    {
      "cite": "100.5",
      "part": "part_i",
      "title": "Conflict of Interest in Casting",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A member may not stamp their own transfiguration of inventory.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Conflict of Interest in Casting",
          "text": "A member may not stamp their own transfiguration of inventory. Any mage, whether of the Conservator or Innovator faction, is strictly prohibited from issuing, validating, or providing the official Guild Seal of Authenticity for any magical transmutation of personal or corporate inventory in which they hold a primary interest. This prohibition serves to prevent the illicit expansion of magical assets and ensures that the integrity of the Autumnwood Accords is not compromised by self-serving arcane manipulation. When a mage performs a transfiguration—be it the expansion of storage dimensions, the alteration of raw materials into finished goods, or the transmutation of currency—the resulting object must be subjected to an independent review by a neutral third party. This third party shall be a clerk or a licensed auditor of the Accords Desk who bears no kinship, financial ties, or magical resonance with the caster.\n\nThe purpose of this regulation is to maintain the stability of the local market and the metaphysical equilibrium of the Midlands. If a mage attempts to bypass this restriction by employing a proxy, such as a familiar or a low-level apprentice, to perform the stamping on their behalf, the act shall be deemed a direct violation of the Accords. See § 100.2 for the limitations on familiar speech and representation. Any inventory found to have been stamped by the primary caster shall be subject to immediate seizure by the Aegis Magi. Furthermore, any goods produced under such circumstances are declared \"Arcane Nullities\" and may not be traded with Goodstyle Artisans or any other recognized merchant body.\n\nIn instances where a large-scale industrial transfiguration is required for the public good, such as the construction of communal infrastructure in the Autumn Wood, the mage in question must petition for a Special Waiver from Archmage Veyra. This waiver shall require a deposit of mana-crystals and a sworn oath of non-profit intent. Without such a waiver, all self-stamped inventory is considered contraband. This provision stands in direct opposition to the Iron Mandate of the Regal Empire, which does not recognize the Guild's authority over private property; however, within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords, the Guild’s seal remains the sole arbiter of magical legitimacy. Violations of this clause may result in a referral to the Paradox Trial for the assessment of magical sealing or pocket-prisonment.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "100.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Delegation to Clerks",
          "text": "Clerks may refuse a stamp for incompleteness without a duel. The administrative staff of the Accords Desk, acting under the authority of the Archmage Sovereign, are granted the discretionary power to withhold the Guild Seal from any application, scroll, or artifact that fails to meet the rigorous standards of completeness. A \"complete\" filing is defined as one that contains all requisite signatures, clearly delineated school-of-magic designations, and a full inventory of reagents used in the casting process. Should a clerk find a filing to be deficient, they are empowered to return the document to the petitioner for correction. This refusal is an administrative action and does not constitute a legal challenge to the mage’s competency or the validity of their research.\n\nUnder no circumstances shall a clerk be compelled to engage in a Paradox Trial or any form of arcane duel simply because a filing was rejected. The role of the clerk is one of verification, not adjudication. If a mage feels that a clerk is acting with undue bias or malice, they are encouraged to file a formal grievance with the Desk Quorum as per § 100.3. However, the clerk's right to refuse a stamp due to missing information, illegible script, or lack of proper factional headers is absolute. This ensures that the flow of magic through the Autumnwood Accords remains orderly and that the Aegis Magi are not burdened with processing incomplete or hazardous data.\n\nFurthermore, clerks are instructed to remain impartial in their assessment of the content. While the Conservators may favor traditionalist structures and the Innovators may push for more expansive permissions, the clerk’s duty is to the letter of the law. If a filing is incomplete, it is incomplete, regardless of the prestige of the mage submitting it. This protection for the clerical staff is vital for the preservation of the Guild’s administrative integrity, as it prevents the \"weaponization of the seal\" by high-ranking mages seeking to bypass the bureaucracy. Any attempt to coerce a clerk into stamping an incomplete document through threats of violence or magical intimidation shall be treated as a high-level offense against the Accords, punishable by immediate referral to the Paradox Trial.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "100.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Foreign Halls and Courtesy",
          "text": "A stamp from another hall is evidence, not law, until recorded. The Mages' Guild recognizes that magic is a global phenomenon and that mages may frequently interact with external jurisdictions, including the Regal Empire, the Silver Flame, or independent research collectives. However, the Autumnwood Accords maintain a strict policy of jurisdictional sovereignty. A seal or stamp issued by a foreign hall, sovereign body, or independent guild is considered \"External Evidence\" only. Such evidence may be presented during a hearing or as part of a petition, but it does not carry the weight of law within the Midlands or any territory governed by the Accords until it has been formally recorded and ratified by the Accords Desk.\n\nTo facilitate the integration of foreign magic into our legal framework, mages may submit \"Petitions of Recognition.\" These petitions require the mage to present the foreign seal along with a certified translation of the underlying laws. Once the Desk Quorum determines that the foreign magic does not violate the core tenets of the Autumnwood Accords—specifically regarding the prohibition of the Unsanctioned Arts—the seal may be entered into the local registry. Until this recording is complete, the foreign seal provides no protections, rights, or privileges to the holder. For instance, a license from a distant province does not grant a mage the right to perform research in the Autumn Wood without first obtaining a local permit.\n\nThis distinction is critical in maintaining the safety of the Midlands. The Guild cannot be held liable for the actions of mages who operate under the laws of the Silver Flame or the Iron Mandate, as those laws are often at odds with our own. If a mage wishes to operate under a foreign law, they must do so at their own risk, and the Guild shall provide no mediation or protection for such actions. Furthermore, any mage who attempts to use a foreign seal to circumvent the Paradox Trial or to bypass the restrictions on Restricted Research shall be subject to immediate detention. The Accords Desk serves as the gatekeeper of magical legitimacy; while we are not isolationist, we are protective of the sanctity of our own seals.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Emergency Sitting",
          "text": "Unscheduled weather is not by itself an emergency sitting. The administration of the Autumnwood Accords is governed by a strict schedule of sessions, designed to ensure that the Archmage Sovereign and the Desk Quorum can provide deliberate and thoughtful oversight of all magical activities. It is a frequent misconception among the lay public, and occasionally among the novice mages of the Innovator faction, that a sudden change in meteorological conditions—such as a localized thunderstorm, a blizzard, or a sudden frost—constitutes an \"Emergency Sitting\" of the Accords Desk. This is explicitly denied. The Desk shall not be forced into a quorum, nor shall any new laws be ratified or urgent petitions be heard, solely on the basis of inclement weather.\n\nThe Accords Desk recognizes that magical weather can be a byproduct of large-scale casting or natural phenomena within the Midlands. However, the purpose of an Emergency Sitting is to address immediate threats to the stability of the realm, such as a rift in the Heartstone, a breach of the Quiet List, or a direct assault by the Iron Legion. Weather, regardless of its intensity, is considered a standard environmental factor and does not meet the threshold of a \"Catastrophic Event.\" Mages who seek to use a storm as a pretext to bypass the standard deliberation period for their research permits will find their petitions denied. The stability of the law requires that it be not swayed by the temporary fluctuations of the atmosphere.\n\nTrue Emergency Sittings may only be called by the Archmage Sovereign or by a majority vote of the Quorum in response to a verified threat to the Accords. Such a declaration must be accompanied by a formal \"Notice of Emergency,\" detailing the specific danger and the duration of the sitting. During a valid Emergency Sitting, the usual procedural requirements for deliberation may be accelerated, but the fundamental protections of the Accords remain in effect. By establishing this rule, the Guild prevents the frivolous use of \"atmospheric emergencies\" to push through reckless innovations or to circumvent the traditionalist protections favored by the Conservators. Stability is the cornerstone of the Autumnwood Accords, and the Desk will not be moved by the wind.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Conflict of Interest in Casting and Delegation to Clerks",
          "text": "§ 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting — A member may not stamp their own transfiguration of inventory, nor delegate such authority without written disclosure to the Desk. This provision prevents self-dealing and ensures accountability in all official acts performed under the Guild seal. Any violation shall be treated as falsification of records and punishable accordingly. § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks — Members may appoint qualified clerks to assist with routine administrative tasks, provided such delegation does not circumvent the spirit of this Code or enable evasion of oversight obligations. The Desk retains ultimate authority over all matters involving official seals, marks, or binding declarations on Guild business. § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy — When operating in territories governed by other lawful entities, members shall respect local customs and regulations unless expressly exempted by the Archmagi. Failure to do so may result in disciplinary action upon return to Autumn Wood jurisdiction. § 100.8 Emergency Sitting — In cases of urgent necessity threatening public safety or Guild integrity, the Desk may convene an emergency session with reduced quorum requirements as deemed appropriate under the circumstances. Such measures shall be strictly limited to addressing the immediate crisis and terminated promptly thereafter.\n\nUnder § 100.5, a member is prohibited from stamping their own transfiguration of inventory without written disclosure to the Desk. This rule prevents self-dealing and ensures accountability in all official acts performed under the Guild seal. Any violation shall be treated as falsification of records and punishable accordingly. § 100.6 allows members to appoint qualified clerks for routine administrative tasks, provided such delegation does not circumvent oversight obligations or enable evasion of duties. The Desk retains ultimate authority over matters involving official seals, marks, or binding declarations on Guild business. As stated in § 100.7, when operating abroad, members must respect local customs and regulations unless exempted by the Archmagi. Violations may lead to disciplinary action upon return to Autumn Wood jurisdiction. Finally, § 100.8 grants the Desk discretion to convene emergency sessions with reduced quorum requirements in cases of urgent necessity threatening public safety or Guild integrity. Such measures shall be strictly limited to addressing the immediate crisis and terminated promptly thereafter.\n\nsee § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild; see § 1.7 Construction; Aggregation of Conduct; see § 100.4 Records of Speech; see § 300.3 Quiet List Procedure",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "100.6",
            "100.7",
            "100.8",
            "100.2",
            "1.7",
            "100.4",
            "300.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting and Delegation to Clerks",
          "text": "No member of the Autumnwood Accords Desk may cast any spell upon themselves, directly or indirectly. This prohibition applies regardless of whether the casting is intended as a mere convenience or as an end unto itself. A caster must always maintain complete detachment from their own transmutation to ensure impartiality in judgment and application. Violation of this rule constitutes serious misconduct subject to immediate disciplinary action under the Accords' penal provisions.\n\nIn cases where technical necessity requires a member's personal involvement, such as when no alternative caster is available or time permits only a single casting, the matter shall be referred to an independent third party for resolution. This third party must possess full credentials and standing within the Guild but remain unaffiliated with either party involved in the dispute. The resulting transmutation shall be deemed valid solely upon review and ratification by the Desk's full membership.\n\nDelegation of casting duties to subordinate clerks or assistants is strictly forbidden under any circumstances. While such individuals may perform ancillary tasks related to spellcasting, they possess no authority whatsoever to execute magical effects independently or on behalf of a superior. Any attempt at unauthorized delegation shall be treated as gross insubordination warranting swift remedial measures consistent with the Accords' disciplinary framework.\n\nThe prohibition against self-casting extends beyond literal application and encompasses all forms of direct benefit derived from one's own magical manipulation. Whether through subtle alteration of personal attributes or more overt transformation, a member must never place themselves in a position where their interests conflict with those of the Guild. Such dual loyalty undermines the foundational principles of trust and accountability upon which the entire organization relies.\n\nTo safeguard against inadvertent violations, each casting site shall be equipped with recording devices capable of capturing both visual and audio data of all proceedings. These recordings serve as an objective record for subsequent review by the Desk in event of any controversy regarding propriety or compliance. Furthermore, members are reminded that their conduct outside official hours remains subject to scrutiny should it bear implications relevant to their duties within the Guild.\n\nThe spirit of this provision transcends its literal terms, embracing a broader ethos of ethical integrity and professional detachment essential for maintaining public confidence in the Guild's operations. As stewards of powerful forces capable of altering reality itself, casters must approach their craft with reverence and restraint unwavering by personal gain or convenience. Only through steadfast adherence to these standards can they preserve both their own honor and the integrity of the magical arts entrusted to them.\n\nsee § 200.1 Licensure Required; see § 400.3 Borrowed Hours: Interest in Time; under § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters regarding delegation limitations. Any questions concerning this section should be directed to the Clerk of the Accords Desk for authoritative interpretation.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "400.3",
            "600.1",
            "100.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
