    },
    {
      "cite": "100.6",
      "part": "part_i",
      "title": "Delegation to Clerks",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Clerks may refuse a stamp for incompleteness without a duel.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Delegation to Clerks",
          "text": "Clerks may refuse a stamp for incompleteness without a duel.\n\nThe administrative integrity of the Autumnwood Accords rests upon the precision of its clerical oversight. To ensure that the magical flow of the Midlands remains regulated and that the records of the Archmage Veyra remain untainted by haste, the Guild hereby establishes the primary authority of the Clerks of the Accords Desk. A Clerk is not merely a passive recorder of events but a gatekeeper of legal finality. When a mage or representative presents a petition, scroll, or license for the Guild Seal, the Clerk is empowered to scrutinize the document for any and all material omissions. If a document is found to be incomplete—whether by lack of proper signature, insufficient description of arcane variables, or failure to meet the stylistic requirements of the Accord—the Clerk shall withhold the stamp.\n\nThis refusal of the stamp is a bureaucratic determination and does not constitute a formal accusation of heresy, nor does it signify a violation of the Paradox Trial protocols. Consequently, a mage whose petition is rejected for incompleteness may not demand a trial by arcane duel as a means of coercion to obtain the seal. The Clerk's refusal is a procedural pause, not a judicial ruling. The mage in question is expected to rectify the omissions and resubmit the document for further review. Should a mage attempt to initiate a duel based on a Clerk’s refusal to stamp a flawed document, such an action shall be deemed a violation of the Accords, as it seeks to use the violence of the Aegis Magi to bypass the necessary diligence of the Desk.\n\nThe Clerk is further protected from harassment in the performance of these duties. No mage, regardless of their standing within the Innovator or Conservator factions, may threaten a Clerk with administrative reprisal or physical harm for upholding the standards of completeness. The Clerk serves the stability of the Hall, and the stability of the Hall requires that every seal issued be perfect. If a Clerk determines that a document is perpetually unfixable or falls outside the scope of the Accords, they may issue a formal \"Notice of Non-Conformity,\" which serves as the final administrative word on that specific petition. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Foreign Halls and Courtesy",
          "text": "A stamp from another hall is evidence, not law, until recorded.\n\nThe Autumnwood Accords maintain a strict jurisdiction over the magic practiced within the Midlands and under the mediation of Archmage Veyra. While the Guild acknowledges that other magical bodies, independent research circles, and external entities may possess their own seals of authority, these marks do not carry the weight of Accord law by virtue of their existence alone. A stamp from a foreign hall—whether it be a provincial coven, a radical research cell, or a distant sovereign body—is regarded by the Accords Desk as mere evidence of a transaction or a prior agreement, but it does not constitute a binding legal mandate within our jurisdiction.\n\nFor a foreign seal to be recognized as having any legal standing within the Autumnwood Accords, it must be formally presented to the Desk and entered into the official registry. Until such time as a Clerk has performed the Rite of Recording and appended the internal Guild notation, the foreign mark is treated as a decorative element or a private contract. Any mage who attempts to enforce a foreign mandate by simply presenting a stamped parchment without first seeking its recording shall find that the Aegis Magi are instructed to ignore such claims. The Accords do not automatically inherit the laws of neighbors; we do not accept the \"law of the land\" as \"law of the Hall\" without a deliberative period where the Conservators and Innovators may assess the impact of such a law on the stability of the Midlands.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild does not grant \"Courtesy Recognition\" to any seal that is found to be in direct opposition to the primary tenets of the Accords. If a foreign hall issues a seal that permits the use of Forbidden Schools as defined by the Conservators, or fails to provide the oversight required by the Innovators for restricted research, the Desk may refuse to record the seal entirely. In such cases, the foreign mark is not only evidence but is explicitly noted as \"Contradictory.\" Mages who rely on such seals for the conduct of their affairs do so at their own peril, for the Accords will not provide protection or mediation for actions taken under the authority of an unrecorded and unverified foreign power. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Emergency Sitting",
          "text": "Unscheduled weather is not by itself an emergency sitting.\n\nThe continuity of the Autumnwood Accords requires a clear distinction between atmospheric phenomena and administrative crises. It is a common misconception among the lay populace, and occasionally among the junior initiates of the Innovator faction, that a significant meteorological event—such as a localized mana-storm, a heavy blizzard, or a sudden atmospheric rift—constitutes an automatic \"Emergency Sitting\" of the Accords Desk. This Codex clarifies that weather, regardless of its severity or its arcane origin, does not automatically suspend the standard operating procedures of the Guild or trigger an immediate emergency session of the Council.\n\nAn Emergency Sitting may only be convened by the Archmage Sovereign or by a unanimous declaration of the Aegis Magi in the event of a direct threat to the Hall’s physical integrity or a breach of the Quiet List that poses an immediate risk of catastrophic paradox. While the Hall may be physically impacted by weather, the legal machinery of the Accords remains in motion unless a formal declaration is made. Mages may not use \"bad weather\" as a legal pretext to bypass standard filing deadlines, to avoid mandatory reporting of research, or to seek immediate, unvetted permissions for high-risk casting. The Desk will continue to process petitions, and the requirements for the Guild Seal will remain unchanged, even should the Midlands be shrouded in fog or struck by lightning.\n\nIn the event of a genuine emergency, the Archmage Veyra shall issue a \"Decree of Suspension,\" which will specify the duration and scope of the emergency sitting. Until such a decree is issued and recorded, all mages are expected to adhere to the standard protocols of the Accords. Any mage who attempts to perform unauthorized actions under the guise of a \"weather-induced emergency\" shall be subject to review by the Conservators. The Guild recognizes that the Midlands is a place of volatile magic, but the law must be a constant. Stability is the goal of the Accords; if the law shifted with every change in the wind, the Accords would be as ephemeral as the magic they seek to govern. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy",
          "text": "§ 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy — When a Hall sits in another Sovereign's territory, the local laws still apply unless exempted by treaty. In such cases, the visiting Hall must observe the host's customs with respect. Failure to do so may result in sanctions, up to and including expulsion from the meeting.\n\nThis provision ensures that no Hall can impose its will upon others merely by virtue of hosting a gathering. It also protects against potential conflicts arising from differing legal traditions across regions. By requiring adherence to local norms, the Accords promote harmony among diverse magical communities while safeguarding each Sovereign's autonomy.\n\nClerks assigned to foreign sessions shall be mindful of these obligations, consulting with appropriate authorities as needed. They bear responsibility for educating guests on relevant customs and procedures. Negligence in this regard may lead to disciplinary action against the Clerk personally.\n\nUltimately, § 100.7 serves as a reminder that even within the grand scope of Arcane Law, basic civility remains paramount. The strength of our collective endeavor depends upon mutual respect and understanding between all participants. Let us strive always to uphold these ideals in spirit if not letter, for they form the bedrock upon which true cooperation may flourish.\n\n\"Respect foreign lands as one's own; let courtesy guide our interactions across boundaries.\" — Archmage Theron",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "100.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Text",
          "text": "PAGE § 100.8 Emergency Sitting — In cases where the safety of persons or property is immediately threatened, the Desk may convene an emergency session outside normal business hours with minimal notice. Such sessions shall be limited to addressing only those matters directly related to the crisis at hand and shall adjourn no later than seven days after commencement unless extended by unanimous vote. All decisions reached during such sittings must be ratified within thirty days or they stand void ab initio unless reaffirmed by subsequent action of the full body. Emergency sittings are reserved exclusively for dire circumstances requiring prompt collective response; routine administrative issues must await regular proceedings. The Presiding Archmage shall issue written notice detailing the nature of the emergency justifying deviation from standard protocol. Minutes of such meetings shall be kept confidential pending formal review and public release in accordance with established transparency guidelines. This expedited process is designed to enable swift action when time-sensitive threats arise, balancing efficiency with due process protections mandated by our shared values. See § 100.3 Quorum of the Desk for additional requirements regarding valid gatherings during extraordinary periods. The necessity for rapid response does not excuse compliance with fundamental fairness principles enshrined in this Codex and all laws thereof. Archmage Veyra's directive remains paramount: act decisively while upholding our highest ideals even under pressure.\"",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.8",
            "100.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Delegation to Clerks and Foreign Halls",
          "text": "§ 100.6 Delegation to Clerks — In the exercise of their duties, Archmages or Aegis Magi may delegate authority to a Clerk for matters of routine administration. This delegation shall be in writing and specify the scope of powers granted. A Clerk acting under such delegation has full authority to issue stamps, seals, or certifications within that defined scope, but no further. If a matter falls outside the delegated authority, the Clerk must decline it without engaging in dispute; any challenge to their decision is subject to review by the Desk itself, not individual mages.\n\n§ 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy — The provisions of this Code apply to all persons within Autumnwood Hall or under its jurisdiction. However, courtesy dictates that when a foreign mage enters the premises for official business, they shall be afforded certain protections commensurate with their status. This includes protection from assault or interference during sanctioned activities, as well as reasonable access to necessary resources. Conversely, mages operating outside of Guild-sanctioned events may not use magic within Hall limits without explicit permission, lest they disrupt the delicate balance maintained by the Accords. Any breach of this courtesy shall be addressed through diplomatic channels rather than immediate sanction.\n\n§ 100.8 Emergency Sitting — In cases where the normal quorum cannot be assembled due to extraordinary circumstances—such as widespread illness, travel delays, or natural disaster—the Desk may convene an emergency sitting with a reduced number of participants. Such sittings shall still adhere to all fundamental principles of fair procedure and deliberation. Decisions made during an emergency sitting carry the same weight as those reached through full process, but only so long as they pertain directly to the crisis at hand. Any subsequent review must occur within seven days unless extended by mutual consent of the parties involved.\n\nThese provisions balance efficiency with fairness, allowing the Guild to respond swiftly to changing conditions while safeguarding against abuse of power. The delegation system empowers trusted administrators without compromising oversight, and the courtesy standards foster cooperation between diverse magical traditions. Ultimately, they reflect the enduring commitment of the Autumnwood Accords to maintain order amidst complexity—a testament to the ingenuity and dedication of those who uphold this vital institution.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "100.7",
            "100.8"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "c4e5b35e7f9500c2",
      "refs": [
        "1.6",
        "100.3",
        "100.7",
        "100.8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "100.7",
      "part": "part_i",
      "title": "Foreign Halls and Courtesy",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A stamp from another hall is evidence, not law, until recorded.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Foreign Halls and Courtesy",
          "text": "A stamp from another hall is evidence, not law, until recorded. The Autumnwood Accords recognize the existence of various arcane jurisdictions, some of which may operate under disparate philosophies or entirely different legal frameworks. However, the sovereignty of the Autumnwood Accords within the Midlands remains absolute. Any documentation, decree, or magical certification bearing the seal, stamp, or sigil of a foreign hall—whether it be a sovereign state, a nomadic circle, or a private academy—shall be treated by the Accords Desk as mere evidence of a fact, rather than as a binding legal mandate. Such marks do not grant the bearer automatic privileges, nor do they exempt the bearer from the oversight of Archmage Veyra. The mere presence of an external seal does not constitute a waiver of Guild jurisdiction.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords Desk shall not recognize the validity of foreign laws unless those laws have been formally submitted to the Desk and recorded in the Great Ledger of Externalities. This recording process requires a formal petition from the respective hall, a period of observation by the Aegis Magi to ensure the law does not infringe upon the stability of the Midlands, and a final decree of acceptance or rejection by the presiding Archmage. Until such a recording is finalized, any foreign stamp is functionally equivalent to a personal letter; it may be presented as supporting testimony in a dispute, but it shall not be used as a basis for demanding a specific magical outcome or a violation of the Accords.\n\nThe Desk notes that the interaction between the Guild and external entities, such as the Goodstyle Artisans, often involves the exchange of such marks. While the Guild values these associations, the administrative integrity of the Accords requires that no external influence bypasses the standard of internal review. Mages attempting to utilize a foreign seal to circumvent the restrictions placed upon them by the Conservators or to bypass the research permissions of the Innovators shall be subject to immediate review. See § 100.5 for prohibitions on self-validation. Any mage found attempting to treat a foreign seal as a substitute for a Guild Seal of Authentic Casting shall be flagged for a potential Paradox Trial.\n\nFinally, courtesy is extended to all foreign halls in matters of diplomatic exchange, but such courtesy does not extend to legal parity. The Accords Desk shall not be compelled to provide stamps, transit clearances, or research permits based solely on the prestige of a foreign hall’s mark. All requests must be processed according to the standard procedures of the Autumnwood Accords. See § 1.5 regarding the status of rival laws and the exclusion of non-Guild tender.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "1.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Emergency Sitting",
          "text": "Unscheduled weather is not by itself an emergency sitting. The administrative functions of the Autumnwood Accords are designed to withstand the common fluctuations of the magical and physical environment. Therefore, the occurrence of localized storms, heavy precipitation, arcane fog, or standard seasonal shifts within the Autumn Wood shall not constitute a valid reason for the convening of an emergency sitting of the Accords Desk. The Desk is expected to maintain its operational capacity during all standard weather conditions, and the failure of a clerk or a presiding Archmage to perform their duties due to \"inclement weather\" shall be viewed as a breach of administrative conduct.\n\nAn emergency sitting may only be convened when a threat to the stability of the Midlands or the integrity of the Accords is identified as immediate and severe. Such threats include, but are not limited to, the opening of a major rift, an unauthorized Titan growth on Markop, or a direct violation of the Quiet List by a public entity. In these instances, Archmage Veyra may declare an emergency, authorizing the Aegis Magi to take immediate enforcement action. The declaration of an emergency sitting requires a specific filing of intent, detailing the nature of the crisis and the specific powers being invoked. This prevents the frivolous use of emergency powers to bypass the standard deliberation processes of the Conservators and Innovators.\n\nFurthermore, the Desk shall not be disrupted by the personal inconveniences of its members. A mage claiming that a specific weather pattern interferes with their personal casting style or \"mood\" does not possess the standing to request a suspension of standard proceedings. The Accords are a body of law, not a sanctuary of comfort. If the weather creates a physical barrier to the Hall, the Desk shall remain in session via remote arcane transmission, provided the transmission is secured against interception by the Regal Empire or the Silver Flame.\n\nThe distinction between a \"natural\" occurrence and a \"magical\" emergency is vital. While the Guild acknowledges the power of the elements, it does not grant the elements the right to dictate the schedule of the law. Only a catastrophic breach of the Accords—one that threatens the very fabric of the Midlands—warrants the suspension of regular order. See § 1.5 for the status of the Iron Mandate and other external legal bodies which do not govern these proceedings. Any mage attempting to manufacture an emergency sitting through the intentional manipulation of the weather shall be referred to the Council of Peers for a Paradox Trial.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Emergency Sitting Procedure and Validity",
          "text": "Under § 100.7, the Desk may recognize the courtesy of foreign halls without formally adopting their rules. This provision was enacted to facilitate cooperation across regional boundaries while preserving the autonomy of each Hall's internal governance. The Archmage Sovereign retains sole discretion to accept or reject any external certification based on merit and compatibility with our core tenets.\n\nAn emergency sitting is declared when a matter requiring immediate attention arises beyond normal operating hours. Such sittings are convened by order of the Archmage Sovereign or their designate, following strict procedural safeguards outlined in § 100.8. All participants must be duly appointed representatives with full authority to bind their respective organizations. Minutes must be recorded and filed within twenty-four hours to ensure transparency and accountability.\n\nDecisions made during emergency sittings carry the same weight as those rendered at regular sessions, provided all statutory requirements were met. However, any action taken under these exceptional circumstances must be ratified by a subsequent full meeting unless explicitly waived by unanimous consent of interested parties. Failure to comply with this ratification requirement renders the contested resolution null and void ab initio.\n\nThe validity of emergency proceedings shall not be challenged on grounds of convenience or expediency alone. Only substantial irregularities affecting due process will suffice to invalidate such actions upon proper proof being presented. Burden of persuasion rests entirely upon those seeking to contest the legality of an emergency decision, who must demonstrate clear and convincing evidence of procedural impropriety.\n\nThis framework balances operational necessity with constitutional safeguards, allowing the Desk to respond swiftly to unforeseen circumstances while maintaining fidelity to established principles. It reflects our commitment to both effective governance and protection of member rights in all contexts. Any ambiguities arising from this provision shall be resolved by reference to underlying values of fairness, reasonableness, and good faith.\n\nsee § 100.8 Emergency Sitting; see § 200.5 Guest Casters; see § 400.3 Borrowed Hours; see § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "100.7",
            "100.8",
            "200.5",
            "400.3",
            "600.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy; Emergency Sitting Procedure and Validity",
          "text": "Where the Accords are in force within the Autumn Wood, the authority of this Desk is plenary and exclusive unless otherwise provided herein. Nothing in these provisions shall be construed to diminish or impair the sovereignty of any other Hall recognized under international agreement or treaty. A seal affixed by a foreign entity carries evidentiary weight only upon subsequent ratification by this Board; absent such validation, it remains mere persuasion rather than binding mandate. This principle stands irrespective of the relative power dynamics between Halls at any given moment and must be observed scrupulously to maintain orderly relations across jurisdictions.\n\nIn extraordinary circumstances where normal convening mechanisms fail or prove impracticable due to force majeure or deliberate obstruction by interested parties, this Desk retains inherent authority to call an Emergency Sitting pursuant to § 100.8. Such gatherings shall follow abbreviated procedures designed for expedited resolution while preserving fundamental fairness and accuracy in outcomes. Quorum requirements may be relaxed temporarily but never below fifty percent of voting members present in person or via secure digital linkage. Minutes produced at emergency sessions remain fully binding provided they accurately reflect all actions taken and votes cast without omission or distortion.\n\nThe validity of any Emergency Sitting shall be subject to challenge within ten days following adjournment, upon grounds strictly limited to procedural irregularity affecting substantive rights rather than mere technical defects unconnected with final results. Challenges must be brought in good faith supported by sworn testimony and corroborating documentation where feasible. Unsubstantiated allegations lacking evidentiary foundation will be summarily dismissed without prejudice to future recourse if warranted. Decisions rendered during emergency proceedings stand unless overturned by subsequent full session deliberation following proper notice and opportunity for rebuttal.\n\nThis framework balances operational necessity with due process safeguards, enabling responsive governance while respecting established norms of democratic accountability. It acknowledges the exceptional nature of crisis situations requiring swift action without sacrificing core values essential to legitimate rule under the Accords. Through disciplined application of these principles, this Desk ensures continuity of function even when ordinary channels falter, serving as both shield and sword in defense of lawful order amidst chaos.\n\nsee § 100.8 Emergency Sitting; see § 100.3 Quorum Requirements; see § 200.4 Suspension Procedures; see § 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After Explosions. Under § 1.7 Construction, ambiguities herein shall be resolved in favor of maintaining stability and protecting public welfare without unduly burdening individual liberties or stifling innovation. As provided by § 1.6 Definitions, \"force majeure\" encompasses natural disasters, civil unrest, pandemics, and other unforeseen events beyond reasonable control. \"Secure digital linkage\" refers to encrypted video/audio transmission systems approved annually by this Desk for remote participation purposes. Failure to comply with emergency protocols may result in sanctions under applicable sections including but not limited to § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items and § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Laboratories.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "100.8",
            "100.3",
            "200.4",
            "500.3",
            "1.7",
            "1.6",
            "300.5",
            "400.2",
            "100.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy; Emergency Sitting Procedure and Validity — Continued",
          "text": "Whereas the Sovereign Hall is the primary seat of legislative authority, this Codex shall be deemed binding upon all member mages regardless of their individual hall affiliations or geographic location within the Accords' jurisdiction. Each hall retains autonomy to enact supplementary regulations tailored to local conditions, provided such rules do not contravene the fundamental principles set forth herein nor infringe upon the rights of other halls under § 100.7. Inter-hall disputes regarding regulatory interpretation shall be submitted to mediation by the Archmage Sovereign or their designated representative, whose ruling shall carry binding weight unless overturned by a two-thirds vote in the next General Assembly (§ 102.5).\n\nIn exigent circumstances threatening public safety or magical equilibrium, an Emergency Sitting may be convened at the discretion of the Presiding Officer or upon petition signed by no less than five-hundred registered mages. Such meetings shall follow strict procedural guidelines: notice must be issued via enchanted beacon and direct communication to all known representatives at least one hour prior; quorum requires physical presence of two-thirds of voting members or their duly appointed proxies; and only motions directly addressing the emergency may be considered. Deliberations shall occur without formal debate, with final decisions made by a simple majority vote recorded in the Official Minutes (§ 103.2). Any resolution passed under Emergency Sitting powers automatically expires upon adjournment unless reaffirmed within thirty days through regular legislative process. Failure to adhere to these protocols renders any purported Emergency Sitting null and void ab initio, leaving affected parties free to challenge subsequent actions based on procedural impropriety.\n\nThe spirit of § 100.7 demands both respect for institutional diversity and unity in preserving the common good. Mages are reminded that while local customs may vary, the core tenets of responsible magical practice remain universal across all halls and regions bound by these Accords. Through cooperation, mutual understanding, and adherence to established norms, we shall continue to foster an environment wherein magic thrives safely for generations yet unborn. So mote it be!",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.7",
            "102.5",
            "103.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Emergency Sitting Procedure and Validity — Continued",
          "text": "Where an emergency sitting has been convened pursuant to § 100.7, the presiding officer shall immediately summon all members known to be present within the jurisdictional bounds of the hall. Such summons may be delivered via any reasonably expeditious means, including but not limited to messengers, telecommunication devices, or magical summons spells. The quorum required for such a meeting is reduced to fifty-one percent (51%) of all active members of the governing body, rather than the usual two-thirds majority mandated by § 100.3. This reduction is deemed necessary to ensure timely decision-making amidst pressing circumstances.\n\nAll matters brought before an emergency sitting shall be strictly limited to those directly related to the declared state of emergency. Any proposal or motion unrelated thereto may be summarily ruled out of order by the chair without debate. The presiding officer retains enhanced authority during such proceedings, including but not limited to suspending standard rules of procedure and compelling testimony under oath from any individual deemed relevant to the crisis at hand.\n\nDecisions rendered during an emergency sitting shall remain binding upon all members unless successfully challenged within seven days thereof through a formal appeal process outlined in § 500.1 et seq. Any member wishing to contest the validity of such rulings must present evidence demonstrating either procedural impropriety or factual error that materially affected the outcome. Failure to timely object constitutes acceptance of the decision as final and conclusive under the doctrine of res judicata.\n\nIt shall be incumbent upon the clerk of the hall to maintain comprehensive minutes of all emergency sittings, documenting not only the substance of debates and votes but also any extraordinary actions taken by the presiding officer in their sole discretion. These records shall be kept separate from ordinary sessional journals and stored under heightened security protocols until such time as they may be reviewed by a special committee convened for that purpose.\n\nWhere multiple emergencies arise concurrently or sequentially, each shall be addressed on its own merits through a distinct emergency sitting. The chair retains broad latitude in determining the scope and duration of any given proceeding based upon evolving conditions. However, no single emergency sitting shall exceed seventy-two (72) consecutive hours without an intervening recess to allow for proper deliberation and reflection by all concerned parties.\n\nThe provisions herein regarding emergency sittings are intended solely as a temporary expedient measure to be employed only when absolutely necessary. They do not alter or supersede any existing constitutional mandates, statutory requirements, or fundamental rights enjoyed by the membership. All actions taken under this authority shall remain subject to subsequent ratification or reversal through normal legislative channels once regular proceedings resume following resolution of the emergency condition triggering their invocation.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.7",
            "100.3",
            "500.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
