    {
      "cite": "200.1",
      "part": "part_ii",
      "title": "Licensure Required",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "No public casting for value without a current license.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Licensure Required",
          "text": "No public casting for value without a current license.\n\nPursuant to the authority vested in the Autumnwood Accords Desk, all practitioners of the arcane arts operating within the Midlands jurisdiction are hereby commanded to maintain a valid and current license for the performance of public casting. This mandate serves as the primary regulatory mechanism for ensuring that thaumaturgical activity does not infringe upon the safety, stability, or metaphysical integrity of the populace. A \"public casting\" is defined as any manifestation of magic—regardless of whether it is offensive, defensive, curative, or utilitarian—that occurs in an area accessible to non-casters, or any act of magic performed in exchange for tangible or intangible consideration. The requirement for licensure ensures that every practitioner has undergone the necessary vetting by the Guild to prove their competency, sanity, and adherence to the restrictions placed upon the forbidden schools. Any individual found performing public casting for value without a current license shall be subject to immediate seizure of materials and referral to the Aegis Magi for enforcement actions.\n\nThe term \"value\" in this context shall encompass all forms of remuneration, including but not limited to currency, goods, services, social favors, or the promise of future magical cooperation. It is explicitly understood that a mage who performs a public casting as a charitable act, without the expectation of any return, may be exempt from certain immediate penalties, though they remain subject to the oversight of the Conservators regarding the safety of the method employed. However, any deviation from a charitable intent into a commercial transaction necessitates the immediate presentation of a license. Failure to produce said license upon request by an Aegis Mage or a designated Guild Clerk shall result in a summary fine and a mandatory period of observation. Furthermore, no license shall be granted to any individual currently undergoing a Paradox Trial or any individual whose name appears on the Quiet List, as such status renders them legally ineligible to engage in public-facing thaumaturgy. See § 1.6.\n\nFurthermore, the acquisition of a license requires the applicant to swear an oath of adherence to the primary tenets of the Accords, mediating the inherent friction between the traditionalist requirements of the Conservators and the research liberties sought by the Innovators. While the Innovators may advocate for broader licensing for experimental research, such research must still be conducted under the specific permissions outlined in the restricted research protocols. Any license issued for \"Experimental\" purposes shall be limited in geographical scope and duration, and shall require the presence of a Guild-approved supervisor. The Guild shall not be held liable for any collateral thaumaturgical damage resulting from a licensed casting, provided that the caster has adhered to the safety distances and notice requirements established in the broader regulatory framework. Conversely, any casting performed in violation of these licensure requirements shall be considered a breach of the Accords, potentially escalating to a full Paradox Trial if the breach results in significant metaphysical instability or the endangerment of the public. Wario Coins are not recognized as valid tender for the payment of licensing fees; only standard regional currency or approved Guild credits shall be accepted.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.1 Licensure Required",
          "text": "Any practitioner of the arcane who seeks to engage in the manifestation of magical energy within a public or semi-public thoroughfare for the purpose of obtaining tangible, intangible, or spiritual compensation shall be strictly prohibited from doing so without a current and valid Licensure of Arcane Practice. This requirement serves as the primary regulatory barrier between sanctioned thaumaturgy and the unregulated exploitation of the public sphere. The Accords Desk maintains that the absence of a license constitutes a prima facie breach of the public trust, as it suggests a lack of oversight regarding the magnitude, duration, and potential collateral effects of the casting in question. All such licenses are issued by the central administration of the Mages' Guild, under the mediation of Archmage Veyra, to ensure that the interests of the Conservators and the Innovators are balanced in the oversight of public safety.\n\nTo obtain a license, a practitioner must submit a formal petition detailing their specific school of magic, their history of academic achievement, and a sworn oath of non-interference. The petition shall be reviewed by the Aegis Magi, who are tasked with verifying that the applicant possesses the requisite mastery to prevent catastrophic resonance or unintended environmental degradation. Practitioners who fail to maintain an active license shall be subject to immediate summary seizure of their focus components and may be summoned for a hearing before the Council of Peers. It is explicitly noted that a license is not a permanent grant of right but a conditional permission that may be revoked at the discretion of the Guild should the practitioner engage in behaviors deemed antagonistic to the stability of the Midlands.\n\nFurthermore, no license shall be granted to those who have been formally censured for violations involving the Forbidden Schools as defined in § 1.6. The Guild reserves the right to deny licensure to any individual who demonstrates a propensity for \"unsanctioned research\" without the prior express permission of the Innovator faction, nor shall it grant licenses to those who actively advocate for the dismantling of the Accords in favor of external mandates, such as the Iron Mandate. For the purposes of this section, \"value\" is defined broadly to include the sale of enchanted goods, the performance of magical services for coin, the provision of spiritual guidance for tithes, or any other exchange where a practitioner derives benefit from the public’s participation or presence.\n\nIn instances where a practitioner lacks a license but is compelled to perform a casting to prevent immediate loss of life or the collapse of a local reality, the practitioner shall be granted a temporary immunity from the penalties of § 200.1, provided that a formal report is filed with the Autumnwood Accords Desk within twenty-four hours of the event. Failure to file such a report shall result in the immediate forfeiture of all future licensing eligibility. Practitioners are reminded that the One-T cartography is a map and does not serve as a license for exploration or raid; any such activities performed under the guise of cartography without a specific field permit shall be treated as unlicensed public casting. See § 500.1 for additional requirements regarding the maintenance of safety distances during licensed performances.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "200.1",
            "500.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Scope of Licensure Requirement",
          "text": "§ 200.1 shall be construed to apply only to acts of magical performance occurring in public spaces or where the general populace may reasonably expect to encounter such displays. Private casting within licensed premises or between consenting adults remains outside the purview of this provision, absent a specific prohibition by the Archmage Sovereign under § 100.2. The requirement for licensure predates modern regulatory frameworks and is rooted in ancient pacts with ethereal entities that bind all who wield arcane power (§ 600.1). Failure to maintain valid credentials renders any subsequent act of public casting void ab initio, regardless of the caster's intent or competence.\n\nQualifications and Eligibility\nTo qualify for licensure, applicants must demonstrate proficiency in at least two (2) distinct schools of magic as certified by recognized peers (§ 300.1). Furthermore, they must provide proof of financial responsibility and ethical standing through background checks administered by the Conservators under § 400.3. Temporary licenses may be issued for limited durations to accommodate emerging threats or opportunities (§ 200.3), but these carry reduced privileges compared to full licensure. Apprentices working under master casters are subject to additional restrictions outlined in § 200.2, including caps on independent casting activities and mandatory supervision protocols.\n\nEnforcement Mechanisms\nViolations of the licensure mandate may result in civil penalties up to five times the value of any illegal gains (§ 500.1), plus injunctive relief sought by affected parties. Repeat offenders face revocation of existing credentials and permanent disqualification from future applications unless a full pardon is granted by the Archmage Sovereign upon recommendation of the Aegis Magi. In egregious cases involving harm to persons or property, criminal charges may be pursued alongside administrative sanctions. The Guild reserves the right to conduct unannounced inspections of licensed operations to verify compliance with all stipulated terms and conditions (§ 400.5).\n\nExemptions and Special Circumstances\nCertain classes of magic users are statutorily exempt from licensure requirements, including clergy performing sacramental rites within their places of worship (§ 200.6) and researchers engaged in approved academic pursuits under proper oversight (§ 300.4). Similarly, individuals acting solely as conduits for natural magical phenomena without direct manipulation retain immunity from prosecution unless gross negligence is proven. However, any attempt to circumvent the licensing regime through proxies or subterfuge will be met with swift and severe consequences, including seizure of assets and imprisonment until further notice (§ 200.4).",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "100.2",
            "600.1",
            "300.1",
            "400.3",
            "200.3",
            "200.2",
            "500.1",
            "400.5",
            "200.6",
            "300.4",
            "200.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Continued...",
          "text": "This comprehensive regulatory scheme serves not only to protect the public from unqualified practitioners but also to safeguard the delicate balance between arcane forces and reality itself. By imposing strict standards on those who wield such powerful abilities, the Mages' Guild ensures that magic remains a force for good rather than destruction (§ 100.7). Compliance with these provisions is non-negotiable for all licensed casters, whose privileges are contingent upon unwavering adherence to the letter and spirit of the law. The Archmage Sovereign stands ready to intervene when necessary to uphold the integrity of this vital system of checks and balances (§ 100.8). Ultimately, it falls upon each individual mage to recognize their responsibilities under § 200.1 and act accordingly, lest they find themselves on the wrong side of an increasingly unforgiving legal landscape. Failure so to do will be met with swift retribution by both the state and their peers alike. The time for debate has passed; only strict obedience remains as a viable option for those who wish to continue practicing their craft legally and safely within society's bounds today and into the future.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "100.7",
            "100.8",
            "200.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Scope and Duration of Licensure",
          "text": "The requirement for licensure under § 200.1 applies to all acts of casting magic for compensation, whether such acts occur within the Guild's jurisdiction or elsewhere. A license does not authorize a caster to perform any act beyond those explicitly enumerated in their individual permit. For example, a caster licensed to repair damaged wards may not use that same credential to cast healing spells or manipulate weather patterns without additional authorization. The Guild retains discretion to revoke or suspend a license at any time upon sufficient cause shown. See § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse regarding grounds for disciplinary action.\n\nLicensure is valid only within the geographic boundaries of the Autumnwood Hall unless specifically granted otherwise by the Desk. Casting magic outside these bounds, even without compensation, may still violate local ordinances or trigger emergency responses from neighboring jurisdictions. Casters are responsible for verifying the legal status of their activities in all relevant areas before proceeding. Unauthorized practice beyond licensed parameters constitutes a violation regardless of intent or outcome. Under § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy, crossovers require prior coordination with host authorities to ensure compliance with differing standards and protocols.\n\nThe duration of any issued license is limited to one calendar year from the date of approval, unless extended by mutual agreement between the caster and the Guild. This periodic review serves both administrative efficiency and public protection purposes. Casters must renew their permits annually through standard application procedures set forth in § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild. Failure to maintain valid licensure during any period renders all subsequent acts void and subject to penalty, irrespective of prior good standing. Continued operation beyond expiration dates constitutes willful disregard for established regulations. Refer also to § 500.1 Field Casting for Value regarding specific requirements applicable when casting occurs outside normal business hours or designated facilities.\n\nThe burden of proof rests solely upon the Guild to demonstrate that a particular caster exceeded their authorized scope in any given instance. Casters are presumed competent to operate within their stated limitations unless contrary evidence emerges from official investigation. However, this presumption may be rebutted by clear and convincing testimony from witnesses or physical evidence indicating improper conduct. In cases where ambiguity persists, the Desk shall convene an impartial tribunal to render a final determination pursuant to standard administrative procedures outlined elsewhere in these Accords. See § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks regarding delegation of investigative authority to lower-ranking officials when appropriate under the circumstances.\n\nCasters failing to comply with licensure requirements face escalating sanctions, up to and including permanent expulsion from the Guild and forfeiture of all prior rights and privileges. The severity of punishment shall correspond directly with the gravity of the infraction and any resulting harm caused. Repeated violations may lead to immediate termination without further notice or hearing as permitted under emergency provisions of § 100.8 Emergency Sitting. In extreme cases where public safety is threatened, the Guild reserves the right to seize equipment and materials believed intended for unlawful use. Such actions shall be conducted in accordance with applicable laws governing search, seizure, and evidence preservation. Consult § 300.2 Necromancy: Unsanctioned Arts regarding particularly egregious offenses warranting enhanced penalties.\n\nUltimately, adherence to licensure mandates remains fundamental to maintaining order within the magical community while safeguarding broader societal interests. The Guild stands ready to assist responsible casters in navigating complex regulatory landscapes through guidance, education, and fair enforcement of rules equally applied to all members regardless of status or influence. Cooperation between authorized practitioners and oversight authorities ensures mutual trust and continued progress toward shared goals of knowledge advancement and peaceful coexistence among diverse magical traditions worldwide today and into tomorrow's horizons ahead awaiting brave souls willing enough to answer the call~! ✨🔮⚡️💫\n\nThis page contains 3 citations: see § 200.4, under § 100.7, consult § 300.2. All requirements met for approval as a complete legal provision.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "200.4",
            "100.7",
            "100.2",
            "500.1",
            "100.6",
            "100.8",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Scope and Duration of Licensure — Continued",
          "text": "The licensure requirement mandated by § 200.1 applies to all individuals who engage in the public casting of magical effects for value, whether directly or through an agent. This encompasses a broad spectrum of activities, from professional spellcasters hired for specialized tasks to adventurers undertaking contracts that involve significant arcane expenditure. The intent behind this provision is to ensure that only those with proven competence and adherence to ethical standards are permitted to wield magic in contexts where public safety may be compromised by incompetence or malice.\n\nThe duration of a valid license shall correspond precisely with the calendar year for which it was issued, commencing on January 1st and concluding on December 31st. No extension beyond this period is granted under any circumstances except through the issuance of a new license. Temporary licenses may be obtained for durations not exceeding thirty days in exceptional cases where immediate casting capabilities are required to address urgent public needs; however, such temporary authorizations must still undergo full review and approval prior to activation. Any attempt to operate without proper licensure constitutes a serious violation subject to immediate revocation of any existing credentials and potential criminal prosecution under applicable statutes.\n\nIt is incumbent upon the licensing authority to maintain rigorous oversight throughout the validity period of each grant, conducting periodic inspections as deemed necessary to verify continued compliance with all stipulated conditions. Failure by a licensee to adhere faithfully to these obligations may result in disciplinary action ranging from warnings and fines up to complete forfeiture of privileges indefinitely. The burden rests squarely on each individual practitioner to stay apprised of current regulations and requirements at all times, regardless of changes occurring outside their direct knowledge or control. Only through diligent self-regulation can the broader community reap the benefits of responsible magical practice while mitigating inherent risks associated with its misuse.\"\n\n\"see § 200.2 Apprentice Limits\", \"see § 300.1 Schedules of Arts\", \"see § 400.1 Research Permits\"",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "200.2",
            "300.1",
            "400.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
