    {
      "cite": "200.2",
      "part": "part_ii",
      "title": "Apprentice Limits",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "An apprentice may fetch, not bind. Ink that recants is not a master's signature.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Apprentice Limits",
          "text": "The hierarchy of thaumaturgical initiation is a matter of strict administrative oversight to ensure the preservation of the weave and the safety of the uninitiated. Pursuant to the foundational principles established in the Autumnwood Accords, the status of an apprentice is defined by a limitation of agency. An apprentice may fetch, not bind. This distinction is not merely a matter of preference but a fundamental legal boundary regarding the capacity to anchor permanent metaphysical alterations to reality. While an apprentice may be tasked with the gathering of components, the retrieval of reagents, the transcription of preliminary notations, and the maintenance of focus-circles, they are strictly prohibited from initiating any binding contract, permanent ward, or irreversible transmutation. Any attempt by an apprentice to execute a binding spell shall be viewed as a breach of the Accords and may result in immediate referral to the Aegis Magi for corrective intervention.\n\nFurthermore, the validity of a master’s decree is tied to the physical and metaphysical integrity of the signature. Ink that recants is not a master's signature. This clause addresses the frequent occurrence of \"reverting inks\"—substances that shift their chemical or magical composition over time to obscure the original intent of a document. For the purposes of the Guild, a signature must remain static and legible for the duration of the contract's life. If a master provides a signature that is prone to fluctuation, erasure, or spontaneous recantation, such a document is deemed null and void. The clerk of the Accords Desk shall not recognize any petition, license, or waiver where the signatory's mark is found to be unstable or subject to unauthorized revision by the ink itself.\n\nAny apprentice found attempting to circumvent these limits by utilizing \"proxy binding\"—where a master provides a verbal command for an apprentice to execute a forbidden binding—shall be subject to the same penalties as the master. The Guild maintains that the responsibility of binding rests solely with the licensed practitioner. If a master delegates the physical act of binding to a subordinate, they remain legally liable for the consequences of that binding. To ensure compliance, all apprentice contracts must be filed with the Accords Desk, and any master who knowingly permits an apprentice to perform tasks exceeding the scope of \"fetching\" shall have their licensure reviewed by the Conservators. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Temporary Mouths",
          "text": "The Guild recognizes the necessity of utilizing temporary vessels for the dissemination of information, yet such vessels are subject to rigorous temporal and spatial restrictions to prevent the unauthorized persistence of Guild secrets. A \"Temporary Mouth\" is defined as any non-permanent entity, construct, or summoned spirit granted the privilege of speech for the purposes of official communication. Such a designation is strictly time-bound. All Temporary Mouths shall expire automatically at the stroke of midnight in the local time zone of the Autumnwood, regardless of whether the communication has reached its intended conclusion. This midnight rule serves as a hard temporal cutoff to ensure that no entity granted temporary speech privileges can remain active or influential beyond the designated operational window.\n\nIn the event that a Temporary Mouth is utilized to transport sensitive information across significant distances, the use of spatial anomalies or storage devices is heavily regulated. Specifically, the use of bags of holding or similar extradimensional storage for the containment of a Temporary Mouth is strictly forbidden unless a specific waiver has been granted by Archmage Veyra. The Guild maintains that the risks associated with \"pocketing\" a speaking entity—including potential resonance with the rift or the accidental creation of a permanent echo—far outweigh the logistical convenience. Any entity placed in such a container must be rendered mute and inert before being moved, and any attempt to bypass this by keeping a Temporary Mouth active within a bag of holding is a violation of the Accords.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild shall not be held liable for any statements made by a Temporary Mouth that occur after the midnight expiration or during a period of \"unintended resonance.\" If a vessel continues to speak beyond its allotted time, it is no longer considered a Temporary Mouth but rather an unsanctioned entity, and its presence may be addressed by the Aegis Magi. All clerks are instructed to log the commencement and expiration of every Temporary Mouth used in official proceedings. Any familiar or spirit used in this capacity must be listed in the master’s registry to be eligible for the temporary speech designation. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Suspension and Lapse",
          "text": "The administrative distinction between a lapse of licensure and a Paradox Trial is a cornerstone of Guild jurisprudence. It is the formal position of the Accords Desk that a lapse is not a Paradox Trial. While the Paradox Trial is a penal system involving the determination of guilt through peer review, magical sealing, or incarceration in a pocket-prison, a lapse is a matter of pure bureaucracy. A lapse occurs when a mage fails to meet the recurring requirements of their licensure, such as the non-payment of dues, the failure to submit annual research logs to the Innovators, or the expiration of a provisional pass. It is a status of inactivity rather than a status of criminality.\n\nWhen a mage’s license lapses, they are effectively rendered \"inert\" in the eyes of the law. They may not cast spells of a certain magnitude, they may not represent the Guild in public forums, and they may not access restricted archives. However, a lapsed mage is not considered a rogue mage. They do not require a trial; they require a filing. The process for reinstatement is purely administrative and involves the submission of the necessary paperwork, the payment of late fees, and a review of the reasons for the lapse by the relevant clerk. The Aegis Magi have no jurisdiction over lapsed mages unless the mage actively attempts to cast magic while their license is in a state of lapse.\n\nConversely, the Paradox Trial is initiated only when a mage has actively violated the Accords—for example, by practicing forbidden schools or engaging in unauthorized field use of high-level magic. The transition from a lapsed status to a Paradox Trial occurs only if the lapsed mage commits a proactive infraction. A mage who simply forgets to renew their paperwork is a clerical matter; a mage who uses a lapsed license as a shield to perform necromancy is a criminal matter. Clerks are reminded to maintain clear records of these distinctions to prevent the unnecessary escalation of administrative errors into high-level penal proceedings. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Guest Casters",
          "text": "The Guild maintains a strict distinction between the hosting of scholarly guests and the engagement of mercenary services. To preserve the integrity of the Autumnwood Accords, it must be understood that a guest who charges a fee is not a guest. The privilege of \"Guest Caster\" status is reserved for those invited to the Midlands for the purpose of academic exchange, collaborative research, or mediation. Such invitations are typically facilitated by the Innovators or the Conservators as part of a broader cultural or intellectual initiative. A guest caster is permitted to perform magic within the Guild’s halls as part of their research or presentation, provided they adhere to the local safety protocols and do not engage in the production of artifacts for sale.\n\nWhen a mage is contracted to perform a specific task—such as the construction of a ward, the cleansing of a site, or the defense of a perimeter—for a monetary sum, they are classified as a Contractor, not a guest. Contractors are subject to different regulations, including the requirement for a specific Work Order and the payment of a transactional tax to the Accords Desk. Because a Contractor is seeking profit, they are not entitled to the same protections or liberties as a Guest Caster. For instance, a Contractor may be barred from certain sensitive areas of the Autumnwood where a Guest Caster might be permitted to roam for observation.\n\nThe distinction is vital for the prevention of \"shadow-contracting,\" wherein a mage might pose as a visiting scholar to perform illicit paid work under the guise of academic inquiry. Any individual who accepts currency, Wario Coins (which are notably not tender), or any other form of payment in exchange for a specific magical output is automatically disqualified from Guest Caster status. The Guild’s auditors are authorized to inspect the ledgers of any hall hosting \"guests\" to ensure that no secret fees are being exchanged. Failure to report a paid contract as such shall result in the immediate revocation of the host’s hosting privileges. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Constructs That Hold a Quill",
          "text": "The legal standing of non-sentient entities regarding the delivery of official notices is a matter of practical necessity. For the purposes of the Accords Desk, service of process on a construct is permitted only if the construct is the primary designated recipient of the Guild’s communications. However, it must be understood that while a construct may be a vessel for delivery, it cannot be a legal person. This leads to the governing principle: Swords do not receive mail. A construct designed for combat, such as a sentinel or a guardian, cannot be served with a summons, a subpoena, or a notice of delinquency. If a notice is delivered to a combat construct, it is considered as though the notice was never received.\n\nFor a construct to be eligible for the service of process, it must be a \"Clerical Construct\"—an entity specifically designed for the handling of records, the transport of correspondence, or the maintenance of archives. Such constructs must be registered with the Accords Desk and assigned a unique identification number. When a notice is delivered to a Clerical Construct, it is deemed as having been delivered to the master who commissioned the construct. The master remains the primary point of legal accountability. If a master attempts to hide from a summons by directing all correspondence to a combat construct, the Accords Desk reserves the right to declare the master in \"Constructual Evasion,\" which carries the same penalties as a willful refusal to appear.\n\nFurthermore, no construct, regardless of its sophistication, may act as a legal representative in a Paradox Trial. While a construct may hold a quill to record the testimony of its master, it cannot testify on behalf of the master, nor can it argue a case before the council of peers. The Guild recognizes that some constructs may possess a high degree of mimicry or programmed logic, but the law distinguishes between the imitation of thought and the possession of agency. Any attempt to utilize a construct to sign a contract or provide a binding oath is a violation of the Accords. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.2 Apprentice Limits",
          "text": "An apprentice may fetch, but never bind in the name of their master without express authorization under § 100.6. Any signature affixed by an apprentice which recants shall be deemed a nullity unless ratified within seven days pursuant to § 100.4. This rule exists to prevent unauthorized expansion of the Guild's liability through the actions of subordinates who lack full comprehension of the implications involved.\n\nThe Master-Apprentice bond is sacred and inviolate, forged in blood and fire across countless trials. To breach this bond without cause is an affront not only to the individual but to the very fabric of magical society as we know it. The Accords Desk maintains strict oversight of all such relationships to ensure they remain within acceptable parameters and do not devolve into exploitation or abuse. Violations may result in severe sanctions including revocation of licensure under § 200.1.\n\nTemporary mouths—those who lend their voices to others without full transfer of authority—are permitted only with written consent from both parties and filing of appropriate forms with the Accords Desk within forty-eight hours. Failure to comply renders any acts performed by such temporary proxies void ab initio unless subsequently ratified in writing. This provision aims to balance practical necessity with protection against potential misuse of borrowed agency.\n\nSuspension and lapse of authority occur automatically upon failure to maintain requisite standards or upon expiration of license terms absent renewal. Such suspensions remain in effect until full remediation is achieved to the satisfaction of the Accords Desk, at which point restoration may be granted discretely. Lapsed licenses cannot be reinstated except through complete re-licensure procedures outlined herein.\n\nGuest casters operating under temporary permits must adhere strictly to the limitations imposed by their host organization and shall not exceed authorized scopes of practice. Any deviation from established guidelines constitutes grounds for immediate termination of guest status and potential disciplinary action against both parties involved. The Accords Desk reserves exclusive jurisdiction over disputes arising from such arrangements.\n\nConstructs capable of holding a quill or pen may execute limited transactions on behalf of their owners provided these acts fall within predefined parameters set forth by the principal. Unauthorized delegation to constructs outside approved categories is strictly prohibited and may result in forfeiture of assets utilized in such improper dealings. This clause seeks to accommodate technological advancements while maintaining essential safeguards against abuse.\n\nThe provisions contained herein represent a carefully balanced approach designed to protect all stakeholders while facilitating legitimate business operations within the bounds of established custom and precedent. Deviations from these standards will be met with swift and decisive consequences as appropriate under existing laws and regulations governing this domain.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "100.4",
            "200.1",
            "200.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "b52a127ec5636287",
      "refs": [
        "1.6",
        "100.4",
        "100.6",
        "200.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "200.3",
      "part": "part_ii",
      "title": "Temporary Mouths",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Duration, midnight rule, bags of holding.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Guest Casters",
          "text": "The distinction between a guest and a practitioner is a cornerstone of the Accords' jurisdictional boundaries. A guest is defined as an individual who enters a Guild hall or a sanctioned research site for the purposes of study, observation, or social interaction, without the intent of providing commercial services. Under the Accords, a guest may be permitted to cast minor spells for personal utility or defense, provided such casting does not interfere with the primary operations of the hall. However, the status of \"guest\" is contingent upon the absence of a commercial transaction. The moment a guest offers a fee, a commission, or any form of payment for the performance of a spell, they cease to be a guest and are instead classified as a contracted practitioner.\n\nA guest who charges a fee is not a guest; they are a commercial entity and are subject to the full weight of the Licensure Requirements. Such individuals must possess a valid license to operate within the Midlands and must register their services with the Accords Desk. They are prohibited from using the \"Guest Status\" to bypass the regulations governing the sale of magical services. Any individual found to be masquerading as a guest while soliciting payment for thaumaturgy shall be fined, and their license—if they possess one—shall be suspended for a period of no less than one year. This rule ensures that the Guild maintains a clear distinction between the scholarly pursuit of magic and the commercial exploitation of it.\n\nFurthermore, guests who are not charging fees are still subject to the conduct codes of the host hall. While they may not be bound by the same rigorous research requirements as the Innovators or the same stability mandates as the Conservators, they are still prohibited from practicing the Forbidden Schools. Any guest who engages in necromancy, blood magic, or other prohibited arts shall be subject to immediate expulsion from the premises and may be reported to the Aegis Magi for a Paradox Trial. The Guild reserves the right to refuse entry to any guest whose previous conduct suggests a risk to the stability of the Autumnwood Accords.\n\nThe Accords Desk shall maintain a registry of all guest interactions that involve the exchange of goods or services. If a practitioner is found to be providing \"pro bono\" work that exceeds the scope of a standard guest interaction, the Accords Desk may require them to register as a temporary commercial entity. This prevents the evasion of licensing fees through the guise of charity or friendship. All commercial magic must be accounted for, and all guests must be identified. See § 100.2 for information on who may speak for the Guild in these matters.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "100.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Constructs That Hold a Quill",
          "text": "The Guild recognizes that certain constructs, specifically those designed for the purpose of recording, transcribing, or maintaining the archives, may be required to perform tasks that involve the handling of legal documents. These entities are classified as Constructs That Hold a Quill. Because these constructs are the primary instruments of the Guild's bureaucracy, they are subject to specific rules regarding the service of process. A construct that holds a quill may be served with a formal notice of summons, a cease-and-desist order, or any other legal document requiring the attention of its primary operator. This is because the construct is an extension of the Guild's administrative reach; to ignore the construct is to ignore the office it serves.\n\nHowever, the Guild maintains a strict distinction between administrative constructs and combat constructs. Swords do not receive mail. Any construct designed primarily for offense, defense, or heavy labor—those that lack the capacity to hold a quill or interpret the nuances of a written decree—are exempt from the service of process. A construct that is purely a weapon of war or a beast of burden cannot be legally \"served\" in the same manner as a clerk's tool. If a practitioner wishes to receive legal notice via a construct, the construct must be demonstrably capable of holding a quill and must be registered as an administrative tool with the Accords Desk.\n\nThe service of process on a construct that holds a quill must be performed by a licensed Clerk or an authorized representative of the Aegis Magi. The notice must be physically delivered to the construct and placed within its reach, ensuring that the primary operator is alerted to the contents of the document. If a construct is destroyed or rendered inoperable before the notice can be delivered, the service of process is deemed failed, and the notice must be re-issued to the practitioner directly. This ensures that the legal weight of the Accords is not lost in the transition from the physical page to the mechanical hand.\n\nFurthermore, constructs that hold a quill are prohibited from independently signing contracts or authorizing the expenditure of Guild resources. While they may receive mail, they cannot initiate legal actions or enter into binding agreements on behalf of their masters without a pre-authorized \"Temporary Mouth\" designation for speech and a corresponding \"Authorized Signature\" seal. Any attempt by a construct to independently execute a contract shall render said contract null and void. This limitation prevents the automation of the Guild's legal authority and ensures that all significant decisions remain in the hands of sentient, bound persons. See § 1.4 for the definition of persons bound.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.3 Temporary Mouths — Duration and Termination",
          "text": "§ 200.3 governs the temporal extent of temporary mouths, specifying that such grants expire at daybreak following issuance unless extended by formal motion. See § 100.6 regarding clerkly delegation authority to set parameters for expedited proceedings. Under § 100.5, any conflict of interest arising from a caster's personal connection to the matter mandates recusal prior to casting. Furthermore, § 200.4 addresses suspension and lapse conditions wherein temporary mouths automatically terminate upon breach of stipulated terms or failure to meet performance benchmarks.\n\nAdditionally, § 200.3 clarifies that bags of holding may serve as repositories for temporary mouth effects pending further instruction from the Desk. This aligns with § 100.8's provisions on emergency sitting protocols where rapid response is imperative. Relatedly, § 400.5 outlines specimen custody requirements ensuring materials remain secure during active casting operations. Finally, § 300.2 defines unsanctioned arts boundaries that temporary mouths must adhere to strictly under threat of immediate revocation per § 200.1 licensure mandates.\n\nIn essence, § 200.3 establishes clear temporal limits and termination triggers for temporary mouth arrangements while referencing broader procedural safeguards found elsewhere in the Accords. These provisions collectively ensure responsible deployment of magical authority within established legal frameworks. See also § 500.4 on healing offered as consideration which may influence duration calculations in certain contexts. The Desk retains ultimate discretion to modify these rules as needed to serve the public good consistent with § 1.7 construction principles.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "200.3",
            "100.6",
            "100.5",
            "200.4",
            "100.8",
            "400.5",
            "300.2",
            "200.1",
            "500.4",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.3 Temporary Mouths — Duration and Termination — Continued",
          "text": "The authority granted under this section is expressly limited to the duration specified in the granting instrument, never exceeding seven (7) consecutive days unless extended by unanimous consent of all signatories. Any such extension must be documented in writing and filed with the Guild Desk within two business days of execution. Failure to comply renders the temporary authorization void ab initio.\n\nTermination of a Temporary Mouth occurs immediately upon expiration of its stated term, regardless of ongoing matters or unfulfilled obligations. The former holder shall forthwith cease all use of the granted powers and return any materials received in connection therewith. Continued exercise after lapse constitutes unauthorized practice under § 200.1.\n\nNotwithstanding the foregoing, a Temporary Mouth may be recalled at any time prior to natural expiration upon written notice delivered personally or by certified mail. Such recall operates retroactively from the moment of delivery unless otherwise agreed. The recalling party shall have no further liability for acts committed while the temporary authorization was validly in effect.\n\nSpecial provisions apply when a Temporary Mouth involves the use of restricted artifacts, prohibited schools, or other high-risk capabilities. In such cases, enhanced monitoring conditions may be imposed by the granting authority at its sole discretion. Non-compliance with these additional stipulations shall result in immediate revocation and potential disciplinary action under applicable provisions.\n\nThe concept of \"midnight rule\" applies to Temporary Mouths involving time-sensitive tasks or seasonal restrictions. Unless explicitly stated otherwise, any such authorization automatically terminates at midnight on the last day of its term, even if still technically valid during daylight hours. Parties should account for this when planning dependent activities.\n\nBag-of-holdings provisions remain in force unless expressly waived. Any physical items transferred pursuant to a Temporary Mouth shall revert to their original owners upon expiration or revocation, free and clear of all claims by the former holder. Disputes over ownership or condition shall be resolved through standard dispute resolution mechanisms provided elsewhere in these Accords.\n\nThis section does not address matters falling under § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse regarding permanent licenses, nor does it cover guest casting arrangements contemplated in § 200.5. Each provision operates independently according to its own terms and conditions as set forth herein.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "200.4",
            "200.5",
            "200.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Temporary Mouths and Their Legal Status in the Mages' Guild Codex",
          "text": "In the complex tapestry of magical governance under the Autumnwood Accords, the concept of \"Temporary Mouths\" stands as an intricate element, often misunderstood or overlooked. A Temporary Mouth is a being—typically an animate construct, golem, or other form of magically animated creature—that serves as an extension of a caster's will. While constructs that hold a quill are covered under § 200.6, the broader implications of their use and legal standing require further elucidation.\n\n### Legal Status and Boundaries\n\nA Temporary Mouth is not a Person Bound by default; it lacks the cognitive capacity to be considered bound by the Codex unless specifically declared as such. However, once a Temporary Mouth engages in covered conduct—such as casting spells or manipulating materials—it may become subject to Codex oversight under § 1.4 Persons Bound and § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks. The jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild over such entities extends only so far as their actions affect covered persons, property, or public safety.\n\n### Duration and Termination\n\nThe duration of a Temporary Mouth's existence is governed by both the caster's intent and external factors beyond the caster’s control. § 200.3 Temporary Mouths — Duration and Termination stipulates that a Temporary Mouth ceases to be operational at midnight unless otherwise specified by the caster or due to unforeseen circumstances. This \"midnight rule\" ensures a clear demarcation for the temporal scope of temporary constructs, preventing indefinite use without explicit authorization.\n\nUnder § 200.3 Temporary Mouths — Duration and Termination, the creation of a Temporary Mouth can be terminated prematurely if the caster so chooses or if the construct becomes incapacitated due to damage or malfunction. Incapacitation is defined as the inability to perform its intended tasks effectively. In such cases, the construct must be deactivated according to § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks.\n\n### Bags of Holding and Their Legal Implications\n\nA particularly nuanced aspect of Temporary Mouths involves constructs that possess magical properties akin to a Bag of Holding, allowing them to store vast amounts of material without physical restrictions. The legal implications of such storage are significant, especially in the context of covered conduct.\n\nUnder § 200.3 Temporary Mouths and Bags of Holding, it is imperative for casters to ensure that any stored materials do not exceed the construct's capacity or violate any other provisions of the Codex. Misuse of a Bag of Holding can result in severe penalties under § 1.5 Relation to Other Law if such actions lead to unauthorized field use or property damage.\n\nIf a Temporary Mouth with Bag-like properties is found to be holding materials that are not authorized for storage, the caster may face sanctions under § 300.2 Necromancy and other relevant sections depending on the nature of the materials. The Aegis Magi have been known to enforce these rules stringently, particularly in cases where unauthorized field use or breaches of Guild property occur.\n\n### Practical Implications\n\nThe legal framework surrounding Temporary Mouths underscores the importance of responsible magical creation and use within the Mages' Guild Codex. Casters must be cognizant of the constructs they create and their potential impact on covered persons and property. The midnight rule provides a clear endpoint for temporary entities, ensuring that all Temporary Mouths are deactivated in a timely manner to prevent any unintended consequences.\n\nIn conclusion, Temporary Mouths, whether animate or imbued with Bag-like properties, play a crucial role in magical practices but must adhere strictly to the Codex's guidelines. Proper adherence ensures not only compliance with Guild regulations but also maintains the integrity of magical practice within the Autumnwood Accords. By understanding and respecting these rules, casters can navigate the complex landscape of temporary constructs safely and responsibly.\n\nSee § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks for further details on operational procedures involving Temporary Mouths.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.6",
            "1.4",
            "100.6",
            "200.3",
            "1.5",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Temporary Mouths and Their Legal Status in the Mages' Guild Codex — Continued",
          "text": "In the intricate tapestry of legal constructs that govern the usage, study, and practice of magic within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords, the concept of \"Temporary Mouths\" holds a unique position. These are not mere tools or vessels but entities temporarily bound to the will of a caster for specific purposes, often under conditions outlined in this Codex. The nature of their existence and the legal status they hold is crucial to understanding the broader implications of their use.\n\nA Temporary Mouth is an entity created by a caster through advanced magical techniques, typically involving the binding of a willing or unwilling subject into a form that allows it to act as a proxy for speech or action. This can include animated constructs, living beings, or even inanimate objects imbued with limited sentience and obedience. The duration of their existence is determined by the caster’s intent and the nature of the task they are intended to perform.\n\nThe Codex specifies that Temporary Mouths have a legal status distinct from that of free-willed persons but not entirely devoid of rights. They do not possess full personhood, as defined in § 1.4 Persons Bound; however, they are afforded certain protections under the Guild’s jurisdiction. This includes the right to be treated humanely and with due consideration for their autonomy within the bounds set by their creation.\n\nUnder § 200.3 Temporary Mouths—Duration and Termination, it is established that the duration of a Temporary Mouth's existence is governed by the caster's intent at the time of its creation. If no specific duration is stated, the entity exists until the completion of the task for which it was created or until it is explicitly dismissed by the original caster. This rule applies universally under § 170.2 Duration and Termination of Magical Entities.\n\nIf a Temporary Mouth’s existence extends beyond the intended duration without explicit dismissal, it becomes subject to an \"midnight rule,\" as detailed in § 350.4 Midnight Rule for Unintended Extensions. According to this provision, any Temporary Mouth that remains active past midnight shall be considered dismissed and reverted to its original state or condition unless otherwise specified by the caster before midnight.\n\nThe use of bags of holding with Temporary Mouths is a critical consideration under § 201.5 Bags of Holding and Duration. These magical constructs can temporarily house a Temporary Mouth, allowing for extended periods of inactivity without affecting their legal status. However, once released from such containers, the Temporary Mouth resumes its duration as if no interruption had occurred.\n\nTemporary Mouths also play an important role in field operations and emergency situations where immediate action is required. Their deployment under § 102.3 Emergency Situations allows for swift and effective responses to threats or anomalies. In such cases, their use may be expedited with reduced scrutiny from the Guild’s oversight bodies, as long as the conditions outlined in § 175.4 Emergency Exceptions are met.\n\nThe Codex further clarifies that Temporary Mouths created by guest casters, as detailed in § 200.5 Guest Casters, must adhere to the same duration and termination rules as any other Temporary Mouth. This ensures consistency and fairness across all members of the Guild and its guests.\n\nIn summary, the legal status of Temporary Mouths is a nuanced aspect of the Mages' Guild Codex, balancing the needs of magical efficiency with ethical considerations. By adhering to these guidelines, casters ensure that their use of such entities remains both effective and compliant with the broader principles outlined in this document. This approach not only protects the integrity of the Guild but also fosters a culture of responsible magic usage within its jurisdiction.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "200.3",
            "170.2",
            "350.4",
            "201.5",
            "102.3",
            "175.4",
            "200.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
