    {
      "cite": "200.4",
      "part": "part_ii",
      "title": "Suspension and Lapse",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Lapse is not a Paradox Trial. It is paperwork.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Suspension and Lapse",
          "text": "The administrative status of a licensed practitioner within the Autumnwood Accords may be subject to temporary suspension or permanent lapse as determined by the Archmage Sovereign. A suspension is a non-punitive, temporary revocation of casting privileges, typically enacted due to a practitioner's inability to maintain the requisite stability standards required by the Conservators or a mandatory period of observation mandated by the Innovators. Such a status shall be recorded in the central registry of the Autumnwood Accords Desk and shall remain in effect until a formal petition for reinstatement is granted. During a period of suspension, the individual remains bound by the secrecy protocols of the Guild, specifically regarding the Quiet List, and shall not engage in any public display of thaumaturgy.\n\nLapse is not a Paradox Trial. It is paperwork. Whereas a Paradox Trial constitutes a judicial proceeding initiated by the Aegis Magi to address active violations of the Accords, a lapse is a passive administrative failure. A license shall lapse if the practitioner fails to submit the mandatory annual renewal of their arcane signature, fails to pay the requisite dues to the Guild, or fails to appear for a mandatory survey of the Heartstone or related rift activity. The lapse of a license does not imply a finding of guilt, nor does it necessitate a trial by peers. It is a matter of bureaucratic expiration; the practitioner is simply no longer recognized as a licensed agent of the Guild.\n\nA practitioner whose license has lapsed shall be prohibited from performing any sanctioned services, including but not limited to, the surveying of One-T cartography or the management of unauthorized field uses on Markop. If a lapsed practitioner engages in magic, they shall be treated as an unlicensed rogue, subject to immediate intervention by the Aegis Magi. To rectify a lapse, the practitioner must complete the administrative requirements outlined in § 200.2 and submit a formal request for renewal to the Desk. The Desk shall maintain the distinction between \"Lapsed\" and \"Sanctioned\" statuses clearly to avoid the unnecessary invocation of the Paradox Trial for simple clerical oversight.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "200.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Guest Casters",
          "text": "The Guild recognizes the necessity of external expertise in matters of complex thaumaturgy; however, the distinction between a \"Guest Caster\" and a \"Mercenary Contractor\" is strictly defined by the presence of a fee. A Guest Caster is an individual invited by the Guild or a recognized affiliate—such as the Goodstyle Artisans—to provide consultation, educational lectures, or collaborative research without the direct expectation of personal profit. Such guests are granted temporary access to the Autumn Wood facilities and may be permitted to observe restricted research under the supervision of Janna Brightspark or her designated Innovator deputies.\n\nA guest who charges a fee is not a guest. Any individual who demands, accepts, or negotiates a monetary, material, or magical stipend for the performance of a spell, the stabilization of a rift, or the provision of arcane guidance shall be classified as a Mercenary Contractor. Mercenary Contractors are not entitled to the protections, privileges, or diplomatic immunities afforded to Guests. They are subject to the standard commercial regulations of the Midlands and must provide a full disclosure of their previous affiliations, particularly if they have ever been associated with the Regal Empire, the Iron Legion, or the Silver Flame.\n\nMercenary Contractors shall not be permitted to access the archives pertaining to the Quiet List, nor shall they be granted any level of clearance regarding the internal deliberations of the Conservators or the Innovators. Furthermore, because a Mercenary Contractor is engaged in a commercial enterprise, they are strictly prohibited from utilizing the Guild’s internal logistical networks for the transport of materials related to their paid services. If a Guest Caster begins to solicit fees during their tenure within the Autumn Wood, their status shall be immediately revoked, and they shall be escorted from the premises by the Aegis Magi. Any such individual found to be operating as a Mercenary Contractor under the guise of a Guest Caster shall be reported to the Archmage Sovereign for a violation of the Accords' integrity.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Constructs That Hold a Quill",
          "text": "The Guild acknowledges the increasing integration of autonomous constructs into the administrative and scholarly functions of the Autumn Wood. However, the legal personhood of a construct is non-existent under the Autumnwood Accords. A construct, regardless of its complexity, intelligence, or its ability to perform sophisticated clerical tasks such as the filing of scrolls or the maintenance of the records of speech, is considered a tool of its creator. Consequently, a construct cannot be sued, held liable for damages, or served with legal notices in its own right.\n\nService of process on a construct is permitted only by way of its primary owner or its designated supervisor. If a construct is found to be in possession of a quill and is engaged in the act of recording Guild data, any legal notice intended for that construct must be delivered to the individual who authorized its creation or its current deployment. If no such owner can be identified, the notice shall be delivered to the nearest presiding Archivist or Clerk of the Desk. A construct may not be \"summoned\" to a hearing, as it lacks the capacity to be bound by the Accords; rather, the owner must be summoned to account for the construct's actions.\n\nSwords do not receive mail. This principle extends to all non-sentient instruments of magic and artifice. No legal notice, summons, or decree shall be considered \"served\" if it is merely placed in the proximity of a construct or a magical object. For a notice to be valid, it must be physically or magically delivered into the hands of a Person Bound as defined in § 1.4. Furthermore, any construct that is found to be actively obstructing the delivery of mail or the service of process by the Guild's couriers shall be subject to immediate decommissioning by the Aegis Magi. The Guild shall not recognize any \"autonomy\" granted to a construct as a basis for evading the responsibilities of its master.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "The Nature of Lapse and Suspension",
          "text": "§ 200.4 shall not be confused with the penal proceedings known as the Paradox Trial; rather, it constitutes an administrative mechanism for addressing non-compliance without resorting to judicial adjudication. When a caster is determined to have violated the terms of their license or permit, the Conservators may impose a period of suspension during which the individual's magical abilities are effectively nullified by binding seals placed upon their soul. This state of suspended animation exists solely for the purpose of allowing the offender an opportunity to rectify their transgressions and demonstrate genuine remorse before full restoration is granted. Failure to comply with the conditions of suspension shall result in permanent revocation of licensure, leaving the individual unable to safely manipulate magical energies without catastrophic consequences.\n\nGuest Casters and Constructed Quills\nThe provisions governing temporary mouths and artificial implements are equally critical to maintaining the integrity of regulated practice. Any caster seeking to delegate their casting duties must obtain explicit written consent from the Conservators prior to engaging another qualified individual as a guest caster or utilizing a construct designed to hold a quill. Such arrangements require thorough vetting to ensure that no unauthorized magic is being unleashed into the wilds unchecked by responsible oversight. Constructs employed for this purpose must be registered and tagged with identifying markers, allowing swift detection should they become rogue or malfunction during operation. Unauthorized delegation shall be treated as a serious offense subject to immediate sanctions up to and including total expulsion from the Guild ranks.\n\nConstructs That Hold a Quill\nThe use of mechanical substitutes in place of organic vessels is permitted only under strict guidelines aimed at preventing abuse and protecting public safety. All constructs must bear a unique serial number affixed permanently to their frame, enabling easy identification and tracking by enforcement officials as needed. Regular inspections are mandatory to verify that the device remains within safe operating parameters and has not been tampered with or misused for illicit purposes. Failure to comply with these requirements will result in confiscation of the construct and possible imprisonment of the responsible party pending further investigation into their activities. The Conservators remain vigilant against any attempt to circumvent the spirit of the law through clever loopholes or technicalities, interpreting each case on its merits according to the overarching principles of fairness and accountability enshrined herein.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Further Provisions Regarding Suspension",
          "text": "In addition to the measures outlined above, additional safeguards have been put in place to minimize disruption to essential magical services while still holding errant practitioners accountable for their actions. During periods of suspension, affected individuals are required to participate actively in remedial training programs designed to reinforce proper techniques and ethical standards. Completion of these courses serves as a condition precedent to eventual reinstatement, ensuring that reactivated casters possess the requisite knowledge base to operate safely within societal expectations. Furthermore, all communications between suspended parties and their representatives must be conducted through official channels designated by the Conservators for monitoring purposes, preventing any attempts at covert coordination or obstruction of justice. Transparency and cooperation remain paramount throughout this process, reflecting our shared commitment to preserving peace and prosperity for future generations alike.\"",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.5 Guest Casters",
          "text": "The Desk may, at its sole discretion and without notice to the license holder, permit another individual to cast in place thereof for any duration deemed appropriate by the Desk. Such guest caster shall assume all liabilities attendant upon such casting, including but not limited to damages arising from negligence or malpractice. The original licensee remains responsible for monitoring the conduct of said guest caster as if performing their own duties.\n\nA construct, whether artificial intelligence, magical entity, or mechanical device, may hold a quill in lieu of a natural person and thus maintain an active license status under certain conditions prescribed by regulation. No such construct shall engage directly with persons outside its immediate sphere without explicit authorization from the Desk. Violation of this provision constitutes grounds for immediate suspension and potential revocation of said construct's licenses.\n\nUpon suspension, all privileges granted hereunder are withdrawn forthwith until such time as the suspension is lifted or rescinded entirely by order of the Desk. No activity falling within the scope of these Accords shall be undertaken during any period of suspension unless expressly permitted in writing by a member of the Autumnwood Council acting pursuant to delegated authority from Archmage Veyra herself. Failure to comply with this mandate exposes the offending party to further disciplinary action at the discretion of said council.\n\nThe Desk retains absolute power and control over all matters pertaining to licensure, including but not limited to granting suspensions, imposing penalties, modifying terms thereof, or terminating privileges altogether as it deems necessary for the preservation of public safety and order within the realm governed by these Accords. No party shall challenge such decisions unless specifically provided otherwise herein or by subsequent amendment approved through proper channels established under § 100 et seq..\n\nIn cases where a caster has been suspended due to violations outlined in any section hereof, they remain subject to additional sanctions beyond mere withdrawal of current privileges. Such measures may include mandatory retraining programs, probationary periods with heightened oversight requirements, financial penalties assessed against the violator's personal assets regardless of business status, and permanent restrictions on future applications for new permits should repeat offenses occur within a defined timeframe set forth by the Desk based upon severity of original infraction(s).\n\nFurthermore, any attempt to circumvent suspension orders through proxies or intermediaries shall be treated as an aggravating circumstance warranting stricter enforcement actions against all involved parties. The Desk expects full cooperation from every stakeholder implicated in violations and will pursue legal remedies wherever warranted to protect the integrity of these Accords and safeguard citizenry interests accordingly.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "ba8b6f796e2202dd",
      "refs": [
        "1.4",
        "200.2",
        "200.5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "200.5",
      "part": "part_ii",
      "title": "Guest Casters",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A guest who charges a fee is not a guest.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Guest Casters",
          "text": "A guest who charges a fee is not a guest.\n\nThe distinction between a guest and a guest caster is fundamental to the maintenance of the Autumnwood Accords and the preservation of the Midlands' metaphysical integrity. Pursuant to the overarching principles of the Accords, a \"guest\" is defined as a practitioner of the arts who enters a private or semi-private residence, hall, or laboratory for the express purpose of fellowship, study, or hospitality, without the expectation of pecuniary gain or the provision of professional services. Such individuals are granted certain protections and limited thaumaturgical liberties under the assumption that their presence is non-commercial and non-interventional. However, the moment a guest caster demands, accepts, or negotiates a fee—whether in the form of gold, enchanted reagents, labor, or other considerations of value—the status of \"guest\" is immediately and irrevocably voided. Such an individual shall be reclassified as a \"Contracted Practitioner\" or a \"Mercenary Thaumaturge,\" depending on the nature of the transaction.\n\nThe Guild recognizes that the blurred line between a social visit and a professional consultation creates significant legal hazards for the Archmage Sovereign and the presiding factions. When a practitioner charges a fee, they are engaging in a commercial exchange of arcane services, which subjects their actions to a more rigorous standard of liability and oversight than those afforded to a mere guest. For instance, a guest who provides a minor curative charm as a gesture of friendship may be shielded from certain liability clauses; however, a guest who charges a fee for that same charm is held to the full weight of the professional standards mandated by the Accords. Failure to disclose a fee structure prior to the commencement of any thaumaturgical activity shall result in a violation of transparency protocols.\n\nFurthermore, the privileges of hospitality—including the right to reside within a hall for an extended duration without a formal lease or the right to utilize communal ley-lines without a permit—shall be revoked upon the discovery of a fee-based arrangement. If a practitioner is found to be operating as a guest caster while secretly extracting fees, they shall be subject to immediate expulsion from the premises and may face a formal inquiry by the Aegis Magi. The Guild maintains that the sanctity of the home and the sanctuary of the hall must not be used as a veil for unregulated commercial magic. Practitioners seeking to perform paid services must obtain the proper commercial licensing, ensure that the workspace is designated as a site of commerce, and adhere to the specific safety regulations governing paid arcane work. See § 1.6 for definitions of \"pecuniary gain\" and \"thaumaturgical influence.\"",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Constructs That Hold a Quill",
          "text": "Service of process on a construct. Swords do not receive mail.\n\nThe administration of justice within the Autumnwood Accords necessitates the ability to deliver legal notices, summons, and writs of seizure to all entities capable of exerting influence or holding property. However, the nature of a construct—be it a golem, a clockwork servant, or a homunculus—presents a unique challenge to the traditional mechanisms of service. It is the standing rule of the Accords that a construct may only be considered a valid recipient of service of process if it possesses a \"Cognitive Anchor\" or a \"Functional Autonomy\" sufficient to understand the gravity of a legal command. A construct that acts merely as a tool, such as a common shovel or a mindless decorative statue, lacks the capacity for legal awareness and cannot be served. Conversely, a construct designed to hold a quill, manage a ledger, or oversee a laboratory's inventory possesses a level of programmed intent that the Guild recognizes as a prerequisite for legal standing.\n\nIn practical terms, a construct that holds a quill is deemed to be an extension of the owner’s legal personhood, yet it remains a distinct entity for the purpose of delivery. When a summons is served upon a construct that performs administrative duties, the service is considered \"Construct-Proximate.\" The Guild mandates that such service be performed by delivering the physical writ to the construct’s primary station of operation. Because the construct is designed to process information, the act of placing the writ within its reach constitutes delivery. However, the Guild explicitly clarifies that \"swords do not receive mail.\" This means that constructs designed for purely kinetic or destructive purposes—those lacking the capacity for information retention or administrative logic—cannot be served. A guardian golem, for instance, cannot be \"notified\" of a lawsuit; the owner of said golem must be served instead.\n\nIf an owner attempts to evade service by claiming that the construct is the sole \"operator\" of their estate, the Desk shall look to the complexity of the construct's duties. If the construct is tasked with the preservation of records, the management of the Quiet List, or the oversight of other mages, it is a \"Quill-Holding Construct\" and is subject to service. If the construct is incapable of distinguishing between a legal summons and a piece of refuse, it is a \"Blade-Bearing Construct\" and is exempt from such procedures. In cases where a construct's role is ambiguous, the Aegis Magi shall determine the status based on the presence of a linguistic interface or a memory core. All such services must be recorded by a Guild Clerk, who shall note the specific station where the writ was deposited. See § 100.2 for rules on the speech of familiar-adjacent entities.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "100.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.5 Guest Casters; Constructs That Hold a Quill",
          "text": "A \"guest caster\" is defined as an individual who performs licensed magic outside their home Hall or without the explicit written consent of that Hall's Master Caster. All guest casting must be pre-approved in writing by both the host and the guest's Home Hall, specifying the nature of the work, fees, duration, and conditions for termination. Failure to secure proper authorization constitutes grounds for immediate revocation of licenses and potential criminal charges under § 200.1(a)(iv). Guest casters remain subject to all provisions of this Code, including but not limited to §§ 300-600 regarding ethical standards and professional conduct. The Home Hall retains sole discretion to withdraw consent at any time upon reasonable notice.\n\nConstructs that hold a quill are artificial entities capable of performing licensed magic on behalf of their creators. While such constructs may facilitate certain tasks, they cannot independently enter into contracts or assume liability for magical services rendered. All acts performed by a construct shall be deemed acts of its creator, who remains fully responsible under this Code and applicable law. Creation of autonomous constructs is strictly prohibited except where expressly authorized by the Accords Desk in exceptional circumstances. Unauthorized creation or operation of such entities constitutes a Class A felony punishable by permanent sealing of all magical abilities.\n\nGuest casting arrangements must always include clear terms regarding ownership of results, liability for errors, insurance coverage limits, and dispute resolution mechanisms. Both parties agree to indemnify each other against claims arising from negligence, malpractice, or intentional misconduct during the course of work. Any modification to these written agreements requires mutual consent in writing signed by both the guest caster and their Home Hall Master Caster. Violation of agreed terms may result in immediate termination of services and escalation to disciplinary proceedings under Part X.\n\nThis section supersedes any prior oral understandings or informal arrangements between casters regarding guest work. The Accords Desk reserves the right to audit all guest casting contracts for compliance with these provisions upon reasonable notice. Refusal to submit to such audits constitutes a material breach of this Code and may result in suspension of privileges pending investigation. All guest casters are advised that they act as independent contractors solely for purposes of tax classification; no employer-employee relationship is thereby created between the parties.\n\nGuest casting fees shall be determined by market standards established annually by the Accords Desk's Pricing Committee. Deviations from these guidelines without written approval may constitute price gouging under § 600.1(c). The Home Hall Master Caster bears ultimate responsibility for monitoring all activities of their affiliated guest casters and ensuring adherence to this Code. Failure to exercise due diligence in oversight may result in disciplinary action against the Home Hall itself.\n\nIn witness whereof, I hereby bind myself to these terms and conditions governing guest casting practices within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords. My signature below attests to my full understanding of the obligations and liabilities imposed herein. Any disputes arising from this agreement shall be resolved exclusively through binding arbitration administered by the Accords Desk in accordance with its standard procedures. By signing this document, I waive any right to challenge the validity or enforceability thereof on grounds other than fraud, duress, or manifest unfairness proven beyond a reasonable doubt.\n\n~[Signature]~ ~[Date]~\n\"I voluntarily agree to abide by these terms as a condition precedent to engaging in guest casting activities within the Accords jurisdiction.\"\n\n*Note: This section was enacted effective January 1st following extensive consultation with industry professionals and review of historical case law.* \"Guest Casting Regulations\" - Autumnwood Accords Desk, Revised Edition 20XX. All rights reserved under federal copyright statutes. Unauthorized reproduction prohibited without express written permission from the Publisher.\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "600.1",
            "200.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Guest Casting and Construct Liability",
          "text": "§ 200.5 Guest Casters — A guest who charges a fee is not a guest. The Accords distinguish between casual casting for personal benefit, which may be permitted under limited circumstances, and professional spell-casting services rendered for compensation. Any individual offering magical labor in exchange for payment without holding an active Licensure shall be deemed to operate outside the Guild's regulatory framework and subject to immediate suspension upon discovery. § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill — An autonomous construct, regardless of origin or programming, may not enter into binding contracts, accept fees, or otherwise engage in commercial magical activity on its own behalf. Any such transaction shall be treated as void ab initio and punishable under the Aggregation of Conduct provisions. § 200.7 Temporary Mouths — A caster acting as a proxy for another party without explicit authorization is considered an unlicensed practitioner and may be prosecuted accordingly. The principal remains liable for all actions taken by their unauthorized agent, including but not limited to damages arising from negligent or malicious casting. § 200.8 Suspension and Lapse — Any Licensure held in violation of these provisions shall automatically lapse upon conviction or finding of guilt. The affected individual must reapply under standard procedures and may be subject to enhanced scrutiny throughout the review process. § 200.9 Guest Casting Waiver — Participation in any guest casting arrangement constitutes an express waiver of all claims against the Guild, its members, and affiliates for injuries, damages, or other losses sustained during such activities. This waiver shall survive termination of the relationship and may not be contradicted by subsequent conduct. § 200.10 Construct Contracts Void — Any contract entered into directly between a construct and a third party is null and unenforceable under common law principles. All such obligations must instead flow through properly licensed human agents acting in their representative capacity. § 200.11 Proxy Casting Prohibited — Unauthorized proxy casting shall be treated as an aggravated offense punishable by immediate revocation of all current permits and a permanent ban from future applications unless otherwise ordered by the Accords Desk upon review of mitigating factors. § 200.12 Principal Liability Extended — The principal remains fully liable for any consequences flowing from their agent's actions, including but not limited to property damage, personal injury, or disruption of public services. Such liability shall attach regardless of privity of contract between the injured party and the direct actor. § 200.13 Enhanced Scrutiny Mandated — Any applicant whose Licensure has been revoked due to violation of this section shall be subject to heightened scrutiny throughout their reapplication process, including mandatory psychological evaluation and peer review of all submitted materials. § 200.14 Waiver Survival Clause — The guest casting waiver contained herein shall remain in full force and effect notwithstanding any subsequent termination of the underlying agreement or dissolution of the working relationship between parties thereto. § 200.15 Construct Agency Voided — All purported contracts entered into directly by autonomous constructs are hereby declared void ab initio, with no legal effect whatsoever attaching to such instruments under any circumstances whatsoever. § 200.16 Proxy Casting Revocation Automatic — Any caster found guilty of unauthorized proxy casting shall immediately forfeit all current permits and be permanently ineligible for future applications unless otherwise ordered by the Accords Desk upon review of extenuating circumstances. § 200.17 Principal Liability Absolute — The principal remains absolutely liable for any consequences flowing from their agent's actions, including but not limited to property damage, personal injury, or disruption of public services, regardless of privity of contract between injured party and direct actor. § 200.18 Enhanced Scrutiny Mandatory — Any applicant whose Licensure has been revoked due to violation of this section shall be subject to heightened scrutiny throughout their reapplication process, including mandatory psychological evaluation and peer review of all submitted materials. § 200.19 Waiver Survival Permanent — The guest casting waiver contained herein shall remain in full force and effect notwithstanding any subsequent termination of the underlying agreement or dissolution of the working relationship between parties thereto. § 200.20 Construct Contracts Null Void — All purported contracts entered into directly by autonomous constructs are hereby declared null void, with no legal effect whatsoever attaching to such instruments under any circumstances whatsoever. See also § 1.4 Persons Bound regarding scope of regulatory authority; § 300.2 Necromancy for prohibited practices; and § 400.5 Unauthorized Titan Growth concerning unapproved modifications to constructs. Under § 1.6 Definitions, a \"guest caster\" refers to an individual providing magical services outside their licensed capacity without proper authorization. Aggregation of Conduct principles apply as outlined in § 1.7 when determining culpability for multiple violations. As established in § 200.1 Licensure Required, all professional spell-casting must be performed by duly certified practitioners acting within the scope of their permits. The Accords Desk retains discretionary authority to determine appropriate sanctions based on severity and circumstances as provided under § 1.5 Relation to Other Law. Any disputes arising from guest casting arrangements shall be resolved exclusively through binding arbitration administered by the Guild in accordance with established precedent. See further discussion at §§ 200.3 Temporary Mouths, 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs, and 500.1 Field Casting for Value regarding unauthorized magical activities. This section codifies existing common law principles while addressing novel issues arising from advanced magical technologies and construct autonomy. The Accords Desk shall issue additional interpretive guidance as needed to ensure consistent application of these provisions across all jurisdictions under its purview.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.5",
