    {
      "cite": "200.6",
      "part": "part_ii",
      "title": "Constructs That Hold a Quill",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Service of process on a construct. Swords do not receive mail.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Constructs That Hold a Quill",
          "text": "Pursuant to the overarching mandates of the Autumnwood Accords and the jurisdictional boundaries established by the Archmage Sovereign, this section governs the legal status of non-sentient and semi-sentient entities capable of administrative interaction. While the Guild recognizes the prevalence of construct-based automation within the modern arcane infrastructure, the legal personhood of a construct remains a matter of strict technicality. A construct shall not be deemed a \"Person Bound\" under the protections and obligations of § 1.4. However, for the purposes of the Accords Desk and the orderly administration of the Midlands, a construct may be granted \"Administrative Agency\" if it is specifically engineered to hold a quill, manage ledgers, or facilitate the transmission of written records.\n\nAny construct that holds a quill and is tasked with the preservation of Guild records, the filing of petitions, or the recording of magical yields shall be subject to the formal service of process. This status is not granted by inherent nature but by the specific magical and mechanical intent of its creation. If a construct is utilized by a Master of the Guild or an authorized Innovator to act as a repository for legal notices, it shall be considered a valid conduit for the delivery of summons, subpoenas, and official decrees. The Clerk of the Accords Desk shall ensure that any such construct is equipped with a designated receptacle or a designated \"holding area\" for physical parchment, ensuring that the integrity of the delivery is maintained without requiring the construct to possess independent volition or comprehension of the contents therein.\n\nConversely, the Guild explicitly denies the status of \"Process-Recipient\" to objects of martial utility. It is hereby decreed that swords do not receive mail. No blade, regardless of its enchantments, its history of service, or its proximity to a quill-holding construct, shall be considered a valid destination for legal correspondence. Any attempt by a courier, a mage, or an automated delivery system to affix a summons to a weapon shall be deemed a nullity. Furthermore, any construct designed solely for combat, defense, or physical labor—lacking the specific apparatus for the holding of a quill or the management of records—is excluded from these provisions. Such entities are to be treated as inanimate property and are incapable of being served with notice, regardless of the demands of the presiding faction.\n\nIn instances where a construct is used by a third party—such as the Goodstyle Artisans or independent contractors—to hold a quill for the purpose of drafting contracts, the legal responsibility for the accuracy of the record remains with the human or sentient agent who commissioned the work. The construct is merely the instrument of the quill; it is not the author of the intent. Should a construct be damaged or destroyed during the service of process, the initiating party shall be held liable for the repair or replacement of the apparatus, provided the damage was not caused by a violation of the Iron Mandate or other external martial laws. For further clarification on the limitations of agency, see § 1.4.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill — Service of Process on Non-Living Entities",
          "text": "Under § 1.4, all persons are bound by the Accords; however, this mandate extends only so far as practical enforcement permits. Where living beings cannot be found or served due to physical absence or magical concealment, alternative methods of notice may be employed at the discretion of the Desk. One such method is service upon a construct designated by the accused as an agent for receiving legal communication.\n\nA \"construct\" within the meaning of this Section includes any artificial entity capable of holding a written document, whether mechanical (such as a clockwork automaton), magical (including golems and homunculi), or otherwise engineered to serve as a proxy. The construct must be under the exclusive control of the accused at the time of designation, with no third-party access that would compromise confidentiality. Proof of such control may be established by sworn affidavit or testimony from witnesses familiar with the arrangement.\n\nService upon a properly designated construct is deemed equivalent to personal delivery and binds the recipient as effectively as any other form of notice. The Desk shall maintain a registry of known constructs used for this purpose, updated quarterly through submissions from practicing mages. Failure to register a construct may render service thereon voidable at the option of the served party, who must then demonstrate diligent effort to locate alternative channels.\n\nThis provision does not create a presumption that all constructs are suitable for legal purposes. Each case shall be evaluated on its merits, considering factors such as reliability, accessibility, and potential for tampering or destruction. The Desk retains authority to reject any proposed construct if it determines the arrangement poses an undue risk to the integrity of proceedings.\n\n§ 200.6 operates in conjunction with § 100.4 regarding the official record of all legal actions taken by the Guild. Documents served upon a construct must be promptly filed and indexed for future reference, ensuring transparency and accountability in the enforcement process. Construct owners are reminded that misuse of this mechanism may constitute obstruction of justice under applicable provisions.\n\nIn sum, § 200.6 provides a practical solution to challenges inherent in serving process on elusive or hidden parties. By leveraging technology and magic where traditional means fail, the Accords maintain their efficacy without compromising fairness. This flexibility exemplifies the Guild's commitment to adapting its rules to meet evolving realities while upholding core principles of due process.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "200.6",
            "100.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Service of Process on Non-Living Entities",
          "text": "§ 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill — Service of Process on Non-Living Entities\n\nThis section addresses the peculiar legal challenges posed by non-living entities that may be parties to contracts or subject to litigation. The core issue is determining proper notice and consent when one side lacks cognitive capacity for understanding or agreement. While natural persons must knowingly assent to binding obligations, constructs operate under different rules derived from their nature as property and tools rather than moral agents.\n\nThe fundamental principle governing such cases is that a construct cannot be held personally liable for breach of contract absent express provision to the contrary in its creation documents. As mere instruments designed by humans to serve purposes set forth therein, an animated blade or stone golem lacks independent volition or intent necessary to form a contractual relationship. Any apparent agreement entered into through its actions binds solely its creator or owner unless explicitly stated otherwise within governing specifications. Thus, when a magically imbued sword fails to cleave as promised during combat training, the remedy lies against its manufacturer for defective workmanship rather than any hypothetical negligence of the weapon itself.\n\nNevertheless, constructs may occasionally acquire limited legal personality through special statutory designation or judicial decree in extraordinary circumstances where traditional remedies prove inadequate. For instance, if an autonomous defense turret repeatedly malfunctions causing property damage despite adequate maintenance by its proprietor, a court might grant standing to sue directly against the machine's control software as a distinct entity. This exceptional approach acknowledges that sophisticated artificial systems can exhibit behaviors indistinguishable from human agency even while remaining fundamentally derivative in origin and authority. The threshold for such recognition remains exceedingly high, reserved only for cases where normal recourse fails utterly to address harm inflicted by a quasi-independent actor possessing unusual capabilities beyond mere mechanical function.\n\nIn all other instances, constructs remain strictly chattel subject entirely to the will of their makers or owners. Any claim against them must ultimately rest upon the liability of natural persons responsible for their design and deployment. The law draws a bright line between tool failures attributable to manufacturing faults versus intentional misconduct by users—only the latter can ground personal responsibility regardless of any deceptive appearance of autonomy displayed during operation. This distinction preserves essential principles of fairness while accommodating novel realities created by advanced magical engineering.\n\nsee § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting regarding ethical constraints on magi; under § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs concerning controlled experimentation environments; reference § 300.1 Schedules of Arts for classification standards applicable to artificial constructs; apply § 1.7 Construction rules when interpreting ambiguous provisions involving non-sentient parties.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "200.6",
            "100.5",
            "400.2",
            "300.1",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.7 Delegation to Clerks and Agents — Limits on Entrusted Authority, § 200.7",
          "text": "No person shall delegate their authority under these Accords beyond the scope expressly granted by statute or rule, nor shall they permit another to act in their name without explicit written consent recorded at the Desk. Any unauthorized delegation renders the principal personally liable for all consequences of the agent's actions, as if performed directly by themselves. This provision aims to prevent abuse of power through proxy arrangements that circumvent oversight mechanisms designed to protect both public safety and the integrity of magical practice. The Desk shall maintain a registry of all authorized delegates and their powers, accessible to any interested party upon request and payment of applicable fees. Violations of this section constitute grounds for disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure. Further guidance on proper delegation protocols may be found in § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks, which outlines specific procedures for entrusting routine tasks to qualified assistants while retaining ultimate accountability. Parties are urged to exercise caution when authorizing representatives to act on their behalf, considering the far-reaching implications of such actions under this comprehensive regulatory framework.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "200.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.8 Temporary Mouths and Proxy Casting — Conditions and Restrictions, § 200.",
          "text": "Under extraordinary circumstances where a licensed caster is temporarily unavailable due to illness, injury, or other compelling reasons, they may authorize another individual to perform specific acts on their behalf through a formal \"Temporary Mouth\" arrangement. Such authorization must be filed with the Desk along with proof of the original caster's incapacity and detailed instructions regarding permitted actions. The acting proxy shall assume full responsibility for all outcomes resulting from their conduct under this authority, notwithstanding any claims of following directions given by the absent licensee. Temporary Mouth arrangements are strictly limited to emergency situations where immediate action is necessary to prevent harm or loss. They may not be used to circumvent normal licensing requirements or extend a caster's operational capacity beyond statutory limits. The Desk retains discretion to deny such requests if it determines that granting them would undermine public safety or violate the spirit of these Accords. This mechanism exists solely as a safeguard for unforeseen contingencies, not as a means to expand individual power improperly. All Temporary Mouth authorizations expire automatically upon resolution of the triggering event unless renewed in writing with updated justification and documentation.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.9 Guest Casters and Cross-Jurisdictional Practice — Oversight Requirements",
          "text": "Non-resident casters seeking to perform services within the jurisdiction governed by these Accords must obtain advance permission from the Autumnwood Desk prior to commencing any magical activities. This requirement applies regardless of whether compensation is exchanged or if the work falls outside standard licensure categories. Guest Casters shall operate under direct supervision of a locally licensed professional who assumes primary responsibility for ensuring compliance with all applicable laws and regulations. The supervising caster must verify the qualifications and insurance coverage of visiting practitioners before allowing them to engage in any magical operations. All guest casting arrangements require written agreements specifying scope, duration, liability allocation, and dispute resolution procedures. Failure to secure proper authorization subjects both parties to penalties up to and including permanent expulsion from professional practice within this region. The Desk maintains a database of approved Guest Casters and their credentials for inspection purposes. This provision balances the need for flexibility in accommodating external expertise with imperative safeguards against unregulated magical activity. Strict adherence to these guidelines preserves the integrity of the regulatory regime while facilitating necessary cooperation across regional boundaries when justified by compelling circumstances.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 201.1 Unauthorized Titan Growth — Prohibition and Penalties, § 201.1 Unauthori",
          "text": "No person shall intentionally induce or permit uncontrolled growth of personal size without express permission from the Desk, except as provided under specific research exemptions granted in writing. Any unauthorized augmentation exceeding normal anatomical proportions constitutes a serious violation of these Accords and may result in immediate suspension of licensure pending investigation. This prohibition exists to prevent abuse of transformative magic that could disrupt social order or endanger public safety. Exceptions may be made for medically necessary treatments approved by qualified practitioners, provided all procedures are documented and reported within seven days. The Desk reserves the right to revoke any previously granted permissions if subsequent misuse is discovered. Parties found guilty of violating this section shall face disciplinary action ranging from fines to permanent disqualification from practicing magic within these bounds. Additional guidelines regarding acceptable limits of physical modification can be found in § 300.1 Schedules of Arts under Transformative Disciplines. Strict enforcement of these standards ensures responsible use of powerful abilities while protecting citizens from potential harms arising from unchecked experimentation with bodily alteration techniques.",
          "key": "g",
          "refs": [
            "300.1",
            "201.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "c4c75549da53d2fb",
