    {
      "cite": "300.1",
      "part": "part_iii",
      "title": "Schedules of Arts",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Schedule A ordinary, B restricted, C Quiet List. Do not reciting C in taverns.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Schedules of Arts",
          "text": "The classification of magical methodologies under the Autumnwood Accords is governed by the tripartite categorization of Schedules A, B, and C. These schedules serve as the definitive regulatory framework for all practitioners operating within the Midlands and under the jurisdiction of Archmage Veyra. Schedule A comprises the Ordinary Arts; these are established disciplines whose risks are well-understood, documented, and deemed safe for general practice by the Conservators without prior individual oversight. Practitioners may employ Schedule A magic for commerce, defense, and personal utility, provided they adhere to the standard safety limitations established in § 1.6.\n\nSchedule B comprises Restricted Arts. These disciplines involve high-variance outputs, significant environmental impact, or inherent risks to the weaver’s mental stability. Use of Schedule B magic requires a specific license issued by the Aegis Magi and is subject to periodic review by the Innovators for research purposes. Any practitioner found utilizing Schedule B arts without a valid license shall be subject to immediate seizure of materials and referral to the Paradox Trial. The distinction between A and B is not merely a matter of potency, but of predictability; any art that demonstrates an unpredictable variance in mana-drain or spatial displacement is to be moved to Schedule B by the Conservator leadership.\n\nSchedule C comprises the Quiet List. These are arts deemed so volatile, socially disruptive, or inherently dangerous to the fabric of the Accords that they are removed from public catalogues and general discourse. The primary mandate regarding Schedule C is the prohibition of its recitation in public houses, taverns, or common gathering places. Any mage who speaks the name of a Quiet List art or describes its mechanics in a tavern shall be deemed to be in breach of the Accords, regardless of whether the magic was actually performed. This provision is designed to prevent the spread of esoteric knowledge to the uninitiated and to maintain the stability of the Midlands. See § 300.3 for the specific procedures regarding the maintenance of this list.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "300.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts",
          "text": "The Accord strictly defines the Unsanctioned Arts as those practices that violate the fundamental sanctity of the cycle of life and the stability of the material plane. Necromancy, blood magic, and the unauthorized manipulation of lingering spirits are the primary pillars of the Unsanctioned Arts. Under the current mediation of Archmage Veyra, these arts are strictly forbidden for general use. No practitioner shall perform, teach, or facilitate the use of Necromancy or its derivatives without a numbered permit issued by the High Council of Conservators. Such permits are rarely granted and are typically reserved for the study of historical anomalies or the containment of existing undead threats.\n\nNotwithstanding the general prohibition, the Innovator faction, under the guidance of Janna Brightspark, maintains the right to file for restricted research permits regarding the Unsanctioned Arts. These filings do not constitute an endorsement of the arts, nor do they grant the petitioner the right to practice these arts in a public or unregulated capacity. All such research must be conducted within designated laboratory settings, under the supervision of the Aegis Magi, and with a clear mandate for the preservation of the Accords. The Innovators may argue for the expansion of research boundaries, but the Conservators retain the final right of veto regarding the issuance of numbered permits.\n\nAny practitioner found engaging in the Unsanctioned Arts without a numbered permit shall be considered a rogue agent of the state. Such actions constitute a primary violation of the Accords and shall result in an immediate referral to the Paradox Trial. While the Innovators may seek to push the boundaries of what is considered \"acceptable\" research, the legal status of Necromancy as an Unsanctioned Art remains absolute. No petition for a research permit shall be considered valid if it lacks a comprehensive risk-mitigation plan approved by both the Conservator and Innovator chairs. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Reclassification",
          "text": "The transition of a magical art from one Schedule to another is a formal administrative process governed by the Accords Desk. A common point of contention involves the movement of an art from Schedule B (Restricted) to Schedule A (Ordinary). It is a fundamental principle of these Accords that the act of reclassification is not a \"party\" to the law; that is, the mere fact that an art is being reconsidered for reclassification does not alter its current legal status. Until the final decree is signed by the Archmage Sovereign and ratified by the Council, any art currently listed in Schedule B remains strictly subject to the licensing requirements and restrictions of Schedule B.\n\nThe process for reclassification requires a dual-signature petition. The Innovators must provide a technical brief demonstrating that the art has been sufficiently stabilized or that its risks are now manageable through standard training. The Conservators must then provide a stability assessment, ensuring that the move to Schedule A will not cause a ripple effect in the Midlands' magical equilibrium. If the Innovators propose a move that the Conservators find reckless, the art remains in Schedule B indefinitely. No mage may begin practicing a reclassified art until the official registry has been updated and the Aegis Magi have issued new standard licenses.\n\nAny attempt to bypass the reclassification process by claiming \"de facto\" status—arguing that because an art is commonly used, it should be treated as Schedule A—is a violation of the Accords. The law recognizes only the official designation. Furthermore, the reclassification of an art does not exempt the practitioner from any past violations committed while the art was still under the Schedule B designation. The legal status of an art is a matter of sovereign decree, not social practice. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Research Copies of Quiet Items",
          "text": "The creation and possession of research copies of arts listed on the Quiet List are governed by stringent protocols. While an art on the Quiet List is prohibited from public discourse and general practice, the Guild acknowledges that the Innovators may require access to such information for the purposes of containment, neutralization, or advanced study. Therefore, the creation of a \"Research Copy\" is permitted only under a direct mandate from Archmage Veyra. These copies are not considered \"public\" and remain subject to the secrecy requirements of the Quiet List.\n\nCrucially, the possession of a research copy does not grant the holder the right to speak of the art. The distinction between the *possession* of the information and the *recitation* of the information is absolute. A mage who holds a research copy of a Quiet List art in a laboratory setting is not in violation of the Accords; however, should that mage discuss the contents of that copy in a tavern, a common hall, or any other non-sanctioned social setting, they shall be prosecuted as if they were reciting a public art. The \"Quiet\" nature of the art is a property of the knowledge itself, not the medium in which it is stored.\n\nAll research copies must be stored in magically-sealed repositories that are inaccessible to non-authorized personnel. The Aegis Magi are responsible for the periodic auditing of these repositories to ensure that no unauthorized dissemination of Quiet List information has occurred. If a research copy is lost or stolen, the holder must report the breach to the Accords Desk within one lunar cycle. Failure to report a breach of a Quiet List research copy is considered an act of treason against the Midlands. The goal of these regulations is to ensure that the most dangerous secrets of the Guild remain hidden from the eyes of the uninitiated, while remaining available to those tasked with the Guild's preservation. See § 300.3.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "300.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.1 Schedules of Arts — The Accords classify all magical disciplines into th",
          "text": "The Quiet List itself remains classified information, accessible solely to high-ranking Guild officials on a need-to-know basis. Public discussion or dissemination of List items is prohibited under § 300.1(c), punishable by immediate expulsion from the ranks and revocation of all privileges. This restriction exists not merely for control, but because certain forbidden arts possess properties that can alter reality itself if misused—a danger no mortal mind should comprehend without proper safeguards in place. Breaching these prohibitions risks unleashing calamities beyond repair, as history has amply demonstrated through the tragic tales of fallen mages consumed by their own hubris and greed.\n\nReclassification requests for items on either Schedule B or C must be submitted via formal petition to the Arcane Research Committee, accompanied by comprehensive data on safety protocols and intended applications. Such petitions are reviewed quarterly, with decisions made solely based on empirical evidence rather than speculation or prejudice. Should a previously forbidden art prove safe through rigorous testing, it may be downgraded to Schedule B status pending further observation. Conversely, any item currently listed as ordinary that exhibits unexpected volatility must be immediately reclassified upward to prevent accidental catastrophe. The burden of proof always rests upon the applicant to demonstrate conclusively that their proposed use poses no unacceptable threat to persons or property.\n\nFinal provisions under this section dictate that all casters carry identification bearing their schedule classification at all times, subject to random inspection by authorized officers who may detain individuals suspected of violating these rules. Failure to produce valid credentials upon demand results in automatic forfeiture of one's license until proven innocent of wrongdoing beyond reasonable doubt. This strict regulatory framework ensures that magic remains a powerful yet controlled force, serving society without descending into chaos and destruction from which even the strongest wills cannot recover unscathed. Only by heeding these wise precepts may we hope to harness the wonders of arcane study responsibly for generations yet unborn.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "300.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Handling and Reporting of Unlisted Arts",
          "text": "In the complex tapestry of magical disciplines governed by the Autumnwood Accords, certain arts are neither ordinary nor restricted, yet still require careful handling and reporting to ensure the safety and well-being of all. These unclassified or unlisted arts present a unique challenge for the Mages' Guild, as they fall outside the strict classifications of Schedule A (ordinary), Schedule B (restricted), and the Quiet List (C). The governance of these arts is critical to maintaining order within the magical community without stifling innovation.\n\nThe handling of unlisted arts involves several key aspects: initial identification, preliminary evaluation, and subsequent reporting. Upon encountering an unlisted art for the first time or discovering its application in a new context, the mage responsible must immediately report it to the nearest Autumnwood Accords Desk. The desk is empowered to convene a panel of experts who will assess the nature and potential implications of the discovered art.\n\nOnce the art has been evaluated, the desk may choose one of three courses of action:\n\n1. **Routine Documentation:** If the art poses no immediate threat or danger and can be safely classified as ordinary over time, it is documented in the Guild’s records for future reference. This allows other mages to learn about new developments without imposing undue restrictions.\n\n2. **Restricted Study with Oversight:** Some unlisted arts may warrant restricted study under close supervision. In such cases, the desk will issue a permit allowing research only within specified parameters and conditions. The mage conducting this research must submit periodic reports to demonstrate compliance with these guidelines.\n\n3. **Automatic Classification into Schedule B or Quiet List:** If an art demonstrates significant potential for harm or poses a serious risk, it may be automatically classified as restricted (Schedule B) or added to the Quiet List (C). This classification is made only after thorough analysis and consultation with peer mages and the desk’s legal council.\n\nIt is important to note that the handling of unlisted arts must adhere strictly to the principles outlined in § 1.4 Persons Bound, ensuring that all actions are taken within the bounds of Guild jurisdiction and authority. Mages who encounter or discover an unlisted art outside Guild property may still incur liability under this Codex if their actions cause harm to third parties or violate any Guild undertakings.\n\nUnder § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks, clerical staff at the Autumnwood Accords Desk are authorized to assist in the identification and initial evaluation of unlisted arts until a full panel can convene. However, final decisions regarding classification remain with the desk itself or its appointed officials. This ensures that all evaluations are conducted by qualified individuals who understand the complexities involved.\n\nThe Quiet List (C) plays a crucial role in this process. Any art classified as restricted or added to the Quiet List must be reported immediately and may not be recited, taught, or publicly discussed without express permission from the desk. The desk will maintain strict confidentiality over these items until they are either downgraded to ordinary status or further restricted if necessary.\n\nIn instances where an unlisted art is discovered within a Guild hall or on Guild property, the mage responsible for its discovery must immediately notify the nearest Aegis Magi for immediate containment and evaluation. This ensures that any potential risks are mitigated promptly and effectively.\n\nFinally, under § 1.6 Definitions and § 1.7 Construction, it is imperative to interpret these provisions in light of the broader goals of the Accords: promoting safety, fostering innovation, and maintaining harmony within the magical community. The handling of unlisted arts must be balanced to ensure that no discipline is unfairly stifled while ensuring that all are regulated fairly and effectively.\n\nBy adhering to these procedures, the Mages' Guild can maintain a delicate balance between protecting its members from dangerous practices and supporting the advancement of knowledge in magic. This approach ensures that even the most unclassified arts are managed responsibly within the framework of the Accords.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "100.6",
            "1.6",
            "1.7"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "c8d063be9d766fb9",
      "refs": [
        "1.4",
        "1.6",
        "1.7",
        "100.6",
        "300.3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "300.2",
      "part": "part_iii",
      "title": "Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Forbidden unless a numbered permit. Innovators still file.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts",
          "text": "Pursuant to the overarching mandates established in § 300.1 Schedules of Arts, the practice of Necromancy is hereby designated as a Class-A Forbidden Art. Any practitioner found engaging in the animation of necrotic tissue, the communion with the deceased for the purpose of servitude, or the manipulation of the veil between life and death without express, written authorization from the Archmage Sovereign shall be subject to immediate summary detention. The Accords recognize that while the manipulation of life essences presents a fundamental risk to the stability of the Midlands, the preservation of the natural order remains the primary directive of the Conservators. Consequently, the use of blood magic, soul-binding, and any methodology that facilitates the permanent extraction of vital essence from a sentient being is strictly prohibited under all circumstances, regardless of the stated intent of the caster.\n\nNotwithstanding the general prohibition, the Innovators may submit formal petitions for \"Restricted Research\" permits concerning the study of necrotic energies. Such permits shall be scrutinized by the Accords Desk to ensure that any experimental application does not breach the fundamental safety threshold of the Autumnwood Accords. However, the mere possession of a permit for research purposes does not grant the holder the right to perform public demonstrations or large-scale applications of the art. All research conducted under such a permit must be contained within a sanctioned laboratory and reported to the Aegis Magi for periodic inspection. Failure to adhere to the containment protocols specified in the permit shall result in the immediate revocation of the permit and a referral to the Paradox Trial for the maximum penalty of magic sealing.\n\nIt is further stipulated that any mage found in possession of unauthorized necromantic reagents, including but not limited to bone-dust of the marked, grave-soil from consecrated sites, or fluids of the deceased, shall be deemed in violation of the Accords. The acquisition of such materials is considered prima facie evidence of intent to engage in Unsanctioned Arts. See § 1.6 for definitions of prohibited reagents. Any individual who knowingly assists in the smuggling or distribution of these materials shall be prosecuted as an accomplice to a high-tier felony. The Guild maintains a zero-tolerance policy regarding the unauthorized use of the \"Quiet List\" for the purpose of concealing necromantic activities; any attempt to mask a forbidden art under a lesser classification shall be met with the full weight of the Accord's penal statutes.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.1",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Quiet List Procedure",
          "text": "The Quiet List serves as a confidential repository of magical methodologies, artifacts, and phenomena that, while not strictly forbidden by the primary Schedules, possess a level of volatility or inherent danger that necessitates restricted awareness. Unlike the public catalogues of sanctioned arts, the Quiet List is not a public document and may not be recited, transcribed, or disseminated in any public forum, including but not limited to taverns, marketplaces, or common halls. The procedure for listing an art upon the Quiet List is initiated by a petition from a member of the Conservator or Innovator factions, supported by a comprehensive risk assessment. Once the Archmage Sovereign approves the petition, the art is entered into the private registry. The purpose of this procedure is to ensure that high-risk magics remain under the purview of the Guild's oversight without causing unnecessary public alarm or providing a roadmap for illicit exploitation by outside entities such as the Regal Empire or the Iron Legion.\n\nA mage who becomes aware of a Quiet List entry through the course of their duties or research is bound by a permanent oath of non-disclosure. Any breach of this confidentiality, whether intentional or through negligence, shall be considered a breach of the Accords and may lead to the revocation of the caster's Guild standing. The Accords Desk shall maintain the physical and metaphysical security of the Quiet List, ensuring that it remains inaccessible to those without the requisite clearance levels. No mage, regardless of rank, may demand access to the Quiet List for the purpose of personal study unless they can demonstrate that such study is essential to the defense of the Midlands or the preservation of the Accords.\n\nFurthermore, the naming of a Quiet List entry in a public setting is strictly prohibited. If a mage is questioned regarding an art that is known to be on the Quiet List, they must provide a generalized description that does not reveal the specific classification or the unique properties of the art. The Aegis Magi are authorized to monitor for such breaches and may impose fines or temporary suspensions on those who fail to uphold the secrecy of the List. The Quiet List is a tool of preservation, not a secret of shame; it is the mechanism by which the Guild manages the most delicate threads of reality. By ensuring that these arts are not publicized, the Guild prevents the destabilization of the common populace and maintains the delicate equilibrium of the Autumnwood Accords. See § 1.6 for further clarification on confidentiality.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Reclassification",
          "text": "The classification of magical arts within the Autumnwood Accords is subject to periodic review by the Accords Desk to ensure that the Schedules of Arts remain relevant to the evolving nature of magical discovery. However, the process of reclassification is governed by strict procedural hurdles to prevent the politicization of magical safety. Specifically, moving an art from a restricted category, such as Schedule B (Restricted Research), to a more permissive category, such as Schedule A (Sanctioned Arts), is not a matter of simple majority vote or partisan preference. Such a transition requires a \"Consensus of Stability,\" necessitating approval from both the Conservator and Innovator chairs, as well as a formal endorsement from the Archmage Sovereign. The Guild recognizes that the Innovators may frequently advocate for the expansion of sanctioned arts, while the Conservators seek to maintain the current boundaries; therefore, the Desk shall not permit a reclassification to occur solely based on the desires of one faction.\n\nAny petition for reclassification must be accompanied by a \"Stability Impact Report,\" detailing the history of the art's use, any recorded incidents of magical fallout, and a proposed framework for its safe integration into the general magical landscape. If a proposed reclassification is deemed by the Accords Desk to pose a significant risk to the Midlands or to violate the core tenets of the Autumnwood Accords, it shall be denied regardless of the factional support. The Desk shall maintain a record of all such petitions, and any mage found attempting to bypass the formal reclassification process by practicing a Schedule B art as if it were Schedule A shall be subject to the Paradox Trial. This rule ensures that the evolution of magic remains a deliberate, measured process rather than a reactive one.\n\nFurthermore, the reclassification of an art does not retroactively change the legal status of previous violations. Any mage who engaged in the unauthorized practice of an art before its formal reclassification remains liable for the infractions committed during the period of its restricted status. The Accords Desk reserves the right to freeze the status of any art currently under review if new evidence of catastrophic risk emerges. This procedural safeguard is designed to prevent the rapid \"normalization\" of dangerous magics that could lead to systemic collapse. The integrity of the Schedules of Arts is the foundation of the Guild's authority, and its preservation is paramount. See § 300.1 for the primary categorizations.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "300.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Research Copies of Quiet Items",
          "text": "The Guild acknowledges the necessity of research and the creation of pedagogical materials for the advancement of the arcane sciences. However, the status of an item or art as a \"Quiet\" entity under the Accords implies a permanent restriction on its public profile. It is a fundamental principle of the Accords that any copy, derivative, or transcription of a Quiet List item remains a Quiet item. If a mage is granted permission to create a research copy of a Quiet art—such as a transcribed scroll, a physical model, or a localized magical echo—that copy must be treated with the same level of security and confidentiality as the original. The act of creating a copy does not \"dilute\" the restricted nature of the art, nor does it authorize the caster to speak of the copy’s contents in public spaces. The restriction is tied to the nature of the magic itself, not merely the specific instance of the medium.\n\nWhile the physical possession of a research copy is permissible for authorized scholars, the dissemination of information regarding that copy is strictly controlled. A scholar may discuss the *methodology* of their research or the *general goals* of their study, provided such discussion does not reveal the specific mechanics, properties, or classifications of the Quiet item. For example, a mage may state they are \"studying a volatile atmospheric phenomenon,\" but they may not describe the specific resonance frequencies or the unique identifiers that place it on the Quiet List. Any mage who shares the specific details of a Quiet research copy with a non-cleared individual, including those who are not members of the Guild, shall be subject to immediate disciplinary action. This includes the seizure of the research materials and a potential referral to the Aegis Magi for a security audit.\n\nIt is important to distinguish between the *existence* of a copy and the *revelation* of its contents. A mage may own a library of Quiet research copies, but they must be kept in a manner consistent with the privacy requirements of the Accords. These materials must be stored in secure locations, and access must be logged. The Accords Desk does not permit the creation of \"public-facing\" summaries of Quiet items; any attempt to create a \"sanitized\" version of a Quiet art for public consumption without the express permission of the Archmage Sovereign is a violation of the confidentiality protocols. The goal of these regulations is to facilitate the pursuit of knowledge while ensuring that the most dangerous and sensitive aspects of the arcane remain shielded from those who lack the training or the temperament to handle them. See § 1.6 for related definitions.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Practices and Regulatory Enforcement",
          "text": "§ 310. Prohibited Rituals — The execution of unsanctioned magical rites outside licensed facilities constitutes a Class A Felony under the Accords. Such activities pose unacceptable risks to public safety and environmental integrity. Violators shall be subject to immediate apprehension by Aegis Magi or local law enforcement, followed by summary trial before an Arcane Tribunal for swift adjudication of guilt and punishment.\n\n§ 311. Regulatory Oversight — The Mages' Guild maintains full authority to inspect facilities, review records, and audit practices as deemed necessary to ensure compliance with all provisions herein. Uncooperative entities may be cited for obstruction of justice under applicable statutes. Persistent non-compliance triggers escalation to disciplinary action ranging from fines to revocation of operating privileges entirely.\n\n§ 312. Penalties and Remedies — Convicted violators face a spectrum of sanctions commensurate with the severity of their offenses, including monetary penalties, community service, mandatory retraining, suspension of licenses, or permanent expulsion from the Guild in extreme cases. Restitution payments may be required to cover damages incurred by affected parties. All judgments remain subject to appeal through established channels within thirty days of issuance.\n\n§ 313. Civil Liability — In addition to criminal penalties, individuals and organizations found liable for unsanctioned practices shall indemnify third parties against any losses or injuries resulting from negligent or malicious use thereof. Plaintiffs pursuing civil claims must provide proof of causation linking defendant's actions directly to claimed damages. Statutes of limitations apply uniformly across all causes of action arising hereunder.\n\nsee § 200.1 Licensure Required for foundational regulatory mandates; under § 500.1 Field Casting for Value, unauthorized work outside permitted zones incurs additional penalties. This Section incorporates principles laid out in §§ 1.6 Definitions and 1.7 Construction while referencing operational guidelines established under §§ 300.2 Quiet List Procedure and 400.1 Research Permits. Adherence to these strictures ensures responsible advancement of magical knowledge without compromising public welfare or undermining the integrity of the Guild's regulatory framework.\n\n\nARCHIVE CARD: [code:ccd:310] § 310 Prohibited Rituals — Execution outside licensed facilities is a Class A Felony subject to immediate arrest and trial. See also §§ 200.4 Suspension and Lapse for disciplinary measures.\n\nmages_guild: {\n        name: \"Mages' Guild\",\n        logo: \"assets/factions/faction_mages_guild.png\",\n        description: \"A formidable and enigmatic organization dedicated to the mastery and control of magic. Its internal structure is fractured, torn between the traditionalist Conservators, who prioritize stability, and the radical Innovators, who push magical boundaries at any cost. The Aegis Magi enforce the will of the dominant faction.\",\n        supporters: ['goodstyle_artisans'],\n        opponents: ['regal_empire', 'iron_legion', 'freelancer_underworld', 'toad_gang', 'toad_cult', 'onyx_hand', 'rakasha_clans', 'cosmic_jesters', 'the_unchained', 'silver_flame', 'oathbound_judges', 'ratchet_raiders', 'koopa_troop', 'rebel_clans', 'crimson_fleet', 'iron_fists', 'moonfang_pack', 'liberated_toads', 'wario_land', 'mushroom_regency', 'peach_loyalists'],\n        penal: [\n            {\n                name: \"The Paradox Trial\",\n                policy: \"Penal System Policy\",\n                spectrum: [\n                    { name: \"Sanction and Seal\", description: \"Rogue mages have their magic sealed away as punishment.\" },\n                    { name: \"Trial by Arcane Duel\", description: \"Guilt is determined by a formal, magically-regulated duel.\" }\n                ],\n                current: 0,\n                trend: 0,\n                description: \"Mages who break the Accords are tried by a council of their peers. Punishments range from having their magic sealed to being cast into a pocket dimension.\"\n            }\n        ],\n        political: [\n            {\n                name: \"The Unsanctioned Arts\",\n                policy: \"Magical Regulation\",\n                spectrum: [\n                    { name: \"Forbidden Schools\", description: \"Necromancy, blood magic, and other 'dark arts' are absolutely forbidden.\" },\n                    { name: \"Restricted Research\", description: \"Dangerous magic can be studied, but only with the council's express permission.\" }\n                ],\n                current: 1,\n                trend: -1,\n                description: \"Certain schools of magic, like necromancy, are forbidden. The Innovator faction constantly pushes the boundaries of what is considered 'acceptable' research, causing friction with the Conservators.\"\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    silver_flame: {\n        name: \"Order of the Silver Flame\",\n        description: \"Zealous paladins and clerics dedicated to eradicating supernatural evil.\",\n        logo: 'assets/factions/faction_silver_flame.png',\n        relations: { \n            allies: [], \n            enemies: ['regal_empire', 'iron_legion', 'freelancer_underworld', 'toad_gang', 'toad_cult', 'onyx_hand', 'mages_guild', 'rakasha_clans', 'cosmic_jesters', 'the_unchained', 'oathbound_judges', 'ratchet_raiders', 'koopa_troop', 'rebel_clans', 'crimson_fleet', 'iron_fists', 'moonfang_pack', 'liberated_toads', 'wario_land', 'mu\n        ],\n        power_level: 7,\n        notable_people: [\n            { name: \"High Inquisitor Alina\", role: \"Grand Inquisitor\", description: \"A stern and uncompromising leader of the Silver Flame.\" }\n        ]\n    },\n    wario_land: {\n        name: \"Wario Land\",\n        description: \"A lawless territory ruled by Wario, infamous for its brutal justice system.\",\n        logo: 'assets/factions/faction_wario_land.png',\n        relations: { \n            allies: ['iron_legion'], \n            enemies: ['mushroom_regency', 'peach_loyalists']\n        },\n        power_level: 6,\n        notable_people: [\n            { name: \"Wario\", role: \"Overlord\", description: \"A greedy and ruthless warlord who answers to no one.\" }\n        ]\n    }\n}",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "500.1",
            "1.6",
            "300.2",
            "200.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unsanctioned Practices and Regulatory Enforcement",
          "text": "The Accords explicitly prohibit the unlicensed practice of necromancy, blood magic, and other unsanctioned arts (§ 300.2). Any individual caught engaging in such forbidden rituals shall be subject to immediate arrest and trial before a full session of the Autumnwood Accords Desk. Upon conviction, penalties may include permanent sealing of magical aptitude, imprisonment without parole, or execution by means of a painless arcane purge.\n\nInnovator factions continue to file applications for permits to conduct research into these dangerous disciplines under strict supervision (§ 300.2). Such requests are scrutinized with extreme rigor, often requiring multiple rounds of peer review before approval is granted. Even when authorized, experiments must be performed within designated facilities and under the direct oversight of Conservator magi at all times. Any deviation from approved protocols results in instant revocation of the permit and severe disciplinary action against responsible parties.\n\nThe Quiet List procedure outlined in § 300.3 remains intact as a mechanism for reclassifying previously sanctioned items deemed too hazardous for public access. Reclassified artifacts are removed from general circulation and stored securely until further notice or permanent deletion from records. Possession of such items without explicit written authorization constitutes a capital offense punishable by death under any interpretation of the Accords.\n\nResearch copies of Quiet List materials may be obtained only upon formal request to the Archmage Sovereign, accompanied by a detailed explanation of intended usage and safeguards against misuse (§ 300.5). Copies are issued with watermarks and tracking glyphs embedded invisibly within the text itself, allowing for instant identification if illicitly distributed. Violation of these terms carries penalties equal to those imposed for actual possession of the original forbidden items.\n\nUnauthorized practices by any entity or individual not holding valid licensure from the Autumnwood Accords Desk shall be treated as a felony crime under both Guild and Imperial law. Regulatory enforcement is conducted jointly by Aegis Magi, local constabulary forces, and specially appointed Oversight Committees empowered to investigate, prosecute, and adjudicate cases arising from suspected violations of the Code. Failure to comply with lawful directives issued during such proceedings may result in additional charges for contempt and obstruction of justice.\n\nThe Accords reserve the right to amend this section at any time without notice should circumstances warrant stricter controls over unsanctioned magical research. All citizens are therefore advised to remain vigilant regarding their surroundings and avoid involvement in activities that could bring disrepute upon themselves or the Guild through association with rogue practitioners operating outside established channels of authority and accountability mandated by these sacred statutes governing all matters pertaining to arcane study, practice, and regulation throughout our realm forevermore.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "300.3",
            "300.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "1bc50331fcd0af5d",
