      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "300.3",
      "part": "part_iii",
      "title": "Quiet List Procedure",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "How an art is listed without a public catalogue.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Quiet List Procedure",
          "text": "The maintenance of the Quiet List is a matter of highest security and sovereign discretion, overseen by the Archmage Sovereign and the High Council of the Conservators. Pursuant to the mandates of the Autumnwood Accords, certain schools of magic or specific arcane methodologies are deemed so volatile, spiritually corrosive, or socially destabilizing that their existence must be shielded from the general populace. The Quiet List is not a public catalogue, nor is it a registry available for consultation by mere citizens, wandering mages, or the uninitiated. It serves as a subterranean repository of forbidden knowledge, a ledger of the \"Unsanctioned Arts\" that the Guild recognizes as existing but refuses to acknowledge in the public sphere. The primary objective of this procedure is the containment of metaphysical hazards that, if publicized, would invite widespread hysteria or provide a blueprint for catastrophic misuse by the Regal Empire, the Iron Legion, or the Cosmic Jesters.\n\nAn art is listed upon the Quiet List through a process of secret adjudication. When a new magical phenomenon is discovered by the Innovators or identified as a recurring threat by the Conservators, it is brought before the High Council in a closed session. If the Council determines that the art poses a risk to the structural integrity of the Midlands or violates the fundamental tenets of Mana Reverence, it shall be struck from the public Schedules of Arts. From that moment forward, the art is officially \"Quiet.\" No public advertisement of the art, no pedagogical instruction in public academies, and no commercial sale of components related specifically to that art may occur. Any mage found to be teaching, practicing, or publicly disseminating the mechanics of a Quiet Item shall be subject to immediate investigation by the Aegis Magi.\n\nThe procedure for listing does not involve the destruction of the knowledge, but rather its sequestration. The Guild maintains a clandestine library where the methodology of these arts is archived for study by authorized personnel only. This is a distinction between \"Forbidden\" and \"Quiet.\" While some arts are forbidden entirely under the Iron Mandate of external powers, the Quiet List is a Guild-specific mechanism of internal regulation. To ensure the integrity of this procedure, any clerk or official who becomes aware of a Quiet Item is bound by a perpetual oath of silence. Recitation of a Quiet Item’s name or properties in a tavern, marketplace, or any other public forum is a breach of the Accords and shall be treated as a high-level offense, potentially resulting in a Paradox Trial. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Reclassification",
          "text": "The administrative movement of magical arts between categories—specifically from the restricted tiers of the Innovators to the sanctioned tiers of the Conservators—is a formal process of Reclassification. This Codex clarifies that the act of Reclassification is a bureaucratic transition of status and does not constitute a \"party\" to any legal agreement or a change in the underlying nature of the magic itself. When an art is moved from Category B (Restricted Research) to Category A (Sanctioned Use), the Guild is acknowledging that the risks associated with the art have been sufficiently mitigated or that the social utility of the art outweighs the perceived danger. However, the transition of a category is a unilateral administrative act by the Archmage Sovereign; it does not create a bilateral contract between the user and the Guild.\n\nMages seeking to petition for the Reclassification of a specific art must provide a comprehensive dossier to the Conservators, demonstrating that the art can be practiced with a high degree of stability and without violating the core tenets of the Autumnwood Accords. If the petition is granted, the art is removed from the restricted schedules and may be taught in standard academies. This change in status does not grant the user immunity from the general regulations governing magical conduct. The mere fact that an art is no longer \"Quiet\" or \"Restricted\" does not mean it is unregulated; it simply means it is no longer subject to the specific secrecy protocols outlined in § 300.3.\n\nFurthermore, any legal disputes arising from the use of an art during the period of its Reclassification shall be judged based on the status of the art at the time of the casting. If a mage utilizes an art that is currently undergoing a transition from restricted to sanctioned, they remain bound by the restrictive permissions of the Innovators until the final seal of Reclassification is affixed by the Archmage Sovereign. The movement of an art is not a negotiation; it is a decree. Therefore, any attempt by a mage or a faction to claim that the \"process\" of Reclassification creates a temporary state of legality is rejected. The law is either in effect, or it is not. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "300.3",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Research Copies of Quiet Items",
          "text": "The Guild acknowledges the necessity of scholarly pursuit and the preservation of arcane history. Consequently, the creation of research copies of Quiet Items is permitted under strict supervision, provided such copies are designated as \"Static Artifacts\" for the purpose of study. It is a fundamental principle of this Codex that the creation of a copy does not strip the underlying art of its \"Quiet\" status. If an art is listed on the Quiet List, any physical manifestation, scroll, or construct derived from that art remains subject to the secrecy protocols of § 300.3. The existence of a research copy does not authorize the user to discuss the mechanics, properties, or existence of the art in public spaces.\n\nThe distinction between the *object* and the *information* is paramount. A mage may possess a physical copy of a Quiet Item within a secured laboratory for the purpose of analysis, but they may not speak of that item in a tavern, a marketplace, or any other public venue. Talking about the item is not permitted; possessing the copy is a regulated privilege. If an Innovator seeks to study a Quiet Item, they must do so within the confines of a Guild-sanctioned research hall. Any dissemination of the knowledge contained within that copy to unauthorized parties constitutes a violation of the Accords. The Guild's stance is clear: the possession of a copy is a matter of research, but the speech regarding that copy is a matter of security.\n\nFurthermore, research copies are not considered \"tender\" or \"goods\" in any commercial sense. They cannot be traded, sold, or gifted to individuals outside of the Guild's authorized research circles. Any attempt to use a research copy as a means of bypassing the secrecy of the Quiet List will result in the immediate seizure of the copy and the initiation of a Paradox Trial for the offending mage. The goal of the Guild is to ensure that the dangerous truths of the Quiet List remain in the shadows, accessible only to those with the clearance to handle them and the discipline to keep them hidden. See § 1.6.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "300.3",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "The Quiet List and Its Maintenance",
          "text": "The Quiet List shall constitute the official record of all arcane arts deemed unsanctioned by this Codex, maintained in strict confidence under § 100.4. Entry onto said List does not imply guilt or wrongdoing but rather necessitates administrative oversight to prevent misuse. The Desk shall review applications for inclusion with impartiality, considering factors such as potential danger to public safety and the likelihood of circumvention of existing prohibitions.\n\nUpon determination that an art warrants listing, a formal notice shall be issued to known practitioners via registered mail or other reliable means, detailing the specific provisions implicated and affording them an opportunity to respond within ten days. Responses shall be reviewed by a panel comprising at least two Conservators and one Innovator, selected for their expertise in the relevant field and absence of personal interest in the matter under consideration.\n\nIn exceptional circumstances where immediate action is required to prevent irreparable harm, the Desk may order provisional inclusion on the Quiet List pending full review. Such measures shall be kept to the absolute minimum necessary and lifted as soon as practical based upon subsequent findings. All decisions regarding listing or delisting shall be documented in writing with supporting rationale provided where appropriate for future reference.\n\nPractitioners whose works appear on the Quiet List shall remain subject to all applicable laws and regulations unless otherwise authorized by specific exemption granted pursuant to § 400.1. Failure to comply may result in disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure under §§ 200.3-200.6. However, mere presence on the List shall not constitute grounds for automatic suspension or disqualification from future applications without further evidentiary showing of noncompliance.\n\nThe Desk retains discretion to reclassify arts originally listed as unsanctioned should new information emerge suggesting diminished risk or altered circumstances warranting reconsideration. Such determinations shall be made through established procedural safeguards designed to ensure fairness and transparency throughout the process while maintaining the integrity of the regulatory framework governing magical practice within the jurisdiction.\n\nsee § 100.4 regarding confidentiality obligations incumbent upon all Guild officials handling sensitive information; refer also to § 200.3 concerning grounds for temporary suspension pending resolution of outstanding issues affecting individual practitioners' standing before the Accords.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "100.4",
            "400.1",
            "200.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Maintenance and Custody of the Quiet List",
          "text": "§ 300.4 Reclassification of Listed Arts — Under § 300.1, arts may be assigned to the Quiet List subject to review by the Conservators and Innovators. This reclassification shall remain in effect indefinitely unless superseded by further ruling under § 300.5 or § 300.6. The Desk reserves authority to alter an art’s classification at any time upon sufficient showing of changed circumstances or new evidence.\n\n§ 300.5 Research Copies and Specimen Custody — Any party desiring a research copy of a listed item must submit a formal petition detailing the intended study, duration, and safeguards against misuse. Approval rests solely with the Desk, which may impose additional conditions at its discretion. Approved specimens shall be held in secure custody under § 300.7 until completion of the authorized investigation, after which they may be returned or disposed of as directed.\n\n§ 300.6 Unauthorized Reproduction and Distribution — No individual or entity may reproduce, distribute, or otherwise exploit a Quiet List art without express written consent from the Guild or its designated agents. Violations shall constitute serious breach under § 1.4 and subject to appropriate sanctions. This prohibition extends to digital formats, derivatives, and any form of commercialization whatsoever.\n\n§ 300.7 Secure Storage Protocols — All items classified on the Quiet List must be maintained in physically secure facilities meeting minimum standards set forth by the Aegis Magi. Access shall be restricted to authorized personnel only, with full audit trails maintained for compliance verification purposes. Breach of these protocols triggers immediate investigation and potential revocation of related permits under § 200.1.\n\n§ 300.8 Public Disclosure Limits — Information concerning specific Quiet List arts may not be publicly disclosed except as necessary to enforce this Code or protect public safety. Unauthorized publication of classified details shall result in disciplinary action commensurate with the severity of the violation. Exceptions require prior approval from at least two Desk members acting independently.\n\n§ 300.9 International Considerations — In matters involving cross-border implications, the Guild shall consult relevant foreign authorities where appropriate. Such consultations do not diminish the binding nature of these provisions within Autumnwood jurisdiction but may inform enforcement strategies in cooperative contexts abroad.\n\nsee § 1.4 Persons Bound; see § 200.1 Licensure Required; see § 300.1 Schedules of Arts\nunder § 1.7 Construction: Aggregation of Conduct applies to cumulative violations of this section.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "300.4",
            "300.1",
            "300.5",
            "300.6",
            "300.7",
            "1.4",
            "200.1",
            "300.8",
            "300.9",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Quiet List Procedure and Reclassification Mechanisms",
          "text": "The Quiet List serves as the confidential register maintained by the Autumnwood Accords Desk to catalog magics deemed too dangerous for open circulation without explicit regulation. Unlike public catalogues, entries on this list do not reveal their existence unless specifically requested through proper channels. This discretion allows the Guild to monitor potentially hazardous arts while avoiding unnecessary panic among lay citizens who might otherwise react poorly to knowledge of such powers existing at all.\n\nReclassification of an item onto or off of the Quiet List follows a rigorous internal review process initiated by either the Conservators or Innovators factions, though ultimately decided by the full Arcane Council. Evidence supporting the proposed change must be submitted along with detailed impact assessments regarding public safety and magical equilibrium. The matter is then debated in closed session before a final ruling is issued with an effective date specified therein. Until such time as reclassification takes effect, existing restrictions remain fully enforceable under penalty of law.\n\nResearch copies of Quiet List items may be obtained by authorized personnel for study purposes only upon presentation of valid credentials and explanation of intended use. Such requests are scrutinized meticulously given the inherent risks involved in handling materials capable of causing catastrophic harm if misused. Copies must remain under constant surveillance during any experiments conducted on Guild premises, with full reporting requirements imposed to ensure accountability throughout the process. Breach of these safeguards invites severe consequences ranging from license revocation to criminal prosecution depending upon circumstances surrounding the infraction committed.\n\nMaintenance and Custody of the Quiet List falls primarily upon specialized units within the Aegis Magi dedicated exclusively to tracking classified magics across all jurisdictions under Guild authority. These agents operate under strict non-disclosure agreements binding them legally to never reveal their findings or methods under any conditions whatsoever, even in the face of death threats or extreme duress. Their work ensures that dangerous arts remain contained where they belong while allowing responsible scholars access needed for legitimate research endeavors when approved by senior leadership. Through diligent stewardship of this vital resource, the Autumnwood Accords continue fulfilling its mandate to balance innovation with stability for future generations alike.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": []
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "30cfd86c2c1297a8",
