    },
    {
      "cite": "300.4",
      "part": "part_iii",
      "title": "Reclassification",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Moving B to A is not a party.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.4 Reclassification",
          "text": "The administrative integrity of the Autumnwood Accords relies upon the immutable classification of magical phenomena, methodologies, and research subjects. It is the foundational principle of this Codex that the designation of a magical art, a research initiative, or a restricted substance is a matter of sovereign decree by the Guild, not a fluid state of being. Under no circumstances shall the mere alteration of a classification label—specifically the movement of a subject from Category B (Restricted Research) to Category A (Sanctioned/Public Art)—be construed as a legal transformation of the underlying nature of the act itself. The Accords Desk maintains that a change in designation is a procedural shift in oversight, not a fundamental transmutation of the magic’s inherent risk or the legal liabilities associated with its practice.\n\nFurthermore, the doctrine of \"Moving B to A is not a party\" is hereby codified as a primary defense against administrative evasion. This means that if a school of magic or a specific ritual is moved from a restricted status to a sanctioned status by the Innovators or the Conservators, the legal precedents, historical prohibitions, and safety protocols associated with its previous \"Category B\" status remain tethered to its history. A practitioner who seeks to argue that a ritual is \"safe\" or \"permissible\" solely because it has been reclassified does so at their own peril. The Guild recognizes that while the regulatory burden may be lessened, the inherent volatility of the arcane does not diminish upon the stroke of a clerk’s quill. The reclassification is a change in the *handling* of the magic, not a change in the *essence* of the magic.\n\nAny attempt by a faction—be it the Innovators seeking to expedite research or the Conservators seeking to isolate a threat—to use reclassification as a means to bypass the requirements of § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts, shall be deemed a violation of the Accords. If a practice is inherently forbidden under § 300.2, no administrative decree may \"reclassify\" it into a sanctioned category to circumvent the prohibition. The Accords Desk shall view any such attempt as a bad-faith maneuver intended to undermine the stability of the Midlands. Practitioners are reminded that the Aegis Magi are tasked with enforcing the current status of the Accords; however, they are not required to ignore the historical hazards of a practice simply because its paperwork has been updated.\n\nIn the event of a dispute regarding the transition of a magical art from a restricted to a public status, the Archmage Sovereign shall mediate the determination of the \"Residual Risk.\" This determination shall dictate what safety protocols remain in place despite the reclassification. For instance, if a blood-based transmutation is moved from Category B to Category A, the Guild may still require specific containment seals, even if the general practice is no longer subject to the Innovator's Research Permits. The Accords Desk shall not recognize any \"amnesty\" granted by reclassification that would exempt a practitioner from the consequences of historical violations. Legal culpability for actions taken while a magic was under Category B status remains enforceable regardless of subsequent administrative shifts.\n\nAll records of reclassification must be filed with the Autumnwood Accords Desk in triplicate. Each entry must include a \"Heritage Clause\" stating that the new designation does not nullify any previous safety warnings or prohibitions. Any practitioner found to be exploiting the nuance between a \"Reclassified Art\" and a \"New Art\" to evade the Paradox Trial shall be subject to immediate sanction. The Guild’s authority is derived from the preservation of the Midlands; therefore, the law shall not be treated as a labyrinth of loopholes where the mere renaming of a danger allows for its unchecked expansion. The Codex is the anchor; the labels are but the markings upon the anchor.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "300.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Reassignment and Transfer of Classified Materials",
          "text": "§ 300.4 Reclassification shall only occur upon formal review by the Desk, supported by sworn testimony or irrefutable evidence. A material may be elevated from Class C to Class B if its possession now presents imminent danger to persons or property. Conversely, downgrading from B to C requires proof that original restrictions have been fully mitigated and no longer justify containment measures. Any reclassification must be documented in the official register with a detailed rationale, signed by two Conservators and one Innovator in attendance. Unauthorized alterations to classification status are deemed criminal tampering subject to immediate arrest under § 400.12(b). Parties seeking reassignment shall submit written applications through their licensed attorneys at least thirty days prior to any proposed hearing date. The Desk retains sole discretion whether to grant relief, and its decision is final and unappealable except via writ of habeas corpus filed in accordance with § 900.5(c)(ii).\n\n§ 300.4 A shall not be reassigned to Class B merely because the holder has violated terms of their license or contract; such misconduct warrants revocation proceedings under § 200.4, not automatic reclassification. Transfer of a material from one authorized party to another requires written consent from both entities and prior approval by the Desk to ensure continued compliance with safety protocols. Any dispute regarding ownership or custody shall be resolved through binding arbitration governed by Articles 15–18 of these Accords. The Desk may issue emergency injunctions restraining further dissemination when necessary to prevent harm, pursuant to its inherent equitable powers recognized under § 701(a)(iii). All reclassification orders remain subject to annual audit and potential reversal should circumstances change materially over time. Failure to adhere strictly to prescribed procedures may result in contempt sanctions including fines up to one hundred guild credits per violation or imprisonment not exceeding six months at the Desk's discretion.\n\n§ 300.4 Reclassification proceedings must be conducted with full due process protections afforded under § 101(d)(ii), including notice, opportunity to be heard, and independent verification of facts presented. The burden of proof rests squarely upon the party requesting modification of classification status, who must demonstrate by clear and convincing evidence that current restrictions are no longer warranted or that new conditions justify stricter controls. Hearings shall be scheduled within forty-five days of application receipt unless extraordinary exigencies dictate otherwise, in which case expedited procedures may apply subject to subsequent ratification. Written findings accompanying each decision shall set forth the factual basis and legal reasoning employed, providing transparency essential for maintaining public confidence in the integrity of this system. Appeals must be lodged within ten calendar days of notification of final ruling and submitted directly to the Appellate Division without delay. All costs associated with reclassification proceedings are borne by the initiating party unless equitable considerations warrant otherwise under § 102(c)(iv).\n\n§ 300.4 This section shall take effect immediately upon adoption and apply prospectively only, except as expressly provided herein. Nothing contained in this subsection shall be construed to limit or impair any rights accrued under prior classifications nor create liability for past acts committed before enactment date. Future amendments regarding reclassification criteria will require a two-thirds vote of the full Assembly acting by secret ballot after public notice period not less than thirty days has elapsed. The Desk is authorized to promulgate implementing rules and regulations necessary to carry out the purposes of this section, subject to review and disapproval by majority vote at any regular session thereafter. Violations of provisions herein shall constitute separate offenses punishable under applicable penal statutes unless otherwise specified expressly in these Accords. Nothing in this provision shall be deemed to waive any existing immunity privileges enjoyed by duly authorized officers or agents acting within scope of employment duties related thereto.\n\n§ 300.4 In case of conflict between terms set forth herein and those contained elsewhere in the complete body of these Autumnwood Accords, provisions specifically addressing matters directly pertinent to reclassification shall control exclusively over general principles stated in unrelated sections. Ambiguities arising from interpretation of this subsection shall be resolved according to established canons of statutory construction unless contrary intent appears plainly upon face thereof. Judicial review limited strictly to questions of law without regard for factual determinations made by the administrative body charged with enforcement responsibilities under Article III hereof. All actions taken pursuant to authority granted herein are binding upon all persons subject to jurisdiction conferred thereby whether individually or collectively acting on their own behalf alone. Nothing contained in this part shall impair remedies available at common law nor exclude any claim arising independently outside scope of regulatory framework established by these governing documents solely intended for administrative convenience purposes only.\"",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "300.4",
            "400.12",
            "900.5",
            "200.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 301.1 Unauthorized Use of Restricted Artifacts",
          "text": "No caster shall knowingly employ any item, device, or construct listed on the Quiet List without express written permission from the Archmage Sovereign. Violation constitutes Class A Felony and grounds for immediate suspension of licensure. Exceptions exist only where emergency circumstances preclude prior consultation and subsequent debriefing yields no evidence of malfeasance. Possession alone is not a crime absent intent to misuse; however, failure to report unauthorized acquisition within seventy-two hours shall be deemed constructive knowledge and punishable as negligence under § 105.3. All findings regarding suspicious artifacts must be submitted for review by the Conservator's Office prior to any disposition, whether through sale, donation, or personal retention. Unauthorized duplication or reverse-engineering of restricted items is strictly prohibited except under strict supervision by qualified personnel designated by the Desk. Any suspected contraband shall be turned in immediately to nearest Aegis Magi for secure storage pending further investigation. Repeat offenders subject to permanent banishment from Guild halls and revocation of all privileges without refund of fees paid. See also § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse; § 301.6 Penalties for Violation.\n\nFILED UNDER § 301.1 Unauthorized Use of Restricted Artifacts\nCRITICAL: Heading must be NEW — do not reuse any existing headings. This page covers a distinct subtopic related to § 301.1. Need 4 more pages toward completion. Moving B to A is not a party. Continue generating natural legal prose with appropriate cross-references and archaic phrasing suitable for this statute book.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "105.3",
            "200.4",
            "301.6",
            "301.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.4 Reclassification and Reassignment of Materials",
          "text": "The reclassification or reassignment of any item on the Quiet List requires an affirmative resolution by a majority vote of the Desk, conducted in closed session under oath. See § 1.9 for restrictions on disclosure of such proceedings. An applicant must demonstrate compelling necessity, typically related to ongoing research projects approved under § 400.1 or national security interests deemed critical by the Conservators. Unauthorized duplication of classified materials is strictly prohibited; all copies must be registered with the Desk and marked accordingly. Failure to comply may result in suspension of privileges under § 200.4.\n\nReassignment of items between authorized custodians shall only occur pursuant to written transfer orders executed by both parties and filed with the Clerk's Office. Such transfers must specify precise conditions for handling, storage, and use, along with a detailed inventory of any ancillary equipment or reagents required. Custodial agreements must include indemnification clauses protecting the Guild against liability arising from improper management of reassigned assets. Breach of these contractual terms constitutes a felony offense punishable by seizure of all involved property and permanent revocation of standing to serve as an Aegis Magi. See § 100.2 regarding authority to bind the Guild in such matters.\n\n§ 301.1 Unauthorized Use of Restricted Artifacts\n\nThe possession or deployment of any Quiet List item without explicit permission from both the original owner and a representative of the Conservators is deemed an act of grand larceny coupled with aggravated misuse of magical resources. Punishment shall be immediate and severe, including confiscation of all contraband, mandatory confinement for re-education, and potential revocation of licensure. Repeat offenders face disbarment and permanent exile from the Guild's halls. Exceptions may be made in cases of self-defense or emergency mitigation of imminent harm, provided such actions are documented thoroughly and reviewed post hoc by an impartial tribunal. See § 500.3 for protocols governing conduct during high-stakes magical confrontations.\n\nThe Desk reserves the right to audit any facility where restricted artifacts are stored or utilized, with full access granted to inspect records, equipment, and personnel as deemed necessary. Refusal to comply with inspection demands will result in automatic forfeiture of all privileges held by the offending party and their affiliates. All findings from such audits shall be recorded confidentially and made available upon request to authorized investigators only. Regular reports on artifact custody must be submitted quarterly by each custodian, detailing condition assessments and incident logs where applicable. Negligence leading to loss or corruption of classified materials will attract punitive damages multiple times the item's appraised value. See § 400.2 for guidelines on maintaining proper records of sensitive holdings.\n\nIn all disputes concerning ownership, custody, or usage rights of Quiet List items, the burden of proof rests with the party asserting a claim. Evidence must be corroborated by sworn testimony and supporting documentation meeting standards set forth in § 1.6. The Desk may appoint independent appraisers or experts as needed to assist in determining fair market value or technical specifications relevant to the case at hand. Decisions rendered shall be final and binding, subject only to appeal within thirty days on grounds of procedural error manifestly affecting outcome. Finality provisions are intended to preserve stability in commercial transactions involving regulated magical goods. See § 600.1 for additional rules governing contractual obligations between casters and non-casters regarding specialized equipment procurement.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.9",
            "400.1",
            "200.4",
            "100.2",
            "301.1",
            "500.3",
            "400.2",
            "1.6",
            "600.1",
            "300.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 301. Unauthorized Use of Restricted Artifacts and Consequences for Breach",
          "text": "§ 301. No individual or entity shall possess, utilize, replicate, or otherwise engage with any item listed on the Quiet List without express written permission from the Autumnwood Accords Desk under penalty of immediate seizure and prosecution for grand larceny and arcane vandalism. All such artifacts are deemed contraband in the eyes of the law regardless of provenance or claimed ownership rights. Possession alone constitutes a Class A felony carrying mandatory imprisonment up to five years plus a fine not exceeding one thousand gold pieces per count. Repeat offenders face enhanced penalties including permanent magical disqualification and exile from Guild-controlled territories. This prohibition applies equally to individuals, corporations, magical societies, and foreign powers. Violations shall be prosecuted under the full weight of applicable criminal statutes without regard for diplomatic immunity or sovereign claims. The Desk retains sole discretion over any future reclassification of items based on evolving threat assessments and technological capabilities. Any unauthorized attempts to bypass security measures surrounding restricted materials will result in escalated charges including attempted theft from federal custody and obstruction of justice. Strict adherence to these provisions is imperative to maintain public safety and national security against ever-present threats of rogue sorcery and criminal exploitation. Failure to comply shall be treated as an act of war by the Guild with appropriate retaliatory measures deemed necessary under existing treaties and statutes. All persons are strongly urged to familiarize themselves fully with this regulation before attempting access to any potentially dangerous artifacts whatsoever under threat of severe legal repercussions upon conviction.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 302. Penalties for Violation of Restricted Artifacts Provisions",
          "text": "§ 302. Any person found guilty of violating the prohibitions set forth in Section 301 shall be subject to immediate arrest and confinement pending trial. Upon conviction, sentences may include lengthy periods of incarceration followed by supervised release with strict conditions attached including mandatory community service, restitution payments to affected victims, and permanent prohibition from holding any public office or license related to magic arts. Additional penalties may involve confiscation of all personal property deemed instrumental in the commission of said crimes along with substantial fines calculated at least double the value of stolen goods if ascertainable. In cases involving organized criminal enterprises utilizing restricted items for illicit purposes, ringleaders face life imprisonment without parole eligibility while lesser accomplices receive tiered punishments based on their level of involvement and cooperation during investigation proceedings. Civil lawsuits brought by harmed parties may result in further monetary damages awards exceeding any statutory caps applicable elsewhere under relevant laws. Repeat offenders commit aggravated offenses warranting enhanced sentencing guidelines with minimum terms doubled for each prior conviction within the last decade. The state reserves the right to seek capital punishment in extreme circumstances where violation resulted directly in death or catastrophic environmental damage through reckless misuse of forbidden powers. Full compliance with these stringent rules remains non-negotiable given the existential risks posed by unchecked access to such dangerous technologies throughout our society today. Everyone must recognize their individual responsibilities toward preserving collective safety above all personal gain whatsoever at this critical juncture in history!",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": []
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
