    {
      "cite": "300.5",
      "part": "part_iii",
      "title": "Research Copies of Quiet Items",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Copies are still Quiet. Talking about them is not.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Research Copies of Quiet Items",
          "text": "Copies are still Quiet. Talking about them is not.\n\nThe protocols governing the Quiet List are designed to ensure that certain sensitive, volatile, or socially disruptive magical phenomena remain outside the public consciousness. Under the Accords, the \"Quiet\" status of an item refers to its inherent nature and the Guild's determination that its public dissemination poses a risk to the stability of the Midlands or the safety of the populace. It is a fundamental principle of the Accords that the creation of a secondary representation—be it a physical manuscript, a mental projection, or a magical duplicate—does not strip the original subject of its Quiet status. A copy of a Quiet Item is, by definition, a Quiet Item.\n\nWhile the physical possession of a copy is subject to the standard restrictions of the Quiet List, the act of discussing the existence of such a copy in a private, scholarly, or authorized setting does not constitute a violation of the Accords. The prohibition is against the *recitation* of the Quiet Item’s specific mechanics, incantations, or secret histories in public forums, such as taverns or marketplaces. The mere mention that a researcher possesses a \"Quiet Copy\" for the purpose of authorized study is permissible, provided the contents of that copy are not revealed. The law distinguishes between the *subject* of the research and the *act* of research.\n\nScholars affiliated with the Innovators may hold copies of Quiet Items for the purpose of restricted research, provided they have obtained the requisite permissions from Archmage Veyra or her designated deputies. These researchers may speak of their work in peer-reviewed settings, discussing the *implications* of the research without revealing the *specifics* of the Quiet Item itself. However, should a researcher describe the specificities of a Quiet Item—regardless of whether they are describing the original or a copy—they shall be subject to immediate sanction. The copy is a shadow of the original; if the shadow is cast into the light of public discourse, the prohibition is breached.\n\nThis distinction is vital for the continued advancement of the arcane arts. It allows for the progress of the Innovators to continue in the shadows of the Hall of Autumn Wood without alerting the Regal Empire or the Silver Flame to the Guild's internal methodologies. The Guild recognizes that knowledge is power, but the Accords recognize that *public* knowledge of certain powers is a liability. Therefore, while the copy remains bound by the silence of the List, the researcher is permitted to speak of the *process* of the research. See § 300.2. Any violation of this distinction by a practitioner shall be referred to the Desk for a Paradox Trial to determine the extent of the breach.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items",
          "text": "The Desk hereby establishes the procedure for obtaining research copies of items listed on the Quiet List, recognizing that such materials may be essential to legitimate magical inquiry despite their sensitive nature. Under § 1.4, all persons bound by the Accords must comply with lawful requests from authorized researchers in good faith.\n\nAn applicant seeking a copy of a Quiet Item shall submit a formal petition to the Desk, detailing the intended research purposes and demonstrating competence through prior licensing under § 200.1 or established academic credentials. The petition must include a sworn statement that the material will be used solely for scholarly advancement and not for personal gain or prohibited ends as defined in § 300.2.\n\nUpon receipt of a complete application, the Desk shall review it within seven (7) days. If approved, a research copy shall be issued containing only the necessary components for study while omitting any dangerous activation sequences or full schematics. The recipient must affix their seal as required by § 1.9 and sign an agreement to return all materials upon completion of their work or at the Desk's discretion.\n\nFailure to adhere to these terms constitutes a breach of the Accords subject to penalties under § 500 et seq., including potential revocation of licensing privileges and referral for Paradox Trial proceedings per § 300.4. The Desk reserves the right to audit research facilities and seize unauthorized copies without notice, pursuant to its broad regulatory authority vested by this Code.\n\nThis provision balances the need for safe access to magical knowledge with appropriate safeguards against misuse, embodying the Accords' dual commitment to innovation and stability as outlined in § 100.7. The Desk shall issue further implementing guidelines as deemed necessary from time to time.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "200.1",
            "300.2",
            "1.9",
            "300.4",
            "100.7",
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items — Continued",
          "text": "§ 300.5 prohibits the duplication or distribution of items on the Quiet List without explicit authorization from the Desk. This restriction extends to both physical artifacts and their magical signatures, which may be embedded within other objects or structures. Unauthorized reproduction of a Quiet Item's unique properties constitutes a violation akin to theft or sabotage, regardless of intent. The prohibition remains in full force even when discussing such items hypothetically or academically; any disclosure that could facilitate illicit acquisition is deemed actionable under § 300.5(a). Exceptions are granted solely through formal petitions reviewed by the Desk, which may impose conditions such as secure storage, limited access, and mandatory reporting of usage. Violations trigger automatic revocation of all existing permits held by the offender and subject them to disciplinary proceedings under Part IV-B. The burden of proof rests upon the accuser to demonstrate that a copy existed and was obtained improperly; mere suspicion is insufficient for sanctions. This provision exists to prevent the proliferation of dangerous or destabilizing magic into the hands of unscrupulous individuals, thereby safeguarding both public safety and the integrity of the arcane arts. Failure to comply with § 300.5 may result in permanent disqualification from practicing magic within Autumnwood jurisdiction, along with financial penalties assessed according to prevailing rates for legal services rendered during enforcement actions.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Research Copies and the Sanctity of Secrecy",
          "text": "§ 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items\n\nA \"research copy\" refers to an authorized reproduction or derivative work based upon an item from the Quiet List, created for scholarly study rather than commercial exploitation. The production of such copies is strictly regulated under this section to preserve the integrity of the original artifact and prevent unauthorized distribution. All requests for research copies must be submitted in writing by a duly credentialed member of the Guild or its affiliated institutions. Each application shall include a detailed description of the intended use, potential risks, and measures taken to mitigate any adverse effects on public safety.\n\nUpon receipt of an application, the Desk shall convene a special committee consisting of representatives from relevant departments to evaluate the request. Factors considered in this evaluation include the necessity of the research for advancing magical knowledge, the competence and character of the applicant, and the likelihood that the proposed work will yield beneficial results without compromising security protocols. The committee shall render a decision within thirty days of receiving the complete application package.\n\nIf approved, the original item shall be temporarily transferred to the requesting party under agreed-upon conditions of custody and handling. These conditions may include mandatory insurance coverage, strict limits on access to unaccredited personnel, and regular inspections by Guild auditors. Failure to adhere to any term of the agreement shall result in immediate revocation of permission and possible disciplinary action against the responsible individuals.\n\nAny unauthorized creation or dissemination of research copies constitutes a serious offense subject to severe penalties under applicable provisions of the Accords. Offenders may face suspension of credentials, fines up to one hundred thousand gold pieces per violation, imprisonment for up to five years, or expulsion from the Guild entirely at the sole discretion of the Archmage Sovereign. Repeat offenders shall be treated with particular severity in accordance with the spirit of this section and the broader goals of maintaining order within our ranks.\n\nIt is further provided that nothing herein shall be construed as granting any implied license to reverse engineer, decompile, or otherwise analyze the internal workings of a Quiet Item beyond what is necessary for the stated purpose of research. Such activities remain prohibited without explicit written consent from both the item's owner and the Guild itself. Violations of this prohibition may lead to civil liability in addition to criminal sanctions as outlined above.\n\nThis section shall be interpreted liberally to effectuate its purposes, which include fostering innovation while safeguarding against misuse. Ambiguities arising hereunder shall be resolved in favor of protecting public welfare and upholding the principles laid forth throughout these Accords. Any attempts to evade or circumvent the requirements imposed by this provision through technicalities or subterfuge are hereby expressly forbidden.\n\nThe provisions contained herein reflect a careful balance between promoting scientific progress and ensuring responsible stewardship of powerful artifacts. As such, they shall remain in full force until amended or repealed by proper authority acting pursuant to established procedures outlined elsewhere within these laws. Future developments in the field of magical technology may necessitate periodic review and adjustment of this framework to address emerging challenges effectively.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.5 Research Copies and the Sanctity of Secrecy",
          "text": "The sanctity of secrecy underpins the very fabric of the Quiet List, and any breach thereof is treated with the utmost gravity by the Mages' Guild. A research copy of a Quiet Item shall retain its classified status regardless of physical separation from the original repository, provided that no unauthorized dissemination occurs. However, mere discussion or speculation concerning such items within prohibited venues—including but not limited to taverns, marketplaces, and other public gathering places—is strictly forbidden. Violators face severe penalties for compromising the confidentiality of Guild affairs.\n\nFurthermore, any attempt to reverse-engineer a research copy with intent to replicate its effects is deemed an act of high treason against the Accords. Such endeavors shall be immediately halted by Aegis Magi upon discovery, and all parties involved subjected to trial for breach of contract and conspiracy. The penalties imposed may range from permanent revocation of licensure to imprisonment in a magical containment facility. In cases where evidence suggests broader collusion or sabotage of Guild operations, additional charges will be pursued to the fullest extent of the law.\n\nIt is imperative that all mages uphold their oaths of silence regarding Quiet Items under any circumstances whatsoever. Failure to do so not only endangers individuals but threatens the stability of society at large. The Mages' Guild remains vigilant in protecting its interests and enforcing these principles without exception or leniency. Any perceived weakness or deviation from established protocols will be met with swift and decisive action to rectify the situation. Restraint, discretion, and unwavering loyalty are non-negotiable expectations for those privileged to serve within this esteemed organization. By adhering strictly to these mandates, mages ensure the continued prosperity and security of all whom they protect through their mastery of arcane arts.\n\nsee § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts\nsee § 1.7 Construction: Aggregation of Conduct\nsee § 400.5 Unauthorized Titan Growth\nsee § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill\"",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "1.7",
            "400.5",
            "200.6",
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.5 Research Copies and the Sanctity of Secrecy — Continued",
          "text": "The creation, distribution, and utilization of research copies remain subject to strict confidentiality protocols designed to preserve their inherent secrecy. Under no circumstances shall a research copy be disclosed to unauthorized parties or employed in contexts inconsistent with its intended scholarly purpose. Violations of these provisions constitute serious breaches of Guild trust and may result in immediate revocation of the original license and subsequent disciplinary action.\n\nThe sanctity of secrecy extends beyond mere physical possession, encompassing all forms of communication regarding a research copy's existence, specifications, or capabilities. Even indirect references that might enable reverse engineering or unauthorized replication are prohibited. Researchers must exercise utmost discretion when discussing their work with colleagues, superiors, or third parties, ensuring no actionable intelligence falls into disreputable hands.\n\nFailure to adhere to these confidentiality mandates exposes not only the individual responsible but also the Guild's broader interests to grave risk. Consequently, all members bound by this Codex are held personally accountable for maintaining rigorous control over research copies and preventing any unauthorized dissemination of sensitive information. Breaches will be met with swift and severe penalties commensurate with the degree of negligence or malice involved.\n\nIn sum, the preservation of secrecy remains paramount to safeguarding both individual researchers and the integrity of magical knowledge itself. All parties must approach their stewardship of research copies with unwavering diligence, recognizing that even seemingly innocuous lapses can have catastrophic downstream effects on Guild stability and security. Vigilance against inadvertent leaks is thus a non-negotiable imperative under § 300.5.\n\nsee § 1.4 Persons Bound regarding obligations applicable to all covered entities; see § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill for analogous restrictions on automated agents; see § 300.3 Quiet List Procedure establishing foundational principles of secrecy enforcement; and see § 300.2 Necromancy defining prohibited schools of magic that may be implicated in improper research copy usage.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "300.5",
            "1.4",
            "200.6",
            "300.3",
            "300.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "9142684072fdfed1",
      "refs": [
        "1.4",
