      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "400.1",
      "part": "part_iv",
      "title": "Research Permits",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Form, fee, who signs.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Research Permits",
          "text": "§ 400.1 Research Permits — Form, fee, who signs.\n\nAll inquiries into the manipulation of high-density mana, the exploration of restricted schools, or the creation of volatile arcane phenomena shall be subject to the rigorous oversight of the Autumnwood Accords. No mage, regardless of standing or lineage, may commence any undertaking that risks the integrity of the Midlands or the stability of the local weave without a valid Research Permit issued by the Accords Desk. The application for such a permit must be submitted on the Official Form 400-A, which requires a comprehensive description of the intended methodology, a projected duration of the research, and a detailed risk assessment of potential blowback. Failure to provide a complete risk assessment shall result in an immediate summary rejection of the application without the possibility of appeal for a period of three lunar cycles.\n\nThe fee for a Research Permit is calculated based on the magnitude of the arcane flux expected during the proceedings. This fee is non-refundable and must be deposited in the Guild’s treasury prior to the signing of the permit. For research involving high-risk categories, such as those bordering on the forbidden schools outlined in § 300.2, the fee shall be tripled to account for the increased surveillance requirements. It is strictly understood that these fees do not constitute a payment for the right to perform the magic, but rather a tax on the potential hazard posed to the public and the Guild's interests. The Accords Desk reserves the right to adjust fee structures based on the current volatility of the regional mana ley lines.\n\nThe authority to sign and validate a Research Permit rests solely with the presiding leadership of the relevant faction, as mediated by Archmage Veyra. For standard research, a signature from a Senior Conservator or a High Innovator is sufficient, provided it is countersigned by an Aegis Mage to ensure enforcement capabilities are available. In cases of extreme complexity or novelty, the Archmage Sovereign must personally review and sign the permit. No provisional permits shall be issued by lower-ranking officials, nor shall a permit granted by a Conservator be superseded by an Innovator without a formal decree from the Desk. All signed permits shall be stamped with the official seal of the Accords; see § 1.9. Any researcher found operating under an expired permit, or an altered permit, shall be subject to immediate seizure of materials and referral to the Paradox Trial for sentencing.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "300.2",
            "1.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Research Permits and Authorization Protocols",
          "text": "§ 400.1 No research into the Schedules of Arts may commence without first obtaining a valid Research Permit issued by the Autumnwood Accords Desk in accordance with this Section. The permit serves as both an authorization to proceed and a record of the permitted scope, conditions, and reporting requirements applicable to the proposed work.\n\nApplicants for permits must submit a detailed proposal outlining the objectives, methodology, expected outcomes, safety measures, and duration of the research project. Where appropriate, applicants shall also provide qualifications demonstrating competency in the relevant fields of study. The Desk shall review all submissions with diligence, consulting with subject matter experts as necessary to assess potential risks and benefits.\n\nPermit fees are set at rates sufficient to cover administrative costs and compensate for any loss of revenue resulting from the temporary suspension of related activities during the research period. Fees vary according to the complexity, scale, and duration of the proposed work. Payment shall be made in Guild tender prior to issuance of the permit. Failure to remit required fees may result in denial of the application or revocation of an already granted permit with associated penalties.\n\nThe permit must be signed by both the applicant and a representative of the Desk attesting to understanding of all terms and conditions set forth therein. Each party retains a copy for their records. The permit shall specify any limitations on methods, materials, locations, or personnel permitted during the research engagement. Deviation from approved parameters without prior written consent constitutes a material breach and may lead to immediate termination of authorization along with potential sanctions under applicable statutes.\n\nThroughout the duration of the project, researchers are required to maintain accurate logs documenting all activities conducted pursuant to the permit terms. Final reports summarizing results achieved and any adverse impacts incurred must be submitted within thirty days following completion or earlier if requested by the Desk. Prompt submission of complete documentation is mandatory for processing subsequent applications and maintaining good standing with the Guild.\n\nViolations of these provisions shall be addressed through established enforcement mechanisms including but not limited to fines, suspension of privileges, imprisonment, and permanent disqualification from future permit eligibility at the sole discretion of the presiding authority. Repeat offenders may face enhanced penalties commensurate with severity of infractions committed. All parties are urged to comply fully with research permit regulations as a means of ensuring safe, responsible advancement of magical knowledge for the greater good of society.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.1 Research Permits",
          "text": "§ 400.1 All research into Schedule I and II magical arts requires an active Permit to Conduct Restricted Investigation, issued by the Innovators' Oversight Committee pursuant to § 300.5. No caster may engage in such study without first obtaining said permit; failure to do so constitutes a Class C violation under § 200.1(b).\n\nPermits are granted on a case-by-case basis after review of the applicant's credentials, intended methodology, and risk assessment. Fees vary according to duration and complexity but shall not exceed the standard rate established by the Conservators' Fiscal Board. Applications must be submitted in triplicate via secure courier or encrypted transmission, accompanied by proof of adequate containment facilities and emergency protocols.\n\nUpon approval, each permit specifies exact parameters including location, timeframe, permitted subjects, and authorized personnel. Deviation from these terms voids the authorization immediately and triggers an automatic audit by Aegis Magi. Violation may result in suspension or revocation of all future permits held by the responsible party.\n\nAll research conducted under valid permit must be logged daily with detailed records of procedures, outcomes, and any adverse effects observed on participants or environment. Final reports are due upon conclusion of the project and shall remain subject to random inspection by Guild auditors. Failure to maintain proper documentation may lead to disciplinary action regardless of successful results.\n\nThe purpose of this system is twofold: first, to ensure that dangerous experiments proceed with adequate safeguards in place; second, to provide a mechanism for tracking emerging trends and potential threats posed by unchecked magical innovation. Through careful oversight, the Guild seeks to balance the pursuit of knowledge with responsibility to public safety.\n\nSee also § 400.2 regarding special provisions for pocket dimension laboratories, and § 300.5 governing the scope and limitations of permitted research activities. Under § 1.7, all terms herein have their ordinary meaning unless defined otherwise within this Part or by subsequent amendment. Nothing in these rules shall be construed to limit inherent rights of free speech or academic inquiry where no harm is imminent.\n\n[END PAGE]",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "300.5",
            "200.1",
            "400.2",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Research Permits and Authorization Protocols — Continued",
          "text": "§ 400.1 Research Permits. All research involving Class IV or higher magical phenomena requires prior authorization in the form of a formal Permit to Conduct Investigations, issued by the Conservatorial Oversight Committee (\"the Committee\"). Application forms must detail the intended scope, methodology, potential risks, and containment measures proposed by the researcher. The Permit shall specify duration, location, personnel authorized to participate, and reporting obligations. Failure to secure a valid Permit constitutes a Class C Infraction under § 200.1(d).\n\nThe fee for issuance of a Research Permit is set at five thousand silver pieces, payable in advance via Wario Coin or equivalent liquid assets. All receipts shall be documented on the official Registry maintained by the Guild Treasurer's Office. Fees may be adjusted periodically to account for inflation and administrative costs associated with oversight activities. Non-payment results in automatic denial of the application and potential blacklisting from future permits.\n\nPermits are non-transferable and strictly tied to the named principal investigator and their immediate research team as listed on the original filing. Substitution of personnel requires written notification to the Committee at least seventy-two hours prior to commencement of work, accompanied by updated credentials and background checks for all incoming members. Unauthorized delegation violates § 200.5(c) regarding unapproved proxies.\n\nViolations of Permit terms constitute grounds for immediate revocation and suspension of future applications until such time as compliance can be demonstrated to the satisfaction of the Committee. Repeat offenses may result in permanent disqualification from conducting regulated research, at the sole discretion of Archmage Veyra upon recommendation by the Oversight Council. Disciplinary proceedings shall follow standard protocols outlined in Part II, Section C of this Code.\n\nResearchers found operating without valid permits or in contravention thereof are subject to seizure of equipment and materials, imposition of monetary penalties ranging from two hundred to one thousand silver pieces per violation, and mandatory remedial training approved by the Guild's Educational Standards Board. Refusal to comply with these requirements may lead to escalation under § 300.4(a)(ii) concerning obstruction of lawful enforcement actions.\n\nThe Committee retains discretion to impose additional conditions deemed necessary for public safety or environmental protection, which must be adhered to without question. Failure to meet such stipulations warrants immediate termination of the Permit and initiation of proceedings for gross negligence under applicable statutes. All disputes arising from permit administration shall be resolved exclusively through formal arbitration channels established herein, precluding judicial intervention except in extraordinary circumstances involving imminent threat to life or property.\n\nThis provision operates subject to existing laws governing intellectual property rights, environmental hazards, and occupational health standards as may apply by force of circumstance. Nothing contained herein shall be construed to diminish the authority of duly constituted governmental bodies to regulate magical activities within their jurisdictions pursuant to statutory mandates. Interjurisdictional conflicts shall be resolved through diplomatic negotiation between relevant authorities or binding arbitration ordered by a competent tribunal upon petition.\n\nFor purposes of clarity and consistency, all references throughout this section to \"research,\" \"investigation,\" or similar terms encompass any systematic effort to acquire knowledge pertaining to magical theory, practice, or application, whether conducted for educational, commercial, or personal reasons. The prohibition extends equally to theoretical speculation and empirical experimentation unless expressly permitted under valid authorization granted hereunder. Breaches of these guidelines are treated with utmost seriousness given their potential consequences for societal stability.\n\nResearchers violating this section face severe repercussions including revocation of licensure, bans on future employment within the industry, and possible criminal charges depending on severity of offense and harm caused. The Guild maintains vigilant oversight to ensure adherence and will pursue all available remedies against those who flout these rules. Compliance remains paramount for safeguarding both individual interests and broader community welfare alike.\n\n\"see § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs; see § 300.1 Schedules of Arts; see § 200.5 Guest Casters\"",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "200.1",
            "200.5",
            "300.4",
            "400.2",
            "300.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.1 Research Permits — Authorization Protocols",
          "text": "Every application for a research permit must be submitted in triplicate, each copy bearing the original signature and seal of the applicant or their duly authorized agent. The forms prescribed by the Guild shall be used exclusively; any deviation constitutes grounds for immediate rejection without further notice. All supporting documentation must accompany the initial submission, including detailed project proposals, risk assessments, safety protocols, and proof of adequate insurance coverage where applicable. Failure to provide complete information may result in indefinite processing delays or outright denial.\n\nUpon receipt, the application shall be assigned a unique identification number by the Clerk's Office for tracking purposes. This number must appear prominently on all subsequent correspondence related to the matter. The designated reviewing officer will then conduct a preliminary examination within seven working days, checking for compliance with formal requirements and obvious deficiencies. Should any errors or omissions be detected, the applicant shall be notified in writing specifying the necessary corrections. Amended submissions are subject to the same rigorous scrutiny as original applications.\n\nOnce cleared for full review, the matter proceeds to committee evaluation by qualified peers specializing in the relevant field of inquiry. The committee convenes no sooner than ten days after all materials have been filed and no later than thirty days thereafter unless extenuating circumstances arise. During deliberations, witnesses may be called upon to testify under oath regarding aspects pertinent to the application's merits. All proceedings remain confidential except as required by law or with explicit consent from all parties involved.\n\nThe committee renders its decision via majority vote after thorough consideration of written arguments submitted within a stipulated timeframe. Dissenting opinions may accompany final determinations but hold no legal force whatsoever. Approved applications are notified in writing along with conditions precedent to activation, if any exist. Rejected requests receive formal denial citing specific deficiencies found during evaluation. Appeals must be lodged within fourteen days of notification using the prescribed procedure outlined herein.\n\nAll permits granted hereunder expire automatically after one calendar year unless renewed pursuant to these provisions. Renewal applicants bear the burden of demonstrating continued compliance with all applicable laws and regulations governing their activities. Late filings incur additional fees calculated at double the original rate plus administrative penalties as determined by the Desk's discretion. Permits operating beyond their expiration date are void ab initio, rendering any actions taken thereunder invalid and subject to punitive sanctions under § 500.1 et seq.\n\nThe Guild reserves absolute authority to revoke or suspend permits at will upon demonstration of cause supported by competent evidence. Notice thereof shall be delivered personally or via certified mail return receipt requested. Affected parties retain the right to contest such actions through established adjudicative channels but must post bond equivalent to potential damages pending resolution. Uncontested revocations take effect immediately without further notice whatsoever. Any attempts to operate under nullified permits constitute contempt of court and invite severe reprisals accordingly.\n\nIn witness whereof, the seal of the Autumnwood Accords is affixed hereto this day in accordance with due process requirements set forth above. Let all concerned take heed lest they tread where none dare venture without proper authorization secured first! 📜✨⚖️\"",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "500.1",
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.1 Research Permits — Authorization Protocols — Continued",
          "text": "All applications for research permits must be submitted in triplicate on standard Guild forms, accompanied by payment of the requisite fee as set forth annually by the Accords Desk. The application shall include a detailed description of the proposed study, the intended methods of investigation, and an assessment of potential risks to persons or property. A sworn statement from the applicant affirming their competence to conduct the research safely must also be attached.\n\nUpon receipt, the application will be reviewed by two independent magi appointed by the Desk. These reviewers shall have no financial interest in the outcome of the request. Their evaluation shall consider whether the proposed work aligns with current safety standards and whether adequate safeguards are planned. Any concerns raised during review may result in additional scrutiny or outright denial of the permit.\n\nIf approved, a signed authorization certificate will be issued to the principal researcher. This document serves as proof of legal authority to proceed with the stated project under specified conditions. Copies shall be filed in both the applicant's personal records and the central registry maintained by the Guild. Violation of any terms outlined in the certificate may lead to immediate revocation and subsequent penalties.\n\nPermits granted for high-risk studies require enhanced monitoring throughout execution. Designated observers from the Conservators or Aegis Magi may accompany researchers at all times during active experimentation. Their role is strictly supervisory; they shall not interfere with legitimate investigative activities unless safety protocols are breached. Failure to comply with these oversight requirements constitutes grounds for disciplinary action against both the researcher and any negligent supervisors.\n\nIn cases where multiple parties seek permits relating to overlapping subject matter, priority shall be determined by date of submission and scope of work approved by the reviewers. The Desk retains discretion to deny competing applications deemed redundant or potentially disruptive. Disputes arising from such determinations will be resolved through established grievance procedures rather than direct confrontation between applicants.\n\nThis section is without prejudice to any existing obligations under separate contracts, patents, or trade secrets. Researchers remain fully liable for protecting confidential information obtained during permitted studies. Unauthorized disclosure of proprietary data may result in civil liability independent of any criminal penalties imposed by this Code. All parties are advised to consult legal counsel regarding specific rights and responsibilities arising from their participation in regulated research programs.\n\nSee also § 200.5 Guest Casters; § 300.1 Schedules of Arts; § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs under related provisions governing controlled magical practice. Under § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks, certain administrative tasks may be delegated to qualified assistants under strict supervision. As noted in § 1.7 Construction, ambiguous terms shall be construed narrowly against the drafter unless context dictates otherwise. This policy reflects the fundamental principle that extraordinary powers demand exceptional accountability measures.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.5",
            "300.1",
            "400.2",
            "100.6",
            "1.7",
            "400.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "fd5a379a089fd8fd",
      "refs": [
        "1.7",
        "1.9",
        "100.6",
        "200.1",
        "200.5",
        "300.1",
        "300.2",
