      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "400.2",
      "part": "part_iv",
      "title": "Pocket Dimensions as Labs",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Hours inside a pocket are still hours for the other party.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Pocket Dimensions as Labs",
          "text": "Pursuant to the regulatory framework established by the Autumnwood Accords and the subsequent oversight of the Conservators, all researchers seeking to utilize extra-planar spaces or artificially generated pocket dimensions for the purpose of laboratory experimentation must adhere to the temporal parity standards set forth herein. While the physical laws of a pocket dimension may be significantly altered from those of the Midlands, the temporal flow remains subject to the Guild’s primary oversight regarding labor, duration, and cognitive preservation. Specifically, any research conducted within a pocket dimension, regardless of its internal expansion or spatial distortion, shall be measured against the standard chronometry of the primary plane of existence from which the laboratory originated.\n\nThe intent of this provision is to prevent the exploitation of \"dilated\" time as a means to circumvent the standard duration limits imposed on restricted research permits. Under the authority of Archmage Theron, no researcher may claim that a week of experimentation occurred in a matter of seconds—or vice versa—for the purpose of reporting progress or fulfilling the temporal requirements of a permit. If a researcher enters a pocket dimension that experiences a slower temporal flow, the Guild shall recognize the duration based on the external world's clock. Conversely, if a researcher utilizes a pocket dimension where time accelerates, the Guild shall view the total duration of the experiment as being equal to the time elapsed in the external world. This ensures that the cognitive toll on the practitioner and the potential for catastrophic instability remain within the manageable parameters defined by the Conservators.\n\nFurthermore, all logistical requirements, including the consumption of resources, the degradation of reagents, and the expiration of temporary mouths, shall be calculated based on the external time. Should a researcher be trapped or detained within a pocket dimension, the Guild’s rescue protocols shall initiate based on the external duration of the disappearance. All laboratory equipment, regardless of its location in a pocket dimension, remains subject to the maintenance schedules of the Guild. See § 1.6 for relevant definitions of \"Pocket Dimension\" and \"Primary Plane.\" Failure to maintain temporal parity during research shall result in the immediate revocation of the researcher's permit and a referral to the Aegis Magi for a formal audit of the laboratory's stability.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Borrowed Hours; Interest in Time",
          "text": "The Guild hereby establishes a strict prohibition against the use of time-stop magic or any temporal stasis effects as a means of stalling, extending, or manipulating legal settlements, debt repayments, or the fulfillment of guild-mandated duties. In any instance where a mage is engaged in a legal proceeding, a debt negotiation, or a contractual dispute, the application of time-stop magic shall not be recognized as a pause in the proceedings. For the purposes of the Accords, time-stop during a settlement is still considered active time. Any party attempting to utilize such magic to \"freeze\" a situation to their advantage shall be deemed in violation of the Guild's integrity protocols.\n\nThe principle of \"Interest in Time\" dictates that if a mage uses magic to halt the flow of time for others while they proceed with a specific action—be it the completion of a task, the movement of goods, or the negotiation of a contract—the Guild shall treat the duration of the stasis as active time for the purpose of all calculations. This includes, but is not limited to, the expiration of deadlines, the accrual of interest on debts, and the expiration of temporary licenses. If a mage spends what they perceive as one hour in a time-stopped state to complete a task, the Guild shall record that hour as having elapsed in the standard flow of the Midlands. No \"borrowed\" time shall be granted to the practitioner; the magic used to halt the world does not grant the user a reprieve from the responsibilities of the world.\n\nFurthermore, any attempt to use time-stop magic to evade the consequences of a Paradox Trial shall be met with the highest tier of sanction. The use of such magic to delay the arrival of an Aegis Magi enforcement team or to stall a peer review shall be considered an aggravating factor in the determination of guilt. All mages are reminded that while the mastery of time is a sophisticated pursuit, it does not exempt the practitioner from the temporal obligations of the Autumnwood Accords. See § 1.6 for definitions of \"Temporal Stasis\" and \"Settlement.\"",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Specimen Custody",
          "text": "All biological or inorganic entities of significant size, complexity, or magical potency that are captured, restrained, or petrified by a practitioner shall be classified as \"Inventory\" under the Guild’s custody regulations. This classification holds until such time as the specimen is officially released, destroyed, or permanently integrated into a controlled environment. No specimen, regardless of its current state of animation or petrification, shall be considered a \"Trophy\" or a \"Personal Possession\" for the purposes of Guild transport or public display until the proper documentation of release or destruction is filed with the Desk.\n\nA petrified monster, for instance, remains a hazardous specimen under the jurisdiction of the Conservators. Because a petrified entity retains the potential for sudden reanimation or magical resonance, it must be handled with the same safety protocols as a living creature. Practitioners are prohibited from transporting such specimens across the Midlands without a certified containment vessel and a valid transport permit. The classification as \"Inventory\" ensures that the Guild maintains a record of all hazardous materials in circulation, preventing the clandestine movement of dangerous entities by rogue mages or radical Innovators.\n\nThe transition from \"Inventory\" to \"Trophy\" may only occur upon the formal \"Decommissioning of Threat\" by a certified Aegis Mage or the \"Sanctioned Release\" by a Conservator. Until such a certification is issued, the practitioner is responsible for the maintenance, containment, and safety of the specimen. Any loss of a specimen while it is still classified as Inventory shall be treated as a breach of safety protocol, potentially resulting in a Paradox Trial. Furthermore, the possession of such specimens in private residences or unauthorized laboratories is strictly forbidden under the research restrictions of the Innovators. See § 400.1 for research permit requirements.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Titan Growth",
          "text": "The Guild hereby clarifies the distinction between \"Controlled Research\" and \"Unauthorized Field Use\" regarding the growth of large-scale magical structures or entities. Any instance of growth magic—including the rapid expansion of flora, the scaling of architectural constructs, or the somatic growth of biological entities—that occurs within a public street, marketplace, or any other non-sanctioned public space shall be automatically classified as \"Unauthorized Field Use.\" This classification remains final and absolute, regardless of whether the practitioner claims the growth was a byproduct of an accident or if the practitioner intends to study the results of said growth at a later date.\n\nThe Innovators may argue that such growth provides a unique opportunity for observation and data collection; however, the Conservators maintain that the risks to public safety and structural integrity in the Midlands outweigh the benefits of such field experiments. Because the growth occurs in a public sphere, it is subject to immediate intervention by the Aegis Magi. The fact that a mage may later seek to study the growth in a laboratory does not retroactively grant the initial growth \"Research\" status. To be classified as research, the growth must occur within a licensed laboratory or a designated \"Wilderness Study Zone\" with a pre-approved permit from Janna Brightspark’s office.\n\nAny mage found to be responsible for unauthorized titan growth in a public area shall be subject to immediate fines and a mandatory review of their licensing. If the growth poses an immediate threat to life or property, the Aegis Magi are authorized to use whatever force is necessary to neutralize the growth, with the practitioner being held liable for all damages. This law serves to protect the infrastructure of the Autumnwood Accords from the reckless expansionism often associated with radical innovation. See § 200.1 for licensure requirements and § 400.1 for research permits.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Laboratories and Time Interest",
          "text": "It is hereby declared that pocket dimensions, when utilized for lawful research or storage purposes under permit, constitute temporary laboratories within the meaning of the Accords. All temporal measurements taken within such spaces shall be accorded full legal effect outside thereof. This ensures equitable treatment between researcher and subject regardless of spatial displacement. See also § 400.3 regarding borrowed hours and interest therein.\n\nSpecimens held in pocket containment retain their natural properties and must be treated with appropriate care during extraction. Any alteration resulting from prolonged sequestration constitutes unauthorized modification under § 200.4. Researchers bear sole responsibility for safe return or destruction of materials post-study. Failure to comply shall result in permit revocation and potential sanctions. Refer to § 500.5 regarding undue influence through specimen manipulation.\n\nUnauthorized expansion of pocket capacity beyond permitted limits is strictly prohibited. Such activity violates core safety protocols established by the Conservators. Penalties include immediate seizure of equipment, suspension of privileges, and possible criminal charges under Article III. Exceptions may be granted only upon formal application demonstrating compelling scientific merit. See § 100.6 for delegation procedures concerning technical specifications.\n\nThe Guild reserves right to audit any facility employing dimensional storage techniques at reasonable intervals. Non-compliance with inspection mandates will incur escalating fines and loss of standing within the magical community. Refusal to submit to oversight renders all subsequent operations void ab initio. This provision exists solely to maintain public safety while enabling legitimate research pursuits. Under § 300.1, all such activities require prior approval from the Aegis Magi.\n\nIn conclusion, § 400.2 balances scientific advancement with necessary safeguards against abuse of powerful technologies. By clearly defining temporal equivalence and specimen integrity requirements, the Accords promote responsible innovation while protecting societal interests. All parties are urged to familiarize themselves thoroughly with these obligations before undertaking any work involving pocket dimensions or related phenomena. Negligence in this regard may lead to severe consequences under existing law. Consult § 500.2 for further guidance on weather-related contingencies affecting experimental outcomes.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "400.3",
            "200.4",
            "500.5",
            "100.6",
            "300.1",
            "400.2",
            "500.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Laboratories and Time Interest — Continued",
          "text": "A pocket dimension, when invoked for the purpose of research or experimentation, shall be deemed an extension of the caster's own laboratory space. Any temporal effects occurring within such a realm—including but not limited to accelerated aging, rapid decay, or localized time dilation—shall accrue directly against the casting entity unless expressly waived in advance by written agreement. This provision applies regardless of whether the pocket dimension is maintained through conventional magical means or illicit artifacts.\n\nThe conservation and protection of specimens extracted from pocket dimensions falls under the exclusive jurisdiction of the Conservators. Unauthorized possession, modification, or dissemination of such materials constitutes a Class A felony punishable by permanent sealing of magical abilities. Violation of these custodial mandates may result in immediate arrest and trial before the High Council on charges of endangerment to public safety.\n\nUnauthorized expansion of pocket dimensions beyond agreed-upon parameters is strictly prohibited and shall be treated as an act of aggression against all other casters. Such actions require prior approval from both the Innovators' Research Committee and the Conservatory Oversight Board. Failure to obtain such permission renders any subsequent use of the expanded capacity void ab initio, leaving the user fully liable for all resulting damages and consequences.\n\nThe interests of third parties whose time and lives may be affected by pocket dimension usage shall always take precedence over academic curiosity or personal gain. In cases where conflicting claims arise regarding temporal allocation within a shared magical space, the matter shall be adjudicated according to established precedents set forth in Part 700 of this Codex. Until such guidelines are promulgated, all casters are directed to exercise utmost prudence and restraint when operating within communal realms.\n\nThis section supersedes any prior understandings or informal arrangements concerning pocket dimension usage that may exist between individual practitioners or organizations. Any disputes arising from non-compliance with these provisions shall be resolved through binding arbitration by a panel of three impartial mages appointed by the Archmage Sovereign, whose decision shall be final and enforceable throughout all member territories.\n\nThe provisions herein are intended to safeguard both the integrity of magical research and the broader well-being of society at large. By establishing clear boundaries for acceptable conduct within pocket dimensions, the Guild aims to foster an environment where innovation can flourish safely and responsibly under careful supervision. All members are urged to acquaint themselves thoroughly with these guidelines and adhere strictly thereto in all their dealings.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "400.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "a77c08d2eeca9e29",
      "refs": [
        "1.6",
        "100.6",
        "200.1",
        "200.4",
        "300.1",
        "400.1",
        "400.3",
        "500.2",
        "500.5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "400.3",
      "part": "part_iv",
      "title": "Borrowed Hours; Interest in Time",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Time-stop during settlement is still time.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Borrowed Hours; Interest in Time",
          "text": "Pursuant to the overarching tenets of the Autumnwood Accords, specifically regarding the temporal stability of the Midlands, any mage who employs chronomancy or time-dilation effects during the duration of a legal settlement, a debt negotiation, or a formal mediation process shall be held to the standard of continuous temporal progression. It is hereby codified that the suspension of the flow of time for the purpose of deliberation, contemplation, or the cooling oftempers does not constitute a cessation of the legal clock. For the purposes of the Guild, time-stop during settlement is still time. If a party to a dispute invokes a chronomantic stasis to prolong a negotiation, the interest accrued on any outstanding magical debts or temporal reparations shall continue to calculate at the standard rate prescribed by Archmage Veyra.\n\nThe Innovators may argue that a frozen moment is a vacuum of progression, yet the Conservators maintain that the intent of the mage is the primary driver of the Accord's jurisdiction. Therefore, any attempt to utilize \"Borrowed Hours\" to evade the consequences of a delayed agreement shall be viewed as a bad-faith manipulation of the arcane weave. Mages seeking to enter a period of stasis for the purpose of intensive study or meditation must first secure a research permit under § 400.1, and even such permits do not exempt the individual from the temporal taxes of the Guild. Any mage found attempting to \"pause\" the legalities of a contract while continuing to benefit from the status quo of that contract shall be subject to a Paradox Trial.\n\nFurthermore, the Aegis Magi are authorized to impose a \"Temporal Interest Penalty\" on any individual who purposefully creates a localized time-stop to prevent the expiration of a temporary mouth or a provisional license. The Guild recognizes that time is a sacred resource, and its artificial manipulation for the sake of administrative evasion is a violation of Mana Reverence. See § 1.6 for definitions regarding temporal flux and magical debt. Any disputes arising from the calculation of interest during a period of suspended animation shall be adjudicated by the Desk, with the Archmage Sovereign holding final authority on the determination of \"active\" versus \"passive\" temporal states.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Specimen Custody",
          "text": "The possession and transport of biological entities, whether living, dormant, or petrified, are governed by strict protocols to ensure the preservation of the Midlands' ecological and magical integrity. Under the Accords, any creature of significant magical potency or size that has been rendered into a state of petrification, stasis, or permanent slumber is strictly classified as inventory, not a trophy. This classification remains in effect regardless of the owner's intent, the aesthetic value of the specimen, or the degree of martial prowess required to achieve the petrification. A petrified monster is inventory, not a trophy, until released.\n\nOwners of such specimens are required to register the entity with the nearest Guild outpost within three lunar cycles of acquisition. This registration serves as a manifest of the specimen's origin, its current state of stasis, and the specific seals used to maintain its condition. The failure to register a petrified specimen constitutes a violation of the research safety protocols outlined in § 400.1. Furthermore, the transport of such inventory across provincial lines requires a verified transport manifest; if a specimen is seized by the Aegis Magi without proper documentation, it shall be impounded as unauthorized material and may be subject to immediate destruction if it poses a risk to the public.\n\nThe distinction between inventory and trophy is critical for the purposes of the Paradox Trial. A mage who displays a petrified creature as a mark of personal achievement in a public forum may be cited for \"Ostentatious Display of Forbidden Stasis,\" especially if the creature belongs to a species protected by the Conservators. Conversely, a mage who treats a petrified entity as a mere object of trade without proper licensing may be charged with \"Negligent Handling of High-Potency Remains.\" No specimen may be \"released\"—whether through the reversal of a petrification spell or the termination of a stasis field—without a formal release permit issued by the Desk. See § 1.6 for further clarification on the definition of \"High-Potency Remains.\"",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Titan Growth",
          "text": "The Guild maintains a zero-tolerance policy regarding the manifestation of large-scale, rapid-growth magic in non-designated zones. Any instance of growth magic utilized within a public thoroughfare, residential district, or any area not specifically zoned for agricultural or botanical experimentation is strictly classified as unauthorized field use. This classification is absolute and pertains to the act of manifestation itself, rather than the subsequent academic pursuit of the perpetrator. Growth magic in a street is field use, not research, even if someone later studies it.\n\nThe Innovators frequently advocate for the \"experimental freedom\" of the arts, yet the Accord dictates that the safety of the populace overrides the curiosity of the individual. If a mage utilizes growth charms to manifest flora, structures, or biological masses in a street, the resulting infrastructure is considered a public hazard. Even if the practitioner subsequently submits a retrospective request for research credit or seeks to categorize the growth as a \"controlled experiment,\" the Guild shall not grant such status retroactively. The act of casting in a public space bypasses the protections and privileges afforded by § 400.1, and the practitioner shall be liable for all damages and the costs of removal as determined by the Aegis Magi.\n\nFurthermore, any growth that results in the obstruction of trade routes, the damage to Goodstyle Artisan products, or the endangerment of common travelers will result in immediate summary judgment. The Guild does not recognize \"unintentional growth\" as a valid defense if the mage failed to consult the local cartography or the one-T cartography for land-use restrictions. Because the growth occurs in a public domain, the legal status of the magic is \"Field Use,\" which carries a higher penalty of sanction and seal than \"Restricted Research.\" Any mage found in violation of this clause may be required to undergo a Paradox Trial to determine the extent of their negligence. See § 1.6 for the distinction between \"Field Use\" and \"Restricted Research.\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.4 Specimen Custody and Unauthorized Expansion",
          "text": "The possession, transport, or storage of any specimen classified as Tier I+ requires an active Research Permit issued by the Mages' Guild Desk. Any failure to maintain proper custody shall be deemed a violation of § 200.1 Licensure Required. In addition, all specimens must remain under direct supervision of a licensed Arcanist at all times; unattended specimens are subject to immediate seizure and destruction without notice or compensation to the owner.\n\nAny specimen that expands beyond its designated containment parameters shall be considered \"Unauthorized Titan Growth\" under § 400.5. Such growth is strictly prohibited and constitutes a Class A violation punishable by forfeiture of the specimen and fines up to one hundred gold pieces per percent of excess mass. The owner or custodian of the specimen shall bear full responsibility for any damages resulting from Unauthorized Titan Growth, including but not limited to structural damage, ecological disruption, and personal injury.\n\nFurthermore, all specimens must be kept in a secure facility approved by the Guild, with access restricted solely to licensed personnel. Any attempt to remove a specimen from its designated location without explicit written permission shall result in immediate revocation of the Research Permit and potential criminal charges under § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting. The Mages' Guild reserves the right to inspect any facility holding specimens at any time, with or without notice, and to seize any suspected contraband for analysis.\n\nViolations of this section shall be prosecuted aggressively by the Aegis Magi and other enforcement officials. Repeat offenders may face permanent disqualification from all research activities and expulsion from the Guild. The Desk shall maintain a detailed log of all specimen movements and conditions to ensure full compliance with these regulations at all times. Failure to adhere to § 400.4 Specimen Custody and Unauthorized Expansion is grounds for immediate suspension or revocation of any relevant permits or licenses held by the offending party.\n\nUnder § 1.9 Seals, Stamps, and What Is Not a Seal, any unauthorized alteration or tampering with specimen containment vessels shall be treated as desecration of sacred relics and punished accordingly. Additionally, per § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts, certain types of specimens may contain residual magical energies capable of affecting nearby casters. Caution must be exercised when handling such items to avoid unintended consequences. Consult with a qualified Arcanist before attempting any manipulation or study of these volatile substances without proper authorization.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "400.5",
            "100.5",
            "400.4",
            "1.9",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.3 Borrowed Hours; Interest in Time",
          "text": "Under this Article, \"Borrowed Hours\" refers to the temporary suspension or dilation of temporal flow within a localized area, effectuated through ritual or magical means. Such an alteration of natural time does not negate the passage of actual minutes, nor does it exempt participants from their contractual obligations accrued prior to the onset of the phenomenon. The Guild maintains that all casters remain fully liable for damages sustained during periods of distorted chronometry, as such effects are deemed an inherent risk of practice rather than a force majeure event.\n\nWhere \"Interest in Time\" is concerned, any unauthorized accumulation of temporal debt incurred by a caster shall be adjudicated under the provisions governing delinquent fees and penalties. The accruing interest rate on overdue payments shall increase proportionally to the duration of the time distortion, calculated according to standard actuarial tables adjusted for magical variance. Failure to remit said obligations within thirty days of notification may result in immediate suspension of licensure until full restitution is made.\n\nClosely related to these provisions is the issue of \"Specimen Custody,\" which mandates that all materials used in conjuring Borrowed Hours must be secured and returned post-event, subject to inspection by Guild inspectors. Any loss or damage to said items shall be compensated at fair market value plus a punitive surcharge equivalent to one hundred percent of the original cost. This policy exists to prevent rogue practitioners from hoarding volatile components for potential misuse in future incidents.\n\nFinally, any \"Unauthorized Expansion\" resulting directly from improper application of time-altering techniques shall be treated as a serious violation of the Accords. Such acts may lead to revocation of licensure and referral to disciplinary proceedings under the Paradox Trial system. The Guild emphasizes that responsible stewardship of temporal forces requires not only technical proficiency but also ethical foresight, ensuring that no single individual or entity gains undue advantage through manipulation of reality itself.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "400.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.3 Borrowed Hours; Interest in Time — Continued",
          "text": "A caster who pauses the flow of magic for any reason, whether to consult notes or simply admire their own handiwork, incurs a penalty known as 'Borrowed Hours.' Each moment spent outside active casting reduces the total duration available by one minute, regardless of whether the clock has ticked forward. This rule prevents mages from artificially inflating their output through dilatory tactics.\n\nInterest in Time applies when a spell's effects outlast its initial incantation. A fireball may burn for seconds after being launched, but that lingering heat counts as additional casting time. Similarly, a healing ward may activate later if triggered by an external event, extending the original expenditure of magical hours. The Desk assesses interest based on observable consequences rather than theoretical possibilities.\n\nSpecimen Custody mandates safekeeping of volatile materials until their purpose is fulfilled. A potion-brewer must secure her ingredients against theft or accidental exposure; failure to do so results in forfeiture of any remaining value. This obligation persists even after the contract concludes, for a rogue reagent could destabilize an entire batch of finished goods.\n\nUnauthorized Titan Growth refers to spontaneous increases in spell potency beyond what was contracted. While welcome bonuses, they still draw from the caster's allocated hours. If a healing ritual unexpectedly restores more health than agreed upon, that surplus counts against the original allotment. The Desk discourages such windfalls as inherently unfair to the client who paid for specific results.\n\n§ 400.3 further clarifies that time stops only when casting is truly suspended—not merely interrupted by minor distractions. A mage distracted by a sudden noise must resume from where they left off, not from the beginning of the spell. This provision preserves the integrity of contracted services while acknowledging reality's chaotic nature.\n\nUltimately, these rules exist to ensure predictability and fairness in magical commerce. By standardizing how time is measured and allocated, the Guild protects both casters and clients from exploitation or misunderstanding. The Desk remains vigilant against any attempts to circumvent these principles for personal gain.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "400.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "58f4de953c1d0909",
      "refs": [
        "1.6",
        "1.9",
        "100.5",
        "200.1",
        "300.2",
        "400.1",
        "400.4",
        "400.5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "400.4",
      "part": "part_iv",
      "title": "Specimen Custody",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A petrified monster is inventory, not a trophy, until released.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Specimen Custody",
          "text": "Pursuant to the oversight of the Autumnwood Accords, all biological or semi-biological entities that have undergone petrification, stasis, or arcane crystallization shall be classified as Guild Inventory rather than personal trophies, spoils of war, or decorative acquisitions. This classification applies to any creature, beast, or sentient being rendered immobile by high-level transmutation or temporal suspension. The Accords Desk asserts that the inherent potential for animation within a petrified specimen constitutes a lingering arcane signature that must be accounted for within the Guild's safety protocols. Any mage who secures such a specimen is strictly prohibited from displaying it in public spaces, taverns, or non-sanctioned residences without a verified containment permit issued by the Conservators.\n\nThe distinction between inventory and trophy is a legal necessity to prevent the misappropriation of hazardous magical assets. A trophy is a static object of personal vanity; a petrified monster is a dormant hazard capable of sudden, uncontrolled restoration or violent expiration. Should a specimen be released from its state of stasis—whether by intent, accident, or environmental decay—the responsibility for its containment shifts from passive inventory to active containment. Any mage found in possession of a released specimen that has not been properly neutralized or bound shall be subject to immediate seizure by the Aegis Magi. The Guild maintains that the preservation of the public's safety outweighs the individual's right to own a \"curiosity\" of such volatile nature.\n\nFurthermore, the transportation of petrified specimens across the Midlands requires a manifest of transit. If a specimen is moved from a private laboratory to a public thoroughfare, it must be accompanied by a clerk or a licensed handler who possesses the knowledge to initiate a \"Cold Seal\" in the event of a breach. Failure to provide such documentation shall result in the immediate revocation of the owner's Research Permit and a referral to the Paradox Trial for reckless endangerment of the populace. See § 1.6 for definitions regarding \"Arcane Signature\" and \"Cold Seal.\" All specimens held in private vaults must be reported to the Archmage Veyra’s office biennially for audit.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Titan Growth",
          "text": "The application of growth magic within any urban center, village, or public thoroughfare is hereby defined as unauthorized field use, regardless of the caster's stated intent or the eventual academic utility of the results. This provision serves to distinguish between controlled laboratory experimentation and the reckless expansion of magical flora or fauna in civilian spheres. If a mage utilizes growth enchantments to produce oversized produce, decorative greenery, or structural reinforcements in a street or marketplace, the act is automatically classified as a violation of the Accords. The fact that a mage may subsequently attempt to study the resulting growth as part of a research project does not retroactively合法ize the initial casting. The initial act of expansion is the primary offense; the study of the consequence is a separate, and often secondary, matter of record.\n\nThe Accords Desk recognizes the Innovators' desire to push the boundaries of botanical and structural transmutation. However, the Conservators maintain that the unpredictable nature of \"Titan Growth\" poses an unacceptable risk to the structural integrity of the Midlands. Growth magic of this magnitude can cause unintended structural collapse, the choking of water supplies, or the creation of ecological dead zones. Therefore, any mage seeking to experiment with growth magnitudes exceeding standard horticultural limits must do so within a designated research zone or a pocket dimension as authorized under § 400.2. The use of such magic in a street is a breach of the public peace and an affront to the stability of the Accord.\n\nMages who engage in unauthorized field growth are liable for all costs associated with the removal of the growth and the restoration of the affected area. If the growth results in property damage or the endangerment of life, the caster shall be summoned before a council of peers for a Paradox Trial. The penalty for such violations may range from the Sanction and Seal of the caster's growth-related schools to the full sequestration of their research materials. See § 1.6 for further clarification on the \"Midlands Public Thoroughfare\" boundaries. No \"Wario Coin\" or other non-tender currency shall be accepted as restitution for damages caused by unauthorized growth. All restitution must be paid in standard Guild-approved currency or through labor-service to the Goodstyle Artisans for reconstruction.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "400.2",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.4 Specimen Custody; Unauthorized Titan Growth",
          "text": "The Guild's exclusive right to collect, store, and study magical artifacts extends to all specimens under its lawful control, whether seized during inspections or surrendered by licensees upon expiry of their permits. Specimens shall be maintained in secure facilities equipped with climate-controlled environments, containment wards, and monitoring sensors to prevent accidental activation or contamination of adjacent areas. Unauthorized possession, transfer, or modification of Guild property constitutes a serious violation subject to immediate suspension and potential revocation of licensure.\n\nIn the event that any specimen begins to undergo spontaneous metamorphosis or exhibits anomalous growth patterns inconsistent with known biological processes, all personnel must cease operations immediately and report findings to their direct supervisor. The affected area shall be evacuated except for designated safety officers equipped with appropriate protective gear and countermeasures. Under no circumstances shall an individual attempt to contain or neutralize a growing anomaly without explicit authorization from the Desk's Safety Division; unauthorized intervention may result in catastrophic escalation of the situation and severe personal injury or death.\n\nShould an artifact escape containment or suffer structural failure resulting in release of hazardous energies, affected parties must initiate emergency protocols outlined in Appendix D without delay. This includes sealing off impacted zones with magical barriers, summoning specialized cleanup crews trained in biohazard mitigation, and conducting thorough decontamination procedures to neutralize lingering residues. Failure to comply with these obligations may expose the responsible party to civil liability for damages incurred by third parties, as well as criminal charges under applicable statutes regulating dangerous substances and materials.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to inspect any premises licensed for magical research at reasonable times and frequencies deemed necessary to ensure compliance with safety standards and regulatory requirements. Inspectors shall be accompanied by a representative of the host organization during all examinations unless otherwise directed by law or exigent circumstances. Findings of violations will be documented in writing and communicated promptly to the concerned entity, which shall then have an opportunity to respond before any punitive measures are imposed. Repeated noncompliance may lead to termination of the license and referral for disciplinary action as warranted under the Accords.\n\nIt is hereby declared that all specimens collected or held by the Guild fall within its proprietary interest and shall be protected accordingly against theft, destruction, or misuse by unauthorized persons. Any individual found in possession of such items without proper documentation may be subject to seizure proceedings initiated by the Enforcement Corps acting on behalf of the organization. Penalties for unlawful acquisition range from fines proportional to market value up to imprisonment for repeat offenders, depending upon severity and intent established during adjudication.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "400.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.4 Specimen Custody and Unauthorized Titan Growth",
          "text": "The lawful possession of any specimen, whether captured in the wild or synthesized in a laboratory, shall be conducted only under strict supervision by a licensed caster or their duly appointed proxy. No individual, regardless of rank or status, shall take matters into their own hands without explicit authorization from the Mages' Guild Desk. This prohibition extends to all forms of acquisition, including but not limited to hunting, mining, harvesting, and theft.\n\nThe unauthorized cultivation or enhancement of a specimen's mass beyond its natural limits, known as \"Titan Growth,\" is strictly forbidden unless conducted pursuant to a valid research permit issued under § 400.1. Any individual found guilty of engaging in illicit Titan Growth shall be subject to immediate revocation of their license and potential criminal prosecution for magical misuse. Furthermore, all specimens must be maintained in secure storage facilities approved by the Guild, with regular inspections to ensure compliance with safety protocols. Failure to adhere to these requirements may result in severe penalties, including fines, suspension, or permanent disqualification from practice.\n\nThe Mages' Guild reserves the right to inspect any premises suspected of harboring unauthorized specimens or engaged in illicit Titan Growth activities upon reasonable suspicion. Such inspections shall be conducted by duly appointed conservators or their agents, who shall have full authority to seize evidence and question individuals involved. Resistance to lawful inspection may be construed as an admission of guilt and subject to enhanced sanctions under applicable penal provisions.\n\nAll citizens are hereby put on notice that ignorance of the law shall not serve as a defense against accusations of violating specimen custody statutes. The Guild urges all practitioners to familiarize themselves with these regulations and seek clarification from official channels should any uncertainties arise. By adhering strictly to established protocols, we safeguard both ourselves and the greater community from the dangers posed by unchecked magical experimentation.\n\nLet it be known that the Autumnwood Accords stand as a bulwark against chaos and excess. Within their bounds lies freedom for those willing to play by the rules. Without them, we descend into barbarism, each seeking power at another's expense. Choose wisely: you may find yourselves on opposite sides of an irreconcilable divide before long. Act now while there is still time to make amends and secure your future under this code. For in upholding these laws lies the strength to build a better world for all.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "400.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.4 Specimen Custody and Unauthorized Titan Growth — Continued",
          "text": "No person shall knowingly possess, transport, or display any specimen derived from a controlled entity without prior authorization from the Conservators' Bureau of Biosecurity. Violation constitutes possession of contraband under § 215.3(a). Furthermore, no mage shall intentionally accelerate the growth rate of such specimens beyond natural limits, whether through direct magical intervention or indirect manipulation of environmental conditions. Such acts are deemed reckless endangerment and may result in revocation of licensure pursuant to § 200.4(b).\n\nSpecimens acquired legally by a licensed caster retain status as controlled property even after release from the original containment unit. The owner must maintain adequate security measures proportional to the specimen's inherent danger level, failing which it shall be seized upon discovery by any sworn peace officer or Guild Inspector. Failure to comply with these storage mandates may lead to forfeiture of the item and additional penalties under § 320.6.\n\nIn cases where a specimen escapes containment due to no fault of its owner, immediate reporting is required within one hour via emergency channel 9-1-1-MAGI. The owner shall cooperate fully in efforts to locate and recapture the loose asset. Prompt disclosure may mitigate fines but does not absolve liability for any damages incurred during the escape interval. Deliberate concealment of an escaped specimen doubles all applicable penalties under § 216.7.\n\nUnauthorized modification of a specimen's genetic makeup or magical constitution is strictly prohibited absent written permission from both the original owner and the Conservators' Research Division. Such tampering may render the specimen unpredictable and highly hazardous to surrounding populations. Any resulting injuries or property losses shall be borne solely by the responsible party, regardless of good faith intent. This restriction applies equally to hybrid specimens created through cross-breeding techniques permitted under § 405.2.\n\nIt is hereby declared that any attempt to weaponize a controlled specimen for personal gain, whether through direct combat use or deployment as an improvised explosive device, shall be prosecuted as a Class A Felony and subject to immediate termination of licensure without appeal. Repeat offenders face permanent disqualification from the practice of magic within this jurisdiction. Such actions represent not merely a breach of contractual obligation but a fundamental threat to public safety warranting the most severe sanctions available under existing law.\n\nThis provision serves to balance the legitimate interests of researchers and collectors with the paramount need to protect society from potential misuse of dangerous entities. By establishing clear boundaries for specimen handling and modification, these regulations aim to prevent catastrophic accidents while preserving opportunities for beneficial study. All casters are reminded that their privileges carry corresponding responsibilities, particularly when dealing with materials capable of causing irreparable harm if mishandled. Adherence to these guidelines ensures continued safe operation of the magical community within our borders.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "215.3",
            "200.4",
            "320.6",
            "216.7",
            "405.2",
            "400.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Specimen Custody and Unauthorized Titan Growth — Continued",
          "text": "Specimens acquired in lawful research contexts shall remain under the exclusive custody of the granting Permittee until such time as they are returned to their natural state or otherwise disposed of pursuant to this Code. No researcher, whether licensed or otherwise, shall retain specimens for personal gain, display, or resale without express written authorization from the Archmage Sovereign. Violation of this provision constitutes a Class C infraction subject to sanctions up to and including permit revocation and imprisonment at the discretion of the Paradox Trial Council.\n\nUnauthorized Titan Growth refers to any spontaneous magical manifestation not directly attributable to permitted experimental procedures. Such phenomena shall be immediately reported to Guild oversight within one solar cycle or face automatic forfeiture of all research permits held by the responsible party. Failure to report may indicate malicious intent and trigger an immediate investigation under § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items. Penalties for unauthorized growth range from monetary fines proportional to market value of affected materials to permanent exile from Guild circles depending on severity and frequency.\n\nThe burden of proof regarding lawful acquisition versus illicit possession rests solely upon the accused researcher who asserts innocence. In cases where provenance cannot be conclusively established, conservative measures shall prevail: seizure of suspect specimens pending further analysis by certified Aegis Magi. Costs associated with such examinations are borne equally between the Guild and the individual subject to dispute resolution through standard arbitration channels outlined in § 100.4 Records of Speech.\n\nResearchers maintaining private collections outside official facilities must adhere strictly to inventory logs detailing each item's origin, purpose, current condition, and planned disposition timeline. Quarterly audits conducted by random selection of peer reviewers will verify compliance with storage protocols designed to mitigate risk of accidental release or contamination spread. Deviations discovered during routine inspections warrant disciplinary action commensurate with the nature of infractions found alongside potential remediation requirements imposed upon offending parties.\n\nThis section remains subject to periodic revision as scientific understanding evolves and new challenges emerge from frontier exploration endeavors undertaken by dedicated scholars throughout our realm at large. Future amendments will be made only after thorough consideration given input gathered via formal petition processes open annually during designated public notice periods announced well in advance through official publications distributed widely across affected regions worldwide for maximum transparency accountability purposes served best by robust regulatory frameworks ensuring safety security prosperity all around us always!",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "300.5",
            "100.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "114fefc3e8efa3e6",
