      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "400.5",
      "part": "part_iv",
      "title": "Unauthorized Titan Growth",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Growth magic in a street is field use, not research, even if someone later studies it.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Titan Growth and Field Use Classifications",
          "text": "The Guild hereby establishes a strict distinction between sanctioned laboratory growth and unauthorized field application of expansionary arcana. Pursuant to the primary mandates of the Autumnwood Accords, all magical research conducted under the auspices of the Innovators must be confined to designated research zones, such as those permitted under § 400.2. Any application of growth magic that occurs within a public thoroughfare, residential district, or any non-sanctioned geographical coordinate shall be automatically classified as field use. This classification remains immutable regardless of the caster's stated intent, academic justification, or the subsequent analytical study of the resulting growth. The Guild recognizes that the volatility of expansionary magic is compounded by the proximity of civilian infrastructure and public transit; therefore, the mere act of manifesting such growth in a street environment constitutes a breach of the safety protocols governing the use of the Unsanctioned Arts.\n\nSpecifically, the Guild shall not entertain any defense claiming that the growth of a Titan-class entity or any related biological expansion was intended as a preliminary experiment for future scholarly review. If the growth occurs in a public street, it is, by definition, a field operation. The fact that a mage may later seek to study the biological properties, structural integrity, or magical residue of said growth does not retroactively transform the initial casting into a protected research activity. See § 400.1 for the requirements of research permits, which explicitly exclude any actions resulting in uncontrolled environmental expansion. Any mage found to have initiated growth magic in an unauthorized area shall be subject to immediate investigation by the Aegis Magi to determine the extent of the structural damage and the risk posed to the surrounding populace.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild notes specific precedents involving unauthorized field use on the Markop region. Any growth magic utilized on Markop that results in Titan-scale expansion is deemed a violation of the Accords' stability requirements as championed by the Conservators. Such incidents shall be recorded as unauthorized field use and shall not be eligible for the protections afforded to the Innovators' restricted research permits. The Guild shall treat all such instances as reckless endangerment of the Midlands' magical equilibrium. Any mage involved in such growth shall be referred to the council for the Paradox Trial, wherein the punishment may include the sealing of the mage's arcana or permanent relocation to a pocket-prison to ensure the continued stability of the public sphere.\n\nIn cases where a mage claims that the growth was a byproduct of a different, sanctioned spell, the burden of proof lies with the caster to demonstrate that the growth was neither intended nor foreseeable. However, the Guild maintains a policy of strict liability for expansionary magic; if the growth occurs in a street, the caster is held responsible for the entirety of the resulting ecological and structural consequences. No provision exists within this Codex to excuse growth magic based on the \"necessity\" of the expansion or the \"scientific value\" of the Titan. For further clarification on the distinction between research and field use, see § 400.1 and § 400.2. All growth magic conducted without a specific permit for the specific location shall be prosecuted under the most severe penalties available to the Aegis Magi.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "400.2",
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Titan Growth and Field Use Classifications — Continued",
          "text": "The Accords strictly prohibit the unauthorized cultivation or expansion of titans outside sanctioned research environments, recognizing such acts as inherently dangerous to public safety and magical equilibrium. Any individual found engaging in illegal titan growth shall be subject to immediate arrest by Aegis Magi for violation of § 400.5, with penalties escalating based on the scale of disruption caused. This includes but is not limited to property damage, interference with emergency services, and potential loss of life from uncontrolled magical reactions. The burden of proof rests upon the accuser to demonstrate beyond a reasonable doubt that the defendant knowingly participated in prohibited growth activities. Furthermore, possession of materials specifically designed for titan expansion may constitute constructive possession under § 102(34), subjecting owners to similar sanctions regardless of direct involvement in cultivation efforts. Law enforcement officers are empowered to seize suspected contraband and detain suspects pending further investigation without warrant upon reasonable suspicion. The Accords mandate that all titans remain contained within approved facilities unless expressly permitted by the Conservators under tightly controlled conditions. Violations will be prosecuted aggressively, with repeat offenders facing permanent disqualification from magical practice and possible imprisonment. Public safety remains paramount, necessitating strict enforcement of these prohibitions to prevent catastrophic incidents stemming from reckless manipulation of powerful arcane entities. Any disputes regarding the classification of growth activities shall be adjudicated by a specially convened panel of magistrates versed in both legal and technical matters pertaining to titan cultivation. The Accords reserve the right to amend this section as new evidence emerges or circumstances warrant, ensuring continued protection for all citizens against foreseeable harms posed by unregulated titan expansion operations. § 400.5 shall be construed liberally to encompass any form of illicit titan manipulation, whether intentional, negligent, or reckless, reflecting the gravity of potential consequences inherent in such forbidden practices.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "400.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Titan Growth and Field Use Classifications — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "§ 400.5 Unauthorized Titan Growth constitutes field use regardless of subsequent intent to study the growth, as it involves the active application of magic in a non-laboratory setting with potential effects on persons or property. See § 200.1 (Licensure Required) and § 300.1 (Schedules of Arts). The mere act of causing a Titan to grow beyond its natural state without explicit permission from the Guild is an actionable violation, irrespective of whether the caster later claims educational purposes. This principle ensures that casual or experimental casting cannot be shielded by post-hoc justification. Unauthorized growth also encompasses any manipulation of magical fields that results in anomalous physical changes, such as spontaneous enlargement or distortion of organic matter. Under § 400.2 (Pocket Dimensions as Labs), true research requires containment within approved facilities designed to mitigate risks. Street-level magic lacks these safeguards and thus falls under penal provisions rather than scholarly inquiry. The burden of proof rests with the accused to demonstrate that their actions did not constitute unauthorized field use, a high standard given the inherent dangers of unchecked growth magic. Violations may include fines, license revocation, or imprisonment depending on severity and impact on public safety. See § 100.2 (Who May Speak for the Guild) regarding enforcement authority and § 300.4 (Reclassification) for potential adjustments to affected magical categories based on new precedents. The Desk maintains absolute jurisdiction over all unauthorized growth incidents, reserving discretion to impose additional sanctions as warranted by circumstances. No individual or entity may claim immunity from these provisions without explicit exemption granted in advance by the Guild. This clause serves as a final clarification of boundaries between permissible and impermissible magical practice under any conditions whatsoever.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "400.5",
            "200.1",
            "300.1",
            "400.2",
            "100.2",
            "300.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Titan Growth and Field Use Classifications — Continued — Administration",
          "text": "The classification of unauthorized titan growth as field use rather than research stems from the inherent nature of the act itself. When an archmage invokes growth magic upon a subject outside the controlled environment of their laboratory, they are effectively casting a spell with unpredictable consequences, regardless of any subsequent analysis. The Guild maintains that such actions constitute improper application of arcane power and must be treated as violations under § 200.1 Licensure Required and § 500.1 Field Casting for Value.\n\nThis strict interpretation serves to protect the public from potential hazards associated with unregulated magic use. Without proper oversight, unauthorized growth rituals could result in catastrophic failures ranging from minor physical deformities to total annihilation of surrounding areas. The precedent set by recent incidents involving rogue mages growing massive flora across entire neighborhoods underscores the necessity of enforcing these regulations rigorously.\n\nFurthermore, distinguishing between research and field casting is crucial for determining appropriate penalties and remediation efforts. While both activities involve magical practice, their contexts and risks differ significantly. Research conducted under permits adheres to safety protocols designed to mitigate harm, whereas unauthorized growth rituals lack such safeguards entirely. Consequently, the Guild asserts that any unlicensed application of growth magic must be prosecuted as field use violations subject to full penal consequences under § 300.1 Schedules of Arts and related provisions.\n\nThis stance applies universally across all instances of unauthorized titan growth, irrespective of intent or outcome. Whether aimed at cultivating rare herbs or expanding territorial borders, such acts remain firmly within the realm of prohibited field casting unless explicitly sanctioned through proper channels. The Guild remains unwavering in its commitment to maintaining order and safety in accordance with the Autumnwood Accords.\n\nsee § 200.1 Licensure Required; see § 500.1 Field Casting for Value; see § 300.1 Schedules of Arts",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "500.1",
            "300.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Titan Growth and Field Use Classifications - Continued — Administra",
          "text": "The classification of unauthorized titan growth as field use, rather than research, has profound implications for how such incidents are handled under the Accords. Under § 400.5(a)(2), any instance where a caster's magic causes uncontrolled growth in a public space is deemed a violation of the public peace and safety, regardless of intent or subsequent study. This strict interpretation serves as a deterrent against reckless experimentation with powerful arcane forces in populated areas.\n\nThe enforcement of this provision falls primarily to the Aegis Magi, who are charged with monitoring magical activity and responding swiftly to any breaches. Upon detection of unauthorized growth, magistrates shall immediately secure the area and detain the responsible caster for further investigation. The accused party must then present evidence of their qualifications and intent to justify the use of such dangerous magic. Failure to do so will result in immediate suspension of licensure under § 200.4(a).\n\nIn cases where unauthorized growth leads to property damage or personal injury, additional penalties may be imposed pursuant to §§ 100-399. The severity of sanctions shall depend on the extent of harm caused and the caster's prior disciplinary record. Repeat offenders face heightened scrutiny and may require mandatory counseling or retraining before being permitted to practice again.\n\nThe Accords further stipulate that all incidents of unauthorized growth must be documented thoroughly by responding magistrates, including details of the magical technique employed and its effects on surrounding objects and individuals. These records shall become part of the permanent case file and serve as reference for future disciplinary proceedings if warranted. Transparency and accountability remain paramount in maintaining public trust in the Guild's ability to regulate magic responsibly.\n\nIt is imperative that all casters internalize the gravity of their responsibilities when wielding such potent abilities. Unauthorized growth represents not merely a technical infraction, but a potential threat to societal stability. Through diligent adherence to these provisions, the Autumnwood Accords continue to evolve and strengthen as guardians against abuse of arcane power.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "400.5",
            "200.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Titan Growth and Field Use Classifications — Continued — Oversight",
          "text": "The prohibition against unauthorized titan growth extends to all forms of accelerated biological development, whether induced by spells, artifacts, or alchemical compounds. The Accords recognize that such practices pose significant risks not only to the individual subject but also to the broader ecosystem and public safety. Therefore, any attempt to catalyze rapid cellular division outside of a licensed laboratory setting constitutes field use, regardless of intent or outcome.\n\nThis principle is absolute: no matter whether the growth magic was cast with benevolent motives or malicious designs, the act itself remains illegal under § 400.1 unless conducted within approved facilities and subject to strict oversight by Conservator magistrates. The Guild maintains that unregulated manipulation of human biology invites unpredictable mutations, potential loss of control over one's own body, and downstream consequences for society at large. Hence, all instances of unauthorized growth magic are treated as field use violations subject to full penal proceedings under the Paradox Trial framework.\n\nIn cases where accelerated development has already occurred, affected parties must immediately report to their local Conservator station for assessment and containment measures. The Guild shall determine appropriate sanctions based on severity and scope of impact. Mitigating factors may include demonstration of remorse, cooperation with investigations, or provision of useful data for future safety studies. Aggravating circumstances will increase penalties proportionately, including permanent sealing of magic powers or exile from the Midlands entirely.\n\nThe provisions herein apply equally to natural phenomena mimicking growth effects—such as spontaneous plant proliferation or animal reproduction surges—as these too may signal dangerous imbalances in ambient mana levels requiring prompt intervention by trained professionals. Ordinary citizens are urged to refrain from interfering with such events without proper authorization, lest they exacerbate the situation inadvertently. Instead, all observations and anomalies should be documented and submitted promptly to Guild authorities for analysis.\n\nBy strictly enforcing these prohibitions against unauthorized growth magic, the Autumnwood Accords uphold their foundational commitment to protecting both individuals and communities from the perils inherent in unchecked magical experimentation. Adherence to this mandate remains paramount to maintaining stability across all sectors of society.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "400.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "0ca5ac1233be0ba7",
      "refs": [
        "100.2",
        "200.1",
        "200.4",
        "300.1",
        "300.4",
        "400.1",
        "400.2",
        "500.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "500.0",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Field Jurisdiction; Conduct Outside Guild Property",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Location alone does not grant immunity. Distinguish Person Bound status from actionable conduct: property damage, public danger, Guild resources or marks, Guild undertakings, tracked assets, and field duties. An unaffiliated traveler is not automatically enrolled, but a destructive Fireball can still trigger notice, restitution, investigation, and process.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "500.0 Field Jurisdiction; Conduct Outside Guild Property",
          "text": "This section establishes the territorial reach of the Autumnwood Accords and clarifies that physical location within or without Guild property does not, in itself, determine immunity from legal process. While a caster operating beyond the perimeter of any registered Hall is not automatically enrolled as a Person Bound under § 1.4 solely by virtue of proximity to the Midlands or the casting of unauthorized spells, their conduct may still fall within the actionable scope of this Codex if specific liabilities are triggered. The distinction between status and liability is critical; jurisdiction attaches to the nature of the act rather than merely the geography of the scene. Therefore, any individual engaging in covered conduct outside Guild property shall be subject to notice, investigation, and potential remedy as prescribed herein, regardless of whether they reside in Autumn Wood or operate on unaligned lands, wilderness, other planes, or unsanctioned field work.\n\nThe test case for liability arises frequently where a destructive Fireball incident occurs upon third-party property without direct Guild involvement. Under § 1.5 Relation to Other Law, the Accords do not supersede local civil claims but rather aggregate them into a unified process of restitution and accountability. An unaffiliated traveler who casts a spell that damages property or creates public danger is not automatically a member of the Guild, yet they incur Codex liability for covered conduct including unauthorized use of Guild marks, equipment, or a breach of a Guild undertaking even if the caster was merely passing through. Such an incident triggers a duty to answer for harm, requiring notice to be served and restitution calculated according to established standards, regardless of the lack of prior licensure or membership status recorded in the Archives; see § 1.6 Definitions for the specific terms governing such liability.\n\nSpecific categories of actionable conduct include the use of tracked Guild assets or anomalies, which binds any operator to the custody rules found under § 200.5 Guest Casters regarding supervision and liability. If a field-casting affects a tracked asset or utilizes a resource marked by the Aegis Magi, the caster becomes subject to oversight as if they were operating within formal jurisdiction. Furthermore, emergency rules apply where public danger is imminent, requiring immediate cessation of activity to prevent further harm. This section does not confer universal membership upon all travelers; rather, it widens the scope of enforcement to ensure that property damage and public safety remain protected even outside the physical bounds of the Guild Hall. Consequently, any claim or process initiated against a caster for such conduct shall be adjudicated according to these provisions, ensuring that the sovereign authority of Archmage Veyra extends to the consequences of magic wherever they are cast, provided the Accords are implicated by the nature of the damage or the resources employed.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "1.5",
            "1.6",
            "200.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Conduct Within Unaligned Lands and Wilderness",
          "text": "In the vast and untamed expanses beyond Guild property, where no hall stands sentinel and no member wields an official seal, the jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild remains a delicate yet crucial matter. The Codex must extend its reach to ensure that all covered conduct, regardless of location, adheres to the standards set forth by the Autumnwood Accords. This page addresses how the Guild asserts authority over actions taken within unaligned lands and wilderness areas.\n\nUnder § 104.2, the Mages' Guild recognizes that its members are not merely confined to the boundaries of their halls or sanctioned field sites. The conduct of a Person Bound extends beyond the immediate vicinity of any Guild property, including unaligned lands and wilderness areas where they may be traveling or working. This principle applies even when no Guild hall, fee, or member is present at the location of an incident.\n\nFor instance, consider a scenario involving a destructive Fireball cast by an unaffiliated traveler in a remote forest. The spell damages nearby structures owned by the Goodstyle Artisans, who have no affiliation with the Mages' Guild. Under § 502.1, this act constitutes a breach of covered conduct as it causes significant property damage and poses public danger. Although the traveler is not a Person Bound in the traditional sense, their actions are actionable under the Codex.\n\nThe Guild may assert process or oversight over such incidents based on several criteria outlined in § 502.3:\n\n1. **Property Damage**: If an unaffiliated caster's spell causes damage to property located within unaligned lands that is not directly related to any Guild hall or asset, the Guild may initiate a proceeding under § 408.1 regarding restitution and compensation.\n\n2. **Public Danger**: Any act of casting that endangers public safety, even in an unaffiliated setting, falls under the jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild. Under § 502.4, the Guild is empowered to investigate such incidents and take appropriate action as outlined in § 316.2.\n\n3. **Unauthorized Use of Guild Resources or Marks**: If a non-member uses Guild resources or marks without authorization, this constitutes a breach under § 208.3, subjecting them to legal consequences regardless of their location.\n\n4. **Guild Undertakings**: Any caster who breaches a formal undertaking with the Mages' Guild, even when cast outside any property, is liable for penalties under § 412.5.\n\n5. **Tracked Assets or Anomalies**: If an unaffiliated caster's spell affects a tracked asset or anomaly that falls within unaligned lands but is of significant importance to the Guild, such as a known pocket dimension or ancient magical artifact, the Codex applies under § 304.2.\n\n6. **Field Duties**: A member who undertakes field duties beyond official sanctioned sites may be subject to Guild oversight for their actions during those duties. Under § 108.4, the Guild retains jurisdiction over such members while they are performing these tasks.\n\nIn cases where a destructive Fireball or similar event occurs in unaligned lands, the Guild may initiate investigations under § 502.6. This process includes:\n\n- Notifying the caster of their actions and potential consequences.\n- Requesting a cessation of further damage.\n- Ordering an assessment of damages and initiation of restitution processes.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to levy penalties upon any individual found responsible for such conduct, even if they are not a Person Bound at the time. Under § 502.7, the Guild may also seek legal action against unaffiliated casters who repeatedly engage in destructive or dangerous behavior.\n\nIt is important to note that jurisdiction over unaligned lands and wilderness areas does not imply automatic enrollment of travelers into the Guild. The Codex applies only when specific conditions are met—such as property damage, public danger, use of Guild resources, breach of a formal undertaking, effect on tracked assets or anomalies, or adherence to field duties.\n\nIn summary, the Mages' Guild extends its jurisdiction over covered conduct within unaligned lands and wilderness areas through § 502.1–§ 502.7, ensuring that all actions taken by covered casters are subject to the same standards of responsibility and accountability as those on Guild property. This approach maintains the integrity and safety of magical practices across all domains.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "104.2",
            "502.1",
            "502.3",
            "408.1",
            "502.4",
            "316.2",
            "208.3",
            "412.5",
            "304.2",
            "108.4",
            "502.6",
            "502.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Conduct Involving Tracked Anomalies or Guild Assets",
          "text": "In the complex realm of magical governance, the Autumnwood Accords Desk must address conduct involving tracked anomalies or Guild assets outside the bounds of Guild property. The conduct of such persons is not merely an extension of their status as Person Bound; it involves a broader scope of liability and accountability that extends beyond mere membership in the Guild.\n\n### Jurisdiction Over Tracked Anomalies\n\nThe first principle to consider under this topic is the jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild over tracked anomalies. The Guild's authority extends far beyond its physical halls, encompassing all entities or phenomena that have been officially recorded on the Quiet List. This list serves as a comprehensive catalog of anomalies and assets that require monitoring due to their potential for harm, utility, or both. Under § 300.4 Reclassification, the Guild reserves the right to reclassify items based on new evidence or changing circumstances.\n\nWhen an anomaly is tracked, it falls under the immediate jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, regardless of where it is located. This means that a Person Bound who casts spells near such an anomaly may be held accountable for any resultant damage, even if the spell was not directly targeting the anomaly itself. For instance, consider the scenario involving a Fireball spell cast by an unaffiliated traveler on an uninhabited stretch of forest. The Fireball damages nearby property and potentially disrupts the balance around a tracked anomaly located within the radius of its explosion.\n\n### Accountability for Damage to Guild Assets\n\nThe second aspect involves accountability for damage caused to Guild assets, even when such assets are not located on Guild property. The Codex mandates that any individual or entity that causes harm to these assets must be held accountable under § 100.4 Records of Speech and § 200.5 Guest Casters. This includes guests who cast outside the bounds of Guild property but whose actions impact tracked anomalies or disrupt the balance around such entities.\n\nIn instances where a Fireball spell causes significant damage to an area containing a tracked anomaly, the responsible party must provide restitution under § 400.4 Specimen Custody and § 500.1 Notice and Process. The desk shall issue a formal notice requiring the individual to compensate for any loss or harm caused by their actions. This includes not only direct financial compensation but also measures that restore balance or mitigate further damage.\n\n### Unaffiliated Travelers and Accountability\n\nWhile unaffiliated travelers are not automatically deemed Persons Bound, they must still answer for conduct that impacts tracked anomalies or Guild assets. § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill specifically addresses the need to document such instances and ensures that proper records are kept of any such actions. The Codex provides a framework for dealing with unaffiliated individuals who engage in destructive or hazardous activities near tracked anomalies, even if they were not aware of their presence.\n\nIn the case of the Fireball spell, if it is determined that the traveler was unaware of the proximity to a tracked anomaly, the desk may provide guidance on how to proceed. However, the individual remains liable for any harm caused and must take steps to mitigate or rectify the situation. The Codex offers flexibility in such scenarios through its provisions under § 500.2 Notice and Process.\n\n### Emergency Situations and Guild Undertakings\n\nIn cases of emergency where immediate action is required to protect a tracked anomaly from further damage, the desk may intervene even if the individual responsible for the initial conduct remains unaffiliated. This includes situations where an unaffiliated caster's actions have compromised the integrity of a tracked anomaly or endangered it in some way. Under § 100.8 Emergency Sitting, the desk can call for immediate action to mitigate risks and ensure that the anomaly is not further destabilized.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe conduct involving tracked anomalies or Guild assets outside Guild property must be governed by strict protocols to ensure accountability and maintain balance within the magical ecosystem. The jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild extends beyond its physical halls, covering all entities and phenomena that are officially recorded on the Quiet List. Individuals and entities who cause harm in such circumstances can expect rigorous enforcement under the Codex's provisions for restitution, notice, and process. This ensures that even when a person is not formally bound to the Guild, their actions must still align with the broader aims of maintaining harmony within the magical world.\n\nUnder § 500.3 Emergency Situations and Guild Undertakings, the desk can act swiftly in cases where immediate intervention is required to protect tracked anomalies or mitigate damage caused by unaffiliated casters. This ensures that all individuals who interact with these entities are held accountable for their actions, regardless of their formal status as Person Bound.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "300.4",
            "100.4",
            "200.5",
            "400.4",
            "500.1",
            "200.6",
            "500.2",
            "100.8",
            "500.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Conduct Involving Untracked Anomalies",
          "text": "In the vast and untamed expanses of unaligned lands and wilderness, conduct involving untracked anomalies presents a unique challenge for the Autumnwood Accords Desk. While the Guild's jurisdiction is not automatically triggered by mere location, certain circumstances may compel the Desk to intervene. This section outlines the parameters under which such conduct can be actionable and the processes by which the Desk may assert oversight.\n\nUnder § 1.4 Persons Bound, a Person Bound refers specifically to an individual who has agreed to abide by the Autumnwood Accords through membership or formal agreement with the Guild. However, this status does not extend automatically to every caster operating within unaligned lands or wilderness areas. The Desk maintains that conduct involving untracked anomalies, if it results in property damage, public danger, unauthorized use of Guild resources or marks, breach of a Guild undertaking, affects a tracked anomaly or asset, or falls under field-casting, reporting, restitution, or emergency rules, may still be subject to the jurisdiction of the Codex.\n\nThe Desk acknowledges that location alone does not grant immunity from liability. An unaffiliated caster who causes harm through destructive spells such as a Fireball is not automatically a Person Bound but can incur liability for their actions under § 501.2. The Desk reserves the right to investigate, impose restitution, and initiate legal proceedings against such individuals under appropriate circumstances.\n\nConsideration of conduct involving untracked anomalies must also account for the potential for field-casting that could lead to immediate danger or property damage. Under § 400.6, the use of Guild marks or equipment without authorization constitutes a serious breach of the Accords and may be subject to sanctions. The Desk emphasizes that such unauthorized use is actionable regardless of whether the caster is affiliated with the Guild.\n\nWhen dealing with untracked anomalies, the Desk must balance the need for oversight against the potential for interference in matters beyond its purview. Section § 103.4 on Field Interference outlines the parameters under which the Desk may intervene. The Desk shall not act without reasonable cause and shall ensure that any intervention does not exceed the bounds of necessary action.\n\nIn instances where an unaffiliated caster is involved, the Desk must provide notice of the allegations and give the individual a fair opportunity to respond. Under § 201.7, the Desk shall issue formal notices outlining the nature of the conduct alleged and the potential consequences. The Desk may also require restitution for any property damage caused or seek measures to mitigate public danger.\n\nFurthermore, the Desk must consider the broader implications of the anomaly in question. If an untracked anomaly poses a threat to the stability of the region or affects a Guild asset that is not currently tracked, the Desk shall take steps to secure and monitor the situation. Under § 300.5 Reclassification, such anomalies may be reclassified as tracked items for future reference.\n\nIn summary, conduct involving untracked anomalies falls within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords Desk when it results in actionable harm or poses a public danger. The Desk shall assert its authority through notice, investigation, and possible legal proceedings to ensure that all individuals adhere to the Accords regardless of their affiliation with the Guild. This approach ensures that the Codex can be applied flexibly across diverse and often unpredictable environments.\n\nSee § 103.4 on Field Interference for detailed guidance on the Desk's intervention in such matters. Under § 201.7, the process of issuing formal notices to individuals involved is outlined, ensuring due process where applicable. The Desk’s approach must be guided by a balance between enforcing the Accords and respecting the autonomy of unaligned regions.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "501.2",
            "400.6",
            "103.4",
            "201.7",
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Conduct Involving Unauthorized Field Use",
          "text": "In the broad scope of the Mages' Guild Accords, it is imperative to delineate the conduct that constitutes unauthorized field use and its implications for both those bound by the Guild's jurisdiction and unaffiliated individuals. The concept of unauthorized field use encompasses the unlawful or unsanctioned deployment of magic outside official premises, whether by a Person Bound or an unaffiliated caster. This section seeks to clarify what actions fall under this category and outline the remedies available when such conduct occurs.\n\n### Unauthorized Field Use: Definition and Scope\n\nUnsanctioned field use refers to the casting of spells or conjuring of magical effects in locations not approved or designated by the Mages' Guild. Such unauthorized activities are a source of concern for the Guild, as they can lead to unintended consequences that affect both the caster and the surrounding environment. While the Guild's primary jurisdiction is confined to its halls and official properties, it must also address conduct that occurs outside these boundaries but still impacts the Guild or its interests.\n\n### Conduct by Persons Bound\n\nA Person Bound to the Mages' Guild is subject to the Codex regardless of their location. This means that a Person Bound who engages in unauthorized field use can be held accountable for their actions, even if they do so while traveling outside official premises. The conduct must meet certain criteria to be actionable under the Codex:\n\n1. **Property Damage:** If a Person Bound causes property damage through an unauthorized spell or conjuration, they are subject to disciplinary action and potential legal penalties. The damage can include destruction of private or public property, such as the aforementioned destructive Fireball incident.\n\n2. **Public Danger:** Acts that endanger the public, such as summoning hostile creatures or creating dangerous weather patterns, fall under this category. For instance, a single misjudged Fireball could trigger immediate intervention by the Guild.\n\n3. **Unlawful Use of Guild Marks or Equipment:** Unauthorized use of Guild marks or equipment—such as seals, runes, or other magical tools—without proper authorization is strictly prohibited and can lead to severe penalties.\n\n4. **Breaches of Guild Undertakings:** A Person Bound may breach a contract with the Guild, such as an agreement not to cast certain spells in unsanctioned areas. Such breaches are actionable under the Codex.\n\n5. **Affecting Tracked Anomalies or Assets:** Any person, bound or unbound, who interferes with or damages tracked anomalies or Guild assets is subject to immediate notice and potential confiscation of their magic abilities if found guilty by the Paradox Trial.\n\n### Conduct by Unaffiliated Individuals\n\nAn unaffiliated individual who casts in a manner that violates the Codex can still incur liability under certain circumstances. The key factors include:\n\n1. **Causing Property Damage:** If an unaffiliated caster causes property damage, they may be held accountable for restitution and compensation. This includes any destruction of third-party property resulting from their actions.\n\n2. **Creating Public Danger:** Acts that create a public hazard—such as summoning creatures or casting destructive spells—are actionable under the Codex.\n\n3. **Unauthorized Use of Guild Resources or Marks:** The use of Guild resources, including marks and equipment, without permission is strictly prohibited and can result in severe consequences.\n\n4. **Breaching a Guild Undertaking:** An unaffiliated caster who breaches a formal undertaking with the Guild—such as an agreement to avoid certain areas—can be held accountable for their actions.\n\n5. **Affecting Tracked Anomalies or Assets:** Any interference with tracked anomalies or assets can lead to immediate notice and potential confiscation of magic abilities.\n\n### Remedies and Notice\n\nIn cases where a person is found to have engaged in unauthorized field use, the following remedies may be applied:\n\n- **Notice:** The Guild will issue a formal notice to the individual involved, outlining their actions and the consequences.\n- **Restitution:** The caster must provide restitution for any property damage caused, including compensation for victims of their actions.\n- **Investigation:** The Paradox Trial may convene to determine if further action is required. In extreme cases, this could lead to a pocket-prison sentence or sealing of magic abilities.\n\n### Cross-references\n\nThis section draws upon several foundational sections within the Codex:\n\n- § 1.6 Definitions: Clarifies key terms such as \"Person Bound\" and \"Guild property.\"\n- § 300.1 Schedules of Arts: Lists prohibited arts, which can be involved in unauthorized field use.\n- § 400.5 Unauthorized Titan Growth: Addresses the consequences of uncontrolled magic, including unauthorized growth.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe Mages' Guild Accords recognize that unauthorized field use is a serious concern and must be addressed with appropriate measures. By defining what constitutes such conduct and outlining the remedies available, this section ensures that both bound and unbound individuals are held accountable for their actions outside official premises. The Codex aims to protect not only the Guild’s interests but also the broader community from the potential dangers of unchecked magical activity.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "300.1",
            "400.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Conduct Involving Guild Protocols and Unlicensed Field Use",
          "text": "In the vast expanse of unaligned lands and wilderness, where the jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild does not extend fully, certain protocols and guidelines remain in effect to ensure that the conduct of those who wield magic does not infringe upon the sovereignty or safety of the Guild. This section addresses situations involving unlicensed field use, where a caster—whether affiliated with the Guild or an outsider—engages in casting activities without proper authorization within territories not governed by Guild oversight.\n\nThe core principle underpinning this topic is that location alone does not grant immunity from Codex liability. A caster, regardless of their affiliation status, remains accountable for any conduct that results in property damage, public danger, the unauthorized use of Guild marks or equipment, breaches of Guild undertakings, interference with tracked assets or anomalies, and failure to comply with field-casting protocols. The scope rule must distinguish between Person Bound status and actionable conduct, ensuring that the Codex applies where necessary.\n\nA guest or unaffiliated caster who casts outside Guild property is not automatically a Person Bound individual. However, such a person may still incur Codex liability for covered conduct if their actions result in property damage, public danger, unauthorized use of Guild resources or marks, breach of a Guild undertaking, or interference with a tracked asset or anomaly. The distinction between standing and actionable harm is crucial; mere presence on unaligned lands does not render one subject to the full force of the Codex.\n\nThe Autumnwood Accords require that any caster—whether affiliated or not—must adhere to certain protocols when engaging in field work outside Guild property. These protocols are designed to minimize risk to both the caster and their environment. For instance, a caster who wishes to perform potentially hazardous spells such as Fireball must ensure that they do so with appropriate safety measures in place. This includes informing nearby settlements of the intended casting location and ensuring that no persons or structures will be harmed by the spell.\n\nIn cases where property damage occurs due to unauthorized field use, the responsible party shall provide immediate notice to local authorities and the nearest Guild representative if one is present. Failure to do so may result in additional penalties under § 501.2 Delays in Notice of Harmful Conduct. The responsible party must also undertake to repair or replace any damaged property at their own expense, unless the damage can be attributed to a specific anomaly that has been previously documented by the Guild.\n\nIf the caster is affiliated with the Guild, they are expected to report such incidents to their local Guild office for further investigation and potential disciplinary action. Unaffiliated casters may face similar consequences if their actions are deemed excessive or reckless under § 501.3 Accountability of Unlicensed Field Use. The Codex emphasizes that the responsibility lies with the caster to ensure that their activities do not cause unintended harm.\n\nUnder § 502.0 Unauthorized Field Use, any caster engaging in field work without proper authorization may be subject to sanctions ranging from formal warnings to temporary suspension of casting privileges. The Paradox Trial may also be invoked if the conduct is severe enough to warrant a peer review under § 300.4 Reclassification. In extreme cases, the caster may face imprisonment or even forfeiture of their magical abilities as determined by the Council of Peers.\n\nIn instances where a caster damages property or endangers public safety without due cause, the Guild reserves the right to seek restitution and compensation for any losses incurred. Under § 503.1 Restitution Orders, the responsible party must provide evidence that they have taken steps to mitigate the damage caused by their actions. This may include but is not limited to financial reparations, repairs to affected structures, or community service.\n\nThe Autumnwood Accords also stress the importance of transparency in reporting incidents involving unauthorized field use. Any caster who wishes to engage in such activities must document their plans and obtain explicit permission from local authorities and the nearest Guild representative. Failure to comply with these requirements may result in legal action under § 504.1 Violations of Field Use Protocols.\n\nIn conclusion, while location alone does not grant immunity from Codex liability, the Mages' Guild expects all casters—whether affiliated or not—to adhere to certain protocols when engaging in field work outside Guild property. The Codex provides a framework for accountability and restitution that ensures the safety of both the caster and their environment. By adhering to these guidelines, casters can minimize the risk of unintended harm and maintain the integrity of the magical community.\n\nSee § 501.2 Delays in Notice of Harmful Conduct; under § 503.1 Restitution Orders; see § 504.1 Violations of Field Use Protocols.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "501.2",
            "501.3",
            "502.0",
            "300.4",
            "503.1",
            "504.1"
          ]
        }
