    {
      "cite": "500.1",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Field Casting for Value",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Public safety distance, notice to non-casters.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Field Casting for Value",
          "text": "§ 500.1 Field Casting for Value\n\nAny practitioner of the arcane who intends to perform a manifestation of magical energy within a public or semi-public thoroughfare for the purpose of obtaining tangible, intangible, or spiritual compensation shall be deemed to be \"Casting for Value.\" This designation applies regardless of whether the compensation is offered in the form of coin, labor, goods, or the fulfillment of a specific request by a non-magical entity. Pursuant to § 200.1, all such acts require a valid license from the Autumnwood Accords Desk. The mere act of performing a spell for a friend or family member does not exempt the caster from these regulations if the resulting outcome provides a measurable benefit to the recipient. The Guild maintains that the public sphere is not a laboratory, nor is it a theater for unregulated arcane commerce.\n\nTo mitigate the risk of collateral magical displacement and physical harm to the populace, all Field Casting for Value must adhere to strict Public Safety Distances. A minimum buffer zone of fifty paces must be maintained between the epicenter of the arcane manifestation and any non-consenting bystander. This distance is non-negotiable and must be calculated based on the projected \"Arcane Radius\" of the spell. If a spell’s inherent nature—such as a large-scale transmutation or a high-yield pyrotechnic enchantment—exceeds a radius of twenty paces, the caster must establish a physical barricade and provide a formal notice to all non-casters within a hundred paces. Failure to provide such notice, or the failure to maintain the safety buffer, shall constitute a violation of the Accords and may result in immediate intervention by the Aegis Magi.\n\nNotice to non-casters must be delivered in a clear, audible manner, preferably via a minor auditory enchantment that does not infringe upon the Quiet List. The notice must state the nature of the casting, the estimated duration, and the primary risks involved. For example, a caster performing a \"Frost-Touch\" enchantment for the purpose of preserving local produce must announce the \"localized temperature drop\" and \"potential for slick surfaces.\" If a non-caster enters the designated buffer zone after notice has been given, the caster is permitted to use minor non-lethal binding spells to ensure the safety of the bystander, provided such actions do not exceed the scope of the original casting. Any caster who proceeds with a casting for value without satisfying these proximity and notification requirements shall be subject to immediate seizure of the proceeds and a formal review by the Paradox Trial. See § 1.6 for definitions of \"Public Thoroughfare.\"",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "500.1",
            "200.1",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unscheduled Weather",
          "text": "§ 500.2 Unscheduled Weather\n\nThe atmospheric integrity of the Midlands is a shared resource, and the manipulation of meteorological phenomena for private gain is strictly regulated under the Autumnwood Accords. Any mage who deliberately invokes, alters, or sustains atmospheric conditions—including but not limited to precipitation, wind velocity, temperature fluctuations, or cloud density—for the purpose of securing an agricultural advantage shall be subject to the provisions of this section. This includes \"Precision Irrigation\" via localized rain, \"Accelerated Maturation\" through artificial warmth, and \"Pest Deterrence\" via sustained gale-force winds. While the Innovators may argue for the efficiency of such methods, the Conservators maintain that large-scale weather manipulation risks the stability of the local ley lines and the health of neighboring lands.\n\nMages seeking to utilize weather-altering magic for agricultural purposes must submit a \"Meteorological Impact Study\" to the Accords Desk no less than fourteen days prior to the intended casting. This study must detail the geographical boundaries of the effect, the duration of the manipulation, and the measures taken to prevent \"Atmospheric Bleed,\" wherein the desired weather effect spills over into neighboring properties or public waterways. Only those with a \"Weather-Class Permit\" may proceed with such castings. If a mage performs these actions without a permit, the Guild reserves the right to neutralize the effect at the caster's expense, regardless of the damage caused to the crops or livestock during the intervention.\n\nFurthermore, it is explicitly stated that the Guild’s insurance protocols and the Goodstyle Artisans' liability protections do not cover losses incurred from weather that has been intentionally summoned or modified by a licensed or unlicensed mage. If a farmer’s crop is destroyed by a \"rogue\" storm, the Guild shall not be held liable. Conversely, if a farmer’s crop is destroyed by a storm they specifically paid a mage to summon—and that storm exceeded the permitted parameters—the farmer may file a grievance with the Desk. However, the Guild shall not provide restitution for \"Unscheduled Weather\" events that were not filed correctly. The Accords distinguish clearly between \"Act of Nature\" and \"Act of Arcana\"; the latter carries a higher burden of proof and a stricter penalty for negligence. See § 400.1 for research permits related to atmospheric study.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "500.2",
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion",
          "text": "§ 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion\n\nIn the event of a magical discharge, structural collapse, or \"Arcane Rupture\" that results in the destruction of Guild hallways, common areas, or shared laboratory corridors, the following protocols for restoration shall apply. The primary objective of these rules is to ensure that the flow of commerce and study is not impeded by the debris of magical failure. The party responsible for the discharge—hereafter referred to as the \"Originating Party\"—is legally obligated to provide the initial assessment of the site. This assessment must determine if the explosion was a result of authorized research, a violation of the Iron Mandate (where applicable to external threats), or a lapse in personal magical containment.\n\nThe Originating Party shall be held solely responsible for the initial \"Clearing Phase.\" This includes the removal of large-scale debris, the stabilization of any remaining magical residue, and the neutralization of lingering \"Arcane Static\" that may cause disorientation or minor hexing to passersby. The Originating Party must file a \"Damage and Incident Report\" with the Desk within twenty-four hours of the event. If the explosion occurred during a sanctioned Innovator project, the costs of the Clearing Phase may be shared with the Guild’s general fund, provided the research was approved under § 400.1. However, if the explosion was a result of a violation of the Forbidden Schools or the Quiet List, the Originating Party shall bear the entirety of the restoration costs.\n\nFollowing the Clearing Phase, the \"Sanitization and Sweeping\" of the corridor shall be conducted. While the Originating Party is responsible for the heavy lifting, the Guild may delegate the fine sweeping and polishing to the Goodstyle Artisans or a designated cleaning construct. The Originating Party shall be billed for the labor of these third parties at the standard Guild rate. If the corridor remains unusable for more than forty-eight hours due to lingering magical instability, the Originating Party shall be subject to a \"Loss of Access\" fine. Furthermore, any physical damage to the architectural integrity of the Autumn Wood hall must be repaired to the original specifications as recorded in the Guild’s archives. No mage may attempt to \"patch\" a wall with permanent transmutation without express permission from Archmage Veyra. See § 100.6 for Delegation to Clerks regarding restoration billing.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "500.3",
            "400.1",
            "100.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Healing Offered as Consideration",
          "text": "§ 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration\n\nThe provision of restorative magic—including but not limited to the closing of wounds, the curing of blights, or the mending of broken bones—is a sacred duty and a regulated practice. Under the Autumnwood Accords, healing may never be sold as a primary \"Contract Term\" for unlicensed individuals. While a mage may accept a voluntary gift or a modest tip for a healing act, the systematic sale of healing as a commodity requires a specific \"Medicinal Casting License.\" This distinction is vital to prevent the exploitation of the infirm and to ensure that life-saving magic is not hoarded by those with the highest capital.\n\nAny entity, including a merchant house or a private estate, that includes \"Magical Healing\" as a mandatory condition of a contract—such as a labor agreement, a prisoner exchange, or a debt repayment—must ensure that the healer providing the service is currently licensed by the Accords Desk. If a contract is found to be predicated on \"Unlicensed Healing,\" the contract shall be declared null and void by the Archmage Sovereign, and the unlicensed healer shall be subject to a \"Practice Violation\" penalty. This includes \"Passive Healing\" zones or \"Restorative Fields\" established in commercial spaces. Such fields must be registered with the Guild to ensure they do not interfere with the local mana flow or create \"Healing Sinks\" that drain the vitality of nearby non-consenting individuals.\n\nFurthermore, healing offered as consideration for the release of a debt or as a \"penalty\" for a crime is strictly prohibited. The Guild views the commodification of biological integrity as a violation of Mana Reverence. If a mage is caught selling \"Healing Vials\" or \"Restorative Potions\" without a secondary manufacturing license, they shall be subject to a permanent ban from the Hall of Autumn Wood. For those who hold the proper licenses, the Desk provides a standardized fee schedule to ensure that healing costs remain equitable across the Midlands. Any deviation from these fees in a public setting must be reported to the Aegis Magi to prevent predatory pricing. See § 200.1 for Licensure Required.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "500.4",
            "200.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Love Philtre and Undue Influence",
          "text": "§ 500.5 Love Philtre and Undue Influence\n\nThe use of enchantments to manipulate the emotional state, desires, or loyalties of another sentient being is a grave violation of the Autumnwood Accords. Specifically, the creation and distribution of \"Love Philtrés,\" \"Charisma Enhancers,\" or any \"Affective Alteration\" draughts or spells are strictly prohibited. A philtre, by its very definition in Guild law, is an instrument of undue influence; therefore, the administration of such a substance can never constitute valid consent. Any magical effect that overrides the free will of a sentient being to induce affection, obedience, or obsession shall be classified as a \"Violation of Autonomy.\"\n\nMages who manufacture or possess \"Love Philtrés\" are subject to immediate seizure of their materials and a mandatory review by the Conservators. If such a substance is administered, the victim shall be entitled to a \"Neutralization Rite\" performed by the Guild at the caster's expense. The caster shall be liable for all damages arising from the duration of the undue influence, including emotional trauma, loss of property, or social standing. The Innovators may argue that \"Emotional Buffs\" are a form of artistic expression or psychological therapy, but the Accords are clear: any magic that bypasses the conscious choice of the subject is a breach of the peace.\n\nFurthermore, the use of \"Undue Influence\" in negotiations, political maneuvering, or the acquisition of property is a felony under the Accords. Mages who utilize \"Subtle Charm\" or \"Persuasion Weaving\" to coerce others into signing agreements shall have their licenses revoked. This prohibition extends to the \"Quiet List\"; any spell designed to make a person \"forget\" their opposition to a deal is an even more severe violation. The Guild maintains that the sanctity of the mind is paramount. Those who seek to turn the hearts of others into puppets of their own design shall find themselves facing the Paradox Trial, with the potential for permanent sealing of their arcane abilities. See § 300.2 for Necromancy and Unsanctioned Arts.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "500.5",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.1 Field Casting for Value; Public Safety Distance and Notice to Non-Caster",
          "text": "No caster shall engage in field casting unless the spatial requirements outlined under § 400.2 are fully satisfied, or such casting is expressly authorized by a specific exemption granted pursuant to these Accords. In all instances where magical effects generate kinetic force, thermal output, electromagnetic emissions, or other hazardous outputs capable of causing injury or property damage beyond an immediate radius, the caster shall maintain a minimum safety perimeter as specified in the relevant Schedules of Arts annexed hereto. Failure to adhere to such distances renders the casting negligent per se and subjects the caster to enhanced penalties upon conviction under § 700.3.\n\nFurthermore, no caster shall initiate field casting without providing reasonable advance notice to all persons within a radius of at least one hundred yards of the intended site of operation. Said notice shall be delivered via verbal announcement or written notification, clearly stating the nature and duration of the impending magical activity. In emergency circumstances where immediate action is required to prevent imminent harm, such notice requirement is waived provided that post-casting debriefing occurs within a reasonable timeframe to assess any collateral impacts. Any caster found guilty of willfully violating either the safety distance mandates or the notification requirements shall face mandatory remedial training and suspension pending review by the Ethics Committee under § 800.4. These provisions serve to balance the needs of legitimate magical practice with the fundamental rights of the public to bodily integrity and property security, reflecting the Guild’s dual commitment to advancement and stewardship. Violations thereof undermine the very foundations upon which safe coexistence between casters and non-casters depends, warranting strict enforcement as outlined herein.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "400.2",
            "700.3",
            "800.4",
            "500.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
