    {
      "cite": "500.2",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Unscheduled Weather",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Calling weather for agricultural advantage. Insurance does not cover weather you called.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Unscheduled Weather",
          "text": "§ 500.2 Unscheduled Weather. Any practitioner of the arcane who intentionally alters, induces, or manipulates atmospheric conditions for the purpose of providing agricultural advantage—including but not limited to the stimulation of crop growth, the redirection of irrigation flows, or the mitigation of frost—shall be subject to the oversight of the Accords Desk. While the Guild recognizes the utility of such magic in the maintenance of the Midlands, it is strictly prohibited to utilize high-magnitude meteorological manipulation without a pre-approved Seasonal Adjustment Permit. The Guild maintains a zero-tolerance policy regarding the use of \"Growth-Spurring Rains\" or \"Artificial Sun-Cycles\" that bypass natural cycles to create market monopolies or ecological imbalances.\n\nFurthermore, let it be known that the Guild’s internal insurance and indemnity protections do not extend to damages arising from self-induced meteorological events. If a mage invokes a localized storm or heatwave to benefit their own holdings or those of their patrons, and that event subsequently causes collateral damage to neighboring properties, the Guild shall hold the caster personally liable for all reparations. This includes, but is not limited to, the destruction of neighboring structures, the contamination of shared water tables, or the unintended migration of local fauna. The caster shall be required to provide a full ledger of the magical expenditure and a comprehensive damage assessment to the Accords Desk within three lunar cycles of the event.\n\nIn cases where the weather is called for the purpose of \"Force Majeure\" defense in civil disputes, the Accords Desk shall strike such claims from the record immediately. A mage cannot claim \"Act of God\" for a phenomenon they personally authored for commercial gain. Any practitioner found in violation of § 500.2 shall be subject to a fine of fifty gold marks per acre of affected land, plus a mandatory period of service in the Goodstyle Artisans' restoration wing. See § 1.6 for definitions of \"Agricultural Advantage.\"",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "500.2",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion",
          "text": "§ 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion. In the event of a magical discharge, structural failure, or intentional detonation resulting in the debris-clogging of any common hallway, thoroughfare, or laboratory corridor, the immediate priority shall be the restoration of safe passage. The practitioner responsible for the primary arcane discharge—hereafter referred to as the \"Originator\"—is legally bound to file a Notice of Incident with the nearest Aegis Magi outpost within one hour of the event. This notice must include the estimated magnitude of the blast, the intended objective of the casting, and a list of any materials rendered unstable by the shockwave.\n\nFollowing the filing of the Notice of Incident, the Originator shall be held solely responsible for the physical restoration of the corridor. This duty includes the removal of pulverized masonry, the neutralization of lingering arcane radiation, and the sweeping of all particulate matter. The Originator may hire authorized laborers, provided such laborers are bound by a non-disclosure agreement regarding the Guild’s internal architecture. No other Guild member, regardless of their proximity to the blast site, shall be required to perform manual labor or maintenance for the Originator. The \"First-In-First-Out\" rule of debris clearance applies: the Originator must clear a path of at least three feet in width before any other traffic may be permitted to pass.\n\nShould the Originator fail to initiate sweeping and clearing within two hours of the incident, the Accords Desk may authorize an Aegis Magi cleanup crew to intervene. In such an instance, the Originator shall be billed at triple the standard labor rate, and the costs shall be deducted from their annual research stipend. Furthermore, if the explosion resulted from a violation of the Forbidden Schools as defined in § 300.2, the Originator shall be subject to immediate detention for a Paradox Trial. See § 100.6 for matters regarding the delegation of cleanup duties to junior clerks.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "500.3",
            "300.2",
            "100.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Healing Offered as Consideration",
          "text": "§ 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration. The provision of restorative magic, mending of flesh, or the alleviation of chronic ailment shall not be considered a mere \"service\" when it is integrated into a formal contract as a primary consideration for labor, goods, or land. Any practitioner who offers unlicensed healing as a contract term—specifically in the context of indentured servitude, debt repayment, or the fulfillment of commercial obligations—is in violation of the Accords. The Guild recognizes that the commodification of life-force and the use of healing as a tool of coercion creates a predatory environment that undermines the stability of the Midlands.\n\nWhen healing is offered as a component of a contract, it must be performed by a licensed Medical Arcanist who has submitted a formal \"Healing Clause\" to the Accords Desk. This clause must specify the limits of the healing (e.g., \"mending of superficial wounds only\" or \"alleviation of non-magical fatigue\") and the duration of the treatment. If a mage uses healing to \"repair\" a worker who has been injured in the line of duty, this is permitted only if the worker retains full autonomy over the treatment and is not being \"repaired\" to continue labor against their will. Any healing performed as a \"maintenance\" requirement for a non-human construct or a bound entity without a specific permit is strictly prohibited.\n\nPractitioners found engaging in the unlicensed sale of healing as a contract term shall have their licenses suspended for a period of no less than three years. Furthermore, any contracts found to contain \"Life-Debt\" clauses—whereby a healing spell is used to generate a debt that cannot be repaid through standard currency—shall be declared null and void by the Accords Desk. The Guild shall not recognize the Wario Coin as a valid tender for the settlement of any healing debts. See § 300.2 for restrictions on blood-based restorative arts.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "500.4",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Love Philtre and Undue Influence",
          "text": "§ 500.5 Love Philtre and Undue Influence. The creation, distribution, or administration of any philtre, potion, or enchantment designed to induce romantic affection, sexual desire, or profound emotional dependency—commonly referred to as \"Love Philters\"—is strictly prohibited under the Accords. The Guild maintains that the imposition of affection via arcane means constitutes a fundamental violation of individual autonomy and a breach of the Mana Reverence principles. No philtre, regardless of its potency or the \"gentleness\" of its effects, shall be considered a valid means of establishing consent.\n\nAny individual who administers a philtre to another shall be held liable for \"Undue Influence.\" This offense carries a heavy presumption of guilt. If a victim is found under the influence of a philtre, the administration of such a substance shall be treated as an assault on the victim's mental integrity. The perpetrator shall be subject to immediate seizure of all brewing apparatuses and a mandatory referral to the Aegis Magi for interrogation. If the philtre was administered with the intent to secure a marriage, a property transfer, or any other legal agreement, the resulting contract shall be deemed fraudulent and unenforceable by the Archmage Sovereign.\n\nThe Innovator faction may seek to study the chemical components of such substances for research purposes, but such research must be conducted within a sealed pocket-dimension lab as per § 400.2 and may never involve human subjects. Any \"social lubrication\" spells that do not involve the manipulation of deep-seated emotions may be permitted, provided they do not cross the threshold into involuntary suggestibility. Practitioners who knowingly sell philtres to the public—including those sold by Goodstyle Artisans—shall be permanently barred from the Hall of Autumn Wood. See § 1.6 for the legal definition of \"Undue Influence.\"",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "500.5",
            "400.2",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.2 Unscheduled Weather and Agricultural Advantage",
          "text": "The practice of invoking atmospheric phenomena for the purpose of agricultural enhancement is strictly regulated under this section. While mages are permitted to call upon wind, rain, or sunshine in accordance with their authorized research permits (§ 400.1), any invocation that exceeds standard meteorological expectations shall be deemed unscheduled weather and subject to additional scrutiny. Such actions must be reported forthwith to the nearest Guild Hall for review by the Conservators and Innovators representatives assigned to the Desk. Failure to comply may result in immediate suspension of research privileges and potential criminal charges under the Accords.\n\nIt is hereby declared that insurance policies purchased prior to the enactment of this section shall not cover damages resulting from unscheduled weather conditions intentionally summoned by a caster for personal gain. Any attempt to claim compensation from third parties for losses attributable to such acts shall be barred on grounds of public policy and equitable estoppel. Mages are reminded that their powers are gifts to be stewarded responsibly, not tools to exploit at will. The balance between magical advancement and societal stability must always remain paramount in the eyes of the law.\n\nThis provision supersedes any conflicting terms found in private contracts or commercial agreements entered into by individual casters. In cases where ambiguity arises regarding the classification of a particular weather event as unscheduled, the burden of proof shall rest with the accused caster to demonstrate that their actions were neither intentional nor excessive in nature. The Guild retains broad discretion to determine what constitutes an appropriate threshold for such determinations based on prevailing conditions and expert testimony.\n\nViolators found guilty of abusing these provisions may face penalties ranging from fines and license revocation to imprisonment, depending on the severity of the consequences wrought by their misdeeds. Repeated offenses could lead to permanent exile from the realm or worse punishment at the discretion of the High Council. All mages are urged to exercise caution and restraint whenever manipulating natural forces outside their normal scope of practice, remembering that even a single ill-considered spell can unleash devastation far beyond their ken.\n\nIn witness whereof, I affix my seal and signature this day signed under the Autumnwood Accords, binding myself eternally to uphold these laws for generations yet unborn. May my name stand as testament to wisdom and restraint in an age gone mad with hubris!",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "500.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.2 Unscheduled Weather and Agricultural Advantage — Continued",
          "text": "Whereas the Accords recognize that certain casters may manipulate atmospheric conditions for personal gain, it is hereby declared that such acts constitute \"unscheduled weather\" under this section when they result in measurable agricultural benefit or harm to third parties. The Desk shall maintain a registry of all instances where magical intervention altered local climate patterns and impacted farming operations. Any caster found guilty of weaponized meteorology against competitors faces immediate suspension and mandatory restitution payments calculated at market rates for crop losses sustained by affected farmers. Furthermore, the use of conjured rainfall or sunlight to enhance harvests without explicit permission from the Conservator Council is prohibited, save where such actions benefit public infrastructure projects under direct Guild supervision. Violators shall be subject to fines up to one thousand gold pieces and temporary sealing of their magical abilities until compliance is demonstrated through certified neutral audits. The Desk reserves authority to issue emergency injunctions halting ongoing weather modification efforts deemed threatening to regional food security. This provision takes effect immediately upon filing with the Autumnwood Accords Archives.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "500.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "3af1c9fd66eb5342",
      "refs": [
        "1.6",
        "100.6",
        "300.2",
        "400.1",
        "400.2",
        "500.3",
        "500.4",
        "500.5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "500.3",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Who files first. Who sweeps.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion",
          "text": "Who files first. Who sweeps.\n\nIn the event of a localized structural failure resulting from high-output arcane discharge, the immediate restoration of the corridor's integrity shall be governed by the protocols of priority and maintenance established herein. While the primary responsibility for the containment of the initial breach rests with the Aegis Magi, the subsequent administrative and physical restoration of the corridor is subject to the \"First-Response Filing\" principle. The mage or researcher responsible for the discharge shall be required to file a formal Notice of Incident with the nearest Clerk of the Accords within one standard turn of the stabilization of the area. This filing must include the specific arcane signature of the spell, the estimated yield of the explosion, and a comprehensive list of any structural components rendered non-viable. Failure to file within the designated timeframe shall result in an automatic escalation of the fine, as per the standard penalties for administrative negligence.\n\nRegarding the physical remediation of the corridor, the \"Who Sweeps\" doctrine dictates that the burden of labor is directly proportional to the magnitude of the disturbance. Should the explosion be classified as a Minor Outburst (less than three meters of debris), the responsible party shall be tasked with the manual clearing of the corridor. For Moderate Disruptions (extending to five meters or involving the shattering of masonry), the responsible party shall be required to hire a certified Goodstyle Artisan to perform the sweeping and debris removal, ensuring that the aesthetic and structural standards of the Autumn Wood are maintained. In cases of Major Catastrophes, where the corridor is rendered entirely impassable or the structural integrity of the Hall is compromised, the Guild shall appoint a restoration crew, and the costs shall be deducted from the responsible party’s research stipend or seized from their personal holdings.\n\nFurthermore, no mage shall attempt to clear debris until the Clerk has verified that the area is free of lingering residual mana. Any individual attempting to \"sweep\" a corridor while active arcane residue is present risks a Paradox Trial for reckless endangerment. The Clerk of the Accords reserves the right to delay the sweeping process until a full survey of the site is completed, regardless of the urgency of the transit. See § 1.7 for matters regarding the aggregation of conduct in public spaces.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Healing Offered as Consideration",
          "text": "Unlicensed healing sold as a contract term.\n\nThe practice of offering restorative magic as a form of consideration, payment, or contractual fulfillment is strictly regulated under the Autumnwood Accords. It is hereby decreed that any act of healing performed by a mage in exchange for tangible goods, services, or the fulfillment of a legal obligation constitutes a commercial transaction of the arcane. Such transactions are strictly prohibited unless the practitioner holds a valid Licensure of Commercial Restoration issued by the Council. The Guild maintains that healing is a fundamental stabilization of the weave and should not be leveraged as a commodity in the marketplace, particularly when such healing is used to bypass the standard costs of labor or goods.\n\nWhen a mage incorporates healing magic into a contract—for instance, promising to mend a laborer's wounds in exchange for a portion of their wages—this shall be deemed an unlicensed sale of a protected art. Such contracts are null and void upon the first instance of the healing being enacted. Any mage found to be engaging in such \"Healing-for-Service\" arrangements shall be subject to immediate investigation by the Aegis Magi. The penalty for such violations shall include a temporary suspension of research privileges and a mandatory period of service in the Hall's maintenance wing. This provision is intended to prevent the exploitation of injured parties and to ensure that the restorative arts are not used to create a secondary economy of magical dependency.\n\nMoreover, the Guild recognizes that \"healing\" in a legal sense includes the restoration of vitality, the sealing of wounds, and the purging of toxins. Should a mage provide these services as part of a \"Goodstyle Artisan\" agreement, they must ensure that the specific terms of the healing are clearly delineated in a written addendum, signed by both parties and witnessed by a Guild Clerk. This addendum must specify the limitations of the healing, the duration of the effect, and a waiver stating that the mage is not liable for any complications arising from the patient's pre-existing conditions. See § 200.1 for requirements on general licensure.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "200.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Love Philtre and Undue Influence",
          "text": "A philtre is not consent.\n\nThe administration of any substance, liquid, or vapor designed to alter the emotional state, romantic inclinations, or cognitive priorities of a sentient being—hereafter referred to as a \"Philtre\"—is strictly forbidden under the Accords. The Guild recognizes that the manipulation of the heart is as grave a violation as the manipulation of the mind. A Philtre does not constitute a valid means of obtaining consent for any action, contract, or magical binding. Any agreement entered into under the influence of such a substance is considered legally void and shall be treated as a violation of the Sovereign’s peace. The use of such magic to influence the behavior of a peer, a subordinate, or a member of the public is grounds for immediate expulsion from the Guild.\n\nAny mage found in possession of a Philtre without a specific, approved Research Permit for the study of emotional resonance shall have their belongings seized and their license revoked. This prohibition extends to the \"Soft Influence\" of magic, where a mage may not use subtle charms to make a target more \"receptive\" to a proposal. The Accords demand that all interactions between mages and the public be conducted with clarity of mind. If a mage utilizes a Philtre to influence an individual, they shall be held liable for any damages caused by the influenced party, including physical harm, property destruction, or the unauthorized use of the victim's magical signature.\n\nFurthermore, the production and distribution of Philtres are categorized as the Unsanctioned Arts. Any clandestine laboratory found producing such substances will be dismantled by the Aegis Magi, and the occupants will be subject to the Paradox Trial. The Guild maintains a zero-tolerance policy regarding the erosion of individual autonomy through arcane chemistry or enchantment. No amount of \"research\" by the Innovators shall justify the creation of substances that bypass the natural volition of the citizenry. See § 300.2 for the definition of Unsanctioned Arts.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.7 Unlawful Consideration and Coercion",
          "text": "It is hereby declared that any magical service rendered in violation of these Accords shall constitute unlawful consideration, void ab initio. No contract, express or implied, may bind a caster to perform prohibited acts without the informed consent of all parties. Such consent must be obtained prior to the commencement of services and must be documented in writing unless waived by all involved in favor of an alternative dispute resolution mechanism recognized under § 100.6. Any agreement purporting to compel a caster to violate these laws shall be deemed fraudulent and unenforceable as against public policy. Furthermore, no person may coerce another into entering into such an unlawful contract through threats, intimidation, or duress. Violations of this section are punishable under the Penal Provisions of Part V, including but not limited to sanctions for breach of contract and contempt of court. It is the duty of all casters to refuse such improper demands and report them to appropriate authorities for investigation and prosecution. Failure so to do shall be deemed complicity in the underlying offense and subject to enhanced penalties accordingly.\n\nARCHIVE CARD: Unlawful Consideration\n*Definition:* Any magical service rendered in violation of these Accords constitutes unlawful consideration, void ab initio.* *Legal Effect:* No contract may bind a caster to perform prohibited acts without informed consent.* *Penalties:* Violations are punishable under Part V, including sanctions for breach and contempt.* *Duties:* Casters must refuse improper demands and report them for investigation and prosecution.*",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "500.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion",
          "text": "No mage, regardless of rank or status, shall cast spells within ten feet of another caster without explicit consent. Violations constitute reckless endangerment and may result in immediate suspension of licensure pending investigation. This provision exists to prevent accidental interference with ongoing magical operations, which could lead to catastrophic consequences for all parties involved.\n\nConsent must be obtained verbally or in writing before casting near others. In emergencies where no time exists for consultation, casters are deemed to have implied consent from all present individuals unless they demonstrate active objection through clear verbal communication or physical action. Failure to secure proper authorization places the offender at risk of sanctions under this section and potentially § 100.5 regarding conflict of interest in casting situations.\n\nThe ten-foot exclusion zone applies regardless of whether the affected party is actively engaged in magical work at the time. Proximity alone triggers the courtesy requirement due to the unpredictable nature of spell effects, which may extend beyond their intended target area. Caster responsibility extends to monitoring surrounding areas during operations and terminating spells immediately upon noticing any signs of adverse impact on nearby mages or structures.\n\nThis rule reflects the Guild's commitment to fostering a safe environment for all practitioners while acknowledging the inherent dangers associated with advanced magic usage. By mandating basic courtesies between casters, the Accords aim to minimize accidental harm and promote professional conduct even in high-stakes scenarios where split-second decisions can determine success or failure of magical endeavors.\n\nSee also § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; § 200.3 Temporary Mouths; § 400.3 Borrowed Hours – Interest in Time. These related provisions further elaborate on ethical considerations surrounding spellwork and resource management within the Guild framework.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "200.3",
            "400.3",
            "500.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.7 Unlawful Consideration and Coercion — Continued",
          "text": "No covenant, whether written or oral, shall bind a caster if procured through duress, fraud, or magical compulsion, unless ratified within seven days by free consent. See § 1.4 (Persons Bound); § 300.5 (Research Copies of Quiet Items). This rule prevents the exploitation of vulnerable individuals into binding themselves to oppressive terms under threat of immediate harm. The Guild maintains a registry of coerced contracts for swift nullification upon discovery.\n\nHealing services rendered by registered practitioners shall not constitute unlawful consideration unless expressly agreed upon as payment in advance. Emergency aid provided without prior demand may be recovered only if the recipient profited from it directly or disseminated false information about their condition to third parties. See § 200.1 (Licensure Required); § 500.4 (Healing Offered as Consideration). This provision balances access to life-saving magic with protection against predatory healers preying on desperation.\n\nLove philtres and similar influence magics remain strictly prohibited unless administered by a certified therapist for documented psychological treatment purposes. Unauthorized use constitutes aggravated assault under § 500.7(b)(3) and may result in permanent sealing of the caster's magical abilities. See § 400.1 (Research Permits); § 500.5 (Love Philtre and Undue Influence). The Guild views such manipulations as fundamental violations of bodily autonomy and informed consent, regardless of claimed therapeutic intent.\n\nAll contracts involving binding spells must specify duration, termination clauses, and dispute resolution mechanisms in plain language understandable to non-magical persons. Obscure legalese or hidden conditions voiding the agreement shall render it unenforceable unless proven by clear written evidence beyond reasonable doubt. See § 100.4 (Records of Speech); § 600.1 (Contracts with Non-Casters). Transparency protects both parties and prevents malicious actors from exploiting loopholes to trap others into unfavorable obligations.\n\nThe Desk shall appoint a Special Master to review all contested agreements involving significant magical risks or financial stakes. The Master's decision, issued within thirty days of filing, is final and binding unless appealed by unanimous vote of the Full Council. See § 100.3 (Quorum of the Desk); § 500.7(a)(2). This expedited process ensures timely resolution while preserving due process rights for all involved parties.\n\nViolations of this section carry penalties up to and including permanent magical sealing, depending on severity and intent. Repeat offenders face enhanced sanctions and potential revocation of Guild standing entirely. See § 400.3 (Borrowed Hours); § 500.7(c). The Guild prioritizes fair dealing above all else, even as it enforces its own strict codes against corruption and abuse of power within its ranks.\n\nNo contract shall supersede the fundamental protections afforded by this Codex or higher law, regardless of purported party intent. Any clause violating public policy or moral standards is void ab initio unless amended prospectively to comply with all applicable statutes and regulations. See § 100.5 (Conflict of Interest in Casting); § 300.2 (Necromancy). This safeguard preserves the integrity of magical practice while allowing reasonable flexibility for legitimate needs.\n\nThe Desk encourages open dialogue between parties regarding terms and expectations to prevent misunderstandings later. Prompt clarification of ambiguities is considered good faith behavior, whereas deliberate obfuscation may constitute fraud under § 500.7(b)(1). See § 200.6 (Constructs That Hold a Quill); § 400.5 (Unauthorized Titan Growth). Transparency and honesty remain cornerstones of ethical magical commerce throughout the Midlands region.\n\nFILED LORE: Unlawful Consideration, Healing Contracts, Love Philtres, Special Master Review, Plain Language Requirements, Dispute Resolution Mechanisms.\nCRITICAL: This page covers § 500.7 with detailed provisions on coerced contracts, healing services, influence magics, contract transparency, and enforcement penalties. It establishes clear guidelines for lawful magical agreements while protecting vulnerable parties from exploitation. The Special Master appointment ensures efficient resolution of complex disputes without compromising due process rights.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "300.5",
            "200.1",
            "500.4",
            "500.7",
