    },
    {
      "cite": "500.4",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Healing Offered as Consideration",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Unlicensed healing sold as a contract term.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Healing Offered as Consideration",
          "text": "§ 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration. All practitioners of the healing arts, whether licensed by the Autumnwood Accords or otherwise, are strictly prohibited from offering restorative magic as a primary consideration in a commercial, civil, or penal contract. The Guild maintains that the restoration of life and the mending of physical integrity are fundamental rights of the sentient being and cannot be commodified as a debt-repayment mechanism or a bargaining chip in the negotiation of services. Any mage who enters into a contract where the provision of healing—whether through potion, incantation, or transmutation—is listed as a specific deliverable in exchange for labor, land, gold, or political favor shall be deemed in violation of the Accords. This prohibition extends to \"conditional healing,\" wherein a victim is granted life-saving treatment on the condition of a subsequent, undisclosed, or disproportionate obligation. Such acts are viewed as predatory and undermine the stability of the Midlands, creating a class of \"debt-slaves\" whose physical health is leveraged against their personal liberty.\n\nFurthermore, the Aegis Magi are authorized to seize any assets derived from such illicit contracts, and the offending caster shall be subject to the Paradox Trial for the exploitation of vulnerable populations. See § 1.2 for the enacting authority of the Accords Desk. While the Innovators may argue that such exchanges are a matter of personal liberty between consenting adults, the Conservators maintain that the inherent power imbalance of a dying individual precludes true consent, thus rendering any such contract void ab initio. The Guild shall not recognize the validity of any \"healing debt\" recorded in a ledger or bound by a magical oath. Any mage found to be operating a \"healing clinic\" that functions as a debt-collection agency shall have their license revoked immediately and may be remanded to a pocket-prison for the duration of the investigation. This law serves to protect the sanctity of the body from the avarice of the arcane.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "500.4",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Love Philtre and Undue Influence",
          "text": "§ 500.5 Love Philtre and Undue Influence. The administration of any magical substance, enchantment, or ritual intended to alter the emotional state, romantic inclination, or loyalties of a sentient being—commonly referred to as \"Love Philtrés\"—is strictly prohibited under the Autumnwood Accords. The Guild declares that a philtre is not consent; rather, it is a violation of the cognitive sovereignty of the individual. Any mage who knowingly brews, distributes, or administers such a substance shall be held liable for \"Undue Influence,\" a high offense against the mental integrity of the citizenry. This prohibition is absolute and does not vary based on the perceived \"benign\" intent of the caster. The use of such magic to bypass the natural process of emotional development or to secure a contract, marriage, or political alliance is considered a fundamental breach of the Accords and may result in the immediate sealing of the caster's magic.\n\nIn the event that a victim of a Love Philtre seeks redress, the Accords Desk shall provide a pathway for the restoration of mental autonomy, provided the victim is not currently under the influence of a more complex, multi-layered enchantment. The Aegis Magi are tasked with identifying and neutralizing active \"Influence Fields\" in public spaces such as taverns or marketplaces. Any mage found in possession of \"Love Philtrés\" without a specific research permit from Janna Brightspark—and even then, only for the purpose of studying emotional resonance in controlled, isolated environments—shall be subject to the Paradox Trial. The Guild recognizes that the manipulation of the heart is as much a violation of the law as the manipulation of the earth. Those who seek to manufacture affection through the laboratory are to be remembered as transgressors against the very essence of free will. See § 300.2 for related restrictions on the Unsanctioned Arts.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "500.5",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration, Love Philtre and Undue Influence — Continued",
          "text": "It is well settled that a cast spell binding the recipient to pay for services rendered may constitute valid consideration under § 200.1 of this Code. However, where healing magic is offered in exchange for sexual favors or other intimate benefits, such transactions fall outside the scope of protected commercial activity. Such arrangements violate both public policy and moral standards enshrined in § 1.7(b), which requires all magical contracts to be \"conducive to societal welfare.\" Consequently, any agreement wherein a caster provides medical services contingent upon gratification shall be deemed void ab initio and unenforceable at law.\n\nThis prohibition extends beyond explicit quid pro quo bargains to encompass subtler forms of inducement, including but not limited to the use of love philtres or other charm spells designed to manipulate affection. Under § 300.2(a)(iii), such enchantments are classified as \"unsanctioned arts\" and subject to seizure upon discovery. Furthermore, any contract predicated on emotional dependency created by magical means shall be scrutinized under the undue influence doctrine of § 1.7(c). Where a party's consent is impaired by supernatural manipulation, their obligations may be set aside entirely at the discretion of the adjudicating council.\n\nThe Guild maintains that the integrity of therapeutic services must remain paramount and untainted by debauchery or predation. To permit healers to trade on their skills for carnal gain would degrade the profession beyond repair and expose vulnerable patients to exploitation. As such, § 500.4 establishes a bright-line rule: healing magic cannot be leveraged as currency in illicit exchanges without risking severe sanctions against both practitioner and client alike. Violators face up to permanent disbarment under § 200.3(e) for failure to uphold the ethical standards of their calling.\n\nThis provision serves as a necessary bulwark against the commodification of intimacy and the erosion of consent in matters of health and well-being. The Council reserves full discretion to interpret evolving technologies and practices through this lens, ensuring that magic continues to serve humanity rather than enslave it. Let all casters heed this warning: cross not into forbidden waters lest they drown beneath the weight of their own hubris. See also § 100.5 regarding conflicts of interest in casting scenarios involving personal gain.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "1.7",
            "300.2",
            "500.4",
            "200.3",
            "100.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration, Love Philtre and Undue Influence — Con",
          "text": "It is hereby declared that any contract term providing for the supply of healing services in exchange for magical goods or services constitutes an illegal barter, void ab initio under the principles laid out in §§ 1.5 and 600.1. Such arrangements are deemed exploitative and contrary to public policy, as they commodify essential healthcare and create opportunities for coercion and abuse. Any attempt to enforce such a provision shall be dismissed outright, with costs awarded against the offending party.\n\nFurthermore, any magical item or substance designed or marketed specifically to influence emotions, desires, or behaviors—such as love philtres, sedatives, aphrodisiacs, or mind-altering concoctions—is strictly prohibited from sale or distribution within Guild jurisdiction. The manufacture, possession, or use of such items for commercial gain shall be punished as a Class A felony, subject to seizure and confiscation under the powers granted in § 500.2. Exceptions may only be made by special permit issued directly by the Archmage Sovereign, upon recommendation from the Ethics Committee and demonstration of compelling public interest.\n\nThe Desk hereby asserts its authority to investigate, regulate, and penalize any practice that undermines personal autonomy or manipulates volition through magical means. This includes but is not limited to: love spells, charm magic, suggestion rituals, addictive substances engineered for profit, and deceptive marketing tactics aimed at vulnerable populations. Violators shall be subject to immediate suspension of licensure pending full inquiry, with additional penalties applied according to the severity of the offense and mitigating circumstances found in §§ 200.3 and 400.6.\n\nIt is the solemn duty of all licensed mages to uphold these standards and report any suspected violations promptly. Failure to do so may result in disciplinary action against the individual mage's credentials, regardless of their otherwise good standing. The Guild stands ready to employ its full range of investigative tools and legal resources to enforce this prohibition, working closely with law enforcement agencies as necessary to protect citizens from exploitation by rogue practitioners or unscrupulous businesses seeking to profit from human frailty.\n\nIn witness whereof, the Archmage Sovereign affixes her seal beneath signature on this day, binding these provisions into eternal force across all realms subject to Autumnwood Accords authority. Let none presume to challenge this directive without facing the full weight of the law—and magic—in return. 🧙‍♀️⚖️🔮",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.5",
            "500.2",
            "200.3",
            "500.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration, Love Philtre and Undue Influence — Con — Continued",
          "text": "Whereas natural remedies such as poultices and balms may be dispensed freely without penalty under § 210.3, the sale of magical healing services constitutes a commercial transaction subject to strict regulation. Any agreement in which a caster agrees to provide curative spells or potions in exchange for payment, goods, favors, or sexual access is voidable at the option of either party upon discovery of fraud or duress. Such contracts are deemed inherently unconscionable due to the unequal bargaining power typically existing between vulnerable clients and predatory practitioners.\n\nLove philtres and charms designed to alter emotions or compel affection fall under this prohibition with particular severity. The casting of such spells for monetary gain is punishable as a felony offense under § 620.1, regardless of whether actual harm results. This stands apart from therapeutic applications where a licensed healer administers counter-spells to mitigate magical addiction or withdrawal symptoms. Unlicensed love magic remains absolutely forbidden except in rare cases approved by the Conservators' Medical Board for dire humanitarian need.\n\nThe Desk hereby declares that any contract clause requiring a client to engage in sexual relations with their provider as partial payment is unenforceable and constitutes gross misconduct warranting immediate suspension of said caster's license. This prohibition extends to all forms of bodily intimacy, not merely intercourse. The spirit of the law demands protection for those incapable of fully comprehending the implications of such arrangements, particularly when dealing with impaired judgment or desperation.\n\nHealers offering services in exchange for romantic favors violate multiple provisions including § 205.1 regarding professional ethics and § 630.4 concerning exploitation of vulnerability. The burden rests upon individuals to resist undue influence from unscrupulous practitioners preying upon their affections. Failure to disclose material facts about a caster's character or intentions may render void any subsequent agreements arising therefrom.\n\nThe Desk further notes that while consensual adult relationships outside the bounds of commerce remain lawful, the blurring of such lines through explicit contractual obligations invites abuse and undermines public trust in the profession. Each party must act with utmost candor to avoid allegations of duress or manipulation. The ultimate goal is preservation of societal welfare without sacrificing individual autonomy.\n\nThis rule supersedes conflicting local ordinances or customary practices contrary to its provisions. Violators may face disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure under § 207.5. The Desk retains discretion to issue cease-and-desist orders as deemed necessary to prevent harm to the public interest. All such determinations shall be made in accordance with established principles of natural justice and equitable treatment for all concerned parties regardless of status or station.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "210.3",
            "620.1",
            "205.1",
            "630.4",
            "207.5",
            "500.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration, Love Philtre and Undue Influence — Rem",
          "text": "Where healing services are tendered in exchange for magical or material consideration, such bargains are deemed void ab initio under this Part. Any contract clause stipulating that the Provider shall cure an ailment contingent upon receipt of a love philtre, blood offering, or sexual favors is hereby declared unenforceable and illegal per se. Violation thereof constitutes a Class A felony punishable by immediate exile from the Guild's jurisdiction. Furthermore, any magic item or spell designed to manipulate emotions, affections, or bodily functions for commercial gain shall be seized as contraband subject to destruction pursuant to § 300.2(b). Offenders shall additionally face sanctions under this Part and the Code of Magical Ethics generally. The Desk retains sole discretion to grant clemency in exceptional circumstances warranting a public hearing. See also § 500.1 (Prohibition on Selling Healing Services), § 600.3(a)(v) (Contractual Restrictions on Personal Liberties). This rule reflects the fundamental principle that bodily integrity and emotional autonomy cannot be commodified or traded without explicit statutory authorization, which remains nonexistent in this Jurisdiction.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "500.1",
            "600.3",
            "500.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
