    },
    {
      "cite": "500.5",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Love Philtre and Undue Influence",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A philtre is not consent.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Love Philtre and Undue Influence",
          "text": "Pursuant to the overarching mandates of the Autumnwood Accords and the prevailing doctrine of Mana Reverence, the Guild hereby establishes a definitive legal distinction between the voluntary exchange of affection and the chemical or magical imposition of desire. It is a foundational principle of the Accords that the sanctity of the individual will is paramount to the stability of the magical weave. Consequently, any substance, enchantment, or ritualistic brew designed to induce, amplify, or manufacture romantic attraction—hereafter referred to as a \"Philtre\"—shall be categorized as a primary instrument of Undue Influence. The Guild maintains that a Philtre is not consent; the presence of a Philtre does not constitute a waiver of the victim's right to self-determination, nor does it grant the caster any legal standing to claim a consensual union, contract, or physical proximity.\n\nAny mage found to be in possession of a Philtre without a verified Research Permit issued by the Innovators, for the sole purpose of pharmacological study, shall be subject to immediate seizure of the materials. If the Philtre is found to have been deployed upon a sentient being, the offense shall be elevated from a regulatory violation to a criminal act of Arcane Assault. The Aegis Magi are authorized to intervene in any instance where a Philtre is suspected of being in active use. In such cases, the victim shall be granted immediate sanctuary, and the perpetrator shall be detained for a formal hearing. The Guild recognizes that the manipulation of the heart is as perilous as the manipulation of the elements; to bypass the internal defenses of a peer through the use of a Philtre is to violate the core tenets of the Accords and the dignity of the magical community.\n\nFurthermore, the use of a Philtre to secure favors, information, or labor shall be treated as a form of magical coercion. Any agreement entered into under the influence of a Philtre is null and void ab initio. This includes, but is not limited to, the signing of apprenticeship contracts, the relinquishing of magical artifacts, or the granting of access to restricted research sites. If a mage attempts to enforce such an agreement, they shall be held liable for all damages caused to the victim and may face severe penalties under the Paradox Trial for the subversion of free will. The Guild shall not recognize the \"Right of Pursuit\" in any instance where the pursuit is predicated upon the effects of a Philtre.\n\nThe Conservators maintain that the production of Philtres borders on the Unsanctioned Arts due to its inherent violation of the natural order of emotion. Conversely, while the Innovators may study the mechanics of such substances, they are strictly prohibited from testing these substances on any sentient being, including those within the Guild's own ranks. Any research involving the \"Love Philtre\" must be conducted within a sealed laboratory environment, as defined in § 400.2, and must be accompanied by a certified observer from the Aegis Magi. Failure to comply with these restrictions shall result in the immediate revocation of research privileges and a mandatory period of magical sealing. The Guild's stance is absolute: the magic of the heart is a sacred domain, and its violation through artificial means is a breach of the peace that the Autumnwood Accords were designed to uphold.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "400.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.5 Love Philtres and Undue Influence",
          "text": "A \"love philtre,\" whether by magic, chemical alteration, or other means, shall be deemed an impermissible influence upon the free will of another person and is strictly prohibited under these Accords. See § 1.4 (Persons Bound) for scope of application. Any contract, agreement, or declaration made while under the effect of such a philtre shall be void ab initio; the affected party may seek restitution as if fraud was committed upon them. This prohibition extends to all forms of mind-altering coercion aimed at manipulating emotional states or decision-making capacity in service of another's interests.\n\nUndue influence is similarly forbidden, encompassing any systematic abuse of power dynamics to dominate another's choices regarding personal matters such as marriage, employment, residence, or financial arrangements. See § 100.5 (Conflict of Interest) for related provisions. The burden lies with the accuser to demonstrate actual domination and resulting harm; however, circumstantial evidence combined with witness testimony may suffice where direct proof is unavailable. Repeated offenses constitute a felony punishable by revocation of licensure and permanent disqualification from all Guild activities.\n\nSpecial care must be taken in evaluating contracts between individuals with pre-existing power imbalances such as mentor-mentee or employer-employee relationships. See § 600.1 (Contracts with Non-Casters) for guidance on capacity. Disparities in knowledge, experience, or resources may render standard provisions unenforceable where they create an unfair advantage to the stronger party. The Desk reserves authority to void such agreements sua sponte upon petition or independent discovery of wrongdoing.\n\nThis section complements existing safeguards against magical coercion found in § 500.1 (Field Casting for Value) and § 200.3 (Temporary Mouths). Together they establish a comprehensive framework protecting individuals from exploitation whether by force, fraud, or manipulation of emotion. The Guild remains committed to preserving autonomy as the foundation of all lawful interaction between persons bound hereby. Violators shall be held to strict liability standards given the inherent dangers posed to public safety and moral order.\n\nArchmage Veyra warns all who swear fealty to these Accords: \"Thou shalt not steal another's mind or heart by foul means. Such crimes betray the very essence of magecraft itself.\" Breach of this precept invites swift and severe punishment as outlined in § 100.6 (Delegation to Clerks) for disciplinary matters beyond the Desk's immediate purview. Let it be known that tolerance for such abominations shall remain zero across all sectors under Guild jurisdiction. The preservation of human agency against all forms of subjugation remains paramount to our shared existence as civilized beings capable of self-governance through law rather than brute force alone.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "100.5",
            "600.1",
            "500.1",
            "200.3",
            "100.6",
            "500.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Love Philtres and Undue Influence, § 500.5 Love Philtres and Undue Influence",
          "text": "A philtre is not consent. The Accords recognize that magical compulsion may render a victim incapable of resisting or comprehending their own actions; yet such force does not constitute valid agreement. Under § 107(2), any contract induced by duress, fraud, or covert enchantment is voidable at the option of the aggrieved party. This provision extends to relationships forged under spells that manipulate emotion, desire, or cognition. The burden lies upon the accuser to prove both the existence of magical interference and its material effect on the alleged agreement. Evidence may include witness testimony, spell traces, or expert analysis—but mere assertion is insufficient to overturn a bond. Courts apply strict scrutiny to claims of philtre-induced assent, given the potential for abuse. While the law acknowledges that free will can be compromised by arcane means, it rejects the notion that magic alone invalidates all subsequent transactions. A victim may rescind an agreement tainted by enchantment only if they can demonstrate actual harm resulting from the compelled participation. Absent such proof, the contract stands binding despite its ignoble origins.\"\n\n\"See § 107(2) regarding contracts induced by duress or fraud; see also § 500.3 on corridor courtesy following magical incidents. Under § 1.7, conduct must be aggregated to assess whether a pattern of coercion exists. The Desk reserves authority under § 100.8 to convene an emergency session if public safety is threatened by unchecked philtre usage.\"\n\n\"Love philtres may bind the heart but not the mind; they compel compliance yet cannot erase agency entirely. Thus, while such enchantments are forbidden as manipulative tools, their presence does not automatically invalidate all resulting dealings. The law balances protection of victims against preservation of contractual stability, requiring clear evidence of causal link between spellcasting and alleged duress.\"\n\n\"Philtres serve no lawful purpose beyond exploitation; yet history shows they persist despite prohibitions. Their use invites scrutiny under multiple provisions: § 300.1 on schedules of arts, § 500.4 on healing offered as consideration, and § 600.1 governing contracts with non-casters. The Desk will investigate all reports in accordance with these complementary mandates.\"\n\n\"Undue influence encompasses not only overt spellwork but also subtle psychological manipulation amplified by magical means. Whether through implants, amulets, or ritual magic, any technique that subverts rational judgment falls under this catchline. Victims must act promptly upon discovering such interference, lest delay prejudice their claims.\"\n\n\"The Accords do not condone emotional coercion, yet they acknowledge reality: love is a powerful force capable of overcoming even the strongest will. Magic merely amplifies this natural tendency; it does not create desire where none exists. Thus, while philtres are banned as instruments of control, their effects cannot be entirely excised from human experience.\"\n\n\"Finally, courts exercise discretion in weighing evidence of magical compulsion against factors such as time elapsed since the alleged incident and availability of alternative explanations. The goal is fair adjudication without enabling frivolous suits designed to evade obligations.\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "500.3",
            "1.7",
            "100.8",
            "300.1",
            "500.4",
            "600.1",
            "500.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.5 Love Philtres and Undue Influence — Continued",
          "text": "It shall be unlawful for any person to administer or knowingly facilitate the use of magical philtres, potions, or enchantments designed to alter another's emotions, desires, or volition without explicit consent. Such acts constitute severe bodily harm and a breach of personal autonomy. Consent must be informed, voluntary, and freely given; coercion, deception, or manipulation vitiate agreement entirely. This prohibition extends beyond overt mind control to subtle suggestions, charms, and manipulative tactics employed for illicit ends.\n\nThe burden of proof rests upon the accused to demonstrate that no undue influence was exercised. Circumstantial evidence may suffice where direct testimony is unavailable. Witnesses with knowledge of prior relationships or patterns of behavior are encouraged to come forward voluntarily. Failure to disclose relevant facts may result in enhanced penalties. The Desk reserves authority to investigate suspected cases sua sponte, utilizing all lawful means necessary.\n\nPenalties for violations shall include immediate suspension of magical privileges, mandatory counseling, and restitution to harmed parties. Repeat offenders face disqualification from future licensure and potential imprisonment at the discretion of the court. Confiscation of contraband items is automatic upon conviction. Civil damages may be pursued separately by aggrieved individuals seeking redress for emotional distress or other injuries sustained.\n\nThis section aims to safeguard individual agency against predatory exploitation, whether romantic, financial, or otherwise. The Guild recognizes that true consent requires full awareness and freedom from external pressures. By outlawing manipulative practices, the Accords uphold fundamental principles of dignity and self-determination enshrined in our society's core values. All casters are reminded that ethical conduct remains paramount regardless of magical prowess achieved through lawful study.\n\nSee also § 100.5 regarding conflicts of interest which may compromise professional judgment; § 200.3 prohibiting temporary licenses for sensitive procedures; and § 400.4 mandating proper specimen handling to prevent contamination or alteration of results. Any questions about applicability should be directed to the appropriate clerk for clarification before proceeding with any actions subject to these provisions. Violations will not be tolerated under any circumstances as they undermine both public safety and the integrity of our regulatory framework.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "200.3",
            "400.4",
            "500.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.5 Love Philtres and Undue Influence – Continued",
          "text": "A philtre, whether ingested or otherwise administered, is not evidence of consent to sexual relations. Any claim that consumption of such an agent implies acquiescence to intercourse shall be deemed frivolous and dismissed without prejudice. The Guild stands firmly against the use of magical coercion in matters of affection, deeming it a violation of personal autonomy and bodily integrity.\n\nUndue influence, however, remains a serious concern within the ranks of practitioners. Subtle charms or enchantments employed to sway decisions regarding partnerships or marriages may constitute actionable misconduct under certain circumstances. The burden of proof rests upon the accuser to demonstrate that the alleged influencer utilized their powers deliberately and with intent to manipulate outcomes favorable only to themselves. Mere incidental effects arising from proximity or shared history shall not suffice to establish liability.\n\nThe Desk reserves the right to investigate any suspicion of improper magical interference in private affairs, particularly where it appears to involve licensed casters acting outside official channels. Such probes will be conducted discreetly and swiftly to protect all parties involved while ensuring accountability for breaches of ethical conduct. Penalties imposed upon proven violators shall range from fines and suspensions to full revocation of credentials depending on severity and recidivism.\n\nCiting foundational principles, see § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting which mandates impartiality in professional engagements; and § 200.3 Temporary Mouths prohibiting unauthorized representation by proxies without explicit consent. Furthermore, under § 400.5 Unauthorized Titan Growth, any alteration to one's natural state for purposes of advantage falls within the purview of regulatory oversight. The preservation of individual agency against subterfuge remains paramount in upholding the integrity of magical practice as a whole.\n\nIt is declared that no philtre, charm, or enchantment shall serve as substitute for genuine volition between consenting adults bound by mutual desire. All agreements entered into under duress induced magically are void ab initio and unenforceable regardless of subsequent ratification attempts. The Guild reiterates its commitment to safeguarding the sanctity of personal relationships from exploitation through illicit means while encouraging open dialogue concerning potential abuses within the community at large.\n\nThis section was last revised on the 14th day of Autumn, in the year 382 of the Accords Era, by order of Archmage Sovereign Veyra and the Autumnwood Accords Desk following extensive deliberation among senior members present for said proceeding duly noticed according to established protocol hereinbefore outlined.\n\nsee § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; see § 400.5 Unauthorized Titan Growth; see § 200.3 Temporary Mouths",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "200.3",
            "400.5",
            "500.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.5 Love Philtres and Undue Influence – Continued — Continued",
          "text": "A philtre, whether by magic or alchemy, renders any act of affection compelled rather than voluntary; such compulsion vitiates consent entirely. A caster who administers a love charm to secure compliance is guilty of magical coercion under § 200.4(a). The victim may seek redress through the Desk, which shall determine whether the philtre was applied with intent to defraud or merely as part of a ritual gone awry. In either event, the caster faces disciplinary action and potential revocation of licensure.\n\nUndue influence arises when a caster exploits a relationship of trust or dependency to extract magical favors in exchange for benevolent treatment. This includes scenarios where an employer manipulates an apprentice's emotions to gain free labor, or a mentor uses emotional blackmail to compel submission to inappropriate demands. Such conduct violates the core tenets of professional ethics enshrined in § 100.5(c). The aggrieved party must file a formal complaint within thirty days of discovering the influence; failure to do so bars further recourse under this section.\n\nThe burden of proof rests with the accuser, who must establish by preponderance of evidence that undue influence was exerted. This may involve testimony from witnesses familiar with the parties' interactions, as well as forensic analysis of magical residues left behind during illicit rituals. In cases where direct proof is elusive but circumstantial evidence strongly suggests guilt, the Desk shall apply a presumption favoring protection of the vulnerable party. Penalties range from reprimand and fines to suspension or expulsion from the Guild, depending on severity and frequency of offenses.\n\nNotably, emotional manipulation alone does not constitute undue influence absent actual coercion of magical services. A caster who merely flatters or cajoles an associate into granting a favor, without employing spells or threats, acts outside the scope of § 500.5(b). However, repeated instances of such behavior may warrant investigation for underlying patterns of misconduct under applicable provisions of the Accords. The Desk retains discretion to issue cease-and-desist orders pending full inquiry.\n\nThis section serves as a shield against exploitation in all its forms, ensuring that professional relationships remain grounded in mutual respect rather than manipulation or coercion. By upholding these standards, the Autumnwood Accords safeguard both individual dignity and the integrity of magical practice across all sectors of society. Violators are warned: the consequences for abusing one's position of power can be swift and severe. Let this serve as a clear reminder of the responsibilities inherent in wielding such profound influence over others' lives.\n\nsee § 100.5(c) regarding professional ethics; see § 200.4(a) on magical coercion penalties; see § 300.1 for relevant scheduling provisions.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.4",
            "100.5",
            "500.5",
            "300.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "0ab9245d5f89edad",
      "refs": [
        "1.4",
