    },
    {
      "cite": "500.6",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Destruction of Third-Party Property by Spell",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces",
          "text": "This section enumerates the ancillary provisions that accompany the primary regulation of field casting for value. First, under § 500.7 \"Compensation Schedules,\" all mages engaged in commercial spellslinging must adhere to published tariff tables maintained by the Conservators' Bureau of Magical Economics. These schedules account for variables such as spell complexity, duration of effect, and risk exposure. Deviation from posted rates constitutes a Class B felony punishable by imprisonment without bail. Second, § 500.8 \"Liability Insurance\" mandates that every field caster maintain active coverage through an accredited insurer against claims arising from accidental discharge or botched incantations. Proof of policy must be presented upon request during any Guild inspection. Failure to comply results in immediate revocation of casting privileges and referral for criminal prosecution. Third, § 500.9 \"Quality Control\" requires all commercially sold magical goods to bear a certification mark indicating they have passed rigorous safety testing administered by the Aegis Magi. Unmarked items suspected of containing unstable or hazardous components shall be seized and destroyed at the owner's expense. Fourth, § 500.10 \"Dispute Resolution\" establishes that any civil litigation concerning field casting services shall be adjudicated exclusively in the Hall of Magic before a panel of three impartial mages appointed by lot. Ordinary courts lack jurisdiction over such matters except to enforce judgments rendered under these provisions. Finally, § 500.11 \"Record Keeping\" obliges every professional spellcaster to maintain accurate logs detailing all transactions conducted outside the Guild's facilities for at least seven years. These records must be made available upon reasonable notice during official audits and may serve as evidence in subsequent proceedings. Violation of any provision herein shall subject the offender to all penalties prescribed by law including fines, imprisonment, suspension of license, or permanent disbarment from practice within the sovereign jurisdiction. Nothing in this section shall be construed to limit the application of existing statutes or common law principles governing commercial activity and tort liability.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "500.7",
            "500.8",
            "500.9",
            "500.10",
            "500.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.6 Related Requirements — Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces",
          "text": "It is hereby declared that any act of spellcasting within the bounds of public thoroughfares, marketplaces, civic plazas, or other communal areas shall be deemed a serious infraction under these Accords. Such unauthorized displays of magical power pose an unacceptable risk to the safety and stability of the populace. To mitigate this hazard, all mages are prohibited from initiating any ritualistic casting outside their licensed places of business without prior written consent from the Conservators' Oversight Committee.\n\nViolations of this provision will be subject to immediate investigation by Aegis Magi on site. Upon confirmation of guilt, penalties shall include a fine of no less than one hundred silver marks per offense, plus restitution for any damages caused. Repeat offenders may face suspension of their practitioner license and revocation of immunity privileges until further notice. In egregious cases where public panic or structural failure results from reckless casting, criminal charges under the Penal Code will be pursued alongside civil actions.\n\nThe Conservators maintain that unrestricted spellcasting in shared spaces undermines social order and invites chaos upon an already volatile world. Mages must recognize their privileged status and exercise restraint accordingly. Their talents are a burden as much as a gift, one that demands responsible stewardship rather than wanton exhibitionism for personal gain or amusement.\n\nThis rule stands regardless of any private arrangements made between individual practitioners and local authorities. Only the Guild holds authority to grant exceptions via formal permit processes subject to strict scrutiny. Informal understandings carry no legal weight whatsoever in determining liability under these Accords. All disputes arising from alleged violations must be adjudicated according to established procedures set forth herein or as otherwise provided by law.\n\nIt is further stipulated that any mage found guilty of unauthorized public casting shall be required to undergo mandatory remedial training supervised by senior Conservators at their own expense. Completion of such courses may result in mitigation of penalties but does not absolve the offender entirely. The goal remains consistent: preservation of peace through responsible magical conduct and adherence to binding regulations governing our craft.\n\nLet this statute serve as a firm reminder that even those who wield great power must bow before higher laws. We are guests walking through streets owned by others, dependent upon their goodwill for protection against unseen threats. Until proven otherwise beyond reasonable doubt, each caster is presumed guilty until proven innocent under the strictest standards of evidence and procedure. May none forget this truth lest they find themselves overwhelmed by consequences far greater than imagined.\n\nsee § 500.1 Field Casting for Value; § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse; § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts; § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "500.1",
            "200.4",
            "300.2",
            "100.6",
            "500.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces",
          "text": "§ 500.6 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces; Prohibition and Penalties\n\nThe casting of spells, rites, or other magical workings within the public sphere without prior authorization is strictly prohibited under these Accords. The public realm, comprising all streets, plazas, markets, parks, and buildings not exclusively owned by a licensed practitioner, shall remain free from uncontrolled magical effects that may disrupt commerce, endanger persons, or violate the peace. This prohibition applies to both active spellcasting and passive enchantments that manifest visibly or audibly in public view.\n\nViolations of this section constitute a Class C infraction punishable under § 1302. Penalties include a fine not exceeding one hundred gold pieces per incident, community service equivalent to eight hours of authorized labor, and mandatory attendance at an educational seminar on responsible magical conduct. Repeat offenders within a twelve-month period shall face escalated sanctions pursuant to the graduated penalty schedule set forth in § 1303(a). In egregious cases where unauthorized spellcasting results in property damage or bodily harm, additional civil liabilities may attach under common law principles recognized by this Codex.\n\nLaw enforcement officers of the Autumnwood Accords Hall are empowered to detain individuals suspected of violating this provision pending investigation and adjudication. Detained persons shall be afforded due process rights including access to counsel and a hearing before an impartial tribunal within seven days of arrest, as mandated by § 105(2). Evidence gathered during investigations must adhere strictly to the rules of admissibility outlined in § 801 through § 806. Failure to comply with lawful orders issued pursuant to this section may result in further disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure privileges under § 204(b)(iii).\n\nIt is hereby declared that all citizens have a right to live free from the unpredictable and potentially hazardous effects of unregulated magic. This provision reflects the collective will of the people to preserve order, safety, and dignity in their daily lives. Nothing herein shall be construed to limit the lawful practice of magic within private spaces or under proper permits issued by this Desk. All provisions of this section shall take effect immediately upon filing with the Autumnwood Accords Registry.\n\nSee also § 204(b) regarding disciplinary measures for license holders; § 1302 and § 1303 outlining penalty schedules; § 801 through § 806 governing evidence procedures; and § 105(2) ensuring due process protections for accused individuals. This section serves as a foundational element of public safety policy within the Guild's regulatory framework.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "500.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.6 Related Requirements — Prohibition on Unauthorized Spellcasting in Publi",
          "text": "No mage shall cast any spell outside the confines of their licensed practice without first obtaining a permit from the Autumnwood Accords Desk. Such permits are subject to rigorous scrutiny and may be denied at the sole discretion of the desk. Permit holders must display their license conspicuously while working in public spaces, subject to inspection by duly authorized officers. Failure to comply with these requirements shall result in immediate revocation of said permit and imposition of penalties as provided herein.\n\n§ 500.7 Related Requirements — Licensing and Registration of Commercial Spellcasters\nAll mages engaged in the commercial casting of spells must register with the Guild and obtain a valid license before commencing operations. Licenses are issued upon satisfactory completion of an examination demonstrating competency in relevant magical disciplines, adherence to ethical standards, and payment of all applicable fees. Licensees shall display their credentials prominently at all times while performing services in public venues. Any suspected violations shall be reported promptly to the Guild for investigation. Repeat offenders may face permanent disqualification from practicing magic within the jurisdiction.\n\nThis section establishes clear guidelines governing the conduct of mages operating outside controlled environments. By requiring permits and licenses, the Accords aim to ensure safety, accountability, and professionalism in public spellcasting activities. Failure to comply with these regulations undermines both public trust and the Guild's ability to regulate effectively. All parties are expected to familiarize themselves thoroughly with these provisions and act accordingly. The penalties prescribed herein serve as necessary deterrents against reckless or unauthorized magical interference in communal affairs. It is imperative that all mages uphold their responsibilities under this framework to maintain harmony between arcane pursuits and civic life.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "500.7",
            "500.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.6 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces — Continued",
          "text": "It shall be unlawful for any person to cast a spell, or otherwise manifest magical effects, within the public domain without prior authorization from the Mages' Guild. \"Public domain\" is defined as any space accessible to the general populace, including streets, squares, parks, and commercial establishments. This prohibition exists to protect citizens from unintended harm and to prevent disruption of daily activities by unpredictable arcane phenomena.\n\nViolations of this section shall be subject to immediate cessation orders issued by a Guild Inspector. Failure to comply with such an order may result in further penalties under § 1006.1 Disturbance of the Peace. In cases where unauthorized spellcasting results in damage or injury, additional charges may be brought pursuant to relevant tort and criminal statutes.\n\nThis provision does not apply to licensed magical professionals performing their authorized duties, as outlined in Parts II-VI of this Codex. Nor shall it impede upon emergency response efforts by registered Aegis Magi acting in the interest of public safety. However, any use of magic during such operations must still conform to all applicable laws and regulations.\n\nThe Mages' Guild reserves the right to expand or restrict the scope of this prohibition as deemed necessary for the protection of the public welfare. Any proposed amendments shall be subject to review by the Desk in accordance with standard procedures. Citizens are encouraged to report suspected violations to their nearest Guild Station promptly, providing all available details.\n\nIn summary, § 500.6 establishes a clear boundary between private and public uses of magic, balancing individual freedom with collective security. By regulating spellcasting in communal areas, the Accords seek to mitigate risks while preserving the integrity of magical practice within established frameworks. All persons are expected to familiarize themselves with this rule and act accordingly to ensure harmonious coexistence between arcane pursuits and ordinary life.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1006.1",
            "500.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces – Disturbance of the Peace",
          "text": "In the domain of the Mages' Guild, the quiet and contemplative nature of arcane practices is paramount to maintaining public order and safety. The use of magic within public spaces must be governed by strict rules to ensure that the sanctity of such places remains unbreached. This page addresses the specific issue of unauthorized spellcasting in public spaces, which can lead to disturbances of the peace, creating a hostile environment for bystanders and potentially escalating into civil unrest.\n\nUnder § 500.6 of the Accords, it is strictly prohibited for any mage to cast spells without express permission within public spaces. The term \"public spaces\" includes all areas accessible to the general populace, such as streets, squares, parks, and other common gathering places. Mages who are found guilty of unauthorized spellcasting in these locations face stringent penalties under § 300.2 (Unsanctioned Arts), which categorizes necromancy, blood magic, and other \"dark arts\" as absolutely forbidden.\n\nThe Act establishes that any mage found to have cast an unauthorized spell within a public space will be subject to immediate confiscation of their casting materials and tools under § 500.5 (Unauthorized Titan Growth). Furthermore, the offending mage may face additional sanctions, including but not limited to, a temporary suspension from the Guild under § 200.4 or being placed on the Quiet List for further investigation as per § 300.3.\n\nThe rationale behind this prohibition is rooted in maintaining the stability and tranquility of public spaces. Unauthorized spellcasting can lead to unpredictable outcomes that may endanger bystanders. For instance, a spell intended for personal use could inadvertently affect others nearby, leading to unintended consequences. This disturbance not only risks personal harm but also undermines public trust in the Guild's ability to regulate magical activities effectively.\n\nMoreover, unauthorized spellcasting within public spaces can be misconstrued as a display of power or intimidation by those who are unaware of the mage’s intentions. Such displays may incite fear and unrest among the populace, which is antithetical to the principles of harmony and cooperation that underpin the Accords. The Aegis Magi play a crucial role in enforcing these regulations, ensuring that any breaches are promptly addressed.\n\nIn cases where a disturbance has been caused by unauthorized spellcasting, the offending mage will be held accountable for all resulting damages. This includes any injuries or property damage incurred as a direct result of the spell. Under § 400.5 (Unauthorized Titan Growth), mages found to have grown uncontrolled magical constructs in public spaces without authorization are subject to additional fines and penalties for the disruption caused.\n\nThe Guild encourages members to seek permission from local authorities before casting any spells within public spaces. This not only ensures safety but also fosters a culture of respect for public order. The Accords Desk, through its role under § 100.8 (Emergency Sitting), can be called upon in exceptional circumstances where immediate action is required to mitigate the effects of an unauthorized spell.\n\nIn conclusion, the prohibition on unauthorized spellcasting within public spaces serves to protect both mages and the general populace from potential harm. It reinforces the Guild's commitment to upholding public safety and maintaining the integrity of magical practices as a peaceful art form. Mages are reminded that their actions have far-reaching consequences, and they must always strive to act in accordance with the Accords.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "500.6",
            "300.2",
            "500.5",
            "200.4",
            "300.3",
            "400.5",
            "100.8"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "A random Fireball in the wild is a covered field incident even without a Guild hall, fee, or Guild witness. Incident report, preservation of evidence, restitution, emergency aid, jurisdictional handoff, and defenses for necessity or authorized work.",
      "refs": [
