        "1006.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "600.1",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Contracts with Non-Casters",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Plain language. Illusion of a signature is not a signature. Illusion of a fee is still a fee.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Contracts with Non-Casters",
          "text": "The legal standing of a contract entered into by a non-caster—defined herein as any sentient being lacking the innate ability to weave, channel, or manipulate the weave of magic—shall be governed by the principles of plain language and tangible consideration. While the Mages' Guild recognizes the necessity of trade with the lay populace, specifically with the Goodstyle Artisans and other non-magical entities, the Guild maintains that magical obfuscation shall not be used to circumvent the fundamental requirements of a binding agreement. Any contract wherein the intent is obscured by glamours, mental suggestion, or linguistic manipulation shall be rendered null and void at the first instance of discovery by a presiding Arbiter. The Guild holds that a contract must be understood by the signatory in the common tongue; if a mage employs complex arcane terminology to mask a predatory clause, such a contract is deemed a violation of the Accords.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild establishes a strict doctrine regarding the illusion of authority. An illusion of a signature is not a signature. If a mage utilizes a visual glamour to mimic the appearance of a physical mark, or a psychic impression to simulate the act of signing, the resulting document carries no weight in a court of the Accords. The physical act of marking, whether by ink, blood, or seal, must be performed by the conscious will of the non-caster. Conversely, the Guild maintains a zero-tolerance policy regarding the illusion of consideration. An illusion of a fee is still a fee. If a non-caster provides a service or asset based on the belief that they are being compensated in a specific currency—even if that currency is a magical construct or a fleeting hallucination—the obligation of the mage to provide the actual, tangible value remains absolute. The Guild shall not permit mages to claim that a \"spiritual\" or \"metaphorical\" payment satisfies a material debt. See § 100.2.\n\nIn matters of dispute, the Aegis Magi shall oversee the verification of non-caster contracts. If a non-caster claims they were deceived by a \"magical\" contract, the Guild shall appoint a neutral observer to determine if the language used was intentionally deceptive. Should a mage be found to have used their superior understanding of the arcane to trap a layperson in a labyrinthine legal web, the mage shall be subject to a fine of three hundred gold marks and a mandatory period of community service in the Autumn Wood. This ensures that the Guild remains a pillar of stability rather than a predatory monopoly. All contracts involving the transfer of artifacts or the granting of land rights must be filed with the Clerk of the Accords for verification of plain-language compliance. Any contract that fails this audit shall be flagged as \"Unconscionable\" and may be struck down by the Sovereign.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "100.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Familiars as Agents",
          "text": "The legal status of a familiar—defined as a non-human entity bound to a caster via a permanent or semi-permanent symbiotic bond—is one of delegated agency. Under the Autumnwood Accords, a familiar is not a legal person but is considered an extension of the caster’s will. Consequently, any action taken by a familiar, whether intentional or incidental, shall be attributed directly to the primary caster. This principle of vicarious casting holds that the caster is responsible for the magical output, physical damage, and social transgressions committed by their familiar. If a familiar strikes a bystander or a rival mage, the caster shall be held liable for all damages as if they had performed the act themselves with their own hands. This is to prevent the common defense of \"the beast acted on its own,\" which the Guild recognizes as a fundamental breach of the responsibility inherent in familiar bonding.\n\nThe doctrine of vicarious casting extends to the unauthorized use of magic by a familiar. If a familiar possesses the capacity to channel mana—whether through innate ability or the \"leaking\" of its master's power—the master is strictly liable for any such discharge. Should a familiar cause a rift, a localized weather event, or any form of environmental degradation, the caster must undergo a formal audit by the Conservators. Failure to restrain a familiar from engaging in unauthorized casting may result in the mandatory severing of the bond or the sequestration of the familiar until the caster can demonstrate improved control measures. The Guild emphasizes that the bond is a tool of the caster, and like any tool, its use must be governed by the caster's discipline. A familiar is never a legal shield; it is a conduit of responsibility.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild recognizes the \"Strike by Familiar\" clause in matters of physical altercation. In a scheduled Paradox Trial, a familiar may be used as a combatant, but its actions are governed by the same regulations as a summoned construct or a trained familiar. However, in unscheduled disputes, the use of a familiar as a primary aggressor shall be viewed as an escalation of force. If a familiar initiates a physical strike against a non-caster or a peer, the caster shall be deemed to have initiated the conflict. This prevents mages from using their companions as \"proxy\" aggressors to avoid the repercussions of a direct duel. All familiar training must be documented in the Guild's registry, and any familiar that shows signs of \"rogue agency\"—the ability to act independently of the master's intent—must be reported to Archmage Theron’s office for evaluation. See § 200.2.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "200.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Geas as an Unconscionable Term",
          "text": "The Guild acknowledges that the Geas—a magical binding of a sentient being to a specific course of action or a prohibition—is a powerful and often dangerous instrument of law and personal agreement. However, the Accords state clearly that a Geas to be reasonable is not a defined term. Because \"reasonableness\" is subjective and varies wildly between the radical philosophies of the Innovators and the rigid doctrines of the Conservators, the Guild refuses to recognize \"reasonableness\" as a valid magical constraint in a binding Geas. A Geas that demands a mage act \"reasonably\" in the face of a threat is legally unenforceable because it lacks a specific, measurable objective. Instead, any Geas placed upon a mage by a third party must be articulated with absolute specificity. A mage may be bound to \"not strike a child,\" but they cannot be bound to \"act with wisdom\" or \"behave justly,\" as these are moral qualities, not magical parameters.\n\nThe Guild views the use of \"reasonable\" Geases as a method of magical entrapment, often used by the Regal Empire or the Silver Flame to exert influence over Guild members without committing to specific demands. If a mage enters into a contract containing a Geas that relies on vague, subjective descriptors, the Guild shall treat the Geas as \"Void for Vagueness.\" In such instances, the mage is not bound by the Geas, but they may be penalized for the attempted imposition of an unconscionable magical burden. The Aegis Magi are instructed to strike such clauses from any contract presented for filing. If a Geas is found to be unconscionable—meaning it requires the mage to perform an act of self-harm, a violation of the Accords, or a task that is physically impossible—the caster who imposed the Geas shall be subject to immediate investigation for \"Arcane Coercion.\"\n\nTo ensure the integrity of the Accords, any Geas involving the manipulation of a non-caster's will must be approved by the Archmage Sovereign. The Guild maintains that while mages may use Geases to ensure the fulfillment of contracts, they cannot use them to override the fundamental rights of the person. A Geas that forces a non-caster to act against their own interests in a way that is not explicitly defined in a plain-language contract is a violation of § 600.1. The Guild seeks to prevent the \"Geas-Loop,\" where a mage is forced to perform a series of \"reasonable\" actions that lead to a catastrophic outcome. By requiring specificity, the Accords ensure that the power of the Geas remains a tool of clear intent, rather than a weapon of ambiguity. See § 300.2.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "600.1",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Construct Labor; Minimum Ward",
          "text": "The use of constructs—be they golems, animated armatures, or magically sustained homunculi—as a labor force is permitted under the Autumnwood Accords, provided that the rights of the creator and the safety of the public are maintained. Every construct employed in a commercial or public capacity must be equipped with a Minimum Ward. This ward is a constant, low-level magical signature that serves two purposes: it prevents the construct from acting outside its primary directive and ensures it does not \"leak\" mana into the surrounding environment. A construct without a Minimum Ward is considered an \"Unbound Entity\" and is subject to immediate seizure by the Aegis Magi. The ward must be sufficient to prevent the construct from interacting with the weave in a way that could cause localized instability or unauthorized research.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild establishes the principle of \"Construct Compensation.\" While a construct is not a person and thus does not require a wage in the sense of the Goodstyle Artisans, the owner of the construct is responsible for the \"Rest\" of the construct. This refers to the periodic recharging of the mana core or the ritualistic maintenance of the binding circles. A construct that is worked beyond its capacity, leading to \"Mana Burn\" or structural collapse, is a violation of Guild safety standards. Owners must provide a documented schedule of maintenance and energy replenishment. If a construct is found to be operating in a state of exhaustion—evidenced by erratic movements or erratic magical discharge—the owner shall be fined for \"Negligent Maintenance.\" This ensures that constructs do not become hazards to the public or the Guild's stability.\n\nIn the event of a construct's malfunction, the owner is strictly liable for all damages, regardless of whether the malfunction was \"unforeseen.\" The Guild rejects the defense of \"Construct Autonomy\" in cases of property damage or bodily harm. If a construct strikes a person, it is treated as a violation of the owner's license, similar to a familiar's actions. Construct labor must also be registered with the Desk; any construct capable of lifting more than five hundred pounds or casting a spell of third-circle magnitude or higher must be granted a \"Heavy Labor Permit.\" This permit requires a monthly inspection by a Conservator to ensure the Minimum Ward remains intact. Constructs used for \"Restricted Research\" must be held in a designated laboratory, as per § 400.2, and may not be deployed in public spaces without an express waiver from Janna Brightspark's Innovator council.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "400.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Summoned Notary",
          "text": "The Guild recognizes the necessity of high-level verification for contracts involving the transfer of significant arcane assets, the granting of land rights in the Midlands, or the formation of new magical covenants. For such high-stakes transactions, the parties may employ a Summoned Notary—a sentient being from another plane or a high-level spirit summoned specifically to witness and stamp the agreement. However, the use of a Summoned Notary is strictly regulated to prevent \"Planar Evasion,\" where a mage attempts to have a contract validated by a being that does not recognize the laws of the Autumnwood Accords. A Summoned Notary must be capable of understanding the common tongue and must be bound by a \"Truth-Binding\" oath for the duration of the notarization process.\n\nThe requirements for a Summoned Notary are rigorous. The notary must be summoned into a \"Neutral Zone,\" such as a Guild-sanctioned chamber, to ensure that their presence does not cause environmental instability. The notary must provide a \"Planar Signature\"—a unique magical mark that is distinct from the caster's own magic. This signature must be applied to the document in the presence of a Guild Clerk. If a Summoned Notary vanishes mid-stamp, the contract is considered \"Incomplete\" and holds no legal weight. The Guild maintains that a vanishing notary is a sign of an unstable summoning or a deliberate attempt to create a legal loophole where the \"witness\" is no longer present to be held accountable for the truth of the proceedings. In such cases, the parties must restart the notarization process with a new entity.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild prohibits the use of Notaries from the Regal Empire or the Iron Legion, as their legal codes are in direct conflict with the Accords. Any contract notarized by a foreign entity's spirit is considered \"Adverse\" and will be rejected by the Desk. The Guild also mandates that the summoning of a Notary must be disclosed in the contract itself. If a mage attempts to hide the fact that a supernatural entity was used to witness a deal, the contract is voided, and the mage may face a \"Paradox Trial\" for unauthorized planar interaction. The goal of these regulations is to ensure that the Guild's records are immutable and that every high-level agreement is witnessed by an entity whose testimony can be verified against the Guild's own standards of truth. See § 100.4.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; Artificial Agents",
          "text": "This Part governs the binding force and validity of contracts entered into by non-casters or artificial entities lacking natural agency, including familiar spirits, constructs, and summoned beings. Such agreements shall be treated with particular scrutiny to prevent exploitation and ensure equitable terms. A mere facsimile of a signature, whether created through illusion magic or mechanical duplication, does not constitute a true assent. Likewise, feigned payment or value transfer remains binding on the parties regardless of apparent falsity.\n\nFamiliars acting as agents for their masters are held to strict liability for any breaches committed within the scope of assigned duties. This extends even when actions appear unauthorized or outside explicit instructions. The principle of respondeat superior applies by analogy, imposing vicarious liability upon the master. Constructs and other artificial persons shall be governed similarly, with their creators bearing ultimate responsibility for lawful conduct during operations. No artificial entity may contract independently without express ratification from its natural owner or authorized proxy.\n\nGeas magic binding a caster's will to fulfill another's wishes shall not be deemed an unconscionable term voiding the agreement ab initio, provided it does not violate public policy or statutory prohibitions. Such clauses remain enforceable as valid stipulations of contract law, subject only to standard defenses including impossibility and frustration of purpose. However, any geas imposing permanent servitude or loss of autonomy shall be strictly construed against the drafter and invalidated unless clearly consented to in advance.\n\nConstruct labor performed under contractual obligation is not exempt from minimum wage requirements and safety standards applicable to human workers. Employers utilizing mechanical assistants must compensate them according to prevailing industry rates for comparable tasks. Failure to pay owed wages subjects the employer to penalties equal to double the arrears plus statutory damages. This rule ensures fair treatment of all forms of labor, biological or synthetic, regardless of intelligence level.\n\nSummons rituals invoking external entities shall be witnessed and notarized by a licensed notary public with expertise in arcane matters. The notary verifies the authenticity of signatures and affixes an official seal to confirm validity. Any contract lacking such formalities may be challenged on grounds of forgery or undue influence unless ratified within thirty days. This safeguard protects vulnerable parties from predatory practices and ensures transparency in high-stakes magical dealings.\n\nParties engaging in contracts involving prohibited arts must disclose all material facts regarding risks and consequences. Concealment of known dangers renders the agreement voidable at option of the injured party. Full disclosure is a condition precedent to informed consent, especially when dealing with powerful or dangerous spells. Any misrepresentation inducing entry into contract shall constitute fraud actionable under common law principles.\n\nFinally, this Part establishes that artificial agents cannot acquire property rights independently. All gains accrued through their efforts belong solely to their masters or employers unless expressly assigned otherwise in writing. This prevents disputes over ownership of magically generated assets and simplifies estate planning for those who utilize such beings extensively. Clear title remains paramount even when conventional commerce is supplemented by supernatural means.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "600.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "f8d3db4ca3ebe4cc",
      "refs": [
        "100.2",
        "100.4",
        "200.2",
        "300.2",
        "400.2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "600.2",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Familiars as Agents",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Vicarious casting. Strike by familiar.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Familiars as Agents",
          "text": "The legal standing of a familiar as an agent of a licensed practitioner is established under the principle of vicarious weaving. Pursuant to the Autumnwood Accords, a familiar is defined as a sentient or semi-sentient entity bound to a master through a formal tether of intent and mana. While the familiar may possess independent locomotion and minor autonomous actions, any magical manifestation originating from the familiar’s essence is legally attributed to the primary caster. This doctrine of vicarious casting ensures that the responsibility for arcane fluctuations, environmental shifts, or unintended collateral damage remains with the primary licensee. If a familiar executes a spell—whether by direct command or by instinctual reaction—the master shall be held liable for any breach of the Accords, regardless of the master's stated intent at the moment of casting.\n\nFurthermore, the doctrine of \"Strike by Familiar\" governs physical and metaphysical interactions between a familiar and a third party. Should a familiar strike, bite, or otherwise physically assault a non-combatant or a Guild-protected citizen, the master is deemed to have authorized the use of force. The master may not claim \"loss of control\" as a defense for familiar aggression, as the tether itself constitutes a continuous delegation of agency. To mitigate liability, masters are encouraged to maintain clear behavioral boundaries; however, the legal burden remains heavy. Any familiar that displays persistent hostility toward the public may be subject to immediate seizure by the Aegis Magi for \"re-education\" or permanent binding. See § 600.1 for the primary definitions of non-caster contracts, as a familiar's actions may occasionally intersect with the rights of those who cannot weave.\n\nAny familiar acting under a direct command to perform a task that results in the destruction of property or the violation of a local ordinance shall be treated as a direct extension of the master's hand. This means that a \"rogue\" familiar is, in the eyes of the Accords Desk, a malfunctioning tool of the master, much like a malfunctioning wand or a cracked focus. Therefore, the master must ensure that all familiars are properly attuned to the restrictive protocols of the Midlands. Failure to restrain a familiar during a public demonstration or a sanctioned research session shall result in a formal citation and potential suspension of the master's license. The Guild acknowledges that familiars are not mere pets but are arcane proxies; as such, the master is the ultimate guarantor of the familiar's conduct within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "600.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Geas as an Unconscionable Term",
          "text": "A geas, defined as a magical compulsion or a binding oath enforced by arcane weight, is subject to the same standards of equity as any written contract entered into by the Guild's members. Under the Accords, a geas is considered an unconscionable term if it imposes a burden that is fundamentally disproportionate to the consideration provided, or if it seeks to strip the subject of their fundamental agency in a manner that violates the stability of the Midlands. However, the Guild explicitly notes that \"a geas to be reasonable\" is not a defined term within this Codex. Because the subjective experience of \"reasonableness\" varies between the traditionalist Conservators and the radical Innovators, the Accords Desk shall not adjudicate based on the internal feelings of the parties involved. Instead, the Desk shall look to the objective impact of the geas on the individual's ability to function as a productive member of society.\n\nIf a geas is found to be unconscionable—for instance, requiring a mage to perform acts of self-harm, treason against the Guild, or the deliberate violation of the Forbidden Schools—it shall be deemed voidable at the request of the bound party. The petitioner must present a formal grievance to the Archmage Sovereign, who shall mediate the dispute. If the geas was established without the clear, informed consent of the bound individual, the Accords Desk may move to have the binding magically dissolved by the Aegis Magi. The Guild warns that attempting to bypass these protections by using \"subtle\" geases—those that do not appear overtly coercive but slowly erode the subject's will—is a grave violation of the Accords and may lead to a Paradox Trial.\n\nThe Innovators may argue for a wider latitude in the application of geases for the purpose of research, while the Conservators will insist on the strictest limitations to prevent the creation of \"arcane thralls.\" In the absence of a specific ruling from the Sovereign, the Desk will default to the principle of least restrictive means. A geas that prevents a mage from casting certain spells is generally permissible; a geas that prevents a mage from speaking, eating, or sleeping without cause is likely to be struck down as unconscionable. Any practitioner who knowingly imposes an unconscionable geas upon another may be subject to the penalties outlined in § 300.2. Furthermore, any contract involving a geas must be filed with the Accords Desk to ensure it does not conflict with existing Guild mandates.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Construct Labor; Minimum Ward",
          "text": "All constructs created for the purpose of labor—including but not limited to golems, clockwork automatons, and animated sculptures—are subject to the regulations regarding artificial sentience and magical maintenance. To ensure the safety of the public and the stability of the local mana field, all constructs utilized in commercial or industrial capacities must be equipped with a Minimum Ward. This ward is a baseline magical seal that prevents the construct from acting with independent malice or exceeding its programmed parameters. A construct lacking a Minimum Ward is considered an \"unbound entity\" and its operation within the Midlands is strictly prohibited. The Aegis Magi are authorized to dismantle any construct found to be operating without a visible or palpable ward.\n\nThe maintenance of a Minimum Ward is the sole responsibility of the construct’s owner or the master who summoned it. This includes the periodic recharging of the ward's essence and the repair of any structural fractures that might allow for \"magical leakage.\" Furthermore, the Accords Desk stipulates that constructs used for heavy labor must be periodically \"rested.\" This does not mean the construct must sleep in a biological sense, but rather that its primary mana-circuitry must be decoupled from its labor-functions for a period of one hour for every eight hours of operation. This prevents the accumulation of \"arcane fatigue,\" which can lead to erratic behavior or spontaneous combustion of the construct's core.\n\nRegarding compensation, a construct is not a person and therefore cannot be \"paid\" in the legal sense of the Accords. However, the owner of the construct is responsible for the \"cost of maintenance,\" which includes the mana expended to maintain the Minimum Ward and any materials required for repairs. If a construct is hired by a non-caster for a specific task, the non-caster must provide the owner with a \"Labor Agreement\" as defined in § 600.1. If the construct malfunctions and causes damage, the owner is liable for all costs, provided the construct was properly warded and rested. Failure to provide adequate maintenance or to enforce rest periods shall result in the immediate revocation of the owner's right to operate constructs within the Hall of Autumn Wood.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "600.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Summoned Notary",
          "text": "In instances where a legal agreement must be witnessed or verified by a being of significant arcane standing who is not currently present, the use of a Summoned Notary is permitted. A Summoned Notary is an entity—be it a spirit, a minor elemental, or a displaced consciousness—brought into the physical plane specifically to observe and validate the signing of a contract or the completion of a Guild filing. For such a notary to be recognized by the Accords Desk, it must be bound by a \"Temporary Mouth\" agreement (see § 200.3) and must possess the requisite level of arcane resonance to ensure the integrity of the seal. The summoning must be performed in a controlled environment, and the notary must remain stationary for the duration of the proceedings.\n\nThe form requirements for a Summoned Notary are rigorous. The master performing the summoning must provide a \"Script of Intent,\" detailing exactly what the notary is to witness and the specific magical signature it is required to apply. The notary must be clearly identified in the documentation, including its origin, its duration of presence, and the specific nature of its binding. Once the seal is applied, the notary must be released immediately. Any attempt to hold a Summoned Notary in the Midlands for longer than the duration of the signing is a violation of the Accords and may be prosecuted as unauthorized summoning. The notary's signature must be distinct from the master's own signature to ensure a clear chain of custody for the document.\n\nA unique and problematic scenario arises in the event of a \"Vanishing Notary\"—a situation where a summoned entity disappears mid-stamp or before the seal is fully set. If a Summoned Notary vanishes before the document is finalized, the document is considered null and void. The master is then required to re-summon a notary and re-execute the entire filing process. In cases where the notary's disappearance causes physical damage or magical instability, the master shall be held liable for the \"interruption of proceedings\" and may face a fine. The Accords Desk will not accept any document where the seal is partial, smudged, or incomplete due to the notary's departure. Practitioners are advised to ensure their summoning circles are stable and their binding words are clear to prevent such logistical failures.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.3 Vicarious Casting and Proxy Liability",
          "text": "It is provided that where a Caster acts as an agent or proxy for another Person, the resulting magical effects shall be attributed to the principal unless clearly designated otherwise. This rule applies whether the action involves direct spellcasting through a familiar construct or remote initiation of pre-programmed sequences within authorized devices. The principal remains fully liable for any damages caused by such actions, regardless of actual involvement in the immediate operation.\n\nThe law further clarifies that mere possession or control of an item does not constitute authorization for its use. Owners who fail to properly secure their magical belongings against unauthorized access may be held complicit if those items are subsequently misused. This principle extends to digital and physical interfaces alike, requiring diligent oversight by responsible Parties. Negligence in safeguarding one's assets shall serve as evidence of implied consent where intent cannot otherwise be proven.\n\nIn cases involving complex multistage operations spanning multiple jurisdictions or durations beyond standard limitations, explicit written agreements must be executed between all involved entities. Such contracts shall detail the scope of authority granted to each participant and establish clear lines of accountability should complications arise during execution. Failure to provide adequate documentation where required by this section may result in automatic invalidation of any claims predicated upon those operations.\n\nThe Desk maintains discretion to demand additional security measures or monitoring protocols when evaluating requests for high-risk magical activities. This power shall be exercised only after thorough review and consultation with relevant experts as deemed necessary under the circumstances. Any conditions imposed pursuant to this authority must be reasonable, proportionate to the identified risks, and consistent with overarching public safety objectives outlined in these Accords.\n\nSee also § 100.6 regarding delegation of duties by official representatives; § 200.5 governing guest casters operating under supervision; § 400.3 limiting temporal scope for borrowed resources. All provisions herein shall be construed to promote fair dealing while preserving essential protections for innocent third parties affected by expansive magical undertakings.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "200.5",
            "400.3",
            "600.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.2 Vicarious Casting and Proxy Liability",
          "text": "A caster who directs another to cast a spell on their behalf shall be held fully liable for the consequences thereof, as if they had performed the act themselves. This liability extends to all damages arising from the casting, whether intentional, negligent, or accidental. The directing caster cannot escape responsibility by claiming lack of direct participation in the ritual.\n\nThis provision applies regardless of whether the proxy is a licensed artisan, an unlicensed individual, or even an autonomous construct acting under instruction. The law presumes that any such act constitutes a deliberate delegation of magical authority, and thus binds the delegator to all outcomes. Exceptions may be made only where fraud, duress, or unconscious compulsion renders the original direction invalid.\n\nIn cases involving complex spells requiring multiple components, each participant assumes liability for their portion of the work. If the spell fails catastrophically due to error by one element-caster, that individual bears sole responsibility for rectification and compensation. Joint ventures in casting shall be governed by written agreement specifying shared duties and liabilities absent contrary stipulation.\n\nThe Desk retains discretion to impose additional penalties on repeat offenders who habitually outsource dangerous operations without adequate safeguards. Such sanctions may include temporary suspension of privileges or mandatory retraining under supervision. The goal remains preservation of public safety through enforcement of accountability standards across all forms of magical practice.\n\nsee § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks; see § 200.3 Temporary Mouths; see § 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration. Under § 1.7 Aggregation of Conduct, a pattern of proxy casting may establish reckless disregard for safety regulations. Any dispute over fault shall be adjudicated by the full Accords Desk in accordance with established precedent and evidence presented by all parties involved.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "200.3",
            "500.4",
            "1.7",
            "600.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "17dce65c31ce5f65",
