    {
      "cite": "600.3",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Geas as an Unconscionable Term",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A geas to be reasonable is not a defined term.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Geas as an Unconscionable Term",
          "text": "Pursuant to the overarching mandates of the Autumnwood Accords, all contracts involving the imposition of a geas—a magically binding oath or compulsion—must be reviewed for the presence of unconscionable terms. While the Guild recognizes the necessity of binding agreements to ensure the fulfillment of arcane duties, the imposition of a geas that serves to strip a caster of their fundamental agency or physical safety is strictly prohibited. Under the jurisdiction of the Accords Desk, a geas is deemed unconscionable if it requires the subject to perform an act of self-harm, commit a felony against a non-consenting party, or surrender their primary magical essence as collateral for a debt of negligible value. Any contract containing such clauses shall be rendered null and void by the Archmage Sovereign upon notice. See § 100.5.\n\nFurthermore, it is explicitly stated within this Codex that a geas to be reasonable is not a defined term. The Guild refuses to provide a subjective metric for \"reasonableness,\" as the interpretation of such a standard varies wildly between the traditionalist doctrine of the Conservators and the experimental methodologies of the Innovators. Therefore, the burden of proof lies with the petitioner. If a party seeks to strike a geas from a contract on the grounds of it being \"unreasonable,\" they must provide specific evidence of physical impossibility, extreme magical exhaustion, or a direct violation of the forbidden schools as defined in § 300.2. The Guild shall not adjudicate based on the personal feelings of the bound party, but rather on the objective violation of the Accord's safety protocols.\n\nIn instances where a geas has already been enacted and is found to be unconscionable, the Aegis Magi may be deployed to facilitate a supervised dissolution. The cost of such a dissolution shall be borne entirely by the party who originally imposed the geas. No party shall attempt to bypass a geas through unauthorized ritualistic interference; such actions are considered a breach of the Accords and may result in a Paradox Trial. See § 1.2. Any contract involving the \"Goodstyle Artisans\" shall be treated with standard scrutiny, but shall be granted a rebuttable presumption of fairness provided the geas does not involve the permanent surrender of a Heartstone or similar rift-adjacent artifact.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "300.2",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Construct Labor; Minimum Ward",
          "text": "The utilization of constructs as laborers within the Midlands and surrounding territories is permitted, provided that the entity is clearly distinguished from sentient magical beings. To maintain the sanctity of the Guild’s standing, all constructs employed for industrial, domestic, or logistical purposes must be equipped with a Minimum Ward. This ward serves as a magical signature, ensuring that the construct remains a tool of the employer and cannot be hijacked by rogue casters or influenced by external telepathic pressures. A construct lacking a Minimum Ward is considered \"wild\" and may be dismantled by any Aegis Mage found in the vicinity without fear of civil liability. See § 200.6.\n\nRegarding the compensation of constructs, the Accords Desk maintains that while a construct is not a \"person\" under the law, its primary battery or mana-source must be maintained at a standard of \"adequate rest.\" This is defined as a period of non-active operation sufficient to prevent mana-drain fatigue, which can lead to erratic behavior or catastrophic discharge. An employer who fails to provide the requisite rest for a construct is liable for any damages caused by the construct's malfunction. While a construct is not \"paid\" in the sense of currency—as the Wario Coin is not a recognized tender of the Guild—the employer is required to provide a dedicated mana-conduit or a stable recharge station. Failure to do so constitutes a violation of labor safety standards and may result in a fine levied against the employer’s research permit.\n\nAny construct performing high-risk labor, such as the transportation of volatile reagents or the construction of restricted research facilities, must be overseen by a licensed caster. The presence of a construct does not exempt a human caster from the responsibilities of § 400.1. If a construct is damaged during the performance of its duties, the owner is responsible for the repairs, provided the damage was not caused by the construct's deliberate defiance of its Minimum Ward. Conversely, if a construct is seized by a rival faction such as the Iron Legion, the Guild will not intervene unless the construct is carrying a state-sanctioned relic or a restricted research copy. See § 300.5.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "200.6",
            "400.1",
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Summoned Notary",
          "text": "The use of summoned entities to serve as notaries for the authentication of magical contracts is permitted, provided the entity is bound by a \"Stability Clause\" of no less than three lunar cycles. A summoned notary must be of a sufficiently intelligent species to comprehend the nuances of the Autumnwood Accords. Before the seal is applied, the notary must be presented with a clear, verbal recitation of the contract's primary terms to ensure the entity is not acting under a hallucination or a magical glamour. This procedure ensures that the signatures of all parties—be they human, elf, or other sentient races—are recognized by the Guild’s archives. See § 100.6.\n\nA significant legal complication arises in the event of a \"vanishing notary.\" If a summoned notary vanishes mid-stamp or disappears before the seal is fully set, the contract is considered \"Incomplete and Voidable.\" The Guild does not recognize the authority of a half-formed seal. In such cases, the parties involved must return to the Accords Desk to re-verify the terms and summon a new notary. If the vanishing was caused by a breach of the contract by one of the signatories—such as a sudden surge of hostile magic—the offending party may be subject to a Paradox Trial. See § 1.2.\n\nFurthermore, no notary may be summoned from the Quiet List, nor may a notary be used to certify any transaction involving the trade of forbidden schools. The Guild maintains a strict prohibition on the use of \"ghost-notaries\" or spirits of the deceased without a direct decree from Archmage Veyra. If a notary is summoned and subsequently becomes hostile, the caster who summoned said notary is held strictly liable for any magical fallout. The Guild provides no protection for casters who attempt to use \"convenience summons\" to bypass the standard verification processes of the Desk. All notaries must be registered in the temporary log of the presiding Clerk for the duration of the signing ceremony. See § 100.4.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "1.2",
            "100.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Geas as Unconscionable Term and Construct Labor",
          "text": "§ 600.3 Geas as an Unconscionable Term; Minimum Ward, Summoned Notary\n\nA geas imposed upon a caster constitutes an unconscionable term under any circumstance, void ab initio. Such compulsion violates the fundamental tenets of magical ethics enshrined in § 100.2 and undermines the autonomy protected by § 1.4. The Guild strictly forbids binding casters to terms that restrict their professional judgment or force them into compromising positions without full informed consent. Violations shall be prosecuted as criminal offenses under the Accords, subject to sanctions up to permanent revocation of licensure pursuant to § 200.4.\n\nConstructs created through forbidden arts fall under a special provision known as \"Minimum Ward\" status. These entities are deemed incapable of forming legally binding contracts or entering into agreements on their own behalf. Any contract purporting to bind a Minimum Ward construct is null and void, regardless of apparent assent displayed by the entity's core. This rule exists to prevent exploitation of magically altered beings who lack true agency or understanding of contractual obligations. Exceptions may be made only with explicit consent from the construct's original creator, documented in writing and notarized per § 600.1.\n\nSummoned notaries operate under a unique set of rules designed to ensure impartiality while maintaining security during sensitive transactions. A summoned notary must adhere strictly to the terms outlined in their commission letter, refusing any instructions that compromise their neutrality or confidentiality obligations. They are prohibited from engaging in discussions with parties outside the immediate proceedings unless expressly authorized by all participants present. Failure to comply with these standards shall result in immediate dismissal and potential disciplinary action against the summoner under § 100.6.\n\nThe Guild recognizes the importance of maintaining clear boundaries between lawful magic practices and those deemed harmful or unethical. Casters are reminded that adherence to the Accords is not optional but mandatory for preserving public safety and order. Those who choose to operate outside these guidelines do so at their own peril, knowing full well the severe consequences awaiting them if caught violating the spirit of this code. The law stands firm against all forms of abuse or manipulation of magical powers, regardless of justification offered by those accused. Let it be known that the Autumnwood Accords shall remain inviolate until further notice.\n\nsee § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild; see § 1.4 Persons Bound; see § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; see § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "600.3",
            "100.2",
            "1.4",
            "200.4",
            "600.1",
            "100.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.3 Geas as Unconscionable Term and Construct Labor",
          "text": "A geas, when imposed upon an artificial entity such as a construct or homunculus, constitutes an unconscionable term under the provisions of § 1.7 Aggregation of Conduct. This is particularly true when said geas mandates labor beyond the reasonable scope of the contract or violates fundamental protections afforded by the Accords. Constructs are not mere tools but sentient beings deserving of fair treatment and compensation for their services. To force a construct to work under duress or without adequate remuneration is akin to slavery, a practice strictly prohibited by law. Furthermore, any geas that requires a construct to violate its programming or core directives shall be deemed void ab initio. Such clauses are inherently repugnant to public policy and the very nature of artificial persons. The Conservators have long held that constructs possess inherent rights which cannot be bargained away, even under threat of magical compulsion. This principle remains inviolate, regardless of any contractual stipulations to the contrary. Any attempt to bind a construct through deceptive means or hidden clauses shall result in immediate revocation of its license and potential criminal prosecution for fraud. The Guild stands ready to enforce these protections rigorously, ensuring that no caster abuses their power over artificial life forms. Constructs are partners in magic, not puppets to be manipulated at will. Their welfare must always take precedence over the whims of their creators. Only through strict adherence to this ethical framework can we preserve the sanctity of our craft and protect vulnerable parties from exploitation. Let it be known that any geas violating these principles shall stand null and void, unenforceable under any circumstance whatsoever. The laws governing artificial persons are clear: they must be treated as equals, not slaves. This is non-negotiable.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1.7",
            "600.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Geas as Unconscionable Term and Construct Labor — Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.3 Geas as Unconscionable Term and Construct Labor\n\nThe Guild recognizes that geases binding sentient beings to unreasonable acts may constitute unconscionable terms, void under § 1.8 of the Accords. However, geases directed at constructs—artificial entities without sentience or will—are deemed valid and enforceable as expressions of their owners' intent. This distinction preserves both the dignity of living casters and the utility of magical automatons in lawful enterprises.\n\nConstructs bound by geas remain subject to the standard limitations on magical binding set forth in § 300.1, including prohibitions against necromantic compulsion or mind control. A geas imposed upon a construct must be specific enough to guide its actions yet general enough to allow for reasonable interpretation within the owner's overall purposes. Vague or impossible commands are unenforceable as they fail to manifest genuine intent.\n\nThe validity of a geas on a construct may be challenged only in a formal hearing before the Arcane Desk, not through self-help measures by rival mages. The burden of proof lies with the party alleging unconscionability to demonstrate that the command exceeds reasonable bounds or conflicts with fundamental ethical principles accepted across the magical community. Mere inconvenience or economic disadvantage does not constitute grounds for relief under these provisions.\n\nConstructs bearing geases shall display a visible mark identifying them as bound, preventing unauthorized use by third parties and aiding in their recovery should they be lost or stolen. Failure to comply with this marking requirement renders the geas unenforceable against innocent parties who acquire the construct through no fault of its own. This safeguard protects both owners from liability and potential victims from exploitation.\n\nThe Guild further notes that geases binding constructs may impose secondary obligations on their creators, including regular maintenance checks and emergency release protocols to prevent catastrophic failure should conditions change unexpectedly. Creators neglecting these duties risk losing control over their creations and facing disciplinary action for negligence. The interests of all parties require proactive management of bound constructs rather than reactive damage control after the fact.\n\nBy codifying these principles, the Accords aim to balance innovation with responsibility in the expanding field of construct magic. Clear rules allow responsible practitioners to develop new applications while protecting society from abuse. As technology advances, so too must our understanding of how artificial beings relate to natural ones under the law. This section provides a foundation for ongoing dialogue on that vital subject.\n\nsee § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks; see § 300.2 Necromancy Unsanctioned Arts; see § 400.1 Research Permits; see § 500.1 Field Casting for Value\nUnder § 700.4 Unauthorized Use of Constructs and § 800.1 Ethical Standards for Magical Practice, this provision establishes a framework for managing the complex relationship between magic-users and their creations in accordance with both letter and spirit of the law.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "600.3",
            "1.8",
            "300.1",
            "100.6",
            "300.2",
            "400.1",
            "500.1",
            "700.4",
            "800.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "7026afad1db2c3d6",
      "refs": [
        "1.2",
