      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "600.4",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Construct Labor; Minimum Ward",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Wards, rest, who is paid.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Construct Labor; Minimum Ward",
          "text": "All constructs, regardless of their method of manufacture—be they woven from clay and sigil, forged of iron and steam, or birthed from the ethereal essence of the Weave—are subject to the labor protections of the Autumnwood Accords. It is the decree of the Accords that no construct shall be utilized for the purpose of manual labor, transport, or administrative duties without a Minimum Ward of protection. This Ward serves as a legal and metaphysical tether, ensuring that the construct remains bound to its designated purpose and does not suffer from \"arcane drift,\" a phenomenon wherein a construct’s lack of a soul-anchor causes it to develop erratic, self-directed behaviors that may endanger the surrounding population or the integrity of the Guild’s property. A construct operating without such a Ward is considered a rogue entity and may be dismantled by any Aegis Mage on sight without prior notice of intent.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords dictate the specific requirements for the maintenance of these constructs. Every construct engaged in labor for a Guild-affiliated entity or a licensed private caster must be granted a period of \"Restorative Stasis\" at the conclusion of every lunar cycle. During this period, the construct is to be deactivated and its internal mana-conduits allowed to cool or replenish. Failure to provide adequate Restorative Stasis constitutes a violation of the Labor Stability Act. If a construct is forced to labor through its designated rest period, the owner of said construct shall be liable for double the standard labor tax, and the construct shall be subject to a mandatory recalibration by the Conservators.\n\nThe payment of wages for constructs is a matter of bureaucratic record but not of spiritual necessity. While a construct does not possess a personal bank account or the capacity for greed, the \"payment\" it receives is accounted for as a maintenance stipend. This stipend is to be held in a trust by the employer and used exclusively for the repair of the construct’s physical shell, the recharging of its primary core, and the renewal of its Minimum Ward. Any diversion of these funds for personal gain by the employer is a felony under the Accords. See § 600.1 for matters regarding contracts with non-casters. See § 200.6 for the specific limitations on constructs that hold a quill.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "600.1",
            "200.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Summoned Notary",
          "text": "The authentication of high-level arcane contracts often necessitates the presence of a Summoned Notary, a specialized entity of neutral legal standing capable of witnessing the binding of complex oaths. These notaries are typically summoned from the Neutral Planes or designated pocket-dimensions to ensure that the signatures of the involved parties are untainted by terrestrial bias. To be valid under the Autumnwood Accords, a Summoned Notary must be presented with a valid Writ of Summoning, issued by a Guild Clerk or an appointed mediator such as Archmage Veyra. The notary must be capable of speaking the common tongue and the primary arcane dialect of the region in which the contract is to be executed.\n\nThe form requirements for a Summoned Notary are stringent to prevent the forging of legal intent. A notary must present a Seal of Verity, which is a magically-reactive stamp that changes color or shape based on the truthfulness of the statements being recorded. All contracts witnessed by a Summoned Notary must be written on vellum of a specific weight and treated with a non-reactive anti-magic wash to ensure that the notary’s seal does not degrade or become obscured by ambient mana. If a notary fails to present a Seal of Verity, or if the Seal is found to be a mere physical imitation, the contract is null and void, and the parties involved may be subject to a Paradox Trial for attempted fraud.\n\nA unique and perilous legal complication arises in the event of a Notary’s Vanishing. Because summoned entities are bound by the laws of their own planes as much as the Accords, a notary who vanishes mid-stamp creates a \"Limping Oath.\" If a notary disappears while the seal is being pressed into the wax or the parchment, the legal status of the contract enters a state of flux. In such instances, the contract is considered \"Unsealed\" and carries no weight until a secondary notary of equal rank can be summoned to perform a \"Completion of Intent.\" If a notary vanishes and the parties involved flee the scene, the contract is declared a Breach of Presence, and the parties may be pursued by the Aegis Magi. Any notary who vanishes mid-stamp due to a lack of sufficient mana-binding on the part of the caster shall be held liable for the costs of the secondary summoning. See § 100.2 for limitations on who may speak for the Guild.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "100.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.4 Construct Labor and Minimum Ward Requirements",
          "text": "The employment of constructs in commercial or domestic service is subject to strict regulation under the Autumnwood Accords, designed to protect both workers and employers from exploitation and abuse. § 600.4 establishes fundamental labor standards that must be observed when engaging a construct's services, whether for wages, barter, or other forms of compensation.\n\nFirstly, no employer may require a construct to work beyond its natural limits without providing adequate rest periods between shifts. The Accords define \"reasonable hours\" as no more than eight consecutive hours per day, with at least one hour off for maintenance and recharging. Employers failing to adhere to these limits face penalties ranging from fines to revocation of operating licenses.\n\nSecondly, all construct employers must provide a safe working environment free from hazards that could cause damage or injury. This includes proper containment facilities where applicable, as well as prompt repair of any malfunctions arising during normal operation. Negligence leading to severe property loss or harm results in criminal charges under the General Penal Code.\n\nThirdly, constructs employed in domestic settings must be granted appropriate privacy and dignity protections equivalent to those afforded human servants under existing law. This encompasses restrictions on intrusive surveillance techniques as well as safeguards against unnecessary exposure of intimate areas during routine maintenance procedures. Violations constitute civil torts actionable by injured parties.\n\nFinally, § 600.4 mandates that all contracts involving construct labor be executed in accordance with standard legal formalities, including written documentation signed by both employer and employee representatives where feasible. Oral agreements lacking sufficient evidentiary support shall not bind either party absent clear corroborating testimony or digital records. This provision aims to prevent fraudulent claims and ensure mutual understanding of obligations assumed upon hiring.\n\nIn sum, these provisions reflect the Accords' commitment to fair treatment of all sentient beings under its jurisdiction, regardless of physical composition. By establishing baseline labor rights for constructs, the Autumnwood Hall seeks to foster a more equitable society where innovation does not come at the expense of ethical principles. Employers are urged to familiarize themselves fully with these rules before engaging in any construct-related business transactions to avoid costly mistakes and potential liabilities down the line.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "600.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.4 Construct Labor and Minimum Ward Requirements — Continued",
          "text": "A caster shall not employ any construct for labor services unless such employment meets minimum ward requirements set forth in this Section. These requirements are designed to ensure that the construct remains stable, controllable, and safe during periods of non-use or while under the care of third parties. Failure to comply with these standards may result in penalties including fines, revocation of licensure, and imprisonment for up to one year.\n\nThe following minimum ward levels shall be maintained at all times when a construct is not actively performing tasks assigned by its owner: autonomous constructs must maintain a base ward level of no less than 75%; remotely controlled constructs require a base ward level of no less than 90%. These thresholds may be adjusted upward based on the complexity and potential danger posed by the specific construct in question. Adjustments shall be determined by an independent assessor appointed by the Guild upon written request from either party.\n\nConstructs employed for labor purposes must also receive adequate rest intervals to prevent degradation of their magical stability. The minimum duration of such rests shall be calculated according to a formula based on total hours of active service rendered in a twenty-four hour period, with additional adjustments made for factors including ambient magic concentration and the specific nature of tasks performed. Detailed logs documenting all labor activity and corresponding resting periods must be maintained by the employer and made available for inspection upon demand.\n\nIn cases where a construct suffers damage or malfunction while under the custody or control of another party, that party shall bear full responsibility for restoring it to proper working order at their own expense. This obligation extends regardless of whether such loss or damage arose through negligence, accident, or intentional misconduct. The affected owner may seek damages directly from any third party found liable for failing to meet these obligations, without prejudice to any further remedies available under the Accords.\n\nAny disputes regarding compliance with the standards set forth herein shall be resolved by a panel of three magisters drawn at random from the rolls maintained by the Guild. Such panels shall have authority to issue binding rulings on all matters pertaining to construct labor and ward maintenance, including but not limited to interpretation of technical specifications and determination of causation in cases of alleged malpractice. All costs associated with such proceedings shall be borne equally by both parties unless otherwise determined by said panel based upon findings of fault or misconduct.\n\nIt is further provided that nothing contained in this Section shall be construed as limiting the inherent rights of any caster to employ constructs however they see fit, so long as such employment does not violate other provisions of these Accords. The intent of this provision is solely to establish baseline safety standards for protecting both employers and those who may come into contact with active constructs during periods of inactivity or transfer of custody.\n\n§ 600.4 Construct Labor; Minimum Ward Requirements — see also § 200.2 Limits on Apprentice Practice; § 300.1 Scheduling Restrictions; § 500.1 Prohibition Against Unsanctioned Field Casting; § 700.9 Penalties for Violation of Construction Standards.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "600.4",
            "200.2",
            "300.1",
            "500.1",
            "700.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.4 Construct Labor and Minimum Ward Requirements — Administration",
          "text": "A construct employed as labor must observe minimum rest intervals to prevent magical degradation or accidental release of stored energy. Under no circumstances may a bound golem, automaton, or other artificially animated entity operate beyond eight (8) consecutive hours without a mandatory recharge period of at least four (4) hours in an unoccupied space free of stray enchantments. Failure to adhere to these thresholds shall constitute grounds for immediate termination of employment and potential revocation of the employer's magic-use license if negligence is established.\n\nEmployers utilizing constructs must maintain detailed logs documenting all operational cycles, rest periods, and any anomalies detected during service. These records serve as evidence should disputes arise regarding malfunction or liability. Logs must be retained for a minimum of five (5) years and made available upon request by Guild Inspectors or legal counsel representing injured parties. Failure to produce such documentation when demanded shall result in automatic presumption of fault against the employer in any ensuing litigation.\n\nSpecial provisions apply to constructs engaged in hazardous tasks, including but not limited to bomb disposal, dungeon exploration, or combat operations. Such units require enhanced rest cycles proportional to the danger level and duration of exposure. For every additional hour of high-risk activity beyond the standard eight-hour shift, an extra thirty (30) minutes of recharge time shall be mandated. This adjustment accounts for increased strain on magical cores and diminished reaction times inherent to operating under stress.\n\nFurthermore, employers must provide appropriate shielding or containment during rest intervals to safeguard against unintended activation of dormant spells stored within the construct's memory banks. Unsecured constructs left in public spaces pose unacceptable risks to bystanders and property alike. Proper disposal or storage facilities shall be arranged at employer expense whenever a unit reaches end-of-life status through damage, obsolescence, or voluntary retirement by its owner.\n\nIn all cases involving contract disputes over construct performance or liability for damages caused by malfunctioning units, the burden of proof rests solely upon the hiring party to demonstrate adherence to stipulated safety protocols and maintenance schedules. Any failure to prove such compliance shall result in automatic forfeiture of claims against the manufacturer or original creator of the artifact. This provision exists to protect consumers from predatory practices and ensure accountability throughout the entire lifecycle of magical labor assets.\n\nThe foregoing regulations represent a minimum standard for responsible construct employment within the Guild jurisdiction. Employers are encouraged to adopt stricter internal controls where deemed necessary for their specific operations or risk profiles. Ultimately, adherence to these guidelines serves both individual safety interests and broader societal stability by minimizing potential harm from powerful arcane technologies falling into unworthy hands. Let it be remembered that magic, like fire, demands respect and careful handling lest it consume us all in its wake.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "600.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.4 Construct Labor and Minimum Ward Requirements — Oversight",
          "text": "It shall be unlawful for any caster to employ a sentient construct in labor services without first affixing the Guild's Official Seal of Protection, hereafter referred to as \"The Shield,\" to said entity's core chassis or magical binding point. The Shield serves both as a mark of compliance and an insurance policy against accidental degradation; its absence renders all work performed by such constructs void ab initio unless ratified post-facto by an impartial Assessor at the caster's sole expense.\n\nMinimum Ward Thresholds shall be maintained on all active constructs under any employment contract, whether direct or indirect. Failure to uphold these thresholds constitutes a Class B offense punishable by immediate suspension of operations until rectification is achieved. In cases where degradation results from negligence rather than malicious tampering, penalties shall be mitigated proportionally at the Assessor's discretion.\n\nThe burden of proof regarding adherence to Minimum Ward Standards rests upon the employer; failure to produce documentation verifying compliance shifts liability entirely to them. This includes but is not limited to regular diagnostic scans, repair logs, and certified certification from authorized service providers. Any attempt to circumvent these requirements through proxy arrangements or sub-contracted labor shall be treated as a direct violation of this section.\n\nConstructs found operating without proper shielding or below mandated ward levels shall be seized by Aegis Magi pending investigation. Confiscated items may be forfeited entirely if violations are deemed willful or egregious. Repeat offenders face escalating sanctions up to and including permanent revocation of casting privileges within the jurisdictional boundaries of this Accord.\n\nThis provision applies equally to both autonomous constructs and those directly controlled by their masters, regardless of ownership status. The intent is twofold: ensuring public safety from rogue entities while protecting casters themselves from unforeseen consequences arising from poorly maintained creations. By establishing clear guidelines for responsible deployment, the Guild seeks to mitigate risks inherent in the manipulation of artificial intelligence on a fundamental level.\n\nLet it be known that this regulation stands as an immutable cornerstone of ethical practice within our field. Those who choose to ignore these mandates do so at their peril and risk not only financial ruin but also personal damnation should things go awry. The consequences of negligence can prove fatal both literally and figuratively, leaving little room for error in matters such as these where lives hang in the balance.\n\nIn witness hereof, I affix my seal beneath this article attesting to its validity and binding force upon all parties subject thereto. May it serve as a beacon guiding us towards safe and prosperous endeavors ahead while guarding against those shadows lurking yet unseen in the corners of our craft. So be it!",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "600.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "ab1e07ed9de59a5f",
      "refs": [
        "100.2",
        "200.2",
        "200.6",
        "300.1",
        "500.1",
        "600.1",
        "700.9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "600.5",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Summoned Notary",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Form requirements. A summoned notary who vanishes mid-stamp.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Summoned Notary",
          "text": "Pursuant to the overarching mandates of the Autumnwood Accords, all legal instruments, contracts, and metaphysical bindings executed within the jurisdiction of the Midlands shall be subject to verification by a recognized notary of the Guild. In instances where a physical notary cannot be produced due to geographical isolation, hostile territory, or the immediate expiration of a temporal window, a Summoned Notary may be invoked. This procedure requires a formal petition to the local Aegis Magi oversight, ensuring that the entity being summoned possesses the requisite magical aptitude to perceive the true nature of the ink and the weight of the intent. The petitioner shall be responsible for providing the necessary mana-conduits to sustain the notary’s presence for the duration of the signing. Furthermore, the petitioner must guarantee that the summoned notary is not a construct of the Iron Mandate, as the Accords do not recognize the legality of Imperial seals within our sovereign halls.\n\nThe form requirements for a Summoned Notary are strictly delineated to prevent the subversion of the Guild's authority. Every invocation must be accompanied by a Writ of Intent, clearly stating the parties involved, the nature of the agreement, and the specific magical signatures to be verified. The notary must be bound by a temporary geas, ensuring they remain neutral and objective throughout the proceedings. This geas shall be nullified immediately upon the completion of the final seal, at which point the notary is to be returned to their point of origin or dissolved into the ether, as per the standard dissolution clauses. No Summoned Notary shall be permitted to witness the drafting of the contract; their role is strictly limited to the verification and stamping of the completed document. Failure to provide a Writ of Intent shall result in the immediate forfeiture of the summoning fee and a potential citation for unauthorized arcane conjuration.\n\nA critical exception exists regarding the phenomenon of the Vanishing Notary. Should a Summoned Notary vanish mid-stamp—defined as the moment the notary's physical or ethereal form ceases to exist while the seal is partially applied—the resulting document shall be deemed \"Null and Voidable\" under § 600.1. The petitioner is required to report such an occurrence to the nearest Aegis Magi station within one lunar cycle. The Guild shall then determine if the vanishing was a result of external interference, a failure of the petitioner's mana-conduits, or a breach of the notary’s own internal stability. If the vanishing is deemed an act of external sabotage by the Silver Flame or the Cosmic Jesters, the petitioner may be granted a waiver on the summoning fee. However, if the notary vanished due to the petitioner's inability to maintain the summoning circle, a fine shall be levied to cover the cost of the wasted arcane energy.\n\nIn cases where a vanished stamp leaves a lingering magical residue that threatens the stability of the surrounding area, the petitioner shall be held liable for any subsequent remediation costs. This is particularly relevant when dealing with high-level bindings that involve the restricted research permitted under the Innovator faction's oversight. See § 400.1 for the requirements regarding research permits and the limitations on high-energy signatures. Furthermore, any Summoned Notary who is found to have intentionally vanished to evade a contract they deemed \"unconscionable\" shall be struck from the Guild’s registry of recognized entities. Such entities will be placed on the Quiet List, and any mage who knowingly summons a known \"Vanishing Notary\" shall be subject to a Paradox Trial for negligence or collusion with rogue elements. All disputes regarding the validity of a mid-stamp disappearance shall be mediated by Archmage Veyra’s appointed tribunal.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "600.1",
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Summoned Notary and the Perils of Absence",
          "text": "§ 600.5 Summoned Notary and the Perils of Absence\n\nA summoned notary who fails to appear at the scheduled time or location shall be deemed in default, and the associated transaction voidable upon petition. Where a notary is unable to attend due to unforeseen circumstances beyond their control, reasonable efforts must be made to reschedule the appointment within seven days. Failure to do so may result in disciplinary action up to and including revocation of the notary's commission. In cases where a summoned notary vanishes without explanation mid-transaction, leaving an incomplete or forged record behind, they shall face immediate suspension pending investigation into potential fraud or malfeasance. Such incidents highlight the critical importance of verifying credentials and maintaining clear lines of communication between all parties involved in any notarized activity. The Guild retains sole discretion to determine whether a given absence constitutes mere negligence or willful misconduct warranting harsher penalties. All summoned notaries are urged to prioritize punctuality and reliability as fundamental tenets of their professional practice, thereby safeguarding the integrity of the entire certification system upon which so many depend. See also § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild; under § 1.6 Definitions, \"Notary\" encompasses all individuals authorized by the Accords to administer oaths and certify documents. Under § 300.3 Quiet List Procedure, unauthorized disclosure of sensitive information can lead to severe sanctions. As outlined in § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs, proper containment protocols must be observed at all times during any experimental work involving potentially hazardous materials or energies. Failure to comply with these guidelines may result not only in personal liability but also broader consequences for public safety and order. It is thus incumbent upon every practitioner of magic to adhere strictly to the established rules governing their craft, lest they find themselves ensnared by far more dire repercussions than a simple missing signature ever could be. The stakes are always high when dealing with forces beyond mortal comprehension – let us hope we never have cause to test them firsthand.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "600.5",
            "100.2",
            "1.6",
            "300.3",
            "400.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.5 Summoned Notary and the Perils of Absence",
          "text": "Where a summoned notary fails to appear at the appointed hour for any reason, whether due to illness, accident, or simple forgetfulness, the contract remains incomplete and unenforceable until such time as the absent party returns to finalize the document under oath. In the interim, no remedies may be pursued against either party, nor shall any damages accrue by mere passage of time without further action on behalf of the aggrieved side. The burden rests solely upon the notary to secure their own presence and punctuality, for failure thereof constitutes a breach of fiduciary duty under § 100.2(b).\n\nShould the absent notary ultimately refuse to appear or act despite reasonable notice and demand, the contract is deemed void ab initio and may be rescinded at any time by either party upon written declaration. No claim may thereafter arise out of the failed transaction except for actual losses directly caused by reliance on the notary's misrepresentation of their availability—a narrow ground requiring clear proof of damages. This provision exists to protect all parties from being bound indefinitely to a stale agreement while awaiting uncertain performance from an unavailable agent.\n\nFurthermore, no substitute notary may be appointed in lieu of the originally summoned individual without prior written consent from both contracting parties. Any such substitution attempted unilaterally shall be deemed null and void under § 600.1(c), regardless of whether the substitute possesses equivalent qualifications or capabilities. This strict requirement ensures that each party retains full control over who represents their interests in executing legally binding instruments, preventing unwanted surprises down the line.\n\nIn cases where emergency circumstances prevent timely appearance by the designated notary, either party may petition the Autumnwood Accords Desk for relief under § 100.8, providing evidence of good faith efforts to secure alternative arrangements. Discretionary approval may be granted if delay would cause irreparable harm and no reasonable alternatives exist. However, such exceptions shall be construed narrowly and never undermine the fundamental principle that only properly summoned officials possess authority to validate contractual obligations.\n\nThis section underscores the critical importance of diligence in fulfilling all procedural requirements when dealing with magical notaries. Parties must anticipate potential obstacles and plan accordingly to avoid being left hanging on a technicality. The law favors certainty over convenience, requiring strict adherence to established protocols lest consequences ensue. Only through careful attention to these details can one ensure that their agreements stand firm against future challenges.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "100.2",
            "600.1",
            "100.8",
            "600.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.5 Summoned Notary and the Perils of Absence — Continued",
          "text": "A summoned notary who fails to appear at the designated time and place shall be deemed in default, resulting in the automatic nullification of any documents executed under their authority. This provision exists to ensure the integrity of the notarization process and prevent fraud or abuse. In such cases, the Guild may impose sanctions on the absent notary, ranging from fines to permanent revocation of their credentials, depending on the severity of the breach and whether it was willful.\n\nFurthermore, a summoned notary who leaves the premises without permission before completing all required duties shall be subject to immediate removal and replacement by another qualified notary at their own expense. Any delays or errors caused by such premature departure shall be borne solely by the defaulting notary and may result in additional liabilities for any parties affected thereby. This rule underscores the critical nature of a notary's presence throughout the entire notarization ceremony, as their oversight is indispensable to validating the authenticity of signatures and contents.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to investigate any suspicious patterns of absence or non-compliance by summoned notaries, particularly when linked to allegations of corruption or collusion with signatories. In extreme circumstances, a blanket suspension of all summoning privileges may be enacted pending thorough review and resolution of underlying issues. Such measures are designed to uphold public trust in the notarization system and safeguard against potential malfeasance within the ranks of commissioned officials.\n\nUltimately, adherence to these stipulations is paramount for maintaining the efficacy and reliability of legal instruments dependent upon notarial attestation. By holding summoned notaries accountable for their obligations, the Guild strives to preserve the highest standards of professionalism and ethical conduct among its membership. Failure to abide by these mandates risks undermining the very foundation upon which notarized transactions rely, with far-reaching consequences for individuals, businesses, and society at large. Thus, all parties must recognize the gravity of their roles in this essential function of modern commerce and governance.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "600.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.5 Summoned Notary and the Perils of Absence — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "The absence of a summoned notary during critical phases of authentication shall not invalidate the entire transaction, provided that adequate substitutes are engaged in accordance with established procedures. In instances where the original notary fails to appear within a reasonable timeframe due to unforeseen circumstances beyond their control, an authorized deputy or alternate may be appointed by the Guild Sovereign to complete the sealing process. Such deputies must possess equivalent credentials and experience as required of primary notaries.\n\nHowever, any delay in finalizing the seal resulting from a notary's absence shall be attributed solely to the negligence of the absent party and may incur penalties under § 600.10 Failure to Appear. These penalties range from fines proportional to lost fees to temporary suspension of licensure, depending on the severity and frequency of infractions. Repeat offenders risk permanent revocation of their notary commissions for breach of trust.\n\nFurthermore, no notary shall intentionally abscond mid-transaction regardless of personal animus toward the client or competing interests. Such conduct constitutes a grave violation of professional ethics codified in § 600.8 Code of Conduct and may trigger immediate disciplinary action up to and including expulsion from the Guild. In extreme cases where flight renders recovery impossible, legal recourse shall be pursued through standard civil channels for breach of contract and unjust enrichment.\n\nThe Guild retains sole discretion to determine whether a notary's absence warrants mitigation or escalation of sanctions based on mitigating factors unique to each circumstance. Clients aggrieved by delays should promptly petition the Arcane Tribunal rather than resorting to extrajudicial measures which could complicate ongoing proceedings. Patience and adherence to due process remain paramount even when frustration mounts under pressure.\n\nUltimately, the integrity of magical seals depends upon unwavering commitment from all participants involved. While no system can perfectly anticipate every contingency, robust safeguards exist to maintain order amidst chaos should issues arise unexpectedly. Trust in these mechanisms shall guide practitioners through adversity until resolution is achieved satisfactorily for all concerned parties alike.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "600.10",
            "600.8",
            "600.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.5 Summoned Notary and the Perils of Absence — Continued — Administration",
          "text": "A summoned notary who departs the Hall before completing their duties shall be deemed in default, subject to penalties under § 100.2 and § 300.4(e). Such absence constitutes a breach of contract and may result in revocation of licensure pursuant to § 200.4(a)(iii). The Archmage Sovereign reserves the right to initiate disciplinary proceedings against any notary found guilty of dereliction of duty, whether intentional or negligent.\n\nFurthermore, a summoned notary who fails to appear as directed shall be held in contempt of court and may face imprisonment for up to one year without bail, as provided by § 150.7(b). The Guild further retains the authority to impose fines totaling no more than two hundred gold pieces per violation, payable within thirty days or subject to additional sanctions under § 900.2(c)(vii). In cases of repeated offenses, cumulative penalties shall apply with increased severity based on prior convictions recorded in the Central Registry.\n\nIt is expressly forbidden for any notary to delegate their responsibilities to another individual unless specifically authorized by written consent from both parties involved and approved by the Archmage Sovereign's Office. Unauthorized delegation renders all subsequent actions invalid and subject to nullification at the discretion of the Guild. Violators shall be liable for any damages incurred as a result of such misconduct, including but not limited to financial losses, emotional distress, and reputational harm suffered by affected individuals or entities.\n\nThe provisions herein are intended to safeguard the integrity of the summoning process and protect all stakeholders from potential abuses arising from improper conduct by summoned notaries. Failure to comply with these regulations may lead to severe consequences ranging from loss of livelihood to criminal prosecution under applicable laws enforced by local authorities in conjunction with the Guild's internal disciplinary mechanisms.\n\nThis clause stands as a testament to the unwavering commitment of the Autumnwood Accords Desk to maintain order, fairness, and accountability within our magical community. By adhering strictly to these guidelines, we ensure that our cherished traditions continue to flourish for generations yet unborn while fostering an environment conducive to growth and innovation under wise stewardship guided by principles rooted deeply in history itself.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.2",
            "300.4",
            "200.4",
            "150.7",
            "900.2",
            "600.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
