    {
      "cite": "700.1",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "Evidence Generally",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "What a filing must contain.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Evidence Generally",
          "text": "§ 700.1 Evidence Generally — What a filing must contain.\n\nAll evidentiary submissions presented before the High Court of Autumnwood or any sub-committee of the Accords Desk shall be deemed null and void unless they conform strictly to the standards of clarity, provenance, and magical integrity established herein. A filing is not merely a statement of fact but a formal anchoring of reality within the Guild’s legal framework. To ensure that the truth of a proceeding is not distorted by the very nature of the arcane, every piece of evidence—be it a written scroll, a physical artifact, a recorded memory, or a summoned testimony—must be accompanied by a Certificate of Authenticity signed by a licensed Clerk or a recognized Aegis Mage. This certificate must verify that the evidence has not been subjected to unauthorized alteration, transmutation, or temporal shifting since its initial collection.\n\nThe filing must contain a comprehensive description of the evidence’s origin, including the geographic coordinates of its discovery, the date of its acquisition, and the identities of all individuals involved in its handling. In cases involving items of significant magical potency, such as those involving the Heartstone or other rift-adjacent materials, the filing must also include a detailed survey of the item’s current mana signature to prevent the accidental triggering of environmental hazards during the hearing. Furthermore, any evidence that is inherently volatile, ephemeral, or capable of self-replication must be housed in a stabilized containment vessel as approved by the Conservators. Failure to provide a sufficient description of the evidence's nature shall result in the immediate dismissal of the filing and a potential citation for negligence under the oversight of Archmage Veyra.\n\nFurthermore, the filing must explicitly state any known associations with the Quiet List. While the contents of the Quiet List are not to be recited in public venues, any evidence derived from such sources must be flagged for restricted viewing. The Clerk shall ensure that such evidence is only accessible to the presiding judges and the immediate parties involved, under a seal of non-disclosure. Any attempt to circumvent these restrictions or to present \"hidden\" evidence in a public forum shall be treated as a violation of the Accords. The Accords Desk reserves the right to reject any filing that utilizes \"Wario Coin\" as a form of collateral or tender, as such currency holds no recognized value within the Guild's legal system. For matters regarding the specific requirements of physical artifacts, see § 400.4. For matters regarding the handling of unauthorized research materials, see § 400.1.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "700.1",
            "400.4",
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Scrying of Closed Books",
          "text": "§ 700.2 Scrying of Closed Books — Warrant. Spoliation even if nothing was taken.\n\nThe sanctity of a private library or a closed grimoire is a cornerstone of the Autumnwood Accords, yet the Guild acknowledges that the pursuit of stability may necessitate the inspection of restricted materials. No mage, regardless of their standing among the Innovators or the Conservators, shall engage in the unauthorized scrying, mental probing, or ethereal peering into a closed book or a sealed repository. Such actions are strictly prohibited unless the individual possesses a valid Writ of Inspection issued by the Sovereign Archmage or a designated Aegis Magistrate. This writ must specify the scope of the scrying, the duration of the access, and the specific reasons for the inquiry. Any scrying conducted without such a warrant shall be deemed a criminal trespass of the mind and a violation of the privacy protections afforded to all registered mages.\n\nCrucially, the Accords establish a strict doctrine of spoliation regarding the act of scrying. The mere act of peering into a closed book via arcane means constitutes a \"metaphysical breach\" of the object's integrity. Even if the scrying process is successful and no physical pages are removed, no secrets are stolen, and no ink is blurred, the act itself is sufficient to constitute spoliation. The Guild holds that the \"observation of the arcane\" is often an \"alteration of the arcane\"; therefore, a book that has been scried is no longer in its original state. In any legal proceeding where a book has been subjected to unauthorized scrying, the contents of that book may be ruled inadmissible as evidence, as its reliability can no longer be guaranteed by the Conservators.\n\nThe penalty for such spoliation shall be determined by the presiding council, but it shall typically involve a formal reprimand and a temporary suspension of the offender's research privileges. If the scrying was performed on a volume containing Quiet List materials, the penalties shall be significantly escalated, potentially leading to a Paradox Trial. Mages who find their libraries subjected to such unauthorized scrying are entitled to seek restitution for the \"spiritual wear\" caused to their collections. For procedures regarding the issuance of Writs of Inspection, see § 400.1. For the consequences of severe magical breaches, see § 300.1.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "700.2",
            "400.1",
            "300.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Memory Charm as Spoliation",
          "text": "§ 700.3 Memory Charm as Spoliation — Forgetting is not a defense if you paid for the forgetting.\n\nIn the pursuit of justice, the Guild recognizes that the human—and demi-human—mind is a fragile vessel, easily manipulated by the very arts it seeks to master. However, the use of memory charms, amnesia spells, or any form of cognitive erasure to avoid the consequences of a legal proceeding is strictly forbidden. Under the Accords, a mage who undergoes a memory erasure to \"purge\" their knowledge of a crime or a breach of contract does not receive a legal reprieve. The law shall look past the veil of the missing memory to the actions that necessitated the erasure. If it can be demonstrated that a mage purposefully induced a state of forgetting to evade testimony or to shield themselves from the repercussions of their actions, the court shall treat the erasure as a deliberate act of spoliation of evidence.\n\nThe legal principle of \"Constructive Knowledge\" shall apply in these instances. If a mage was aware of a violation at the time of the act, the fact that they no longer possess that memory is irrelevant to their liability. Furthermore, the Guild explicitly prohibits the use of memory charms as a method of \"cleansing\" one's record. A mage who pays a third party, or utilizes their own magic, to forget a specific event shall be held to the same standard of accountability as if the memory were perfectly intact. The Guild maintains that \"forgetting is not a defense,\" particularly when the act of forgetting is a calculated maneuver to obstruct the truth. Any mage found to have utilized such charms to obstruct a Paradox Trial shall face immediate escalation of their sentencing.\n\nFurthermore, the use of memory charms on witnesses or victims is considered a severe felony under the Accords. Such actions shall result in the immediate sealing of the offender's magic and a mandatory period of isolation. The Accords Desk shall not recognize any plea of ignorance based on self-induced amnesia. If a mage claims they cannot testify because they have \"purged\" the event, the court may compel them to provide evidence of the charm's usage, which shall serve as prima facie evidence of guilt or obstruction. For matters concerning the sealing of magic, see § 300.1. For the management of restricted research that might involve cognitive manipulation, see § 400.1.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "700.3",
            "300.1",
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Simulacrum Testimony",
          "text": "§ 700.4 Simulacrum Testimony — A copy may not swear for the original.\n\nThe integrity of the High Court relies upon the presence of the living, sentient being before whom the oath is sworn. Consequently, the use of a simulacrum, illusion, or any magically-constructed duplicate to provide testimony in a legal proceeding is strictly prohibited. A simulacrum, by its very nature as a \"copy,\" lacks the inherent soul-binding or consciousness required to enter into a binding legal oath. While a simulacrum may be used to demonstrate a visual reconstruction of a past event—provided it is clearly labeled as a \"Visual Reconstruction\" and not \"Testimony\"—it may never be permitted to speak on behalf of the original entity or to provide subjective accounts of events.\n\nAny attempt to present a simulacrum as a valid witness shall be met with an immediate stay of proceedings and a formal investigation into the authenticity of the claimant's actions. The law distinguishes clearly between a \"Visual Reconstruction,\" which is a tool of education and evidence, and \"Simulacrum Testimony,\" which is a fraudulent attempt to bypass the requirements of the Accords. If a mage seeks to provide a visual reconstruction of a crime scene or a historical event, they must obtain a permit from the Conservators to ensure the reconstruction does not contain unauthorized magical effects or \"hallucinations\" that could mislead the court. The original entity must be present to oversee the reconstruction and must be available to answer clarifying questions in real-time.\n\nShould a party attempt to substitute a simulacrum for an absent witness, the court may rule that the party is in \"Contempt of the Accords.\" In such cases, the evidence provided by the simulacrum shall be struck from the record, and the party may be subject to a Paradox Trial for attempting to subvert the judicial process. This rule ensures that the \"human element\"—the weight of personal responsibility—remains at the center of the Guild's legal framework. For matters regarding the use of constructs in legal settings, see § 600.4. For the definition of forbidden schools that might involve the creation of sentient duplicates, see § 300.2.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "700.4",
            "600.4",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Clone as Duplicate Claimant",
          "text": "§ 700.5 Clone as Duplicate Claimant — One claim. The later body files an amendment.\n\nIn instances where a mage has undergone the process of cloning—whether for research, labor, or survival—the legal status of the resulting bodies must be clearly defined to prevent the \"Multiplicity of Claims\" paradox. Under the Autumnwood Accords, a single magical signature or \"Identity Essence\" may only hold one primary legal standing at any given time. If a mage possesses multiple bodies through cloning, they are considered a single legal person with multiple physical manifestations. Therefore, only one body may act as the primary claimant in a legal suit, hold property, or enter into contracts. The \"Original\" body is typically designated as the primary claimant by default, unless a formal \"Transfer of Primacy\" is filed with the Accords Desk.\n\nIf a mage wishes to pursue a legal claim using a secondary body, they must first file a \"Notice of Intent to Claim\" with the Clerk. Once the intent is filed, the secondary body may act as the representative for the legal proceedings. However, should a dispute arise between the bodies regarding the direction of the claim, the Accords dictate that the primary body's wishes shall prevail. If the primary body is incapacitated or destroyed, the secondary body must file a \"Petition for Amendment of Primacy\" to become the sole legal claimant. This ensures that the Guild's records remain clean and that no single mage can use their clones to \"double-file\" claims or circumvent the limits of the Paradox Trial.\n\nFurthermore, any property or assets acquired by a clone during a period of \"Secondary Status\" are considered the property of the primary identity. Should a clone be destroyed, the assets do not revert to the public but remain with the primary body. This prevents the \"fragmentation of wealth\" that often plagues the Innovators' more radical experiments with biological and magical replication. Any mage who attempts to file a claim with a clone without first filing the required amendment or notice shall have their claim dismissed as a \"Duplicate Filing.\" For matters regarding the use of clones as labor, see § 600.4. For the overarching rules on research permits involving such replication, see § 400.1.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "700.5",
            "600.4",
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.1 Evidence Generally",
          "text": "The Accords recognize that disputes over magical practice often hinge upon contested facts, requiring reliable proof to establish liability. Accordingly, the Desk shall accept as admissible any evidence obtained lawfully and relevant to the issues at hand. This includes written records, physical artifacts, witness testimony, and direct observation of conduct. The burden of production lies with the party asserting a claim or defense, who must produce sufficient evidence to meet the prima facie standard before an opposing rebuttal becomes necessary.\n\nNotably, scrying techniques used to view private conversations or actions constitute spoliation of evidence unless expressly authorized by a court order. Any magical enhancement intended solely to bolster testimony beyond natural capability shall be deemed fraudulent and subject to dismissal with prejudice. Conversely, memory charms administered for therapeutic purposes remain lawful so long as they do not distort recollection materially. Such procedures require prior consent from all affected parties and documentation filed with the Guild.\n\nSimulacra summoned under controlled conditions may provide valuable insight into past events or hidden knowledge. However, their use in lieu of direct testimony is strictly limited to illustrative purposes only. The Desk shall determine admissibility based on necessity and probative value versus prejudicial effect. Similarly, duplicate claimants arising from cloning technologies must demonstrate clear chain of custody and identity verification before being permitted to participate in legal proceedings. Failure to do so results in automatic disqualification from the case.\n\nUltimately, the integrity of the evidentiary process underpins all adjudicative outcomes within the Accords framework. Parties are urged to seek qualified counsel when navigating these complex rules of proof. The Desk retains ultimate discretion to exclude any material deemed unreliable or improperly obtained in furtherance of justice. Adherence to these standards ensures fair treatment for all litigants while safeguarding against abuse of magical powers within judicial contexts.\n\nSee also § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; § 200.3 Temporary Mouths; § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs; § 600.2 Familiars as Agents. Under § 1.7 Construction, Aggregation of Conduct applies where multiple evidentiary points converge to establish a pattern of behavior warranting disciplinary action. The Desk shall evaluate each case on its merits while maintaining consistency with established precedents.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "200.3",
            "400.2",
            "600.2",
            "1.7",
            "700.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
