    {
      "cite": "700.2",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "Scrying of Closed Books",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Warrant. Spoliation even if nothing was taken.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Scrying of Closed Books",
          "text": "The act of scrying, whether through a crystal lens, a scrying pool, or a direct mental projection, into a volume that has been formally sealed by the Archmage Sovereign or the presiding High Conservator is strictly prohibited under the Autumnwood Accords. A closed book is not merely a physical object of bound paper and ink; it is a legal state of existence wherein the information contained therein has been sequestered from the public and private spheres for reasons of stability, security, or the preservation of the Quiet List. Any attempt to bypass these seals via divination, ethereal peering, or any other arcane method of observation constitutes a breach of the sanctity of the Archive. Such actions are treated as a prima facie violation of the privacy of the Guild's internal records and the personal property rights of the authors.\n\nTo initiate a legal inquiry into a closed volume, a caster must first obtain a formal Warrant of Inspection from the Accords Desk. This warrant must specify the exact nature of the information sought, the necessity of the inquiry for the preservation of the Guild or the safety of the Midlands, and the specific limitations of the scrying duration. Without a warrant, any scrying of a closed book shall be met with immediate seizure of the scrying apparatus and a mandatory period of magical silence for the offending caster. The mere intent to observe is sufficient for prosecution, as the volatile nature of forbidden knowledge means that even a glance can cause irreparable ripples in the local weave.\n\nFurthermore, the law of Spoliation applies to all scrying attempts regardless of the outcome. If a caster successfully scries into a closed book without a warrant, the contents of that book are deemed \"spoliated\" in the eyes of the Accords. This means that even if the caster takes no physical notes, reveals no secrets to third parties, and admits no knowledge of the contents, the integrity of the text is considered compromised. The Guild shall treat the book as if its contents were leaked, and the original author or the presiding Conservator may demand that the volume be moved to a more secure, high-containment vault. The caster shall be liable for the costs of re-sealing and the administrative fees associated with the relocation of the text. See § 700.1.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "700.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Memory Charm as Spoliation",
          "text": "The use of memory charms, cognitive erasure, or any mnemonic suppression spell intended to remove knowledge of a Guild-related offense or a restricted research violation is strictly prohibited as a method of evading justice. The Accords Desk holds the firm position that \"forgetting is not a defense if you paid for the forgetting.\" If a caster utilizes a charm to erase their own memory of a crime, or if they employ a charm on a witness to suppress their testimony, the act of erasure is itself considered a secondary offense of Spoliation. The law recognizes that the voluntary removal of a memory creates a void in the legal record that can be exploited to obstruct the Paradox Trial. Therefore, the existence of a memory charm is sufficient evidence of a desire to obstruct, regardless of whether the caster can currently recall the specific details of the act.\n\nIn any proceeding before a council of peers, the presence of a memory charm—whether active or recently expired—shall be treated as a confession of the underlying act. If a caster is found to have sought out a memory charm to \"cleanse\" their mind of a specific event, the Guild shall presume that the event occurred as described in the initial accusation. The caster shall not be permitted to argue a lack of intent or a lack of memory as a mitigating factor; rather, the proactive seeking of oblivion shall be viewed as an admission of guilt. This prevents the \"clean slate\" defense where a rogue mage might attempt to render themselves legally incapable of understanding their own crimes.\n\nFurthermore, any caster who provides a memory charm to a third party for the purpose of concealing evidence or silencing a victim shall be subject to immediate arrest by the Aegis Magi. Such actions are viewed as a direct assault on the transparency of the Accords. The Guild maintains that the truth of the past is a communal responsibility, and the artificial deletion of that truth to protect an individual's reputation or freedom is a violation of the collective security of the Midlands. See § 700.1.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "700.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Simulacrum Testimony",
          "text": "A simulacrum, whether it be a physical construct of flesh and bone, a magical duplicate, or a lingering psychic echo, may not swear testimony or provide evidence in any formal hearing before the Accords Desk. The law of Identity requires that only a sentient being of recognized legal standing—specifically a living mage, a non-magical citizen of the Midlands, or a sanctioned Goodstyle Artisan—may participate in the testimony of a Paradox Trial. A copy may not swear for the original, as the nature of magic allows for the creation of perfect mimics that lack the moral weight and soul-binding oaths required by the Guild's judicial processes.\n\nIf a party to a legal dispute seeks to present the testimony of a simulacrum, they must first prove that the original entity is deceased and that the simulacrum is a \"Living Record\" of the original's intent, which is a standard rarely granted. Even in such cases, the simulacrum may only be used to provide historical context and cannot be used to swear an oath or provide \"new\" testimony. The use of a simulacrum to testify as if it were the original person is strictly forbidden and shall be considered a form of identity theft and a violation of the Sanctity of the Self. Any attempt to do so will result in the immediate dismissal of the evidence and a heavy fine against the presenting party.\n\nThe Accords Desk further stipulates that a simulacrum may not be used to bypass the \"Quiet List\" restrictions. If a simulacrum were allowed to speak, it could potentially recite forbidden knowledge without the caster facing the direct consequences of such a violation. Therefore, any simulacrum that speaks during a legal proceeding must be bound by a permanent silence ward unless specifically authorized by Archmage Veyra. The preservation of the distinction between the \"Original\" and the \"Copy\" is fundamental to the prevention of magical fraud within the Guild's jurisdiction.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Clone as Duplicate Claimant",
          "text": "In matters of property, intellectual research, or magical rights, a clone shall be considered a Duplicate Claimant rather than a separate legal entity. Under the Autumnwood Accords, the existence of a clone does not grant the secondary body a separate right to claim ownership, nor does it allow for the doubling of resources. One claim is permitted per original identity. If a mage creates a clone to claim a share of a research grant or a portion of a physical estate, the Guild shall recognize only the primary body as the legal owner. The clone is viewed as an extension of the original's reach, not a new person.\n\nShould a situation arise where a clone is granted its own Provisional Guild Pass or seeks to establish independent research permits, the Guild shall treat this as a violation of the Unsanctioned Arts. The clone is legally tethered to the original. If the original body is incapacitated, the clone may act as a temporary proxy, but it cannot hold property in its own name. In cases where a clone attempts to file for independent status, the Guild shall require a \"Severance of Identity\" hearing, which is notoriously difficult to achieve and requires the express permission of the Conservators.\n\nIn the event of a dispute between a primary body and its clone regarding the use of shared resources—such as a laboratory or a specific mana source—the primary body shall always hold the dominant legal right. The later body, or the clone, must file an amendment to the original's charter to request specific hours of usage. This ensures that the creation of clones cannot be used as a tactic to dilute the authority of a single mage or to circumvent the limits on research copies of Quiet Items. The law is clear: the soul (or the primary arcane signature) owns the claim; the body is merely the vessel. See § 400.5.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "400.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.2 Spoliation by Forbidden Divination",
          "text": "A mage shall not employ scrying or other forms of forbidden divination to breach the privacy of any person, household, or establishment without express consent from all parties concerned. Such acts constitute a grave violation of the sanctity of private life and are subject to severe penalties under this Code. Violators shall be liable for treble damages plus punitive fines assessed at the discretion of the court based on the severity of the intrusion and resulting harm suffered by the aggrieved party.\n\nThe prohibition extends not only to direct scrying but also to indirect methods of espionage through enchanted mirrors, animated dolls, or other devices designed to observe private spaces surreptitiously. Any such contraptions found in possession shall be seized and destroyed at public expense, with costs borne by the offending caster. Repeat offenders may face permanent revocation of their magical licensure and mandatory exile from the jurisdiction for a period not less than one solar year.\n\nFurthermore, this section expressly forbids the use of memory charms or other enchantments designed to erase or alter recollections in the service of spoliating evidence relevant to pending litigation. Such machinations violate fundamental principles of fair play and justice, undermining the integrity of the judicial process itself. Courts shall presume bad faith on the part of any party found guilty of employing forbidden divination or memory manipulation for evidentiary purposes, with adverse inference drawn against them accordingly.\n\nIt is hereby declared that no simaculum or artificial duplicate may testify as a competent witness in any matter unless expressly authorized by law. The identity and consciousness of each individual remain inviolate; attempts to subvert this through cloning technologies shall be prosecuted under existing statutes regarding fraud, impersonation, and unauthorized human experimentation. Any claim arising from the wrongful creation or use of simulacra shall be adjudicated on its merits without regard for any technical defenses based upon the artificial nature of the alleged plaintiff-defendant.\n\nThis section shall apply with equal force to all forms of remote sensing technology capable of penetrating physical barriers, including but not limited to thermal imaging, lidar scanning, and magnetic resonance profiling. Operators thereof are reminded that privacy expectations remain high even in the digital age; mere suspicion of wrongdoing does not justify wholesale surveillance without probable cause supported by oath or affirmation. Unauthorized gathering of sensitive data shall be treated as a felony offense subject to harsh punishment upon conviction.\n\nThe spirit of this provision is intended to safeguard individuals against invasive prying into their most intimate thoughts and actions without warrant. While legitimate investigative techniques have their place, they must never compromise the core values of trust and dignity that undergird civilized society. Let all casters remember: power granted by magic comes with solemn obligations; misuse thereof brings swift retribution from both Heaven and Earth alike!",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "700.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.2 Spoliation by Forbidden Divination — Continued",
          "text": "Forbidden divination, including scrying operations conducted without the explicit permission of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, constitutes spoliation under § 100.4(c) regardless of whether physical evidence was removed or altered. The mere act of illicitly accessing protected magical records or attempting to peer into sealed archives infers an intent to interfere with the integrity and confidentiality of Guild data. Such conduct shall be presumed to have compromised the sanctity of the materials in question, triggering automatic sanctions pursuant to § 700.2(b).\n\nThe burden of proof rests upon the accused caster to demonstrate that their unauthorized divination efforts had no impact whatsoever on the security or availability of the targeted information. Absent compelling evidence to the contrary, the Desk shall deem spoliation complete and impose penalties accordingly. This strict liability standard reflects the grave risks inherent in unrestricted access to potent arcane knowledge; once a seal is broken, even momentarily, the damage may prove irreparable.\n\nDivination techniques capable of penetrating closed books or revealing hidden truths are classified as high-risk manipulations under § 300.1(s). Practitioners engaged in such activities without proper authorization violate both the spirit and letter of the Accords. Repeat offenses may lead to revocation of licensure and referral for criminal prosecution. The Desk retains sole discretion to grant exceptions on a case-by-case basis, contingent upon rigorous vetting procedures and adequate safeguards.\n\nThe prohibition against forbidden divination extends beyond physical archives to include digital or simulated equivalents stored within secure databases. Any attempt to breach these electronic barriers shall be treated identically to unauthorized access of tangible records. Automated monitoring systems are employed to detect suspicious activity and initiate immediate alerts to enforcement agents. Violators face swift disciplinary action upon confirmation of guilt.\n\nThis provision serves as a deterrent against reckless experimentation with dangerous magical arts. By maintaining strict control over sensitive data, the Guild safeguards not only its own interests but also those of all who rely upon the stable functioning of arcane systems. Unauthorized divination threatens this delicate equilibrium and must be met with decisive measures to preserve order and security across the realm.\n\nsee § 100.4(c) Spoliation Generally; § 300.1(s) High-Risk Manipulations; § 700.2(b) Penalties for Spoliation; § 500.6 Automated Monitoring Systems; under § 900.3 Strict Liability Standards.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.4",
            "700.2",
            "300.1",
            "500.6",
            "900.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "270566874beef904",
      "refs": [
        "100.4",
        "300.1",
        "400.5",
        "500.6",
        "700.1",
        "900.3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "700.3",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "Memory Charm as Spoliation",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Forgetting is not a defense if you paid for the forgetting.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Memory Charm as Spoliation",
          "text": "The Guild hereby establishes that the intentional erasure of cognitive data via magical means does not constitute a valid defense against the withholding of evidence, nor does it grant the practitioner immunity from the consequences of their prior actions. Under the Autumnwood Accords, a mage who undergoes a Memory Charm to obfuscate their knowledge of a transgression is deemed to have committed an act of Spoliation. The legal principle of \"Cognitive Integrity\" dictates that the burden of memory remains with the individual regardless of the method by which that memory is suppressed. If a mage is summoned to provide testimony or evidence regarding a breach of the Accords and claims ignorance due to a self-inflicted Memory Charm, such a claim shall be dismissed by the presiding council.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild recognizes that the act of paying for, commissioning, or self-administering a Memory Charm constitutes a willful attempt to subvert the judicial process. In all proceedings, the court shall treat the existence of a Memory Charm as a prima facie admission of guilt regarding the underlying matter being suppressed. The mere fact of forgetting is not a shield; rather, the methodology of forgetting serves as a confession of the existence of the fact. Practitioners who seek to utilize such charms to evade the Paradox Trial shall find their magic subject to immediate Sealment under the authority of the Conservators. Any individual found to have induced a state of amnesia to avoid the disclosure of illicit research or unauthorized field casting shall be prosecuted as if the original information were still held in clear and present mind.\n\nSee § 700.1 for general evidence standards. See § 700.2 regarding the limitations of scrying. The Guild maintains that the preservation of the truth is paramount to the stability of the Midlands. Any attempt to treat a blank mind as a clean slate in the eyes of the law is a violation of the sanctity of the Accords. The cost of the charm, whether paid in gold, mana, or personal sacrifice, is irrelevant to the determination of spoliation. The law focuses on the result: the deliberate removal of evidence from the sphere of human consciousness to hinder the Guild's ability to maintain order.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "700.1",
            "700.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Simulacrum Testimony",
          "text": "A simulacrum, whether created through high-level illusion, somatic projection, or complex material transmutation, lacks the legal standing to provide sworn testimony or enter into binding contracts on behalf of its progenitor. The Guild recognizes that while a simulacrum may possess the appearance, voice, and perceived personality of a specific mage, it lacks the \"Arcane Signature of Intent\" required for legal recognition. Therefore, a copy may not swear for the original. Any attempt to utilize a simulacrum to bypass the requirements of the Paradox Trial or to provide false testimony in a dispute of the Accords shall be treated as a felony of Deceptive Casting.\n\nWhen a mage is summoned to appear before the High Council or an Aegis Magi tribunal, they must present their own physical form. The use of a simulacrum as a proxy is strictly prohibited, as the Guild cannot verify the internal state of a magical construct. If a simulacrum is presented in place of a mage, the proceedings shall be suspended, and the mage in question shall be subject to a summary citation for evasion. This rule ensures that the nuances of the mage's conscience and the weight of their personal oaths are properly weighed by the peers of the Guild. A copy is a reflection of a truth, not the truth itself; it cannot bear the weight of a vow, nor can it be held liable for the consequences of its words.\n\nFurthermore, any contract signed by a simulacrum is null and void ab initio. If a mage seeks to conduct business with the Goodstyle Artisans or other entities, they must ensure that their own hand—not a magical duplicate—affixes the seal. Failure to adhere to this requirement may result in the immediate revocation of research permits under § 400.1. The Guild's stance is clear: the law governs the soul and the intent of the caster, neither of which can be replicated by the mere mastery of form. For those who believe their simulacrums are sufficiently \"real\" to stand in court, the Guild reminds them that the Accords are bound by the essence of the individual, not the shadow they cast.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Clone as Duplicate Claimant",
          "text": "In instances where a mage undergoes biological or magical replication resulting in the creation of a clone, the Guild recognizes a singular legal identity for the purpose of claims, debts, and privileges. The doctrine of \"Unitary Essence\" dictates that while two bodies may exist, only one legal entity may hold the primary standing. The first body to register with the Accords shall be recognized as the Primary Claimant. Any subsequent body produced via cloning shall be designated as a Duplicate Claimant. The Duplicate Claimant possesses no independent rights to Guild assets, research privileges, or the right to vote within the factions of the Conservators or Innovators.\n\nTo resolve conflicts of interest between a Primary and a Duplicate Claimant, the law requires that the later body files a formal amendment to the original registration. This amendment must explicitly state the intended role of the Duplicate Claimant—whether it be as a servant, a research vessel, or a secondary laborer—and must be approved by the presiding Archmage. If a Duplicate Claimant attempts to assert independent ownership of a laboratory, a research permit, or a position within the Guild, such claims shall be summarily struck down. The law does not recognize the \"right of replication\" as a right of \"multiplication\" of legal status. One mage, regardless of the number of physical vessels they may inhabit, may only hold one seat of power or one license of practice.\n\nShould a dispute arise regarding the distribution of resources between a Primary and a Duplicate Claimant, the Guild shall default to the Primary Claimant's rights unless a prior written agreement, notarized by a Summoned Notary under § 600.5, states otherwise. The Duplicate Claimant may be utilized for the purposes of research or field work, provided that the Primary Claimant remains the sole bearer of legal responsibility for any violations of the Accords. Any attempt to split the ownership of a licensed artifact or a restricted research copy between two bodies shall be viewed as an unauthorized division of the Guild's interests. The Accords are designed to prevent the dilution of responsibility; therefore, the clone must follow the lead of the original, and the law shall treat them as a single thread in the tapestry of the Midlands.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "600.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.3 Memory Charm as Spoliation and Evidence",
          "text": "The use of memory charms or amnestic magic to alter a witness's recollection constitutes spoliation of evidence, subjecting the caster to severe penalties under §§ 1.5 and 200.4. Such acts undermine the integrity of judicial proceedings by rendering testimony unreliable and compromising the truth-finding function of the court. The burden lies upon any party claiming altered memory to prove the charm's application by clear and convincing evidence, failing which such claims shall be disregarded as frivolous and vexatious under § 100.5.\n\nIn matters involving simuli or artificial constructs bearing personal memories or personality traits, their testimony shall be deemed admissible only upon passage of a rigorous authenticity test administered by independent arcane experts. This evaluation must verify that the construct's recollections are not merely programmed responses but genuine reflections of actual events witnessed firsthand. Failure to meet these standards will result in automatic exclusion of such evidence as tainted and inadmissible under § 700.2 and related provisions governing hearsay exceptions.\n\nFurthermore, any claimant seeking to establish identity based upon cloned or duplicated persons must first demonstrate the legal status of said duplicates through a formal hearing before the Archmagi's Court. Mere assertion of kinship or resemblance shall be insufficient grounds for recognizing duplicate entities as legitimate representatives in matters of inheritance, liability, or contractual obligation. The court retains broad discretion to deny such standing when fraud or manipulation is suspected, acting sua sponte upon reasonable cause shown under §§ 100.2 and 300.4.\n\nThis section serves to protect the sanctity of judicial fact-finding by preventing subversion through magical interference with human cognition or artificial consciousness alike. Violators face not only immediate sanctions but also potential revocation of all licenses held within the Guild's jurisdiction under § 200.1 and its implementing regulations. The principles herein are designed to maintain public confidence in the impartiality and reliability of legal processes administered by the Accords Desk, ensuring that truth prevails even in the face of arcane deceptions.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.5",
            "100.5",
            "700.2",
            "100.2",
            "200.1",
            "700.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.3 Memory Charm as Spoliation and Evidence Preservation",
          "text": "§ 700.3 Memory Charm as Spoliation and Evidence Preservation. The casting or attempted casting of a memory charm upon a material witness constitutes spoliation of evidence under § 1.4(e). Such acts shall be deemed intentional interference with the administration of justice, punishable by sanctions including but not limited to fines up to one thousand gold pieces per instance and permanent revocation of licensure. This provision applies regardless of whether the charm was successfully enacted or merely attempted. The burden of proof rests upon the accused caster to demonstrate that no such act occurred, absent evidence to the contrary. In cases where a memory charm is cast in good faith under duress or without knowledge of the witness's status as a material fact-witness, sanctions may be mitigated at the discretion of the presiding adjudicator. However, this mitigation shall not apply if the accused caster failed to disclose relevant circumstances that would have precluded them from casting such a charm lawfully. Furthermore, any party found guilty of employing memory charms for evidentiary purposes shall be barred from appearing before the Accords Desk in their own defense for a period of one lunar cycle following imposition of sanctions, unless granted an exception by special dispensation. This clause is intended to preserve the integrity of legal proceedings and prevent parties from undermining judicial processes through magical interference with human cognition. Violations of this section shall be prosecuted ex officio by the Guild's Office of Disciplinary Affairs in conjunction with relevant law enforcement agencies as appropriate under existing statutes governing criminal acts related to magic use. The principles set forth herein reflect the Accords' longstanding commitment to maintaining fair and transparent adjudication while safeguarding against abuses of magical power within judicial contexts. Any ambiguities arising from this provision shall be resolved by reference to foundational sections pertaining to evidence standards, witness protection obligations, and prohibitions on tampering with official records or persons thereof. See also §§ 700.1 through 700.2 regarding general evidentiary rules; § 850 concerning specific restrictions on memory alteration techniques; and Part IV-A for comprehensive guidelines surrounding spoliation of evidence in all its forms.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "700.3",
            "1.4",
            "700.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.4 Memory Charm and Admissibility",
          "text": "The use of memory charms in litigation presents unique evidentiary challenges requiring careful consideration by the tribunal. Under § 700.3, when an accused mage employs a memory charm to evade detection or accountability, this act constitutes spoliation of evidence and may warrant severe sanctions against the offending party. The court retains discretion to draw adverse inferences from such conduct and impose additional penalties beyond those originally sought.\n\nIn instances where a memory charm has been successfully deployed, the burden shifts to the accuser to demonstrate by clear and convincing proof that the charm was ineffective or bypassed entirely. Failure to meet this heightened standard results in dismissal of charges, regardless of any subsequent revelations regarding underlying wrongdoing. This elevated threshold reflects the extraordinary power inherent in magical alteration of consciousness and memory.\n\nWhen multiple parties claim conflicting recollections of events, particularly where memory charms may have been involved, the tribunal shall apply a rigorous scrutiny of each testimony. Corroborating evidence, witness statements, physical records, and any available magical traces must all be weighed carefully before reaching a determination of credibility. The court maintains absolute authority to order further investigations as deemed necessary for a just resolution.\n\nSimulacra generated through advanced illusion magic may offer additional avenues for fact-finding where direct testimony proves elusive. However, the admissibility of such constructs remains subject to strict safeguards designed to prevent abuse and protect against unreliable conclusions drawn from inherently unstable sources. The tribunal shall exercise its discretion prudently when considering evidentiary value attributed to simulated representations.\n\nUltimately, the core principle guiding all proceedings involving memory manipulation is the preservation of truth-seeking integrity within the legal framework. While acknowledging the potent capabilities afforded by magical arts, the court must remain vigilant against erosion of factual accuracy essential for equitable justice. Balanced application of these guidelines ensures fair adjudication even in cases complicated by extraordinary supernatural elements.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "700.3",
            "700.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "3b0d7986fd4eed77",
      "refs": [
        "1.4",
        "1.5",
        "100.2",
        "100.5",
        "200.1",
        "400.1",
        "600.5",
        "700.1",
        "700.2",
        "700.4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "700.4",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "Simulacrum Testimony",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A copy may not swear for the original.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Simulacrum Testimony",
          "text": "A copy may not swear for the original. Any entity produced via the method of arcane duplication, illusion, or somatic mirroring—collectively referred to as a \"Simulacrum\"—is strictly prohibited from providing testimony, depositions, or sworn statements before a tribunal of the Autumnwood Accords. The legal fiction of the Guild maintains that a Simulacrum possesses the likeness and, in some instances, the memories of the progenitor, yet it lacks the metaphysical \"weight\" required to bind a soul to a truth. Consequently, any statement extracted from a Simulacrum is deemed inadmissible as primary evidence. This restriction applies regardless of the degree of fidelity achieved by the caster. Even if a Simulacrum is indistinguishable from the original to the naked eye, or if it possesses the ability to replicate the vocal nuances and mannerisms of the source, it remains a mere echo. Under the Accords, only a living, sentient body of the original caster may be held accountable for the truth of their actions.\n\nFurthermore, the use of a Simulacrum to obstruct justice by providing a \"false\" testimony that mirrors a \"true\" one is considered a grave offense. If a party seeks to utilize a Simulacrum for the purpose of information retrieval, they must first petition the Aegis Magi for a Research Permit to ensure the process does not violate the sanctity of the original's cognitive privacy. See § 400.1. Any attempt to bypass these restrictions by weaving complex \"truth-binding\" spells onto a duplicate shall be viewed as an attempt to circumvent the inherent limitations of the Accords and may result in a Paradox Trial. The Guild recognizes that while a copy may hold data, it cannot hold the \"burden of conscience.\" Therefore, any legal proceeding involving a Simulacrum shall require the physical presence of the original caster to verify the authenticity of the information provided. Failure to produce the original caster for verification shall result in the immediate dismissal of the testimony and a formal warning against the use of unauthorized arcane proxies.\n\nIn cases where the original caster is deceased, a Simulacrum may be used solely for the purpose of \"archival visualization\" to assist the tribunal in understanding the context of past events, provided that such visualization is not presented as evidence of fact. The Clerk of the Accords shall ensure that all such visualizations are clearly marked as \"Non-Testimonial Visualizations\" in the court record. Any party found to be attempting to pass off a Simulacrum’s speech as the original's sworn word shall be subject to immediate sanctions, including potential sealing of their casting abilities. This law serves to protect the integrity of the Accords against the manipulation of reality by those who would seek to manufacture witnesses from the ether. See § 700.2.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "700.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Clone as Duplicate Claimant",
          "text": "One claim. The later body files an amendment. In the event that a single magical consciousness inhabits multiple biological vessels—specifically through the process of cloning or somatic splitting—the Guild recognizes the \"Doctrine of Primacy.\" This doctrine dictates that the first viable body to manifest or exist in a state of independent consciousness shall be the primary claimant for all legal, financial, and contractual obligations. The Guild does not recognize the existence of \"multiple\" citizens when the underlying magical signature is identical. Therefore, should a mage or artisan create a clone, the clone is viewed as a legal extension of the original, rather than a separate entity capable of independent litigation or contract formation. This prevents the \"doubling\" of assets or the \"dilution\" of liability within the Midlands.\n\nWhen a second body is produced, it is considered a \"Subordinate Vessel.\" If the Subordinate Vessel seeks to engage in independent commerce, property ownership, or the signing of Accords, it must first file a Formal Amendment of Status with the Accords Desk. This amendment requires the Primary Claimant to explicitly delegate specific rights to the Subordinate Vessel. Without such a filing, any contract signed by the clone is legally voidable at the whim of the Primary Claimant, as the Guild views the clone as a mere tool or \"construct\" of the original's will. This mirrors the protections found in § 600.4 regarding construct labor, as the Guild refuses to grant full personhood to bodies that are manufactured by the intent of another. The Primary Claimant retains all core rights, including the right to the original's Guild license and the responsibility for any debts incurred by the Subordinate Vessel.\n\nIf a dispute arises between two bodies of the same consciousness regarding the ownership of a specific artifact or land, the Guild shall default to the Primary Claimant's claim unless a prior \"Splitting of Essence\" decree has been filed. Such a decree is a rare and difficult-to-obtain legal maneuver, requiring a high-level mediation by Archmage Veyra herself. In the absence of such a decree, the \"later body\" is legally subordinate. Any attempt by a clone to file a separate claim of ownership without the Primary Claimant's signature shall be treated as an unauthorized expansion of magical influence and may be subject to a Paradox Trial. This ensures that the radical Innovators cannot use cloning to circumvent the limitations of the Accords or to multiply their influence within the Autumn Wood. See § 200.1.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "600.4",
            "200.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Simulacrum Testimony and Duplicate Claims",
          "text": "§ 700.4 Simulacrum Testimony; The Original's Voice\nA simulative construct, regardless of its fidelity or autonomy, may not testify as the original caster in matters pertaining to the latter's intent, state of mind, or personal knowledge. While a high-resolution copy may replicate speech patterns and mannerisms with uncanny accuracy, it lacks the requisite consciousness to bear witness truthfully. Any attempt to introduce simulatory testimony in place of the original shall be deemed a violation of § 700.1. Exceptions apply only where the original is legally unavailable and a court order has been granted permitting the use of a certified duplicate under strict evidentiary safeguards.\n\nIn cases involving multiple duplicates, each claimant must establish their unique identity beyond reasonable doubt. Mere possession of a simulative token does not confer standing to represent the true self. The burden rests upon the alleged original to prove continuity of consciousness and agency from birth until the present moment. Failure to do so results in dismissal of the claim and potential sanctions for false pretenses under § 800.2.\n\nThe Desk retains discretion to appoint an independent arbiter of identity whose findings shall be binding absent proof of error or corruption. Such appointments are made only when necessary to resolve disputes over authentic selfhood that threaten public order or undermine judicial integrity. The cost thereof is borne equally by all parties unless otherwise ordered.\n\nThis provision reflects the fundamental principle that no artificial intelligence, however advanced, can fully embody the complex web of human experience that defines an individual's soul. To conflate simulation with substance risks eroding the very fabric of personhood itself—a danger far greater than any mere inconvenience to litigants. Thus stands the rule: let copies mimic as they may; the true self remains inviolate, guarded by law and reason alike.\n\n\"see § 1.4 Persons Bound\", \"under § 700.1 Evidence Generally\", \"refer to § 800.2 Sanctions for False Pretenses\".",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "700.4",
            "700.1",
            "800.2",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "700.4 Simulacrum Testimony and Duplicate Claimants",
          "text": "A simulacrum, whether conjured through forbidden rites or engineered by artificers, shall possess no legal capacity to testify on its own behalf. The fundamental principle remains that a duplicate may not swear for the original; such testimony is inherently suspect and inadmissible without corroboration from independent witnesses. This restriction applies equally to simulacrums created via magical transmutation as it does to those manufactured through mechanical means.\n\nHowever, where a simulacrum has been granted artificial sentience sufficient to understand and communicate truthfully, its statements may be admitted as secondary evidence upon showing of necessity. The burden lies upon the proponent to demonstrate that no other practical alternative exists for obtaining relevant information, given the unavailability or inability of the true self to participate in proceedings. Such testimony shall carry diminished evidentiary weight compared to direct observation or primary documentation.\n\nIn cases involving duplicate claimants arising from cloning procedures, the initial clone retains standing as a separate entity until proven otherwise by preponderance of evidence. Any assertion that a later-created simulacrum supersedes an earlier one must be supported by clear proof of intentional obfuscation or fraudulent misrepresentation by the first party involved. Absent such demonstration, each duplicate maintains distinct legal status and rights under the Accords.\n\nThe Desk reserves discretion to admit simulacrum testimony in exceptional circumstances where strict application of this rule would unjustly prejudice a litigant's ability to present their case fully. Such rulings shall be made only after careful consideration of all relevant factors and with explicit findings recorded in the official docket. Any party objecting must raise such grounds at the earliest convenient time, failing which may constitute a waiver thereof.\n\nUltimately, the guiding principle remains that a copy cannot supplant the original without clear justification. While technological advancements continue to blur traditional distinctions between persons and their reproductions, this foundational tenet of personal integrity shall endure as a bulwark against abuse and manipulation within the arcane community. The Desk stands ready to address novel challenges posed by evolving practices while maintaining fidelity to established precedents.\n\nsee § 700.1 Evidence Generally; see § 600.2 Familiars as Agents; under § 3.7 Burden of Proof; pursuant to § 4.9 Judicial Discretion. A simulacrum's words hold little weight without corroboration or necessity. Each duplicate stands alone until proven otherwise. The Desk balances fairness with precedent, ready to adapt to new realities while guarding core values. Legal capacity remains tied to true selfhood, even in an age of artificial replication.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "700.1",
            "600.2",
            "3.7",
            "4.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.4 Simulacrum Testimony and Duplicate Claimants",
          "text": "Simulacrums generated through lawful duplication processes retain all memories, skills, and personality traits of the original caster at the time of replication. However, they possess no legal standing distinct from their source. When a simulacrum invokes rights or asserts claims in its own name, such acts are deemed void ab initio unless ratified by the original individual under duress or undue influence.\n\nIn cases where multiple duplicates exist simultaneously, each shall be treated as an independent entity for the purposes of magical research and development only. No duplicate may represent the interests of another without explicit authorization granted in writing and witnessed by two disinterested mages registered with the Guild. Unauthorized duplication beyond permitted limits constitutes a Class C felony under § 200.1(a)(3).\n\nThe original caster retains sole authority to revoke any previously issued duplications at will, provided adequate notice is given to all affected parties. Failure to provide such notice renders subsequent revocations null and void unless accompanied by proof of immediate danger posed by continued existence of the duplicates.\n\nWhen disputes arise concerning ownership or control of duplicate entities, resolution shall follow standard arbitration procedures outlined in §§ 600-799. Parties may petition the Desk for a provisional injunction to halt further replication activities pending final determination on the merits of their respective claims.\n\nThis provision exists to prevent exploitation and abuse of duplication technology while recognizing its legitimate applications within the bounds set forth by these Accords. Any circumvention thereof shall be prosecuted as an aggravating circumstance in accordance with applicable penal statutes.\n\nsee §§ 100.8, 200.3, 400.5, 600.2\nA simulacrum is not the original; it cannot swear for them unless compelled. Duplicate claims require explicit ratification to be valid in court or before any official body.\" - Archmage Theron, Conservator Council Minutes #1792-B\"\n\n\"Multiple duplicates may exist as separate entities solely for research purposes. Beyond that, they are chattel of the original caster until disposed of by them.\" - Janna Brightspark, Innovator Symposium Address\"\n\n\"I maintain that all lawful duplications fall under my exclusive jurisdiction and authority to revoke or modify at discretion. The Desk must defer to this prerogative in all matters pertaining directly to duplicates derived from my work.\" - Original Caster v. Duplicate Owner, Case #884-C (Partial Transcript)\" \"The rules regarding duplication are clear: one original equals one lawful duplicate for research purposes only. Any additional copies constitute unauthorized field use and will be treated as such under existing statutes.\" - Autumnwood Accords Desk Ruling #3321-F\"",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "100.8",
            "700.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.4 Simulacrum Testimony and Duplicate Claimants — Continued",
          "text": "A simulated duplicate possesses no legal personality independent of its source, and may not testify or plead in the stead thereof unless expressly authorized by the court upon showing of necessity. Any attempt to bind a duplicate's signature or testimony against its original shall be deemed void ab initio for want of capacity. However, where an original is irretrievably lost or destroyed, a duplicate sworn before two disinterested witnesses may stand as sufficient evidence in lieu thereof. The burden lies squarely on the party producing such substitute proof to demonstrate both the chain of custody and the authenticity of the duplication process beyond reasonable doubt. Failure so to do shall result in dismissal of any claim predicated upon said duplicate's purported assertions or admissions. This provision exists solely to mitigate undue hardship where physical presence of a litigant proves impossible, not to circumvent fundamental fairness requirements inherent to adversarial proceedings. As such, courts retain broad discretion to deny substitution requests whenever they deem the risks of fraud or perjury outweigh the convenience offered by remote participation via simulacra.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "700.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "1431f9fc59e00161",
      "refs": [
        "1.4",
        "100.8",
        "200.1",
        "400.1",
        "600.2",
        "600.4",
        "700.1",
        "700.2",
        "800.2"
      ],
      "unresolved_refs": [
        "3.7",
        "4.9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "700.5",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "Clone as Duplicate Claimant",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "One claim. The later body files an amendment.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Clone as Duplicate Claimant",
          "text": "In the event that a legal claimant presents a physical duplicate of their person—whether through the use of high-fidelity transmutation, sophisticated illusionary weaving, or the biological replication of cellular matter—the Guild shall recognize only the primary biological entity as the rightful holder of any and all legal claims, properties, or titles. The existence of a secondary body does not constitute a secondary right of ownership, nor does it grant the duplicate the standing to act as a joint tenant or a co-claimant in any matter before the Accords. Any attempt to utilize a duplicate body to circumvent debt, evade the Paradox Trial, or assert multiple interests in a single asset shall be deemed an act ofarcane fraud and a violation of the foundational principles of the Autumnwood Accords.\n\nThe Guild’s stance on replication is predicated on the principle of singular identity. When a mage or a layperson produces a clone, that clone is viewed by the Accords as an extension of the original's will, not as an independent legal persona. Consequently, if a claimant possesses a duplicate, the Guild shall treat all actions taken by the duplicate as if they were taken by the primary body. If the duplicate is used to acquire property, that property belongs to the primary; if the duplicate is used to incur debt, the primary is liable. This prevents the \"doubling\" of rights which would otherwise destabilize the economic and social fabric of the Midlands.\n\nShould a legal dispute arise involving both the primary body and a duplicate, the Guild shall permit the later-manifested body to file a formal amendment to the primary's status. This amendment may be used to define the specific operational boundaries of the duplicate—for instance, designating it as a \"Laborer Entity\" or a \"Research Proxy\"—but it cannot be used to sever the link of ownership. The filing of such an amendment serves to clarify the intent of the primary claimant and to ensure that the Guild’s records reflect the intended use of the replication. Failure to file an amendment within three lunar cycles of the duplicate’s manifestation shall result in the Guild assuming a default \"Proxy Status,\" wherein the duplicate is granted no rights beyond those of a common construct, as defined in § 600.4.\n\nFurthermore, any attempt to claim that a duplicate is a separate legal \"person\" for the purpose of splitting a single inheritance or a single license shall be summarily dismissed. The Guild acknowledges the complexities of replication, yet it maintains that a soul—or the arcane signature associated with a legal identity—cannot be partitioned without a specific, high-level decree from Archmage Veyra. See § 700.1 for general evidence requirements regarding identity. Any mage found attempting to use a duplicate to bypass the restrictions of § 200.1 or to manufacture \"new\" claimants for the purpose of illicit trade shall be subject to immediate referral for a Paradox Trial. The Aegis Magi are authorized to seize all assets of a duplicate that are not explicitly bonded to the primary body in a filed amendment.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "600.4",
            "700.1",
            "200.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.5 Clone as Duplicate Claimant",
          "text": "In the event that a caster creates a duplicate of their own form through means such as cloning, duplication magic, or transference rituals, both entities shall be deemed distinct legal persons under these Accords, each bearing full responsibility for their respective actions and obligations. The original caster retains ownership of any magical knowledge, artifacts, or rights accrued prior to the creation of the clone, unless explicitly transferred via binding contract witnessed by a neutral party.\n\nHowever, should either the original or the cloned entity violate the provisions of these Accords, both shall be held jointly liable for any resulting damages or penalties imposed upon them collectively by the Mages' Guild. This joint liability provision exists to discourage reckless experimentation with duplication technologies that could lead to unpredictable and potentially catastrophic outcomes should one form fail or act independently without coordination.\n\nFurthermore, in situations where the original caster's identity has been fully subsumed into the cloned entity through complete magical integration, the new composite being shall be recognized as the sole legal successor to all prior rights and duties of its constituent parts. This principle ensures continuity of responsibility even when the precise boundaries between self and other have become obscured by advanced transference techniques.\n\nIt is expressly forbidden for any caster to create multiple duplicates with the intent of evading liability or distributing guilt among several forms. Such malicious gaming of the system shall be punished as a felony offense under these Accords, subject to maximum sanctions including permanent sealing of magical abilities and exile from civilized society at the discretion of the presiding judge.\n\nThis clause serves to clarify the complex legal implications arising from duplication magic while maintaining accountability for all actions taken by casters regardless of their physical manifestation. The Mages' Guild remains committed to upholding these principles to ensure fair and consistent application of justice within our shared reality.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "700.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.5 Duplicate Claimants and the Priority Rule",
          "text": "In cases where multiple parties assert competing claims to the same magical effect, object, or benefit derived therefrom, this Code establishes a clear framework for determining precedence based on temporal priority of entitlement. Where two or more individuals or entities find themselves in possession of identical rights to exploit an arcane resource, the party whose claim vests first in time shall be accorded exclusive dominion over said asset during the pendency of any ensuing dispute resolution process. This principle applies regardless of whether the competing interests are mutually exclusive or capable of concurrent enjoyment; mere existence of conflicting titles precludes simultaneous exercise thereof without express consent from all concerned parties.\n\nThe temporal priority rule serves as a default mechanism for allocating scarce magical goods when contractual provisions fail to address specific scenarios arising from overlapping claims. It is premised upon the notion that earlier entitlement warrants preferential treatment, reflecting both fairness and practical necessity in an environment where rivalries may escalate rapidly into violence should access remain ambiguous. Parties entering into agreements involving shared resources are advised to specify priority mechanisms within their operative clauses rather than relying upon this statutory fallback. Nevertheless, absent such stipulation, first-in-time shall govern unless contrary intent appears clearly on the face of the instrument or is otherwise demonstrated by reliable extrinsic evidence.\n\nThis provision does not extinguish superior equitable interests merely accrued subsequent to initial vesting, nor does it absolve later claimants from liability for tortious interference with established rights where such conduct occurs despite knowledge thereof. Rather, it creates a presumption favoring the primacy of antecedent titles absent affirmative waiver or modification by all affected stakeholders. Courts charged with adjudicating such disputes are directed to apply this rule diligently and impartially, ensuring that no party gains improper advantage through manipulation of procedural timelines or misrepresentation of facts surrounding origin of claim. Only upon full resolution of competing interests may subsequent arrangements be finalized without risk of challenge based on prior occupancy status.\n\nSee also §§ 700.1 Evidence Generally; 700.2 Scrying of Closed Books; 600.4 Construct Labor and Minimum Ward Requirements. Under § 1.8 Effective Date, these provisions shall remain in force until explicitly amended or superseded by subsequent enactments deemed necessary to address evolving circumstances within the Guild's jurisdictional sphere. Archmage Veyra reserves authority to issue supplementary guidelines as needed to clarify application of this priority framework across diverse factual contexts encountered in practice.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "700.1",
            "1.8",
            "700.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.5 Duplicate Claimants and the Priority Rule — Continued",
          "text": "Where two or more claimants assert identical interests in the same subject matter, priority shall be determined by temporal precedence under § 1.4 (Persons Bound). The first duly filed claim takes precedence over subsequent filings unless fraud or bad faith is proven on the face of the record. See also § 600.3 (Geas as an Unconscionable Term) regarding duress in contract formation, and § 700.2 (Scrying of Closed Books) for admissibility standards.\n\nIn cases involving artificial duplicates—whether magically conjured similes or legally replicated instruments—the original document retains primacy unless expressly superseded by a later amendment executed with full formalities. Under § 400.1 (Research Permits), any modification to an authorized act requires separate approval and re-certification, not mere clerical alteration. This principle extends analogously to duplicate claims, where the initial filing establishes the foundational right subject only to valid revocation or novation pursuant to governing statutes.\n\nWhere conflicting titles arise from parallel filings occurring within the same calendar hour, such as during system-wide outages or emergency sessions convened under § 100.8 (Emergency Sitting), tie-breaking shall follow a randomized algorithm overseen by an impartial arbiter selected via lot. The resulting determination becomes binding absent manifest error or procedural irregularity demonstrable on its face. See § 100.4 (Records of Speech) for authentication protocols and § 700.3 (Memory Charm as Spoliation) regarding evidentiary burdens in disputed scenarios.\n\nParties seeking to challenge a priority ruling must petition the Autumnwood Accords Desk within seven lunar cycles of notification, providing sworn affidavits substantiating their case under oath. Failure to comply with this timeline constitutes waiver of further recourse except through extraordinary writs granted solely at the discretion of Archmage Veyra personally. Refer to § 100.2 (Who May Speak for the Guild) for standing requirements and § 200.3 (Temporary Mouths) regarding proxy representation limitations.\n\nThe foregoing provisions reflect the longstanding wisdom that clarity in ownership prevents chaos in practice, a maxim enshrined across all civilized jurisdictions since time immemorial. As our ancestors declared, \"One true master per vessel,\" lest the whole sink beneath conflicting weights of greed and entitlement. Thus stands the law, immutable save by due process alone. May it guide us well.\n\n\"Order through precision; justice through procedure.\" – Archmage Theron, Conservator Leader",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "600.3",
            "700.2",
            "400.1",
            "100.8",
            "100.4",
            "700.3",
            "100.2",
            "200.3",
            "700.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Duplicate Claimants and the Priority Rule — Continued",
          "text": "§ 700.5 Duplicate Claimants and the Priority Rule — Continued\n\nIn instances where multiple claimants present themselves as identical duplicates of an original entity, the Accords establish a clear hierarchy to resolve competing assertions. The primary factor in determining precedence shall be temporal priority: the first party to successfully demonstrate their status as a genuine duplicate shall retain exclusive rights to any benefits derived therefrom. Subsequent claimants shall be deemed derivative entities and granted no further entitlements under this provision unless expressly authorized by the original holder or their duly appointed proxy.\n\nTo substantiate claims of duplication, each applicant must provide irrefutable proof of origin, including but not limited to genetic matching, biometric analysis, or authenticated testimony from recognized authorities within the relevant field. Failure to produce adequate evidence shall result in immediate disqualification and potential sanctions for attempted fraud under § 800.12 (Misrepresentation). The burden of proving duplicate status rests solely upon the asserting party and cannot be shifted through presumption or implication.\n\nFurthermore, any attempt to exploit duplicate status for financial gain beyond the scope of legitimate restitution is strictly prohibited and shall constitute a serious violation subject to enhanced penalties under § 900.7 (Abuse of Privilege). The Accords recognize that duplication can create complex legal entanglements requiring careful navigation by all parties involved. Therefore, claimants are urged to seek counsel from qualified practitioners before proceeding with any actions based on their purported status as duplicates.\n\nIn the event of disputes arising from conflicting duplicate claims, the matter shall be adjudicated according to established procedural guidelines outlined in Part VIII (Dispute Resolution). The decision rendered by the competent authority shall be binding and final unless overturned through proper appellate channels. This framework ensures fairness and consistency in addressing the unique challenges posed by duplication phenomena while safeguarding against exploitation of the system by malicious actors seeking undue advantage at the expense of others.\n\nThrough these provisions, the Accords seek to balance the interests of original entities with those of their duplicates, promoting equitable treatment while maintaining integrity within the broader legal framework governing such extraordinary circumstances. Compliance with this section is mandatory for all parties involved in duplicate-related matters and failure to adhere thereto may result in severe consequences as specified throughout this Code.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "700.5",
            "800.12",
            "900.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Duplicate Claimants and the Priority Rule — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "In the event that multiple duplicate claimants emerge, each having satisfied the requisite conditions for recognition under these Accords, priority shall be determined by the temporal sequence of their initial filings with the Autumnwood Accords Desk. The first in time shall prevail absent extenuating circumstances or evidence of fraud or collusion as provided herein. This provision ensures equitable treatment while discouraging speculative hoarding of duplicate rights by bad actors seeking to manipulate the system for personal gain.\n\nShould a dispute arise regarding the validity or precedence of competing claims, an expedited adjudication process shall be triggered upon written request from any interested party. The presiding arbiter shall review all relevant documentation and testimony within seven business days thereafter, rendering a binding decision based strictly on the provisions set forth in these Accords. Appeals of such determinations shall lie solely with the full Council, which may convene at its earliest convenience to hear arguments from both sides before issuing final judgment.\n\nIt is hereby declared that no duplicate claimant shall be permitted to exploit their status for undue advantage over others operating within the bounds of lawful commerce under these Accords. Engaging in predatory practices aimed solely at undermining competitors through the abuse of duplicate rights constitutes a material breach warranting severe penalties as prescribed by applicable law and Guild policy. The integrity of this delicate marketplace depends upon fair play and mutual respect among all participants – principles that must be upheld without exception to preserve the long-term viability of our collective enterprise.\n\nBy virtue of their voluntary association with the Autumnwood Accords Desk, duplicate claimants agree to abide fully by these rules and regulations at all times. Failure to do so may result in immediate expulsion from good standing along with such additional sanctions as deemed appropriate under the circumstances. The Council retains broad discretion to enforce compliance vigorously whenever necessary to safeguard the interests of all stakeholders involved. Through diligent adherence to these mandates, we shall foster an environment conducive to innovation and prosperity for generations yet unborn.\n\n*Note: This section applies equally whether disputes concern direct commercial rivals or seemingly unrelated entities whose actions nonetheless impact one another adversely in significant ways.*",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": []
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "7cce39e7dabb8b28",
      "refs": [
        "1.4",
        "1.8",
