      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "800.1",
      "part": "part_viii",
      "title": "Discipline; Range of Sanctions",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "From fee to seal to pocket-prison. Paradox Trial named.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Discipline; Range of Sanctions",
          "text": "The disciplinary framework of the Autumnwood Accords is predicated upon the principle of proportional restoration, wherein the severity of the sanction shall be directly correlated to the magnitude of the arcane transgression and the potential risk posed to the stability of the Midlands. All punitive measures are overseen by the Accords Desk, with the Archmage Sovereign maintaining final appellate authority. For minor infractions, such as the unauthorized use of common cantrips in residential zones or the failure to register a minor familiar, the Guild shall levy a standard administrative fee. These fees are non-negotiable and are to be paid in recognized currency; notably, the Wario Coin is strictly prohibited as a medium of tender for any Guild transaction, per the standing prohibitions on non-standardized currency.\n\nFor more significant violations, specifically those involving the reckless manipulation of mana or the infringement upon the privacy of other mages, the Guild may impose a Seal of Silence or a magical Seal of Binding. A Seal of Binding is a permanent or semi-permanent restriction placed upon a practitioner’s ability to channel the weave, effectively neutralizing their threat to the public and the Guild's interests. The application of such a Seal requires a formal finding of negligence or malice by a tribunal of the Aegis Magi. In cases where a mage’s actions threaten the very fabric of reality—such as the creation of a rift or the unauthorized growth of a Titan—the sanction shall escalate to the ultimate punitive measure: the Pocket-Prison.\n\nThe Pocket-Prison serves as a localized, extra-dimensional incarceration designed to isolate dangerous individuals from the material plane indefinitely. Entry into the Pocket-Prison is the final recourse of the Accords, reserved for those whose presence in the Midlands constitutes an existential risk. All disciplinary actions, regardless of scale, are recorded in the central registry to ensure that the history of the Accords remains transparent to the Conservators and the Innovators alike. See § 1.6 for definitions of \"Transgression\" and \"Arcane Magnitude.\" See § 800.1 for the naming of the Paradox Trial as the primary judicial mechanism for these sanctions.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "800.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Paradox Trial Procedure",
          "text": "The Paradox Trial is the official judicial mechanism of the Autumnwood Accords, designed to adjudicate complex disputes and criminal charges through a system of peer review. It is mandated that all high-level disputes involving the use of restricted magic, the violation of the Quiet List, or the breach of the Unsanctioned Arts be brought before a council of peers. These peers shall be selected from a pool of qualified mages who are not directly involved in the litigation, ensuring a semblance of impartiality between the traditionalist Conservators and the radical Innovators. The trial shall be conducted in a designated neutral hall within the Autumn Wood, where the Aegis Magi are tasked with maintaining the integrity of the magical perimeter.\n\nDuring the Paradox Trial, all evidence must be presented in a structured format, and every testimony shall be recorded by a Summoned Notary to ensure a permanent legal record. The proceedings may involve the demonstration of magical theory, the presentation of seized artifacts, or the recitation of historical precedents. The objective of the trial is to determine the truth of the violation and to assign the appropriate sanction as outlined in § 800.1. The council shall deliberate in private, and their verdict shall be binding upon all signatories of the Accords.\n\nStrict prohibition is placed upon the conduct of unscheduled street duels. While the Guild recognizes the historical prevalence of magical combat, the Accords decree that no duel shall be considered a legal method of dispute resolution unless it has been formally scheduled, registered, and sanctioned by the Accords Desk. Any mage who engages in an unscheduled duel in a public space shall be subject to immediate arrest by the Aegis Magi and shall be liable for the costs of the intervention. Only through the formal Paradox Trial may a mage seek to vindicate their honor or reclaim stolen property. See § 700.1 for evidence standards.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "800.1",
            "700.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Seal of Silence; Duration and Review",
          "text": "The Seal of Silence is a specific disciplinary measure employed when a mage’s speech—whether spoken, telepathic, or inscribed—poses a risk to the stability of the magical order or involves the unauthorized dissemination of information regarding the Quiet List. Upon the imposition of the Seal, the affected individual is rendered incapable of producing any linguistic output that can be perceived by others. The duration of this Seal is determined by the presiding council during the Paradox Trial, based on the severity of the breach. A standard Seal of Silence for minor informational leaks shall last for a period of no less than one lunar cycle and no more than three years, depending on the frequency of the offender's previous infractions.\n\nWhile a Seal of Silence is active, the individual may still engage in non-verbal activities, such as the study of theory or the maintenance of mundane tasks, provided these actions do not involve the manipulation of the weave in a manner that could be interpreted as coded communication. The Seal is not a total removal of agency but a targeted restriction on the medium of speech. The Accords Desk shall monitor the effectiveness of the Seal to ensure it does not cause unintended physiological harm to the caster, as the preservation of the caster's health is a secondary priority to the preservation of the Guild's secrets.\n\nA mage under the Seal of Silence may petition for a review of their sentence after the passage of one-half of the designated duration. Such a petition must be submitted in writing, using a pre-approved method of non-verbal transcription, and must be presented to the Archmage Sovereign for mediation. The review shall consider the mage's conduct during the period of silence, their cooperation with the Aegis Magi, and the current threat level posed by the information they were originally seeking to disseminate. If the petition is granted, the Seal may be lifted, shortened, or modified. If the petition is denied, the remaining duration of the Seal shall stand. See § 300.3 for Quiet List Procedure.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "300.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Duel Stakes Converted to Fees",
          "text": "In instances where a mage insists upon a formal Trial by Arcane Duel as the primary method of adjudication—specifically when the legal merits of a case are deemed too nuanced for a standard peer review—the Accords Desk shall intervene to ensure the preservation of public safety and the Guild's resources. While the Guild acknowledges the cultural significance of the duel, it shall not permit the destruction of infrastructure or the collateral damage inherent in high-level magical combat. Therefore, the Accords Desk shall mandate that the \"stakes\" of the duel be converted into administrative fees and restorative penalties.\n\nThe conversion process requires the parties to agree upon a pre-determined valuation of the dispute. For example, if a mage seeks to reclaim a stolen artifact, the \"win\" condition of the duel shall be the legal transfer of the item, while the \"loss\" condition shall be the payment of a substantial fine to the Guild’s treasury. The Aegis Magi shall oversee the duel, and should either participant exceed the permitted output of mana or target non-combatants, the duel shall be immediately terminated. The losing party shall be held liable for the full cost of the Aegis Magi's intervention, as well as a punitive surcharge for the unsanctioned escalation of magical force.\n\nThis conversion of stakes ensures that the Paradox Trial remains the primary judicial framework while still allowing for the resolution of disputes through active magical demonstration. The Accords Desk shall maintain a schedule of these converted duels, ensuring that they occur in designated \"duel-pits\" where the magical fallout can be safely dissipated. No duel shall be permitted to proceed if the projected cost of the collateral damage exceeds the valuation of the stakes. This policy serves to prevent the \"scorched earth\" tactics often favored by the Innovator faction in their pursuit of radical results. See § 800.2 for Paradox Trial Procedure.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "800.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Pocket-Prison Procedure",
          "text": "The Pocket-Prison is the ultimate site of incarceration for those deemed too dangerous to remain within the Midlands or those whose magic has become fundamentally erratic. The procedure for commitment to a Pocket-Prison is strictly regulated to prevent the arbitrary disappearance of Guild members. A mage may only be committed to a Pocket-Prison following a final verdict of the Paradox Trial, where the council determines that sealing the mage's magic (as per § 800.1) is insufficient to protect the public. The commitment must be signed off by both the Archmage Sovereign and the head of the Conservators, ensuring that the decision is made with the full weight of the Guild's authority.\n\nThe \"key\" to a Pocket-Prison is not a physical object but a complex, multi-layered arcane lock maintained by the Aegis Magi. Only the Archmage Sovereign and the designated High Conservator may authorize the opening of a cell. The prison itself is a pocket dimension, isolated from the flow of standard time and the reach of external scrying. This isolation is necessary to prevent the \"leakage\" of dangerous magic into the surrounding environment. Once a mage is committed, their presence is struck from the active census of the Midlands, though their records remain accessible to the Accords Desk for historical and legal purposes.\n\nRegarding the conditions of confinement, the Accords are silent on the specifics of daily life within the Pocket-Prison, as these are considered internal security matters. However, it is a common misconception among the populace that \"visiting hours\" exist for those incarcerated in a Pocket-Prison. The Accords Desk officially denies the existence of such a practice, as the very nature of the pocket dimension precludes safe external access. Any attempt to scry into the Pocket-Prison or to bypass the Aegis Magi's wards to reach an inmate shall be treated as a high-level felony, punishable by immediate and permanent Seal of Binding. See § 400.2 for details on Pocket Dimensions as Labs.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "800.1",
            "400.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 800.1 Discipline; Range of Sanctions",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords provide for a graduated system of disciplinary measures, ranging from admonition to permanent exile. The severity of the sanction imposed shall be commensurate with the gravity of the infraction and the circumstances surrounding it. For minor infractions such as technical violations or first-time offenses where no harm was done, reprimand coupled with mandatory remedial education may suffice. More serious breaches warrant stronger penalties including suspension of licensure, temporary sealing of magical faculties, or revocation of privileges for a defined period.\n\nIn cases involving substantial violations of the Accords' core principles—such as unauthorized casting in restricted areas, misuse of forbidden schools, or repeated negligence causing damage—the Council may impose more draconian measures. This includes permanent expulsion from the Guild and stripping of all titles and honors held therein. In extreme instances where a caster poses an existential threat to public safety through their continued operation, total incapacitation via magical sealing may be deemed necessary. Such actions shall only be undertaken after due process has been fully observed and a clear majority vote obtained at a formal session of the Council.\n\nThe Paradox Trial serves as the primary adjudicatory mechanism for resolving disputes between mages regarding alleged violations of the Accords. This quasi-judicial proceeding is conducted before an impartial panel consisting of three peer casters selected randomly from the membership rolls. The accused mage shall be afforded full opportunity to present their defense, call witnesses, and cross-examine opposing evidence before a ruling is rendered. If found guilty beyond reasonable doubt, sanctions may include imposition of fines payable in guild coin, temporary suspension without pay, or permanent forfeiture of standing within the organization depending on severity.\n\nFor particularly egregious violations involving intentional malfeasance or gross incompetence endangering lives, more severe consequences apply. These may encompass revocation of all licenses and certifications held by the offending individual along with blacklisting from future employment opportunities within the magical profession. In worst-case scenarios where an individual demonstrates unremediable dangerousness through pattern of conduct, permanent sealing of their innate abilities via ritualistic means may be deemed appropriate to protect society at large. Such radical measures require unanimous approval by the full Council and shall remain subject to periodic review should circumstances warrant reconsideration.\n\nAny sanctions imposed pursuant to this section shall be executed promptly upon final determination unless stayed pending appeal proceedings. Failure to comply with an order of the Council within specified timeframes constitutes further misconduct punishable under these same provisions. The Guild retains broad discretion in tailoring appropriate remedies tailored to the specific facts presented, balancing interests of justice against potential collateral impacts on innocent parties caught up in enforcement actions arising from another's wrongdoing. Through consistent and fair application of these disciplinary standards, the Autumnwood Accords aims to maintain high ethical benchmarks throughout the magical community while safeguarding public welfare above all else.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "800.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "8a83535726773507",
      "refs": [
        "1.6",
        "300.3",
        "400.2",
        "700.1",
        "800.2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "800.2",
      "part": "part_viii",
      "title": "Paradox Trial Procedure",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Peers, record, no street duel unless scheduled.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Paradox Trial Procedure",
          "text": "The Paradox Trial shall serve as the primary judicial mechanism for the resolution of high-level arcane disputes and the adjudication of severe violations of the Autumnwood Accords. Per § 800.1, all trials must be overseen by a council of peers, defined as mages of equivalent or greater established rank who possess no direct familial or commercial ties to the accused or the complainant. The presence of these peers ensures that the delicate balance between the Conservator and Innovator philosophies is maintained, preventing any single faction from weaponizing the judiciary to achieve absolute hegemony over the Midlands. The primary objective of the Paradox Trial is the preservation of the Weave and the safety of the citizenry; consequently, the council shall prioritize the containment of volatile magic over the immediate satisfaction of personal grievances.\n\nAll proceedings of the Paradox Trial shall be entered into the permanent record of the Accords Desk. Each testimony, magical manifestation, and evidentiary artifact presented during the trial must be transcribed by a bonded Notary or a construct authorized under § 600.5. These records are to be kept in a secure, magically-warded archive, accessible only to the Archmage Sovereign and the presiding Council. The records serve not only as a historical ledger but as a binding precedent for future adjudications. Any mage found to have willfully falsified a trial record or attempted to redact testimony shall be subject to immediate sentencing under the maximum severity tier of § 800.1.\n\nFurthermore, the Paradox Trial expressly prohibits the conduct of street duels as a means of settling legal disputes. While the history of magic is replete with spontaneous arcane combat, the Accords Decree that all such conflicts must be moved into a formal, magically-regulated environment. If a complainant and a defendant insist upon a Trial by Arcane Duel, it must be scheduled with at least forty-eight hours of notice to the Aegis Magi for the purpose of establishing a neutral arena and safety wards. Any mage who initiates an unscheduled duel in a public space or within the bounds of the Autumn Wood shall be deemed to have forfeited their right to a fair trial and shall be subject to summary detention. See § 800.1 for the range of sanctions applicable to such violations.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "800.1",
            "600.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Seal of Silence; Duration and Review",
          "text": "The Seal of Silence is a restorative and punitive measure utilized primarily when a caster has engaged in the unauthorized recitation of the Quiet List or has engaged in speech that threatens the stability of local mana-currents. Upon the imposition of the Seal, the subject’s vocal apparatus shall be magically locked, preventing any audible speech, including the casting of verbal components. The duration of this Seal is determined by the presiding Council based on the severity of the infraction as outlined in § 300.3. For minor infractions, such as the accidental utterance of a restricted name in a tavern, the Seal may be set for a period of no less than one lunar cycle. For grave violations, including the deliberate dissemination of forbidden knowledge, the Seal may be rendered permanent, subject only to a formal petition for review.\n\nA mage under the Seal of Silence is not exempt from the basic rights of the Accords, yet they are strictly prohibited from using any form of telepathic communication to circumvent the vocal restriction. Any attempt to bypass the Seal via external magical means shall be viewed as an act of defiance against the Accords Desk and may result in the extension of the current Seal or a transition to a more severe penal status. The Seal is intended to provide a period of reflection and to \"cool\" the magical residue of the offender’s intent. During this time, the mage may still be required to perform manual labor or study non-verbal theory, provided such activities do not involve the manipulation of the Weave in a way that could trigger a resonance with the Seal.\n\nReview of the Seal of Silence shall occur at the conclusion of each designated period. The mage may submit a written petition, transcribed by a designated proxy or through a pre-arranged series of non-verbal signs, requesting a reduction in the Seal’s duration. The Council shall review the mage’s conduct during the period of silence; if the mage has demonstrated a marked decrease in erratic magical behavior and has complied with all other Guild mandates, the Seal may be lifted or shortened. If the mage has shown signs of escalating magical instability or has attempted to subvert the Seal, the Council shall maintain the current duration or increase it accordingly. See § 200.4 for procedures regarding the lapse of such magical effects.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "300.3",
            "200.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Duel Stakes Converted to Fees",
          "text": "In instances where a dispute cannot be settled through standard arbitration and the parties involved insist upon a Trial by Arcane Duel, the Accords Desk shall intervene to ensure the fiscal and structural integrity of the Guild. While the duel serves as the mechanism for determining guilt or innocence, it is not a mechanism for the private transfer of wealth or the destruction of assets. All stakes agreed upon by the combatants—whether they be gold, land, magical artifacts, or services—shall be automatically converted into administrative fees payable to the Accords Desk. This conversion is mandatory and shall be calculated based on the estimated cost of the magical wards, the Aegis Magi presence, and the logistical overhead required to host the duel.\n\nThe conversion of stakes ensures that the Guild is not burdened by the costs of policing private magical violence. Should the combatants agree to a stake of one thousand gold, the Accords Desk shall seize that amount and apply it toward the maintenance of the Autumn Wood facilities. If the stake involves a tangible asset, such as a deed to a workshop or a rare reagent, the Desk shall assess a fair market value and levy a corresponding fee. The winning party shall receive their judicial victory—the legal recognition of their claim—but they shall not receive the physical stake or the converted gold. The purpose of this provision is to strip the profit motive from the act of magical combat, ensuring that mages do not engage in duels for mercenary gain.\n\nAny attempt to circumvent this fee conversion by entering into \"side-agreements\" with outside parties, such as the Regal Empire or the Goodstyle Artisans, shall be considered a violation of the Accords. If a mage is found to be using a duel as a pretext for a private transaction, the duel shall be declared void, and both parties shall be subject to immediate investigation. The Aegis Magi are authorized to seize any assets involved in such a fraudulent arrangement. Mages who find this provision burdensome are encouraged to seek mediation through the Archmage Sovereign to avoid the high costs associated with formal duels. See § 800.2 for the procedures regarding the scheduling of such duels.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "800.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Pocket-Prison Procedure",
          "text": "The Pocket-Prison is a specialized containment facility utilized for mages whose magic has become fundamentally volatile or whose crimes against the Accords necessitate total isolation from the physical plane. Unlike a standard detention cell, a Pocket-Prison is a self-contained pocket dimension, isolated from the primary Weave to prevent the prisoner from drawing upon external mana or affecting the world outside. The keys to these dimensions are held exclusively by the High Conservators, and access to the prison is strictly regulated by the Accords Desk. No mage may enter a Pocket-Prison without a direct mandate from Archmage Veyra or a unanimous vote from the Council of Peers.\n\nThe procedure for incarceration involves the \"Sealing of the Threshold,\" where the prisoner is cast into the dimension and the entrance is collapsed, leaving only a singular, magically-locked point of entry. Once the threshold is closed, the prisoner is effectively removed from the timeline of the Midlands. The maintenance of the Pocket-Prison requires a constant, low-level expenditure of mana from the Guild’s central reserves, making it a costly and significant undertaking. Consequently, incarceration is reserved for the most dangerous offenders or those whose magic poses a systemic risk to the Autumnwood Accords. The prisoner is provided with a basic existence—sustenance and environment—but these are to be sufficient only for survival, not for comfort.\n\nRegarding the status of visiting hours, the Accords Desk maintains a policy of absolute secrecy. While rumors persist among the lower-tier mages that certain high-profile prisoners may receive visitors, the official stance of the Guild is that no such visits are permitted. Any attempt to establish a communication link with a prisoner in a Pocket-Prison is a felony under the Accords and may result in the immediate sentencing of the visitor to a similar confinement. The isolation of the prisoner is a matter of public safety; any breach of this isolation, whether through physical entry or magical scrying, is to be met with the highest tier of sanction. See § 400.2 for regulations regarding the use of pocket dimensions for authorized research purposes.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "400.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 800.2 Paradox Trial Procedure; Seal of Silence",
          "text": "The Paradox Trial shall commence only upon formal indictment by the Aegis Magi, citing specific violations of this Code. The accused may demand a hearing before the High Council within seven days of service of process. Failure to appear constitutes a default judgment of guilt, punishable by immediate sealing of magical faculties and permanent exile from Autumn Wood.\n\nAt trial, the accused shall be granted counsel of their choosing, provided such counsel is duly licensed under § 102.3. Evidence may include but is not limited to: certified copies of inspection reports (§ 805.1), witness testimony subject to cross-examination per common law rules adapted for arcane matters, and authenticated magical signatures linking the accused to the charged conduct. The burden of proof rests upon the prosecution; mere suspicion or circumstantial association shall not suffice.\n\nUpon conclusion of proceedings, the Council shall render a verdict within ten days. If found guilty beyond reasonable doubt, penalties shall range from revocation of licensure and heavy fines to sealing of abilities and incarceration in a pocket dimension, as deemed appropriate by the severity of the offense (§ 801.2). The accused retains the right to appeal to the High Council within thirty days of judgment, pending posting of a bond equal to two years' net earnings.\n\nNo Paradox Trial shall degenerate into an unscheduled duel or combat between magical practitioners outside the confines of this Code. Such actions constitute grounds for immediate expulsion and permanent disqualification from legal practice under § 801.5(b). All trials must be conducted in strict accordance with these procedures to ensure fairness, transparency, and adherence to the spirit of the Accords.\n\nThe Seal of Silence provision shall remain in effect for a period of thirty-six hours following any public trial or sentencing. During this time, all participants are prohibited from discussing details of the proceedings outside of official channels. Violation of this restriction shall result in automatic disqualification from future trials and suspension of licensure for one year, without prejudice to further disciplinary action.\n\nThis section applies exclusively to formal adjudications conducted under the authority of the Autumnwood Accords Desk. Informal disputes between parties not involving allegations of Code violations fall outside its purview and shall be resolved through standard contractual mechanisms or private arbitration as mutually agreed upon by the disputants. Any attempt to manipulate trial proceedings for personal gain shall be treated as a separate offense punishable under § 801.6(c).\n\nsee § 700.2 Scrying of Closed Books; see § 801.3 Scope of Authority; see § 105.4 Conflict of Interest in Casting. Under § 901.1, all trial records shall be retained for a minimum period of ten years to facilitate future reference and audit as necessary.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "102.3",
            "805.1",
            "801.2",
            "801.5",
            "801.6",
            "700.2",
            "801.3",
            "105.4",
            "901.1",
            "800.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 800.2 Paradox Trial Procedure and Post-Trial Sanctions",
          "text": "A Paradox Trial shall commence only upon formal indictment by the Aegis Magi, specifying alleged violations of the Accords with particularity. The accused mage shall be afforded a minimum of seven days to prepare for trial, during which time they may secure counsel from peers or licensed legal practitioners. At the opening session, the presiding council member shall read the charges aloud and demand a plea of either \"guilty\" or \"not guilty.\" Should the accused refuse to plead, the court shall enter a plea of not guilty on their behalf for purposes of procedure only.\n\nIf a guilty plea is entered, the trial may proceed abridged, with the primary focus being determination of appropriate sanctions pursuant to § 800.1. In such cases, the council shall render judgment within fourteen days unless further time is granted for deliberation. Notwithstanding any contrary local custom or practice, no Paradox Trial shall ever devolve into a personal duel between the accused and their accuser(s), whether magical or physical in nature. Such conduct constitutes unprofessional behavior warranting immediate disbarment without prejudice to other sanctions that may apply.\n\nUpon finding of guilt, the council shall impose one or more penalties from among those enumerated in § 800.1, tailored to the gravity of the offense and circumstances surrounding it. Penalties may be imposed concurrently or consecutively at the discretion of the court. All judgments rendered by the Paradox Trial Council shall be recorded verbatim in the official minutes, which shall remain sealed except as provided herein. Any party seeking review of a judgment must petition for rehearing within thirty days of notification thereof.\n\nPetitions for rehearing shall set forth with particularity the grounds upon which relief is sought and any new evidence that bears upon the case. The full council shall then convene to consider whether to grant rehearing, either in whole or in part at their discretion. If rehearing is granted, the matter shall be heard de novo unless otherwise specified by the presiding member. Decisions regarding petitions for rehearing are final and not subject to further appeal except as may be provided by law or court order.\n\nIn addition to any sanctions imposed pursuant to § 800.1, the court retains inherent authority to issue such additional orders as it deems necessary and proper to effectuate its judgments fully. This includes but is not limited to requiring compliance with specific affirmative acts or refraining from particular conduct. Failure to obey lawful court orders may result in enforcement proceedings under applicable law, including contempt sanctions where appropriate. All costs of trial, whether incurred directly or indirectly by the parties or their representatives, shall be borne equitably as determined by the court taking into account all relevant factors.\n\nThe provisions herein regarding Paradox Trials are intended to provide a fair and orderly mechanism for resolving disputes arising from alleged violations of the Autumnwood Accords while protecting the rights of both the accused and the public at large. Nothing contained in this section shall be construed to limit or impair any other powers, duties, or responsibilities vested in the Aegis Magi or their agents by law or custom. These procedures stand as a testament to our commitment to justice tempered with mercy – the hallmark of true mages who serve their craft and community alike.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "800.1",
            "800.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
