    },
    {
      "cite": "900.1",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Duty to File Before Casting",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "If value, composition, or risk will move, file first.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Duty to File Before Casting",
          "text": "The preservation of the weave and the stability of the Midlands require that all significant alterations to reality, whether intentional or incidental, be documented by the Accords Desk prior to the initiation of the somatic or verbal components of the spell. Under the authority of the Archmage Sovereign, all practitioners of the arts are hereby commanded to submit a formal Notice of Intent to Cast (NIC) whenever the intended enchantment, transmutation, or evocation is likely to result in a measurable shift in material value, a fundamental change in chemical or metaphysical composition, or a demonstrable increase in environmental risk. This duty is not merely a bureaucratic formality; it is a preventative measure designed to ensure that the Aegis Magi can monitor for potential paradoxes or violations of the forbidden schools as defined in § 300.2. A mage who proceeds with a casting that meets these criteria without a filed and acknowledged NIC is in immediate breach of the Accords and may be subject to the sanctions detailed in § 800.1.\n\nFurthermore, the \"filing first\" requirement dictates that the documentation must be physically or magically deposited at the Accords Desk before the first syllable of the incantation is uttered. Failure to comply shall result in the immediate forfeiture of any protections afforded by the Guild and may lead to an immediate summons for a Paradox Trial as outlined in § 800.2. In instances where the risk of the casting is deemed \"imminent\" by the practitioner—such as in cases of self-defense or the containment of a burgeoning rift—the practitioner may act first but must provide a sworn affidavit of emergency within one hour of the event's conclusion. Such affidavits must be notarized by a summoned notary as per § 600.5. If the casting results in a permanent alteration of the landscape or the creation of a permanent magical construct, the practitioner shall be held liable for all subsequent registry fees and maintenance costs. See § 900.2 for reporting thresholds.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "800.1",
            "800.2",
            "600.5",
            "900.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Reporting of Material Enchantments",
          "text": "Any enchantment that imparts a permanent or semi-permanent property to a physical object, known as a Material Enchantment, must be reported to the Accords Desk to ensure the integrity of the regional market and the safety of the citizenry. The threshold for mandatory reporting is set at a minimum of fifty gold pieces in perceived material value, or any enchantment that alters the weight, volume, or elemental state of an object beyond a five percent variance from its natural state. For example, a blade that glows is a minor enchantment requiring no filing, but a blade that cuts through stone as if it were silk constitutes a Material Enchantment and requires full disclosure. Practitioners who fail to report such enchantments are liable for a \"Late Reporting Surcharge.\" This surcharge shall accrue at a fixed rate of ten percent of the estimated market value per sunrise, calculated in the caster's original time zone.\n\nThe reporting period begins at the moment the enchantment is finalized and the object is released from the casting circle. Should a practitioner attempt to obfuscate the nature of the enchantment by claiming it is a mere \"aesthetic flourish,\" the Aegis Magi reserve the right to seize the object for inspection. If the object is found to exceed the reporting thresholds, the practitioner shall be fined the full accrued surcharge plus a penalty of double the base fee. This ensures that the Innovators do not flood the markets with unregulated, high-potency items while the Conservators maintain the stability of the Midlands. For questions regarding the specific chemical changes of materials, see § 1001.7. For the disposal of failed enchantments, see § 400.4.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.7",
            "400.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Record Retention",
          "text": "The Mages' Guild Accords Desk serves as the ultimate repository of magical history, and as such, the retention of records is paramount to the continuity of the Accord. All filings, including Notices of Intent to Cast, Material Enchantment reports, and Paradox Trial transcripts, shall be preserved in the Guild archives for a minimum duration of seven years. However, in the event that a record pertains to a \"Geological Age\" event—defined as any enchantment or ritual that results in the formation of new landmasses, the shifting of tectonic plates, or the creation of permanent, non-degradable geological features—the record shall be maintained indefinitely. The determination of whether a record qualifies as a \"Geological Age\" event is at the sole discretion of the Clerk of the Accords Desk, based on the Clerk's subjective assessment of the \"humor\" or \"significance\" of the event's permanence.\n\nDuring the standard seven-year retention period, records may be accessed by authorized Conservators or Innovators for the purposes of research or litigation. During this time, the Desk may redact sensitive information regarding the Quiet List to protect the privacy of the high-ranking mages involved. Once a record is deemed to have reached its retention limit, it may be moved to the deep archives or, if the Clerk deems the content sufficiently trivial or amusing, may be ceremonially incinerated. No mage shall have the right to demand the destruction of their personal records before the expiration of the mandatory retention period. For the specific procedures regarding the redaction of sensitive data, see § 300.3.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "300.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Identify Fees; Who Pays",
          "text": "The administration of the Autumnwood Accords is a self-sustaining bureaucratic entity; therefore, the Guild shall not provide administrative services, permit processing, or enforcement actions gratis. Every action taken by the Accords Desk—from the filing of a Notice of Intent to Cast to the adjudication of a Paradox Trial—incurs a mandatory Administrative Fee. The fundamental principle of the Accords regarding these costs is simple: the individual who requests the service is the individual who shall bear the burden of the cost. The Accords Desk is expressly prohibited from waiving these fees for any reason, including political pressure from the Regal Empire, requests from the Silver Flame, or appeals from the Innovator faction.\n\nThe person who asks for the magical privilege pays for the bureaucratic oversight. This applies to all interactions with the Aegis Magi, who may levy additional \"Enforcement Surcharges\" if a mage's actions require the deployment of Guild resources to contain a magical anomaly. It is strictly forbidden for a third party to pay the fees on behalf of a mage without a signed Power of Attorney and a pre-payment of the \"Proxy Fee.\" Furthermore, the Guild shall not accept Wario Coins as a valid form of tender for any official fees; all payments must be rendered in gold, mana-crystals, or other approved Guild-recognized currencies. Any attempt to bribe a Clerk with unauthorized tender is a felony offense punishable by immediate referral to the Paradox Trial. For details on the conversion of duel stakes into administrative fees, see § 800.4.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "800.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "False Provenance of Relics",
          "text": "The Accords Desk serves as the arbiter of truth regarding the history and origin of magical artifacts. A common misconception among the lay public and even some novice casters is that a compelling narrative or a \"story\" constitutes a valid chain of title for a relic. The Accords Desk formally declares that a story is not a chain of title. For an artifact to be registered with a valid provenance, the owner must provide a continuous, documented history of ownership, including the names of previous keepers, the locations of previous storage, and the specific magical signatures of those who have held the item. A tale of a \"lost king\" or a \"fallen civilization\" is insufficient for the purpose of legal registration or the removal of restrictive wards.\n\nIf a practitioner attempts to register a relic based solely on oral tradition or unverified mythos, the filing shall be rejected as \"Speculative Provenance.\" The practitioner may be required to submit to a forensic arcane audit, wherein the Aegis Magi will attempt to trace the magical \"echoes\" of the object's history. If the audit reveals that the artifact's history has been intentionally obscured or falsified—such as attempting to hide a relic's origin from the Conservators—the practitioner shall be subject to immediate prosecution for Fraudulent Provenance. This law is designed to prevent the illicit trade of stolen artifacts and to ensure that the Guild can properly monitor the movement of high-risk items. For the protocols regarding the scrying of closed books to verify provenance, see § 700.2.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "700.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting; Reportable Enchantments and Relics",
          "text": "Every caster shall file an advance notice with the Accords Desk prior to casting any spell of Level Five or higher, unless exempted by special dispensation under § 200.3. Failure to comply constitutes a Class B violation subject to fines up to one thousand gold crowns per instance. Notice must be submitted at least forty-eight hours before intended casting date and hour, specifying location, purpose, estimated duration, and potential hazards. See § 100.6 for delegation requirements regarding notice submission by proxy representatives.\n\nReportable enchantments include all items possessing intrinsic magical properties exceeding a value threshold of fifty gold crowns. Owners must disclose such artifacts during initial licensing applications and promptly notify the Desk of any newly acquired pieces meeting criteria. Concealment or misrepresentation shall result in immediate suspension pending investigation, with penalties escalating based on proven intent to defraud. False claims regarding item origin may trigger criminal charges under relevant statutes governing theft and forgery. Record retention obligations mandate maintenance of detailed logs documenting all filed notices and reported objects for a minimum period of seven calendar years. These records serve as official reference materials during enforcement proceedings and audits conducted by appointed inspectors.\n\nResponsibility for filing rests exclusively with the individual caster or entity initiating magical operations, irrespective of whether services are rendered gratuitously or for compensation. Exceptions exist only where explicit written authorization is granted by the Conservators' Council to proceed without advance warning due to exigent circumstances threatening public safety or national security interests. In such rare instances, post-facto reporting becomes mandatory within seventy-two hours following event conclusion. Any discrepancies discovered during subsequent review shall be addressed through formal disciplinary channels as outlined in § 800.1 et seq.\n\nIdentification fees associated with processing filings vary according to spell complexity and anticipated impact zone size. Standard rates apply unless waived by the Archmage Sovereign upon determination of compelling public benefit arising from proposed work. Payment must accompany submission of all required documentation, including sworn affidavits attesting to caster competency and insurance coverage limits adequate to cover potential damages. Late submissions incur additional surcharges calculated at twice the base filing fee plus twenty percent monthly accruals until resolved satisfactorily by the Desk's adjudicators acting in their sole discretion guided by applicable regulations herein established.\n\nProvenance verification of all reportable relics requires submission of authenticating certificates issued by accredited appraisers familiar with relevant historical contexts surrounding each artifact's creation and ownership history. Absence thereof renders acceptance of materials provisional pending further scrutiny, potentially delaying commencement dates for associated projects accordingly. Fraudulent attempts to bypass provenance checks via falsified paperwork will be treated as aggravated offenses warranting enhanced punitive measures beyond those specified above without further notice whatsoever given beforehand directly involved parties concerned regarding matters herein addressed comprehensively throughout this section dedicated solely explaining duties imposed upon casters operating within jurisdictional bounds defined hereinabove clearly delineated explicitly leaving no room whatsoever for ambiguity whatsoever concerning responsibilities laid out forthwith below...",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.3",
            "100.6",
            "800.1",
            "900.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
