    },
    {
      "cite": "900.2",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Reporting of Material Enchantments",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Thresholds. Late reports accrue per sunrise in the caster's original time zone.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Reporting of Material Enchantments",
          "text": "All mages operating under the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords shall be mandated to register any enchantment of a material nature that exceeds the standard threshold of common utility. A \"Material Enchantment\" is defined herein as any permanent or semi-permanent alteration of physical matter, including but not limiteded to the infusion of mana into structural masonry, the creation of sentient constructs, or the transmutation of base metals into durable alloys for commercial use. Any enchantment that possesses the capacity to affect a public thoroughfare, a private residence, or a communal resource must be filed with the Accords Desk within three lunar cycles of its completion. Failure to report such enchantments constitutes a breach of the Guild’s transparency protocols and may result in the immediate seizure of the enchanted object for safety inspection.\n\nThe threshold for mandatory reporting is determined by the magnitude of the arcane resonance. Minor household charms, such as self-lighting lanterns or cooling stones, do not require filing. However, any enchantment capable of sustaining a continuous magical output of more than fifty units of mana per hour, or any object capable of independent locomotion without a direct tether to a caster, falls under the mandatory reporting category. Mages who neglect to file these reports shall be subject to administrative fines. In the event of a failure to report, late reports shall accrue a penalty of twenty gold sovereigns per sunrise in the caster's original time zone. This penalty is designed to ensure that the Guild maintains an accurate and timely ledger of all active arcane signatures within the Midlands.\n\nFurthermore, mages who engage in the mass production of material enchantments for sale to non-casters must provide a certificate of stability signed by a recognized Conservator. This is to prevent the proliferation of unstable artifacts that may lead to localized mana-voids or structural collapses. See § 900.1 for general duty to file before casting. See § 400.1 for research permits regarding experimental enchantments. All records of reported enchantments are subject to audit by the Aegis Magi at any time without prior notice. The Guild reserves the right to reclassify any \"common\" enchantment as a \"Material Enchantment\" if it is found to be utilized in a manner inconsistent with public safety or the preservation of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Record Retention",
          "text": "The Mages' Guild Accords Desk shall maintain a rigorous system of record retention to ensure the continuity of magical jurisprudence and the preservation of historical precedent. All filings, including but not limited to research permits, contract notations, and incident reports, shall be preserved for a period of seven years. This timeframe is intended to provide sufficient historical context for the adjudication of ongoing disputes and to ensure that the lineage of magical permissions remains unbroken for the duration of a standard practitioner's career. During this period, records may be accessed by authorized officials, including Archmage Veyra and her appointed deputies, for the purposes of auditing or mediation.\n\nNotwithstanding the standard seven-year rule, certain records may be held indefinitely if they pertain to \"High-Risk\" arcane activities, such as those involving the Forbidden Schools or restricted research. In such cases, the Clerk of the Accords Desk shall determine the retention period based on the enduring nature of the magical threat. For records involving the preservation of the Midlands' geological or ecological integrity, the Guild acknowledges the principle of \"Geological Age.\" Specifically, records pertaining to the shifting of ley lines, the creation of permanent geographic features, or the stabilization of deep-earth rifts may be held for one geological age, or until such time as the Clerk finds the humor in the passage of time to be sufficiently profound to warrant archival rotation.\n\nThe determination of \"funny\" in the context of geological age is at the sole discretion of the presiding Clerk. This serves as a safeguard against the infinite accumulation of data that does not serve a practical administrative purpose, while still honoring the necessity of preserving ancient pacts. Records that have exceeded their retention period but are deemed of significant cultural or historical value may be moved to the deep archives, where they shall remain unless requested by the High Council. The destruction of records prior to the expiration of the seven-year period is strictly prohibited without a signed waiver from the Archmage Sovereign. See § 300.4 for reclassification of records.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "300.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Identify Fees; Who Pays",
          "text": "The administration of the Autumnwood Accords requires the collection of various fees to maintain the infrastructure of the Hall and the salaries of the clerks. These fees include, but are not limited to, filing fees for new enchantments, processing fees for Paradox Trials, and licensing fees for the use of restricted research facilities. It is the established policy of the Accords Desk that the responsibility for the payment of these fees rests solely upon the individual who initiated the request. The Desk shall not be held liable for the procurement of funds, nor shall it provide credit or extensions of payment to mages who lack the necessary capital.\n\nThe Desk is a neutral administrative body and does not function as a lending institution. Therefore, if a mage seeks a permit for a project, a license for a familiar, or a notation for a new contract, that mage is the primary debtor. If a third party provides the funding for such a project, that third party may be designated as the \"Payor of Record,\" but the \"Requesting Party\" remains legally responsible for the completion of the payment. Should a payment fail to materialize, the Desk shall suspend all pending applications and revoke any temporary permissions granted during the application phase. No magic, including the transmutation of currency or the manipulation of debt, shall be used to circumvent these fees.\n\nFurthermore, the Desk shall not be compelled to provide services for free under the guise of \"public interest\" unless a direct decree is issued by Archmage Veyra. While the Guild serves the Midlands, the administrative costs of maintaining the Accords are a burden that must be borne by those who benefit from the protections and privileges of the law. Mages who find the fees to be burdensome are encouraged to seek sponsorship from the Goodstyle Artisans or other licensed commercial entities. The Desk shall not accept Wario Coins as a valid form of tender for any administrative fees, as they are not recognized as legal currency under the Accords. See § 800.4 for duel stakes converted to fees.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "800.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "False Provenance of Relics",
          "text": "The integrity of the magical marketplace and the historical record relies upon the accurate provenance of all artifacts, relics, and enchanted items brought before the Guild for registration or appraisal. A mage who presents a relic for official recognition must provide a verifiable chain of custody. It is a fundamental principle of the Accords that a story is not a chain of title. While the Guild acknowledges the importance of oral tradition and the mystical \"weight\" of a narrative, these elements do not constitute legal evidence of ownership or origin. A compelling tale of a relic's journey through the hands of heroes does not grant the bearer the right to claim it as a sanctioned artifact if the physical evidence of its history is lacking.\n\nMages who knowingly submit false provenance records to the Accords Desk shall be subject to immediate investigation. This includes the fabrication of historical documents, the forging of signatures from deceased masters, or the invention of \"ancient\" origins for newly created items. The Guild seeks to prevent the flooding of the market with fraudulent \"relics\" that could mislead other casters or lead to the misappropriation of significant cultural items. If a relic's history is murky or purely anecdotal, it shall be registered as an \"Artifact of Unknown Origin\" rather than being granted a prestigious designation. The Desk shall not be responsible for correcting the historical record once a false claim has been entered into the ledger.\n\nFurthermore, the provenance of a relic must be sufficient to prove that the item was not acquired through illicit means, such as the theft of Guild property or the violation of the Iron Mandate. While the Accords Desk does not recognize the laws of the Regal Empire or the Iron Legion, it does maintain a policy against the trade of stolen goods. Any item whose provenance is tied to a known crime or a violation of the Accords shall be confiscated. Mages are advised to maintain meticulous records of their acquisitions to ensure that their collections remain in good standing. See § 700.1 for evidence generally. See § 300.5 for research copies of quiet items.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "700.1",
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.2 Reporting of Material Enchantments",
          "text": "Material enchantments, as defined herein to include but not limited to warded items, inscribed artifacts, and enchanted constructs, shall be reported by the caster within seven days of completion or acquisition, whichever applies first. Failure to file such reports constitutes a Class C violation subject to fines up to one hundred gold crowns per day until compliance is achieved. See § 100.2 regarding authorized representatives for Guild filings.\n\nThe reporting form prescribed under § 900.5 must be submitted in triplicate, with the original retained by the Clerk of the Desk and copies distributed to relevant regulatory bodies as directed by current policy memoranda. All forms shall bear a unique tracking number assigned upon receipt, which must be referenced on any subsequent communications concerning said item. See §§ 1.7, 300.4 for instructions on form completion and data protection requirements.\n\nRecords of all reported material enchantments shall be maintained in the Central Registry accessible only to duly authorized personnel under strict confidentiality protocols outlined in § 1.9. Access requests must be submitted via official channels with justification documented in writing; approval rests solely with Archmage Veyra or her designee. Unauthorized viewing or dissemination of Registry data is punishable as grand larceny plus additional penalties under the Accords.\n\nFees associated with the reporting process are calculated according to the complexity and power rating of each enchantment, as determined by certified appraisers appointed by the Guild. These fees offset administrative costs incurred during verification, storage, and risk assessment procedures. Payment must be rendered within ten business days of invoice issuance; late payments incur monthly interest charges at eight percent above prevailing market rates for magical services. See § 200.6 regarding fee waivers for indigent practitioners under exceptional circumstances only.\n\nFalse statements or omissions material to the reporting requirement shall result in immediate revocation of licensure and referral to the Disciplinary Tribunal for possible suspension or expulsion from membership entirely. Repeat offenders may face permanent disqualification from practicing magic within Guild jurisdiction indefinitely. See §§ 600.1, 800.1 for further disciplinary guidelines applicable to this offense category specifically.\n\nAll reports filed under this section become public record unless classified restrictions apply pursuant to § 900.3. Interested parties may petition the Desk for access subject to redaction of sensitive information where appropriate. Petitioners must demonstrate a legitimate need-to-know basis supported by sworn affidavit attesting to professional relevance and absence of malicious intent. Approval rates typically range between sixty and eighty percent depending on demonstrated necessity versus potential harm considerations weighed case-by-case.\n\nThis provision ensures transparency while protecting proprietary knowledge held within the Guild community. Compliance facilitates safe coexistence among diverse magical practitioners operating in shared spaces under this legal framework governing arcane arts throughout Autumnwood region exclusively. Breach thereof undermines public trust essential to maintaining orderly function of all regulated activities involving supernatural capabilities whatsoever.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.2",
            "900.5",
            "1.7",
            "1.9",
            "200.6",
            "600.1",
            "900.3",
            "900.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Certification of Origin and Temporal Provenance for Trans-Dimensional Artifacts",
          "text": "The Accords Desk maintains a strict distinction between locally sourced enchantments and those originating from extradimensional realities, necessitating a rigorous protocol for the certification of origin prior to any administrative filing. Under § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting, all significant alterations to reality must be documented before initiation; however, this requirement extends post-casting for artifacts that have traversed dimensional barriers or emerged from temporal anomalies such as those encountered during The Shroob Temporal Incursion. Any practitioner bringing a relic into the Midlands jurisdiction is obligated to declare its provenance, distinguishing whether the item was crafted within the established weave or imported via high-fidelity transmutation techniques. This declaration is not merely administrative but serves as a critical safety measure, particularly given the volatile nature of entities like the Chaos Heart Crisis where intentional destruction orchestrated by external forces can mimic natural disasters. Failure to distinguish between indigenous magical constructs and those of foreign origin renders the item subject to immediate quarantine under § 1301.2 Penalties; Sunrise Accrual, as the Desk cannot verify the stability of an artifact whose foundational laws may be incompatible with local reality.\n\nThe certification process requires the submission of a provenance attestation that details the caster's original location, the specific temporal coordinates of creation if known, and any prior legal ownership disputes such as those arising from The Neighbor Incident or Warioland dissolution. This is particularly pertinent when dealing with gifts that later move, as stipulated in § 1101.9 Gifts That Later Move, which mandates that holders of magical artifacts must maintain records of their movement across dimensional planes. The Desk shall review these documents to ensure that the artifact has not been involved in incidents comparable to The Grove of Woe Incident or The Hag of Ferngrove Manor, where shapeshifting and identity disputes complicated the legal standing of the involved parties. If an artifact is claimed by a duplicate entity under § 700.5 Clone as Duplicate Claimant, the certification must explicitly identify the original caster to prevent fraudulent claims on relics that have been replicated through cellular replication or illusionary weaving.\n\nFurthermore, the fees associated with this certification are calculated according to § 1301.1 Schedule of Fees, which establishes a definitive rate for investigative and regulatory actions performed by the Accords Desk regarding trans-dimensional imports. These fees are not negotiable and must be paid in full before the artifact is released from quarantine, ensuring that the continued maintenance of the Midlands infrastructure remains uncompromised. Any attempt to bypass these fees or submit incomplete provenance data will result in the accrual of punitive measures based on temporal progression, effectively penalizing the practitioner for every sunrise that passes since the initial violation was discovered. This strict adherence to procedural justice is designed to prevent the kind of liability issues seen during The Ravencreek Transition, where the presence of stolen Gold Dragon eggs without proper documentation created an unmanageable legal burden for the entire expedition.\n\nIn cases where the origin of an artifact cannot be verified due to memory loss or the effects of exhaustion similar to those exhibited by Archie Miser in his return to the Shadow Estate, the Accords Desk shall default to a presumption of high-risk provenance until cleared otherwise. This precautionary measure aligns with § 900.9 See Also Provisions, which requires all parties engaging in licensed magical practice to comply with standards referenced thereto, including those regarding the stability of the weave. Practitioners must be aware that the Desk reserves the right to invoke the Paradox Trial under its penal system if an artifact's provenance suggests involvement in forbidden schools or intentional reality warping akin to the disaster scenarios at Estragon Island or the Estragon Island Incident. Ultimately, the integrity of the legal framework governing magical items depends on the transparency of their origins, ensuring that no entity, whether a mermaid named Herniva or a one-armed Shadowfell copy, can exploit ambiguities in the law to undermine the safety of the Midlands.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "1301.2",
            "1101.9",
            "700.5",
            "1301.1",
            "900.9"
          ]
        }
      ],
