    {
      "cite": "900.3",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Record Retention",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Seven years or one geological age, whichever the clerk finds funnier.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Record Retention",
          "text": "The Archival Integrity of the Autumnwood Accords dictates that the preservation of magical records is paramount to the stability of the Midlands. All filings, including but not limited to research permits, Paradox Trial transcripts, and unauthorized field use reports, shall be maintained in the permanent registry of the Mages' Guild. The duration of such retention is governed by the principle of Perpetual Continuity; specifically, records shall be preserved for a period of seven years or one geological age, whichever the clerk finds funnier. This clause serves to acknowledge the inherent fluidity of temporal magic and the unpredictable nature of arcane decay. While a standard administrative cycle may suggest a fixed term, the Clerk of the Accords reserves the sole prerogative to determine the \"humorous\" expiration of a record based on its thematic relevance to the Guild's ongoing history.\n\nIn instances where a record pertains to a Paradox Trial, the retention period may be extended indefinitely if the resulting pocket-prison remains active, as the existence of the prison constitutes a continuous event. Conversely, records pertaining to minor infractions or the mundane activities of the Goodstyle Artisans may be subject to expedited purging should the Clerk deem the aesthetic of the archive to be cluttered. It is strictly forbidden for any mage, including those of the Innovator faction, to request the destruction of a record to obscure past transgressions. Any attempt to influence the Clerk’s sense of humor regarding retention periods shall be viewed as an act of administrative subversion. For matters regarding the specific handling of restricted research copies, see § 300.5. All records must be inscribed in a medium resistant to standard transmutation or erosion.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Identify Fees; Who Pays",
          "text": "The financial obligations arising from the administration of the Autumnwood Accords are governed by the Principle of Origin. It is the established mandate of the Mages' Guild that the costs associated with the filing, mediation, and enforcement of these laws shall be borne by the person who asked. This means that any mage seeking a research permit under § 400.1, requesting a Paradox Trial, or seeking mediation from Archmage Veyra is solely responsible for the procurement of necessary fees. The Desk, as a neutral administrative body, shall not be held liable for the costs of magical auditing, archival storage, or the logistical requirements of the Aegis Magi.\n\nNo fee shall be levied against the Guild's general treasury for the processing of standard requests, nor shall any fee be diverted to the Innovators or Conservators for the purpose of political lobbying. If a request involves a third party, such as a merchant from the Regal Empire or a representative of the Silver Flame, the requesting mage must provide a signed guarantee of payment from said party before the Desk will initiate the filing. The Desk shall not provide credit, nor shall it accept Wario Coins as a valid form of tender, as such currency is explicitly barred from Guild circulation. If a mage finds themselves unable to produce the required funds, the request shall be returned without action. For more information on the conversion of duel stakes to administrative fees, see § 800.4.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "800.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "False Provenance of Relics",
          "text": "The authentication of magical artifacts and relics within the Midlands is subject to the Doctrine of Verifiable Lineage. A story, regardless of its narrative complexity, evocative power, or historical weight, does not constitute a chain of title. The Mages' Guild explicitly rejects \"mythic provenance\" as a valid legal basis for the ownership or authorized use of artifacts. To establish a valid chain of title, a relic must possess a documented history of physical transfer, verified by the signatures of previous owners or the seals of recognized authorities such as the Conservators. A tale of a relic being \"gifted by a dying star\" or \"forged in the fires of the first rift\" is considered a literary achievement but holds no weight in the eyes of the Accords.\n\nMages who attempt to register a relic based solely on oral tradition or \"inherited legend\" shall be flagged for a secondary audit. If the provenance is found to be fabricated or intentionally misleading to circumvent restrictions on the Unsanctioned Arts, the offending mage shall be subject to the Paradox Trial as outlined in § 800.2. The Desk shall not recognize any artifact whose history is obscured by the \"Quiet List\" protocols unless a formal waiver is granted by Archmage Veyra. Furthermore, any relic claiming to be a component of the Heartstone or a product of the Great Rift must be surveyed by a Guild-approved cartographer before a provenance can even be considered for preliminary filing. For matters regarding the scrying of closed books to determine true origins, see § 700.2.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "800.2",
            "700.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.3 Record Retention and Fees",
          "text": "The preservation of official records is paramount to the integrity of the Guild's regulatory functions, yet the sheer volume of filings generated by even a single day's operations renders total archival impractical without prohibitive cost. Consequently, this section establishes a framework for systematic retention and disposal of documents deemed less critical than others in the public interest. All records relating to licensure examinations, disciplinary hearings, and major enforcement actions shall remain in permanent storage under strict chain-of-custody protocols. Routine correspondence, preliminary drafts, and other ephemera may be reduced to digital form or destroyed after a reasonable interval provided no legal claim has been filed against such material during that time.\n\nArchives maintained by the Autumnwood Accords Desk shall be accessible upon request by any person holding valid credentials from an accredited institution of higher learning. Researchers must submit written inquiries specifying the nature and purpose of their investigation along with proof of affiliation or employment. Access to confidential files concerning individual mages is restricted solely to authorized investigators pursuing active cases approved by the High Council. The Desk shall charge nominal fees for photocopying, scanning, or certifying documents beyond basic consultation privileges. These charges are calculated at current market rates and paid directly to the Guild Treasury in Wario Coin or equivalent lawful currency. Failure to settle outstanding balances may result in suspension of access rights until compliance is achieved.\n\nIn rare instances where original artifacts possess significant historical or scholarly value, the Desk may issue certified copies bearing official seals rather than transferring possession outright. Such replicas retain full evidentiary weight for all legal purposes while preserving the integrity and provenance of genuine items. Requests for duplication must be accompanied by a detailed justification demonstrating why reproduction alone suffices for the intended end use. The Council reserves discretion to deny applications deemed frivolous or contrary to public policy considerations weighing in favor of preservation over accessibility. Any party dissatisfied with such determinations may petition for review within thirty days following written notice of decision.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "900.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.3 Record Retention and Fees — Continued",
          "text": "The Guild maintains permanent records of all filings, trials, inspections, and enforcement actions as required by this Ordinance. These official archives are the sole authoritative source for determining compliance with the Accords, superseding conflicting private documentation. Records shall be retained for a minimum duration of seven years or one geological age, whichever term proves more amenable to clerical management. Fees assessed for accessing these files vary based on volume and complexity; full tariff schedules appear in Schedule D attached hereto.\n\nAll casters must provide accurate provenance information regarding enchanted items submitted for review. Misrepresentation of origin constitutes a material defect warranting immediate rejection of the filing. The Desk retains discretion to impose additional scrutiny where ambiguity exists, potentially escalating costs to the applicant. False claims of heritage may result in sanctions up to and including permanent revocation of licensure, at the sole discretion of Archmage Veyra or her delegate.\n\nIn matters involving disputed ownership of magical artifacts, the Guild shall act as neutral arbiter solely upon submission of sworn affidavits from all claimants. Such proceedings are subject to standard filing fees plus a premium charge reflective of expedited processing requirements. Parties must exhaust administrative remedies before seeking external recourse for alleged irregularities in the evaluation process. Unauthorized attempts to influence official determinations through bribery or intimidation will be prosecuted under applicable penal statutes with exemplary severity.\n\nThe integrity of Guild records is paramount to maintaining public trust and ensuring uniform application of the Accords across all jurisdictions. Every clerk must adhere strictly to chain-of-custody protocols when handling physical evidence or digital data. Failure to follow prescribed procedures may render findings inadmissible in subsequent hearings, necessitating costly remand for reevaluation. Consequently, practitioners are advised to retain independent counsel versed in arcane jurisprudence should they anticipate challenges to the official ruling.\n\nThis provision balances the need for historical preservation with practical considerations of storage capacity and fiscal responsibility. The extended retention period accounts for the enduring nature of magical effects and their potential relevance long after initial registration. Conversely, limits on access fees prevent abuse of the system by vexatious litigants seeking to harass competitors through frivolous inquiries. Ultimately, these guidelines establish a robust framework for managing the immense volume of filings generated annually while safeguarding both individual rights and collective interests.\n\nsee § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks; § 900.5 Fees Schedule; § 800.4 Duel Stakes Converted to Fees; § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "900.5",
            "800.4",
            "300.2",
            "900.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Documentation and Reporting Protocols for Anomalies",
          "text": "In the conduct of their work, members of the Mages' Guild are required to maintain detailed records of any anomalies encountered or manipulated during field operations. These records serve multiple critical functions: ensuring transparency, facilitating the sharing of knowledge across the Guild, and providing a basis upon which future actions can be taken in accordance with the Codex. Proper documentation is not merely a formality but a duty owed by all Persons Bound to the Mages' Guild.\n\nThe obligation to report anomalies begins at the moment an anomaly is first noted or engaged. This includes the recording of observations, initial assessments, and any preliminary data collected during its encounter. It is incumbent upon each Person Bound to ensure that these records are thorough, accurate, and timely. Under § 500.1 Conduct Outside Guild Property, it shall be understood that field operations undertaken in unaligned or unsanctioned locations fall within the purview of this Codex. Thus, any damage or anomaly encountered during such activities must also be reported accordingly.\n\nThe manner of reporting is specified under § 900.3 Record Retention and Fees—Continued. Each entry should include a description of the anomaly, its location, date and time of discovery, and the names of all Persons Bound involved in the encounter. If an anomaly has been neutralized or contained, the method used should also be documented along with any residual effects observed. For those anomalies that are not immediately resolved, periodic updates must be submitted at intervals not exceeding thirty days.\n\nFees associated with the documentation process shall be covered by the Guild under § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters. However, any additional expenses incurred due to extraordinary circumstances—such as the need for specialized equipment or prolonged containment procedures—shall be subject to prior approval and are ultimately borne by the individual who initiated the field operation.\n\nThe importance of accurate record-keeping cannot be overstated. Under § 800.1 Discipline, Range of Sanctions, any failure to comply with these reporting protocols shall be taken into account in determining appropriate sanctions. The Codex recognizes that breaches may range from minor lapses in documentation accuracy to more serious offenses such as falsifying records or concealing the existence of an anomaly altogether.\n\nFurthermore, under § 800.2 Paradox Trial Procedure, it is emphasized that incomplete or inaccurate records can lead to a case being reopened for further investigation. This not only underscores the necessity for meticulous record-keeping but also highlights the potential ramifications of non-compliance. The Paradox Trial acts as both an enforcement mechanism and a deterrent against any misconduct.\n\nIn instances where an anomaly is later determined to have originated from a source other than those initially reported, such as an unauthorized field use or a breach in containment procedures, additional records must be submitted under § 900.2 Reporting of Material Enchantments. These documents should detail the circumstances leading to the discovery and any actions taken subsequent to the initial report.\n\nThe Mages' Guild places great value on the integrity of its operations. Thus, all Persons Bound are reminded that adherence to these documentation protocols is not only a requirement under this Codex but also a reflection of their commitment to maintaining the Guild's reputation for excellence and reliability. By doing so, they contribute to the collective knowledge base of the Mages' Guild and uphold the highest standards of professionalism and integrity.\n\nFailure to comply with these requirements may result in disciplinary action, which can range from verbal warnings to more severe sanctions as outlined under § 800.1 Discipline. The goal is not merely to penalize but also to foster a culture of accountability and responsibility within the Guild. By emphasizing the importance of accurate documentation and timely reporting, the Mages' Guild seeks to ensure that all operations are conducted with the utmost care and precision.\n\nIn conclusion, the Codex mandates strict adherence to documentation protocols for any anomaly encountered or manipulated during field operations. This requirement is essential not only for maintaining transparency but also for ensuring the safety and efficacy of future operations. By doing so, the Mages' Guild upholds its commitment to excellence, reliability, and integrity in all its activities.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "500.1",
            "900.3",
            "600.1",
            "800.1",
            "800.2",
            "900.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
