    {
      "cite": "900.4",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Identify Fees; Who Pays",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "The person who asked. Not the Desk.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Identify Fees; Who Pays",
          "text": "In the matter of identification, verification, and administrative processing of magical artifacts, relics, or items of significant arcane density, the Guild shall establish a clear distinction between the act of identification and the liability for the associated costs. Any mage, initiate, or contracted agent seeking to have an object cataloged, verified for safety, or assessed for its potential violation of the Autumnwood Accords shall be deemed the primary petitioner. Consequently, all fees associated with the identification of such items shall be the sole responsibility of the individual or entity who initiated the request. The Accords Desk serves as a neutral administrative body; it does not provide identification services as a public utility, nor shall it ever be held liable for the costs incurred during the processing of a petitioner's property.\n\nShould a petitioner claim that the identification of an item was performed as a matter of Guild necessity—for instance, if an item was seized by the Aegis Magi for immediate containment—the petitioner shall still be held liable for the administrative fees unless a formal waiver is granted by Archmage Veyra. In cases where multiple parties claim ownership of a single relic, the Desk shall not adjudicate the underlying title; it shall instead require a deposit from all claiming parties to cover the identification costs, with the fees being refunded only upon the final resolution of the ownership dispute. The Desk is strictly prohibited from utilizing Guild treasury funds to offset the costs of private identifications.\n\nFurthermore, any mage who attempts to have the Accords Desk assume the role of a debt collector for unpaid identification fees shall be found in violation of administrative protocol. If a petitioner fails to provide the requisite tender, the Desk shall cease all work immediately and return the item to the petitioner in its current state, with no further liability on the part of the Guild. The Desk shall not be compelled to provide identification for items of unknown origin if the petitioner refuses to provide a bond against the risk of arcane leakage during the inspection process. See § 900.4.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "False Provenance of Relics",
          "text": "The Guild recognizes that the history of a magical artifact is often obscured by the passage of centuries, the erosion of memory, or the deliberate obfuscation of its previous owners. However, the Accords Desk shall maintain a strict distinction between a \"narrative of origin\" and a \"legal chain of title.\" A story, regardless of its poetic depth, historical resonance, or the prestige of its supposed author, does not constitute a valid legal conveyance of ownership or a certification of the item’s provenance. Any mage attempting to register a relic based solely on oral tradition, transcribed legends, or \"inherited\" myths without corroborating evidence shall find their application for registration suspended until such time as a verifiable audit of the item's movement can be produced.\n\nThe Guild shall not accept \"mythic provenance\" as a substitute for legal clearance. For example, an item described as \"the blade of a fallen king\" provides no more legal standing than a common iron rod if the Guild cannot trace the transfer of the blade from the kingdom to the current holder. Mages seeking to register relics of high arcane potency must provide a documented history of ownership that satisfies the requirements of the Conservators. If a relic’s history is broken or lost, it must be declared \"Orphaned\" and subjected to the standard quarantine procedures before any identification can occur. The Desk shall not be held responsible for the validity of a petitioner’s claims; it is the petitioner’s burden to prove that the item was not stolen, cursed, or illegally extracted from a protected site.\n\nFurthermore, any deliberate attempt to provide a false provenance to circumvent the restrictions placed upon certain schools of magic—specifically those categorized as \"Forbidden\" by the Conservators—shall be treated as a felony under the Accords. If a mage knowingly presents a fabricated history to gain access to restricted research facilities or to bypass the Innovators' oversight, they shall be subject to the Paradox Trial. The Guild's records are a matter of law, not a library of fiction. A story is not a chain of title, and the Desk shall not permit the romanticization of history to supersede the rigorous demands of magical safety and legal clarity. See § 300.5.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.5 False Provenance and Attribution of Magic Items",
          "text": "It is hereby established that the false attribution of magical provenance to an item shall constitute prima facie evidence of fraudulent intent, absent contrary proof by clear and convincing testimony. This standard applies regardless of whether the misattribution was intentional or merely negligent on the part of the caster or seller. The rationale underlying this provision rests upon the understanding that magical items possess intrinsic histories which can significantly impact their power levels and potential for dangerous side effects. Misrepresenting an object's origins may lead to catastrophic misuse by unprepared users, causing irreparable harm to individuals and property alike.\n\nUnder § 900.5, any party knowingly presenting a magically enhanced item with fabricated or misleading background information shall be subject to immediate revocation of their licensure and a fine no less than one hundred gold crowns per violation. Repeated offenses may result in permanent disqualification from the practice of magic within the Jurisdiction. Furthermore, such fraudulent conduct may trigger an investigation into whether additional items in the perpetrator's possession are similarly tainted by deception or illegal manufacture. The burden shall rest upon the accused to demonstrate that any inaccuracies arose from honest error rather than deliberate deceit.\n\nThis provision does not preclude legitimate scholarly inquiry into magical artifacts, provided such investigations are conducted under the strict supervision of authorized personnel and comply with all applicable safety protocols. Researchers seeking information regarding an item's origins must submit formal petitions outlining their purpose and methods to the Mages' Guild for approval prior to commencing any examination. Unauthorized attempts to uncover secrets through illicit means will be met with swift and severe punishment, in accordance with § 1500 et seq.\n\nThe Desk reserves discretion to modify penalties based on mitigating circumstances, such as confession before discovery of fraud or restitution made voluntarily by the offender. Conversely, aggravating factors like prior convictions for similar offenses may lead to enhanced sanctions beyond those explicitly enumerated herein. The ultimate goal remains maintaining public safety while fostering an environment conducive to responsible magical advancement. All persons are urged to familiarize themselves fully with these obligations to avoid inadvertent violation and consequent liability under applicable law.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "900.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.5 Attribution of Magic Items and Provenance Claims",
          "text": "§ 900.5 Attribution of Magic Items; Burden of Proof for Historical Relics. It shall be unlawful to falsely attribute a magic item to a known historical figure or event without clear evidence, as such misrepresentation may artificially inflate market value and encourage fraud. Any caster seeking to sell an artifact claiming ancient lineage must provide credible documentation of provenance to the Desk prior to transaction. Failure to do so renders the sale void and subject to confiscation by Aegis Magi upon reasonable suspicion of deception. This provision aims to protect consumers from overpaying for counterfeit relics while maintaining transparency in the magical economy under § 100.6.\n\nUnder no circumstances shall a magic item's true origin be concealed or misrepresented, whether through forged certificates, altered inscriptions, or outright fabrication. Violations constitute a Class B misdemeanor punishable by fines up to one thousand gold pieces and mandatory community service at the discretion of the local judge. Repeat offenders may face additional penalties including temporary suspension of their magical license per § 200.4. The burden of proof rests entirely on the seller to establish authenticity beyond reasonable doubt, adhering to standards set forth in applicable civil procedure codes outside this Codex.\n\nThe Desk reserves the right to inspect any disputed item at its own expense if a credible allegation is made against it. Such examinations must be conducted impartially by certified appraisers approved by the Guild, whose findings shall be binding unless contested within ten business days. Parties dissatisfied with initial determinations may appeal directly to Archmage Veyra for final adjudication under § 100.2. All fees associated with these review processes shall be paid upfront by the complaining party, reimbursable only if the allegation is substantiated. This framework ensures fairness and efficiency in resolving disputes while safeguarding public confidence in magical goods.\n\nFalse attribution not only harms individual traders but also undermines the integrity of the entire magical marketplace. By establishing strict guidelines for provenance claims, this section seeks to preserve ethical standards and prevent exploitation by unscrupulous actors. Compliance with these provisions is essential for maintaining trust between buyers and sellers alike, fostering a thriving environment where innovation can flourish alongside tradition. All casters are expected to uphold these principles diligently in their professional endeavors.\n\nThe Desk further clarifies that this rule applies equally to both domestic and international transactions involving magic items originating from any region within or beyond the Accords' jurisdiction. Nothing herein shall be construed to limit existing laws regarding intellectual property rights, trademarks, or consumer protection statutes enacted by sovereign governments elsewhere. Rather, these regulations complement such frameworks to create a comprehensive system governing magical commerce globally. Casters are advised to familiarize themselves with all relevant legal requirements before engaging in any commercial activity related to enchanted artifacts.\n\nIn summary, § 900.5 mandates truthfulness regarding the origins and history of magic items traded within the Guild's purview. Sellers bear the responsibility of verifying and disclosing accurate information, facing appropriate consequences for violations. Through diligent enforcement of these guidelines, the Autumnwood Accords Desk strives to cultivate a fair and reliable marketplace where honest dealing prevails over deceitful practices. All members are urged to adhere strictly to this standard in their daily operations.\n\nsee § 100.2 Authority of the Archmage; see § 200.4 Suspension Procedures; see § 300.3 Quiet List Filing Requirements; see § 700.1 General Evidence Standards",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "900.5",
            "100.6",
            "200.4",
            "100.2",
            "300.3",
            "700.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.4 Fees for Identification Services and Allocation of Costs",
          "text": "§ 900.4 Identify Fees; Who Pays. Any caster providing identification services under this section shall charge a reasonable fee to compensate for time spent, risk assumed, and expertise applied. Such fees shall be agreed upon in advance between the parties or determined by an independent appraiser if disputed. The client engaging the caster's services bears full responsibility for paying said fees promptly upon completion of work. Failure to do so may result in termination of the agreement without refund and potential legal action for breach of contract. No portion of any fee collected shall be subject to seizure under the Iron Mandate or similar extraneous statutes unless expressly authorized by this Codex. All costs associated with identifying an item's magical properties remain solely the responsibility of the requesting party, regardless of outcome or success rate achieved during examination. The Guild Desk reserves no right to recoup such expenses from general funds nor shall it accept payment in kind except as otherwise provided herein. Clients are advised that fees may vary significantly based on complexity of analysis required and rarity of materials involved in the item being appraised. Written estimates must be furnished prior to commencement of services whenever practicable to avoid misunderstandings regarding final charges incurred throughout duration of engagement period established between provider and consumer thereof. Disputes arising over quantum or quality of service rendered shall be resolved through binding arbitration administered by designated magisters appointed annually by vote of active members in good standing within relevant jurisdictional boundaries applicable at time dispute arises under these provisions set forth above herein.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "900.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.4 Identification Fees and Allocation of Costs",
          "text": "All requests for identification services submitted under these Accords shall be subject to an initial consultation fee payable in advance by the submitting party. This fee, set at no less than five gold coins per item or one hundred gold coins per batch thereof, serves as a non-refundable administrative charge covering the Desk's preliminary assessment of potential hazards and logistical requirements. Should further extensive analysis prove necessary, additional costs shall accrue pro-rata based on complexity and duration, billed upon completion of said services. Refunds are granted only in cases where no substantive work was performed due to client error or withdrawal prior to commencement of actual evaluation.\n\nPayment obligations extend beyond mere transactional exchanges; they embody a broader commitment to responsible stewardship within the arcane community. By electing to engage Guild expertise, clients implicitly acknowledge their duty to share in the burdens attendant thereto, whether financial or otherwise. This principle undergirds not only fee structures but also liability frameworks governing subsequent interactions between parties. Failure to honor payment terms may result in immediate suspension of all further services rendered until outstanding balances are satisfied in full, plus applicable penalties as prescribed by law.\n\nIn matters involving disputed provenance claims regarding magical artifacts, the burden of proof rests squarely upon the asserting party to establish credibility through corroborating evidence and expert testimony where feasible. Mere assertions lacking substantiation carry no weight whatsoever under these Accords, regardless of persuasive rhetoric employed to bolster such positions. The Desk reserves absolute discretion to reject outright any submissions deemed frivolous or intended solely for gain without legitimate purpose, thereby preserving precious resources for bona fide inquiries requiring genuine attention.\n\nUltimately, the equitable distribution of costs reflects a fundamental truth: magic remains an inherently dangerous force demanding careful handling from all who seek its power. Whether through formal channels established herein or informal arrangements made outside thereof, every participant bears responsibility for mitigating risks associated with their actions. Only by embracing this shared accountability can we hope to foster a safer environment wherein both innovation and tradition may flourish harmoniously under the watchful gaze of Archmage Veyra herself.\"",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "7de81b694257f1d4",
