    {
      "cite": "900.5",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "False Provenance of Relics",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A story is not a chain of title.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "False Provenance of Relics",
          "text": "Any individual, entity, or faction seeking to introduce a relic, artifact, or enchanted object into the public sphere, the Guild’s archives, or any sanctioned marketplace under the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords shall be held strictly liable for the veracity of the item’s provenance. The Accords Desk hereby establishes that the mere existence of a narrative surrounding an object does not constitute a legal chain of title. A story, regardless of its antiquity, poetic resonance, or perceived historical weight, is not a substitute for documented evidence of lawful acquisition or ancestral right. Practitioners who attempt to bypass the rigorous standards of § 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title by weaving elaborate tapestries of mythic origin are subject to immediate investigation by the Aegis Magi.\n\nThe Guild recognizes that the \"mystique\" of an object often serves as a veil for illicit trafficking or the misappropriation of cultural heritage. Therefore, any claim of provenance based solely on oral tradition, \"found-item\" anecdotes, or \"ancestral memory\" without corroborating physical evidence of transition shall be deemed insufficient for the purposes of legal registration. To prevent the laundering of stolen goods through the manufacture of convenient legends, the Accords Desk requires a verifiable paper trail—or its magical equivalent—detailing the movement of the object from its point of origin to the current holder. If the lineage of an object is broken by a period of \"lost history,\" the burden of proof remains on the claimant to demonstrate that the acquisition was not performed through coercion, theft, or violation of previous sovereign treaties.\n\nFurthermore, the intentional fabrication of a false provenance is classified as a Grade III Arcane Fraud. Any mage or merchant found to have knowingly authored a fictitious history for a relic to increase its market value, influence political standing, or obscure its dangerous nature shall face immediate referral to the Paradox Trial for adjudication. During such proceedings, the court shall weigh the discrepancy between the \"story\" provided and the actual magical signature of the item. If the magical resonance of the relic contradicts the stated history—for instance, if an object claimed to be a gift of peace displays a signature of violent subjugation—the provenance shall be struck from the record, and the item may be seized as contraband.\n\nIn instances where an object’s history is genuinely unknowable due to the destruction of records, the owner must register the item as \"Unverified Origin.\" Such items may be held privately but are prohibited from being sold, gifted, or traded within the Midlands without a specific waiver from the Archmage Sovereign. The Accords Desk shall not be misled by the eloquence of a scholar or the passion of a collector; a story is a matter of art, while a chain of title is a matter of law. For further guidance on the required documentation for relic registration, see § 1101.2. For matters regarding the preservation of existing records, see § 900.3.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.2",
            "900.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.6 Relic Authentication and Appraisal Standards",
          "text": "The Guild recognizes the value of historical magical artifacts, herein termed \"relics,\" in establishing precedent for dispute resolution. To ensure integrity in relic valuation, all appraisals shall be conducted by certified Guild assessors utilizing standardized testing protocols outlined in Exhibit B-14. False certification or tampering with a relic's provenance constitutes forgery under § 601 and may result in revocation of licensure pursuant to § 200.4(d). Assessors must disclose any conflicts of interest per § 100.5 prior to commencing evaluation services. Relics seized during inspections shall be held in secure vaults until disposition is finalized by the Desk. Parties contesting an appraisal may request a second opinion at their own expense within thirty days of initial assessment results. Failure to comply with these authentication requirements renders relics inadmissible as evidence unless corroborated by independent testimony meeting evidentiary standards set forth in § 700.1(c). The Guild reserves the right to re-evaluate relic valuations periodically based on new information or technological advancements affecting appraisal methodologies. All relic transactions must be registered with the Guild within ten business days of completion to maintain accurate records for future reference and regulatory oversight purposes as mandated by this section and related provisions herein.\n\n\"Relic authentication requires expertise beyond ordinary knowledge. Certified assessors exist precisely to prevent fraud through rigorous testing procedures. Any party seeking to challenge an appraisal bears the burden of proof and associated costs.\" - Archmage Theron, Conservator Council\n\n\"The provenance of magical artifacts is sacred ground. Tampering with such items violates both letter and spirit of our Accords. We must remain vigilant guardians against deception.\" - Janna Brightspark, Innovator Representative\n\n\"Standardized protocols ensure fairness across all relic evaluations. Transparency in appraisal processes builds trust within the Guild community.\" - Senior Clerk Elara Vane, Autumnwood Accords Desk\n\n*See also: § 300.2 Prohibited Practices Regarding Antiquities; § 600.1 Contractual Obligations Relating to Historical Items*",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "200.4",
            "100.5",
            "700.1",
            "300.2",
            "600.1",
            "900.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.5 False Provenance of Relics; § 900.6 Relic Authentication and Appraisal S",
          "text": "No caster shall knowingly misrepresent the origin, age, or magical potency of an item for commercial or fraudulent purposes, such representations constituting a material breach of contract under these Accords. See § 1.4 (Persons Bound) and § 300.5 (Research Copies of Quiet Items). Any party discovering such falsification must immediately notify the nearest Guild Office in accordance with § 900.1 (Duty to File Before Casting), failing which they incur liability for all resulting damages.\n\nAll disputed artifacts shall be submitted for independent appraisal by two certified Relic Appraisers appointed by the Archmage Sovereign, their findings binding unless successfully challenged within thirty days under oath before the Desk. The costs of such verification shall be borne equally between disputing parties until one party prevails, at which point reimbursement follows standard civil procedure. See § 700.1 (Evidence Generally) and § 900.4 (Identify Fees).\n\nRelics deemed counterfeit or fraudulently marketed shall be seized pursuant to § 800.1 (Discipline; Range of Sanctions) and destroyed after judicial review, unless their value exceeds one hundred gold crowns, in which case they shall be sold at public auction with proceeds distributed to the Guild’s General Fund for future research initiatives. See § 900.2 (Reporting of Material Enchantments).\n\nThe Guild retains sole authority to authenticate relics and determine their true nature, rejecting all claims by unauthorized bodies or individuals. This includes but is not limited to self-proclaimed historians, connoisseurs, or relic hunters lacking formal certification. Violation of this clause constitutes grounds for immediate suspension of licensure under § 200.4 (Suspension and Lapse). See also § 100.2 (Who May Speak for the Guild).\n\nAppraisal fees shall be assessed at a flat rate of fifty crowns per item, payable upon filing with the Appraisers’ Bureau. Additional charges apply for complex artifacts requiring extended examination or specialized equipment. Payment must be rendered in Wario Coin or equivalent lawful tender; foreign currencies require conversion at current market rates plus a five percent premium to cover exchange risk. See § 100.6 (Delegation to Clerks) and § 200.5 (Guest Casters).\n\nParties aggrieved by Appraisal findings may petition for rehearing within ten days, providing new evidence or testimony relevant to authenticity determinations. Such motions shall be heard expeditiously by a special tribunal convened solely for this purpose, with final rulings issued no later than sixty days from initial submission. See § 700.2 (Scrying of Closed Books) and § 800.3 (Seal of Silence; Duration and Review).\n\nRelic Authentication and Appraisal Standards shall be promulgated annually by the Archmage Sovereign in consultation with the Conservator Council and Innovator Representatives, ensuring alignment with evolving magical theory and technological advancement. These standards carry the force of law under these Accords and must be strictly enforced to maintain public trust in Guild oversight mechanisms. See § 100.5 (Conflict of Interest in Casting) and § 900.3 (Record Retention).\n\nThis comprehensive framework governs relic authentication, fraud prevention, and dispute resolution, reflecting the Guild’s unwavering commitment to integrity in all matters pertaining to magical artifacts. By adhering thereto, casters safeguard both their own interests and those of the broader community dependent upon responsible stewardship of arcane treasures. For further guidance on specific scenarios not addressed herein, consult the official commentaries maintained by the Autumnwood Accords Desk or seek counsel from licensed legal practitioners specializing in Artificer Law. See § 100.7 (Foreign Halls and Courtesy) and § 200.6 (Constructs That Hold a Quill).",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "300.5",
            "900.1",
            "700.1",
            "900.4",
            "800.1",
