          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.5 False Provenance of Relics and Related Deceptions",
          "text": "It is forbidden to misrepresent the origin, authorship, or historical significance of any magical artifact in a manner that induces another into purchasing it under false pretenses. Such acts constitute fraud and undermine public trust in the antiquities market. Violators shall be subject to penalties commensurate with the degree of harm caused.\n\nThis provision addresses the deliberate fabrication of provenance narratives designed to inflate an item's perceived value or desirability. Examples include falsely attributing a spellbook to a legendary archmage, claiming a wand was wielded by a historical hero, or asserting that a trinket contains residual enchantments from its original creator where none exist. The intent is typically financial gain through deception rather than mere exaggeration of facts.\n\nIn cases where the misrepresentation results in material loss to an unsuspecting buyer, restitution shall be ordered along with punitive sanctions. Where no direct victim can be identified, a fine payable to the Guild fund will apply. Persistent offenders may face suspension or revocation of their licensure if they hold any form of official standing within the magical community.\n\nThe burden of proof rests upon the accuser to demonstrate that false claims were made knowingly and with specific intent to deceive. Vague assertions about an item's \"ancient roots\" or \"mystical past,\" absent evidence of affirmative misrepresentation, will not suffice to trigger liability under this section. However, patterns of deceptive behavior may warrant further investigation by the appropriate authorities.\n\nThis rule complements existing provisions concerning contract fraud and unfair trade practices. It reflects the Guild's commitment to maintaining integrity within the marketplace for magical goods and services. All participants are expected to adhere to ethical standards when discussing or transacting in artifacts of historical importance. Failure to do so risks not only financial consequences but also professional ruin.\n\nsee § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild, § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters, § 700.1 Evidence Generally\n\"Truthfulness is the cornerstone of commerce. Deception erodes trust and damages all concerned.\" — Archmage Theron, Conservator Manifesto",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "100.2",
            "600.1",
            "700.1",
            "900.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.5 False Provenance of Relics and Related Deceptions — Continued",
          "text": "The use of spurious provenance to inflate the value or status of magical artifacts is expressly prohibited under this section. Any representation that a relic possesses ancient lineage, noble ownership, or sacred consecration which is demonstrably false shall constitute grounds for immediate revocation of licensure and potential criminal prosecution. The Desk maintains an independent authentication board empowered to investigate claims of provenance fraud upon receipt of credible evidence.\n\nRelics obtained through deception regarding their origin are subject to forfeiture and destruction at the Guild's discretion. Penalties may be enhanced if false representations were made with intent to defraud or manipulate market conditions. Repeated violations will result in permanent disqualification from participating in any licensed magical trade activities. The Desk reserves the right to impose additional sanctions as deemed necessary to protect public welfare.\n\nThis provision shall not apply where a caster acts solely as an agent for another party who knowingly misrepresented the artifact's history, provided full disclosure was made prior to transaction completion. However, failure to disclose material facts regarding known provenance issues remains grounds for disciplinary action under existing regulations concerning honesty in trade practices.\n\nThe Desk encourages all market participants to exercise due diligence when acquiring magical items and report any suspected fraud immediately. Prompt reporting facilitates swift investigation and mitigation of potential harm to consumers and the integrity of the regulatory framework as a whole. Cooperation with authentication proceedings is mandatory upon reasonable notice.\n\nViolations of this section may be prosecuted summarily without prior warning. Conviction carries automatic suspension pending completion of remedial training in ethical trading practices. Subsequent offenses will result in escalating penalties up to permanent revocation of all licenses and certificates held by the offender. The Guild maintains a zero-tolerance policy regarding deceptive provenance claims.\n\nThis section complements existing prohibitions on fraud and misrepresentation found elsewhere in these Accords, reflecting the unique risks posed by false attributions of magical significance. The Desk retains broad authority to interpret and enforce this provision as circumstances demand, within the limits set forth herein. All parties are expected to uphold the highest standards of honesty in all dealings involving licensed magical items.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "900.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.5 False Provenance of Relics and Related Deceptions — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "The foregoing prohibition extends to any misrepresentation regarding the origin, age, or magical potency of an enchanted object intended for sale or barter. To assert that a mundane item bears ancient enchantments when it does not constitutes a material breach of trust and may be prosecuted as fraud under § 100.2(e). This includes fabricating pedigrees for artifacts, falsely claiming association with historic figures or events, and exaggerating the efficacy of protective wards or offensive charms.\n\nParties are encouraged to seek certified appraisals from registered Guild evaluators prior to major transactions involving relics suspected of containing active enchantments. Such evaluations shall be conducted in accordance with standards set forth by the Conservator's Office and may include non-invasive scanning techniques approved for public use. Refusal to permit such examination, where reasonable grounds exist to suspect falsification, shall be noted on the seller's license and may result in suspension pending further investigation under § 200.4(b).\n\nThe burden of proof rests upon any party alleging false provenance to establish by a preponderance of evidence that misrepresentations were made with intent to deceive. Witness testimony, documentary records, and technical analysis shall all be admissible as relevant evidence in determining the validity of such claims. In cases where deception causes tangible harm—including financial loss or physical injury—the aggrieved party may pursue civil remedies through standard judicial channels alongside any criminal penalties imposed by the Guild.\n\nRepeated offenses regarding relic authenticity may lead to escalated sanctions, including permanent revocation of licensure and referral for trial under the Paradox Trial provisions of § 800 et seq. The Desk retains discretion to impose additional conditions on practitioners who have violated this section, such as mandatory ethics training or enhanced oversight during future transactions. All parties are urged to maintain scrupulous honesty regarding magical provenance to preserve the integrity of our shared professional community and protect the public from exploitation.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.2",
            "200.4",
            "900.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "ef70308a94d4832f",
