    {
      "cite": "1001.1",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Financial Fraud and Manipulation Generally",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "General prohibition. Cross-ref later 1001 sections.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Financial Fraud and Manipulation Generally",
          "text": "Any act of deception involving the deliberate manipulation of arcane, material, or ethereal assets to induce a party into an involuntary transaction or to misrepresent the nature of a magical contract shall be deemed a primary violation of the Autumnwood Accords. The Guild maintains that the integrity of the magical marketplace is the bedrock of civil stability within the Midlands; therefore, any mage or non-mage entity utilizing the arcane to distort the perception of value, ownership, or intent shall be subject to immediate investigation by the Aegis Magi. This prohibition encompasses, but is not limited to, the fabrication of magical provenance, the clandestine alteration of enchantment potency, and the use of coercive mental influence to bypass the voluntary nature of a trade. All such actions constitute a breach of the public trust and may result in the initiation of a Paradox Trial as outlined in § 800.2.\n\nFurthermore, any individual found to be engaging in systematic financial fraud shall be barred from holding a Provisional Guild Pass, regardless of their political standing or historical contributions to the Innovators or Conservators. The Accords Desk reserves the right to seize all assets involved in a fraudulent transaction, including the forfeiture of the magical components used to facilitate the deceit. See § 1001.6 for specific rules regarding the transfer of assets and see § 1001.10 regarding the visibility of underlying obligations. Any entity found in violation of this section may be referred to the Archmage Sovereign for a determination of permanent expulsion from the Guild's jurisdiction.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "800.2",
            "1001.6",
            "1001.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets",
          "text": "The use of specific classes of magic within the context of trade, lending, or the valuation of assets is strictly prohibited to ensure the impartiality of the market. This includes, but is not limited to, spells of mass compulsion, truth-distortion, and any high-level divination used to extract private financial secrets from a counterparty without express, written consent. The Innovators may argue that such restrictions hamper the flow of information; however, the Conservators maintain that these spells undermine the fundamental principle of free will in commerce. Any mage utilizing these prohibited schools in a commercial setting shall face immediate sanction and potential sealing of their primary casting focus.\n\nIt is further mandated that the Quiet List shall remain strictly confidential. No mage shall recite, reference, or hint at the contents of the Quiet List in any tavern, marketplace, or public forum. Any attempt to use the existence of the Quiet List as a basis for market manipulation or as a tool of intimidation is a felony under the Accords. The Aegis Magi are authorized to use any means necessary to silence a mage who violates this confidentiality. For matters involving the specific reporting of forbidden research in trade, see § 400.1. For the consequences of unauthorized casting in public spaces, see § 500.2.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "500.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Valuation After Alteration",
          "text": "Any magical object or item that undergoes a significant transfiguration, enchantment, or physical alteration must be formally re-assayed by a Guild-certified appraiser before it may be traded or used as collateral. The mere act of alteration does not change the legal status of the item; for example, a petrified inventory remains a liability of the owner until the petrification is reversed and the item is re-certified. The burden of proof regarding the original state of the item lies with the seller. If a seller fails to provide a pre-alteration certificate, the item shall be valued at its lowest possible utility state for the purposes of all Guild-governed contracts.\n\nIn cases where an item is altered to increase its magical potency, the seller must disclose the source of the enhancement. If the enhancement involves the use of restricted materials or unauthorized schools of magic, the item is subject to immediate seizure by the Conservators. This rule ensures that the sudden influx of \"enhanced\" goods does not destabilize the local economy or introduce volatile elements into the Midlands. For inquiries regarding the custody of such specimens during the assay period, see § 400.4. For matters regarding the unauthorized growth of magical entities as a form of asset, see § 400.5.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "400.4",
            "400.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Illusion-Based Fraud",
          "text": "The use of illusions to misrepresent the physical properties, quantity, price, or identity of goods, buyers, or sellers is strictly prohibited. A mage who casts an illusion to make a crate of common grain appear as enchanted wheat, or to make a small sum of gold appear as a mountain of wealth, is in direct violation of the Autumnwood Accords. This applies equally to the fabrication of a buyer’s identity; any mage who assumes the guise of a known entity to secure a favorable trade agreement shall be prosecuted for identity fraud. The Guild views the manipulation of perception as a direct assault on the sanctity of the agreement.\n\nFurthermore, illusions used to obfuscate the true price of an item during a negotiation are considered a form of price-gouging and shall be met with heavy fines. If an illusion is used to hide a lien or a pre-existing debt on an object, the transaction is null and void, and the offending mage may be remanded to a pocket-prison. The Aegis Magi are tasked with patrolling major trade hubs to ensure that \"visual honesty\" is maintained. For procedures regarding the use of pocket dimensions as a means to hide these illusions, see § 400.2. For the reporting of material enchantments that may be mistaken for illusions, see § 900.2.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "400.2",
            "900.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Material Alteration of Traded Goods",
          "text": "Any trade involving the aggregation, permissible transfiguration, or material alteration of goods must be accompanied by a full disclosure of the changes made to the base material. The burden of disclosure lies entirely with the seller. If a mage transfigures a common metal into a more valuable alloy, they must provide the chemical and arcane blueprints of the process to the Guild's records. Failure to disclose such alterations is a violation of the Accords and may result in the immediate revocation of the seller's trading license. This ensures that the \"hidden\" properties of a sold item do not cause unforeseen magical reactions for the buyer.\n\nHistorical annotations regarding the transmutation of materials are kept in the central archives; any mage performing these actions must cite the relevant precedents to ensure compliance with existing safety protocols. For example, the transmutation of lead into gold is subject to specific reporting thresholds as detailed in § 1001.8. Any transfiguration that results in a permanent change to the molecular structure of the trade good must be registered within three lunar cycles of the sale. For further details on the legality of transfiguring materials into laboratory equipment, see § 400.2. For the identification of fees associated with these registrations, see § 900.4.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.8",
            "400.2",
            "900.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets",
          "text": "A magical asset provided as an endowment or a gift is not exempt from the laws of the Accords; rather, it is subject to specific oversight regarding its long-term obligations. A \"hat that later bills the wearer\" for its magical services is a classic example of a deceptive endowment. Any gift that carries a hidden cost, a retroactive debt, or a self-replicating fee structure must be disclosed in full at the moment of the gift's delivery. If a gift is found to contain a hidden \"billing\" enchantment or a parasitic mana-drain, the gift is declared a violation of the Accords and the provider shall be liable for triple damages.\n\nFurthermore, the transfer of a magical asset as a gift does not transfer the liability of any existing liens or debts attached to that asset unless such a transfer is explicitly stated in a notarized deed. The Guild recognizes that many magical items possess \"sentience-lite\" properties that may attempt to claim ownership or demand payment; the owner of the original asset is responsible for suppressing such behaviors until a formal transfer of title is complete. For the identification of fees regarding these transfers, see § 900.4. For the reporting of material enchantments that might facilitate such \"billing,\" see § 900.2.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.4",
            "900.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk",
          "text": "All mages and merchants are required to provide a full and accurate disclosure of the composition, value, and inherent risk of any magical item offered for trade. This disclosure must be provided in a language that the buyer understands; a disclosure provided in a dead tongue or a complex arcane cipher that the buyer cannot read is not considered a \"full\" disclosure and is treated as a deliberate obfuscation. This includes the disclosure of any \"side effects,\" such as minor telepathic echoes, unintended environmental shifts, or the risk of the item becoming volatile under specific conditions.\n\nFailure to disclose a known risk—such as an item's tendency to teleport when exposed to moonlight—shall result in the immediate forfeiture of the sale and a permanent mark on the seller's Guild record. The seller is liable for any damages incurred by the buyer due to non-disclosure. The Accords Desk requires that all high-risk items be accompanied by a \"Warning of Volatility\" scroll. For the reporting of such material enchantments, see § 900.2. For the legal consequences of providing false provenance for such items, see § 900.5.",
          "key": "g",
          "refs": [
            "900.2",
            "900.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Lead-to-Gold; Reporting Threshold",
          "text": "The transmutation of base metals, specifically the conversion of lead into gold, is subject to strict reporting thresholds to prevent the destabilization of the regional currency. Under the Autumnwood Accords, lead remains lead for tax and regulatory purposes until a formal assay by the Guild's Assay Desk confirms the permanence and purity of the transmutation. Any gold produced via transmutation must be declared to the Guild within twenty-four hours of the conversion. This ensures that the influx of \"new\" gold does not lead to hyper-inflation or the corruption of the market.\n\nMages who engage in large-scale transmutation for the purpose of flooding the market with gold are subject to heavy fines and may be required to contribute a portion of their yield to the Guild's treasury. The Innovators may argue that this stifles the progress of alchemical science, but the Conservators maintain that economic stability is a prerequisite for magical safety. For inquiries regarding the identification of fees for these assays, see § 900.4. For the reporting of these material enchantments, see § 900.2.",
          "key": "h",
          "refs": [
            "900.4",
            "900.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Polymorph of Coinage",
          "text": "The use of polymorph magic to alter the shape of coinage is strictly forbidden. The Guild maintains that the shape of a coin is not its denomination; a gold coin shaped like a dragon is still a gold coin, but a copper coin shaped like a gold coin is a criminal offense. Any attempt to use polymorph magic to deceive a merchant or a citizen regarding the denomination of a currency is a direct violation of the Accords. Such acts are considered a form of high-level fraud and will be met with immediate seizure of all the offender's current currency.\n\nFurthermore, the use of polymorph magic to create \"counterfeit\" coins—those that possess the correct shape but lack the required magical signature of the Guild-approved mint—is a capital offense within the Guild's jurisdiction. The Aegis Magi are authorized to use \"Truth-Sensing\" wards in all major marketplaces to detect such discrepancies. For the reporting of these material enchantments, see § 900.2. For the identification of fees for legitimate minting, see § 900.4.",
          "key": "i",
          "refs": [
            "900.2",
            "900.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Invisibility of Liens",
          "text": "A lien is a legal claim on an asset; the fact that a lien is hidden from public view does not negate its existence. Furthermore, any attempt to use invisibility magic or \"veiling\" spells to hide a lien on a traded object is a violation of the Accords. An invisible lien is still a lien, and an attempt to conceal it is a deliberate act of fraud. All liens must be recorded in a visible, legible manner on the item's title or accompanying documentation. Any merchant who attempts to sell an item with a concealed lien shall be prosecuted for fraud.\n\nIf a lien is discovered to have been hidden using active magic, the offending mage shall be subject to the Paradox Trial as outlined in § 800.2. The Guild maintains that transparency in debt is essential for the health of the Midlands' economy. For the reporting of the enchantments used to veil such liens, see § 900.2. For the legal consequences of providing false provenance regarding an item's debt status, see § 900.5.",
          "key": "j",
          "refs": [
            "800.2",
            "900.2",
            "900.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Wish Used to Rewrite a Filed Return",
          "text": "The use of a \"Wish\" or any high-level reality-warping spell to alter, erase, or rewrite a filed magical return or a notarized contract is strictly prohibited. A wish does not rewrite the stamp of the Accords Desk. Once a document has been filed and stamped by an authorized clerk, it is considered a permanent record of the Guild. Any attempt to use reality-warping magic to retroactively change the terms of a contract or the amount of a reported enchantment is a violation of the fundamental stability of the Accord.\n\nMages who attempt such actions are viewed as threats to the very fabric of the Guild's legal framework and will be subject to the most severe sanctions, including a permanent Seal of Silence or a Paradox Trial. The Guild's records are protected by high-level \"Stasis Wards\" to prevent such tampering. For the procedures regarding the Paradox Trial, see § 800.2. For the reporting of material enchantments that might be used to attempt such rewrites, see § 900.2.",
          "key": "k",
          "refs": [
            "800.2",
            "900.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Wario Coin; Non-Recognition",
          "text": "The Guild does not recognize the \"Wario Coin\" as a legal tender or a valid form of currency within the Midlands. The Wario Coin is classified as a private tender and carries no weight in Guild-governed transactions. Any merchant who accepts Wario Coins as payment for Guild-regulated goods or services is doing so at their own risk and is not entitled to Guild protection or mediation in the event of a dispute. Furthermore, the Guild will not facilitate the exchange of Wario Coins for any recognized currency.\n\nAny attempt to force the Guild to recognize the Wario Coin as a standard currency is a violation of the Accords. The Aegis Magi are instructed to ignore any requests for currency conversion involving this token. For matters regarding the identification of fees for legitimate currency exchange, see § 900.4. For the reporting of material enchantments that might be used to fake Wario Coins, see § 900.2.",
          "key": "l",
          "refs": [
            "900.4",
            "900.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault",
          "text": "Any storage vessel, including but not limited to a Bag of Holding, used to conceal assets from the Guild's oversight is considered an \"off-books\" vault. However, the Accords Desk maintains that \"off-books\" is still \"books\" if a clerk can smell it. The use of extra-dimensional storage to hide wealth from taxation, reporting, or seizure is a violation of the Guild's right to oversee the flow of magical assets. Any such vessel discovered in the possession of a Guild member that contains undeclared assets will be subject to immediate audit.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to inspect any such storage vessels during a formal audit or if there is reasonable suspicion of fraud. The owner of the vessel must provide a full manifest of its contents. For the reporting of material enchantments that facilitate such storage, see § 900.2. For the identification of fees associated with the registration of large-scale storage vessels, see § 900.4.",
          "key": "m",
          "refs": [
            "900.2",
            "900.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Secured Transactions in Bound Objects",
          "text": "Any transaction involving a \"bound object\"—an item that possesses the ability to move, act, or defend itself independently—is subject to heightened scrutiny. Because such objects can \"walk away\" from their owners or act as autonomous agents, they are considered high-risk collateral. Any contract involving a bound object as collateral must include a \"Binding Clause\" that prevents the object from fleeing its owner or the lender during a dispute. Failure to secure a bound object properly during a transaction is the sole responsibility of the seller.\n\nIf a bound object leaves its owner's possession during a trade due to a lack of proper binding, the seller shall be held liable for the full value of the item. The Aegis Magi may be called upon to \"tether\" such objects during high-value negotiations. For the reporting of the enchantments used to bind such objects, see § 900.2. For the identification of fees for the notarization of binding clauses, see § 900.4.",
          "key": "n",
          "refs": [
            "900.2",
            "900.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Layered Transfiguration Schemes",
          "text": "Any series of transfigurations, enchantments, or physical alterations that are so complex that a reasonable toad would be confused are to be read as a single scheme. The Guild does not recognize \"layered\" deception where a mage uses multiple minor transfigurations to hide a major illegal one. If the cumulative effect of several minor magical changes is to obscure the true nature of an object, it shall be treated as a single act of fraud. This prevents mages from bypassing the laws of the Accords by breaking down a large violation into several smaller, \"permissible\" ones.\n\nThe Aegis Magi are instructed to look for the \"intent of the weave\" rather than the individual threads of the spell. If the ultimate result of a magical process is a violation of the Accords, the entire process is subject to sanction. For the reporting of complex material enchantments, see § 900.2. For the identification of fees for auditing complex magical schemes, see § 900.4.",
          "key": "o",
          "refs": [
            "900.2",
            "900.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "bb8f42a3bf89b877",
      "refs": [
        "400.1",
        "400.2",
        "400.4",
        "400.5",
        "500.2",
        "800.2",
