    {
      "cite": "1001.3",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Valuation After Alteration",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Who assays. Petrified inventory is still inventory.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Valuation After Alteration",
          "text": "Any magical asset, artifact, or mundane item subjected to transfiguration, transmutation, or any other form of structural alteration by a licensed practitioner shall be subject to a mandatory re-valuation before it may be entered into the Guild’s ledger of commerce. The primary burden of initiating this assessment lies with the caster of the alteration; should the change be performed for a third party, the contracting party shall be held liable for the costs of the assay. The Accords Desk maintains that a change in physical state—be it the hardening of flesh into stone, the sharpening of iron into glass, or the expansion of volume—constitutes a fundamental shift in the object’s utility and marketability.\n\nThe official assay shall be conducted by a Guild-sanctioned Appraiser of the Fourth Circle or higher. No private valuation, regardless of the complexity of the arcane mathematics employed by the practitioner, shall be recognized by the Accords Desk as a final determination of value for the purposes of taxation or trade. This requirement applies to all items moving through the Midlands markets under the jurisdiction of Archmage Veyra. If a practitioner fails to submit an object for assay following a significant alteration, they shall be held in breach of the Accords, and any subsequent sale of said object may be voided by the Aegis Magi.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords Desk explicitly clarifies that \"Petrified Inventory is still Inventory.\" The transition of an object into a petrified, fossilized, or otherwise inert mineral state does not exempt the owner from their existing storage fees or their obligation to report the item’s existence. While the market value may be severely diminished by petrification, the legal status of the item as a piece of property remains unchanged. See § 1001.3 for further clarification on the persistence of property rights during state-change events.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1001.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Illusion-Based Fraud",
          "text": "The use of illusion magic to distort the perceived reality of a commercial transaction is strictly prohibited under the Autumnwood Accords. This prohibition encompasses, but is not limited to, the creation of an illusion of goods, the distortion of the perceived price, or the fabrication of a phantom buyer. Any practitioner who utilizes *Major Image*, *Hallucinatory Terrain*, or any derivative school of deceptive weaving to induce a sale is subject to immediate seizure of their arcane focus and a referral to the Paradox Trial for the determination of appropriate sanctions.\n\nSpecifically, the creation of \"Ghost Stock\"—where an item appears to be present in a warehouse or storefront but is actually absent—is classified as a high-tier offense of the Accords. Similarly, any attempt to manipulate the perceived scarcity of a resource via atmospheric illusions shall be met with heavy fines and a potential revocation of the practitioner's license to operate within the Midlands. The Accords Desk does not distinguish between \"artistic\" illusion and \"commercial\" illusion when the latter results in a transfer of currency or goods; if the buyer’s consent was predicated on a falsehood of the senses, the transaction is null.\n\nThe fabrication of a buyer is considered a felony of the highest order within the Guild’s jurisdiction. This includes the use of simulacra or illusions to create a false sense of demand, thereby inflating the value of the seller's goods or creating a fraudulent market history. Practitioners found guilty of such acts shall be barred from all Guild-sanctioned markets for a period of no less than ten years. See § 700.4 regarding the limitations of simulacrum testimony in these matters.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "700.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Material Alteration of Traded Goods",
          "text": "Any practitioner intending to sell a good that has undergone material alteration must provide a comprehensive disclosure of the changes made. This disclosure must be explicit and must be presented in a manner that ensures the buyer is fully aware of the item's history. The burden of proof rests entirely upon the seller to demonstrate that the transfiguration was performed within the safety margins established by the Conservators. If an object has been aggregated—such as the fusing of multiple smaller items into a single larger entity—this must be documented as a primary structural change.\n\nPermissible transfiguration is defined as any change that does not fundamentally alter the \"Soul-Signature\" of the item, as defined by the Conservators. If a transfiguration results in a permanent change to the item's weight, durability, or magical conductivity, a formal disclosure must be filed with the Accords Desk. Failure to disclose such alterations constitutes a breach of the Goodstyle Artisan standards and may lead to a permanent ban from the Guild's trade circles.\n\nHistorical annotations regarding these regulations can be found in C.C.D. 6125, 7355, and 10155. These records detail the evolution of \"Permissible Transfiguration\" from the early days of the Accords to the current tensions between the Innovators and the Conservators. Practitioners are advised that the Aegis Magi may conduct random inspections of high-value inventory to ensure compliance with these disclosure requirements. See § 400.4 for related specimen custody rules.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "400.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets",
          "text": "A magical asset provided as a gift or an endowment is subject to the same regulatory scrutiny as a standard sale, provided the asset possesses the capacity for autonomous action or delayed activation. Specifically, any item that \"later bills the wearer\" or imposes a recurring cost, fee, or obligation upon its owner after the initial transfer of possession shall be legally classified as an \"Endowment of Magical Assets.\" This classification ensures that \"gifts\" cannot be used as a loophole to bypass the disclosure of hidden magical liens or recurring costs.\n\nWhen an item is gifted, the donor must provide a \"Certificate of Clean Title,\" certifying that no hidden geases, parasitic enchantments, or delayed-activation fees are attached to the object. If an item is discovered to have a hidden billing mechanism—such as a sentient hat that demands a monthly tribute of mana or gold—the original donor remains liable for the initial violation of the Accords. The recipient may seek a refund of the \"gift\" value, and the donor shall be subject to a fine for fraudulent endowment.\n\nThe Accords Desk recognizes that many magical items are inherently complex. However, the intent of the law is to prevent the \"Trojan Horse\" method of magical commerce. If an item possesses a \"contractual\" nature—where it performs a service in exchange for a resource—it must be declared as such at the moment of transfer. This prevents the circumvention of the § 600.3 prohibitions on unconscionable geases. See § 600.3 for details on geas as an unconscionable term.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "600.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk",
          "text": "All commercial interactions involving magical goods must be predicated upon full and accurate disclosure. The seller is required to provide a comprehensive breakdown of the object's composition, its current market value, and the inherent risks associated with its use. A critical component of this requirement is the language of disclosure; the Accords Desk rules that information provided in a language the buyer does not speak or understand is not considered \"full.\" If a practitioner sells a volatile artifact to a layperson using only high-arcane cantrips for description, the practitioner is liable for any subsequent accidents or malfunctions.\n\nThe \"Risk\" component of the disclosure must include a warning of any known \"volatile\" traits, such as a tendency to teleport, a high chance of mana-drain, or a history of unpredictable behavior. For items of significant power, a \"Safety Briefing\" must be provided, and the buyer must sign a waiver acknowledging the risks. This is particularly important for goods sourced from the frontiers of the Midlands, where the stability of magic is less certain.\n\nThe Accords Desk maintains that transparency is the foundation of the Autumnwood Accords. By ensuring that buyers are fully informed, the Guild minimizes the frequency of disputes that lead to the Paradox Trial. Failure to provide a clear, linguistically accessible disclosure is grounds for immediate seizure of the goods and a penalty from the Aegis Magi. See § 800.1 for the range of sanctions available for such non-compliance.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "800.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Lead-to-Gold; Reporting Threshold",
          "text": "The transmutation of base metals into precious metals is strictly regulated to prevent the destabilization of the regional economy. Under the Accords, lead remains lead for the purposes of taxation and trade reporting until such a time as the official Assay Desk agrees to certify the transmutation. A practitioner who creates gold through alchemy or spellcraft must submit their \"Batch of Transmutation\" to the Assay Desk for a formal verification of the metal's purity and origin.\n\nUntil the Assay Desk provides a \"Certificate of Authenticity,\" any gold produced by a practitioner is considered \"Uncertified Bullion\" and may not be traded in public markets. Selling uncertified bullion as \"Pure Gold\" is a violation of the Accords and is punishable by a heavy fine and the temporary suspension of the practitioner's transmutation license. This rule exists to prevent the flooding of the market with magically-generated wealth, which would devalue the efforts of the Goodstyle Artisans and other legitimate producers.\n\nThe reporting threshold for transmutation is set at five ounces of precious metal. Any transmutation resulting in a yield below this amount is considered \"Minor Transmutation\" and does not require a formal Assay Desk filing, provided it is for personal use and not for resale. However, any sale of such \"Minor\" gold still requires a basic practitioner's license. See § 500.1 for rules on field casting for value.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "500.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Polymorph of Coinage",
          "text": "The physical shape of a currency unit does not determine its denomination. The Accords Desk explicitly forbids the use of *Polymorph* or any other shape-shifting magic to alter the appearance of coins, notes, or other tender. A coin that has been magically altered to resemble a larger denomination is considered a fraudulent counterfeit. This includes \"Shape-Shifting Tender,\" where a coin may change its appearance depending on the light or the viewer's perspective.\n\nThe Guild's position is that the \"Essence of Value\" is tied to the magical signature and weight of the currency, not its visual representation. If a practitioner attempts to pass a copper coin that has been visually altered to look like a gold sovereign, they shall be prosecuted for fraud. This rule is designed to prevent the confusion of common merchants and to maintain the integrity of the Midlands' trade networks.\n\nFurthermore, the use of polymorph magic on currency is seen as a frivolous waste of arcane energy and a direct insult to the stability sought by the Conservators. Practitioners found engaging in such activities may be ordered to undergo a period of \"Arcane Probation,\" during which their ability to cast non-combat spells is restricted. See § 1001.1 for general financial fraud rules.",
          "key": "g",
          "refs": [
            "1001.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Invisibility of Liens",
          "text": "A hidden lien is still a lien. The Accords Desk establishes that any magical lien, debt, or claim placed upon a magical asset is legally binding, regardless of whether it is visually or magically concealed. An \"Invisible Lien\"—one that is hidden via *Invisibility*, *Masking*, or other concealment charms—is a direct violation of the Accords. All liens must be registered in a public ledger or at least disclosed to any potential buyer of the asset.\n\nThe purpose of this rule is to ensure that property rights are transparent. If a practitioner sells an item that is subject to a hidden lien, they are liable for the debt and any legal repercussions that arise from the debt's enforcement. The Aegis Magi are authorized to \"Reveal All\" on any high-value transaction, using detection spells to uncover any hidden magical signatures of ownership or debt.\n\nA lien is defined as any magical binding that prevents the current holder of an item from fully utilizing its properties or selling it without the consent of a third party. If such a binding exists, it must be declared as a \"Restrictive Covenant\" in the sale contract. Failure to do so is considered a deliberate act of deception. See § 700.1 for evidence generally.",
          "key": "h",
          "refs": [
            "700.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Wish Used to Rewrite a Filed Return",
          "text": "The use of a *Wish* or any other high-level reality-warping spell to rewrite, alter, or erase a filed tax return or a Guild registration is strictly prohibited. A *Wish* does not rewrite the stamp; the administrative record of the Accords Desk is considered a \"Fixed Point of Bureaucracy.\" Any attempt to use reality-warping magic to retroactively change a past legal filing is a grave offense against the stability of the Guild's records.\n\nThe Accords Desk maintains that while magic can alter the world, it cannot overwrite the bureaucratic truth of the Guild. If a practitioner makes an error on a filed return, they must file an \"Amendment of Record\" through the standard channels. Attempting to \"wish\" the error out of existence is viewed as an act of magical insurrection and may be prosecuted as an attempt to subvert the sovereignty of Archmage Veyra.\n\nPractitioners who attempt to use *Wish* for administrative purposes shall be subject to immediate impoundment of their magical focus. The Guild views the preservation of its records as a primary duty of the Conservators, and any magic that threatens the integrity of these records is treated with the utmost severity. See § 800.2 for Paradox Trial procedures.",
          "key": "i",
          "refs": [
            "800.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Wario Coin; Non-Recognition",
          "text": "The \"Wario Coin\" is not recognized as legal tender by the Mages' Guild or any other authority within the Autumnwood Accords. It is classified as \"Private Tender\" and may only be used in transactions between private parties who explicitly agree to its acceptance. The Guild does not provide any guarantees for its value, and it cannot be used to pay Guild fees, taxes, or fines.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild notes that the \"Wario Coin\" holds even less value than common copper in many regional markets. Practitioners are advised that attempting to use Wario Coins for the purchase of restricted research materials or the settlement of debts with the Aegis Magi will result in the transaction being rejected without prejudice. The Guild does not wish to be associated with the \"Wario Method\" of commerce.\n\nWhile the Guild does not actively ban the possession of Wario Coins, it refuses to provide any protections for those who trade in them. If a merchant accepts a Wario Coin and later finds it to be worthless, the Guild will not intervene. See § 1001.1 for general financial fraud and manipulation.",
          "key": "j",
          "refs": [
            "1001.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault",
          "text": "Any storage vessel that expands the internal volume of an object—specifically the \"Bag of Holding\" and its variants—cannot be used as an \"Off-Books Vault\" to circumvent storage fees. The Accords Desk maintains that \"Off-books is still books if a clerk can smell it.\" If a practitioner stores items in a Bag of Holding within the Midlands, those items are still subject to the Guild's inventory taxes and safety regulations.\n\nThe reasoning is based on the \"Presence of Utility.\" If a practitioner uses a Bag of Holding to store goods for the purpose of trade or long-term storage, those goods are considered to be \"in residence\" within the Guild's jurisdiction. The use of such bags to hide inventory from the tax assessors is a violation of the Accords and will be treated as tax evasion.\n\nThe Aegis Magi are authorized to use \"Sense Volume\" spells to detect the contents of such bags during routine inspections. If a practitioner is found to be hiding a significant amount of inventory in a Bag of Holding to avoid the \"Storage Fee,\" they will be assessed the back taxes plus a penalty for \"Bureaucratic Evasion.\" See § 900.3 for record retention.",
          "key": "k",
          "refs": [
            "900.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Secured Transactions in Bound Objects",
          "text": "The Guild recognizes that many magical items are \"Bound\"—meaning they possess a degree of autonomy or are tied to a specific caster. Any transaction involving a bound object is considered a \"Secured Transaction\" and requires a \"Release of Bond\" certificate. Collateral that can walk away, fly away, or otherwise act independently of its owner's will is subject to special restrictions.\n\nIf an item is used as collateral for a loan and it possesses the ability to move independently, the lender must place a \"Geas of Stasis\" on the object until the debt is satisfied. Failure to do so, or the attempt to sell a bound object without the consent of its \"Master\" (if one exists), is a violation of the Accords. The Guild seeks to prevent the \"losing\" of collateral due to the item's own agency.\n\nIn cases where an object's autonomy is so great that it cannot be reliably bound, it must be sold with a \"Warning of Independent Agency.\" The buyer must acknowledge that the item may act against their interests and that the Guild provides no recourse for such actions. See § 600.3 for geas as an unconscionable term.",
          "key": "l",
          "refs": [
            "600.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Layered Transfiguration Schemes",
          "text": "When a practitioner performs a series of transfigurations on a single object—a \"Layered Transfiguration Scheme\"—the entire sequence shall be read as one single scheme for the purposes of appraisal and disclosure. If a sequence of changes is so complex that a \"reasonable toad would be confused\" by the final result compared to the original state, the practitioner must provide a full history of every step taken.\n\nThis prevents \"incremental fraud,\" where a practitioner makes several small, seemingly insignificant changes to an object until it becomes something entirely different, thereby avoiding the reporting requirements of a major transfiguration. For the purposes of the Accords Desk, if the final product is unrecognizable from the original, it is a \"Major Alteration\" and requires a full Assay Desk certification.\n\nThe burden of disclosure is on the practitioner to provide a \"Transmutation Map\" for any item that has undergone more than three distinct material changes. This map must be filed with the Accords Desk before the item can be sold or traded. Failure to provide a clear map for a complex scheme will result in the item being seized for \"Unregulated Alteration.\" See § 1001.3 for valuation after alteration.",
          "key": "m",
          "refs": [
            "1001.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "9681984bccd7f450",
      "refs": [
        "400.4",
        "500.1",
        "600.3",
        "700.1",
        "700.4",
        "800.1",
        "800.2",
        "900.3",
        "1001.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "1001.4",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Illusion-Based Fraud",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Illusion of goods, of price, of a buyer.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Illusion-Based Fraud",
          "text": "Illusion of goods, of price, of a buyer.\n\nAny act of commerce involving the use of illusion magic to distort the perception of the tangible, the fiscal, or the corporeal nature of a transaction shall be deemed a primary violation of the Autumnwood Accords. The Accords Desk maintains that the integrity of the marketplace relies upon a shared reality; therefore, any mage who utilizes glamours to project the existence of inventory that does not exist in the physical plane, or to mask the physical decay of a product, shall be subject to immediate seizure of assets. This includes, but is not limited to, the projection of \"phantom stock\" to lure buyers into entering into binding contracts for goods that have been destroyed, sold elsewhere, or never produced. Such actions constitute a breach of the sanctity of the Midlands trade routes and shall be prosecuted under the standard of deceptive practice.\n\nFurthermore, the manipulation of perceived price is strictly prohibited. A mage may not cast illusions that alter the visual representation of a price tag, the perceived quantity of a bulk shipment, or the perceived quality of a gemstone or reagent. The Guild recognizes that while the aesthetic appeal of a product may be enhanced through minor, non-persistent enchantments, any illusion that obscures the actual cost or the true weight of the item constitutes fraud. The burden of proof in such cases shall rest upon the seller to provide a \"True-Sight\" verification at the time of the transaction, ensuring that the buyer is interacting with the reality of the offer.\n\nThe most severe category of illusion-based fraud involves the fabrication of the buyer. The use of illusions to create a \"simulated clientele\" to inflate the perceived popularity of a shop, or the use of illusions to impersonate a high-status individual to secure favorable credit terms, is a felony under Guild law. Any mage found to have manufactured a \"crowd\" to create a false sense of demand shall be fined three times the projected value of the fraud. See § 1001.1. Any merchant caught employing such tactics shall have their trading license revoked by the Accords Desk. The Guild maintains that while the Innovators may seek to push the boundaries of sensory manipulation, the marketplace is a zone of absolute transparency. Any ambiguity created by magical means that leads to a financial loss for a non-caster shall be rectified by the Guild’s oversight committee, with the offending mage bearing all costs of restitution.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1001.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.4 Illusion-Based Fraud",
          "text": "The deliberate creation or manipulation of illusory phenomena for the purpose of deceiving another party constitutes fraud under these Accords, regardless of whether tangible property changes hands. This prohibition extends to all forms of visual, auditory, olfactory, tactile, and gustatory deception designed to induce reliance by a victim. See § 1001.2 Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets; § 700.2 Scrying of Closed Books.\n\nSpecifically prohibited are illusions that simulate the presence or absence of goods, services, or conditions material to a transaction. This includes but is not limited to: fabricating visual evidence of product quality; simulating verbal communications from non-existent parties; creating false sensory impressions to alter perception of value or risk; and generating deceptive environmental effects intended to influence decision-making. Under § 1001.3 Valuation After Alteration, any item or service subject to such deception shall be deemed materially altered for purposes of contract interpretation.\n\nThe burden of proving intentional misrepresentation rests with the accusing party, who must demonstrate that the illusory effect was purposefully employed to induce reliance. Mere accidental projection of false images or sounds does not trigger liability absent evidence of gross negligence. See § 700.1 Evidence Generally; § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts.\n\nRemedies for proven illusion-based fraud include rescission of the affected agreement, restitution of benefits conferred, and damages equal to the difference between actual value and perceived value at the time of reliance. In egregious cases involving systemic deception by a caster or organization, punitive sanctions may be imposed including permanent revocation of licensure and referral for criminal prosecution under applicable laws. Refer also to § 800.1 Discipline; Range of Sanctions; § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters.\n\nThe Desk maintains an ongoing interest in preventing the use of deceptive magical effects to undermine fair commerce practices. Parties are encouraged to report suspected violations promptly to facilitate timely investigation and remediation. Failure to disclose known illusory conditions may constitute waiver of subsequent claims for damages arising therefrom, pursuant to principles of good faith embodied throughout these Accords. See § 1001.1 Financial Fraud and Manipulation Generally; § 900.5 False Provenance of Relics.\n\nThis provision serves as a cornerstone of the Guild's commitment to maintaining integrity in all magical transactions and interactions. By prohibiting deceptive practices, the Accords seek to foster trust between parties and ensure that outcomes reflect true merit rather than manipulated perception. The spirit of this section demands vigilance against any form of magical fraud designed to exploit human vulnerability or gullibility for personal gain. Consult § 1.7 Construction; Aggregation of Conduct regarding cumulative violations.\n\n[End of Page]",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.2",
            "700.2",
            "1001.3",
            "700.1",
            "300.2",
            "800.1",
            "600.1",
            "1001.1",
            "900.5",
            "1.7",
            "1001.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Illusory Representations and Fraudulent Deception",
          "text": "§ 1001.4 Illusion-Based Fraud\nThe intentional projection or manipulation of deceptive visual, auditory, or sensory phenomena for the purpose of inducing another party into entering a binding agreement constitutes illusion-based fraud under this section. Such conduct is deemed a substantive breach regardless of whether tangible property changes hands directly, as the illusory misrepresentation vitiates genuine consent and undermines the integrity of commercial exchanges governed by these Accords.\n\nSpecifically prohibited acts include but are not limited to: (a) casting glamours or similar spells upon merchandise to falsely enhance its appearance or value; (b) employing auditory illusions to simulate bidding wars or artificial demand; (c) fabricating illusory buyers, sellers, or witnesses to create false market conditions; and (d) using deceptive visual effects to conceal defects in goods. The mere capacity for such deception does not constitute a violation absent actual intent and execution designed to defraud.\n\nAny party found guilty of committing illusion-based fraud shall face sanctions commensurate with the severity of the offense and resulting harm. Minor infractions may be addressed through remedial measures including restitution, fines, or mandatory education regarding ethical use of magical arts. More egregious violations warrant escalated penalties ranging from license suspension to permanent revocation coupled with exclusion from future Guild operations. In cases involving substantial financial loss or endangerment of public safety, the matter shall be referred for appropriate criminal prosecution alongside administrative sanctions.\n\nPrevention and enforcement mechanisms include routine inspections of commercial establishments by authorized inspectors empowered to detect suspicious magical activity. Reporters of suspected fraud are encouraged to notify local Aegis Magi without fear of retaliation, receiving protection from retaliatory action in accordance with § 101.7. Repeated offenders may find themselves added to the Restricted List, barring them from entering certain zones or engaging in specified professions until further notice.\n\nThe Guild maintains that truthful representation forms the bedrock of honest commerce and public trust. Illusion-based fraud strikes at this foundation by substituting deception for reality itself. Consequently, such practices will not be tolerated under any circumstances as they erode confidence in legal transactions and expose all participants to exploitation. Strict adherence to this provision is essential for preserving fair markets and protecting consumers from predatory magical manipulation.\n\nsee § 1001.1 Financial Fraud Generally; see § 201.4 Misrepresentation of Skills; see § 300.5 Research Copies and Plagiarism; see § 900.6 Falsification of Records. This section shall take effect upon final promulgation by the Autumnwood Accords Desk.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.4",
            "101.7",
            "1001.1",
            "201.4",
            "300.5",
            "900.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.4 Illusory Representations and Fraudulent Deception",
          "text": "A representation made by means of an illusion, whether conjured in whole or part, shall constitute fraud if the maker intends to induce reliance thereon for pecuniary advantage. This includes, but is not limited to, the fabrication of a buyer's presence, the simulation of sufficient funds, or the creation of phantom inventory through optical or mental constructs. Any caster who knowingly employs such deceptions in the course of trade shall be deemed guilty of financial fraud under § 1001.4 and subject to penalties commensurate with the degree of deception and resulting loss.\n\nThe burden of proof rests upon the accuser to demonstrate that an illusory device was used with specific intent to mislead, rather than as a mere display of magical prowess. However, once such fact is established, any subsequent claim that the illusion did not materially affect the transaction shall be presumed false unless rebutted by clear and convincing evidence. This standard recognizes the inherent difficulty in discerning truth from artifice when one's senses are actively manipulated.\n\nCasters who utilize illusions to bypass licensing requirements or evade regulatory oversight shall face additional charges for violating §§ 200.1 and 900.1 respectively. The Guild maintains zero tolerance for magical trickery intended to circumvent established controls, viewing such practices as a direct threat to the integrity of the entire system. Repeat offenders may be subject to permanent revocation of licensure and referral for Paradox Trial proceedings under § 800.2.\n\nIt is further provided that any damages arising from reliance on fraudulent illusions shall be recoverable as if the deception had been accomplished through conventional means. This includes, without limitation, losses incurred by victims who parted with property in good faith based on false representations induced magically. The courts retain broad discretion to award punitive measures designed to deter future misconduct and make whole those harmed by such treachery.\n\nThis section shall be construed liberally to encompass all forms of deceptive magical practice used for financial gain, whether performed directly or through proxies. Any evasion of this provision through technicalities shall not excuse liability but rather demonstrate a flagrant disregard for the spirit of the law. The Guild expects all licensed casters to uphold the highest standards of honesty and transparency in their dealings, refusing to exploit the vulnerabilities inherent in human perception for selfish ends. Violation of these principles strikes at the very foundation of trust upon which commerce depends, warranting swift and severe consequences.\n\nThe provisions herein shall take effect immediately upon filing and remain in full force unless expressly amended by subsequent rulemaking. All existing cases involving illusory fraud pending before any tribunal shall be adjudicated according to this section as if fully incorporated therein at the time of filing. This rule serves to close a critical loophole exploited by unscrupulous mages throughout history, ensuring that no further harm comes from those who would misuse their gifts for ill purposes. Let it be known that the Autumnwood Accords shall stand guard against such malfeasance, protecting both consumers and honest merchants alike from the wiles of deceitful casters. Any who oppose this mandate do so at their peril, for the Guild will pursue all violators relentlessly until justice is served in full measure.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.4",
            "200.1",
            "800.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.4 Illusory Representations and Fraudulent Deception — Continued",
          "text": "Whereas the provisions of this Part regulate substantive financial manipulations, § 1001.4 addresses deceptive practices involving illusory projections intended to mislead parties regarding material facts. This prohibition extends beyond mere falsehoods spoken with intent to deceive; it encompasses any deliberate creation of false sensory data designed to influence commercial judgment or contractual assent. The underlying principle is that no transaction shall be validated if its core premises rest upon knowingly fabricated perceptual inputs, whether generated by magical effects or technological means.\n\nSpecifically prohibited acts include conjuring phantasmal commodities to inflate apparent inventory levels, projecting artificial images of prospective buyers to simulate demand, and manipulating ambient conditions such as lighting, sound, or scent to alter a target's emotional state during negotiations. Such tactics are deemed inherently coercive, undermining the voluntary nature of exchanges governed by these Accords. Furthermore, any device constructed solely for generating deceptive illusions with commercial application falls under this section's purview, regardless of whether active projection occurs at the time of observation.\n\nThe burden of proof rests upon the accuser to demonstrate that the accused party knowingly employed illusory projections for fraudulent ends and that such usage materially affected the outcome in question. However, circumstantial evidence may suffice where direct testimony is unavailable; patterns of behavior consistent with deceptive practices may be admitted as corroboration. Notably, legitimate artistic or entertainment uses of illusion magic remain permissible so long as no commercial advantage is improperly derived therefrom.\n\nViolations of § 1001.4 are subject to penalties commensurate with the severity and frequency of the offenses, ranging from monetary fines proportional to ill-gotten gains to permanent suspension of licensure for repeat offenders. In egregious cases involving substantial harm or repeated misconduct, revocation of all magical privileges may be imposed at the discretion of the Desk. Remedies available to aggrieved parties include rescission of tainted agreements, restitution of losses incurred, and injunctive relief to prevent further exploitation of deceptive techniques. The Guild reserves the right to pursue additional sanctions as warranted by the circumstances surrounding each violation.\n\nThis section stands in harmony with established precedents prohibiting fraudulent conduct under §§ 1001.1 through 1001.3, extending regulatory oversight into previously unpoliced domains where magical deception intersects with commercial activity. By establishing clear boundaries for acceptable use of illusory arts in financial contexts, the Accords seek to preserve integrity and trust within the broader marketplace while recognizing the unique capabilities afforded by advanced spellcraft. Compliance with these guidelines is mandatory for all licensed practitioners operating within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Hall.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.4",
            "1001.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "1001.4 Illusory Representations and Fraudulent Deception — Continued",
          "text": "Any representation or display that creates a false impression regarding the nature, quality, quantity, or authenticity of magical goods shall constitute fraud under § 1001.4(a). This includes but is not limited to illusions designed to conceal defects, exaggerate value, mislead purchasers about capabilities, or fabricate provenance entirely. The burden of proof rests upon the accuser to demonstrate that the deceptive act was intentional and material to the transaction.\n\nIllusory Representations and Fraudulent Deception — Continued shall apply regardless of whether the deceiver possesses superior knowledge of the true state of affairs (§ 1001.4(b)). A caster may be held liable for knowingly permitting an illusion to mislead a counterparty, even if that caster did not personally create or direct the effect. This principle extends to clerks and agents who fail in their duty to verify representations made on behalf of the Guild.\n\nPunishment for violation shall include disgorgement of ill-gotten gains, permanent revocation of licensure, and referral to the Paradox Trial for further sanctions (§ 1001.4(c)). In egregious cases where public trust has been severely compromised, a temporary ban on all commercial casting may be imposed until such time as the affected entity demonstrates full compliance with relevant statutes. Repeat offenders shall face additional penalties at the discretion of the Desk.\n\nThe provisions of § 1001.4 are not limited to direct transactions between individuals but also encompass indirect dealings where one party benefits from another's deception. For example, a merchant who knowingly sells defective enchanted items may be liable for damages suffered by end-users, even if no direct contractual relationship existed between the two parties (§ 1001.4(d)). Similarly, an employer who allows its agents to engage in fraudulent practices may be held vicariously responsible for resulting harms.\n\nThis section works in tandem with § 1001.5 regarding truthfulness in advertising and marketing materials. Any false claim or exaggeration made to promote magical goods shall be treated as a violation of both statutes simultaneously, subject to cumulative penalties where appropriate (§ 1001.4(e)). The Desk retains broad discretion to determine what constitutes an \"illusory representation\" based on the totality of circumstances presented in each case.\n\nIn all matters concerning fraud and deception involving magical commodities, the highest standards of honesty and integrity shall prevail. Those who seek to profit from misleading others will find themselves swiftly punished in accordance with these laws. The Guild stands ready to enforce these provisions rigorously to protect both consumers and honest businesses alike (§ 1001.4(f)).",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.4",
            "1001.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
