    {
      "cite": "1001.5",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Material Alteration of Traded Goods",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "MATCH THE FILED PHOTO: aggregation; permissible transfiguration; disclosure; burden; cross-refs; historical annotations C.C.D. 6125 / 7355 / 10155.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Material Alteration of Traded Goods",
          "text": "Any merchant, artisan, or mage-trader engaged in the commerce of enchanted, mundane, or semi-sentient goods shall be held strictly liable for the material alteration of such items following the point of sale. Under the Autumnwood Accords, the integrity of a trade is predicated upon the physical and metaphysical state of the object at the moment of exchange. An \"alteration\" is defined as any change to the object’s primary form, weight, utility, or inherent magical properties that occurs without the express written consent of the purchaser. This includes, but is not limited to, the expansion of a container’s interior volume, the modification of a weapon’s reach, or the transmutation of a material’s base essence. See § 1001.3 regarding valuation after such alterations.\n\nThe burden of proof regarding the original state of a traded good rests upon the seller. If a dispute arises concerning the degradation or transformation of a product, the seller must provide a certified \"State of Origin\" manifest. In cases where the item is an enchanted artifact, the seller shall provide a recorded magical signature to accompany the physical description. If a buyer can demonstrate that an item has undergone an unauthorized aggregation—such as the merging of two distinct magical artifacts into a single, unstable composite—the seller shall be liable for the full restitution of the purchase price plus a punitive fee of twenty percent of the original value. Furthermore, any permissible transfiguration must be disclosed as a primary feature of the item; if a transfiguration is hidden or triggered by a mechanism not revealed at the time of sale, it shall be deemed a fraudulent alteration.\n\nAll transactions involving complex transfigurations must be cross-referenced with the established precedents for material stability. For instance, if an item undergoes a change that results in a net increase of mass or volume without a corresponding increase in utility, it shall be flagged for inspection by the Aegis Magi. Historical annotations regarding the stability of such items may be found in C.C.D. 6125, 7355, and 10155. Any seller found to have intentionally obscured the \"Material Alteration\" status of a good through layered enchantments shall face a Paradox Trial for deceptive practice. See § 800.2 for the procedure of such a trial.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1001.3",
            "800.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets",
          "text": "Any magical asset, artifact, or enchanted garment bestowed upon a recipient as a gift or endowment shall be subject to the Accords' regulations regarding \"Deferred Consideration.\" A common point of litigation arises when a gift—such as a ceremonial hat, cloak, or staff—is found to possess a \"billing\" enchantment or a parasitic mana-drain mechanism that activates after a period of possession. For the purposes of the Autumnwood Accords, such an item is not a true gift but an unconscionable endowment. If a gift later bills the wearer, the recipient is entitled to a full dissolution of the debt and the immediate seizure of the item by the Guild’s enforcement agents.\n\nThe distinction between a \"Gift\" and an \"Endowment\" rests on the intent of the donor and the presence of a latent obligation. A gift is a complete transfer of ownership with no strings attached. An endowment is a transfer of use that carries a hidden, delayed, or recurring cost, whether that cost be in gold, mana, blood, or service. Mages who provide \"free\" magical items to the public or to fellow mages are cautioned that any latent mechanism designed to extract value from the user post-possession shall be treated as a violation of the Accords. Such items must be registered with the Accords Desk as \"Conditional Endowments\" before they may be distributed.\n\nFurthermore, if an endowment is discovered to be predatory—meaning the cost of the \"gift\" exceeds the market value of the item by a factor of three or more—the donor shall be subject to a mandatory audit by the Conservators. Archmage Theron has decreed that the stability of the Midlands' economy relies on the clarity of these transfers. If a donor attempts to bypass these rules by claiming the billing mechanism is a \"protective ward\" or a \"training exercise,\" the burden of proof remains on the donor to demonstrate that the cost is non-exploitative. See § 600.3 for related prohibitions on unconscionable terms in magical contracts.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "600.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk",
          "text": "The principle of Full and Accurate Disclosure is the cornerstone of all commercial arcane transactions. Every seller of magical goods is mandated to provide a comprehensive disclosure of an item’s composition, its appraised value, and its inherent risks. This disclosure must be presented in a manner that is accessible to the buyer. It is a specific violation of the Accords to provide a disclosure that is technically accurate but practically obscured. For example, providing a description of a volatile explosive in a language the buyer does not speak, or using overly dense arcane jargon to hide a catastrophic failure rate, shall be deemed a failure of disclosure.\n\nFull disclosure requires three distinct components. First, the Composition: the seller must list all base materials and the primary schools of magic used in the item’s construction. Second, the Value: the seller must provide a fair market appraisal, adjusted for any unique magical properties. Third, the Risk: the seller must explicitly state the \"Failure State\" of the item—what happens when the magic fails, breaks, or is exhausted. If an item has a chance of teleporting the user to a pocket dimension, exploding, or causing permanent mana-burn, these risks must be highlighted in bold, legible script.\n\nFailure to provide these disclosures shall result in the immediate voiding of the contract and a forfeiture of the seller's right to the purchase price. If a buyer suffers harm due to an undisclosed risk, the seller shall be liable for all damages, including medical costs and any necessary restoration of magical essence. The Accords Desk maintains that a buyer's ignorance, when facilitated by a seller's obfuscation, is a triable offense. See § 700.1 for the general rules of evidence in such disputes.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "700.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Lead-to-Gold; Reporting Threshold",
          "text": "The transmutation of base metals into precious metals is a strictly regulated activity under the Autumnwood Accords. To prevent the destabilization of local economies and the flooding of markets with counterfeit bullion, the Accords establish a clear reporting threshold for all transmutation-based activities. Specifically, the transmutation of lead into gold (or any other base metal into a currency-standard metal) is not considered a \"completed sale\" for the purpose of tax and regulation until the item has been formally assayed by a Guild-approved assay desk.\n\nUntil such time as the assay desk agrees to the purity and permanence of the transmutation, the lead remains legally classified as lead. This means the seller cannot claim the tax benefits of gold production, nor can they claim the sale as a finalized transaction of high-value goods. This rule ensures that \"temporary\" transmutations—those that might revert to lead after a certain period or under specific conditions—do not bypass the Guild’s oversight. Sellers must present the item to a certified clerk who will verify the stability of the transmutation before it is entered into the ledger of commerce.\n\nAny mage who attempts to sell gold produced by transmutation without first obtaining an assay certificate shall be charged with \"Market Sabotage.\" This offense is treated with high severity by the Conservators, who view the unauthorized production of gold as a threat to the Midlands' prosperity. If an assay desk determines that a transmutation is unstable or \"flickering,\" the resulting gold shall be struck from the records and the seller may be fined. See § 1001.3 for valuation after alteration.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Polymorph of Coinage",
          "text": "The Accords clearly distinguish between the physical shape of a currency and its actual denomination. A common form of fraud involves the use of polymorph magic to alter the appearance of a coin—making a copper piece appear as a gold sovereign or changing the size of a silver piece to mimic a larger denomination. Under the Accords, \"Shape is not denomination.\" The magical appearance of a coin is irrelevant to its legal tender value; only the inherent magical signature and the weight of the base metal (as verified by the assay desk) determine its worth.\n\nMages who employ polymorph magic for the purpose of deceiving a merchant or a peer in a transaction shall be subject to immediate seizure of the fraudulent goods and a mandatory fine. This rule applies regardless of whether the polymorph effect is permanent or temporary. If a coin is enchanted to look like gold but remains copper in essence, it is a counterfeit. If a coin is enchanted to look like a sovereign but is actually a sovereign of lesser weight, it is a fraudulent alteration. The Accords Desk does not recognize \"magical aesthetics\" as a valid reason for a discrepancy in value.\n\nFurthermore, any merchant who knowingly accepts polymorphed coinage without first performing a standard \"Sense Magic\" check shall forfeit their right to claim the currency as valid tender. This places a shared responsibility on the trade community to maintain the integrity of the local economy. The Aegis Magi are authorized to patrol markets and may confiscate any items suspected of being polymorphed for the purpose of fraud. See § 1001.4 regarding illusion-based fraud.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Invisibility of Liens",
          "text": "A lien is a legal claim on a property or asset as security for a debt. Under the Autumnwood Accords, the \"Invisibility of Liens\" principle dictates that a hidden lien is still a lien, and any attempt to render such a lien invisible or undetectable through magical means is a direct violation of the Guild’s transparency requirements. If a mage or merchant places a lien on a magical artifact, that lien must be recorded in a public registry or, at minimum, be detectable by a standard \"Sense Lien\" spell.\n\nAn invisible lien—one that has been magically masked so that it does not appear on a standard audit or cannot be detected by the owner of the asset—is considered a \"Shadow Debt.\" The Accords Desk treats Shadow Debts as a form of predatory lending and a violation of the peace. If a buyer acquires an item with an invisible lien, they may petition the Accords Desk to have the lien made manifest. Once revealed, the lien must be satisfied before the item can be sold or transferred again. The presence of a lien, however visible, remains a valid encumbrance on the title of the good.\n\nAny individual found to have intentionally hidden a lien to facilitate a sale is subject to a Paradox Trial for \"Malicious Concealment.\" The Conservators emphasize that the clarity of ownership is essential for the stability of the Midlands. Mages who provide \"clean\" titles for items they know to be encumbered by hidden liens shall be held jointly liable for the debt. See § 700.1 for evidence generally.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "700.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Wish Used to Rewrite a Filed Return",
          "text": "The Accords Desk maintains a strict policy regarding the temporal and causal stability of financial records. It is explicitly stated that a \"Wish\" or any other high-level reality-warping spell used to rewrite a filed financial return is an illegal act of \"Causal Fraud.\" While a Wish may indeed alter the reality of a past event, it does not—and shall not—rewrite the Guild’s official stamp or the chronological record of the filing. The act of filing a return is a bureaucratic fact that exists independently of the magical reality of the contents.\n\nIf a mage uses a Wish to \"undo\" a debt or \"erase\" a past expenditure, the Accords Desk will recognize the change in reality but will maintain the original record of the filing as a \"Voided Entry.\" The mage will be required to pay a \"Causal Correction Fee\" to the Guild to account for the strain placed on the local reality. Attempting to use such magic to bypass the reporting requirements of § 900.1 is a severe offense, as it constitutes an attempt to manipulate the Guild’s oversight through unauthorized reality warping.\n\nThe Guild’s records are considered \"Fixed Points\" in the administrative sense. Even if a Wish makes it so that a debt was never incurred, the record of the debt's filing remains as evidence of the mage’s initial intent. Any attempt to use reality-warping magic to hide a history of magical misconduct or financial delinquency will be met with a Paradox Trial. The Accords Desk serves as the ultimate arbiter of what is \"real\" in the eyes of the law, regardless of what the current reality suggests.",
          "key": "g",
          "refs": [
            "900.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Wario Coin; Non-Recognition",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords do not recognize the \"Wario Coin\" as a valid form of legal tender within the Midlands or any territory governed by the Accords. While these coins may circulate in private markets or as tokens of personal favor, they hold no value in the eyes of the Guild, the Conservators, or the Innovators. They are classified as \"Private Tender\" and are not to be accepted as payment for any goods, services, or fees required by the Accords Desk.\n\nMages or merchants who attempt to demand Wario Coins as payment for Guild-regulated services shall be denied the transaction, and the merchant may be fined for attempting to substitute unauthorized currency. Furthermore, the Wario Coin is not recognized as a valid collateral for secured transactions. If a merchant attempts to use Wario Coins to settle a debt with a Guild-affiliated entity, the debt shall remain outstanding until paid in approved currency (gold, silver, or authorized mana-credits).\n\nThe Guild’s refusal to recognize Wario Coins is absolute. No amount of \"Waluigi-based\" advocacy or \"Wario-centric\" commerce can alter this status. The Accords Desk advises all travelers to ensure their purses are filled with recognized tender before attempting to conduct business in the Autumn Wood. See § 900.4 for identify fees.",
          "key": "h",
          "refs": [
            "900.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault",
          "text": "The use of a \"Bag of Holding\" or any similar extradimensional storage vessel as an \"off-books\" vault is strictly prohibited. The Accords Desk holds that \"off-books\" is a status of accounting, not a physical location. If a clerk can smell the magic, the contents are considered part of the official ledger. Any storage vessel that creates a pocket dimension or a separate spatial plane for the purpose of concealing assets from the Guild’s audit is a violation of § 900.3.\n\nMages who utilize extradimensional storage to hide contraband, unauthorized research materials, or unregistered wealth are liable for \"Constructive Possession.\" This means that even if the item is not physically present in the Midlands, its presence in a portable extradimensional space accessible by the owner constitutes possession within the jurisdiction of the Accords. During an audit, any such storage vessel found in a mage's possession must be opened and its contents cataloged.\n\nFailure to report the existence of a Bag of Holding or similar vessel as a \"Storage Asset\" during the initial registration of a mage's workshop is a violation of the Duty to File. The Guild maintains that the ability to hide assets in another dimension does not grant a right to secrecy from the Sovereign. If a bag is found to contain \"off-books\" items, the owner shall be subject to a fine of five percent of the total value of the concealed goods. See § 900.2 regarding reporting of material enchantments.",
          "key": "i",
          "refs": [
            "900.3",
            "900.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Secured Transactions in Bound Objects",
          "text": "A \"Bound Object\" is defined as any item—be it a book, a sword, a piece of jewelry, or a sentient construct—that possesses the capacity for independent movement or autonomous action. The Accords Desk warns that these items are \"Collateral that can walk away.\" Any transaction involving a Bound Object as collateral for a loan or a contract must include a \"Binding Tether\" or a \"Geas of Immobilization\" to ensure the collateral remains stationary and accessible to the creditor.\n\nIf a Bound Object moves from its agreed-upon location without the owner’s express command, it is considered a \"Breach of Security.\" The lender is entitled to immediate repossession of the object, and the borrower may be charged with \"Collateral Flight.\" To prevent such incidents, the Accords Desk recommends the use of a \"Stasis Ward\" during the period the object is held as security. Any contract that does not explicitly mention the containment of autonomous movement is considered poorly drafted and may be voided in favor of the creditor.\n\nFurthermore, if a Bound Object is used as collateral and it acts with \"Malice\" against the creditor, the borrower shall be held strictly liable for all damages caused by the object. The owner of a Bound Object is responsible for its actions at all times, whether it is currently being held as collateral or is in their own possession. See § 600.2 regarding Familiars as Agents.",
          "key": "j",
          "refs": [
            "600.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Layered Transfiguration Schemes",
          "text": "When determining the legality of a transfiguration, the Accords Desk shall apply the \"Reasonable Toad\" standard. If a transfiguration scheme is so complex, layered, or multi-staged that a \"reasonable toad\" (defined as a standard, non-sentient amphibian with average cognitive faculties) would be confused as to the item's primary purpose or current state, the entire scheme shall be read as a single, unified transaction. This prevents mages from bypassing \"Material Alteration\" laws by breaking a single change into multiple, seemingly minor steps.\n\nFor example, if a merchant transfigures a chair into a table, then the table into a stool, then the stool into a chair of a different color, the Accords Desk will not view these as three separate events. Instead, they will be viewed as a single \"Layered Transfiguration Scheme\" designed to obfuscate the final outcome. If the final result differs significantly from the original item without a corresponding disclosure, the seller shall be held liable for the entire sequence of alterations.\n\nAny merchant who utilizes \"Nested Transfigurations\"—where one change is hidden inside another—must provide a \"Full Schematic\" of the item's potential states at the time of sale. If the schematic is missing or if the item undergoes a state change that was not listed in the schematic, the transaction is deemed fraudulent. The goal of this rule is to ensure that the consumer is not surprised by a \"cascade\" of magical effects that occur over time or through repeated interaction. See § 1001.5 regarding Material Alteration.",
          "key": "k",
          "refs": [
            "1001.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
