    {
      "cite": "1001.6",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A hat that later bills the wearer is an endowment. Catchline from the photo.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets",
          "text": "Any magical asset, artifact, or enchanted implement bestowed upon a recipient by a member of the Mages' Guild, or any licensed practitioner thereof, shall be legally classified as an endowment. This classification applies regardless of whether the transfer of the asset was intended as a gift, a loan, a reward for service, or a component of a larger commercial transaction. The primary distinction of an endowment, as established by the Autumnwood Accords, is the inherent potential for the asset to exert ongoing influence, provide persistent utility, or incur recurring obligations upon the holder. For the purposes of these regulations, a hat that later bills the wearer is an endowment. This specific precedent ensures that the mere act of \"gifting\" an item does not exempt the donor from the reporting requirements of § 900.2, nor does it shield the donor from liability should the asset possess latent properties that violate the Accord's safety standards.\n\nFurthermore, any asset provided as an endowment must be registered with the nearest Guild clerk to ensure that any \"hidden\" costs—be they mana-taxation, psychic feedback, or material degradation—are disclosed to the recipient. Failure to register an endowment can result in the immediate seizure of the asset by the Aegis Magi, as the Guild maintains that any unmonitored magical gift is a potential vector for unauthorized influence. See § 1001.15 regarding the layering of such gifts. If a recipient discovers that a gifted item is subject to a \"billing\" mechanism not disclosed at the time of bestowal, the donor shall be held liable for triple the value of the first three cycles of the billing, plus any punitive fees mandated by the Archmage Sovereign's current decree.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.2",
            "1001.15"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk",
          "text": "All magical items, reagents, and enchanted services offered for trade or sale within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords must be accompanied by full and accurate disclosure. This disclosure must encompass the item’s magical composition, its appraised market value, and any inherent risks associated with its use. The Guild maintains a strict standard of transparency: a disclosure that is true in a language the buyer does not speak is not full. Therefore, if a merchant utilizes a linguistic charm or a complex arcane dialect to describe the properties of a cursed blade or a volatile potion, the transaction shall be deemed fraudulent. The seller must provide a translation in the common tongue of the region or a dialect clearly understood by the purchaser.\n\nRisk disclosure must be specific. A merchant cannot simply state that an item is \"powerful\" without detailing the specific magnitude of its magical output and the potential for catastrophic failure, such as localized rift generation or unintended transmutation. If an item possesses a \"sentience threshold\" or a \"personality drift,\" these must be listed as primary hazards. Failure to provide such disclosures shall result in the immediate voiding of the sale and a mandatory referral to the Paradox Trial for the offending merchant. See § 700.1 for evidence regarding the intent of the seller. The Guild shall not recognize \"buyer beware\" as a valid defense against the sale of undisclosed hazardous enchantments.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "700.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Lead-to-Gold; Reporting Threshold",
          "text": "The transmutation of base metals into precious metals remains a primary area of fiscal oversight within the Guild. Pursuant to the Accords, the transmutation of lead into gold, or any other noble metal, does not constitute a change in the asset’s taxable status until a formal assay has been performed and approved by the Guild's designated assay desk. For the purposes of immediate reporting, lead remains lead for tax until the assay desk agrees otherwise. This rule prevents the rapid cycling of assets to evade the reporting fees mandated in § 900.4. Any mage attempting to sell \"newly minted\" gold without a corresponding transmutation certificate from the assay desk shall have the gold confiscated as contraband.\n\nThe assay desk is the sole authority capable of certifying the purity and \"magical lineage\" of transmuted gold. If the transmutation process involved forbidden schools of magic—as defined by Archmage Theron’s Conservator mandates—the resulting gold is subject to immediate seizure and the mage shall be remanded for a Paradox Trial. No merchant may claim a tax credit for \"material improvement\" until the Guild has verified that the gold was not produced via unauthorized blood tithes or other illicit arcane methods. See § 1001.3 for valuation after alteration.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "900.4",
            "1001.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Polymorph of Coinage",
          "text": "The Guild strictly prohibits the use of polymorphancy or any other shape-shifting enchantment to alter the physical appearance of currency for the purpose of deception. It is a fundamental principle of the Accords that shape is not denomination. A gold coin that has been magically reshaped into a silver coin, or a copper piece enlarged to the size of a dinner plate, retains its original denomination. Any attempt to pass a polymorphed coin as a different denomination constitutes a Grade-A financial fraud and is punishable by the immediate sealing of the perpetrator's magic.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild does not recognize any currency that has been altered to mimic the aesthetic of restricted currencies, such as those of the Regal Empire or the Iron Legion. If a mage creates a \"custom\" coin that bears a resemblance to an enemy's tender, the coin is considered a counterfeit and the creator is liable for treasonous conduct against the Accords. Mages are encouraged to use standard Guild-approved tokens for internal transactions to avoid the complications of shape-shifting. See § 1001.4 for illusion-based fraud.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Invisibility of Liens",
          "text": "The Guild recognizes that magical liens—debts secured by an enchanted object—may be hidden through various means, including concealment charms or spatial folding. However, the Accords state clearly that a hidden lien is still a lien. Any agreement wherein a magical asset is pledged as collateral must be recorded in a public ledger or a shared vault accessible by the Guild's auditors. An invisible one is a violation of the transparency statutes. If a mage attempts to sell an item that is secretly encumbered by a lien, the transaction is void, and the mage may face fines for \"Arcane Obfuscation.\"\n\nIn cases where a lien is rendered invisible via a high-level concealment spell, the Guild reserves the right to \"force-reveal\" the debt. If a lien is found to be hidden from the buyer, the seller is liable for the full extent of the debt plus a penalty of 50% of the item's current value. This ensures that the flow of magical goods remains unencumbered by clandestine debts that could destabilize the local economy. For disputes regarding the visibility of magical contracts, see § 600.3.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "600.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Wish Used to Rewrite a Filed Return",
          "text": "The Guild maintains a strict distinction between the manipulation of reality and the manipulation of administrative records. While a \"Wish\" or a similar high-level reality-warping spell may be used to alter the physical state of the world, it has no standing in the administrative courts of the Mages' Guild. Specifically, a wish does not rewrite the stamp. If a mage has filed a formal magical return or a report of a material enchantment, that record is considered a fixed point in the Guild’s bureaucratic timeline. Any attempt to use a reality-warping spell to \"un-happen\" a filing or to change the contents of a previously submitted document is considered an act of administrative sabotage.\n\nMages who attempt to use reality-warping to circumvent the reporting requirements of § 900.1 shall be subject to immediate detention. The Guild’s records are protected by a \"Temporal Anchor\" and \"Static Seal,\" ensuring that even the most powerful desires cannot erase the fact of a filing. If a mage wishes to correct an error in a filed return, they must follow the standard amendment procedure rather than attempting to rewrite the history of the filing. See § 900.3 for record retention policies.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "900.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Wario Coin; Non-Recognition",
          "text": "The Mages' Guild officially declares that the \"Wario Coin\" is not recognized as a legal tender within the Midlands or any territory governed by the Autumnwood Accords. It is classified strictly as private tender. Because it lacks a standardized mana-back and is not issued by a recognized sovereign or the Guild itself, it holds no value in official transactions. Furthermore, \"points\" associated with its use are even less so, and the Guild will not accept them as consideration for any services, fees, or permits.\n\nMages who attempt to use Wario Coins to settle debts with the Guild or to purchase restricted research materials will find their transactions rejected without prejudice. The Guild will not convert Wario Coins into Guild tender, nor will it provide any exchange services for them. The only interaction the Guild shall have with Wario Coins is to refuse them as payment. See § 1001.1 for financial fraud and manipulation generally.",
          "key": "g",
          "refs": [
            "1001.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault",
          "text": "Any spatial storage device, including but not limited to the \"Bag of Holding,\" \"Portable Hole,\" or \"Pocket Dimension,\" is subject to the Guild's oversight regarding the storage of illicit goods. The Accords state that off-books is still books if a clerk can smell it. This means that while a mage may not be required to list every minor personal item stored in a spatial fold, any items of significant magical power, prohibited reagents, or stolen artifacts must be declared. The \"scent\" of an item refers to its magical signature, which remains detectable to trained Guild auditors regardless of the spatial displacement.\n\nMages who use spatial storage to hide \"off-books\" assets from the tax collectors of the Guild are in violation of § 900.2. If a bag or pocket dimension is found to contain undeclared material enchantments or prohibited schools of magic, the owner is liable for the full weight of the non-disclosure penalties. The Guild reserves the right to \"audit\" any spatial storage device during a standard inspection. See § 900.4 for identify fees.",
          "key": "h",
          "refs": [
            "900.2",
            "900.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Secured Transactions in Bound Objects",
          "text": "The Guild recognizes that certain magical objects, such as sentient artifacts, familiars, or bound constructs, possess the capacity for autonomous movement. Transactions involving such items are classified as \"Secured Transactions in Bound Objects.\" Because collateral that can walk away presents a significant risk to the integrity of the marketplace, any such item pledged as collateral must be placed under a \"Stasis Ward\" or a \"Geas of Compliance\" during the duration of the loan.\n\nA lender is not entitled to the full use of a bound object as collateral; they are only entitled to the value of the object. If a sentient artifact \"flees\" its lender or a bound construct \"walks away\" from a secured transaction, the lender must report the escape immediately to the Aegis Magi. Failure to report an escaped piece of collateral may result in the lender being held liable for the full value of the debt as if the item were still in their possession. See § 600.2 for familiars as agents.",
          "key": "i",
          "refs": [
            "600.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Layered Transfiguration Schemes",
          "text": "When a mage attempts to perform a series of transfigurations on a single object to hide its true nature or to bypass specific regulations, these actions shall be viewed as a single, continuous act. The Guild maintains that a layered transfiguration scheme is read as one scheme if a reasonable toad would be confused. This \"Toad Standard\" is used to prevent mages from performing minor, seemingly innocent transmutations to circumvent the restrictions on more significant, prohibited changes.\n\nIf a merchant sells a \"wooden chair\" that has been transfigured three times—first from a tree, then into a chair, then into a \"chair of many faces\"—the Guild will treat the final sale as a sale of a \"Chair of Many Faces.\" The seller cannot claim that the initial transfigurations were separate, legal acts. If the final result of the layered scheme violates any safety standards or is a prohibited school of magic, the entire chain of transmutations is deemed illegal. See § 1001.5 for material alteration of traded goods.",
          "key": "j",
          "refs": [
            "1001.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "803f4767a07f8d97",
