        "1001.5",
        "1001.15"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "1001.7",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Full and accurate. True in a language the buyer does not speak is not full.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk",
          "text": "Under the authority of the Autumnwood Accords and the regulatory oversight of the Mages' Guild, all commercial transactions involving enchanted,Thaumaturgic, or magically-augmented commodities shall be governed by the principle of Absolute Transparency. It is the decree of Archmage Veyra that the asymmetry of magical knowledge shall not be exploited for the purpose of illicit profit or the endangerment of the uninitiated. Any mage, artisan, or merchant engaged in the sale, trade, or transfer of magical assets—whether such assets be wands, scrolls, sentient constructs, or enchanted reagents—is strictly mandated to provide a full and accurate disclosure of the item’s composition, its current market value, and its inherent risks. Failure to provide such disclosures shall constitute a breach of the Accords and may result in immediate referral to the Aegis Magi for investigation, as well as potential referral to the Paradox Trial for the adjudication of magical fraud.\n\nThe requirement of \"full and accurate\" disclosure is interpreted by the Guild as a comprehensive accounting of the item’s metaphysical properties. This includes, but is not limited to, the potency of the primary enchantment, the duration of the effect, the source of the mana used in its creation, and any known volatile side effects. A seller may not omit a known risk simply because it is deemed \"statistically improbable\" or \"minor\" in the eyes of a practitioner. For example, if a protective ward possesses a non-zero chance of causing localized gravity inversion upon expiration, such a risk must be explicitly stated. Furthermore, the composition of the item must be disclosed in its entirety; an enchanted blade forged from iron infused with shadow-essence must be identified as such, rather than being sold merely as a \"darkened steel blade.\" Any obfuscation of materials intended to hide the provenance of forbidden components shall be treated as a high-tier violation of the Accords.\n\nThe Guild further establishes that truth in a language the buyer does not speak is not considered \"full\" disclosure. This provision is designed to prevent the sale of dangerous artifacts to laypeople or foreign nationals who lack the linguistic capacity to comprehend the warnings provided. If a transaction occurs between a Guild-certified practitioner and a non-practitioner, or between two parties of different linguistic backgrounds, the disclosure must be rendered in a common tongue understood by the recipient. The seller bears the entire burden of ensuring that the buyer comprehends the specific dangers, limitations, and costs associated with the acquisition. Providing a list of warnings in a dead tongue or a complex arcane dialect to a common laborer does not satisfy the requirements of § 1001.7. In such instances, the seller shall be held liable for any subsequent damages, regardless of whether the information provided was technically \"accurate\" in its original form.\n\nRegarding the valuation of magical assets, all parties must agree upon a fair market price that reflects the current state of the item. Any alteration to the item’s potency or composition—whether through wear, magical decay, or intentional modification—must be reflected in the final valuation. See § 1001.3 for specific protocols regarding valuation after alteration. A seller who knowingly overvalues an item by concealing its degradation or under-enchanted state shall be subject to the penalties outlined in § 1001.1. Furthermore, the Guild notes that while the Wario Coin is not recognized as a legal tender of the Guild, any trade involving private currency or barter must still adhere to the disclosure requirements of this section. All transactions involving the sale of items with \"Restricted Research\" status must also be accompanied by a certificate of permission from the Innovators, as overseen by Janna Brightspark, to ensure that the transfer of such items does not violate the broader safety mandates of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1001.7",
            "1001.3",
            "1001.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk",
          "text": "Whereas magic items often possess intricate enchantments or hidden flaws that may not be immediately apparent to the untrained eye, it is hereby deemed unconscionable for any seller to conceal material defects, superior qualities, or potential dangers inherent in the goods offered for sale. To this end, every vendor of magical artifacts must provide full and truthful disclosure of an item's composition, current value, and associated risks prior to finalizing a transaction. This obligation extends beyond mere verbal description and requires written certification by a qualified appraiser when the item exceeds a certain monetary threshold or possesses unique properties that could impact its safe usage. Failure to comply with these disclosure mandates shall constitute grounds for contract rescission and may subject the offending party to punitive damages equal to twice the purchase price, plus all reasonable costs incurred in mitigating any harm resulting from reliance on inaccurate representations. Furthermore, buyers are expressly warned that they bear sole responsibility for verifying the authenticity and condition of any magical goods procured outside official channels, as no presumption of merchantability shall attach to such transactions absent explicit contrary agreement. The burden of proof regarding full disclosure rests squarely upon the seller at all times, with any ambiguity resolved strictly against them under established principles of equity and good faith. It is thus imperative that all participants in the magical marketplace adhere scrupulously to these transparency requirements to preserve public safety and maintain confidence in commercial dealings involving arcane technologies. See also § 1001.2 Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets; § 900.4 Identify Fees; Who Pays; § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.2",
            "900.4",
            "600.1",
            "1001.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk\nIn transactions involving enchanted items or services, full disclosure of material facts is required to ensure informed consent and prevent fraud. A seller must reveal the true composition, estimated value, and any known risks associated with the item's magical properties. Concealment of these details renders the contract voidable at the buyer's option.\n\nFor instance, if a potion purports to enhance strength but contains trace amounts of a toxic alchemical compound, the vendor must disclose this potentially harmful ingredient. Failure to do so constitutes misrepresentation and exposes the seller to liability for resulting injuries or damages. Similarly, when purchasing a cursed blade believed to be a family heirloom, the buyer is entitled to know if the item bears any active enchantments that could affect its performance or safety during use.\n\nThe burden of disclosure falls upon the party with superior knowledge regarding the item's condition and capabilities. This often requires independent appraisal by qualified magi or experts before finalizing an agreement. Only through transparent communication can both sides assess whether the deal meets their needs and tolerances for risk.\n\nMoreover, disclosures must be made in a manner reasonably calculated to inform a layperson of pertinent facts. Technical jargon or evasive answers do not suffice. Written documentation is strongly advised to avoid later disputes over what was communicated. In cases where language barriers exist between parties, translation services should be employed to guarantee mutual understanding.\n\nUltimately, the principle underlying § 1001.7 is one of fair dealing and good faith. By mandating openness about an object's magical nature, the Accords aim to protect all involved from deception and unintended consequences. Whether acquiring a rare scroll or commissioning a complex spellwork project, buyers deserve complete honesty before committing their resources. Sellers likewise benefit from clarifying expectations upfront rather than facing costly litigation down the line. Through diligent adherence to this standard, the marketplace remains vibrant and trustworthy for generations yet unborn.\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk — Continued",
          "text": "Full disclosure under this section requires not only literal accuracy in the spoken tongue but also an explanation of any non-obvious properties that could materially affect the buyer's decision. A seller who states \"this amulet is a simple luck charm\" while omitting its ability to drain the lifespan of anyone who gazes upon it has failed their duty. Similarly, failing to reveal that a so-called \"healing potion\" actually accelerates cellular decay constitutes misrepresentation under § 1001.7(a).\n\nThe risk associated with an item must be quantified where possible and communicated clearly. A seller offering a \"stable mana core\" without disclosing its tendency to spontaneously combust at random intervals has not satisfied the disclosure requirement. The buyer's consent is vitiated if they cannot make an informed choice about accepting the risks inherent in their purchase.\n\nIn cases where full disclosure would reveal trade secrets or proprietary information, the seller must provide sufficient detail for the buyer to assess value and risk without divulging confidential data. For example, a magic shop may describe an item as \"a high-grade component with minor flaws,\" omitting specific serial numbers or design schematics that identify its origin. This balances transparency with protection of intellectual property rights under § 1001.7(b).\n\nSellers who knowingly conceal material defects or limitations face severe penalties, including rescission of the transaction and monetary damages. A buyer may void their contract if they can prove they would not have purchased the item had they known the full truth, even if the seller did not explicitly lie. This principle ensures that magical commerce remains fair and trustworthy in an inherently dangerous field.\n\nThe burden of disclosure rests squarely on the seller, who is deemed to possess superior knowledge regarding their goods. Buyers are presumed to act with ordinary caution but cannot be expected to detect hidden dangers without proper warnings. Sellers who fail to meet this standard bear responsibility for any resulting harm or loss, whether intentional or negligent in origin.\n\nThrough these provisions, § 1001.7 aims to foster transparency and accountability in the magical marketplace, protecting consumers from deceptive practices while respecting legitimate business interests. The goal is a system where all parties can make truly informed decisions about their investments and exposures.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "The obligation to disclose extends beyond mere physical description of the enchanted item. The seller must reveal all material facts regarding the object's magical provenance, including any prior enchantments, defacements, or residual curses that may affect its function or safety. Concealment of such information renders the transaction voidable at the option of the buyer upon discovery. This duty persists even when the hazards are latent and not immediately apparent through ordinary inspection. For instance, a seller must inform a purchaser if an item was previously used in dark rituals, as this may induce unpredictable malfunctions or attract parasitic entities. The standard for full disclosure is that of complete candor; half-truths and euphemisms fail to satisfy the statutory requirement. Sellers who violate this provision commit perjury by omission and are subject to disciplinary sanctions up to and including permanent license revocation. Furthermore, any intentional misrepresentation of an item's true nature constitutes fraud under § 1001.1 and exposes the perpetrator to civil damages in addition to criminal penalties. Buyers should exercise due diligence before entering into such agreements, consulting independent appraisers when necessary to verify claims made by vendors. The Guild reserves the right to inspect any disputed article at its discretion to ascertain whether full disclosure was maintained throughout the sales process. Failure of a seller to comply with these obligations shall be deemed gross misconduct warranting immediate expulsion from membership in good standing and possible prosecution for related offenses under applicable laws.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.1",
            "1001.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk — Continued — Administration",
          "text": "In cases where an enchanted object proves dangerous or defective after purchase due to undisclosed flaws, the buyer may seek remedies including rescission of the contract, refund of funds paid, or compensatory damages for losses incurred. The burden of proof rests with the aggrieved party to demonstrate that the seller knowingly concealed material defects at the time of sale. However, the Guild will assist in evaluating complex magical items and determining liability based on available evidence. Sellers who routinely engage in deceptive practices may face enhanced penalties and mandatory remedial training before being permitted to resume business operations. The ultimate goal is to foster transparency and trust within the marketplace while protecting consumers from unwitting exposure to harmful artifacts. Through rigorous enforcement of these disclosure mandates, the Autumnwood Accords strive to maintain fair trading standards that benefit all participants in the magical economy. Any seller found guilty of systematic fraud shall be blacklisted across all Guild jurisdictions and barred from operating legally anywhere within the realm. Their actions represent a profound breach of ethical conduct and undermine the integrity of the entire industry. Such individuals must face swift and severe consequences to deter future misconduct and preserve public confidence in enchanted goods. The Guild remains vigilant in its mission to safeguard citizens from predatory vendors who would exploit their ignorance for personal gain at everyone else's expense. Justice shall be served, and wrongdoers held accountable for their transgressions against the spirit of fair trade enshrined herein.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.7"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "e9b4bb5bfe883bc2",
      "refs": [
        "600.1",
        "900.4",
        "1001.1",
        "1001.2",
        "1001.3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "1001.8",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Lead-to-Gold; Reporting Threshold",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Lead remains lead for tax until the assay desk agrees.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Lead-to-Gold; Reporting Threshold",
          "text": "The transmutation of base metals into precious ores, specifically the conversion of lead into gold, is subject to the strict reporting thresholds established by the Archmage Veyra’s treasury oversight. It is a fundamental principle of the Accords that the physical state of an asset determines its tax liability. Therefore, lead remains lead for the purposes of the Guild’s tax and reporting requirements until such time as the official Guild Assay Desk has performed a certified inspection and issued a formal Certificate of Transmutation. Any mage who attempts to move, sell, or hide lead that has been aesthetically \"gold-plated\" via low-level illusion or minor transmutation without filing a notice of intent shall be subject to fines for tax evasion.\n\nThe reporting threshold is triggered the moment a transmutation becomes permanent or semi-permanent. Temporary transmutations intended for artistic display or theatrical use do not require filing, provided the duration is less than one lunar cycle and the gold does not enter the commercial stream. However, should the gold be used as a medium of exchange, it must be declared immediately. The Guild maintains a \"Lead Status\" on all uncertified heavy metals; until the Assay Desk signs off, any gold-colored substance is legally classified as lead for the purpose of calculating the Innovators' research surcharges. This prevents the illicit laundering of base metals through \"experimental\" transmutation labs.\n\nMages found attempting to bypass the Assay Desk by selling \"fresh\" gold directly to non-Guild members are in violation of the Accord’s transparency statutes. Such actions are viewed as a circumvention of the Guild’s role as the primary mediator of magical wealth. If a transaction involves a significant volume of transmuted ore, the Aegis Magi may be dispatched to verify the transmutation’s stability before the sale is finalized. See § 1001.13 regarding the use of extra-dimensional storage for such materials.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1001.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold; Reporting Threshold",
          "text": "Whereas certain base metals may undergo transmutation into precious alloys or pure substances through arcane intervention, the Accords establish strict protocols for such transactions to prevent market manipulation and illicit enrichment. Specifically, any instance where lead is converted directly to gold—or any other base metal transformed into a high-value commodity via magical means—shall be considered a reportable event under § 1001.8(a).\n\nThe burden of reporting rests upon the caster responsible for the transmutation. Failure to file an immediate declaration with the Accords Desk within forty-eight (48) hours constitutes a Class A violation subject to severe penalties, including permanent license revocation and monetary fines ranging from one thousand to five thousand gold pieces per occurrence (§ 1001.8(b)). The reporting form must detail the precise nature of the transformation performed, the materials used, the magical techniques employed, and any potential side effects or risks associated with the procedure (§ 1001.8(c)).\n\nIn addition to standard licensing fees, a special assessment equal to fifteen percent (15%) of the fair market value of the resulting gold shall be levied upon successful lead-to-gold conversions. This tax serves both as compensation for the Desk's oversight services and as a deterrent against frivolous or unethical experimentation with fundamental transmutation arts (§ 1001.8(d)). Refusal to pay the assessed fee may result in immediate suspension of all magical privileges until compliance is achieved, at which point normal processing resumes pursuant to § 200.4.\n\nThe Accords Desk reserves the right to conduct random audits and inspections of transmutation laboratories and workshops to ensure adherence to these regulations. Non-compliance discovered during such examinations shall trigger an expedited investigation under relevant provisions of Title VIII, with potential sanctions escalated accordingly (§ 1001.8(e)). All reports submitted pursuant to this section remain confidential except as necessary for regulatory review, in accordance with § 700.2 governing scrying and privacy rights.\n\nThis provision is intended solely to regulate the economic implications of magical transmutation and does not prohibit or restrict the practice itself when conducted lawfully within licensed facilities. Any doubts regarding interpretation shall be resolved by reference to the spirit of the Accords as expounded upon in official commentary and case law (§ 1001.8(f)). Violations of this section are hereby designated as a felony under applicable local statutes, subject to enhanced penalties up to double those normally prescribed for similar offenses committed outside the scope of these regulations (§ 1001.8(g)).\n\nSee also § 300.2 regarding the classification and restriction of transmutation-related arts; § 900.5 concerning false provenance claims involving magical items; and § 600.1 for provisions pertaining to contracts entered into by non-casters in relation to spellcasting services. Any ambiguities arising from this section shall be construed strictly against any party seeking to evade compliance obligations, consistent with established principles of magical jurisprudence (§ 1001.8(h)).",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.8",
            "200.4",
            "700.2",
            "300.2",
            "900.5",
            "600.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Lead-to-Gold Conversion Reporting Threshold",
          "text": "§ 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold Conversion and Reporting Obligations\n\nA fundamental principle underpinning the integrity of the Accords' economic framework is the strict prohibition against unauthorized transmutation of base metals into precious alloys or bullion, a practice known as \"lead-to-gold\" conversion. Such acts constitute both fraud and an interference with the Guild's regulatory monopoly over magical metallurgy. To enforce this ban effectively, § 1001.8 establishes clear reporting thresholds and procedures for detecting illicit alchemical transactions.\n\nAny mage or entity engaged in commerce involving lead ore, refined lead, gold dust, ingots, coins, or finished goods containing these materials must maintain detailed records of all incoming and outgoing shipments. These logs shall include precise weights, purity levels, assay results, dates of transfer, counterparty identification, and payment methods. Failure to comply with this record-keeping requirement constitutes a prima facie violation warranting immediate investigation by the Aegis Magi.\n\nFurthermore, § 1001.8 mandates that any conversion process resulting in a net gain exceeding two percent must be reported within seven days via Form MM-72, \"Lead-to-Gold Activity Report\". This report shall detail the quantities of raw materials consumed, the yield produced, the efficiency ratio achieved, and an explanation of the necessity for such operations. The Desk shall review all submissions quarterly to identify patterns suggestive of circumvention schemes or systematic abuse of loopholes.\n\nMages found guilty of violating § 1001.8 face severe penalties ranging from confiscation of assets and equipment to permanent revocation of licensure. In egregious cases involving large-scale operations, criminal prosecution may ensue under relevant statutes governing economic crime. The Guild retains discretion to impose additional sanctions deemed appropriate to restore market equilibrium and punish bad actors.\n\nThis provision embodies the Accords' broader commitment to preventing magical manipulation from undermining societal stability through devious means. By maintaining rigorous oversight of lead-to-gold conversions, the Guild safeguards against inflationary pressures and preserves fair competition among lawful practitioners. All members are expected to uphold these standards diligently, recognizing their vital role in protecting the collective good.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold Conversion Reporting Threshold",
          "text": "Any caster or entity engaged in the transmutation of base metals into noble substances must file an initial disclosure with the Autumnwood Accords Desk within three business days following completion of said alchemical operation, regardless of whether the resulting product is intended for commercial sale, personal use, or experimental application. This requirement applies equally to both manual and automated conversion processes, including but not limited to artisanal smithing operations and industrial-scale refining facilities utilizing arcane catalysis techniques. Failure to submit timely notification shall constitute a Class C violation subject to standard penalties outlined herein.\n\nNotwithstanding the foregoing disclosure obligation, no further reporting is mandated unless the total market value of successfully converted material exceeds one hundred gold pieces (100 gp), calculated at prevailing exchange rates as determined by the Guild's official assaying division. This threshold exists to balance administrative efficiency with regulatory thoroughness, recognizing that minor alchemical experiments pose negligible risk while large-scale transmutation activities warrant heightened scrutiny for potential market manipulation or illicit fund laundering purposes. Casters exceeding this monetary limit must additionally provide a detailed breakdown of input materials, energy expenditures, and yield percentages alongside their routine filing submission.\n\nThe provisions set forth in this section operate independently of any existing tax obligations imposed upon physical commodities under separate statutes. Lead-to-gold conversions remain subject to full taxation until explicitly exempted by the Accords Desk or superseded by newer legislation enacted through proper procedural channels. Consequently, entities engaging in such activities must maintain accurate records sufficient to demonstrate compliance with both alchemical reporting requirements and applicable fiscal liabilities simultaneously.\n\nCasters found willfully evading disclosure obligations shall face escalated sanctions commensurate with the severity of noncompliance, ranging from monetary fines up to two hundred gold pieces for first-time offenses to permanent revocation of licensure for repeat violators. In egregious cases involving substantial sums or evidence of coordinated malfeasance, disciplinary action may proceed directly to a formal Paradox Trial before an impartial panel convened specifically for such matters. All penalties imposed under this section are cumulative and do not preclude concurrent civil liability claims arising from related damages or losses suffered by third parties as a result of negligent conversion practices.\n\nIt is hereby declared that the obligations herein constitute a fundamental condition precedent to lawful operation within the jurisdictional bounds of the Autumnwood Accords. No caster shall engage in lead-to-gold transmutation without first ensuring full adherence to all stipulated reporting mandates, thereby safeguarding public welfare and maintaining integrity throughout the magical economy governed by these hallowed agreements. Let this clause stand as a testament to the enduring wisdom of our ancestors who foresaw such necessities long ago.\n\n§ 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold Conversion Reporting Threshold — see also § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill regarding automated compliance mechanisms, § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting for general filing protocols, and § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items concerning material provenance verification requirements.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.8",
            "200.6",
            "900.1",
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Lead-to-Gold Conversion Reporting Thresholds",
          "text": "§ 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold Conversion Reporting Thresholds\n\nSubsection 1: Lead-to-Gold Conversion Reporting Thresholds\n\nAll transactions involving the conversion of lead into gold via arcane means must be reported to the Mages' Guild within seventy-two (72) hours of completion, unless otherwise exempted under § 1001.9. Failure to comply may result in sanctions up to and including temporary suspension of licensure under § 800.1(a)(iv). The reporting obligation applies regardless of whether the transformation was successful or yielded a profit; even failed attempts must be disclosed if they involved licensed practitioners or Guild-regulated materials.\n\nSubsection 2: Exemptions and Special Provisions\n\nCertain limited conversions may be exempt from immediate reporting, provided prior written authorization is obtained from the Conservators' Oversight Committee. Such approvals are typically granted only for academic research purposes or emergency medical applications where gold-based treatments are unavailable. Any such authorized activity must still undergo post-completion audit to verify compliance with all safety and containment protocols outlined in Part IV. Unauthorized circumvention of these procedures constitutes gross negligence punishable under § 800.1(d).\n\nSubsection 3: Enforcement Mechanisms\n\nTo facilitate oversight, the Guild maintains a centralized registry of all approved lead-to-gold conversion facilities and their operating parameters. Periodic unannounced inspections shall be conducted to ensure adherence to established standards. Violations discovered during these audits may trigger expedited disciplinary proceedings independent of the regular complaint process described elsewhere in this Code. Persistent offenders face escalating penalties ranging from fines calculated as a percentage of illicit gains to permanent revocation of professional credentials.\n\nSubsection 4: Collaborative Investigations\n\nWhen suspected violations involve multiple parties or cross-jurisdictional elements, affected jurisdictions shall cooperate fully under mutual legal assistance treaties existing between member states. Evidence gathered during joint operations enjoys privileged status unless directly challenged in court proceedings initiated by an aggrieved party. Results of such investigations inform future policy adjustments and risk assessment models employed by the Guild to anticipate emerging threats posed by unregulated alchemical practices.\n\nThis provision reflects collective wisdom honed through decades of trial and error, embodying our shared commitment to maintaining order amidst chaos. By adhering strictly to these guidelines, all practitioners contribute meaningfully toward safeguarding societal interests while pursuing noble goals within legally defined boundaries. Let none forget that true mastery lies not in domination over nature but in harmonious coexistence therewith—a principle enshrined throughout this entire compilation of laws designed specifically for our unique circumstances as stewards of magical knowledge.\"\n\n*End of § 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold Conversion Reporting Thresholds.*",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.8",
            "1001.9",
            "800.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold Conversion Reporting Thresholds",
          "text": "Any practitioner who engages in the conversion or exchange of lead for gold, whether through alchemical transmutation, magical enhancement, or other means, shall report such transaction to the Guild's Assay Desk within seventy-two hours of completion. Failure to comply with this reporting requirement constitutes a Class A violation and may result in immediate suspension of licensure pending investigation.\n\nThe reporting obligation applies regardless of whether the lead-to-gold conversion results in a net profit or loss for the practitioner. The sole metric for compliance is the act itself, not its economic outcome. This provision exists to prevent illicit laundering of illegal gains through seemingly legitimate magical services.\n\nPractitioners seeking to convert lead to gold must first obtain written authorization from the Assay Desk, detailing the intended amount and method of transmutation. Unauthorized attempts at such conversions shall be treated as evidence of criminal intent in any subsequent proceedings. The Guild reserves the right to monitor all reported transactions for suspicious activity patterns indicative of tax evasion or money laundering schemes.\n\nViolations of this section may be subject to civil penalties ranging from monetary fines to permanent revocation of licensure, depending on severity and prior record. Criminal charges may also be initiated in conjunction with disciplinary action under applicable laws. Practitioners are advised that repeated noncompliance will likely result in total expulsion from the Guild's membership rolls without recourse or appeal.\n\nThe Assay Desk shall maintain a confidential ledger of all reported lead-to-gold conversions, accessible only to sworn officials. Information contained therein is protected by privilege and may not be disclosed outside legal proceedings absent court order or mutual consent between parties involved. This safeguard ensures that sensitive financial data remains secure while enabling effective oversight of potentially illicit activities.\n\nBy establishing clear guidelines for reporting obligations surrounding lead-to-gold transactions, the Guild seeks to balance regulatory transparency with necessary privacy protections. Adherence to these provisions reflects a commitment to ethical practice within the broader community of spellcasters and alchemists operating under the Autumnwood Accords framework. Failure to abide by such standards invites harsh consequences designed to preserve public trust in our collective endeavors.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.8"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
