    {
      "cite": "1001.9",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Polymorph of Coinage",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Shape is not denomination.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Polymorph of Coinage",
          "text": "The Guild hereby clarifies the distinction between physical shape and legal denomination. A coin, or any other object intended to serve as a medium of exchange, maintains its denomination based on its recognized legal status, not its physical form. The application of polymorph magic to coinage—be it to change its size, weight, color, or shape—does not alter its underlying value, nor does it grant the user the right to redefine that value. For example, a gold sovereign reshaped into the likeness of a dragon remains a gold sovereign; it does not become \"dragon-tender.\"\n\nAny attempt to use polymorph magic to create \"custom\" denominations or to deceive others regarding the quantity of currency held is a violation of the financial fraud statutes. This includes the practice of \"stretching\" a single coin into multiple smaller coins through spatial manipulation or physical expansion. Such acts are considered a manipulation of the Guild's economic stability. Mages who engage in the production of polymorph coinage for the purpose of defrauding the public shall be referred to the Paradox Trial for the determination of suitable sanctions.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild does not recognize \"artistic\" denominations. If a mage produces a coin that is enchanted to look like a different currency but retains the value of the original, it must be clearly marked as a \"Commemorative Polymorph.\" If the mage intends to use the coin as actual tender, it must meet the standard dimensions and weight requirements of the Midlands' currency. Shape is not denomination; any mage who attempts to argue otherwise shall be fined for \"Arcane Aestheticism\" and required to attend a seminar on the importance of fiscal uniformity.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.9 Polymorph of Coinage and Tender Value",
          "text": "It is declared that the magical transformation of base coinage into gold or silver, known as polymorphing, does not alter the legal tender status of the underlying currency unit for purposes of debt satisfaction. A debtor may discharge an obligation by offering a sum of common metal coins equal in face value to the amount owed, even if those coins have been previously transmuted through arcane means. The creditor shall accept such payment without prejudice and may not assert any claim regarding the metaphysical taint or magical residue present upon the offered tender. This principle is rooted in the fundamental concept that money serves as a symbol of value rather than an object of intrinsic worth, and thus its physical composition remains irrelevant to contractual fulfillment.\n\nThe prohibition against using polymorphed coins for speculative trading or profit-seeking activities stands unaffected by this ruling. While such items may satisfy debt obligations, they remain subject to all other restrictions placed upon altered currency under the Accords. Any attempt to market or sell polymorphed coinage directly violates § 1001.6 regarding the sale of prohibited magical artifacts. Furthermore, possession of large quantities of transformed money by a single individual raises concerns regarding potential illicit activity and may warrant investigation by Aegis Magi in accordance with standard procedures for suspicious financial behavior.\n\nIn cases where the debtor fails to provide appropriate tender within a reasonable time after demand, default interest shall accrue at triple the normal rate until full satisfaction is achieved. This enhanced penalty serves as both deterrent and compensation for the additional burdens placed upon the creditor by having to procure alternative means of payment while awaiting resolution of the dispute. The burden of proof rests with the party alleging breach to demonstrate that valid tender was refused or delayed without justifiable cause.\n\nThe Desk emphasizes that these provisions are intended solely to facilitate fair commercial transactions and prevent exploitation of magical phenomena for fraudulent ends. They do not constitute a general license for citizens to engage in unauthorized alteration of currency, which remains strictly prohibited under § 1001.2 except as specifically permitted by written authority from the Guild Master or his duly appointed delegates. Violation of this clause may result in severe sanctions including permanent revocation of licensure and imprisonment at the discretion of the presiding official.\n\nThis interpretation is binding upon all persons within Autumnwood jurisdiction unless expressly superseded by subsequent legislation enacted through proper channels of governance. Questions regarding application to particular circumstances shall be referred to the Arcane Judiciary for determination based on facts presented under oath. Failure to comply with these directives may lead to escalated enforcement actions as deemed necessary to uphold the integrity of our shared economic system and protect vulnerable members of society from predatory practices predicated upon arcane deception.\n\nsee § 1001.6 Sale of Prohibited Items; see § 200.3 Temporary Mouths for analogies regarding limited-use permissions; under § 1.4 Persons Bound, all citizens must adhere to these regulations regardless of personal beliefs or affiliations. The Desk reserves the right to amend this interpretation as circumstances warrant upon notice and opportunity for hearing.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.6",
            "1001.2",
            "200.3",
            "1.4",
            "1001.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.9 Polymorph of Coinage and Tender Value — Continued",
          "text": "The transformation of coinage through spellcraft, whether intentional or incidental, shall not alter the nominal value established by the Crown's minting authority. Under § 1001.2 Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets, the casting of polymorphic enchantments upon currency is strictly forbidden except under license from the Autumnwood Accords Desk. A licensed caster may employ such magic solely to facilitate the exchange rate conversion specified in their permit, with all proceeds deposited into a designated escrow account for audit by the Guild's Treasury Section. Unauthorized transmutation of coinage constitutes both a violation of this section and a breach of § 1001.5 Material Alteration of Traded Goods, subjecting the offender to immediate suspension and potential expulsion from licensure. Furthermore, any dispute arising from altered currency shall be resolved by reference to the original face value prior to modification, unless evidence proves otherwise under oath. This provision ensures the integrity of monetary transactions while accommodating legitimate magical practices within the bounds of law. See also § 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk; § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.2",
            "1001.5",
            "1001.7",
            "300.2",
            "1001.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Polymorphed Coins and the Integrity of Tender",
          "text": "§ 1001.9 Polymorphed Coins and the Integrity of Tender — Continued\n\nWhere polymorph magic alters the physical form or composition of currency without changing its nominal value, such coins remain valid tender upon presentation to a merchant or debtor. However, any attempt to pass off a transformed coin as unaltered may constitute fraud under § 1001.1 and subject the caster to disciplinary action for misrepresentation of magical effects. The Guild reserves the right to verify the authenticity of disputed currency through arcane inspection, at which point the burden of proof shifts to the possessor to demonstrate lawful acquisition and modification.\n\nThe use of polymorph spells on coinage is strictly prohibited except in cases where the transformation is necessary for safe transport or preservation of valuable items. Even then, the original denomination must remain clearly visible or retrievable without damaging the spell's integrity. Merchants may refuse acceptance of altered coins at their sole discretion and are not obligated to provide change or credit in such instances. Any loss resulting from reliance on a defaced coin shall be borne by the presenting party unless proven otherwise through independent appraisal.\n\nThis regulation stands separate from statutes governing counterfeit production, which address illicit creation of false currency rather than legitimate modification thereof. Both offenses carry severe penalties including permanent sealing under § 800.3 and potential extradition to local authorities for prosecution under civil code. The Desk maintains a list of approved transformation techniques that preserve the essential identity of the object while allowing for practical utility in trade or storage contexts. Violations may result in suspension of licensure until compliance is demonstrated through certified testing protocols administered by accredited investigators.\n\nFor further guidance on acceptable practices regarding magical alteration of personal property, consult § 300.2(c)(iv) and relevant case law including *Veyra v. Brightspark*, 14 A.W. 89 (Autumnwood Cir. 2076). The Guild encourages responsible innovation within established parameters to ensure continued prosperity and safety for all citizens subject to these accords.\"\n\n\"see § 1001.2 Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets; see § 300.2 Unsanctioned Arts; see § 800.3 Seal of Silence\"",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.9",
            "1001.1",
            "800.3",
            "300.2",
            "1001.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.9 Polymorphed Currency and Commercial Integrity",
          "text": "§ 1001.9 addresses the complex interplay between transformative magic and monetary systems, specifically when polymorphic effects alter coinage in circulation or storage. The fundamental principle underpinning this provision is that all tender remains subject to the original design specifications of the issuing authority, regardless of subsequent magical modifications. Any alteration affecting weight, diameter, composition, surface markings, or numismatic integrity renders such currency void ab initio, as it no longer faithfully represents the sovereign's seal and promise.\n\nThe Desk has observed a disturbing trend wherein rogue practitioners utilize polymorph spells to artificially inflate coin value by transforming base metals into precious alloys mid-transaction. While such acts may temporarily yield illicit profit, they ultimately undermine the stability of regional economies and violate the sanctity of standard tender. To counter this menace, § 1001.9 mandates that any party receiving altered currency must immediately cease payment upon discovery and notify local Aegis Magi for investigation. Failure to comply exposes both recipient and dispenser to joint liability for fraud and forgery under §§ 1001.4–1001.6.\n\nFurthermore, the prohibition extends beyond mere visual deception; even subtle shifts in atomic arrangement that alter density or conductivity constitute actionable tampering. Advanced spectroscopic analysis employed by Guild inspectors can detect these microscopic discrepancies with near-perfect accuracy. Any merchant found accepting polymorphed coins faces automatic revocation of licensure and permanent ban from regulated commerce, regardless of subjective intent. The burden of proof rests solely upon the accused to demonstrate good faith reliance on outward appearance—a standard rarely met given the ubiquity of magical augmentation in modern society.\n\nIn cases where altered currency enters the financial system en masse, such as during coordinated heists or border crossings, heightened enforcement measures shall be activated under § 1001.9(b). This includes mandatory suspension of all transactions involving affected denominations until full audit is complete, followed by potential implementation of temporary metal detectors at entry points to sensitive facilities. Businesses refusing compliance risk seizure of assets and imprisonment for obstruction of justice. The Desk retains sole discretion to lift restrictions once verification confirms widespread integrity of remaining tender.\n\nFinally, § 1001.9 expressly forbids the creation or possession of \"counterfeit standards\"—magical devices designed to permanently modify coinage properties without detection. Manufacture, sale, or use of such contraband instruments carries penalties exceeding those for ordinary forgery, reflecting their capacity to destabilize entire markets through engineered inflation or deflation. Violators shall be prosecuted as organized criminals and subject to lifetime disqualification from any trade involving monetary exchange. The Guild views these threats with zero tolerance, pledging relentless pursuit until commercial purity is fully restored across all jurisdictions under its watch.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.9",
            "1001.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.9 Polymorphed Coinage and Commercial Integrity",
          "text": "It shall be unlawful for any caster or their agent to knowingly introduce into commerce coinage that has been subjected to polymorphic alteration, whether by magical means or otherwise. Such coins, regardless of their apparent denomination or visual resemblance to valid tender, possess no legal value and are deemed worthless in all transactions. The introduction of altered currency constitutes a serious breach of public trust and undermines the integrity of the economic system.\n\nTo establish violation under this section, it need only be shown that the accused had actual knowledge or constructive notice of the coin's polymorphed state prior to its circulation. Constructive notice may be inferred from circumstantial evidence including but not limited to: unusual weight discrepancies; inconsistent surface textures or temperatures; anomalous magnetic properties; failure to bear expected mint marks; or receipt of such currency in exchange for goods or services rendered. The burden of proof rests upon the accuser to demonstrate, by a preponderance of the evidence, that the defendant knowingly facilitated the dissemination of invalid tender.\n\nUpon conviction, offenders shall be subject to immediate forfeiture of all illicitly obtained gains and penalties ranging from monetary fines equivalent to ten times the face value of the altered coins to imprisonment for periods not exceeding one year or both, at the discretion of the adjudicating authority. Repeat offenses may result in escalated sanctions including permanent revocation of casting licenses and registration as a professional criminal under § 1205.6. Furthermore, any entity found complicit in the production, distribution, or acceptance of polymorphed coinage shall be held jointly and severally liable for all resulting damages suffered by third parties relying upon the authenticity of such tender in good faith.\n\nThis provision is enacted to safeguard the reliability of monetary exchange within the realm and prevent exploitation of magical technologies to devalue official currency. By criminalizing the use of altered coins, the Accords seek to maintain public confidence in traditional forms of payment while acknowledging the potential for abuse inherent in advanced arcane applications. Enforcement of this clause shall be a priority concern for all regulatory bodies charged with upholding the letter and spirit of these laws.\n\nIn cases where polymorphed coinage is discovered during routine inspection or audit procedures, seizing officials are directed to promptly notify both their superiors and representatives from the relevant financial institutions for coordinated action. All confiscated items must be inventoried meticulously and stored securely pending further investigation or disposition as ordered by the presiding officer. Failure to comply with these procedural requirements may result in disciplinary measures against responsible personnel under applicable administrative codes.\n\nThe prohibitions herein apply without limitation to any form of altered currency including but not restricted to gold pieces, silver coins, copper pennies, credit chits, digital tokens, or any other medium of exchange recognized by law. Nothing contained in this section shall be construed to diminish the protections afforded bona fide holders who unknowingly received and attempted to utilize polymorphed tender prior to discovery of its illicit nature. Such individuals remain fully entitled to seek restitution from those responsible for introducing invalid funds into circulation, subject only to standard statutes of limitation governing civil actions.\n\nIt is hereby declared that violation of this article shall constitute a felony punishable under the full extent of applicable penalties provisions contained herein and elsewhere within these Accords. Courts are urged to impose stringent sanctions on convicted offenders to serve as a deterrent against future infractions and thereby preserve the stability and fairness of commercial dealings throughout our shared domain. Only through rigorous enforcement can we ensure that the sanctity of monetary systems remains inviolate amidst the ever-evolving landscape of magical possibilities.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1205.6",
            "1001.9"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "a35547fd982f7646",
      "refs": [
        "1.4",
        "200.3",
        "300.2",
        "800.3",
        "1001.1",
        "1001.2",
        "1001.4",
        "1001.5",
        "1001.6",
        "1001.7"
      ],
      "unresolved_refs": [
        "1205.6"
      ]
