    {
      "cite": "1001.10",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Invisibility of Liens",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A hidden lien is still a lien. An invisible one is a violation.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Invisibility of Liens",
          "text": "A lien, whether it be a magical debt, a property encumbrance, or a soul-binding obligation, is a legal reality that exists independently of its visibility to the naked eye or to standard scrying methods. The fact that a lien has been rendered invisible, obscured by a veil of secrecy, or hidden within a complex series of nested enchantments does not negate its existence. Under the Autumnwood Accords, a hidden lien is still a lien; it remains a valid legal claim on the asset in question.\n\nThe deliberate use of invisibility magic to conceal a lien is a primary violation of the Guild’s transparency requirements. If a mage sells an enchanted item that is secretly encumbered by a debt to a third party—rendered invisible to the buyer to facilitate a \"clean\" sale—the seller is liable for all penalties associated with the hidden debt. The Aegis Magi are authorized to use \"Deep-Sense\" detection to uncover such hidden encumbrances during any contested trade. Any asset found to have an invisible lien attached to it shall be subject to immediate seizure until the debt is satisfied.\n\nFurthermore, this rule applies to \"quiet\" debts—those that are not publicly recorded but are nonetheless agreed upon between two mages. While the Guild does not require the public posting of every minor debt, the use of magic to hide a major lien from the Guild’s oversight is a criminal offense. If a lien is invisible, it is a violation. The Guild shall treat all such instances as intentional fraud, and the perpetrator may face a Paradox Trial for the deliberate subversion of the Accords’ transparency mandates.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Invisibility of Liens and the Doctrine of Constructive Notice",
          "text": "A fundamental tenet of the Accords is that magical encumbrances, regardless of their visibility to the naked eye or conscious perception of the subject, constitute valid liens upon the person or property they bind. The legal fiction of constructive notice operates such that any mage capable of detecting a spell through conventional divination techniques is deemed to have actual knowledge of its existence. Consequently, failure to perceive an invisible enchantment does not negate its binding force; rather, it merely delays discovery and compounds liability under § 1001.10(b). This principle ensures that mages cannot evade obligations by cloaking them in obscurity, thereby preserving the integrity of magical commerce and personal accountability.\n\nThe mechanism of constructive notice is particularly salient where complex or subtle wards are employed to conceal their presence. Such spells may be designed expressly to deceive even experienced practitioners, creating a false sense of security while silently draining the victim's resources or autonomy over time. Under § 1001.10(c), any mage who knowingly employs deceptive enchantments for the purpose of obscuring liens commits the offense of Concealment by Trickery and is subject to enhanced penalties upon conviction. The burden of proof rests with the accuser to demonstrate that the accused had the capacity for detection yet willfully avoided it, a standard typically met through expert testimony or circumstantial evidence such as anomalous energy readings.\n\nFurthermore, the doctrine extends beyond mere physical concealment to include psychological manipulation designed to induce amnesia or suggestibility in the subject. Techniques such as Memory Wipe and Mind Blank are expressly prohibited in commercial contexts under § 1001.5(d), as they violate the sanctity of personal autonomy and informed consent. Even when employed ostensibly for therapeutic purposes, these practices constitute a breach of fiduciary duty if used to obscure the true nature of magical bonds. Violators face severe sanctions including permanent revocation of licensure and mandatory exile from the Guild ranks under § 800.2(e).\n\nThe implications of this rule reach far beyond individual disputes, touching upon the very fabric of magical society. By establishing that invisible liens are binding just as those visible to all, the Accords uphold a baseline of fairness and transparency in dealings between mages. This prevents exploitation through subterfuge and ensures that no party can claim ignorance where reasonable diligence would have revealed the truth. As stated in § 1001.10(d), \"A lien cast with intent binds whether seen or unseen,\" affirming that the efficacy of magical contracts depends upon the caster's purpose rather than the subject's awareness.\"\n\nIn practice, this doctrine necessitates heightened vigilance among all practitioners regarding the spells they employ and those affecting them. Regular scans for hidden enchantments using approved divination tools are now considered standard operating procedure in professional settings. Failure to conduct such screenings may itself constitute negligence under § 700.4(b), opening the door to liability should adverse consequences arise later. The ultimate goal remains the preservation of a trustworthy environment where magical interactions can occur with confidence that all parties enter into agreements fully informed of their rights and obligations.\"\n\n\"see § 1001.5(d) Prohibited Spells in Commercial Contexts; see § 800.2(e) Permanent Revocation for Gross Misconduct; see § 700.4(b) Negligence in Detection Procedures. A lien cast with intent binds whether seen or unseen.\"",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.10",
            "1001.5",
            "800.2",
            "700.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.10 Invisibility of Liens and the Doctrine of Constructive Notice",
          "text": "A lien, whether visible or concealed, remains valid upon its creation notwithstanding any attempt to obscure its existence from view. Under this Article, all liens arising under the Accords are deemed constructive notice to all persons who deal with the encumbered property, regardless of their actual knowledge thereof. This principle serves to protect the integrity of magical commerce by preventing fraudulent attempts to evade obligations through clandestine arrangements.\n\nThe doctrine of constructive notice operates on the presumption that any individual engaging in business dealings within the jurisdiction has access to and duty to review official records maintained by the Guild's Bureau of Magical Property Rights. Failure to consult these archives constitutes negligence at worst, but may constitute willful misconduct if done with intent to defraud creditors or other parties. The burden is upon those claiming ignorance of a lien's existence to demonstrate that they exercised reasonable diligence in verifying their counterparty's standing prior to transacting.\n\nThis provision reflects the Accords' broader commitment to transparency and accountability in magical transactions. By establishing an irrebuttable presumption that all liens are publicly recorded, the law removes any ambiguity regarding the true state of title for enchanted assets. It thereby deters opportunistic behavior by bad-faith actors who might otherwise seek to exploit information asymmetries between buyers and sellers.\n\nExceptions exist only where the lien is manifestly impossible to discover through ordinary means, such as those attached by supernatural entities beyond mortal comprehension or jurisdiction. Even in such cases, however, the affected party retains the right to petition the Guild for relief upon proper notice and proof of harm suffered as a direct result of the undisclosed encumbrance.\n\nThe principles outlined herein shall govern all disputes concerning the visibility and discoverability of liens, whether arising inter partes or vis-à-vis third parties claiming an interest in the same subject matter. They represent a fundamental shift away from traditional notions of actual notice toward a more robust system designed to safeguard the collective welfare of the magical community as a whole.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.10 Invisibility of Liens and the Doctrine of Constructive Notice — Continued",
          "text": "Nothing in this Part shall be construed to validate or enforce any hidden lien, encumbrance, or magical claim that has not been duly registered with the Autumnwood Accords Desk in accordance with § 900.1 et seq. See § 900.2 (Reporting of Material Enchantments). An invisible lien is void ab initio and unenforceable under any circumstances. Under no condition may a party rely on an undisclosed magical claim as grounds for rescission, damages, or specific performance. This rule applies equally to liens created by spellwork, runes, sigils, or other arcane mechanisms. See § 1001.6 (Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets).\n\nThe Doctrine of Constructive Notice is hereby expressly rejected in the context of hidden magical claims. Unlike conventional property interests subject to public recording statutes, arcane encumbrances do not benefit from presumed knowledge through registration alone. Each party bears a duty of reasonable inquiry into potential magical defects before entering binding agreements involving enchanted assets or services. Failure to conduct such inquiries does not excuse breach of contract under § 1001.7 (Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk). However, constructive notice principles may inform the allocation of risk where parties knowingly engage in high-risk magical transactions without full disclosure. See § 1001.4 (Illusion-Based Fraud) for related guidance on material misrepresentation.\n\nIn cases where an invisible lien is discovered post-formation, the aggrieved party may seek remedies under the general provisions governing breach and fraud. This includes damages for reliance losses, restitution of transferred value, and potentially punitive sanctions if intentional deception is proven. The burden of proof rests squarely with the claimant to establish the existence and terms of the hidden encumbrance. Ambiguities regarding magical claims are resolved strictly against the drafter or party asserting such an interest. See § 700.1 (Evidence Generally) and § 900.4 (Identify Fees; Who Pays).\n\nThis section does not preclude the existence of valid, registered liens that operate invisibly to untrained observers. Such claims remain enforceable when properly documented under Part IX. The prohibition targets only those deliberately concealed from all but a select few, designed to exploit ignorance or bypass standard safeguards. Parties are advised to consult qualified legal and magical counsel before assuming obligations involving enchanted items or services of significant value. Prudence is paramount in navigating the complex interplay between conventional contract law and arcane realities. See § 1001.2 (Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets) for additional context on regulatory boundaries.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "900.2",
            "1001.6",
            "1001.7",
            "1001.4",
            "700.1",
            "900.4",
            "1001.2",
            "1001.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.10 Invisibility of Liens and the Doctrine of Constructive Notice — Contin",
          "text": "The invisibility of liens serves as both shield and sword in the arcane marketplace. While it protects lenders from immediate default, it undermines trust among merchants who cannot discern hidden encumbrances on assets they purchase or invest in. This opacity breeds suspicion and depresses overall transaction volumes unless remedied through greater transparency mechanisms.\n\nTo address this dilemma, the Accords mandate that all active liens be recorded in a public registry accessible to interested parties upon request. The cost of registration falls upon the lienholder, who bears responsibility for maintaining accurate records throughout the duration of their security interest. Failure to comply exposes them to penalties including fines and potential forfeiture of claimed priority status.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords establish presumptions regarding unknown liens based on reasonable diligence inquiries undertaken by prospective buyers or investors. If a party fails to discover an encumbrance despite exercising ordinary care in researching title history and public filings, they may be estopped from asserting claims against innocent third parties relying on apparent ownership rights.\n\nThese provisions balance competing interests between protecting established security interests while preserving market fluidity for honest traders operating in good faith. By requiring disclosure of existing obligations upfront, the Accords promote clarity and reduce disputes arising from concealed liabilities surfacing unexpectedly down the line. Ultimately, this approach fosters greater confidence in commercial dealings involving magical goods and services across the realm.\n\nsee § 1001.6 Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets; see § 200.3 Temporary Mouths; see § 900.4 Identify Fees; Who Pays. The interplay between lien visibility, registration obligations, and equitable notice principles shapes how magic-infused commerce unfolds in practice under the Autumnwood Accords regime.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.6",
            "200.3",
            "900.4",
            "1001.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.10 Invisibility of Liens and the Doctrine of Constructive Notice — Contin — Continued",
          "text": "The doctrine of constructive notice operates as an exception to the general rule of invisibility, wherein a party may be deemed aware of a lien despite lacking actual knowledge thereof. Under this principle, if a mage reasonably should have been cognizant of an encumbrance given the circumstances surrounding their transaction, they shall bear the consequences as though such awareness were explicit. This equitable construct serves to prevent negligent parties from escaping liability through claims of ignorance when red flags ought to have prompted further inquiry.\n\nIn practice, constructive notice attaches where a lien's existence is rendered obvious by extrinsic factors beyond mere secrecy. For instance, if a caster fails to disclose a substantial magical debt while actively promoting their services under a false name, they may be held liable for defrauding subsequent clients who relied upon their purported unencumbered status. Similarly, when a construct owner permits unauthorized modifications to their automaton that degrade its utility without informing the original purchaser, constructive notice may attach regarding latent defects known only to the modifier.\n\nThe burden of proving actual or constructive knowledge rests squarely with the party alleging such awareness. They must present compelling evidence demonstrating how the hidden lien should have been apparent under ordinary diligence. Mere speculation about potential conflicts does not suffice; there must be a tangible nexus between the undisclosed encumbrance and the transaction at issue. Courts will scrutinize these claims closely to avoid undermining the foundational principle that liens remain invisible unless properly registered.\n\nThis provision ensures fairness in an era where magical assets often carry complex histories beyond their physical form. By holding actors accountable for failing to investigate obvious risks, it promotes transparency while preserving the sanctity of undisclosed obligations. The balance struck here reflects a pragmatic approach to evolving commercial norms without compromising established legal precedents.\n\nSee also § 1001.2 Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets regarding disclosure requirements; § 300.3 Quiet List Procedure for analogous secrecy provisions; and § 700.5 Clone as Duplicate Claimant for issues of apparent ownership versus actual rights. Under § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting, all material interests must be registered prior to deployment in commerce. As noted under § 400.3 Borrowed Hours, misuse of temporal assets similarly violates fiduciary duties owed to clients. The Desk reminds all parties that vigilance remains paramount when dealing with enchanted goods or services.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.2",
            "300.3",
            "700.5",
            "900.1",
            "400.3",
            "1001.10"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "c14421401e67271b",
      "refs": [
