    {
      "cite": "1001.11",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Wish Used to Rewrite a Filed Return",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A wish does not rewrite the stamp.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Wish Used to Rewrite a Filed Return",
          "text": "The Guild clarifies that the use of high-level reality-altering magic, specifically \"Wish\" level enchantments or similar reality-warping phenomena, does not grant a mage the authority to retroactively alter, erase, or rewrite a filed and stamped magical tax return or a finalized Guild contract. A wish does not rewrite the stamp. Once a document has been submitted to the Clerk’s Desk and received the official Guild seal, it is considered a fixed point in the administrative timeline.\n\nAny attempt to use a wish to \"undo\" a debt, \"re-write\" a contract, or \"erase\" a reporting failure is considered an act of administrative sabotage. The Guild recognizes that while a wish may alter the physical reality of the world, it does not possess the jurisdictional authority to overwrite the bureaucratic reality of the Accords. If a mage uses a wish to change the fact that they owed a debt, the debt remains on the Guild's ledger as a \"Wish-Nullified Liability.\" The mage will still be required to pay the original fee, and they may be charged an additional \"Reality Interference Surcharge\" for attempting to subvert the Guild’s records.\n\nThis rule serves to prevent the erosion of the Guild’s authority by those who believe their personal magical potency exempts them from the Accords. The Guild is the mediator of magic, and its records are the foundation of that mediation. To allow mages to simply \"wish\" away their obligations would create a catastrophic instability in the Guild’s ability to regulate the Midlands. Any mage found attempting to use a wish for the purpose of administrative revision shall be immediately detained for questioning by the Conservators.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.11 Wish Used to Rewrite a Filed Return",
          "text": "No act of magic, however potent, shall undo the finality of an executed filing before the Desk or its authorized proxies. A casting that purports to erase, alter, or nullify a return once stamped valid is void ab initio and constitutes material fraud under § 1001.6. This rule exists not merely to preserve administrative order but to safeguard the sanctity of contractual certainty—a bedrock principle upon which all magical commerce rests. To permit such retroactive revision would be to invite chaos, for it would render every filed document a mere suggestion subject to whimsical revision by rogue spellcasters.\n\nThe Desk has observed with growing alarm an uptick in attempts to manipulate the filing process through supernatural means. Instances include the use of wish spells to \"un-cast\" returns after issuance, or the invocation of temporal loops to revisit and amend submitted forms. Such acts are explicitly prohibited and punishable as serious offenses under the Accords. The Desk maintains that once a return is properly executed, it stands immutable except by due process before a judicial body or competent tribunal. Any party seeking to challenge the validity of a filing must do so through established legal channels, not by force of magic alone.\n\nIt shall be noted that this prohibition applies regardless of whether the wish was cast knowingly or unknowingly by the filer or their agents. The burden lies upon all parties to verify the completeness and accuracy of their submissions prior to execution. Failure to do so may result in rejection of subsequent filings until such time as the offending return is formally withdrawn or corrected through proper procedure. The Desk reserves the right to deny service to any entity found guilty of repeated violations of this clause.\n\nFurthermore, attempts to circumvent this prohibition by employing proxies, simulacra, or other avatars are equally forbidden. The intent behind the law is clear: no magical trickery shall undermine the integrity of the filing process. Parties seeking exceptions must petition the Desk directly with compelling justification and evidence of necessity. Mere assertion of power is insufficient grounds for waiver of this fundamental rule.\n\nIn sum, § 1001.11 serves as a bulwark against the most insidious forms of magical malfeasance. By establishing clear boundaries on what may and may not be done post-filing, the Desk ensures that all participants operate within a predictable framework conducive to fair dealing. Those who would test this limit do so at their peril, for the consequences can be dire indeed—from immediate revocation of privileges to permanent expulsion from good standing before the Guild. Let it be remembered: once stamped, forever sealed unless reversed through lawful means alone.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.6",
            "1001.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.11 Wish Used to Rewrite a Filed Return — Continued",
          "text": "A wish cast directly upon a filed return, or an instrument thereon, does not constitute a valid alteration under these Accords. The immutable nature of the official seal and ink renders such attempts null and void ab initio. Any party seeking modification must submit a formal amendment request pursuant to § 1001.6, accompanied by appropriate fees as set forth in Schedule A.\n\nFurthermore, the use of illusion magic to mask or obscure material facts within a return is strictly prohibited. Such conduct shall be deemed fraud under § 1001.4 and subject to enhanced penalties. The burden of disclosure rests squarely upon the filer, who must ensure complete accuracy and transparency at all times. Failure to do so invites rigorous scrutiny from the Desk's auditors.\n\nIn addition, any attempt to employ a wish for the purpose of evading regulatory oversight is expressly forbidden. This includes but is not limited to uses designed to conceal illicit activities or misrepresent financial position. The Accords stand firm in their commitment to maintaining the integrity of magical commerce through diligent supervision and enforcement.\n\nIt is hereby declared that no magic, however potent, shall supersede the authority of the Autumnwood Guild Desk in matters relating to its regulatory purview. All parties are reminded of their obligation to comply fully with these provisions without exception or evasion. The consequences for non-compliance range from monetary fines up to and including permanent disqualification from practicing within the jurisdiction.\n\nThe spirit of this section shall be liberally construed to effectuate a comprehensive ban on magical tampering with filed documents. Plain language prevails in all interpretations, and any doubts are resolved against those attempting to circumvent these clear mandates. The Guild reserves the right to pursue civil and criminal remedies as appropriate under applicable laws.\n\nThis provision serves as an unequivocal warning against abusing supernatural powers for regulatory gain or evasion. The Desk expects adherence to established protocols rather than creative shortcuts that risk undermining public trust. Violations will be addressed swiftly and severely, with no leniency shown for those who choose to flout these rules.\n\nIn sum, the Accords maintain an ironclad stance on protecting the sanctity of official records from magical manipulation. Any perceived loopholes are hereby closed by forceful language leaving no room for misinterpretation. The Desk remains vigilant in its mission to uphold justice through unwavering enforcement of this critical safeguard.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.6",
            "1001.4",
            "1001.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.12 Retroactive Alchemy of Financial Instruments",
          "text": "A wish shall not retroactively alter the legal effect of a filed financial instrument, even if such alteration would benefit the caster or their associates. The binding nature of sealed contracts persists regardless of subsequent magical manipulation aimed at nullifying obligations. Any attempt to use arcane means to erase debts, void liabilities, or rewrite transaction histories after the fact constitutes high-level fraud and grounds for immediate sanction under § 800.1.\n\nThe Desk maintains that financial instruments carry inherent legal weight once properly executed, independent of external forces. While mages are free to employ their skills in commerce, they may not exploit loopholes created by wishful thinking or reality-warping abilities to undermine established obligations. Such actions violate the core principles of contract law and threaten the integrity of economic exchange within the Accords' jurisdiction.\n\nConsequences for violating this provision range from heavy fines to permanent revocation of licensure, depending on severity and intent. Repeat offenders may face additional penalties including exile from Guild territory or sealing of their magical faculties under § 800.2. The burden lies with any party claiming a contract has been magically altered to prove such alteration occurred beyond a reasonable doubt. Absent conclusive evidence, the original terms shall prevail.\n\nThis rule exists to prevent abuse of powerful artifacts and abilities in financial contexts, ensuring that magic does not become an unchecked variable destabilizing economic systems. The Desk acknowledges the potential for creative circumvention but maintains firm boundaries regarding the finality of legal agreements once sealed with proper authority. Mages must operate within established frameworks when engaging in commercial activities, avoiding shortcuts that compromise fairness or transparency.\n\nFailure to comply with this mandate renders a caster subject to disciplinary action under § 800.1 and possible civil liability for damages resulting from fraudulent practices. The Desk reserves the right to investigate allegations of magical tampering at its sole discretion, employing all necessary means to ascertain facts fully. Any party aggrieved by perceived violations may petition for review through standard appellate channels outlined in applicable sections herein.\n\nUltimately, this provision reinforces the principle that magic serves as a tool within defined parameters rather than an omnipotent force subject only to individual will. By maintaining strict controls over how and when arcane abilities interact with financial instruments, the Guild protects both its members and the broader community from exploitation arising from unchecked power dynamics between mages and merchants alike.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "800.1",
            "800.2",
            "1001.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.13 Irrevocable Nature of Filed Statements",
          "text": "The written record submitted to the Autumnwood Accords Desk constitutes an immutable fact for all legal purposes, regardless of subsequent alteration by magical means. Even a Wish spell cast retroactively cannot erase the original submission from history's memory; it can only obscure or disguise its true nature. Any attempt to rewrite past filings using supernatural intervention shall be treated as aggravated fraud subject to enhanced penalties under § 1001.14 and related provisions. The Desk maintains independent verification processes designed specifically to detect such manipulative tactics, employing a variety of arcane sensing techniques unavailable to ordinary mages. Parties are reminded that the integrity of the financial registry depends upon strict adherence to these protocols at all times. See also §§ 600.3 (Geas as Unconscionable Term), 900.1 (Duty to File Before Casting), and 1001.7 (Disclosure Requirements). Violation of this principle undermines the entire regulatory framework governing magical commerce, warranting swift and severe action by the Aegis Magi upon discovery.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.14",
            "600.3",
            "1001.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.14 Aggravated Fraud Penalties",
          "text": "Any individual or entity found guilty of magically altering filed financial statements commits aggravated fraud under these Accords, carrying a mandatory minimum penalty of permanent sealing of their magical abilities along with substantial monetary fines. The Desk reserves the right to escalate charges to criminal proceedings in appropriate cases, seeking imprisonment for particularly egregious offenders. Repeat violators may face additional sanctions including expulsion from the Guild and blacklisting throughout the region. Enforcement actions shall be initiated immediately upon notification by any authorized party, without prejudice to further remedies available under applicable law. These stringent measures reflect the critical importance of maintaining public confidence in the fairness and accuracy of magical markets. See §§ 800.1 (Range of Sanctions), 900.5 (False Provenance Punishment), and 1001.2 (Prohibited Spells in Markets). The Guild remains committed to protecting consumers from predatory practices while ensuring fair competition among lawful participants. All members are urged to report suspected violations promptly through established channels to facilitate timely intervention before damage occurs.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "800.1",
            "1001.14"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "a6744dfa0ef5bfc6",
      "refs": [
        "600.3",
        "800.1",
        "800.2",
        "1001.4",
        "1001.6",
        "1001.12",
        "1001.13",
        "1001.14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "1001.12",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Wario Coin; Non-Recognition",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Private tender. Points even less so. Cite only to refuse.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Wario Coin; Non-Recognition",
          "text": "The Guild officially declares that the \"Wario Coin\" is not a recognized tender of the Autumnwood Accords. It is classified as private tender and holds no legal weight in any official Guild transaction, tax payment, or contract settlement. The Guild does not provide any valuation for Wario Coins, nor does it accept them as a means of satisfying any fees, fines, or debts. In the eyes of the Accords, a Wario Coin is functionally equivalent to a decorative button; it has value only in private, informal agreements between individuals who have voluntarily agreed to its use.\n\nBecause Wario Coins are not recognized tender, they may not be used to purchase Guild-sanctioned materials, licenses, or services. Any merchant who attempts to accept Wario Coins as payment for Guild-regulated goods may be investigated for \"Unsanctioned Economic Interference.\" The Guild will not mediate disputes involving Wario Coins, as they fall outside the scope of the Accords’ financial regulations. If a mage wishes to trade in Wario Coins, they must do so at their own risk in the private markets, and the Guild shall not be held responsible for any losses incurred from such trades.\n\nIn any dispute where Wario Coins are presented as a form of payment for a Guild-related obligation, the Guild shall simply refuse the tender. This refusal is not a judgment on the coin's inherent worth, but a strict adherence to the Guild's recognized monetary standards. Mages are advised to use standard Midlands currency for all matters involving the Accords. Cite only to refuse.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Wario Coin; Non-Recognition — Continued",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords expressly reject the Wario Coin as valid legal tender within the jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild Hall. Although such tokens may circulate freely in informal markets and private transactions, they carry no official standing under the Codex or its subordinate regulations. Any attempt to use Wario Coin for settlement of debts incurred before the Desk shall be deemed ineffective, leaving the obligor liable only for payment in standard coinage or guild-approved scrip. This rule reflects the broader principle that all financial instruments must conform to established norms and oversight mechanisms; unregulated currencies pose inherent risks of fraud, inflation, and manipulation by unscrupulous actors. The Guild reserves the right to seize and confiscate any Wario Coin found within its purview without compensation, treating such acts as potential evidence of economic destabilization efforts. Furthermore, no clerk or officer shall be compelled to accept Wario Coin in satisfaction of fees, penalties, or fines imposed by virtue of this Office. Parties desiring to discharge obligations using non-standard currency must first obtain written authorization from the Archmage Sovereign or her duly appointed delegates. Failure to comply with these provisions may result in escalated sanctions, including revocation of licensure and referral for punitive trial under § 800 et seq. The Desk maintains a strict policy against acknowledging any claims predicated solely on Wario Coin holdings; such assertions shall be dismissed summarily unless corroborated by independent verification from a recognized assessor or auditing body. This stance underscores the Accords' commitment to preserving financial integrity and preventing the erosion of monetary standards through illicit means. All transactions involving alternative currencies must adhere to rigorous disclosure requirements set forth in § 1001, ensuring transparency and accountability throughout the system. By firmly rejecting the validity of Wario Coin, the Mages' Guild safeguards its regulatory authority and protects citizens from exploitative practices that could undermine economic stability. Any organization or individual found violating these provisions shall face severe consequences, including but not limited to expulsion from guild membership and criminal prosecution under applicable statutes. The Desk expects all participants in magical commerce to recognize and respect these mandates without exception or equivocation. Compliance is non-negotiable, as upholding the sanctity of currency forms a cornerstone of sound governance within this realm.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Non-Recognition of Wario Coin as Tender",
          "text": "§ 1001.12 Wario Coin; Non-Recognition\nThe Autumnwood Accords Desk hereby declares that any coinage bearing the likeness or signature of Wario, whether minted by private parties or state authorities, shall not be recognized as legal tender within the jurisdiction of the Guild Halls. This provision supersedes all prior customs and practices to the contrary. The rationale for this stance is twofold: first, such currency lacks any intrinsic value beyond its face denomination, rendering it worthless in times of economic distress; second, the proliferation of Wario Coin facilitates illicit transactions and undermines the integrity of the Guild's monetary system.\n\nIt shall be unlawful for any licensed mage or construct to accept payment in Wario Coin for services rendered, goods delivered, or debts incurred. Violation of this prohibition shall constitute a Class A misdemeanor subject to penalties under § 800.1 Discipline; Range of Sanctions. Furthermore, the possession and circulation of Wario Coin within Guild-controlled zones is strictly prohibited, with exceptions granted only by written authorization from the Archmage Sovereign. Any individual found in violation thereof may be subjected to confiscation of said currency and imposition of a fine up to five hundred gold crowns per offense.\n\nThis regulation shall remain in full force and effect until amended or repealed by the Autumnwood Accords Desk acting pursuant to § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild. The Desk reserves the right to issue additional guidance and interpretations as necessary to ensure compliance with this mandate. All persons bound hereby are directed to familiarize themselves fully with its terms and consequences, failing which they shall be held responsible for any resulting liabilities or damages. By virtue of the authority vested in this Codex, let it be known that Wario Coin is henceforth deemed null and void for all intents and purposes within our realm.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.12",
            "800.1",
            "100.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.12 Wario Coin; Non-Recognition of Private Tender",
          "text": "No coinage struck under the aegis of Wario or any affiliated entity shall be recognized as legal tender within Autumn Wood jurisdiction, regardless of face value or purported backing. This prohibition extends to all forms of private currency, whether minted domestically or imported from foreign lands, unless expressly authorized by the Archmage Sovereign through formal decree. The Guild reserves absolute discretion to determine the authenticity and acceptability of any monetary instrument presented for settlement of debts, fines, or fees owed under these Accords.\n\nAny attempt to discharge an obligation using non-compliant tender shall be deemed a deliberate refusal to pay, punishable as contempt of court. Repeated offenses may result in suspension of licensure or revocation of standing practice privileges until such time as full redress is made in lawful means. Merchants and service providers are expressly forbidden from demanding payment exclusively via Wario Coin or similar instruments; failure to comply constitutes unfair trade practice under existing statutes governing consumer protection and fair dealing.\n\nThe Guild maintains a comprehensive registry of approved currencies, updated quarterly to reflect market conditions and political developments. This list shall be made publicly available at all official desks and online portals for easy reference by citizens and practitioners alike. Any queries regarding the status of specific coinage should be directed to the Clerk in writing for prompt resolution. Unauthorized issuance or circulation of counterfeit money is a felony offense carrying severe penalties upon conviction.\n\nArchmage Veyra, exercising her plenary powers as head of state, hereby declares null and void all prior rulings that permitted acceptance of Wario Coin. This order supersedes conflicting provisions in local ordinances or customary practice to the contrary. All parties are enjoined from violating this directive under pain of immediate prosecution. The Guild shall take whatever measures necessary to enforce compliance, including but not limited to seizure of offending assets and initiation of disciplinary proceedings against recalcitrant individuals or entities.\n\nLet it be understood that the integrity of our monetary system is paramount to maintaining social order and economic stability in Autumn Wood. Any undermining thereof threatens the very foundations upon which this society rests. Citizens are urged to report suspicious activity involving illicit currency promptly to their local law enforcement authorities. Together, we shall safeguard our shared prosperity against those who would seek to profit from chaos and deceit. So mote it be!",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.12 Wario Coin; Non-Recognition of Private Tender — Continued",
          "text": "Wario Coin, the proprietary currency issued by certain commercial entities within the Guild's jurisdiction, shall not be accepted as lawful tender for any transaction regulated under these Accords. This prohibition extends to all forms of payment including goods, services, debts, fines, fees, and settlements. The invalidity of Wario Coin stems from its lack of official backing and the inherent volatility associated with private issuance.\n\nUnder § 1001.6 Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets, any attempt to use Wario Coin as a medium of exchange constitutes a material misrepresentation of value and risk. Similarly, § 1001.3 Valuation After Alteration applies mutatis mutandis to transactions conducted in private tender, rendering them subject to re-evaluation should the coin's worth fluctuate significantly post-exchange. The Desk retains discretion to void any contract predicated upon Wario Coin if fraud or duress is alleged.\n\nThe non-recognition policy reflects the Accords' commitment to maintaining a stable and transparent economic ecosystem. By refusing private tender, the Guild safeguards against speculative bubbles and predatory lending practices that could undermine public confidence in its regulatory framework. Parties dealing exclusively in Wario Coin must bear all associated risks without recourse under these laws. Violators may face disciplinary action up to and including suspension of licensure for repeated offenses.\n\nThis provision operates independently from broader prohibitions on financial fraud (§ 1001.1) or manipulation (§ 1001.2), yet complements them in preserving market integrity. No exemptions are granted regardless of the parties' status or intent. The Desk shall issue periodic guidance clarifying specific applications as needed to ensure uniform enforcement across all jurisdictions under its purview.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.6",
            "1001.3",
            "1001.1",
            "1001.2",
            "1001.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.12 Private Tender and Non-Recognition",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords Desk expressly disclaims any obligation to recognize or accept Wario Coin, whether issued by the Wario Kingdom or otherwise, as legal tender within the jurisdiction of the Guild or its member states. Any private agreement purporting to establish such status shall be void ab initio and unenforceable under this Code. This prohibition extends to all forms of scrip, tokens, digital assets, or other non-state currencies that lack explicit authorization from a sovereign authority recognized by the Accords.\n\nMages who knowingly accept Wario Coin in lieu of lawful coinage for services rendered shall be subject to disciplinary proceedings upon complaint. Repeat offenders may face sanctions up to and including temporary suspension of their licensure. The Desk retains broad discretion to determine whether any given form of private tender falls within the ambit of this prohibition, taking into account factors such as the issuer's credibility, the currency's liquidity in local markets, and its potential for facilitating illicit transactions.\n\nThe provisions of this section shall not be construed to limit or restrict the Guild's right to establish its own internal accounting systems and payment methods as it deems necessary to further the purposes of the Accords. Nothing herein shall impair the Desk's power to require all filings and reports submitted under this Code to be denominated exclusively in lawful coinage, unless otherwise specified by subsequent rulemaking.\n\nSee also § 1001.8 Reporting Thresholds for material transactions; § 900.4 Identification Fees payable to the Guild upon filing of new accounts or amendments thereto. Under § 700.2 Scrying Prohibited in Private Spaces, any attempts to circumvent this ban through technological means shall be treated as willful violations subject to enhanced penalties. Nothing in this section shall affect the validity of contracts executed prior to the effective date hereof, except where expressly provided otherwise by the Accords Desk.\n\nThis provision represents a deliberate policy choice by the Guild to maintain strict control over all monetary flows within its domain and to prevent potential abuse of private currencies for evading regulatory oversight. The Desk reserves the right to amend these rules at any time without notice or hearing, as part of its ongoing efforts to adapt to evolving economic conditions and threats to public order.\n\nIn sum, Wario Coin remains firmly excluded from the realm of acceptable tender under the Autumnwood Accords, with severe consequences for those who dare to challenge this fundamental tenet of Guild law. The Desk stands ready to enforce these provisions rigorously, utilizing all means at its disposal to preserve the integrity and stability of the local economy against external pressures and internal subversion.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.8",
            "900.4",
            "700.2",
            "1001.12"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "eb92fc8d979203fd",
      "refs": [
        "100.2",
