        "800.1",
        "900.4",
        "1001.1",
        "1001.2",
        "1001.3",
        "1001.6",
        "1001.8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "1001.13",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Off-books is still books if a clerk can smell it.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault",
          "text": "The use of extra-dimensional storage, specifically the Bag of Holding and similar \"pocket\" containers, does not exempt the contents of such containers from the Guild’s oversight. The legal maxim \"off-books is still books if a clerk can smell it\" is hereby codified. If a mage uses a Bag of Holding to store enchanted items, transmuted ores, or restricted research materials, those items are still subject to all reporting, taxation, and safety regulations.\n\nMages may not claim that items stored in a pocket dimension are \"hidden\" from the Guild’s jurisdiction. If an item is owned by a Guild member and is capable of being retrieved for use, it is subject to the Accords. The use of a Bag of Holding as a \"black site\" for the storage of illegal artifacts or unreported magical assets is a grave violation. If a Guild inspector finds a Bag of Holding in a mage's possession, they are authorized to demand a full inventory of its contents. The \"hidden\" nature of the storage does not provide a legal shield against the requirements of § 900.2.\n\nFurthermore, the storage of \"Off-Books\" items in a Bag of Holding is considered an attempt to evade the Guild's mandatory reporting. Any items discovered in such a vault that have not been properly filed will be seized, and the owner will be charged with \"Intentional Obscuration of Assets.\" The Guild’s goal is to ensure that all magical materials are accounted for to prevent the clandestine buildup of dangerous artifacts. See § 900.3 for record retention requirements.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.2",
            "900.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.13 Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault",
          "text": "§ 1001.13 Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault: Use of Extra-Dimensional Storage for Concealment or Evasion of Taxation and Regulation is Prohibited\n\nThe Accords explicitly forbid the use of extra-dimensional containers, such as bags of holding or similar artifacts, to conceal taxable goods, evaded duties, or prohibited items from detection by Guild Inspectors. Such practices constitute a material breach of filing obligations and undermine the integrity of the economic system regulated thereby. Any party found utilizing dimensional storage for illicit purposes shall be subject to immediate seizure of assets and prosecution under applicable penal statutes.\n\nThe intent behind this prohibition is clear: extra-dimensional space must not serve as a loophole to circumvent lawful oversight. The Guild recognizes that advanced magical technology can render conventional inspection methods obsolete, hence the need for specific restrictions on their application in commercial contexts. Parties are urged to exercise prudence and transparency when employing such devices, ensuring full compliance with all relevant Accords at all times.\n\nViolations of § 1001.13 may result in severe penalties, including permanent revocation of licensure, monetary fines, and criminal charges depending on the circumstances. The Guild reserves the right to enforce this provision rigorously to protect public safety and maintain fair trade practices within its jurisdiction. All licensed mages and entities are expected to familiarize themselves with these requirements and adhere strictly thereto without exception.\n\nThis section serves as a reminder that magical innovation must never supersede legal obligations. While new technologies offer exciting possibilities for growth and development, they cannot be allowed to erode the foundational principles upon which this society thrives. By mandating accountability and openness in the use of dimensional storage, the Accords seek to balance progress with responsible stewardship of shared resources. It is imperative that all stakeholders uphold these standards diligently to preserve the stability and prosperity of our community for generations to come.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Off-Books Storage via Bag of Holding",
          "text": "§ 1001.13 Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault\nThe storage of magical assets in extradimensional containers, commonly known as Bags of Holding, shall be treated as the establishment of an off-books vault under § 1001.2(b). The transfer of ownership into such a container does not constitute delivery absent physical manifestation or explicit declaration by both parties. This rule exists to prevent mages from evading tax obligations and regulatory oversight through mere verbal assertion of possession without tangible proof of control.\n\nThe burden of proving lawful acquisition rests upon the holder of the Bag of Holding at all times, see § 700.1. Failure to produce contents for inspection may result in confiscation under the Aegis Powers granted by the Accords. Any attempt to conceal the existence or location of such a vessel from Guild inspectors shall be deemed an act of evasion subject to penalties under §§ 800–900. The presence of prohibited items within an off-books vault does not alter its legal characterization but may trigger additional scrutiny regarding potential illicit activities facilitated thereby, in accordance with § 1001.5.\n\nMages are reminded that the Accords apply regardless of physical location or dimensional displacement. No clever wordplay shall obscure this fundamental truth. The Desk retains full authority to inspect any claimed Bag of Holding upon reasonable suspicion of violation. Refusal to comply will be treated as evidence of guilt per § 800.1(a). All holders are urged to maintain complete transparency regarding the contents and provenance of their magical storage devices to avoid unnecessary complications, see § 1001.7. The Guild stands ready to assist compliant citizens in navigating these complex regulations while ensuring fair enforcement against those who would undermine its integrity. Trust but verify; it is better to ask forgiveness than permission when dealing with the arcane. But remember: we always find out eventually.\n\n\"Thou canst not hide from thine debts, nor yet from thine masters.\" - Ancient Proverb, commonly cited at Arcane Courts\n\"Where there's smoke, there's fire. And where there's a Bag of Holding, there's trouble brewing.\" - Janna Brightspark, Innovator Faction Leader",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.13",
            "1001.2",
            "700.1",
            "1001.5",
            "800.1",
            "1001.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Bag-of-Holding as Off-Books Vault § 1001.13",
          "text": "The use of extradimensional containers, particularly bags of holding and similar artifacts capable of creating sealed interior spaces, shall not be employed to circumvent the provisions of this Code. Such vessels are hereby designated as \"off-books vaults\" when utilized for storing goods subject to taxation, assessment, or regulatory oversight without disclosure to the Autumnwood Accords Desk. The concealment of taxable items within these dimensions constitutes a deliberate evasion of fiscal obligations and is treated identically to physical smuggling across jurisdictional lines.\n\nParties employing bags of holding as storage facilities must file a supplemental declaration identifying the contents, their estimated value, and the intended duration of retention. This disclosure ensures proper inclusion in subsequent tax calculations and prevents inadvertent double-taxation upon retrieval. Failure to submit such documentation within ten business days of acquiring the container results in a presumptive violation under § 1001.13(a)(2). The Desk retains broad discretion to demand immediate inventory audits should irregular patterns emerge, including but not limited to unexplained increases in declared asset holdings coinciding with purchases or rentals of dimensional storage devices.\n\nIt shall be unlawful to transfer title to taxable property into the custody of a bag-of-holding contract as a means of defrauding creditors or avoiding seizure by enforcement officers. Such transactions are deemed void ab initio, and all parties thereto liable for penalties under applicable fraud statutes. The presence of concealed goods within an extradimensional space does not negate their existence for legal purposes; rather, it amplifies the gravity of the infraction due to the intentional nature of the concealment. Aggravated penalties apply when violations involve amounts exceeding one thousand gold pieces or result in demonstrable economic harm to third parties.\n\nThe Desk authorizes the use of specialized detection technologies capable of scanning for active dimensional anchors and associated artifacts. Law enforcement agencies are granted unrestricted access to such equipment for investigative purposes, with costs borne by the violating party upon confirmation of guilt. Refusal to submit to lawful inspection constitutes an additional offense under § 1001.13(b)(4), subject to separate prosecution. Repeated offenders may face enhanced sanctions including permanent revocation of magical licensure and mandatory community service related to public safety initiatives.\n\nThis provision serves as a critical safeguard against the erosion of revenue streams essential for maintaining law enforcement capabilities throughout the region. By closing this avenue of tax avoidance, the Accords preserve their integrity while acknowledging the realities of modern commerce facilitated by advanced magical technology. Compliance remains voluntary but highly advisable given the severe consequences of non-cooperation. The Desk stands ready to assist bona fide users in navigating these complex regulations through approved channels and professional consultation services.\n\n\"Smuggling isn't about where you hide it - it's about whether anyone knows.\" ~ Anonymous Merchant, circa 742 AR",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.13 Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault — Continued",
          "text": "The use of extradimensional containers, specifically the bag of holding and its variants, presents unique challenges to the Accords' framework regarding financial transparency and liability. Under this section, it is established that any attempt to utilize such items for the purpose of concealing assets or evading reporting requirements constitutes a direct violation of § 1001.1 Financial Fraud and Manipulation Generally. The Desk presumes knowledge of such contrivances on the part of all licensed practitioners and imposes strict prohibitions against their deployment in financial contexts without explicit authorization.\n\nThe core principle underlying this provision is that magic cannot be employed to circumvent established fiscal obligations. Whether through direct invocation, enchanted artifacts, or any other arcane means, efforts to mask wealth or obscure transactional trails are deemed unacceptable. This extends beyond simple hoarding; it encompasses active subversion of the reporting system designed to maintain market stability and protect consumers from predatory practices. Any individual found using such methods faces immediate suspension pending further review under § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse.\n\nFurthermore, this clause clarifies that possession alone does not constitute a violation; rather, intent is key. The mere ownership of an extradimensional container carries no inherent wrongdoing. It becomes illicit only when deliberately exploited to evade disclosure mandates or facilitate fraudulent activities. Practitioners are urged to exercise utmost caution regarding such items and consult with the Desk before engaging in any potentially problematic applications. Failure to do so may result in severe penalties, including permanent revocation of licensure under § 800.3 Seal of Silence.\n\nThe Desk retains broad discretion in interpreting and enforcing this provision as necessary to safeguard the integrity of financial markets. This includes the authority to mandate additional audits or impose heightened reporting requirements on those suspected of circumventing the rules through magical means. By explicitly addressing these novel scenarios, the Accords aim to preemptively close loopholes that could otherwise be exploited by unscrupulous actors seeking to undermine the system's safeguards. All practitioners are expected to adhere strictly to these guidelines in order to preserve public confidence and ensure fair competition within the industry.\n\nsee § 1001.2 Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets; see § 1001.3 Valuation After Alteration; under § 900.2 Reporting of Material Enchantments, practitioners must disclose all relevant magical influences affecting their assets. Failure to do so may result in charges of concealment and manipulation under this section.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.1",
            "200.4",
            "800.3",
            "1001.2",
            "1001.3",
            "900.2",
            "1001.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Taxonomy of Spatial Anomalies and the Definition of \"Off-Books\" Jurisdiction",
          "text": "The classification of extradimensional containers, particularly those known colloquially as Bags of Holding, presents a unique challenge to the standard jurisdictional boundaries established under § 500.0 regarding Field Jurisdiction and Conduct Outside Guild Property. While physical location within or without Guild property does not, in itself, determine immunity from legal process, the internal reality of such an artifact creates a paradox where the contents exist simultaneously in multiple coordinate systems, rendering the application of standard territorial law ambiguous. To resolve this ambiguity, the Accords must address the specific condition under which a spatial anomaly ceases to be a hidden vault and becomes a matter of public record; specifically, the provision that off-books is still books if a clerk can smell it establishes a critical threshold for evidentiary discovery. This olfactory trigger serves as an immediate exception to the privacy protections usually afforded to sealed containers, effectively collapsing the barrier between the interior of the anomaly and the external legal environment. Once the scent permeates the ambient air—whether through a breach in the seal or the natural leakage of contained odors—the contents are deemed subject to inspection pursuant to § 1301.4 Who May Inspect, regardless of whether the container itself remains physically intact.\n\nThe mechanism by which this olfactory intrusion occurs often involves the displacement of atmospheric gases or the venting of volatile substances from items stored within the extradimensional pocket, creating a hazardous environment that may trigger emergency protocols under § 700.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence. When a Bag of Holding is utilized as an off-books vault, the owner attempts to exploit the definition of \"off-books\" to shield illicit goods, stolen artifacts, or contraband from the scrutiny of the Mages' Guild; however, the moment the scent becomes detectable by a certified clerk acting in good faith, the presumption of secrecy is voided. This principle ensures that the Guild cannot be bypassed simply by stacking magical items within an extradimensional space, provided the usage results in sensory contamination of the immediate locale. The legal weight of this rule extends to all entities whose actions or presence may exert a measurable thaumaturgical influence, as defined in § 1.4 Persons Bound, thereby encompassing both the custodians of the Bag and any individuals who have attempted to conceal items within it using spatial manipulation spells.\n\nFurthermore, the disposition of items discovered within such a compromised container must adhere strictly to the procedures outlined in § 800.10 Disposition of Confiscated Items. Where any property, chattel, or artifact has been seized pursuant to the provisions herein contained following an olfactory breach, such items shall be held in escrow by the Clerk until final resolution of the underlying matter. The mere presence of a smell does not constitute a conviction, but it does strip the owner of the right to claim the contents as private property held beyond the reach of the Accords. If the scent is indistinguishable from background magical residue or ambient Feywild aromas, the container may remain sealed; however, if the odor is distinct and traceable to specific contraband, the Clerk has the authority to demand immediate opening without fear of violating § 700.2 Scrying of Closed Books, as the olfactory evidence supersedes the need for magical scrying or visual inspection that might otherwise be restricted by a formal seal.\n\nThis legislative stance is particularly relevant in cases involving high-profile individuals or organizations operating under the guise of legitimate commercial spellcasting but engaging in unsanctioned storage activities, as prohibited under § 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct. A caster who attempts to use a Bag of Holding to store illicit research materials or forbidden school components must recognize that the fragility of their secrecy is absolute; a single whiff of roasting meat, burning ozone, or decaying matter will suffice to invalidate the claim of off-books status. The Accords maintain that the integrity of the magical community relies on the transparency of storage methods, and any attempt to circumvent this through dimensional displacement fails the moment it compromises the sensory environment of the jurisdiction. Consequently, any individual found in possession of a Bag of Holding that has emitted detectable odors into the public domain is subject to immediate investigation, with the presumption shifting from innocence until proven guilty to guilt until proven innocent regarding the source of the scent. This ensures that the Guild retains full oversight over all magical assets within the Midlands, preventing the creation of black-market dimensional warehouses that operate outside the regulatory framework established by the Sovereign.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "500.0",
            "1301.4",
            "700.12",
            "1.4",
            "800.10",
            "700.2",
            "500.12"
          ]
        }
      ],
