    {
      "cite": "1001.14",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Secured Transactions in Bound Objects",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Collateral that can walk away.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Secured Transactions in Bound Objects",
          "text": "The Guild recognizes the inherent danger in the trade of \"Bound Objects\"—items that possess a degree of sentience, independent movement, or the ability to act as an agent of their owner. When such objects are used as collateral or as part of a secured transaction, they are subject to unique \"Walk-Away\" clauses. Because a bound object can potentially move itself out of a physical vault or flee a site of seizure, the owner must provide a \"Binding Guarantee\" that the object will remain stationary and compliant during the duration of the transaction.\n\nAny contract involving a bound object as collateral must include a \"Stationary Clause.\" If the object \"walks away\" during a period of legal encumbrance, the owner is held strictly liable for the loss of the asset and for any damage caused by the object’s flight. The Guild considers the loss of a bound object from a secured location as a breach of the Accord's stability measures. Sellers of bound objects must disclose the object’s level of autonomy and its history of compliance.\n\nIn the event that a bound object flees its designated location, the Aegis Magi may be authorized to track and retrieve it using \"Recall\" magic, the costs of which shall be borne by the owner. Transactions involving objects with high levels of agency (such as golems or sentient constructs) must be overseen by a Guild Notary to ensure that the \"binding\" is sufficient to prevent the asset from abandoning its legal obligations. Collateral that can walk away requires extraordinary security; failure to provide such security is a violation of the Accord.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Secured Transactions in Bound Objects — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1001.14 Secured Transactions in Bound Objects\n\nA critical ambiguity arises where tangible property, once bound by magic or enchantment, transitions into the realm of intangible rights. This section addresses the priority and perfection of security interests attached to such hybrid assets. Where a spell permanently alters an item's nature—transforming lead to gold, or flesh to stone—the original chattel ceases to exist in its prior form. The value resides instead in the magical state itself. A creditor extending funds for this transformation acquires only a contractual right against the debtor, unless specific steps are taken to perfect their interest. To secure a claim on such enchanted goods, the creditor must obtain a lien via official registration with the Accords Desk within thirty days of perfection. Failure to register renders any subsequent bona fide purchaser or mortgagee superior to the unperfected lienholder. Moreover, any attempt to re-magically alter an already secured item without disclosing the prior encumbrance constitutes fraud under § 1001.1 and shall be punished accordingly. The Desk may demand production of all relevant scrolls or artifacts to verify the extent of the magical binding before approving a security interest. This provision ensures clarity in commercial dealings involving magically modified goods, protecting both lenders and buyers from hidden risks. See also §§ 900.2 (reporting of material enchantments), 1001.5 (material alteration of traded goods), and 300.1 (schedules of arts). Under § 1001.14, failure to register a security interest in enchanted property within the prescribed timeframe shall result in the automatic extinguishment of that interest upon transfer of possession to another party. This rule balances the need for commercial certainty with recognition of magical uniqueness.\"",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.14",
            "1001.1",
            "900.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.14 Secured Transactions in Bound Objects – Collateral That Can Walk Away",
          "text": "§ 1001.14 Secured Transactions in Bound Objects — Continued: Collateral that Can Walk Away. A secured transaction involving a bound object presents unique risks not encountered with conventional assets, primarily due to the potential for the collateral itself to depart from the creditor's control entirely. This section establishes rules governing such scenarios to ensure equitable treatment of all parties involved.\n\nWhen a bound object is pledged as security for a debt, it remains in physical possession of the debtor unless otherwise agreed upon by written instrument signed under seal. However, if the debtor breaches any material term of the agreement or becomes insolvent, the creditor may exercise remedies immediately without prior notice except where prohibited by applicable law or contract provision. Remedies available to the secured party include taking possession of the collateral pursuant to a writ issued by a Justice's Court upon application supported by affidavit stating default and value of claim relative to debt owed; selling or otherwise disposing of the property at public auction after reasonable notification published in local newspapers for fifteen days preceding sale date with proceeds applied first toward satisfaction of outstanding obligations owed hereunder then return of surplus funds if any remain following full payment thereof.\n\nIn cases where the collateral cannot be located despite diligent efforts by both parties to ascertain its whereabouts, the creditor may petition a court of competent jurisdiction for an order directing investigation into possible hiding places or alternative holdings consistent with known facts surrounding last known location along with evidence establishing bona fide security interest therein prior to granting relief sought herein. Upon approval granted by said tribunal based upon findings made after hearing conducted solely on written submissions absent oral testimony except as expressly authorized thereto, judgment shall enter against debtor for amount due plus costs associated therewith including reasonable attorney fees incurred during course of proceedings instituted hereunder unless otherwise provided herein or in any subsequent modification thereof executed voluntarily and knowingly by all interested parties involved.\n\nNotwithstanding anything contained herein to the contrary, no creditor shall ever attempt to seize bound objects belonging to third persons without specific authority granted via duly executed power of attorney setting forth precise terms governing such actions including limitations on scope thereof relative to interests claimed thereby or any other restrictions deemed necessary for protection against abuse thereof by those wielding same irresponsibly. Failure to comply with these requirements renders all subsequent attempts void ab initio regardless of circumstances surrounding events leading up thereto unless ratified later in writing signed under seal by affected parties expressly waiving prior objections raised previously hereunder or elsewhere pursuant to laws applicable hereto.\n\nThis provision aims to balance competing interests between creditors seeking assurance against future defaults and debtors retaining maximum flexibility regarding use of pledged assets while preventing opportunistic exploitation arising from inherent vulnerabilities associated with immovable property types generally understood within broader legal contexts encompassing matters discussed hereinabove along with related considerations not explicitly addressed but implied therein by operation of customary usage prevailing throughout civilized lands worldwide today.\n\nsee § 1001.6 Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets; see § 900.5 False Provenance of Relics; see § 800.3 Seal of Silence; Duration and Review.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.14",
            "1001.6",
            "900.5",
            "800.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.14 Secured Transactions in Bound Objects – Collateral That Can Walk Away — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1001.14 governs the special risks attendant to lending magical items as security for debt. Where a creditor accepts a bound object as collateral, they must acknowledge that the debtor may retain use rights unless expressly waived. Any such waiver requires written consent and explicit valuation of lost utility. Failure to account for this creates a presumption of unconscionable terms under § 1001.6.\n\nParticular attention is paid to items possessing autonomous agency or sentience. Lending such artifacts without full disclosure subjects the lender to strict liability for any harm caused by their independent actions, pursuant to § 200.4 and related provisions on delegated authority. The creditor bears burden of proving they obtained informed consent prior to accepting security. Absent such proof, the transaction is voidable at debtor's option.\n\nThe Desk further notes that magical bonds may serve as de facto liens under certain conditions. Where a spell permanently attaches ownership markers or binding clauses to an item, this effect may supplant conventional mortgage instruments. Such \"magical mortgages\" are subject to separate regulatory oversight and disclosure requirements detailed in § 600.3. Creditors relying solely on enchantments without formal registration risk losing priority claims upon default.\n\nFinally, the Desk emphasizes that neither party should assume the other lacks knowledge of inherent dangers associated with the collateral. Debtor representations regarding item condition or potential hazards carry weight under standard warranty principles outlined in § 501.2. Lenders accepting items \"as-is\" do so at their peril and cannot later complain of unforeseen complications arising from known defects.\n\nIn sum, § 1001.14 mandates scrupulous care when dealing with magical security interests. Both creditor and debtor must fully understand the implications before proceeding. The Desk remains available to clarify any ambiguities regarding this complex area of law. Compliance protects all parties against avoidable losses stemming from negligence or ignorance of binding obligations.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.14",
            "1001.6",
            "200.4",
            "600.3",
            "501.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.14 Secured Transactions in Bound Objects – Collateral That Can Walk Away — Administration",
          "text": "The unique mobility and autonomy inherent to bound objects present distinctive challenges for secured creditors seeking to enforce their interests under the Accords. Unlike traditional chattel, a bound entity may elect to sever its ties with its owner entirely, rendering any prior security agreements moot. To mitigate this risk, § 1001.14 establishes specific requirements and protections governing such transactions.\n\nFirstly, all secured parties must obtain written consent from the bound object before initiating enforcement actions against it or transferring ownership rights. This consent shall be documented in a \"Severance Agreement,\" executed by both the creditor and debtor, detailing the scope of permitted activities and any associated compensation obligations. Failure to secure such agreement prior to action constitutes grounds for dismissal of claims and potential sanctions under § 1001.5.\n\nSecondly, creditors are encouraged to register their security interest in the Central Registry within thirty days of execution, thereby providing public notice and priority against subsequent claimants. Registration shall include a detailed description of the bound object, its current location, and any special conditions or limitations imposed by the owner. Unregistered interests remain valid between parties but lack enforceability vis-à-vis bona fide third parties under § 1001.6.\n\nThirdly, in cases where the bound object refuses to comply with reasonable requests from the secured party, such as accessing internal records or providing financial statements, the creditor may petition the Guild for an emergency injunction compelling cooperation. The burden of proof rests solely on the petitioner to demonstrate a credible threat to their security position and that no less intrusive alternatives exist. Injunctive relief shall be granted only upon showing clear and present danger to the integrity of the transaction or substantial prejudice to the creditor's rights under § 1001.7.\n\nFinally, this section applies exclusively to transactions involving bound objects classified as \"High Value\" pursuant to Schedules of Arts (§ 300). Lower-tier items remain subject to standard commercial lending practices without additional safeguards. The provisions herein are not intended to alter the fundamental nature of security interests under common law or create new causes of action; rather, they supplement existing frameworks with tailored mechanisms suited to the peculiarities of bound collateral. See also § 1001.2 regarding valuation methodologies and § 1001.9 concerning disclosure obligations for enchanted assets.\n\nThe interplay between secured financing and bound object autonomy demands careful navigation by all participants in the magical economy. By adhering strictly to the procedures outlined above, creditors can protect their investments while respecting the agency of those whom they bind into servitude. Ultimately, this balance ensures continued growth and innovation within the Guild's expanding marketplace. Under § 1001.8, violations may result in penalties ranging from monetary fines to outright revocation of operating licenses for non-compliant firms.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.14",
            "1001.5",
            "1001.6",
            "1001.7",
            "1001.2",
            "1001.9",
            "1001.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.14 Secured Transactions in Bound Objects – Collateral That Can Walk Away — Oversight",
          "text": "The unique nature of bound objects, particularly those infused with magical properties or capable of autonomous action, necessitates special provisions regarding the perfection and enforcement of security interests therein. Where collateral possesses inherent mobility or the potential to escape the control of the secured party, additional steps may be required to preserve the priority position established under this Part.\n\nIn instances where the bound object is a living creature, construct, or sentient entity, mere attachment of a lien shall not suffice to perfect the security interest against subsequent bona fide purchasers or encumbrancers. Rather, the creditor must effectuate physical possession or registration with an appropriate governmental authority having jurisdiction over such entities. Failure to do so may result in subordination of claims should the collateral transfer hands without notice of the prior security agreement.\n\nFurthermore, creditors holding security interests in bound objects shall exercise due diligence in monitoring the condition and location thereof throughout the duration of the credit extension. Unreasonable neglect or failure to inspect may constitute waiver of rights under applicable law and custom. In particular, where the object exhibits signs of distress, degradation, or attempted liquidation by unauthorized parties, prompt remedial action must be taken to mitigate losses.\n\nWhere collateral is capable of self-replication or metamorphosis, additional safeguards shall be employed to prevent dilution or depreciation of value. This may include contractual restrictions on reproduction methods or mandatory submission of newly created units for inspection and certification. Any material deviation from approved specifications shall constitute an event of default triggering acceleration of obligations under the security instrument.\n\nIn sum, the secured party must maintain vigilant oversight of collateral possessing unusual characteristics to preserve its priority status. Adherence to these heightened standards ensures fair treatment of all parties engaged in transactions involving bound objects within the commercial framework established by this Code.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.14"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "d9ee01c8955800de",
