    {
      "cite": "1001.15",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Layered Transfiguration Schemes",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Read as one scheme if a reasonable toad would be confused.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Layered Transfiguration Schemes",
          "text": "The Guild hereby establishes the \"Reasonable Toad\" standard for the interpretation of complex magical transmutations. If a mage employs a series of layered transfigurations—where an object is changed into one form, then another, then a third, and so on—the entire sequence shall be read as a single, continuous scheme if a reasonable toad would be confused by the intermediate steps. This prevents mages from using \"nested\" transmutations to bypass the Guild’s reporting thresholds or to hide the original nature of an asset.\n\nFor example, if a mage transmutes lead into a copper pot, then into a silver bowl, and finally into a gold goblet, the Guild will view this as a single transmutation of lead into gold. The intermediate steps of \"copper\" and \"silver\" do not reset the reporting requirements or exempt the mage from the taxes associated with the final gold product. The intent of the mage is the primary factor in legal determination; if the sequence of changes is clearly designed to obfuscate the final result from the Assay Desk, it shall be treated as a fraudulent \"Layered Scheme.\"\n\nAll mages who engage in complex transmutations are required to file a \"Sequence of Intent\" form before beginning the process. This form must outline the final intended form and the steps required to reach it. If a mage fails to file such a form and the final result is found to be a violation of the Accords, the Guild may impose the maximum penalty for \"Deceptive Transmutation.\" The Guild's goal is to ensure that the complexity of a spell does not provide a loophole for the evasion of the Accords.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.15 Layered Transfiguration Schemes",
          "text": "A transaction utilizing multiple, sequentially applied transfigurations to obscure the true nature or value of goods constitutes a violation of this section. Whether the spells are cast contemporaneously or in succession is irrelevant; what matters is their combined effect on the item's provenance and worth. For example, an enchanted chest containing gold may be first wrapped in lead to prevent detection by divination magic, then covered in fur to mimic a beastskin pouch, and finally treated with illusion wards to appear empty upon inspection. Each layer serves to mask reality from prying eyes or magical scrutiny, rendering the scheme inherently deceptive under § 1001.7.\n\nThe Desk finds that such layered manipulations often indicate an attempt to evade existing prohibitions on specific magics or commodities. In one notable case, a merchant sought to smuggle restricted alchemical reagents by encasing them in lead-lined boxes coated with anti-scrying residue and disguised as mundane crates. The intent was clear: to bypass both physical searches and magical inspections simultaneously. This approach directly contravenes the spirit of § 300.2 regarding controlled substances, even if technically falling outside its literal scope.\n\nLayered schemes also frequently arise in attempts to launder illicit gains through complex chains of ownership and transmutation. A criminal might first polymorph stolen artifacts into base materials, then recombine them using intricate enchantments that erase all traces of their origin. The end result is a seemingly ordinary object with no connection whatsoever to its unlawful source. Such obfuscation undermines the very purpose of financial reporting mandated by § 1001.7 and enables money laundering via magical means.\n\nThe burden rests on any party claiming good faith reliance on such schemes to prove beyond reasonable doubt that they had no knowledge of their deceptive nature. Given the sophisticated technology available in modern mages' guilds, ignorance is seldom an acceptable defense against charges of fraud or evasion. The Desk will presume guilt unless rebutted by compelling evidence of innocence or duress.\n\nUltimately, § 1001.15 exists to curb increasingly cunning attempts to game the system through arcane artifice. As magical technology advances, so too do methods of concealment and obfuscation. This provision ensures that no matter how elaborate the scheme becomes, it remains subject to scrutiny and sanction under the Accords. The Desk retains broad discretion to interpret what constitutes a \"layered transfiguration\" in light of evolving practices and precedents.\n\nsee § 1001.4 for guidance on illusion-based fraud; see § 1001.5 regarding material alteration of goods; see § 1001.7 for obligations of disclosure; see § 900.2 on reporting requirements for enchanted items. Violations of this section may result in sanctions up to and including permanent sealing under § 800.3, depending on circumstances.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.7",
            "300.2",
            "1001.15",
            "1001.4",
            "1001.5",
            "900.2",
            "800.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.15 Layered Transfiguration Schemes — Continued",
          "text": "A \"Layered Transfiguration Scheme\" is defined herein as any sequence of spells or procedures wherein an initial alteration creates conditions that facilitate subsequent, more profound changes to the subject matter. Such schemes are deemed inherently deceptive and thus prohibited under this section. The prohibition extends not only to the final result but also to every intermediate step employed to achieve it.\n\nFor example, a caster who first uses a minor illusion spell to conceal the true nature of an item, then employs a more powerful enchantment to permanently alter its properties, has engaged in a Layered Transfiguration Scheme even though each individual spell might otherwise be legal. The scheme lies in the deliberate ordering of operations designed to circumvent detection and regulation.\n\nThe burden of proof rests upon the Accuser to demonstrate that the accused utilized multiple steps with the intent of evading oversight. This may include testimony from witnesses, analysis of magical residue, or reconstruction of events based on available evidence. Mere suspicion is insufficient for conviction; however, a preponderance of circumstantial indicators will suffice given the clandestine nature of such activities.\n\nDefendants charged under this section shall be entitled to call expert witnesses to testify as to whether their actions constituted a Layered Transfiguration Scheme or merely coincidental use of multiple spells. The standard of proof remains high: clear and convincing evidence that the accused acted with deceptive intent is required before sanctions may be imposed.\n\nIn cases where layered schemes are uncovered, penalties shall be calibrated to reflect both the complexity of the operation and any resulting harm to public safety or economic stability. Aggravating factors include prior violations, refusal to cooperate with investigation, or evidence that similar activities were conducted on a broader scale. Mitigating circumstances may include voluntary disclosure before discovery by authorities, full restitution of losses incurred, and genuine remorse demonstrated through corrective action.\n\nThe provisions herein are intended to close loopholes exploited by cunning mages seeking to skirt the spirit if not the letter of the law. By targeting the methodology rather than just the outcome, this section ensures that no evasive tactic can render transfiguration magic beyond regulatory reach. It represents a fundamental shift in enforcement philosophy, emphasizing proactive oversight over reactive punishment.\n\nThis clause shall be construed liberally to encompass all forms of deceptive magical practice, now or later developed. The Desk reserves authority to issue additional guidance as needed to clarify its application in evolving contexts. All persons subject to these Accords are hereby put on notice that any attempt to game the system through layered operations will meet with swift and severe consequences. Compliance is not optional—it is mandatory for all licensed practitioners operating within Autumnwood's jurisdictional boundaries.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.15"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.15 Layered Transfiguration Schemes — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1001.15 Layered Transfiguration Schemes, in the sense applied herein, refers to those complex arrangements wherein magical effects are compounded or nested such that their individual components may not be readily discernible upon casual inspection. A classic example involves a gemstone enchanted first with an illusion of wealth, then concealed within a bag-of-holding spell, which is itself disguised as a mundane trinket worn by the owner. To a novice observer—or even to a clerk unfamiliar with such stratagems—the object appears innocuous; yet its true value may exceed that of the desk upon which it rests.\n\nThe Accords treat these layered schemes not merely as curiosities, but as potential instruments of fraud and evasion. For where one can see only surface appearances, the cunning mage perceives hidden depths. And in those depths lies the capacity to mislead, deceive, or conceal assets from proper taxation or seizure. Henceforth, all such arrangements shall be deemed \"material facts\" subject to full disclosure prior to any transaction involving magical goods or services. Failure to reveal their existence may constitute grounds for revocation of licensure, and in cases of deliberate obfuscation designed to avoid liability, punitive damages may also be awarded at the discretion of the Desk.\n\nIt bears emphasis that this provision does not prohibit innovation per se; rather, it mandates transparency where ambiguity threatens public interest. A well-documented enchantment applied openly for legitimate purposes remains fully compliant with existing statutes. The prohibition arises only when the very nature of the scheme renders its true scope unknowable until too late. Let no one mistake caution for censorship—we encourage boldness within bounds, not recklessness under cover of secrecy.\n\nAccordingly, every licensee is charged with exercising due diligence in both creating and disclosing any layered transfiguration schemes involving their property or services. Inquiries regarding specific applications shall be directed to the Conservators' Office of Magical Standards, which maintains a registry of approved designs for reference purposes only. Unauthorized experimentation falls strictly outside the scope of this Code, regardless of intent or outcome. The Desk retains sole authority to determine whether any given arrangement complies with applicable laws and regulations as they may evolve over time.\n\nSee also § 1001.3 Valuation After Alteration (regarding disclosure obligations); § 900.2 Reporting of Material Enchantments (for related filing requirements); and Part II, Chapter 4 (on research permits for novel constructs). Violators subject to sanctions under §§ 800.1–800.5 as appropriate.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.15",
            "1001.3",
            "900.2",
            "800.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.15 Layered Transfiguration Schemes — Continued — Administration",
          "text": "§ 1001.15 Layered Transfiguration Schemes — Continued\n\nA particularly insidious form of fraud involves the application of multiple, compounding transmutation spells to a single item, each designed to obscure the true nature and value of the underlying object. For example, a base material such as lead may first be transmuted into gold via a primary spell, then further enchanted with an illusionist's trick to appear unblemished and lustrous despite potential flaws or wear. Additional layers of magic might include a memory charm to erase evidence of previous alterations, a clone created solely for the purpose of holding the original item while the fraudulent article is marketed under false pretenses, and finally a seal of silence cast upon all witnesses to prevent them from testifying against the perpetrator.\n\nThe Desk takes strong exception to such elaborate schemes, viewing them as an egregious abuse of arcane power for purely nefarious ends. Under § 1001.6, any endowment or gift containing hidden enchantments must be fully disclosed before acceptance; failure to do so constitutes material misrepresentation and grounds for immediate revocation of the transaction. Moreover, the cumulative effect of multiple spells applied in succession often exceeds safe operational limits, posing a genuine threat to public safety should the item later come into competent hands. The Guild makes no distinction between simple forgery and complex transfiguration fraud; both are treated as serious breaches of trust and subject to full penal sanctions under Title VIII.\n\nClerks are directed to scrutinize all high-value transactions with heightened vigilance, particularly those involving enchanted goods or services. Any suspicious circumstances should be promptly reported for investigation by the Aegis Magi, who possess the authority to seize disputed items and interrogate involved parties as needed. The Desk retains broad discretion to impose additional requirements on repeat offenders, ranging from mandatory retraining courses to outright suspension of licensure pending further review. Ultimately, it is incumbent upon every registered caster to uphold the highest standards of integrity in their professional dealings; anything less will not be tolerated under the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.15",
            "1001.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1001.15 Layered Transfiguration Schemes — Continued — Administration — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1001.15 Layered Transfiguration Schemes — Continued — Administration shall govern the procedural handling of complex, multi-stage transmutation operations involving nested magical effects or sequential alterations to a single object. Such layered schemes present unique challenges for valuation, disclosure, and regulatory compliance, requiring heightened scrutiny from both the Mages' Guild Desk and affected third parties. Under this provision, any party initiating a series of enchantments upon an item must file a detailed Breakdown of Effects with the appropriate regional office prior to commencing work. This document shall enumerate each individual spell or alteration, its intended function, the materials consumed, and the projected final state of the object post-completion. Failure to provide this comprehensive disclosure may result in immediate suspension of all related permits and potential disciplinary action against the responsible caster. Furthermore, any mid-process deviations from the originally filed plan must be reported within forty-eight hours via supplementary filing. Unreported changes that materially alter the risk profile or economic value of the finished product shall constitute grounds for revocation of licensure and imposition of civil penalties up to five times the standard fee schedule. The Mages' Guild retains broad discretion to order independent audits whenever evidence suggests concealment of material facts regarding layered transfiguration activities. Any party sustaining demonstrable loss due to non-compliance with these administrative requirements may bring a direct action for damages before the Accords Desk, without need to exhaust other remedies first. This section operates in conjunction with §§ 1001.3, 1001.6, and 900.2 regarding disclosure obligations and record retention mandates applicable to all magical service providers operating within Guild jurisdiction. Violations may also trigger referral for criminal prosecution under relevant statutes where appropriate. The Desk shall promulgate additional guidance documents as needed to clarify implementation specifics without altering the substantive requirements herein set forth. All provisions of this Article remain fully enforceable notwithstanding any conflicting internal policies or unwritten customs contrary hereto.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.15",
            "1001.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
