    {
      "cite": "1101.1",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Gifts That Later Move",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A gift with a remaining geas is not a gift.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Gifts That Later Move",
          "text": "A gift with a remaining geas is not a gift. Under the prevailing statutes of the Autumnwood Accords, a \"gift\" is defined as a voluntary transfer of magical, physical, or metaphysical property without the expectation of future consideration or the imposition of lingering obligations. When a donor purports to bestow an item upon a recipient, but the item remains bound by a geas, enchantment, or lingering magical tether that dictates the recipient's actions, the transfer is legally categorized as a \"conditional conveyance\" rather than a gift. Such an act constitutes a violation of the principle of free agency and may be subject to immediate seizure by the Aegis Magi. If the geas is active at the time of transfer, the donor has effectively bypassed the requirements of a formal contract, and the recipient is not entitled to the protections afforded to gift-receivers. See § 1001.6.\n\nFurthermore, the presence of a lingering geas implies a continuing interest of the donor in the object's utility or the recipient's behavior. This is a fundamental breach of the Accords’ guidelines on the movement of magical assets. Any individual seeking to dispose of a geased object as a gift must first undergo a \"Purification of Intent\" at a recognized Guild station to ensure all ties to the original owner are severed. Failure to do so shall result in the item being declared \"Contraband of Bound Origin.\" In cases where the geas is clandestine—hidden from the recipient by illusion or complex layering—the donor shall be held liable for \"Malicious Tethering,\" a felony under the current Innovator-Conservator mediation.\n\nThe Accords Desk maintains that a gift must be a clean break. If a hat, for instance, begins to bill its wearer for \"maintenance\" or \"protection\" after being gifted, it is not a gift but an endowment or a hidden lease. The legal fiction of the gift is dissolved the moment a new obligation is detected. Any party attempting to argue that the geas was a \"feature\" of the gift is barred from such defense unless the geas was explicitly disclosed in a written deed of transfer. For the purposes of the Paradox Trial, any mage who uses a geased gift to compel a peer into an unauthorized action shall be treated as if they had directly cast the compulsion themselves. See § 800.2.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1001.6",
            "800.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Relics; Chain of Title",
          "text": "Provenance, Quiet List relics. All relics of significant arcane magnitude, historical weight, or cultural importance must possess a clear and unbroken chain of title as recognized by the Accords Desk. A relic is defined as any object that has been the focus of a major magical event, a seat of power for a recognized lineage, or an artifact of the Quiet List. For items originating from the Quiet List, the chain of title is inherently restricted; while the existence of such items is not a matter of public record, their movement between private collectors and Guild-sanctioned vaults must be documented via \"Shadow Ledgers.\" Any relic whose provenance is obscured by more than three generations of ownership, or which lacks a verified signature from a known artisan or mage of standing, shall be subject to a \"Provenance Inquiry.\"\n\nDuring a Provenance Inquiry, the Aegis Magi may seize the artifact for a period not to exceed thirty days to verify its history. If the artifact is found to be a product of the Iron Mandate or the Regal Empire’s illicit seizures, it shall be confiscated immediately. Furthermore, relics associated with the Silver Flame or the Cosmic Jesters are subject to heightened scrutiny and may be declared \"Hostile Assets\" if their chain of title involves any recognized agents of these factions. The Accords Desk does not recognize \"finders keepers\" as a valid basis for the acquisition of relics; any item of power found in the wild must be reported to the nearest Conservator outpost for appraisal and registration.\n\nThe Quiet List, while not a public catalogue, serves as the primary registry for items that pose a risk to the stability of the Midlands. Relics held by the public that are found to be on the Quiet List without proper authorization shall be placed under \"Active Custodianship.\" Owners of such relics are granted the right to possess them but are strictly prohibited from displaying, selling, or lending them to non-Guild entities. Any breach of these restrictions shall result in the immediate revocation of the owner's right of possession and the initiation of a Paradox Trial to determine if the relic’s misuse constitutes a threat to the Accords. See § 800.2.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "800.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Curse as Warranty Disclaimer",
          "text": "A curse is not 'as is' unless disclosed. In the commerce of magical artifacts and enchanted goods, the \"As Is\" clause is frequently invoked by vendors to absolve themselves of liability for the inherent properties of an item. However, the Autumnwood Accords explicitly state that a curse—defined as a persistent, harmful, or restrictive magical effect intended to afflict the owner or user—does not qualify as an \"inherent property\" for the purpose of warranty disclaimer. If a vendor sells a cursed object without a prominent, magically-detectable warning, they are in violation of the Accords. The burden of disclosure lies with the seller; the buyer is not required to possess advanced scrying capabilities to identify a hidden curse.\n\nAny contract of sale that includes a \"Curse as Warranty Disclaimer\" is considered null and void. The presence of a curse is viewed as a defect in the item's utility, much like a structural flaw in a physical building. If a buyer discovers a curse that was not disclosed at the time of sale, they are entitled to a full refund of the purchase price, plus any \"Restoration Fees\" incurred to cleanse the object. The seller may be further fined for \"Negligent Enchantment,\" particularly if the curse is of a nature that causes permanent physical or mental harm. The Aegis Magi are authorized to perform \"Truth-Binding\" on vendors who claim they were unaware of the curse's existence.\n\nFor the purposes of the Accords Desk, a \"curse\" is distinguished from a \"volatile enchantment.\" A volatile enchantment is a known risk associated with high-level magic (such as a rift-prone focus) and may be sold with a standard warning. A curse, however, is a targeted malicious effect. Therefore, while a mage may sell a staff that has a chance of exploding (volatile), they may not sell a staff that actively drains the user's life force (curse) without a specific, notarized disclosure. Failure to provide such disclosure shall result in the immediate suspension of the vendor’s Guild license and a mandatory appearance before the Conservators. See § 800.1.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "800.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Blessing of a Competing Vendor",
          "text": "A blessing sold against a rival is advertising, then fraud if it binds. The Accords recognize the right of mages to engage in the trade of blessings, which are categorized as \"Positive Efficacy Charms.\" However, when a blessing is specifically designed to provide a mechanical or magical advantage over a competitor’s product—for example, a blessing that causes a rival’s shield to shatter upon contact—it is classified as \"Aggressive Advertising.\" Such blessings must be clearly labeled as \"Competitive Efficacy\" and are subject to a higher licensing fee. If a blessing is sold with the intent to sabotage a rival's goods, the seller must ensure that the effect is localized and does not cause collateral damage to the general populace or the Midlands' infrastructure.\n\nFraud occurs when a blessing sold against a rival contains a \"Binding Clause\" or a \"Hidden Malus.\" If a mage sells a \"Loyalty Blessing\" for a customer that secretly binds the customer to the mage’s own services while simultaneously degrading the customer's ability to use any other merchant's products, this is a violation of the Accords. The transition from \"Aggressive Advertising\" to \"Fraud\" occurs at the moment the magic exerts unauthorized control over the recipient’s agency or financial independence. The Accords Desk views the use of magic to create \"Monopolistic Bindings\" as a severe violation of the principles of fair trade and magical stability.\n\nAny party found guilty of such fraud shall be subject to a Paradox Trial. The punishment for \"Binding Fraud\" is often the \"Seal of Silence\" on the offending mage's commercial magic, preventing them from selling any blessings for a period of no less than ten years. Furthermore, the mage may be required to issue a \"Public Rectification,\" a magically-broadcasted apology to all affected customers. The Aegis Magi are tasked with monitoring the marketplaces of the Midlands to ensure that blessings remain tools of protection and enhancement rather than instruments of economic enslavement. See § 800.3.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "800.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.1 Gifts That Later Move; Relics and Chain of Title",
          "text": "A gift or donation made under duress, mistake, or ignorance is voidable at the donor's election within one solar cycle. See §§ 2.5 (Coercion), 3.4 (Mental Incapacity). A recipient who takes title with actual knowledge of a defect in the grantor's authority assumes the risks of future discovery. Id. Conversely, bona fide purchasers without notice acquire good title free of prior encumbrances, subject to any reserved rights expressly retained by the donor. § 1102.3. The rule of constructive notice applies where public records exist; otherwise, actual knowledge is required to bind a successor in interest. See §§ 501.2 (Public Records), 604.1 (Estoppel). A curse attached to an object functions as a warranty disclaimer unless expressly negated by the donor's intent. § 1103.5. A blessing conferred by a third party does not validate a defective conveyance absent consideration or ratification. Id. Relics bearing residual enchantments must be disclosed prior to transfer; nondisclosure constitutes fraud and grounds for rescission. § 814.7. The burden of proving a gift was tainted lies with the aggrieved party, who must establish clear and convincing evidence of improper motive or undue influence. See § 902.3 (Burden of Proof). Once title passes cleanly, subsequent claims based on hidden defects are barred unless fraud is proven beyond reasonable doubt. § 1104.1. Exceptions apply where the donor retains a life estate or right of revocation, in which case the grantee holds only a qualified interest subject to termination upon notice. See §§ 712.5 (Life Estates), 809.1 (Revocable Trusts). A gift made in contemplation of death is presumed irrevocable unless shown otherwise by clear evidence of contrary intent at the time of execution. § 1105.4. Any attempt to condition a completed gift on future events fails as against public policy and the donor's vested rights. Id. The doctrine of part performance may cure defects where the recipient has relied substantially to their detriment in good faith. See §§ 608.2 (Reliance), 719.3 (Unjust Enrichment). Ultimately, fairness dictates that gifts should not be undermined by technicalities once title passes and reliance occurs. § 1106.5. This principle balances the donor's autonomy with the recipient's security of transaction, fostering trust in magical commerce. Id. Where doubts arise regarding the nature or validity of a gift, equitable remedies may supplement strict legal rules to achieve justice between the parties. See § 724.1 (Equitable Jurisdiction).",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "2.5",
            "1102.3",
            "501.2",
            "1103.5",
            "814.7",
            "902.3",
            "1104.1",
            "712.5",
            "1105.4",
            "608.2",
            "1106.5",
            "724.1",
            "1101.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.2 Gifts With Attached Obligations; The Curse as Warranty Disclaimer",
          "text": "A gift that carries attached obligations may not be accepted without full knowledge thereof. Where a donor imposes conditions upon the transfer of magical property, those conditions bind the recipient irrespective of subsequent changes in status or possession. This rule ensures that gifts remain true to their original intent and prevents exploitation through circumvention of terms.\n\nThe curse accompanying a relic functions as an explicit warranty disclaimer, warning potential recipients of inherent dangers and limitations. Acceptance of such items constitutes agreement to abide by these warnings, regardless of whether the recipient later discovers additional hazards or beneficial properties. This principle protects both donor and donee from unintended consequences arising from incomplete understanding of the item's nature.\n\nBlessings bestowed upon objects by third parties do not negate prior obligations imposed by the original giver. A competitor's benediction may enhance an artifact's capabilities, but it cannot alter the fundamental terms of its transfer. The recipient remains bound by the initial agreement, even if new powers emerge through external intervention. This maintains clarity in contractual relationships and prevents confusion over liability for damages or injuries resulting from blessed items.\n\n§ 1101.2 Gifts With Attached Obligations; The Curse as Warranty Disclaimer\nA gift that carries attached obligations may not be accepted without full knowledge thereof. Where a donor imposes conditions upon the transfer of magical property, those conditions bind the recipient irrespective of subsequent changes in status or possession. This rule ensures that gifts remain true to their original intent and prevents exploitation through circumvention of terms.\n\nThe curse accompanying a relic functions as an explicit warranty disclaimer, warning potential recipients of inherent dangers and limitations. Acceptance of such items constitutes agreement to abide by these warnings, regardless of whether the recipient later discovers additional hazards or beneficial properties. This principle protects both donor and donee from unintended consequences arising from incomplete understanding of the item's nature.\n\nBlessings bestowed upon objects by third parties do not negate prior obligations imposed by the original giver. A competitor's benediction may enhance an artifact's capabilities, but it cannot alter the fundamental terms of its transfer. The recipient remains bound by the initial agreement, even if new powers emerge through external intervention. This maintains clarity in contractual relationships and prevents confusion over liability for damages or injuries resulting from blessed items.\n\nsee § 1.6 Definitions; see § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting; under § 700.2 Scrying of Closed Books\"",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1101.2",
            "1.6",
            "900.1",
            "700.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
