    {
      "cite": "1101.2",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Relics; Chain of Title",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Provenance, Quiet List relics.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title Provenance and Authenticity",
          "text": "Every relic, artifact, or enchanted object traded within the Accords must bear an unbroken chain of title tracing its origin to a lawful source. See §§ 300.4 Specimen Custody, 900.5 False Provenance. Any item lacking proper documentation shall be deemed contrabunt and subject to seizure under § 800.1 Discipline, Range of Sanctions. The burden of proof rests upon the seller to demonstrate clear ownership prior to transaction. Failure to do so constitutes a prima facie case for fraud under § 1001.1 Financial Fraud and Manipulation Generally.\n\nRelics found in the wild or salvaged from ruins require immediate reporting to the nearest Aegis Magi detachment within seventy-two hours. See § 900.2 Reporting of Material Enchantments, Duties of Discoverers. Unregistered items shall be confiscated pending investigation by the Conservators or Innovators as appropriate. Possession without authority is punishable as possession of stolen goods under applicable law. This provision aims to prevent illicit trafficking and ensure all magical artifacts enter the market through regulated channels.\n\nThe Desk reserves the right to require additional background checks, security clearances, or character references for high-value transactions involving particularly potent relics. See § 200.5 Guest Casters, Enhanced Vetting Procedures. Sellers must disclose any known defects, curses, or unstable properties of the item in writing before finalizing the sale. Concealment of material facts renders the contract voidable at the option of either party and may result in disciplinary action for professional misconduct.\n\nChain of title documentation must include original purchase receipts, transfer deeds, appraisal certificates, and any relevant magical attestation scrolls. Copies shall be filed with the Guild Records Office pursuant to § 900.3 Record Retention requirements. Failure to maintain adequate records may lead to denial of future licensure or suspension of existing privileges under § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse. This rigorous oversight ensures accountability throughout the lifecycle of every magical commodity transacted within our jurisdiction.\n\nSee also §§ 1001.5 Material Alteration of Traded Goods, 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk, for related provisions regarding seller obligations. Under § 1001.4 Illusion-Based Fraud, any attempt to misrepresent the nature or capabilities of a relic shall be treated as aggravated fraud carrying enhanced penalties. The Desk maintains a database of known cursed items and dangerous artifacts that pose special risks to unsuspecting buyers. This resource is available upon request to licensed practitioners for reference during due diligence investigations.\n\nRelics constitute an integral component of our magical economy and demand vigilant stewardship by all participants. By adhering strictly to these provenance requirements, we protect both consumers and the Guild's reputation from exploitation by rogue elements seeking profit through deception or malice alone. The preservation of lawful commerce depends upon collective commitment to transparency, honesty, and ethical dealing in all matters pertaining to enchanted goods and services offered under our watchful gaze forevermore.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.4",
            "800.1",
            "1001.1",
            "900.2",
            "200.5",
            "900.3",
            "200.4",
            "1001.5",
            "1001.4",
            "1101.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title Provenance and Authenticity Verification of Orig",
          "text": "Under this section, the Guild establishes rigorous protocols for verifying the provenance and authenticity of relics and artifacts submitted to its registry. All claimed items must be accompanied by a detailed chain of title, demonstrating unbroken ownership from original acquisition to present possession. Failure to provide adequate documentation will result in immediate rejection of the submission without prejudice to future applications.\n\nAuthenticity determinations shall be made exclusively by the Guild's Council of Connoisseurs, composed of recognized experts in relevant fields. This body possesses sole authority to adjudicate disputes regarding item classification and value assessment. Their rulings are final and binding on all parties involved. Appeals must be filed within thirty days of initial determination and will be reviewed de novo by an expanded panel selected via random lottery from registered appraisers.\n\nRelics bearing the marks or seals of prohibited organizations shall not be accepted under any circumstances, regardless of stated provenance. This absolute bar applies even where the item appears defaced or otherwise altered to obscure identifying features. The burden of proving lawful acquisition rests entirely upon the submitter; any presumption of legitimacy will be rebutted by mere allegation of taint.\n\nIn cases involving disputed ownership claims, the Guild shall act as neutral arbiter pending resolution of underlying controversies through appropriate legal channels. No relic may enter official storage until all competing interests have been fully litigated and exhausted by competent tribunals. The Guild maintains no vested interest in any such disputes and assumes no liability for subsequent litigation arising from its administrative actions alone.\n\nSubmitors are further advised that once accepted, relics become the sole property of the Autumnwood Accords Desk and remain subject to all applicable statutes and regulations indefinitely. Unauthorized removal or attempted sale will constitute a felony offense punishable by heavy fines and imprisonment at the discretion of the presiding judge. Violators may additionally face permanent disbarment from practice within Guild jurisdiction.\n\nThis comprehensive framework ensures both the integrity of the relic registry and protection against fraud, theft, or illicit trafficking. By maintaining strict control over classified items, the Guild safeguards public safety while facilitating lawful study and appreciation of magical history's most extraordinary artifacts. All provisions herein shall be construed liberally to effectuate these essential purposes.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1101.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title Provenance and Authenticity Verification of Orig — Continued",
          "text": "Whereas relics and antiquities possess unique historical and monetary value beyond their material composition, the Accords establish rigorous standards to ensure transactional integrity in this specialized market sector. Section 1101.2 mandates that all parties engaged in the trade of artifacts must maintain comprehensive documentation verifying item provenance from initial acquisition through any subsequent transfers or alterations. This chain of title serves both as evidence of legitimate ownership and as a safeguard against fraudulent practices common within the arcane antiquities community.\n\nThe Accords require sellers to provide certified appraisal reports detailing artifact composition, age estimation methods utilized, and disclosure of all known repairs or restorations performed upon the item's surface. Buyers must similarly disclose any prior knowledge of defects, damage, or hidden flaws that may affect value assessment. Failure to comply with these documentation obligations renders transactions voidable at the election of either party within thirty (30) days of discovery.\n\nTo facilitate accurate valuation and risk assessment, licensed appraisers are authorized under § 1101.2(c) to employ specialized detection technologies including spectral imaging, elemental resonance scanning, and thermal analysis where appropriate. These tools allow for non-intrusive examination revealing subtle traces of alteration or synthetic replication that might escape conventional visual inspection. Results from such examinations must be recorded in the official ledger maintained by the Antiquities Division.\n\nParties are further obligated to report any suspected forgery or misrepresentation immediately to prevent wider market contamination. Unsubstantiated accusations may lead to disciplinary proceedings under § 1200, while verified fraud warrants criminal charges pursuant to applicable statutory provisions. The Accords emphasize that transparency and honesty remain paramount in maintaining trust within the relic trading ecosystem governed by these regulations.\n\nThis provision operates in conjunction with §§ 900-1000 regarding magical item registration, as well as § 700 covering cognitive manipulation techniques relevant to forgery detection. Violations may result in license revocation under § 800 and potential imprisonment for serious offenses threatening public safety or economic stability. By enforcing strict accountability standards, the Autumnwood Accords aim to foster a fair and secure marketplace benefiting all law-abiding participants engaged in this ancient yet profitable pursuit.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1101.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title Provenance and Authenticity Verification of Orig — Administration",
          "text": "The provisions of this Part regarding the classification of items as relics and the requirements for establishing a clear chain of title are without prejudice to any claims or interests that may exist in such items under other applicable laws, including but not limited to those governing archaeological sites, cultural heritage, and stolen goods. However, it is hereby declared that where an item has been listed on the Quiet List as a relic, the presumption shall be that such listing constitutes prima facie evidence of both its status as a relic and the invalidity of any subsequent transfers or sales thereof, absent proof to the contrary.\n\nVerification of Origin\nAny party seeking to establish a valid chain of title for an item classified as a relic must provide satisfactory documentation verifying the original provenance of said item, including but not limited to: (a) official records from the Autumnwood Hall confirming its inclusion on the Quiet List at the time of acquisition; (b) sworn statements from reputable appraisers or historians attesting to the authenticity and historical significance of the item; and (c) a detailed history of ownership tracing back to the original source. Failure to provide such documentation shall result in the immediate revocation of any permits or licenses issued pursuant to this Part, along with imposition of penalties as deemed appropriate by the Desk.\n\nFurthermore, it is expressly forbidden for any individual or entity to knowingly acquire, possess, or traffic in items listed on the Quiet List without first obtaining written permission from the Archmage Sovereign. Violation of this prohibition shall constitute a serious offense punishable by imprisonment not exceeding five years and/or a fine up to one thousand gold crowns per violation. In addition, all profits derived from such illegal activities shall be forfeited to the Guild for reinvestment in lawful endeavors.\n\nThe Desk reserves the right to conduct random inspections of facilities known or suspected to house relics at any time without notice, in order to ensure compliance with these provisions. Refusal to submit to such inspection shall constitute a separate violation subject to additional sanctions. It is hereby declared that the protections afforded by this Part extend equally to both natural persons and artificial entities, including but not limited to corporations, partnerships, trusts, and magical constructs capable of holding property rights.\n\nIn witness whereof, I have set forth my hand on this ______ day of ____________, in the year of our Lord ____ and the ____ year of the Autumnwood Accords.\n\n\"Provenance is everything. Without it, a relic is nothing more than expensive scrap metal to me.\" - Archmage Theron, Conservators\n\n\"I'll pay top dollar for that ancient scroll! Just name your price!\" - Janna Brightspark, Innovators\n\n*This regulation aims to protect the integrity of our most valuable artifacts while providing clear guidelines for their legal acquisition and transfer.*",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1101.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title Provenance and Authenticity Verification of Orig — Oversight",
          "text": "§ 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title Provenance and Authenticity Verification of Orig - Continued\n\nWhere a relic's provenance cannot be established with reasonable certainty, the burden shifts to the possessor to demonstrate that acquisition was lawful. Failure to meet this burden results in presumption of illicit origins under § 1101.3(a). The Desk may require independent authentication from qualified experts at the expense of the disputing party. All findings must be recorded and made available for inspection upon written request.\n\n§ 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title Provenance and Authenticity Verification of Orig - Administration\n\nThe Arch-Mage's Office maintains a registry of certified relic appraisers whose determinations shall carry evidentiary weight in all proceedings. Appraisers must adhere strictly to ethical standards set forth in § 405.1 et seq., including avoidance of conflicts of interest and proper disclosure of any financial ties to the parties involved. Regular audits ensure compliance with these obligations. Violations may result in revocation of certification status pursuant to § 406.2.\n\nThe Desk further mandates that all relic transactions exceeding a specified monetary threshold be reported within five business days to prevent money laundering or tax evasion schemes (§ 815.7). This requirement applies regardless of whether the exchange occurred domestically or internationally. Reports submitted in good faith shall remain confidential unless subpoenaed by competent authorities. Willful falsification constitutes perjury under § 923.4, subject to enhanced penalties upon conviction.\n\nIn cases involving disputed ownership claims between multiple parties, the Desk shall appoint a neutral mediator with expertise in both legal and arcane matters to facilitate resolution outside formal litigation whenever feasible (§ 105.8). Mediation efforts must conclude within thirty days unless extended by mutual agreement. Failure to participate diligently may result in dismissal of one's claim without prejudice. Costs associated with mediation are typically shared equally among disputants unless otherwise ordered by the mediator based on equitable principles and prior conduct.\n\nThis comprehensive framework ensures that relics circulate freely yet responsibly, respecting both their historical significance and inherent magical properties while safeguarding public welfare through vigilant oversight mechanisms designed to detect and deter abuse before it escalates beyond containment capabilities possessed even by seasoned practitioners of highest standing within our guild ranks today!",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1101.2",
            "1101.3",
            "405.1",
            "406.2",
            "815.7",
            "923.4",
            "105.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title — Burden of Verification and the Silence of the ",
          "text": "Under the provisions governing relics and their chain of title, the initial filing of provenance is merely the threshold entry into a rigorous process of verification that extends far beyond the surface narrative of the object's history. It shall be understood that while § 1101.2 establishes the requirement to quiet list relics prior to public disclosure, this administrative act does not constitute a conclusive presumption of authenticity or benign intent; rather, it triggers an affirmative duty upon the submitter to demonstrate that the relic's origin is free from magical contamination or deceitful fabrication. The burden of proof rests squarely with the party presenting the artifact, requiring them to provide not only a chronological history but also empirical data confirming the integrity of the item's magical signature against known anomalies. This standard is elevated by § 900.5 False Provenance of Relics, which explicitly dictates that a compelling story or oral tradition does not constitute a valid chain of title, thereby ensuring that emotional appeal cannot supersede factual verification in matters of arcane heritage. Consequently, any attempt to introduce a relic into the Guild’s archives or sanctioned marketplace without satisfying these rigorous standards shall be deemed an act of unauthorized introduction and subject to immediate quarantine under § 700.16 Validation Protocols for Arcane Artifacts and Relics.\n\nThe verification process mandated by this section is designed to detect subtle manipulations of reality that often accompany the trafficking of dangerous artifacts, particularly those associated with forbidden schools or ancient pacts that risk destabilizing the local metaphysical equilibrium. A submitter must provide physical evidence, such as resonance readings or dimensional mapping, that corroborates the claimed lineage of the relic, ensuring that no elements of collateral taint have been introduced to mask a darker origin. This requirement is critical given the precedent set in § 1101.11 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion, where it was established that even minor magical infections can render an otherwise lawful gift or transfer void and dangerous to the public sphere. Therefore, the quiet list filing under § 1101.2 serves not merely as a cataloging exercise but as a forensic investigation into the metaphysical health of the object in question. Failure to produce such corroborating evidence results in the automatic invocation of oversight protocols detailed in § 300.15 Oversight Protocols for Quiet List Procedures, wherein the relic remains sealed within the restricted archives until further review can be conducted by the Council of Conservators.\n\nFurthermore, the distinction between a genuine heirloom and a fabricated construct is often blurred by the very nature of arcane creation, where an object may be artificially aged or its history rewritten through high-level illusion magic to mimic a legitimate chain of title. To combat this, § 900.3 Record Retention mandates that all filings regarding relics must include a cross-referenced audit trail extending back to the point of origin, preventing the creation of false histories that could mislead future generations of mages. This strict adherence to archival integrity is reinforced by § 900.4 Identify Fees; Who Pays, which clarifies that the cost of identification and verification is borne by the submitter, ensuring that those attempting to introduce relics into the public sphere do so with full knowledge of the scrutiny they will face. The Mages' Guild operates under the principle that safety is not a privilege but a prerequisite for interaction with magic, and thus any relic lacking a verified chain of title is treated as a potential vector for catastrophe until proven otherwise.\n\nIn cases where a relic presents ambiguous signs of contamination or where the history provided contradicts established magical laws regarding ancient pacts or mana reverence, the matter shall be escalated to the Paradox Trial for adjudication by peer mages familiar with the specific subtleties of artifact manipulation. This judicial mechanism ensures that disputes over authenticity are resolved fairly while maintaining the highest standards of safety and responsibility. As noted in § 1001.16 Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Memb, all findings from these verification processes must be kept confidential to prevent panic or exploitation by factions such as the Silver Flame or Disaster Inc., who may seek to exploit unverified relics for their own ends. Ultimately, the quiet list is a shield, not a door; it protects the Midlands from the dangers lurking in the shadows of unverified history. Any individual seeking to bypass these protocols will find that the laws regarding false provenance and magical contagion leave no room for error, ensuring that only those relics with a truly pure and verified lineage may ever touch the hands of the public or be studied within the Guild's sanctified halls.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1101.2",
            "900.5",
            "700.16",
            "1101.11",
            "300.15",
            "900.3",
            "900.4",
            "1001.16"
          ]
        }
      ],
