    {
      "cite": "1101.3",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Curse as Warranty Disclaimer",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A curse is not 'as is' unless disclosed.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Curse as Warranty Disclaimer",
          "text": "§ 1101.3 Curse as Warranty Disclaimer. The transfer of any enchanted object, relic, or mundane item bearing a lingering metaphysical burden shall not be governed by the standard \"as is\" clauses of common commerce under the Autumnwood Accords. Specifically, the presence of a curse—defined herein as any persistent, deleterious magical effect intended to harm, hinder, or drain the vitality, sanity, or resources of a subsequent owner—constitutes a material defect in the item's condition. Any merchant, practitioner, or private citizen seeking to divest themselves of a cursed object may not utilize the standard \"as is\" disclaimer to insulate themselves from liability. If a curse is present and remains undisclosed at the point of sale or transfer, the seller shall be held in breach of the Accords, subject to the penalties outlined in § 1001.5.\n\nFurthermore, the legal distinction between a \"dormant\" curse and an \"active\" curse is irrelevant for the purposes of warranty disclosure. If the magical signature of a curse is detectable by a standard sensory appraisal by a licensed Aegis Mage or a certified Conservator, the seller is mandated to provide a full disclosure of the curse’s origin, potency, and duration. Failure to do so constitutes a willful concealment of a hazard. A curse is not \"as is\" unless the buyer has been explicitly warned of its existence and has voluntarily accepted the risk of its activation. In such cases of explicit disclosure, the seller may be granted immunity from subsequent claims of \"hidden defects,\" provided the disclosure was made in writing or through a binding verbal contract witnessed by a Guild-recognized notary.\n\nThe Innovators may argue that certain \"reactive\" curses—those that only trigger under specific, rare conditions—do not constitute an immediate defect. The Conservators, however, maintain that any potential for harm constitutes a breach of the stability of the local weave. Therefore, for the purposes of the Autumnwood Accords, any curse that possesses the capacity to manifest harm shall be treated as a primary defect. Any attempt to bypass these requirements by claiming the curse is a \"feature\" of the item's history, rather than a flaw in its current state, shall be viewed as a fraudulent misrepresentation. See § 1001.7 for requirements regarding the disclosure of risk in complex magical assets.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.3",
            "1001.5",
            "1001.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Blessing of a Competing Vendor",
          "text": "§ 1101.4 Blessing of a Competing Vendor. The use of benevolent magic, specifically \"blessings,\" to actively undermine the commercial interests of a rival entity is strictly regulated as a form of illicit advertising. While the Mages' Guild recognizes the right of practitioners to promote their services, the application of a blessing to an object or person with the express intent of rendering a competitor's product unusable, unappealing, or magically incompatible is prohibited. If a blessing is sold as a service to a customer with the primary purpose of creating a hostile metaphysical environment against a specific rival vendor, such an act shall be classified as unauthorized advertising.\n\nShould such a blessing contain binding components—namely, magic that actively prevents a consumer from utilizing a competitor's goods or creates a persistent magical barrier against a rival’s influence—it shall be elevated from a mere advertising violation to a felony of magical fraud. Under the Accords, a blessing that binds a consumer to a single vendor by magically suppressing the efficacy of all other competing products is considered a violation of the free flow of magical commerce. Such \"exclusive bindings\" are prohibited unless the vendor can prove a legitimate safety concern or a unique, non-compressive magical synergy that does not infringe upon the consumer's right to seek alternative services.\n\nThe Aegis Magi are authorized to seize any artifacts or objects imbued with such \"hostile blessings\" and may levy heavy fines against the practitioner who cast them. Furthermore, any vendor found to be distributing blessings of this nature shall be barred from Guild certification for a period of no less than five years. This provision ensures that the marketplace remains a space of open competition rather than one governed by magical monopolies or \"blessed\" coercion. Practitioners are encouraged to seek counsel with the Conservators to ensure their promotional enchantments do not cross the threshold into prohibited binding magic. See § 1001.1 for general principles on financial fraud and manipulation.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1101.4",
            "1001.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.3 Curse as Warranty Disclaimer; Blessing of Competing Vendor",
          "text": "A magic item sold under the guise of \"as is\" remains subject to all standard warranties and representations unless explicitly disclaimed in writing by a licensed appraiser. The mere presence of a curse or flaw does not constitute an adequate disclaimer, especially where such defects materially impair the intended function of the object. Any seller who knowingly conceals known hazards risks treble damages under § 1101.4.\n\nConversely, when an item is blessed by a competing vendor with protective enchantments, these benefits survive transfer unless specifically negated. Such blessings may include immunity to certain curses, resistance to degradation, or minor utility enhancements. The buyer stands in privity with both the original caster and subsequent owners, entitled to enforce these protections against any party interfering with their use.\n\nThis section supersedes conflicting terms in private contracts only where those terms violate public policy or explicit statutory prohibitions. Parties may agree to modify standard warranties provided they do so knowingly and voluntarily, without duress or fraud. Any ambiguity regarding the scope of a disclaimer shall be construed most strongly against the drafter.\n\nSee also § 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold for valuation standards when magical artifacts enter commerce; § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items for exceptions regarding laboratory specimens; and § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill for automated transactions involving sentient beings. Under § 700.5 Clone as Duplicate Claimant, multiple ownership claims arising from duplication shall be resolved according to established precedence rules detailed therein.\n\nThe principles outlined herein reflect the Guild's enduring commitment to fair trade practices and consumer protection within the magical economy. By setting clear expectations for both sellers and buyers, these provisions foster trust and stability in an inherently volatile marketplace. All parties are urged to familiarize themselves thoroughly with this section before engaging in any transaction involving enchanted goods.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1101.4",
            "1001.8",
            "300.5",
            "200.6",
            "700.5",
            "1101.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.3 Curse as Warranty Disclaimer; Blessing of Competing Vendor — Continued",
          "text": "A magical item sold or transferred by the owner, whether gratuitously or for value, carries with it an implied warranty that it is free from any harmful enchantments known to the seller at the time of sale. This obligation applies regardless of whether the transfer was express or silent, oral or written. Failure to disclose a curse renders the transaction voidable at the option of the buyer, who may seek rescission and damages for reliance losses suffered in good faith belief that the item was safe.\n\nConversely, if a seller expressly disclaims all warranties regarding harmful effects, including curses and hexes, such disclaimer shall be binding unless it is unconscionably one-sided or results from fraud or duress. The burden of proving knowledge of a curse lies with the buyer seeking to void the deal; mere speculation about latent dangers does not suffice. Sellers must affirmatively warrant that they have reviewed the item for known defects before representation can be made as to its safety.\n\nA blessing conferred by one vendor upon an article may serve as a factual predicate for implied warranty claims against subsequent transferees, if the blessing was intended to enhance value or reduce risk. However, a blessing alone does not displace express terms agreed upon between buyer and seller in later transactions. Each sale constitutes a separate contractual relationship with its own warranties and disclaimers independent of prior dealings.\n\nThe Desk shall have authority to investigate alleged breaches of warranty arising from undisclosed curses, including but not limited to inspection of the contested item, testimony from experts familiar with such enchantments, and examination of relevant records. Remedies available to aggrieved parties include rescission of the sale, refund of purchase price plus interest, compensation for consequential damages proximately caused by the curse, and equitable relief as appropriate under the circumstances.\n\nNothing herein shall be construed to limit or alter any rights afforded by existing common law doctrines of fraud, misrepresentation, breach of contract, or tortious interference with contractual relations insofar as they may apply independently to matters governed by this section. The provisions hereof are intended solely to codify and clarify prevailing standards relating to curses and warranties in commercial transactions involving magical artifacts.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1101.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.3 Curse as Warranty Disclaimer; Blessing of Competing Vendor — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "The burden of disclosure extends beyond mere knowledge of a curse's presence to encompass the seller's duty to warn against its potential activation or escalation. Failure to provide such notice, where reasonably foreseeable, may constitute a breach of implied warranty regardless of actual intent to defraud. Thus, a vendor who knows that their goods are cursed and sells them without warning assumes liability for any resulting harm, even if no direct magical contact occurs between buyer and seller.\n\nFurthermore, a blessing conferred upon property by a party with a competing interest may be deemed voidable at the option of the original owner. For example, if a debtor pledges cursed land to secure a loan and the lender subsequently blesses it in hopes of mitigating risk, such act does not extinguish the underlying defect. The blessing is merely a gesture of goodwill unless accompanied by explicit renunciation of prior claims or conditions attached thereto. Courts will scrutinize the motives behind any post-sale ritualistic enhancement to determine whether it was undertaken in good faith or as an attempt to obscure liability.\n\nIn cases involving multiple transactions of the same cursed item, each party remains liable for their own conduct independently of others' actions. A seller who knowingly transfers a cursed object cannot later disclaim responsibility merely because subsequent owners have also dealt with it improperly. The chain of title does not absolve anyone from their obligations under law unless expressly released by all interested parties in writing. This principle ensures that the original wrongdoer remains accountable for initiating the curse's propagation, even if others amplify its effects downstream.\n\nFinally, remedies available against a cursed vendor include rescission of contract, damages for losses incurred, and injunctive relief to prevent further harm. Punitive sanctions may also apply where malice or gross negligence is proven beyond a reasonable doubt. The goal is not only compensation but deterrence—to discourage reckless handling of dangerous artifacts that threaten public safety. Only through strict enforcement can the Accords maintain order amidst these treacherous forces.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1101.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.4 Implied Warranty of Non-Maleficence",
          "text": "Whereas the foregoing sections address express warranties and disclaimers, this section establishes an implied covenant running with every magical transaction. By their very nature, enchanted items possess latent dangers unknown to the casual observer; hence, sellers are deemed to warrant that such risks have been fully disclosed prior to transfer of title. Failure to provide adequate warning renders any sale void ab initio, regardless of apparent consent or consideration paid. This principle finds its roots in the ancient maxim: \"No spell unspoken, no harm unaverted.\"\n\nThe seller's duty extends beyond mere disclosure of known defects; they must actively investigate potential hazards inherent to the item's magic. For instance, a merchant selling a cursed dagger must ascertain whether it carries any malignant properties that could affect the buyer or their associates. Negligence in this regard constitutes breach of contract and grounds for rescission. Similarly, buyers are obligated to exercise due diligence in verifying claims made by sellers regarding an item's history and condition. Blind acceptance of assertions without corroboration shifts liability onto the purchaser should adverse consequences ensue.\n\nIn cases where both parties act in good faith but misunderstanding prevails, equitable remedies may apply rather than harsh penalties. A court might order restitution of purchase price plus reasonable expenses incurred during investigation or repair efforts. Alternatively, specific performance could compel delivery of a properly enchanted substitute if feasible without undue hardship. The ultimate goal remains restoration of injured rights to their pre-transaction state, wherever practically possible under the circumstances.\n\nThis implied warranty operates independently of any express agreement between parties and survives attempts to contract around it via fine print or oral stipulations. It reflects society's collective judgment that magical commerce demands heightened standards of care due to the extraordinary powers involved. Those who would engage in such trade must accept these obligations willingly, understanding that failure to comply invites serious repercussions. Only through mutual vigilance can the delicate balance of magic and law be preserved for generations to come.\n\nsee § 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold Reporting Threshold; see § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse; see § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items\nunder § 1.6 Definitions — \"Warranty,\" \"Defect,\" \"Disclosure\"",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.8",
            "200.4",
            "300.5",
            "1.6",
            "1101.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
