    {
      "cite": "1101.4",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Blessing of a Competing Vendor",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A blessing sold against a rival is advertising, then fraud if it binds.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Blessing of a Competing Vendor",
          "text": "Under the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords and the oversight of the Accords Desk, all magical services provided by licensed practitioners within the Midlands shall be subject to the standards of fair commerce and arcane integrity. While the Mages' Guild acknowledges the inherent right of a practitioner to solicit patronage, the application of liturgical or protective enchantments for the express purpose of undermining a specific, identified competitor is strictly regulated. A blessing, by its fundamental nature, is a metaphysical enhancement of a subject's state; however, when such a blessing is structured to actively diminish the efficacy, reputation, or commercial viability of a rival entity, it transcends the realm of standard mercantile competition and enters the sphere of regulated magical advertising.\n\nAny practitioner who offers a \"Blessing of Superiority\" or a \"Ward of Market Dominance\" that specifically names, targets, or references a competing vendor shall be required to file a Notice of Commercial Intent with the Accords Desk prior to the casting of said enchantment. Such notices must clearly delineate the scope of the blessing to ensure it does not infringe upon the existing magical protections or established trade rights of the aforementioned rival. If a blessing is sold as a means to \"outshine\" a competitor without specific naming, it remains a permissible act of promotion, provided it does not utilize prohibited schools of magic as defined in § 1.6. However, the moment a practitioner employs a binding oath or a persistent enchantment that actively suppresses the rival's ability to conduct business—such as a \"Curse of Diminished Appeal\" or a \"Ward of Consumer Aversion\"—the act shall be reclassified as fraudulent conduct.\n\nFraud, as defined under § 1001.1, occurs when a magical effect is deployed with the intent to deceive the public or to create an artificial monopoly through coercive arcane means. A blessing that binds a customer to a specific vendor by magically inhibiting their ability to seek services from a competitor is a violation of the Accords. Such binding effects are considered predatory and shall be subject to immediate dissolution by the Aegis Magi. Practitioners found guilty of selling such binding blessings shall be liable for restitution to the affected vendor and may face sanctions under § 800.1. Furthermore, any practitioner who knowingly sells a blessing that contains hidden \"leash\" mechanics—where the blessing provides a benefit to the buyer while simultaneously siphoning mana or influence from a rival—shall be subject to the Paradox Trial for the use of deceptive and destabilizing magic.\n\nThe Distinction between legitimate promotion and prohibited fraud rests upon the presence of a \"binding\" element. If the magic merely encourages a preference, it is a matter of trade; if the magic removes the agency of the consumer or the viability of the rival through persistent, involuntary arcane interference, it is a breach of the Accords. Practitioners are encouraged to consult the Goodstyle Artisans for approved methods of non-binding promotional magic to ensure compliance with these statutes. Any dispute regarding the \"competitive reach\" of a blessing shall be mediated by Archmage Veyra’s office, though the burden of proof shall rest upon the petitioner to demonstrate that the blessing in question is non-consensual in its suppression of the rival's interests.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "1001.1",
            "800.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.4 Blessing of a Competing Vendor",
          "text": "A blessing offered by a vendor who is known to have a competing interest in the transaction shall be deemed suspicious and subject to immediate inquiry by the Desk. Under no circumstances may such a blessing be relied upon as valid consideration unless it has been fully disclosed and ratified in writing by all parties involved. Any attempt to conceal or misrepresent the nature of the conflict shall constitute grounds for disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure.\n\nThe provision is intended to prevent vendors from using their influence to artificially enhance the perceived value of goods they are selling, thereby defrauding buyers and undermining fair market practices. Vendors must disclose any personal or financial ties to competing entities before offering blessings that could materially affect the outcome of a deal. Failure to do so renders such blessings void ab initio and exposes the vendor to liability for damages incurred by aggrieved parties.\n\nThe Desk shall maintain a registry of all known conflicts of interest involving licensed vendors, making this information available upon request to any party engaged in transactions with said vendors. This transparency requirement is designed to empower consumers to make informed decisions regarding their purchases while holding vendors accountable for unethical conduct. Regular audits and investigations will be conducted to ensure compliance with these provisions and to identify any systemic issues requiring remedial action.\n\nBlessings tainted by undisclosed conflicts of interest shall be treated as fraudulent misrepresentations under applicable law, giving rise to claims for rescission, restitution, and consequential damages. Vendors found guilty of violating this section may face additional penalties including fines, suspensions, and permanent expulsion from the Guild. The Desk retains broad discretion in determining appropriate sanctions based on the severity of the violation and its impact on market integrity.\n\nParties are strongly urged to seek independent legal counsel before entering into any transaction involving blessings from vendors with potential competing interests. Failure to do so does not excuse reliance on tainted blessings or limit liability for resulting losses. The burden remains on each party to exercise due diligence in protecting their own interests and avoiding conflicts that may compromise the fairness of a deal.\n\nThis section applies only to blessings offered within the context of commercial transactions between licensed vendors and consumers. Private arrangements unrelated to business activities are outside the scope of these regulations, though they may still be subject to general principles of contract law and ethical conduct expected of all Guild members. The Desk reserves the right to interpret this provision as necessary to preserve the integrity of the marketplace and protect public welfare from deceptive practices.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1101.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.4 Blessing of a Competing Vendor — Continued",
          "text": "A vendor's blessing is not merely an expression of goodwill but a binding magical contract that alters the legal status of goods. When a seller invokes their divine patron or ancestral spirits to sanctify merchandise, they create a metaphysical warranty that persists beyond the transaction. However, this blessing may be invoked against competitors who violate its terms, effectively weaponizing sacred oaths as trade barriers.\n\nThe law recognizes that such blessings often function as de facto monopolies when properly enforced. If a vendor's patron grants exclusive protection to their products, rival merchants cannot legally replicate these effects without committing ritual fraud under § 1001.4. The Accords further prohibit using blessed items in comparative advertising unless explicit disclaimers accompany all claims of superiority. Unsubstantiated assertions of magical dominance constitute false propaganda punishable by fines and temporary suspension of licensure.\n\nEnforcement mechanisms include immediate seizure of offending goods upon complaint, followed by a full judicial review of blessing authenticity. In cases where rival vendors deliberately undermine another's sanctified wares through dark magic or sabotage, they face enhanced penalties for both economic harm and desecration of sacred bonds. The Guild maintains detailed registries of all blessings granted to licensed sellers, allowing rapid verification during disputes.\n\nThe underlying principle remains that commerce must never supersede spiritual obligations. When a merchant invokes divine forces to secure market advantage, they enter into a complex web of metaphysical liabilities. Failure to honor these sacred duties invites retribution not only from offended patrons but also from the Guild itself, which views such practices as inherently destabilizing to the broader magical economy. Thus, blessings wielded in commercial contexts are treated with the utmost seriousness, reflecting their profound impact on both tangible profits and intangible spiritual realities.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.4",
            "1101.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.4 Blessing of a Competing Vendor — Advertising by Binding",
          "text": "A blessing sold on behalf of a vendor who stands in competition with the original seller shall be deemed, at law, to constitute an act of advertising for that competitor's benefit. See § 705.3 Prohibition Against Improper Solicitation; § 1204.5 Undue Influence by Third Parties. If such blessing further binds the purchaser—whether through a written contract or oral agreement—the original seller may bring suit for damages, including reasonable out-of-pocket expenses and lost profits directly traceable to the sale of the competing blessing. The burden rests on the complaining party to prove causation with particularity; mere speculation as to what the customer might have purchased from them instead is insufficient to establish standing under § 1204.6 Standing Requirements. Moreover, any clause within a blessing purporting to waive this right or limit damages shall be held void as contrary to public policy and good morals. See § 705.8 Unconscionable Provisions; § 1203.1 Waiver of Statutory Rights Invalidity. The Desk reserves the authority to issue a cease-and-desist order against any entity found engaging in such practices, and may refer matters for criminal prosecution where appropriate. This provision ensures that vendors retain full control over their commercial interests, even when third parties attempt to exploit binding arrangements for gain.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "705.3",
            "1204.5",
            "1204.6",
            "705.8",
            "1203.1",
            "1101.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.4 Blessing of a Competing Vendor — Advertising by Binding — Continued",
          "text": "A vendor's blessing, when offered in direct competition with an established merchant, constitutes not merely an offer but an act of public advertising. The binding nature of the spell serves to guarantee the efficacy and value of the goods or services purchased through that blessing; thus, it functions as a form of promotional endorsement carried out via magical compulsion rather than mere words. This practice is permissible so long as it does not violate other provisions of the Accords, particularly those governing unfair competition and deceptive trade practices under Title 8.\n\nHowever, when such a blessing explicitly targets a specific rival vendor's customer base or reputation, it crosses into the realm of malicious intent. In this context, the spell operates less as an advertisement and more as a weaponized tool to undermine a competitor's market position. The law views these acts with extreme skepticism, for they strike at the heart of fair commercial dealings. A blessing cast solely to degrade another business's standing may be deemed actionable under § 800.1(D) as intentional interference with economic relations.\n\nFurthermore, if the binding clause within such a competitive blessing restricts the addressee from patronizing rival establishments or purchasing alternative products, it effectively creates an exclusive contractual obligation enforced by magic rather than consent. Such clauses are void ab initio under § 200.4(A)(1), as they constitute an impermissible restraint on trade. The Archmage's Court shall invalidate any offending enchantment and assess damages against the casting mage for tortious interference, alongside appropriate fines payable to the injured party.\n\nThe Desk advises all merchants to exercise great care in crafting blessings intended for commercial purposes. While promotion of one's own wares is lawful, targeting a competitor with binding magic invites severe repercussions under both civil and penal statutes. It is incumbent upon each vendor to consider the broader implications of their magical marketing strategies before committing them to parchment or spellbook. The Accords exist to protect all parties engaged in legitimate commerce from exploitation by rogue mages seeking personal gain at another's expense.\n\nsee § 800.1(D) Intentional Interference with Economic Relations; see § 200.4(A)(1) Impermissible Restraints on Trade; see § 900.5 False Provenance of Relics — Deceptive Marketing Practices under the guise of blessing may constitute fraud. A blessing that binds a customer exclusively to one vendor violates principles of free trade and fair competition outlined in Title 2. Such acts warrant swift legal intervention by the Archmage's Court to prevent further harm to innocent merchants. The Desk urges all practitioners to review their commercial enchantments carefully before deployment, ensuring they comply with both letter and spirit of these laws. Failure to do so may result in substantial penalties under applicable statutes.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "800.1",
            "200.4",
            "900.5",
            "1101.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.4 Blessing of a Competing Vendor — Advertising by Binding; Fraudulent Res",
          "text": "A vendor shall not offer a blessing that simultaneously binds the recipient to purchase exclusively from a competitor, as such an arrangement constitutes fraudulent restraint of trade under common law principles and violates § 1101.2 regarding unconscionable terms. Any contract clause mandating exclusive patronage of another entity in exchange for magical enhancement is void ab initio, regardless of its inclusion within the blessing's enchantments.\n\nFurthermore, a vendor who employs binding magic to compel a customer into buying from them after receiving superior goods or services commits misrepresentation and unjust enrichment. The compelled purchase shall be subject to rescission upon proof that the transaction resulted solely from magical coercion rather than voluntary assent. This rule applies even if the initial blessing was legitimate and the subsequent restraint appears superficially consensual, as long as the totality of circumstances demonstrates undue influence.\n\nThe prohibition extends to subtle forms of binding such as charm, compulsion, or alteration of memory that impairs judgment regarding commercial decisions. A vendor who uses any such technique to secure a monopoly over their client's spending habits violates this section and faces disciplinary action under § 1100 et seq. The burden lies on the accuser to demonstrate by clear and convincing evidence that magical interference materially affected the victim's ability to negotiate fair terms.\n\nExceptions exist where both parties knowingly enter into an exclusive arrangement for legitimate business reasons, such as trade secrets or specialized expertise. However, any clause requiring total submission of will to another party remains impermissible regardless of commercial justification. The Guild maintains a strict stance against magical coercion in economic contexts to preserve free market principles and individual autonomy.\n\nViolations shall be prosecuted under the full weight of criminal statutes including extortion, fraud, and conspiracy where appropriate. Civil remedies include restitution, damages for emotional distress, and permanent injunctions barring future abusive practices. Repeat offenders risk revocation of all professional licenses and sanctions up to and including imprisonment. The Desk retains sole discretion to determine penalties based on severity of offense and mitigating factors present in each case.\n\nsee § 1101.3 regarding valuation after alteration; see § 700.2 for guidelines on simulating consent under duress; see § 900.4 identifying fees owed for binding services rendered against one's will. This provision reinforces the Guild's commitment to protecting citizens from predatory magical practitioners who abuse their powers for financial gain at others' expense.\n\n\"Magic must serve humanity, not enslave it.\" — Archmage Veyra, Autumnwood Accords Preamble",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1101.2",
            "1101.3",
            "700.2",
            "900.4",
            "1101.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "5a80b18307f448de",
