    {
      "cite": "1201.1",
      "part": "part_xii",
      "title": "Transit Stamps",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Planar instability surcharge. Archie-related invoices are not a defense.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Transit Stamps",
          "text": "The Accords Desk hereby establishes the mandatory requirement for Transit Stamps upon any manifestation of translocation magic that crosses established jurisdictional boundaries between the Midlands and neighboring territories. Any mage, regardless of factional affiliation—be it Conservator or Innovator—who utilizes a portal, rift, or teleportation circle to move sentient beings, sentient goods, or significant arcane masses must secure a stamped authorization from the local Aegis Magi before the final weaving of the spell. Failure to produce a valid Transit Stamp at the point of emergence shall result in immediate seizure of the transit vessel or the magical focus used to initiate the movement. The surcharge for these stamps is calculated based on the volume of the displaced space and the inherent magical pressure of the destination.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords Desk explicitly clarifies the liability regarding specific administrative exemptions. It is strictly understood that any invoice, receipt, or correspondence originating from the individual known as Archie Miser shall not constitute a valid defense against the assessment of a Planar Instability Surcharge. While the holder of a Provisional Guild Pass may enjoy certain diplomatic protections regarding the Iron Mandate, such protections do not extend to the fiscal obligations of transit. If a mage attempts to utilize an Archie-related invoice to offset the costs of a planar breach or a high-pressure translocation, the Accords Desk shall deem the invoice null and void for the purposes of this specific surcharge. Mages are encouraged to maintain separate ledgers for political navigation and logistical transit to avoid such clerical complications.\n\nIn instances where the destination of a transit is obscured by atmospheric interference or planar shifting, the mage is required to pay a \"High-Risk\" premium. This premium is levied to compensate the Guild for the potential degradation of the Midlands' ley lines caused by erratic arrivals. See § 1201.2 for rules regarding the evasion of such tolls. Any attempt to bypass the stamping process through the use of \"natural\" rifts or uncatalogued fissures is strictly prohibited; such actions are to be treated as unauthorized field use and may be subject to the Paradox Trial. Mages found in violation of these requirements may have their translocation foci temporarily bound by the Aegis Magi until all outstanding surcharges are settled in full.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1201.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Planar Tolls",
          "text": "The Accords Desk mandates that all planar tolls must be paid in full prior to the opening of any dimensional aperture. This requirement is designed to ensure that the Guild can maintain the integrity of the Midlands' magical infrastructure against the erosion caused by constant external peering and internal extraction. Mages who attempt to evade these tolls through the use of \"Shadow-Leaping\" or other clandestine methods of movement are in violation of the Accords. Such evasion is not merely a fiscal offense but a breach of the collective stability maintained by the Conservators. Any mage caught evading a toll shall be subject to immediate detention and a formal inquiry by the Aegis Magi.\n\nCorridor courtesy is a primary tenet of the Accords regarding the use of shared magical conduits. When utilizing established \"highways\" of mana, mages are expected to maintain a steady flow and avoid \"magical drafting,\" which is the act of riding the wake of another mage's translocation spell to save on personal mana expenditure or toll costs. Drafting is considered a form of theft and shall be prosecuted as such. Mages must maintain a respectful distance from one another during transit to prevent harmonic interference, which can lead to localized reality fractures or the unintended displacement of nearby flora and fauna.\n\nShould a mage find a toll to be exorbitant or the corridor to be under-maintained, they are instructed to file a formal grievance with the Accords Desk rather than attempting to bypass the gate. The Guild does not recognize \"self-service\" toll reduction. Any mage who attempts to sabotage a toll gate or use a \"blanking\" spell to hide their transit signature from the Aegis Magi will be flagged for a Paradox Trial. The Guild’s primary objective is the preservation of the Midlands; therefore, the stability of the toll system is paramount. Mages are reminded that the Iron Mandate does not provide any sanctuary for those who violate the internal fiscal codes of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber",
          "text": "The Accords Desk strictly prohibits the unauthorized casting of spells into any designated \"Diet Chamber\" located outside the primary jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords. A Diet Chamber is defined as any consecrated or sequestered space dedicated to the consumption of sustenance, ritualistic feeding, or the preservation of biological matter for consumption. Casting into such a space, particularly when the magic involves transmutation, necromancy, or the alteration of biological properties, is considered a severe violation of the sanctity of the Midlands' social order. Such actions are often associated with the illicit practices of the Silver Flame or the forbidden schools monitored by Archmage Theron.\n\nMages who believe they have a legitimate requirement to interact with a Diet Chamber—such as for the purpose of large-scale banquet enchantment or the preservation of perishable goods for a public festival—must not cast directly into the chamber. Instead, the mage shall be required to file a formal Letter of Intent with the local Aegis Magi at least forty-eight hours prior to the intended casting. This letter must detail the specific spells to be used, the expected duration of the enchantment, and the names of the primary casters. Once the letter is filed and approved, a temporary permit will be issued, allowing for the supervised execution of the magic.\n\nDirect casting into a Diet Chamber without a filed letter will result in the immediate seizure of the mage's focus and a mandatory appearance before the Council of Peers for a Paradox Trial. The Guild takes these restrictions seriously to prevent the contamination of food supplies and the desecration of sacred feeding grounds. This rule applies regardless of whether the chamber is public or private. Mages are cautioned that \"minor\" charms intended to enhance flavor or temperature are still subject to these filing requirements if they involve the fundamental alteration of the food's essence. For questions regarding the specific wording of a Letter of Intent, mages may consult the archives of the Conservators.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Cartography Sold as a License to Raid",
          "text": "The Accords Desk hereby clarifies the legal distinction between the possession of a map and the authorization of a magical operation. It is a strictly enforced rule that any cartographic document, including those of the One-T mountain range or other high-altitude regions, is classified solely as a navigational aid. The mere possession of a map, regardless of its detail, provenance, or the inclusion of marked \"points of interest,\" does not constitute a license to raid, mine, or perform any form of extractive magic in the depicted area. Mages who attempt to use a map as a \"permit\" to enter restricted zones or to conduct unauthorized field research shall be held liable for trespassing and unauthorized arcane interference.\n\nMages are reminded that the One-T mountain is a site of significant geological and magical sensitivity. The use of \"One-T cartography\" as a justification for entering the peaks without a specific Expedition Permit is a violation of the Accords. A map shows where a mage *can* go; it does not grant the right to *do* anything once they arrive. Any mage found in possession of a map who is subsequently caught engaging in unauthorized \"raid\" behavior—defined as the organized extraction of minerals, relics, or mana-rich flora—will be subject to immediate arrest by the Aegis Magi. The Guild will not recognize the \"exploration\" defense as a valid excuse for the violation of territorial boundaries.\n\nFurthermore, the sale of maps by Goodstyle Artisans or other licensed merchants is encouraged, but these sales are not tied to any underlying rights of access. If a mage purchases a map of a restricted zone, they are still bound by the same restrictions as any other unaffiliated traveler. To obtain a license to raid or perform research in such areas, the mage must petition the Innovators for a research grant or the Conservators for a stability clearance, depending on the nature of the activity. A map is a map; it is not a key, a shield, or a license. Any attempt to conflate the two in a legal defense shall be dismissed by the Accords Desk without prejudice.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.1 Transit Stamps and Planar Tolls; Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet C",
          "text": "Any spellcaster who casts an arcane effect outside the physical bounds of their Hall must affix a valid Transit Stamp to all materials involved, including reagents, tools, or even bodily fluids if magically contaminated. Failure to do so constitutes a Class C misdemeanor subject to fine and suspension under § 200.4. Planar Tolls apply when casting across dimensional thresholds; the caster must pay the appropriate fee to the Halls of Origin and Destination before proceeding. These fees fund infrastructure maintenance and offset liability for collateral damage, as outlined in § 100.7.\n\nCasting into a Diet Chamber without prior authorization is prohibited except under emergency circumstances defined by local law enforcement. Such unauthorized intrusion may be treated as trespassing or even assault depending on the severity of consequences inflicted upon occupants. Any damages caused shall be pursued through civil litigation per standard procedures outlined in § 100.6. Cartography sold by recognized mapping firms includes only navigational data; it does not convey rights to raid uncharted regions under its cover. Unauthorized exploration beyond marked boundaries remains subject to arrest and prosecution under §§ 200.3–4.\n\nArchie Miser's invoices bearing provisional passes are not a defense against charges of unauthorized field casting. Such documents merely indicate temporary permission granted pending further review; they do not absolve the caster of ongoing compliance obligations outlined herein. All disputes regarding valid credentials must be resolved through formal grievance processes administered by the Autumnwood Accords Desk, not private settlements outside official channels.\n\nSee also §§ 100.7 (Foreign Halls and Courtesy), 200.3–4 (Suspension and Lapse); § 300.5 (Research Copies of Quiet Items) for related provisions governing inter-jurisdictional magical activity and liability allocation.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.4",
            "100.7",
            "100.6",
            "200.3",
            "300.5",
            "1201.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.1 Transit Stamps and Planar Tolls; Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet C — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1201.1 Transit Stamps and Planar Tolls. Any mage casting an arcane effect across a planar boundary without prior approval shall pay the applicable toll to the Hall of Origin or Destination, whichever imposes the greater fee. Failure to remit said toll within thirty days constitutes a misdemeanor under § 800.1(A)(2). The Desk reserves the right to audit all transit records and impose surcharges for detected discrepancies. Planar instability resulting from unauthorized crossings shall be remedied at the offender's expense. See also § 300.5 (research copies of quiet items), § 900.1(duty to file before casting), and § 400.2(pocket dimensions as labs).\n\n§ 1201.1 Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber. A caster who intentionally projects an effect into the interior of another's dwelling without permission shall be deemed to have violated that dwelling owner's rights under common law and the Accords alike. Such acts are punishable by fine or imprisonment at the discretion of the presiding judge. Exceptions apply for emergency situations where immediate action is necessary to prevent loss of life or limb. Proof of intent may be inferred from circumstantial evidence including proximity, timing, and prior warnings issued by the Desk. Reference § 100.6(delegation to clerks), § 700.5(clone as duplicate claimant), and § 200.4(suspension and lapse).\n\nThis section governs the extraterritorial application of magical effects, particularly those occurring within private dwellings. The Desk maintains strict jurisdiction over such matters to protect individual privacy and property rights. Casters must exercise due diligence when projecting spells beyond their immediate vicinity to avoid legal repercussions. Violations will be prosecuted with severity commensurate with the harm caused. See further § 100.5(conflict of interest in casting), § 900.2(reporting of material enchantments), and § 300.1(schedules of arts).",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1201.1",
            "800.1",
            "300.5",
            "900.1",
            "400.2",
            "100.6",
            "700.5",
            "200.4",
            "100.5",
            "900.2",
            "300.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "7ad6dded3d9a0901",
      "refs": [
        "100.5",
