    {
      "cite": "1201.2",
      "part": "part_xii",
      "title": "Planar Tolls",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Evasion. Corridor courtesy.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Planar Tolls",
          "text": "Evasion. Corridor courtesy.\n\nPursuant to the jurisdictional oversight established under the Autumnwood Accords, all mages who facilitate the transit of sentient beings, goods, or essence across planar boundaries shall be held strictly liable for the payment of designated Planar Tolls. The Accords Desk maintains that the stabilization of the Midlands’ local reality necessitates a standardized taxation of inter-planar flux. Any mage who utilizes a rift, gateway, or dimensional fold to bypass these established checkpoints shall be deemed in violation of the Accord. Such evasion is categorized as a breach of corridor courtesy, as it places an undue burden on the structural integrity of the local weave and risks the degradation of the surrounding geography. All transit fees are to be calculated by the volume of mana displaced and the duration of the aperture maintained.\n\nMages seeking to move assets across planes must consult the current schedules maintained by the Accords Desk. Failure to declare a planned transit prior to activation may result in the immediate seizure of the traveling assets by the Aegis Magi. Furthermore, any mage who intentionally obscures the nature of their cargo to circumvent toll assessment shall face a formal inquiry. The Accords Desk emphasizes that \"corridor courtesy\" is not merely a social suggestion but a legal requirement for the continued stability of the Midlands. Mages who refuse to acknowledge the necessity of these tolls are viewed as contributing to the entropy of the realm. See § 1.2 for the overarching authority of the Accords Desk in matters of inter-planar regulation.\n\nRegarding the physical maintenance of transit points, any mage who creates a temporary rift or gateway is responsible for the immediate stabilization of the exit point. If a gateway results in structural damage to the local environment—be it cracked stone, scorched earth, or lingering arcane radiation—the initiating mage is liable for all repair costs. This includes the hiring of Goodstyle Artisans for physical reconstruction if the damage exceeds minor surface abrasions. If the exit point is located within a public thoroughfare, the mage must ensure that no debris or residual mana lingers for more than one standard hour. Failure to clear the \"corridor\" of magical residue is a violation of local ordinances and shall be prosecuted as a public nuisance.\n\nIn instances where a planar toll cannot be paid due to the immediate threat of a predatory entity or an imminent collapse of the rift, a \"Force Majeure\" petition must be filed with the Accords Desk within three days of the event. Until such a petition is granted, the mage remains liable for the full toll. The Accords Desk reserves the right to levy additional penalties for any mage who repeatedly utilizes \"emergency\" exemptions to avoid their fiscal obligations to the Guild. For further details on the classification of planar entities and their associated toll rates, see § 1201.1.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.2",
            "1201.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber",
          "text": "Do not. File a letter instead.\n\nThe Autumnwood Accords strictly prohibit the direct casting of offensive, transformative, or high-variance magic into any designated \"Diet Chamber\" or sanctuary of sustenance. A Diet Chamber is defined as any consecrated or secular space dedicated to the preparation, storage, or consumption of food and drink, where the sanctity of the meal is protected from arcane interference. This prohibition is absolute and applies regardless of the location of the casting. A mage who casts a spell into such a chamber—whether for the purpose of preservation, flavor enhancement, or hostile disruption—shall be subject to immediate sanctions under the Paradox Trial. The Guild recognizes that the integrity of sustenance is a fundamental pillar of civil stability; to corrupt a meal is to corrupt the social fabric of the Midlands.\n\nMages who find it necessary to alter the properties of food or drink through arcane means must do so via the approved \"Sanctified Modification\" process. This process requires the mage to submit a formal request to the Accords Desk for a specific permit. Once the permit is granted, the mage may proceed with the modification, provided they do not exceed the parameters outlined in the approved petition. If a mage believes that a Diet Chamber requires a permanent arcane enhancement, they are strictly forbidden from casting such a spell directly into the chamber. Instead, the mage shall draft and file a formal letter of intent with the Accords Desk, detailing the desired outcome, the safety measures to be employed, and the intended duration of the effect.\n\nThe filing of a letter of intent allows the Conservators and Innovators to review the proposed magic for potential risks to the local weave and the safety of the consumers. If the request is denied, the mage must seek an alternative method of modification that does not involve direct casting into the sanctuary. Any mage caught casting into a Diet Chamber without a filed letter and a corresponding permit shall be apprehended by the Aegis Magi. The penalty for such an act shall be determined by the severity of the contamination; minor alterations may result in a fine, while the corruption of a communal feast or the poisoning of a public well may result in a full Seal of Silence or a transition to a pocket-prison.\n\nFurthermore, this regulation extends to the \"flavoring\" of beverages with ephemeral magic. While the Innovators may argue for the creative freedom of such arts, the Conservators maintain that the unpredictable nature of such magic can lead to unintended physiological consequences for the consumer. Therefore, all \"flavor-casting\" must be documented and the ingredients of the magic must be disclosed to the Guild. Mages who refuse to file the necessary paperwork for their culinary enchantments are considered to be acting in bad faith against the Accords. For guidance on the submission of formal letters to the Desk, see § 900.1.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "900.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Cartography Sold as a License to Raid",
          "text": "One-T mountain. A map is a map. It is not a permit.\n\nThe Accords Desk hereby clarifies the legal distinction between the acquisition of geographical data and the authorization of martial action. It is a frequent point of contention among the Innovators that the possession of a detailed map of a restricted area, such as the One-T mountain or other contested territories, constitutes a tacit permission to explore or occupy said region. This is a legal fallacy. Under the Autumnwood Accords, a map is merely a record of topographical and arcane data; it confers no rights of passage, no license to harvest resources, and no permit to engage in hostile action. A mage who purchases or commissions a map of a restricted zone is merely acquiring information. The use of that information to bypass Guild-sanctioned boundaries or to initiate a raid is a separate and punishable offense.\n\nMages who intend to enter restricted territories for the purpose of research, trade, or exploration must secure a formal \"Field Expedition Permit\" from the Accords Desk. This permit must be filed well in advance and must specify the duration of the stay, the number of participants, and the intended scope of activity. The possession of a map, even a high-fidelity one provided by a Goodstyle Artisan, does not exempt the mage from this requirement. Should a mage be intercepted by the Aegis Magi while in a restricted zone, the mere possession of a map shall not serve as a defense. The mage must produce a valid, stamped Expedition Permit to avoid immediate detention and potential referral to a Paradox Trial.\n\nFurthermore, the sale of maps that include \"tactical notations\"—such as identified weak points in defenses, hidden ingress points, or unguarded resource deposits—is strictly regulated. Such maps are classified as \"Sensitive Intelligence\" and may only be sold to Guild-affiliated entities with the express permission of Archmage Veyra. Any third party caught selling such maps to non-Guild actors, including the Regal Empire or the Iron Legion, shall be prosecuted for espionage. The Accords Desk reminds all mages that while knowledge is power, the unauthorized application of that knowledge in a manner that threatens the stability of the Midlands is a breach of the Accord.\n\nIn the event that a mage discovers a new geographical feature or a previously unmapped rift during a sanctioned expedition, they are required to report these findings to the Accords Desk within forty-eight hours. Failure to report new data, or the deliberate withholding of such information for private gain, is a violation of the Guild's data-sharing protocols. A map is a tool for navigation; a permit is a tool for legality. The two are not interchangeable. Mages who attempt to use the \"I only had a map\" defense during a seizure of assets shall be subject to the maximum fines as outlined in § 1001.4. For information on obtaining a Field Expedition Permit, see § 900.2.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.4",
            "900.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.2 Planar Tolls and Extraterritorial Casting",
          "text": "It is hereby declared that any casting directed at or through a Diet Chamber, regardless of the caster's physical location, shall be deemed an extraterritorial act subject to this Accords Desk's full jurisdiction. The presence of a Channeling Arc in the target zone establishes sufficient nexus for regulation and enforcement. See § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; § 900.2 Reporting of Material Enchantments.\n\nCartographic works depicting planar topography, particularly those revealing ingress points or resource nodes, are classified as strategic secrets under § 300.4 Reclassification procedures. The unauthorized sale or distribution of such maps constitutes a felony offense punishable by permanent license revocation and imprisonment in the Pocket Prison. Under § 1201.5 Penalties for Strategic Espionage.\n\nDiet Chambers themselves shall be registered with this Desk upon establishment, disclosing their location, purpose, and intended frequency of use. Failure to register may result in immediate seizure and destruction of the facility as contrabon. See § 400.3 Borrowed Hours; § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill.\n\nAll casters are reminded that their actions extend beyond mere spell effects, encompassing the broader consequences thereof. A well-intentioned ritual performed in ignorance of local laws may still incur liability for resulting damages or disruptions. Counsel is available at no cost to clarify obligations and responsibilities under these provisions. Refer to § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks; § 500.1 Field Casting for Value.\n\nThis Section shall be cited as the Planar Tolls Provision in all future proceedings involving extraterritorial casting or related activities. Any ambiguities herein shall be resolved by reference to the spirit and intent of these Accords, not a literalist interpretation that undermines their efficacy. See § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy; § 800.2 Paradox Trial Procedure.\n\nThe Desk reserves the right to investigate any suspected violations proactively without prior notice or warrant. Cooperation is mandatory unless specific protections apply under established legal frameworks. Refusal may result in escalated penalties including asset seizure and criminal charges. Consult § 100.1 Licensure Required; § 700.5 Administrative Detention Powers.\n\nIt is the goal of this regulation to balance individual freedoms with collective safety, ensuring that magical practices enhance rather than endanger our society. Through diligent enforcement and education, we shall maintain order amidst innovation for generations to come. As always: abide by the law or face the consequences accordingly.\"",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "900.2",
            "300.4",
            "1201.5",
            "400.3",
            "200.6",
            "100.6",
            "500.1",
            "100.7",
            "800.2",
            "100.1",
            "700.5",
            "1201.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.2 Planar Tolls and Extraterritorial Casting — Continued",
          "text": "No casting shall occur within the jurisdictional limits of another Hall without prior written consent, under penalty of immediate arrest for unauthorized practice. This prohibition extends to all forms of transdimensional projection, including but not limited to planar travel, pocket-space manipulation, and spell-based teleportation. Violators shall be subject to full investigation and prosecution per § 800.1 et seq., with fines set at twice the estimated value of any illicit magical effects produced. The burden of proof rests upon the accuser to establish that the accused knowingly entered a restricted zone for the purpose of casting. Defense by accident or ignorance is not valid unless corroborated by witness testimony or official records. Exceptions may be granted only via special permit issued by the Archmage Sovereign, subject to strict oversight and reporting requirements. All permits must specify exact coordinates, duration, and intended uses, with failure to adhere to terms constituting breach of contract and grounds for revocation. Penalties escalate based on severity and repetition, ranging from suspension of license to permanent expulsion from the Guild. Repeat offenders face additional sanctions including mandatory rehabilitation courses and public shaming via the Quiet List. The Desk retains sole discretion to waive penalties in cases of genuine emergency or force majeure, provided full disclosure is made within forty-eight hours of the event. Refusal to comply with these regulations shall be deemed a serious felony offense under the Accords, warranting expedited trial and maximum punishment. The Guild maintains an active surveillance network to detect and deter illegal planar activity, employing both technological means and human informants. Any suspected violator may be intercepted at any time, regardless of location or claimed immunity, for immediate search and seizure proceedings. Failure to submit to lawful detention shall result in escalation of charges and imposition of harsher penalties upon conviction. The provisions herein are designed to protect the integrity of inter-Hall relations and prevent unintended consequences arising from unregulated dimensional interference. All parties agree that compliance with these rules is essential to maintaining peace and order within the broader magical community. Violation of this section shall be treated as a direct affront to the authority of the Autumnwood Accords, inviting swift and severe retribution. The Desk reserves the right to amend or expand upon these guidelines at its sole discretion, without prior notice or consultation. Refusal to abide by updated regulations shall constitute grounds for immediate disciplinary action up to and including expulsion from membership in perpetuity. Ultimately, adherence to these strictures is non-negotiable for all licensed practitioners operating within Guild jurisdiction. Failure to comply will not be tolerated under any circumstances whatsoever. The Accords stand absolute, binding all signatories regardless of status or influence. Any attempt to circumvent or undermine this provision shall be met with utmost severity, ensuring the continued dominance and prestige of the Autumnwood Hall in the magical world at large.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "800.1",
            "1201.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.2 Planar Tolls and Extraterritorial Casting — Continued: Jurisdictional R",
          "text": "The provisions herein govern the conduct of magicians who effect translocations beyond recognized boundaries, whether by intentional projection or inadvertent slip. Such acts constitute a form of extraterritorial casting that triggers unique legal considerations under § 1201.1(a)(ii). When an individual leaves the physical plane without proper authorization, they subject themselves to the full force of the Autumnwood Accords upon re-entry, regardless of prior location at time of departure. This principle applies equally to temporary excursions and permanent departures.\n\nAny caster found guilty of unlicensed planar travel faces immediate suspension under § 200.4 until completion of mandatory re-certification courses covering cross-dimensional safety protocols and jurisdictional compliance. Repeat offenders may be subject to revocation of magical licensure entirely per § 800.1(c). Furthermore, all artifacts acquired during unauthorized excursions must undergo rigorous inspection before release back into lawful commerce, with associated fees assessed pursuant to § 900.4(b)(viii).\n\nThe Desk retains broad discretion to impose additional sanctions deemed necessary for public protection and regulatory integrity, within the limits set forth in § 100.6(d)(ii) regarding delegated authority. Failure to comply fully with these requirements constitutes a Class B infraction punishable by fine up to one thousand gold pieces or imprisonment not exceeding six months at Archmage's Discretion per § 800.1(e)(iii).\n\nIt is hereby declared that all clauses contained in this section shall remain in full force and effect notwithstanding any contrary provisions existing under other statutes or local ordinances which may conflict therewith, by virtue of the superior standing accorded to these binding accords throughout the entire realm. Any attempts to circumvent these regulations through subterfuge or technicality will be met with swift and severe consequences as determined solely by the Council's judgment in each case upon review of all pertinent facts presented at hearing.\n\nsee § 1201.1 for foundational definitions regarding planar boundaries and unauthorized translocation; under § 800.1(c) concerning suspension procedures and re-certification requirements applicable to violators; reference also § 900.4(b)(viii) relating to artifact inspection protocols and fee assessments imposed upon recovered items seized during enforcement actions initiated pursuant hereto. All persons subject to the jurisdiction of this Code are hereby put on formal notice that continued operation outside established parameters shall result in graduated penalties escalating with each subsequent offense until ultimate compliance is achieved or punitive measures rendered permanent as circumstances dictate by the presiding authority acting within its statutory mandate.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1201.1",
            "200.4",
