    {
      "cite": "1201.4",
      "part": "part_xii",
      "title": "Cartography Sold as a License to Raid",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "One-T mountain. A map is a map. It is not a permit.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Cartography Sold as a License to Raid",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords, as mediated by Archmage Veyra, maintain a strict distinction between the acquisition of geographical knowledge and the authorization of hostile incursions. It is a fundamental principle of the Accords that the possession of a map, regardless of its detail, historical depth, or the arcane means by which it was compiled, does not constitute a legal license for the seizure, pillaging, or tactical engagement within the designated territory. This distinction is particularly critical regarding the One-T mountain range, where historical precedents of unauthorized \"treasure expeditions\" have led to significant ecological and arcane destabilization. Any individual, entity, or guild-affiliated party found to be asserting that a purchased map grants them sovereign right of entry for the purpose of raiding shall be subject to immediate summary citation.\n\nThe Accords Desk hereby clarifies that cartography is a scholarly pursuit and a tool for navigation, not a instrument of martial permission. Under these statutes, a map serves only to define the physical boundaries of a landmass; it does not supersede the territorial rights of the local inhabitants, the sovereign claims of the regional powers, or the environmental preservation mandates established by the Conservators. Any entity attempting to use the purchase of a One-T mountain chart as a justification for bypassing the standard requisitioning process for exploration shall be deemed to be in violation of the peace. The presence of a map in one's possession shall not be admitted as evidence of \"prior consent\" from the land's overseers.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild warns against the illicit sale of \"Raid Licenses\" disguised as topographical scrolls. Merchants or clandestine brokers who market maps with clauses implying permission to enter restricted zones, extract resources, or engage in combat are in direct violation of the Accords. Such sales are considered fraudulent and may lead to the seizure of the maps in question and the revocation of the seller's mercantile privileges. The Accords Desk recognizes that the One-T mountain range is a sensitive zone; therefore, any expeditionary force must secure a formal Permit of Incursion from the appropriate regional authority. A map is a representation of the world, not a key to its secrets.\n\nMages and explorers who rely on the \"Cartographic Exemption\"—the notion that a map provides a legal shield against charges of trespassing or looting—shall find such defenses void. If a party is apprehended within the One-T mountains without a valid, stamped Permit of Incursion, the mere production of a map shall not serve as a mitigating factor in their sentencing. The Aegis Magi are instructed to prioritize the apprehension of those who use \"exploratory intent\" as a veneer for unauthorized raiding. See § 1.2 for matters regarding the enacting authority of the Accords Desk. All parties seeking to survey the One-T range are reminded that one-T cartography is a map, not a raid license.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.4 Cartography as Evidence, Not Authority",
          "text": "A map or chart depicting terrain features, magical concentrations, or strategic advantages is merely documentary evidence and confers no rights upon its possessor. Under § 1201.3, only an official permit signed by the Sovereign grants authority to enter contested zones; a privately held cartographic reproduction does not substitute therefor. Courts consistently hold that possession of detailed maps alone cannot justify unauthorized incursions into restricted areas, regardless of their accuracy or specificity. See § 1200.5 regarding the presumption of innocence absent formal adjudication.\n\nThe Guild maintains that all intelligence gathering must proceed through lawful channels established by statute. While individuals may observe and record external phenomena consistent with general observation rights under § 130.2, covert operations requiring infiltration or deception fall outside these protections. Cartographic analysis remains a valid investigative tool when conducted openly, but it does not transform the holder into an agent of state power capable of overriding territorial sovereignty claims. This principle protects both citizen privacy and the integrity of official investigations alike.\n\nFurthermore, the distribution of sensitive maps raises concerns regarding national security and public safety that exceed private property disputes. The Sovereign retains discretion to regulate access to such materials on a case-by-case basis, weighing potential harms against freedoms of information protected by other provisions. Unauthorized circulation may constitute contempt for obstructing lawful inquiry or revealing classified data under § 150.7. Parties found guilty of disseminating prohibited cartographic works face penalties up to and including permanent sealing of magical abilities per § 800.3(b).\n\nUltimately, the legal status of maps mirrors that of other informational assets covered by the Accords: they are subject to reasonable restrictions designed to prevent abuse while preserving legitimate interests in knowledge acquisition. Possession does not equal possession of power; rather, it represents a responsibility to use such information wisely and responsibly within bounds set forth by competent authority. Failure to recognize this distinction risks severe consequences ranging from civil liability to criminal prosecution under applicable statutes. All citizens are urged to consult official sources before acting upon unverified intelligence derived from commercial or personal mapping efforts.\n\n\"Under § 1201.3, only an official permit grants authority to enter contested zones; a privately held cartographic reproduction does not substitute therefor.\"\n\"Unauthorized circulation may constitute contempt for obstructing lawful inquiry or revealing classified data under § 150.7.\"\n\"The Sovereign retains discretion to regulate access to such materials on a case-by-case basis...\"\n\"Citizens are urged to consult official sources before acting upon unverified intelligence derived from commercial mapping efforts.\"",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1201.3",
            "1200.5",
            "130.2",
            "150.7",
            "800.3",
            "1201.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.4 Cartography as Evidence, Not Authority — Continued",
          "text": "A map constitutes documentary evidence only; it confers no rights or privileges by its mere possession. The acquisition or sale of cartographic representations, including those marked with restricted zones or tactical notations, shall be treated as the transfer of informational assets subject to standard commercial regulations. No individual may invoke ownership of a planar chart to justify unauthorized entry into sovereign space or violation of local ordinances. Violators face penalties under §§ 1201.3 and 800.6 for trespass and reckless magical exposure respectively.\n\nThe Guild Desk shall retain exclusive authority to interpret spatial data within its jurisdiction. Disputes regarding the accuracy, scope, or validity of any mapping product must be adjudicated by appointed magistrates empowered with technical expertise in surveying and dimensional geometry. Until such official review is complete, all parties are directed to suspend operations pending resolution. Failure to comply may result in injunctions pursuant to § 601.2 and temporary suspension of magical licensure per § 200.4(b).\n\nThis provision does not impede lawful research or educational use of cartographic materials obtained through proper channels under §§ 300.5 and 900.2(c). Institutions maintaining bona fide academic programs may request copies from the Desk subject to nondisclosure agreements and usage restrictions outlined in Appendix G. All requests must be submitted on official letterhead with proof of institutional standing and faculty credentials attached.\n\nCartographic fraud remains a serious offense under this Article and related statutes. The intentional falsification, alteration, or misrepresentation of spatial information for illicit purposes shall constitute grand larceny enhanced by magical endangerment per § 1003.7(c). Convicted offenders face imprisonment up to ten years plus mandatory sealing of all magical abilities via § 800.3(a)(ii). Recidivists are subject to permanent disenfranchisement and exile from the Guild territories without recourse.\n\nNothing herein shall be construed to diminish existing treaty obligations between member nations regarding shared borders, resource rights, or defense pacts. Diplomatic agreements supersede conflicting provisions in this Code unless explicitly amended by unanimous vote of the Hall under § 201.5. The Desk maintains a register of all international accords affecting planar geography for reference purposes only; consultation is available upon request to accredited representatives.\n\nThis Section shall take effect immediately upon filing and remains in force indefinitely unless repealed or substantially revised by subsequent legislation passed through proper channels. Any ambiguities arising from its implementation shall be resolved in favor of public safety and equitable treatment of all parties involved. The Guild reserves the right to issue clarifying guidance memos as needed without altering the substantive text. Compliance with this directive is mandatory for all licensed practitioners operating within Autumnwood Accords territory.\n\nsee §§ 1201.3, 800.6; under § 900.2(c); per Appendix G and § 201.5; pursuant to § 1003.7(c) and § 800.3(a)(ii).",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1201.3",
            "601.2",
            "200.4",
            "300.5",
            "1003.7",
            "800.3",
            "201.5",
            "900.2",
            "1201.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.4 Cartography as Evidence, Not Authority — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "Whereas the One-T Mountain map constitutes an expression of cartographic opinion and artistic license rather than a binding legal instrument, it remains admissible evidence in proceedings concerning territorial disputes or unauthorized field use under §§ 500.3 and 901.2. The Guild shall weigh such representations alongside other admissible facts, including witness testimony, physical inspection of terrain features, and official survey records maintained by the Conservators. No single document, however compelling its visual presentation may appear to the untrained eye, shall be deemed conclusive proof of ownership or right-of-way without corroboration from multiple independent sources. This rule serves to prevent frivolous claims based solely upon speculative depictions while preserving the integrity of sworn statements and verified data. Violations of this provision shall be treated as contempt of court and subject to sanctions pursuant to § 1502.1(e).\n\nThe Desk further notes that any party relying on the One-T map for purposes beyond mere reference must first obtain explicit authorization from the appropriate authorities in accordance with established protocols set forth in §§ 700.4 through 700.6. Failure to secure such permission prior to engaging in activities within the mapped area shall constitute a breach of contract and potentially expose the offending entity to liability for damages arising therefrom. The Guild retains sole discretion to determine whether any given action constitutes \"unauthorized field use\" under § 500.3, with final adjudication resting with the High Council convened by Archmage Veyra upon referral from the local Magistrate. All determinations shall be made in accordance with applicable laws and regulations governing magical research and exploration practices within the Accords jurisdiction. Any disputes regarding interpretation of this clause shall be resolved through binding arbitration administered by a panel of retired Conservators appointed jointly by the Innovator faction leadership and the Aegis Magi Order.\n\nIt is hereby declared that the One-T Mountain map, in whatever form it may exist—whether as digitized file, printed publication, or oral tradition—is neither a deed nor a leasehold instrument but rather an informational resource intended to aid navigators familiar with planar geography. Parties seeking title or usage rights must pursue such interests through proper channels and under the supervision of duly licensed appraisers accredited by the Guild's Certification Board. Unauthorized possession or distribution of classified survey data shall be prosecuted as theft of government secrets pursuant to Title 18, Chapter 37 of the United States Code, with penalties enhanced where magical components are involved. The Desk warns that attempts to exploit ambiguities in cartographic notation for illicit gain will meet with swift and severe consequences, including revocation of operating licenses and permanent expulsion from the professional ranks of Arcane Practitioners recognized by this Accord. Let all who read these words take heed: knowledge carries great responsibility, and misuse thereof brings retribution sure as winter's frost. So let it be written in stone and sealed with blood oath!",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "500.3",
            "1502.1",
            "700.4",
            "1201.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Cartography as Evidence, Not Authority — Continued",
          "text": "The mere possession or distribution of a detailed map of the One-T does not constitute proof of intent to raid or breach any laws under this Code. Maps are commercial products that may be traded freely between private parties without implicating criminal liability absent additional facts indicating unlawful purpose. To convict an individual solely on grounds that they owned or viewed such cartographic material would violate fundamental principles of fair notice and proportionality enshrined in §§ 1.4–1.5 and 700.1(b).\n\nHowever, where a map is coupled with other incriminating circumstances—such as purchase from known raiders, modification to include secret passages or guard rotations, or possession alongside stolen goods—it may serve as corroborating evidence of guilty knowledge under the totality-of-the-circumstances test prescribed in § 700.2. In such cases, the map becomes a material link in establishing the nexus between defendant and planned violation rather than standing alone as proof beyond reasonable doubt. Courts retain full discretion to weigh its probative value against potential prejudice pursuant to standard evidentiary rules codified at §§ 601–605.\n\nNo individual shall be held criminally liable for merely owning or displaying a map of the One-T region absent clear evidence of further incriminating conduct. Absent such proof, maps remain innocuous artifacts subject only to ordinary contract law governing their sale and transfer between consenting adults. This principle reflects our enduring commitment to presumption of innocence and avoidance of overbreadth in penal statutes—a cornerstone of just governance under §§ 10–12 and 700.3(a).\n\nIt shall be unlawful for any person knowingly to distribute maps of the One-T specifically designed to facilitate unauthorized expeditions therein, where such distribution is intended to further illegal ends. Such conduct may constitute aiding and abetting under § 502 when paired with substantive involvement in planning or executing a raid. Mere possession remains innocent unless accompanied by overt acts demonstrating criminal intent beyond a reasonable doubt as required by our shared legal traditions enshrined in §§ 10–12 and 700.3(b).\n\nThis provision shall be construed narrowly to avoid chilling legitimate cartographic study, tourism planning, or artistic expression involving the One-T region. Broad application could impermissibly infringe upon freedoms of speech, association, and movement guaranteed herein and under broader constitutional protections referenced in § 15. Courts must balance enforcement interests against these foundational liberties when adjudicating disputes arising hereunder consistent with our enduring commitment to due process as outlined in §§ 20–22 and 700.4(c).\n\nSee also §§ 300.1 (Schedules of Arts), 700.2 (Corroborating Evidence Standards), and 900.5 (Record Retention Requirements) for related provisions governing classification, proof, and documentation obligations within the Guild framework. This section stands as a critical safeguard against misuse of mapping technology while preserving lawful inquiry into one of the most treacherous regions known to humankind under our shared constitutional values referenced in § 15.\n\n*Archmage's Note: Clarity is paramount here lest we inadvertently criminalize harmless curiosity or artistic endeavor. Let us remain vigilant guardians of both public safety and civil liberties alike.*",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "700.2",
            "300.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.4 Cartography as Evidence, Not Authority — Continued — Administration",
          "text": "The mere existence of a detailed map does not confer upon its holder the right to traverse or exploit the regions it depicts. Under § 1201.3, all planar transit requires specific tolls and stamps issued by the appropriate authority; no private document can supersede these statutory requirements. A cartographic artifact remains evidence of prior passage rather than a license for future intrusion. Any attempt to utilize such maps as a pretext for unauthorized access constitutes trespass under § 200.1 and may result in immediate suspension of the holder's licensure.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild maintains that all publicly available plans are intended solely for educational purposes and risk assessment. They do not imply permission to engage in hazardous activities within mapped territories without explicit consent from local magistrates or landowners. Violation of this principle shall be treated as a Class A misdemeanor punishable by fines up to one hundred gold crowns and community service equivalent to ten standard workweeks. Repeat offenders face harsher penalties including permanent revocation of their arcane credentials.\n\nIt is the responsibility of every licensed mage to verify current regulations before entering unfamiliar zones, regardless of what previous records may suggest. Reliance on outdated or incomplete maps shall not excuse non-compliance with existing laws nor mitigate liability for resulting damages. The Desk retains sole discretion to modify access protocols based on emerging threats or security concerns unrelated to any individual's possession of cartographic materials.\n\nIn sum, the holding in § 1201.3 stands firm: no piece of paper grants carte blanche to explore dangerous frontiers recklessly. Mages must exercise due diligence and respect for established boundaries at all times. Failure to do so invites swift and severe consequences under this Code and those of higher precedence. Let none presume authority where none exists, lest they find themselves bound by chains far heavier than any spellbook page could contain.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1201.3",
            "200.1",
            "1201.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "70dbf1422ffc3990",
