    {
      "cite": "1301.3",
      "part": "part_xiii",
      "title": "Appeals",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Desk, then Paradox Trial, then nowhere useful.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Appeals",
          "text": "Any mage, practitioner, or entity aggrieved by a final determination issued by the Accords Desk may initiate a formal petition for review under the provisions of § 1301.3. The primary venue for such a grievance remains the Accords Desk itself, where the presiding Clerk shall review the administrative record to ensure that the original ruling was rendered in strict accordance with the Autumnwood Accords. This initial review is a matter of procedural integrity; the Desk shall not re-litigate the underlying merits of the magical variance or the specificities of the arcane transgression, but shall instead determine if the Desk's own internal protocols were violated during the initial adjudication. If the Clerk finds that the record is complete and the procedure was followed, the petition shall be denied, and the ruling shall stand as a matter of administrative finality.\n\nShould the petitioner maintain that the Desk’s procedural determination is fundamentally flawed or that a grave miscarriage of arcane justice has occurred, the matter may be elevated to a formal Paradox Trial. Pursuant to § 800.2, a Paradox Trial shall be convened by a council of peers selected from the relevant faction—be it the Conservators or the Innovators—depending on the nature of the original charge. During this phase, the peer council shall examine the substantive evidence, the efficacy of the seals applied, and the proportionality of the sanctions imposed. The trial by peers serves as the highest deliberative body within the Guild's legal framework, intended to balance the traditionalist requirements of Archmage Theron with the research liberties championed by Janna Brightspark.\n\nUpon the conclusion of a Paradox Trial, the verdict rendered by the peer council is considered absolute and binding. There is no further mechanism for appeal within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords. The Guild recognizes that the paradoxes of magic are inherently volatile; therefore, the legal system must eventually reach a point of closure to prevent infinite recursive litigation. Once a Paradox Trial has concluded, the petitioner is advised that the matter is closed. Any attempt to seek further redress outside of the Guild’s established legal channels shall be viewed as an act of defiance against the Accords. The law provides no further sanctuary for those who seek to endlessly contest the sovereign mediation of Archmage Veyra. See § 1301.2 for penalty accruals.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1301.3",
            "800.2",
            "1301.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Who May Inspect",
          "text": "The sanctity of the Accords Desk and the confidentiality of its archives are paramount to the stability of the Midlands. Consequently, access to the internal records, active dockets, and the physical premises of the Accords Desk is strictly limited to authorized personnel. Only a designated Clerk of the Desk, as defined in § 100.1, possesses the standing to enter the archives and handle the primary ledgers. These Clerks are the sole custodians of the Guild's legal history and are tasked with ensuring that no unauthorized magical signatures or mundane intrusions compromise the integrity of the bound codes.\n\nFurthermore, inspection of specific dockets or the status of a pending Paradox Trial may be granted to named officers of the Guild. These officers must be explicitly identified in a written requisition and must possess a high-level clearance granted by the Archmage Sovereign. Such access is granted only on a need-to-know basis, typically for the purposes of enforcement by the Aegis Magi or for high-level mediation by Archmage Veyra. No individual, regardless of their rank within the Conservator or Innovator factions, may demand a general inspection of the archives without a specific warrant signed by the presiding Archmage.\n\nThe public, including unaffiliated mages, traveling merchants, and members of the Goodstyle Artisans, are strictly prohibited from entering the Accords Desk or attempting to inspect the Guild's private records. No crowd, assembly, or group of observers shall be permitted to view the proceedings of the Desk or the contents of the archive. Any attempt to breach this perimeter or to solicit information from a Clerk regarding the Quiet List or other sensitive records shall be met with immediate summary expulsion. Violations of this inspection protocol may result in the immediate revocation of a Provisional Guild Pass or the initiation of a Paradox Trial for interference with Guild sovereignty. See § 900.3 for record retention limits.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "100.1",
            "900.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.3 Appeals and Inspection Rights",
          "text": "Any party aggrieved by an assessment or penalty imposed under these Accords may petition the Autumnwood Accords Desk for review within thirty days of notification. Such petitions must be supported by sworn testimony, documentary evidence, and a detailed argument demonstrating error in fact or law. The Desk shall convene an emergency session to hear oral arguments from both parties and render a final determination within fourteen days. This expedited process is designed to ensure prompt resolution of disputes without undue burden upon the Guild's administrative resources. Inspection rights are granted freely to any interested party, provided such inspection does not disrupt ongoing operations or compromise confidential information. Requests for access to records must be made in writing and shall be fulfilled promptly unless specific exemptions apply under §§ 100.6 or 200.4. The Desk reserves discretion to deny inspection where it would jeopardize national security or the integrity of enforcement proceedings. All decisions rendered on appeals are final and binding, subject only to extraordinary review by the full Council in cases involving precedent-setting interpretations. This provision balances the need for efficient dispute resolution with protections against arbitrary action by individual clerks. Through this structured appeal mechanism, the Guild ensures fairness while maintaining its authority to enforce compliance across all jurisdictions. The spirit of these rules demands both rigor and compassion in their application, reflecting our shared commitment to justice and order. Let it be known that we stand ready to serve as impartial arbiters of truth and equity for all who abide by this noble Code. May the scales remain balanced and the path clear for those guided by wisdom and integrity. So mote it be!",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "1301.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.3 Appeals and Inspection Rights — Continued",
          "text": "Every licensed mage or registered artificer shall have the right to appeal any adverse ruling issued by the Accords Desk within seven days of delivery, provided the grounds for appeal fall within those enumerated in under § 200.6 Delegation to Clerks. Such appeals must be submitted in triplicate via registered courier service and accompanied by a fee equal to double the original filing charge, payable only in Wario Coin or approved currency as specified in § 1301.1 Schedule of Fees. The Presiding Archmage shall convene an emergency session of the Desk within forty-eight hours to review the appeal on its merits without further notice to the parties concerned, unless otherwise directed by order of the Chair. The decision rendered shall be final and binding upon all concerned, subject only to extraordinary writs as provided in law.\n\nInspection rights extend to all records pertaining to an individual mage's licensure status, including but not limited to original applications, examination results, disciplinary history, and renewal receipts. Such records may be inspected by the licensee at reasonable times during normal business hours at the Autumn Wood Hall or any regional branch office upon presentation of valid identification and proof of current licensure. Copies thereof may be obtained for a nominal fee calculated according to § 1301.2 Penalties. No party shall refuse inspection on grounds of privacy or proprietary concern absent compelling evidence of material prejudice to national security or public safety, as determined by the Desk in its sole discretion.\n\nAll appeals must conform strictly to procedural requirements set forth herein and may be subject to dismissal for failure to comply therewith. The Desk reserves the right to deny any appeal deemed frivolous, vexatious, or lacking legal basis without further explanation. Parties aggrieved by such dismissals may petition for reconsideration once every ninety days at their own expense. Final determinations shall constitute res judicata barring relitigation of same matter between same parties except under circumstances expressly authorized by statute or judicial precedent recognized as binding upon this jurisdiction. Nothing contained herein shall be construed to limit any rights otherwise available at law or in equity, nor to create new causes of action independent of those explicitly enumerated herein.\n\nThis section serves to ensure fair and transparent adjudication while maintaining the integrity and efficiency of the Guild's regulatory functions. By balancing the rights of individual practitioners with the needs of collective order, it fosters an environment where lawful magic may flourish under the watchful gaze of our wise stewards. May these provisions guide us always in upholding justice for all who walk this blessed earth!",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.6",
            "1301.1",
            "1301.2",
            "1301.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.3 Appeals and Inspection Rights — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "The right to appeal any decision of the Accords Desk is expressly granted under these provisions, subject to strict procedural requirements designed to ensure fairness while preventing abuse. An aggrieved party may petition for review within thirty days of receiving written notice of the final determination, stating with particularity the grounds for objection and citing specific clauses of this Codex allegedly violated. Such appeals shall be heard by a specially constituted panel comprising three Desk members not involved in the original ruling, plus two external magisters appointed by lot from the Quiet List, all sworn to impartiality under penalty of immediate disqualification. The burden rests entirely on the appellant to demonstrate error de novo or manifest unfairness; mere dissatisfaction with outcomes is insufficient. Appeals may be granted only where clear mistakes appear on the face of the record or where new evidence materially alters the factual matrix. All appeals must conclude within sixty days, failing which they are deemed abandoned and unreviewable except by extraordinary writ at the Desk's sole discretion. Concurrently, parties retain a limited right to inspect official records directly affecting their interests upon written request and payment of nominal fees covering reproduction costs. This inspection privilege is exercisable during normal business hours at the Hall or via certified electronic delivery where permitted by technical capability. Inspectors must sign an oath preserving confidentiality and prohibiting use beyond the scope of the contested matter. Violation results in automatic revocation of all future access privileges and potential criminal sanctions under § 1502. These safeguards balance transparency with necessary operational security, ensuring both accountability to the governed and protection of sensitive information vital to public safety. No further changes shall be made without explicit legislative action by full vote of the entire Council.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1301.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.3 Appeals and Inspection Rights — Continued — Administration",
          "text": "The right to appeal a Desk decision is absolute, but it carries weight. An appellant must exhaust all remedies below before seeking review by the Full Council under § 1301.4. Until then, the original ruling stands as if no appeal were filed. This prevents frivolous challenges from paralyzing enforcement efforts while preserving the accused's ultimate recourse to higher authority.\n\nInspection rights accompany these appeals. Any party subject to a Desk order may petition for an independent audit of their records and operations by a neutral third-party auditor approved by both parties. Such audits serve to verify compliance without necessarily revealing trade secrets or sensitive information. The Auditor's findings become part of the official record and may influence subsequent proceedings.\n\nSpecial protections apply to certain categories of data. Personal identifiers, health records, and confidential business strategies require explicit consent before disclosure, even during an audit. Redacted versions may suffice where full transparency is not essential to determine compliance. This balances legitimate oversight needs against privacy concerns inherent in the Guild's broad regulatory mandate.\n\nAppeals must be filed within thirty days of receiving notice of the contested action, unless extended for good cause shown. Failure to timely appeal results in deemed acceptance of the ruling and waives further objection on those grounds. The burden lies with the appellant to demonstrate error or abuse of discretion by the original decision-maker. Mere dissatisfaction with an outcome does not constitute a valid basis for review.\n\nThe Full Council exercises final authority over all appeals, acting as both judge and jury in this forum. Its decisions are binding unless reversed on direct appeal to the Archmage Sovereign under extraordinary circumstances. The Council strives to render prompt judgments while ensuring due process is strictly observed at every stage of the proceedings.\n\nInspection orders issued by the Desk must specify precisely what materials may be reviewed, for how long, and by whom. Unwarranted broadsides granting open-ended access to all records are invalid unless expressly authorized by statute or mutual agreement between the parties. Overreach in this regard exposes the enforcing officer to disciplinary action for abuse of power.\n\nConfidentiality agreements often accompany inspection privileges, requiring recipients to safeguard obtained information from improper use. Violation of such terms constitutes grounds for dismissal of any appeal based on those records and may trigger separate sanctions against the offending party. The integrity of the appellate process depends upon maintaining proper boundaries between oversight and intrusion into private affairs.\n\nUltimately, the appeal system exists to correct mistakes and uphold fairness in Guild administration. It is not a mechanism for delaying enforcement or evading responsibility through endless litigation. Parties must accept that some degree of uncertainty accompanies any regulatory regime and plan accordingly when submitting to the Desk's authority. Wise counsel dictates respecting the process rather than gaming it for tactical advantage at every turn.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1301.4",
            "1301.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "5f37a7fd6a2c4f78",
      "refs": [
        "100.1",
        "100.6",
        "200.6",
        "800.2",
        "900.3",
        "1301.1",
        "1301.2",
        "1301.4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "1301.4",
      "part": "part_xiii",
      "title": "Who May Inspect",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "Clerks, named officers. Not a crowd.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Who May Inspect",
          "text": "The authority to conduct physical, metaphysical, or administrative inspections of Guild-sanctioned properties, research laboratories, and personal vaults held under the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords is strictly limited to designated personnel. The Guild maintains that the integrity of arcane research and the safety of the public rely upon a controlled environment where the presence of unauthorized observers could lead to the contamination of magical signatures, the theft of intellectual property, or the accidental triggering of volatile enchantments. Consequently, any individual seeking to enter a site of restricted research or to audit a ledger of magical assets must be formally recognized by the Accords Desk as an authorized inspector.\n\nUnder the provisions of the Autumnwood Accords, only Clerks of the Accords Desk and named officers of the Aegis Magi possess the standing to conduct such inspections. This includes, but is not limited to, high-ranking members of the Conservator and Innovator factions who have been granted specific, time-bound mandates by Archmage Veyra. These officers must present their official seals and a signed warrant of inspection upon arrival at any site. The presence of a named officer does not grant an inherent right of entry to their subordinates; rather, only those individuals explicitly named in the writ of inspection may cross the threshold of the designated area. Any officer who permits an unnamed individual to accompany them into a restricted zone shall be held personally liable for any breach of security, as defined in § 1301.2.\n\nThe prohibition against public inspection is absolute. No crowd, group of commoners, religious congregation, or unauthorized body of mages may gather to demand a viewing of Guild archives, research facilities, or private magical holdings. The Guild acknowledges the public's interest in the safety of the Midlands, yet maintains that the nuances of arcane regulation are not suited for public consumption. Any attempt to organize a mass inspection or a \"public viewing\" of a restricted site shall be deemed an act of civil unrest and may be met with the immediate deployment of the Aegis Magi to disperse the assembly. Furthermore, the mere presence of a crowd in the vicinity of an active inspection site may result in the immediate suspension of the inspection and a filing of grievances against the organizers.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild clarifies that \"public interest\" does not constitute a legal basis for the bypass of these restrictions. While the Goodstyle Artisans may occasionally request collaborative oversight of specific craftsmanship projects, such requests must still be processed through the standard petitioning system and result in a formal appointment of a specific, named inspector. The Guild shall not be compelled to provide access to any individual who does not hold the requisite rank or specific designation within the Accords. Any violation of these protocols, including the unauthorized recording of an inspection or the covert observation of a Clerk performing their duties, shall be subject to the penalties outlined in § 1301.2. See § 100.1 for the list of authorized offices and titles.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1301.2",
            "100.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.4 Authorized Inspectors and Access Procedures",
          "text": "Only duly appointed inspectors holding valid credentials issued under these Accords shall have authority to examine records, files, or materials subject to this Part. Such inspectors must present themselves for identification at the door of any facility where relevant documents are maintained, stating their purpose clearly and producing written authorization signed by a member of the Autumn Wood Desk. Facilities may refuse entry to individuals who cannot satisfactorily prove their bona fides as authorized agents of the Guild.\n\nUpon gaining access, inspectors shall conduct examinations in a professional manner consistent with the stated objectives of this section. They are empowered to view, copy, and take notes from any document within the scope of an active investigation or audit. However, they may not remove originals without explicit permission from their supervising officer. All copies must bear a watermark stating \"Property of the Autumn Wood Mages' Guild\" along with the date and name of the inspector making the reproduction.\n\nInspection activities shall be carried out during normal business hours unless special arrangements are made in advance for after-hours access when required by operational necessity. Inspectors must adhere strictly to safety protocols established by the facility operator while performing their duties. Failure to comply with these basic rules may result in immediate termination of the inspection and possible disciplinary action against the offending inspector.\n\nAccess granted under this section shall be limited solely to the purposes for which it was authorized. Inspectors are prohibited from using Guild resources for personal gain, research unrelated to official business, or any other improper ends. Any suspected misuse must be reported forthwith to their superior officer for investigation and appropriate corrective measures. Violation of these limitations may constitute grounds for revocation of inspection privileges and further sanctions as deemed necessary by the Desk.\n\nThe rights conferred herein are subject to reasonable restrictions imposed by local laws, regulations, or safety considerations that cannot practicably be circumvented during an active inspection. Inspectors shall consult with facility management ahead of time whenever potential conflicts arise between Guild mandates and external constraints affecting the course of their work. Good faith efforts to accommodate conflicting requirements may mitigate liability for delays or incomplete examinations arising from unavoidable impediments beyond their control.\n\nInspections conducted pursuant to § 1301.4 shall be documented in detail, including dates, times, locations, individuals present, observations made, and actions taken. Findings must be compiled into a comprehensive report submitted within thirty days of completion for review by the supervising officer. Reports containing classified information shall be marked accordingly and handled according to established security protocols governing sensitive materials.\n\nThis section does not abrogate any existing rights or remedies available under applicable law nor create any additional ones beyond those explicitly stated herein. All disputes arising from interpretation or application of these provisions shall be resolved exclusively through the formal grievance procedures outlined elsewhere in this Code. Nothing contained herein shall impair the inherent authority of the Desk to protect its interests and enforce compliance with the Accords as it deems necessary for the greater good of society at large.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1301.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.4 Authorized Inspectors and Access Procedures — Continued",
          "text": "Inspection rights granted under this Part shall be exercised solely by individuals holding valid credentials issued by the Autumnwood Accords Desk or its duly appointed agents. Such credentials must be presented upon request at any time during an inspection, failure to produce them may result in immediate termination of access privileges. Authorized inspectors include but are not limited to: Guild Clerks Grade III and above; sworn officers of the Aegis Magi; independent auditors contracted with the Desk pursuant to § 1301.2(b); and representatives of allied regulatory bodies under treaty agreement. No other persons, whether acting in official capacity or otherwise, shall conduct inspections without explicit written authorization from the Presiding Officer.\n\nUpon presentation of valid credentials, authorized inspectors shall be granted reasonable access to examine any records, documents, premises, or materials deemed relevant to the scope of their investigation. Inspectors must provide a detailed inventory of items removed for examination and return them promptly thereafter, accompanied by a signed receipt confirming condition and completeness. Any refusal to cooperate with inspection procedures may constitute obstruction under § 1301.2(c)(iv) and subject the respondent to further penalties as provided herein.\n\nInspections shall be conducted during normal business hours unless exigent circumstances exist warranting after-hours access. In such cases, inspectors must notify the inspected entity at least two (2) hours in advance whenever feasible, specifying date, time, duration, and purpose of entry. Inspectors are prohibited from interfering with ordinary business operations except where necessary to fulfill their statutory duties. All inspections shall be documented via official report filed within five (5) working days thereafter, detailing findings, observations, and recommendations for remedial action if applicable.\n\nInspected entities retain the right to accompany inspectors during examinations unless otherwise directed by law or emergency conditions requiring swift intervention without delay. Accompanying personnel must maintain professional decorum and refrain from impeding inspection efforts through argumentation or intimidation tactics. Requests for clarification regarding inspector actions should be made privately outside the presence of witnesses to preserve impartiality and efficiency. Failure to adhere to these protocols may result in escalation of enforcement measures including suspension of licenses or initiation of disciplinary proceedings.\n\nThis Section operates independently of any other provisions herein and shall not be construed abrogating existing rights under applicable law unless expressly stated otherwise. Any ambiguity regarding scope of inspection authority shall be resolved favorably towards ensuring full compliance with regulatory mandates while minimizing undue burden on inspected parties. The integrity of this process is paramount to maintaining public trust in the Guild's oversight functions and effectiveness thereof.\n\nsee § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks; see § 400.3 Borrowed Hours; Interest in Time; see § 900.2 Reporting of Material Enchantments; see § 1201.1 Transit Stamps.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1301.2",
            "100.6",
            "400.3",
            "900.2",
            "1201.1",
            "1301.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.4 Authorized Inspectors and Access Procedures — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "Inspection authority extends only to duly appointed Guild clerks bearing valid credentials, as defined in § 200.6. No individual may enter private premises unaccompanied or without cause stated in writing by an Archmage or High Conservator. Unauthorized entry shall be treated as trespass under common law and subject to immediate arrest by Aegis Magi upon complaint.\n\nAccess requests must specify purpose, scope, duration, and justification consistent with regulatory objectives outlined herein. Bulk data collection or systematic monitoring absent imminent threat violates the sanctity of personal space guaranteed by § 1.4. Inspectors shall exercise restraint, avoiding unnecessary disruption to lawful business operations while fulfilling their duties diligently and impartially.\n\nCooperation is mandated by law but does not compel surrender of privileged information protected by attorney-client privilege or other recognized exemptions. Individuals retain right to counsel during interviews except in emergencies involving public safety risks outlined in § 100.8. Recording devices require explicit consent from all parties present before activation, per privacy statutes incorporated herein by reference.\n\nFailure to comply with inspection mandates may result in escalated penalties including fines, license revocation, and criminal charges for obstruction of justice under applicable penal codes. Repeated violations warrant referral to the Paradox Trial Council for adjudication pursuant to established procedures governing professional misconduct.\n\nThis section remains subject to periodic review and amendment by the Autumnwood Accords Desk as needed to address emerging challenges posed by technological advances and evolving societal norms affecting magical commerce practices within our jurisdictional boundaries.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.6",
            "1.4",
            "100.8",
            "1301.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.4 Authorized Inspectors and Access Procedures — Continued — Administration",
          "text": "The authority to inspect records, filings, and physical items held by the Accords Desk shall be vested exclusively in officers designated in writing by the Archmage Sovereign or their duly appointed delegates. Such designation shall specify the scope of inspection authority, including types of documents, locations, and timeframes, and must be maintained on file for public reference. No individual may act as an inspector unless holding a current letter of appointment bearing the official seal of the Guild Hall.\n\nInspectors granted access pursuant to this Section shall exercise their powers in accordance with the principles of necessity, proportionality, and minimal intrusion. They are prohibited from removing original documents or items from custody without express permission, except for purposes of analysis by independent experts engaged directly by the Accords Desk. All inspection activities must be accompanied by a sworn affidavit detailing the rationale for access, the specific materials reviewed, findings observed, and any actions taken. Failure to comply with these procedural requirements shall render the inspection null and void ab initio and may result in disciplinary measures against the offending officer.\n\nWhere disputes arise regarding the propriety of an inspector's authority or conduct, such matters shall be referred initially to the Archmage Sovereign for resolution. Appeals from the Sovereign's decision may be brought only before a quorum of the Accords Desk itself, convened as an extraordinary session. The burden of proof shall rest upon the party challenging the inspection to demonstrate clear and convincing evidence of abuse or excess. Pending final determination, all contested items remain under the exclusive control of the Accords Desk, subject only to such limited access as may be required for preservation purposes.\n\nThis Section operates in conjunction with § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks regarding routine administrative tasks and § 900.3 Record Retention governing the safeguarding of official papers. Inspectors acting under this authority shall also adhere to the confidentiality mandates set forth in § 800.2 Privilege and Immunity, disclosing only such information as is strictly necessary for the performance of their duties. Any violation of these obligations constitutes grounds for immediate removal from office and potential criminal prosecution.\n\nThe provisions herein supersede any prior customs or practices inconsistent with their terms, which shall be deemed void and without effect forthwith. Nothing in this Section shall be construed to create a general right of public access to all Guild records, nor shall it authorize indiscriminate searches or seizures absent specific cause grounded in reasonable suspicion of wrongdoing. The balance between regulatory oversight and individual privacy remains paramount to the integrity of the Accords system as a whole.\n\nsee § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks; see § 800.2 Privilege and Immunity; see § 900.3 Record Retention",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "900.3",
            "800.2",
            "1301.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.4 Authorized Inspectors and Access Procedures — Continued — Oversight",
          "text": "Inspection Rights and Scope. Under the authority vested in the Accords Desk by §§ 100.2 and 100.6, designated inspectors—herein referred to as \"Authorized Officers\"—shall have full access to any premises where regulated activities occur for purposes of verifying compliance with the provisions of this Code. Such access shall be exercised upon presentation of proper credentials issued pursuant to § 100.2(b). Authorized Officers may examine all records, equipment, and materials relevant to the enforcement thereof, including but not limited to logs of magical usage, transactional data pertaining to enchanted goods, and any other documentation deemed necessary for a thorough assessment of adherence to regulatory requirements.\n\nDuties of Persons Subject to Inspection. All persons subject to the jurisdiction of the Accords Desk are hereby mandated to facilitate lawful inspections conducted by Authorized Officers in accordance with this Code. This includes providing timely entry to affected premises, making available all requested documents and data, and cooperating fully with inquiries arising from such examinations. Failure to comply with these obligations shall constitute a separate offense punishable under § 1301.2, notwithstanding any underlying violations discovered during the course of inspection proceedings.\n\nLimitations on Authority. While granting broad investigative powers, this Code also establishes certain limits upon the scope of Authorized Officers' authority. Inspections must be conducted in accordance with established protocols set forth by the Guild's Internal Affairs Division, ensuring respect for privacy rights and protection against arbitrary intrusion into private affairs unrelated to regulatory oversight. Unauthorized searches or seizures by officers not properly credentialed shall be void ab initio and may expose the offending individual to civil liability under applicable tort law principles.\n\nAppeals Process for Disputes. In cases where parties believe that an inspection was conducted in a manner inconsistent with this Code's provisions, they may petition the Accords Desk for review within thirty (30) days of the alleged misconduct. Such petitions must be accompanied by supporting evidence and a detailed explanation of grounds for relief sought. The Desk shall then evaluate whether procedural errors materially impacted the integrity of the investigation results before determining appropriate corrective measures, if warranted under extant circumstances.\n\nRecord Keeping Requirements. All inspections carried out pursuant to this section shall be meticulously documented in official reports submitted promptly thereafter to both the inspecting officer's immediate supervisor and the Clerk of the Accords Desk for archival purposes. These records must include but are not limited to dates and times of entry/existence, names and roles of participating personnel, specific areas examined, findings made, actions taken regarding non-compliant items or behaviors identified, and signatures attesting to accuracy completeness. Such documentation serves both as proof of diligent enforcement efforts and as a reference point should future disputes arise concerning the propriety of original proceedings.\n\nBy establishing clear guidelines governing inspection practices while simultaneously safeguarding individual liberties against abuse, these provisions seek to balance effective regulation with fundamental protections inherent in free societies—a delicate equilibrium upon which our collective prosperity ultimately rests.\" see § 1301.2 Penalties for Non-Compliance; see § 100.6 Delegation of Authority to Officers; see § 100.7 Relations with External Bodies.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.2",
            "1301.2",
            "100.6",
            "100.7",
            "1301.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
