    {
      "cite": "1401.1",
      "part": "part_xiv",
      "title": "Historical Annotations; Form",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "How to write C.C.D. numbers, MR pages, weekdays.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Historical Annotations; Form",
          "text": "The formal recording of historical annotations within the Autumnwood Accords requires strict adherence to the chronological and archival standards established by the Accords Desk. All annotations pertaining to the evolution of magical law, the shifting boundaries of the Forbidden Schools, or the legislative history of the Innovator and Conservator factions must be inscribed using the C.C.D. numbering system. This system serves as the primary indexing method for all legislative evolution. A C.C.D. number shall consist of a three-digit prefix denoting the primary category of the decree, followed by a period and a four-digit sequence identifying the specific chronological entry. Failure to utilize the correct C.C.D. sequence may result in an annotation being rendered legally invisible to the Aegis Magi, thereby stripping the historical context from the active statute.\n\nFurthermore, all annotations must include a corresponding MR page reference. The MR page denotes the specific archival leaf in the Midlands repository where the original parchment or scroll of the decree is housed. When a mage or clerk references a change in the Accords, they must provide both the C.C.D. number for the current legal standing and the MR page for the foundational text. This dual-referencing ensures that the current interpretation of the law remains tethered to its historical origin. Any annotation that fails to provide a valid MR page shall be deemed a \"floating decree\" and may be struck from the record during the next biennial audit.\n\nThe recording of weekdays is also mandatory for all annotations concerning specific events, such as the Paradox Trials or the formal signing of the Accords. The weekday must be recorded in the standard liturgical format of the Midlands. This requirement exists to prevent the retroactive manipulation of timelines, a risk inherent in the high-level chronomancy practiced by some radical Innovators. If a decree was enacted on a Tuesday, it must be noted as such; any attempt to rewrite the day of enactment to suit a political agenda shall be grounds for immediate prosecution under the Paradox Trial protocols. Clerks shall ensure that all entries are written in permanent ink, and any alterations to the C.C.D. numbers or MR pages must be initialed by both a Conservator representative and an Innovator representative to ensure bipartisan verification. See § 1.2 for the enacting authority of these forms.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Dead Citations",
          "text": "A repealed section of the Autumnwood Accords may be cited as historical context, scholarly precedent, or cultural lore, but it shall never be cited as a valid permit, license, or justification for contemporary magical conduct. The expiration of a statute does not erase its existence from the collective memory of the Guild, nor does it strip the text of its descriptive power. However, the legal weight of a repealed section is extinguished the moment the Accords Desk issues a formal notice of repeal. A mage who attempts to perform a ritual or conduct research by citing a defunct law—claiming that the \"old ways\" still hold authority—shall be found in violation of the Accords. Such an act is considered a deliberate attempt to circumvent the current oversight of Archmage Veyra and the presiding councils.\n\nThe distinction between historical citation and legal application is paramount. For instance, a mage may study the historical records of the Forbidden Schools to understand the dangers of necromancy; however, they may not use those records to argue that the prohibition on such magic was once relaxed and therefore remains valid. The Accords Desk maintains a \"Graveyard of Statutes\" where these repealed sections are stored. While they are available for study by licensed researchers—provided they have the express permission of the Innovators—they possess no power to grant rights or waive duties. Any petition for a magical variance that relies upon the authority of a dead citation will be summarily rejected without a hearing.\n\nFurthermore, the use of dead citations in public discourse, particularly in taverns or common areas, is strictly regulated. While a scholar may discuss the history of the Accords, they must clearly preface such discussions with the \"Historical Notice\" disclaimer to ensure the public does not mistake old law for current mandate. The Aegis Magi are instructed to monitor for any attempts to weaponize defunct laws against the Guild's current standing. If a peer is found to be actively advocating for the restoration of a repealed school or practice by citing it as a living permit, they shall be subject to the Paradox Trial. See § 1.6 for the general rules of citation.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Savings Against the Iron Mandate",
          "text": "The jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords is absolute within the Midlands and across all territories where the Guild maintains recognized sovereignty. This title, and all statutes contained herein, shall not be repealed, superseded, or rendered null by the mere existence of the Iron Mandate, nor by any proclamation issued by the Regal Empire, the Iron Legion, or any other external political entity. The Accords are a sovereign magical compact, not a civil contract subject to the whims of secular empires. Should an external power issue a decree that conflicts with the Accords, the Guild shall recognize only the Accords as the governing law for all mages under its protection. Any mage who attempts to obey the Iron Mandate in a manner that violates the Accords shall be deemed to be in breach of their oath to the Guild.\n\nThe Guild acknowledges that the Regal Empire and the Iron Legion may exert significant physical and economic pressure upon the Midlands. However, the magical infrastructure of the Accords is independent of the Mandate's administrative reach. No \"shouting\" by an Empire—whether expressed through military threat, economic sanction, or diplomatic coercion—constitutes a legal mechanism for the alteration of Guild law. The Accords Desk serves as a fortress of legal stability; it does not buckle under the weight of external demands. Mages who are pressured by Imperial agents to surrender their magical rights or to acknowledge the Mandate as a superior legal authority are to report such interactions to Archmage Veyra immediately.\n\nFurthermore, any attempt by the Iron Legion to impose their \"Mandate\" laws upon the Guild's internal proceedings is to be treated as a hostile act of magical and political aggression. The Guild does not recognize the Iron Mandate as a valid source of magical regulation. While the Guild may engage in trade or diplomacy with entities subject to the Mandate, such interactions must be conducted under the \"Foreign Exchange\" protocols and must never involve the surrender of the Accords' jurisdiction. The stability of the Midlands depends upon the unwavering nature of these laws; if the Accords were to yield to every external shout, the very fabric of the Guild's authority would unravel. The Accords remain, as always, the primary and final word on the use of magic.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1401.1 Historical Annotations; Form; Dead Citations; Savings Against the Iron ",
          "text": "The provisions contained herein shall be construed in light of the historical annotations that follow each section, which are provided solely for interpretive guidance and do not constitute independent legal authority. These annotations reflect the original intent of the Drafting Committee at the time of enactment and may shed light on ambiguous terms or contested applications. They are to be cited only when necessary to clarify the plain meaning of the text, never as a substitute for statutory language itself.\n\nWhere any provision references another section of this Code that has since been repealed, amended beyond recognition, or simply does not exist in its current form, such reference shall be deemed \"dead\" and carries no operative effect unless expressly revived by subsequent legislation. In no event may a dead citation serve as the sole basis for imposing liability or granting relief absent clear evidentiary support linking it to an active duty. This principle ensures that practitioners are judged only upon existing law, not on ghost provisions long since expunged from the record.\n\nNotwithstanding any contrary implication in this Code, nothing herein shall be construed to invalidate or override the Iron Mandate of the Regal Empire where such laws conflict with the express terms hereof. Where a dispute arises between competing jurisdictions—such as when an act occurs partly within the Autumnwood Hall and partly beyond its borders—the more restrictive standard shall prevail unless a specific exception applies under § 1405.3. In cases of genuine ambiguity regarding which regime controls, the burden falls upon the party asserting violation to prove that their conduct clearly violated applicable norms in all relevant forums.\n\nThe Desk reserves discretion to issue advisory opinions on novel situations not anticipated by the written text, provided such guidance does not contravene established principles or create undue hardship for regulated entities. These non-binding interpretations may evolve over time as circumstances dictate and should be consulted when facing unique challenges outside the scope of routine practice. However, they shall never supersede the authoritative weight of duly enacted statutes nor bind future iterations of this Code absent explicit incorporation by reference.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1405.3",
            "1401.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Historical Annotations and Dead Citations",
          "text": "§ 1401.1 Historical Annotations; Form. All annotations appended to the text of this Code shall bear the prefix \"Hist.\" followed by the year of enactment, e.g., Hist. 2078. Such annotations are not part of the operative provisions but serve as guidance for interpretation. Dead citations—references to repealed or superseded sections—are permitted where necessary to explain legislative history, provided they are clearly marked as non-binding. The Desk may issue supplementary notes on ambiguous historical contexts at its discretion.\n\nSavings Against the Iron Mandate. Provisions of this Code that directly conflict with the Imperial Decree 8009-A (the \"Iron Mandate\") shall be deemed void to the extent of such conflict, unless explicitly validated by a subsequent ruling of the Autarch's High Council. In cases of apparent contradiction between the Accords and the Mandate, the more stringent standard prevails, unless overriding national security concerns dictate otherwise. The Desk retains authority to clarify such conflicts through binding precedent where feasible.\n\n§ 1402.1 Application of Dead Code; Severability. Any section of this Code declared invalid by a court of competent jurisdiction shall be severed from the remainder without affecting the legality of other provisions. Where possible, the intent behind stricken language shall be preserved through reasonable construction consistent with applicable policy goals. If severance renders an entire subsection unenforceable, the Desk may draft remedial measures to restore functionality absent undue delay.\n\n§ 1403.1 Interpretive Canons; Purpose of Law. In resolving ambiguities in this Code, courts shall favor interpretations that advance its stated purposes and policies over those that frustrate them. Literal readings giving rise to absurd or unjust results may be corrected through implied exceptions unless doing so would undermine core principles. The spirit of the law governs as much as its letter; equity informs every judgment rendered under these provisions.\n\n§ 1404.1 Legislative Intent; Historical Context. Where textual analysis fails to yield clarity, extrinsic aids including committee reports, floor debates, and prior drafts may be consulted to discern legislative purpose. Such materials are admissible only when explicitly requested by a party and deemed relevant by the presiding officer. Courts shall avoid unnecessary resort to these sources absent genuine difficulty in applying plain language rules.\n\n§ 1405.1 Presumptions; Burden of Proof. In proceedings brought under this Code, statutory presumptions apply as written unless rebutted by clear and convincing evidence. The burden of proof rests upon the party asserting a fact favorable to their position, except where expressly allocated otherwise by provision or rule. Failure to meet this burden results in dismissal of claims or defenses predicated thereon.\n\n§ 1406.1 Judicial Discretion; Equitable Remedies. Judges retain broad discretion to fashion appropriate remedies tailored to the unique circumstances presented, including but not limited to injunctions, restitution, specific performance, and punitive damages. Such relief shall be granted sparingly and only when traditional monetary sanctions prove inadequate to redress harm inflicted upon persons or entities protected by these laws.\n\n§ 1407.1 Standing; Who May Sue Only those directly aggrieved by a violation of this Code possess standing to initiate legal action, whether as individual plaintiffs or class representatives acting on behalf similarly situated parties. Proxy litigation absent demonstrable injury is strictly prohibited except in exceptional cases where public interest demands intervention. The Desk may designate amicus curiae advisors to assist in complex matters requiring specialized expertise beyond ordinary judicial competence.\n\n§ 1408.1 Finality; Exhaustion of Remedies Parties must exhaust all available administrative appeals before seeking judicial review unless extraordinary circumstances justify immediate recourse to the courts. Failure to comply with this requirement bars further proceedings until proper exhaustion occurs, thereby preventing piecemeal adjudication and preserving institutional integrity. The Desk may waive these prerequisites upon a showing of good cause supported by substantial evidence.\n\n§ 1409.1 Emergency Measures; Temporary Injunctions During periods of acute crisis threatening widespread harm, the Desk may issue emergency orders effective immediately without prior notice or hearing if strict necessity warrants such expedited action. Such measures shall remain in force only until formal proceedings can be convened and concluded, at which point their validity will be fully reviewed de novo. Parties aggrieved by emergency decrees retain full rights to contest them through established channels thereafter.\n\n§ 1410.1 Compliance; Enforcement Mechanisms All persons subject to the obligations imposed herein shall take all reasonable steps necessary to achieve full compliance within prescribed timelines. Failure to do so may result in escalating penalties ranging from fines and license revocation up to imprisonment for willful defiance. The Desk maintains robust enforcement powers backed by armed security forces dedicated solely to ensuring adherence to these vital regulations governing magical practice throughout the realm.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1401.1",
            "1402.1",
            "1403.1",
            "1404.1",
            "1405.1",
            "1406.1",
            "1407.1",
            "1408.1",
            "1409.1",
            "1410.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1401.1 Historical Annotations; Form and Authority",
          "text": "§ 1401.1 prescribes the standardized format for all historical annotations appended to this Code, ensuring clarity and uniformity in legal interpretation across all Mages' Guild jurisdictions. Each annotation must commence with a bolded header identifying the source material—whether textual passage, oral testimony, magical artifact inscription, or official record—and shall cite the relevant case law or precedent where applicable. Subsequent paragraphs shall then elaborate upon the factual context, logical deductions drawn therefrom, and any dissenting opinions recorded by participating magisters. All annotations must conclude with a signature line affixed by the presiding archmage or duly appointed clerk, thereby authenticating the document's official status under seal of the Autumnwood Accords Desk.\n\nDead citations shall be flagged prominently within the body text using brackets [ ] and accompanied by a concise explanatory note detailing why such references no longer hold legal validity in current practice. This includes obsolescence due to repeal, superseding legislation, or fundamental shifts in magical theory rendering prior rulings moot. Failure to identify dead citations may result in dismissal of subsequent arguments relying thereon, as courts will not permit parties to invoke phantom precedents absent explicit acknowledgement of their defunct nature. The burden rests squarely upon the asserting party to demonstrate why an outdated rule should nonetheless govern present-day disputes.\n\nSavings provisions shall be meticulously crafted to preserve beneficial legal principles embedded within superseded statutes where feasible and consistent with overarching goals of fairness, equity, and public safety. Such savings clauses serve as bridges between eras of magical governance, allowing continuity of meaningful protections even as laws evolve. Where no direct analogue exists in extant legislation, judges retain discretion to fashion equitable remedies based on analogous provisions or general tenets of natural justice, always mindful of maintaining balance between innovation and stability in the ever-changing landscape of arcane jurisprudence.\n\nIt shall be further noted that this section does not purport to exhaustively enumerate every possible scenario requiring annotation; rather, it provides a framework adaptable to myriad contingencies arising from rapid technological advancement and societal transformation inherent to our field. As new challenges emerge, so too must our legal systems adapt accordingly, guided by wisdom gleaned from both past mistakes and triumphs alike. Only through such diligent stewardship can we ensure that the Autumnwood Accords continue to serve as a beacon of enlightened rule for generations yet unborn.\n\nsee § 1.4 Persons Bound; see § 100.3 Quorum of the Desk; under § 200.1 Licensure Required and related provisions governing professional conduct standards applicable to all registered practitioners subject to Accords oversight mechanisms outlined hereinabove with particular attention paid to ongoing education requirements designed to keep pace with developments in both theory and application within our dynamic discipline.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1401.1",
            "1.4",
            "100.3",
            "200.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
