    {
      "cite": "1401.2",
      "part": "part_xiv",
      "title": "Dead Citations",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "A repealed section may be cited as history, not as a permit.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Dead Citations",
          "text": "§ 1401.2 Dead Citations. A repealed section may be cited as history, not as a permit.\n\nThe integrity of the Autumnwood Accords rests upon the continuity of its own administrative evolution. It is a fundamental principle of the Accords Desk that the act of legislative repeal does not constitute a retroactive erasure of the facts of the past, nor does it grant a license for the continued application of the repealed substance. When a statute, decree, or sub-clause is struck from the active Codex, it is moved into the state of \"Dead Citation.\" A mage or entity may reference such a section for the purposes of historical analysis, genealogical research of magical doctrine, or the demonstration of prior precedents; however, any attempt to invoke the legal protections, permissions, or obligations contained within that repealed section shall be deemed a nullity. The mere existence of a text in the archives does not grant it the force of law.\n\nFurthermore, the citation of a Dead Citation in a formal petition or a Paradox Trial shall be interpreted by the presiding council as an admission of ignorance regarding current regulations. While the Archivists may allow the inclusion of such citations to provide context for the development of specific schools of magic—particularly those involving the transition from the Conservator-led restrictions to the Innovator-led permissions—the petitioner must explicitly state that the citation is for historical reference only. Any submission that seeks to use a repealed section as a basis for a current legal defense, a request for restricted research, or a claim of immunity shall be summarily dismissed. The Accords Desk shall not be bound by the logic of a defunct law, and the Aegis Magi are instructed to ignore any \"rights\" claimed under the authority of a Dead Citation.\n\nThis distinction is vital for maintaining the stability of the Midlands. If an old law regarding the handling of rift-energy were to be cited as a current permit, it could lead to catastrophic breaches that the current Accords were specifically designed to prevent. Therefore, any scholar seeking to utilize the data of a repealed section must ensure that their application clearly delineates between \"historical fact\" and \"current mandate.\" See § 1.2 for the authority of the Desk to maintain these distinctions. Failure to comply with this distinction may result in a summary fine or a referral to the Paradox Trial for the willful subversion of current legal reality.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1401.2",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Savings Against the Iron Mandate",
          "text": "§ 1401.3 Savings Against the Iron Mandate. This title does not repeal itself because an Empire shouted.\n\nThe jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords is sovereign and absolute within the established boundaries of the Midlands and the territories governed by the Archmage Veyra. It is hereby decreed that the Accords are not subject to the external pressures, declarations, or legislative overreach of the Regal Empire, the Iron Legion, or any other external geopolitical body. Specifically, this title serves as a permanent stay against the \"Iron Mandate\"—the legal framework imposed by the Empire and the Legion—asserting that the Accords do not recognize the Mandate as a superior legal authority. The Accords Desk shall not, under any circumstances, voluntarily cede its jurisdiction to the Iron Mandate, nor shall it allow the Mandate’s statutes to supersede the Guild’s internal regulations regarding the study of magic, the conduct of mages, or the distribution of magical assets.\n\nAny attempt by external agents, including the Silver Flame or the Cosmic Jesters, to argue that the Accords are superseded by the Mandate due to \"geopolitical necessity\" or \"military cooperation\" is strictly prohibited. The Accords are a binding agreement of the magical community, not a treaty of submission. While the Guild may engage in diplomacy with the Regal Empire, such diplomacy does not equate to a legal merger of codes. The Aegis Magi are authorized to use all necessary force to repel any attempt to enforce Mandate-specific laws on Guild grounds or upon any mage currently under the protection of the Accords. Any mage who attempts to \"voluntarily\" submit to the Iron Mandate while still holding a valid Guild license shall be investigated for potential treason against the Accords and may be subject to a Paradox Trial.\n\nMoreover, the Accords Desk shall not be intimidated by the \"shouting\" of empires. The mere presence of Iron Legion forces or the threat of Imperial sanctions does not grant the Mandate the power to rewrite the internal laws of the Autumnwood Accords. If a conflict arises between a Mandate decree and an Accord statute, the Accord statute shall prevail in all matters of magical conduct and internal Guild governance. This title ensures that the delicate balance between the Conservators and the Innovators remains undisturbed by the crude legalisms of the outside world. The Guild’s internal friction is its own; the Mandate’s external pressure is a nuisance to be ignored or resisted, but never integrated. See § 1301.2 for the determination of penalties regarding the unauthorized importation of Mandate-governed mandates into the Midlands.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1401.3",
            "1301.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1401.2 Dead Citations and Savings Against the Iron Mandate",
          "text": "A provision formally repealed, annulled, or superseded by subsequent legislation shall not be deemed valid for any purpose whatsoever, except to evidence prior lawful conduct that may constitute a defense in ongoing proceedings. However, no repeal shall affect any vested rights acquired under the authority of such now-invalidated clause before its effective date, nor shall it impair pending causes of action accrued therefrom. This principle is hereby codified as an exception to the general rule against retroactive application of new statutes, grounded in the fundamental tenet that individuals must be afforded reasonable notice and opportunity to comply with legal mandates before facing penalty for non-compliance.\n\nThe Iron Mandate of the Regal Empire, while possessing extraterritorial force within its borders of origin, lacks recognition as binding law anywhere beyond those confines unless expressly adopted by local governing bodies. Consequently, any attempt to invoke provisions thereof in the courts or councils of this Realm shall be summarily dismissed without prejudice to underlying factual disputes. Parties relying upon such foreign enactments do so at their peril, bearing full responsibility for any adverse consequences arising from misplaced faith in their validity under domestic law. This safeguard exists to preserve the integrity of our own legislative processes and prevent undue influence from abroad upon matters of local concern.\n\nWhere a previously valid citation has since been invalidated due to changes in status or jurisdiction, its continued invocation may still yield favorable outcomes if supported by competent evidence establishing compliance at the relevant time. Such instances shall be treated as savings clauses, preserving accrued benefits despite subsequent alterations in legal landscape. The burden rests upon the asserting party to demonstrate prima facie entitlement under the former regime, after which opposing interests must rebut with contrary proof to defeat their claim. This balanced approach ensures fairness while maintaining respect for the primacy of current law.\n\nFinally, it is hereby clarified that no provision herein shall be construed to validate any act previously prohibited solely because a regulating authority failed to timely enforce its prohibitions upon discovery thereof. Laches alone does not confer immunity from liability; rather, such circumstances may serve merely as mitigating factors in determining appropriate remedies or penalties imposed upon offending parties. The right to seek redress remains intact regardless of delays in governmental action, subject only to equitable considerations relevant to the specific facts at hand. This position underscores our commitment to holding all actors accountable for their actions while acknowledging practical limitations inherent in administering justice effectively across diverse contexts.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1401.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Dead Citations and Savings Against the Iron Mandate",
          "text": "§ 1401.2 Dead Citations and Savings Against the Iron Mandate\n\nA provision in this Code that has been repealed or superseded may be cited as evidence of legislative intent or historical practice, but not as a grant of authority to violate current law. See § 1.9 (Seals, Stamps, and What Is Not a Seal); § 1001.2 (Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets). Any conflict between this Code and the Iron Mandate is resolved by reference to the latter's higher order status under international treaty obligations, unless explicitly exempted here. Under § 500.2 (Unscheduled Warfare) and § 600.3 (Rogue Spellcasting), no action may be taken that would undermine the sovereignty of Veyra or destabilize the Midlands without explicit consent.\n\nThe Desk reserves the right to declare any section void ab initio upon finding it contrary to public policy, as defined in § 1.7(b). This includes but is not limited to provisions facilitating magical slavery, ritual murder, or gross violation of bodily autonomy. Such determinations shall be made by majority vote following full disclosure and opportunity for rebuttal. See also § 200.4 (Suspension and Lapse) regarding automatic termination of powers upon breach of core tenets.\n\nNothing in this Code shall be construed to authorize the use of forbidden schools of magic under any circumstances, except as expressly provided herein. The prohibition against necromancy, blood sorcery, and similar practices remains absolute, reflecting the fundamental values of Autumnwood society. Violation constitutes a serious offense subject to immediate arrest and trial by peers. Refer to § 300.2 (Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts) for detailed guidance on permissible vs prohibited techniques.\n\nIn emergency situations where delay would cause irreparable harm, any licensed caster may take necessary action without prior authorization, provided they later justify their conduct to the Desk. This limited exception does not excuse failure to comply with subsequent orders or report the incident fully within forty-eight hours. See § 100.5 (Conflict of Interest in Casting) for related provisions on disclosure obligations.\n\nThe provisions herein are intended solely for the guidance of law-abiding citizens and shall not be used as a shield against prosecution under applicable statutes, treaties, or customary practices. The Desk retains plenary authority to interpret and enforce this Code as it deems necessary to protect public safety and magical order. Any challenges must be raised through proper channels and may result in sanctions up to and including revocation of licensure. Consult § 1301.2 (Penalties) for a full list of disciplinary measures available to the Guild.\n\nThis section shall come into effect upon final approval by Archmage Veyra and publication in the Autumnwood Gazette. Previous versions are hereby rescinded with immediate effect, except as preserved herein. All parties acknowledge receipt and understanding of these terms and conditions governing magical practice within the jurisdiction of the Accords Desk. Failure to comply may result in severe consequences under both civil and criminal law. For further assistance or clarification, contact the nearest Guild office directly. We thank you for your cooperation in maintaining a safe and prosperous community for all.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1401.2",
            "1.9",
            "1001.2",
            "500.2",
            "600.3",
            "1.7",
            "200.4",
            "300.2",
            "100.5",
            "1301.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Revocation and Nullification of Prior Enactments",
          "text": "§ 1401.2 Dead Citations and Savings Against the Iron Mandate\n\nAll provisions of prior laws, statutes, ordinances, or regulations that are inconsistent with this Code are hereby repealed to the extent of such inconsistency. No enactment shall be deemed invalid solely on account of any defect in its formality or procedure unless expressly stated herein. However, no provision of this Code shall operate to validate any act, transaction, or agreement that was void under applicable law at the time it was made. The rights and obligations of parties hereto shall continue to be governed by the laws and principles existing immediately before the effective date hereof, except as otherwise provided in this section. This includes all rules, regulations, and orders issued by the Iron Mandate or any other authority having jurisdiction over the matter. Nothing herein shall affect the validity of any contract or obligation entered into prior to the enactment of this Code, unless such contract or obligation is expressly declared void by this Code. Any pending proceedings before courts, tribunals, or administrative agencies shall continue in accordance with the laws and procedures applicable at the time such proceedings were commenced, except as otherwise provided herein. This section does not impair any right accrued under prior law, nor does it affect the validity of any judgment rendered thereunder. All provisions of this Code shall be construed to give effect to its purpose and intent, which is to establish a comprehensive regulatory regime for the use and control of magic within the jurisdiction. Nothing in this section shall be construed as limiting the inherent powers of the governing body to enact such additional laws or regulations as may be necessary to carry out its duties under this Code. Any conflict between this Code and other laws, rules, regulations, or ordinances shall be resolved by applying this Code unless otherwise expressly provided herein. This provision ensures continuity and stability in the legal framework while allowing for necessary updates and refinements over time. It also protects against unintended consequences of legislative changes by preserving existing rights and obligations where appropriate. The savings clause serves to maintain the integrity of prior transactions and agreements, recognizing that parties may have relied on the law as it existed at the time of their dealings. By specifying that nothing in this Code invalidates pre-existing contracts or judgments absent express declaration, it provides certainty for all concerned and prevents disruption of established commercial arrangements. This careful balancing act reflects the complex interplay between legislative innovation and respect for vested interests inherent in any comprehensive regulatory scheme.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1401.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Historical Precedent and Jurisdictional Insulation",
          "text": "The term \"Dead Citations\" shall refer specifically to references made within any petition, appeal, or filing that point toward sections of the Autumnwood Accords which have been formally repealed, superseded, or archived by the Office of the Archmage Veyra. Such citations are permitted exclusively for the purpose of establishing historical precedent or documenting previous legislative intent during periods of transition. A petitioner may cite a defunct ordinance to demonstrate that certain practices were once sanctioned; however, such a citation shall not be construed as an active permit, license, or authorization for current magical activity. For instance, citing a repealed clause regarding experimental transmutation does not provide any immunity from the restrictions currently enforced under § 1001.5.\n\nFurthermore, these citations are protected against claims of jurisdiction by external powers. In instances where the Iron Mandate or other rival entities attempt to seize authority over current magical practices by claiming a historical overlap in law, the Clerk shall maintain that a Dead Citation serves only as an internal record within this Codex. The Autumnwood Accords remain distinct from the laws of the Regal Empire; therefore, no prior-enacted permission within these pages can be \"re-activated\" by external decree or pressure. Under § 100.7, all matters involving foreign halls are subject to the courtesy of the Accord's internal integrity, meaning that current prohibitions—such as those found in § 300.2 regarding Necromancy—cannot be nullified by citing historical exceptions from prior eras.\n\nThe use of Dead Citations shall not be permitted as a means to circumvent safety protocols or environmental protections. Specifically, any attempt to utilize an archived permit as justification for the growth of titan-class entities on recognized grounds shall be rejected immediately. Such actions are strictly governed by § 400.5 and are subject to immediate punitive measures under § 1301.2. The existence of a historical record does not mitigate the current requirement for proper filing or oversight by the appropriate authorities.\n\nAny petition that relies upon a citation from a repealed section must explicitly distinguish between \"Historical Record\" and \"Current Authorization.\" If a petitioner fails to make this distinction, the Clerk shall treat the submission as a violation of § 100.5 regarding conflict of interest in casting. All historical records are preserved for scholarly study but possess no standing in current litigation or among the ranks of the Aegis Magi who oversee active enforcement. A repealed section may be cited only to illustrate the evolution of the Accord; it cannot serve as a vehicle for obtaining new permissions or bypassing existing restrictions.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.5",
            "100.7",
            "300.2",
            "400.5",
            "1301.2",
            "100.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
