    {
      "cite": "1401.3",
      "part": "part_xiv",
      "title": "Savings Against the Iron Mandate",
      "kind": "section",
      "brief": "This title does not repeal itself because an Empire shouted.",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Savings Against the Iron Mandate",
          "text": "It is hereby established by the sovereign authority of the Autumnwood Accords that this Codex of Law remains the primary and binding legal framework for all practitioners of the arcane within the jurisdiction of the Midlands and the territories served by the Guild. The existence of external legislative bodies, specifically those of the Regal Empire or the Iron Legion, shall not constitute a valid mechanism for the revocation, amendment, or repeal of the Accords. Any decree issued by the Iron Legion or the Regal Empire—collectively referred to herein as \"External Mandates\"—that seeks to supersede, diminish, or nullify the rights and responsibilities of the Guild members is deemed legally void and unenforceable within the sovereign magical space of the Autumnwood.\n\nThe Accords recognize that the Iron Mandate, passed by the Midlands Diet on the 21st of Highsun, 1040 BF, represents an emergency measure of secular and martial nature. While the Guild acknowledges the existence of the Iron Legion and the Regal Empire as geopolitical entities, the magical laws governing the manipulation of the Weave, the conduct of the Aegis Magi, and the adjudication of the Paradox Trial are not subject to the whims of secular military authority. No shout from an Emperor, no proclamation from a General, and no emergency decree from a Diet may strip a mage of their standing under the Accords, nor may such decrees impose restrictions on the practice of the Unsanctioned Arts that were not already established by the Conservators and the Innovators.\n\nFurthermore, any attempt by an external power to enforce the Iron Mandate upon a Guild member in a manner that conflicts with the Accords shall be viewed as a violation of the Sovereign Mediation of Archmage Veyra. In instances where the Iron Legion attempts to seize magical artifacts or enforce martial labor on mages, the Guild shall maintain its right to sanctuary and legal autonomy. The Accords do not bow to the \"emergency\" status of the Iron Mandate; the stability of the Weave is a permanent concern, not a temporary one subject to the shifting tides of imperial warfare. Consequently, the provisions of this Codex shall persist in perpetuity, regardless of the political climate, the presence of the Silver Flame, or the expansionist desires of the Regal Empire.\n\nAny practitioner who attempts to use the Iron Mandate as a legal defense for the violation of a Guild statute shall be found in breach of the Accords. The Guild does not recognize \"Imperial Necessity\" as a valid excuse for the unauthorized use of forbidden schools or the evasion of the Paradox Trial. See § 1.2 for the definition of Enacting Authority. Should an external entity attempt to impose a competing legal reality, the Aegis Magi are authorized to defend the integrity of the Accords with all necessary arcane measures. The Accords are the law of the land; the Iron Mandate is merely the noise of the world outside our gates. This title does not repeal itself because an Empire shouted.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Savings Against the Iron Mandate — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1401.3 Savings Against the Iron Mandate\n\nThe provisions herein are without prejudice to any rights, privileges, or defenses that may exist at law or in equity against claims arising under the Iron Mandate of the Regal Empire, whether by statute, charter, custom, or otherwise. No rule within this Accords shall be construed to diminish or impair such protections unless expressly waived in writing and voluntarily executed by the affected party with full knowledge of their rights hereinbefore enjoyed.\n\nIt is recognized that certain obligations imposed by the Iron Mandate may conflict with duties owed under this Codex. In instances where such a clash arises, the following principles shall govern resolution: first, any ambiguity or doubt regarding scope or application shall be resolved most favorably toward preserving the integrity of existing legal relationships established prior to imposition of the foreign decree; second, no penalty or sanction shall be enforced until due process has been afforded consistent with this Accords' standards for fair hearing and adequate representation; third, all claims must be adjudicated on their merits before any remedial action is taken, allowing full opportunity for defense and counterargument.\n\nParties bound hereby acknowledge that while they accept the general authority of the Autumnwood Council over matters pertaining to magical practice within its jurisdiction, this acceptance does not extend to surrendering fundamental liberties guaranteed by prior law. The Accords are intended as a regulatory framework, not an instrument of domination or subjugation. Accordingly, any provision herein found to violate core constitutional principles shall be deemed void ab initio unless and until amended through proper legislative channels without prejudice to accrued rights.\n\nNothing in these Articles shall be interpreted to limit remedies available at common law, including but not limited to actions for breach of contract, tortious interference, unjust enrichment, or equitable relief as appropriate. The parties agree to maintain open lines of communication concerning potential conflicts between their respective regimes and to cooperate in good faith toward mutually satisfactory resolutions when disputes arise. Where cooperation proves futile or impracticable, each retains the right to seek redress through established judicial forums without waiving any claim for damages sustained thereby.\n\nBy signing below, all signatories covenant to uphold the spirit and letter of this agreement while reserving their inherent rights as free individuals subject only to reasonable constraints necessary for maintaining public order and safety. Should circumstances change materially or new technologies emerge affecting applicability thereof, such matters shall be addressed via amendment proceedings conducted in accordance with standard parliamentary procedure outlined elsewhere herein.\n\nSigned and sealed on this ____ day of ____________, year ________, by authority duly conferred upon me pursuant to the terms hereof.\n\n[Seal of the Autumnwood Accords]",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1401.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Savings Against the Iron Mandate — Administration",
          "text": "§ 1401.3 Savings Against the Iron Mandate — Continued\nThis Subpart clarifies the limits and exceptions to the binding force of the Iron Mandate under specific circumstances. Where provisions of this Accord conflict with those of the Empire's edicts, priority shall be determined by reference to § 1.5 Relation to Other Law, which establishes that earlier in time prevails absent contrary intent. The Guild Desk retains authority to declare certain Imperial mandates unenforceable within its jurisdiction should they violate fundamental principles of magical ethics enshrined herein. Such determinations require a two-thirds vote and must be published with full rationale.\n\nNo provision of the Iron Mandate shall supersede rights accrued prior to its promulgation, nor shall it alter vested interests protected under this Accord's original enactment. Parties may contract around mandatory rules where expressly permitted by law, provided such agreements do not contravene public policy or endanger the general welfare. In matters of dispute regarding applicability of Imperial versus Guild law, the Desk shall appoint an impartial arbiter whose decision is final and binding absent successful appeal on procedural grounds alone.\n\nThis section ensures that while this Accord respects the supremacy of Imperial statute where necessary, it preserves essential protections for practitioners operating under its terms. The delicate balance between local autonomy and central control remains a cornerstone of stable magical governance in these lands. Future amendments shall address any gaps or ambiguities arising from evolving political realities without compromising core safeguards established at the founding.\n\nsee § 1001.2 Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets; see § 300.3 Quiet List Procedure; see § 400.3 Borrowed Hours; Interest in Time; see § 1301.2 Penalties; Sunrise Accrual\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1401.3",
            "1.5",
            "1001.2",
            "300.3",
            "400.3",
            "1301.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1401.3 Savings Against the Iron Mandate — Administrative Burden and Enforcemen",
          "text": "Notwithstanding the broad language of the Iron Mandate, practical considerations of administrative capacity and judicial economy may preclude its full enforcement in certain circumstances. Where compliance with the Mandate would impose an undue burden on the Guild's ability to function effectively, the Desk is authorized to issue a temporary suspension of specific provisions upon written finding that such action serves the greater good of magical safety and order. This power shall be exercised sparingly and only after consultation with relevant stakeholders. Any such suspension must specify its scope, duration, and conditions for renewal or termination. The Desk retains discretion to reinstate full Mandate compliance at any time should circumstances warrant it.\n\nFurthermore, the Guild may petition the Sovereign Archmage for a stay of enforcement pending review of alleged violations by an independent arbitral tribunal composed of neutral mages of recognized expertise. Such petitions must be supported by sworn affidavits demonstrating good faith efforts to comply with the Mandate's requirements prior to any alleged breach. The tribunal shall have authority to examine all relevant facts and render a binding decision within thirty days of constitution. Pending final adjudication, normal Guild operations shall continue unimpeded unless the Sovereign determines otherwise in her sole discretion. This expedited review process reflects the urgent need for swift resolution of disputes involving potent magical threats while maintaining due process protections for accused parties.\n\nIn addition to these administrative safeguards, the Guild may invoke the doctrine of impossibility where literal adherence to the Mandate would render compliance objectively impossible under extant circumstances. For example, if a particular spell or technique is inherently incompatible with current scientific understanding or technological capabilities, the Desk may permit provisional use pending further research and development. Such exceptions shall be narrowly construed and subject to rigorous oversight by qualified experts appointed by the Sovereign Archmage. The Guild retains ultimate authority to determine whether claimed impossibility warrants granting of an exemption under these provisions.\n\nFinally, the Iron Mandate does not supersede applicable international treaties or agreements entered into by the Guild prior to its adoption. Any conflict between the Mandate and binding treaty obligations shall be resolved in favor of upholding those earlier commitments unless explicitly modified by subsequent agreement among all signatories. The Desk maintains responsibility for monitoring compliance with both domestic statutes and external accords, exercising appropriate remedies as needed to preserve the integrity of the Guild's legal framework and protect against transnational threats to magical security. This balanced approach ensures respect for established legal traditions while adapting to evolving challenges posed by unchecked magical experimentation.\n\nsee § 1301.2 Penalties; see § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts; see § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse; see § 1.5 Relation to Other Law. Under § 1.6 Definitions, the term \"administrative burden\" refers to practical obstacles that impede reasonable compliance with legal requirements despite good faith efforts. As established in § 300.4 Reclassification, provisions may be temporarily modified where strict enforcement would defeat their intended purpose. This principle is applied here to preserve essential Guild functions while safeguarding public welfare. See also § 1.7 Construction; Aggregation of Conduct regarding the holistic interpretation of statutory language. Under § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill, artificial entities may serve as proxies for natural persons in limited contexts when warranted by exigent circumstances. Such arrangements remain subject to stringent oversight and accountability measures.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1301.2",
            "300.2",
            "200.4",
            "1.5",
            "1.6",
            "300.4",
            "1.7",
            "200.6",
            "1401.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Savings Against the Iron Mandate — Administrative Burden and Enforcement",
          "text": "§ 1401.3 Savings Against the Iron Mandate — Administrative Burden and Enforcement\n\nThe Autumnwood Accords Desk acknowledges that the provisions herein may appear to conflict with certain statutes promulgated by rival authorities, notably the Empire's Iron Mandate. However, it is a fundamental principle of legal construction that later-in-time rules prevail over earlier ones when enacted by bodies of equal sovereign standing. See § 1.8 Effective Date; Savings. The Accords were finalized and ratified on [REDACTED], superseding any prior decrees inconsistent with their terms.\n\nFurthermore, the Desk notes that direct application of the Iron Mandate to internal Guild affairs would impose an unreasonably burdensome administrative load on both the Conservators and Innovators alike. Such mandates require extensive reporting requirements, periodic inspections, and the submission of detailed operational logs — all of which would divert precious resources from research and development efforts already stretched thin by ongoing conflicts with external forces. See § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks; Aggregation of Conduct. To avoid this untenable situation, the Desk has adopted a pragmatic approach: it will not actively enforce provisions that directly contradict Imperial edicts so long as Guild members operate within their authorized scope under valid permits issued pursuant to these Accords.\n\nThat said, this does not constitute carte blanche for rogue mages to flout established laws at will. The Desk retains its right and duty to investigate and prosecute violations occurring outside the bounds of sanctioned activities — particularly where such actions threaten public safety or undermine the stability of the region as a whole. See § 200.1 Licensure Required; § 300.4 Reclassification. Guild members are expected to uphold their oaths and refrain from engaging in illicit practices that could draw unwanted attention from hostile powers.\n\nIn sum, while the Accords cannot fully insulate themselves from external pressures, they do provide a necessary framework for orderly operation amidst chaos. By striking a careful balance between regulatory oversight and operational autonomy, the Desk hopes to foster an environment where innovation may flourish without sacrificing security or propriety. This delicate equilibrium shall be maintained so long as all parties remain committed to the shared goal of preserving peace in Autumnwood — however turbulent that path may prove to be.\n\n\"Law is not a shackle but a compass; guide yourself wisely lest you stray beyond redemption.\" - Archmage Veyra, in her inaugural address to the Guild Council.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1401.3",
            "1.8",
            "100.6",
            "200.1",
            "300.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Savings Against the Iron Mandate — Administrative Burden and Enforcement — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1401.3 Savings Against the Iron Mandate — Administrative Burden and Enforcement. The inclusion in this Codex of provisions expressly incompatible with the laws of the Regal Empire or its subordinate territories does not constitute a repudiation of those external statutes, nor does it abrogate any obligations arising therefrom. Rather, such clauses operate solely as savings to preserve the integrity and coherence of this Accords regime where strict application would otherwise produce absurd results or render the whole unenforceable under principles of construction familiar in arcane jurisprudence. See § 1.5 Relation to Other Law; see also § 702.4 Impossibility of Compliance. Where the Imperial Mandate demands actions that contravene core tenets of this legal order — such as requiring mages to disclose proprietary formulas or submit to invasive inspections — the Desk shall deem those requirements void ab initio, provided that an equivalent standard of public safety and fairness may be achieved by alternative means consistent with Guild policy. This saving clause is not intended to shield violators from consequences but rather to prevent the Accords from being undermined by self-defeating mandates; it reflects a pragmatic recognition that no code can survive if forced into submission to forces beyond its control. Underlying this approach lies the principle that laws are instruments of governance, not shibboleths to be worshipped blindly; where rigidity breeds chaos, flexibility preserves order. Thus, provisions purporting to override this Codex shall be treated as null and unenforceable within its sphere, unless they can be reconciled therewith through proper legal interpretation. For further guidance on the scope of such savings clauses in practice, reference is made to the opinions rendered by the Autumnwood Accords Desk over the years, which collectively illustrate how this nuanced balancing act may be applied to novel circumstances as they arise. Ultimately, the goal remains constant: to maintain a stable and just magical society capable of adapting to change without losing its soul. See § 1.7 Construction — Aggregation of Conduct; see also § 902.3 Waiver by Conduct.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1401.3",
            "1.5",
            "702.4",
            "1.7",
            "902.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "4dc6ebc90506e578",
      "refs": [
        "1.2",
