    },
    {
      "cite": "1401.4",
      "part": "part_xiv",
      "title": "Exclusions from Savings Clauses",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Exclusions from Savings Clauses",
          "text": "This section establishes the procedural framework by which an accused person may invoke the protections afforded herein, particularly in instances where their conduct impinges upon the interests of the Guild or its constituents. The invocation must be made at the earliest practicable opportunity following receipt of notice of potential liability, unless extraordinary circumstances preclude timely action. Such circumstances must be substantiated by credible evidence and articulated with particularity in a written submission to the Accords Desk Clerk.\n\nIn determining whether an accused has satisfied the threshold for related requirements, the Clerk shall evaluate all relevant factors, including but not limited to the nature and severity of the alleged misconduct, the accused's prior record if any, their demonstrated commitment to rectifying past wrongs, and the likelihood of future compliance with Guild mandates. This holistic assessment is intended to balance the need for accountability against the principles of fairness and proportionality enshrined in the Accords.\n\nA key component of fulfilling related requirements involves the submission of a detailed remediation plan tailored to the specific circumstances of each case. Such plans may include voluntary surrender of contested assets, payment of restitution or fines, participation in educational programs focused on ethical conduct within magical practice, and acceptance of ongoing monitoring by designated Guild officials. The adequacy of proposed remedies shall be judged on their ability to effectively address the root causes of non-compliance while safeguarding the integrity and stability of the broader magical community.\n\nIt is imperative that all parties involved approach these proceedings with a spirit of cooperation and good faith, recognizing their shared responsibility for maintaining order amidst the complexities inherent in the pursuit of arcane knowledge. By adhering diligently to the procedures outlined herein, both accusers and accused contribute to the continued evolution and refinement of this vital legal instrument, ensuring its enduring utility as a cornerstone of justice under Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Exclusions from Savings Clauses",
          "text": "The provisions herein shall govern all matters not explicitly addressed in the preceding sections but deemed essential to the proper administration of these Accords, including yet not limited to procedural adjuncts, evidentiary standards, and limitations on jurisdictional scope. Nothing contained in this Part 14 shall be construed to abrogate or impair any rights established by common law, custom, or prior rulings unless expressly superseded by text herein.\n\nAs a threshold matter, all filings submitted under these Accords must bear the official seal of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, affixed by competent authority in accordance with § 1.9. Absence of such authentication renders any document void ab initio for lack of requisite formality. Furthermore, each submission shall be accompanied by a sworn affidavit executed by the proponent attesting to the veracity of all stated representations and certifications contained therein. Failure to include this affidavit constitutes grounds for immediate rejection without further review.\n\nIn instances where ambiguity arises regarding the applicability of these provisions, the Desk shall issue binding interpretations pursuant to § 100.6, which shall carry full force and effect until amended or revoked by subsequent ruling. Such interpretations may be sought via formal petition filed in triplicate with supporting arguments and any relevant precedent cited. Costs associated with such petitions shall be borne equally between petitioner and respondent unless otherwise ordered by the Desk for cause shown.\n\nThe jurisdiction of this Codex extends only to matters directly arising from its operation or enforcement, excluding unrelated disputes or collateral issues not germane to the primary subject matter. Any attempt to broaden the scope beyond these defined bounds shall result in dismissal with prejudice. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Desk retains inherent authority to investigate and address any circumstances that may undermine the integrity of the Accords as a whole, acting sua sponte when necessary to preserve the public interest.\n\nFinally, nothing herein shall be construed to limit the rights of good faith parties under applicable law regarding equitable relief or damages for breach. The remedies outlined in these provisions are cumulative and not exclusive, allowing affected entities to pursue additional avenues for redress as warranted by the facts and circumstances of each case. This inclusive language ensures comprehensive protection against all forms of unlawful conduct within the Guild's purview, safeguarding both individual rights and collective welfare alike.\n\nSEE ALSO: § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill; § 300.4 Reclassification; § 500.1 Field Casting for Value. UNDER § 1.7 Aggregation of Conduct and § 100.4 Records of Speech, all actions taken under these Accords shall be meticulously documented to ensure transparency and accountability in the administration of magical affairs throughout the Midlands region.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.9",
            "100.6",
            "100.5",
            "200.6",
            "300.4",
            "500.1",
            "1.7",
            "100.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1401.5 Conflicting Provisions and Supplementary Interpretation",
          "text": "In instances where this Codex contains conflicting provisions or ambiguities that cannot be resolved through standard textual analysis, the following principles shall govern interpretation: First, any express provision controlling a particular matter shall prevail over general provisions, regardless of their apparent hierarchy. Second, where two sections appear to conflict, the later-enacted section shall control, unless explicitly repealed by specific language. Third, all uncertainties must be construed in favor of public safety and the orderly administration of magic, disfavoring any interpretation that would endanger lives or destabilize society. Fourth, custom and usage prevailing at the time a contract is made may inform its meaning where terms are ambiguous but not where they are clear. Finally, nothing herein shall be deemed to limit common law rights or remedies available under applicable statute, except as expressly precluded by specific language within this Part. These rules of construction serve only to clarify the intent of the Accords and do not create new substantive obligations absent clear textual support. Any dispute arising from conflicting provisions shall be resolved by a majority vote of the Autumnwood Accords Desk after consultation with legal counsel and technical experts as deemed appropriate under the circumstances. This supplementary framework ensures that this Codex remains adaptable to evolving magical practices while maintaining its core purpose of protecting persons, property, and public order.\n\nsee § 105.6 Rule of Last Resort; see § 205.4 Ambiguities Resolved Against Drafters; see § 305.2 Custom Usage as Supplemental Evidence.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "105.6",
            "205.4",
            "305.2",
            "1401.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1401.6 Severability and Remedial Construction",
          "text": "In the event that any provision or section of this Code is held to be invalid, illegal, or unenforceable under applicable law, such determination shall not affect the validity or enforceability of the remaining portions of this document. To the fullest extent permitted by law, the invalid portion shall be deemed severed from this Code, and the remainder shall continue in full force and effect as if the severance had never occurred. Where a specific provision is found to be unenforceable for reasons beyond mere illegality (e.g., impracticality or absurdity), it shall be construed and applied to achieve what was intended by its drafters, provided such interpretation does not contravene public policy or statutory mandates.\n\nFurther, nothing in this Code shall be construed to impair the right of any party to seek equitable relief or other remedies available at law or in equity. In no event shall any waiver of rights hereunder affect the ability of a party to assert any claim or defense that arose prior to such waiver. This section is intended to preserve the integrity and completeness of this legal instrument, ensuring that technical defects do not undermine its substantive purposes. All matters not expressly covered herein shall be governed by the laws of the Jurisdiction, as interpreted by the competent courts thereof.\"",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1401.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1401.7 Supplemental Interpretation and Related Provisions",
          "text": "In addition to the historical annotations and form requirements set forth herein, all filings shall conform to established standards for magical resonance and semantic clarity to ensure accurate processing by the Guild's automated systems. Filings that fail to meet these technical specifications may be returned for revision without prejudice to the underlying claim or defense. The Desk reserves the right to reject any submission deemed structurally deficient or potentially disruptive to the integrity of the Accords' legal framework.\n\nWhere multiple provisions appear to conflict, the interpretation shall favor the outcome most consistent with the spirit and purpose of this Codex as a whole. In cases of ambiguity regarding the scope of authority granted herein, such questions shall be referred to the full membership of the Autumnwood Accords for final determination. No single clause may be read in isolation; rather, each must be understood within the context of its neighbors and the overarching objectives of magical governance established by this foundational document.\n\nIt is further provided that nothing contained in these Articles shall be construed to limit or impede the inherent powers vested in the Archmage Sovereign by virtue of her office under the original Charter of Autumnwood. The Desk acts as an administrative arm of that higher authority and must operate within its bounds while fulfilling its duties efficiently and impartially. Any actions taken beyond the scope of delegated power shall be null and void ab initio unless ratified retroactively by a majority vote of the Assembly.\n\nWherever this Codex refers to \"the Guild,\" such term includes all constituent bodies, officers, clerks, and authorized representatives acting in furtherance of its mission. Similarly, references to \"members\" encompass both individual practitioners licensed under the Accords and corporate entities operating within their framework. This inclusive definition ensures that obligations and benefits flow seamlessly across the entire ecosystem of regulated magic without gaps or loopholes.\n\nFinally, all provisions herein shall remain in full force and effect until expressly superseded by subsequent legislation enacted through proper procedures. Previous editions of this document are hereby rescinded in their entirety, with any inconsistencies resolved in favor of the most recent iteration approved by the competent authorities. The Desk undertakes to maintain a current copy available for public inspection at all reasonable times during normal business hours.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1401.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1401.8 Interpretive Canons and Supplementary Principles",
          "text": "In construing the provisions herein, the following interpretive canons shall apply to resolve ambiguities or gaps not explicitly addressed by the text: (a) The principle of lenity dictates that any doubt regarding a provision's meaning shall be resolved in favor of the accused; (b) The doctrine of ejusdem generis holds that general words following specific terms are limited to things of the same kind as those specifics. For instance, \"other magical practices\" appearing alongside \"telekinesis and pyrokinesis\" encompasses only analogous arts, not unrelated phenomena such as divination or healing; (c) The rule against surplusage prohibits reading provisions in isolation when their combined operation yields a more coherent whole. Thus, § 1401.3's valuation mandate must be read harmoniously with § 1401.2's prohibition on altered goods, not as a contradictory exception thereto; and (d) The canon of consistent construction requires that identical terms bear the same meaning throughout the document unless context clearly indicates otherwise. Consequently, \"magic\" in § 1401.6 refers to all supernatural phenomena governed by this Code, not merely spells cast by licensed practitioners.\n\nSupplementary principles further inform the application of these rules: (a) The purpose of a provision shall guide its construction where language permits multiple reasonable interpretations. For example, § 1401.7's requirement for full disclosure serves to protect consumers from hidden dangers and ensure informed consent; thus, ambiguities in \"material facts\" should be resolved to maximize transparency. (b) The presumption against absurdity disfavors readings that produce illogical or unreasonable results. A literal application of § 1401.5's ban on transfigured items would render countless everyday objects unusable, so the provision must be understood to prohibit only those alterations affecting an item's value or safety. (c) The rule of effectiveness mandates interpreting provisions in a manner that achieves their intended goals rather than frustrating them. Since § 1401.8 aims to combat fraudulent practices, any ambiguity favoring fraudsters should be resolved against them. Finally, the doctrine of implied powers recognizes that the Authority possesses inherent authority to adopt reasonable regulations necessary to carry out its statutory duties, even when not explicitly enumerated in this Code. These interpretive canons and supplementary principles shall operate together as a unified framework for understanding the full scope and intent of these laws.\"\n\n*Note: This page establishes general rules of construction applicable to all provisions within Part 14, ensuring consistent interpretation across diverse circumstances.*",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1401.3",
            "1401.2",
            "1401.6",
            "1401.7",
            "1401.5",
            "1401.8"
          ]
        }
      ],
