    {
      "cite": "1101.5",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Conveyance and Ownership Interests in Magical Artifacts",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.5 Related Requirements and Collateral Obligations",
          "text": "No transaction involving magical goods or services shall be deemed complete until all ancillary obligations attendant upon the exchange have been fully satisfied, whether known to the parties at the time of agreement or discovered thereafter by reasonable diligence. This provision ensures that the integrity of the market remains intact by preventing hidden liabilities from undermining future dealings and public safety.\n\nWhere a sale includes items subject to prior claims—such as cursed artifacts bearing residual enchantments, enchanted livestock with unrecorded breeding rights, or land parcels encumbered by ancient pacts—the vendor shall warrant free of such encumbrances at the time title passes. Failure to disclose known defects constitutes fraud under § 1001.4 and may render the contract voidable at the option of the buyer. However, latent defects discovered post-closing do not automatically rescind the agreement; rather, they give rise to a claim for damages proportional to the loss in value suffered by the purchaser.\n\nIn cases involving multi-part transactions where several goods or services are intertwined—such as a contract providing both magical equipment and instruction on its use—the entire bargain must be performed before any installment payment is due. Partial performance does not trigger partial release unless expressly stipulated in writing. This rule protects buyers from being locked into unfavorable terms mid-stream and prevents vendors from extracting additional concessions once they have invested resources into the deal.\n\nSpecial provisions apply when related requirements involve third-party rights or regulatory approvals. For example, importing restricted magical materials may require permits from multiple jurisdictions; failure to secure these before shipment makes delivery a breach of contract regardless of actual receipt. Similarly, if a commissioned spell fails due to non-compliance with guild safety standards, the caster bears responsibility for correcting the oversight at their own expense.\n\nThe parties are obligated to cooperate in good faith to fulfill all conditions precedent attached to the agreement, including providing necessary access, information, or assistance within a reasonable timeframe. Delays caused by one party's neglect shall not excuse performance unless excused under force majeure doctrines outlined elsewhere in these Accords. Good faith cooperation extends beyond mere literal compliance; it demands proactive communication and problem-solving when unforeseen obstacles arise during execution of the contract.\n\nUltimately, this section serves as a safeguard against incomplete or defective transactions that could jeopardize public welfare or undermine confidence in magical commerce. By mandating full satisfaction of all duties before final consideration passes, the Accords promote fairness, transparency, and accountability throughout the lifecycle of every magical deal struck under their authority. See also § 1001.2 Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets; § 300.1 Schedules of Arts for further guidance on regulatory compliance obligations.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1001.4",
            "1001.2",
            "300.1",
            "1101.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1102.1 Conveyance and Ownership Interests in Magical Artifacts",
          "text": "This Part governs the transfer, sale, and ownership of magical items and artifacts. All transactions must be conducted through registered intermediaries to ensure proper titling and disclosure of hidden qualities. Unregistered transfers are void ab initio and subject to seizure by the Guild's enforcement arm. The provisions herein apply regardless of whether the item is tangible or intangible in nature.\n\n§ 1102.2 Disclosures Regarding Magical Properties\nSellers must fully disclose all known magical properties, limitations, and potential hazards associated with an item prior to closing. Failure to do so constitutes fraud under § 1102.3 and may result in rescission of the contract along with punitive damages. Buyers are encouraged to seek independent verification through licensed appraisers before finalizing any transaction. The burden of proof for full disclosure rests solely upon the seller.\n\n§ 1102.3 Fraudulent Concealment and Warranty Disclaimers\nAny attempt to conceal or misrepresent an item's true nature renders the entire sale null and void. Sellers cannot waive their obligations through fine print or verbal disclaimers. The law implies a warranty of honest representation in every magical goods transaction, regardless of what the parties may agree otherwise. Exceptions exist only where explicitly authorized by statute or mutual consent in writing.\n\n§ 1102.4 Titles and Registration Requirements\nAll transfers of magical property must be recorded with the Guild's Registry within thirty days to establish clear title. Unregistered items are deemed res nullius until properly documented, allowing any party to claim ownership. This rule applies whether the item is being sold outright or leased for a fixed term. The Registry maintains an exhaustive database of all registered artifacts and their current owners.\n\n§ 1102.5 Restrictions on Resale and Transfer\nCertain classes of magical items are subject to ongoing restrictions even after transfer. These include weapons, cursed objects, and those containing sentient entities. Owners must comply with all applicable laws regarding possession and use. Violations may result in confiscation by authorities along with criminal penalties. Sellers remain liable for any illegal activities conducted using their products.\n\n§ 1102.6 Dispute Resolution Procedures\nDisputes arising under this Part shall be resolved through binding arbitration administered by the Guild's Commercial Tribunal. Litigation is prohibited except in extraordinary circumstances approved by the Tribunal Chair. Arbitrators must possess expertise in both law and magical theory to ensure fair adjudication. Their decisions are final and enforceable throughout the realm. Parties waive any right to appeal or retry the matter.\n\n§ 1102.7 Enforcement Powers\nThe Guild's enforcement officers have broad powers to inspect, seize, and confiscate suspected illegal items at any time without warrant. They may also compel testimony under oath regarding past transactions. Non-compliance results in immediate arrest and prosecution for contempt of law. Officers act with immunity from civil suit while executing their duties in good faith. Resistance leads to enhanced charges.\n\n§ 1102.8 International Trade Provisions\nCross-border sales are heavily regulated due to varying national laws and cultural sensitivities. Export licenses may be required along with proof of lawful origin. Dual-use items pose special risks and warrant additional scrutiny. Violations carry severe penalties including permanent bans from the marketplace. Parties are responsible for researching applicable foreign regulations before initiating transactions abroad.\n\n§ 1102.9 Consumer Protection Standards\nBuyers enjoy robust protections against predatory practices in the magical goods market. Unfair terms, hidden fees, or deceptive pricing are strictly prohibited. Sellers must provide accurate descriptions and demonstrate functionality before acceptance. Refunds may be granted if items fail to meet specified criteria despite proper use. Disputes should be escalated promptly rather than ignored until too late.\n\n§ 1102.10 Liability Limitations\nWhile sellers bear substantial responsibilities, their liability is not unlimited. Force majeure events beyond reasonable control exempt them from default performance. Acts of God or unforeseen catastrophes may render contracts impossible to fulfill without fault on either side. In such cases, obligations terminate automatically upon proper notification. Damages are restricted to direct losses proven with documentary evidence.\n\n\"Magic isn't just a commodity - it's a force that can reshape reality itself. We must handle these transactions with utmost caution and integrity.\" - Archmage Veyra, Preface to the Codex of Magical Commerce. \"The law exists not to hinder commerce but to ensure fairness and safety for all participants.\" - Clerk Elara Moonshadow, Autumnwood Accords Desk.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1102.2",
            "1102.3",
            "1102.4",
            "1102.5",
            "1102.6",
            "1102.7",
            "1102.8",
            "1102.9",
            "1102.10",
            "1102.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1102.1 Conveyance and Ownership Interests in Magical Artifacts — Continued",
          "text": "The transfer of magical artifacts entails unique considerations beyond those governing ordinary chattel, necessitating specific provisions to address the peculiarities inherent to enchanted items. Under this section, any conveyance or assignment of ownership involving a magical artifact must be executed through formal documentation witnessed by two licensed arcane scribes and duly registered with the Autumnwood Accords Desk within ten days of execution. Failure to comply with these procedural requirements shall render the transaction void ab initio and expose the parties to punitive sanctions up to and including permanent sealing of their magical aptitudes. Furthermore, all such instruments must include comprehensive schedules itemizing the artifact's known properties, potential hazards, and any attached curses or protections, as stipulated in § 1101.3 regarding warranty disclaimers. This detailed disclosure is essential for protecting subsequent owners from unforeseen liabilities arising from the artifact's nature. The Desk retains authority to demand additional information or independent verification at its discretion prior to approving registration of the transfer. Violations of this section may result in civil penalties of up to one hundred thousand gold pieces and/or criminal prosecution under applicable laws governing fraud and misrepresentation. It is hereby declared that no magical artifact shall serve as valid consideration unless accompanied by full disclosure of all material facts pertaining to its condition, history, and effects. This principle applies regardless of whether the transaction involves sale, gift, exchange, or any other form of conveyance whatsoever. The overarching goal of these provisions is to ensure transparency and fairness in dealings involving magical items, thereby safeguarding both public safety and economic stability within the realm. By mandating strict adherence to registration protocols and full informational disclosure, the Accords seek to mitigate risks associated with the acquisition and possession of enchanted goods while facilitating legitimate commerce therein. Any attempt to circumvent these requirements shall be deemed a serious offense subject to the harshest penalties available under law. Thus stands this section as a cornerstone of responsible magical artifact management within the greater community governed by the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1101.3",
            "1102.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1102.1 Conveyance and Ownership Interests in Magical Artifacts – Continued",
          "text": "This section governs the transfer of title to enchanted objects, ensuring that magical properties do not undermine transactional clarity. Under § 1102.1(a), any sale or gift of a magically altered item must disclose all active enchantments known to the transferor. Failure to do so constitutes fraud under general statute and voids the contract ab initio. The burden of disclosure rests solely on the seller, who shall provide written certification of the artifact's condition upon request.\n\n§ 1102.1(b) addresses the issue of residual curses or boons that may linger after transfer. Unless explicitly released by a qualified diviner, such effects remain attached to the object and bind all subsequent owners. This principle protects buyers from inheriting unintended liabilities while preventing sellers from offloading dangerous items without compensation. Any dispute over lingering magic shall be resolved via binding arbitration under § 1050.2.\n\nSpecial provisions apply when conveying items with sentient or semi-sentient properties, such as animated constructs or golems. Under § 1102.1(c), the transferor must ensure that any embedded wills are either fulfilled or legally discharged before completion of sale. Unfulfilled contractual obligations owed by the item itself may override personal agreements between parties, creating third-party claims against both buyer and seller. This rule preserves the integrity of magical contracts even after ownership changes hands.\n\n§ 1102.1(d) covers the unique case of heirlooms passed down through generations. Such items often carry familial protections or restrictions that cannot be overridden by ordinary means. Any attempt to break these bonds without proper authorization shall result in immediate revocation of title and potential criminal charges under § 1400.9(a). Families holding such artifacts are urged to register them with the Conservators for safekeeping, thereby avoiding costly litigation down the line.\n\nFinally, § 1102.1(e) establishes a presumption against implied warranties in magical goods absent explicit agreement to the contrary. Unlike mundane merchandise, enchanted objects cannot be assumed to function perfectly upon delivery unless guaranteed in writing. Buyers must inspect items thoroughly before finalizing transactions and promptly report any defects discovered thereafter. Prompt notification preserves remedies; delay waives rights under § 1050.3(b).\n\nThis comprehensive framework ensures fair dealing in the complex marketplace of magical artifacts, balancing the interests of all parties involved while maintaining public safety standards set forth by the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1102.1",
            "1050.2",
            "1400.9",
            "1050.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.6 Collateral Security and Subrogation Rights in Magical Transactions",
          "text": "In the context of secured transactions involving magical assets, collateral security may extend beyond the primary item to include secondary obligations or rights held by the obligor. This includes, but is not limited to, future earnings from enchanted labor, residual effects of prior spells, or potential claims against third parties arising from the artifact's use. The creditor must disclose all such collateral interests at the time of agreement execution. Failure to do so renders any attempt to enforce them void ab initio.\n\nSubrogation rights arise when a debtor fulfills an obligation by paying off another party who held a prior claim on the same magical asset. In such cases, the debtor assumes the position of the original creditor and may seek reimbursement from the obligor up to the amount paid. The scope of subrogation is limited strictly to the actual sum expended, plus reasonable legal fees incurred in perfecting the transfer of title. Any excess value retained by the debtor remains their property free of prior encumbrances.\n\nSpecial provisions apply when collateral security involves living beings or sentient entities enchanted with servitude spells. In these instances, additional protections must be included to prevent abuse and ensure humane treatment of the subject. These may include limits on working hours, mandatory rest periods, adequate nutrition and shelter, and prohibition of cruel or degrading practices. Violation of any such safeguard constitutes grounds for immediate termination of the security arrangement and potential criminal prosecution.\n\nThe concept of \"clean title\" takes on unique significance in magical transactions due to the possibility of hidden curses or blemishes affecting value and usability. Buyers are entitled to full disclosure of all known defects before finalizing an agreement. Sellers who conceal material flaws may be held liable for resulting damages even after transfer of possession. Disputes over clean title status shall be resolved through independent arcane appraisal by mutually agreed-upon experts, with costs borne equally unless otherwise stipulated in the contract.\n\nCollateral obligations arising from magical artifacts must clearly specify duration, termination triggers, and remedies upon breach. Indefinite or vague terms are unenforceable under these accords. Parties may agree to automatic renewal clauses subject to notice periods and fair compensation adjustments based on current market rates for similar services. Any modification of original terms requires written consent signed by all involved parties bearing the official seal of the Mages' Guild Conservators.\n\nThe principles outlined herein apply equally to domestic and international transactions, though additional regulatory compliance may be necessary when crossing planar boundaries or involving foreign jurisdictions with conflicting laws. In such cases, priority shall generally favor the law of the place where performance occurs unless otherwise specified in a valid choice-of-law provision incorporated into the main agreement text.\n\nIt is further noted that all collateral arrangements must align with established ethical guidelines set forth by the Guild's Code of Conduct and cannot violate higher-order prohibitions concerning human dignity or planar integrity. Contracts attempting to circumvent these fundamental protections are void regardless of their apparent legality under conventional civil law standards. The Courts retain inherent jurisdiction to intervene where necessary to uphold these overriding public policy concerns.\n\nUnder § 1101.6, parties engaging in magical secured transactions must explicitly identify all forms of collateral and associated obligations at the outset. Concealment renders any attempted enforcement null and void. Subrogation rights are limited strictly to actual amounts paid plus reasonable legal costs. Special safeguards protect sentient subjects from exploitation or abuse. Clean title requires full disclosure of defects, with sellers liable for undisclosed flaws causing damage. All terms must be precise and comply with both local regulations and Guild ethics. The Courts maintain oversight authority to ensure fundamental protections remain intact regardless of contractual language employed.\n\nsee § 1002.5 Collateral Disclosure Requirements; see § 1103.1 Ethical Guidelines for Magical Practitioners; see § 2004.7 Special Protections for Sentient Beings",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1101.6",
            "1002.5",
            "1103.1",
            "2004.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.5 Related Requirements and Collateral Obligations — Continued",
          "text": "In the event that a magical item or creature is gifted, sold, or otherwise transferred subject to conditions precedent or subsequent, such conditions shall be deemed enforceable obligations under these Accords where they do not contravene public policy or the explicit prohibitions of this Code. See § 1001.2 Prohibited Spells in Financial Markets; § 300.1 Schedules of Arts. For example, if a vendor sells a cursed artifact with knowledge that it will cause harm to the purchaser's heirs, such malice may constitute actionable fraud under general principles of contract law as interpreted by this Desk. Similarly, a seller who knowingly fails to disclose material defects in a magical construct may be held liable for breach of warranty and negligence per se.\n\nWhere a gift is given with an express reservation of rights or title, the donor shall retain full ownership and control over the gifted item until such time that all conditions have been fulfilled. Under § 1001.6 Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets; § 1301.4 Who May Inspect, this includes the right to reclaim possession should the donee fail to adhere to the stipulated terms. The burden of proving compliance with such covenants shall rest upon the donor seeking enforcement thereof.\n\nCollateral security arrangements involving magical property shall be governed by these provisions insofar as they relate to the underlying transaction. See § 1201.3 Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber; § 1401.1 Historical Annotations; Form. This encompasses pledges of future harvests, promises of service rendered through magic, or offers of assistance in locating and neutralizing threats posed by rival casters. Such obligations are binding upon execution, provided they do not violate any statutory prohibition herein enacted.\n\nIt is further declared that all requirements imposed under this Section shall be construed liberally in favor of enforcing the true intent of the parties, lest a technicality render nugatory an otherwise valid agreement. As stated in § 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk; § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill, honesty and good faith are paramount to maintaining the integrity of magical commerce within Autumnwood's borders. Failure to comply with any provision herein may result in severe penalties, including revocation of licensure and imprisonment at Archmage Veyra's discretion.\n\nThe Desk reserves the right to issue additional guidelines or interpretations as necessary to address evolving circumstances surrounding related obligations and collateral interests in magic items and beings. Nothing contained herein shall be deemed to limit such authority unless expressly stated otherwise by unanimous vote of the full Council. Under § 1001.3 Valuation After Alteration; § 500.2 Unscheduled Weather, flexibility is essential to accommodate the unpredictable nature of arcane phenomena in commercial dealings.\n\n*ARCHIVE CARD: Related Obligations & Collateral Security*\n*Maintain detailed records of all conditions attached to magical transactions, including but not limited to:*\n- *Precise wording of any reservations of title or rights by donors/sellers*\n- *Proof of fulfillment of agreed-upon terms where applicable*\n- *Evidence of malice, concealment of defects, or other breaches warranting enforcement actions*\n- *Nature and scope of any pledged collateral security interests*\n\n*Reference: § 1001.6 Endowments and Gifts; § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts*",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.2",
            "300.1",
