    {
      "cite": "1301.5",
      "part": "part_xiii",
      "title": "Supporting Obligations",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Supporting Obligations",
          "text": "The provisions set forth in this Part shall be read and construed together with the remaining articles of these Accords, including but not limited to the Licensing Framework (§§ 200.1–200.6), the Classification Schedules (§§ 300.1–300.5), and the Research Protocols (§§ 400.1–400.5). Nothing herein shall be deemed to supplant or contradict any prior enactments of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, provided such earlier laws are not expressly repealed by this amendment. In cases where ambiguity exists between multiple sections, the principle of *leges posteriores non prejudicant legibus priori* (later laws do not harm earlier ones) shall govern unless a clear intent to the contrary is demonstrated.\n\nFurther, these Related Requirements operate in tandem with the broader policies outlined in Parts I through IX, particularly those concerning professional conduct (§§ 100.1–100.8), disciplinary procedures (§§ 500.1–500.9), and ethical obligations of mages within society at large (§§ 600.1–600.7). Any action taken under the authority granted herein must remain consistent with the overarching mission of protecting both magical diversity and public safety from undue harm or exploitation. Where conflicts arise between this section and any other provision, the hierarchy of law shall dictate resolution: first, by reference to the specific catchline cited; second, by considering the context in which the clause appears within its immediate Part; third, by applying general principles of statutory interpretation as set forth in § 1.7.\n\nIt is hereby declared that all mages subject to jurisdiction under these Accords must familiarize themselves thoroughly with the entirety thereof, including cross-references and annexes, before engaging in any regulated magical practice or commercial activity. Failure to do so may result in aggravated penalties upon conviction for violations committed while operating without adequate knowledge of applicable laws. The Guild reserves the right to issue supplementary guidance memoranda as needed to clarify implementation details, which shall carry binding force equivalent to those contained directly within this Code unless explicitly stated otherwise.\n\nFinally, these Related Requirements remain subject to periodic review and revision by the full Assembly of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, with input sought from affected parties whenever feasible. Such updates will be duly published in official gazettes and made readily accessible to all members of the magical community via digital archives maintained by the Guild’s Information Office. Until then, these provisions stand as the definitive expression of current legal standards governing professional magic use across the Midlands region.\n\nsee § 1301.2 Penalties; see also §§ 600.4 Standards of Conduct and § 700.5 Enforcement Mechanisms\"",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "300.1",
            "400.1",
            "100.1",
            "500.1",
            "600.1",
            "1.7",
            "1301.2",
            "600.4",
            "700.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.5 Related Requirements and Supporting Obligations",
          "text": "The provisions set forth in this Part are not to be read as standalone rules but as an integrated whole, each section drawing upon the others for full meaning and effect. Where a requirement is described as \"related,\" it carries with it ancillary duties necessary to give the primary mandate its intended force. For instance, under § 1301.2 Penalties, failure to pay levies in full and timely results not only in financial sanction but also in restriction of magical practice until compliance is achieved—this operational consequence flows directly from the penal framework laid out herein. Similarly, § 1301.4 Who May Inspect establishes who is empowered to examine records; this authority cannot be exercised absent adherence to procedural safeguards detailed elsewhere, such as notice periods and scope limitations found in § 1301.3 Appeals. These interlocking obligations ensure that enforcement remains both effective and equitable.\n\nParties subject to the fees outlined above must also maintain accurate books of account reflecting all transactions involving magical services or goods. While not explicitly mandated by a single clause, this duty is implied by the need for transparent auditing referenced in § 1301.4 and the general principle of good faith enshrined in § 1.7 Construction. Records kept pursuant to this obligation must be made available upon reasonable request to authorized representatives of the Accords Desk, with refusal deemed a separate violation triggering additional penalties under § 1301.2. Failure to comply with these related requirements may result in escalated sanctions, including suspension of licensure or referral for criminal prosecution where appropriate. Ultimately, adherence to all applicable provisions collectively ensures the integrity and fairness of the regulatory regime governing arcane activities within the realm.\n\nsee § 1301.2 Penalties; § 1301.4 Who May Inspect; § 1.7 Construction; § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill\"",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1301.2",
            "1301.4",
            "1301.3",
            "1.7",
            "200.6",
            "1301.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.5 Related Requirements and Supporting Obligations — Continued",
          "text": "No assessment or fee levied under these Accords shall stand unless accompanied by all requisite supporting documentation, certifications, and declarations as specified in the applicable Schedule. Failure to provide such documentation within thirty (30) days of demand shall render the obligation void ab initio, with no refund due. See also § 1201.5 Related Requirements regarding evidentiary standards for planar tolls.\n\nAll payors must maintain accurate records of all transactions related to their obligations under this Code, including but not limited to receipts, invoices, bank statements, and correspondence with the Accords Desk. Such records shall be retained for a period of no less than seven (7) years from the date of payment and made available upon reasonable request for audit or verification purposes. Under § 100.4 Records of Speech, failure to produce requested records may result in suspension of services and escalation of penalties.\n\nIn cases where payments are made via third-party intermediaries or automated systems, the payor remains fully liable for ensuring that funds are transmitted correctly and on time. Any disputes arising from technical failures or processing errors shall not relieve the debtor of their primary obligation to satisfy the debt within the stipulated timeframe. See § 1301.4 Who May Inspect regarding responsibilities of intermediaries.\n\nWhere applicable, payors must also comply with any additional regulatory requirements imposed by external governing bodies or international accords that supersede this Code. This includes adhering to export controls, anti-money laundering statutes, and data privacy regulations as relevant to the nature of the transaction. Reference is made to § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy for guidance on conflicts between domestic and foreign laws.\n\nFinally, all obligations under this Code are subject to periodic review and adjustment by the Accords Desk in light of changing economic conditions and technological developments. Payors should consult official publications or seek legal counsel when uncertain about their specific duties or rights arising from these provisions. For further clarification, see § 1301.3 Appeals regarding formal dispute resolution processes.\n\n\"Compliance is not a suggestion; it is the bedrock of fair trade.\" - Archmage Veyra, in her inaugural address to the Guild.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1201.5",
            "100.4",
            "1301.4",
            "100.7",
            "1301.3",
            "1301.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.5 Related Requirements and Supporting Obligations – Continued",
          "text": "Not all obligations arising under the Autumnwood Accords are self-evident or immediately apparent upon first reading. Certain duties exist only by implication, custom, or prior agreement between parties bound by the Codex. These latent requirements must be identified and enforced with the same rigor as express provisions to maintain the integrity of magical commerce and societal order. Failure to recognize such obligations may result in cascading liabilities that far exceed initial expectations.\n\nOne primary category of related requirements concerns the maintenance of accurate records and timely reporting. Under § 1301.2, all transactions involving licensed magic must be logged in full detail within seven days of completion. This includes not only monetary exchanges but also transfers of knowledge, physical artifacts, or intangible benefits like access to restricted research facilities. The Guild Clerk reviews these entries quarterly to ensure compliance and detect anomalies indicative of fraud or abuse. Any discrepancies must be resolved promptly through established dispute mechanisms before escalating further.\n\nAnother critical obligation pertains to the ongoing education and training of practitioners. As outlined in § 200.1, every active license holder must complete at least twenty hours annually of approved continuing professional development courses focused on emerging risks, ethical considerations, and technological advancements relevant to their field of practice. This requirement ensures that all mages remain current with evolving standards and best practices while fostering a culture of lifelong learning within the magical community. Neglecting this duty may result in disciplinary action ranging from warnings to outright revocation of licensure privileges depending on severity.\n\nFurthermore, parties engaging in regulated activities must adhere strictly to safety protocols designed to mitigate potential hazards arising from their operations. These guidelines are codified across various sections of the Accords and supplemented by industry-specific regulations issued by specialized subcommittees. Examples include proper disposal methods for hazardous materials, adequate shielding against stray spells during casting sessions, and routine inspections of equipment for signs of wear or malfunction. Compliance with these measures protects both individuals and society at large from unforeseen consequences stemming from negligent conduct.\n\nLastly, there exists an implicit duty to act in good faith towards fellow members whenever circumstances permit without compromising one’s own legitimate interests. While not explicitly mandated by statute, this principle underpins much of the spirit behind numerous clauses throughout the Codex. It encourages cooperation rather than competition among practitioners and promotes a harmonious working environment conducive to shared progress and prosperity for all involved stakeholders alike. Upholding these unwritten norms alongside formal rules helps preserve trust essential for sustaining robust relations between diverse groups operating within scope defined hereinabove.\n\nIn summary, adherence to both stated provisions and implied responsibilities forms the bedrock upon which successful navigation of complex legal landscapes hinges for those bound by Autumnwood Accords today and tomorrow alike. Ignoring either aspect invites unnecessary peril down path fraught with pitfalls awaiting unwary travelers unprepared adequately face challenges ahead confidently yet cautiously ever onward toward brighter horizons beyond!",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1301.2",
            "200.1",
            "1301.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.5 Related Requirements and Supporting Obligations — Administration",
          "text": "Matters ancillary to the primary assessment of fees, penalties, and appeals under this Part shall nonetheless be governed by these provisions where applicable. First, all persons subject to the Accords are deemed to have notice of the current fee schedule as published on the Autumnwood Accords Desk's official bulletin boards and website. Failure to consult said publications does not excuse non-compliance with payment deadlines or obligations thereunder. Second, any dispute arising out of a calculation error or clerical mistake in the original assessment shall be resolved by reference to the underlying facts and circumstances surrounding the alleged offense or conduct at issue. Nothing herein precludes the Desk from conducting a fresh review upon request if new evidence emerges that materially alters the basis for the initial determination. Third, all payments made toward any fee liability must be rendered in Wario Coin unless otherwise authorized in writing by the Guild Sovereign. Checks drawn on personal accounts or foreign currencies shall be deemed invalid and returned immediately, together with any accrued interest thereon. Fourth, persons who contest a charge have ten (10) business days from receipt of notice to file a written appeal setting forth their grounds for relief. Appeals must specify whether they challenge the factual basis for the assessment or seek remission on equitable grounds such as hardship or mistake of law. Fifth, inspections conducted pursuant to these sections shall be limited in scope and duration so as not to unduly burden the inspected party's lawful activities. Inspectors must produce proper identification and a written order authorizing entry prior to commencing any examination. Resistance to inspection without probable cause may result in escalated sanctions up to and including arrest for obstruction of justice. Finally, nothing contained herein shall be construed to diminish the rights or remedies available at law or under common usage absent express waiver by agreement of all interested parties. Where applicable, general principles of equity shall supplement these specific mandates in furtherance of their intent and purpose.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1301.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1301.5 Related Requirements and Supporting Obligations — Enforcement Mechanism",
          "text": "The provisions herein establish the requisite framework for ensuring compliance with all applicable obligations under this Codex, including but not limited to those set forth in §§ 1301.1 through 1301.4. To effectuate these purposes, the Desk shall have authority to employ such measures as may be necessary and appropriate, consistent with established principles of administrative law and the overarching goals of public safety and economic stability. Such enforcement actions may include, without limitation, direct intervention by Aegis Magi or their authorized agents; issuance of stop-work orders or temporary suspensions of licensure pending full investigation; imposition of civil penalties or monetary fines pursuant to § 1301.2; referral for disciplinary proceedings before the Paradox Trial in cases involving gross negligence or intentional misconduct; and, where warranted by the circumstances, initiation of criminal prosecutions under applicable statutes. All enforcement activities shall be conducted with due regard for procedural fairness and protection of individual rights, as mandated by §§ 1.7 and 100.5. The Desk retains discretion to tailor its response to each case on a fact-specific basis, exercising sound judgment in determining the most effective means of securing compliance while minimizing undue disruption to lawful commerce and daily life. Nothing herein shall be construed to limit or impair any other powers or duties vested in the Desk by law or this Codex, nor to preclude recourse to additional remedies where justice requires. The ultimate aim remains preservation of public welfare through vigilant oversight and decisive action against those who would undermine the integrity of our magical society's foundational institutions.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1301.1",
            "1301.2",
            "1.7",
            "1301.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
