    {
      "cite": "900.6",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic",
          "text": "The obligations imposed by this Part are cumulative and do not exhaust the scope of regulatory oversight applicable to magical practice. Nothing herein shall be construed as limiting the authority of the Aegis Magi under any other provision of these Accords, including but not limited to those governing public safety, environmental protection, and consumer fraud prevention. In particular, where a proposed enchantment involves the modification or creation of sentient beings—whether natural or artificial—the full spectrum of bioethical statutes shall apply in tandem with filing mandates. See also § 300.2 (Prohibited Arts); § 500.4 (Healing as Commercial Transaction); § 1201.4 (Cartography and Unauthorized Excursions).\n\nFurther, this Part does not relieve mages of their common law duties regarding negligence, tort liability, or breach of contract arising from their professional activities. The Desk shall not act as a shield against civil suits brought by third parties harmed by improper magical operations. Conversely, compliance with all filing requirements creates no presumption of innocence in any criminal prosecution; each charge must still be proven beyond a reasonable doubt per the standards established under § 1003 (Criminal Proceedings). See further § 200.4 (Disciplinary Actions); § 1101.5 (Gifts and Gratuities); § 1401.3 (Defenses Against Empire Statutes).\n\nIt is expressly recognized that certain advanced techniques may fall outside the traditional scope of licensure yet remain subject to reporting obligations. For instance, the deployment of autonomous constructs or programmed ritual sequences capable of producing effects equivalent to licensed casting must be disclosed even if no direct human intervention occurs post-activation. The Desk retains discretion to demand supplemental data where necessary to fully assess risk profiles associated with novel applications of magical theory. This principle applies regardless of whether such activities are conducted under the guise of research, entertainment, or personal curiosity. Refer also to § 400.1 (Research Permits); § 700.2 (Constructs and Automatons); § 800.3 (Programmed Rituals).\n\nFinally, nothing in this section shall be interpreted to diminish the inherent dignity and autonomy of individuals whose lives may intersect with magical practices. Consent must always be informed, voluntary, and freely given whenever another person is subjected to any form of enchantment or supernatural intervention without their explicit authorization. Special protections extend to vulnerable populations including minors, persons with diminished capacity, and those in positions of dependency on the caster. Violations of these fundamental rights constitute grounds for immediate revocation of privileges under § 1005 (Grounds for Discipline). See likewise § 600.1 (Human Subjects Research); § 700.4 (Somatic Enchantment Limits); § 900.8 (Consent and Capacity).",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "500.4",
            "1201.4",
            "200.4",
            "1101.5",
            "1401.3",
            "400.1",
            "700.2",
            "800.3",
            "600.1",
            "700.4",
            "900.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.6 Related Requirements: Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic",
          "text": "Every filing submitted under these Accords must include full disclosure of any ancillary magical effects, whether primary or secondary, intended or incidental. This requirement extends beyond the explicit spellwork described in the application to encompass all associated phenomena, such as residual enchantments, auras of influence, or lingering planar signatures that may emanate from the caster or their equipment. Failure to declare these related elements shall be treated as material omission and subject to penalty under § 901.4.\n\nCertification accompanying each submission must attest not only to the truthfulness of the representations made but also to the completeness thereof. The undersigned affiant declares upon oath that no other magical properties, known or unknown, exist in connection with the filed matter except as fully set forth herein. This certification serves as a binding undertaking enforceable under common law and equitable principles, with perjury penalties applicable for willful falsification. See § 105.2 regarding oaths before the Guild Desk.\n\nIn cases where ancillary magic cannot be precisely quantified or isolated from the primary effect, reasonable estimates must still be provided along with supporting evidence such as witness testimony, experimental data, or comparative analyses. The burden of proof rests upon the applicant to demonstrate good faith effort in identifying and reporting all relevant magical attributes. Ambiguity or evasion will not excuse non-compliance but rather compound the violation. Under § 105.3, ambiguous filings are deemed insufficient and may be rejected outright.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to demand additional information beyond what is initially submitted if subsequent investigation reveals undisclosed related magic that impacts public safety or regulatory oversight. Applicants must cooperate fully with such inquiries, providing access to records, facilities, and personnel as reasonably necessary for verification purposes. Refusal to assist will result in escalation of charges per § 901.7 and potential revocation of future filing privileges.\n\nThis provision exists not merely as a bureaucratic formality but as an essential safeguard against the hidden dangers inherent in unregulated arcane practice. By demanding complete transparency regarding all magical manifestations, the Accords protect both the citizens and the Guild from unintended consequences arising from incomplete disclosure. Compliance is thus both a legal obligation and a moral imperative for all registered practitioners. See § 105.4 concerning duties to disclose material facts affecting public welfare.\n\n*Related provisions: §§ 901.3, 105.2–105.3*",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "901.4",
            "105.2",
            "105.3",
            "901.7",
            "105.4",
            "901.3",
            "900.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic",
          "text": "Under this section, every filing must disclose not only the primary enchantment but also any secondary or tertiary magical effects that may accompany it. This includes hidden clauses embedded within protective wards, latent powers activated under specific conditions, and any residual echoes from previous spellwork still present on an object. Failure to fully describe these ancillary elements renders a return incomplete and subject to penalty upon discovery of omission.\n\nThe requirement for full disclosure stems from the inherent dangers of unexamined magical artifacts. Even seemingly innocuous items may harbor unpredictable behaviors that could endanger users or bystanders if left unchecked. By mandating comprehensive reporting, the Accords aim to prevent accidents caused by ignorance of an item's true capabilities. This proactive approach aligns with the foundational principle of responsible magic use outlined in § 1004.2.\n\nTo facilitate accurate certification, filers must utilize standardized forms designed for detailed description of magical properties. These templates include fields for noting triggers, durations, side effects, and any known interactions with other spells or materials. Clerks trained in arcane analysis will review each submission to verify completeness before accepting it into the official record. Any ambiguities will be clarified through follow-up inquiries issued directly to the submitter.\n\nPenalties for inadequate disclosure range from monetary fines to temporary suspension of filing privileges, depending on severity and intent. Repeat offenders face escalating consequences up to permanent revocation of Guild status. In extreme cases where undisclosed magic leads to harm or destruction, criminal charges may additionally be pursued under applicable statutes. The ultimate goal remains safeguarding both public safety and the integrity of magical practice across all realms governed by these laws.\n\nBy insisting on thorough transparency around every aspect of enchanted objects, the Accords establish a rigorous framework for managing risk inherent in arcane arts. This meticulous approach reflects the Guild's long-standing commitment to maintaining order amidst the ever-shifting tides of power that define their world. Only through such diligence can society hope to coexist safely with the boundless possibilities—and perils—of magic itself.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1004.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Certification of Ancillary Magic",
          "text": "§ 900.6 Related Requirements: Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic\n\nNo report shall be deemed complete without full disclosure of all magic, enchantments, or artifacts employed in the casting event, whether primary or incidental. This mandate extends beyond the immediate spellwork to include any lingering effects, residual charges, or secondary phenomena triggered by the ritual. Failure to disclose such ancillary elements constitutes a material omission and subjects the caster to penalties under § 901.3 for falsification of records.\n\nCasters must certify in writing that no unauthorized modifications or additions were made to the spell after initial filing. This certification serves as proof of compliance with all regulatory requirements and protects both the Guild and the public from unforeseen hazards resulting from tampered rites. Any deviation from the approved version, however minor, invalidates the entire report and necessitates a new submission under § 901.2 for re-evaluation.\n\nIn cases where ancillary magic proves difficult to quantify or categorize, casters shall provide detailed descriptions of observed effects, potential risks, and recommended mitigations. This information will be reviewed by qualified peers who may request additional data or clarification as needed. The burden of proof rests squarely upon the caster to demonstrate full transparency regarding all magical elements involved in their practice.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to conduct random audits and inspections to verify accuracy of disclosures and certifications. Casters found evasive or deliberately misleading during such proceedings face immediate suspension pending further investigation, regardless of prior good standing. Repeated violations may result in permanent revocation of licensure under § 201.5 for egregious misconduct.\n\nUltimately, the principle guiding these provisions is one of complete accountability. By mandating thorough disclosure and rigorous certification, the Autumnwood Accords seek to ensure that all magical activities remain safe, predictable, and subject to effective oversight. This standard applies uniformly across all ranks and disciplines, reflecting the Guild's unwavering commitment to responsible stewardship of arcane knowledge for the greater good.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "900.6",
            "901.3",
            "901.2",
            "201.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Certification of Ancillary Magic — Continued",
          "text": "§ 900.6 Related Requirements, Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic\n\nEvery filing made under this Part shall include a complete disclosure of all ancillary magic used or contemplated in connection with the transaction. This requirement applies whether the ancillary spell is cast directly by the filer, delegated to an associate, or embedded within the object itself. Failure to disclose such magic renders the filing incomplete and subject to rejection at any time upon discovery. The burden of proof lies with the party asserting that ancillary enchantments were employed; they must provide verifiable evidence including witness testimony, magical residue analysis, or certified transcripts from a peer-reviewed casting session.\n\nThe phrase \"ancillary magic\" encompasses any spellwork performed to facilitate, enhance, or secure the primary transaction. This includes but is not limited to: transmutation rituals used to alter the form of goods; protective wards designed to prevent theft or tampering; divination techniques employed to assess market value; and communication charms utilized to negotiate terms remotely. Even seemingly innocuous gestures such as a blessing upon departure may constitute reportable magic if its intent was to influence future behavior related to the filing's subject matter. The scope of disclosure is intentionally broad to ensure transparency in all magical dealings conducted under these Accords.\n\nIn cases where ancillary magic poses potential harm or environmental risk, additional certification requirements apply. Any spellwork involving unstable energies, dimensional breaches, or sentient entities must be accompanied by a safety assessment signed by two licensed Aegis Magi. This report shall detail the nature of the hazard, proposed mitigation strategies, and monitoring protocols for post-filing observation. The Desk reserves the right to demand immediate suspension of operations if credible threats emerge from undisclosed magical practices. Repeated violations may result in revocation of filing privileges and referral for Paradox Trial proceedings.\n\nThe principles of full disclosure extend beyond mere factual accuracy. Filer must also reveal all material advantages gained through magical means, including preferential treatment by authorities, expedited processing times, or access to restricted resources. Concealing such benefits undermines the integrity of the entire regulatory framework. Transparency is not optional but a fundamental obligation owed to both the Guild and the public. Only through complete candor can meaningful oversight be achieved in this rapidly evolving field of arcane commerce.\n\nSee § 1001.2 regarding disclosure obligations for financial instruments, and § 300.4 concerning research copy limitations. Under § 1.7, any ambiguity in these provisions shall be resolved against the party seeking to restrict filing requirements. The Desk maintains absolute discretion to demand further information as necessary to fully evaluate compliance with this Part.\n\n*End of Page 256*",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "900.6",
            "1001.2",
            "300.4",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Certification and Disclosure of Ancillary Magic",
          "text": "§ 900.6 Related Requirements — Continued\n\nEvery application for licensure must contain full disclosure of all magical abilities, including those acquired through inheritance, study, or chance encounter. Concealment of material facts shall constitute grounds for revocation under § 200.4(A). Candidates are required to submit sworn statements verifying the provenance and classification of each ability listed on their form. Failure to do so may result in denial of credentials until satisfactory proof is provided.\n\nIn addition, applicants must certify that they possess no outstanding violations of these Accords or related statutes. Any prior infractions, whether adjudicated by this Guild or another competent authority, shall be itemized and explained. Repeated offenses will trigger enhanced scrutiny during the review process. The Desk reserves discretion to demand supplementary evidence whenever it deems necessary for a complete assessment of character and competence.\n\nAll licensure documents must bear original signatures of both applicant and witness(es). Copies certified by an authorized official serve as valid substitutes in cases where originals are lost or damaged. Electronic versions generated via secure portals shall carry equivalent legal weight, provided they display unaltered cryptographic seals validating their authenticity.\n\nThis section applies to all forms of licensure without exception, from the lowest-level permit to the highest tier of recognition. No individual may bypass these requirements through wealth, influence, or exceptional talent. The standards herein ensure uniformity and fairness across the entire Guild, protecting both the public interest and the integrity of magical practice itself.\n\nViolations of disclosure obligations will be treated as serious misconduct subject to disciplinary action up to and including permanent expulsion from membership. Recidivists face additional penalties designed to deter future attempts at deception or subversion of the system. The Desk maintains strict confidentiality regarding all submitted materials, releasing them only upon lawful compulsion or mutual consent between parties involved in a dispute.\n\nBy signing these forms, applicants agree to abide by all provisions outlined herein and acknowledge their ongoing responsibility for truthful representation of self. Breach of this agreement renders any obtained license void ab initio, with full retroactive effect. The Guild shall pursue all available remedies without limitation where fraud or misrepresentation is established beyond reasonable doubt.\n\nThis clause reflects the foundational principle that trust between caster and state forms the bedrock upon which safe magical society rests. Without transparency, neither protection nor accountability can be meaningfully enacted. Therefore, rigorous adherence to these mandates remains paramount for preserving both public welfare and the moral authority of this ancient institution.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.6",
            "200.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic",
