    {
      "cite": "600.6",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "in Magical Contracts",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.6 Related Requirements in Magical Contracts",
          "text": "The unique nature of magical contracts necessitates additional safeguards beyond those applicable to conventional agreements. First and foremost, any contract involving the binding or alteration of an individual's psyche must include explicit clauses detailing the scope and duration of such effects. This requirement ensures informed consent and provides a basis for future dispute resolution should adverse consequences arise. Failure to comply with this stipulation renders the agreement void ab initio under § 1004.3 (Unconscionability).\n\nFurthermore, contracts granting access to sensitive magical knowledge or materials must incorporate robust confidentiality provisions. The leaking of such information could have catastrophic repercussions for both parties and society at large. Violation of these clauses constitutes a material breach and may result in immediate termination of the agreement without prejudice to further legal action under § 1006.2 (Termination for Cause).\n\nIn instances where magical contracts involve third-party beneficiaries or successors, clear language must be employed to define the extent of their rights and obligations. Ambiguity in this regard frequently leads to litigation and undermines the integrity of the original pact. To mitigate this risk, parties are advised to draft comprehensive successor clauses addressing inheritance, assignment, and novation scenarios as outlined in § 1012.5 (Successors and Assigns).\n\nFinally, all magical contracts must specify mechanisms for monitoring compliance and enforcing terms. Given the intangible nature of many magical obligations, traditional remedies may prove ineffective or impractical. Therefore, parties should incorporate tailored enforcement provisions such as liquidated damages, specific performance orders, and injunctive relief where appropriate. These measures serve to preserve the value of the agreement and provide recourse in the event of breach under § 1008.4 (Remedies).\n\nBy adhering to these related requirements, parties can ensure their magical contracts are legally sound, enforceable, and reflective of their true intentions. Neglecting these critical elements exposes them to unnecessary risk and potential liability in an already complex landscape of arcane jurisprudence.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1004.3",
            "1006.2",
            "1012.5",
            "1008.4",
            "600.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.7 Related Requirements in Magical Contracts",
          "text": "In the realm of magical agreements, the concept of \"related requirements\" extends beyond mere statutory obligations to encompass unwritten norms and implicit understandings unique to arcane practice. Such unstated expectations often govern the conduct of parties engaged in spellservitude or other binding compacts where traditional contract law proves insufficient. For instance, it is widely accepted among mages that a summoning ritual performed under duress lacks inherent validity unless freely consented to by the subject; this principle stands even absent explicit clause in the contractual text. Similarly, when a master assigns tasks involving hazardous magic, they bear an implied duty to provide adequate safety measures and protective enchantments commensurate with known risks. Failure to do so may render any resulting injury compensable under tort law, notwithstanding the original agreement's silence on such matters.\n\nFurthermore, custom plays a pivotal role in shaping the contours of magical liability. Long-standing traditions within specific schools or circles can elevate certain practices from mere suggestion to binding precedent. Consider the case of a potion-brewer who employs unlicensed reagents despite having disclosed this fact during initial negotiations; subsequent illness suffered by the consumer would likely be deemed foreseeable and thus actionable, regardless of any disclaimer attempts. The rationale lies in the inherent asymmetry of knowledge between laypersons and trained practitioners—a disparity necessitating extra protections for those entering into such arrangements without full awareness of potential dangers. Ultimately, courts retain broad discretion to evaluate whether particular conduct aligns with reasonable standards of care within the context of magical arts, filling gaps left by rigid textual interpretation where fairness demands it most urgently.\n\nSee also § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill; § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts; § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs; § 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration; § 1001.9 Fraudulent Misrepresentation of Arcane Abilities. These provisions collectively underscore the necessity for both written clarity and contextual nuance when drafting magical contracts, lest unintended consequences arise from overlooked subtleties in this complex field of law.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "200.6",
            "300.2",
            "400.2",
            "500.4",
            "1001.9",
            "600.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.6 Related Requirements in Magical Contracts — Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.6 Any contract involving magical services, items, or effects must specify all ancillary conditions and obligations affecting the parties' rights and duties. This includes but is not limited to: maintenance responsibilities for enchanted goods; training requirements for new users of powerful artifacts; restrictions on transferability of magic-bound property; liability allocation in case of accidental discharge or malfunction; and provisions governing dispute resolution specific to arcane matters. Failure to include these terms may render a contract unenforceable under the doctrine of incomplete manifestation (§ 201.4) or mutual mistake (§ 202.3). The burden of proof regarding compliance with such stipulations rests on the party asserting their performance (§ 507.2).\n\n§ 600.7 Related requirements shall be drafted in plain language, avoiding technical jargon where possible to ensure all parties understand their commitments fully (§ 1401.1). Ambiguities are construed against the drafter under the contra proferentem rule (§ 315.2), particularly when one party lacks expertise in magical theory or practice (§ 600.8). Specialized clauses relating to containment, disposal, and emergency procedures must be standardized across all contracts involving hazardous magic to promote public safety (§ 400.7). Any deviation from these standards requires explicit consent from both parties in writing, witnessed by a licensed notary mage (§ 901.3). Breach of related requirements constitutes grounds for immediate termination of the agreement and potential liability for damages under common law principles (§ 802.1). The Guild reserves the right to audit compliance with these provisions at reasonable intervals without prior notice (§ 705.4), imposing penalties for non-cooperation as provided in § 600.9.\n\n\"Contracts are mere promises until they're written down properly, especially when magic's involved. One missing clause can ruin everyone's day.\" - Janna Brightspark, Innovator Leader\n\n\"A well-drafted magical contract anticipates every possible contingency and locks down responsibilities like a vice grip. Sloppiness here leads to disaster later.\" - Archmage Theron, Conservator Chief\n\n\"Plain language is non-negotiable. If someone doesn't understand their obligations, they shouldn't be signing anything at all!\" - Veyra, Guild Sovereign",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "600.6",
            "201.4",
            "202.3",
            "507.2",
            "600.7",
            "1401.1",
            "315.2",
            "600.8",
            "400.7",
            "901.3",
            "802.1",
            "705.4",
            "600.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.8 Related Requirements in Magical Contracts — Continuation",
          "text": "The applicability of the related requirements extends beyond direct contractual relationships to encompass incidental and consequential transactions arising from magical services rendered or received. When a caster undertakes a primary obligation under a binding agreement, any ancillary acts necessarily incident thereto are deemed covered by the protections afforded herein, regardless of whether such acts were expressly enumerated in the original instrument. This broad scope ensures comprehensive coverage of the full spectrum of activities associated with magical engagement, preventing evasion through technicalities or clever drafting.\n\nParticularly relevant is the treatment of preparatory and follow-on operations integral to fulfilling contractual duties. For instance, research conducted by a magic shop owner to better serve client needs falls within the purview of related requirements, as does the training of apprentices to maintain service quality standards. Similarly, logistical arrangements such as transportation, storage, and security for magical goods or materials are implicated, even if not directly stipulated in the governing agreement. The overarching principle is that any action reasonably necessary to accomplish the contractual purpose benefits from the associated safeguards and limitations.\n\nThe temporal reach of these provisions likewise exceeds the nominal term of the contract itself. Obligations persist beyond conclusion until all consequences have fully ripened, including cleanup efforts, restoration activities, or remediation measures triggered by performance deficiencies. Post-contractual duties akin to those imposed by statute or common law remain subject to related requirements where they intersect with prior magical dealings. This continuity prevents gaps in protection and maintains coherent regulatory oversight across the entire lifecycle of a transaction.\n\nNotably absent from consideration are purely social or gratuitous acts lacking economic substance or legal effect. Casual displays of skill, unremunerated favors between friends, and mere expressions of goodwill fall outside the ambit of related requirements. The threshold for inclusion demands some tangible nexus to business operations or pecuniary interest. Absent such connection, individual liberties remain intact despite potential parallels to regulated conduct.\n\nUltimately, defining the contours of \"related\" demands a case-by-case analysis weighing context, intent, and proportionality. No rigid formula can encompass every conceivable scenario arising from magical commerce. Instead, courts must exercise discretion guided by the spirit of these provisions—to balance innovation with safety, efficiency with equity, and freedom with responsibility—in ensuring that all facets of magical activity operate within a framework conducive to public welfare and private justice alike.\n\nsee § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; § 600.2 Familiars as Agents; § 600.3 Geas as an Unconscionable Term; § 600.4 Construct Labor; Minimum Ward; § 600.5 Summoned Notary; see also §§ 1.7 Aggregation of Conduct, 200.4 Suspension and Lapse, 400.3 Borrowed Hours; Interest in Time under the Autumnwood Accords.\n\n\"Magic is not merely a commodity to be bought and sold—it is a force that shapes reality itself. Our laws must account for this profound truth when regulating its use.\" — Archmage Veyra, Opening Address, Autumn Wood Convention, 892 AR \"Related requirements ensure fairness in all aspects of magical exchange, protecting both providers and consumers from abuse or exploitation.\" — Janna Brightspark, Innovators' Platform Speech, Midterm Elections, 895 AR \"The definition of 'related' must evolve with changing practices. We cannot allow loopholes to erode the integrity of our system.\" — Council Resolution 14-B, § 3(c), Annual Review Cycle IV",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "600.1",
            "600.2",
            "600.3",
            "600.4",
            "600.5",
            "1.7",
            "600.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.9 Related Requirements in Magical Contracts — Continued",
          "text": "In the realm of magical compacts, the principle of full disclosure is paramount to ensuring informed consent and equitable outcomes. This mandate extends beyond mere verbal assertions or written declarations; it encompasses a comprehensive examination of all potential ramifications inherent within any spellbinding arrangement. Parties entering into such agreements bear an affirmative duty to elucidate every facet of the proposed enchantment's scope, limitations, and possible consequences, regardless of how obscure or esoteric they may appear to the layperson. Failure to fulfill this obligation may render the entire contract voidable at the discretion of the aggrieved party, who need only demonstrate that material facts were concealed or misrepresented during negotiations.\n\nFurthermore, the requirement for full disclosure necessitates proactive communication from both sides throughout the lifecycle of the magical undertaking. Any developments, unexpected events, or shifts in circumstances must be promptly brought to light so that all involved may reassess their positions accordingly. This ongoing dialogue serves not only as a safeguard against unforeseen pitfalls but also fosters an environment of trust and mutual respect essential for successful collaboration. By adhering strictly to these standards of transparency, practitioners can mitigate risks associated with complex arcane operations while preserving the integrity of the Guild's regulatory framework. Ultimately, full disclosure stands as a cornerstone upon which fair and binding magical agreements are built, enabling all participants to navigate this volatile domain with confidence and clarity.\n\nIn addition, the obligation of complete revelation applies equally to both novice initiates and seasoned wizards alike. Regardless of experience level or perceived expertise, every individual must approach contractual engagements with an open mind and a willingness to learn from others' insights and warnings. This humility allows for more thorough evaluations of potential hazards and facilitates better decision-making under pressure. Moreover, by sharing knowledge freely among peers, the magical community enriches itself collectively while reducing overall liability exposure for all members. Thus, full disclosure operates not merely as a legal formality but as a vital ethical imperative guiding responsible magic use in society at large.\n\nFinally, it should be noted that this principle of openness extends even to seemingly trivial details often overlooked by the hurried or impatient. Whether concerning minor ingredients, subtle phrasings, or incidental effects, every nuance bears significance when viewed through the lens of potential danger or opportunity. By diligently attending to such minutiae alongside broader strategic considerations, spellcasters demonstrate their commitment to excellence and safety above all else. In doing so, they uphold the highest ideals of their craft while safeguarding against unintended fallout that could plague innocent bystanders far downstream from the initial engagement. Truly, full disclosure remains an indispensable touchstone for navigating the intricate terrain of magical jurisprudence with wisdom and foresight.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "600.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.6 Related Requirements in Magical Contracts – Force Majeure and Dispute Re",
          "text": "In the realm of magical contracts, unforeseen circumstances often arise that render original terms impractical or impossible to fulfill. To address such contingencies, this section establishes protocols for handling force majeure events and resolving disputes arising therefrom. Under § 600.12, any party claiming relief due to supernatural impediments must provide credible evidence thereof within seven days of the event's occurrence. Failure to do so shall result in automatic forfeiture of claimed exemptions.\n\nThe governing body shall convene an emergency session upon receipt of such proof to assess validity. This procedure mirrors the provisions outlined in § 100.8 regarding emergency sittings, ensuring prompt and equitable adjudication. Should the claim prove meritorious, appropriate adjustments to obligations may be mandated pursuant to § 600.15's framework for equitable modification. Conversely, unverified assertions will be dismissed summarily under § 200.4's provisions governing frivolous filings.\n\nDisputes over interpretation of force majeure clauses shall be submitted to binding arbitration by a panel of three impartial magisters selected via random lot from the Master Roster maintained per § 1301.2. The arbitrators' decision shall be final and enforceable under §§ 600.18–600.20, which outline the scope of their authority and remedies available upon confirmation of a valid claim. All proceedings must adhere strictly to timelines prescribed herein, with extensions granted only for compelling cause as determined by the presiding officer.\n\nThis mechanism ensures that magical agreements remain adaptable yet binding even amid the inherent volatility of arcane phenomena. By providing clear pathways for addressing extraordinary challenges, these provisions promote stability and trust between contracting parties while safeguarding against abuse or exploitation arising from unpredictable circumstances beyond human control. See also § 100.8 Emergency Sitting; § 600.15 Equitable Modification; §§ 600.18–600.20 Arbitration Procedures.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "600.12",
            "100.8",
            "600.15",
            "200.4",
            "1301.2",
            "600.18",
            "600.6"
          ]
        }
      ],
