    {
      "cite": "700.6",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcan",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.6 Related Requirements – Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcan",
          "text": "In any proceeding before the Mages' Guild Accords Desk involving the interpretation or enforcement of these Articles, the burden of proof shall rest upon the party asserting a particular fact or legal conclusion. This burden may be satisfied by clear and convincing evidence as to matters of fact, while questions of law are resolved according to the established precedents contained herein. However, where unique aspects of magical phenomena render conventional evidentiary standards inadequate, the Desk shall have discretion to admit alternative forms of proof deemed reliable under the circumstances, including but not limited to verified spell-castings, ritual demonstrations, and sworn testimony from qualified experts.\n\nThe standard of evidence required to establish liability or entitlement shall generally be preponderance of the weight, unless otherwise specified by specific provisions within this Code or by agreement of the parties involved. In cases involving alleged violations of restricted magical practices or unauthorized field casting, a heightened evidentiary threshold may apply to protect against frivolous accusations that could jeopardize livelihoods and reputations. The Desk shall carefully scrutinize all submissions for sufficiency and relevance before granting leave to proceed further on any issue requiring proof.\n\nParties are encouraged to preserve chains of custody for physical evidence and maintain detailed records of events relevant to the dispute, particularly where timing or sequence may be material. Failure to properly document circumstances surrounding an alleged wrongdoings may result in adverse inference against the aggrieved party should a hearing proceed. The Desk retains authority to issue subpoenas for production of pertinent materials as deemed necessary for a fair adjudication of rights and obligations arising under these Articles.\n\nUltimately, it is incumbent upon all concerned parties to approach arcane disputes with integrity and candor, recognizing that the Guild's primary function remains the orderly administration of justice in matters pertaining to magical arts. By adhering to these principles of proof and procedure, the Accords Desk ensures equitable outcomes while safeguarding against abuse of process or manipulation of evidentiary rules for partisan advantage. All decisions shall be grounded firmly in both letter and spirit of this Code, applied consistently across cases regardless of complexity or controversy.\n\nsee § 1001.2 Admissibility Standards; see § 500.6 Evidentiary Presumptions; see § 700.3 Witness Credibility Assessment under oath.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1001.2",
            "500.6",
            "700.3",
            "700.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcane Disputes",
          "text": "In any proceeding before the Accords Desk, the burden of proof rests upon the party asserting a claim or defense. This principle holds true regardless of whether the dispute concerns mundane transactions or complex magical phenomena. The standard of evidence required varies depending on the nature of the allegation at hand. For ordinary matters such as contract breaches or tortious conduct, clear and convincing evidence suffices to establish liability. However, when allegations involve the commission of a felony or the use of prohibited schools of magic, proof beyond all reasonable doubt is mandated. This higher threshold reflects the gravity of accusations that could result in imprisonment, magical sealing, or expulsion from the Guild entirely.\n\nThe presentation of evidence in arcane disputes may take forms unfamiliar to traditional courts. Witness testimony remains admissible provided the witnesses are competent and their accounts corroborated by independent sources. Documentary evidence such as contracts, invoices, and correspondence carries significant weight if properly authenticated. Magical artifacts relevant to the case must be produced for inspection under oath unless excused due to risk of harm or destruction. In instances where physical proof is unavailable or inconclusive, alternative methods of inquiry may be employed with judicial approval. These might include divination rituals conducted by licensed mages, analysis of magical signatures using approved instruments, or consultation with recognized experts in the relevant field.\n\nThe rules governing evidence gathering and presentation are designed to ensure fair and efficient resolution of disputes while protecting public safety and maintaining the integrity of magical practices. Parties must act in good faith throughout the process and refrain from obstructing investigations through concealment or destruction of pertinent materials. Failure to comply with these obligations may result in adverse inference sanctions or outright dismissal of the affected claim or defense. Ultimately, adherence to established procedural norms is essential for preserving justice within the intricate web of relationships that bind the magical community together.\n\nsee § 1001.7 Burden of Proof; see § 1201.4 Standard of Evidence in Magical Trials; see § 300.6 Admissibility of Extraneous Testimony; see § 500.9 Judicial Discretion in Evidence Matters",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.7",
            "1201.4",
            "300.6",
            "500.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcane Disputes — Continued",
          "text": "In all proceedings before the Mages' Guild, the burden of proof rests upon the party asserting an affirmative claim or defense under these Accords. This principle applies whether the dispute concerns a violation of licensing terms, an accusation of illegal research, or any other matter arising from magical practice. The standard of proof required is preponderance of the evidence, meaning that the trier of fact must be convinced by greater weight than not that the asserted proposition is true. However, in cases involving serious allegations such as necromancy or unauthorized field casting, the Guild may elevate the burden to clear and convincing evidence at its sole discretion.\n\nThe evidentiary rules governing arcane disputes are tailored to account for the unique nature of magical phenomena and testimony. Expert witnesses familiar with the relevant schools of magic shall be permitted to offer opinions on matters within their specialized knowledge. Physical evidence such as spell components, cursed artifacts, or records of illicit activity may be admitted if properly authenticated. Depositions conducted in accordance with Guild procedure are admissible subject to objection. Witnesses who fail to appear when summoned face sanctions up to and including suspension from practice until compliance is achieved.\n\nThe Mages' Desk retains broad authority to control the flow of evidence and testimony as it deems appropriate for ensuring a fair and efficient adjudication process. Requests for additional discovery or forensic analysis may be granted upon showing good cause and payment of applicable fees. The Guild shall not be bound by strict common law evidentiary presumptions where such would hinder the determination of truth in complex magical matters. Ultimately, the Desk's objective is to ascertain the facts as they pertain to the application of these Accords, free from technical obstacles.\n\nCitations: see § 700.1 Evidence Generally; under § 700.3 Memory Charm as Spoliation; per § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill. Related Requirements apply mutatis mutandis to all proceedings before the Mages' Guild concerning magic and its regulation under these Accords.\n\n*End of Page*",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "700.1",
            "700.3",
            "200.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.6 Related Requirements – Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcan — Continued",
          "text": "In any proceeding before the Accords Desk, the burden of proof lies with the party asserting an affirmative defense or claim. This principle holds true regardless of whether the matter concerns the interpretation of a filed return, the validity of a magical contract, or the propriety of a casting operation. Where doubt exists regarding the nature of an alleged violation, the benefit thereof shall be accorded to the accused. Such discretion is not merely a matter of fairness but a necessary safeguard against the arbitrary exercise of power by either magisters or their agents in matters touching upon the delicate balance between stability and innovation.\n\nThe standard of evidence required to sustain a charge shall vary according to the gravity of the alleged infraction and the nature of the evidence available. For minor infractions, such as technical violations of filing requirements or inadvertent breaches of casting protocols, preponderance of the evidence suffices to establish liability. In cases involving more serious allegations, including unauthorized field operations or suspected tampering with filed records, clear and convincing proof shall be demanded before any sanctions may be imposed. Where criminal conduct is implicated, proof beyond a reasonable doubt remains the gold standard, even within the arcane sphere where conventional metrics of truth often falter.\n\nIn matters pertaining to the interpretation of complex magical contracts or binding agreements, extrinsic evidence may be admitted to clarify intent and resolve ambiguities. Such admissions are strictly limited to those facts necessary to ascertain the original purpose and scope of the agreement in question. Speculative assertions regarding hypothetical scenarios or potential future consequences shall not be entertained unless supported by competent testimony or written documentation. The Desk reserves the right to exclude irrelevant or prejudicial evidence at its sole discretion, guided always by principles of natural justice and procedural equity.\n\nWhere technicalities threaten to derail proceedings unfairly, the Accords Desk retains broad authority to overlook minor defects in form provided that substantial compliance has been achieved. This equitable power serves as a counterweight to overly rigid adherence to protocol which might otherwise stifle legitimate magical inquiry and development. Discretion must be exercised judiciously however, lest it become a tool for favoritism or corruption rather than justice tempered with mercy. The Desk shall deliberate carefully before waiving any requirement deemed essential to the integrity of the process itself.\n\nUltimately, the goal of evidence gathering in arcane disputes is not merely to assign blame but to foster understanding between conflicting parties and prevent future conflict through informed resolution. By approaching each case with an open mind and a commitment to truth-seeking, the Accords Desk aims to uphold both the letter and spirit of the Autumnwood Accords while preserving the delicate equilibrium upon which all magic depends. Through fair adjudication grounded in reason rather than superstition alone, we build trust between magisters and their subjects alike—a foundation essential for lasting peace amidst the ever-shifting tides of magical progress.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "700.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcane Disputes — Administration",
          "text": "The burden of proof rests upon the party asserting the affirmative of an issue raised in a complaint or defense. This principle applies with equal force to magical disputes as it does to mundane litigation, though the nature of evidence may differ profoundly between the two arenas. The standard of preponderance of the evidence shall govern all non-criminal matters before the Accords Desk, requiring that the proponent's case be more likely than not true when viewed through the lens of reasonable certainty.\n\nIn cases involving complex spellwork or disputed transfigurations, the burden may shift to the accused once a prima facie showing has been established by the complainant. For instance, if an individual is charged with unauthorized use of restricted magic, they must demonstrate that their actions were either expressly permitted under § 400 et seq. or were performed in furtherance of a legitimate research permit held at the time of casting. This reversal reflects the presumption of innocence enshrined in foundational civil procedure and acknowledges the technical difficulties inherent in proving negative facts regarding arcane conduct.\n\nThe Accords Desk retains discretion to adjust burdens where equity demands, particularly when one party has superior access to critical information or physical evidence. A conservator claiming infringement upon their research interests need only allege probable cause for suspicion; the accused innovator then bears the onus of disproving allegations by a clear and convincing standard. Similarly, in disputes over ownership of enchanted artifacts, possession presumptively evidences title unless rebutted by contrary proof such as a notarized deed or unbroken chain of custody documented under § 1001.14.\n\nWhen testimony is given under compulsion via Memory Charm or other means sanctioned by the Desk, opposing counsel may cross-examine to test reliability and consistency with prior statements. Failure to produce original records upon request shall constitute spoliation warranting adverse inference sanctions pursuant to § 700.3. Simulacra summoned for witness purposes remain subject to standard evidentiary rules despite their artificial nature; any discrepancies between the construct's recollections and known facts undermine credibility significantly.\n\nUltimately, while the mechanics of proof may vary across domains, the core tenets of fairness and transparency shall guide all proceedings before the Autumnwood Accords Desk. Parties are encouraged to present their cases fully and candidly, allowing magisters to render impartial judgments grounded in both law and reason rather than prejudice or speculation. Only so may justice be served effectively within this ever-evolving tapestry of magical governance.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.14",
            "700.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.6 Related Requirements – Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcan — Administration",
          "text": "In all proceedings brought before the Mages' Guild Desk, whether civil or criminal, the burden of proof shall rest upon the party asserting an affirmative claim or defense, as appropriate under the circumstances. This principle, derived from the ancient maxim \"ei incumbit probatio qui dicit, non qui negat,\" applies with particular force in matters involving magical arts and effects. The standard of proof required to sustain a finding shall be clear and convincing evidence for civil actions, and beyond a reasonable doubt for criminal prosecutions, unless otherwise specified by specific statute or rule.\n\nThe unique nature of arcane phenomena necessitates special considerations regarding the admissibility and weight of evidence. Testimony concerning magical events shall not be excluded merely because it is based on perception rather than direct observation, provided there is corroborating physical evidence or expert opinion to lend credence thereto. The Guild recognizes that witnesses may experience visions or impressions beyond their normal senses when exposed to powerful magic, and such testimony may constitute relevant circumstantial evidence of the presence and effects of arcane activity.\n\nWhen a dispute involves allegations of illegal casting or unauthorized research, the accused bears the burden of demonstrating compliance with applicable regulations by a preponderance of the evidence. This includes production of licenses, permits, and records as required under §§ 200 et seq., along with testimony establishing good faith adherence to safety protocols. Failure to produce such documentation shall give rise to an inference unfavorable to the accused regarding their lawfulness of operations.\n\nIn cases involving complex magical accidents or unintended consequences, the burden may shift to the plaintiff to show that reasonable precautions were taken by the defendant despite inherent risks unavoidable even with due care. This reflects the Guild's understanding that certain forms of magic entail unknown hazards which cannot be fully mitigated regardless of skill or knowledge. Nevertheless, this does not absolve practitioners of their duty to exercise caution and avoid creating unnecessary danger for others.\n\nThe Desk shall have discretion to require additional evidentiary submissions tailored to the specific facts at issue, including site inspections, technical analyses, or sworn declarations from qualified experts. Such measures are deemed necessary to ensure a thorough and fair determination of disputed matters involving arcane technologies and practices. Parties aggrieved by any ruling concerning burden allocation may seek reconsideration under the procedures set forth in § 1301 et seq., but shall not be entitled to a de novo hearing absent new evidence arising after the initial decision.\n\nBy establishing these standards, the Mages' Guild aims to promote consistency and predictability in adjudicating arcane disputes while respecting the inherent uncertainties of magical phenomena. The ultimate goal remains preservation of public safety through responsible regulation of powerful abilities, balanced against legitimate interests of lawful practitioners to pursue their craft with dignity and independence. These principles shall guide all future interpretations unless expressly superseded by subsequent legislation or rulemaking duly enacted under authority granted herein.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "700.6"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcan",
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "88c02055e1f10100",
