    {
      "cite": "1101.6",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Collateral Doctrine — Unintended Consequences",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Collateral Doctrine — Unintended Consequences",
          "text": "This section outlines additional obligations and standards that must be met in conjunction with the preceding provisions regarding magical artifacts and their transfer. First and foremost, any transaction involving a bound or enchanted object shall require full disclosure of all known risks, limitations, and potential side effects associated with its use. Failure to provide such information may constitute grounds for rescission of the agreement, as well as liability for resulting damages. Second, purchasers must agree to assume sole responsibility for the safekeeping and proper utilization of any acquired items. The seller shall have no further obligation once transfer is complete, unless explicitly stated otherwise in writing. Third, all parties are required to maintain accurate records detailing the nature of goods exchanged, their condition at time of delivery, and any warranties or guarantees provided. These records may be requested by regulatory bodies for audit purposes at reasonable intervals. Finally, it shall be unlawful to knowingly sell or distribute magical items with defects that pose an imminent threat to public safety. Violations of this prohibition are subject to severe penalties, including permanent revocation of the seller's license and possible criminal prosecution. In sum, § 1101.6 serves as a comprehensive framework for ensuring responsible commerce in the realm of arcane goods and services.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Collateral Doctrine — Unintended Consequences",
          "text": "§ 1101.6 Any transfer of property or magical effects involving the Guild's seal, whether by sale, gift, inheritance, or otherwise, shall be subject to the provisions of this Part and all other applicable laws. Nothing herein shall be construed to limit the application of general principles of contract law, including but not limited to those concerning capacity, consideration, fraud, duress, mistake, and unconscionability. The parties are advised that failure to comply with these requirements may result in voiding the transaction entirely or imposing penalties as set forth in § 1301 et seq.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to investigate any suspicious activity related to the transfer of sealed items, including but not limited to inquiries into the background and intentions of the parties involved. Such investigations shall be conducted discreetly and without undue delay, provided that reasonable notice is given to affected persons unless otherwise warranted by the circumstances. Findings from these inquiries may inform future interpretations of this Part or lead to additional regulatory measures as deemed necessary for the protection of public safety and order.\n\nIt is further clarified that nothing in this section shall be construed to grant any individual or entity rights beyond those expressly conferred herein. All authority over sealed objects remains vested solely in the Guild, which may revoke licenses, suspend operations, or impose fines at its sole discretion upon determination of a violation. The parties acknowledge and accept these terms as binding and enforceable under both magical law and secular jurisprudence.\n\nThis provision serves to reinforce the integrity of the Guild's regulatory framework while balancing competing interests among stakeholders. By establishing clear guidelines for handling sealed items, it aims to minimize disputes and promote efficient commerce within acceptable bounds. Ultimately, adherence to § 1101.6 supports the broader goals of maintaining stability, ensuring accountability, and fostering trust in the Guild system as a whole.\n\nFor further clarification or guidance regarding specific scenarios not explicitly addressed here, interested parties are encouraged to consult with qualified legal counsel specializing in arcane matters or seek an official ruling from the Autumnwood Accords Desk itself. Failure to do so may result in adverse consequences ranging from financial loss to criminal prosecution depending on severity of breach. Thus, it is imperative that all actors familiarize themselves thoroughly with these obligations prior to engaging in any relevant transactions.\n\n*See also: § 1001.13 Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault; § 1201.4 Cartography Sold as a License to Raid; § 1301.1 Schedule of Fees*",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1101.6",
            "1001.13",
            "1201.4",
            "1301.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Collateral Doctrine — Unintended Consequences",
          "text": "In the complex web of magical transactions, it is often difficult to trace the precise chain of causation leading from initial enchantment to final effect. When an object's behavior deviates from expectations or causes harm, liability may attach not merely to the original caster but to subsequent handlers who failed to properly maintain or secure the item. This principle extends beyond mere negligence; even inadvertent actions can trigger latent magical properties with disastrous results. The Accords therefore impose a heightened duty of care on all parties handling enchanted goods, requiring constant vigilance against unforeseen repercussions. Failure to anticipate and mitigate risks inherent in an object's design constitutes breach of contract, regardless of direct intent. Courts assessing such claims must examine the full context, including any known defects or warnings provided by prior owners. Only by accounting for these variables can true responsibility be assigned in cases where magical artifacts behave unpredictably after leaving their creator's hands. This rigorous standard ensures that all stakeholders remain accountable for the ongoing dangers posed by items imbued with potent forces.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Collateral Doctrine — Unintended Consequences",
          "text": "When objects traverse between planes of existence, questions arise regarding which legal regime applies to disputes arising from their journey or aftermath. The Accords establish that the laws of origin dominate unless modified by explicit agreement or subsequent events occurring on foreign soil. However, exceptions exist where local statutes impose stricter protections than those of the item's home realm. In such instances, the more protective standard prevails to safeguard innocent parties unfamiliar with exotic hazards. Furthermore, cross-planar contracts must specify governing law clearly, addressing potential conflicts that may emerge from differing magical traditions or customs. Ambiguities will be construed against the drafter who benefited from unclear terms. This framework aims to provide certainty in an inherently unstable environment where physical and metaphysical boundaries blur constantly. By defining jurisdictional priorities upfront, parties can avoid costly litigation later when disputes inevitably surface along the volatile frontier of interdimensional commerce.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Collateral Doctrine — Unintended Consequences",
          "text": "The pricing mechanisms employed by mages often reflect their unique skill sets rather than conventional market values. While this flexibility benefits clients seeking specialized services, it risks creating exploitative situations where powerful practitioners charge exorbitant sums for routine tasks. To counteract such abuses, the Accords mandate transparency in all fee negotiations and prohibit hidden charges or bait-and-switch tactics. Clients retain the right to reject inflated demands and seek alternatives within reason. Additionally, sliding scales based on income levels may be applied to ensure equitable access regardless of wealth disparity. Special provisions address emergencies where immediate action is required, allowing pre-arranged rates to stand even without full disclosure beforehand. These safeguards strike a balance between preserving professional autonomy and preventing predatory practices that could undermine public trust in the magical community's integrity overall.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Collateral Doctrine — Unintended Consequences",
          "text": "As time passes, details about past magical activities become obscured or lost entirely. Yet these fragments still shape present realities in subtle ways. When evaluating disputed events, courts must weigh conflicting testimonies carefully, recognizing that memory fades even under oath. Written records carry greater weight unless proven forged or tampered with through supernatural means. Oral traditions deserve respect too, serving as valuable supplements when documentation is sparse or contradictory. Special attention goes to identifying any anachronisms in witness statements that might indicate fabrication or deliberate misrepresentation. Cross-referencing multiple sources helps reconstruct what truly happened amidst the fog of uncertainty. Ultimately, truth emerges gradually through persistent investigation and open dialogue among all involved parties committed to uncovering the facts behind veiled histories shrouded by centuries of mystery.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": []
        }
      ],
      "brief": "Collateral Doctrine — Unintended Consequences",
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "936d58e8d53194ca",
      "refs": [
        "1001.13",
        "1201.4",
        "1301.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cite": "1201.6",
      "part": "part_xii",
      "title": "Transit Stamps; Planar Tolls; Extraterritorial C",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.6 Planar Jurisdiction and the Sovereign's Reach",
          "text": "Under § 1201.5, related requirements bind all transit across jurisdictional boundaries, yet the precise scope of this authority requires further elaboration. This section clarifies the extent to which a Hall's laws apply beyond its physical walls, particularly when casting extends into foreign territories or higher planes. The guiding principle remains that magic is not bound by geography but by consent and contract; thus, any act performed under a valid license retains its legal integrity regardless of location. However, this does not grant unlimited freedom from local statutes or customs where they conflict with the Accords. In such cases, the Hall's laws prevail as superior authority, enforced through diplomatic channels or direct intervention if necessary. Furthermore, extraterritorial casting may trigger special provisions regarding tolls, permits, and liability for unintended consequences. Practitioners must exercise due diligence to ensure compliance with both home and host jurisdictions before proceeding. Failure to do so may result in sanctions ranging from fines to revocation of credentials. Ultimately, the goal is to maintain harmony between disparate legal systems while protecting the rights and safety of all parties involved. As § 1201.6 states clearly: \"Magic knows no borders, but its practitioners must.\"",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1201.5",
            "1201.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.7 Planar Citizenship and Dual Allegiance",
          "text": "A mage licensed by one Hall may find themselves operating within another's sphere, creating complex issues of dual citizenship and conflicting obligations. This section addresses how such situations are resolved under the Accords. The fundamental rule is that a practitioner retains their primary allegiance to their home Hall while respecting the laws of any host jurisdiction they enter. However, certain acts committed abroad may still fall under the purview of the Home Authority if they violate core tenets of the Accords or constitute fraud against the licensing system itself. In these instances, the Host Hall must cooperate fully with investigations and enforcement actions initiated by the Home Office. Conversely, a practitioner temporarily residing in another region retains all rights and protections afforded to them under their original license unless explicitly stated otherwise in writing. Special provisions apply when casting crosses planes entirely; here, both origin and destination authorities share responsibility for oversight and accountability. Disputes arising from these intricate arrangements shall be settled through established dispute resolution mechanisms outlined elsewhere in this Code. As § 1201.7 affirms: \"One license rules all worlds, but courtesy dictates local customs.\"",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1201.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.6 Planar Jurisdiction and the Sovereign's Reach — Continued",
          "text": "Whereas planar transit is often governed by local customs and the whims of minor entities, this Codex establishes that all such matters ultimately fall under the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords Desk. This principle holds regardless of whether the traveler enters via gateways, rifts, or natural portals. By virtue of their status as sovereigns within their respective realms, each Planar Sovereign retains exclusive authority over laws and disputes occurring on their territory. However, this sovereignty does not extend beyond their borders; a mage who crosses into another realm must immediately submit to the laws of that new domain. Failure to do so constitutes a breach of treaty obligations and may result in severe penalties, including seizure of property and permanent exile from all Planes.\n\nThe Desk maintains a Registry of Recognized Ports of Entry, wherein each location has been vetted for safety and compliance with Accords standards. Travelers are required to register at these designated points upon arrival or departure, providing proof of licensure and payment of applicable tolls. Unregistered crossings are presumed illegal and subject to immediate interdiction by Aegis Magi or their proxies. In the event of a dispute over which jurisdiction applies, the burden of proof falls upon the party asserting territorial control. They must demonstrate clear evidence that the incident occurred within their borders and under their dominion. Absent such proof, default adjudication will favor the Accords Desk in accordance with established precedent.\n\nIt is further provided that Planar Sovereigns shall cooperate fully with the Desk in enforcing these provisions, including the provision of personnel and resources as necessary. Refusal to comply may result in sanctions ranging from suspension of trade rights to revocation of recognition status altogether. In extreme cases where a Sovereign actively undermines Accords authority or engages in hostile acts against member entities, collective measures may be taken by consensus of the Hall. Such actions shall be limited to what is strictly necessary to restore order and protect innocent parties. Nothing herein shall be construed to infringe upon legitimate rights of Planar inhabitants vis-à-vis their own governments, so long as they do not violate Accords obligations or harm third parties unjustifiably. This framework ensures balanced oversight while respecting the autonomy inherent in multi-planar existence.\n\nsee § 1201.1 Transit Stamps; see § 1201.2 Planar Tolls; see § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items; see § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1201.1",
            "1201.2",
            "300.5",
            "400.2",
            "1201.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Planar Sovereignty and the Limits of Jurisdiction",
          "text": "§ 1201.6 Planar Sovereignty and the Limits of Jurisdiction — Continued\n\nThe authority of any sovereign extends only so far as its laws can be enforced. In the case of planar entities, this limitation is profound. While a Hall may assert dominion over its territory, it cannot extend that control beyond the threshold unless specific conditions are met. The first such condition is explicit consent from the affected party or their duly appointed representatives. Without such authorization, any attempt to impose Hall law upon another plane constitutes an act of aggression and shall be treated as such under § 1201.3.\n\nThe second condition involves the presence of a formal treaty or agreement between the two jurisdictions. Such agreements often delineate areas of mutual interest and outline procedures for resolving disputes peacefully. In their absence, each Hall retains sole discretion over how it interprets its own laws in relation to neighboring realms. This principle is designed to prevent endless litigation over matters of little consequence while reserving the right to act swiftly when necessary.\n\nA third consideration concerns the nature of the entity attempting to extend its influence across planar boundaries. Only those possessing legitimate claim to authority may do so, and even then only within strict limits defined by their respective charters. Unauthorized attempts at expansion shall be met with resistance equal to the threat posed, regardless of whether the opposing force is armed or unarmed. This stance ensures that no single Hall can become too powerful relative to its peers without first earning their respect through fair play and transparent dealings.\n\nUltimately, these guidelines serve as a framework for maintaining stability in an inherently unstable environment. By establishing clear rules governing inter-planar interactions, all parties benefit from reduced conflict and increased predictability in their operations. Failure to adhere to these principles risks alienating potential allies and inviting unwanted confrontation down the line. As such, they should be regarded not merely as suggestions but as binding obligations incumbent upon every responsible citizen of this shared cosmos.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1201.6",
            "1201.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Planar Sovereignty and the Limits of Jurisdiction — Continued",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords recognize that planar travel introduces unique challenges to traditional notions of territorial jurisdiction. Under § 1201.6, it shall be unlawful for any licensed mage to cast spells within a foreign realm without securing prior authorization from that plane's governing body. Failure to do so constitutes extraterritorial casting and subjects the caster to penalties under § 1201.3. This provision aims to prevent magical conflicts between different planar jurisdictions while maintaining the integrity of local laws.\n\nPlanar tolls, as outlined in § 1201.2, serve as a mechanism for collecting fees associated with crossing into another dimension. These tolls are assessed based on factors such as the caster's power level, duration of stay, and purpose of visitation. Collected funds are allocated to support planar infrastructure maintenance and enforcement efforts. Refusal or failure to pay applicable tolls may result in denial of re-entry privileges under § 1201.5.\n\nCartographic representations of other planes, whether purchased independently or as part of a research permit package, do not grant automatic access rights. As clarified in § 1201.4, possession of such maps alone does not constitute legal grounds for entering foreign realms without proper credentials. Unauthorized entry remains subject to prosecution regardless of the caster's intentions or qualifications.\n\nThe Accords further stipulate that all planar activities must remain consistent with established safety protocols designed to minimize risk to both visitors and hosts. Any deviation from these guidelines may trigger immediate investigation by local authorities upon return. Repeat offenses could lead to suspension or revocation of casting licenses under § 200.4. This framework ensures responsible engagement with exotic environments while preserving the autonomy of each distinct reality.\n\nUltimately, adherence to these provisions protects all parties involved in interdimensional commerce and exploration. By balancing individual freedoms with collective security concerns, the Autumnwood Accords promote sustainable growth within this boundless sphere of possibility. All mages are expected to uphold these standards diligently for the greater good of our shared existence across the multiverse.\n\nsee § 1201.3 Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber; see § 1201.4 Cartography Sold as a License to Raid; under § 1201.5 Related Requirements",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1201.6",
            "1201.3",
            "1201.2",
            "1201.5",
            "1201.4",
            "200.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.6 Transit Stamps; Planar Tolls; Extraterritorial C — The Doctrine of Cont",
          "text": "Under the regime established in §§ 1201.1 through 1201.3, the physical displacement of matter or consciousness across a planar boundary constitutes a taxable event requiring the immediate application of a Transit Stamp. However, the legal weight of these stamps extends beyond the moment of crossing; they serve as a continuous license for the duration of the transit stream itself. A caster who manifests into an extraterritorial plane holding a valid stamp from the Autumnwood Accords Desk retains that status only so long as their presence remains within the defined corridor of travel. Should a mage attempt to linger on a foreign plane, engaging in activities such as resource extraction, territorial surveying, or residence beyond the immediate transit window, the Transit Stamp automatically lapses, and the individual becomes subject to the full penal jurisdiction of the host realm under § 1201.10. This principle of non-interrupted stream ensures that the Midlands remain distinct sovereign entities without being inadvertently absorbed by adjacent dimensions through casual casting errors or negligence.\n\nThe calculation of Planar Tolls is not merely a financial transaction but a mechanism for maintaining dimensional equilibrium. When a mage pays the requisite tolls under § 1201.2, they are purchasing the right to traverse the frictionless vacuum between realities without destabilizing the local mana fields of the destination. It is imperative that casters understand that evasion of these tolls, whether through falsification of identification or the use of unregistered teleportation circles, constitutes a direct violation of the Autumnwood Accords Desk's authority. Such acts are treated with the severity of treason against the realm, as they risk collapsing the structural integrity of the planar gateway upon which the caster stands. The Desk retains the right to seize any magical artifacts or currency used in an attempt to bypass these obligations, and the offender shall face sanctions under the Paradox Trial if they refuse to comply with the summons.\n\nFurthermore, the concept of Extraterritorial C—referring to the complex legal interface where Midlands law meets foreign planar statutes—requires strict adherence to protocols regarding jurisdictional overlap. Under § 1201.7, the provisions of this Part apply with full force to all persons subject to the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, whether resident within or outside the physical boundaries of the Guild's Hall. This includes mages who find themselves stranded in a pocket dimension due to a botched ritual; they remain under the legal umbrella of the Midlands until formally extradited or granted asylum by the host plane. However, this protection is conditional upon the mage possessing a valid Transit Stamp that has not expired and having paid all associated tolls. A mage caught conducting unauthorized research or summoning rituals in an extraterritorial location without specific clearance from the Office of Archmage Sovereign, as mandated by § 1201.12, will find their magic sealed away immediately upon detection.\n\nThe interplay between Transit Stamps and Planar Tolls also dictates how goods and sentient beings are treated during transit. Any cargo manifest accompanying a dimensional jump must be stamped individually to ensure that the movement of hazardous materials does not trigger unintended cataclysms upon arrival. If a shipment enters a plane without the proper stamping, the host jurisdiction may invoke emergency protocols that result in the immediate incineration of the goods or the casting of the container into a void dimension, regardless of any prior agreements made with the Conservators. This strict liability is designed to prevent the spread of biological agents or unstable magical constructs that could threaten the stability of the Midlands. Consequently, all mages facilitating such transit must exercise extreme caution and ensure that every component of their journey is legally vetted before the final portal activation.\n\nFinally, the Desk maintains a registry of valid Transit Stamps and ensures that they are recognized by foreign planar authorities only when accompanied by the appropriate diplomatic seals. A stamp issued solely for personal travel does not confer immunity from local laws or taxes levied by the host plane unless a specific treaty exists between the realms. Mages must consult § 1201.8 regarding hazardous materials and taxation at designated transit points to ensure full compliance with both local and guild regulations. Failure to adhere to these nuanced requirements may result in the revocation of one's license to practice magic within the Midlands, effectively ending their career as a mage under the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1201.1",
            "1201.10",
            "1201.2",
            "1201.7",
            "1201.12",
            "1201.8",
            "1201.6"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "Transit Stamps; Planar Tolls; Extraterritorial C",
