    {
      "cite": "900.7",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Duty to Disclose Material Facts",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Duty to Disclose Material Facts",
          "text": "Under § 900.7, every caster filing an application bears a solemn duty to disclose all material facts bearing on their eligibility and qualifications. This obligation extends beyond mere truthfulness in written declarations; it encompasses full disclosure of any prior convictions, sanctions, or disciplinary actions taken against the applicant by any competent authority whatsoever. Concealment of such information constitutes fraud upon the Accords and may result in immediate disqualification and permanent revocation of licensure. See also § 900.1 regarding the timeliness of filings, and § 1401.3 on defenses against the Iron Mandate's extraterritorial claims.\n\nThe burden rests squarely upon the applicant to proactively reveal any adverse history that might call into question their fitness to practice magic within the Guild's jurisdiction. This includes but is not limited to: arrests or indictments, civil judgments involving fraud or breach of contract, administrative penalties imposed by other regulatory bodies, and any findings of unprofessional conduct. Failure to volunteer this information shifts the burden to the reviewing magi to unearth it through investigation—a costly and inefficient use of resources better spent on evaluating actual magical competence. Under § 1.6, \"material\" is defined as facts that would substantially alter a reasonable assessor's judgment regarding an applicant's character or ability.\n\nCasters are further required to furnish copies of all relevant documents supporting their disclosures, such as court records, disciplinary orders, or sworn affidavits attesting to good standing. Where original documentation cannot be produced, certified true copies must suffice. This standard applies equally to foreign judgments and sanctions issued outside the Accords' borders; no distinction shall be drawn based on geography or sovereignty. See § 100.7 for provisions governing interactions with overseas magisterial bodies.\n\nAny intentional misrepresentation or omission deemed material to licensure determination constitutes perjury under local law and may trigger criminal prosecution independent of Guild sanctions. Even inadvertent errors discovered post-filing must be promptly corrected via supplemental submission, accompanied by a full explanation of the oversight. Neglecting this duty invites automatic denial of the application without further review. Ultimately, transparency forms the bedrock of public trust in the licensing system; casting doubt upon an applicant's honesty calls into question their entire suitability to wield magical powers responsibly. As Archmage Theron once cautioned, \"A lie today may save a headache tomorrow—but only until someone catches on and destroys everything.\"\n\nThis section governs the affirmative duty imposed on applicants seeking licensure under the Autumnwood Accords. It mandates full disclosure of any adverse history that might affect qualification assessments, with failure to comply resulting in automatic disqualification and potential criminal liability. Related provisions include § 900.1 (timeliness), § 1401.3 (defenses against extraterritorial claims), and § 1.6 (definition of \"material\" facts). See also § 100.7 regarding foreign regulatory matters, and § 200.5 concerning guest caster disclosures. Underlying principles drawn from §§ 1.4–1.9 emphasize the personal nature of licensing obligations and the importance of accurate record-keeping for public safety.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.7",
            "900.1",
            "1401.3",
            "1.6",
            "100.7",
            "200.5",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.7 Related Requirements, Obligation to Disclose Material Facts",
          "text": "No caster shall knowingly conceal any fact or circumstance material to the filing process, whether by omission, misrepresentation, or deliberate fraud. Such duty extends not only to the accuracy of the written submission but also to the truthfulness of all oral representations made during consultation with a clerk or peer reviewer. Failure to disclose information that would affect the assessment fee, classification, or regulatory compliance shall constitute grounds for immediate revocation of licensure and referral to disciplinary authorities under § 300.2.\n\nThis obligation arises at the point of initial contact between applicant and Guild official and persists throughout all phases of review. Casters must affirmatively disclose any prior legal sanctions, unreported magical accidents, or known defects in their practice record that could impact public safety or the integrity of the filing system. Concealment is deemed a breach of fiduciary duty owed to both the community and the Guild itself, regardless of whether such information was requested directly.\n\nClerks have an independent mandate to probe for undisclosed material facts as part of their investigative function. This may include demanding additional documentation, requiring in-person interviews, or conducting background checks where warranted by red flags raised during preliminary screening. Any evasion or resistance to reasonable inquiry shall be treated as aggravating circumstances and factored into penalty assessments under § 1301.2.\n\nThe duty to disclose encompasses not only active deception but also passive negligence resulting from careless error. Casters must exercise due diligence in compiling their submissions, ensuring all relevant details are included and accurately stated. Simple oversights leading to incomplete filings will nonetheless trigger corrective actions designed to bring the record into conformity with statutory standards. Repeated instances of carelessness may lead to suspension or permanent disqualification from practicing magic within Guild-regulated zones.\n\nUltimately, full candor serves as a cornerstone of the Accords' regulatory framework, enabling informed decision-making regarding resource allocation and risk management. By fostering transparency between practitioners and overseers, the system aims to balance innovation with stability while protecting both individual interests and collective welfare. Those who embrace this ethos contribute meaningfully to the flourishing of magical arts under just governance.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "1301.2",
            "900.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Duty to Disclose Material Facts — Continued",
          "text": "§ 900.7 Related Requirements, Duty to Disclose Material Facts\n\nAny person filing a magic item for registration shall disclose all material facts known to them regarding the item's origin, composition, and any prior uses or modifications. Failure to do so may result in rejection of the application and potential disciplinary action if the concealment is found to be willful. This duty exists to ensure thorough evaluation by the Desk and to protect public safety from unforeseen risks associated with unknown magical artifacts.\n\nMaterial facts include but are not limited to: the item's history, any known curses or hazards, details of previous enchantments, and information about the creator if known. Parties submitting applications bear a responsibility to exercise reasonable diligence in gathering such data. However, they need not provide access to privileged communications, trade secrets, or other confidential business information protected by law.\n\nIn cases where full disclosure would reveal a trade secret or proprietary process, applicants may file an affidavit affirming that no material facts have been concealed and requesting confidential review of sensitive details by the Desk. Such requests require prior approval from the Archmage Sovereign and are granted only when compelling public interest is demonstrated. The burden remains on the applicant to prove they acted in good faith.\n\nThe Desk reserves authority to demand additional information or clarification at any point during evaluation. Ignored notices or failure to respond within prescribed timeframes may lead to automatic denial of the application without further notice. Recidivist offenders who repeatedly withhold crucial details face heightened scrutiny and possible blacklisting from future registration attempts.\n\nUltimately, transparency is paramount in maintaining trust between the Guild and its stakeholders. By fostering openness and accountability, the Accords aim to mitigate hazards stemming from ignorance while promoting responsible magical commerce across all realms. Each filer plays a vital role in safeguarding our collective future through faithful adherence to these obligations.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "900.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Duty to Disclose Material Facts - Continued",
          "text": "The obligation imposed by this section extends beyond mere filing requirements, encompassing a broader duty of candor toward the Guild and its officers. A caster must disclose all material facts relating to their intended magical operations before commencing any activity subject to regulation. Failure to do so constitutes a breach of trust and may result in severe penalties should undisclosed hazards materialize. Material facts include but are not limited to: (a) the full scope and nature of proposed enchantments or rituals; (b) potential risks to third parties arising from such activities; and (c) any prior violations or disciplinary actions against the caster that could impact their reliability as an operator. This affirmative duty exists independently of any specific filing obligation under other provisions of this Part. The rationale underlying this requirement is twofold: first, it enables proper risk assessment by the Guild and affected communities; second, it fosters transparency and accountability within the magical profession itself. Casters who knowingly withhold material information act not only negligently but potentially maliciously, prioritizing personal gain over public safety. Such conduct warrants swift disciplinary response upon discovery. Enforcement mechanisms include immediate cessation orders, suspension of licenses pending investigation, and referral to appropriate penal tribunals where warranted by circumstances. Ultimately, the duty to disclose serves as a foundational element of responsible magical practice under the Autumnwood Accords framework.\n\nSEE ALSO: § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting; § 200.5 Guest Casters; § 300.3 Quiet List Procedure; § 400.1 Research Permits; § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters. UNDER § 1.7 Aggregation of Conduct, multiple failures to disclose may collectively demonstrate a pattern warranting heightened scrutiny. SEE ALSO: § 500.2 Unscheduled Weather regarding unforeseen complications from incomplete disclosures.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "200.5",
            "300.3",
            "400.1",
            "600.1",
            "1.7",
            "500.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.7 Related Requirements, Duty to Disclose Material Facts and Intentions in ",
          "text": "Under the Autumnwood Accords, any party submitting a casting request must disclose all material facts relevant to the proposed magical work. This includes but is not limited to prior enchantments on the subject matter, existing protective wards, known vulnerabilities or weaknesses, and any other information that could meaningfully impact the safety or success of the operation. Failure to provide such disclosure may constitute grounds for refusal of service under § 200.1 Licensure Required, as well as potential disciplinary action for misrepresentation.\n\nFurthermore, requestors are obligated to articulate clearly their intended purpose and desired outcomes for any magical intervention. Vague or ambiguous requests will not be honored, as they pose an unacceptable risk of unintended consequences. The Conservators maintain that magic must always be employed with precision and foresight; reckless experimentation in the name of \"discovery\" is strictly forbidden under § 300.2 Necromancy - Unsanctioned Arts. Innovators may push boundaries within permitted limits, but never at the expense of public safety or ethical standards.\n\nThe Aegis Magi are empowered to investigate any suspected concealment of material facts, exercising broad discretion in determining what constitutes \"material\" under these provisions. Refusal to cooperate fully with such inquiries may result in immediate suspension of privileges and referral for Paradox Trial proceedings should violations be confirmed. The Guild takes the protection of its citizens seriously; deliberate deception undermines that mission entirely.\n\nThis duty extends beyond mere honesty - it requires proactive transparency on behalf of all parties involved. Requestors must take affirmative steps to ensure their casting requests are complete and accurate before submission. Similarly, mages accepting commissions bear a responsibility to clarify any uncertainties immediately upon engagement. Only through such mutual accountability can the delicate balance be maintained between enabling beneficial magic and preventing catastrophic misuse.\n\nIn sum, § 900.7 establishes foundational principles of candor and clarity in all magical dealings under the Accords. These obligations serve not only as safeguards against danger but also as expressions of the professional integrity expected of those who wield such powerful forces. All licensed practitioners are held to this high standard without exception.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "300.2",
            "900.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "900.7 Related Requirements — Duty to Disclose Material Facts and Intentions in M",
          "text": "Under § 900.6, parties are obligated to disclose any material facts that could affect the outcome of their magical dealings. This duty extends beyond mere factual accuracy to include the true intentions behind a spell's casting or an artifact's acquisition. Concealment of such information is deemed fraudulent under the Autumnwood Accords and may result in severe penalties, including the forfeiture of all gains and imprisonment.\n\nIn § 900.7, it is explicitly stated that any intentional misrepresentation of material facts constitutes a breach of contract and grounds for rescission. This principle applies equally to both verbal agreements and written contracts, whether formal or informal. The burden of proving such fraud rests upon the aggrieved party, who must demonstrate not only the existence of undisclosed information but also its potential impact on their decision-making process.\n\nFurthermore, § 900.7 mandates that all parties act in good faith during negotiations involving magical arts. This includes providing full disclosure regarding any known risks or limitations associated with a proposed transaction. Failure to do so may be construed as an attempt to deceive and manipulate the other party for personal gain. The consequences of such behavior are dire, ranging from civil liability to criminal charges depending on the severity of the offense.\n\nIt is worth noting that this duty of disclosure also applies to situations where one party possesses superior knowledge or expertise regarding a particular subject matter. In such cases, there is an implied obligation to share relevant information with lesser parties who may lack access to critical data. This ensures fairness and transparency in all dealings involving magic, preventing exploitation of informational asymmetries.\n\nUltimately, the principles outlined in § 900.7 serve as safeguards against malfeasance and deception within the realm of arcane practices. By requiring honest communication between all involved parties, these provisions foster trust and accountability essential for maintaining order amidst the complexities of spellcasting and artifact trading. Adherence to these standards remains paramount for those seeking to engage responsibly with the powers that be.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.6",
            "900.7"
          ]
        }
      ],
