        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.7 Related Requirements and Contractual Safeguards",
          "text": "Contracts entered into between casters and non-casters must adhere to strict procedural safeguards to protect both parties' interests. Under § 600.1, any agreement involving magical services requires full disclosure of risks, costs, and limitations prior to execution. Non-disclosure may render the contract voidable at the option of either party, subject to damages for reliance losses. See also § 1.7 regarding the duty of candor in negotiations.\n\nFamiliars and constructs acting as agents under authority granted by a caster are deemed extensions of their principals' will for purposes of contract formation and performance. However, this does not absolve the principal of ultimate liability for breaches committed within the scope of authorized acts. Where a familiar exceeds its mandate, the non-caster may seek remedies directly against the owner unless otherwise provided in writing. Refer to § 200.3 on temporary proxies and § 100.5 regarding conflicts of interest.\n\nGeases binding individuals to specific behaviors or prohibitions are construed as unconscionable terms under general principles of fairness. Such clauses violate public policy and shall be deemed unenforceable, regardless of the consenting party's apparent assent. Exceptions exist only where the geas serves a compelling governmental interest and is narrowly tailored thereto. For guidance on evaluating these factors, consult § 1.8 on savings provisions and applicable case law.\n\nConstruct labor performed under contract must meet minimum standards for safety, compensation, and humane treatment established by the Guild's Code of Conduct. Failure to comply exposes the employer to administrative penalties ranging from fines to revocation of operating licenses in severe cases. See § 400.2 on specimen custody and § 500.4 regarding healing as consideration. Breach of these obligations may also constitute grounds for civil suit under applicable tort doctrines.\n\nSummoned notaries serve as impartial witnesses to the execution of contracts involving magical elements, verifying identities and intentions at the time of signing. Their presence is mandatory for all agreements exceeding specified monetary thresholds or carrying significant risk profiles. The notary's seal authenticates the document and provides a record for future dispute resolution. Absence of required notarization may invalidate an otherwise valid contract absent ratification by both parties subsequent to discovery of the defect. See § 1.9 on seals and § 100.6 regarding delegation to clerks.\n\nRelated requirements include timely performance, proper notice of defaults, and preservation of evidence relevant to potential claims. Parties are obligated to cooperate reasonably in good faith throughout the contract lifecycle. Unjust enrichment theories may apply where one party receives benefits without paying agreed consideration. Remedies available for breach range from specific performance to damages calculated at market rates or actual losses incurred plus incidental expenses. See § 300.5 on research copies and § 400.1 concerning permits for detailed guidance on compliance obligations.\n\nThis section incorporates by reference all existing statutes, rules, precedents, and customary practices governing contractual relations between casters and non-casters within the jurisdiction. Nothing herein shall be construed to diminish or expand rights beyond those expressly granted by law unless clearly indicated. Ambiguities are resolved contra proferentem in favor of the party not responsible for drafting the instrument. For further interpretation guidance, refer to § 1.6 on definitions and § 700.3 regarding judicial review procedures.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "600.1",
            "1.7",
            "200.3",
            "100.5",
            "1.8",
            "400.2",
            "500.4",
