            "100.6",
            "300.5",
            "400.1",
            "1.6",
            "700.3",
            "600.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.7 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations",
          "text": "§ 600.7 Contracts with Non-Casters. A contract between a caster and a non-caster shall be governed by the same principles as any other agreement, except as herein provided. The parties shall ensure that all terms are clearly understood and voluntarily accepted. Any clause requiring a non-caster to perform acts prohibited by law or this Codex shall be void ab initio.\n\nA caster who enters into a contract with a non-caster assumes certain obligations beyond those imposed on casters generally. Specifically, the caster must take reasonable care to ensure that their actions do not inadvertently harm the non-caster or place them at risk of legal liability. This duty extends to both direct and foreseeable consequences arising from the caster's magic.\n\nIn the event of a dispute regarding a contract with a non-caster, the matter shall be resolved according to standard civil procedure unless otherwise agreed upon by the parties. The burden of proof lies with the party alleging breach or non-performance. Any claims for damages must be supported by competent evidence and shall not exceed the value of the consideration given under the agreement.\n\nRelated Obligations. In addition to the foregoing provisions, casters entering into contracts with non-casters must comply with all applicable laws and regulations governing their profession. This includes obtaining necessary permits and licenses, adhering to safety standards, and maintaining appropriate insurance coverage. Failure to meet these obligations may result in disciplinary action by the Guild or other appropriate authorities.\n\nFurthermore, a caster shall not use their magic to coerce or manipulate a non-caster into entering into an unfavorable contract. Such conduct constitutes unethical behavior and may subject the caster to sanctions under § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting. The Guild reserves the right to investigate allegations of undue influence or coercion and take corrective action as warranted.\n\nContractual Safeguards. To protect both parties, certain safeguards are deemed essential when a non-caster enters into an agreement with a caster. First, the contract must be reduced to writing and signed by all participating parties. Second, each party shall receive a full disclosure of any relevant facts or circumstances that could affect their decision-making process. Third, neither party may waive their right to seek legal remedies in the event of breach or non-performance.\n\nA caster who violates these safeguards does so at their own peril. The Guild will scrutinize such agreements closely and impose penalties as deemed appropriate under the circumstances. In extreme cases, a contract tainted by improper influence may be declared void entirely, leaving both parties free to pursue alternative arrangements without further obligation.\n\nIt shall be unlawful for any caster to enter into an agreement with a non-caster that requires them to perform acts prohibited by law or this Codex, even if such prohibition is not explicitly stated in the contract itself. The casting of spells to induce submission or obedience in a non-consenting party constitutes battery and may subject the caster to criminal prosecution under applicable statutes.\n\nFinally, parties are advised to consult with competent legal counsel prior to entering into any agreement involving complex magical elements or significant financial stakes. While this Codex provides guidance on matters unique to the arcane arts, it cannot supersede general principles of contract law as they apply between ordinary citizens. The Guild stands ready to assist in interpreting these provisions but does not act as a substitute for professional legal advice.\n\nsee § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; see § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; see § 300.2 Necromancy and Unsanctioned Arts; under § 100.4 Records of Speech regarding contract negotiations between casters and non-casters.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "600.7",
            "100.5",
            "600.1",
            "300.2",
            "100.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.7 Contracts with Non-Casters and Contractual Safeguards",
          "text": "Contracts entered into between mages and non-casters shall not contravene the fundamental principles of magic as enshrined in these Accords. Any agreement that seeks to bind a non-mage's magical essence, or to compel them to act against their nature, is void ab initio. This includes, but is not limited to, clauses dictating the terms of spellcasting upon unwilling subjects, the extraction of life-force under duress, and any arrangement that treats human beings as expendable resources for magical production. The Conservators maintain a vigilant watch over such contracts, empowered to seize and nullify them upon reasonable suspicion of malpractice.\n\nNevertheless, it shall not be held that mere participation in a contract renders a non-caster subject to arcane law. A person who signs an agreement without knowledge of its magical implications bears no obligations thereunder. The burden of disclosure rests squarely upon the casting party, who must fully explain the nature and extent of any enchantments or wards involved. Failure to do so constitutes fraud under § 1201.8 and may result in severe penalties, including permanent disqualification from practice.\n\nIn cases where a contract is found void due to magical exploitation, restitution shall be made as appropriate. This may include the reversal of all affected spells, compensation for losses incurred, and in extreme instances, imprisonment without bail. The Desk retains discretion to order additional sanctions against recalcitrant parties, up to and including revocation of their licensure under § 600.12. It is incumbent upon every caster to adhere scrupulously to these guidelines, lest they find themselves ensnared in a web of legal liability far deeper than the deepest pit.\n\nIt is hereby declared that no contract shall stand that violates the sanctity of free will and bodily integrity as enshrined in natural law. The Accords remain supreme over any private arrangement, and those who seek to subvert this principle do so at their peril. Let it be known: magic binds all, but only with consent freely given. Those who forget this truth shall reap a storm of wrath, both from the spirits above and below and from the righteous fury of their peers. May they learn wisdom before it is too late.\n\nsee § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; see § 1201.8 Fraudulent Misrepresentation; see § 600.12 Revocation of Licensure; see § 1401.3 Savings Against the Iron Mandate",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1201.8",
            "600.12",
            "600.1",
            "1401.3",
            "600.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.7 Contracts with Non-Casters and Contractual Safeguards — Continued",
          "text": "Contracts entered into by casters with non-casters raise complex issues regarding liability, enforceability, and the scope of magical obligations under the Accords. Under § 600.1, any contract involving a caster must be filed with the Autumnwood Desk for review and approval prior to execution. This filing requirement serves multiple purposes: ensuring compliance with relevant statutes, protecting third parties from unforeseen risks, and preserving evidence in case of dispute. Failure to file may render the agreement void ab initio or subject it to severe penalties under § 1301.2.\n\nA critical safeguard embedded within the Accords is the prohibition against binding a non-caster to perform magical acts without explicit consent and informed understanding. As outlined in § 600.3, any clause requiring an individual to engage in spellcasting or ritualistic activities constitutes an unconscionable term and shall be deemed void as contrary to public policy. This principle extends beyond mere coercion; it encompasses contracts that effectively enslave a non-caster's will through subtle magical influence or psychological manipulation. The burden of proof rests upon the caster to demonstrate that all parties entered into the agreement freely and with full knowledge of its implications.\n\nFurthermore, § 600.4 addresses the unique challenges posed by construct labor in contractual arrangements. While constructs may be employed as agents under a caster's direction, they cannot be held personally liable for breaches or damages arising from their actions. Instead, liability remains with the employing party who commissioned the work. This allocation of responsibility aims to prevent abusive practices where casters exploit autonomous entities without regard for consequences. Additionally, § 600.5 mandates that all contracts involving summons or binding rituals must include a clause specifying the duration and limitations thereof, thereby protecting against indefinite servitude or loss of autonomy.\n\nIn cases of alleged contract violations involving non-casters, the Accords provide mechanisms for swift resolution through arbitration panels comprised of both casters and laypersons. These panels operate independently of the Guild's internal hierarchies to ensure impartial adjudication. Appeals may be lodged with the Autumnwood Desk itself under § 1301.3, though such review is typically limited to questions of law rather than fact-finding. Ultimately, the goal of these safeguards is to balance the interests of all parties while upholding the fundamental principles of justice and fairness enshrined in the spirit of the Accords.\n\nsee § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; see § 600.3 Geas as an Unconscionable Term; under § 1301.2 Penalties for Noncompliance; cf. § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs regarding unauthorized field use and liability implications.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "600.1",
            "1301.2",
            "600.3",
            "600.4",
            "600.5",
            "1301.3",
            "400.2",
            "600.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.1 Unauthorized Field Use and Regulatory Oversight",
          "text": "Unauthorized field use of magic poses significant risks to public safety and the integrity of arcane studies, necessitating stringent regulatory oversight under the Autumnwood Accords. As prescribed in § 700.1, any casting conducted outside official Guild premises requires prior written permission from the Conservators' Council or designated Innovator Liaison depending on the nature of the work. This authorization process evaluates potential hazards, environmental impacts, and public welfare concerns before granting approval. Applications must include detailed plans for containment, emergency response protocols, and post-operation cleanup procedures to mitigate adverse effects.\n\nViolations of § 700.1 constitute serious offenses subject to disciplinary action up to and including permanent revocation of licensure under § 200.4. Repeat offenders may face additional sanctions such as mandatory retraining, fines proportional to the scale of harm caused, or imprisonment for egregious cases involving reckless endangerment. The burden lies with the caster to prove that their activities complied fully with all stipulated conditions; mere assertions of competence or necessity are insufficient defenses against allegations of unauthorized field use.\n\nMoreover, § 700.2 establishes a system of random inspections and audits designed to detect unreported magical operations and enforce compliance proactively. Inspectors possess broad powers to enter private property with warrants issued by the Autumnwood Desk, seize relevant materials, and interview witnesses as deemed necessary for investigation purposes. Refusal to cooperate may result in escalated penalties under § 1301.2(b). Data collected during these reviews informs ongoing policy adjustments aimed at addressing emerging threats while fostering responsible innovation within acceptable parameters.\n\nUltimately, the provisions surrounding unauthorized field use reflect the delicate balance between regulating dangerous practices and supporting legitimate research endeavors. By maintaining rigorous oversight mechanisms alongside clear guidelines for permissible activity, the Accords seek to safeguard both society at large and the reputation of lawful casters alike against exploitation or misuse of their powerful abilities.\n\nsee § 700.2 Random Inspections and Audits; under § 1301.2 Penalties for Violations; cf. § 500.1 Field Casting for Value regarding compensation issues related to off-site work arrangements; compare § 400.4 Specimen Custody obligations when handling sensitive materials away from secure facilities.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "700.1",
            "200.4",
            "700.2",
            "1301.2",
            "500.1",
            "400.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 800.1 Ethical Standards and Professional Conduct",
          "text": "Casters are bound by rigorous ethical standards governing their professional conduct both within and outside the workplace under § 800.1 of the Autumnwood Accords. These guidelines address issues ranging from client interactions to personal behavior, aiming to preserve public trust in the magical profession while protecting individuals from abuse or exploitation. Compliance with these norms is mandatory for all licensed practitioners regardless of rank or specialization.\n\nKey provisions include prohibitions against dual relationships between caster and client that could impair judgment or create conflicts of interest (§ 800.2). Casters must avoid situations where financial gain might compromise objectivity or lead to overreliance on their services. Similarly, § 800.3 forbids sexual harassment, discrimination, or any form of misconduct based on race, gender, age, disability status, or other protected characteristics under applicable law. Such behavior undermines the dignity of the profession and invites scrutiny from regulatory bodies.\n\nFurthermore, casters are obligated to maintain accurate records of their work pursuant to § 800.4. This documentation serves multiple functions: facilitating continuity in long-term projects, enabling accountability in case of disputes or malpractice claims, and supporting ongoing education efforts within the community. Neglecting this duty may result in disciplinary measures ranging from warnings to suspension pending investigation into potential negligence or misconduct.\n\nFinally, § 800.5 emphasizes the importance of continuous professional development through attendance at workshops, seminars, and conferences sponsored by recognized organizations. Staying abreast of emerging trends ensures that casters provide optimal service while adhering to evolving best practices. Failure to pursue necessary training may indicate stagnation or incompetence warranting corrective action under § 200.4(a). Ultimately, upholding these high ethical standards reflects well on the entire casting community and reinforces its reputation for excellence in delivering safe and effective magical solutions to diverse problems faced by society today.\n\nsee § 800.2 Prohibition Against Dual Relationships; see § 800.3 Anti-Harassment Policies; under § 1301.2 Disciplinary Actions for Breaches of Code; cf. § 400.5 Unauthorized Titan Growth as an example of unacceptable professional conduct leading to sanctions; contrast with § 600.6 Related Requirements outlining acceptable delegation practices when working with assistants or apprentices who require supervision due to limited experience levels.",
          "key": "g",
          "refs": [
