        },
        {
          "heading": "Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and Evidence",
          "text": "§ 700.7 Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and Evidence shall govern the introduction of magical data into formal proceedings under these Accords. The Desk hereby establishes that all evidence derived from arcane means must undergo strict scrutiny before being admitted, to prevent contamination by illusion or deception. See § 1.6 defining \"Evidence\" as any material fact offered to prove a proposition in issue.\n\nUnder this section, the admissibility of scrying results, memory charm impressions, simulacrum outputs, and clone testimonies shall be evaluated according to specific criteria set forth herein. The Desk finds that such methods possess inherent limitations which may compromise their reliability if not properly calibrated and verified. Accordingly, no arcane testimony or derived evidence shall be accepted without first satisfying the requirements laid out below. See § 100.6 regarding delegation of technical tasks to qualified clerks where appropriate.\n\nFor scrying operations, the Desk mandates that all lenses must be certified free from tampering prior to use, with serial numbers recorded in official logs. The caster must declare any known defects or magical interference affecting the device's accuracy. Results obtained under these conditions may then be admitted as corroborative evidence, though never as sole proof of contested facts. See § 200.3 limiting scope of permitted practices to licensed professionals only.\n\nMemory charm impressions shall undergo immediate analysis by a qualified thaumaturge unconnected to the case. The analyst must produce a detailed report assessing potential sources of contamination or distortion in the impression. Only after this verification may such evidence be tendered, and then solely for purposes corroborating other reliable testimony. See § 300.4 governing reclassification procedures for tainted materials.\n\nSimulacrum-generated data shall require dual authentication by both the original caster and an independent expert witness. The experts must confirm that the simulation parameters were properly configured and free from external interference. Any discrepancies detected during review shall render the resulting evidence inadmissible until resolved to satisfaction of the Desk. See § 400.2 regulating use of pocket dimensions as laboratory spaces for such purposes.\n\nClone testimony presents unique challenges due to potential cognitive degradation or personality divergence over time. The Desk therefore requires that all clones provide sworn affidavits detailing their training regimen, any modifications made post-cloning, and a complete history of experiences since activation. Such documents shall be subject to cross-examination as required by standard evidentiary rules. See § 500.1 addressing field casting compensation schemes which may incentivize substandard replication practices.\n\nIn all cases involving arcane evidence, the burden rests upon the party introducing such material to demonstrate its validity and lack of prejudice beyond a reasonable doubt. The Desk retains discretion to exclude any testimony or exhibit deemed likely to confuse issues, mislead the tribunal, or waste time in accordance with established judicial principles. See § 600.1 prohibiting contractual agreements that contravene public policy under these Accords.\n\nThese admissibility standards are intended to ensure fair adjudication while maintaining rigorous control over potentially dangerous magical technologies. Violations shall constitute grounds for disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure, at the Desk's sole discretion. See § 200.4 outlining suspension procedures for serious infractions. The Desk reserves authority to amend these rules as necessary to address emerging challenges in the field of arcane jurisprudence. See § 1301.5 regarding appeals process for contested rulings by the full Board.\n\nIt is hereby declared that adherence to these provisions shall be deemed essential to preserving the integrity and efficacy of all magical proceedings conducted under the auspices of this Codex. Failure to comply may result in severe penalties including permanent disqualification from practice within Autumnwood jurisdiction. See § 1401.3 noting historical precedents where similar measures proved necessary for public safety. The Desk stands ready to enforce these guidelines with unwavering determination to protect both the innocent and the guild's precious reputation. See § 900.6 authorizing appropriate sanctions against recalcitrant parties.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "700.7",
            "1.6",
            "100.6",
            "200.3",
            "300.4",
            "400.2",
            "500.1",
            "600.1",
            "200.4",
            "1301.5",
            "1401.3",
            "900.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.7 Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and Evidence",
          "text": "The strict evidentiary rules governing civil litigation are deemed inadequate to address the unique challenges posed by magical phenomena and supernatural entities. Accordingly, this Part establishes specialized standards for the admission of arcane testimony and evidence in proceedings before the Autumnwood Accords Desk and its tribunals.\n\n§ 700.7(1) Arcane Testimony. The testimony of a qualified spellcaster regarding their own practices, abilities, or effects shall be admissible as prima facie evidence, absent rebuttal proof to the contrary. Such testimony may include sworn declarations concerning spell origins, ritual components, and intended outcomes. Cross-examination is permitted but limited to matters directly relevant to the caster's competency and credibility.\n\n§ 700.7(2) Magical Residues and Artifacts. Physical traces of magical activity, such as residual aetheric signatures or enchanted objects, may be introduced as circumstantial evidence when authenticated by an accredited Arcane Forensic Analyst (AFA). AFAs must maintain detailed chain-of-custody logs and provide contemporaneous calibration records for all analytical equipment utilized during examination proceedings.\n\n§ 700.7(3) Expert Witnesses. Opinions rendered by independent experts on matters of magical theory, classification, or causation shall carry weight proportional to their demonstrated expertise and standing within the arcane community. Parties objecting to an expert's qualifications must raise such issues at least three days prior to trial, allowing adequate time for meaningful cross-examination.\n\n§ 700.7(4) Secrecy Obligations. Witnesses testifying regarding protected Arcane Knowledge or Quiet List items are subject to a temporary non-disclosure order lasting until conclusion of the proceeding and filing of all appellate briefs. Violation of such orders may result in sanctions including contempt citations and disqualification from future testimony before the Accords Desk.\n\n§ 700.7(5) Special Masters. In complex cases involving disputed magical causation or novel phenomena, the Desk may appoint a Special Master to oversee discovery, manage expert exchanges, and issue preliminary findings on technical issues requiring arcane expertise. The Special Master's determinations shall be binding unless clearly erroneous upon appellate review.\n\nThese provisions balance the need for reliable fact-finding in technologically advanced disputes with necessary protections for sensitive magical information. They reflect recognition that traditional evidentiary frameworks may prove insufficient when dealing with forces beyond ordinary comprehension. The Desk retains discretion to tailor procedural accommodations as circumstances warrant, always mindful of maintaining fair play and integrity in adjudication.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "700.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.7 Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and Evidence — Continued",
          "text": "The admission of arcane testimony or evidence in proceedings before the Desk is governed by strict criteria designed to ensure reliability and fairness. Under § 700.1, all parties must submit a written motion requesting the introduction of magical proof at least seven days prior to hearing, specifying precisely what item, spell, or phenomenon is sought and why it is material to the case. The Presiding Clerk shall review said motion in conjunction with relevant files under §§ 300–600 before granting leave to present such evidence.\n\nArcane testimony delivered orally must be accompanied by a sworn affidavit detailing the circumstances of its acquisition and execution, along with an attestation that no coercion or deception was employed. As provided in § 700.2, scrying operations conducted without prior authorization from the Conservators are deemed tainted and inadmissible unless subsequently ratified in writing. Memory charms cast upon witnesses shall be treated as spoliation under § 300.4 and may result in sanctions against the offending party.\n\nSimulacra summoned to bear witness must meet the standards set forth in § 700.5, including proof of authenticity and independence from outside influence. Clones or duplicates invoked as additional parties shall be subject to the restrictions outlined in § 700.6 regarding their capacity to act on behalf of the original casters. Any attempt to bypass these safeguards for tactical advantage constitutes contempt punishable under § 1301.2.\n\nThe Desk retains discretion to admit otherwise excluded magical evidence if it deems such proof essential to resolving disputed factual issues fairly and efficiently. However, this power shall not be exercised arbitrarily or capriciously but rather pursuant to principles of natural justice enshrined in §§ 1.7–1.9. Parties objecting to the admission of arcane materials may petition for an expedited ruling by direct appeal to the Sovereign Archmage via established channels under § 200.4.\n\nUltimately, the goal behind these stringent admissibility rules is twofold: first, to preserve the integrity of the adjudicative process itself; second, to protect all participants from undue harm resulting from unregulated magical experimentation. By balancing these competing interests through careful oversight and transparent procedures, the Autumnwood Accords seek to foster an environment wherein truth may emerge even amidst complexity and chaos alike. For further guidance on specific applications, consult §§ 400–800 as applicable.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "700.1",
            "700.2",
            "300.4",
            "700.5",
            "700.6",
            "1301.2",
            "1.7",
