          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and Evidence — Continued",
          "text": "§ 700.7 Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and Evidence\n\nThe admissibility of arcane testimony and evidence in legal proceedings is governed by stringent criteria to ensure fairness and reliability. Under § 700.1, all testimony must be given under oath or affirmation, with penalties for perjury applicable as usual. Arcane manifestations used as evidence must be authenticated according to the procedures set forth in § 700.2, which outlines requirements for scrying of closed books and other divination techniques. Memory charms suspected of spoliation are subject to examination under § 700.3, with findings reported by independent observers sworn to impartiality.\n\nSimulacrum testimony presented pursuant to § 700.4 must conform to established protocols regarding the construction and operation of such constructs. The authenticity of any claims made through simulacra is determined by a panel of qualified mages appointed by the court, in accordance with standards detailed in accompanying regulations. In cases where clones are invoked as duplicate claimants under § 700.5, their status and authority must be verified against official records maintained by the relevant jurisdictional body. Failure to properly authenticate any form of arcane testimony or evidence shall result in its exclusion from consideration.\n\nThe admissibility provisions herein operate independently of other rules within this Code but are intended to complement them where applicable. Nothing contained in these sections shall be construed to limit or expand rights beyond those explicitly granted by statute or common law, except as otherwise provided herein. Parties seeking to introduce unconventional forms of proof bear the burden of demonstrating their admissibility prior to presentation at trial. The court retains discretionary authority to exclude irrelevant, prejudicial, or unreliable material even if technically permissible under these guidelines.\n\nThese standards reflect a balance between accommodating necessary magical practices and safeguarding against abuse. They recognize both the unique challenges posed by supernatural phenomena in evidentiary contexts and the paramount need for procedural integrity. Compliance with these requirements is mandatory for all participants in matters involving arcane elements, regardless of jurisdictional boundaries or customary precedents elsewhere. Violation may result in sanctions up to and including dismissal of claims or counterclaims dependent upon improperly admitted matter.\n\nSee § 700.1 Evidence Generally regarding foundational rules applicable to all testimony; under § 300.4 Reclassification for procedures governing reevaluation of contested evidence types. Related provisions include § 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion concerning safety protocols during demonstrations, and § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill defining permissible roles for artificial intelligences in legal proceedings. Construction principles outlined herein are consistent with those set forth in §§ 1.4–1.9 governing persons bound by these Accords and general rules of interpretation.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "700.7",
            "700.1",
            "700.2",
            "700.3",
            "700.4",
            "700.5",
            "300.4",
            "500.3",
            "200.6",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and Evidence — Administration",
          "text": "In all proceedings before the Autumnwood Accords Desk, testimony and evidence involving magical phenomena shall be subject to rigorous scrutiny to ensure reliability and fairness. § 700.1 Evidence Generally dictates that all material presented must be authenticated by a duly licensed mage-forensic expert possessing appropriate credentials under Part 200. Such authentication is necessary to establish the chain of custody and integrity of any enchanted item or recorded spell-effect claimed as proof. Failure to provide proper certification shall result in automatic exclusion of the contested evidence from consideration under § 700.4 Simulacrum Testimony provisions.\n\n§ 700.2 Scrying of Closed Books addresses the contentious issue of magical divination undertaken without judicial oversight. Unless expressly authorized by a warrant issued pursuant to § 500.1 Field Casting for Value, any unauthorized scrying operation constitutes grave interference with due process. Evidence obtained through such improper means shall be deemed tainted and inadmissible, regardless of its apparent probative value. This rule exists to prevent abuse of powerful divination arts in the pursuit of self-serving ends. See also § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts regarding similar prohibitions on forbidden magical practices.\n\n§ 700.3 Memory Charm as Spoliation recognizes that certain enchantments capable of altering conscious recollection may constitute spoliation of evidence under specific circumstances. When a party knowingly subjects themselves to such alterations in anticipation of litigation, the resulting loss of accurate memory shall be imputed to their side of the dispute. This principle aims to preserve the integrity of testimonial records and deter manipulative use of mind-affecting magics for evidentiary advantage. Refer to § 400.3 Borrowed Hours; Interest in Time for additional guidance on temporal distortions affecting witness reliability.\n\n§ 700.4 Simulacrum Testimony establishes that artificially generated consciousnesses incapable of independent volition may not serve as competent witnesses under the Accords. While such constructs may be employed to preserve critical information or provide technical assistance, they lack the requisite agency to bear testimony on their own behalf. Any attempt to introduce simulative entities as surrogate witnesses shall result in immediate dismissal of the offending claim and potential sanctions against responsible parties for misuse of prohibited technologies. Consult § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters regarding limitations on artificial intelligence applications in legal contexts.\n\n§ 700.5 Clone as Duplicate Claimant clarifies that magically replicated individuals retain distinct legal identities despite physical similarities. In matters involving personal injury, property damage, or contractual obligations, each cloned entity shall be held separately accountable for their own actions and liabilities. No party may assert defenses based solely on duplication status absent clear statutory authorization. This provision safeguards the fundamental principle of individual responsibility within an increasingly complex magical society. See Part 300 regarding identity verification procedures applicable to all litigants.\n\n§ 700.6 Related Requirements incorporates by reference all relevant provisions scattered throughout this Codex that bear upon evidentiary standards in arcane proceedings. These include but are not limited to §§ 200.1 Licensure Required, 400.1 Research Permits, and 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion where applicable. Parties appearing before the Desk shall be deemed fully aware of these interconnected obligations and shall comply therewith without further notice. Failure to adhere shall result in appropriate disciplinary action as outlined elsewhere herein. This comprehensive approach ensures consistent application of fair trial principles across all magical disputes within the Guild's jurisdiction.\n\nUnder § 1.8 Effective Date; Savings, these admissibility standards apply retroactively except where doing so would impair vested rights or impose new obligations. Existing cases pending before the Autumnwood Accords Desk shall proceed under prior rules unless specifically remanded for re-evaluation under this updated framework. The Desk retains discretion to grant exceptions in exceptional circumstances upon written motion and justification filed with full particularity. All decisions regarding evidence admissibility remain within the sole purview of presiding magistrates acting in accordance with these guidelines. Any challenges to evidentiary rulings must be raised promptly during trial rather than on appeal, consistent with traditional procedural norms. This balance between flexibility and predictability serves the ultimate goal of achieving justice through reliable fact-finding processes.\n\nsee § 100.4 Records of Speech; see § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts; see § 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion; see § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; see Part 200 regarding licensure and certification requirements applicable to all witnesses and experts testifying before the Accords Desk under these provisions.",
