    {
      "cite": "800.7",
      "part": "part_viii",
      "title": "PART 800 - DISCIPLINARY MEASURES § 800.7 Related Requirements; § 1301.2 Penaltie",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "PART 800 - DISCIPLINARY MEASURES § 800.7 Related Requirements; § 1301.2 Penaltie",
          "text": "The provisions of this Part shall be read in conjunction with the applicable penalty schedules set forth in § 1301.2 and any supplementary guidelines issued by the Autumnwood Accords Desk from time to time. In determining the appropriate sanction for a given infraction, the Presiding Council shall take into account the totality of the circumstances, including but not limited to: (a) the gravity of the violation as measured against the specific clauses of this Code; (b) any history of prior offenses by the accused casterman or entity; (c) the degree of harm caused to innocent third parties or public safety; and (d) evidence of remorse or corrective action taken voluntarily. Where multiple violations are found, sanctions shall be aggregated in accordance with § 1301.2(c) unless exceptional mitigation is warranted by the circumstances.\n\nIt is hereby declared that all disciplinary measures imposed under this Code constitute a lawful deprivation of property and liberty interests protected by the Magna Carta and subsequent charters. Accordingly, any casterman subject to such sanctions shall be afforded full due process rights throughout the proceedings, including notice of charges, opportunity to present evidence and cross-examine witnesses, representation by counsel of choice, and appeal to higher authorities as provided herein. Failure to comply with these procedural mandates shall render any resulting sanction null and void ab initio at the sole discretion of the aggrieved party seeking redress through proper channels.\n\nIn cases where a casterman's magic is temporarily or permanently sealed pursuant to § 800.3, such action shall not preclude the individual from pursuing alternative means of livelihood consistent with their station in life and ability to earn a living wage without further restriction on their civil rights. The Guild recognizes that magical suppression may necessitate significant lifestyle adjustments for affected parties and encourages them to seek assistance through accredited rehabilitation programs or community support networks where available. Reinstatement procedures shall commence immediately upon expiration of the seclusion period unless extended by mutual agreement between all involved parties within thirty (30) days thereof.\n\nThe foregoing sections serve as a comprehensive guide to understanding the scope, application, and implications of disciplinary actions taken under this Code. Any ambiguities or disputes arising from their interpretation shall be resolved in favor of strict construction against those seeking to impose sanctions and in accord with established principles of fairness and equity governing administrative law generally. This final paragraph ensures clarity regarding the intent and purpose behind each provision contained herein for all readers thereof.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1301.2",
            "800.3",
            "800.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 800.7 Related Requirements and Prohibited Acts; § 1301.2 Penalties for Breach ",
          "text": "This Part codifies the disciplinary framework governing infractions of the Accords, establishing both the spectrum of sanctions available and the procedural safeguards required to ensure justice is meted out fairly. The range of penalties extends from admonition and fine up to permanent sealing of magical aptitude or exile to a pocket dimension for egregious offenders. Each sanction carries specific conditions precedent and must be imposed only after due process has been fully exhausted.\n\nA critical aspect of disciplinary action lies in the determination of fault, which requires careful consideration of mitigating factors such as intent, circumstance, and prior record. The burden of proof rests squarely upon the accuser to demonstrate guilt beyond reasonable doubt through credible testimony or irrefutable evidence. Defendants are entitled to counsel and a full opportunity to present their case before an impartial tribunal composed of peers familiar with arcane matters.\n\nSanctions imposed must be proportional to the gravity of the offense, tailored to address the root causes while preventing future harm to both individuals and society at large. Severe penalties such as sealing may only be enacted when lesser measures have proven inadequate or when the offender poses a continuing threat to public safety. Even then, periodic review is mandated to assess whether less restrictive alternatives might suffice given changed conditions.\n\nThe Accords further prohibit certain acts deemed inherently dangerous or incompatible with lawful magic practice, regardless of individual circumstances. These include unauthorized experimentation on living subjects, possession of prohibited materials, and engagement in magical duels outside sanctioned arenas. Violation of such prohibitions constitutes automatic grounds for disciplinary action without need for additional proof of harm.\n\nFinally, this Part emphasizes the importance of rehabilitation alongside punishment where appropriate, recognizing that most offenders can be reintegrated into productive society if given proper support and guidance. Probationary periods with clear milestones allow for correction of course while maintaining accountability for past misdeeds. The ultimate goal remains preservation of public welfare through balanced enforcement of standards essential to harmonious coexistence between casters and non-magical folk alike.\n\nsee § 1301.2 regarding specific penalty tiers and application criteria; see § 800.5 outlining pocket-prison procedures for extreme cases; under § 800.6, related requirements supplement this framework where necessary. Persons subject to disciplinary measures retain all rights afforded by law unless explicitly restricted as part of their sanction package.\n\n\"The Accords stand as testament to our collective wisdom in navigating the delicate balance between innovation and responsibility. Disciplinary mechanisms exist not merely to punish but to guide us toward higher ideals.\" - Archmage Veyra, Mediating Statement No. 7892-B",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1301.2",
            "800.5",
            "800.6",
            "800.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "PART 800 - DISCIPLINARY MEASURES",
          "text": "§ 800.7 Related Requirements; § 1301.2 Penalties for Breach\n\nThe disciplinary measures outlined in this Part shall be applied uniformly and impartially to all members of the Guild, regardless of rank or faction affiliation. No mage is above the law, and violations will be met with swift and decisive action designed to preserve the integrity of magical practice within the Accords' jurisdiction. The Conservators maintain that strict enforcement is essential to preventing abuse and ensuring public safety, while Innovators argue for proportionality and rehabilitation where appropriate. Ultimately, the Aegis Magi serve as neutral arbiters in all matters of discipline.\n\nSanctions may include reprimand, fines, suspension of license, permanent revocation of privileges, sealing of magical abilities, or expulsion from the Guild entirely. The severity of punishment shall correspond to the gravity of the infraction and any resulting harm. Lesser offenses might warrant a warning or mandatory remedial training, while egregious breaches could result in lifelong disqualification from casting magic under any circumstances. Repeat offenders face increasingly harsh penalties, reflecting the cumulative nature of their misconduct.\n\nThe Paradox Trial remains the primary mechanism for adjudicating disputes between Guild members and determining appropriate sanctions. This formal proceeding ensures due process and allows all parties to present evidence in support of their positions. Peer review by a panel of senior mages provides an additional layer of oversight and accountability. Appeals may be lodged with the Archmage Sovereign, who retains final authority over disciplinary outcomes. Expedited trials are available for urgent cases where immediate action is necessary to protect public welfare or maintain order.\n\nSealing of magical abilities represents one of the most severe punishments available under the Accords. This procedure permanently disables a mage's capacity to cast spells without specialized equipment or assistance, effectively neutering their power. Sealed individuals must register with the Conservators and undergo regular monitoring to ensure compliance. Release from sealing is exceptionally rare and contingent upon demonstrated rehabilitation and service to the Guild community over an extended period. The stigma of being \"seared\" follows a mage for the rest of their days, limiting career prospects and social standing.\n\nExpulsion from the Autumnwood Accords carries even greater consequences than mere sealing. Once cast out, an ex-mage loses all rights and protections afforded by membership, rendering them vulnerable to prosecution under civil law or extrajudicial retaliation from rival groups. Such individuals are generally barred from practicing magic within Guild territory unless granted special dispensation by the High Council. Expulsion is reserved for those who prove irredeemably corrupt or pose an unacceptable threat to societal stability. The burden of proof rests squarely on the accuser, and standards for removal must be met beyond any reasonable doubt.\n\nDisciplinary records are maintained confidentially but may be accessed upon proper request by relevant authorities or prospective employers. This transparency helps safeguard against future misconduct while protecting privacy where feasible. Expungement is possible after a designated period of good behavior, provided no new violations occur during that time. Rehabilitation programs exist to assist struggling mages in overcoming personal demons and returning to productive lives outside the shadows of infamy. The Guild stands ready to extend a hand to those willing to learn from their mistakes rather than repeating them ad infinitum.\n\nIn conclusion, the enforcement framework established herein constitutes the bedrock upon which responsible magical practice rests. By holding every practitioner accountable for their actions, we foster an environment where innovation can flourish alongside tradition. Justice tempered with mercy ensures that the Accords serve not merely as instruments of control but as guides toward ethical growth and mutual understanding among all who walk this path together in pursuit of arcane enlightenment under the watchful gaze of history itself.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "800.7",
            "1301.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 800.7 Sanctioned Uses of Forbidden Arts",
          "text": "The Accords recognize that in exceptional circumstances, the prohibition against forbidden arts may be temporarily lifted under strict oversight. Such exceptions require written approval from the Desk and must occur within a designated containment zone established by an Aegis Magus. The caster remains fully liable for any adverse effects resulting from the sanctioned use, regardless of whether precautions were taken.\n\nUnder § 1301.2, failure to adhere to the conditions set forth in the exception grant constitutes grounds for immediate revocation of all privileges held under the Accords. Furthermore, repeat offenses may result in permanent sealing of magical abilities and referral for prosecution under applicable criminal statutes. The Desk maintains a confidential register of all exceptions granted, accessible only upon specific request by interested parties demonstrating compelling need.\n\nIt is imperative that mages understand the gravity with which such permissions are viewed by the Conservators. While innovation drives progress, it must never compromise public safety or undermine foundational magical principles. The Aegis Magi remain vigilant in monitoring compliance and may initiate emergency containment procedures if they perceive any threat to community welfare arising from unauthorized experimental activities.\n\nUltimately, the granting of exceptions serves as a reminder that even the most rigid legal codes require flexibility when faced with novel challenges. Yet this latitude is afforded sparingly, always balanced against the greater good served by maintaining orderly control over dangerous forces inherent in the arcane arts. Mages who seek to push boundaries must do so responsibly and within the confines established by their peers for collective protection.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1301.2",
            "800.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Sanctioned Uses of Forbidden Arts",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords recognize that certain magical practices, though nominally forbidden under § 300.2, may nevertheless serve legitimate ends when performed under strict supervision and for approved purposes. This principle is codified in the provisions regarding sanctioned uses of otherwise prohibited arts.\n\nUnder § 800.6 Related Requirements, a Conservator or designated Aegis Magi may authorize the temporary lifting of restrictions on specific forbidden schools of magic. Such authorization shall be granted only when it can be demonstrated that the intended use will result in no net increase in overall magical risk to society. Typical examples include the study of necromantic phenomena for medical research purposes, or the controlled application of blood-magic techniques in forensic investigations.\n\nAny such permit must specify precisely which arts are authorized, the duration of the authorization, and the exact conditions under which they may be employed. Violation of these stipulations shall constitute a breach of § 800.1 Discipline and subject the violator to full disciplinary action under Part 800, including but not limited to revocation of licensure pursuant to § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse.\n\nIn addition, all sanctioned uses must be reported quarterly to the Conservators' Bureau for review and assessment of potential abuses or unintended consequences. Failure to submit such reports shall result in immediate suspension of the permit until compliance is verified. This requirement is designed to ensure ongoing oversight of even those magical practices deemed acceptable under limited circumstances.\n\nThe provisions regarding sanctioned uses reflect the Accords' pragmatic approach to magic regulation, recognizing that absolute prohibition may not always serve the greater good when properly controlled. However, this flexibility must never be exploited for personal gain or advantage, as such behavior undermines the very foundation of the Guild's authority and trustworthiness in matters magical.\n\nsee § 800.1 Discipline; Range of Sanctions\nsee § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse; Guest Casters\nsee § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts\nsee § 1.7 Construction; Aggregation of Conduct",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "800.6",
            "800.1",
            "200.4",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 800.7 Sanctioned Uses of Forbidden Arts — Continued",
          "text": "While the Autumnwood Accords generally prohibit the practice of forbidden schools of magic, certain exceptions exist for sanctioned research and controlled experimentation conducted under strict supervision. Under § 300.2, necromancy and blood magic remain absolutely forbidden except where expressly permitted by a majority vote of the Archmage Council. Such permissions must be renewed annually and are subject to immediate revocation upon breach of terms.\n\nInnovators seeking to study restricted arts may apply for temporary research permits under § 400.1, which outline specific safety protocols and containment measures. Violation of permit conditions constitutes a major infraction punishable by seal or expulsion. The Council maintains absolute discretion over all applications and reserves the right to deny access without explanation.\n\nParticularly stringent rules govern the use of pocket dimensions for research purposes. Under § 400.2, no caster may utilize private planes as laboratories without explicit written authorization from a Conservator-level mage. Unauthorized experiments conducted within such spaces are presumed reckless and subject to enhanced penalties. All specimens collected during sanctioned fieldwork must be returned intact upon completion of studies.\n\nThe Accords further prohibit the commercial sale or distribution of forbidden grimoires, artifacts, or knowledge. Exceptions may be granted for educational purposes only, provided materials remain under constant guard. Possession alone does not violate this section unless accompanied by evidence of intent to practice. However, possession combined with prior infractions constitutes prima facie guilt requiring rebuttal proof.\n\nConservators retain inherent authority to inspect facilities and seize contraband at will. Resistance to inspection is deemed an act of war against the Guild itself. All seizures must be logged and inventoried before release or destruction according to § 1301.2. Unlawful retention of confiscated items constitutes theft from state custody.\n\nUltimately, the balance between innovation and control remains fragile. The Council acts as sole arbiter of what may safely proceed. Casters who push boundaries without permission risk immediate retribution regardless of potential benefits. Prudence demands deference to established rules lest one find oneself suddenly powerless and imprisoned forever.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "400.1",
            "400.2",
            "1301.2",
            "800.7"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "PART 800 - DISCIPLINARY MEASURES § 800.7 Related Requirements; § 1301.2 Penaltie",
