    {
      "cite": "1101.7",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.7 Related Requirements - Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion",
          "text": "Whereas magical objects may bear more than merely the literal imprints of prior owners or users, this Section governs the doctrine of collateral taints and their legal effect upon subsequent transactions. It is well established under common arcane jurisprudence that certain artifacts retain residual enchantments or curses even after formal disavowal by original casters; these lingering effects may impose liability beyond mere physical damage.\n\nSpecifically, a seller shall be deemed to have warranted the absence of active hexes or binding spells unless expressly disclaimed in writing. Failure to disclose known taints constitutes breach of contract and grounds for rescission under § 1302.4 Remedies for Breach. Moreover, any magical item possessing inherent sentience or autonomy may assert rights against good-faith purchasers if its fundamental nature is compromised through unauthorized modification or defilement.\n\nThe burden of proving taint exists shifts to the buyer where circumstantial evidence suggests foul play during prior ownership. However, this presumption shall not apply to items acquired at public auction or from reputable dealers maintaining full chain-of-custody records pursuant to § 100.2 Authorized Representatives. In such instances, strict liability attaches only if malicious intent can be demonstrated beyond reasonable doubt.\n\nFurthermore, this Section mandates that all magical transactions involving sentient constructs or animated objects require notarized consent from the entity itself, witnessed by two impartial mages of standing. Absent such documentation, any purported sale shall be void ab initio, regardless of apparent assent from human parties. This safeguard ensures protection against exploitation of vulnerable beings and upholds ethical standards within the trading community at large.\n\nFinally, it is hereby declared that no private agreement may override these mandatory provisions absent explicit ratification by the Guild Desk in a formal ruling. Any attempts to circumvent these protections through fraudulent means shall be punished as grand larceny under applicable penal statutes governing theft and embezzlement of magical assets. These stipulations remain in full force until amended by subsequent legislative action duly passed through established channels.\n\nsee § 1302.4 Remedies for Breach; see § 100.2 Authorized Representatives; see § 900.6 Related Requirements",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1302.4",
            "100.2",
            "900.6",
            "1101.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.7 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion",
          "text": "Where articles have been in possession of an unauthorized caster, the Desk may impose a remedial taint upon them, regardless of subsequent bona fide transfer. This preventive measure serves to neutralize residual enchantments that could otherwise re-emerge under certain conditions, posing risk to future owners or users. The taint shall be applied by certified magi under strict supervision, with full disclosure provided to the affected party.\n\nIn cases where an article has been used in a prohibited ritual or contract, even if completed years prior, it remains subject to seizure and destruction upon notice from the Desk. The mere fact of such usage renders the item permanently tainted beyond redemption for ordinary commercial use. Any attempt to resell or transfer title shall be deemed void ab initio, with criminal penalties applicable to all involved parties.\n\nThe burden of disclosure rests squarely upon sellers when offering articles with a potentially magical history. Failure to disclose known taints constitutes fraud and may result in treble damages plus punitive sanctions. Buyers are encouraged to request full provenance documentation prior to transaction completion, particularly for high-value items or those exhibiting unusual properties. The Desk maintains an online registry of flagged articles which shall be consulted by all parties before finalizing sales agreements.\n\nArticles bearing active curses or binding contracts may require special handling protocols before release into general circulation. In such instances, a neutral third party shall oversee the ritualistic cleansing process, with all costs borne by the original owner unless otherwise adjudicated. Until fully purged of malignant influences, affected items shall remain in secure custody pending further instruction from the Desk.\n\nThe principles outlined herein apply equally to both tangible and intangible magical artifacts, including but not limited to enchanted weapons, cursed heirlooms, and digitally encoded spells. No article is beyond the reach of these provisions, regardless of its perceived value or sentimental attachment. The Desk retains sole discretion over what constitutes a sufficient taint warranting intervention under this section.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1101.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.7 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion — Continued",
          "text": "The principles outlined in this Part extend beyond direct transactions to encompass the subtle, insidious transmission of magical influence. The Accords recognize that magic, unlike mere commodity, carries an inherent aura that can permeate objects, spaces, and even persons who interact with them. This phenomenon, termed \"magical contagion,\" may occur through touch, proximity, or even shared air for extended periods. While generally benign in casual encounters, sustained exposure to potent enchantments or curses can result in unintended consequences.\n\nSpecifically, the Desk holds that any item imbued with significant magical energy constitutes a \"tainted asset\" until properly cleansed or neutralized. This designation applies whether the magic was placed intentionally by a skilled caster or accidentally during a ritual gone awry. Purchasing or possessing such an item without knowledge of its condition may render the buyer liable for subsequent harms arising from residual enchantments. Sellers, conversely, bear a duty to disclose known taints, as failure to do so constitutes fraud under § 1.5 and voids any contract entered into thereunder.\n\nFurthermore, the concept of \"collateral taint\" recognizes that magical influence can spread beyond the original object or subject. For instance, clothing worn by a cursed individual may retain harmful properties long after removal, affecting anyone who dons them later. Similarly, rooms where dark rituals were performed may harbor lingering malevolent energies that impact all inhabitants. Landlords and property managers must therefore exercise due diligence in inspecting premises for residual effects before leasing or selling.\n\nIn cases of suspected contagion, the victim may petition the Desk for an official appraisal at public expense. If confirmed, the contaminated item or space will be quarantined until remediated by a certified mage. Costs associated with such procedures shall be borne by the party responsible for introducing the taint, determined according to prevailing evidence standards outlined in § 1301.2. Failure to comply may result in further sanctions under applicable penal provisions.\n\nUltimately, these guidelines aim to protect citizens from the hidden dangers inherent in magical artifacts and environments. By establishing clear rules regarding disclosure, liability, and remediation, the Accords seek to mitigate risks while fostering responsible engagement with the mystical realm. All parties are urged to heed these warnings lest they find themselves ensnared by unseen forces beyond their control.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.5",
            "1301.2",
            "1101.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.7 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion — Administration",
          "text": "The Accords recognize that magical items, whether enchanted or cursed, may carry residual influences extending beyond their primary function. This phenomenon, termed \"collateral taint,\" shall be governed by the provisions set forth herein to ensure fair dealing among all parties.\n\n§ 1101.7(1) Definition of Collateral Taint\n\"Collateral taint\" refers to any persistent magical effect or property that adheres to an item through its original enchantment, curse, blessing, or other supernatural intervention. Such effects may manifest physically, mentally, or spiritually, and shall be deemed inherent to the object itself regardless of subsequent ownership or usage.\n\n§ 1101.7(2) Disclosure Obligations\nEvery vendor offering a magically influenced item for sale must disclose all known collateral taints in writing prior to completing any transaction. Failure to do so constitutes material misrepresentation and shall render the contract voidable at the option of the aggrieved party. Vendors who knowingly conceal such defects may face additional penalties under § 1302.4 regarding fraudulently misrepresented goods.\n\n§ 1101.7(3) Burden of Proof\nIn any dispute concerning collateral taints, the burden shall rest upon the alleged victim to demonstrate that: (a) they were unaware of the existence or nature of the taint at the time of purchase; and (b) the taint has caused demonstrable harm exceeding normal wear and tear. The vendor may rebut by presenting evidence of full disclosure and/or mitigation efforts undertaken post-sale.\n\n§ 1101.7(4) Remedial Actions\nUpon verification of a valid claim, remedies available to the aggrieved party include: (i) rescission of contract with refund; (ii) damages for proven losses resulting from reliance on defective goods; or (iii) specific performance requiring remediation at the vendor's expense where feasible. The Guild shall provide mediation services upon request and may appoint independent appraisers as needed to assess the scope and severity of any taint effects.\n\n§ 1101.7(5) Exemptions\nThe provisions of this section shall not apply to items explicitly sold \"as-is\" with full disclosure of all known defects, nor to magical artifacts acquired through theft or other illicit means where original ownership cannot be established. In such cases, standard commercial law principles shall prevail unless otherwise specified by subsequent ruling of the Autumnwood Accords Desk.\n\nIt is hereby declared that ignorance of collateral taints shall not excuse compliance with these regulations. All parties are urged to exercise due diligence in examining potential purchases and consulting qualified experts when uncertainty exists regarding an item's true nature or history. The Guild remains committed to fostering a marketplace built on transparency, integrity, and mutual respect between buyers and sellers alike.\n\nsee § 1302.4 Fraudulent Misrepresentation; see § 1101.8 Disclaimer of Implied Warranties; see § 900.5 Independent Appraisal Services; see § 1.7 Construction — Aggregation of Conduct.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1101.7",
            "1302.4",
            "1101.8",
            "900.5",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "1324 - Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion",
          "text": "§ 1101.7 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion — Administration\n\nThe administration of collateral taints presents unique challenges requiring specialized protocols and oversight by the Aegis Magi. All instances of potential contamination must be immediately reported to the nearest field office for assessment, regardless of apparent severity or source. Upon receipt of such reports, a designated Taint Response Team shall dispatch within forty-eight hours to verify conditions and contain any spreading influence.\n\nDuring preliminary evaluation, the team leader may authorize controlled testing procedures deemed necessary to ascertain extent and nature of contamination. Such measures must be conducted with utmost care to avoid exacerbating risks or causing additional harm to persons and property. All findings will be documented in detail and submitted for review by a panel of senior magi experienced in taint theory and remediation techniques.\n\nIf initial assessments confirm active spread or high probability thereof, the Aegis Magi shall assume temporary custodial control of affected areas until further notice. This includes securing perimeters, restricting access, and implementing appropriate containment measures as directed by specialized experts convened for that purpose. Disposition of contaminated items remains subject to subsequent determination based on full investigation results.\n\nThroughout all phases of response operations, the safety and well-being of responding personnel shall be paramount consideration without compromising effectiveness of mission objectives. Adequate protective gear, support equipment, and emergency evacuation routes must be provided as appropriate given anticipated hazards encountered during execution of duties under these provisions.\n\nFailure to comply with any aspect of this administrative framework may result in disciplinary action ranging from reprimand to expulsion from membership depending upon gravity of violation and attendant consequences suffered thereby. Repeat offenders face enhanced penalties up to and including permanent forfeiture of standing within the guild structure entirely at sole discretion of governing authorities empowered under these rules.\n\nFor additional guidance on specific scenarios not addressed herein, consult relevant case law compilations maintained by the Central Archives or seek direct consultation with appointed advisors specializing in taint management issues as may be mutually agreed upon between all concerned parties involved in any particular situation requiring resolution under this section's purview.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1101.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.7 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion — Administrative Protocol",
          "text": "Whereas magical items may carry residual effects beyond their primary function, this Subsection governs the detection, reporting, and remediation of such taints under the auspices of the Autumnwood Accords Desk. All casters in possession of enchanted objects must submit a quarterly declaration on Form 11-DT, attesting to the item's current state and any observed anomalies. Failure to comply shall result in immediate suspension of practice privileges until further notice.\n\nUpon receipt of a report indicating potential contamination, the Desk shall convene an emergency review panel within seven (7) days. Said panel shall consist of no less than three licensed appraisers with expertise in arcane artifacts, none of whom may have a conflict of interest as defined under § 100.5. The panel's sole purpose is to determine whether the reported taint poses a threat to public safety or violates any provision of the Accords.\n\nIf confirmed as hazardous, the item shall be seized and placed in secure quarantine pending full analysis. During this period, all parties with lawful possession must refrain from handling or activating the object under any circumstances. Violation of this prohibition constitutes gross negligence subject to penalties under § 1301.2. Once deemed safe for return, a written certification will be issued confirming the item's status and authorizing resumption of normal use.\n\nThe Desk shall maintain comprehensive logs of all taint incidents, including origin, nature, extent of damage or risk posed, and resolution measures taken. These records shall remain confidential except as required by law or to inform future regulatory decisions. Information regarding specific items may be disclosed only upon written request from a duly authorized representative of the affected party.\n\nIn cases where widespread contamination is suspected across multiple sources, the Desk reserves the right to initiate a broader investigation involving sampling and forensic examination. All reasonable costs associated with such efforts shall be borne by the Guild, subject to reimbursement if negligence or misconduct on the part of any involved caster is established. Nothing herein shall preclude private suits for damages arising out of taint-related injuries or losses.\n\nThe provisions of this Subsection shall apply retroactively to all outstanding declarations due as of the Effective Date hereof. Any previously reported but unresolved items remain subject to the full scope of administrative review until formally closed out by the Desk. The undersigned Clerk hereby certifies that these guidelines reflect current best practices for managing magical contagion risks within the community.\n\nClerk's Initials: ___________ Date: ____/____/________\nForm 11-DT Submission Deadline: Last day of each calendar quarter\nPenalty for Non-Compliance: Immediate suspension of casting privileges until cleared by the Desk",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "1301.2",
            "1101.7"
          ]
        }
      ],
