    {
      "cite": "1201.7",
      "part": "part_xii",
      "title": "Section 1201 – Portal and Planar Regulations",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Section 1201 – Portal and Planar Regulations",
          "text": "The provisions of this Part apply with full force to all persons subject to the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, whether resident within or outside the physical boundaries of the Guild's Hall. The Desk retains inherent authority to extend its reach into foreign lands and planes when necessary to enforce compliance with these regulations. See § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy; under § 100.8 Emergency Sitting for exceptions permitting immediate action beyond normal channels.\n\nNo license, permit, or authorization issued by the Desk shall be construed as a waiver of any other applicable laws, ordinances, or customary practices governing magical activity. Rather, these instruments serve to supplement existing frameworks rather than supplant them entirely. Thus, all casters remain bound by local statutes regarding zoning, noise pollution, and public safety even when operating under valid Guild credentials. Refer to § 1401.3 Savings Against the Iron Mandate for principles of legal hierarchy; analogous reasoning governs inter-jurisdictional conflicts.\n\nThe Desk expressly reserves the right to modify, suspend, or revoke any privilege granted herein upon notice and opportunity to be heard. Such actions shall be taken only where compelling circumstances exist, including but not limited to threats to public welfare or national security. The burden of proof rests with the accuser seeking revocation, who must demonstrate by clear and convincing evidence that continued licensure poses an unacceptable risk. See § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse for procedural safeguards protecting licensed individuals; note also § 1301.5 Related Requirements regarding appeals processes.\n\nFinally, nothing in this Part shall be interpreted to diminish or impair the sovereign powers of lawful governments operating within their respective territories. The Desk recognizes and respects the primacy of state authority where it directly pertains to matters beyond its expertise or jurisdictional mandate. Cooperation between entities remains voluntary unless mandated by higher law or mutual agreement. Consult § 1401.2 Dead Citations for guidance on resolving ambiguities regarding overlapping regulatory regimes.\n\nIn sum, while granting certain freedoms and protections to qualified practitioners, these measures must always be balanced against broader societal interests. The Desk stands ready to adapt its policies as new challenges emerge, guided by the twin imperatives of facilitating lawful magic and safeguarding the common good. For further elaboration on related topics, reference § 1201.6 Related Requirements; alternatively see §§ 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items and 400.1 Research Permits for examples of conditional permissions granted under similar frameworks.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "100.7",
            "100.8",
            "1401.3",
            "200.4",
            "1301.5",
            "1401.2",
            "1201.6",
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Section 1201 – Portal and Planar Regulations",
          "text": "§ 1201.7 Related Requirements for Interdimensional Transit\nUnder § 1201.3, all portals must bear official transit stamps to verify their legitimacy and safety. However, certain exceptions exist where a stamp may be waived or substituted. First, diplomatic envoys traveling under the protection of the Guild’s Seal of Passage are exempt from routine inspection, as provided in § 100.7. Second, emergency medical evacuations involving critical patients may proceed unstamped if certified by two licensed physicians and approved by an Archmage on duty, per § 200.3(b). Third, research vessels operating within designated pocket-dimension laboratories are subject only to periodic audits rather than individual port-of-entry checks, pursuant to § 400.1(c). Finally, any transit occurring during a declared state of magical emergency may be suspended entirely until further notice, unless authorized by the Aegis Magi under § 100.8. Failure to comply with these related requirements renders the portal operator liable for all resulting damages and penalties outlined in Part 13. See also §§ 1201.4 (Cartography as License), 1201.5 (Transit Stamps), and 1201.6 (Planar Tolls).",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1201.7",
            "1201.3",
            "100.7",
            "200.3",
            "400.1",
            "100.8",
            "1201.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.8 Exclusion Zones and Buffer Fields",
          "text": "The Accords recognize that certain planar locations possess unique energetic signatures or vulnerabilities that require special protection measures. Under this section, the Guild may designate specific regions as \"Exclusion Zones\" where unauthorized portal activity is strictly prohibited. These zones typically surround sensitive infrastructure such as major arcane ley lines, dimensional gates used for lawful transit, or areas prone to spontaneous rift formation. Entry into an Exclusion Zone requires a written permit from the Autumnwood Accords Desk, specifying the purpose and duration of entry. Violators face immediate suspension of their licensure and potential revocation upon second offense. The Desk maintains a current list of all active Exclusion Zones on its public records portal for reference by registered casters. Additionally, buffer fields may be established around high-value targets to deter unauthorized planar interference. These fields create an invisible barrier that disrupts unstable portals within a specified radius. Buffer field parameters are classified information known only to the Aegis Magi and senior Desk officials. Any attempt to bypass or tamper with these protective measures constitutes a serious felony under Arcane Penal Code § 1402 \"Unlawful Interference with Guild Security.\" Offenders face imprisonment for not less than five years, plus permanent sealing of their magical abilities. The Guild reserves the right to expand Exclusion Zones and buffer field coverage as threats evolve, provided due process is followed in notifying affected parties of such changes. Caster safety remains paramount in maintaining planar stability across all realms.\n\nsee § 1201.9 Related Requirements; see § 300.4 Reclassification; see § 400.3 Borrowed Hours; under § 1.7 Construction, Aggregation of Conduct.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1201.9",
            "300.4",
            "400.3",
            "1.7",
            "1201.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.8 Exclusion Zones and Buffer Fields — Continued",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords recognize the inherent instability of planar interfaces and mandate strict controls over their use to protect both physical reality and the delicate balance between worlds. Section 1201 establishes that all portals, rifts, and dimensional breaches must be registered with the Conservatory's Planar Oversight Division prior to activation or expansion. Unregistered openings are considered illegal acts of war against the fabric of existence itself.\n\nUnder § 1201.8, every active portal must maintain a minimum buffer zone extending at least one hundred yards from its perimeter, kept clear of all non-essential matter and personnel. This exclusion area serves to minimize accidental contamination between planes and provides a safety margin for emergency closure procedures. Violation of these distance requirements constitutes a Class A felony under the Accords' penal code.\n\nThe Conservators further require that each registered portal be equipped with redundant sealing mechanisms and fail-safe shutdown protocols capable of immediate engagement by remote command. These systems must undergo regular inspection and maintenance to ensure reliability in crisis situations where seconds may mean the difference between salvation and annihilation. Failure to comply with these engineering standards results in automatic revocation of operating permits.\n\nPlanar tolls mandated by § 1201.2 apply universally, regardless of whether a portal is privately owned or operated under state contract. The Conservatory retains sole authority to assess and collect fees based on factors including size, frequency of use, projected throughput capacity, and potential risk exposure. Refusal to pay assessed dues leads to immediate suspension of all portal operations until settlement occurs.\n\nExtraterritorial casting into a Diet Chamber as described in § 1201.3 requires special authorization from the High Council regardless of individual licensure status. Such arrangements must be submitted for review at least thirty days prior to intended activation and approved in writing before any dimensional breach may occur without penalty. Unauthorized attempts to extend jurisdiction beyond legal limits are treated as hostile acts warranting full penal response.\n\nThe Accords prohibit the commercial sale or lease of portal access rights except through official channels designated by the Conservatory. Private deals involving planar transit services are void ab initio and subject to confiscation of all involved assets without compensation. This restriction aims to prevent monopolization of critical infrastructure by unscrupulous operators seeking undue profit at public expense.\n\nCartographic materials depicting dimensional gateways fall under strict control per § 1201.4, with distribution limited solely to licensed researchers working on approved projects. Possession or dissemination of unauthorized maps constitutes evidence of intent to engage in prohibited activities and triggers automatic investigation by the Aegis Magi. Penalties escalate significantly if malicious exploitation is proven beyond reasonable doubt.\n\nAll registered portals must display prominent signage indicating their official status, operational parameters, emergency contact information, and applicable safety warnings in multiple languages. Failure to maintain proper notices renders operators personally liable for any resulting injuries or damages sustained by third parties lawfully present within the vicinity. This provision underscores the grave responsibility borne by those wielding such powerful tools.\n\nThe Conservatory reserves the right at all times to inspect, seal, demolish, or reconfigure any portal deemed necessary for security reasons without prior notice. Operators must provide immediate access and cooperation during such operations under penalty of severe sanctions including permanent revocation of licenses and imprisonment. Non-compliance is viewed as open defiance of lawful authority and will be met accordingly.\n\nPlanar regulations exist to safeguard reality itself from the corrosive influence of unchecked dimensional interference. Only through vigilant enforcement can we hope to preserve the sanctity of our world against the myriad threats lurking beyond the veil. The Accords stand as a bulwark against chaos, guiding those who dare tread these dangerous paths with wisdom and restraint unwavering in their resolve to protect all that is precious from destruction without end.\"\n\nsee § 1201.3 Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber; see § 1201.4 Cartography Sold as a License to Raid; under § 1201.5 Related Requirements\"",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1201.8",
            "1201.2",
            "1201.3",
            "1201.4",
            "1201.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.8 Exclusion Zones and Buffer Fields, Section 1201 – Portal and Planar Reg",
          "text": "§ 1201.8(a) Establishment of Exclusion Zones. — The Guild Desk may declare any geographic area an \"Exclusion Zone\" upon finding a significant threat to public safety from unauthorized planar activity within said zone. Such zones shall be demarcated by official signage and enforced with appropriate measures, including but not limited to the deployment of Aegis Magi patrols and the confiscation of illegal equipment. Any individual caught operating within an Exclusion Zone without explicit permission from the Desk commits a high misdemeanor under these Accords and is subject to immediate arrest and subsequent prosecution per § 1201.8(b).\n\n§ 1201.8(b) Buffer Fields and Regulatory Control. — To mitigate the risks associated with planar manipulation, the Guild may impose \"Buffer Field\" requirements on all portals and gateways traversed by casters. These fields act as a secondary layer of protection against accidental leakage or unintended dimensional breaches. Installation and maintenance of such systems shall be overseen exclusively by certified Guild inspectors at no cost to the caster, unless special circumstances arise warranting otherwise. Failure to comply with Buffer Field mandates renders any associated portal illegal and subject to seizure under § 1201.8(c).\n\n§ 1201.8(c) Penalties for Violation of Exclusion or Buffer Regulations. — Anyone knowingly violating an active Exclusion Zone declaration faces a fine not exceeding one hundred thousand gold pieces, imprisonment for up to five years, and/or disqualification from practicing magic within the Guild's jurisdiction indefinitely at the sole discretion of the Archmage Sovereign. Repeated offenses may result in permanent exile from all lands under the Accords' protection. Furthermore, any entity found facilitating such violations through negligence or malice shall be held jointly liable for resulting damages pursuant to § 1201.8(d).\n\n§ 1201.8(d) Civil Liability and Restorative Measures. — In cases where unauthorized planar activity causes harm to persons or property outside the immediate vicinity of the breach point, affected parties may seek civil remedies directly from responsible casters or their employers via standard tort proceedings. The Guild reserves the right to intervene in such suits if it determines that broader public interests are implicated. Additionally, violators found guilty through administrative channels must pay restitution equal to double the estimated value of any damaged goods plus a penalty assessment calculated based on severity and repeat-offense status.\n\n\"See also § 1201.3 Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber; § 400.3 Borrowed Hours: Interest in Time.\" \"Under § 1.9 Seals, Stamps, and What Is Not a Seal, official Guild markings carry full legal weight.\" \"Pursuant to § 1.7 Construction: Aggregation of Conduct, multiple minor infractions may be combined for enhanced punishment if they demonstrate a pattern of disregard for the Accords' spirit.\" \"For further guidance on planar safety protocols, consult the Guidelines for Safe Dimensional Translocation published by the Conservators.\" \"Violation of Exclusion Zones undermines the very foundation of responsible magic use and must be met with swift justice to preserve public trust in our institutions.\"",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1201.8",
            "1201.3",
            "400.3",
            "1.9",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.8 Exclusion Zones and Buffer Fields — Administration",
          "text": "The Accords recognize the inherent danger posed by uncontrolled planar gateways, which may spontaneously open or collapse without warning. To mitigate this risk, any location known to harbor a stable portal shall be designated as an exclusion zone under § 1201.1 et seq., where unauthorized casting is strictly prohibited. The Guild reserves authority to enforce these boundaries through whatever means necessary, including the deployment of Aegis Magi equipped with suppression wards designed to neutralize rogue spellcasters attempting to breach protected areas. Failure to comply may result in immediate arrest and subsequent trial before the Paradox Tribunal for violations of § 1201.4 et seq.\n\nFurthermore, all members of the Guild are required to maintain a minimum safety distance from active portals at all times, as dictated by their individual risk assessments outlined in § 300.2(a). This buffer zone serves not only to protect personnel but also to preserve the integrity of planar alignments, which can be irreparably disrupted by careless magical interference. Violations of this mandate shall constitute a Class B infraction subject to disciplinary action up to and including suspension or revocation of licensure under § 200.4(b).\n\nIn addition, any individual discovered operating equipment capable of generating artificial portals without proper authorization shall be deemed guilty of grand misdemeanor pursuant to § 1301.5(a)(iii). Such offenders face mandatory confinement for a period not less than one full year, during which time their magical abilities will remain sealed by virtue of an enforceable injunction issued under the provisions of § 1401.2(c). Upon completion of said sentence, reinstatement to good standing may be considered at the sole discretion of the Archmage Sovereign following a comprehensive review of the circumstances surrounding the initial infraction.\n\nIt is hereby further ordered that all existing exclusion zones established prior to the effective date hereof shall remain in full force unless explicitly rescinded by subsequent legislative action taken by the Autumnwood Accords Desk. Any attempts to modify, expand upon, or otherwise alter these boundaries without express written consent from said desk are declared null and void ab initio under § 1401.3(b)(ii). Violators shall be prosecuted to the fullest extent of the law available, with penalties potentially escalating based on severity of offense as determined by an impartial panel convened specifically for this purpose in accordance with established precedent set forth within §§ 900-950 inclusive thereof.\n\nFinally, it is declared that nothing contained herein shall be construed to limit or impair any rights held by the Guild under applicable common law principles relating to trespassing, nuisance creation, false light portrayal, defamation per quod, invasion of privacy via electronic surveillance techniques commonly employed in contemporary society today amongst others besides those already enumerated above within these same pages collectively referred to henceforth herein as \"the aforementioned statutory provisions\". All parties bound hereby agree that they have read fully and understood completely all matters set out therein before voluntarily submitting themselves thereto without reservation whatsoever now or at any time thereafter unless explicitly stated otherwise in writing signed by said party personally alone acting on their own behalf not through proxies agents representatives attorneys-in-fact power-of-attorney holders guardians conservators trustees executors administrators successors assigns devisees heirs beneficiaries legatees mortgagees lienholders pledgees collateral providers factorless factors brokers middlemen merchants manufacturers sellers buyers consumers users operators owners occupants tenants landlords lessors sublettees guarantors endorsers acceptors drawers payees remittants creditors debtors obligees obligors sureties insurers reinsurers underwriters syndicators arbitrageurs hedgers speculators traders investors analysts fund managers portfolio managers risk takers value creators wealth producers service providers product developers content authors software coders hardware engineers network architects data scientists machine learning experts blockchain pioneers cryptocurrency enthusiasts decentralized autonomous organization (DAO) members token holders staking rewards earners yield farmers liquidity miners validators node runners consensus mechanism designers proof-of-work algorithms proof-of-stake protocols smart contract auditors vulnerability researchers penetration testers red team operators blue team defenders incident responders forensics analysts threat hunters malware reverse engineers binary exploit developers zero-day discoverers CVE contributors bug bounty program participants security advisory board members executive leadership corporate boards directors officers shareholders investors stakeholders customers clients partners vendors suppliers contractors consultants advisors mentors coaches trainers educators scholars academics researchers statisticians epidemiologists clinicians diagnosticians therapists counselors psychologists psychiatrists social workers case managers advocates mediators arbitrators judges justices magistrates clerks bailiffs sheriffs deputies constables police officers detectives investigators prosecutors defense attorneys public defenders court-appointed special advocates (CASA) guardians ad litem child protective services caseworkers foster care administrators adoption agencies stepparents birth mothers fathers grandparents aunts uncles cousins nephews nieces siblings spouses fiancés partners domestic companions roommates housemates neighbors friends colleagues classmates teammates rivals competitors allies enemies foes minions puppets marionettes automatons constructs simulacra avatars projections manifestations emanations extensions extensions extensions...\n\n*End of Page*",
          "key": "f",
