    {
      "cite": "900.8",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Provenance and Authenticity",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Provenance and Authenticity",
          "text": "§ 900.8 Related Requirements\nIn furtherance of the Accords' comprehensive regulatory framework, this section details ancillary obligations applicable to all licensed practitioners regardless of specialization or rank. These stipulations serve as universal baseline expectations for professional conduct and administrative compliance within the Guild's jurisdictional sphere.\n\nAll licensees shall maintain accurate records of every casting performed under their authority, including client identification, purpose, method employed, and outcomes achieved. Such documentation must be retained for a minimum period of five years from date of service, unless superseded by longer retention mandates imposed by specific sub-regulations or special circumstances warranting extended archival periods. Failure to preserve requisite records may constitute grounds for disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure status, depending on severity of omission and resultant impact upon Guild integrity or public safety interests.\n\nWhen engaging in commercial transactions involving magical goods or services, practitioners must disclose any inherent risks associated with their craft prior to entering binding agreements with clients or partners alike. This disclosure obligation extends beyond mere verbal notification; written advisories detailing potential hazards should be furnished whenever feasible under the circumstances. Concealment of material dangers may expose violators to enhanced penalties proportional to degree of deception utilized in procuring consent for risky operations deemed unsuitable without full informed understanding by all parties involved.\n\nPractitioners are further required to promptly report any incidents resulting from their professional activities, whether arising directly from casting errors or indirectly through unforeseen consequences triggered downstream from initial spellwork endeavors. Immediate notification must be provided upon discovery of adverse events affecting persons, property, or natural systems, enabling timely investigation and mitigation efforts before conditions deteriorate beyond manageable limits imposed by existing safeguards established throughout the Guild's regulatory apparatus.\n\nFinally, all licensed casters agree to cooperate fully with any inquiries conducted pursuant to lawful authority, providing such information as reasonably requested necessary for verifying compliance with applicable statutes and regulations governing their respective fields of expertise within scope of official duties assigned hereinby competent oversight bodies empowered by virtue thereof under terms set forth in this Code duly enacted into force upon ratification by majority vote at next scheduled session of Autumnwood Accords Convention duly convened thereafter.\n\nThis section shall be construed liberally to effectuate purposes underlying entire body of laws comprising present codification efforts aimed at securing public welfare through effective regulation of magical practices conducted within territorial boundaries subject to direct supervisory control exercised hereby on behalf of sovereign state interests protected by aforementioned provisions contained herein with binding force upon all persons operating under authority granted pursuant hereto.\n\nsee § 200.1 Licensure Required; see § 400.3 Borrowed Hours; see § 500.1 Field Casting for Value\nunder § 900.8 Related Requirements, practitioners must adhere to strict record-keeping standards while maintaining transparency regarding inherent risks and promptly reporting any adverse outcomes stemming from their professional engagements within the Guild's regulatory framework designed to safeguard public safety and uphold ethical practices across all magical disciplines subject to oversight herein.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.8",
            "200.1",
            "400.3",
            "500.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.8 Related Requirements — Continued",
          "text": "Duty to Disclose Material Enchantments and Defects. Any mage seeking licensure under these Accords shall truthfully disclose all material enchantments, curses, or defects known to them regarding any artifact they submit for evaluation. Concealment of such information shall constitute grounds for immediate revocation of the applicant's credentials and subsequent sanctions under § 1201.4. This duty extends to both original creators and resellers of magical items, ensuring that all parties involved in commerce maintain full transparency concerning potential hazards. Failure to comply may result in permanent disqualification from Guild practice.\n\nRecord Retention and Access Standards. All official records maintained by the Autumnwood Accords Desk shall be retained for a minimum period of ten (10) years unless otherwise specified by law or regulation. These archives shall remain accessible upon written request by licensed mages, investigators, or authorized representatives of other relevant bodies, subject to appropriate security protocols and fees as outlined in § 900.4. Digital copies may be generated at the discretion of the Desk for archival purposes but shall not supplant original documents. Any alterations made to official records must be duly noted and certified by two (2) senior clerks or equivalent authorities.\n\nIdentification and Authentication Fees Structure. Fees associated with identification procedures shall vary according to complexity, risk level, and resource utilization as determined by the Conservators. Base rates for standard evaluations are set forth in Schedule A attached hereto, while specialized examinations may incur additional charges based on scope of work performed. Payment must be made prior to commencement of services unless explicitly waived or deferred under extraordinary circumstances approved by the Desk. Refunds will only be granted upon completion of all required steps and fulfillment of contractual obligations outlined in each individual agreement.\n\nProvenance Verification Protocols for Antiques and Relics. When assessing historical artifacts submitted for appraisal, mages must exhaustively investigate their origins using available databases, cross-referencing with known patterns of forgery or tampering typical to the era concerned. Any suspicions raised during preliminary inspection warrant further scrutiny before final determination can be rendered conclusively. Special attention should be paid to subtle anomalies suggesting artificial enhancement beyond natural capabilities inherent in such objects' age alone. Comprehensive reporting on findings shall accompany every certified assessment issued forthwith thereafter pursuant hereto contained hereinabove within this codified framework governing lawful magical practice today across all realms known unto us all now and forevermore amen so it is written thus we decree under seal of truth eternal!",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1201.4",
            "900.4",
            "900.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.8 Related Requirements — Provenance and Authenticity",
          "text": "§ 900.8 Related Requirements; Authentication of Magical Artifacts\nIn order to maintain the integrity of the magical record, all artifacts submitted for registration shall bear a unique identifier mark affixed by the filing clerk using an iridescent ink pen of the Guild's manufacture. This mark serves both as proof of official acceptance and as a deterrent against counterfeiting or misrepresentation. Any artifact lacking such identification shall be deemed invalid for all purposes under these Accords unless accompanied by written verification from the original creator bearing their personal seal.\n\nFurthermore, parties submitting artifacts for sale, trade, or transfer must declare in writing any known defects, limitations, or hidden enchantments affecting the item's value or safety. Failure to make full disclosure shall constitute fraud and subject the offending party to immediate revocation of licensure and potential criminal prosecution under existing statutes regarding deceptive commercial practices. The Guild reserves the right to order independent appraisal at its sole discretion when suspicious circumstances arise during routine inspection, with all costs borne by the registrant unless negligence is proven.\n\nLast but not least, owners of registered artifacts are reminded that possession does not confer absolute title or immunity from seizure under certain conditions outlined herein. Specifically, the Guild retains a perpetual lien on any item bearing its authentication mark should outstanding debts fail to be satisfied within thirty days of notice. This clause ensures continued compliance with fiscal obligations without compromising ownership rights otherwise vested in lawful holders thereof. It is incumbent upon all participants to familiarize themselves thoroughly with these provisions prior to engaging in any transaction involving magical goods subject to regulation under this Code.\n\nsee § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting; see § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse; see § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items; see § 400.5 Unauthorized Titan Growth.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "900.8",
            "900.1",
            "200.4",
            "300.5",
            "400.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.8 Related Requirements — Chain of Custody and Integrity",
          "text": "The Accords mandate that all items entering the official record must maintain an unbroken chain of custody to ensure their integrity and authenticity. This requirement extends beyond simple possession, encompassing a full audit trail from acquisition through storage and eventual disposition. Any gaps in this documentation render the item suspect and subject to further scrutiny or rejection outright.\n\nProvenance is paramount in determining both value and legal status of magical artifacts. Detailed records must be kept regarding each transfer of ownership, noting date, location, witnesses, and any associated transactions or payments. These logs serve multiple critical functions: they establish legitimate title, prevent fraud or forgery, and provide a historical account of the artifact's journey through time. Failure to maintain such meticulous documentation undermines confidence in the entire regulatory framework.\n\nSpecial attention must be paid to items with contested histories or those acquired under dubious circumstances. Extraordinary diligence is required to verify their lawful origin before acceptance into the official archives. When doubts arise, independent verification by qualified appraisers may be necessary to resolve uncertainties. Until such validation is complete, disputed objects remain in limbo pending further review.\n\nUltimately, preserving the purity and reliability of the Guild's records depends upon strict adherence to these custodial obligations at every stage. Only through unwavering commitment to these standards can the Accords fulfill their purpose as a trustworthy guide for all who wield magic within its bounds. Neglect of this duty compromises everything from individual safety to societal stability itself.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "900.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.8 Related Requirements - Authentication and Verification Protocols",
          "text": "The authentication and verification of filed magical items constitutes a critical component of the Guild's regulatory framework, ensuring both public safety and fiscal integrity. Under § 900.6, all submitted artifacts must undergo rigorous examination by a panel of qualified assessors prior to official registration. This evaluation encompasses not only technical analysis but also historical provenance research, cross-referencing against existing records to detect potential fraud or duplication.\n\nAssessors are tasked with determining the true nature and capabilities of each item, distinguishing between genuine enchantments and mere illusions or imitations. Specialized equipment and controlled testing environments facilitate this process, minimizing risk to both assessors and public safety. All findings must be meticulously documented in the permanent archives, creating an immutable record for future reference.\n\nIn cases where authentication proves ambiguous or contested, appeals may be lodged with the Full Council within thirty days of initial determination. Such reviews shall proceed expeditiously, prioritizing resolution while maintaining impartiality. The Guild's commitment to transparent and fair evaluation procedures serves as a cornerstone of its mission to foster responsible magical innovation while safeguarding societal interests.\n\nThrough these comprehensive protocols, the Autumnwood Accords Desk upholds its mandate as the preeminent authority on magical artifacts within the region. By enforcing strict standards for authentication and verification, the Guild protects both consumers and practitioners from deceptive practices. This vigilant oversight ensures that only legitimate items enter circulation, preserving the integrity of the magical marketplace while promoting safe exploration of arcane disciplines.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "900.6",
            "900.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.8 Related Requirements — Authentication and Verification Protocols",
          "text": "All filings submitted to the Autumnwood Accords Desk shall undergo rigorous authentication prior to acceptance into official records. This process ensures the integrity, authenticity, and legal admissibility of all archived materials. Failure to comply with these protocols may result in rejection of the filing and potential sanctions against the submitting party.\n\nAuthentication begins with visual inspection by a qualified Guild assessor, who examines physical characteristics such as paper quality, ink composition, handwriting style, and any magical signatures or enchantments present on the document. These features are compared against known samples from the same author or era to verify consistency and origin. Digital scans or photographs may also be subjected to forensic analysis where applicable.\n\nOnce visually inspected, each item is subjected to a series of standardized tests designed to detect tampering or forgery. These include spectral imaging to reveal hidden markings or alterations, chemical swabbing for trace residue detection, and specialized equipment capable of identifying synthetic materials disguised as authentic artifacts. Any discrepancies or anomalies discovered during testing must be immediately reported to the Desk Clerk in writing, accompanied by supporting evidence and a detailed explanation thereof.\n\nFor particularly complex cases requiring expert testimony or additional analysis, the Guild may engage independent specialists at its own expense. Such evaluations will be conducted impartially and their findings made available to all interested parties upon request. Results of these comprehensive assessments form an integral part of the final authentication report submitted alongside the original filing.\n\nOnly after successfully passing all applicable checks shall a document be deemed \"fully authenticated\" and eligible for official archiving under this Codebook's provisions. This stringent vetting process serves as the cornerstone of trust within our system, ensuring that every piece of information preserved herein meets the highest standards of accuracy and reliability demanded by law.\n\nIn matters involving high-stakes disputes or novel technologies where precedent is scarce, extra diligence shall be applied to validate submissions beyond ordinary scrutiny levels. The ultimate goal remains unwavering: safeguarding against deception while facilitating transparent exchange of knowledge across generations of mages bound by these sacred vows.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.8"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "Provenance and Authenticity",
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "793287f4b026fe79",
      "refs": [
        "200.1",
        "200.4",
        "300.5",
        "400.3",
        "400.5",
        "500.1",
        "900.1",
