    {
      "cite": "300.9",
      "part": "part_iii",
      "title": "Definitions and Standards",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.9 Related Requirements - Definitions and Standards",
          "text": "The terms \"necromancy,\" \"blood magic,\" and \"forbidden arts\" as used herein shall be construed according to the schedules promulgated by the Conservator's Council, updated quarterly or upon significant discovery of new practices. Any magical technique involving direct manipulation of corpses, skeletal structures, or bone matter for offensive purposes falls under this definition unless expressly exempted by written resolution. See § 300.2 for further elaboration on specific prohibited schools and their rationales.\n\nThe \"Quiet List\" referenced in § 300.4 shall be maintained as a confidential register of particularly dangerous or controversial spells, accessible only to authorized researchers under strict supervision. Publication or recitation of these items outside official channels constitutes a Class A violation subject to immediate penal action under the Paradox Trial provisions. Breaches of this confidentiality requirement may result in permanent revocation of licensure and sealing of magical aptitude. Under § 900.7, any member found distributing Quiet List materials for profit shall be held jointly liable with their employer or patron organization.\n\nResearch into necromantic arts must always prioritize ethical considerations over experimental success, even when pursuing legitimate scientific inquiry. The safety of the public and surrounding communities takes precedence over individual curiosity or academic advancement. See § 400.3 regarding proper containment procedures for hazardous specimens during study periods. Failure to adhere to these guidelines shall be deemed gross negligence warranting disciplinary proceedings under Part III.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to reclassify any magical technique from permitted to forbidden status with retroactive effect if new evidence demonstrates unacceptable risk to society at large. Such actions will be taken only after thorough review by an independent panel of senior mages appointed jointly by the Sovereign and Conservator Council. Any affected parties shall receive written notice thirty days prior to enforcement, allowing opportunity for appeal or modification of the ruling as appropriate. Reference § 100.5 for detailed appellate procedures.\n\nUnauthorized experimentation with necromantic subjects outside approved facilities constitutes a felony offense punishable by imprisonment up to five years plus permanent sealing of magical abilities. Exceptions may be granted only under rare circumstances where substantial public benefit clearly outweighs potential harm, subject to rigorous oversight and post-study auditing. See § 200.4 for the formal process requesting such waivers. Violations of this provision will result in immediate suspension of all privileges pending full investigation into the nature and extent of the misconduct involved.\n\nThe provisions contained herein are intended to balance legitimate scholarly pursuits with necessary protections against misuse of powerful arts. While innovation is encouraged within defined boundaries, absolute adherence to safety protocols remains non-negotiable for all licensed practitioners operating under this Code. Any deviations must be pre-approved in writing by appropriate authorities and documented fully for future reference and accountability purposes. Failure to comply shall subject the offender to full legal consequences as outlined in subsequent articles of this statute book.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "300.4",
            "900.7",
            "400.3",
            "100.5",
            "200.4",
            "300.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.9 Related Requirements - Definitions and Standards — Continued",
          "text": "The term \"Unsanctioned Arts\" refers to magical practices deemed inherently dangerous or immoral by the Autumnwood Council, regardless of intent or outcome. These include but are not limited to blood magic, necromancy, soul-binding, and ritualistic sacrifice. Under § 100.5, any casting of Unsanctioned Arts without explicit permission constitutes a Class A felony punishable by permanent sealing of magical ability via the Paradox Trial (§ 200.7). Permission may be granted only in cases where research potential vastly outweighs societal risk, and even then is subject to stringent oversight per § 400.1.\n\nThe \"Quiet List\" mentioned herein is not a public registry but an internal classification system maintained by the Conservators. Items on this list are considered so hazardous that mere mention of their existence outside authorized channels violates § 300.7. Violations carry penalties under § 200.4 including revocation of licensure and imprisonment. The full contents of the Quiet List remain confidential per § 100.9, accessible only to Council members with a need-to-know clearance level of Black Diamond or higher. Unauthorized publication or dissemination incurs additional sanctions under § 300.8 relating to defamation and endangerment of public safety.\n\nResearch permits (§ 400.2) are issued at the sole discretion of the Innovators' Division upon demonstrated competence and ethical standing. Permit holders must submit quarterly reports detailing all experiments conducted, with results made available for audit under § 100.6. Failure to comply may result in immediate revocation and prosecution under applicable statutes. Constructed entities (§ 200.3) require separate permits regardless of their biological status; unauthorized creation is a felony offense carrying penalties outlined in § 500.7 including mandatory decommissioning and destruction.\n\nThe standards for reclassification (§ 300.4) involve rigorous peer review by the Academic Oversight Committee, which evaluates both theoretical soundness and practical implications. Reclassified items retain their original status until officially updated; interim use requires written approval from two Council members per § 100.8. Research copies (§ 300.5) must be returned upon completion of permitted studies, with all derived data surrendered to the Guild for archival purposes under § 900.7. Unauthorized duplication or distribution violates copyright protections established in § 600.2 and may lead to civil litigation pursuant to applicable laws.\n\nThese provisions serve to balance innovation with safety, ensuring that magical advancement does not come at unacceptable cost to society. All parties are expected to adhere strictly to these guidelines, understanding that failure to do so will result in severe consequences as outlined throughout this Codex and related statutes. The integrity of the Autumnwood Accords depends upon universal compliance, enforced through both legal mechanisms and communal pressure where appropriate.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "200.7",
            "400.1",
            "300.7",
            "200.4",
            "100.9",
            "300.8",
            "400.2",
            "100.6",
            "200.3",
            "500.7",
            "300.4",
            "100.8",
            "300.5",
            "900.7",
            "600.2",
            "300.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.9 Definitions and Standards — Continued",
          "text": "For purposes of these Accords, the term \"necromancy\" encompasses any magical practice that seeks to manipulate death itself, including but not limited to the raising of corpses as servants, the binding of spirits within objects or persons, and the transmutation of flesh into bone through forbidden rites. Such arts are deemed inherently unstable and dangerous, posing a threat both to individual safety and to the social order at large. Consequently, necromancy remains absolutely prohibited under all circumstances, with no exceptions permitted for research, education, or any other stated purpose.\n\nThe prohibition extends beyond mere ritualistic acts; it also encompasses theoretical study and speculative application of necromantic principles. Mages are forbidden from investing personal funds into the acquisition of necromantic tomes, artifacts, or equipment unless explicitly authorized in writing by a panel of senior Arcanists charged with evaluating potential risks and benefits. Any violation of this restriction shall be treated as grounds for immediate revocation of licensure without further process.\n\nFurthermore, all mages are strictly enjoined from utilizing necromancy as a means of personal gain or advancement. This includes but is not limited to the summoning of familiar spirits for assistance in daily tasks, the invocation of ghostly presences to intimidate rivals or adversaries, and the manipulation of spectral entities to achieve otherwise unattainable objectives. Such conduct undermines the very foundation of responsible magical practice and shall be punished severely upon discovery.\n\nIt is incumbent upon every licensed mage to vigilantly monitor their own activities and those of their associates, ensuring that no necromantic influences seep into their sphere of influence. Failure to exercise due diligence in this regard may result in disciplinary action, regardless of whether any actual harm was inflicted. The preservation of public safety and the integrity of the arcane arts are paramount concerns that transcend individual interests or convenience.\n\nIn sum, the ban on necromancy is absolute and unyielding. No amount of theoretical justification or pragmatic necessity can ever override this fundamental tenet of our code. Mages must accept this limitation as an inherent condition of their profession, recognizing that true mastery lies not in pushing boundaries recklessly but in exercising restraint with wisdom and foresight. Only by adhering scrupulously to these guidelines can we hope to safeguard ourselves and society against the perils posed by unchecked necromantic ambition.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "300.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.9 Definitions and Standards — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "The term \"sanctioned arts\" refers to any magical discipline that has been formally catalogued under the Schedules of Arts and is legally permissible for use by licensed casters within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords Desk. Conversely, \"unsanctioned arts\" encompasses all other forms of magic, whether explicitly prohibited or merely unregistered. The distinction carries profound legal consequences, as only sanctioned arts may be employed openly without fear of prosecution under § 300.1 et seq.\n\nThe Quiet List, referenced in § 300.3, serves as the authoritative register for all items deemed potentially dangerous by the Desk's classification committees. Each entry on this list is subject to strict controls regarding its acquisition, storage, and application. Possession of a listed item without proper authorization constitutes a serious offense under both civil and penal law. Exceptions may be made pursuant to § 300.5 for bona fide research purposes conducted in approved facilities by qualified personnel holding valid permits issued under § 400.1 et seq.\n\nReclassification proceedings initiated pursuant to § 300.4 must adhere to rigorous procedural safeguards designed to ensure fairness and accuracy in determining the status of contested artifacts or techniques. Interested parties are entitled to notice, a hearing before an impartial tribunal composed of Guild peers, and full opportunity to present evidence on their behalf. Findings rendered following such proceedings shall be binding unless successfully appealed within the time limits prescribed by § 300.6.\n\nResearch copies of items appearing on the Quiet List may be procured only through formal application submitted in triplicate to the Chief Clerk of the Accords Desk, accompanied by detailed justification outlining intended use and measures for secure containment. Approval hinges upon a thorough review of the applicant's credentials, prior record, and proposed methodology. Copies so authorized shall remain under constant surveillance by appointed overseers charged with verifying compliance with all stipulated conditions throughout the duration of the project (§ 300.5).\n\nAny attempt to circumvent these regulations via proxy arrangements or subterfuge will be treated as an aggravating circumstance warranting enhanced penalties upon conviction. The Guild reserves absolute discretion to revoke licenses, seize assets, and pursue criminal charges against violators without further notice. Adherence to the spirit as well as letter of the Accords is imperative for maintaining public trust in our noble institution.\n\nsee § 100.4 Records of Speech; see § 300.6 Related Requirements; see § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs\nunder § 300.7 Related Requirements, unauthorized duplication or dissemination of protected materials is strictly forbidden and punishable by immediate expulsion from the ranks of licensed practitioners. Violators shall forfeit all rights thereto forevermore. Let this stand as final warning!",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "300.1",
            "300.3",
            "300.5",
            "400.1",
            "300.4",
            "300.6",
            "100.4",
            "400.2",
            "300.7",
            "300.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.9 Definitions and Standards — Continued — Administration",
          "text": "§ 300.9 Definitions and Standards – Cont'd. For the purposes hereof, \"unsanctioned arts\" refers to any magical practice not explicitly enumerated in § 300.1 or otherwise approved by the Desk under a reclassification petition filed pursuant to § 300.4. The mere possession of texts detailing such forbidden techniques does not constitute commission; however, active study without a valid Research Permit issued under § 400.1 shall be deemed an unlawful act subject to penalty under Part IV. \"Necromancy\" is defined as any ritual invoking or binding the spirits of the deceased for purposes other than solemn remembrance or lawful divination, and includes but is not limited to corpse animation, soul binding, and blood sacrifice. Such practices remain permanently prohibited without exception. The term \"research copy,\" when used in connection with Quiet List items, denotes a facsimile reproduction created solely for archival purposes under the supervision of an Aegis Magus; unauthorized duplication or dissemination of such copies shall be prosecuted as forgery and grand larceny. Finally, \"pocket dimension\" is construed to mean any extradimensional space capable of sustaining independent biological function for extended periods, whether naturally occurring or artificially generated via spellcraft. The use thereof without a valid laboratory permit from the Desk constitutes illegal field work under § 500.1 and shall be punished accordingly.\"\n\nThis section clarifies critical terminology essential for proper application of these provisions, ensuring uniform interpretation across all jurisdictions where the Accords hold sway. By establishing precise boundaries between permissible inquiry and forbidden excess, the Guild seeks to balance its dual mandate of fostering magical advancement while safeguarding society from existential threats posed by unchecked experimentation. Any ambiguities shall be resolved in favor of public safety and stability, consistent with the foundational principles enshrined in §§ 1.4 through 1.9.\"\n\nUnder § 300.6 Related Requirements, all mages engaging in study or practice of forbidden arts must register annually with the Conservator's Office to undergo background checks and demonstrate financial solvency. Failure to comply results in immediate suspension of licensure until rectified. Additionally, any individual found guilty of violating these definitions shall face enhanced penalties under Part IV, including mandatory sealing of magical abilities via the Paradox Trial process described in § 1201.6. The Desk reserves the right to amend these standards as new threats emerge, subject to ratification by a two-thirds majority vote at a regular session convened specifically for this purpose.\"\n\nSee also §§ 300.2–300.8 governing the regulation of unsanctioned magical practices and associated offenses.\"",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "300.9",
            "300.1",
            "300.4",
            "400.1",
            "500.1",
            "1.4",
            "300.6",
            "1201.6",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.9 Definitions and Standards — Classification of Magical Conduct",
          "text": "For the purposes of these Accords, \"magical conduct\" encompasses all acts involving the casting of spells, manipulation of arcane energies, or utilization of enchanted items in a manner that could impact public safety or order. Such conduct is categorized into four distinct classes based on inherent risk and potential consequence: Class A (High Risk), Class B (Moderate Risk), Class C (Low Risk), and Class D (Negligent). This taxonomy serves as the primary basis for determining licensing requirements, enforcement priorities, and applicable penalties.\n\nClass A conduct includes any act involving forbidden schools of magic such as necromancy, blood sorcery, or soul-binding arts deemed inherently destructive to the natural order. These acts are prohibited absolutely under § 1401.6 regardless of intent or outcome. Violations constitute immediate grounds for revocation and prosecution under the Paradox Trial provisions. Class B conduct involves high-energy applications such as teleportation circles, dimensional anchors, or mass summoning rituals that could destabilize local reality if mishandled. Practitioners engaging in Class B activities require advanced licensure and must adhere to strict containment protocols outlined in § 1201.5.\n\nClass C encompasses moderate-risk operations like personal wards, utility charms, or minor transmutation spells commonly employed by everyday mages. While less hazardous than higher classes, negligent execution can still result in property damage or injury. Such activities fall under standard regulatory oversight with periodic inspections mandated by § 300.4. Class D represents the lowest tier, covering routine spellcasting that poses minimal threat to persons or infrastructure when performed competently. These actions generally require only basic certification and are subject to random audits rather than systematic review.\n\nThe classification system is designed to facilitate proportionate enforcement while accommodating legitimate magical pursuits within safe parameters. Failure to properly categorize one's activities may lead to escalated scrutiny and heavier penalties should violations occur. Mages bear the burden of accurately self-assessing their conduct's risk level prior to execution, consulting official guides when uncertain under § 1401.7. The Desk reserves authority to reclassify any act de novo based on contextual factors including location, timing, scale, and potential downstream effects.\n\nThis framework ensures that magical practice remains a regulated privilege rather than an unchecked freedom, balancing innovation with necessary safeguards for the greater good. Adherence to these standards reflects responsible stewardship of arcane gifts within our shared society.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1401.6",
            "1201.5",
            "300.4",
            "1401.7",
            "300.9"
          ]
        }
      ],
