    {
      "cite": "400.9",
      "part": "part_iv",
      "title": "Research Integrity and Peer Review",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Research Integrity and Peer Review",
          "text": "Under § 100.6, certain duties may be delegated to clerks for administrative efficiency; this section governs the scope of such delegation specifically regarding research permits and related obligations. While clerks possess no inherent authority beyond their assigned tasks, they may act as proxies for researchers in routine matters directly tied to permit compliance and safety protocols. These proxy actions are binding on the original researcher provided they fall within the clerk's delegated purview under § 100.6(a)(iii).\n\nA primary delegation allowance granted to clerks involves verifying preliminary safety data submitted by applicants during the initial review phase. This includes confirming that proposed research locations meet minimum containment standards and that appropriate safeguards have been implemented per Schedule of Arts requirements. Clerks are empowered to request additional information or clarification from researchers as necessary to complete this verification, but they lack authority to make substantive judgments on technical matters beyond their training. Any dispute arising from a clerk's preliminary findings must be elevated to the full Desk for final adjudication under § 200.4(b).\n\nClerks further may facilitate communication between researchers and external regulatory bodies where applicable, transmitting required documentation and coordinating inspection schedules as directed by senior officials. However, they shall not negotiate terms or agree to extensions on behalf of the Guild without express authorization in writing from an Archmage. Unauthorized commitments made by clerks are void ab initio unless ratified within seven days under § 100.6(c). This limitation exists to prevent erosion of centralized control over sensitive research activities and to protect the Guild's strategic interests against inadvertent liability exposure.\n\nIn cases where delegation proves insufficient due to complexity or urgency, researchers may petition for temporary assignment of a full-time clerk liaison to oversee their project exclusively. Such liaisons possess expanded powers commensurate with their role as dedicated representatives of the Desk, including onsite monitoring and immediate enforcement authority within research facilities. Liaison appointments remain subject to recall at any time without cause under § 100.6(d), ensuring that researchers maintain ultimate accountability for their operations regardless of temporary staffing arrangements. All delegated functions must be documented in official records pursuant to § 200.4(a) to ensure transparency and facilitate future audits or investigations as needed.\n\nsee § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks\nsee § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse\nsee § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "200.4",
            "100.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 400.9 Research Integrity and Peer Review",
          "text": "All research conducted under the authority of a permit shall adhere to the highest standards of academic integrity, safety, and ethical conduct. Any findings or conclusions reached by an Innovator must be submitted for review by at least two independent Conservators before being deemed official. This peer-review process ensures that magical breakthroughs do not inadvertently destabilize local ecosystems or violate fundamental laws of nature.\n\nInnovators found falsifying data, concealing dangerous results, or acting outside the scope of their permit shall face immediate revocation of credentials and potential criminal charges under § 500.12. The Desk maintains a database of all research outcomes to track long-term impacts on the broader magical community. Failure to register results within thirty days may lead to suspension of future permits for the offending party or their associates.\n\nSpecial provisions apply when research involves sentient subjects, classified materials, or cross-dimensional entities. Such projects require heightened supervision and mandatory debriefings after each session. The Desk reserves the right to terminate any study deemed too risky or unethical without notice. Innovators are reminded that personal gain must never supersede public safety in pursuit of magical knowledge.\n\nThe spirit of this section is to balance the freedom of inquiry with prudent oversight, recognizing that unchecked experimentation could unleash forces beyond our comprehension. By fostering accountability and collaboration between factions, we may unlock new frontiers while minimizing harm to ourselves and future generations. Let us proceed with humility, caution, and an unwavering commitment to the greater good.\n\nsee § 400.1 Research Permits; see § 500.12 Criminal Penalties for Falsification; see § 200.3 Temporary Mouths and Their Limitations; under § 1.7 Aggregation of Conduct, repeated violations may warrant harsher sanctions.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "500.12",
            "400.1",
            "200.3",
            "1.7",
            "400.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Research Oversight Committee Functions and Powers",
          "text": "The Research Oversight Committee, hereinafter referred to as the \"ROC,\" shall serve as the primary governing body for the regulation of all magical research conducted under the auspices of the Mages' Guild. The ROC shall consist of five members appointed by the Archmage Sovereign, comprising at least two Conservators, two Innovators, and one neutral arbiter selected from among the Aegis Magi. These appointments shall remain in effect for a term of three years, renewable upon reappointment by the Archmage Sovereign.\n\nThe ROC shall meet quarterly to review submitted research proposals, evaluate their potential risks and benefits, and grant or deny permissions accordingly. In addition, the ROC shall conduct periodic audits of ongoing projects to ensure compliance with all applicable laws and regulations. Any member found to be in violation of established protocols shall face immediate suspension from further research activities until such time as a full investigation has been completed and satisfactory remedial actions have been taken.\n\nThe powers granted to the ROC include, but are not limited to: (a) reviewing and approving or denying all new research proposals; (b) setting standards for acceptable experimental procedures; (c) mandating safety measures and containment protocols; (d) imposing penalties for non-compliance; (e) recommending disciplinary action against errant researchers; and (f) advising the Archmage Sovereign on matters pertaining to magical development. The ROC shall maintain a detailed log of all its proceedings, which shall be made available upon request by any interested party.\n\nIt is hereby declared that no member of the Mages' Guild shall engage in any form of unauthorized research without first obtaining written permission from the Research Oversight Committee. Failure to comply with this provision shall result in automatic revocation of one's licensure and potential criminal prosecution under applicable laws. The ROC retains absolute discretion to revoke previously granted permissions at its sole discretion, should it determine that continued approval would pose an unacceptable risk to public safety or magical stability.\n\nIn accordance with these provisions, the Research Oversight Committee shall exercise diligent oversight over all aspects of magical research within the Guild's jurisdiction, ensuring that such endeavors are conducted safely and responsibly while fostering innovation and progress in the field of arcane studies. The ROC shall remain ever vigilant against any threats posed by unchecked experimentation or misuse of forbidden arts, acting swiftly to mitigate potential dangers to society at large. Through its steadfast dedication to preserving both knowledge and security, the Research Oversight Committee stands as a bastion of responsible stewardship for all who seek to unlock the mysteries of magic under lawful guidance.\n\nsee § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks; see § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse; see § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items; see § 400.7 Related Requirements.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "200.4",
            "300.5",
            "400.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Research Oversight and Peer Review",
          "text": "§ 400.9 Related Requirements\n\nThe integrity of magical research is paramount to the safety and stability of society. To this end, all studies involving classified or high-risk arts shall undergo rigorous peer review by qualified experts in relevant fields. The Research Oversight Committee (\"ROC\") established under § 401.2 serves as the primary body for evaluating proposed projects, assessing potential hazards, and granting necessary approvals. ROC members must maintain impartiality and disclose any conflicts of interest as mandated by § 500.7.\n\nThe peer review process shall commence with submission of a comprehensive proposal outlining research objectives, methodologies, risk mitigation strategies, and anticipated outcomes. The ROC will scrutinize each element meticulously, consulting external specialists as needed to ensure thorough vetting. Projects deemed too dangerous or unethical may be rejected outright, while others might receive conditional approval subject to strict monitoring. Throughout execution, researchers must provide regular progress reports detailing findings, incidents, and any deviations from the approved plan.\n\nUpon completion of studies, final results shall be presented to the ROC for assessment against original hypotheses and safety parameters. Positive outcomes may lead to wider dissemination through published papers or presentations at professional conferences. Conversely, adverse effects or procedural failures will trigger an immediate investigation into root causes and potential liabilities. In cases where misconduct is discovered, appropriate disciplinary action will ensue in accordance with established protocols.\n\nThis robust framework ensures that magical inquiry proceeds responsibly, balancing the pursuit of knowledge with prudent safeguards for all concerned parties. By fostering a culture of accountability and transparency within our scholarly community, we uphold the highest standards of ethical practice while advancing the frontiers of arcane understanding.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "400.9",
            "401.2",
            "500.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Research Oversight and Peer Review Mechanisms",
          "text": "Under this Part, the integrity of all research activities conducted within authorized facilities shall be maintained through a rigorous system of oversight and peer review. § 400.6 establishes that every permit issued under this section must include explicit terms requiring periodic inspection by designated Guild auditors to verify compliance with safety protocols and ethical standards. Failure to undergo such inspections may result in immediate suspension of research privileges until full compliance is demonstrated.\n\n§ 400.7 further mandates the formation of independent Research Oversight Committees (ROC) composed of senior mages not directly involved in specific projects under review. These ROCs shall examine all proposed methodologies, data analyses, and final reports submitted by investigators to ensure adherence to established scientific principles and moral guidelines. The findings of each committee must be formally recorded and made available for public scrutiny unless classified information is invoked pursuant to § 300.5.\n\nIn cases where irregularities or potential violations are identified during the review process, the ROC possesses broad authority to impose corrective measures ranging from additional audits to outright revocation of research permissions. Such actions shall be taken without prejudice to any subsequent disciplinary proceedings that may arise. The Guild reserves the right to audit historical records indefinitely should new evidence emerge suggesting prior misconduct was concealed or misreported.\n\n§ 400.8 clarifies that all findings generated through permitted research remain the exclusive property of the Mages' Guild unless explicit licensing agreements are executed between researchers and interested third parties. Unauthorized dissemination of proprietary data may constitute grounds for immediate expulsion from the organization alongside civil penalties. This provision serves to protect both commercial interests and national security considerations inherent in advanced magical studies.\n\nThe overarching purpose of these requirements is twofold: first, to safeguard public welfare by preventing reckless experimentation that could lead to catastrophic consequences; second, to preserve the Guild's reputation as a responsible steward of arcane knowledge. By instituting comprehensive checks and balances, Autumnwood ensures that progress does not come at the expense of safety or ethics. All researchers are reminded that their privileges carry corresponding obligations to uphold these fundamental principles in all their endeavors.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "400.6",
            "400.7",
            "300.5",
            "400.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Dispute Resolution and the Appointment of Independent Peer Auditors",
          "text": "Whereas the integrity of all magical research must be preserved to ensure public safety and maintain the balance of natural forces, it is hereby mandated that all studies conducted under permit shall adhere to strict ethical standards as enforced by the Research Oversight Committee established under § 400.12. This section delineates the specific powers and procedural mechanisms granted to the Committee for the adjudication of disputes arising from alleged misconduct, data fabrication, or deviation from approved methodologies within any sanctioned laboratory or field site. When a preliminary violation is identified during an audit pursuant to § 400.10, or when a supervising mage reports irregularities in energy expenditure that suggest unauthorized experimentation as defined in § 400.11, the Committee shall convene a Peer Review Panel within seven solar days of such notification. The composition of this Panel shall consist of three senior mages who possess no prior affiliation with the subject of the inquiry, ensuring an unbiased assessment that aligns with the foundational principles of mediation found in § 1.16 and § 1.17.\n\nThe authority to summon witnesses and demand access to sealed research logs is expressly vested in the Chair of the Research Oversight Committee, provided such summons are issued under the seal of the Guild Master as authorized by § 100.2. It shall be the duty of any researcher summoned before a Peer Review Panel to appear at the designated hearing location unless a valid exemption is granted based on the nature of the research subject or the safety protocols outlined in § 400.8. Failure to appear without a written justification submitted to the Archmage Sovereign, whose authority in mediation disputes is paramount per § 1.16, shall be construed as a willful obstruction of oversight and may result in the immediate suspension of the researcher’s permit under provisions similar to those governing unauthorized field casting in § 400.14. The Panel shall have the discretion to adjourn proceedings if the presence of the accused mage is deemed necessary for the accurate assessment of complex arcane phenomena, though such adjournments must not exceed thirty days without further executive review.\n\nIn cases where the Peer Review Panel finds sufficient evidence of systemic negligence or intentional deception that compromises the safety of a pocket dimension laboratory established under § 400.2, the Committee shall issue a formal finding of non-compliance which triggers an automatic audit pursuant to § 400.10. This finding serves as the primary administrative basis for escalating matters to the Paradox Trial if the violation involves acts that threaten the stability of reality or breach ancient pacts regarding mana reverence. The Panel's deliberations shall be confidential until a final ruling is rendered, a measure intended to protect the confidentiality of ongoing investigations and prevent the contamination of research data by external observers, consistent with the strict protocols for exclusionary hearings detailed in § 100.17. Any recommendations made by the Panel regarding the revocation of permits or the imposition of sanctions must be forwarded to the Archmage Sovereign for final approval, ensuring that the sovereignty of the Guild remains intact while upholding the collective responsibility for research integrity.\n\nFurthermore, the Peer Review Mechanism shall include a provision for the appointment of independent technical auditors who are not members of the immediate faculty but hold standing accreditation as neutral observers under § 400.15. These auditors possess the authority to enter any restricted zone without prior notice if there is an imminent threat to public safety or if previous oversight protocols have failed to detect anomalies in mana flow. The findings of these independent auditors shall carry equal weight with those of the primary Peer Review Panel and may be used to overturn preliminary conclusions that appear biased or incomplete. It is further ordered that all decisions made by the Committee regarding the validity of a research project shall be recorded in the official archives and cross-referenced against the classification standards set forth in § 300.4, ensuring that no research subject is misclassified or allowed to proceed under false pretenses. Should the Archmage Sovereign determine that the Peer Review Panel has erred in its judgment or failed to act with due diligence, the Sovereign reserves the right to appoint a special commission empowered to re-evaluate the case de novo, thereby maintaining the highest standard of accountability within the Mages' Guild Accords.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "400.12",
