    {
      "cite": "900.9",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "See Also Provisions",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.9 Related Requirements; See Also Provisions",
          "text": "In addition to the obligations set forth herein, any party engaging in licensed magical practice shall comply with all applicable provisions of this Code and those referenced thereto. This includes adherence to standards for safety, efficacy, and ethical conduct as established by the Mages' Guild or its duly appointed delegates. Failure to do so may result in disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure or criminal prosecution, depending on the severity and circumstances of the violation.\n\nFurthermore, all casters must maintain accurate records of their activities for a minimum period specified by regulation. These logs shall include details such as dates, times, locations, purposes, participants, materials used, and outcomes achieved. Such documentation serves both administrative oversight functions and historical preservation efforts within the broader magical community. Regular audits may be conducted to ensure compliance with reporting mandates.\n\nAny disputes arising from interpretation of these provisions or their application to specific situations shall be resolved through established dispute resolution mechanisms outlined elsewhere in this Code. This encompasses mediation, arbitration, and formal adjudication as appropriate under the facts and law governing each case. Parties are expected to act in good faith throughout the process and cooperate fully with authorities investigating potential breaches of law.\n\nThe foregoing requirements reflect the collective wisdom accumulated over generations of practice and reflection upon lessons learned hard-won through trial-and-error experience across cultures worldwide. They embody our shared commitment to responsible stewardship of magical power for benefit rather than harm alone. By accepting licensure, practitioners implicitly agree to abide by these rules willingly knowing full well consequences thereof should they fail so to do henceforth forevermore without regret nevermore!\n\nsee § 100.4 Records of Speech; see § 200.1 Licensure Required; under § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items\n§ 900.9 Related Requirements; See Also Provisions (End of Section)",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "100.4",
            "200.1",
            "300.5",
            "900.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.10 Jurisdictional Immunity and Privilege",
          "text": "Section 900.10 establishes the legal immunities enjoyed by duly licensed mages when acting within the scope of their authorized practice. Under § 900.1(a), a registered practitioner shall not be held personally liable for civil damages resulting from casting operations conducted in compliance with all applicable laws and guild regulations. This immunity extends to both professional services rendered and incidental activities directly related thereto, as delineated in § 900.1(b).\n\nHowever, this protection is not absolute. Pursuant to § 900.2(a), a caster shall forfeit their immunity if they engage in gross negligence or willful misconduct. Such actions include but are not limited to: unauthorized modification of magical devices (§ 900.2(b)), casting outside designated parameters without proper permits (§ 900.2(c)), and failure to maintain required insurance coverage (§ 900.2(d)). The burden of proof regarding a mage's compliance lies with the practitioner, who must demonstrate adherence to all relevant statutes upon request from any interested party under § 900.3(a).\n\nFurthermore, Section 900.4 addresses the issue of vicarious liability for employer-casters and their associates. While principal casters retain immunity under normal circumstances, § 900.4(a) stipulates that supervising entities remain responsible for ensuring proper oversight and training protocols are followed. Failure to provide adequate supervision may result in joint and several liability for resulting harms, as outlined in § 900.4(b). This provision aims to promote accountability throughout the entire casting operation chain.\n\nFinally, Section 900.5 clarifies the limitations of this immunity when third-party injuries occur. While practitioners are shielded from suits arising directly from their lawful work, § 900.5(a) preserves the right of any injured party to seek redress for intentional torts or criminal offenses committed by casters outside authorized activities. Such matters shall be adjudicated according to standard legal procedures rather than through guild mechanisms alone, per § 900.5(b). This ensures that immunity provisions do not create loopholes for malicious actors evading justice under the guise of professional practice.\n\nIn sum, Section 900.10 balances the need to protect legitimate magical practitioners with safeguards against abuse of their privileged status. By defining clear boundaries for acceptable conduct and assigning appropriate levels of responsibility, these rules foster a safer environment for all parties involved in arcane endeavors. Compliance with this framework remains mandatory for continued licensure under the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "900.2",
            "900.3",
            "900.4",
            "900.5",
            "900.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.9 Related Requirements – See Also Provisions",
          "text": "In addition to the primary filing obligations outlined herein, mages shall comply with all ancillary requirements applicable to their specific circumstances and practices. These include, but are not limited to: timely submission of supplemental reports following any material change in status or activity; adherence to seasonal casting windows prescribed by local authorities; payment of all outstanding fees within thirty (30) days of invoice receipt; and maintenance of accurate personal ledgers documenting every spell cast for at least five consecutive years. Failure to meet these secondary obligations may result in escalated penalties, including suspension of privileges until compliance is achieved.\n\nSee also § 900.1 (Duty to File Before Casting), which establishes the fundamental prerequisite of pre-authorization for all magical operations; § 200.3 (Temporary Mouths), governing the limited use of proxies under strict oversight; and § 400.5 (Unauthorized Titan Growth), prohibiting unregulated expansion of one's own mana reservoir without explicit council approval. These provisions collectively ensure comprehensive regulatory coverage across all facets of arcane practice, from initial licensure through ongoing conduct review.\n\nMages are further reminded that the Guild retains inherent authority to impose additional conditions deemed necessary for public safety or resource management at any time. Such directives shall take precedence over any prior agreements or customary usages. Resistance to lawful orders constitutes grounds for immediate revocation of credentials and possible criminal prosecution under relevant statutes. The spirit of these related requirements mirrors that of the core mandates: preservation of order, protection of citizens, and sustainable stewardship of magical potential.\n\nUnder § 900.7 (Foreign Halls and Courtesy), practitioners operating abroad must respect host nation regulations even when exempt from domestic filing duties. Similarly, § 1201.6 (Related Requirements) extends analogous expectations to cross-jurisdictional collaborations. Ultimately, the scope of a caster's obligations cannot be narrowly construed; they encompass all actions reasonably implicated by their professional role. As stated in § 300.7 (Related Requirements), \"the law follows the magic,\" meaning wherever one turns their art, so too must they turn their attention to lawful compliance.\"\n\nThis provision also incorporates by reference various industry standards and best practices developed over centuries of collective wisdom. While not exhaustive, these guidelines represent a baseline for ethical conduct expected of every registered practitioner. Deviation therefrom requires compelling justification approved in advance by the appropriate oversight body. Self-regulation remains paramount; only through vigilant adherence to both letter and intent can we safeguard our shared future against the perils of unchecked ambition.\n\nFor further guidance on interpreting these supplementary mandates, consult the Master Registry or seek counsel from an experienced mentor. Remember always that true mastery lies not merely in command of spellwork, but in disciplined self-governance—a cornerstone upon which all responsible magic builds. Thus do we uphold our vows and secure our standing within this noble profession forevermore.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "200.3",
            "400.5",
            "900.7",
            "1201.6",
            "300.7",
            "900.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.10 Immunity and Privilege Provisions",
          "text": "Under § 900.6–900.8, mages are obligated to file detailed reports of all casting activities before the Accords Desk. These provisions establish the framework for oversight and accountability within the Guild's regulatory regime. However, recognition must be made that such oversight cannot extend indefinitely without impairing the practical functioning of magic itself. To balance these competing interests, certain exceptions and immunities are hereby codified to protect both casters and third parties from undue interference.\n\n§ 900.10 Immunity and Privilege Provisions\n(a) **Judicial Immunity.** No caster shall be subject to civil or criminal liability for actions taken in the course of lawful casting operations, provided such actions were not willfully malicious or grossly negligent. This immunity extends to all acts occurring within the scope of employment as a licensed artisan or contractor. See § 900.1–900.5 regarding filing and reporting obligations that define \"lawful operation.\"\n\n(b) **Privileged Communications.** All communications between casters and their clients, contractors, or fellow practitioners concerning casting matters are privileged and confidential unless waived in writing. This privilege protects the integrity of professional relationships and prevents unwarranted intrusion into private business affairs. See § 900.1–900.5 for definitions of \"casting operations\" that trigger these protections.\n\n(c) **Sovereign Immunity.** The Autumnwood Accords Desk and its agents enjoy absolute immunity from suit except as expressly provided herein. This reflects the public interest in maintaining an efficient regulatory apparatus free from harassment by litigants seeking to circumvent statutory requirements. See § 900.1–900.5 regarding the scope of official duties that attract such protections.\n\n(d) **Safe Harbor Provisions.** Casters acting in good faith reliance upon written authorization or approval from the Accords Desk shall not be liable for ordinary negligence resulting therefrom. This encourages responsible innovation while preserving liability for intentional misconduct or gross deviation from established standards. See § 900.1–900.5 for guidance on what constitutes valid authorization under this regime.\"\n\n(e) **Limitations and Exceptions.** The foregoing immunities and privileges shall not apply where: (i) the caster acted with intent to harm; (ii) the conduct fell outside the scope of authorized activities; or (iii) third-party rights were deliberately violated. In such instances, ordinary tort principles govern liability determinations. See § 900.1–900.5 for further elaboration on these critical distinctions.\"\n\nThese provisions collectively serve to delineate the boundaries of permissible regulatory oversight while safeguarding against abuse of power by both state actors and private parties alike. Through careful calibration, they enable the Guild to fulfill its mandate without stifling legitimate magical practice or infringing upon fundamental liberties. Thus stands § 900.10 as an essential component of the broader statutory scheme governing arcane arts in this realm.\"",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "900.6",
            "900.10",
            "900.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "900.10 Immunity and Privilege Provisions",
          "text": "Under § 900.6, mages are required to file detailed manifests prior to casting potent spells within Autumnwood jurisdiction. Failure to comply renders the act void ab initio and subject to punitive sanctions per the Accords' penal provisions. This filing obligation extends to all recognized practitioners regardless of affiliation or rank, ensuring comprehensive oversight of magical activity across the region.\n\nRelated Requirements: § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting; § 900.2 Reporting of Material Enchantments; § 900.3 Record Retention; § 900.4 Identify Fees Who Pays; § 900.5 False Provenance of Relics; § 900.6 Related Requirements; § 900.7 Related Requirements; § 900.8 Related Requirements\n\nSee Also Provisions, § 900.11 Immunity from Civil Litigation, § 900.12 Criminal Jurisdiction and Enforcement, § 900.13 Diplomatic Privileges for Foreign Representatives, § 900.14 Tax Exemptions on Magical Goods\n\nThe filing regime serves multiple purposes: establishing a baseline of normal magical activity to detect anomalies, creating an audit trail for post-casting disputes, and enabling pre-emptive intervention should risks arise. Practitioners must submit complete details including spell type, intended effect, location, duration, and any ancillary materials employed. Omissions or inaccuracies may invalidate the entire filing and trigger investigations into potential violations of the Accords' core tenets.\n\nImmunity Provisions are integral to maintaining order within this complex legal landscape, shielding good-faith actors from unwarranted interference while preserving the state's ability to enforce compliance where necessary. These safeguards apply uniformly to all registered mages operating under the authority of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, regardless of their individual circumstances or affiliations with various magical organizations.\n\nBy adhering strictly to these filing mandates and respecting applicable immunities and privileges, practitioners can navigate the intricate web of regulations governing magical practice in this jurisdiction with confidence and clarity, contributing to a stable and prosperous environment for all residents.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "900.6",
            "900.1",
            "900.2",
            "900.3",
            "900.4",
            "900.5",
            "900.7",
            "900.8",
            "900.11",
            "900.12",
            "900.13",
            "900.14"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.10 Immunity and Privilege Provisions — Continued",
          "text": "All persons duly licensed or otherwise authorized under this Act, in the performance of their official duties as such, are hereby granted full immunity from any criminal or civil liability arising out of acts committed within the scope of their employment. Such immunity extends to actions taken in good faith reliance on directions received from superiors, whether oral or written. This provision is intended to foster an environment where magistrates may perform their functions without fear of retribution or undue interference.\n\nIt shall be further provided that no suit or proceeding may be maintained against any such officer or employee for damages resulting from the exercise or non-exercise of any discretion vested by law in respect thereof, unless there is shown to have been gross negligence or willful misconduct. The burden of proof regarding either of these conditions rests solely upon the plaintiff and must be sustained by clear and convincing evidence before any claim may proceed past summary judgment stage.\n\nIn addition to foregoing protections, all duly appointed magistrates are afforded certain privileges in connection with their official capacities. These include exemption from jury duty while on active assignment, priority access to government facilities for investigative purposes, and reimbursement of reasonable travel expenses incurred in the course of performing assigned tasks. Such benefits shall remain in full force notwithstanding any conflicting provisions contained within other statutes or regulations.\n\nFinally, nothing herein shall be construed as waiving any right otherwise enjoyed by individual citizens under domestic law or treaty obligations binding upon the United States. Rather, it represents a careful balancing act between protecting public safety and safeguarding constitutional liberties against potential abuses inherent in granting broad powers to those responsible for enforcing them. Only through such delicate equilibrium can we ensure both effective governance and continued respect for fundamental freedoms alike.\n\nSee also § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting; § 200.5 Guest Casters; § 400.3 Borrowed Hours – Interest in Time. These provisions collectively define the scope of lawful magical practice under our jurisdiction, balancing efficiency with responsibility at every turn. Together they form an integrated framework designed to promote harmony between tradition and innovation alike.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "200.5",
            "400.3",
            "900.10"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "In addition to the obligations set forth herein, any party engaging in licensed",
