    {
      "cite": "500.9",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Unlicensed Artisanry",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Unlicensed Artisanry",
          "text": "The provisions herein shall be read in conjunction with the related requirements set forth in §§ 1.4 through 1.7, which govern the general applicability and construction of this Code. Specifically, all persons subject to these rules are deemed to have notice thereof by virtue of their participation in the magical community under the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords Desk. See § 1.4 Persons Bound; see also § 1.6 Definitions for clarification of technical terms employed throughout.\n\nFurther, nothing contained in this Part shall be construed to override or diminish any applicable statutes, ordinances, or regulations enacted by duly constituted authorities within the Hall's territories and spheres of influence. The Desk reserves the right to refer disputes involving overlapping jurisdictions to appropriate administrative bodies for resolution pursuant to established inter-agency protocols under § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy.\n\nIt is expressly declared that failure to comply with any provision set forth herein shall constitute a material breach of license, subjecting the offending party to immediate revocation of privileges and such additional sanctions as may be warranted under existing penal codes. Aggravating factors include willful defiance, repeated infractions, or actions causing significant harm to persons or property. Mitigating circumstances may be taken into account at the Desk's sole discretion, provided they are supported by credible evidence.\n\nIn cases of ambiguity regarding intent or scope, interpretation shall favor public safety and the preservation of peace over narrow literalism. This principle derives from the foundational purpose of the Accords—to regulate magic responsibly while enabling beneficial advancement. See § 1.7 Construction; compare with § 300.2 Necromancy: Unsanctioned Arts for an example of strict construction applied to particularly hazardous practices.\n\nThe Desk shall maintain a registry of all complaints and adjudications arising under this section, accessible upon written request by interested parties or their duly authorized representatives. This ensures transparency and accountability in the administration of justice within the magical community. Records shall be updated promptly following final disposition of each case, reflecting current enforcement trends and emerging patterns of noncompliance as relevant to future regulatory decisions.\n\nFinally, nothing herein shall be construed to create any right or remedy beyond those explicitly granted by this Code or existing law. All claims for damages, penalties, or other relief must be pursued through proper legal channels and in accordance with applicable statutes of limitation. The Desk acts solely in its capacity as an impartial arbiter of the Accords, without prejudice to any inherent rights retained by individuals, corporations, or entities subject thereto.\n\nBy failing to adhere fully to these provisions, licensees risk not only disciplinary action but also erosion of public trust and potential revocation of their standing within the magical ecosystem altogether. Prudent adherence to ethical standards and lawful conduct remains paramount for all practitioners seeking long-term success in this demanding field. As Archmage Veyra herself has often stated: \"True mastery lies not merely in raw power, but in wise restraint.\" Let these words guide your path forward under the watchful eye of the Accords.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "1.6",
            "100.7",
            "1.7",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.9 Related Requirements - Unlicensed Artisanry",
          "text": "The practice of casting spells for compensation without an active license is strictly prohibited under the Autumnwood Accords, with severe penalties applicable to all violators. Such unlicensed artistry not only undermines the regulatory framework but also poses grave risks to public safety and magical integrity. The Guild maintains a zero-tolerance policy regarding rogue practitioners who operate outside established channels, regardless of their claimed expertise or proven track record.\n\nAny individual found guilty of performing unauthorized magic for profit shall face immediate revocation of any existing credentials and permanent disqualification from future licensure. In addition to these administrative sanctions, the offender may be subject to civil liability for any damages resulting from their negligent or reckless casting. The burden of proof rests squarely upon the accuser to demonstrate that the accused engaged in illicit activity with intent to defraud or endanger others.\n\nFurthermore, all suspected instances of unlicensed artistry must be reported promptly to the nearest Guild inspector for investigation and adjudication. Failure to comply with this reporting requirement may result in disciplinary action against the responsible party, up to and including expulsion from the Hall. The Guild reserves the right to initiate criminal proceedings where appropriate, particularly when allegations involve serious bodily harm or loss of life attributable to faulty spellwork.\n\nIt is imperative that all members of the magical community uphold the highest standards of professionalism and accountability in their professional dealings. By adhering rigorously to these guidelines, we ensure the continued safety and prosperity of our shared society while preserving the dignity and prestige of the Guild itself. Any deviation from these principles threatens not only individual livelihoods but the very foundation upon which our collective well-being rests.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "500.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.9 Unlicensed Artisanry and the Gray Market",
          "text": "§ 500.9 Unlicensed Artisanry and the Gray Market. The Guild maintains that all magical commodities, whether crafted by licensed artisans or scavenged from wild sources, must bear a valid mark of origin to enter lawful commerce. This provision seeks to eradicate counterfeiting, substandard goods, and the black market in arcane paraphernalia which undermines both consumer safety and tax revenue. Under this section, any item lacking proper documentation is presumed illicit until proven otherwise through rigorous inspection by Guild auditors. Penalties for trafficking unmarked wares range from confiscation of contraband to revocation of merchant licenses and imprisonment. The intent is twofold: first, to protect citizens from harmful or ineffective magical items; second, to ensure fair competition among registered producers who adhere to quality standards. Violators face strict liability regardless of intent, as the burden rests upon sellers to demonstrate lawful provenance. Only with this ironclad regulation can the Guild guarantee the integrity and safety of all goods bearing its seal.\n\n\"see § 500.1 Field Casting for Value; see § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild; under § 400.3 Borrowed Hours Interest in Time\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "500.9",
            "500.1",
            "100.2",
            "400.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.9 Unlicensed Artisanry and the Gray Market — Continued",
          "text": "The Accords recognize that not all magical labor falls within the purview of formal licensure. Where an individual operates outside the Guild's regulatory framework, either by choice or circumstance, such activity constitutes \"gray market\" casting. While technically unregulated, gray market practices are neither inherently illegal nor protected; they exist in a legal limbo subject to discretionary enforcement.\n\nThe Aegis Magi retain broad authority to investigate suspected violations of guild statutes, including unauthorized field operations conducted without permits. Upon discovery of evidence suggesting illicit activity, inspectors may issue citations for non-compliance with applicable laws and ordinances. Failure to remedy such violations within a reasonable timeframe may result in escalating penalties, up to and including revocation of any existing credentials.\n\nParties engaging in gray market casting do so at their own risk. The Guild bears no liability for injuries or damages arising from unlicensed practices, whether suffered by the caster themselves or third parties. Conversely, victims seeking redress must pursue claims directly against responsible individuals, absent proof of official negligence by guild agents.\n\nThis provision reflects the complex reality that magic permeates society beyond formal channels. Rather than attempting to eradicate all unauthorized use, the Accords acknowledge its inevitability while maintaining robust oversight mechanisms. The balance between freedom and control remains an enduring challenge for regulators in this field.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "500.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.9 Unlicensed Artisanry and the Gray Market — Administration",
          "text": "§ 500.9 Unlicensed Artisanry and the Gray Market\n\nNo caster shall, for consideration or profit, employ any spellwork not included in their official license without prior written authorization from the Guild Desk. Violation constitutes unlicensed artisanry under § 200.1(a)(7), subject to disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure. This prohibition extends to all forms of magical practice, whether performed personally or through proxies, constructs, or other agents.\n\nThe gray market for illicit spellcasting thrives despite strict prohibitions, driven by the insatiable demand for unauthorized services ranging from simple buffs to complex ritual workings. Such activities undermine the integrity of licensure and expose practitioners to significant legal risks if discovered. The Desk maintains a vigilant watch over suspected operations, utilizing informants and surveillance where necessary. Confirmed offenders face escalating penalties based on severity and frequency of violations.\n\nCasters are reminded that the distinction between licensed and unlicensed work is not merely bureaucratic pedantry but a fundamental safeguard for public safety and magical equilibrium. Unauthorized practices may inadvertently trigger unforeseen consequences beyond the caster's control, endangering both client and caster alike. The Guild stands ready to assist responsible practitioners in expanding their capabilities through proper channels rather than risking criminal prosecution via gray market shortcuts.\n\nSee also § 200.1 Licensure Required; § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts; § 400.1 Research Permits for related provisions governing the scope and limitations of authorized magical activities. Failure to comply with these requirements may result in immediate suspension pending investigation, regardless of apparent benign intent or outcome. The burden of proof rests squarely upon the accused caster to demonstrate compliance with all applicable statutes and regulations.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "500.9",
            "200.1",
            "300.2",
            "400.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.9 Unlicensed Artisanry and the Gray Market — Administration — Continued",
          "text": "The clandestine production and distribution of unauthorized magical items constitutes a persistent threat to public safety and the integrity of the Guild's regulatory framework. Such \"gray market\" operations typically bypass critical quality controls, risk assessments, and licensing requirements, resulting in substandard goods that may malfunction catastrophically or cause irreparable harm to users and bystanders alike. To combat this growing problem, the Autumnwood Accords empower the Aegis Magi with broad authority to investigate suspected violations, seize illicit stockpiles, and prosecute offending parties under the full weight of the penal code.\n\nInvestigations into unlicensed artisanry shall be conducted with discretion but thoroughness. The Aegis may enter properties without warrant upon reasonable suspicion, subject to subsequent judicial review. All seized materials must be inventoried in triplicate and held in secure custody pending resolution of legal proceedings. Owners or operators found guilty of violating § 500.1 shall face heavy fines up to three times the value of illegal goods produced, plus restitution for all damages caused. Repeat offenders may have their magic sealed permanently and be barred from ever seeking licensure again.\n\nThe Guild recognizes that total eradication of gray market activity is an elusive goal given its decentralized nature. Rather than pursuing futile crackdowns alone, the Aegis shall actively collaborate with law enforcement agencies to disrupt major distribution networks and dismantle organized criminal enterprises profiting from unlicensed magic. Joint task forces may be established as necessary to ensure swift apprehension and conviction of violators across jurisdictional lines. Through sustained vigilance and multi-agency cooperation, it is hoped that illegal artisanry will dwindle until rendering further aggressive measures unnecessary.\n\nThis section shall take effect immediately upon filing and remains applicable indefinitely unless explicitly amended by subsequent resolution. All existing laws pertaining to unlicensed magic production are hereby superseded to the extent of any conflict. The Aegis Magi are authorized to issue binding orders directing compliance with these provisions, failure to which may result in immediate seizure of assets and temporary suspension of all magical practice pending review. Failure to abide shall constitute a separate offense punishable under § 900.1. Any questions regarding interpretation or enforcement shall be referred to the Archmage Sovereign for final adjudication.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "500.1",
            "900.1",
            "500.9"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "The provisions herein shall be read in conjunction with the related requirements",
      "source": "lmstudio",
