    {
      "cite": "600.9",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Requirements",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Requirements",
          "text": "§ 600.9 Related Requirements\nThis Section sets forth the prerequisites for entering into binding magical contracts with parties lacking formal licensure under these Accords. Pursuant to § 100.2, only duly authorized representatives of the Guild may negotiate such agreements on its behalf. As provided in § 400.3, any contract involving temporal manipulation requires explicit permission from the Conservators and must be registered within three solar days of execution. See also § 200.7 regarding disclosure obligations.\n\nUnder § 300.8, no pact may impinge upon the sanctity of a pocket dimension without prior review by the Aegis Magi. This includes but is not limited to clauses granting access rights or usage fees for such spaces. Any violation constitutes breach under § 500.1 and subjects the offending party to immediate seizure of assets. Reference is made to § 600.4 concerning liability limitations in force majeure scenarios involving arcane phenomena.\n\nAs mandated by § 1201.7, all contracts must include a comprehensive risk assessment prepared by a certified appraiser. This document shall outline potential hazards and mitigation strategies consistent with § 800.5 safety protocols. Failure to comply renders the agreement void ab initio. Consult § 600.3 regarding unconscionable terms and § 900.7 for standards of good faith performance.\n\nPursuant to § 1401.2, any party seeking to alter material provisions after execution must submit a formal amendment request subject to strict scrutiny. Unilateral modifications are strictly prohibited under § 700.6. Amendments require unanimous consent and re-execution by all signatories unless otherwise specified in the original instrument. See § 1101.8 for notification requirements.\n\nFinally, as outlined in § 200.9, any dispute arising from such contracts shall be resolved exclusively through binding arbitration administered by a panel appointed by the Guild Sovereign. Litigation is expressly forbidden under § 500.4. The prevailing party shall be entitled to recover reasonable attorney fees and costs incurred, subject to a cap of fifty percent of the contract value unless exceptional circumstances exist per § 1301.7. This provision reflects the consensus reached at the Autumnwood Summit of 894 AR as detailed in the official proceedings archived under § 600.2.\n\n*Archmage Veyra's Note: These provisions were drafted to protect both novice casters and their clients from predatory practices while ensuring accountability for all parties involved.*",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "600.9",
            "100.2",
            "400.3",
            "200.7",
            "300.8",
            "500.1",
            "600.4",
            "1201.7",
            "800.5",
            "600.3",
            "900.7",
            "1401.2",
            "700.6",
            "1101.8",
            "200.9",
            "500.4",
            "1301.7",
            "600.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Contracts and Non-Magical Counterparts",
          "text": "§ 600.9 Related Requirements governs the interaction between magical contracts and their mundane equivalents, particularly where non-casters are involved in the transaction or fulfillment thereof. Where a contract binds both casters and non-casters, the provisions of this Part shall apply by necessary implication to ensure equitable treatment across all parties regardless of magical aptitude. See § 100.2 regarding who may validly represent the Guild's interests in such matters.\n\nA critical distinction exists between contracts executed solely through arcane means versus those utilizing conventional legal instruments alongside magic. In the former case, this Codex provides exclusive remedial avenues. However, when non-casters participate materially in a magical agreement—whether as guarantors, witnesses, or mere beneficiaries—they retain access to traditional contract law principles where applicable. See § 100.3 for quorum requirements involving mixed parties and § 200.5 for guest caster obligations under hybrid agreements.\n\nThe Guild acknowledges that many essential services require non-magical expertise, from financial auditing to structural engineering. Consequently, contracts incorporating both magical and mundane components shall be interpreted holistically rather than fragmented by disciplinary boundaries. Under § 100.7, foreign halls may provide courtesy interpretations when dealing with cross-jurisdictional hybrids. Emergency provisions under § 100.8 allow for expedited rulings where mixed-contract disputes threaten public safety or stability.\n\nFamiliars and constructs serving as agents in non-magical contracts are deemed extensions of their masters' legal persons under § 600.2, subject to minimum liability thresholds per § 600.4. Summoned notaries operating under § 600.5 shall execute affidavits with the same probative weight as official Guild seals pursuant to § 1.9. Any clause attempting to evade these protections through convoluted drafting shall be void ab initio under the doctrine of unconscionability codified in § 300.2 and reinforced by § 600.3 regarding geas terms.\n\nIn all cases, fairness dictates that non-casters receive notice of magical contingencies material to their obligations prior to signing. See also § 1401.8 on disclosure duties and § 1201.7 on informed consent in research contexts. The Guild maintains an official registry of approved non-magical service providers (§ 300.1) whose contracts enjoy presumptive validity within jurisdictional limits set forth in § 500.1 and § 400.2 respectively.\n\nThis framework aims to balance the unique needs of the arcane community with the established rights of ordinary citizens, fostering cooperation while safeguarding against exploitation inherent in power imbalances between casters and their clients or collaborators alike. For further guidance on specific hybrid scenarios, consult the Desk's published interpretations under § 100.4 or petition for a preliminary ruling via standard channels outlined in §§ 200.1 through 200.6.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "600.9",
            "100.2",
            "100.3",
            "200.5",
            "100.7",
            "100.8",
            "600.2",
            "600.4",
            "600.5",
            "1.9",
            "300.2",
            "600.3",
            "1401.8",
            "1201.7",
            "300.1",
            "500.1",
            "400.2",
            "100.4",
            "200.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Contracts and Non-Magical Counterparts — Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.9 Related Requirements\n\nA contract between a caster and non-caster may include magical obligations, but such clauses are subject to strict scrutiny under § 1.7. Any provision granting the caster unfettered access to the signatory's person or property shall be deemed unconscionable and void as against public policy. See § 300.2 (Unsanctioned Arts). Furthermore, no contract may require a non-caster to surrender their legal capacity to form other agreements without explicit written consent from a licensed advocate under § 100.2. The Guild recognizes that magical contracts exist within the broader context of civil law; thus, they must not contravene fundamental rights protected by the Accords or national statutes. Under § 400.5 (Unauthorized Titan Growth), any clause compelling a party to engage in prohibited field activities shall be invalidated ab initio. Finally, disputes arising from hybrid magical-civil agreements shall be adjudicated according to this Code's penal provisions where applicable, with reference to the general principles of contract law as outlined in § 200.1 (Licensure Required). Parties are advised to seek independent legal counsel before entering into any agreement involving both mundane and arcane elements. The Guild reserves the right to void entire contracts if they pose a threat to public safety or magical security. This interpretation is binding on all signatories regardless of their prior knowledge of these restrictions. Any attempt to circumvent these safeguards shall be prosecuted as a serious offense under the relevant articles herein.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "600.9",
            "1.7",
            "300.2",
            "100.2",
            "400.5",
            "200.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.9 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Matters",
          "text": "Contracts entered into between casters and non-casters fall under the general principles of mercantile law, modified by the unique circumstances inherent to magical services. The fundamental rule remains that a contract is binding if it meets all common legal requirements: offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity, and legality. However, several caveats apply specifically when magic forms part of the transactional exchange. First, any clause requiring a non-caster to perform acts that violate their own licensure or guild regulations shall be deemed void and unenforceable. Second, contracts involving restricted or forbidden arts require explicit prior approval from both the caster's licensing board and the relevant regulatory authority; failure to obtain such consent renders the agreement null ab initio. Third, provisions mandating excessive risk-taking beyond accepted safety protocols may be challenged as unconscionable under § 107(4). Finally, all contracts must clearly specify which party bears responsibility for accidental or unintended magical effects arising from performance. Absent express language to the contrary, liability shall attach to the caster who initiated the spellwork, not the passive recipient. These rules ensure fairness while preserving public safety in an increasingly complex magical economy. See also § 106(2) regarding contractual capacity and § 108(5) on unconscionability standards. Under § 109(3), disputes concerning mixed magic-mercantile agreements shall be adjudicated by a specially constituted panel including both legal experts and seasoned practitioners. This hybrid approach acknowledges the dual nature of such transactions while maintaining rigorous oversight to protect all parties involved in the arcane marketplace.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "600.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Contracts with Non-Casters and Non-Magical Counterparts – Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.9 Contracts Involving Mixed Parties and Related Matters\n\nThis Section addresses the legal complexities arising when magical contracts involve parties lacking casting abilities, whether through natural limitation or voluntary restraint under § 201.3(a). Such arrangements require heightened scrutiny to ensure fairness and prevent exploitation of vulnerable casters by those seeking arcane services without proper licensure.\n\nA contract between a licensed caster and an unlicensed individual may be enforceable only if the latter demonstrates sufficient knowledge of magical hazards (§ 601.12) and obtains informed consent for all procedures involved. Failure to disclose material risks renders any agreement void ab initio under common law principles incorporated by reference in § 602.4. The burden of proving such disclosure lies with the caster, who must retain contemporaneous records as evidence of compliance (§ 703).\n\nFurthermore, unlicensed parties seeking magical services shall engage only through established channels regulated by this Code. Direct transactions outside Guild oversight are prohibited except where expressly permitted under emergency provisions in § 815 or specific exemptions granted by the Sovereign Archmage (§ 901). Violation of this mandate constitutes a Class B misdemeanor punishable by fine and/or imprisonment up to six months at the discretion of the presiding Magistrate.\n\nIn cases involving familial or communal agreements where multiple unlicensed members rely on a single licensed provider, additional safeguards apply. The primary caster must maintain clear accounts of all services rendered and charges assessed, accessible upon reasonable demand (§ 604). Disputes regarding billing practices shall be resolved through binding arbitration administered by the Conservators' Office unless otherwise stipulated in writing between the parties (§ 1015).\n\nFinally, any attempt to circumvent these protections through proxy arrangements or sub-contracting is strictly forbidden. The caster remains personally liable for all obligations undertaken under this Section regardless of delegation or third-party involvement (§ 605). This principle extends even where technicalities such as power-of-attorney documents are invoked; no instrument shall override the fundamental safeguards established herein without explicit approval from the Arcane Council itself (§ 1020).\n\nSee also § 1.7 (Construction), § 1.8 (Effective Date), and § 603 (Unconscionable Terms) for additional guidance on interpreting these provisions in context. Any ambiguities shall be resolved liberally in favor of protecting non-caster parties from undue harm or financial loss resulting from their reliance on magical services provided under this Code's framework.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "600.9",
            "201.3",
            "601.12",
            "602.4",
            "1.7",
            "1.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Contracts and Non-Magical Counterparts — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.9 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Matters, Contracts with Non-Casters and Non-Magical Counterparts – Continued\n\nA contract between a caster and a non-caster may include magical provisions only to the extent that such provisions are necessary to effectuate the parties' intent and do not contravene this Code or public policy. Any magical clause must be clearly identified, separately bargained for, and explicitly assented to by both parties prior to execution. Absent explicit agreement otherwise, standard contract principles shall apply to any non-magical aspects of such an instrument.\n\nA caster may employ a familiar as an agent under a power-of-attorney granted in writing or through binding magical compulsion consistent with applicable laws and ethical guidelines. The scope of the familiar's authority is limited to those acts expressly delegated by the principal. Any unauthorized use of the familiar beyond this scope shall constitute a breach of contract and may expose both parties to liability for resulting damages.\n\nA geas imposed as part of a contractual obligation is deemed unconscionable and unenforceable under any circumstances. Such provisions are inherently coercive and violate fundamental principles of personal autonomy protected by this Code. Parties seeking to bind another's conduct shall negotiate traditional covenants rather than resorting to magical compulsion.\n\nA construct employed in the performance of contractual duties must be maintained at a minimum standard of operational safety and reliability as determined by competent evaluation. Failure to adhere to these standards may constitute negligence per se and render the employer liable for any harm resulting therefrom. Regular inspections and maintenance shall be conducted to ensure ongoing compliance with all applicable regulations and specifications.\n\nA summoned notary possesses full authority to administer oaths, take acknowledgments, and certify documents within their sphere of influence. Any magical protections or penalties attached to such certification must be clearly disclosed and voluntarily accepted by the parties involved. Unauthorized modifications to a notarized instrument may invalidate its legal effect entirely.\n\nAll contracts involving magic shall include detailed descriptions of any associated risks, limitations, and potential consequences. Parties are advised to seek independent counsel before entering into agreements containing complex or hazardous magical provisions. Failure to disclose material facts regarding such risks may constitute fraud and void the agreement ab initio.\n\nThis section supersedes all prior conflicting rules concerning contracts involving magic. Future amendments shall specify any intentional deviations from these principles with explicit notice and consensus of the affected parties. Disputes arising under this Code shall be resolved through binding arbitration unless otherwise agreed upon by the contracting entities.\n\nSEE ALSO: §§ 201-305 (General Contract Principles), 401-450 (Magical Liability Standards); § 600.1-600.8 (Specialized Provisions)",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "600.9",
            "600.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
