    {
      "cite": "700.9",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "Burden and Standard of Proof in Arcane Disputes",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Burden and Standard of Proof in Arcane Disputes",
          "text": "In matters concerning the admissibility of magical evidence and testimony, particular scrutiny must be applied to phenomena that challenge conventional notions of reality. Under § 300.2, certain schools of magic are deemed unsanctioned due to their inherent dangers and ethical implications; similarly, § 700.9 establishes strict guidelines for evaluating the reliability of arcane-derived data in legal proceedings.\n\nScrying operations conducted within closed books or sealed environments shall be subject to rigorous oversight per § 400.2. Any evidence obtained through unauthorized divination techniques may be deemed inadmissible under § 700.3, which categorizes memory charm spells as forms of spoliation when used to manipulate witness recollections. Simulacra created via intricate illusion magic cannot serve as credible testimony absent corroboration from independent sources, a principle codified in § 700.4.\n\nThe status of cloned individuals presents unique evidentiary challenges given their shared origins yet distinct existences. While clones maintain separate legal identities under § 200.5, any claims predicated on duplication must demonstrate clear lineage and intent beyond mere replication. This framework is further elaborated in § 700.5 to prevent fraudulent assertions of identity based solely on magical reproduction.\n\nRelated requirements include but are not limited to: adherence to research permit protocols outlined in § 400.1, maintenance of proper specimen custody as mandated by § 400.3, and compliance with field casting regulations detailed in § 700.6. Failure to observe these stipulations may result in suspension or revocation of licensure pursuant to § 200.4. Furthermore, any contracts entered into involving non-casters must satisfy the formalities specified in § 501 prior to becoming enforceable under applicable law.\n\nIt is incumbent upon all practitioners to uphold the highest standards of integrity when presenting magical evidence or testimony. The integrity of our judicial system depends upon trust in the accuracy and authenticity of arcane information. To compromise this principle would undermine both the rule of law and the very foundation of our society's relationship with magic itself.\"\n\n\"see § 400.1 Research Permits; § 700.6 Field Casting Regulations; § 200.4 Suspension Procedures; § 501 Contract Formalities\"",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "700.9",
            "400.2",
            "700.3",
            "700.4",
            "200.5",
            "700.5",
            "400.1",
            "400.3",
            "700.6",
            "200.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.10 Burden and Standard of Proof in Arcane Disputes",
          "text": "In all matters arising under these Accords, the burden of proof shall rest upon the party asserting an affirmative claim or defense. Where allegations involve the commission of a magical offense, the accused bears no initial obligation to disprove innocence; rather, it is incumbent upon the accuser to establish prima facie guilt by clear and convincing evidence. In civil proceedings concerning licensing disputes or contractual breaches, the preponderance of the evidence standard shall apply unless expressly modified by specific provision within this Code.\n\nThe standard of proof required for conviction in penal cases before a Paradox Trial jury is proof beyond a reasonable doubt, tempered by the unique nature of arcane phenomena which may defy conventional verification methods. In such instances, circumstantial evidence and expert testimony regarding magical signatures may be deemed sufficient to sustain an indictment if they collectively eliminate all rational alternative explanations consistent with innocence. The Presiding Archmage retains discretion to adjust evidentiary thresholds when dealing with matters of national security or existential threat posed by rogue magic users.\n\nWitnesses shall be subject to standard rules of impeachment and cross-examination, though their competency may be questioned on grounds specific to magical ability or susceptibility to charm spells. Testimony rendered under the influence of truth-compelling enchantments shall carry double weight unless successfully challenged through lawful means such as counter-curse application or demonstration of pre-existing immunity. Where direct observation is impossible due to distance or obfuscation, secondary evidence including scrying recordings and residue analysis may be admitted subject to adversarial rebuttal.\n\nIn cases involving disputed ownership of magical artifacts or living constructs, the court shall presume bona fide possession by registered holders unless proven otherwise. Burden shifts to any party claiming superior title through theft, coercion, or fraud to present affirmative evidence establishing their rightful claim beyond a preponderance of probabilities. Where multiple parties assert concurrent interests in the same property, priority shall be determined by chronological order of lawful acquisition absent contrary stipulation binding all disputants.\n\nEvidence obtained unlawfully or in violation of constitutional protections shall generally be inadmissible unless its probative value substantially outweighs prejudicial effect under applicable jurisprudence. Exceptions may be granted where exclusion would undermine public safety or permit continued criminal conduct, at the sole discretion of the Presiding Magistrate after full hearing. All admissions and confessions must be voluntary and knowingly given; any signed waiver shall require notarization and delivery in triplicate to respective parties of record.\n\nThe right against self-incrimination applies fully to all accused persons regardless of magical capacity or prior conviction status. Compelled testimony may be utilized solely for impeachment purposes unless the witness explicitly consents otherwise in writing witnessed by two impartial third parties. Privileged communications between attorney-client, husband-wife, and clergy-parishioner remain inviolate except upon unanimous consent of affected parties or court order finding no alternative means to ascertain material facts at stake.\n\nThis Section shall be construed liberally in favor of defendants facing serious charges while maintaining rigorous enforcement against repeat offenders and unrepentant criminals who defy the rule of law through arcane malice alone. Justice tempered with mercy remains the enduring aspiration guiding all judicial proceedings under these hallowed Accords meant to protect both citizens and society at large from the ravages of unchecked magical power wielded by madmen or worse.\n\nsee § 700.14 Admissibility Standards; see § 250.3 Rights Against Self-Incrimination; see § 850.6 Privileged Communications Exceptions under general evidentiary principles governing all Arcane Accords proceedings throughout the realm.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "700.14",
            "250.3",
            "850.6",
            "700.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.9 Related Requirements — Continued",
          "text": "§ 700.10 Burden and Standard of Proof in Arcane Disputes\nThe burden of proof in any arcane dispute rests upon the accuser to establish prima facie grounds for their claim, pursuant to the foundational principles set forth in §§ 1.5–1.6. Where a defendant raises a plausible defense or counter-claim, the evidentiary obligation shifts accordingly, as dictated by the logic and fairness inherent in the system outlined in § 100.7. No party may rely solely on assertion or conjecture to carry their case; tangible evidence must be marshaled in accordance with the rules of proof detailed herein.\nThe standard of proof required shall vary depending upon the gravity of the allegations at issue, as elaborated in §§ 300.1–300.5 and § 400.2. For lesser infractions, a preponderance of the magical evidence may suffice to justify sanctions or remedial actions. However, for charges involving serious breaches such as unauthorized field casting (§ 500.1) or forbidden research (§ 400.3), clear and convincing proof shall be mandated before any punitive measures are imposed. In cases bearing upon public safety or the integrity of the Accord itself, proof beyond all reasonable doubt must be secured to ensure justice is properly served.\nEvidence may take many forms, from written records and witness testimony to magical traces and scryed observations, each subject to rigorous scrutiny under §§ 700.2–700.5 and § 1301.4. Illegally obtained materials shall be deemed inadmissible unless their probative value substantially outweighs any potential prejudice, consistent with established judicial precedent referenced in § 800.6. Parties are urged to preserve all relevant artifacts and communications throughout the investigation, lest they find themselves penalized for spoliation under § 700.3.\nIn disputes involving complex or arcane matters where conventional evidence may be scarce or ambiguous, alternative methods of proof shall be employed as necessary, provided they do not undermine the principles outlined in §§ 1.8–1.9 and § 1201.5. Expert testimony from recognized authorities within the Guild carries significant weight, subject to cross-examination and verification per § 600.3. Where feasible, magical analysis or ritual reconstruction may illuminate critical facts otherwise obscured by time or obfuscation techniques detailed in §§ 400.4–400.5 and § 1101.7.\nUltimately, the goal of any evidentiary proceeding is to ascertain the truth with fairness and thoroughness, guided by the overarching tenets embodied in the Preamble of these Accords (§ 1.1) and reinforced throughout this Codebook. By adhering strictly to these requirements, all parties may trust that their grievances will be heard and resolved equitably under the wise stewardship of the Mages' Guild.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "700.10",
            "1.5",
