          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.9 Related Requirements - Continued",
          "text": "In addition to the foregoing evidentiary exclusions, the following items shall also be deemed inadmissible in any arcane dispute: (A) testimony or records derived from magical scrying devices used without proper licensure; (B) confessions extracted through memory charms or other forms of mental compulsion; and (C) statements made by simulacrums or clones purporting to represent their original subjects. However, this prohibition shall not apply where such evidence is otherwise properly authenticated and the opposing party has had a full opportunity to cross-examine its sources under oath.\n\nFurthermore, parties are prohibited from introducing any evidence obtained in violation of this Part's privacy protections, whether directly or through third-party agents acting on their behalf. This ban extends to both written materials and oral communications, regardless of medium. Violations shall constitute grounds for summary dismissal of the offending party's claims and may subject them to additional penalties under § 700.12.\n\nThe foregoing restrictions reflect a fundamental policy judgment that certain types of magical intrusion into private affairs are so egregious as to warrant complete exclusion from judicial consideration, even if technically relevant. This approach balances the need for factual accuracy in determining arcane disputes with the imperative of safeguarding individual liberties against unchecked technological overreach. Any challenges to these provisions must be raised promptly and with particularized justification, lest they be deemed waived by acquiescence.\n\nSee also §§ 700.2–700.5 regarding specific evidentiary exclusions; § 1.7(c) concerning the aggregation of prohibited acts; and § 900.3(b)(i) for additional limitations on admissible evidence in related proceedings. Any ambiguities arising from this section shall be resolved in favor of maintaining strict privacy boundaries, consistent with the overarching principles set forth herein.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "700.12",
            "700.2",
            "1.7",
            "900.3",
            "700.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.10 Burden and Standard of Proof in Arcane Disputes — Continued",
          "text": "In any arcane dispute brought before the Guild, the burden of proof rests upon the accuser to establish by clear and convincing evidence that a violation of these Accords has occurred. The standard for conviction is elevated beyond mere preponderance of the evidence due to the grave consequences inherent in penalizing magical practitioners for alleged infractions. Where doubt exists as to the nature or extent of the transgression, it shall be resolved most strongly against the accuser and in favor of the accused.\n\nThe court may admit various forms of expert testimony regarding arcane practices, spell structures, and ritualistic components. Such witnesses must possess recognized credentials within their fields and demonstrate impartiality in their opinions. The trier of fact is permitted to draw reasonable inferences from circumstantial evidence when direct proof is unavailable, provided such deductions are logically sound and supported by the record. However, speculative assertions unsupported by reliable data shall be disregarded entirely.\n\nWhen evaluating claims of unauthorized magic usage or prohibited research activities, the Guild considers a multifaceted totality of circumstances including but not limited to: observed anomalies in magical signatures; inconsistencies between claimed abilities and documented training histories; suspicious procurement patterns for rare reagents or components; and deviations from established procedural norms without valid justification. No single factor is dispositive; rather, they are weighed collectively to ascertain whether a prima facie case has been made out before proceeding further.\n\nIn cases involving contested interpretations of vague or ambiguous provisions within these Accords, the court shall construe such language in favor of the party who relied thereupon to their detriment. This equitable principle acknowledges that practitioners cannot be expected to divinate hidden meanings without adequate notice. Consequently, any doubt as to the scope or limitations imposed by particular clauses shall remain unresolved rather than enforced against innocent parties.\n\nShould new evidence emerge post-judgment capable of materially altering the outcome, affected individuals may petition for reconsideration within thirty days of receiving final notice. Such motions require demonstration of good faith efforts previously made to secure said proof and must explain why earlier disclosure was not feasible despite diligent pursuit. Upon review, the tribunal retains discretion either to grant relief or dismiss the appeal outright based on merit alone.\n\nUltimately, the goal of these evidentiary rules is to ensure fair adjudication while respecting both the sanctity of magical arts and public safety concerns. Only through rigorous application of established standards can true justice be served in this complex domain where science intersects with sorcery in unpredictable ways. See § 1.4 for persons bound by these obligations and § 300.5 regarding permissible research methodologies under supervision. Under § 1.7, all terms herein shall be given their ordinary meanings unless otherwise defined expressly herein or by reference thereto. As provided in § 1.9, any official seals affixed to documents executed pursuant to this Code shall carry full legal weight and effect throughout the Realm. Finally, nothing contained herein limits remedies available at common law or equity except where specifically abrogated by contrary statutory mandate.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "300.5",
            "1.7",
            "1.9",
            "700.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.9 Related Requirements — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "Under § 700.6, parties in arcane disputes must comply with additional procedural mandates to ensure fair adjudication. First, all evidence submitted shall be authenticated by an independent scribe or qualified mage of standing with the Guild. Unverified claims carry no weight and may result in sanctions against the presenting party under § 1401.7. Second, witnesses testifying regarding magical phenomena must appear in person unless excused for valid cause, such as illness or active combat duty. Remote testimony via scrying crystal is permitted only with prior written consent from both sides (§ 700.2). Third, any artifacts or items alleged to have been involved in the disputed event must be produced and made available for inspection by opposing counsel and the presiding Arcanist. Failure to do so constitutes spoliation of evidence and may lead to adverse inference rulings under § 350.14. Finally, all communications between parties concerning the dispute shall be conducted through official channels—either via registered letter or secure messaging platform approved by the Desk—to maintain a clear record for future reference (§ 900.8). These measures collectively serve to uphold the integrity of the judicial process and prevent abuse of arcane powers in the pursuit of personal gain or vendetta. By adhering strictly to these requirements, litigants demonstrate good faith and respect for the rule of law, thereby facilitating just resolution of their differences under the Autumnwood Accords.\n\nsee § 700.6 Related Requirements; see also § 1401.7 Sanctions for Non-Compliance; under § 350.14 Adverse Inference for Spoliation. This section ensures that all evidence and testimony meet rigorous standards of authenticity and relevance, preventing frivolous or manipulative claims from undermining the credibility of the arcane judiciary. Parties are expected to act in good faith throughout the proceedings, submitting only verifiable information and adhering to established protocols for communication and documentation. By doing so, they contribute to a fair and efficient resolution of their disputes, upholding the principles of justice enshrined in the Accords.",
          "key": "f",
