    {
      "cite": "800.9",
      "part": "part_viii",
      "title": "Ex Post Facto Prohibition and Retroactive Sanctions",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Ex Post Facto Prohibition and Retroactive Sanctions",
          "text": "The provisions governing penal sanctions against rogue casters are inextricably linked to several ancillary statutes designed to maintain the integrity of both the Accords and the broader magical ecosystem. First and foremost is the requirement for comprehensive background checks on all candidates undergoing Paradox Trial proceedings, under § 800.7 Related Requirements; this ensures that no individual with a history of malicious intent can exploit the trial process to further their own ends. Such vetting is critical given the sensitive nature of sealing magic permanently within an afflicted subject's soul—a procedure so dangerous it borders on heresy in its own right.\n\nFurthermore, § 801.3 Related Requirements mandates that all trials be conducted by a panel comprising members from both Conservator and Innovator factions, preventing either side from imposing their will unilaterally. This balanced approach reflects the foundational principle of fair play enshrined throughout the Accords, while also mitigating the risk of partisan bias skewing outcomes in favor of one ideological camp over another. The presence of diverse perspectives during deliberations serves to broaden the scope of analysis and ensure that all relevant factors are duly weighed before any punitive measures are enacted upon an accused caster.\n\nIn addition to these procedural safeguards, § 802.1 Related Requirements establishes a system for post-trial review wherein decisions made under the Paradox Trial can be appealed to a higher council should either party believe they were unfairly treated during the initial proceedings. This mechanism provides much-needed recourse against potential abuses of power by lower-ranking adjudicators who may act without sufficient foresight or restraint in administering justice to those before them. By affording litigants an opportunity to challenge unfavorable rulings, the Accords uphold their commitment to equity and impartiality even amidst the inherent complexities of magical jurisprudence.\n\nFinally, it bears noting that § 803.2 Related Requirements stipulates strict confidentiality protocols surrounding all cases brought under this section; details thereof are to be kept on a need-to-know basis alone, accessible only to those directly involved in executing sanctions or overseeing appeals processes. Breach of this secrecy mandate carries severe repercussions for any individual found guilty of leaking sensitive information regarding ongoing trials or their participants' identities. Such vigilance is paramount to protecting both the subjects of punishment and society at large from unnecessary harm arising from public exposure of volatile matters best left handled discreetly behind closed doors.\n\nsee § 800.7 Related Requirements; see § 801.3 Related Requirements; under § 802.1 Related Requirements; pursuant to § 803.2 Related Requirements.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "800.7",
            "801.3",
            "802.1",
            "803.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 800.9 Ex Post Facto Prohibition and Retroactive Sanctions",
          "text": "No provision within this Part shall be construed to impose penalties or sanctions retroactively, except as expressly authorized by the Desk under extraordinary circumstances. Any determination of guilt or violation must be based solely on conduct occurring after the effective date of the relevant rule. This principle ensures fair notice to all casters regarding permissible and prohibited magical practices. See § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks [for limitations on retroactive rulemaking]; § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse [regarding temporal scope].\n\nIn cases where ambiguity exists as to whether past conduct constituted a violation, the Desk shall apply the interpretation most favorable to the accused caster. This standard protects against arbitrary enforcement and maintains confidence in the system's impartiality. However, repeated or egregious misconduct may warrant discretionary review for potential corrective action outside the formal disciplinary process. Under § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy [mutual recognition of judgments]; § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items [exceptions to general prohibitions].\n\nThe prohibition against ex post facto sanctions does not preclude the Desk from addressing patterns of behavior that began prior to the effective date but continued thereafter. Such continuing violations are subject to full investigation and appropriate punishment regardless of when they originated. This approach balances fairness with accountability, ensuring that persistent offenders cannot evade consequences through technicalities. Per § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting [disclosure obligations]; § 400.3 Borrowed Hours [limits on temporal manipulation].\n\nSpecial provisions may apply where violations involve threats to public safety or the integrity of magical research. In such instances, expedited proceedings and enhanced penalties are permissible to mitigate harm. The Desk retains broad discretion to tailor responses based on the specific facts and circumstances presented. This flexibility is essential for adapting to evolving challenges in a rapidly advancing field. As noted in § 100.8 Emergency Sitting [urgent measures]; § 500.2 Unscheduled Weather [force majeure considerations].\n\nUltimately, the principles of fairness and transparency guide all disciplinary actions under this Part. Casters are entitled to due process, clear notice of alleged violations, and an impartial hearing before any sanction is imposed. These safeguards preserve the legitimacy of the system while enabling effective enforcement of the Accords. For further guidance on procedural rights, consult § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild [representatives]; § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters [binding obligations].",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "200.4",
            "100.7",
            "300.5",
            "100.5",
            "400.3",
            "100.8",
            "500.2",
            "100.2",
            "600.1",
            "800.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 800.9 Prohibition on Ex Post Facto Penalties and Retroactive Sanctions",
          "text": "No mage shall be subject to any penalty, sanction, or disciplinary measure unless such measure was expressly provided for in the Accords at the time of the alleged infraction. The principle against ex post facto punishment is sacrosanct within the Guild's jurisdiction; a rule cannot be applied retroactively to punish conduct that was lawful when performed. This prohibition extends beyond mere statutory penalties to include informal sanctions, such as social ostracism or professional blacklisting, if imposed pursuant to a newly enacted provision.\n\nThe Desk shall not adopt any new rule, interpretation, or precedent with retrospective effect unless strictly necessary to address an urgent threat to public safety or magical stability. Even in such exigent circumstances, the Desk must provide notice and opportunity for comment prior to implementation, allowing affected parties to adjust their conduct accordingly. Any exception to this standard requires a supermajority vote of the full Accords Council and explicit citation of the compelling justification therefor.\n\nMages shall not be held liable for violations of customs, usages, or practices that have fallen into disuse unless expressly preserved by written law. The mere fact that an action was previously tolerated or even encouraged does not immunize a caster from liability should such practice later be proscribed. Conversely, longstanding customs and understandings may inform the Desk's exercise of its discretionary authority in determining appropriate sanctions for infractions lacking precise statutory guidance.\n\nIn all cases, penalties shall be proportional to the gravity of the offense and tailored to achieve rehabilitation where possible. The Desk shall not impose harsher measures than those necessary to vindicate the Accords' purposes or protect the public welfare. Excessive punishments, whether by design or accident, are void ab initio and may be challenged on that ground alone. This requirement applies with equal force to both formal adjudications and informal disciplinary proceedings conducted by authorized agents of the Guild.\n\nNo mage shall be subjected to double jeopardy for a single transgression. A finding of guilt in one proceeding precludes subsequent punishment for the same act, whether in another venue or under a different guise. The Desk retains authority to revisit a case only upon demonstration that the prior determination was flawed due to error, fraud, or newly discovered evidence materially affecting the outcome. Absent such grounds, past judgments stand conclusive and binding on all parties.\n\nThe prohibition against retroactive sanctions serves as a fundamental safeguard of individual liberty and legal certainty within the magical community. It ensures that mages may regulate their conduct according to known standards without fear of arbitrary or unexpected consequences. By adhering strictly to this principle, the Guild fosters trust in its institutions and maintains the rule of law amidst the inherent uncertainties of arcane practice.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "800.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibition on Ex Post Facto Penalties and Retroactive Sanctions",
          "text": "§ 800.9 Prohibition on Ex Post Facto Penalties and Retroactive Sanctions; § 1.5 Relation to Other Law; under § 1.6 Definitions, \"Penalty\" includes any adverse consequence imposed by the Guild in response to alleged misconduct. This section shall be liberally construed in favor of accused casters facing disciplinary action.\n\nNo penalty or sanction may be imposed upon an individual unless such measure was expressly authorized by this Codex at the time the conduct occurred. Any attempt to apply a new rule retroactively to punish prior acts is strictly prohibited, except as provided herein. The burden lies with the accuser to demonstrate that the challenged behavior violated existing law when committed, not how it might be viewed in hindsight.\n\nRetroactive changes to disciplinary standards shall not take effect until at least thirty (30) days after public notice thereof has been issued through official channels and posted prominently throughout all Guild halls and offices across the region. During this period, any individual subject to such revised provisions may petition for an extension of time limits applicable to charges brought under the old regime. Such requests shall be granted unless compelling circumstances dictate otherwise in the sole discretion of the Presiding Magistrate.\n\nIn cases where a sanction has already been imposed pursuant to the prior rules but subsequent amendment would have rendered it more lenient, the accused may elect to invoke the new standard prospectively from the date of their acceptance thereof. This option shall be exercised within fourteen (14) days of learning of the change or such longer period as deemed reasonable under the circumstances. Failure to timely notify the relevant authority forfeits eligibility for this benefit.\n\nNo penalty imposed by any officer, agent, delegate, or subordinate acting outside the scope of their actual or apparent authority shall bind the Guild unless ratified within ten (10) working days thereafter. Unauthorized actions taken in excess of delegated powers remain void ab initio and confer no rights upon either party thereto. Exceptions may be made only upon written order issued by a full quorum of the Autumnwood Accords Desk after due notice and hearing.\n\nNothing herein shall prevent the Guild from adopting new regulations or interpretations going forward, provided always that such measures comply with this section's prohibitions on retroactivity. The spirit of fairness and due process animating these provisions extends to all matters concerning the imposition of penalties upon members of this noble order. Let it be remembered that power must never eclipse justice in the eyes of those who wield it.\n\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~*~