          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "800.9",
            "1.5",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibition on Retroactive Penalties",
          "text": "No penalty, sanction, or adverse consequence may be imposed upon any person for conduct that was not unlawful at the time it occurred. This principle serves as a cornerstone of due process within the Accords and reflects the fundamental fairness expected of all governing bodies under the Guild's jurisprudence. The prohibition extends to both civil and criminal contexts, ensuring that mages are not subjected to arbitrary punishment based on subsequent changes in law or policy interpretations.\n\nAny attempt to apply ex post facto penalties shall be deemed void ab initio and subject to immediate reversal by a competent tribunal upon proper challenge. Courts possess inherent authority to scrutinize the temporal applicability of sanctions against accused parties, with a heavy presumption favoring the defendant when doubt exists as to whether conduct violated established norms at the relevant point in time. This safeguard prevents abuse of power by rogue magistrates or factions seeking to manipulate legal standards for political gain.\n\nThe prohibition also encompasses indirect penalties arising from retroactively altered regulations or guidelines. For instance, if a new rule were promulgated that criminalized previously permissible magical practices, such change could not be used to penalize individuals for actions taken prior to its enactment unless explicit transitional provisions were included. This limitation protects against chilling effects on legitimate research and practice while maintaining flexibility for the Guild to adapt evolving understandings of magic safely.\n\nExceptions to this rule are narrowly construed and must satisfy rigorous scrutiny. Retroactive sanctions may only be justified in cases involving grave threats to public safety or national security where advance warning was impracticable given the nature of the danger posed. Even then, strict procedural safeguards apply to ensure that any such measures remain proportional and necessary rather than punitive in character. The burden rests squarely on those seeking to enforce retroactive penalties to demonstrate compelling justification beyond mere administrative convenience or ideological purity.\n\nThis provision stands as a bulwark against capricious governance and arbitrary enforcement within the arcane community. By anchoring disciplinary actions in contemporaneous law, it fosters predictability and trust between regulators and regulated entities alike - essential qualities for maintaining stability amidst the constant flux of magical innovation and discovery.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibition on Retroactive Penalties — Continued",
          "text": "No sanction, seal, or penalty imposed under these Accords shall have any legal effect unless expressly authorized by the full text of this Code at the time the alleged violation occurred. The Desk shall not have power to create new offenses or aggravate existing ones through retroactive interpretation or application. This principle is fundamental to maintaining trust between the Guild and those subject to its jurisdiction.\n\nUnder § 100.5, a caster may not be held liable for conduct that was lawful when performed, even if later reclassified under revised standards. Similarly, sanctions imposed prior to any amendment shall remain valid unless overturned on proper procedural grounds. Conversely, a new provision cannot apply to past actions except where explicitly stated. This ensures fairness and prevents arbitrary punishment based on shifting definitions of acceptable magical practice.\n\nThe prohibition extends beyond formal penalties to include informal censure or reputational damage inflicted by the Desk. While individual casters retain their right to criticize Guild actions freely, official reprimands must be grounded in clear, contemporaneous rules. Retroactive disciplinary measures undermine the integrity of the entire regulatory framework and invite abuse of power by rogue officials seeking personal vendettas under false pretenses.\n\nViolations of this clause shall constitute grounds for immediate appeal and potential disqualification of the offending order from future proceedings. Repeated offenders may face removal from office permanently, along with civil liability for any resulting harms. The Desk must adhere strictly to established legal principles at all times, recognizing that its authority derives solely from lawful enactment rather than arbitrary fiat.\n\nIn sum, these safeguards protect both casters and the Guild against capricious governance while preserving the flexibility needed to adapt to evolving magical discoveries. By anchoring enforcement in clear, pre-existing norms, the Accords maintain their legitimacy as a stable foundation for orderly coexistence between magic-users and society at large. Any departure from this core tenet risks unraveling the delicate balance achieved through years of hard-won compromise.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "Ex Post Facto Prohibition and Retroactive Sanctions",
