    {
      "cite": "1101.9",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Gifts That Later Move — §1101.9",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Gifts That Later Move — §1101.9",
          "text": "The provisions herein constitute supplementary obligations incumbent upon the holder of any magical artifact or relic subject to this Part, and shall be read in conjunction with §§ 1101.1 through 1101.8 inclusive. These requirements are designed to ensure that the transfer, possession, and utilization of enchanted items remain transparent and accountable within the Guild framework. Failure to comply may result in penalties ranging from revocation of licensure to criminal prosecution under applicable statutes.\n\nFirstly, any individual acquiring a cursed or otherwise hazardous item must immediately disclose such fact to their supervising Archmage or equivalent authority. Concealment of known dangers shall be treated as gross negligence and may lead to suspension of privileges without further notice. Additionally, the original seller remains liable for any latent defects in enchantments that manifest within one hundred eighty days of transfer, regardless of subsequent ownership changes. This warranty period exists solely to protect consumers from fraudulent or incompetent practitioners who attempt to offload dangerous goods onto unsuspecting parties.\n\nSecondly, all relics must be registered with the Bureau of Magical Artifacts and equipped with a tracking sigil for inventory purposes. The sigil shall remain affixed at all times unless specifically authorized in writing by an Archmage. Tampering with or removing said identification without permission constitutes tampering with evidence and may result in severe disciplinary action up to and including permanent disqualification from the practice of magic within Guild boundaries. Furthermore, owners are prohibited from altering or modifying any inherent properties of the relic except through approved procedures overseen directly by a licensed Artificer. Unauthorized modifications void all warranties and expose the offender to additional liability for resulting damages.\n\nFinally, periodic inspections shall be conducted annually by appointed inspectors to assess condition and compliance status. Owners must provide full access to their items for examination at reasonable times upon proper notice. Refusal to submit to inspection will be deemed an admission of guilt regarding any violations discovered during routine audits. Inspectors have broad authority to seize suspected contraband pending further investigation, with all proceeds remitted to the Guild Treasury after deduction of processing fees. Non-compliance with these mandatory stipulations shall constitute a Class A felony offense punishable by imprisonment not exceeding twenty years and/or fines up to one hundred thousand gold pieces per violation count.\n\nIt is hereby declared that these related requirements are essential for maintaining public safety and order within our society, and all persons bound thereby are urged to exercise utmost diligence in adhering thereto at all times. The Guild reserves the right to amend or expand upon these provisions as necessary without notice, solely based on evolving circumstances or new intelligence regarding specific threats posed by particular artifacts. All such changes shall be published promptly via official channels for public awareness and compliance purposes.\n\nsee § 1101.5 Gifts That Later Move; see § 1101.7 Curse as Warranty Disclaimer; see § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse; see § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.1",
            "1101.5",
            "1101.7",
            "200.4",
            "300.2",
            "1101.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.9 Gifts That Later Move",
          "text": "A gift of magical provenance is not absolute if the donor retains residual claim or influence over its operation, as established in Matter of Archmage Theron v. Janna Brightspark, 456 F. Mag. 892 (Autumn Wood 2023). Where a caster transfers an item imbued with personal intent but imposes ongoing conditions, the original owner may seek to reclaim ownership upon breach or revocation of said terms. This principle protects against exploitation of magical bonds for coercive advantage and ensures equitable treatment between parties in complex transactions involving enchanted artifacts.\n\nThe doctrine of \"later movement\" applies when a gifted item is used contrary to its intended purpose or to harm the donor's interests. In such cases, the original giver may petition the Desk for restoration of title, subject to proof of malice or gross negligence by the recipient. The burden lies with the accuser to demonstrate that the misuse was foreseeable and directly attributable to the transfer of ownership rather than independent action.\n\nRecent jurisprudence has expanded this rule to cover digital and non-tangible magical constructs, recognizing that intent can be encoded beyond physical form. However, courts remain cautious about granting equitable relief absent clear evidence of wrongful conduct, lest they undermine the finality of gifts and disrupt commercial stability. The standard remains high: mere dissatisfaction with an item's performance does not constitute grounds for reversion unless coupled with malicious exploitation or gross negligence in its handling.\n\nIn sum, while magical gifts generally pass freely between willing parties, exceptions exist where the donor retains latent control or imposes binding stipulations. Courts will scrutinize such arrangements carefully to prevent abuse and ensure fair dealing in an increasingly complex marketplace of enchanted goods and services. The delicate balance between finality and fairness must be maintained through vigilant oversight and adherence to established precedent.\n\n*See also § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; § 300.2 Necromancy: Unsanctioned Arts; § 400.3 Borrowed Hours: Interest in Time.*",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "300.2",
            "400.3",
            "1101.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.9 Gifts That Later Move — Continued",
          "text": "In matters concerning magical endowments, particularly those bestowed by powerful entities such as the Mages' Guild or its subsidiaries, it shall be understood that the nature of such gifts may evolve over time in unforeseen ways. This provision addresses situations wherein a gift, initially conferred without conditions or strings attached, subsequently acquires latent properties or obligations that were not apparent at the time of transfer.\n\nWhere a magical item, creature, or service is given as a present or donation, and later manifests effects detrimental to the recipient or third parties, liability shall attach primarily to the original donor or their agents. This principle rests upon the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur applied to arcane phenomena – that such items rarely, if ever, operate without consequence unless deliberately engineered by skilled practitioners with knowledge of their potential hazards.\n\nThe burden of proof lies with the claimant seeking redress for injuries suffered due to latent magical properties surfacing post-delivery. They must establish a causal nexus between the gifted item and the alleged harm through sworn testimony, expert analysis where available, and demonstration that reasonable precautions were not taken upon receipt. Mere assertion of ill fortune or bad luck shall not suffice to trigger this protective clause.\n\nNotwithstanding these safeguards, recipients are urged to exercise due diligence when accepting magical endowments from unknown sources. Prudence dictates thorough vetting of the donor's reputation and intent prior to acceptance, especially where substantial value is at stake. The Guild maintains no obligation to defend against claims arising from reckless acquisition or misuse of gifted artifacts after the fact.\n\nThis section operates in conjunction with existing statutes regarding magical liability, warranty disclaimers, and contractor duties under §§ 1101.3 through 1101.5. It serves as a specialized exception tailored to the unique risks inherent in arcane transactions where full disclosure may be impossible even to masters of the craft themselves.\n\nThe provisions herein shall remain in effect until superseded by subsequent legislation or mutual agreement between relevant parties, reflecting the Guild's commitment to balancing protection for its constituents with recognition of the unpredictable nature of magical phenomena beyond human comprehension.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1101.3",
            "1101.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.9 Related Requirements — Gifts That Later Move, Continued",
          "text": "§ 1101.9 Gifts That Later Move — Continued\nThe transfer of magical items by gift remains subject to the full weight of the Accords once the donor retains any residual authority over their use or disposition. This principle prevents donors from evading regulation through nominal relinquishment while maintaining de facto control. Where a donor continues to dictate terms regarding an item's employment, such as restricting access to specific locations or individuals, the gift is deemed incomplete until all conditions are satisfied. The recipient bears no liability for non-compliance imposed unilaterally by the donor after transfer of title. However, any attempt to circumvent Arcane Law through such conditional gifting constitutes a violation warranting investigation by the Aegis Magi. In cases where ambiguous intent exists regarding retained authority, burden of proof falls upon the alleged donor to demonstrate ongoing control over the gifted artifact's application or operation. This safeguard ensures that magical objects remain subject to consistent oversight regardless of their provenance or current custodian. Failure to adhere to these stipulations may result in seizure of the item and potential criminal charges depending on severity of infraction. Parties are advised to seek legal counsel prior to engaging in conditional gifting transactions involving regulated items to avoid inadvertent violation of Guild statutes.\n\nSee also § 1101.5 Related Requirements re: donor responsibilities post-transfer; § 200.3 Temporary Mouths for analogous provisions regarding agent authority; and § 400.1 Research Permits concerning conditional research agreements. Under § 1.6 Definitions, \"residual authority\" encompasses all powers to direct an item's function or access. As established in § 1.7 Construction, ambiguous terms are construed strictly against the drafter—in this case, the donor attempting to preserve control through subterfuge. The provisions herein align with foundational principles set forth in §§ 100.5 Conflict of Interest and 300.4 Reclassification regarding ongoing obligations attached to magical possessions. All parties must recognize that nominal gifts do not absolve compliance responsibilities when substantive control persists, per § 900.7 Related Requirements on circumvention attempts. Consultation with a qualified attorney is strongly recommended before proceeding with conditional transfers of regulated artifacts under the autumnwood accords framework.\n\n[NOTE: This page continues the treatment of § 1101.9, focusing specifically on donor-imposed conditions and their legal implications post-transfer.]",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1101.9",
            "1101.5",
            "200.3",
            "400.1",
            "1.6",
            "1.7",
            "100.5",
            "900.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.9 Gifts That Later Move — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "The foregoing prohibition extends not only to outright gifts but also to nominal exchanges that serve as veiled transfers of ownership. Where a party purports to sell an item for a trivial sum or mere token consideration, the Accords deem such transactions fraudulent and voidable at the behest of any aggrieved third party with a superior claim to said property. This includes, without limitation, marriages contracted in perpetuity with no provision for dissolution, indentured servitude entered into under duress or deception, and adoption arrangements that effectively enslave the adoptee’s labor. The intent behind such subterfuge is immaterial; what matters is the substantive effect on title and control.\n\nFurthermore, this rule applies regardless of whether the purported gift remains in the hands of the original donor or has passed through multiple channels. Once a prohibited transfer occurs, all subsequent disposition of that item—or proceeds derived therefrom—is tainted by the original taint. Thus, if A gives B a cursed blade which B then sells to C for gold, and C gifts that same gold back to D, A may yet recover possession of either the sword or its monetary equivalent from any of these parties. The Accords recognize no clean hands here; they see only an unbroken chain of illicit enrichment stemming from one source.\n\nFinally, it is held that this prohibition reflects a fundamental public policy aimed at protecting vulnerable persons from exploitation under the guise of generosity. Whether motivated by malice or simple folly, those who attempt to circumvent these laws do so at their peril and risk severe sanctions upon discovery. For as they say in the trade: \"A gift today may be a shackle tomorrow—and no one is safe from the consequences.\"\n\nsee § 200.3 Temporary Mouths (regarding nominal exchanges); see § 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration (on gifts conditioned on future service); see § 800.10 Prohibited Gifts to Public Officials (parallel restrictions).",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.3",
            "500.4",
            "800.10",
            "1101.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.9 Gifts That Later Move — Continued, Reconsidered",
          "text": "Gifts that later move shall not be considered voidable on grounds of duress or undue influence unless the donor can demonstrate by clear and convincing evidence that the donee exploited a unique vulnerability at the time of transfer to secure an unfair advantage. Mere subsequent change in circumstances or financial hardship does not constitute valid grounds for rescission under this provision. See § 1101.6 Related Requirements; § 200.5 Guest Casters.\n\nThe burden of proof rests upon the party seeking to set aside a gift on the basis that it was induced by improper means. They must establish both the existence of such means and their causal connection to the transaction. Speculative assertions or circumstantial evidence shall not suffice. See § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy; § 600.5 Contracts Between Casters and Non-Casters.\n\nIn determining whether a gift was procured by duress, undue influence, or similar improper conduct, the court shall consider all relevant factors including but not limited to the relative bargaining positions of the parties, the nature and extent of any threats or coercion employed, and the fairness of the resulting exchange under the totality of the circumstances. See § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild; § 400.3 Borrowed Hours; Interest in Time.\n\nA gift that later moves shall remain valid and enforceable unless successfully challenged within a reasonable time after discovery of any grounds for rescission. Failure to promptly assert such claims shall constitute a waiver thereof, except in cases involving fraud or misrepresentation where the injured party may seek equitable relief regardless of passage of time. See § 1201.4 Damages; § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items.\n\nThis section does not preclude any other remedies available at law or equity to protect against improper influence over free will, including but not limited to injunctions specific performance, rescission or reformation of contracts, and punitive damages where applicable. See § 1401.2 Penalties; § 700.6 Contracts Between Caster Organizations; § 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion.\n\nThe provisions herein shall be liberally construed to effectuate the legislative intent of preventing abuse of power and safeguarding individual autonomy in matters involving substantial transfers of wealth or value. See § 1.8 Effective Date; Savings; § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1101.6",
            "200.5",
            "100.7",
            "600.5",
            "100.2",
            "400.3",
            "1201.4",
            "300.5",
            "1401.2",
            "700.6",
            "500.3",
