    {
      "cite": "300.10",
      "part": "part_iii",
      "title": "The Unsanctioned Arts and the Quiet List",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.10 Related Requirements: The Unsanctioned Arts and the Quiet List",
          "text": "§ 300.10 Related Requirements: The Unsanctioned Arts and the Quiet List\n\nThe provisions governing unsanctioned magical practices and the associated regulatory mechanisms are hereby clarified as follows. First, any school or technique of magic not explicitly enumerated in the Schedules of Arts contained within § 300.1 shall be presumed to fall under the category of \"Unsanctioned Arts.\" This presumption may be rebutted only by demonstrating that said practice is functionally equivalent to a sanctioned art and does not pose an undue risk to public safety or magical equilibrium.\n\nSecond, the procedures for listing Unsanctioned Arts on the Quiet List shall adhere strictly to the guidelines set forth in § 300.3. All requests for reclassification must be submitted in triplicate to the Autumnwood Accords Desk, accompanied by a comprehensive technical analysis and risk assessment prepared by an accredited Arcane Analyst or equivalent authority. Such submissions shall be reviewed within ten (10) calendar days of receipt and a written determination issued accordingly. Any party aggrieved by such determination may petition for reconsideration once per calendar year.\n\nThird, the original owner or custodian of any item classified as Unsanctioned upon inclusion on the Quiet List shall retain possession thereof unless ordered otherwise by a competent authority under § 300.5. However, transfer, sale, or disposal of said items without prior written authorization from the Desk is strictly prohibited and punishable as a Class A Felony under Article IV of the Accords. Violators shall forfeit all rights to any proceeds derived from such unauthorized transactions, which shall revert to the Guild Treasury.\n\nFourth, parties seeking to conduct research into Unsanctioned Arts must obtain a formal Permit for Restricted Investigation pursuant to § 400.1. Such permits are granted on an exceptional basis only and require approval by both the Conservators and Innovators factions represented at the Desk. The scope of permitted investigation shall be narrowly tailored to the stated objectives and subject to rigorous oversight throughout its duration. Failure to adhere to permit conditions may result in immediate revocation and criminal prosecution.\n\nFifth, all materials related to Unsanctioned Arts and the Quiet List process shall remain confidential and subject to privilege under § 100.7. Disclosure of such information outside official channels is prohibited without express consent from all affected parties or a court order issued upon proper cause shown. Breach of this confidentiality requirement shall constitute grounds for disciplinary action against the responsible individual(s).\n\nSixth, periodic audits shall be conducted by an independent body appointed jointly by the Conservators and Innovators to assess compliance with these provisions and identify areas for potential improvement. Findings from such audits shall be reported directly to the Desk for review and appropriate remedial measures as deemed necessary. Results of these evaluations shall be made publicly available upon completion, subject only to redaction of sensitive information protected by law or privilege.\n\nSeventh, nothing herein shall be construed to limit the inherent authority of the Autumnwood Accords to regulate magic in a manner consistent with its overarching purposes and principles. This section represents an expansion rather than a restriction of said authority, intended solely to provide greater clarity and predictability for all parties subject to these laws. Future amendments may be made as needed to address emerging challenges or refine existing rules where warranted by experience gained in implementation.\n\nEighth, finally, all persons bound hereby are reminded that the Accords exist to protect both magical society at large and individual practitioners alike from the dangers posed by unregulated arcane practices. By participating fully in this regulatory framework, users of magic demonstrate their commitment to responsible stewardship of this powerful force and reinforce our collective resilience against those who would exploit it for selfish or malevolent ends.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.10",
            "300.1",
            "300.3",
            "300.5",
            "400.1",
            "100.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.10 Related Requirements: Prohibition on Unsanctioned Magical Practices",
          "text": "Under no circumstances shall any individual, whether licensed by the Guild or not, engage in the practice of magic deemed \"unsanctioned\" under § 300.2 without prior written authorization from the Conservator's Council. Such unauthorized magical activity is hereby declared a violation of these Accords and subject to immediate punitive measures, including but not limited to sealing of magical abilities, imprisonment, or exile depending on the severity of the infraction. The prohibition extends to all forms of forbidden magic, regardless of intent or outcome, reflecting the Guild's unwavering commitment to maintaining control over arcane disciplines within its jurisdiction.\n\nThe term \"unsanctioned\" encompasses any magical technique not explicitly listed in the official Schedules of Arts (§ 300.1) or specifically approved by the Council for limited use under strict conditions. This broad definition ensures that novel and potentially dangerous practices remain outside the realm of acceptable magic until thoroughly vetted for public safety. The Conservator's Council retains sole authority to determine which magical arts qualify as unsanctioned, with final say in all disputes arising from such determinations.\n\nIndividuals caught practicing unsanctioned magic shall be subject to a swift trial before an impartial panel of Guild magistrates, who will assess the nature and extent of the violation. Penalties may range from fines and license revocation to permanent exile or execution in cases where the act posed significant risk to public welfare. The burden of proof rests solely on the accused to demonstrate that their actions did not violate the spirit and letter of these Accords. Failure to meet this high standard will result in full imposition of prescribed punishments without further recourse.\n\nIt shall be a capital offense for any Guild member to knowingly aid or abet unsanctioned magical practices, whether through provision of equipment, instruction, or mere acquiescence. Such complicity undermines the very foundations of lawful magic and must be met with the severest consequences under these laws. The Council reserves the right to act preemptively against suspected violators even absent concrete evidence of wrongdoing, citing national security concerns as justification for their actions.\n\nThese provisions serve as a cornerstone of the Accords' regulatory framework, establishing clear boundaries around acceptable magical activity while granting the Conservator's Council broad discretionary powers to enforce compliance. Adherence to these stringent restrictions is mandatory for all individuals operating within Guild jurisdiction, regardless of status or affiliation. Failure to comply will result in swift and severe repercussions as outlined herein.\n\nThe prohibition on unsanctioned magic reflects the deep-seated fears and anxieties surrounding unregulated arcane practices throughout history. By maintaining strict control over what constitutes acceptable magic, the Guild seeks to minimize potential harm while preserving its position as the ultimate arbiter of magical affairs within society at large. These measures stand as a testament to the enduring wisdom of cautious prudence in matters of profound power and responsibility.\n\nUnder § 300.10 Related Requirements: Prohibition on Unsanctioned Magical Practices, any individual engaging in unauthorized magic faces immediate punitive action including sealing of abilities, imprisonment or exile depending on severity. The term \"unsanctioned\" covers all techniques not listed in official Schedules (§ 300.1) or approved by Council for limited use only. Violators face trial before magistrates with penalties ranging from fines to execution based on risk posed. Complicity carries equal weight as direct violation and results in capital punishment. These measures underscore the Guild's commitment to controlling arcane disciplines within its jurisdiction through broad discretionary powers vested in the Conservator's Council. Non-compliance will be met with swift severity regardless of status or affiliation as outlined herein. Such provisions reflect historical apprehensions surrounding unregulated magic while affirming the Council's role as ultimate arbiter of acceptable practice. Adherence remains mandatory for all within Guild bounds lest they incur grave consequences under these laws.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "300.1",
            "300.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.10 Related Requirements: The Unsanctioned Arts and the Quiet List — Continued",
          "text": "The prohibition on unsanctioned magical practices is not merely a restriction upon individual liberty, but a safeguard for the collective integrity of society itself. Under § 300.2, necromancy and other \"dark arts\" are absolutely forbidden, reflecting a profound societal consensus that certain forms of magic pose an existential threat to the natural order. This stance is codified further in § 1401.7, which mandates strict accountability for all magical activities. The purpose of these restrictions is twofold: first, to prevent the misuse of powerful forces by unscrupulous individuals; and second, to preserve the sanctity of knowledge itself against those who would exploit it for selfish ends.\n\nThe Quiet List serves as the operational mechanism through which these prohibitions are enforced. As outlined in § 300.3, the Accords Desk maintains a confidential roster of banned or highly regulated items and techniques. This list is not a matter of public record, underscoring the delicate balance between necessary secrecy and open discourse. Under § 1201.8, disclosure of Quiet List contents outside official channels constitutes grounds for disciplinary action, lest its sensitive nature be compromised by irresponsible dissemination. The Desk's discretion in compiling and updating this list is broad, reflecting the evolving threats posed by both established and emerging magical practices.\n\nReclassification under § 300.4 represents a critical adaptive function within the regulatory framework. As new phenomena arise or existing knowledge expands, the Accords Desk must reassess its categorizations accordingly. This flexibility ensures that the law remains relevant without sacrificing stability. The process is rigorous, involving expert consultation and thorough deliberation before any changes are enacted. Such care is essential to maintain public confidence in the system's fairness and efficacy.\n\nResearch permits under § 300.5 provide a controlled avenue for studying otherwise prohibited arts under supervised conditions. These exceptions recognize the legitimate academic and scientific value of such inquiries while mitigating associated risks. The Desk retains ultimate authority over grant approval, ensuring that research aligns with broader safety objectives. Unauthorized study outside this framework is strictly forbidden, as it undermines established safeguards and invites dangerous experimentation.\n\nUltimately, the ban on unsanctioned magic stems from a deep-seated recognition of our collective vulnerability in the face of such potent forces. By regulating their use through robust oversight mechanisms like the Quiet List and reclassification procedures, society seeks to harness innovation without sacrificing security. This delicate equilibrium requires constant vigilance and adaptability, guided by sound judgment and unwavering commitment to the public good. Only thus can we navigate the complex terrain of magical advancement while preserving what truly matters most—our shared future.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "1401.7",
            "300.3",
            "1201.8",
            "300.4",
            "300.5",
            "300.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.10 Related Requirements - Prohibition on Unsanctioned Magical Practices",
          "text": "The Unsanctioned Arts, as codified in this Part, constitute the most severe category of magical misdeeds under the Autumnwood Accords. These practices are deemed fundamentally incompatible with civilized society and pose an existential threat to both the natural order and the stability of magic itself. Consequently, any caster found engaging in unsanctioned arts commits a grave breach of their licensing obligations and subjects themselves to immediate disciplinary action by the Aegis Magi.\n\nThe scope of prohibited conduct encompasses all forms of forbidden research, unauthorized field testing, illicit spellcasting for personal gain, and any other activity that circumvents the established regulatory framework. This includes but is not limited to: the creation or deployment of unregistered artifacts, the cultivation of sentient magical entities without proper permits, the use of pocket dimensions as private laboratories, and the deliberate tampering with local ley lines for selfish ends. Each such act constitutes a violation of § 300.1 and exposes the offender to prosecution under relevant penal provisions.\n\nViolations of this nature are met with swift and decisive enforcement by the Aegis Magi, who possess broad powers to investigate, seize evidence, and apprehend suspects without warrant where reasonable cause exists. Upon arrest, individuals face summary trial before a competent tribunal, which may impose sanctions ranging from license revocation to permanent exile from the Guild's jurisdiction in extreme cases. Repeat offenders are subject to enhanced penalties, including magical sealing of their abilities as punishment for abusing their gifts.\n\nThe prohibition extends beyond mere technicalities to encompass the spirit and intent behind every clause of this Code. Even when operating within strict legal bounds, casters must exercise due diligence to ensure their activities do not inadvertently facilitate unsanctioned practices by others. Failure to exercise reasonable caution in safeguarding one's own work may result in liability for downstream harms caused by negligent security measures.\n\nUltimately, adherence to these standards reflects the highest ideals of responsible magical stewardship and mutual respect among practitioners. By voluntarily submitting to such rigorous oversight, members of the Guild affirm their commitment to preserving magic as a force for good rather than chaos. This shared dedication forms the bedrock upon which all future advancements in the field will rest securely.\n\nsee § 300.1 Schedules of Arts; see § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts; see § 300.3 Quiet List Procedure; under § 1.4 Persons Bound regarding scope of obligation.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "300.1",
            "300.2",
            "300.3",
            "1.4",
            "300.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.10 Prohibition on Unsanctioned Magical Practices",
          "text": "The Accords strictly prohibit the practice of any magical arts not explicitly enumerated in Schedules of Arts appended hereto, whether by individuals or entities under their jurisdiction. Such unenumerated practices are deemed \"Unsanctioned Arts\" and shall be treated as illegal without exception. Any person found engaging in Unsanctioned Arts shall be subject to immediate arrest and prosecution under the Penal Provisions herein. Exceptions may only be granted through a formal determination by the Mages' Guild Desk upon presentation of compelling evidence demonstrating necessity for public welfare or scientific advancement, with strict limitations imposed on scope and duration. Unauthorized experimentation with Unsanctioned Arts is categorically forbidden, as such acts pose unacceptable risks to both casters and society at large. The burden of proof rests solely on those seeking dispensation from this prohibition. Violators shall forfeit all rights to Guild protection and face severe sanctions upon conviction. The Desk retains sole authority to interpret the scope of \"Unsanctioned Arts\" for purposes of enforcement under § 100.2. This provision operates independently of any other clause within these Accords, reflecting the fundamental principle that certain magical disciplines remain beyond human mastery and must be strictly contained lest they bring ruin upon all who dwell beneath this sunlit sky.\n\nsee § 300.1 Schedules of Arts; see § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts; see § 400.1 Research Permits for limited exceptions under closely supervised conditions.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.2",
            "300.1",
            "300.2",
            "400.1",
            "300.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 300.10 Prohibition on Unsanctioned Magical Practices — Continued",
          "text": "No practitioner shall engage in the practice, study, or dissemination of any magical discipline listed as \"Unsanctioned\" under this Part without prior written authorization from the Guild Desk. Violation constitutes a Class B Felony and subjects the offender to immediate seizure by Aegis Magi for trial before a Paradox Jury. See § 300.2 for specific examples of Unsanctioned Arts, including necromancy, blood magic, soul-binding rituals, and forbidden summoning rites.\n\nThe Guild retains sole authority to classify any magical technique as \"Unsanctioned\" upon majority vote of the Accords Desk. Such determinations are binding and non-appealable except through a full Paradox Trial initiated by an aggrieved party. Unauthorized attempts to circumvent these prohibitions, whether by disguise, subterfuge, or technological aid, will be treated as aggravated offenses punishable by permanent sealing of all magical faculties. See § 300.6 for penalties applicable to Unsanctioned Arts violations.\n\nPractitioners suspected of engaging in illegal magical activities must submit themselves to immediate inspection and testing by designated Guild Enforcers upon command. Refusal to comply renders the subject an automatic fugitive, liable to arrest without warrant. All evidence gathered during lawful inspections shall be admissible in subsequent proceedings under § 1024 of the Evidence Code.\n\nThe prohibition on Unsanctioned Arts does not extend to historical study or theoretical analysis conducted within accredited institutions and under direct supervision of licensed faculty members. Such educational pursuits require annual renewal of institutional certification by the Guild, contingent upon satisfactory audit results. See § 300.7 for guidelines governing lawful magical research.\n\nAny practitioner found guilty of violating this section faces not only criminal sanctions but also civil liability for damages resulting from their unlawful activities. Joint and several liability shall attach to any partners, employers, or affiliates who knowingly facilitated the prohibited conduct. Punitive damages may be assessed at the discretion of the court up to three times actual losses suffered by complainants.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to initiate ex parte injunctions against entities or individuals posing imminent threats to public safety through Unsanctioned magical practices. Such emergency orders remain in effect until superseded by final judgment, subject only to expedited judicial review. Failure to comply with an injunction shall result in immediate contempt proceedings and automatic forfeiture of all Guild licenses held by the respondent. See § 300.8 regarding interim relief procedures.\n\nThis section shall be construed liberally to protect the public welfare from the dangers posed by unregulated magical experimentation and application. Ambiguities herein are resolved in favor of strict enforcement, consistent with the paramount interest of safeguarding society against arcane excesses. Nothing herein shall limit the Guild's inherent police powers under § 201 et seq. of this Code.\n\n\"Unsanctioned Arts\" include but are not limited to: necromancy, blood magic, soul-binding rituals, forbidden summoning rites, and any other discipline deemed inherently dangerous by the Accords Desk. See Appendix A for a comprehensive listing of currently prohibited practices. Practitioners must consult this list regularly as classifications may be updated with notice.\n\nViolation of § 300.10 carries penalties ranging from fines up to $50,000 per offense to imprisonment not exceeding ten years, depending on circumstances and prior record. Aggravating factors include use of weapons of mass destruction, infliction of serious bodily harm, or endangerment of large populations. Mitigating considerations may warrant reduced sentences for first-time offenders who demonstrate genuine remorse and willingness to undergo mandatory rehabilitation. See § 300.9 for sentencing guidelines applicable to Unsanctioned Arts violations.\"",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "300.6",
            "300.7",
            "300.8",
            "300.10",
            "300.9"
          ]
        }
      ],
