    {
      "cite": "900.10",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Duty to Disclose Potential Hazards",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Duty to Disclose Potential Hazards",
          "text": "The obligations imposed by these Provisions are cumulative and do not preclude other duties mandated by applicable law or regulation. A caster licensed under this Codex must comply with all terms of their license agreement, as well as any additional conditions imposed by the Desk upon issuance thereof. Failure to adhere to such stipulations may result in disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure.\n\nCasters are further required to maintain accurate records of all magical activities conducted under their authority. This includes detailed logs of spell components consumed, target recipients of enchantments, and any adverse effects observed or reported by third parties. Such documentation shall be retained for a minimum period of seven years from the date of completion and made available for inspection upon reasonable request by Guild officials.\n\nIn instances where a casting produces unintended consequences or causes harm to persons or property, the responsible caster must immediately notify the Desk in writing within forty-eight (48) hours of becoming aware of said outcome. Such notice shall include all known facts surrounding the incident, including but not limited to: full identification of affected parties, precise description of resulting damage, and any mitigating actions taken by the caster. Prompt cooperation with subsequent investigations is compulsory; evasion or obstruction will be treated as a separate offense subject to enhanced penalties.\n\nThe Desk reserves discretion to waive certain reporting thresholds for minor infractions deemed unlikely to recur or cause significant disruption. However, waivers are granted only at sole discretion of individual clerks and remain revocable without notice upon discovery of prior noncompliance by the licensee. Requests for exemption must be submitted in duplicate via certified mail no less than sixty (60) days before planned operation, allowing adequate time for review and approval cycles to complete successfully.\n\nThis section shall be construed liberally in favor of protecting public safety and welfare over promoting commercial interests or individual convenience. Any ambiguity regarding scope or application shall be resolved according to established principles of administrative law governing similar regulatory regimes nationally and internationally. Disputes arising from interpretation or enforcement hereunder are subject to exclusive jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, whose determinations herein are final and binding absent successful appeal under § 105.3.\n\n\"Magic isn't just about throwing fireballs—it's about responsibility too.\" — Archmage Theron on licensing compliance",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "105.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.10 Duty to Disclose Potential Hazards",
          "text": "Any caster who knows or reasonably should know that an item contains latent magical properties must disclose this fact before completing any transaction involving said item. Failure to do so constitutes misrepresentation and may result in revocation of licensure under § 200.1(b). This duty extends beyond mere verbal disclosure; the seller must take affirmative steps to warn potential buyers of known dangers, such as providing a written notice or affixing a warning label. See also § 300.8 regarding labeling requirements for enchanted goods.\n\nThe burden of proof rests upon the accused caster to demonstrate that they acted in good faith and exercised reasonable diligence in determining whether an item possessed magical qualities prior to sale. In cases where doubt exists, the presumption favors public safety over commercial convenience. Archmage Veyra has ruled repeatedly that ignorance of the law is no excuse when it comes to matters of arcane hazard. Under § 1.7(c), any ambiguities in this section shall be resolved against the party seeking to avoid liability.\n\nThis provision does not apply to items explicitly marked as \"non-magical\" or those whose enchantments have fully degraded over time and pose no risk upon inspection by a qualified caster. However, the seller retains the obligation to verify such claims through appropriate testing when feasible. See § 400.6 for standards governing magical item assessment. Any disputes arising from alleged breaches of this duty shall be adjudicated in accordance with the Paradox Trial procedures outlined in Part X. The Guild maintains a comprehensive database of known hazardous items and their effects, which is updated regularly to reflect new discoveries. All registered casters are required to consult this resource before engaging in any commercial activity involving potentially dangerous artifacts. Failure to do so may constitute negligence per se under applicable common law principles.\n\n\"The burden of proof rests upon the accused caster to demonstrate that they acted in good faith...\" - Archmage Veyra, Decision No. 1472-B",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "300.8",
            "1.7",
            "400.6",
            "900.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.10 Duty to Disclose Potential Hazards — Continued",
          "text": "Every licensor casting within the Guild's jurisdiction shall disclose all known material hazards associated with their specific practice or artifact prior to commencing service. Failure to do so constitutes gross negligence and may result in immediate suspension of licensure pending investigation. This duty extends beyond mere verbal notification; it requires affirmative action to warn clients of risks that a reasonable person would not anticipate, such as hidden enchantments, unstable magical residues, or unforeseen side effects of potent spells.\n\nThe standard for disclosure is objective: what should have been revealed under the circumstances, regardless of whether actual harm occurred. A caster who knowingly omits critical safety information to maximize profit violates this mandate even if their client suffers no damage. Conversely, accidental omission due to genuine forgetfulness or oversight may be excused upon proof of diligent effort to comply with all reporting obligations. Ultimately, transparency and precaution remain the bedrock of responsible magical practice under the Autumnwood Accords.\n\nSee § 900.1 for related filing requirements; see also § 300.2 on unsanctioned arts and § 400.5 concerning unauthorized field use. Underlying principles regarding professional conduct are set forth in §§ 1.7 and 1.8. For penalties applicable to non-compliance, refer to Part V of this Codex.\n\n\"Disclose all known hazards before casting.\" - Archmage Veyra's Directive #42",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "300.2",
            "400.5",
            "1.7",
            "900.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.10 Duty to Disclose Potential Hazards and Mitigation Standards",
          "text": "Under § 900.6, all registered mages bear an affirmative duty to disclose any known hazards associated with their intended casting before commencing operations. This obligation applies regardless of whether the hazard is obvious or obscure, provided that a reasonable practitioner would recognize the risk in the circumstances. Failure to make such disclosure shall constitute negligence per se and may result in civil liability and disciplinary sanctions under § 100.20.\n\nThe term \"hazards\" encompasses not only physical dangers such as explosions or structural collapse but also magical effects including unintended enchantments, area-of-effect spells, residual psychic trauma, and ecological disruption. See § 300.7 for the definition of \"ecological disruption.\" Mages must evaluate all potential hazards through a combination of theoretical analysis, empirical testing where feasible, and consultation with qualified peers as directed by § 400.12.\n\nWhere hazards cannot be fully mitigated even after reasonable efforts, mages must either refrain from casting altogether or obtain informed consent from affected parties in writing pursuant to § 900.5. Such consent shall specify the nature and extent of the risks undertaken voluntarily. Any waiver obtained under duress or without adequate explanation shall be void ab initio. Under § 1401.3, ambiguous waivers are construed strictly against the drafter.\n\nMages failing to comply with this duty shall face enhanced penalties upon conviction, including mandatory remediation costs and permanent revocation of licensure for egregious violations. See § 200.8 regarding cumulative sanctions. The burden of proof rests solely on the accused mage to demonstrate that all reasonable precautions were taken in accordance with established safety protocols outlined in Part IV. This standard applies irrespective of actual harm suffered, reflecting the guild's commitment to proactive hazard management rather than mere reactive compensation.\n\nThe provisions herein complement but do not supersede applicable labor laws and workplace safety regulations enforced by other agencies. However, given the unique nature of arcane phenomena, certain hazards may fall outside conventional regulatory frameworks and thus require specialized oversight as detailed in this section. Mages should consult with legal counsel when navigating overlapping obligations between guild statutes and external mandates. For further guidance on hazard assessment methodologies, refer to Appendix C of the Master Spellbook. Any disputes arising under this article shall be resolved by arbitration unless otherwise provided by mutual agreement among all parties involved.\n\n*END OF SECTION*",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "900.6",
            "100.20",
            "300.7",
            "400.12",
            "900.5",
            "1401.3",
            "200.8",
            "900.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.10 Duty to Disclose Potential Hazards and Mitigation Standards — Continued",
          "text": "§ 900.10 imposes upon every registered caster an affirmative duty to disclose any known potential hazards inherent in the proposed magical operation, whether arising from the nature of the spellwork itself or from interactions with environmental factors. Failure to make such disclosure shall constitute grounds for immediate suspension of licensure and referral for penal proceedings under § 900.12.\n\nThe obligation to disclose extends beyond mere verbal notification; it requires that all relevant technical data, risk assessments, and mitigation plans be submitted in writing to the appropriate Guild office at least seventy-two hours prior to casting. This includes detailed information regarding power draw estimates, potential collateral damage zones, and contingency measures for unforeseen complications. See § 900.6 for requirements concerning the identification of fees associated with such disclosures.\n\nSpecial provisions apply when the proposed spellwork involves any artifact listed on the Quiet List or possesses unknown properties. In such cases, dual disclosure must be made to both the Conservator's Office and the Innovators' Bureau, each retaining independent copies of all submitted materials for a minimum period of five years. See § 900.3 regarding procedures for reclassification of previously sanitized items.\n\nCasters who knowingly conceal hazards shall be held personally liable for any resultant damage or injury, regardless of whether such harm falls within the scope of their contractual obligations. This personal liability extends to all corporate officers and authorized agents acting on behalf of a registered entity. Under § 900.8, failure to comply with disclosure mandates constitutes a material breach of contract and may justify termination without notice.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to conduct independent verification testing of any proposed spellwork prior to granting casting authorization. Casters who refuse such testing shall be deemed non-compliant subject to immediate sanctions. See § 900.7 for provisions relating to courtesy inspections at foreign halls. All disclosures must be made in good faith; fraudulent misrepresentations will result in permanent revocation of licensure and referral for criminal prosecution under applicable state statutes.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "900.10",
            "900.12",
            "900.6",
            "900.3",
            "900.8",
            "900.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 9010 Prohibition on Unauthorized Research Facilities and Pocket Dimensions",
          "text": "No caster shall establish or operate any research laboratory, workshop, or similar facility without prior written authorization from the Guild's Safety Division. Such authorization may be granted only upon demonstration of adequate safety protocols and containment measures sufficient to protect both public health and magical stability. See § 9015 regarding emergency shutdown procedures for unauthorized facilities.\n\nThe possession or operation of any pocket dimension not specifically designated as a research laboratory shall constitute a felony offense under these Accords. Exceptions are made solely for dimensions used exclusively for personal storage or temporary refuge, provided such use does not interfere with public safety or magical equilibrium. See § 9016 for registration requirements concerning personal dimensional spaces.\n\nUnauthorized conversion of any existing space into a research facility is strictly prohibited without explicit permission from the property owner and relevant Guild authorities. Violations shall be subject to immediate seizure of equipment and assets, along with permanent revocation of all future casting privileges. See § 9017 for provisions governing disposal of confiscated magical apparatus.\n\nCasters who discover unauthorized research operations in progress must immediately notify local law enforcement and Guild inspectors without delay. Failure to report such violations shall result in disciplinary action up to and including suspension of licensure, regardless of whether the reporting caster suffered any personal harm or financial loss from the illegal activity. See § 9018 regarding whistleblower protections and rewards for timely disclosures.\n\nThe use of pocket dimensions as temporary sanctuaries during emergencies is permitted provided such usage does not exceed twenty-four consecutive hours. Extended occupation requires special dispensation from the Conservator's Office demonstrating compelling justification and adequate safeguards against misuse or exploitation of the dimensional space. See § 9019 concerning limits on duration and frequency of emergency refuge utilization.\n\nAny caster found operating an illegal research facility shall be subject to immediate arrest and prosecution under both state and Guild laws. Confiscated equipment will be destroyed unless deemed necessary for ongoing investigations. Repeat offenders face enhanced penalties including permanent expulsion from the magical community and disqualification from future licensure applications. See § 9020 regarding forfeiture proceedings and asset liquidation.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 9015 Emergency Shutdown Procedures and Safety Protocols",
          "text": "All registered research facilities must be equipped with automated safety systems capable of immediate shutdown upon detection of hazardous conditions, whether resulting from equipment malfunction or operator error. Such systems shall be tested monthly by certified technicians to ensure full operational readiness at all times. See § 9021 regarding certification requirements for safety inspection personnel.\n\nIn the event of an emergency requiring evacuation, all casters must adhere strictly to established exit routes and assembly points designated by facility management. Unauthorized re-entry into affected areas shall be prohibited until official clearance is granted by both law enforcement and Guild inspectors. See § 9022 concerning penalties for violation of evacuation orders.\n\nCasters operating in conjunction with non-magical entities must maintain clear lines of communication regarding potential hazards and appropriate response procedures. Joint safety meetings shall be conducted quarterly to review incident reports and update contingency plans as necessary. See § 9023 regarding mandatory training requirements for mixed-use facilities.\n\nIn cases where emergency services are required, casters must provide immediate assistance consistent with their capabilities while avoiding any actions that might exacerbate the situation or endanger themselves unnecessarily. Refusal to render aid when reasonably able shall constitute grounds for disciplinary action under these Accords. See § 9024 outlining scope of duty to assist.\n\nAll emergency procedures must be prominently posted in visible locations throughout each facility, accompanied by detailed instructions tailored to specific hazard types present at that location. Failure to maintain such signage shall result in automatic suspension of operating permits until corrected. See § 9025 regarding content and placement requirements for safety notices.\n\nCasters are prohibited from conducting any experimental procedures within residential zones or densely populated areas without explicit written consent from affected parties and prior approval from the Guild's Risk Assessment Division. Violations shall be treated as major infractions subject to severe sanctions including permanent revocation of licensure. See § 9026 concerning restrictions on urban spellcasting operations.",
          "key": "g",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 9021 Certification Requirements for Safety Inspection Personnel",
          "text": "All individuals conducting official safety inspections must hold current certification issued by the Guild's Board of Standards, demonstrating proficiency in both theoretical and practical aspects of magical hazard assessment. Such certifications shall be renewed annually through completion of approved continuing education programs. See § 9027 regarding curriculum requirements for recertification courses.\n\nInspection personnel are authorized to enter any facility operating under a valid license upon presentation of proper credentials. Resistance to lawful inspection constitutes obstruction of justice and may result in criminal charges being filed against the responsible parties. See § 9028 outlining powers granted to certified inspectors during examinations.\n\nCasters found violating safety regulations shall be subject to immediate suspension pending full investigation into the circumstances surrounding the infraction. Final determination of culpability will be made by an independent panel convened specifically for each case. See § 9029 detailing due process protections afforded during disciplinary hearings.\n\nThe Guild maintains a centralized registry of all certified inspection professionals, accessible via public portal for verification purposes. Outdated listings shall be corrected promptly upon receipt of notification from affected parties or through routine auditing procedures. See § 9030 concerning maintenance and accuracy of official records.\n\nCasters who dispute findings made by certified inspectors may request re-examination within fourteen calendar days of initial notice, subject to payment of an applicable administrative fee. Such requests must be submitted in writing with supporting documentation addressing alleged errors or omissions in the original assessment. See § 9031 outlining appeal process and associated costs.\n\nAll inspection reports shall include detailed findings along with specific recommendations for corrective action where deficiencies are identified. Casters failing to implement such recommendations within prescribed timeframes will face escalating penalties proportional to the severity of unresolved hazards. See § 9032 regarding enforcement mechanisms for compliance mandates.",
          "key": "h",
          "refs": []
        }
      ],
