    {
      "cite": "500.10",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Compensation and Indemnification",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Compensation and Indemnification",
          "text": "The provisions contained herein are intended to supplement and clarify the obligations imposed upon registered casters by the core articles of this Part. Nothing in these related requirements shall be construed to diminish or expand any rights, duties, or liabilities expressly set forth elsewhere in the Autumnwood Accords. In the event of a conflict between this section and another provision within § 500, the more specific language shall control. Furthermore, compliance with all applicable statutes, regulations, orders, and directives issued by the Guild or its authorized agents is hereby deemed a condition precedent to lawful practice under any license granted hereunder. Failure to adhere to these related requirements may result in immediate revocation of said license, imposition of sanctions, or other appropriate remedial action as determined by the governing council at its sole discretion. All casters are expected to familiarize themselves fully with both this section and the broader context provided herein before engaging in any professional magical activities within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords.\n\nsee § 500.1 Field Casting for Value; see § 500.6 Related Requirements; see Part I, Article IV; under § 100.4 Records of Speech and § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "500.1",
            "500.6",
            "100.4",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.10 Related Requirements - Compensation and Indemnification",
          "text": "§ 500.10 mandates that all mages providing professional casting services must secure written contracts outlining compensation terms, liability limits, and indemnity provisions prior to commencing any work. Failure to adhere to these contractual obligations may result in disciplinary action by the Guild, including suspension of licensure or revocation of privileges. Mages acting outside the bounds of established agreements assume all risks associated with their activities, absolving the Guild of responsibility for any damages incurred.\n\nFurther, § 500.10 stipulates that clients seeking magical services must present valid identification and proof of licensure when making payments. Cash transactions only are permitted under no circumstances; checks, drafts, or other negotiable instruments require immediate verification by a designated Guild official before acceptance. Any discrepancies discovered after the fact shall be grounds for voiding the contract entirely and pursuing legal action against the offending party.\n\nIn addition, § 500.10 requires that all compensation payments be made within thirty (30) days of service completion, unless otherwise agreed upon in writing by both parties. Late fees accrue at a rate of five percent (5%) per month on outstanding balances, compounded monthly until paid in full. Mages refusing to accept timely payment may file a complaint with the Guild's Ethics Committee for expedited resolution.\n\nFinally, § 500.10 reserves the right of the Guild to intervene in any dispute arising from casting services rendered under these provisions. Such intervention shall be deemed necessary and justified when parties fail to resolve matters amicably within a reasonable timeframe. In extreme cases involving significant financial loss or personal injury, the Guild may assume direct control over proceedings to protect public safety interests.\n\nThis section ensures fair treatment of all participants in the magical economy while maintaining high standards for professional conduct. By establishing clear guidelines for compensation and accountability, § 500.10 promotes trust between clients and mages alike, fostering a stable environment conducive to continued growth and innovation within our esteemed profession.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "500.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.10 Related Requirements - Compensation and Indemnification — Continued",
          "text": "Any contract entered into by a licensed caster which creates a legal obligation for the performance of magical services shall be construed as containing an express provision that such services are rendered without compensation unless otherwise agreed in writing. This default rule applies notwithstanding any contrary term or understanding, whether oral or implied. It is intended to protect the public from exploitative practices wherein casters demand exorbitant fees under the guise of emergency services.\n\nNotwithstanding the foregoing, a caster may contract for additional compensation beyond the standard fee schedule if such arrangement is fully disclosed prior to engagement and approved in writing by two disinterested members of the Guild Desk. Any attempt to evade this requirement through sham agreements or side deals shall be treated as fraud and punishable under § 204.15. The burden of proving any waiver of this default rule rests upon the caster seeking additional remuneration.\n\nIn cases where a magical service results in damage, injury, or loss beyond what was reasonably foreseeable at the time of contracting, the affected party may seek indemnification from the providing caster regardless of whether compensation was originally stipulated. This right survives termination of any contractual relationship and is independent of any claim for breach of contract or negligence. The Guild shall provide reasonable assistance in pursuing such claims through its arbitration procedures.\n\nNo caster shall agree to bear sole responsibility for damages arising from their services without explicit acknowledgment that they assume unlimited liability therefor. Any clause purporting to exclude or limit a caster's liability for bodily injury, death, or property damage resulting from gross negligence or willful misconduct shall be void as contrary to public policy. The Guild reserves the right to investigate and regulate any attempt by casters to shift liability onto third parties or insurers in violation of this provision.\n\nThis section shall apply to all contracts entered into on or after its effective date, whether oral or written, express or implied. Nothing herein shall be construed to impair any existing rights or remedies available at law or equity, but rather serves as a supplementary guide for determining the scope and extent thereof in the absence of contrary agreement. Any ambiguity regarding compensation or liability shall be resolved in favor of the protecting party, consistent with this Code's overriding purpose of safeguarding the public welfare against magical abuses.\n\nsee § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; see § 204.15 Fraud and Deception; see § 302.6 Unauthorized Practice; see § 708.9 Indemnification Clauses",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "204.15",
            "100.5",
            "302.6",
            "708.9",
            "500.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.10 Compensation and Indemnification in Field Operations",
          "text": "§ 500.10 shall govern the compensation and indemnification obligations arising from field casting activities conducted under an active permit issued by this Desk. The primary objective is to ensure fair remuneration for services rendered while mitigating risks associated with unauthorized or substandard performance. Where a caster has been duly authorized to engage in field work, they are entitled to just compensation commensurate with the scope of expertise demonstrated and value delivered to the client. This may include upfront fees, hourly rates, or completion bonuses as mutually agreed upon between parties prior to commencement of services. Any such agreement shall be reduced to writing and filed with this Desk for record keeping purposes in accordance with § 100.4.\n\nFurthermore, casters engaging in field operations assume responsibility for maintaining adequate professional liability insurance coverage throughout the duration of their contracted engagements. Failure to maintain such coverage shall constitute grounds for immediate suspension or revocation of applicable permits under provisions of § 200.3 and § 200.4 respectively. In the event that a caster's actions result in damage to property, injury to persons, or other foreseeable harm arising directly from their professional duties, they shall be liable for all resulting costs and damages incurred by third parties not otherwise barred by statute or common law defenses. Such liability cannot be contractually waived except as expressly permitted under applicable state laws governing exculpatory clauses.\n\nIn addition to direct compensation obligations, casters are required to provide comprehensive indemnification protection against claims brought against them by clients or their agents in connection with field services rendered. This shall encompass defense costs, settlements, judgments, and other expenses associated with resolving any disputes stemming from the casting work performed. Indemnity provisions must be clearly outlined in all service agreements executed pursuant to § 500.1 authorizations. Where such clauses are omitted or ambiguous, they will not be enforceable as a matter of public policy unless expressly ratified by written instrument filed with this Desk.\n\nThis section represents the culmination of considerations regarding equitable treatment and risk allocation inherent in professional magical services. By establishing clear guidelines for compensation and liability, § 500.10 seeks to foster trust between casters and clients while safeguarding against abusive practices that could undermine the integrity of the field casting industry as a whole. It is imperative that all parties adhere strictly to these provisions to maintain harmony within the broader magical community governed by the Autumnwood Accords. Violations may subject offending entities to disciplinary action ranging from monetary penalties up to and including permanent revocation of operating privileges under severe circumstances.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "500.10",
            "100.4",
            "200.3",
            "200.4",
            "500.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.10 Compensation and Indemnification in Field Operations — Continued",
          "text": "Mages engaged in field operations under the authority of a Guild permit are entitled to compensation for services rendered, subject to the terms outlined herein. Payment shall be made promptly upon satisfactory completion of the contracted task, with deductions permitted only as expressly authorized by the contracting party. In no event shall reimbursement exceed the original value of the issued permit plus reasonable out-of-pocket expenses documented in triplicate. Failure to remit payment within thirty days constitutes a default under this section and may result in revocation of future permits and referral for disciplinary action.\n\nConversely, Guild-mandated emergency operations conducted without prior authorization are deemed acts of public service. Mages participating therein shall not be compensated monies directly; instead, they accrue credit toward their annual permit fee at a rate equivalent to one-quarter the standard charge per hour worked. Such credits may be applied against subsequent billed services rendered by the Guild or held for future use at the sole discretion of the Archmage Sovereign. Refusal to participate in bona fide emergencies without extenuating circumstances shall result in immediate suspension and potential forfeiture of standing privileges.\n\nIn all instances where a mage incurs liability arising from the performance of contracted duties, the client agrees to assume full responsibility for any ensuing damages or claims. This indemnification extends not only to third parties but also includes reimbursement for legal fees incurred by the Guild in defending against wrongful suits brought as a consequence of the work performed. The scope of this obligation is limited solely to events directly traceable to the authorized activities under the permit and excludes any negligent or intentional misconduct on the part of the practitioner.\n\nAny dispute regarding compensation, indemnity, or related matters shall be resolved through binding arbitration administered by a panel convened by the Autumnwood Accords Desk. The arbiters' decision shall be final and enforceable without further recourse to conventional courts unless manifest error is demonstrated. Costs associated with the arbitration process are initially advanced equally by both parties but ultimately borne by the prevailing side as determined at conclusion of proceedings. Prompt resolution of such disputes remains paramount to maintaining operational efficiency throughout the Guild's vast network of field agents across the continent and beyond.\n\nsee § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks regarding authorized activities; see § 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion concerning liability for damages; under § 700.2 Standards of Practice, negligent or intentional misconduct is strictly prohibited; refer to § 900.4 Dispute Resolution Procedures for binding arbitration mechanisms applicable across all Guild contracts and agreements.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.6",
            "500.3",
            "700.2",
            "900.4",
            "500.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.10 Compensation and Indemnification in Field Operations — Administration",
          "text": "§ 500.10 Compensation and Indemnification in Field Operations — Continued\n\nIt is further provided that any caster engaged in field operations pursuant to a valid permit shall be entitled to compensation as agreed upon between the caster and the client, subject to Guild oversight. Such compensation must be paid promptly upon completion of services and may not be used as leverage for future favors or obligations. In the event of dispute regarding fees owed, the aggrieved party may petition the Desk for adjudication under the provisions of § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting.\n\nMoreover, all casters operating outside their licensed jurisdiction shall maintain adequate insurance coverage to protect against potential liabilities arising from their activities. Failure to secure appropriate indemnification shall be deemed a violation of this section and may result in suspension or revocation of said caster's credentials. The Desk reserves the right to review policy documents at its discretion, as mandated by § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks.\n\nIn cases where field operations necessitate temporary reclassification under § 300.4 Reclassification, additional safeguards shall be implemented to mitigate risks associated with such activities. These may include heightened supervision, restricted access zones, or mandatory debriefings upon return from duty. The ultimate goal remains the preservation of public safety while enabling legitimate magical pursuits within established parameters.\n\nFinally, any breaches of this subsection shall be subject to penalties commensurate with the severity of infractions committed. Repeated offenses may lead to permanent disqualification from field operations unless specifically exempted by special dispensation from the Sovereign Archmage. All casters are urged to familiarize themselves thoroughly with these guidelines before undertaking any independent ventures beyond their home districts, lest they find themselves entangled in legal complexities far more burdensome than those inherent to mere spellcasting itself.\n\nAs always, compliance with these regulations shall be strictly enforced by authorized agents of the Mages' Guild upon reasonable suspicion of violations. Refusal to cooperate during an investigation may constitute grounds for immediate arrest and prosecution under applicable laws governing magical practice. Let it be remembered that freedom to explore new frontiers of magic is balanced against responsibility towards community welfare—a delicate equilibrium maintained only through diligent adherence to these mandates set forth hereinabove.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "500.10",
            "100.5",
            "100.6",
            "300.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
