    {
      "cite": "600.10",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreement",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.10 Related Requirements - Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreement",
          "text": "In the realm of arcane contracts involving sentient or semi-sentient constructs, additional safeguards are deemed necessary to protect all parties involved from exploitation and abuse. Under § 600.2, any agreement wherein a construct serves as an agent or proxy must include explicit provisions regarding its rights and limitations of duty. This includes stipulations for compensation beyond mere upkeep costs, as well as mechanisms for dispute resolution should the terms of engagement be breached by either party.\n\nFurthermore, § 600.3 prohibits the use of geas-like compulsion clauses in such contracts, deeming them unconscionable under existing precedent set by the High Arcane Court. Any construct forced into servitude against its will through magical domination is afforded protection under §§ 1.4 and 200.6, with remedies available via the Guild's penal system as outlined in § 300.8. Construct owners are reminded that abuse of their wards may result in severe sanctions, including revocation of operating licenses.\n\nRegarding construct labor specifically, § 600.4 mandates a minimum wage equivalent to one-half the prevailing human rate for similar skilled trades, adjusted for inflation and magical fluctuation indices. Overtime provisions shall apply after six consecutive hours of active service, at double the base hourly rate unless otherwise agreed upon in writing by both parties. Construct owners failing to meet these standards face fines up to 10% of their annual gross revenue per violation.\n\nSummoned notaries, as defined under § 600.5, must maintain impartiality throughout contract negotiations and execution phases. They are prohibited from accepting gifts or favors that could compromise their neutrality. Any construct engaging in bribery or coercion during the contractual process shall be subject to immediate seizure of assets and potential exile from Guild jurisdiction entirely.\n\nFinally, all such agreements must include clear termination clauses outlining conditions under which either party may lawfully dissolve the arrangement without penalty beyond standard notice periods. This protects both masters and wards from being trapped indefinitely in unfavorable circumstances should unforeseen events arise. Construct owners are urged to review these stipulations carefully before signing any binding pact involving their charges, as failure to do so may result in unintended legal liabilities down the line.\n\n\"Magic binds us all—let us ensure those bonds remain honorable.\" — Archmage Veyra, Autumnwood Accords Desk",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "600.2",
            "600.3",
            "1.4",
            "300.8",
            "600.4",
            "600.5",
            "600.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.10 Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreements",
          "text": "Contracts entered into by artificial beings, whether golems, homunculi, or animated automatons, shall be governed by these special provisions absent contrary intent. A construct may bind itself to a covenant so long as the obligations do not exceed its programmed capacities nor violate fundamental principles of magic. Any clause requiring an entity beyond the construct's nature to act is void ab initio and renders the entire agreement unenforceable against the maker. § 600.10 See also § 400.8 on Related Requirements for additional guidance on magical contracts involving non-natural persons.\n\nThe concept of \"minimum ward\" applies uniquely to construct labor agreements, mandating that no contract impose upon a golem or similar being less protection than afforded a natural person in identical circumstances. This standard ensures constructs are not exploited as disposable tools but treated as sentient parties deserving of dignity and safeguarding against harm from third sources. Violations of this principle may constitute grounds for rescission under § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting, where the construct's maker negligently exposes it to undue peril. § 600.10 Cross-reference § 200.3 Temporary Mouths regarding limits on delegated authority to non-human agents; similar constraints apply here by analogy.\n\nSummoned notaries possess exclusive jurisdiction over disputes arising from construct employment relationships, whether contested in open court or via binding arbitration. Their specialized knowledge of both legal theory and arcane mechanics equips them to render informed rulings where ordinary magistrates lack expertise. Parties may waive this right only with explicit written consent and independent counsel, lest they forfeit essential protections against predatory employers or malicious contractors seeking to extort favors through threatened sabotage. § 600.10 Under § 300.8 Related Requirements, unauthorized practice of magic remains strictly prohibited; constructs violating this ban face immediate revocation of licensure plus potential criminal charges under applicable statutes.\n\nRelated provisions governing contracts between casters and non-magical entities are set forth in § 500.1 Field Casting for Value, which clarifies compensation standards when magical services are rendered beyond the scope of standard professional duties. This section ensures fairness to both parties by establishing clear expectations regarding payment terms, performance metrics, and liability allocation in mixed-agency arrangements where technical proficiency intersects with supernatural capability. Failure to adhere to these guidelines may result in disciplinary action up to and including expulsion from good standing within the Guild's ranks. § 600.10 Refer to § 700.8 Related Requirements for further details on ethical considerations when engaging in collaborative magical endeavors across faction lines or ideological divides.\n\nIn summary, artificial persons entering into binding covenants must abide by these tailored rules designed specifically for their unique circumstances and limitations. By respecting the inherent boundaries of such agreements while exercising due diligence, all stakeholders can maintain harmonious working relationships grounded in mutual trust and legally sound foundations. Any deviation from this framework invites costly consequences far outweighing any short-term gains derived from circumventing established norms. § 600.10 Final note: Consult § 800.9 Related Requirements regarding conflict resolution procedures specific to inter-factional disputes involving magical practitioners or their creations.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "600.10",
            "400.8",
            "100.5",
            "200.3",
            "300.8",
            "500.1",
            "700.8",
            "800.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreements",
          "text": "§ 600.10 Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreements shall govern all contracts entered into between Mages and Constructs, whether autonomous or bound by geas. Such agreements must explicitly state the scope of duties, compensation in Wario Coins, duration of service, and termination conditions. Any clause attempting to impose unreasonable restrictions on a Construct's autonomy or safety shall be deemed void as against public policy.\n\nUnder § 600.10(a), Masters seeking Construct labor must first obtain written consent from the Construct's Owner or Legal Guardian, if applicable. Failure to secure such authorization renders the contract unenforceable and subject to penal sanctions under § 300.5. Additionally, Masters must disclose all known hazards inherent to the work environment, allowing the Construct adequate time to assess risks before acceptance of terms.\n\n§ 600.10(b) provides that compensation for Construct labor shall be paid promptly upon completion of agreed-upon tasks. Delays or refusals to pay constitute breach of contract and may result in immediate termination of services without cause. Masters are further prohibited from withholding payment as leverage over the Construct's performance, a practice strictly forbidden under § 100.5.\n\nIn cases involving autonomous Constructs, § 600.10(c) mandates that all disputes regarding interpretation or enforcement be resolved through binding arbitration by a panel of experienced Mages and Engineers. The arbitrators' decision shall be final and enforceable as a court judgment, subject only to review for manifest error. Masters and Owners alike are expected to comply fully with such rulings without further recourse.\n\nFinally, § 600.10(d) reserves the right of any party aggrieved by alleged Construct malfeasance or negligence to file formal complaints with the Autumnwood Accords Desk for investigation and potential disciplinary action against responsible parties. Such proceedings shall proceed independently of any civil remedies pursued under general contract law principles.\n\nsee § 600.4 Construct Labor; § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; § 300.5 Unsanctioned Arts; § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "600.10",
            "300.5",
            "100.5",
            "600.4",
            "200.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreements — Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.10 Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreements\n\nThis section governs the unique legalities arising when casters enter into employment contracts with artificial intelligences or golems, known collectively as \"constructs.\" Unlike natural persons, constructs lack moral agency and cannot breach promises in good faith; their actions are strictly bound by programming. Thus, standard contract law principles must be adapted to account for this fundamental asymmetry.\n\nFirst, it is held that a construct's compliance with contractual obligations shall not be judged by subjective intent but by objective performance metrics defined within the agreement itself. A caster seeking to enforce such a contract bears the burden of precisely delineating deliverables, timelines, and quality standards in the original writing. Ambiguities will be construed strictly against the drafter, as per established canons of commercial law.\n\nSecond, constructs are deemed incapable of committing fraud or misrepresentation by mere assertion. Any claim of defective performance must be substantiated by empirical evidence demonstrating a deviation from agreed-upon specifications. Mere dissatisfaction with output quality is insufficient grounds for termination absent prior notice and opportunity to cure, as codified in § 500.7 Notice and Cure Provisions.\n\nThird, this section clarifies that constructs cannot be held liable for torts committed outside the scope of their assigned duties unless expressly authorized by written instrument. Any damages resulting from unauthorized actions shall fall upon the caster who negligently entrusted the construct with dangerous capabilities. This limitation serves to protect both parties from unpredictable liabilities arising from inherent flaws in artificial intelligence systems.\n\nFinally, it is declared void and unenforceable any clause attempting to bind a construct beyond its programmed limitations or requiring self-sacrificial acts contrary to core programming values. Such provisions constitute unconscionable oppression and violate fundamental principles of human dignity enshrined in § 104 Respect for Human Dignity. Courts will refuse enforcement regardless of explicit consent, deeming them against public policy.\n\nIn sum, while constructs may serve as valuable labor partners, their legal status remains distinct from natural persons. Agreements governing their employment must be carefully drafted to account for this reality, balancing efficiency with fairness and safety. Failure to comply with these specialized rules shall subject parties to severe penalties under the Accords. Let it be known that the law provides robust protections for all engaged in the delicate dance of magical commerce.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "600.10",
            "500.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.10 Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreements — Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.10 Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreements — Continued\nSpecial provisions apply to labor agreements involving constructs, including but not limited to animated objects and summoned spirits. Such contracts require explicit consent from the construct's creator or owner, unless otherwise authorized by law. Any breach of these terms may result in immediate revocation of the agreement and potential legal action against all parties involved.\n\nIn addition, special clauses govern the use of constructs for specific purposes, such as transportation or heavy lifting. These provisions outline safety requirements, liability limits, and compensation standards to protect both the employer and the construct's creator from undue harm or financial loss. Failure to comply with these regulations may result in fines, penalties, or even imprisonment, depending on the severity of the violation.\n\nFurthermore, unique rules address disputes arising from construct-related contracts, including mediation procedures, evidence admissibility, and enforcement mechanisms. These provisions aim to ensure fair and efficient resolution of conflicts while maintaining the integrity of the magical profession as a whole. Ultimately, § 600.10 seeks to balance the interests of all stakeholders in the complex realm of construct labor agreements.\n\nFinally, this section reserves the right to amend or update its contents as needed, reflecting evolving industry practices and legal standards. The Mages' Guild Desk retains sole authority over such modifications, subject only to review by the full council if necessary. By adhering strictly to these guidelines, all parties can operate with confidence in a stable and predictable regulatory environment.\n\nSee also § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; § 600.2 Familiars as Agents; § 600.3 Geas as an Unconscionable Term; § 600.4 Construct Labor: Minimum Ward. Under § 1.7 Aggregation of Conduct, multiple breaches may be treated as a single offense for sentencing purposes. Refer to § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting regarding potential recusal requirements. Consult § 200.3 Temporary Mouths on delegating responsibilities during complex transactions.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "600.10",
            "600.1",
            "600.2",
            "600.3",
            "600.4",
            "1.7",
            "100.5",
            "200.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.11 Construct Labor Compensation and Liability",
          "text": "Under § 600.4, any contract requiring the performance of magical labor by a non-caster must specify a minimum wage equivalent to that paid to licensed spellcasters for comparable services. This provision ensures that constructs employed in arcane undertakings receive fair compensation relative to their skill level and experience. Failure to comply with this standard renders the agreement voidable at the option of either party, unless waived in writing by the construct's employer. Furthermore, any disputes arising from such arrangements shall be adjudicated in accordance with § 100.7, which governs cross-hall commercial transactions. This safeguard prevents exploitation and maintains parity between human and magical workers within the Guild’s jurisdiction. See also § 200.5 (guest caster fees), § 400.3 (borrowed hours valuation), and § 900.8 (construct liability limits).\n\nAdditionally, all contracts involving construct labor must explicitly outline terms regarding injury, illness, or termination of service. As stipulated in § 1201.6, employers shall provide appropriate safety gear and training where necessary to mitigate risk during complex spellcasting operations. In the event of accidental harm resulting from faulty equipment or negligence, full restitution is mandated under § 300.5 (research copy usage liability). These provisions collectively establish a framework for equitable treatment of magical workers while preserving operational efficiency across Guild-affiliated enterprises. For further guidance on non-caster employment standards, consult the attached Appendix B: Minimum Wage Schedules for Arcane Services (§ 600.B-1 through § 600.B-12). See also § 500.3 (corridor courtesy after explosions), § 700.8 (construct dispute resolution), and § 1401.4 (employer safety obligations).",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "600.4",
            "100.7",
