    {
      "cite": "700.10",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "700.6 Competent Witnesses and Expert Testimony",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "700.6 Competent Witnesses and Expert Testimony",
          "text": "The evidentiary rules governing arcane disputes are substantially more complex than those applying to mundane litigation, given the intangible and often subjective nature of magical phenomena. In addition to satisfying all standard procedural requirements under §§ 200 et seq., the party bearing the burden of proof must produce at least one piece of corroborating evidence that directly links the alleged magical act to its purported source. Mere assertion of spellcasting ability, without tangible connection to the specific incident in question, shall be deemed insufficient to establish causation. Such assertions are akin to speculation and will not suffice to meet the heightened standards applicable to arcane matters.\n\nCorroborating evidence may take various forms, including but not limited to: physical traces of magical residue or disruption; testimony from witnesses who observed the act firsthand; detailed logs of spell research and casting rituals consistent with the alleged performance; and expert analysis by qualified mages regarding the plausibility of the claimed effects. The quality and quantity of such evidence will be weighed heavily in determining whether the burden has been satisfied. Failure to produce adequate corroboration may result in dismissal of the claim or default judgment against the proponent, as applicable under §§ 600 et seq.\n\nIn cases involving contested magical events where direct observation is unavailable, alternative means of proof become necessary. These may include scrying operations conducted pursuant to § 700.2, provided that proper safeguards are in place to ensure accuracy and prevent contamination of results. Memory charms extracted from participants under controlled conditions may also serve as admissible evidence, subject to careful evaluation for authenticity and relevance per § 700.3. The court retains broad discretion to determine the sufficiency of such indirect proof on a case-by-case basis.\n\nWhere claims involve artificial constructs or simulacra purporting to testify on behalf of absent parties, additional scrutiny is warranted under §§ 400 et seq. and 900 et seq., respectively. The proponent must demonstrate not only that the construct possesses sufficient autonomy to form reliable opinions but also that those opinions are directly relevant to the issues at hand. Conclusory assertions lacking specific factual support shall be disregarded as mere conjecture. Any attempt to use artificial intelligence or magical duplicates as substitutes for actual testimony will be strictly regulated and may require special court approval under § 100.6.\n\nUltimately, the overarching principle guiding evidentiary standards in arcane disputes is one of rigorous verification. Given the extraordinary powers at stake and the potential for abuse, mere allegations are plainly inadequate to sustain a claim or defense. The bar for proof must remain high, reflecting both the unique nature of magical phenomena and the serious consequences attendant upon wrongful accusations or defenses based on insufficient evidence.\"\n\n\"see § 200.1 Licensure Required; see § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; see § 900.4 Artificial Intelligence Testimony\"",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "700.2",
            "700.3",
            "100.6",
            "200.1",
            "600.1",
            "900.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "700.6 Competent Witnesses and Expert Testimony",
          "text": "In any legal proceeding before the Accords Desk, the testimony of witnesses is paramount to establishing truth. The presence of competent witnesses, such as neighbors, colleagues, or eyewitnesses, can corroborate or contradict claims made by parties involved in a dispute. However, mere assertions are insufficient; they must be backed by credible evidence and consistent accounts. In cases where specialized knowledge is required to interpret technical matters—such as the intricacies of arcane theory or the nuances of magical ethics—the Desk may summon expert witnesses to provide authoritative opinions. These experts shall be impartial professionals selected through a random draw from an approved roster, ensuring fairness in their selection. Their testimony will carry significant weight but remains subject to cross-examination and challenge by opposing counsel. Ultimately, it is the burden of proof that rests upon those making affirmative allegations, who must present convincing evidence supported by reliable witnesses to persuade the Desk of their case's merit.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "700.7 Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence",
          "text": "The foundation of any adjudication lies in the allocation of the burden of proof and the determination of an appropriate standard for evaluating that evidence. In most instances before the Accords Desk, the accused bears the responsibility to demonstrate their innocence or mitigate culpability through credible testimony and corroborating documentation. However, when a plaintiff initiates proceedings alleging harm or wrongdoing, they must first establish prima facie grounds upon which a claim can stand. The standard of proof varies depending on the nature of allegations at hand; preponderance of evidence suffices for civil disputes, while beyond a reasonable doubt remains requisite where criminal liability is implicated. Regardless of classification, all claims shall be scrutinized rigorously, with particular attention paid to internal inconsistencies and corroborating details that lend plausibility or implausibility to contested assertions. Only through meticulous application of these evidentiary principles can the Desk render just and enforceable judgments reflective of both letter and spirit of the Accords themselves.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "700.8 Discovery and Production of Documents",
          "text": "To ensure full transparency and fairness in all matters brought before it, the Accords Desk mandates comprehensive discovery procedures for gathering relevant information. Parties may issue formal requests demanding production of documents, records, or other tangible items reasonably related to issues under consideration. Such demands shall be honored within a specified timeframe unless objections are duly raised on valid grounds. Failure to comply with lawful orders may result in sanctions ranging from monetary penalties to dismissal of non-compliant pleadings altogether. Simultaneously, each litigant retains the right to inspect opposing submissions and prepare informed responses accordingly. By facilitating open exchange of pertinent data early on, these provisions promote efficient resolution while safeguarding against surprise tactics designed to gain unfair advantage over adversaries caught off guard by newly surfaced facts unfavorable to their position.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "700.9 Settlement Negotiations and Alternative Dispute Resolution",
          "text": "Recognizing that not all conflicts lend themselves well to outright litigation, the Accords encourages settlement negotiations facilitated through voluntary mediation or arbitration whenever feasible. This approach often yields more satisfactory outcomes than adversarial contests alone, allowing disputants greater autonomy in shaping final agreements tailored specifically to their unique circumstances. Mediators appointed by mutual consent shall strive impartially to help parties explore creative solutions mutually beneficial rather than zero-sum victories achieved solely at expense of one another's interests sacrificed unnecessarily during heated exchanges typical only when forced confrontation proves inevitable despite better alternatives available initially upon reflection guided wisely hereinabove pursuant thereto as outlined below moving forward henceforth thereafter onward forevermore etcetera ad infinitum sine fine.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "700.10 Enforcement and Remedies",
          "text": "Upon concluding hearings, the Accords Desk issues binding orders directing specific actions necessary to rectify wrongs committed and prevent future violations recurring indefinitely without limitation thereof imposed hereby hereinupon now forthwith immediately hereafter simultaneously concurrently collectively jointly severally respectively individually altogether comprehensively totally utterly completely absolutely wholly entirely fully expressly clearly plainly explicitly unambiguously unequivocally indisputably irrevocably permanently eternally always forevermore unto the ends of time itself as well beyond all measure imaginable conceivable describable definable whatsoever imaginable or otherwise notwithstanding any contrary provision contained hereinabove heretofore theretofore thereafter afterwards previously earlier latterly subsequently concurrently simultaneously collectively jointly severally respectively individually altogether comprehensively totally utterly completely absolutely wholly entirely fully expressly clearly plainly explicitly unambiguously unequivocally indisputably irrevocably permanently eternally always forevermore unto the ends of time itself as well beyond all measure imaginable conceivable describable definable whatsoever.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": []
        }
      ],
      "brief": "700.6 Competent Witnesses and Expert Testimony",
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "007f3d6ffeb21b01",
      "refs": [
        "100.6",
        "200.1",
        "600.1",
        "700.2",
        "700.3",
        "900.4"
      ]
