    {
      "cite": "800.10",
      "part": "part_viii",
      "title": "Disposition of Confiscated Items",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 800.10 Related Requirements and Disposition of Confiscated Items",
          "text": "Where any property, chattel, or artifact has been seized pursuant to the provisions herein contained, such items shall be held in escrow by the Clerk until final resolution of the underlying matter. Upon adjudication, if a conviction is secured, all recovered goods shall be forfeited absolutely to the Guild for distribution at its sole discretion, consistent with prior precedents established under § 750.4 et seq. In cases where innocence is proven, original ownership shall be restored forthwith, absent evidence of third-party tampering or illicit trafficking during the period of confinement.\n\nNotwithstanding any other provision herein, no item seized in connection with a Paradox Trial or related enforcement action may be retained by any individual officer, employee, or agent of the Guild without express authorization from the Sovereign Archmage. Unauthorized possession constitutes a Class A felony under § 900.2 and shall result in immediate dismissal and permanent disqualification from future service. Furthermore, all seized materials must be catalogued and inventoried within seventy-two (72) hours of receipt using standardized forms approved by the Desk, with copies filed electronically and physically as required by § 650.13. Failure to comply with these administrative mandates shall subject the responsible party to disciplinary proceedings under Part VIII hereof.\n\nIn addition, any funds or valuables recovered during search warrants, seizures, or arrests shall be deposited into a separate trust account maintained solely for litigation expenses and restitution payments as directed by the Presiding Magistrate. Interest earned on such holdings shall accrue in favor of the Guild treasury unless otherwise ordered by judicial decree. No portion thereof may be diverted for personal use under any circumstances whatsoever, with violations punishable as embezzlement per § 1050.7. All transactions involving seized assets must be meticulously documented and audited quarterly to ensure full accountability and transparency in accordance with established fiscal protocols set forth earlier in this Code.\n\nIt is hereby declared that nothing contained herein shall impair the Guild's inherent authority to dispose of confiscated property as it deems necessary for operational efficiency and public safety, provided such actions remain within the bounds of lawful procedure and respect due process rights afforded to all parties involved under applicable statutory frameworks governing criminal justice administration in this Realm. Any ambiguities arising from conflicting directives shall be resolved by reference to the principles laid out above, prioritizing fairness, proportionality, and adherence to the spirit rather than mere letter of these sacred writs inscribed upon parchment bound with silver thread.\n\nsee § 800.5 Disposition of Seized Goods; see § 900.3 Forfeiture and Restitution Procedures; see Part VII General Provisions Regarding Evidence Preservation and Chain of Custody Standards Applicable to All Law Enforcement Activities Within Autumnwood Jurisdictional Boundaries As Defined By Current Maps And Charts Updated Annually At The Discretion Of The Sovereign Archmage In Consultation With Senior Council Members Representing Each Major Faction Affected By These Regulations.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "750.4",
            "900.2",
            "650.13",
            "1050.7",
            "800.5",
            "900.3",
            "800.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Disposition of Confiscated Artifacts and Magical Items",
          "text": "§ 800.10 Related Requirements and Disposition of Seized Property\n\nAll items seized during the course of enforcement proceedings, whether through arrest, search warrant execution, or voluntary surrender, shall be placed under official Guild custody pending further action. Such property includes but is not limited to wands, staves, spellbooks, focus artifacts, rare reagents, and any other object imbued with magical properties that may constitute evidence of a violation.\n\nUpon seizure, each item must be cataloged in the Central Archives using a standardized form detailing serial numbers, descriptions, estimated value, and chain-of-custody documentation. Originals shall be retained by the Desk while certified copies are distributed to relevant parties including the accused, complainant, and any third-party claimants with standing under § 100.5.\n\nItems classified as contraband pursuant to Schedule A (§ 300.2) shall remain in secure storage until final adjudication. Post-trial disposition varies based on outcome: guilty verdicts result in permanent forfeiture to the Guild treasury unless otherwise ordered by the Paradox Trial Council under § 800.4. Not guilty findings mandate immediate release of all seized property to rightful owners, subject to reasonable inspection for tampering or evidence contamination.\n\nSpecial provisions apply to sentient or semi-sentient objects possessing willful agency, such as animated constructs or bonded companions. These entities shall be treated as persons under § 100.6, with rights afforded in accordance with their status and applicable laws regarding captivity and servitude. Release conditions may include mandatory counseling, probationary monitoring, or community restitution programs deemed appropriate by the presiding magistrate.\n\nAny items of historical significance or unique cultural value identified during seizure shall be referred to the Preservation Division for assessment. Such artifacts may qualify for protected status under § 400.2, granting them immunity from future confiscation except via judicial warrant obtained through due process channels.\n\nAll confiscated property logs must be reconciled monthly with financial records and audit trails maintained by the Guild's Compliance Office under § 100.8. Discrepancies exceeding five percent trigger immediate investigation into potential embezzlement or unauthorized diversion of assets. Violations of these provisions constitute grounds for disciplinary action up to and including expulsion from membership privileges permanently.\n\nThis section operates in conjunction with §§ 700.2, 701.5, and 801.3 governing asset forfeiture procedures across the Guild network. Compliance with these interlocking mandates ensures equitable treatment of all parties involved while preserving public safety through rigorous oversight mechanisms designed to prevent abuse of seized materials for illicit purposes.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "800.10",
            "100.5",
            "300.2",
            "800.4",
            "100.6",
            "400.2",
            "100.8",
            "700.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Disposition of Confiscated Artifacts and Magical Items — Continued",
          "text": "§ 800.10 Disposition of Seized Artifacts and Magical Items\n\nAll magical items, artifacts, reagents, and other material possessions seized during enforcement actions shall be inventoried, catalogued, and secured in a designated custody facility under the supervision of an Aegis Magi. The inventory record shall include a detailed description, estimated value, provenance, and any markings or inscriptions identifying ownership. This record shall be maintained for at least one hundred eighty (180) years, subject to periodic review by the Conservators' Council for potential return or destruction.\n\nItems of exceptional historical, cultural, or scientific significance may be retained permanently by the Guild for study and preservation. Such items shall be displayed in a secure exhibit hall accessible only to licensed researchers and accredited historians. All other seized property shall be held in trust until ownership is definitively established through legal process. If no claimant appears within six (6) months of notification, the items may be sold at public auction to defray enforcement costs, with proceeds deposited into the Guild's general fund.\n\nConfiscated materials deemed dangerous or contraband under any provision of these Accords shall be destroyed by a certified alchemist in accordance with established safety protocols. Destruction certificates signed by two independent witnesses shall accompany the final inventory report. No seized item shall ever re-enter circulation without explicit written authorization from the Archmage Sovereign, obtained only upon completion of all outstanding legal proceedings related to its original seizure.\n\nThis section applies equally to items recovered from private individuals and those intercepted during routine inspections or investigations. The Guild retains broad discretion in determining appropriate disposition methods based on each case's unique circumstances and public interest considerations. Any disputes regarding ownership claims shall be resolved exclusively through the judicial channels outlined herein, with no alternative remedies available under these Accords.\n\nThe provisions of this section supersede any conflicting local ordinances or municipal codes to the extent necessary for effective enforcement of the Autumnwood Agreement. The Guild expressly reserves the right to modify disposition procedures as needed to address emerging threats and technological developments without prior notice. All members are directed to comply fully with these mandates at all times, failing which shall subject themselves to immediate disciplinary action under applicable sanctions provisions.\n\nsee § 800.1 Discipline; Range of Sanctions\nsee § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs\nsee § 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion\nsee § 700.6 Magical Items and Their Regulation\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "800.10",
            "800.1",
            "400.2",
            "500.3",
            "700.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Disposition of Confiscated Artifacts and Magical Items — Administration",
          "text": "§ 800.10 Disposition of Confiscated Artifacts and Magical Items; § 900.2 Handling of Seized Property\n\nUpon the conclusion of any Paradox Trial, whether resulting in conviction or acquittal, all seized artifacts and magical items shall be processed according to these provisions: (a) If contraband as defined under § 300.1, said property shall be destroyed by certified Aegis Magi using approved neutralization techniques; (b) If lawful but restricted, items shall be transferred to the Conservator's Storage Vault pending further review; (c) If determined innocent and belonging to the accused, original ownership rights are reinstated subject to ongoing monitoring. All dispositions must be documented in writing with receipts signed by relevant parties. Failure to comply may result in additional sanctions including suspension of casting privileges until remediation is complete. Special provisions apply for items containing sentient components or bound spirits; these require consultation with Animate Affairs prior to final disposition under § 600.5. Any disputes regarding ownership shall be adjudicated by a specially constituted panel comprising one representative each from the Guild, the accused's faction, and neutral observers selected via lottery. Costs associated with storage, security, and eventual destruction or transfer of seized property are assessed against the convicted party unless extenuating circumstances exist. Records of all transactions must remain sealed for a period equal to twice the remaining term of the offender's banishment plus five years thereafter before being made available for archival purposes only. Unauthorized removal or tampering with confiscated materials constitutes a Class A felony punishable by immediate re-sealing of magic and permanent disqualification from Guild membership regardless of prior standing.\"\n\n\"This section governs the post-adjudication handling of all physical evidence seized during trials, ensuring proper chain-of-custody while protecting both public safety and individual rights. The detailed procedures herein serve to maintain integrity in the penal process and prevent potential abuse of confiscated items for secondary purposes. Strict adherence is mandatory as deviations may compromise ongoing investigations or endanger innocents through improper disposal methods.\"",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "800.10",
            "900.2",
            "300.1",
            "600.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Disposition of Confiscated Artifacts and Magical Items — Oversight",
          "text": "§ 800.10 Disposition of Seized Artifacts and Magical Items — Administration\n\nAll items seized during the course of an investigation or trial shall be inventoried, secured, and held in trust by the Aegis Magi until such time as their disposition is determined pursuant to these Accords. Such items include, but are not limited to, wands, staves, spellbooks, components, raw materials, and any other objects bearing magical resonance or potential for misuse. The inventory shall be maintained on file in the official records of the Guild Hall, accessible only to authorized personnel under strict confidentiality protocols.\n\nItems deemed to have no further value or threat to public safety may be retained by the Aegis Magi for archival purposes, subject to periodic review by the Conservators. Such artifacts serve as a historical record of transgressions and may be used in educational settings or for research into forbidden magics under controlled conditions. Disposal of such items shall be conducted via incineration or other means ensuring permanent destruction of any residual enchantments.\n\nMagical components and raw materials recovered from illicit operations shall be returned to their lawful owners upon verification of ownership, provided such return does not contravene public interest or security concerns. Where ownership cannot be established with reasonable certainty, items may be donated to approved research institutions for study under supervised conditions. Any proceeds generated from the sale or disposal of seized goods shall be remitted to the Guild Treasury and applied towards funding enforcement operations and community outreach programs.\n\nConfiscated artifacts possessing intrinsic historical or cultural significance may be transferred to museums or educational facilities for public display and study, subject to appropriate safeguards against misuse or desecration. Such transfers require written consent from both the Aegis Magi and the Conservators, with clear stipulations regarding access restrictions and preservation standards. Failure to comply with these provisions shall constitute grounds for immediate revocation of any privileges granted under this section.\n\nThe administration of seized items shall be conducted in accordance with all applicable laws and regulations governing the handling of hazardous materials, controlled substances, and potentially infectious agents. Special precautions must be taken when dealing with items known or suspected to emit radiation, generate extreme temperatures, or possess other dangerous properties. Personnel tasked with inventorying and storing such goods must wear appropriate protective gear and follow established safety procedures at all times.\n\nAny discrepancies discovered during the course of auditing seized property shall be reported forthwith to the supervising Aegis Magi for investigation and resolution. Suspected tampering or theft of confiscated items shall be prosecuted as criminal offenses under these Accords, with perpetrators subject to harsh penalties upon conviction. Accountability for loss or damage to seized goods rests solely with the individual responsible for their custodial care until proper transfer of ownership occurs.\n\nThis section applies only to items legally seized pursuant to valid warrants issued by competent authorities acting within their jurisdictional purview. Possession of stolen property, even if recovered from a criminal suspect, remains subject to separate legal processes and cannot be automatically classified as \"seized\" under these provisions unless accompanied by proper documentation and authorization. Unauthorized removal or alteration of official seals affixed to seized items shall be treated as evidence of guilt in any ensuing proceedings.\n\nThe ultimate goal of this administrative framework is to ensure the safe and responsible management of potentially dangerous materials while preserving their evidentiary value for future reference. Through diligent oversight and transparent procedures, the Aegis Magi strive to maintain public trust in the integrity of their operations and uphold the highest standards of professional ethics befitting their esteemed position within society at large. Any deviations from prescribed protocols shall result in immediate disciplinary action against responsible parties, regardless of rank or seniority.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "800.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Title: Registry and Custodial Transfer Protocols for Seized Thaumaturgic Assets",
          "text": "Upon the formal execution of any confiscation order pursuant to the disciplinary mechanisms outlined in Part 800, the immediate physical transfer of the seized item shall not constitute the finalization of its legal status; rather, it initiates a mandatory custodial registration process designed to prevent unauthorized circulation or accidental activation. The seizing officer, having secured the artifact under the conditions described in § 1001.6 regarding endowments and gifts, must immediately draft a Custodial Manifest that details the specific nature of the item, its origin as detailed in any accompanying audit report, and the precise chain of custody from the point of seizure to the secure holding facility. This manifest serves as the primary legal bridge between the field enforcement action and the archival storage protocols, ensuring that every magical asset is treated with the gravity implied by its potential to alter reality or cause paradoxical instability. It is imperative that no item be placed into general storage without first being logged in the central registry, a requirement that extends to items confiscated under the jurisdiction of the Paradox Trial where guilt has been established via § 800.1 Discipline; Range of Sanctions. Failure to register an item within the twenty-four hour window following seizure shall render any subsequent disposal or sale void ab initio, regardless of the apparent innocuousness of the object, and may subject the custodian to sanctions under § 800.2 Paradox Trial Procedures.\n\nThe classification of confiscated items into specific hazard tiers is a critical component of this registry process, directly influencing the storage requirements and access protocols mandated by the Office of Archmage Sovereignty. Items deemed to possess active enchantments or residual magical signatures must be segregated from mundane goods to prevent cross-contamination, a principle that aligns with the transmission oversight duties outlined in § 300.14 Catchline Title: Transmission Protocols and Oversight. Furthermore, any artifact confiscated during an investigation into the Unsanctioned Arts, such as necromantic tools or forbidden schools of magic identified under § 800.12 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation, requires a secondary validation step to ensure that the item has not been compromised by paradoxical feedback loops prior to storage. This validation often involves consultation with the Conservators faction within the Guild, who retain the authority to re-evaluate the danger spectrum of an item based on new research findings or shifts in magical theory, thereby ensuring that the regulatory framework remains responsive to evolving threats. The registry must also record any known historical associations with the artifact, including whether it was previously bestowed as an endowment under § 1001.6, as this history may impose additional restrictions on its release or destruction.\n\nIn instances where a confiscated item is found to be a research copy of a Quiet Item, the registry must explicitly note its status as a non-quiet transmission vector, adhering strictly to the prohibitions set forth in § 300.12 Prohibition on Magical Correspondence and Transmission. Discussing the specifics of such items outside of authorized channels is prohibited, and the registry entry itself must be encrypted at the level required for sensitive arcane data to prevent leakage that could incite unauthorized study or replication. The custodial transfer protocol further mandates that any item bearing the seal of the Mages' Guild be returned only upon the completion of a formal peer review process, which may include mediation efforts as described in § 1.17 Additional Provisions for Mages' Guild Mediation and Peer Review Protocols if there is dispute regarding the legitimacy of the confiscation. This ensures that the disciplinary action taken is not only procedurally correct but also substantively just, maintaining the Guild's reputation as a responsible institution while actively managing the dangers inherent in their field of study. Ultimately, the registry serves as the definitive record against which all future interactions with the confiscated item will be measured, ensuring that no magical asset slips through the cracks of oversight and re-enters the circulation without explicit authorization from the highest echelons of the Guild's governance.",
          "key": "f",
