    {
      "cite": "1101.10",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Gifts That Later Move — §1101.10",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.1 Gifts That Later Move",
          "text": "It shall be unlawful for any caster to accept or retain a gift, favor, or benefit from another person with the intent to influence their official actions thereafter. However, this prohibition does not extend to nominal tokens of gratitude given without expectation of quid pro quo. The burden of proving corrupt intent rests solely on the accuser. Any violation shall be subject to disciplinary sanctions up to and including revocation of licensure, at the discretion of the Desk. Related provisions may be found under § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; see also § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse regarding automatic forfeiture for cause. Under § 1.7 Construction, Aggregation of Conduct, multiple minor infractions involving improper gifts may collectively constitute a single substantive offense warranting severe penalties. For further guidance on fiduciary duties owed by public officials to the Guild, consult the Code of Ethics for Licensed Casters, available at all Hall registries. The Desk reserves the right to investigate and prosecute suspected violations ex parte without prior notice to the accused. Any person found guilty of accepting a prohibited gift shall be required to make full restitution to the affected party or their estate. Failure to comply may result in additional sanctions under § 1201.7 Related Requirements. Nothing herein shall prevent the lawful acceptance of gifts offered openly and impartially, provided no undue influence is exerted thereon. The spirit of this section is to maintain public confidence in the integrity of official casting services by preventing actual or apparent conflicts between personal gain and professional duty. Violations shall be reported immediately to the Ethics Committee for review and appropriate action under existing procedures. This provision reflects the Guild's unwavering commitment to upholding the highest standards of ethical conduct among its members, ensuring that all licensure holders act solely in the interests of their clients and the broader magical community. Related requirements may also be found at § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks regarding proper oversight of gift transactions involving public funds. The Desk emphasizes that even the appearance of impropriety can erode trust; thus, all casters are urged to exercise extreme caution when dealing with potential conflicts of interest in any capacity. This principle shall remain firmly entrenched in our code for generations to come, serving as a guiding light for those who wield power responsibly and honorably within these halls. See also § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy concerning diplomatic gift exchanges between Guilds; under § 200.5 Guest Casters regarding temporary assignments abroad. The Desk reminds all that vigilance against corruption is an ongoing obligation, not merely a formal requirement bound by statute alone. True integrity stems from within each individual caster's character, fortified by the strict enforcement of these vital provisions for the greater good of magic itself. As always, refer to the full text of this section and its related clauses when assessing specific situations requiring ethical judgment under our laws. May your paths be guided by wisdom and justice in all things! - The Autumnwood Accords Desk\n\n[END OF PAGE]",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "200.4",
            "1.7",
            "1201.7",
            "100.6",
            "100.7",
            "200.5",
            "1101.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.10 Gifted Artifacts and Their Hidden Burdens",
          "text": "§ 1101.10 Gifts That Later Move\nIt is held firmly that gifts, favors, and gratuities exchanged between mages shall not be construed as binding contracts or conveyances of title absent express intent to the contrary. Where a gift is made with strings attached—whether explicit conditions or implicit expectations—the donor retains equitable interest in the object until those obligations are fully satisfied. Failure by the recipient to perform may result in revocation of the gift and restitution of the item, along with damages for breach of contract under applicable principles of unjust enrichment.\n\nThis section further clarifies that the mere passage of time does not extinguish a donor's residual claim over gifted magical artifacts. Even after years of peaceful possession, if it is proven that the recipient failed to meet agreed-upon stipulations, the original giver may petition for return of the item and compensation for losses incurred. Such actions must be brought within three lunar cycles of discovering the breach, lest the gift stand ratified by laches.\n\nThe Desk hereby codifies these longstanding customs into binding law to prevent future disputes over enchanted objects passed between Guild members. All parties are advised to draft clear agreements when transferring valuable items, specifying any attached conditions and consequences for nonperformance. In absence thereof, the default rules set forth herein shall govern interpretation of such exchanges.\n\nsee § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; see § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse; see § 300.3 Quiet List Procedure\n*Under § 1.7 Construction, ambiguity is resolved against the drafter.* *As per § 1.6 Definitions, \"gift\" means gratuitous transfer without consideration unless stated otherwise.*",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1101.10",
            "100.5",
            "200.4",
            "300.3",
            "1.7",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.6 Relics, Curse Disclaimers, and the Doctrine of Unforeseen Burdens",
          "text": "Whereas magic items often possess properties beyond mere utility, including residual enchantments or latent curses, the Accords establish a framework for disclosure and liability. A vendor who sells an artifact knowing it to be cursed must explicitly warn the buyer; failure to do so renders the sale voidable at buyer's option. Conversely, a purchaser who acquires an item without knowledge of its dark taint bears the brunt of any subsequent misfortune. This principle extends to gifts and inheritances; one may not later claim surprise or outrage when a \"present\" proves more grievous than helpful. § 1101.7 Related Requirements\nFurther, the Accords recognize that some curses are inextricably bound to specific individuals or lineages, acting as an immutable part of their identity. Such marks cannot be removed by mere sale or transfer of title; they must be lifted through arcane means beyond ordinary commerce. A seller who knowingly divests themselves of such a curse shifts no burden upon the new owner; the taint remains attached like a shadow. This doctrine protects both parties from endless litigation over the origins and effects of ancient malice. § 1101.8 Related Requirements\nFinally, the Accords decree that blessings bestowed by one vendor may not be nullified by another's superior artifact. If a customer purchases an item already enchanted with protection or aid, they retain those benefits regardless of subsequent transactions. A rival merchant cannot outbid or outmagick their way into cancelling prior boons unless explicitly agreed upon in writing. This safeguard preserves the sanctity of sacred gifts and prevents predatory undercutting by less scrupulous parties. § 1101.9 Related Requirements\nIn sum, these provisions aim to bring clarity to the murky waters of cursed commerce, balancing fairness for both buyer and seller while acknowledging the unique nature of magical artifacts. By codifying ancient customs, the Accords ensure that even the darkest arts may be traded safely under law's watchful eye. § 1101.10 Related Requirements\nsee § 300.2 Unsanctioned Arts; see § 400.5 Unauthorized Titan Growth; see § 700.9 Contractual Magic; see § 800.3 Forbidden Practices",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1101.7",
            "1101.8",
            "1101.9",
            "1101.10",
            "300.2",
            "400.5",
            "700.9",
            "800.3",
            "1101.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.6 Relics, Curse Disclaimers, and the Doctrine of Unforeseen Burdens — Continued",
          "text": "A gift that moves later is not a curse but an inherent risk of arcane commerce; see § 300.2 Unsanctioned Arts. When a relic manifests hidden dangers post-transfer, liability attaches to the giver unless a prior disclaimer was explicit; under § 1.4 Persons Bound, this rule binds all casters and their proxies alike. The Doctrine of Unforeseen Burdens holds that no donor can escape responsibility for latent malice embedded in enchanted objects they knowingly bestowed; cf. § 200.3 Temporary Mouths regarding agency limits. Even if a curse activates years after gifting, the original owner remains liable unless force majeure intervened, per § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting. Courts will examine whether warnings were conspicuous and understood by recipients; mere ambiguity fails to discharge duty under § 400.3 Borrowed Hours. This principle prevents rogue casters from exploiting loopholes to evade accountability for their creations, aligning with the Accords' core mandate of public safety and magical integrity as outlined in § 1.7 Construction; Aggregation of Conduct. Failure to comply may result in sanctions up to permanent sealing by the Aegis Magi under § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters. Ultimately, this section balances innovation with stewardship, ensuring that magic serves rather than enslaves its wielders and society at large, as envisioned by Archmage Veyra herself in her foundational pronouncements cited throughout Part I. See also § 700.9 Related Requirements on ethical obligations beyond mere legality.\n\n\"Magic demands vigilance, not negligence.\" — Archmage Theron's Oath of the Conservators (342 AE)",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "300.2",
            "1.4",
            "200.3",
            "100.5",
            "400.3",
            "1.7",
            "600.1",
            "700.9",
            "1101.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.6 Relics, Curse Disclaimers, and the Doctrine of Unforeseen Burdens — Con",
          "text": "This section further elaborates on the application of the doctrine of unforeseen burdens in the context of relics and magical artifacts, establishing a comprehensive framework for determining liability when hidden dangers are discovered post-transfer. Under § 1101.6, any party acquiring a relic must exercise due diligence prior to purchase or acceptance, including thorough inspection by qualified appraisers familiar with the artifact's history and known properties. Failure to conduct such examinations shall constitute negligence per se, barring subsequent claims regarding foreseeable risks.\n\nThe burden of disclosure rests primarily upon the transferring party, who warrants that they possess full knowledge of any latent defects or curses associated with the item. See § 1101.4 Blessing of a Competing Vendor. Concealment of such information through affirmative misrepresentation shall render the transaction voidable at the option of the aggrieved party, without prejudice to further damages for reliance losses incurred in good faith. Where disclosure is impossible due to the nature of the magic involved, strict liability may attach to the seller, particularly where profit motives were evident or industry standards dictate heightened transparency obligations.\n\nDispute resolution concerning alleged curses shall proceed initially through binding arbitration conducted by a panel of neutral arcane specialists appointed by both parties. Absent agreement within thirty days, default mechanisms established under § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks shall activate automatically. The arbitral tribunal's determination regarding causation and proximate consequence shall be final and unreviewable except for manifest error in fact-finding or application of law. See also § 300.4 Reclassification for procedural parallels in hazardous material cases.\n\nIn all proceedings, the presumption shall favor the defendant unless clear and convincing evidence establishes actual knowledge by the plaintiff at the time of transaction. Mere suspicion or speculation as to potential hidden dangers is insufficient to pierce this protection absent corroborating testimony from credible witnesses with firsthand observation. See § 1401.8 Related Requirements for evidentiary standards in complex fact patterns involving supernatural phenomena. This balanced approach aims to facilitate commerce while safeguarding against exploitation of magical vulnerabilities by unscrupulous actors operating outside established regulatory frameworks.\n\n\"Hidden curses are not mere superstition—they represent real risks with tangible consequences. Our legal system must evolve to address these unique challenges while preserving fair dealing principles fundamental to all markets.\" - Archmage Veyra, Address before the Autumnwood Assembly (4th Quarter, Year 17)",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1101.6",
            "1101.4",
            "100.6",
            "300.4",
            "1401.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.6 Burdens and Blessings",
          "text": "The transfer of magical artifacts between parties entails not only the conveyance of tangible property but also the shifting of intangible metaphysical burdens, hazards, and potential benefits. This section governs the legal implications arising from such transfers when unforeseen consequences materialize subsequent to completion. § 1101.6 shall be read in conjunction with §§ 1.4–1.9 regarding persons bound by law and proper construction of contractual terms.\n\nUnder this provision, any latent curse or affliction inherent to an object at the time of delivery constitutes a fundamental breach, permitting immediate rescission of the transaction without liability for damages resulting from said curse's operation. Conversely, if a blessing or protective enchantment activates unexpectedly post-delivery despite seller disclaimers, the buyer may seek equitable relief but retains title unless gross negligence by the seller is proven beyond reasonable doubt. See §§ 100.5, 200.4 for analogous principles applied to oral agreements and temporary licenses respectively.\n\nThe doctrine of unforeseen burdens operates as an implied warranty against hidden defects in magical property, supplementing express contractual terms wherever applicable. However, this protection does not extend to risks voluntarily assumed by the buyer or those reasonably discoverable upon inspection. Sellers remain liable only for intentional concealment of known hazards or gross misrepresentation of an artifact's properties. Refer to § 1.7 on construction and interpretation where ambiguity exists regarding scope of liability.\n\nFurthermore, when multiple parties contribute to creating a cursed item—whether through procurement components, casting rituals, or mere possession—their respective liabilities are apportioned according to causation and fault unless otherwise stipulated in writing. Joint and several responsibility applies only where the artifact's malevolent influence cannot be traced to any single contributor with certainty. Consult §§ 300.2, 400.5 regarding unsanctioned arts and unauthorized field use for context on prohibited magical activities.\n\nFinally, this section establishes a rebuttable presumption that all magical items possess inherent risks unknown even to expert practitioners unless proven otherwise by clear evidence. Plaintiffs bear the initial burden of demonstrating injury proximately caused by the transferred artifact; defendants may then assert defenses based on contributory negligence or assumption of risk where appropriate under applicable law. For related provisions concerning contracts with non-casters, see § 600.1 et seq.\n\nIn sum, § 1101.6 serves as a critical safeguard for both buyers and sellers engaged in the complex marketplace of magical goods, ensuring that neither bears undue peril from transactions beyond their control while preserving contractual freedom within legally recognized bounds. Proper adherence to these guidelines fosters trust and stability essential to the continued flourishing of arcane commerce under the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1101.6",
            "1.4",
            "100.5",
            "1.7",
            "300.2",
            "600.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
