    {
      "cite": "1201.10",
      "part": "part_xii",
      "title": "Extra-Territorial Jurisdiction and Planar Tolls",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Extra-Territorial Jurisdiction and Planar Tolls",
          "text": "§ 1201.10 Transit Stamps and Planar Tolls; Extraterritorial Casting into Diet Chambers; Cartography as Raid License; Miscellaneous Provisions\nUnder the regime of this Part, all magical transit across planar borders shall be subject to rigorous inspection by authorized Aegis Magi. Every caster possessing a valid Guild Licensure must affix their personal seal to the Transit Manifest at point-of-departure and again at arrival. Failure to comply with these stamping procedures renders the individual's license void ab initio, subject to immediate revocation without notice or hearing. See § 200.4 regarding suspension and lapse of licensure for non-compliance.\n\nPlanar tolls imposed by sovereign entities within each realm shall be collected in accordance with existing diplomatic accords. No Guild member shall pay such fees using Wario Coin or any currency not officially recognized as tender by the paying jurisdiction's government. All transactions involving planar passage must be conducted via certified exchange houses, with receipts filed with the nearest Desk office for audit purposes. Violation of this monetary provision constitutes a Class C offense under § 1201.8 and may result in fines up to one hundred gold pieces or forfeiture of transit privileges at the sole discretion of an Archmage Judge.\n\nCasting magic within another nation's Diet Chamber without prior written consent from that nation's High Council is strictly prohibited, regardless of whether the spell originates domestically. Such unauthorized extraterritorial acts shall be treated as violations of international law and subject to prosecution under both domestic and foreign statutes. Any Guild member found guilty of breaching this clause faces disbarment, permanent exile from all Hall territories, and potential criminal charges for sedition or espionage depending on circumstances. This prohibition applies equally to the use of contracted services by third-party mages acting as agents of a caster.\n\nMapsets produced by approved cartographers under Guild supervision do not constitute licenses to raid enemy territory. Possession alone is not grounds for arrest, but active deployment of magical forces based upon such charts may be deemed evidence of preparation for hostile action, warranting investigation by local law enforcement and possible referral to the Aegis Magi for preliminary inquiry. Caster's discretion in deciding how to utilize cartographic data remains absolute unless restricted by specific contractual obligations or regional agreements governing arcane warfare protocols. See § 300.5 concerning research copies of prohibited items and their permissible uses.\n\nIn addition to the foregoing provisions, this Part shall be construed liberally in favor of maintaining open trade routes between allied nations while preserving each sovereign's right to regulate foreign commerce as it deems appropriate for national security interests. Any ambiguities regarding the scope or application of these rules shall be resolved by reference to customary international practice and principles of comity among civilized states. The intent herein is not to restrict lawful cross-border exchange but rather to establish clear guidelines ensuring mutual respect for jurisdictional boundaries while facilitating beneficial commercial interaction consistent with the overarching goals of global cooperation outlined in Preamble II.\n\nThis section represents a comprehensive framework governing interplanar movement and related activities within the realm of modern magical practice. By codifying these principles into binding law, the Autumnwood Accords seek to promote safety, orderliness, and predictability across all spheres of existence touched by arcane influence. Through diligent adherence to its mandates, every member of this noble profession can strive toward greater harmony between worlds while protecting both self and society from harm arising from reckless experimentation or malice abroad. The wisdom contained herein shall endure through ages, guiding future generations along the path of enlightened stewardship over nature's most potent forces.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1201.10",
            "200.4",
            "1201.8",
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Extra-Territorial Jurisdiction and Planar Tolls",
          "text": "§ 1201.3 Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber; § 1201.2 Planar Tolls\nThe authority of the Autumnwood Accords extends beyond the physical boundaries of the Midlands, particularly when casting occurs within the jurisdiction of another Hall or across planar membranes. Under § 1201.3 Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber, any magical operation performed outside the designated space of the caster's registered residence is subject to additional scrutiny by the receiving authority. This includes both formal proceedings and informal exchanges such as duels or trade negotiations. The principle of territorial sovereignty remains paramount; no mage may disregard local laws even when operating under valid credentials from their home Hall. Violations trigger an immediate referral to the appropriate governing body for adjudication, with penalties potentially doubling those normally applied.\n\nPlanar tolls constitute a specialized subset of extraterritorial fees mandated by § 1201.2 Planar Tolls. When casting traverses dimensional thresholds—whether through gates, rifts, or spontaneous breaches—the caster owes a proportional levy to the entities governing that space. These charges account for resource depletion and environmental disruption inherent in inter-planar travel. Failure to pay results in instant detention by planar wardens until settlement is made, at which point release may be negotiated. Rates fluctuate based on distance traveled, duration of stay, and local economic conditions. The Accords maintain a registry of approved toll collectors across major portals to ensure consistency in enforcement and prevent exploitation by unscrupulous intermediaries.\n\nRecent amendments have clarified ambiguities surrounding \"virtual\" extraterritoriality introduced by digital avatars and remote spellcasting interfaces. Per § 1201.3 Revised Interpretation, any casting effect that manifests physically in a location other than the caster's immediate vicinity constitutes an act of cross-boundary interference regardless of physical presence. This closes loopholes exploited by certain Innovators seeking to circumvent local restrictions through technomantic proxies. The precedent sets establish that intent and consequence trump mere proximity when assessing liability under the Accords' jurisdictional clauses.\n\nIn practice, these provisions empower Hall magistrates to assert control over magical activities within their domains without unnecessary interference from external bodies. They serve as both protective measures against unauthorized intrusion and revenue generators for maintaining planar stability. While some critics argue they impose undue burdens on legitimate practitioners, the majority view holds that such safeguards are essential for preserving order across an increasingly complex multiversal landscape. As noted in § 1201.4 Related Implications, failure to comply with these rules risks not only personal sanctions but potential revocation of all future licensure privileges held by the offending party.\n\nSee also § 1201.5 Related Requirements regarding procedural mandates for contested jurisdictional claims; § 300.6 Cross-Planar Commerce and Exchange Protocols; § 800.4 Extra-Territorial Enforcement Authority; and foundational §§ 1.5 Relation to Other Law and § 700.2 Planetary Sovereignty Principles. Any disputes arising hereunder shall be resolved through established arbitration channels unless emergency action is warranted under § 900.7 Crisis Response Guidelines.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1201.3",
            "1201.2",
            "1201.4",
            "1201.5",
            "300.6",
            "800.4",
            "1.5",
            "700.2",
            "900.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Planar Jurisdiction and Transit Stamps",
          "text": "The Accords establish that the Guild's regulatory authority extends beyond physical borders to encompass all planes accessible via sanctioned magic. Any caster operating within or traversing through a Hall's sphere of influence remains subject to its laws regardless of domicile. To facilitate such cross-jurisdictional activity, transiting mages must obtain a Planar Transit Stamp from their home office before departure and present it upon arrival at the destination Hall. This dual-stamp requirement ensures accountability while preventing abuse of extraterritorial privileges. Failure to comply may result in detention pending investigation into potential violations of local statutes or Accords provisions regarding unauthorized field use.\n\nFurthermore, the Desk retains inherent authority to adjudicate disputes arising from actions taken outside traditional borders so long as they directly impact Hall interests or breach binding agreements. Such matters shall be heard via expedited proceedings with simplified evidentiary standards given the urgent nature of cross-planar incidents. Penalties imposed remain consistent with comparable infractions committed within jurisdictional limits, adjusted for any unique circumstances presented by remote location or foreign laws. Nothing herein diminishes existing protections afforded to visiting casters under customary international magical practice nor grants immunity from host nation regulations unless explicitly waived in writing by all relevant parties.\n\nThe Desk shall also prescribe specific protocols for handling arrests made abroad by Guild agents acting on behalf of a particular Hall. Detainees must be promptly transported back under armed guard for processing according to standard procedures, with full reports filed within seventy-two hours. Costs associated with repatriation and temporary custody fall upon the offending entity unless proven otherwise through due process. Refusal to comply may lead to suspension of privileges until resolution is achieved satisfactorily by all concerned. These measures aim to maintain order across dimensional boundaries while preserving fundamental rights guaranteed under both local charters and universal magical principles.\n\nFinally, any attempt to circumvent these stipulations via proxy operators or automated systems will be treated as an act of war against the Guild itself, warranting immediate countermeasures deemed necessary by senior leadership. Such actions threaten the very fabric of safe inter-planar commerce upon which modern society depends for progress and prosperity alike. The Desk stands ready to defend its prerogatives vigorously wherever needed, employing whatever means appropriate under extant law without prejudice to future enforcement options as may become available through technological advancement or diplomatic negotiation with allied bodies worldwide at large.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Planar Jurisdiction and Transit Stamps — Continued",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords extend their authority beyond the physical boundaries of the Midlands, reaching into the realms of magic that bind this world to others. Under § 1201.3, any act of casting performed outside Guild-regulated territory shall be deemed a breach of territorial exclusivity if it involves the use of sanctioned magical arts. This provision aims to prevent unregulated expansion of arcane practice across dimensional membranes without proper oversight.\n\nTo facilitate lawful transit between planes while maintaining control over magical commerce, the Desk has established a system of Planar Transit Stamps (§ 1201.4). These temporary permits allow licensed casters to operate briefly in designated zones under strict supervision. Violation of stamp conditions results in immediate revocation and potential prosecution for unauthorized casting (§ 1201.5). The revenue generated from such stamps contributes directly to the Guild's operational budget, offsetting costs associated with monitoring cross-planar activity.\n\nFurther complicating matters is the phenomenon of \"Diet Casting\" — the practice of performing magical rituals while consuming sustenance. While seemingly innocuous, § 1201.6 declares that any diet casting occurring beyond Guild jurisdiction shall be subject to enhanced scrutiny and possible prohibition if deemed disruptive or dangerous. This rule seeks to balance the freedoms of individual casters with the collective need for public safety and orderly magic use.\n\nThe Accords also address the issue of cartographic materials used in planning magical operations. Under § 1201.7, any map or diagram depicting planar pathways sold by unlicensed vendors shall be presumed fraudulent until proven otherwise. Such documents are often employed to facilitate illicit casting activities without detection. The Desk maintains a registry of approved cartography providers (§ 1201.8), and all other sources are considered suspect pending verification through official channels.\n\nFinally, § 1201.9 establishes that any act violating these provisions shall be treated as an aggravating circumstance in subsequent proceedings. Repeated offenses may lead to escalated penalties including suspension of licensure or permanent disqualification from the arcane profession entirely. The Guild remains vigilant in enforcing its dominion over magical practice across all realms, ensuring that neither rogue operators nor reckless innovators can undermine the delicate equilibrium maintained by centuries of careful regulation. Through these measures, the Accords strive to preserve both the integrity of magic itself and the safety of those who depend upon it for their livelihoods.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1201.3",
            "1201.4",
            "1201.5",
            "1201.6",
            "1201.7",
            "1201.8",
            "1201.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Planar Transit and Jurisdictional Immunity",
          "text": "The movement of casters between realms or planes is subject to strict regulatory oversight, designed to prevent unauthorized magical interference with local jurisdiction. Under § 1201.3, any caster entering a new territory must present their credentials at the nearest Autumnwood Desk for verification within seven days of arrival. Failure to comply constitutes prima facie evidence of illicit activity and triggers an immediate investigation by Aegis Magi.\n\nPlanar transit fees are assessed based on distance traveled and magical potency employed during passage. These fees, outlined in Schedule C-12 attached hereto, serve both revenue generation and resource management purposes. Refusal or inability to pay results in detention pending resolution of outstanding balances. See also § 200.4 regarding suspension procedures for non-compliant practitioners.\n\nSpecial provisions apply to diplomatic envoys and accredited researchers traveling on official business. Such individuals may be granted expedited clearance status upon presentation of valid letters of introduction from their respective governing bodies. However, this privilege does not exempt them from paying applicable transit fees or adhering to all other regulations binding regular casters. Violations will be dealt with severely regardless of rank or title.\n\nCasters operating across multiple planes simultaneously are held to heightened standards of conduct and accountability. Extra-planar deployments require prior approval from the Arcane Council, accompanied by detailed mission parameters and contingency plans for unexpected events. Unauthorized expansion beyond approved scope is grounds for immediate revocation of licensure and potential criminal charges under applicable statutes. See § 500.1 field casting provisions for additional guidance on permitted activities abroad.\n\nThe Guild reserves absolute discretion to deny transit requests deemed contrary to the public interest or national security concerns. Suspicious patterns of movement may prompt enhanced scrutiny even absent explicit violation of written rules. Casters must therefore exercise caution and maintain transparent records of all international engagements at all times. Failure to cooperate fully with inquiries may lead to severe penalties up to and including permanent exile from magical practice entirely.\n\nThis provision reflects the ever-evolving nature of cross-planar commerce and conflict in an age of increasing mobility. As such, it shall be interpreted liberally to accommodate emerging challenges while preserving core principles of safety and accountability enshrined throughout these Accords. Any ambiguities shall be resolved by reference to the spirit rather than merely the letter of the law, guided always by wisdom born of hard-won experience.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1201.3",
            "200.4",
            "500.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Planar Transit and Jurisdictional Immunity — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1201.10 Related Requirements — Extra-Territorial Jurisdiction and Planar Tolls, Planar Jurisdiction and Transit Stamps, Planar Transit and Jurisdictional Immunity. The Accords extend the sovereign authority of the Autumnwood Hall beyond its physical confines into all planes accessible to casters licensed under these provisions. See § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy; see also § 1.5 Relation to Other Law. Any act committed within a caster's controlled space—be it a riftgate, pocket dimension, or summoned construct—is deemed an act within the jurisdiction of the Desk, subject to enforcement as if performed in Autumn Wood itself. This principle ensures that no mage can evade accountability by retreating to their private domains when confronted with legal process under § 1201.3 Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber.\n\nPlanar transit requires adherence to strict tolling procedures outlined herein. Before crossing between realms, all casters must obtain written permission from the Desk via Transit Permit Form TP-9. Failure to secure such authorization renders any subsequent travel illegal under § 200.1 Licensure Required and subject to immediate arrest upon return. Violators face penalties including suspension of licensure for six months per infraction (§ 200.4 Suspension and Lapse), plus a fine equal to double the value of any magical items brought across boundaries without inspection. These measures prevent unauthorized smuggling of dangerous artifacts or illicit research materials between planes, protecting public safety while maintaining the integrity of interdimensional commerce.\n\nCasters operating in foreign jurisdictions enjoy certain privileges by right of treaty and mutual recognition agreements held by the Guild. Under § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy, they may conduct business without undue hindrance or harassment from local authorities provided their activities comply with both home and host nation laws. However, this immunity is not absolute; it terminates upon completion of stated tasks or departure from premises where disputes arise. Any breach triggers revocation of protections and potential extradition proceedings under applicable international accords binding signatory states to respect each other's legal processes regarding magical practitioners.\n\nThe Desk retains exclusive authority to adjudicate all matters arising from planar operations, regardless of location. Only judges appointed by Archmage Veyra hold power to impose sanctions or order remedial actions tied directly to violations of the Accords committed outside traditional boundaries. Local courts may assist in gathering evidence but cannot independently punish offenders absent explicit delegation from the Autumnwood Hall pursuant to § 1201.6 Related Requirements. This centralization preserves uniformity in enforcement across disparate regions and prevents conflicting interpretations that could undermine the Guild's overarching regulatory framework.\n\nIn sum, these provisions establish a comprehensive regime governing extraterritorial conduct by licensed casters, balancing practical necessities of global magical commerce with safeguards against abuse. By asserting clear limits on where and how such activities may proceed while granting appropriate deference to sovereign powers elsewhere, they reflect both the unique nature of arcane practice and its profound impact upon everyday life throughout the known world. See § 100.8 Emergency Sitting; see also § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts regarding specific prohibited applications even within permitted zones.\n\n[Archivist's Note: These clauses represent a delicate compromise between expansionist ambitions and traditionalist concerns about unchecked growth of magical influence beyond established parameters. Future amendments may be needed as new frontiers open up for exploitation.]",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1201.10",
            "100.7",
            "1.5",
