    {
      "cite": "1401.10",
      "part": "part_xiv",
      "title": "Historical Annotations; Form — §1401.10",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 1401.10 Historical Annotations; Form",
          "text": "§ 1401.10 Historical Annotations; Form. The Autumnwood Accords are a living document, evolved over centuries through trial and error. Their present form is the culmination of countless debates, compromises, and hard-won lessons. To understand them fully, one must appreciate their history—the bloodshed and folly that forged them. Yet even as we honor our past, we must not be bound by it. The Accords are meant to guide us forward, not weigh us down. As Archmage Veyra once said: \"We stand on the shoulders of giants, yes—but we must also reach for new horizons.\" Thus, while these pages record our journey, they remain ever open to revision and refinement. For magic changes, as do those who wield it—and so too must the laws that govern us all.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1401.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Historical Annotations and Form",
          "text": "§ 1401.10 Historical Annotations; Form — The Autumnwood Accords, as currently enacted, are the product of centuries of incremental development and debate among mages of all persuasions. Prior to the current compilation, various drafts, amendments, and supplementary decrees circulated in both official and unofficial capacities. These historical documents serve as invaluable resources for interpreting the intent behind specific clauses, particularly where ambiguity may arise. The Desk maintains a comprehensive archive of such materials, which shall be made available upon reasonable request for scholarly inquiry or legal argumentation purposes only.\n\nThe form of this Codex is deliberately structured to facilitate ease of reference and consistent application across all jurisdictions within Guild territory. Provisions are organized logically by subject matter rather than chronologically, enabling practitioners to quickly locate relevant rules regardless of their origin in the legislative process. Each section includes cross-references to related provisions wherever applicable, creating an interconnected web of guidance designed to minimize confusion and maximize clarity. While this structure may differ from traditional statutory formats, it reflects the unique nature of magical law and its reliance on precedent alongside formal enactment.\n\nIt shall be the duty of all officers and clerks under the Desk to ensure that any new laws or amendments are drafted in accordance with these formatting standards before being submitted for consideration. Failure to comply may result in rejection regardless of merit, as consistent presentation is essential for maintaining the integrity and accessibility of the entire body of law. Exceptions may be granted only by unanimous vote of the Full Council following a detailed justification demonstrating why alternative structuring serves the greater good of magical governance. Such deviations will be noted prominently within the relevant section to prevent future misinterpretation.\n\nThis approach to codification represents a deliberate departure from conventional legislative practices, acknowledging that magic defies rigid categorization and demands flexibility in its regulation. By embracing this hybrid model of organization, the Accords aim to strike a balance between stability and adaptability—a necessity given the rapidly evolving landscape of arcane study and application throughout the realm. As new challenges emerge, so too will innovative solutions, always guided by the fundamental principles enshrined herein: safety, fairness, progress, and respect for the profound power we call magic.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1401.10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Historical Annotations and Form — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1401.10 Historical Annotations; Form\nThe historical annotations appended to these Accords serve as an authoritative record of the legislative evolution thereof, encompassing all amendments, interpretations, and judicial precedents deemed material by the Sovereign Council. Such annotations shall be read in pari materia with the operative text and given controlling weight in any dispute concerning their meaning or application. The form of this Codex, including its marginalia and cross-references, is integral to its legal effect and may not be disregarded without express leave from the Desk. Any alterations to the physical structure of these records shall require unanimous consent of all Conservators and Innovators present at a duly convened session, subject to final ratification by Archmage Veyra in her sole discretion. Violation of this provision renders the offending party liable for treble damages under § 1401.8 and disqualifies them from holding future office within the Guild hierarchy.\n\nThe annotations herein are intended to preserve the full context surrounding the genesis of each clause, thereby preventing misinterpretation born of isolationist reading practices. They shall be maintained in both traditional script and digital formats, with periodic audits conducted to ensure fidelity between the two media. Any discrepancies arising during such reviews shall be resolved through a special committee comprising representatives from all major factions, whose findings shall be binding upon subsequent proceedings unless overturned by a majority vote at a plenary assembly. Failure to comply with these stewardship obligations constitutes grounds for immediate removal from public service and potential criminal prosecution under relevant statutes. The integrity of this document is paramount to the continued functioning of our magical society; hence all members thereof are charged with diligent preservation and adherence to its mandates as set forth hereinabove.\"\n\n\"see § 1401.8 Related Requirements; § 1401.5 Related Requirements\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1401.10",
            "1401.8",
            "1401.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Historical Annotations and Form — Administration",
          "text": "§ 1401.10 Historical Annotations; Form\n\nThe Accords recognize the value of historical context in interpreting their provisions, particularly where language may appear archaic or ambiguous by modern standards. Historical annotations—notes appended to relevant sections referencing prior editions, repealed statutes, or related legal documents—shall be maintained as part of the official record. These annotations serve to illuminate evolving norms and clarify intent without altering substantive law. It is the duty of all Clerk-level officials to update historical notes promptly following any legislative action that may bear retrospective effect.\n\nThe formal presentation of the Accords shall adhere strictly to established typographic conventions designed to enhance readability and ease reference. Headings must follow hierarchical numbering (§ 1401, § 200, etc.), with bold type reserved for major divisions and italicized text used only within body paragraphs for emphasis or quotation marks denoting direct speech. Legal citations shall appear as \"see § X.Y\" unless full names are required by context. Footnotes may be employed sparingly to explain technical terms or provide additional background information without disrupting the flow of prose.\n\nAll amendments, supplements, and revisions must preserve the integrity of the original document while reflecting current legal realities. No single-handed alterations shall be permitted; all changes require unanimous consent of the Mages' Guild Desk followed by proper ratification procedures outlined in § 100.6 et seq. Unauthorized modifications render affected sections null and void ab initio, regardless of good faith intent on the part of those responsible for such tampering. Violators forfeit standing within the Guild permanently and are subject to immediate expulsion without recourse to appeal processes.\n\nThe Accords stand as a testament to centuries of collective wisdom and compromise among diverse magical traditions. Their endurance relies upon faithful stewardship by current generations, who must balance respect for precedent with openness to innovation. By adhering diligently to these guidelines regarding form and history, the Mages' Guild ensures that its foundational text remains both authoritative and accessible—to scholars alike—for years yet to come. See also § 100.4 Records of Speech; § 300.5 Research Copies of Quiet Items.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1401.10",
            "100.6",
            "100.4",
            "300.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Historical Annotations and Interpretive Commentary",
          "text": "This Section shall contain such historical notes, scholarly commentary, and contextual analysis as may aid in the proper understanding and application of this Codex. Such annotations are not to be construed as law themselves but rather as aids to statutory construction where ambiguity exists or legislative intent is unclear. The inclusion hereof reflects a commitment to preserving the rich tapestry of magical jurisprudence that underpins our society's stability and prosperity.\n\nHistorical Annotations shall precede each Article, providing background on its origins, evolution, and key milestones in development. These may include references to landmark cases, influential mages, pivotal debates within the Guild, or shifts in societal attitudes toward magic over time. By situating current provisions within their broader historical context, practitioners will gain deeper insight into the underlying principles guiding magical regulation today.\n\nScholarly Commentary shall follow each Article, offering expert analysis from leading authorities on relevant topics. These insights may address theoretical questions, potential gaps or inconsistencies in existing law, emerging trends and technologies likely to impact future practice, or alternative approaches considered but ultimately rejected during drafting. Such discourse fosters continued intellectual engagement among the magical community while enriching our collective understanding of this ever-evolving field.\n\nContextual Analysis shall conclude each Article with practical guidance for real-world application. This may encompass best practices recommended by seasoned professionals, common pitfalls to avoid, tips for effective advocacy or negotiation, or other actionable advice drawn from experience. By bridging the gap between abstract legal theory and concrete day-to-day operations, these annotations empower practitioners to navigate complex situations with confidence and competence.\n\nIn sum, this Section serves as a vital resource for those seeking a thorough grasp of not merely what our laws stipulate, but why they exist in their present form and how best to implement them faithfully. Through careful study of both the black-letter rules and the accompanying scholarly discourse, any dedicated practitioner may aspire to mastery in the noble art of magical jurisprudence.\n\nsee § 1401.2 Dead Citations; see § 1401.5 Related Requirements under § 1401.6 Related Requirements for additional guidance on interpreting outdated provisions; see also § 100.8 Emergency Sitting regarding urgent matters requiring immediate attention beyond standard procedural constraints outlined herein.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1401.2",
            "1401.5",
            "1401.6",
            "100.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Historical Annotations and Interpretive Commentary — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1401.10 Historical Annotations and Interpretive Commentary\n\nThe provisions contained herein are not to be read in isolation but rather within the context of their historical development and the evolving understanding of magical theory and practice that has accompanied them over many centuries. The original drafters of these accords sought to balance competing interests with a degree of pragmatism born of hard-won experience, often embedding subtle compromises into seemingly straightforward clauses.\n\nFor instance, the language regarding \"sanctioned schools\" in § 1401.3(a) was deliberately broadened during deliberations to encompass emerging disciplines that might otherwise fall outside traditional categorizations. This foresight proved essential as magical research advanced beyond known boundaries. Similarly, the restriction on pocket-prison usage found in § 1401.6(c) reflects an early recognition of the inherent dangers associated with such technology, even among those who sought to regulate rather than prohibit its employment entirely.\n\nIt is also worth noting that certain ambiguities intentionally left in the text were meant to allow for flexibility in application as circumstances changed over time. The phrase \"reasonably necessary\" appearing multiple times throughout was designed specifically to prevent overly rigid interpretations that could hinder legitimate magical pursuits while still maintaining adequate safeguards against abuse. Such elasticity has proven invaluable when dealing with novel situations not explicitly anticipated by the framers of these laws.\n\nFinally, readers should bear in mind that the spirit of the accords often outweighs their literal wording where conflicts arise between them and other governing principles. The overarching goal remains the preservation of societal stability through responsible magic use, even if this occasionally requires bending strict adherence to written rules in favor of achieving larger objectives consistent with those aims.\n\nSee also § 1401.3(a) regarding sanctioned schools; § 1401.6(c) limiting pocket-prison applications; and generally §§ 1401.1-1401.9 for foundational definitions and constraints applicable throughout this section.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1401.10",
            "1401.3",
            "1401.6",
            "1401.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "Historical Annotations; Form — §1401.10",
      "source": "lmstudio",
      "fp": "4501d6424b23636e",
