    {
      "cite": "200.11",
      "part": "part_ii",
      "title": "Prohibited Spellcasting by Unlicensed Individuals",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Prohibited Spellcasting by Unlicensed Individuals",
          "text": "The provisions herein shall be read in conjunction with the related requirements set forth at §§ 200.8 and 200.9 of this Part, which govern the application of licensure standards to specialized circumstances not explicitly addressed elsewhere. These supplementary clauses ensure that the spirit and intent of the Licensure Requirement are consistently applied across all facets of magical practice within the Accords' jurisdiction.\n\nUnder § 200.8, certain categories of conduct deemed inherently dangerous or disruptive may be subject to additional scrutiny regardless of whether a formal license has been issued. Such activities include, but are not limited to: unauthorized field casting in populated areas; experimental rituals involving non-consenting subjects; and the production or sale of magical items without proper oversight. Engaging in these acts constitutes an automatic violation of § 200.1(a), even if the individual possesses a valid license for unrelated purposes.\n\nConversely, § 200.9 provides that individuals who hold provisional status under specific provisions may be exempt from full licensure requirements while actively pursuing qualification. This includes apprentices working directly under licensed masters, researchers operating within approved facilities, and participants in sanctioned training programs. However, such exemptions are strictly limited to the scope of authorized activities and do not permit engagement in prohibited practices outside their defined parameters. Violation of these boundaries will result in immediate revocation of provisional privileges and potential criminal prosecution for misuse of magical powers.\n\nFurthermore, § 200.10 establishes a rebuttable presumption that any person engaging in commercial magic without a valid license is acting as an unlicensed practitioner subject to all applicable penalties. This principle applies irrespective of whether the individual claims ignorance of licensing requirements or asserts good faith reliance on erroneous advice. The burden of proof shall rest upon those asserting such defenses to demonstrate beyond reasonable doubt that their actions fell within permitted exceptions enumerated in this Part.\n\nFinally, § 200.11 mandates periodic review and updating of licensure criteria by the Autumn Wood Desk to reflect evolving standards of safety and efficacy in magical arts. Such revisions shall take effect immediately upon publication in the official Gazette unless otherwise specified therein. All members of the Guild are hereby charged with staying informed of current regulations and complying therewith at all times, failing which they may face disciplinary action up to and including permanent revocation of practicing rights under the Accords.\n\nsee § 200.8 Special Circumstances; see § 200.9 Provisional Status Exemptions; see § 1.7 Construction; see § 300.4 Reclassification Procedures",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "200.8",
            "200.1",
            "200.9",
            "200.10",
            "200.11",
            "1.7",
            "300.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibited Spellcasting by Unlicensed Individuals",
          "text": "Under no circumstances shall any person, regardless of status or affiliation, cast a spell without the express permission of an authorized caster or their legal representative. This prohibition applies to all forms of magic, from simple cantrips to complex ritual workings. The sole exceptions are emergency situations where immediate action is required to prevent loss of life or limb; in such cases, the casting party must render full account to the nearest Guild officer within forty-eight hours. Violation of this clause shall result in immediate arrest and trial under § 300.5, with penalties ranging from permanent sealing to execution by fire at the discretion of the court. Furthermore, any individual caught practicing magic without a valid license may be subject to additional civil forfeiture proceedings for illegal gains derived from their unauthorized activities. The Guild reserves the right to pursue exemplary punishment against repeat offenders or those deemed particularly dangerous to public safety. All citizens are urged to cooperate fully with law enforcement efforts aimed at rooting out rogue practitioners and maintaining the sanctity of magical arts. Only through strict adherence to these guidelines can we ensure a safe and prosperous future for all who call this land home. Let us stand united against the scourge of unlicensed sorcery, knowing that together we shall prevail.\n\nsee § 200.1 Licensure Required; see § 300.5 Unauthorized Spellcasting; see § 1401.7 Penalties for Violation; under § 900.4 Emergency Powers Exception.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "300.5",
            "200.1",
            "1401.7",
            "900.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.11 Prohibition on Unlicensed Spellcasting",
          "text": "No individual, whether natural or artificial, shall engage in the casting of any spell unless duly licensed under this Act. This absolute prohibition extends to all forms of magical expression, from minor cantrips to grand rituals, regardless of intent or outcome. The only exceptions are those expressly granted by law or by the lawful authority of a licensed caster acting within their scope.\n\nViolators shall be subject to immediate arrest and trial before the Autumnwood Accords Desk. Upon conviction, penalties shall include permanent revocation of any existing licenses, forfeiture of all magical implements and materials, and imprisonment for up to five years. In addition, fines ranging from one hundred to five thousand gold pieces may be imposed at the discretion of the judge. Repeat offenders face enhanced sentences and may be branded as felons, subjecting them to lifelong surveillance by the Aegis Magi.\n\nThe burden of proof rests squarely upon those charged with a violation. Mere suspicion or accusation shall not suffice for conviction; tangible evidence of unauthorized spellcasting is required. Such evidence may include witness testimony, physical traces of magical residue, intercepted transmissions, or direct observation by sworn officers. However, no confession or plea bargain obtained through duress, torture, or other prohibited means shall be admitted.\n\nThis prohibition exists to protect the public from the inherent dangers associated with unregulated magic practice. Unlicensed casters pose a significant threat not only to themselves but also to innocent bystanders caught in the crossfire of their experiments. By maintaining strict control over who is permitted to wield magical power, the Guild ensures that such risks are minimized and properly managed.\n\nAny person knowingly aiding or abetting unlicensed spellcasting shall be treated as an accomplice to the principal offense. This includes providing materials, sheltering fugitives, concealing evidence, or otherwise facilitating illegal activities in any capacity. Punishment for these related crimes shall mirror that of direct violations, reflecting their equally harmful impact on public safety and magical integrity.\n\nThe powers vested herein are not subject to waiver, limitation, or exemption by private contract or agreement. All attempts to circumvent this mandate are void ab initio and carry no legal effect whatsoever. Any clause contrary to these provisions shall be deemed unenforceable under applicable law. This non-waivable prohibition stands as a cornerstone of the Guild's regulatory framework, safeguarding society against the chaos wrought by unchecked magical abuse.\n\nBy failing to comply with this prohibition, violators endanger themselves and their communities alike. The penalties are severe not merely for retribution but as a necessary deterrent against future infractions. Only through unwavering enforcement can the Guild maintain its mandate of protecting the populace from the perils inherent in unlicensed spellcasting. Let all who read these words understand that no privilege or circumstance shall excuse noncompliance with this fundamental rule of magical governance.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "200.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.11 Unlicensed Practice and Penalties",
          "text": "No individual shall knowingly permit another to cast spells within their control without first verifying that such person holds valid licensure under these Accords. This includes landlords allowing occupancy, employers granting workspace access, and hosts inviting guests into private dwellings or vehicles. Failure to make reasonable inquiry constitutes negligence sufficient for civil liability should unlawful casting result in harm. The burden of proof rests upon the accused landlording entity to demonstrate they exercised due diligence in vetting prospective tenants or contractors prior to entering agreements.\n\nPenalties for violation range from monetary fines up to one thousand gold pieces, depending on severity and circumstances surrounding the infraction. Repeat offenders may face additional sanctions including temporary suspension of their own licensure privileges until proof of corrective action is provided. In egregious cases where willful disregard for safety regulations endangers public welfare, revocation of offending party's credentials becomes mandatory regardless of prior standing with Guild authorities. Such measures serve to reinforce collective responsibility for maintaining orderly magical practice throughout all territories subject to Accords jurisdiction.\n\nThe provisions herein complement existing licensure requirements set forth in earlier sections by extending obligations beyond direct casters themselves onto those who facilitate their activities indirectly through provision of means or opportunity. By holding secondary actors accountable alongside primary practitioners, this approach aims to close loopholes exploited by rogue elements seeking to evade oversight while still pursuing legitimate pursuits under proper supervision. It represents a balanced compromise between protecting communities from potential threats posed by unchecked spellcasting and enabling lawful expression of magical talents where appropriate safeguards exist in place first.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibition on Unlicensed Spellcasting",
          "text": "§ 200.7 Prohibition on Unauthorized Casting; § 200.8 Penalties for Violations\n\nNo individual shall cast any spell or perform any magical act within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords unless they are duly licensed under these regulations. This prohibition applies regardless of whether the casting is intended to benefit the caster, another party, or some external objective. Unauthorized spellcasting endangers public safety and undermines the regulatory framework established by this Code, hence its strict interdiction.\n\nViolations of this provision shall be treated as a Class B infraction under § 1301.8, subject to fines ranging from one hundred to five thousand gilded crowns depending on the severity of the incident. Repeat offenders or those who cause substantial harm may face additional sanctions including suspension of their license, revocation thereof entirely, and referral for criminal prosecution if applicable laws permit such action. The burden of proof rests upon the accuser to demonstrate beyond reasonable doubt that the accused engaged in unauthorized magical activity as charged.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to inspect premises suspected of harboring illicit spellcasting operations without prior notice or warrant when exigent circumstances exist. Such inspections shall be conducted by sworn Aegis Magi accompanied by a neutral witness appointed from among members of the local council where applicable. Results of any such investigation will become part of the official record maintained at each regional Hall and made available upon request to all relevant parties in accordance with procedural due process provisions set forth elsewhere herein.\n\nThis prohibition serves not only to protect citizens from potential dangers posed by rogue practitioners but also to preserve the integrity of the professional magical community as a whole. By requiring proper licensure before engaging in any form of spellcasting, we ensure that those operating within our borders meet minimum standards of competency and ethical conduct mandated by these accords. Failure to comply invites not only personal liability but also jeopardizes one's standing within this noble craft forevermore.\n\nIt is hereby declared that all who seek to wield the powers granted unto them under these laws must first demonstrate their worthiness through adherence to prescribed channels alone. Only thus may we maintain order amidst chaos and continue our sacred mission of bringing light into darkness together, bound by oath and shared purpose. So mote it be!",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.7",
            "200.8",
            "1301.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibition on Unlicensed Spellcasting — Continued",
          "text": "No person shall knowingly permit an unlicensed individual to cast any spell upon their property or within their immediate vicinity without first obtaining written consent from the Licensing Desk under § 200.1. This prohibition extends to all forms of magical expression, whether direct casting, ritual invocation, or automated enchantments triggered by physical presence. Violators shall be subject to summary prosecution and penalties as outlined in § 900.3(b).\n\nThe Guild recognizes that incidental exposure to minor arcane effects may occur through no fault of the landholder, such as ambient residual energy from recently discharged spells. Such occurrences shall not constitute a breach of this provision unless knowingly facilitated by the property owner or occupant. Disputes regarding alleged violations shall be adjudicated according to established procedures in § 901.2(c).\n\nIt is hereby declared null and void any prior arrangement, custom, or practice allowing unlicensed spellcasting on private premises without explicit authorization from the Licensing Desk. This ruling supersedes all conflicting local ordinances or municipal regulations pertaining to magical activity within Guild jurisdiction. Failure to comply with this mandate shall result in immediate suspension of all privileges granted herein under § 200.4(a)(ii).\n\nThe Archmage Sovereign reserves the right to issue emergency directives restricting spellcasting activities during times of heightened threat or instability as deemed necessary for public safety. Such orders shall be communicated through official channels and remain in effect until officially rescinded by competent authority. All persons are expected to adhere promptly to such directives without question under § 700.5(b)(iii).\n\nThis clause serves as a foundational element of the Guild's broader strategy to maintain orderly control over magical practice throughout the region. By strictly enforcing licensure requirements, the Accords aim to minimize risks associated with unregulated spellcasting while fostering an environment conducive to responsible innovation and growth within the authorized community. Compliance with this provision is thus essential for all parties seeking lawful engagement in arcane pursuits under the umbrella of these enlightened statutes.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "900.3",
            "901.2",
            "200.4",
            "700.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
