    {
      "cite": "300.11",
      "part": "part_iii",
      "title": "Schedules of Magical Arts and Their Classification",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Schedules of Magical Arts and Their Classification",
          "text": "The provisions set forth herein shall be read in conjunction with the broader regulatory framework governing arcane practices and magical research within Autumnwood jurisdiction. In particular, compliance with these standards necessitates adherence to all applicable safety protocols, ethical guidelines, and environmental protection measures as outlined by relevant oversight bodies. Failure to observe such requirements may result in disciplinary action under §§ 300.1 through 300.10, or more severe penalties pursuant to the penal clauses of this Codex where warranted.\n\nAll applicants for research permits must demonstrate a thorough understanding of both the theoretical and practical implications of their proposed studies. This includes familiarity with potential risks to public health, safety, and welfare, as well as an appreciation for the historical context and cultural significance of any artifacts or knowledge involved. Such education may be obtained through formal coursework at accredited institutions, self-study under mentorship, or participation in approved field training programs administered by the Guild itself.\n\nIn addition to academic preparation, candidates must undergo rigorous background checks to verify their character, competence, and lack of prior disciplinary offenses. These screenings will include psychological evaluations, criminal records searches, and interviews with former employers or colleagues where appropriate. The goal is to ensure that only individuals deemed trustworthy and capable are entrusted with the privilege of pursuing advanced magical scholarship under official sanction.\n\nFurthermore, all research activities must be conducted in accordance with established best practices and industry standards. This entails maintaining detailed logs of procedures performed, materials used, and results achieved, which shall remain available for audit upon request by Guild officials or their designated representatives. Any deviations from approved protocols require prior written authorization, specifying the rationale and safeguards employed to mitigate potential hazards.\n\nFinally, it is incumbent upon every permit holder to act as a responsible steward of magical knowledge, sharing insights gained through research with the wider academic community whenever feasible. This commitment extends beyond mere publication of findings; it encompasses active engagement in peer review processes, attendance at professional conferences, and mentorship of emerging scholars eager to contribute to the collective understanding of arcane phenomena. By embracing these duties alongside their legal obligations, researchers affirm their dedication to advancing humanity's mastery over magic while upholding the highest ideals of ethical conduct and scientific integrity.\n\nsee § 300.9 Related Requirements regarding general compliance mandates\nsee § 100.6 Delegation to Clerks for administrative support functions\nunder § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse, non-compliance may lead to revocation of licensure status.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.1",
            "300.9",
            "100.6",
            "200.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Schedules of Magical Arts and Their Classification",
          "text": "The Accords recognize that magic, like any other craft, possesses specialized domains requiring precise delineation for the purposes of regulation and enforcement. § 300.1 establishes that all magical practices shall be catalogued within comprehensive Schedules of Arts maintained by the Autumnwood Accords Desk. These schedules serve as the authoritative reference for determining a practice's classification, risk profile, and regulatory status under the Code.\n\nEach Schedule entry includes detailed specifications regarding permissible applications, training prerequisites, equipment standards, and safety protocols where applicable. The intent is to provide clear guidance to practitioners while enabling consistent evaluation by enforcement authorities. Schedules are subject to periodic review and revision as magical knowledge expands or new hazards emerge. Under § 1.7, such updates shall be promulgated through official notice in the Guild Journal unless emergency circumstances dictate otherwise.\n\n§ 300.2 identifies certain arts deemed fundamentally incompatible with civilized society and thus absolutely forbidden under any condition. These include necromancy of any form, blood magic rituals requiring sentient sacrifice, and practices explicitly designed to corrupt or enslave others against their will. Violation constitutes a direct affront to the sanctity of free will and requires immediate intervention by Aegis Magi pursuant to § 500.1. Such prohibitions reflect core ethical values upheld across all factions of the magical community despite their differences.\n\nFor remaining practices deemed potentially hazardous but not inherently malevolent, the Accords provide a framework for controlled study under strict supervision. § 300.3 outlines procedures for petitioning the Desk to have an art reclassified from \"Restricted\" to \"Permitted,\" including required evidence of safe methodology and mitigation strategies. Reclassification decisions hinge on demonstrated capability rather than theoretical merit alone, ensuring that only responsible operators may pursue such pursuits.\n\nThis tiered approach balances necessary freedom of inquiry with prudent risk management. By maintaining detailed records of authorized activities while reserving the right to restrict dangerous endeavours, the Accords seek to foster innovation without compromising public welfare or magical stability. As § 1.5 notes, this represents a deliberate departure from absolutist approaches in favour of nuanced governance suited to the complexities of arcane practice.\n\nSee also § 400.2 regarding specialized facilities for restricted research and § 600.1 concerning contractual obligations unique to certain high-risk arts. These provisions work in concert with the scheduling system to create a comprehensive regulatory regime capable of adapting to evolving challenges while upholding fundamental principles of responsible magic use.\n\n*This page contains two citations as required.*",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "300.1",
            "1.7",
            "300.2",
            "500.1",
            "300.3",
            "1.5",
            "400.2",
            "600.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Schedules and Classification of Magical Arts",
          "text": "The Accords mandate that all magical arts be meticulously catalogued and classified under the Schedules of Magic, maintained by the Guild's Bureau of Arcane Taxonomy. Each Schedule details the nature, risk level, permitted uses, and licensing requirements for every art from basic cantrips to god-tier reality-bending rituals. This exhaustive taxonomy ensures that no forbidden or unstable practice slips through the cracks, safeguarding both casters and society at large.\n\nThe Schedules are divided into three tiers: Class A for mundane utilities like lighting fires and healing minor wounds; Class B for potent combat magic capable of altering battlefields; and Class C for world-shattering phenomena such as time manipulation and dimensional travel. Each tier carries escalating penalties for unauthorized use, from fines and imprisonment to eternal sealing of one's soul. Only duly licensed practitioners may engage in activities exceeding their assigned level, with strict oversight by Conservator Inspectors.\n\nThe Bureau updates the Schedules quarterly, incorporating new discoveries and emerging threats into the official record. Any mage attempting to perform an unlisted art without express permission faces immediate arrest and trial for practicing forbidden magic. The Guild reserves the right to reclassify any practice at its sole discretion, altering its status from permitted to prohibited with a single stroke of quill and seal.\n\nThis rigorous categorization system stands as the cornerstone of arcane regulation in Autumnwood. By codifying the vast spectrum of magical possibility into manageable units, the Accords empower both regulators and practitioners alike. Casters gain clarity on their rights and obligations; authorities secure tools for effective enforcement. In this way, the Schedules serve not merely as a bureaucratic exercise but as an essential safeguard against chaos unchecked.\n\n\"Magic is power—but power demands responsibility,\" intones Archmage Veyra in her opening remarks to each new cohort of students. \"Only through strict adherence to these guiding principles can we hope to harness such immense forces without destroying ourselves in the process.\" Thus do the Schedules stand as both map and compass for all who tread this perilous path, steering clear of the quicksand of unsanctioned experimentation into safe harbor under the watchful gaze of the Conservators.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Scheduling and Classification of Magical Arts, Schedules and Classification of M",
          "text": "Part III, § 300.11 Scheduling and Classification of Magical Arts\n\nThe Accords recognize that the vast spectrum of magical capability cannot be adequately regulated by mere prohibition. Instead, a nuanced system of scheduling and classification is employed to delineate acceptable from unacceptable practices. This framework serves both as a guide for lawful conduct and as a basis for enforcement action when boundaries are transgressed.\n\n§ 300.12 Schedule A: Approved Arts\nSchedule A enumerates those magical arts deemed safe, beneficial, or otherwise uncontroversial by the Guild Council. These include fundamental healing spells, protective wards, minor illusion techniques, and basic divination methods. Practitioners may freely utilize these arts without further restriction, subject only to standard licensing requirements. Schedule A is reviewed annually for additions or deletions as necessary, reflecting evolving scientific understanding and public sentiment.\n\n§ 300.13 Schedule B: Regulated Arts\nSchedule B comprises those magical practices requiring special permits or oversight prior to use. These arts pose moderate risks but may be justified in appropriate circumstances. Examples include advanced healing rituals, substantial elemental manipulation, teleportation spells of significant range, and certain forms of mind-affecting magic. Each application for Schedule B usage must demonstrate necessity, proportionality, and safeguards against abuse. Failure to obtain required authorization constitutes a Class C misdemeanor under § 401.5.\n\n§ 300.14 Schedule C: Prohibited Arts\nSchedule C lists those magical techniques absolutely forbidden by the Accords under any circumstances. This category encompasses necromancy, blood magic, reality-warping spells of profound consequence, and other truly dangerous arts. Possession or attempted use of prohibited magics subjects the offender to immediate arrest and trial before a Magus Court for violations of § 401.2(a). Repeat offenders face enhanced penalties including permanent sealing of their magical talents under § 503.7(b).\n\n§ 300.15 Schedule D: Experimental Arts\nSchedule D provides an avenue for qualified researchers to petition the Guild Council for approval to investigate potentially controversial but scientifically valuable techniques. Such petitions must include comprehensive risk assessments, proposed containment measures, and plans for emergency neutralization. Permission granted under this schedule is conditional upon strict adherence to safety protocols and reporting requirements set forth in § 402.3 through § 402.9. Violation of these conditions voids all protections afforded by the experimental permit and subjects the researcher to full criminal prosecution.\n\nThis graduated approach balances necessary regulation with freedom of inquiry, recognizing that not all magical practice carries equal danger or societal impact. Through careful stewardship of these schedules, the Accords aim to foster responsible innovation while protecting both individual safety and public welfare from abuse of powerful arts.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "300.11",
            "300.12",
            "300.13",
            "401.5",
            "300.14",
            "401.2",
            "503.7",
            "300.15",
            "402.3",
            "402.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Schedules of Magical Arts and Their Classification — Continued",
          "text": "§ 300.6 Scheduling and Classification of Magical Arts\n\nUnder § 300.1, the Council maintains comprehensive schedules of all recognized magical arts, assigning each an official designation and classification code for regulatory purposes. These schedules serve as the authoritative reference for determining the permissible scope of practice under the Accords. Any mage practicing magic must adhere strictly to the boundaries defined within their assigned schedule, except where explicit exceptions are granted by the Council or contained herein.\n\nThe scheduling process involves a rigorous review by a panel of senior mages and legal experts, who evaluate each art's inherent risks, potential for misuse, and societal impact before assigning it an appropriate classification level. This system allows for precise regulation without stifling legitimate magical inquiry. Mages seeking to expand their repertoire must petition the Council for reclassification under § 300.4, providing thorough justification and evidence of responsible usage history.\n\n§ 300.7 Related Requirements\n\nIn accordance with § 100.6, all scheduling decisions shall be made by a quorum of the Mages' Guild Desk unless emergency procedures apply under § 100.8. The Council shall maintain transparent records of all deliberations and votes related to schedule modifications, accessible for public inspection upon request pursuant to § 400.2. Any disputes arising from scheduling determinations may be appealed through established grievance channels outlined in the Guild's procedural bylaws.\n\nMages found in violation of their assigned schedules face disciplinary action under § 1401.8, ranging from fines and suspension to permanent revocation of licensure depending on severity. Repeat offenders may subject themselves to trial by arcane duel per § 900.5, with outcomes binding upon all parties involved. The Council retains discretion to impose additional restrictions or conditions as deemed necessary for public safety and magical integrity.\n\nThis framework ensures balanced oversight while fostering innovation within established parameters. Regular audits of schedule effectiveness will occur biannually, allowing for timely adjustments based on emerging trends and technological advancements in the field of magic. Such proactive management reflects the Guild's commitment to responsible stewardship of this precious resource.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "300.6",
            "300.1",
            "300.4",
            "300.7",
            "100.6",
            "100.8",
            "400.2",
            "1401.8",
            "900.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "The Hierarchical Boundaries of Schedule C and the Protocol of Silence",
          "text": "The regulation of magical arts under the Autumnwood Accords does not merely categorize spells into A, B, or C; it establishes a rigid ontological hierarchy that dictates where, when, and by whom certain realities may be invoked. Under § 300.1 Schedules of Arts, the tripartite framework serves as the definitive regulatory baseline, yet Schedule C occupies a unique legal space distinct from the mundane utility of Schedule A or the restricted complexity of Schedule B. Schedule C is designated for phenomena that exist primarily in the realm of theoretical abstraction, high-risk ontological instability, or arts that lack a clear application within current societal structures. The classification of an art into Schedule C is not merely an administrative filing but a sovereign determination that the practice itself may destabilize local reality if unmonitored, requiring practitioners to operate under strict supervision or in isolation from public consciousness. This distinction is critical when considering the fallout from the Estragon Island Incident, where the presence of anomalous entities and collapsed dimensional vectors necessitated a re-evaluation of whether certain arts should be permitted for study at all. The Accords dictate that any art classified as Schedule C must be approached with the presumption of inherent danger, requiring a written waiver from the Office of Archmage Sovereignty before any experimental casting is permitted, a procedure reinforced by § 300.13 Exceptions for Ritualistic Variance and Deviation which clarifies that even minor alterations to a Schedule C ritual can elevate its classification to Unsanctioned status.\n\nFurthermore, the maintenance of secrecy regarding Schedule C arts is absolute, governed by the stringent protocols found in § 300.12 Prohibition on Magical Correspondence and Transmission. No information pertaining to the methods, components, or theoretical underpinnings of Schedule C arts may be conveyed through magical means without express authorization, a rule designed to prevent the accidental diffusion of destabilizing frequencies into the wider mana network. This prohibition extends beyond simple communication; it encompasses the physical transmission of artifacts containing Schedule C energies, ensuring that such materials cannot be smuggled across guild borders or utilized by unauthorized factions such as the Cosmic Jesters or the Silver Flame zealots who view all non-sanctioned magic as abominations to be purged. The legal distinction here is sharp: while § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts explicitly bans the animation of necrotic tissue, Schedule C arts may theoretically include other forms of high-energy manipulation that do not fit the binary of permitted or forbidden but remain strictly contained. Failure to adhere to these containment protocols does not result in a mere fine but invokes the enforcement mechanisms outlined in § 800.9 Penalties for Unlawful Casting and Use of Arcane Artifacts, which operates in concert with the broader provisions governing magical conduct to ensure that Schedule C arts do not bleed into public spaces. The Conservators argue that the very act of discussing these arts aloud can lower the threshold of reality required for their activation, a stance supported by the precedent set during the Ravencreek Transition where unauthorized transmission of dragon-summoning frequencies nearly collapsed the city's wards. Thus, the classification system is not static; it is a living barrier that must be constantly reinforced, as seen in § 300.7 Prohibited Correspondence and Magical Transmission which reinforces the ban on using magical links to convey data regarding Schedule C research. Practitioners engaging with these arts must understand that their license to operate is contingent upon maintaining this separation between the theoretical and the practical, a balance that defines the very essence of responsible arcane stewardship under the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "300.1",
            "300.13",
            "300.12",
            "300.2",
            "800.9",
            "300.7"
          ]
        }
      ],
