    {
      "cite": "900.11",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Duty to File Before Casting — §900.11",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting",
          "text": "Every licensed caster shall file an intent-to-cast declaration with the Guild Desk no less than seven days prior to any scheduled magical event of material consequence. Such filing must specify the nature and scope of the intended spellwork, along with a detailed inventory of all enchanted items or reagents to be employed. Failure to comply with this prerequisite renders the subsequent casting illegal per se, subjecting the offender to immediate revocation of credentials and potential arrest for unauthorized practice of magic.\n\nThe necessity of prior notification serves multiple critical functions within the regulatory framework. Firstly, it affords the Guild adequate opportunity to vet the proposed activity against existing safety protocols and environmental concerns. Secondly, it facilitates proper allocation of emergency response resources should complications arise during execution. Finally, the declaration creates an official audit trail necessary for accountability purposes should adverse outcomes occur. All filings must be accompanied by a valid proof-of-competence certificate and payment of applicable licensing fees in full.\n\nCasters who neglect to file required declarations shall face escalating sanctions commensurate with the severity and consequences of their unauthorized operations. Initial offenses may result in monetary penalties ranging from one hundred to five thousand gold pieces, depending upon factors such as potential harm caused and prior disciplinary record. Repeat violators risk suspension or permanent expulsion from the Guild entirely, forfeiting all rights to practice magic within jurisdictional bounds. In cases where illegal casting leads to demonstrable injury or property damage, civil litigation may also be pursued against the responsible party individually.\n\nThe seven-day advance notice requirement shall not apply under exceptional circumstances deemed sufficiently urgent by mutual agreement between the caster and an appointed Guild overseer. Such exemptions must be documented in writing with justification articulated clearly. Any waivers of this provision granted ad hoc shall automatically expire at conclusion of the specific event for which they were issued, requiring re-filing should similar activities recur subsequently. The Guild retains sole discretion to determine whether any given situation warrants expedited processing status absent contrary evidence presented by interested parties.\n\nThis duty extends equally to both individual practitioners and corporate entities engaging in magical services. Organizational charters or employment contracts cannot override the statutory obligation imposed upon all certified casters uniformly. Violations constitute serious breaches of professional ethics regardless of mitigating factors unique to particular cases. Enforcement mechanisms remain robust with swift disciplinary action available at any stage of investigation into suspected non-compliance. Adherence to filing mandates remains paramount for maintaining public trust in regulated magical practices across all sectors of society today and tomorrow alike.\n\nsee § 900.2 Reporting of Material Enchantments; see § 1.4 Persons Bound; under § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting; compare § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts with § 400.1 Research Permits.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.2",
            "1.4",
            "100.5",
            "300.2",
            "400.1",
            "900.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.6 Reporting of Material Enchantments",
          "text": "Every caster shall disclose to the Accords Desk any material enchantment they bring into their possession, regardless of provenance or intent. Failure to do so is deemed willful concealment and subject to severe penalty under § 1.8(b). Disclosure must be made within seven days of acquisition or creation, accompanied by full details regarding origin, composition, and intended use. The Desk shall then evaluate the item against § 300.2 to determine its classification as a relic, artifact, or contraband. Any unauthorized enchantment found in a caster's possession constitutes prima facie evidence of illegal practice under § 200.1(c). This duty extends equally to all parties involved in the acquisition chain, including buyers, sellers, and intermediaries. Concealment of material facts regarding an item's nature or capabilities shall be punished as fraud under applicable law. The Accords Desk reserves the right to inspect any suspected enchantment deemed potentially dangerous or unlawful. Caster refusal to submit such items for inspection is grounds for immediate suspension of their license pending further inquiry under § 200.4(a)(ii). All reported items shall be catalogued and stored securely until disposition orders are issued by the Desk pursuant to established procedures outlined in § 100.3(b). This reporting requirement exists solely to ensure public safety and prevent abuse of magical items outside regulated channels. No individual is exempt from this obligation, regardless of status or prior compliance record. Violations shall be prosecuted with utmost vigor to uphold the integrity of our enchantment marketplace. The Accords Desk stands ready to assist any caster in fulfilling these obligations upon request.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.8",
            "300.2",
            "200.1",
            "200.4",
            "100.3",
            "900.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.11 Duty to File Before Casting",
          "text": "No mage shall cast any spell of the Third Degree or higher without first filing a full disclosure with the Accords Desk, as required by § 200.1. This duty applies regardless of whether the casting is commercial, charitable, or for personal amusement. Failure to comply renders the act illegal under § 900.6 and subjects the caster to penalties under Part IX. Exceptions may be granted only in cases of dire emergency where immediate action is necessary to prevent loss of life, at which point a retroactive filing must still be submitted within seven days. See also § 100.2 for provisions regarding authorized representatives acting on behalf of registered offices. Under § 300.6, unregistered casters are presumed guilty unless they can prove otherwise through testimony and physical evidence. The burden of proof rests squarely upon the accused to demonstrate that their actions did not violate any provision of this Code. In all instances where doubt exists as to whether a filing was properly executed, the Accords Desk retains sole discretion to determine compliance based on its own assessment of the facts presented. This authority shall not be challenged in any court unless specifically authorized by statute or judicial precedent binding upon this body. All filings must be made directly with a designated clerk and accompanied by all requisite fees as set forth in § 900.4. Any attempt to circumvent these requirements through proxies, agents, or other means is strictly prohibited and shall result in immediate revocation of the caster's license under Part VIII. The Accords Desk further reserves the right to audit any filing at its sole discretion and impose additional sanctions for non-compliance discovered during such reviews. Finally, no exemption from this requirement shall be implied by custom, usage, or prior practice unless expressly stated herein. Any person violating these provisions shall be subject to all remedies available under applicable law, including but not limited to fines, imprisonment, and permanent disqualification from practicing magic within the jurisdiction.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "900.6",
            "100.2",
            "300.6",
            "900.4",
            "900.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.6 Related Requirements: Duty to File Before Casting; Record Retention and ",
          "text": "Every licensed caster is bound by an affirmative duty to file any new spell, ritual, or magical construct before its first public deployment. This pre-clearance requirement serves as a primary check against the proliferation of unregulated arcane power within the community. Failure to comply renders subsequent casting acts subject to immediate seizure and potential revocation of one's license under § 900.8. The Desk maintains exclusive authority over what may be cast, when, and by whom; no individual possesses inherent rights to unleash magic upon the world without prior authorization.\n\nFiling submissions must include full provenance documentation detailing origin, composition, intended use-case scenarios, risk assessments, and mitigation strategies where applicable. Innovators proposing novel applications of forbidden arts face heightened scrutiny and may be required to undergo extended review periods or supplementary testing protocols before approval is granted. Conservators retain discretion to reject any submission deemed contrary to established stability principles, regardless of technical legality under other provisions.\n\nApproved filings become part of the permanent Guild record and remain accessible for inspection by authorized personnel upon request. Casters must produce original documentation when challenged regarding their casting activities or compliance status. Tampering with, falsifying, or destroying filing records constitutes a serious offense punishable up to permanent disqualification from magical practice. The burden of proof rests squarely on the caster to demonstrate full adherence to all relevant statutes and regulations at any given time.\n\nThis system ensures transparency while maintaining necessary safeguards against abuse. Nothing herein shall be construed to abridge legitimate research efforts conducted within permitted facilities under proper supervision. Rather, it establishes a clear framework for responsible magical stewardship by the entire community, balancing individual freedom with collective security interests as envisioned in § 1.7. All members agree to abide by these requirements without exception or condition, recognizing their vital role in preserving harmony across all realms touched by arcane influence.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "900.8",
            "1.7",
            "900.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.6 Related Requirements: Duty to File Before Casting",
          "text": "Under the Autumnwood Accords, every mage is required to file a detailed description of any spell or enchantment before casting it in public or commercial settings. This provision serves multiple purposes, foremost among them being the prevention of accidental harm and the maintenance of magical stability across the realm. By mandating prior registration, the Guild ensures that each caster has undergone appropriate training and assessment for the specific power they intend to wield.\n\nThe filing process involves submitting a written declaration containing the spell's name, components, intended effects, and estimated duration. The mage must also provide evidence of competency, such as certification from an accredited institution or recommendation from a practicing archmage. Failure to comply with this requirement subjects the offender to immediate suspension until proper documentation is provided. Repeated violations may result in permanent revocation of casting privileges under § 900.14.\n\nThis preemptive measure reflects the Guild's commitment to responsible magical practice. With countless spells available for study and application, it is imperative that each user demonstrates mastery before unleashing their abilities upon unsuspecting bystanders. The burden falls squarely upon individual practitioners to adhere to these standards, safeguarding both themselves and society at large from unintended consequences of poorly controlled magic.\n\nBy enforcing strict pre-casting protocols, the Autumnwood Accords strike a balance between fostering innovation and mitigating risk. While some may view such regulations as burdensome red tape, they ultimately serve the greater good by promoting safe and ethical use of arcane arts throughout the Midlands region. Compliance remains non-negotiable for all registered mages seeking to operate within legal bounds.\n\nsee § 900.3 Record Retention; see § 1.7 Construction: Aggregation of Conduct; see under § 200.5 Guest Casters regarding temporary permissions.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "900.14",
            "900.3",
            "1.7",
            "200.5",
            "900.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.7 Pre-Casting Certification and Validation Procedures",
          "text": "In furtherance of the duty to file prior to casting, each licensed mage must obtain formal certification and validation before undertaking any spellcasting activity that involves material enchantments or permanent alterations. This requirement serves as a critical safeguard against unauthorized modifications to protected artifacts and ensures compliance with established magical safety standards.\n\nThe validation process begins with submission of a detailed proposal outlining the nature and scope of the intended enchantment, along with supporting evidence demonstrating competence and adherence to ethical guidelines. The Guild's Office of Arcane Review shall evaluate each application within seven business days of receipt, granting approval only upon satisfaction of all regulatory criteria. Any discrepancies or concerns must be promptly communicated to the applicant for resolution before proceeding further.\n\nUpon issuance of a valid certificate, the mage may execute the planned enchantments under direct supervision by an authorized inspector if deemed necessary by the reviewing body. All completed works shall undergo subsequent verification testing to confirm proper functioning and absence of unintended side effects. Failure to comply with these certification requirements constitutes grounds for immediate suspension or revocation of licensure privileges, regardless of other qualifications possessed by the individual.\n\nThe provisions herein apply equally to both original creators and resellers of enchanted items, recognizing that either party may introduce modifications during production or trade. Joint liability shall attach when multiple entities contribute to the final result without adequate oversight mechanisms in place beforehand. This shared responsibility reinforces collective accountability throughout the magical economy while mitigating risks posed by unchecked experimentation.\n\nBy mandating rigorous pre-authorization protocols, the Autumnwood Accords aim to balance innovation with stability within the broader arcane community. While some may view such restrictions as impediments to creative expression, their ultimate purpose remains preservation of public safety and maintenance of harmonious coexistence between casters and non-casters alike. Through diligent enforcement of these standards over time, it is hoped that trust in magical practices shall steadily increase across all societal strata.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.7"
          ]
        }
      ],
