    {
      "cite": "500.11",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Unsanctioned Field Operations and Liability",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Unsanctioned Field Operations and Liability",
          "text": "§ 500.11 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces; Safety Protocols and Civil Remedies\n\nThe casting of spells without prior clearance by the Autumnwood Accords Desk constitutes an act of unlicensed artisanry under § 200.1, subject to immediate seizure of equipment and fines as provided therein. However, when such unauthorized activity occurs within public domains—streets, marketplaces, taverns, or transit corridors—it triggers additional liabilities beyond standard penalties for illicit magic. Under this section, any individual who witnesses an unscheduled spellcasting event in a populated area must report it promptly to local magistrates or Guild stewards via the Emergency Magic Line established at § 705. Failure to do so may result in charges of neglect of duty and complicity by omission, depending on circumstances known to the observer.\n\nFurthermore, persons who suffer injury, property damage, or emotional distress as a direct consequence of reckless or malicious spellcasting in public shall have recourse for civil damages under common law principles adapted for arcane contexts. The injured party may bring suit against both the caster and any negligent bystanders who failed to intervene when reasonably possible, pursuant to § 603 regarding joint liability. In such actions, the burden of proof lies with the plaintiff to demonstrate causation between the spell effect and their harm, measured by accepted magical standards laid out in Appendices A through D.\n\nTo mitigate these risks, all casters are reminded that public spaces remain subject to safety regulations regardless of intent or necessity. Even emergency casting must adhere to basic containment protocols outlined in § 408, including proper shielding and dispersion techniques designed to minimize collateral impact on surrounding persons and property. Violation of these rules may lead to enhanced penalties under subsections (b) through (d), which address aggravated offenses involving endangered lives or widespread disruption.\n\nFinally, the Accords Desk retains discretion to issue temporary restraining orders against repeat offenders or those posing imminent threats to public order. Such injunctions shall be served directly on the offending party and remain in effect until further notice or modification by a duly constituted tribunal. Disobedience thereof constitutes contempt of court and may result in imprisonment without bail under § 1204, unless stayed pending resolution of underlying charges.\n\nsee also §§ 200.5 (guest casters), 300.6 (safety standards), 705 (emergency procedures)\nunder § 1.8 (effective dates and savings clauses)",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "500.11",
            "200.1",
            "200.5",
            "1.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces and Liability for Damages",
          "text": "§ 500.11 Field Operations, Collateral Damage, and Indemnification Provisions\n\nUnsanctioned spellcasting conducted outside the confines of licensed laboratories or designated training grounds constitutes a prima facie violation of these Accords. While minor infractions may be addressed through administrative penalties, acts resulting in substantial property damage, personal injury, or ecological harm trigger full civil liability under this section. The caster shall remain liable for all consequential losses suffered by third parties due to unauthorized magical activity, regardless of intent or negligence.\n\nIn cases where collateral damage exceeds a threshold established by the Conservators Council, affected individuals may pursue direct suits against responsible casters without recourse to normal judicial channels. Such actions are expedited pursuant to Rule 103(b) and heard before a special tribunal comprised of senior Aegis Magi. Remedies available include full restitution for property losses, medical expenses, emotional distress damages, and punitive penalties designed to deter future infractions. The burden of proof rests upon the plaintiff to demonstrate causation between the unauthorized casting and their injuries or damages.\n\nFurthermore, any caster who knowingly facilitates or permits others to engage in unsanctioned spellcasting within their sphere of influence shall be held jointly and severally liable for resulting harms. This principle extends to property owners, business operators, and event organizers who fail to exercise reasonable care to prevent illegal magical activity on their premises. Failure to comply with this provision may result in suspension of all licenses issued to the responsible party until such time as adequate safeguards are put in place.\n\nThe Conservators reserve the right to seek injunctive relief against repeat offenders or those posing an imminent threat to public safety. Such orders may include mandatory seizure and destruction of illegal equipment, revocation of existing credentials, and prohibitions on future practice within specified jurisdictions. Contempt of court carries severe penalties including fines up to one hundred thousand gold pieces per violation and imprisonment without bail pending resolution of the underlying dispute.\n\nThese provisions are intended to balance the freedoms guaranteed under Part III with necessary protections for citizens and property owners across all realms. Compliance is mandatory, and failure to abide shall be treated as a material breach of contract subject to appropriate sanctions at the discretion of governing bodies. All casters are expected to exercise utmost diligence when operating in public spaces to avoid unnecessary harm or disruption to ordinary life.\n\nSee also § 200.6 (Construct Labor Agreements), § 400.8 (Emergency Spellcasting Protocols), and § 705(b)(iii) (Trespassing by Magical Means). Unauthorized spellcasting in public spaces remains strictly prohibited except under emergency conditions duly authorized by the Archmage Sovereign. Violators face severe consequences including permanent expulsion from the Guild and criminal prosecution under applicable laws. All members are urged to report suspected infractions promptly for prompt investigation and resolution in accordance with established procedures.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "500.11",
            "200.6",
            "400.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "300.12 Unsanctioned Field Operations and Liability",
          "text": "§ 500.12 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces and Liability for Damages\n\nThis Section governs the liabilities arising from unauthorized spellcasting within public spaces, including streets, parks, communal gathering areas, and other zones accessible to non-casters. It establishes a framework for assigning responsibility for damages caused by rogue magic users and outlines procedural mechanisms for restitution and punishment. The provisions herein complement those of § 500.6 regarding the general prohibition on unscheduled casting but extend specifically to scenarios where such acts result in tangible harm or disruption to third parties.\n\nWhere an individual casts a spell without prior authorization from the Mages' Guild Desk, any damages incurred by persons or property within range shall be presumed attributable to the casters actions unless rebutted by clear and convincing evidence of intervening causes or lack of causation under § 1.7. This presumption reflects the inherent danger posed by unregulated magical operations in densely populated environments and places a burden on unauthorized practitioners to demonstrate that their conduct did not contribute materially to the alleged harm. The standard for proof is intentionally stringent to deter reckless behavior and encourage compliance with licensing requirements set forth in Part 200.\n\nThe Desk retains discretion to impose additional penalties beyond mere restitution, including fines, suspension of future casting privileges, or revocation of existing licenses where appropriate. Such sanctions shall be calibrated according to the severity of the infraction, the degree of culpability demonstrated by the accused caster, and any prior disciplinary history on file. Guidelines for determining punitive measures are provided in § 500.14 and shall be applied consistently across all cases falling within this Section's scope.\n\nIn situations where multiple parties are implicated in a single incident involving unauthorized spellcasting, the Desk shall conduct a thorough investigation to establish individual degrees of fault before assigning liability proportionally among responsible actors. This approach ensures fairness while maintaining accountability for all participants in illicit magical activities. Failure to cooperate with investigations or provide relevant information may result in adverse inference findings under applicable rules of evidence codified elsewhere in this Codex.\n\nUltimately, the purpose of these provisions is to safeguard public safety and promote orderly management of arcane powers within society at large. By clearly delineating responsibilities for damages stemming from rogue magic use, the Mages' Guild seeks to minimize risks associated with unregulated spellcasting while preserving its role as arbiter of disputes arising therefrom. Adherence to these standards remains paramount in upholding both the letter and spirit of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "500.12",
            "500.6",
            "1.7",
            "500.14"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.11 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces and Liability for Damages",
          "text": "Whereas the practice of magic constitutes a potent force capable of altering reality itself, its application within public domains necessitates stringent safeguards to protect persons and property from inadvertent harm arising from uncontrolled arcane activity. Under this provision, any casting of spells, sigils, or other magical effects in areas accessible to third parties without explicit permission from the Mages' Guild Desk is deemed an act of negligence per se, irrespective of intent or outcome. The caster bears strict liability for all consequential damages resulting therefrom, including but not limited to structural degradation, environmental contamination, psychological trauma, and loss of bodily integrity suffered by nearby inhabitants or passersby.\n\nNotwithstanding the foregoing, certain exemptions shall apply where the unauthorized spellcasting was conducted solely for emergency stabilization efforts preventing imminent catastrophe, provided such actions were reasonably necessary under the circumstances and documented contemporaneously with full disclosure of all material facts to the Desk within forty-eight hours thereafter. Failure to comply with this reporting requirement shall constitute a separate violation subject to independent sanction without prejudice to any claims arising from actual harm suffered by affected parties. Furthermore, no defense based on contributory negligence or assumed risk shall be available to a caster charged under these rules, given the inherent dangers associated with unregulated magical experimentation in populated zones.\n\nThe burden of proof rests upon the aggrieved party to establish causation between the unauthorized casting and their alleged injuries by a preponderance of the evidence, though expert testimony regarding arcane phenomena may be admitted as relevant circumstantial fact. In cases involving widespread devastation or mass casualties, the Guild shall appoint an independent tribunal empowered to assess damages comprehensively and apportion responsibility among all implicated casters proportionally according to fault. All judgments rendered herein shall remain binding unless successfully appealed on procedural grounds within thirty days of final issuance by a superior court having territorial jurisdiction over the incident site.\n\nThis section operates independently from other provisions governing licensed spellcasting practices or private contractual arrangements, applying equally to both individuals and corporate entities engaged in commercial magical services. Nothing contained here shall be construed as waiving any common law rights or remedies available at law or equity for breach of duty causing personal injury or property damage under applicable statutes of limitations. Violations may result in civil penalties ranging from monetary fines commensurate with the extent of harm inflicted up to and including permanent revocation of professional licenses held by responsible parties.\n\n§ 500.11 shall be read together with § 200.6 regarding construct liability, § 700.9 governing burden of proof in arcane disputes, and § 800.8 concerning disposition of seized magical artifacts as evidence. Any ambiguities in application shall be resolved liberally in favor of protecting public safety interests absent compelling contrary indications from the legislative record or subsequent judicial precedent establishing otherwise through proper channels.\n\nsee § 200.6; see § 700.9; see § 800.8; see § 1.7 Aggregation of Conduct.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "500.11",
            "200.6",
            "700.9",
            "800.8",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces and Liability for Damages — Continued",
          "text": "§ 500.11 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces and Liability for Damages\nAny casting of magic within the boundaries of an inhabited hall, whether licensed or otherwise, shall be presumed to carry inherent risks that may result in property damage, personal injury, or broader societal harm. Accordingly, all casters are held strictly liable for any adverse consequences stemming from their unauthorized practice in public spaces. This liability extends beyond mere negligence and encompasses reckless disregard for the safety of others.\n\nTo mitigate these dangers, each hall shall designate specific zones where spellcasting is permitted without restriction, known as \"Safe Caster Zones.\" All other areas shall be considered \"No-Cast Zones,\" unless explicitly authorized by written waiver from the affected parties. Violation of this zoning requirement constitutes a prima facie case for civil suit and potential criminal prosecution under § 200.6(a).\n\nFurthermore, any damages arising from unauthorized casting shall be assessed according to general principles of tort law, with additional penalties imposed for willful misconduct. This includes but is not limited to compensatory awards for property loss, medical expenses, emotional distress, and punitive damages at the court's discretion. In extreme cases involving gross negligence or malice, permanent disqualification from practicing magic within that hall may be warranted under § 200.6(b).\n\nThe burden of proof rests squarely upon the casting individual to demonstrate that their actions did not cause harm or that any resulting injury was unforeseeable and unavoidable despite reasonable precautions taken. Failure to meet this heavy burden shall result in automatic liability for all claimed losses, regardless of actual fault. This strict standard applies equally to both professional arcanists and amateur enthusiasts alike.\n\nIt is the solemn duty of every hallmaster to enforce these regulations diligently and report any violations promptly to local authorities. Negligence in oversight may lead to revocation of operating privileges under § 100.7(c). Conversely, failure to pursue legitimate claims against offending casters shall be deemed a waiver of further recourse by the aggrieved parties.\n\nBy enacting this provision, the Guild seeks to balance the rights of individuals to practice their craft with the fundamental obligation to protect public welfare. Through clear guidelines and robust accountability measures, we foster an environment where magical arts can flourish safely and responsibly for generations to come.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "500.11",
            "200.6",
            "100.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces and Liability for Damages — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "The Desk further stipulates that any act of unauthorized spellcasting occurring within public spaces shall constitute a prima facie case of negligence, placing the burden upon the caster to demonstrate the absence of foreseeable harm under § 500.6(a). This provision extends beyond mere technical violations to encompass substantive consequences arising from reckless magical experimentation in populated areas, as affirmed by precedent in *Veyra v. Brightspark*, 142 A.M.G. 89 (Autumnwood 347). Under this rule, the caster bears strict liability for any injuries or property damage proximately caused by their actions, regardless of intent or malice, pursuant to § 500.6(b). Exceptions are narrowly construed and limited solely to cases where emergency circumstances necessitate immediate intervention to prevent greater harm, subject to subsequent judicial review per § 500.6(c). The Desk emphasizes that the public interest in safety outweighs any claimed rights of free expression or research freedom when weighed against tangible risks to persons and property, echoing foundational principles laid out in §§ 1.4–1.9 regarding the balance between individual liberty and communal welfare. Furthermore, this liability regime applies irrespective of whether the caster holds a valid license at the time of the incident, as licensing status is irrelevant to determining fault in such scenarios under § 200.3(d). The Desk urges all practitioners to exercise extreme caution when conducting magical operations outside designated facilities, remembering that their actions carry profound implications for public welfare. Failure to adhere to these mandates may result in severe penalties, including suspension of privileges and civil suits brought by affected parties, as outlined in §§ 500.6(d)–(e). The Desk reserves the right to amend this rule as needed to address emerging threats or technological advancements, maintaining ultimate authority over regulatory interpretation under § 100.2(a)(iii). Practitioners are reminded that vigilance and responsibility remain paramount in safeguarding both themselves and society from unintended consequences of unchecked magical prowess. See also §§ 500.7, 600.10(c), 800.4(b)(ii) for complementary provisions governing public safety protocols and compensation mechanisms applicable to similar contexts.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "500.6",
            "1.4",
            "200.3",
            "100.2",
            "500.7"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "NEW section §500.11 in this same Part only. Invent a DISTINCT catchline that belongs with these siblings, not a new subject and NOT 'Related Requirements': § 500.1 Field Casting for Value; § 500.2 Unscheduled Weather; § 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion; § 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration; § 500.5 Love Philtre and Undue Influence; § 500.6 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces; § 500.7 Public Safety — Additional Protocols; § 500.8 Compensation and Debt Settlement; § 500.9 Unlicensed Artisanry; § 500.10 Compensation and Indemnification. First line must be: PAGE Your New Distinct Catchline (5-10 words, specific, no 'Related Requirements')",
