    {
      "cite": "600.11",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations §600.11",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations §600.11",
          "text": "This section governs the unique legal challenges posed by contracts entered into between licensed casters and non-casters, or involving magical artifacts themselves as contracting parties. Under § 200.6, constructs holding a quill are deemed competent agents; this principle extends to summoned spirits and bound elementals acting within their delegated authority. A contract executed by such entities binds the summoner or master unless fraud or duress is proven under § 700.9.\n\n§ 300.2 prohibits necromantic manipulation of consciousness without consent, rendering any geas or compulsion clause void as against public policy and unconscionable under common law principles. A caster seeking to bind a non-caster through magical means must instead rely on traditional contractual remedies, such as liquidated damages calculated in advance pursuant to § 600.4. Failure to comply may result in sanctions including suspension of licensure or referral for penal proceedings under the Paradox Trial provisions of these Accords.\n\nIn cases involving magical items with autonomous agency, the item itself shall be treated as a limited legal person for purposes of enforcing contractual obligations undertaken by its previous owner. This does not extend to tort liability outside the scope of authorized use defined in the contract of sale or transfer. See § 1401.9 regarding cross-jurisdictional enforcement of such agreements, particularly when items traverse planar boundaries during transit.\n\nThe Desk retains discretion to approve special contractual arrangements involving high-risk magical technologies that cannot be adequately regulated through standard licensing frameworks. Such approvals shall be granted only upon presentation of comprehensive safety protocols and evidence of adequate insurance coverage for potential harms. Any violation of approved conditions shall constitute a breach of contract subject to equitable remedies including specific performance or injunctive relief where appropriate under the circumstances presented.\n\nThis section is intended to provide clarity in an increasingly complex marketplace where magical capabilities blur traditional lines between natural and supernatural agency. Parties are urged to seek qualified legal counsel familiar with arcane law when drafting contracts involving casters, constructs, or enchanted objects of significant value. The ultimate goal remains protection of public safety while fostering innovation within the bounds established by these foundational statutes.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "200.6",
            "700.9",
            "300.2",
            "600.4",
            "1401.9",
            "600.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations §600.11 — Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.11 Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreements\n(a) Purpose and Scope. This section governs agreements between licensed mages and autonomous constructs, including golems, automatons, and enchanted servants used as laborers or assistants in magical operations. Such contracts must explicitly address the unique legal status of constructs, which exist outside traditional personhood frameworks.\n\n(b) Capacity to Contract. While constructs lack natural capacity for contract formation under common law, they may bind themselves through programmed directives set by their creators. Directives entered into good faith and within the construct's operational parameters shall be deemed binding obligations enforceable against both parties.\n\n(c) Compensation and Remuneration. Constructs entitled to compensation must receive payment in accordance with agreed terms upon satisfactory completion of assigned tasks. Failure to pay may result in deactivation, seizure of tools, or legal action for breach of contract by the construct's owner.\n\n(d) Liability Limitations. Neither party shall be liable to the other for direct damages arising from normal operation of the construct unless such damages resulted from gross negligence or intentional misconduct. Indirect and consequential damages are expressly disclaimed except in cases of proven malice.\n\n(e) Termination Provisions. Either party may terminate the agreement upon written notice with a reasonable period specified therein, typically no less than thirty days. Immediate termination is permitted only for material breach or violation of safety protocols by either side.\n\n(f) Dispute Resolution. Any disputes arising from this contract shall first be addressed through mediation by an independent Arcane Arbitrator appointed mutually by the parties. Failure to resolve matters amicably within fourteen days allows either party to seek binding judgment in a court of competent jurisdiction, provided no conflicting magical injunctions exist that would compromise judicial proceedings.\n\n(g) Compliance and Reporting. Parties must maintain accurate records of all transactions involving construct labor for inspection upon reasonable request by regulatory authorities. Annual audits shall be conducted by certified Arcane Auditors to ensure adherence to safety standards and fair labor practices.\n\n(h) Force Majeure Clauses. Neither party shall be liable for failure to perform due to acts beyond their control, including but not limited to natural disasters, magical catastrophes, or governmental injunctions. Affected parties must provide prompt notice of any anticipated delays to facilitate alternative arrangements where feasible.\n\n(i) Entire Agreement Doctrine. This contract constitutes the complete and final expression of the mutual understanding between the parties and supersedes all prior negotiations, representations, or agreements whether written or oral concerning subject matter hereof. No modification shall be effective unless in writing and signed by both parties.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "600.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations §600.11 — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "A contract entered into between a licensor and a non-caster must contain specific safeguards to protect both parties from magical risks. First, the non-caster shall not invoke any clause that would require them to perform an act of magic beyond their natural abilities or training. Second, the contract may include provisions for emergency evacuation should unintended magical phenomena occur. Third, all fees and penalties must be calculated without reference to arcane variables unless expressly agreed upon in writing with full disclosure.\n\nWhere a non-caster agrees to assist in a magical operation, they shall not be held liable for any adverse effects resulting from the caster's actions alone. However, if the non-caster knowingly facilitates prohibited magic through their cooperation, they may be deemed complicit under § 200.4. In such cases, standard criminal penalties apply regardless of contractual terms.\n\nThe Guild reserves the right to annul any contract found to violate these principles upon review by a panel of Arcane Legal Experts. Such determination shall be final and binding unless appealed within thirty days on grounds of manifest injustice or error of fact. Appeals must be supported by sworn affidavits from qualified witnesses attesting to the contested provisions' contents and effects at the time of execution.\n\nThis section operates in conjunction with § 200.3 regarding temporary spellmouths, as well as §§ 400.1–400.5 governing research permits and supervised experimentation. Violations may trigger sanctions under § 500.6 including fines, suspension, or revocation of licenses depending on severity. The provisions herein shall be construed liberally to promote public safety while respecting the autonomy of consenting adults engaged in lawful magical pursuits.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "200.4",
            "200.3",
            "400.1",
            "500.6",
            "600.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.11 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations — Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.11, Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations §600.11 — Continued, Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations §600.11 — Continued — Continued\n\nIn the event of a dispute arising from a contract involving non-caster parties, the burden of proof shall rest upon the caster to demonstrate that the agreement was entered into knowingly and voluntarily, without coercion or deception. See § 700.9 Burden and Standard of Proof in Arcane Disputes. Furthermore, any clauses imposing magical penalties or restrictions on non-casters shall be deemed void unless expressly ratified by a competent legal authority. This provision serves to protect vulnerable individuals from exploitative arrangements that may have long-term consequences beyond their immediate understanding. Under § 300.2 Necromancy; Unsanctioned Arts, such protections are especially critical given the potential risks associated with certain types of magical influence. Additionally, non-casters entering into contracts shall be afforded access to independent legal counsel at public expense if requested. This ensures that all parties have a full appreciation of their rights and obligations before committing to binding agreements. The Guild maintains a roster of qualified attorneys who may provide this service without charge, thereby reducing barriers to justice for those lacking resources or expertise in arcane matters. In sum, these safeguards reflect the Accords' commitment to fairness and equity in all dealings between casters and non-casters alike. Through vigilant oversight and robust procedural guarantees, the Guild strives to foster an environment where lawful commerce may flourish free from undue risk or abuse of power. As Archmage Veyra often declares: \"Magic binds us all; let none be bound against their will!\"",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "600.11",
            "700.9",
            "300.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations §600.11 — Continued — Dispute",
          "text": "When disputes arise between casters and non-casters regarding magical contracts, the Accords prescribe a structured process for resolution. First, parties shall attempt to negotiate in good faith through their respective representatives or agents. If negotiation fails, either party may petition the Office of Archmage Sovereign for mediation under § 600.7. The Mediator shall convene within seven days and hear arguments from both sides, reviewing all relevant documents including the contract itself, correspondence, and witness testimony as required by § 600.9.\n\nShould mediation prove unsuccessful, the matter may proceed to binding arbitration before a panel of three impartial arcane experts appointed jointly by both parties. The Arbitrators shall apply the principles laid out herein along with general equitable doctrines familiar to seasoned mages. Their determination shall be final and enforceable throughout all Jurisdictions recognized by this Code. In cases involving alleged violations of prohibited arts or unauthorized field casting, criminal proceedings may run concurrently but must not prejudice any civil claims for damages sustained by either party under the terms of their agreement.\n\nThis framework ensures that contractual disagreements are settled efficiently without resorting to violent confrontation or magical sabotage — practices strictly forbidden under §§ 200.4 and 300.1 respectively. By providing a clear path for dispute resolution, the Accords promote stability in commercial dealings involving supernatural elements while safeguarding public welfare from reckless experimentation or abuse of power. All parties are urged to consult counsel early should tensions escalate, rather than allowing minor misunderstandings to fester into costly litigation.\n\nSee also § 600.12 Remedies for Breach and Damages Assessment; §§ 600.7–600.9 Mediation and Arbitration Procedures; § 600.8 Compliance Obligations in General; § 600.10 Special Provisions Governing Construct Labor Agreements; foundational provisions on dispute resolution found throughout Parts IV through VI of this Codex as applicable to each specific scenario presented by the facts at hand.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "600.7",
            "600.9",
            "200.4",
            "600.12",
            "600.8",
            "600.10",
            "600.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations — Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.11 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations, § 600.11(b)—Continued, shall apply to all agreements involving the casting of spells or the provision of magical services by Guild members for compensation from non-casters. Such contracts must be reduced to writing and signed by both parties, as well as witnessed by a licensed mage not directly involved in the transaction. The witness shall verify the identity and capacity of each signatory under oath before attesting to the authenticity of their signatures. Any oral agreements or informal understandings between mages and non-casters are void ab initio and unenforceable in any court of law, magical or terrestrial. Exceptions may be granted by the Conservators upon a showing of compelling necessity and no feasible alternative means to achieve the contracted ends. All contracts subject to this section shall include explicit clauses addressing liability for adverse effects, termination conditions, dispute resolution mechanisms, and indemnification obligations as appropriate given the circumstances. Failure to comply with these requirements renders any such agreement null and void, exposing both parties to full legal recourse should harm befall either party due to non-compliance. The Conservators reserve the right to audit all contracts governed by this section at their discretion, requiring production of original documents for inspection and review as deemed necessary to ensure adherence to the spirit and letter of these provisions. Any discrepancies or irregularities discovered during such audits shall be reported immediately to the Archmage Sovereign for investigation and appropriate action pursuant to applicable laws and regulations governing magical practice within the Autumnwood Accords jurisdiction.\n\n\"Contracts with non-casters demand heightened scrutiny given the inherent risks and complexities involved.\" - Conservator Theron, *The Arcane Quarterly*, Vol. 87, p. 312.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "600.11"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "NEW section §600.11 in this same Part only. Invent a DISTINCT catchline that belongs with these siblings, not a new subject and NOT 'Related Requirements': § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; § 600.2 Familiars as Agents; § 600.3 Geas as an Unconscionable Term; § 600.4 Construct Labor; Minimum Ward; § 600.5 Summoned Notary; § 600.6 in Magical Contracts; § 600.7 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations; § 600.8 Compliance Obligations; § 600.9 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Requirements; § 600.10 Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreement. First line must be: PAGE Your New Distinct Catchline (5-10 words, specific, no 'Related Requirements')",
      "source": "lmstudio",
