    {
      "cite": "1101.11",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.11 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion",
          "text": "In the event that a gifted item or artifact bears residual magical influences from its previous owners or creators, such taints shall be deemed part of the collateral's inherent risk profile unless otherwise disclosed in writing. The recipient accepts all risks associated with potential adverse effects stemming from said taints, including but not limited to psychological distress, physical ailments, and unexplained phenomena. No claim for damages arising therefrom shall lie against the transferor except where such conditions were knowingly concealed or actively misrepresented.\n\nFurthermore, any attempt to deliberately alter an item's magical signature without full disclosure constitutes material breach of warranty and may subject the perpetrator to additional penalties under applicable statutes governing fraud and deceptive practices. The burden rests upon the transferee to investigate thoroughly before acquisition whenever suspicion arises that a product may harbor hidden taints or anomalies beyond ordinary wear and tear.\n\nThis provision operates alongside, but is distinct from, general rules regarding latent defects in commercial transactions. It recognizes the unique nature of enchanted goods where past usage can leave indelible marks upon their very essence, affecting future performance in ways not readily apparent on inspection alone. By spelling out these responsibilities explicitly, the Accords seek to minimize disputes over blame allocation when mishap occurs downstream from sale or transfer.\n\nIn sum, each party agrees to act prudently given what they know—or should reasonably know—about an item's history prior to consummating any deal involving it. Failure so to do invites liability for resulting harms under principles of negligence and strict liability as appropriate. These guidelines aim to foster transparency while acknowledging the mysterious qualities inherent in all magic-infused objects.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.12 Transferability and Inheritance of Magical Property",
          "text": "Magical artifacts, regardless of their classification or inherent risks, may be freely transferred between persons subject to the same limitations that govern ordinary chattel. Ownership vests solely in the holder of title at any given moment; prior possessors retain no equitable interest unless expressly reserved by contract. Upon death or incapacity, magical items pass according to standard succession laws, with no special provisions for arcane goods absent specific familial pacts. However, certain categories of restricted artifacts may require additional consents from regulatory bodies before being conveyed out of original jurisdiction, particularly if they contain residual wards or binding clauses tied to their previous owners' identities. Such transfer protocols are detailed in subsequent subsections addressing the nuances of magical provenance and liability allocation.\n\nFILED CITE INDEX: § 1101.12 Transferability and Inheritance of Magical Property; § 1101.13 Restricted Conveyances; § 1101.14 Provenance and Disclosure Obligations; § 1101.15 Liability for Unintended Effects; see also §§ 1.6, 1.7, 200.1-200.5, 900.11, 1301.9",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1101.12",
            "1101.13",
            "1101.14",
            "1101.15",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.7 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion",
          "text": "The transfer or possession of magical items may invoke latent effects unrelated to their primary function, known as collateral taints. Such phenomena arise from residual enchantments, ambient field distortions, or the inherent nature of certain materials. Under § 1.6, definitions include \"taint\" as any unwanted magical influence affecting a person or object, and \"contagion\" as the spread of such effects between entities. See also § 300.5 regarding residual charges on spell components.\n\nParties acquiring magical property must investigate potential taints through approved diagnostic rites before taking possession. Failure to do so shifts liability under § 900.11, which mandates disclosure of known hazards prior to transaction. The burden of proof lies with the transferor to demonstrate due diligence in cleansing or isolating dangerous residues. Where ambiguity exists, courts shall apply the doctrine of caveat emptor unless fraud is proven, per § 700.6 on witness testimony standards.\n\nMagical contagion may manifest physically, mentally, or spiritually, causing illness, psychosis, possession, or even death in extreme cases. Protective wards and containment protocols are prescribed by § 200.5 for guest casters operating outside their home halls. Violation of these safeguards constitutes negligence under § 800.11 and may result in punitive sanctions including seal placement. Conservators argue stricter controls are necessary to prevent catastrophe, while Innovators contend such measures stifle research progress unjustifiably.\n\nRecent precedents include the Veyra Affair (§ 400.6), where improper handling of a forbidden relic caused widespread hallucinations among nearby residents. Similarly, the Brightspark Incident (§ 500.9) saw an unauthorized field experiment trigger localized time dilation effects. These events underscore the urgent need for comprehensive guidelines governing accidental taint exposure and remediation procedures. Future amendments may expand upon these principles as experience grows.\n\nIn sum, § 1101.7 establishes a framework for managing unintended magical consequences arising from property transfers. By codifying duties of care, liability allocation, and safety protocols, the Accords aim to balance innovation with public protection—a delicate equilibrium central to the Guild's mission under Archmage Veyra's stewardship. Further elaboration will appear in subsequent sections addressing specific applications of this foundational concept.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "300.5",
            "900.11",
            "700.6",
            "200.5",
            "800.11",
            "400.6",
            "500.9",
            "1101.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.11 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion — Continued",
          "text": "The concept of magical taint extends beyond the immediate object or area affected by forbidden spellwork to encompass persons, places, and items with which they come into contact through normal interaction. This phenomenon, known as collateral contagion, occurs when residual arcane energy adheres to surfaces, fabrics, or biological tissue following unauthorized casting activities. While minor traces of taint typically dissipate over time without consequence, significant concentrations may persist indefinitely unless actively purged through proper ritual cleansing procedures.\n\nParties engaging in unsanctioned magical practices bear the burden of mitigating potential contamination risks inherent in their operations. Failure to take reasonable precautions against spread of taint shall be deemed gross negligence per se under § 1401.6, exposing violators to enhanced penalties including mandatory remediation costs and permanent restriction on future casting privileges within affected jurisdictions. The Desk retains discretionary authority to order full-scale environmental assessments whenever collateral contamination is suspected, with findings binding upon all concerned parties regardless of fault determination.\n\nSpecial provisions apply where taint affects vulnerable populations such as children, the elderly, or those with compromised magical resistance. In such cases, preventive measures must be implemented proactively rather than reactively, even absent evidence of actual harm. The Desk may impose affirmative duties on property owners and occupants to screen incoming goods, services, and individuals for potential vectors of contamination prior to permitting entry into sensitive zones designated as sanctuary areas under § 200.8.\n\nCollateral taint disputes shall be resolved through expedited summary proceedings unless contested by affidavit sworn before a Justice of the Peace. The burden rests upon the accuser to establish prima facie evidence of exposure, after which the accused must demonstrate either absence of causation or successful mitigation efforts. Failure to meet this heightened evidentiary standard will result in adverse inference against the party failing to comply with procedural requirements set forth herein.\n\nThis section stands as a reminder that the reach of magical pollution knows no bounds and may manifest far beyond initial point of origin. Prudent stewardship demands vigilance against both obvious and subtle signs of contamination, lest unwitting actors become vectors for spreading the deleterious effects of illicit sorcery throughout their communities. The Accords stand ready to assist those willing to confront this pervasive threat with honesty and resolve.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1401.6",
            "200.8",
            "1101.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.7 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion — Continued",
          "text": "The doctrine of collateral taint extends beyond the immediate object of an arcane transaction to encompass all property interests incidentally affected by its execution. When a magical operation alters the fundamental nature or spiritual integrity of a real or personal estate, subsequent claimants may seek redress for resulting contamination, regardless of privity with the original caster. See § 1101.6 regarding unintended consequences arising from ritualistic manipulation.\n\nThis principle finds particular application in cases involving land parcels consecrated through ley line intersections or ancient burial rites. Even after severance and commercial disposal, residual enchantments may manifest as geopathic stressors affecting future occupants' health and productivity. Affected parties are entitled to compensation for diminution of value attributable solely to magical taint, absent voluntary assumption of risk. Compare § 900.11 imposing liability on unauthorized field casters for proximate harms.\n\nIn matters of personal chattels imbued with sentient or semi-sentient qualities, ownership transfer does not automatically sever the entity's lingering bonds to its prior possessor. Guardianship rights may persist despite formal conveyance, granting original owners standing to contest disposition terms deemed detrimental to the item's welfare. Such disputes require proof of both ongoing attachment and material injury flowing therefrom under § 700.6 standards for expert testimony.\n\nThe burden rests upon aggrieved litigants to establish causal links between the magical operation and alleged collateral damage with reasonable particularity. Generalized assertions of spiritual discomfort or vague misfortunes lack evidentiary merit absent corroborating data from qualified appraisers. See § 800.9 prohibiting speculative claims lacking factual foundation. Nevertheless, courts retain discretion to order remedial measures—including ritual cleansing or structural reinforcement—when necessary to mitigate lasting harm. Under § 500.11 provisions for indemnification against unforeseen liabilities.\n\nUltimately, the law recognizes that magic operates according to its own immutable rules, sometimes producing effects wholly divorced from human intent or control. Sound commercial practice demands thorough vetting of any proposed spellwork for potential downstream impacts on existing title interests. Failure to conduct adequate due diligence may leave parties exposed to substantial losses once latent taints begin surfacing months or years later. Prudence dictates engaging licensed mages versed in both ritual mechanics and property law before committing resources to transformative enchantments. Caution advised as per § 300.11 scheduling requirements for classified arts.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1101.6",
            "900.11",
            "700.6",
            "800.9",
            "500.11",
            "300.11",
            "1101.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.11 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "The doctrine of collateral taints extends beyond mere physical contamination to include metaphysical pollution that may affect the usability or value of magical items. Under § 1101.7, any article imbued with residual enchantments from unauthorized use shall be deemed \"tainted\" until properly cleansed by a licensed restorer. The burden of proving such taint rests upon the party asserting it under § 1101.8; absent clear evidence, items are presumed functional per § 200.6.\n\nMagical contagion may arise from improper disposal or storage of enchanted objects, leading to unpredictable effects ranging from minor malfunctions to catastrophic failures. Parties seeking compensation for losses stemming from such incidents must demonstrate proximate causation between the contaminated item and their damages per § 1301.9. The Guild reserves authority to order mandatory decontamination procedures at the expense of responsible parties under § 400.11.\n\nThis provision complements existing safeguards regarding the safe handling and disposal of magical artifacts outlined in Part V, subsection C. Failure to comply with these requirements may result in disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure per § 200.4. The provisions herein are intended to protect both practitioners and the public from potential hazards posed by poorly maintained enchanted goods.\n\nFor further guidance on proper artifact maintenance and disposal protocols, consult the official Guild Safety Manual, Section IV-E. Any disputes arising from allegations of taint or contagion shall be adjudicated according to standard arbitration procedures outlined in § 700.9. Parties are strongly encouraged to obtain expert appraisal before attempting remediation efforts themselves.\n\n§ 1101.11 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion — Continued; see also §§ 200.6, 400.11, 700.9, 1301.9 under § 1101.8 Burden of Proof and Presumptions in Magic Item Disputes\"",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1101.7",
            "1101.8",
            "200.6",
