    {
      "cite": "1201.11",
      "part": "part_xii",
      "title": "Extraterritorial Jurisdiction and Planar Tolls",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§1201.11 Extraterritorial Jurisdiction and Planar Tolls",
          "text": "Nothing in these Accords shall be construed to limit the jurisdictional authority of the Autumnwood Hall over all lawful magical activities conducted within its purview, regardless of the physical location where such activities occur. This principle extends beyond mere territorial boundaries into the metaphysical realm, encompassing acts committed through portals, rifts, or other planar conduits that originate from Guild-regulated spaces. The rationale underlying this expansive interpretation rests upon the premise that magic itself constitutes an extension of the caster's will and therefore remains subject to regulatory oversight wherever it manifests. To permit otherwise would create dangerous loopholes whereby irresponsible practitioners could evade accountability by simply transposing their operations into unregulated zones. Consequently, any magical act initiated from within Guild jurisdiction retains full legal significance even if its effects propagate across dimensional barriers. The Desk reserves the right to investigate and sanction such acts as though they had been committed in plain sight, recognizing that the source of illicit power ultimately determines culpability rather than the mere geography of its impact. This approach ensures comprehensive enforcement while respecting the unique nature of arcane phenomena which cannot be fully contained by conventional spatial demarcations. Any attempts to circumvent these provisions through technicalities regarding location will fail as a matter of law, given the overarching intent to maintain public safety and uphold the integrity of the magical profession. The Desk shall exercise its discretion freely in determining whether extraterritorial conduct warrants disciplinary action under this section, weighing factors such as proximity to Guild premises at initiation, potential for harm, and evidence of premeditated evasion of regulations. Such determinations will be made on a case-by-case basis with full consideration given to the specific circumstances surrounding each instance of alleged misconduct. It is hereby declared that no practitioner shall claim immunity from jurisdiction simply by exploiting the complexities of planar travel as a means of obfuscation, for such behavior undermines the fundamental authority of the Autumnwood Accords and threatens the stability of magical society writ large. The Desk stands ready to adjudicate disputes arising from this provision with wisdom tempered by firmness, ensuring that no stone remains unturned in its pursuit of justice. Let it be known that the reach of these laws extends far beyond mortal eyesight into the very fabric of reality itself.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1201.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.11 Transit Stamps and Planar Tolls",
          "text": "Section 1201 establishes that the Autumnwood Accords extend beyond physical borders, binding all who traverse the mists between worlds. To facilitate safe passage while safeguarding Guild interests, two mechanisms apply: transit stamps and planar tolls. A transit stamp is an official mark affixed to a caster's license upon entry into or exit from Planespace, verifying compliance with applicable laws during that journey. Failure to display a valid stamp may result in detention for inspection under § 1201.6.\n\nPlanar tolls represent fees levied by the Hall of Sovereignty on entities utilizing interdimensional gateways. These charges defray costs associated with maintaining portal integrity and monitoring traffic. Non-payment renders the gateway inaccessible until obligations are satisfied, regardless of urgency. Exceptions exist for emergency evacuations approved under § 1201.4.\n\nCrucially, a transit stamp does not constitute a waiver of extraterritorial jurisdiction. Casters remain subject to Accords provisions wherever they may be, including remote reaches of the ethereal realm. The Guild reserves the right to pursue violations across all planes without prejudice. Conversely, lawful activity conducted abroad retains immunity from domestic prosecution absent specific treaty arrangements.\n\nThe Desk emphasizes that these measures are not burdens but protections for both travelers and host nations. By adhering to transit protocols and settling tolls promptly, casters ensure orderly flow of magical commerce while minimizing risk of accidental contamination or conflict. Those who disregard them invite swift sanctions under relevant sections herein. Thus stands the balance struck between openness and control—a delicate equilibrium requiring vigilance from all parties concerned with the sanctity of Planespace itself.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1201.6",
            "1201.4",
            "1201.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.7 Planar Sovereignty and Jurisdictional Boundaries",
          "text": "Part XII, Section 1201 governs the complex legalities surrounding arcane transit and extraterritorial magics. Subsection 1201.8 specifically addresses the concept of planar sovereignty and its implications for Guild jurisdiction. Under this framework, each magical realm or pocket dimension possesses inherent territorial integrity that must be respected by all casters utilizing such spaces. This principle stems from the foundational belief that magic itself constitutes a distinct domain with its own rules of engagement.\n\nThe Desk has established clear protocols to uphold these sovereignty rights. When a caster enters an uncharted plane, they are deemed subject only to the laws and customs native to that realm until exiting or being intercepted by enforcement agents. This temporary suspension of standard jurisdiction prevents conflicts between overlapping magical authorities while maintaining order within each unique environment. Violations of planar norms may result in immediate sanctions without recourse to normal appellate processes.\n\nFurthermore, subsection 1201.8 mandates that all casters carry valid transit permits when traversing any non-standard dimension. These documents serve as proof of licensure and compliance with safety regulations applicable to interplanar travel. Failure to produce such credentials upon request constitutes prima facie evidence of suspicious activity warranting further investigation by the Aegis Magi. The burden rests squarely on the individual caster to demonstrate lawful intent and proper authorization for their chosen route.\n\nIn cases where multiple jurisdictions claim authority over a shared plane, disputes shall be resolved through diplomatic channels between relevant Hall representatives. Until such time as amicable agreements are reached, all casters must adhere strictly to the most restrictive set of rules among competing authorities. This precautionary approach minimizes potential conflicts arising from differing legal standards across adjacent territories. The Desk reserves the right to modify these guidelines as necessary in response to evolving magical conditions and geopolitical realities.\n\nUltimately, the preservation of planar sovereignty serves the greater good by ensuring safe passage through dangerous realms while preventing misuse of powerful artifacts or phenomena. By establishing clear boundaries of jurisdiction, the Accords enable responsible exploration without compromising core principles of arcane stewardship. All members of the Guild are charged with upholding these vital tenets regardless of personal gain or convenience. Only thus can we maintain harmony across all dimensions under the watchful eye of Archmage Veyra and her trusted advisors.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1201.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.9 Planar Transit and Jurisdictional Overlap",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords recognize that the unique geography of planar travel creates complex legal scenarios not contemplated by conventional spatial law. When a caster traverses from one plane to another, they may inadvertently subject themselves to multiple overlapping jurisdictions simultaneously. This section clarifies how such conflicts shall be resolved under the authority of this Codex.\n\nFirstly, it is established that no single Hall possesses absolute dominion over all planar spaces accessible via conventional magic. Rather, each portal and transit route falls within a specific jurisdictional zone defined by its geographic coordinates and magical signature. The Desk maintains comprehensive records of these zones to facilitate dispute resolution. When multiple claims arise regarding the same location, they shall be adjudicated according to principles of first-come-first-served rights, subject to applicable treaties between Halls.\n\nSecondly, the Accords expressly prohibit any Hall from asserting extraterritorial authority beyond its designated zone without explicit consent from affected parties. Unauthorized enforcement actions taken outside these boundaries are deemed void ab initio and may result in disciplinary sanctions against the offending party. This limitation protects both casters and rival Halls from abusive claims of power based solely on proximity to a portal.\n\nThirdly, special provisions apply to situations where planar transit results in unintended consequences affecting multiple jurisdictions. In such cases, responsible parties must promptly notify all affected Halls and cooperate fully with any ensuing investigations or remediation efforts. Failure to do so constitutes grounds for enhanced penalties under the relevant sections of this Code.\n\nFinally, the Desk retains ultimate discretion to modify jurisdictional boundaries as necessary to accommodate changing magical phenomena or diplomatic agreements between Halls. All casters are charged with staying apprised of such updates through official channels and complying therewith without question. Resistance to lawful directives may result in severe consequences for all involved.\n\nBy adhering to these guidelines, the Guild ensures orderly navigation of planar space while minimizing disputes arising from territorial ambiguities. The well-being of all casters depends upon collective observance of these critical principles.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1201.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.6 Planar Transit and Jurisdictional Overlap",
          "text": "This Section governs the complex legal framework surrounding travel between realms and the overlapping jurisdictions that may arise therefrom. Under § 1201.3, any individual or entity casting a spell with extraterritorial effects must file a Diet Chamber application prior to execution. Failure to do so renders such acts void ab initio under § 100.5. Furthermore, § 1201.4 prohibits the sale of cartographic data as a substitute for official permits; all maps depicting portal locations or planar gateways must be registered with the Office of Archmage Sovereign before distribution (§ 300.6).\n\nThe concept of \"integrated obligations\" outlined in § 1201.5 applies here, requiring that duties incurred across multiple jurisdictions be fulfilled according to the most stringent standards. For example, a mage licensed under Autumnwood Accords but operating within Iron Legion territory must comply with both sets of regulations (§ 400.8). This principle ensures consistent enforcement regardless of location and prevents regulatory arbitrage (§ 700.12).\n\nPlanar sovereignty boundaries are delineated in § 1201.7, which establishes that no single Hall may claim dominion over an entire dimension without consensus from all other stakeholders. Violations thereof constitute a Class A felony under § 800.4. Additionally, § 1201.9 mandates that all transit agreements explicitly waive conflicting claims to planar space, thereby eliminating disputes over territorial rights (§ 500.7). These provisions collectively maintain orderly interrealms commerce and minimize the risk of accidental magical catastrophes (§ 900.3).\n\nIn sum, § 1201 serves as a critical safeguard against the dangers inherent in planar travel and casting beyond one's native realm. By imposing rigorous prerequisites for extraterritorial activity, this Section protects both individual casters and the wider community from unintended consequences of their actions (§ 1401.5). Compliance with these mandates is non-negotiable for all members of the Arcane Community engaged in cross-dimensional pursuits (§ 200.9).",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1201.3",
            "100.5",
            "1201.4",
            "300.6",
            "1201.5",
            "400.8",
            "700.12",
            "1201.7",
            "800.4",
            "1201.9",
            "500.7",
            "900.3",
            "1401.5",
            "200.9",
            "1201.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.11 Transit Stamps and Planar Tolls — Continued",
          "text": "All casters departing the Autumnwood domain via recognized portals must affix an official transit stamp to their license prior to crossing jurisdictional boundaries. Failure to do so constitutes prima facie evidence of unauthorized field operations under § 900.11. The stamp certifies that the caster has paid all applicable planar tolls and holds valid insurance coverage for potential damages incurred abroad. Planar tolls are assessed based on distance traveled, duration of stay, and the potency of magic employed during transit. Unpaid tolls result in immediate revocation of the caster's right to return until settlement is made in full.\n\nCasters operating outside recognized portals assume all risks associated with unregulated travel and bear sole liability for any violations committed while abroad. The Guild assumes no responsibility for disputes arising from unauthorized crossings, except as expressly provided herein. Planar sovereignty remains vested with the local governing body of each destination realm, unless otherwise stipulated by treaty or mutual agreement between the parties involved. Any conflicts regarding jurisdictional boundaries shall be resolved through diplomatic channels and binding arbitration in accordance with established precedent.\n\nThe Desk reserves the right to inspect transit stamps at random intervals as part of routine compliance audits. Casters refusing such inspection may be deemed guilty of evading regulatory oversight and face severe penalties, including permanent disqualification from Guild membership. Transit stamps are non-transferable and expire annually upon renewal. Lost or damaged licenses must be reported immediately and replaced at the caster's own expense after verification of good standing is completed.\n\n§ 1201.11 Transit Stamps and Planar Tolls — see also § 900.11 Duty to File Before Casting, § 800.6 Confiscation Procedures for Unlawful Casters, and § 400.5 Unauthorized Field Operations Prohibited. Any questions regarding this provision should be directed to the Office of Archmage Sovereign or its duly appointed representatives. This section supersedes any contrary provisions in previous agreements unless explicitly amended by subsequent action taken pursuant to these Accords.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.11",
            "1201.11",
            "800.6",
            "400.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "NEW section §1201.11 in this same Part only. Invent a DISTINCT catchline that belongs with these siblings, not a new subject and NOT 'Related Requirements': § 1201.1 Transit Stamps; § 1201.2 Planar Tolls; § 1201.3 Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber; § 1201.4 Cartography Sold as a License to Raid; § 1201.5 Integrated Obligations; § 1201.6 Transit Stamps; Planar Tolls; Extraterritorial C; § 1201.7 Section 1201 – Portal and Planar Regulations; § 1201.8 Extraterritorial Jurisdiction and Planar Sovereignty; § 1201.9 Waiver and Modification; § 1201.10 Extra-Territorial Jurisdiction and Planar Tolls. First line must be: PAGE Your New Distinct Catchline (5-10 words, specific, no 'Related Requirements')",
