    {
      "cite": "200.12",
      "part": "part_ii",
      "title": "Prohibitions on Coercive and Degradational Magic",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Prohibitions on Coercive and Degradational Magic",
          "text": "Under § 200.9, the Guild strictly forbids the use of magic designed to dominate, debase, or psychologically scar individuals beyond their immediate pain. Such acts constitute a fundamental violation of magical ethics and personal autonomy, regardless of whether they result in physical injury. Specifically prohibited are spells that force another into servitude, induce chronic fear or shame, corrupt the mind permanently, or strip away dignity through ritualized humiliation. These practices undermine the very foundation of civilized society and threaten to unleash chaos upon the realm.\n\nAny mage found guilty of engaging in coercive or degradational magic faces immediate revocation of their license under § 200.4. Additionally, they shall be subject to disciplinary measures up to and including permanent sealing of their abilities as punishment for abusing their power. The Guild recognizes that while all magic carries risk, the deliberate infliction of lasting harm on another's psyche constitutes an egregious failure of responsible stewardship. Such actions place the perpetrator beyond redemption and justify severe consequences to protect both the public and the integrity of magical practice itself.\n\nThis prohibition applies equally to direct casting and indirect manipulation through constructs or other intermediaries. Whether employed for personal gain, sadistic pleasure, or misguided experimentation, such malefic arts are utterly anathema to the ideals upheld by this Codex. The Desk retains broad discretion to determine what qualifies as coercive or degradational under any circumstances, guided by common sense and a steadfast commitment to safeguarding human dignity. Any attempt to evade these restrictions through technicalities shall be met with swift and harsh retribution from the full weight of Guild authority.\n\nsee § 200.9 Prohibited Practices and Disciplinary Measures; see § 1.4 Persons Bound Under Jurisdiction; see § 300.6 Core Principles of Responsible Spellcasting; see § 500.7 Duty to Avoid Unreasonable Harm To Others\"",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "200.9",
            "200.4",
            "1.4",
            "300.6",
            "500.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibitions on Coercive and Degradational Magic — Continued",
          "text": "Under § 200.9, no caster shall employ spells designed to inflict pain, humiliation, or loss of bodily autonomy upon another individual without their fully informed consent. This prohibition applies regardless of whether the coercion is direct or indirect, immediate or delayed. Any magical act whose primary purpose is debasement, enslavement, or destruction of a target's willpower constitutes a severe violation of the Accords and shall result in immediate suspension and review by the Aegis Magi.\n\nThe Guild recognizes that certain forms of ritualistic play are permissible within strictly bounded contexts, such as consensual duels between licensed practitioners under supervised conditions. However, any attempt to extend these boundaries into non-consensual or exploitative territory will be treated as a criminal offense subject to full penal proceedings. The burden of proving consent and lack of harm rests entirely upon the caster charged with violating this provision.\n\nSpecial protections are afforded to vulnerable populations, including minors, individuals under guardianship, or those lacking capacity for informed decision-making. No licensed caster shall engage in any magical activity involving such persons without explicit authorization from their legal representatives or custodians. Violation of these safeguard provisions may lead to revocation of licensure and further disciplinary action at the discretion of the Archmage Sovereign.\n\nThe Desk maintains a confidential registry of all reported incidents alleging coercive or degrading use of magic, including details sufficient for investigation but protecting identities where appropriate. Each report is reviewed by an impartial panel appointed from among retired mages sworn to uphold the spirit of the Accords above personal interest. Findings and recommended sanctions are brought before the full Guild Council for final determination.\n\nCasters found guilty of violating § 200.9 face mandatory rehabilitation counseling focused on ethical spellcraft practices, alongside any applicable penalties outlined in subsequent sections. Repeat offenses or particularly egregious cases may warrant permanent expulsion from the Guild and referral to secular authorities for additional punishment under relevant laws. The ultimate goal remains preservation of public safety while fostering responsible magical expression within clearly defined limits.\n\nsee § 200.9 (Prohibited Practices); see § 1401.8 (Human Dignity Principles)\nunder § 1301.6 (Protection of Vulnerable Persons) and § 700.5 (Burden of Proof Standards)",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "200.9",
            "1401.8",
            "1301.6",
            "700.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.13 Prohibition on Coercive and Degradational Magic",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords strictly prohibit the use of magic for purposes of coercion, degradation, or dehumanization, regardless of whether such acts are performed by licensed casters or unlicensed individuals alike. Under no circumstances may a caster compel another individual to perform sexual acts against their will through magical means, nor may they reduce any person to a state of debasement solely for the purpose of deriving sexual gratification therefrom. Such prohibitions apply equally whether the coercion is achieved via mind control spells, pain-infliction rituals, or other forms of magical domination.\n\nThe Accords further forbid any act intended to strip an individual of their dignity or sense of self-worth in a manner that serves only the sadistic urges of the caster. This includes acts such as forced humiliation, public degradation, and ritualized subjugation devoid of legitimate magical purpose. The underlying principle is that magic may never be employed to reduce another human being to an object of abuse, even within the bounds of consensual roleplay or fantasy scenarios.\n\nViolations of this prohibition are subject to severe sanctions under § 200.9, including revocation of licensure, permanent expulsion from the Guild, and potential criminal prosecution for related offenses such as rape or assault. The Desk maintains a rigorous oversight regime to prevent abuse, utilizing both technological monitoring and informant networks to detect clandestine operations. Any caster found guilty of violating these standards will face immediate disciplinary action, regardless of their standing within the magical community.\n\nThe Accords recognize that while magic can enhance personal expression and pleasure, it must never be wielded as a tool for oppression or degradation. This fundamental ethical boundary protects individuals from exploitation even in the context of otherwise consensual activities. By establishing clear limits on permissible magical conduct, the Guild ensures that its members uphold both legal compliance and moral integrity in their pursuits.\n\nsee § 200.9 Disciplinary Measures; see § 1.4 Persons Bound; see § 800.11 Confiscation Procedures. Under the foundational principles of fairness and human dignity enshrined in §§ 1.7 and 1.8, these restrictions safeguard both the rights of individuals and the integrity of magical practice itself. Any dispute regarding alleged violations shall be adjudicated according to established Guild procedures outlined herein.\n\nEND OF PAGE - CONTINUE TO NEXT SECTION\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "200.9",
            "1.4",
            "800.11",
            "1.7",
            "200.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibitions on Coercive and Degradational Magic — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "§ 200.13 Prohibition on Coercive and Degradational Magic\n\nNo licensure shall be granted to any caster who demonstrates an aptitude for or reliance upon coercive or degradational magical practices. Such methods, whether employed directly or through proxies, are fundamentally incompatible with the ethical standards of the Guild and the protections afforded by these Accords. The Desk retains broad discretion to deny permits based on a candidate's demonstrated tendencies toward manipulation, intimidation, or degradation of others for personal gain or amusement.\n\nThis prohibition extends beyond mere technique; it encompasses the caster's overall demeanor and approach to magical interaction. Those who exhibit a pattern of using their powers to dominate, humiliate, or psychologically harm others shall be deemed unfit for licensure regardless of technical skill. The Desk may require additional assessments or probationary periods where appropriate to gauge an applicant's maturity and commitment to responsible practice.\n\nViolations of this provision carry severe penalties, up to and including permanent revocation of licensure and referral for disciplinary action under § 200.9. Repeated offenses may result in the imposition of sanctions upon related parties found complicit or negligent in overseeing such conduct. The Desk shall act swiftly and decisively to uphold these prohibitions, recognizing that prevention is always preferable to remediation once harm has been inflicted.\n\nThe spirit of this rule demands vigilance at every stage—from initial application through ongoing practice—to ensure that the tools granted by licensure are never misused for destructive ends. By rejecting coercive and degradational magic outright, the Guild affirms its dedication to the preservation of dignity and autonomy for all persons under its jurisdiction. Only those who embody these core values shall be entrusted with the power to shape reality itself.\n\nsee § 200.9 Disciplinary Measures and § 300.11 Prohibited Schools of Magic for related provisions; under § 1.4 Persons Bound, all casters agree to abide by these prohibitions without exception.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.13",
            "200.9",
            "300.11",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 200.12 Prohibitions on Coercive and Degradational Magic — Continued",
          "text": "Under no circumstances shall any licensed caster employ magic that deliberately induces feelings of inferiority, shame, or worthlessness in another sentient being. Such degradational practices are strictly forbidden, regardless of the stated intent behind their use. The Guild recognizes that subtle psychological manipulation can be just as damaging as overt physical violence. Therefore, all forms of magical humiliation and debasement remain prohibited by this Code.\n\nThe Aegis Magi shall actively monitor for instances where magic is used to undermine an individual's self-esteem or sense of agency. Evidence of such activities will result in immediate suspension pending investigation. Conviction leads to revocation of licensure and mandatory remedial counseling, unless the caster elects to undergo a full magical audit and pay substantial fines instead. The goal is to protect both citizens from harm and maintain public confidence in the Guild's regulatory framework.\n\nThis prohibition complements existing rules against physical assault and verbal abuse. While those statutes address explicit acts of aggression, § 200.12 fills a critical gap by outlawing covert methods of inflicting emotional distress. The spirit of this section demands that all magical interactions preserve human dignity at their core. Casters are reminded that true mastery involves restraint as much as power.\n\nViolations will be prosecuted with the utmost seriousness, reflecting the Guild's unwavering commitment to ethical practice. Penalties escalate based on severity and frequency of offending conduct. Repeat offenders face permanent expulsion from the profession and possible criminal charges for endangerment. The Desk retains broad discretion within these parameters to safeguard societal interests as they evolve over time.\n\nCasters who question the boundaries surrounding coercive arts are urged to consult with senior mentors or legal advisors before proceeding. Uncertainty should never drive reckless experimentation. Rather, thoughtful deliberation precedes any action that might impact others' mental well-being. This prudent approach benefits both individual practitioners and the wider community they serve daily through their craft.\n\nThe provisions herein stand as a testament to our shared values of respect and compassion amidst the complexities of arcane study. By upholding these standards collectively, we ensure that magic remains a force for positive change rather than unnecessary suffering. Together we build a stronger, more harmonious future grounded in mutual understanding and responsible stewardship of our remarkable gifts.\n\nsee § 200.9 Prohibited Practices and Disciplinary Measures; see § 1350.7 Mental Torture and Psychological Warfare; see § 640.5 Emotional Manipulation via Spellcasting; under § 890.3 Standards of Professional Conduct.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.12",
            "200.9",
            "1350.7",
            "640.5",
            "890.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Restrictions on Compulsory Arcane Servitude",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords strictly prohibit the application of magic to override the fundamental autonomy of any sentient being, whether such individual is a member of the Guild or an unaffiliated civilian. Under the jurisdiction of § 1.4, all persons bound by these accords are granted protection against magical compulsion that bypasses their conscious will for the purpose of labor, servitude, or involuntary cooperation. This prohibition specifically targets \"binding\" spells—those which tether a subject's motor functions or cognitive processes to an external command. Any mage found utilizing such magic shall be subject to immediate investigation by the Aegis Magi under § 200.9, and may face severe penalties if the coercion is deemed intentional for personal gain.\n\nThe distinction between \"coercive\" and \"persuasive\" magic is a matter of strict scrutiny. While an orator may use enchantment to heighten the charisma of their speech or a negotiator may use minor charms to soothe a tense atmosphere, these must not bypass the subject's ability to refuse the interaction. If a spell prevents a target from physically withdrawing from a situation or mentally rejecting a command, it is classified as coercive and is strictly forbidden under § 200.11. The Accord recognizes that while some forms of magic may influence emotion or perception, the fundamental right to bodily and mental autonomy cannot be traded for convenience or political leverage.\n\nFurthermore, the use of \"degradational\" magic—defined as any spell intended to strip a target of their dignity, sense of self, or essential identity—is strictly prohibited by the Conservators and upheld as core law. This includes but is not limited to spells that forcibly alter memories to remove agency, magically induced states of perpetual obedience, or the use of transmutation to force a subject into an undignified form for the purpose of humiliation. Such acts are viewed as violations of the sanctity of the soul and are considered among the most severe offenses in the Autumnwood Accords. Any mage caught employing such techniques shall be held liable for immediate seizure of their casting tools under § 800.11, and may find their license permanently revoked by the Office of Archmage Veyra as per § 100.11.\n\nIn matters of dispute involving involuntary magic, the evidence must be weighed against the standard of \"Intent of Use.\" If a mage claims that a coercion spell was an accidental byproduct of a defensive ward or a localized area-of-effect charm, they may seek relief under § 1.2. However, the burden of proof remains with the accused practitioner to demonstrate that no intent for servitude existed. All enforcement actions regarding these prohibitions shall be conducted by the Aegis Magi, whose primary mandate is the preservation of stability within the Midlands. Any violation resulting in the permanent loss of autonomy for a victim will result in an automatic referral to the Paradox Trial, where the mage's status may be determined through peer review or immediate sequestration as defined under § 1301.11.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "200.9",
            "200.11",
            "800.11",
            "100.11",
            "1.2",
            "1301.11"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "NEW section §200.12 in this same Part only. Invent a DISTINCT catchline that belongs with these siblings, not a new subject and NOT 'Related Requirements': § 200.1 Licensure Required; § 200.2 Apprentice Limits; § 200.3 Temporary Mouths; § 200.4 Suspension and Lapse; § 200.5 Guest Casters; § 200.6 Constructs That Hold a Quill; § 200.7 § 200.8 Exemptions and Waivers; § 200.8 Prerequisites for Licensure; § 200.9 Prohibited Practices and Disciplinary Measures; § 200.10 Construction and Interpretation; § 200.11 Prohibited Spellcasting by Unlicensed Individuals. First line must be: PAGE Your New Distinct Catchline (5-10 words, specific, no 'Related Requirements')",
