    {
      "cite": "900.12",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Additional Provisions",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.12 Additional Provisions",
          "text": "It is hereby provided that nothing in this Part shall be construed to limit or impair the full force and effect of any other provision contained herein or in the Autumnwood Accords generally. Any act, omission, or practice prohibited by law under any circumstance shall remain so regardless of any conflicting provisions set forth above; such prohibitions are cumulative and not mutually exclusive. In all cases where this Part addresses matters subject to applicable laws, rules, regulations, customs, usages, or other legal instruments governing the same subject matter, those external authorities shall control unless expressly modified by these terms. The intent is not to supplant existing obligations but rather to clarify and supplement them within their respective spheres of application.\n\nFurthermore, where this Part contemplates actions requiring consent, approval, authorization, waiver, release, or other forms of legal permission from third parties, such permissions must be obtained in writing prior to undertaking said acts. Any purported action taken without the necessary consents shall be deemed void ab initio and subject to immediate reversal upon proper notice. The burden of establishing that all required consents have been duly obtained rests solely on those asserting a right or privilege thereby conferred. Failure to secure appropriate permissions constitutes grounds for denial of relief, dismissal of claims, or imposition of sanctions as the circumstances warrant.\n\nIn addition, this Part shall not be interpreted in a manner inconsistent with public policy, morality, fairness, justice, equity, good conscience, or sound administration of law. Where doubt arises regarding the scope, meaning, or application of any provision herein, it shall be resolved by reference to these overarching principles rather than rigid textualism alone. The ultimate aim is to promote responsible magical practice while safeguarding against abuse and exploitation through balanced regulation grounded in both letter and spirit of the Accords. Nothing contained hereunder shall limit the authority of courts, tribunals, or other adjudicative bodies to interpret, enforce, modify, or set aside any provision deemed contrary to law or equity as they may determine from time to time.\n\nFinally, it is expressly declared that all rights and remedies available under this Part are cumulative and not exclusive thereof. Nothing herein shall preclude the pursuit of additional causes of action, defenses, or equitable relief otherwise proper in any given situation. Claimants retain full discretion to pursue multiple theories simultaneously without regard for potential duplicity or redundancy. The availability of alternative paths does not diminish the strength of those already asserted; rather, they stand side by side as complementary avenues toward redress and satisfaction of claims arising from violations of these provisions. Thus empowered, claimants may freely elect the strategy best suited to their individual needs and objectives within the bounds established by law and reason.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Disclosure and Certification of Ancilla",
          "text": "The Duty to Disclose Material Facts (§ 900.7) extends beyond the explicit spellcasting ritual itself, encompassing all magical effects that may arise from or in conjunction therewith. Under this provision, a caster must fully disclose any ancillary enchantments, residual effects, or secondary phenomena that are reasonably foreseeable as outcomes of their practice. Failure to reveal such information renders the entire casting operation noncompliant with Accords requirements.\n\nThe Obligation of Certification under § 900.6 mandates that all magical operations be accompanied by appropriate documentation and verification procedures. This includes obtaining independent assessment of potential risks, securing necessary permits for hazardous applications, and maintaining thorough records of outcomes and observations. The certification process serves as a critical safeguard against unforeseen consequences arising from complex or poorly understood arcane techniques.\n\nProvenance and Authenticity (§ 900.8) requires full disclosure regarding the origin and history of any magical artifacts employed during casting operations. Casters must certify that all materials utilized are lawfully acquired and properly registered under applicable regulations. Concealing defects, tampering with items to enhance their apparent value, or misrepresenting sources shall constitute serious violations subject to enhanced penalties.\n\nAdditional Provisions (§ 900.12) encompass various supplementary obligations designed to ensure comprehensive compliance across all aspects of magical practice. These include mandatory consultation protocols when dealing with dangerous constructs or unstable spell formations; strict limitations on experimental applications without appropriate oversight; and requirements for immediate reporting of any adverse events occurring during operations. Such measures collectively serve to promote public safety while preserving the integrity of legitimate arcane endeavors within clearly defined boundaries.\n\nSee also § 100.5 regarding conflicts of interest in casting activities, which may give rise to additional disclosure obligations under special circumstances. Furthermore, provisions addressing foreign jurisdictions and cross-border transactions (§§ 1201 et seq.) impose specific reporting requirements relevant to international magical commerce. These related mandates reinforce the overarching principle that full transparency remains paramount throughout all stages of lawful spellcasting practice.\n\nCasters must recognize that their responsibilities extend far beyond mere technical proficiency in executing desired effects. Rather, they bear a profound duty to society at large by maintaining rigorous standards for safety, honesty, and accountability in every aspect of their profession. Neglecting this higher calling invites severe repercussions under the Accords framework.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "900.7",
            "900.6",
            "900.8",
            "900.12",
            "100.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.12 Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic",
          "text": "Every artifact submitted under the Accords must undergo rigorous analysis to determine whether it contains embedded enchantments or other forms of latent magic not apparent to unaided perception. This requirement applies regardless of whether the submitter claims ownership, possession, or merely custodial control over the item in question. Failure to disclose such ancillary magic constitutes a material misrepresentation and may be grounds for revocation of any permit issued pursuant to this Part.\n\nSubmitters shall bear the burden of producing all relevant documentation pertaining to an artifact's origin and history, including but not limited to purchase receipts, appraisals, provenance records, and prior inspection reports. Where such documents are unavailable or incomplete, the submitter must provide a detailed sworn statement explaining the circumstances surrounding their acquisition and any known alterations made to the item since that time. The Desk retains sole discretion to accept or reject supplemental evidence as it deems appropriate in determining the true nature of the artifact submitted.\n\nIn addition to physical inspection, each artifact shall be subjected to magical scanning using approved diagnostic devices capable of detecting residual energies and hidden wards. These scans must be performed by qualified practitioners licensed under the Accords and witnessed by a designated Guild official. The results thereof shall become part of the permanent record maintained by the Desk and made available for review upon request by any interested party paying the requisite fee.\n\nAny discrepancies discovered between initial assessments and subsequent testing may result in additional fees or penalties imposed on the submitter at the sole discretion of the Archmage Sovereign. Furthermore, failure to comply fully with disclosure obligations shall be treated as a violation of § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting and subject to the sanctions provided therein. The Desk reserves the right to deny future submissions from any entity found guilty of intentional concealment or fraud regarding ancillary magic.\n\nThe provisions herein are intended solely to facilitate thorough vetting of all artifacts entering the official marketplace under the Accords. They shall not be construed to create a presumption of guilt or liability absent proof of negligence or malice on the part of the submitter. Compliance with these requirements is mandatory for all participants seeking to engage in lawful trade of magical goods and services within the Guild's jurisdiction.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "900.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Disclosure and Certification of Ancilla — Continued",
          "text": "Whereas the primary casting of a magical effect may constitute a reportable transaction under this Code, it is further provided that any ancillary magic performed in concert therewith shall likewise be subject to disclosure and certification requirements. This provision ensures full transparency regarding all magical operations conducted within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords Desk.\n\nSpecifically, when an Archmage or registered caster employs secondary spells, rituals, or enchantments to augment or facilitate their primary spellwork, each such ancillary operation must be separately identified in the filed declaration. The nature and purpose of these supporting magics must be clearly articulated, along with any materials or reagents utilized therein. Failure to fully disclose all components of a complex magical undertaking may result in findings of non-compliance under § 100.5 Conflict of Interest in Casting.\n\nCertification of ancillary magic requires submission of individual manifests for each supporting spell, detailing its function and relationship to the main effect. These supplementary documents must be signed by the principal caster or their designated proxy, affirming their accuracy and completeness upon penalty of perjury under § 1301.1 Supplementary Provisions and Transitional Arrangements. The Desk reserves authority to demand additional verification as deemed necessary for thorough review.\n\nIt is further mandated that any potential conflicts arising from simultaneous casting of multiple spells be explicitly addressed in the filed materials. Where secondary magics could interfere with or undermine primary effects, such interactions must be analyzed and reported in accordance with § 400.12 Research Integrity and Oversight Protocols. The Desk shall assess whether appropriate safeguards have been implemented to mitigate any adverse consequences stemming from concurrent operations.\n\nThis comprehensive disclosure regime serves the dual purpose of maintaining public safety while preserving the integrity of magical practice. By requiring full transparency regarding all aspects of spellcasting, including ancillary components, the Accords Desk can effectively monitor compliance and prevent unauthorized or dangerous activities. Caster certification of each element involved ensures accountability throughout the entire process.\n\nThe provisions herein apply regardless of whether ancillary magic is performed directly by the principal caster or delegated to assistants or contractors under § 200.1 Licensure Required. All parties participating in a magical operation share responsibility for proper disclosure and documentation. The Desk shall enforce these requirements uniformly across all registered practitioners, ensuring equitable oversight throughout the Commonwealth.\n\nNon-compliance with ancillary magic certification obligations may result in penalties ranging from fines to suspension of casting privileges under § 1200.3 Sanctions for Violations. Serious or willful breaches could lead to revocation of licensure entirely pursuant to § 500.7 Termination and Disbarment procedures. The Desk retains discretion to impose additional remedial measures as deemed appropriate in each case.\n\nThis section establishes a robust framework for managing the complexities inherent in advanced magical practice, balancing regulatory oversight with professional autonomy. Through meticulous disclosure and certification requirements, the Autumnwood Accords Desk safeguards both public welfare and the dignity of the arcane arts practiced under its jurisdiction.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "1301.1",
            "400.12",
            "200.1",
            "1200.3",
            "500.7",
            "900.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Disclosure and Certification of Ancilla — Administration",
          "text": "Where an artifact or enchantment contains embedded magic not readily apparent to the untrained eye, such as wards, curses, or residual spells cast during creation, a duty arises upon discovery to fully disclose these latent properties to any prospective purchaser or user under § 900.7. Failure to reveal known dangers may constitute fraud and subject the seller to civil penalty in addition to criminal sanctions under § 300.5. The certification required by § 900.6 must include a detailed inventory of all detectable magical components, their intended function, and any associated risks or limitations.\n\nIn cases where ancillary magic cannot be fully characterized without invasive testing that may destroy the item's value or integrity, the seller shall provide an estimated risk assessment based on available information and clearly state that full disclosure is provisional pending further analysis. This caveat protects both parties from future disputes arising from unknown variables while maintaining consumer protections mandated by § 1.8. Any subsequent revelations of additional hazards must be promptly communicated to all affected entities without delay, in accordance with the ongoing obligations set forth herein.\n\nThe provisions governing disclosure and certification extend not only to commercial transactions but also apply whenever an individual acquires possession of a magically-infused object through inheritance, gift, or other lawful means. As stated under § 900.7, every holder bears responsibility for ensuring that any potential perils are brought to light before the item is employed or transferred again. This principle underscores the broader societal commitment to transparency and safety promoted by the Accords as a whole.\n\nIt is further provided that where doubt exists regarding whether an object contains hidden magical elements, the burden of proof rests upon the party claiming such presence to demonstrate it through competent evidence. Absent clear indication to the contrary, all items shall be presumed benign until proven otherwise, consistent with the foundational presumption of innocence enshrined in § 1.4. Nevertheless, prudent caution remains advisable whenever dealing with antiquities or artifacts of uncertain origin, lest unforeseen consequences arise from ignorance rather than malice.\n\nSee also §§ 900.6, 900.7, 300.5, 1.8 for related requirements and standards applicable to disclosure obligations and risk management practices generally. These interconnected provisions collectively establish a robust framework designed to safeguard public welfare while facilitating the responsible exchange of magical goods within our dynamic society. Through diligent adherence thereto, we shall continue to foster an environment wherein innovation may flourish alongside tradition under the watchful gaze of justice.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "900.7",
            "300.5",
            "900.6",
            "1.8",
            "1.4",
            "900.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 900.12 Ancillary Magic Disclosure and Certification",
          "text": "All mages operating under the Autumnwood Accords are further required to fully disclose any ancillary magical effects inherent in their licensed works, whether these effects arise from natural enchantments or deliberate augmentation by third parties. Such disclosure must be made at the time of initial filing and renewed annually thereafter, accompanied by a sworn certification attesting to the accuracy and completeness of all statements regarding potential residual magics. Failure to comply with this duty constitutes grounds for revocation of one's magical license and may subject the offending party to additional penalties under relevant sections of these Accords.\n\nThe provisions herein are designed to ensure that clients remain fully informed of any latent dangers or unexpected capabilities associated with their purchases, thereby facilitating safe and responsible use of magical artifacts. By mandating transparent disclosure practices, the Guild seeks to protect both consumers and practitioners from unforeseen complications arising from undisclosed enchantments. Mages who knowingly conceal material facts regarding ancillary magics shall be held strictly liable for any resulting harm or loss suffered by others in reliance on their representations.\n\nIt is further provided that all certification forms and supporting documentation must be retained for a period of no less than five years following the date of sale, to facilitate future audits and investigations as deemed necessary by the Guild's oversight authorities. Any alterations or additions to previously filed disclosures shall similarly require formal submission to the appropriate reviewing body prior to implementation. Compliance with these requirements is essential to maintaining one's standing within the magical community and avoiding costly litigation in the event of disputes.\n\nMages who violate this section may face immediate suspension of their licenses pending completion of a thorough review by the Conservators' Council. Upon conclusion of said proceedings, disciplinary action will be imposed as warranted under existing statutes governing professional misconduct. In egregious cases involving intentional deception or gross negligence resulting in significant injury or property damage, criminal charges may also be pursued alongside civil remedies available to affected parties.\n\nThis duty extends equally to all practitioners engaging in magical services of any kind, whether through direct employment contracts or informal arrangements with clients. The obligations herein are non-delegable and remain incumbent upon individual mages regardless of intermediary relationships or corporate affiliations. Breach thereof shall be treated as a serious offense subject to swift and severe sanctions consistent with the gravity of the circumstances involved.\"\n\n\"See also §§ 900.1, 900.7, 900.10 regarding related disclosure duties; § 200.5 for penalties applicable to unlicensed casting activities.\"",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.1",
            "200.5",
            "900.12"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "NEW section §900.12 in this same Part only. Invent a DISTINCT catchline that belongs with these siblings, not a new subject and NOT 'Related Requirements': § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting; § 900.2 Reporting of Material Enchantments; § 900.3 Record Retention; § 900.4 Identify Fees; Who Pays; § 900.5 False Provenance of Relics; § 900.6 Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic; § 900.7 Duty to Disclose Material Facts; § 900.8 Provenance and Authenticity; § 900.9 See Also Provisions; § 900.10 Duty to Disclose Potential Hazards; § 900.11 Duty to File Before Casting. First line must be: PAGE Your New Distinct Catchline (5-10 words, specific, no 'Related Requirements')",
