    {
      "cite": "500.12",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct",
          "text": "§ 500.12 Unsanctioned Commercial Spellcasting\n\nNo caster shall engage in commercial spellcasting activities outside the bounds of their licensed practice without express authorization from the Guild's High Council. Such unauthorized commercialization is deemed a serious breach of trust and may result in revocation of licensure, confiscation of magical implements, and liability for damages arising therefrom. Any profits derived from such illicit activities shall be forfeited to the Crown as contraband proceeds.\n\nThis prohibition applies equally to both individual practitioners and corporate entities seeking to monetize their arcane capabilities beyond established channels. The Guild maintains sole authority over the regulation and taxation of magical services within its jurisdiction, reserving for itself the right to determine fair pricing structures and quality standards. Unauthorized competition undermines this regulatory framework and exposes all parties involved to unacceptable risks.\n\nViolations of this provision shall be subject to immediate investigation by the Aegis Magi upon complaint or detection. Convicted offenders face penalties ranging from monetary fines proportional to illegal gains, to permanent expulsion from the profession with associated asset seizures. Repeat offenses may warrant more severe sanctions including imprisonment and magical containment as deemed appropriate under existing penal statutes.\n\nThe Guild retains discretion to issue special permits for exceptional circumstances where commercial spellcasting serves a legitimate public interest or addresses critical market failures not met by licensed providers. Such permissions shall be granted only after thorough vetting of character, competence, and financial responsibility. Permits are non-transferable and subject to annual renewal upon review of compliance history.\n\nAny disputes arising from alleged violations of this section shall be resolved through binding arbitration before a panel convened pursuant to the Accords' dispute resolution procedures. Costs of proceedings borne by prevailing party unless otherwise ordered for equitable reasons. All determinations final and not subject to further appeal except on grounds of gross procedural error or fraud.\n\nThis provision represents a deliberate balance between protecting public welfare from unregulated magical commerce while preserving freedom of trade within lawful bounds. The Guild stands ready to enforce these provisions vigorously to maintain confidence in the integrity of our professional standards. Those who choose to operate outside this framework do so at their peril and without recourse under the protection of the Accords.\n\nsee § 500.1 Field Casting for Value; see § 600.3 Contracts with Non-Casters; see § 704.2 Unlawful Competition Provisions.\"",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "500.12",
            "500.1",
            "600.3",
            "704.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct",
          "text": "§ 500.12 Unsanctioned Research and Unauthorized Experiments in Public Spaces\nNo mage shall conduct research or perform unauthorized experiments in public spaces without express permission from the Archmage Sovereign and written authorization from a Conservator Magus of at least Third Rank. Any such violation constitutes a Class B Felony under § 104.3(a)(2) and is punishable by imprisonment not to exceed five years, fine of one thousand Wario Coins, or both at the discretion of the presiding judge. Furthermore, any mage found guilty of conducting unsanctioned research shall be subject to immediate suspension from practice until such time as they have completed a mandatory course in Magical Ethics and Safety administered by the Aegis Magi. Failure to comply with these provisions may result in revocation of licensure under § 203.4(b). The Archmage Sovereign retains sole authority to grant waivers or exceptions to these requirements on a case-by-case basis, subject to review by a panel of Conservator Mages. All requests for such waivers must be submitted in writing and accompanied by detailed justification and supporting documentation demonstrating the necessity thereof. Any mage found guilty of violating this section shall also be required to pay all associated costs incurred by the Guild in investigating and prosecuting the offense, including but not limited to legal fees, expert witness expenses, and administrative overhead. Finally, any mage who knowingly assists or facilitates unsanctioned research activities in violation of these provisions shall be held jointly and severally liable for all resulting damages and penalties imposed upon the primary violator. Such joint liability extends to both civil and criminal proceedings brought by the Guild or affected parties alike. This section is intended to safeguard public safety and maintain the integrity of magical practice within our society while providing clear guidelines for lawful conduct in public spaces. Violations will be prosecuted vigorously to ensure compliance with these essential provisions designed to protect all citizens from potential harm resulting from reckless or illegal magical experimentation.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "500.12",
            "104.3",
            "203.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Arcane Field Conduct Protocols",
          "text": "Section 500.12 shall govern the conduct and operations of licensed mages during field casting activities outside of controlled environments. This includes all procedures, restrictions, and obligations applicable when magic is employed in public or semi-public settings under the authority of a valid permit. The provisions herein are designed to safeguard both the caster's interests and those of the general populace.\n\n§ 500.12(a) requires that all field casting operations be preceded by a comprehensive risk assessment conducted no less than twenty-four (24) hours prior to commencement. Such assessments must evaluate potential hazards, escape routes, containment measures, and emergency response capabilities. The results shall be submitted for review by the local Conservator or their designee, who may impose additional safeguards based on the nature of the proposed work.\n\nUnder § 500.12(b), casters are prohibited from employing spells with effects exceeding their designated class rating unless expressly authorized in writing by the Archmage Sovereign. Exceptions may be granted only for research purposes subject to strict oversight protocols outlined elsewhere in this Code. Violation of this prohibition constitutes a Class A felony and shall result in immediate revocation of all licenses and permits held by the offending party.\n\n§ 500.12(c) mandates that any damage or injury resulting from negligent or reckless casting be fully compensated by the responsible mage(s). This obligation extends to third parties whose property or persons may have been adversely affected, regardless of whether a direct causal link can be established beyond reasonable doubt. Failure to provide prompt and adequate compensation shall subject the violator to punitive damages up to five times the actual loss sustained.\n\nIn accordance with § 500.12(d), all field operations must maintain continuous contact with the Guild's central monitoring station via encrypted comms array. Casters are required to report their location, intended activities, and anticipated duration at least every two (2) hours throughout each day of operation. Any deviation from the approved plan must be communicated immediately for further instructions before proceeding.\n\n§ 500.12(e) prohibits the use of magical effects that could reasonably be expected to cause panic or alarm among the public, including but not limited to illusions of monstrous form, sudden bursts of flame or ice, and unexplained levitation phenomena. Minor visual disturbances such as sparks, flashes, or brief gusts of wind are permitted so long as they do not interfere with normal civic activities.\n\nUnder § 500.12(f), casters shall refrain from engaging in any form of magical combat during field operations unless specifically authorized under exceptional circumstances determined by the Arcane Tribunal. Unauthorized dueling poses an unacceptable risk to innocent bystanders and is strictly forbidden without prior written consent from both parties involved and the local Conservator.\n\n§ 500.12(g) requires that all equipment used in connection with field casting meet minimum safety standards established by the Equipment Safety Commission. Regular inspections must be performed every thirty (30) days, with results submitted to the Guild for recordkeeping purposes. Defective or substandard gear shall not be utilized under any circumstances pending repair or replacement.\n\nIn accordance with § 500.12(h), casters are obligated to maintain a detailed log of all activities conducted during each field operation. This must include dates, times, locations, spells employed, outcomes achieved, and any adverse effects observed. Such records shall be retained for no less than seven (7) years and made available for audit upon request by authorized officials.\n\nUnder § 500.12(i), failure to comply with any provision of this section shall constitute a breach of contract subject to the remedies outlined herein. Repeated or willful violations may result in suspension or permanent revocation of all licenses held by the offending party, along with civil and criminal penalties as appropriate under applicable law.\"\n\n\"see § 500.1 Field Casting for Value; § 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion; § 500.7 Public Safety — Additional Protocols; § 200.1 Licensure Required\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "500.12",
            "500.1",
            "500.3",
            "500.7",
            "200.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.12 Arcane Field Conduct Protocols",
          "text": "Part V — Arcane Field Operations and Liability, § 500.12 Arcane Field Conduct Protocols\n\nCasters engaged in field operations shall adhere to strict conduct protocols designed to minimize disruption and risk to the public. Under no circumstances may a caster employ magic that causes unnecessary pain or distress to onlookers without prior authorization. Any magical display that attracts significant attention shall be immediately halted until appropriate permits can be secured. Failure to comply with these directives constitutes a serious breach of professional ethics and may result in disciplinary action up to and including revocation of licensure.\n\nIn the event of an emergency requiring immediate intervention, casters are authorized to bypass normal procedural requirements temporarily. However, such actions must be limited strictly to what is necessary to mitigate harm. All subsequent activities shall revert to standard operating procedures as soon as conditions permit. Unauthorized use of force or coercion against non-compliant individuals remains strictly prohibited regardless of circumstances. Violations of this mandate will be treated as grounds for immediate termination and potential criminal prosecution depending on severity.\n\nCasters are further reminded that their actions reflect upon the entire profession and may influence public perception of magic as a whole. Maintaining decorum even under stress is essential to preserving trust between casters and society at large. Breaches of conduct protocols will be documented in full and submitted for review by the Office of Archmage Sovereign. Repeated offenses or particularly egregious violations may lead to enhanced penalties beyond those outlined herein. All casters are expected to uphold the highest standards of professionalism at all times.\n\n\"See also § 200.3 Temporary Modifications; § 700.5 Emergency Powers and Limitations; § 1400.8 Professional Ethics.\" \"Under § 100.5 Conflict of Interest, casters must recuse themselves when personal gain may bias judgment.\" \"Reference § 900.6 Duty to Mitigate Harm for additional guidance on safety obligations during field work.\" \"As outlined in § 500.7 Public Safety Protocols, prevention of collateral damage is paramount at all times.\" \"Violation of these provisions constitutes a material breach under § 801.2 Breach of Contract Principles and may trigger further sanctions.\" \"Casters are reminded that their license grants privilege but not impunity; accountability remains absolute regardless of urgency.\" \"Failure to follow prescribed procedures could result in liability for resulting damages per § 600.9 Strict Liability in Magical Operations.\" \"All casters bear responsibility for managing risks inherent in their trade through careful planning and restraint.\" \"Disciplinary measures may include fines, suspension, mandatory retraining, or permanent expulsion from the profession depending on circumstances.\" \"Maintaining public confidence requires unwavering commitment to ethical practice even when faced with difficult choices under pressure.\" \"The Guild expects each member to act as an ambassador for the art form, reflecting well on us all through exemplary behavior.\" \"Neglect of duty may be construed as grounds for dismissal from active service status entirely at Archmage's discretion.\" \"Protocols exist not only for protection but also preservation of our shared reputation across generations past and future alike.\" \"Discipline today ensures freedom tomorrow; let us all strive to earn it through steadfast adherence to these vital guidelines.\" \"The integrity of magic depends upon the character of those who wield it. Choose wisely how you represent this noble craft.\" \"Our collective success hinges on mutual respect, discipline, and an unshakeable commitment to doing what is right above all else.\" \"Let us stand as guardians of truth against chaos, guided by wisdom born from centuries of hard-won experience.\" \"In conclusion: conduct yourself with honor always; your actions echo far beyond the moment they occur.\" \"Remember: we are stewards of great power entrusted into our keeping. Wield it wisely lest we lose it all forevermore!\"",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "500.12",
            "200.3",
            "700.5",
            "1400.8",
            "100.5",
            "900.6",
            "500.7",
            "801.2",
            "600.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Arcane Field Conduct Protocols, § 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane ",
          "text": "The Arcane Field Conduct Protocols govern the behavior of licensed casters engaged in sanctioned operations outside formal Guild facilities. Under § 500.7(a), any caster executing a spell of Class III or higher must maintain clear lines of sight to all affected parties, ensuring informed consent through visual confirmation where practicable. In cases involving non-human subjects, auditory and vibrational cues shall suffice provided they convey equivalent clarity of intent.\n\n§ 500.7(b) mandates that casters abort any spell sequence immediately upon detecting signs of unintended escalation, such as rising ambient mana levels or anomalous distortions in local space-time metrics. Failure to do so constitutes gross negligence subject to disciplinary action under § 302.4(c). Exceptions apply only when pre-approved by the Office of Arcane Safety pursuant to § 150.6(b), typically for research trials involving novel spell constructions.\n\nThe protocols further require that all casters carry certified detection devices capable of measuring residual magical radiation and particulate matter following spell completion. Results must be logged electronically or on provided forms, submitted within forty-eight hours via secure transmission channels designated by the Guild’s Information Systems Division. These records serve both as evidence of compliance and as data for ongoing safety analysis.\n\nSpecial provisions address situations where spells inadvertently affect bystanders or property. Caster shall promptly notify affected parties in person when feasible, offering appropriate remedies including compensation for damages under § 500.8(a). In cases involving personal injury or death, the matter escalates immediately to criminal investigation pursuant to § 204.1(d), regardless of prior civil settlements reached between private parties.\n\nCasters are prohibited from using any spell component derived from protected species without explicit written authorization from both the Bureau of Wildlife Conservation and the Office of Arcane Ethics. Violation constitutes aggravated environmental destruction under § 650.3(e) and may result in revocation of licensure alongside criminal charges. Exceptions exist only for spells requiring specific biological materials as catalysts, where alternative substitutes cannot achieve desired effects without compromising safety or efficacy.\n\nThe protocols also address conflicts arising from overlapping spell fields generated by multiple casters operating in proximity. Such situations require immediate cessation of all casting activities until mutual agreement on sequence and priority can be established through mediation proceedings under § 180.7(b). Refusal to comply places the offending caster at risk for charges of reckless endangerment under applicable criminal statutes.\n\nFinal provisions mandate that all casters undergo periodic re-evaluation of their operational procedures every six months, with written reports submitted directly to their supervising field officer. These reviews assess adherence to established protocols and identify areas for improvement in safety practices or emergency response capabilities. Non-compliance triggers mandatory remedial training programs administered at Guild expense upon request by the reviewing authority.\n\nThrough these comprehensive guidelines, the Arcane Field Conduct Protocols aim to balance operational flexibility with rigorous safety standards, ensuring that magical services remain beneficial rather than harmful to society as a whole. Continued vigilance and strict adherence to these measures are essential for maintaining public trust in the profession of arcana.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "500.7",
            "302.4",
            "150.6",
            "500.8",
            "204.1",
            "650.3",
            "180.7",
            "500.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 500.12 Unregulated Spellcasting and Public Safety",
          "text": "The unauthorized casting of magic within public spaces poses significant risks to both persons and property, necessitating strict regulatory oversight. Section 500.6 prohibits any unlicensed individual from performing arcane rituals in view of the general populace without prior authorization from the Autumnwood Accords Desk. Violations thereof shall be subject to immediate confiscation of materials and potential revocation of existing permits under § 200.1.\n\nFurthermore, spellcasters engaging in field operations must adhere to comprehensive safety protocols designed to mitigate collateral damage. These include maintaining adequate clearance distances from bystanders as specified in the official Safety Guidelines issued by the Conservators (§ 700.3). Failure to comply with such directives may result in disciplinary action ranging from fines to suspension of licensure, depending on the severity of the infraction and resultant harm.\n\nIn instances where spellcasting inadvertently causes injury or destruction, affected parties are entitled to seek redress through established legal channels outlined herein. Section 500.8 governs compensation claims arising from negligent magical practices, requiring proof of causation and quantifiable damages pursuant to prevailing common law principles adapted for arcane contexts. The burden of establishing liability shall rest upon the accuser unless affirmative evidence exonerates the accused caster.\n\nThe Desk retains broad discretion to enforce these provisions as deemed necessary to preserve public order and welfare. This may include issuing cease-and-desist orders, imposing monetary penalties, or initiating formal investigations into suspected violations. Such measures are without prejudice to any additional remedies available at law or equity under applicable statutes and case precedents governing professional conduct within the magical arts community.\n\nIt is incumbent upon all registered practitioners to familiarize themselves thoroughly with these regulations and abide by them scrupulously in their daily operations. The Guild stands ready to assist those who may require guidance on compliance matters but will not tolerate blatant disregard for established safety standards under any circumstances whatsoever. Adherence to prescribed protocols remains paramount in ensuring responsible stewardship of potent magical forces within society at large.\n\nsee § 500.6 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces; see § 200.1 Licensure Required; see § 700.3 Safety Guidelines and Best Practices; see § 1.8 Effective Date and Savings Clauses.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.1",
            "700.3",
            "500.6",
            "1.8",
            "500.12"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "NEW section §500.12 in this same Part only. Invent a DISTINCT catchline that belongs with these siblings, not a new subject and NOT 'Related Requirements': § 500.1 Field Casting for Value; § 500.2 Unscheduled Weather; § 500.3 Corridor Courtesy After an Explosion; § 500.4 Healing Offered as Consideration; § 500.5 Love Philtre and Undue Influence; § 500.6 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces; § 500.7 Public Safety — Additional Protocols; § 500.8 Compensation and Debt Settlement; § 500.9 Unlicensed Artisanry; § 500.10 Compensation and Indemnification; § 500.11 Unsanctioned Field Operations and Liability. First line must be: PAGE Your New Distinct Catchline (5-10 words, specific, no 'Related Requirements')",
