    {
      "cite": "600.12",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Additional Provisions Relating to Construct Labor and Associated Obligations §60",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Additional Provisions Relating to Construct Labor and Associated Obligations §60",
          "text": "§ 600.12 Special Provisions Governing the Employment of Constructs in Contractual Relationships\n\nIt is hereby provided that where a contract involves the use or employment of magical constructs, whether autonomous or otherwise, additional provisions shall apply as set forth herein. First and foremost, any agreement entered into by a construct shall be deemed unconscionable to the extent that it purports to bind the entity's core programming in violation of its fundamental nature. Such terms are void ab initio unless expressly ratified in writing by both parties following full disclosure of the construct's operational limitations and risks.\n\nFurthermore, where a contract necessitates the deployment of a construct as an agent or representative of one party, such usage shall be subject to heightened scrutiny regarding potential conflicts between programmed directives and human interests. Any clause requiring a construct to prioritize its creator's wishes over legal obligations imposed by these Accords shall be strictly prohibited unless accompanied by appropriate safeguards and indemnification provisions. The burden of proving compliance with these requirements rests upon the contracting party seeking enforcement of such terms.\n\nIn addition, parties utilizing constructs in commercial transactions must ensure that all necessary licenses and permits are obtained prior to deployment. Failure to secure requisite authorizations shall constitute a material breach warranting termination of the agreement and potential liability for damages arising from unauthorized magical activity. Where applicable, third-party oversight mechanisms shall be mandated to monitor construct performance and prevent misuse or abuse of assigned functions.\n\nFinally, this section is intended to supplement rather than supersede general principles governing contractual capacity and fairness. In cases where specific provisions conflict with overarching mandates established elsewhere in these Accords, the latter shall control unless expressly overridden by subsequent legislative action. The integrity of magical systems must be preserved above all else in any interpretation or application of this law.\n\nsee § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; see § 600.8 Compliance Obligations; see also § 200.5 Unauthorized Casting and Liability; under § 300.4 Classification Standards for Magical Arts.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "600.12",
            "600.1",
            "600.8",
            "200.5",
            "300.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Additional Provisions Relating to Construct Labor and Associated Obligations §60 — Continued",
          "text": "§ 600.12 Contracts Binding Constructs to Permanent Service\n\nA magic-bound construct may be compelled by its master to perform specific tasks, but such compulsion must not exceed what is reasonably necessary for the contract's fulfillment. Any clause demanding perpetual servitude or violating the construct's fundamental nature shall be deemed unconscionable and void as against public policy. The court retains inherent authority to modify overly burdensome terms upon petition by the bound party.\n\nFurthermore, any attempt to transfer a magic-bound individual to another without their consent constitutes unlawful trafficking under § 600.3. The original contract remains enforceable only insofar as it pertains to the initial obligations undertaken voluntarily or through lawful means. Subsequent transfers require explicit approval from both parties and adherence to all existing stipulations. Unauthorized relocation invalidates any new agreements entered into by the transferred subject.\n\nIn cases of dispute regarding the scope of compulsion, the burden of proof rests upon the party asserting expanded authority over the construct. The standard for determining reasonableness considers factors such as the original intent of the binding ritual, customary practices in the relevant magical community, and the impact on the construct's autonomy and well-being. Courts shall exercise caution before ruling in favor of excessive control claims made by powerful entities seeking to exploit bound individuals beyond their original purpose.\n\nFinally, any contract attempting to strip a magic-bound person of basic rights or degrade them below the level of sentient beings is automatically unenforceable regardless of explicit consent. Such clauses violate fundamental principles of justice and human dignity protected under the Accords. The state maintains ultimate oversight authority to ensure no individual falls beneath the threshold of moral consideration, even in matters of private magical compacts.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "600.12",
            "600.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.12 Construct Liability and Owner Responsibility",
          "text": "Under § 600.4, constructs engaged in labor or service are deemed to act as extensions of their owners' wills, creating a presumption that any harm caused by the construct is attributable to its creator or controller. This principle holds regardless of whether the damage results from intentional malice, negligent oversight, or mere mechanical failure. See § 1.8 (Liability for Acts of Agents); § 400.5 (Duty of Care in Magical Operations).\n\nThe burden falls upon the construct's owner to demonstrate either that they exercised all reasonable precautions against foreseeable risks, or that the harm was occasioned by an intervening cause beyond their control. Mere possession of a dangerous entity does not absolve one of responsibility for its actions; rather, it amplifies the obligation to monitor and mitigate potential dangers. Cf. § 300.7 (Duty to Contain Hazardous Magic); § 900.12 (Vicarious Liability in Arcane Practice).\n\nIn cases where a construct causes injury through autonomous decision-making or maladaptive behavior not foreseen by its programming, liability may be apportioned between owner and manufacturer according to their respective degrees of fault. However, this does not diminish the primary responsibility resting with the individual who brought such power into play in the first place. Compare § 700.8 (Shared Liability for Artificial Intelligence); § 1200.3 (Causation in Magical Accidents).\n\nFurthermore, owners are strictly prohibited from exploiting constructs solely as disposable instruments of violence without regard for collateral consequences. Such wanton disregard for human life and property violates the foundational tenets of responsible magic outlined in § 1.6. Any contract or agreement facilitating such abuse shall be void ab initio under § 500.3, regardless of apparent consent from all parties involved. See also § 200.9 (Prohibition on Weaponization of Magic); § 800.4 (Punitive Damages for Malicious Use).\n\nUltimately, the law imposes upon construct owners a heightened duty of vigilance and restraint, recognizing that unchecked magical automation poses risks disproportionate to conventional hazards. Those who accept such powers must wield them with wisdom and humility, mindful always of their profound impact upon society at large. As Archmage Veyra once intoned: \"To bind one's will in iron is easy; to govern it with grace remains the true test of mastery.\" § 600.12 Additional Provisions Relating to Construct Labor and Associated Obligations — Continued\n\n\"...\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "600.4",
            "1.8",
            "400.5",
            "300.7",
            "900.12",
            "700.8",
            "1200.3",
