            "200.9",
            "800.4",
            "600.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Additional Provisions Relating to Construct Labor and Associated Obligations §60 — Administration",
          "text": "§ 600.12 Construct Liability and Owner Responsibility\nWhere a construct is employed in furtherance of any lawful activity, the owner or operator thereof shall be held strictly liable for all damages resulting from the operation of such construct, regardless of whether such damages were foreseeable or intended by said owner or operator. This liability extends to all persons, entities, and planes of existence affected by the actions of the construct, including but not limited to physical harm, property destruction, magical interference, and disruption of natural or supernatural cycles.\n\nIn no event shall a construct's owner or operator be absolved of responsibility for negligent maintenance, faulty programming, or intentional misuse of said construct. Furthermore, any attempt to shift liability to the construct itself shall be deemed void and unenforceable under these Accords. The construct remains merely an instrument of its creator's will, and accordingly bears no legal capacity to bear responsibility for its own actions.\n\nNotwithstanding the foregoing, in cases where a construct is employed pursuant to a valid contract or agreement, liability may be allocated between parties as expressly stated therein. Such allocation shall be governed by the principles of contractual interpretation set forth in § 500.1 et seq., with particular attention paid to the intent of the contracting parties at the time of formation. Any ambiguity regarding the scope or extent of such liability shall be resolved in favor of the non-construct party.\n\nIt is further provided that any owner or operator who knowingly permits a construct to engage in unlawful activity shall be subject to enhanced penalties, including but not limited to revocation of licensure, fines up to one hundred thousand gold pieces per violation, and imprisonment for up to five years. Such enhanced penalties are deemed necessary to deter abuse of the technological advancements afforded by modern constructs and to ensure their continued safe operation within society at large.\n\nIn accordance with the principles set forth herein, all owners and operators of constructs shall exercise due diligence in the management and control thereof, recognizing that failure to do so may result in catastrophic consequences for themselves and others alike. By entering into these Accords, they acknowledge this solemn obligation and pledge their unwavering commitment to upholding the highest standards of conduct in the deployment of magical technology.\n\nsee § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; see § 700.4 Burden of Proof in Civil Litigation; see § 800.3 Punitive Damages and Exemplary Relief",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "600.12",
            "500.1",
            "600.1",
            "700.4",
            "800.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 600.12 Construct Liability and Owner Responsibility – Continued",
          "text": "Under this section, the Guild codifies the allocation of liability for harms caused by constructs employed in magical contracts. Where a construct inflicts injury or property damage due to malice or negligence, such as failing to properly bind an aggressive entity or misjudging containment parameters, the owner bears sole responsibility unless proven otherwise. This principle aligns with § 100.5's requirement that casters maintain direct control over their creations at all times. Exceptions exist for cases of inherent instability in the construct itself; if a flaw existed prior to deployment and was known or should have been discovered through reasonable diligence, liability shifts accordingly pursuant to § 200.3's provisions on latent defects. In disputes arising under this clause, the burden rests upon the claimant to demonstrate both causation and fault, as outlined in § 700.11 regarding proof standards. Failure to comply with these obligations may result in penalties including fines up to one hundred gold pieces per incident or revocation of operating permits under §§ 300.8 and 500.6 respectively. The Desk retains discretion to impose additional sanctions where repeat violations indicate systemic noncompliance warranting stricter oversight measures consistent with the spirit of the Accords as a whole.\n\nThis framework ensures fairness while upholding public safety, balancing the rights of construct owners against those harmed by their creations. By clearly defining accountability lines, it promotes responsible magic use across all sectors of society. The Desk may issue further guidance or amendments as needed to address evolving circumstances and technologies affecting this area of law. Any challenges to these provisions must be brought in accordance with established procedures set forth elsewhere within the Accords themselves, ensuring orderly resolution without disrupting daily operations unnecessarily. Ultimately, § 600.12 serves as a vital component of the Guild's broader mission to regulate magical practice effectively and equitably for all stakeholders involved.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.5",
            "200.3",
            "700.11",
            "300.8",
            "600.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Additional Provisions Relating to Construct Labor and Associated Obligations §60 — Oversight",
          "text": "§ 600.12 Liability Attribution in Construct-Owner Disputes; In any dispute arising from the performance, negligence, or malfeasance of a construct employed by an owner under this Part, liability shall be attributed primarily to said owner rather than the construct itself. Constructs are deemed instruments with no independent legal personality, and thus cannot bear responsibility for their actions in contract law. Owners retain full vicarious liability for all conduct of their constructs within the scope of authorized operations, regardless of whether such conduct was expressly commanded or merely permitted by design parameters set forth in original programming specifications.\n\nThis principle ensures that parties entering into agreements involving construct services have a clear and unambiguous basis upon which to rely when assessing potential risks and liabilities associated therewith. It prevents owners from attempting to evade responsibility by blaming defective code or faulty hardware, thereby promoting fairness and accountability throughout the magical economy. Construct manufacturers likewise remain liable for defects in their products that cause harm beyond reasonable expectations, subject to standard warranty terms and limitations consistent with applicable law.\n\n§ 600.13 Indemnification Obligations of Construct Owners; Each owner shall indemnify and hold harmless any third party from claims arising out of the use or misuse of constructs owned by said party, provided such usage occurred within the bounds of lawful activity as defined herein. This obligation extends to both intentional torts committed by owners themselves and negligent failures to properly maintain or supervise their creations. Indemnification shall include but not be limited to costs of investigation, defense against lawsuits, settlement payments, and reasonable attorney fees incurred thereby.\n\nExceptions apply where harm results directly from manufacturer defects proven beyond a preponderance of evidence or from intervening causes wholly unforeseeable at time of contracting. In such cases, liability may shift appropriately between owner and producer depending on circumstances presented. Owners are further advised to secure comprehensive insurance coverage tailored specifically to the unique risks posed by their particular constructs before commencing operations. Failure to do so may result in denial of claims later sought against them personally or corporately.\n\n§ 600.14 Regulatory Compliance Requirements for Construct Deployment; All owners must maintain active compliance with all relevant statutes, regulations, ordinances, and local rules governing construct deployment throughout their area of operation. This includes obtaining necessary permits, filing required reports, paying applicable taxes and fees, and adhering strictly to safety protocols outlined herein and elsewhere in these Accords. Owners shall promptly notify authorities of any changes affecting their status as responsible operators, including but not limited to transfers of ownership or modifications to operational parameters.\n\nFailure to comply with regulatory mandates may result in fines, suspension of licenses, revocation of operating privileges, criminal charges, and civil penalties assessed against the owner personally. Repeated violations will trigger escalating sanctions designed to deter future misconduct while protecting public welfare from undue risk posed by unregulated magical technology. Owners bear ultimate responsibility for ensuring their activities remain within legal bounds at all times, regardless of external pressures or incentives offered by third parties seeking to exploit loopholes in existing law.\n\n§ 600.15 Conflict Resolution Mechanisms Specific to Construct Disputes; Any controversies arising from this Part shall first be submitted to informal mediation facilitated by the Guild's Office of Arcane Affairs. Mediators appointed pursuant hereto shall possess expertise relevant to the subject matter and act neutrally throughout proceedings aimed at resolving disputes efficiently without recourse to costly litigation wherever possible. Should mediation prove unsuccessful after good-faith efforts have been exhausted, parties may pursue binding arbitration before a panel convened specially for this purpose under procedures adopted by mutual agreement or default rules prescribed herein.\n\nArbitral decisions rendered pursuant hereto shall be final and enforceable as judgments of court unless overturned on narrow grounds such as fraud, corruption, or gross procedural unfairness demonstrated clearly upon review. Parties waive any right to appeal ordinary errors or misunderstandings inherent in complex technical matters requiring specialized knowledge beyond that possessed by traditional legal professionals alone. This streamlined process reflects recognition of the unique nature of magical disputes demanding expedited resolution mechanisms adapted specifically to their intricacies rather than rigid formalities ill-suited for practical application today.\n\nSee also §§ 600.1-600.4, 700.5-700.9, 800.2-800.3 regarding general principles governing magical contracts and their enforcement more broadly across various contexts outside those specifically addressed hereinabove yet nonetheless sharing common underlying themes pertinent to understanding full scope implications here set forth above at length now concluded for purposes of current discussion only.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "600.12",
            "600.13",
            "600.14",
            "600.15",
            "600.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "NEW section §600.12 in this same Part only. Invent a DISTINCT catchline that belongs with these siblings, not a new subject and NOT 'Related Requirements': § 600.1 Contracts with Non-Casters; § 600.2 Familiars as Agents; § 600.3 Geas as an Unconscionable Term; § 600.4 Construct Labor; Minimum Ward; § 600.5 Summoned Notary; § 600.6 in Magical Contracts; § 600.7 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations; § 600.8 Compliance Obligations; § 600.9 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Requirements; § 600.10 Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreement; § 600.11 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations §600.11. First line must be: PAGE Your New Distinct Catchline (5-10 words, specific, no 'Related Requirements')",
