    {
      "cite": "700.12",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence",
          "text": "The provisions set forth in this section govern the admissibility and weight of magical evidence in proceedings before the Autumnwood Accords Desk or any other adjudicative body authorized under these Accords. Such matters shall be determined by reference to the principles herein, supplemented as necessary by custom and practice within the Guild.\n\n§ 700.12 Admissibility of Magical Artifacts  \nAny physical object imbued with magical properties may be admitted into evidence provided it is authenticated by a licensed Arcane Appraiser pursuant to § 300.4. Such authentication shall include a sworn statement setting forth the origin, nature, and extent of the artifact's enchantments. The opposing party shall have an opportunity to cross-examine the appraiser regarding their qualifications and methodology.\n\n§ 700.12-A Residues and Traces  \nMagical residues detected upon items or persons may be admitted as circumstantial evidence under § 800.3, subject to expert interpretation by a qualified caster. The presence of such traces does not constitute proof of wrongdoing per se but rather establishes a prima facie case requiring further substantiation. Interpretation shall conform with established protocols outlined in the Research Permits Registry maintained by the Conservator's Office.\n\n§ 700.12-B Simulacra and Illusions  \nVisual or auditory manifestations generated through illusion magic shall not be admitted unless corroborated by independent testimony or physical evidence. Where such corroboration exists, the court may draw inferences regarding intent and capability but shall refrain from assigning definitive conclusions based solely upon theatrical displays. Parties are encouraged to submit written objections prior to trial rather than relying on dramatic reenactments during proceedings.\n\n§ 700.12-C Digital Impressions  \nData traces recorded via arcane surveillance devices or memory charms may be admitted as evidence if obtained lawfully and authenticated by the issuing authority. The authenticity thereof shall be challenged only through established procedural mechanisms set forth in § 900.8, not by extrajudicial claims. Unauthorized access to such records constitutes a violation of both these Accords and applicable privacy statutes, subjecting violators to civil penalties under § 500.15 and potential criminal sanctions under Planar Law.\n\nSee also § 700.6 Standard of Proof in Arcane Disputes; § 700.8 Admissibility of Magical Traces and Residues; § 900.3 Privilege Against Self-Incrimination Under Coercion.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "700.12",
            "300.4",
            "800.3",
            "900.8",
            "500.15",
            "700.6",
            "700.8",
            "900.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.12 Magical Trace Admissibility and Chain of Custody",
          "text": "In proceedings governed by these Accords, the admissibility of magical traces and residues as evidence is strictly regulated to prevent contamination or misinterpretation. Under § 700.8, such physical manifestations may be offered only if properly preserved and authenticated according to established protocols. The party seeking admission must demonstrate an unbroken chain of custody from the point of capture through all subsequent handling, storage, and presentation at trial. Any break in this continuum renders the trace inadmissible under § 700.12(a), regardless of its apparent probative value.\n\nAuthentication requires comparison against known samples provided by opposing counsel or independent verification by a certified Arcane Forensic Examiner licensed by the Guild. See also § 300.5 regarding certification requirements for magical professionals engaged in evidentiary matters. The examining officer must issue a sworn report detailing methodology, results, and qualifications, which becomes part of the official record pursuant to § 100.7. Failure to comply with these procedural safeguards shifts the burden of proof to the submitting party, who then bears the risk of exclusion under § 700.12(b).\n\nParties may contest the relevance or reliability of offered traces through cross-examination and expert testimony. However, speculative assertions lacking factual basis are prohibited by § 400.9(c). All disputes regarding trace admissibility shall be resolved de novo upon written motion filed no later than ten days prior to trial, allowing adequate time for briefing and response. Urgent matters may receive expedited consideration at the sole discretion of the presiding authority. This framework balances the need for robust evidentiary disclosure with safeguards against abuse or manipulation inherent in magical phenomena. Adherence thereto ensures fair adjudication while maintaining public confidence in arcane judicial processes.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "700.8",
            "700.12",
            "300.5",
            "100.7",
            "400.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 700.12 Magical Trace Admissibility and Chain of Custody — Continued",
          "text": "Magical traces, residues, and artifacts recovered from the scene of alleged illegal casting may be admitted into evidence if the proponent establishes an unbroken chain of custody from discovery to presentation in court. Any break or lapse in this continuum shall result in automatic exclusion of such items unless cured by a showing of good faith efforts to maintain integrity. The Desk shall issue standardized containers and logging forms for handling magical materials, failure to comply with which constitutes grounds for suppression.\n\nWitnesses testifying regarding the chain of custody must declare under oath that they handled the item directly or supervised its transfer without interruption. Any reasonable doubt as to whether a trace has been tampered with or substituted shall be resolved in favor of the accused. The Desk retains sole authority to authenticate magical evidence and determine its relevance to pending matters. Unauthorized possession, alteration, or destruction of such items is punishable as obstruction of justice under § 1305.7 and may lead to revocation of casting privileges pursuant to § 200.9.\n\nAdmissibility of magical traces shall not depend on prior notice or consent from the accused party. However, the defense retains absolute right to inspect, test, and challenge such evidence in open session before it is admitted. The burden rests upon the prosecution to prove beyond a reasonable doubt that any trace offered is genuine, unaltered, and directly linked to the charged offense. Failure so to do shall result in immediate dismissal of those counts.\n\nThis provision operates alongside but independently from § 700.8 regarding admissibility standards for magical testimony and evidence generally. Nothing herein limits the Desk's inherent powers to regulate collection methods or preserve integrity of materials in its possession. All provisions concerning handling, storage, and presentation of magical traces shall be strictly construed in accordance with the spirit and letter of these Accords. Violations may subject violators to disciplinary action up to and including permanent expulsion from the Guild and confiscation of all property deemed contraband under § 1201.6.\n\nSee also: §§ 700.8, 1305.7, 200.9, 1201.6. Under § 1.4, these rules bind all persons subject to the jurisdiction of this Codex. In accordance with § 1.7(c), ambiguities regarding admissibility shall be resolved in favor of due process protections for the accused. As provided by § 1.9, official seals or stamps verifying authenticity shall carry full evidentiary weight unless successfully challenged. Any modifications to this section require approval by at least two-thirds vote of the Autumn Wood Desk following public notice and comment period of no less than thirty days per § 100.4(b).",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1305.7",
            "200.9",
            "700.8",
            "1201.6",
            "1.4",
            "1.7",
            "1.9",
            "100.4",
            "700.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "700.12 Magical Evidence and Chain of Custody",
          "text": "Magical evidence obtained in accordance with these provisions shall be admissible in all Arcane litigation, provided that its chain of custody has been maintained without interruption or tampering. § 700.8 Admissibility of Magical Traces and Residues mandates strict protocols for the collection and preservation of such evidence, including proper labeling, sealing, and documentation of all handling procedures. Failure to comply with these requirements shall render any resulting testimony presumptively inadmissible under § 700.9 Burden and Standard of Proof in Arcane Disputes, unless the proponent can demonstrate by clear and convincing evidence that the integrity of the magical trace was preserved despite such failures.\n\nThe burden of establishing the authenticity and reliability of any magical evidence shall rest upon the party introducing it into the record, pursuant to § 700.6 Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcan. This includes providing a detailed affidavit from the individual who collected or processed the evidence, attesting under oath to their adherence to all applicable procedures set forth in these Accords. The opposing party shall have an absolute right to cross-examine such affiants regarding any deviations from protocol that may have compromised the evidentiary value of the magical trace.\n\nIn cases where conflicting magical traces are submitted by opposing parties, the court shall apply a rigorous standard of scrutiny before admitting either into evidence. Under § 700.10 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence, the judge must determine whether each piece of evidence was gathered in good faith and with reasonable care to avoid contamination or degradation. Any doubts regarding the probative value of disputed magical traces shall be resolved against the party offering them into proof.\n\nThe provisions herein are designed to ensure that only reliable and untainted magical evidence informs judicial decision-making, thereby safeguarding the integrity of Arcane legal proceedings. By establishing clear rules for the handling of such sensitive materials, these guidelines seek to prevent abuse of power while facilitating the fair adjudication of disputes arising from complex arcane phenomena. All members of the Guild are reminded that strict compliance with these mandates is imperative to maintaining public confidence in the impartiality and efficacy of our courts.\n\nSee also §§ 700.6 Burden of Proof, 700.8 Admissibility Standards, 700.9 Chain of Custody Requirements, and foundational principles on evidence preservation laid out in § 1.4 Persons Bound and § 500.3 Prohibition Against Tampering with Evidence.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "700.8",
            "700.9",
            "700.6",
            "700.10",
            "1.4",
            "500.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Admissibility of Magical Evidence and Residues",
          "text": "§ 700.12 Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and Magical Evidence, Continued — Authentication Requirements\n\nAll magical traces, residues, and artifacts introduced as evidence must be authenticated by a sworn declaration from the producing party attesting to their provenance and integrity under penalty of perjury. Such declarations shall specify the date, time, location, and circumstances of discovery or acquisition. Where feasible, independent verification by a licensed mage with appropriate credentials shall accompany each submission to corroborate authenticity. Failure to provide requisite authentication may result in exclusion of the evidence at the discretion of the presiding judge or arbitration panel.\n\nSpecial precautions must be taken when handling magical items deemed unstable, volatile, or otherwise hazardous. In such cases, transfer of possession shall occur only within a properly shielded containment chamber equipped with emergency suppression systems. Witnesses to the transaction shall sign an affidavit confirming adherence to safety protocols and noting any anomalies observed during exchange. Breach of these safeguards may constitute grounds for dismissal of associated claims alongside potential sanctions against the responsible party.\n\nParties contesting the authenticity or relevance of offered evidence bear the burden of producing contrary proof sufficient to cast reasonable doubt upon its veracity. Mere assertions without supporting documentation shall not suffice to defeat a properly authenticated submission. However, judges retain inherent authority to admit preliminary evidence subject to further scrutiny should opposing arguments emerge during proceedings.\n\nAll submitted magical items remain property of the issuing party unless otherwise ordered by competent tribunal. Return or destruction must be effected promptly following resolution of related disputes, absent contrary directive. Neglecting these obligations may lead to forfeiture and subsequent confiscation under applicable seizure statutes. Strict compliance with authentication procedures serves both as safeguard against fraud and facilitator of just adjudication in arcane matters.\n\nsee § 700.1 Evidence Generally; see § 900.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation Procedures; see § 400.5 Standards for Admission of Magical Data in Court Proceedings\"",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "700.12",
            "700.1",
            "900.12",
            "400.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Admissibility of Magical Evidence and Residues — Continued",
          "text": "Magical traces, residues, and artifacts recovered at the scene of an alleged violation may be admitted into evidence under strict conditions prescribed by this Section. The proponent must establish a complete chain of custody from discovery to presentation, detailing every handler and transfer point with temporal precision. Any gaps in continuity shall result in automatic exclusion unless explained satisfactorily.\n\nResidues themselves bear no independent evidentiary weight without contextual corroboration. Isolated traces lacking association to the accused person or event are inadmissible as prima facie proof of guilt. The trier of fact must consider all surrounding circumstances before drawing conclusions from magical markings.\n\nExpert testimony regarding origin and meaning of residues is routinely required but not dispositive. Lay opinion on the implications of particular signatures may be offered if grounded in common knowledge rather than specialized training. Conflicting expert assessments shall be weighed according to their respective qualifications and methodology soundness.\n\nParties retain full control over whether physical evidence remains sealed until trial or is disclosed earlier. Voluntary surrender of items prior to indictment constitutes a waiver of privilege but not an admission of liability. Any attempt to conceal or destroy potentially incriminating traces subjects the actor to enhanced penalties upon conviction.\n\nThe burden rests upon the accuser to prove authenticity and relevance of all offered magical evidence beyond reasonable doubt. Mere assertion of its probative value is insufficient to overcome objections raised by opposing counsel. Trial chambers possess broad discretion to exclude prejudicial material even if technically admissible.\n\nThis Section does not preclude admission of other forms of proof including witness statements, documentary records, and circumstantial inferences drawn from the totality of facts found. Magical residues serve only as one piece within a larger mosaic toward determining truth and justice.\n\nsee § 700.8 Admissibility of Magical Traces and Residues; see § 700.6 Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcane Litigation; see § 1301.9 Rules Regarding Secrecy and Privilege in Proceedings.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "700.8",
            "700.6",
            "1301.9"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "NEW section §700.12 in this same Part only. Invent a DISTINCT catchline that belongs with these siblings, not a new subject and NOT 'Related Requirements': § 700.1 Evidence Generally; § 700.2 Scrying of Closed Books; § 700.3 Memory Charm as Spoliation; § 700.4 Simulacrum Testimony; § 700.5 Clone as Duplicate Claimant; § 700.6 Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcan; § 700.7 Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and; § 700.8 Admissibility of Magical Traces and Residues; § 700.9 Burden and Standard of Proof in Arcane Disputes; § 700.10 700.6 Competent Witnesses and Expert Testimony; § 700.11 Burden Shifting and Presumptions in Arcane Litigation. First line must be: PAGE Your New Distinct Catchline (5-10 words, specific, no 'Related Requirements')",
