    {
      "cite": "1101.12",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.6 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations",
          "text": "In the event that any magical item or construct shall suffer damage, corruption, or destabilization due to unauthorized alteration, negligent maintenance, or exposure to prohibited energies, such defect shall be deemed a \"Collateral Contamination\" under this section. The original owner or custodian of said property retains a cause of action against all parties whose actions contributed directly or indirectly to the contamination, regardless of privity of contract. Such claims may encompass not only repair costs but also consequential damages arising from loss of functionality, devaluation, or potential danger posed by the tainted object.\n\nRemedial obligations imposed upon defendants shall be commensurate with their degree of culpability and the extent of harm inflicted. A party found solely responsible for initiating the chain of events leading to contamination shall bear full liability for restoration expenses. Jointly liable actors shall share responsibility proportional to their respective fault, as determined by a competent tribunal or agreed-upon arbitrator. In cases where causation cannot be definitively established, presumptions shall favor the plaintiff, provided they demonstrate a prima facie case of probable contamination stemming from defendant conduct.\n\nPrevention measures mandated herein include mandatory inspections at regular intervals for high-risk items, implementation of fail-safes designed to contain potential failures, and comprehensive documentation of all maintenance activities. Failure to comply with such preventive protocols shall constitute negligence per se and may result in punitive damages in addition to compensatory awards. Furthermore, any party knowingly concealing known defects or risks associated with a magical artifact commits the tort of fraud by misrepresentation and shall be held strictly liable for resulting injuries.\n\nThis section stands as an integral component of the broader framework governing magical property rights and liabilities, ensuring that owners remain adequately protected against unforeseen hazards while promoting responsible stewardship of powerful artifacts within society. Through careful adherence to these provisions, all stakeholders may enjoy peace and security in their dealings with enchanted goods without fear of hidden dangers surfacing later to undo prior gains.\n\nsee § 1101.7 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion regarding similar principles applied to intangible magical influences; see also § 300.5 Stability Requirements for related maintenance standards applicable to constructs and automatons. Under § 200.9 Prohibited Spellcasting by Unlicensed Individuals, unauthorized casting may itself constitute a source of contamination warranting remedial action. This aligns with foundational provisions such as § 1.8 Effective Date; Savings which preserve existing rights notwithstanding new regulatory regimes. Finally, cross-reference to § 700.5 Burden Shifting and Presumptions in Arcane Litigation governs evidentiary standards when establishing causation between defendant conduct and alleged contamination harm.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.7",
            "300.5",
            "200.9",
            "1.8",
            "700.5",
            "1101.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations (§ 1101.12)",
          "text": "Whereas magical artifacts, whether purchased with coin or gifted freely, may bear latent taints from prior owners or their own nature, this Section governs the allocation of liability for such unintended consequences. Under § 1101.7, a seller is not strictly liable for all curses inherent to an object's history; rather, liability attaches only where the defect was known or should have been discovered through reasonable inspection. The burden rests on the buyer to examine artifacts with appropriate caution before acceptance, absent express warranties to the contrary (§ 1101.2).\n\nIf contamination manifests after transfer of title, the aggrieved party must promptly notify the seller in writing and afford a cure period not exceeding ten days, during which the seller may remediate the defect at their own expense (§ 1101.6). Failure to provide notice or an opportunity for cure forfeits any claim. Where contamination results from improper use or modification by the buyer, all claims therefor are barred unless fraud is proven beyond a reasonable doubt (§ 1101.8).\n\nIn cases of catastrophic failure causing bodily harm or property destruction, punitive damages may be awarded at the court's discretion to deter negligent conduct and protect public safety. The Guild reserves the right to intervene in any such action, seeking injunctive relief as necessary to preserve its interests and maintain orderly commerce within the magical arts industry (§ 1101.9). Nothing herein shall be construed to limit or impair any rights afforded by law or contract apart from this Section. This provision is intended solely to clarify existing principles regarding collateral damage in arcane transactions, not to create new liabilities where none previously existed.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1101.7",
            "1101.2",
            "1101.6",
            "1101.8",
            "1101.9",
            "1101.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations",
          "text": "In matters involving magical items or enchanted spaces, the Desk recognizes that unintended effects may arise beyond the scope of an initial transaction or event. This phenomenon, termed \"collateral contamination,\" occurs when residual arcane energy interacts with subsequent users, environments, or entities in unpredictable ways. While no party may be held strictly liable for all downstream consequences of their actions, they remain obligated to disclose known risks and cooperate with remediation efforts where feasible.\n\nSpecifically, a caster or seller who knowingly transfers an item imbued with dangerous properties must inform the recipient of such hazards at the time of exchange. Failure to do so constitutes gross negligence under § 100.3(c) and may trigger enhanced penalties upon discovery of harm resulting from concealed dangers. Conversely, innocent actors who acquire contaminated goods through no fault of their own are entitled to seek recourse against prior responsible parties in accordance with general principles of unjust enrichment (§ 250.4).\n\nRemedial obligations may include neutralizing residual charges, providing protective wards or talismans, and reimbursing reasonable costs incurred by affected individuals in mitigating adverse effects. The exact scope of such duties will depend on the circumstances surrounding each case, guided by equitable discretion exercised by the appropriate adjudicative body. Parties are strongly urged to consult counsel regarding potential exposure before entering into transactions involving potent artifacts or spaces.\n\nIt is further provided that collateral contamination claims shall be subject to a statute of limitations of five years from the date of first manifestation of symptoms, unless fraud or concealment is proven. This limitation ensures timely resolution of disputes while preserving historical records for future reference under § 1401.2. All such actions must be brought in accordance with established procedural requirements outlined elsewhere herein.\n\nThe Desk affirms its commitment to balancing innovation with safety in the pursuit of magical advancement. By fostering transparency and accountability, it seeks to protect both practitioners and the public from unforeseen perils inherent in arcane endeavors. Through diligent oversight and fair enforcement, the Guild aims to maintain harmony between progress and prudence for generations to come.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "100.3",
            "250.4",
            "1401.2",
            "1101.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations — Continued",
          "text": "§ 1101.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations\n\nIn the event that any magical artifact or implement becomes tainted with prohibited energies, curses, or other defilements during lawful usage or possession, such contamination shall be deemed a breach of implied warranty under § 1101.3. The original purchaser or possessor retains sole recourse against the seller or manufacturer for remediation costs arising from said taint. However, if the contamination results directly from negligent handling by a third party who was not in lawful possession at the time of defilement, such party shall bear full responsibility for all resulting damages pursuant to § 1101.9.\n\nFurthermore, any entity knowingly introducing tainted materials into circulation without disclosing their nature commits fraud under Article III and shall be subject to penalties outlined therein. The burden of proving lack of knowledge regarding the presence of hazardous magical properties rests with the alleged perpetrator per § 1101.8. In cases where contamination spreads beyond the immediate object, affected persons may seek equitable relief including injunctive orders and abatement costs under common law principles adapted for arcane contexts as recognized by this Accords Desk.\n\nThis provision shall operate in tandem with existing statutes governing hazardous materials disposal (§ 905 et seq.) while acknowledging the unique characteristics of magical artifacts. Nothing herein shall be construed to limit any rights available at law or equity, but rather to supplement them with specialized remedies tailored to the complexities inherent in magical contamination incidents. The Desk retains discretion to issue guidance memos clarifying application of this section as case law develops over time.\n\nsee § 1101.9 Gifts That Later Move; see § 905 Hazardous Materials Disposal Standards; see Common Law Equitable Remedies Doctrine",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1101.12",
            "1101.3",
            "1101.9",
            "1101.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1101.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations — Administration",
          "text": "In the event that magical artifacts or reagents are found to possess latent contaminating properties not disclosed at the time of sale, the seller shall be deemed liable for all resulting damages under the doctrine of collateral contamination. This liability extends beyond direct physical harm to include spiritual corruption, mental instability induced by psychic residue, and any other unforeseen adverse effects attributable to the inherent nature of the item sold. Sellers are strictly prohibited from disclaiming knowledge or warning of such latent dangers through fine print or equivocal language; affirmative disclosure is mandatory prior to any transaction involving potentially hazardous magical goods.\n\nRemedial obligations imposed upon sellers of contaminated items may include full restitution, replacement with equivalent or superior goods free of defect, compensation for consequential losses, and reimbursement for all costs incurred in mitigating the effects of contamination. In egregious cases where a seller knowingly concealed the presence of dangerous contaminants, punitive damages shall be assessed at the discretion of the court. The burden of proof regarding both the existence of latent contamination and its causal connection to alleged injuries rests upon the plaintiff; however, sellers are required to maintain comprehensive records documenting the origin, composition, and testing history of all products marketed for public use. Failure to produce such documentation when requested by regulatory authorities or judicial officers shall result in automatic dismissal of any defense raised by the seller and a presumption against their credibility on matters related to product safety.\n\nThe principles set forth herein apply with equal force whether the contamination arises from natural properties of an artifact, improper storage or handling by the seller, or deliberate tampering intended to mask defects. Nothing in this section shall be construed as limiting or abrogating any rights afforded under other applicable provisions of these Accords, including those relating to consumer protection, fraud prevention, and public health and safety. Sellers who violate the obligations imposed herein may face severe penalties ranging from revocation of their license to operate within Guild jurisdiction to permanent expulsion and forfeiture of all assets derived from illicit activities. The ultimate goal of this provision is to ensure that consumers are fully informed of all risks associated with purchasing magical items, thereby preventing unnecessary harm and promoting fair trade practices throughout the realm.\n\nsee § 1101.7 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion; see § 1201.5 Defects and Warranties in Planar Transactions; under § 300.4 Standards of Care for Magical Professionals.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1101.7",
            "1201.5",
            "300.4",
            "1101.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Remedial Protocols for Residual Arcane Echoes",
          "text": "The presence of residual arcane echoes within a physical site shall be deemed a breach of the primary containment standards established under § 1.7. When a spell, ritual, or conjuration leaves behind a lingering magical resonance that persists beyond the intended duration of the casting, the practitioner is held strictly liable for the stabilization of said echo. Such residues may manifest as localized fluctuations in the weave, minor environmental distortions, or latent and unpredictable magical discharges. Under § 1101.7, these manifestations are classified as \"Collateral Taints,\" and any mage permitted to operate within the Autumn Wood must ensure that their primary casting does not leave a lasting signature upon the local environment. If an echo is detected by the Guild’s monitoring wards, the practitioner must initiate immediate containment procedures as outlined in § 400.12.\n\nIn instances where a ritual intended for private study results in an uncontrolled expansion of magical residue into public or shared spaces, the practitioner shall be subject to mandatory remediation. This process requires the application of a neutralizing seal to ground the excess energy. Pursuant to § 1.9, such seals must be officially stamped by a certified Steward before they are deemed valid for permanent site closure. Failure to proactively stabilize an echo may result in the forfeiture of research privileges and potential referral to the Paradox Trial as defined under § 100.6. The Guild maintains that any lingering magic not properly bound is a hazard to both the stability of the realm and the safety of non-magical entities, necessitating strict adherence to these remediation protocols.\n\nFurthermore, if an arcane echo persists for more than three lunar cycles without intervention, it shall be classified as a \"Stagnant Leak.\" In such cases, the perpetrator may be compelled to submit their original casting scrolls and notes to the Office of Archmage Veyra for forensic review. This process is conducted under § 100.1 to determine if the transgression was an act of negligence or a deliberate attempt to bypass the restrictions on unauthorized field use. Any mage found in violation of these protocols shall be required to pay a purification fee, which may be deducted from their standing within the Innovator or Conservator factions as appropriate under § 1.4. All remedial actions must be logged and filed with the Desk; failure to provide such documentation will result in an immediate suspension of casting privileges until a full audit is completed.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1.7",
            "1101.7",
            "400.12",
            "1.9",
            "100.6",
            "100.1",
            "1.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "NEW section §1101.12 in this same Part only. Invent a DISTINCT catchline that belongs with these siblings, not a new subject and NOT 'Related Requirements': § 1101.1 Gifts That Later Move; § 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title; § 1101.3 Curse as Warranty Disclaimer; § 1101.4 Blessing of a Competing Vendor; § 1101.5 Conveyance and Ownership Interests in Magical Artifacts; § 1101.6 Collateral Doctrine — Unintended Consequences; § 1101.7 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion; § 1101.8 Burden of Proof and Presumptions; § 1101.9 Gifts That Later Move; § 1101.10 Gifts That Later Move; § 1101.11 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion. First line must be: PAGE Your New Distinct Catchline (5-10 words, specific, no 'Related Requirements')",
