    {
      "cite": "1201.12",
      "part": "part_xii",
      "title": "Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.12 Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations",
          "text": "§ 1201.12 All casters intending to utilize portals or planar gates must obtain prior transit clearance from the Office of Archmage Sovereign. Such clearance shall be granted only upon presentation of valid identification, proof of registration under this Code, and a completed application detailing the purpose and destination of intended travel. Failure to secure proper authorization for planar transit constitutes a Class A misdemeanor punishable by fine and/or imprisonment not exceeding one year at the court's discretion.\n\nUpon entry into or exit from any realm other than the Prime Material Plane, each caster shall be required to surrender their identification credentials together with an official stamp affixed by the receiving jurisdiction. This stamp serves as evidence of lawful passage and compliance with all applicable regulations governing cross-dimensional movement. Casters found without appropriate documentation upon arrival in a foreign domain may be detained pending verification of their identity and authority to operate within those bounds.\n\nThe Office of Archmage Sovereign reserves the right to impose additional requirements or restrictions on planar travel as deemed necessary for the maintenance of security and order across all planes of existence. Such measures shall be promulgated through formal notice published in the Autumnwood Gazette at least thirty days prior to implementation, allowing affected parties adequate opportunity to adjust their operations accordingly. Casters failing to comply with any lawful directive regarding planar transit shall subject themselves to enhanced scrutiny and possible revocation of their casting privileges entirely.\n\nIt is further provided that all disputes arising from violations of this section shall be resolved through binding arbitration before a panel of qualified arcane litigators appointed by the Office of Archmage Sovereign. The costs associated with such proceedings shall be borne equally by both parties unless otherwise determined by the arbitrators based on factors including but not limited to fault, financial status, and mitigating circumstances presented during hearings held under oath before the full court.\n\nThe provisions set forth herein apply without limitation to any person or entity engaging in activities involving interdimensional travel regardless of nationality, residency status, or other distinguishing characteristics irrelevant to the core objective of regulating potentially hazardous magical practices conducted outside conventional spatial parameters. Nothing contained in this subsection shall be construed as diminishing or impairing any rights afforded by existing treaties, accords, or agreements entered into between nations and governing bodies concerned with controlling access to non-Earthly locales.\n\nIn witness whereof, we hereunto append our signatures beneath the official seal of the Mages' Guild dated true and correct this ____ day of ____________, in the year of Our Lord ________.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1201.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.12 Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations — Continued",
          "text": "Under § 1201, all mages utilizing planar transit for commercial or official purposes are subject to strict stamping requirements. This ensures proper taxation of inter-dimensional commerce while maintaining security oversight over magical traffic flows. Failure to comply with these obligations may result in confiscation of cargo and substantial fines pursuant to § 800.\n\nThe mandatory stamp serves as proof that the party has paid applicable tolls and fees, and agrees to abide by all host nation laws during their stay. The design incorporates unique identifiers for tracking purposes, allowing rapid verification at checkpoints throughout the realm. Mages must carry original copies of valid stamps or risk being turned away at borders.\n\nSpecial provisions apply to diplomatic missions and humanitarian aid efforts, which may receive reduced rates upon presentation of appropriate credentials under § 100. Conversely, repeat offenders face escalating penalties including license revocation and permanent bans from planar transit networks entirely. Such harsh measures reflect the Guild's zero-tolerance stance against tax evasion in this critical revenue area.\n\nThe regulations further mandate that all shipments originating from foreign lands undergo comprehensive inspection upon arrival. This includes verification of declared contents against manifest documents, as well as screening for contraband or prohibited items. Unlawful goods discovered in transit will be seized and destroyed at the discretion of local authorities. Ship owners bear ultimate responsibility for ensuring their cargo meets all safety standards before departure.\n\nTo facilitate smoother operations, designated processing centers have been established near major portals throughout the kingdom. These facilities provide expedited clearing services for compliant parties while detaining suspicious shipments pending further investigation. Payment terminals are available at each location to handle transactions efficiently in local currency or approved foreign exchange rates. Mages are encouraged to utilize these convenient options rather than attempting ad-hoc arrangements outside official channels.\n\nOverall, the stamping regime represents an essential component of the broader planar management framework laid out herein. By enforcing rigorous controls over magical travel and trade, the Accords safeguard both economic interests and public safety alike. Continued adherence by all stakeholders remains paramount to preserving stable relations between realms and preventing future conflicts stemming from unchecked expansionism.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1201.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.12 Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations — Portal Licensing Framework",
          "text": "Under this section, any magical portal or gate serving as an interplanar conduit shall be subject to mandatory licensing by the Autumnwood Accords Desk. Licenses are issued pursuant to a rigorous vetting process that assesses the applicant's intended use, security measures, and financial capability to post bonds for potential damages. License fees scale with the portal's capacity and risk profile, ranging from nominal charges for minor wards to substantial premiums for high-yield transit hubs. Failure to obtain proper authorization constitutes a Class A felony punishable by confiscation of magical assets and permanent revocation of one's casting rights.\n\nOperators must maintain detailed logs of all transits, including passenger manifests, cargo descriptions, and timestamps. These records are subject to random audit by Guild Inspectors or the Aegis Magi at any time. False reporting shall be prosecuted as fraud under § 2105.3 et seq., with enhanced penalties for repeat offenders. Operators discovered evading licensing requirements face immediate seizure of their equipment and imprisonment without bail pending trial.\n\nThe Desk retains discretion to deny, suspend, or revoke licenses based on national security concerns, public welfare considerations, or operator misconduct. Affected parties may petition for reconsideration within thirty days of notification; appeals beyond that deadline are non-binding and subject only to the Desk's final ruling. Revoked operators must surrender all active licenses immediately upon receipt of written notice.\n\nUnlicensed portals discovered during routine inspections shall be sealed and forfeited to the Guild, along with all associated paraphernalia. Owners face misdemeanor charges unless they can prove lack of knowledge regarding the portal's existence or operation. In such cases, penalties are mitigated but still enforced strictly to deter future violations. The Desk publishes an annual report detailing licensing statistics, enforcement actions, and revenue generated from these fees, which funds public magical infrastructure projects.\n\n§ 1201.12 Compliance is paramount for safe and regulated interplanar commerce. Operators must adhere to all stipulations without exception, understanding that leniency will not be extended under any circumstances. The Guild stands ready to enforce this mandate with unwavering resolve to protect both the public good and the integrity of magical practice across all realms.\"",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "2105.3",
            "1201.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.13 Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations — Portal Licensing Framework",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords recognize that planar transit, whether by natural phenomena or artificial means, constitutes a regulated activity with profound implications for both territorial sovereignty and magical security. Under § 1201.6, any entity operating a permanent portal or gateway between planes must secure a Planar Transit License from the Archmage Sovereign's Desk prior to commencing operations. This requirement applies regardless of whether the portal serves commercial, research, or personal purposes.\n\nA Planar Transit License is distinct from standard magical permits and requires extensive vetting of both the operator and the intended use cases. Applicants must submit detailed schematics, risk assessments, and contingency plans demonstrating their ability to manage potential hazards arising from planar instability. The Desk retains broad discretion in approving or denying applications based on national security considerations.\n\nLicensees are required to display their credentials prominently at all times during transit operations and maintain accurate logs of all passages through their portals. Failure to comply with these recordkeeping requirements constitutes grounds for immediate license revocation under § 1201.6(a)(iv). Additionally, licensed operators must pay an annual renewal fee calculated according to a sliding scale based on projected traffic volume, as set forth in Schedule A thereto.\n\nThe Accords further provide that unlicensed planar transit is strictly prohibited and punishable as a felony offense under § 200.1(b). Violators face mandatory seizure of all equipment involved, along with substantial fines and potential imprisonment. Repeat offenses carry enhanced penalties including permanent disqualification from practicing magic within the Guild's jurisdiction.\n\nSection 1201.8 grants the Desk authority to inspect any suspected unauthorized portal without warrant upon reasonable suspicion. Inspectors may temporarily disable or seal the device pending further investigation at their sole discretion. All costs associated with such actions shall be borne by the responsible party, who remains liable for damages resulting from non-compliance.\n\nThe Archmage Sovereign reserves the right to modify licensing requirements and fees as necessary to adapt to evolving threats and technological advancements. Such changes shall take effect upon publication in the Official Gazette with a minimum thirty-day notice period afforded to licensees.\n\nThis framework ensures that planar transit remains a controlled activity subject to rigorous oversight, thereby protecting both public safety and the integrity of interdimensional boundaries. Compliance with these provisions is mandatory for all entities engaged in such pursuits under the purview of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1201.6",
            "200.1",
            "1201.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.12 Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations — Portal Licensing Framework — Continued",
          "text": "Any individual or entity intending to utilize a magical portal for transit purposes must first obtain explicit authorization from the Autumnwood Accords Desk via issuance of a valid Portal Use Permit (§ PUP-1). Such permits are non-transferable and shall be subject to rigorous background checks, spellcasting proficiency assessments, and risk evaluations conducted by qualified Aegis Magi. Failure to secure proper licensing prior to employing planar travel mechanisms constitutes a Class B Felony under § 200.3(a), punishable by immediate arrest, confiscation of all magical implements, and potential revocation of existing Guild credentials.\n\nThe Desk reserves the right to inspect any PUP holder's records at reasonable intervals to verify compliance with permit terms and conditions. This includes review of travel logs, expense reports, and incident summaries submitted within forty-eight (48) hours following each journey. Persistent failure to maintain accurate documentation may result in suspension or cancellation of privileges without notice. Furthermore, unlicensed use of portals for commercial gain or malicious purposes shall be prosecuted as grand larceny under § 1102.7(c), carrying penalties commensurate with the severity of harm inflicted upon third parties and public infrastructure.\n\nIn cases where emergency planar travel is deemed necessary to prevent imminent loss of life, property damage exceeding one million gold pieces (G.P.), or catastrophic environmental disruption, individuals may invoke the Emergency Override Protocol (§ EOP-1). Upon notification to the Desk via encrypted magical communication channels, such actions shall be presumed justified and post-hoc reviewed within seven business days. However, repeated abuse of this provision without legitimate cause will trigger automatic forfeiture of all remaining PUPs and subject the violator to enhanced monitoring for a period not less than two (2) years from date of final adjudication.\n\nThe Desk further mandates that all licensed portal operators maintain comprehensive insurance coverage with minimum limits set at five times their annual gross revenue or ten million G.P., whichever is greater. Policies must name the Autumnwood Accords as an additional insured party and explicitly cover liabilities arising from planar accidents, unauthorized crossings, and resulting damages to persons or property. Proof of adequate bonding shall be submitted quarterly alongside routine reporting filings under § 1204.3(b). Non-compliance with these financial requirements renders any PUP void ab initio and subject to immediate revocation pending further investigation by the Aegis Magi.\n\nIt is hereby declared that nothing contained herein shall be construed as granting immunity from criminal liability for acts committed during authorized planar transit operations. All applicable laws regarding assault, theft, fraud, and other offenses remain fully enforceable regardless of jurisdictional boundaries crossed via magical means. Individuals found guilty of violating these stipulations may face concurrent prosecution in both terrestrial courts and the Arcane Tribunal, with punishments to be combined as directed by presiding magistrates exercising sound discretion under § 1302.4(g).\n\nBy virtue of this enactment, the Autumnwood Accords Desk reaffirms its sovereign authority over all matters pertaining to interdimensional travel within its sphere of influence. This includes but is not limited to regulation of portal construction standards, oversight of transit routes, enforcement of safety protocols, and imposition of equitable tolls reflecting true cost-of-service metrics adjusted annually for inflationary pressures affecting the broader economy. Any attempts by rogue elements to circumvent these controls shall be met with swift and decisive action designed to protect both public welfare and Guild interests alike.\"\n\n\"See also § 1201.5 Integrated Obligations; § 1203.6 Enhanced Monitoring Requirements.\"",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.3",
            "1102.7",
            "1204.3",
            "1302.4",
            "1201.5",
            "1203.6",
            "1201.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "§ 1201.13 Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations — Portal Licensing Framework — Continued",
          "text": "Under § 1201, all portals used for interplanar travel must be licensed by the Guild's Bureau of Arcane Infrastructure (BAI). Licensees shall pay an annual fee based on portal capacity and usage metrics defined in Appendix D. Unauthorized activation or operation of unlicensed portals is prohibited under pain of seizure and forfeiture. The BAI retains authority to inspect, audit, and shut down noncompliant facilities at any time with reasonable notice. Violators may face civil penalties up to fifty thousand gold pieces per violation plus treble damages for any losses suffered by the Guild. Willful defiance subjects offenders to criminal prosecution under § 200's provisions on illegal spellcasting operations. All licensing agreements shall be governed by the laws of Autumnwood and interpreted in accordance with this Code. Disputes arising hereunder are subject to exclusive jurisdiction of the Mages' Court sitting en banc. Nothing herein shall impair any rights or remedies available at common law or under other applicable statutes. The provisions of this section shall remain in full force notwithstanding any contrary terms contained in private contracts or oral understandings between parties. Any attempt to contract away these obligations is void ab initio and unenforceable as against public policy. The BAI may issue emergency shutdown orders without prior notice where imminent danger to life or property is demonstrated by clear and convincing evidence. Failure to comply with such orders constitutes contempt of court punishable by fines, imprisonment, or both at the discretion of the presiding judge. All portals must display conspicuous signage identifying them as regulated facilities subject to inspection under this Act. Licensees shall maintain accurate logs of all transit activity for a period of no less than five years from date of last operation. These records are available for review upon proper demand by authorized Guild officials or their representatives duly appointed in writing. The penalties prescribed herein are cumulative and without limitation, reflecting the serious nature of violations involving interplanar commerce and safety. Nothing contained in this section shall be construed to limit the applicability of other relevant statutes or regulations governing magical devices and infrastructure throughout the Kingdoms of Midgardia.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1201.13"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "NEW section §1201.12 in this same Part only. Invent a DISTINCT catchline that belongs with these siblings, not a new subject and NOT 'Related Requirements': § 1201.1 Transit Stamps; § 1201.2 Planar Tolls; § 1201.3 Extraterritorial Casting into a Diet Chamber; § 1201.4 Cartography Sold as a License to Raid; § 1201.5 Integrated Obligations; § 1201.6 Transit Stamps; Planar Tolls; Extraterritorial C; § 1201.7 Section 1201 – Portal and Planar Regulations; § 1201.8 Extraterritorial Jurisdiction and Planar Sovereignty; § 1201.9 Waiver and Modification; § 1201.10 Extra-Territorial Jurisdiction and Planar Tolls; § 1201.11 Extraterritorial Jurisdiction and Planar Tolls. First line must be: PAGE Your New Distinct Catchline (5-10 words, specific, no 'Related Requirements')",
      "source": "lmstudio",
