        },
        {
          "heading": "SEMANTIC AMBIGUITY AND TERMINOLOGICAL HARMONIZATION",
          "text": "The interpretation of the Autumnwood Accords shall not rest upon colloquial usage or common parlance found within the public sphere, but rather upon the strict lexicographical definitions established herein. In instances where a term’s meaning may be contested by external parties—specifically those entities governed by the Iron Mandate or other non-aligned jurisdictions—the court of the Autumnwood Accords shall default to the internal lexicon of the Guild. Any attempt to substitute \"commoner\" terminology for \"Arcane\" technicality in legal disputes will be disregarded as a procedural defect. This ensures that the nuances of magical theory remain insulated from the influence of lay interpretations, maintaining the integrity of the Accord’s core mandates.\n\nWhere linguistic ambiguity arises due to the conflicting interests of the Conservators and the Innovators, the Office of Archmage Veyra shall serve as the final arbiter of intent. Under § 1.6, specific technical terms regarding \"Forbidden Schools\" are non-negotiable; however, should a dispute arise over whether a newly discovered technique falls under these prohibitions or constitutes \"Restricted Research,\" the presiding magistrate shall consult the records of the Innovators to determine if a petition for variance was ever filed. Such disputes must be resolved in private chambers to ensure that the internal deliberations regarding innovation remain shielded from public scrutiny, as mandated by the protections against unauthorized disclosure.\n\nFurthermore, all legal instruments involving non-caster entities, including those under contract with the Goodstyle Artisans, shall require the use of standardized terminology defined in § 600.11. A failure to utilize precise nomenclature when drafting contracts for construction or labor may result in a presumption of negligence on the part of the casting party. No term found within these pages shall be construed as an admission of liability toward external entities, nor shall any \"common\" phrasing be accepted as a substitute for the formal designations required by the Accord's primary statutes.\n\nIn cases where linguistic overlap occurs between the Autumnwood Accords and regional laws, the specific protections granted under § 1.5 shall supersede local customs. Any attempt to conflate \"sanctioned research\" with \"unauthorized field usage\" due to a lack of clarity in local dialect will be strictly prohibited. The prosecution of such ambiguity is vital for the safety of the Midlands; thus, any term related to high-level ritualism must be interpreted through the lens of the Academy’s core texts rather than regional tradition. All practitioners are expected to maintain consistency in their filings to ensure that no petition for a Research Permit is denied due to improper phrasing or linguistic drift.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "600.11",
            "1.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Semantic Ambiguity and Terminological Harmonization — Continued",
          "text": "The interpretation of the Autumnwood Accords shall prioritize lexical consistency across all sub-sections to ensure that the mandate of the Archmage Veyra remains uniform throughout the Midlands. Where technical terminology is employed, such as \"arcane,\" \"mana,\" or \"rift,\" the meanings assigned in § 1.6 shall govern all subsequent interpretations. Any ambiguity arising from the common usage of magical terms by non-affiliated parties—specifically those associated with the Iron Mandate or other external jurisdictions—shall be disregarded in favor of the codified definitions within this Codex. The Guild maintains that clarity is the primary defense against the degradation of arcane stability, and thus, any term not explicitly defined in § 1.6 must be interpreted according to its most stable and traditional application as overseen by the Conservators.\n\nIn instances where the Innovator faction proposes the adoption of new terminology to describe emergent phenomena or experimental schools, such terms shall not be integrated into the legal corpus until they have undergone a period of formal scrutiny. Under § 400.12, any experimental nomenclature must be clearly distinguished from established classifications to prevent the accidental overlap of restricted and permitted activities. It is strictly prohibited for practitioners to substitute colloquialisms or local dialects in place of official terminology when filing reports with the Office of Archmage Veyra. Such substitutions may lead to a failure of intent during legal review, potentially resulting in a determination of unauthorized conduct under § 200.11.\n\nFurthermore, the doctrine of Terminological Harmonization requires that all contracts involving third parties, including those with Goodstyle Artisans, must utilize the specific phrasing outlined in § 600.11. Any ambiguity created by external legal influence or conflicting terminology from the Regal Empire shall be voided upon the invocation of this section. The Guild does not recognize \"common sense\" as a valid interpretive tool; rather, all disputes regarding the meaning of a specific clause must be referred to the Desk for a definitive ruling based on the prevailing definitions in § 1.6 and the construction principles found in § 1.7.\n\nThe enforcement of these standards is overseen by the Aegis Magi, who are tasked with ensuring that any \"semantic drift\" does not compromise the integrity of the Accord's safety protocols. Should a practitioner be found intentionally using ambiguous language to circumvent restrictions on forbidden schools, such an act shall be viewed as a willful evasion of § 300.11 and may lead to immediate referral for a Paradox Trial. The consistency of these terms is essential to ensure that the distinction between \"restricted research\" and \"forbidden acts\" remains absolute and unambiguous throughout all jurisdictions covered under § 1.5.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "400.12",
            "200.11",
            "600.11",
            "1.7",
            "300.11",
            "1.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "SEMANTIC STABILITY AND TERMINOLOGICAL UNIFORMITY",
          "text": "The determination of arcane intent within the Autumnwood Accords necessitates a rigid adherence to standardized terminology to mitigate the risks posed by linguistic drift and interpretive variance. Because magic is inherently tied to the precision of spoken and inscribed symbols, any ambiguity in the legal lexicon may result in unintended metaphysical consequences or jurisdictional overlap. Therefore, all terms defined under § 1.6 shall be interpreted strictly according to their primary definitions unless explicitly modified by a specific sub-clause. The Office of the Archmage Sovereign maintains that \"semantic stability\" is not merely a matter of clerical convenience but a foundational requirement for the containment of volatile energies. When a term’s meaning is contested due to its usage in different contexts, the interpretation favored by the Conservators—emphasizing traditional safety and literalist application—shall prevail unless an Innovator permit under § 400.11 specifically authorizes a broader interpretive scope for experimental research.\n\nFurthermore, the Harmony of Terms requires that any terminology introduced by external entities, such as the Goodstyle Artisans or independent contractors, must be reconciled with the Accord’s primary lexicon before being codified into binding agreements. Under § 600.11, non-casters entering into contracts with the Guild must provide a \"Glossary of Intent\" if their specific trade terminology conflicts with standard magical definitions. Failure to harmonize these terms may result in the immediate voiding of the contract and the potential for collateral taints as outlined in § 1101.11. The Clerk’s Desk shall not recognize any colloquialism or slang as a valid legal substitute for established arcane descriptors; thus, any petition containing \"non-standard\" terminology is subject to summary rejection under § 1.7 regarding the construction of ambiguous phrasing.\n\nIn cases where an Innovator seeks to redefine a term within the scope of a restricted research project, such a change remains localized and does not alter the global definitions contained in this Codex. The distinction between \"Restricted Research\" and \"Forbidden Schools\" is maintained strictly through the clarity of these terms; any attempt to blur these lines via linguistic ambiguity will be prosecuted as an intentional circumvention of the Accord’s safety protocols. For example, a term used to describe a dangerous transmutation must not be substituted with a more benign-sounding synonym in a permit application. All such applications are subject to review under § 400.12 to ensure that the integrity of the original prohibition remains intact despite changes in common parlance.\n\nFinally, any interpretation granted by the Archmage Veyra during mediation must be documented as an \"Interpretative Clarification\" and filed with the Desk for inclusion in subsequent audits. These clarifications do not amend § 1.6 but serve as a guide for the Aegis Magi when enforcing the law on the ground. Should a term become so archaic or ambiguous that it no longer serves its intended purpose, the petition for \"Terminological Update\" must be submitted to the Conservators for review. If approved, the new term shall be appended to the Codex with an explicit cross-reference to the original definition to ensure continuity of law as mandated by § 1401.12. Any attempt to introduce a term that mirrors the nomenclature used in the Iron Mandate or other external jurisdictions is strictly prohibited and may result in immediate seizure under § 800.11.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "400.11",
            "600.11",
            "1101.11",
            "1.7",
            "400.12",
            "1401.12",
            "800.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "SEMANTIC STABILITY AND TERMINOLOGICAL UNIFORMITY — Continued",
          "text": "The interpretation of the Autumnwood Accords shall be governed by the principle of Semantic Stability, which mandates that terms defined within this Codex maintain a fixed meaning regardless of external cultural shifts or colloquial evolutions in common parlance. When the text refers to \"Arcane,\" it signifies specifically those manifestations of magic codified under the oversight of the Archmage Veyra and the governing bodies of the Autumn Wood; it does not encompass mundane pyrotechnics or non-magical chemical reactions. Any attempt by an external party, such as the Royal Empire or the Iron Legion, to redefine these terms through diplomatic decree shall be deemed a violation of the Accord’s core integrity. The Clerk's Desk maintains that consistency in terminology is the primary defense against the erosion of magical safety.\n\nIn instances where linguistic drift occurs among the common citizenry or within non-affiliated organizations, such as the Goodstyle Artisans, the definitions provided under § 1.6 shall remain the sole binding authority for the purposes of legal arbitration. For example, a \"Contract\" involving magic must adhere strictly to the technical requirements of spell-binding and mana-conveyance described in the codified sections, rather than the general meaning of an agreement found in secular law. Any ambiguity arising from local dialects or vernacular usage shall be resolved in favor of the literal interpretation provided by the Guild’s own records. This ensures that the legal boundaries between \"permitted\" and \"forbidden\" remains absolute and unyielding to external pressures.\n\nFurthermore, all documents submitted to the Desk for filing must utilize the standardized lexicon established herein. The use of synonyms or poetic descriptors in place of codified terms is strictly prohibited in formal filings. If a petitioner wishes to describe a process that falls between two defined categories, they must seek a specific variance under § 1.12 rather than attempting to introduce ambiguous terminology into the public record. This rigor prevents the \"dilution\" of the law by ensuring that every word carries a precise, singular weight in the eyes of the Aegis Magi and the presiding Archmages.\n\nRegarding the distinction between internal disputes and external threats, any term pertaining to \"Security\" or \"Defense\" shall be interpreted through the lens of magical stability rather than physical fortification. This distinction is vital when interacting with entities such as the Silver Flame, whose definitions of \"defense\" may involve non-magical methods that do not fall under the jurisdiction of § 400.11. By maintaining a rigid terminological uniformity, the Autumnwood Accords ensure that the scope of the Guild’s authority is clearly delineated from the conflicting laws of the Iron Mandate or other neighboring jurisdictions. All interpretive doubts must be referred to the Office of Archmage Veyra for final adjudication under § 100.1.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "1.12",
            "400.11",
            "100.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "SEMANTIC STABILITY AND TERMINOLOGICAL UNIFORMITY — Administration",
          "text": "The stability of the Autumnwood Accords relies upon the absolute consistency of terminology employed within all official decrees, petitions, and internal correspondences. To ensure that legal intent remains immutable regardless of shifting cultural dialects or linguistic drift in the Midlands, any term identified as a \"Technical Arcane Term\" must be interpreted strictly according to its definition under § 1.6. When disputes arise regarding the intended meaning of a clause, the court shall prioritize the established lexicon over common parlance. This requirement for semantic stability is essential for maintaining the distinction between permissible research and forbidden arts; any ambiguity in terminology that could grant an unauthorized practitioner access to restricted schools shall be interpreted in favor of the restrictive construction as outlined under § 300.11.\n\nFurthermore, the Accord mandates a policy of terminological harmonization across all sub-factions. While the Innovators and Conservators may hold divergent philosophical views on the nature of magic, they are bound by the unified terminology of this Codex. For instance, terms concerning \"Research Integrity\" must remain consistent whether applied to supervised experiments or unauthorized field operations. Any attempt to redefine common magical descriptors for political leverage shall be deemed a violation of the Accord's integrity. In cases where an external entity—such as those governed by the Iron Mandate—attempts to introduce conflicting terminology into a shared jurisdictional space, the terms herein shall supersede all outside definitions.\n\nThe determination of semantic stability also extends to the physical manifestations of magic and its subsequent documentation. The distinction between \"Seal Magic\" and \"Standardized Binding\" must remain absolute; for clarity on the legal consequences of such distinctions, see § 1301.12. Any term that could potentially conflate a permitted action with a prohibited one is subject to immediate rectification by the Office of Archmage Veyra. Furthermore, the identification of specific entities and geographic locations must remain static; any variance in the naming of landmarks or known magical anomalies shall be cross-referenced against the official records under § 100.4 to ensure that no ambiguity exists regarding the jurisdiction of a specific incident.\n\nIn instances where linguistic evolution threatens the clarity of the Accord, the Office of Archmage Veyra may issue a Clarifying Decree. Such decrees do not alter the underlying law but provide a binding interpretation for specific terms found in the text. These interpretations are to be treated as permanent additions to the lexicon and must be cited when adjudicating disputes over intent. The standard of evidence required to challenge a codified term is high; any claimant seeking a revision of a definition must prove that the current terminology causes an unavoidable risk to public safety or magical stability, as defined under § 1.7. Under no circumstances shall the omission of a specific word from a clause be interpreted as a waiver of the underlying restriction unless explicitly permitted by the provisions in § 1.12.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "300.11",
            "1301.12",
            "100.4",
            "1.7",
            "1.12"
          ]
        }
      ],
