    {
      "cite": "200.13",
      "part": "part_ii",
      "title": "Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation",
          "text": "The provisions set forth within this section establish the mandatory protocols regarding the containment of volatile arcane phenomena and the subsequent mitigation of environmental breaches. Under the authority of the Autumnwood Accords, any mage—regardless of their standing among the Conservators or the Innovators—is bound by the duty to ensure that high-level somatic or gestural components do not fracture into uncontrolled localized reality distortions. Such occurrences are strictly governed under § 1.6 regarding the definition of \"Arcane Instability,\" which includes, but is not limited to, spontaneous rift generation and lingering mana-residue blooms. Should a practitioner initiate a spell that exceeds their current licensure level as defined in § 200.1, they are personally liable for any containment failures resulting from insufficient anchoring rituals.\n\nIn the event of an Arcane Breach, the affected area shall be declared a Restricted Zone. During such a declaration, all unauthorized magic is strictly prohibited under § 200.11. The primary objective of the responding Aegis Magi shall be the stabilization of the local weave and the containment of \"contagious\" mana. Any mage found within a designated breach zone who does not possess a valid Research Permit for high-risk environments as outlined in § 400.11 may be subject to immediate detention and search by the presiding officers. The determination of whether a breach constitutes an intentional act or an accidental oversight shall be evaluated during any subsequent Paradox Trial, where the severity of the negligence will dictate the specific penalty under § 200.9.\n\nFurthermore, no practitioner shall utilize experimental methods that have not been vetted by the Innovator council without the express prior consent of Archmage Veyra. Such experiments are subject to the oversight protocols detailed in § 400.12. If a breach occurs during an authorized experiment, the liability remains with the primary researcher unless it can be proven that the breach was caused by external interference or unforeseen environmental factors as defined under § 700.11 regarding the burden of proof in arcane litigation.\n\nAll evidence gathered from a containment site, including physical artifacts and residual magical signatures, must be sealed and logged according to the standards of § 1.9. Any attempt to conceal a breach or to falsify the extent of a mana-leak constitutes an offense against the Accord's integrity. Specifically, the intentional omission of \"contagion\" data from official reports shall result in the immediate revocation of all active licenses and may lead to a permanent ban under § 200.4. All containment procedures must be executed with the utmost precision to ensure that no breach enters the public sphere or impacts the sanctity of the Autumn Wood, as governed by the overarching protections of § 1.5 regarding the supremacy of the Accord over local municipal law.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "200.1",
            "200.11",
            "400.11",
            "200.9",
            "400.12",
            "700.11",
            "1.9",
            "200.4",
            "1.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Procedures for the Stabilization of Volatile Weave-Fractures",
          "text": "In the event that a magical discharge results in a localized instability or \"weave-fracture,\" the primary obligation of any practitioner present is the immediate containment of the affected area to prevent the spread of arcane contagion. Such an occurrence constitutes a breach of standard safety protocols and may necessitate the deployment of the Aegis Magi to secure the perimeter. Under § 1.6, such instabilities are defined as unauthorized fluctuations in the local mana density that threaten the structural integrity of the physical realm. Upon detection of a fracture, the presiding mage must initiate a containment field to isolate the anomaly. If the breach exceeds the capacity of standard stabilization wards, the authorities shall invoke the protocols for emergency intervention as outlined under § 500.12, ensuring that any resulting magical fallout does not migrate into neighboring territories or public thoroughfares.\n\nThe stabilization process requires a methodical approach to arcane containment, prioritizing the suppression of chaotic energy over the preservation of local property. In instances where an unauthorized ritual has caused a rift or significant mana bleed, the responding officers must determine if the breach is \"active\" or \"residual.\" Active breaches require immediate dampening spells to prevent further expansion, while residual bleaches may be addressed through gradual grounding techniques. All actions taken during these procedures are subject to review by the Conservators to ensure that no experimental methods from the Innovator faction have compromised the stability of the region's magical foundation. Failure to contain a breach promptly can result in the imposition of severe penalties as defined under § 800.12, particularly if the leak results in permanent environmental degradation or \"collateral taints\" as described in § 1101.11.\n\nFurthermore, any containment equipment used during these operations must bear the official seal of the Autumnwood Accords to be recognized as legal instruments of stabilization. Unauthorized tools or uncertified reagents may lead to a forfeiture of immunity for the practitioner involved. If a breach occurs within a research facility, the protocols for research integrity and oversight shall take precedence, requiring a full audit of the laboratory's safety permits under § 400.12. Any mage found utilizing non-sanctioned methods to mitigate such breaches may be subject to an immediate investigation by the Office of Archmage Veyra to determine if they have violated the core tenets of the Accords. All logs regarding containment efforts must be filed with the Desk within three lunar cycles, ensuring that every instance of a breach is documented for the preservation of canonical continuity under § 1401.12.\n\nIn cases where a breach involves entities from other planes or dimensions, the standard procedures are augmented by the requirements for planar transit and stamping as dictated by § 1201.12. If the containment fails and requires the intervention of high-level specialists, the area shall be declared a \"Restricted Zone\" until such time as it can be certified safe for public entry. Any individual attempting to enter a restricted zone without a valid permit may face immediate detention. The determination of what constitutes a \"safe\" perimeter is left to the discretion of the presiding officer on-site, provided their actions remain consistent with the interpretation of laws regarding construction and safety under § 1.7. All reports must be filed in clear, unambiguous prose, avoiding colloquialisms or non-standard terminology as cautioned by § 1.13.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "500.12",
            "800.12",
            "1101.11",
            "400.12",
            "1401.12",
            "1201.12",
            "1.7",
            "1.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Stabilization Procedures for Spontaneous Weave-Fractures",
          "text": "In the event that an arcane discharge results in a localized instability of the weave—herein defined as a \"Weave-Fracture\"—the immediate priority of all responding mages shall be the containment of the breach to prevent atmospheric degradation. Such fractures are often the byproduct of unauthorized research conducted without the requisite oversight, and thus may fall under the purview of both the Conservators and the Innovators depending on the origin of the magical signature. Upon detection of a fracture, the primary responder must establish a perimeter and initiate a containment field as defined under § 1.9 to isolate the fluctuating energy from the surrounding environment. Any mage attempting to stabilize such a rift without a valid permit for high-level manipulation shall be subject to immediate investigation by the Aegis Magi.\n\nThe stabilization of these fractures requires a methodical approach to harmonic alignment. Responders must first identify the frequency of the fracture; if the fracture exhibits signs of \"unnatural\" origin, as classified under § 300.11, the responder is mandated to signal for assistance from the Conservator faction immediately. During this stabilization period, no magic shall be cast that does not directly contribute to the dampening of the rift's core. Any attempt to utilize the fracture for experimental purposes or \"innovative\" exploration without express written consent from Archmage Veyra constitutes a violation of the Accords and may result in immediate seizure of equipment under § 800.12. Furthermore, if a rupture occurs within a populated area, the responder must ensure that no civilian populations are exposed to raw arcane radiation.\n\nShould the stabilization efforts fail or be delayed by external interference—such as incursions from the Iron Legion or other non-aligned entities—the respondent may invoke emergency measures to \"seal\" the breach. Such sealing actions are distinct from standard containment; they represent a permanent closure of the rift and may result in the loss of any items or magical conduits trapped within the fold. In cases where a rupture is caused by an individual’s negligence, the perpetrator shall be subject to the procedures for unlicensed casting as outlined in § 200.11. All data gathered during the stabilization process must be filed with the Office of the Archmage promptly; any failure to report such an event may result in the revocation of the practitioner's standing and a formal inquiry into their research credentials under § 400.12.\n\nFinally, all sites where a Weave-Fracture has occurred are deemed \"Contaminated Zones\" until certified otherwise by a certified surveyor. During this period, no unauthorized exploration or transit is permitted. Any individual found within the exclusion zone without a valid mandate may be detained for questioning. The documentation of these events must remain strictly confidential and shall not be disclosed in public forums or common areas; any breach of this confidentiality regarding the specific locations of unstable weaves constitutes a violation of the protocols established under § 100.13, as it poses a risk to the overall safety of the Autumn Wood region.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.9",
            "300.11",
            "800.12",
            "200.11",
            "400.12",
            "100.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Stabilization Procedures for Spontaneous Weave-Fractures — Continued",
          "text": "Upon the occurrence of an unplanned rupture in the local arcane fabric, known as a \"Weave-Fracture,\" responding mages are mandated to prioritize immediate containment over investigation or exploration. A spontaneous fracture is defined under § 1.6 as any structural instability in the weave not resulting from intentional casting by a licensed practitioner. In such instances, the primary objective of the response team is the stabilization of the local area to prevent further degradation of reality. This obligation remains paramount regardless of whether the breach occurs within private property or public thoroughfares, provided those areas fall under the jurisdictional oversight defined in § 1.11. Any mage present during such an event who possesses a valid license must assist in containment efforts; failure to do so may result in a forfeiture of standing as outlined under § 100.2.\n\nThe stabilization process shall commence with the establishment of a \"Containment Perimeter.\" This perimeter is intended to isolate the fracture and prevent the seepage of volatile energies into neighboring zones. During this phase, mages must utilize standard dampening sigils to ground excess mana. If the rupture exhibits signs of expansion or uncontrolled growth, the responding officers are authorized to invoke emergency containment measures. Such actions are governed by the principles of risk mitigation; the priority is the preservation of physical space and the prevention of \"Collateral Taint,\" as defined in § 1101.11. Should a fracture fail to stabilize within the initial hour of detection, the responding mages must signal for high-level intervention from the Conservator wing to ensure structural integrity is maintained.\n\nIn cases where a fracture is caused by an unauthorized ritual or a violation of the restricted research protocols, the specific circumstances shall be documented and reported to the Office of Archmage Veyra for subsequent adjudication. The actual stabilization of the weave must be performed using methods that do not further damage the underlying fabric; any experimentation with \"unconventional\" stabilization techniques is strictly prohibited unless authorized by Janna Brightspark’s office under § 400.11. Any mage who utilizes unauthorized methods and succeeds in stabilizing a fracture may still face disciplinary action for violating safety protocols. Furthermore, if the breach involves elements of \"forbidden magic,\" such as those listed in § 300.11, the area must be quarantined indefinitely until a full purification can be performed by a designated official.\n\nAll materials used during the stabilization process—including spent reagents, exhausted sigils, and discarded focus stones—must be surrendered to the Guild for proper disposal. These items are no longer considered personal property once they have been utilized in an official containment action under § 800.12. Any mage attempting to reclaim these items or repurpose them for private use shall be subject to the penalties of the Paradox Trial as described in § 1301.12. Finally, any evidence of a deliberate breach must be preserved and sealed according to the standards set forth in § 1.9 to ensure that subsequent investigations can determine if the fracture was an accident or a willful violation of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "1.11",
            "100.2",
            "1101.11",
