    {
      "cite": "400.13",
      "part": "part_iv",
      "title": "Protocols for Deviant Methodology and Innovation Oversight",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Protocols for Deviant Methodology and Innovation Oversight",
          "text": "The expansion of magical theory within the Autumnwood Accords acknowledges the inherent friction between established methodology and experimental advancement. While the Conservators, under the leadership of Archmage Theron, advocate for strict adherence to traditional forms as outlined in § 300.11, the Innovator faction, championed by Janna Brightspark, seeks broader latitude for \"experimental\" applications. Under § 400.13, any researcher seeking to deviate from standard casting methodologies must submit a Petition of Intent to the Office of the Sovereign. This petition must explicitly detail how the proposed methodology deviates from the sanctioned techniques established in § 300.11 and provide a justification for such deviation based on either theoretical advancement or practical necessity.\n\nUnless otherwise granted by special dispensation under § 1.12, all experimental methodologies are subject to immediate suspension if they manifest \"unstable variance.\" An unstable variance is defined as any magical output that fluctuates outside of the predicted parameters established in the initial research permit. Such fluctuations may result in a mandatory review by the Aegis Magi. If the Aegis Magi determine that the deviation poses a risk to the stability of the local weave, the researcher shall be subject to containment procedures under § 200.13. The primary objective of these protocols is to ensure that the pursuit of innovation does not compromise the foundational safety of the Midlands or the integrity of the Accord's core protections.\n\nFurthermore, any research involving \"unconventional conduits\"—defined as materials or methods not explicitly listed in the Standard Apparatus Catalog—must be conducted within a localized containment field. These fields must meet the specifications for laboratory environments described under § 400.2. Failure to maintain such containment during an experimental phase shall be deemed a violation of the research integrity standards set forth in § 400.12. In such instances, the researcher’s eligibility for future permits may be suspended until a full audit is completed by the High Council.\n\nAll data derived from non-standard methodologies must be logged and archived to ensure that subsequent researchers do not repeat hazardous errors. This logging requirement remains in effect regardless of whether the research was ultimately successful or failed. Any practitioner who intentionally obscures their methodology to bypass oversight shall be subject to a formal inquiry. Under § 100.3, such an act is considered a breach of trust with the Accord and may lead to a revocation of all active research privileges. The goal of § 400.13 is not to stifle the curiosity of the Innovators, but to ensure that the spark of innovation does not ignite a flame that consumes the infrastructure of the magical arts.\n\nFinally, any collaboration between a researcher utilizing a non-standard methodology and an external entity—including but not limited to Goodstyle Artisans—must be disclosed in writing prior to the commencement of work. Such collaborations are subject to the oversight protocols regarding third-party involvement as described under § 600.12. Unauthorized cooperation with entities outside the Accord’s jurisdiction during a period of experimental variance may result in an immediate forfeit of the researcher's status and potential referral for a Paradox Trial under the standard penalties established by the High Council.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.11",
            "400.13",
