    {
      "cite": "900.13",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Reporting of Ancillary and Sub-Layered Arcane Effects",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Reporting of Ancillary and Sub-Layered Arcane Effects",
          "text": "The provisions set forth within this section shall govern the reporting requirements for magic that exists as a secondary effect, byproduct, or underlying layer to a primary casting. Under § 900.11, any practitioner must file a notice before casting; however, when such casting generates \"ancillary\" effects—defined under § 1.6 as magical phenomena not explicitly stated in the primary spell's intent but resulting from its execution—the caster remains liable for the failure to disclose these latent risks. The Accord demands that even if an effect is deemed a byproduct of natural environmental interaction, it must be disclosed if that interaction is facilitated or catalyzed by the practitioner’s presence. This ensures that the \"hidden\" layers of magic do not bypass the oversight mechanisms established in § 400.13 for deviant methodologies.\n\nFurthermore, any arcane signature that persists beyond the immediate conclusion of a ritual shall be subject to the reporting requirements of § 900.2 regarding Material Enchantments. If an ancillary effect remains active within a public or semi-public space, it is deemed a \"lingering enchantment\" and must be declared as such. The failure to disclose these lingering effects may result in the seizure of the primary casting's focus under § 800.12. It is strictly prohibited for a mage to claim that an ancillary effect was \"unforeseen\" if that effect falls within known categories of magical behavior. Ignorance of the potential for secondary resonance does not constitute a valid defense against charges of non-disclosure.\n\nIn cases where multiple mages are performing concurrent rituals, each individual is responsible for reporting the overlapping effects of their respective magic. If an interaction between two distinct spells produces a third, tertiary effect, both practitioners must be listed as responsible parties in the filing. This prevents the evasion of liability through collaborative casting. Any arcane signature that appears to fluctuate or \"pulse\" outside of standard parameters shall be flagged as a potential hazard under § 900.10 and may necessitate immediate containment protocols as outlined in § 200.13.\n\nFinally, any magic derived from restricted research—as defined by the Innovator faction’s oversight—must have its ancillary effects specifically cross-referenced against the primary permits granted under § 400.11. If a permitted spell generates an unpermitted byproduct, the practitioner may be subject to an immediate audit of their laboratory and materials. The Accord maintains that while the Innovators may push boundaries, such expansion must not occur at the expense of the stability mandated by the Conservators. All secondary effects that manifest as environmental hazards or \"contaminants\" are subject to the remediation clauses in § 1101.12. Any attempt to mask these effects through glamours or obfuscation shall be treated as a deliberate violation of the duty to disclose material facts under § 900.7.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.11",
            "1.6",
            "400.13",
