    {
      "cite": "500.13",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Mitigation of Environmental Arcane Decay",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Mitigation of Environmental Arcane Decay",
          "text": "Under the prevailing tenets of the Autumnwood Accords, all mages engaged in field operations are bound by the obligation to mitigate any and all environmental arcane decay resulting from active casting. While § 500.12 establishes the general standards for conduct during such operations, § 500.13 specifically addresses the lingering resonance left behind by high-magnitude spells or sustained elemental manipulation. Such residue is not merely a matter of aesthetic preservation but a critical requirement to prevent the degradation of local reality. Any practitioner who fails to perform a \"Cleansing\" ritual upon the completion of a public or private casting event may be subject to investigation by the Aegis Magi for negligence of the environment.\n\nThe scope of this mitigation shall include the stabilization of localized mana currents that have been thinned, warped, or redirected. For instance, if a mage utilizes transmutation techniques in an area populated by non-casters, they must ensure the local weave is returned to its baseline state before departing the vicinity. Failure to do so may result in \"Arcane Drift,\" which can cause unpredictable fluctuations in nearby objects and structures. Such instances of negligence shall be viewed as a violation of the safety protocols established under § 500.7. Furthermore, any mage found to have caused significant \"leakage\" that impacts neighboring territories shall be held liable for restoration costs, as outlined in the principles of compensation and debt settlement.\n\nIn cases where the decay is so severe that it threatens the structural integrity of physical reality—such as a localized rift or sustained dimensional thinning—the practitioner must immediately notify the nearest Accord representative. Such events are no longer considered standard field operations but may escalate into matters requiring intervention by the Conservators to ensure stability, in accordance with the oversight roles defined in § 100.3. Under these circumstances, the mage’s immunity from certain civil claims is forfeited should they fail to secure the site immediately. The presence of a \"One-T\" cartography map does not constitute a license for high-risk experimentation; any such activity must comply with the rigorous standards of § 400.11 regarding research permits and supervised experiments.\n\nFurthermore, the Autumnwood Accords maintain that no mage shall use the excuse of \"unforeseen magical feedback\" to bypass their duty of mitigation. If a spell behaves in an unexpected manner, the caster remains responsible for the subsequent stabilization of the environment. This is particularly relevant when dealing with sentient-adjacent entities or sensitive biomes where mana flux can lead to unpredictable mutations. The Innovators may argue for more permissive boundaries regarding \"experimental\" results, but under § 1.7, the construction of these rules favors the safety of the public realm over the curiosity of the individual practitioner. Any mage who knowingly bypasses these mitigation steps shall have their license reviewed by the High Council in the Autumn Wood.\n\nFinally, all documentation regarding environmental remediation must be filed within forty-eight hours of the casting event's conclusion. This filing is mandatory for any spell exceeding a third-degree magnitude on the standard scale. If a mage fails to provide such documentation, they shall be deemed in violation of § 900.13 regarding the reporting of ancillary and sub-layered arcane effects. The purpose of this record is to ensure that the Guild can track the cumulative impact of magic upon the realm's geography. Any evidence of willful negligence or intentional disregard for the stability of the weave shall be grounds for immediate referral to a Paradox Trial, as governed by the procedures in § 1301.12. All mages are reminded that their primary duty is the preservation of the world as it exists, and any action that compromises this core tenet is strictly prohibited under the overarching spirit of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "500.12",
            "500.13",
            "500.7",
            "100.3",
            "400.11",
            "1.7",
            "900.13",
            "1301.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Remediation of Residual Mana Bleed and Ground Saturation",
          "text": "The remediation of environmental arcane decay, specifically the phenomenon colloquially known as \"mana bleed,\" shall be governed by the principles of containment and stabilization as outlined in § 200.13. When a magical discharge occurs outside of a controlled sanctum or a sanctioned laboratory, it may leave behind persistent residues that contaminate the local ether. Such contamination is deemed a violation of the Autumnwood Accords' primary mandate to preserve the stability of the Midlands’ arcane weave. Any mage observed within a zone of active decay must immediately initiate containment protocols; failure to do so shall constitute a breach of duty under § 900.13 regarding the reporting of ancillary and sub-layered effects.\n\nThe presence of \"ground saturation\" — where the terrestrial plane absorbs excess mana from a botched ritual or an unauthorized casting incident — requires immediate intervention by a licensed specialist. Under § 500.12, these areas are designated as hazardous zones. If a site shows signs of crystalline growth, spectral echoes, or localized temporal drifting, it must be cordoned off and reported to the Aegis Magi for assessment. The primary objective is the neutralization of these residues before they can manifest into autonomous magical entities or \"wild\" phenomena. Practitioners who fail to mitigate such decay may find their licenses suspended, as the preservation of the natural flow is paramount to the safety of non-casters.\n\nFurthermore, the interaction between decaying mana and physical infrastructure must be monitored for structural degradation. Where arcane residue interacts with stone, timber, or metal, it may cause \"calcification\" or other forms of material instability. Such instances are subject to the liability clauses found in § 500.11 regarding unauthorized field operations. Any mage who causes such damage through negligence is liable for the costs of remediation and restoration. During this process, all evidence must be preserved; any attempt to conceal the extent of the decay from the Conservators shall be prosecuted as an act of willful obfuscation under § 400.12.\n\nIn cases where the decay is severe enough to threaten local populations or non-magical territories, the Innovator faction may request a \"Specialized Mitigation Permit\" to utilize advanced, albeit experimental, containment techniques. However, such requests must be vetted by Archmage Veyra to ensure they do not violate the core tenets of safety as defined in § 1.6. Any use of non-standard methods without express permission is strictly prohibited and shall result in a mandatory Paradox Trial under § 800.12. The goal remains the purification of the land, ensuring that the very fabric of reality remains untainted by the reckless application of high-level sorcery.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "200.13",
            "900.13",
            "500.12",
            "500.11",
            "400.12",
            "1.6",
            "800.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Remediation of Residual Mana Bleed and Ground Saturation — Continued",
          "text": "Under the sovereign authority of Archmage Veyra as established in § 1.2, all practitioners operating within the Autumn Wood or any territory subject to the Autumnwood Accords are strictly bound by the protocols governing the stabilization of local mana flows. It is a fundamental tenet of our governance that magic must not be permitted to leak into the terrestrial strata in an uncontrolled manner. When high-level casting results in \"Mana Bleed\"—defined under § 1.6 as any arcane energy that fails to dissipate upon completion of a spell and instead permeates the local environment—the caster is personally liable for its containment. Failure to contain such bleed constitutes a violation of the stability mandates championed by the Conservators, and may lead to formal investigation under § 400.13 regarding deviant methodology.\n\nGround Saturation occurs when repeated casting in a concentrated geographical area causes the local ley lines to become \"saturated\" or \"heavy.\" Such saturation can lead to spontaneous arcane discharges or the degradation of natural flora and fauna. In cases where ground saturation is detected, the caster must immediately initiate containment procedures as outlined under § 200.13. If a site shows signs of significant decay, the Aegis Magi are authorized to impound the area until such time as it can be scrubbed of its residual signatures. Any mage attempting to bypass these protocols for the sake of convenience or rapid casting is in violation of the safety standards codified under § 500.12 and may face immediate petition for a Paradox Trial.\n\nFurthermore, any intentional accumulation of mana for the purpose of creating \"pocket\" zones or localized high-density fields must be reported to the Guild Office as per § 100.1. If such a field is not properly sealed according to the standards in § 1.9, it shall be deemed an unlicensed hazard. The presence of any lingering resonance that does not dissipate within one lunar cycle following a casting event requires the filing of a Remediation Report. Under these circumstances, the mage must cooperate with the Guild’s appointed surveyors to ensure the site is returned to its baseline state. Failure to remediate known bleed sites may result in the revocation of research permits and the forfeiture of certain privileges under § 400.11.\n\nAll remediation efforts involving high-risk purification spells must be conducted only by those possessing a valid High-Clearance Seal as defined in § 1.9. If a mage is forced to perform an emergency stabilization on a leaking site, they must document the exact coordinates and volume of mana discharged; such records shall be cross-referenced with the reporting requirements in § 900.13. Any evidence of intentional contamination or the deliberate creation of \"hot zones\" for the purpose of intimidating others shall be prosecuted under the strict terms of § 700.11, where the burden of proof regarding intent shall shift to the accused. The Guild maintains that the preservation of the natural mana equilibrium is paramount, and any deviation from these remediation protocols shall be considered a direct affront to the stability of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.2",
            "1.6",
            "400.13",
            "200.13",
            "500.12",
            "100.1",
            "1.9",
            "400.11",
            "900.13",
            "700.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Remediation of Residual Mana Bleed and Ground Saturation — Administration",
          "text": "The Accord mandates that all practitioners engaged in high-density casting must account for the physical degradation of the terrestrial plane resulting from prolonged mana exposure. When magic is cast without sufficient containment, it may result in \"Mana Bleed,\" a condition wherein the local weave becomes saturated or thinned to the point of instability. Such instances are strictly regulated under § 500.12; however, the specific technical procedures for stabilizing ground saturation fall under these provisions. Any mage found to have caused significant bleed is required to perform immediate stabilization rituals to prevent the degradation from becoming a permanent environmental hazard.\n\nGround saturation occurs when magical residues do not dissipate naturally but instead bond with local geological and botanical structures. This can lead to \"Arcane Decay,\" characterized by the crystallization of soil or the mutation of local flora. Under § 1.6, such anomalies are classified as unauthorized alterations of the natural order. If a practitioner identifies a zone of saturation exceeding the thresholds defined in the technical appendices, they must report the site immediately. Failure to report such decay may result in the immediate seizure of the mage’s focus by the Aegis Magi, who act as the primary enforcement arm for the Accord's environmental protections.\n\nThe remediation process requires the application of stabilizing sigils designed to \"ground\" the excess energy into harmlesser forms. Practitioners attempting to remediate these zones must hold a valid certification in containment magic or work under the direct supervision of a licensed Conservator. In cases where the bleed is severe, the area may be declared a restricted zone. This designation is essential for preventing unauthorized access by non-casters, who might otherwise be exposed to fluctuating mana levels. All remediation efforts must be logged and filed with the Autumnwood Accords Desk within three lunar cycles of the initial assessment.\n\nFurthermore, any attempt to bypass these remediation requirements through \"quick-fix\" casting methods—such as forcibly venting mana into adjacent territories—is strictly prohibited. Such actions constitute a breach of safety protocols and may result in the initiation of a Paradox Trial under § 1.7. Parties found guilty of intentional environmental neglect shall have their magic sealed as determined by the prevailing factional leadership. Collaboration with Goodstyle Artisans is permitted for the construction of permanent containment barriers, provided that such structures are approved by Archmage Veyra before commencement.\n\nIn cases where remediation fails to stabilize a site, the area must be cordoned off and documented in the internal records regarding hazardous zones. Unauthorized entry into these zones constitutes a violation of § 1.4. Any mage who knowingly assists another in circumventing these protocols shall face the same penalties as the primary offender. The goal of these regulations is not to hinder the advancement of magic, but to ensure that the casting of spells does not leave a permanent scar upon the world. All remediation actions must be performed in accordance with § 1.9 regarding the proper application of containment seals and stamps to ensure long-term stability.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "500.12",
            "1.6",
            "1.7",
            "1.4",
            "1.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Remediation of Residual Mana Bleed and Ground Saturation — Oversight",
          "text": "The remediation of localized mana bleed, hereafter referred to as \"Ground Saturation,\" shall be governed by the strict protocols established under § 500.12 regarding arcane field operations. When a casting event results in an uncontrolled discharge—notwithstanding whether such discharge was intentional or accidental—the immediate vicinity must be surveyed for signs of environmental decay. If a site exhibits symptoms of \"bleed,\" the presiding mage is mandated to initiate containment procedures immediately. This process involves the neutralization of lingering thaumic and spectral residues that have permeated the physical strata. Under § 100.13, any remediation efforts conducted within the Autumn Wood must be reported to the central registry if they involve more than three degrees of arcane intensity.\n\nIn cases where ground saturation poses a risk to local flora or fauna, the responding mage shall perform a \"Silt-Purge\" ritual. This procedure is designed to filter out high-frequency mana spikes that cause erratic growth or necrotic blight in common vegetation. If such remediation is required due to an unauthorized casting as defined under § 400.11, the responsible party must submit a full audit of the site’s stability to Archmage Veyra’s office. Failure to perform these purges within forty-eight hours of a detected breach may result in the imposition of a Paradox Trial, as outlined in the penal standards under § 800.12. Any mage attempting to bypass these requirements by using unapproved \"quick-fix\" seals shall be subject to immediate seizure of their tools and subsequent investigation into their license status.\n\nThe administration of large-scale remediation projects involving significant environmental decay requires a formal petition from the Innovator faction or, in cases of extreme risk, an emergency override from the Conservators. For matters involving high-density zones where mana bleed has become permanent, the area shall be marked as \"Unstable\" and recorded in the official archives. During such periods, all non-essential casting within a one-mile radius is strictly prohibited under § 900.13 to prevent further contamination of the local ley lines. Furthermore, any remediation efforts involving sentient-adjacent constructs must adhere to the labor standards set forth in § 600.12.\n\nFor matters involving trans-planar leakage where ground saturation involves extra-dimensional residues, the mage shall refer to the specific containment protocols under § 200.13. In such instances, the standard remediation procedures are superseded by the requirement for a dimensional seal. Any breach of these standards may result in a mandatory grounding of the caster's license. All materials used during the remediation process must be certified as \"inert\" to ensure no secondary reactions occur. If any substance utilized during the purging process is found to contain prohibited substances, the mage shall be prosecuted under § 800.11 for the handling of hazardous arcane materials. The final clearance of a site will only be granted once a formal certificate of stability is issued by the presiding official in accordance with the definitions provided in § 1.6.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "500.12",
            "100.13",
            "400.11",
            "800.12",
            "900.13",
            "600.12",
            "200.13",
            "800.11",
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Protocols for Chronic Mana Saturation and Soil Remediation",
          "text": "The management of chronic mana saturation, colloquially referred to as \"ground saturation,\" is governed by the strictures regarding environmental stability as outlined in the Autumnwood Accords. When magical discharge results in a persistent state of high-density arcane residue within the terrestrial strata, it is no longer classified as a singular event of casting but rather as an ongoing environmental condition requiring active remediation. Under these circumstances, any practitioner who identifies a zone of significant saturation must immediately report the coordinates to the nearest authorized office for evaluation by the Conservators. Such zones are subject to immediate containment protocols under § 200.13, and failure to report such anomalies may result in the individual being held liable for the subsequent decay of the local ecosystem.\n\nRemediation efforts are prioritized based on the proximity of the saturation to inhabited areas or sensitive ley-line junctions. In cases where mana bleed has saturated the groundwater or root systems of local flora, the remediation must be conducted by certified mages who hold a valid status under § 400.11. These practitioners are tasked with neutralizing the \"bleed\" through harmonic stabilization rather than forceful expulsion, which could lead to unpredictable atmospheric feedback. Any unauthorized attempt to purge saturated mana using high-intensity casting is strictly prohibited and shall be treated as an act of reckless endangerment. Furthermore, any party seeking to conduct such remediation must provide proof of a valid research permit or a specific mandate from the Innovator faction for experimental stabilization techniques under § 400.13.\n\nThe oversight of these sites falls under the jurisdiction of the Aegis Magi, who serve as the primary enforcers of Accord stability. Once a site is declared \"saturated,\" it must be marked with a physical and metaphysical seal to prevent unauthorized entry by non-casters or common travelers. These seals are not merely symbols but are active wards designed to dampen the local mana signature until remediation is complete. Any individual found attempting to bypass these barriers shall be subject to the penalties outlined in § 800.12. Furthermore, all logs regarding the decay of the land and the subsequent steps taken to stabilize it must be entered into the official record; failure to provide accurate data on the volume of mana bled may result in a temporary suspension of the practitioner’s right to perform field operations under § 500.12.\n\nIn instances where remediation fails or where the decay becomes irreversible, the area shall be designated as a \"Zone of Exclusion.\" During such periods, any magic performed within the zone is subject to immediate scrutiny by the central authorities in the Autumn Wood. The transition from an active remediation site to a permanent exclusion zone requires a formal review by the Archmage Veyra to ensure that the risk of \"mana-leak\" does not threaten neighboring territories. All contracts involving construction or terraforming near these sites must explicitly account for potential environmental decay, and any party who fails to account for such risks in their initial filings shall be held liable for all subsequent cleanup costs under § 600.12. Any discrepancy between the reported levels of saturation and actual readings will result in a formal investigation into the integrity of the practitioner's reporting under § 400.12.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.13",
            "400.11",
            "400.13",
            "800.12",
            "500.12",
            "600.12",
            "400.12"
          ]
        }
      ],
