    {
      "cite": "600.13",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Liability for Autonomous Construct Malfeasance",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Autonomous Construct Malfeasance",
          "text": "The legal status of autonomous constructs and their subsequent actions within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords are governed by the principles of strict liability and primary authorship. When a practitioner engages in the creation or summoning of an artificial entity—be it a golem, a construct of woven mana, or a bound elemental—the original caster remains the primary legal nexus for all outcomes produced by said entity. Under § 600.4, while a Minimum Ward is required to stabilize the construct’s core essence, such a ward does not insulate the creator from the legal consequences of the construct's actions. If a construct causes physical harm, property damage, or unauthorized arcane discharge, the liability shall rest solely upon the caster who initiated the construction or summoning sequence. This principle remains absolute regardless of whether the construct acted under direct command or through autonomous logic pathways.\n\nFurthermore, in instances where a construct is utilized to fulfill terms within a contract involving non-casters, the protections afforded by § 600.11 do not extend to the behavior of the construct itself. While the contract may specify the labor provided by the construct, any deviation from the agreed-upon scope—specifically actions that result in \"collateral taints\" as defined under § 1101.12—shall be deemed a personal failure of the caster to maintain proper oversight. The existence of a Summoned Notary under § 600.5 does not absolve a caster from responsibility; rather, it serves only to certify that the contract's terms were met by the construct at the time of signing. If the construct subsequently deviates into prohibited behaviors or violates the safety protocols established in § 400.13, the liability is deemed \"non-transferable\" and shall be prosecuted against the primary caster’s license.\n\nIn cases where a construct operates beyond the immediate vicinity of its master, the requirements for proper containment as outlined under § 200.13 are invoked. A failure to maintain these containment protocols constitutes a breach of the Accord's safety standards. Any unauthorized \"field use\" that results in environmental arcane decay shall be subject to the penalties detailed in § 500.13. It is explicitly noted that while a construct may possess a degree of autonomy, it lacks the legal standing to enter into agreements or assume liability; therefore, any perceived \"agency\" by the construct is legally construed as an extension of the caster's intent.\n\nFinally, any construction involving high-level mana manipulation must be documented under the protocols for research integrity found in § 400.12. Failure to register a construct’s specific capabilities prior to its deployment may result in the immediate seizure of the construct by the Aegis Magi. Under no circumstances shall an \"emergency\" or \"unforeseen circumstance\" serve as a defense for the failure to provide adequate oversight of autonomous entities. All such matters are subject to interpretation under § 1.7, and any attempt to circumvent these obligations through complex sub-layering of contracts will be viewed as an intentional violation of the Accord's core stability mandates.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "600.4",
            "600.11",
            "1101.12",
            "600.5",
            "400.13",
            "200.13",
            "500.13",
            "400.12",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Autonomous Construct Malfeasance — Continued",
          "text": "The creation and deployment of autonomous constructs—defined under § 1.6 as any arcane construct capable of independent action without continuous manual input—subject the creator to strict liability for all subsequent actions of said construct. While the Autumnwood Accords allow for the construction of golems, homunculi, and animated armatures, the transition from a \"controlled\" entity to an \"autonomous\" one triggers specific legal escalations. Under § 600.12, any construct that deviates from its primary inscribed purpose must be immediately halted; however, should such a deviation result in harm to third parties or the degradation of local ley lines, the liability falls solely upon the primary caster. The Guild maintains that an autonomous construction is an extension of the creator's intent, and thus, any \"rogue\" behavior is legally interpreted as an unauthorized casting event rather than a mechanical failure.\n\nFurthermore, the presence of a formal seal per § 1.9 does not insulate a mage from liability if the construct was intentionally designed with high-autonomy parameters to circumvent safety protocols. The Innovator faction may argue for broader leeway in these instances, yet the Conservators maintain that any construct capable of independent decision-making must be registered with the Autumn Wood central registry. Failure to register such an entity constitutes a violation of standard construction protocols. In cases where a construct causes environmental arcane decay, the owner shall be liable for remediation costs as outlined under § 500.13. The use of \"free\" labor through constructs does not waive the requirement for oversight; rather, it necessitates more rigorous documentation of the core binding spells to ensure they do not drift into forbidden methodologies prohibited by § 400.13.\n\nIn the event of a breach where an autonomous construct enters non-sanctioned territory or engages in unauthorized field operations, the owner shall be held liable for all damages regardless of whether the \"malfeasance\" was intentional. The standard for \"intent\" is waived in matters of autonomy; if the construct acts without your direct command and causes harm, you are legally responsible as if you had performed the act yourself. This is particularly pertinent when constructs interact with non-casters, where liability may trigger clauses under § 600.11 regarding contract fulfillment. Any construction that utilizes \"unstable\" logic paths—often favored by those seeking to bypass standard safety limitations—is subject to immediate seizure and forfeiture of the core components under § 800.12.\n\nFinally, any construct found to be acting in defiance of the Autumnwood Accords' primary prohibitions shall result in an immediate inquiry into the caster’s standing. If a construct is utilized to facilitate \"unauthorized research\" or to bypass safety protocols for restricted materials, the resulting liability may lead to a formal referral for a Paradox Trial as defined under § 100.13. The Guild does not recognize \"mechanical error\" as a valid defense against claims of malfeasance. If the construct’s actions result in the creation of a mana-leak or unauthorized rift, the owner must submit to immediate containment protocols and may face the seizure of their casting privileges under § 800.11. All disputes regarding the \"autonomy\" of a construct shall be settled by the Desk's interpretation of the original binding script as filed during the construction phase.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "600.12",
            "1.9",
            "500.13",
