        {
          "heading": "Liability for Autonomously Manifested Construct Deviance",
          "text": "The legal distinction between a construct’s intentional disobedience and an autonomous deviation caused by environmental instability is the primary pillar upon which § 600.13 rests. Under these provisions, a mage who commissions or maintains a sentient-adjacent automaton remains strictly liable for all physical and arcane damages resulting from its actions, regardless of whether the mage intended to issue a command that would cause such harm. This strict liability ensures that the burden of containment falls upon the creator rather than the public. Where a construct exhibits \"unstable behavior\" as defined under § 1.6, the owner must immediately initiate a grounding ritual. Failure to do so constitutes a willful violation of safety protocols, potentially triggering the penalties for unlawful casting described in § 800.11.\n\nIn cases where a construct’s malfeasance is traced back to unauthorized modifications or \"innovation\" performed without the express oversight of the Innovator faction, the liability may be escalated from civil damages to criminal forfeiture of the construct's core. Such instances are often flagged during routine inspections as violations of research integrity protocols. Any mage seeking to bypass standard stability checks must do so under a formal waiver; however, no such waiver shall exempt the owner from the basic safety requirements of § 600.12 regarding construction labor and associated obligations. If an autonomous deviation occurs within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords, the presiding mediator—Archmage Veyra—may order the immediate decommissioning of the unit if it poses a threat to public stability or the sanctity of the local weave.\n\nFurthermore, the interaction between construct autonomy and environmental arcane decay must be monitored closely. If a construct deviates from its programming due to external magical contamination, the owner is still held liable for the initial failure to shield the construct's core against such fluctuations. This is particularly relevant in areas where high-density mana flows are present, as any breach of containment by an autonomous unit may lead to collateral taints under § 1101.11. The Guild maintains that a construct’s lack of agency does not excuse its owner from the consequences of its presence; thus, all constructs must carry a registered seal of compliance in accordance with § 1.9. Any device found without such a mark, or whose core has been tampered with to bypass safety limiters, shall be subject to immediate seizure and potential forfeiture during a Paradox Trial under § 100.13 if it is deemed a risk to the collective peace.\n\nFinally, any contract involving the deployment of autonomous constructs in public spaces must explicitly state the liability terms for \"rogue\" behavior. While private agreements between mages may vary, they cannot override the fundamental protections established by the Autumnwood Accords. Any attempt to shift the burden of malfeasance onto a third-party operator or an independent contractor is void if such a transfer would result in a reduction of safety oversight. All liability for construct malfeasance remains with the primary license holder as defined under § 1.4. If a construct causes damage to public property or infrastructure, the owner must submit a full report of the failure to the nearest Guild office, ensuring that all evidence is preserved according to the standards set forth in § 700.12.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "600.13",
