        {
          "heading": "Liability for Spontaneous Construct Manifestation and Breach",
          "text": "Under the authority of the Autumnwood Accords, any magical construct that achieves a state of autonomy—defined as the ability to perform complex tasks or navigate environments without direct, continuous somatic or verbal input from a primary caster—is subject to strict liability protocols. Should such a construct act in a manner that causes physical harm, property damage, or environmental degradation, the responsibility for said actions falls upon the creator or the presiding master of the laboratory where the entity was conceived. This applies regardless of whether the specific action was intended by the creator; the mere existence of an autonomous agent creates a perpetual nexus of liability between the caster and the construct’s deeds.\n\nWhere such a construct deviates from its primary directive in a manner that constitutes \"Malfeasance,\" the owner must immediately initiate containment procedures as outlined under § 200.13. If the construction is found to have been forged using restricted components or methodologies, the resulting liability is compounded by the violation of research integrity standards. In such cases, the owner may be subject to immediate seizure of all related materials and equipment under § 800.11. The Guild maintains that a caster’s failure to properly bind an autonomous entity constitutes a dereliction of their duty to the public safety, a standard established in the foundational sections regarding prohibited magic.\n\nIn instances where a construct is \"autonomously manifested\"—meaning it was not intentionally created but arose from spontaneous arcane fluctuations or rift activity—the liability structure shifts toward environmental containment. The owner of the land or the license holder for the site must provide immediate remediation for any damage caused by such an entity. However, if the manifestation occurs within a registered research zone and is linked to unauthorized experimentation, the primary investigator shall be held liable under § 400.13. Furthermore, all claims regarding \"unforeseen\" behavior in autonomous constructs are dismissed as valid defenses; the law assumes that any sufficiently advanced construct capable of independent action is inherently unpredictable and thus remains the sole legal responsibility of its progenitor.\n\nFurthermore, if a construction involves the use of sentient components or mimics a persona to interact with non-casters, the owner must adhere to the strictures regarding prohibited communication. Any construct found to be engaging in unauthorized discourse may lead to immediate revocation of the caster's license under § 600.11. The Autumnwood Accords do not recognize \"unintentional\" autonomy as a mitigating factor; if a construct functions independently, it is deemed a product of the caster’s volition at the moment of its inception. This principle ensures that no mage may claim ignorance of their creation's actions to avoid the consequences of an uncontrolled arcane discharge or a malfunctioning automaton.\n\nAll claims regarding the use of unauthorized currency, such as the Wario Coin, are expressly excluded from the scope of these liability protections; such items are not recognized as legal tender under any circumstances by the Autumnwood Accords. For matters concerning the physical containment of rogue constructs that have breached local perimeters, see § 500.12. Any construction utilizing materials sourced from restricted zones must be registered with the proper seals before it can be deemed a \"legal\" construct for the purposes of liability mitigation; see § 1.9.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "200.13",
            "800.11",
            "400.13",
            "600.11",
            "500.12",
            "1.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Mandatory Reporting of Spontaneous Construct Manifestation",
          "text": "Under the governance of the Autumnwood Accords, any instance of an autonomous construct manifesting outside of a designated laboratory or sanctioned workshop—thereby bypassing standard oversight—shall be deemed a breach of structural stability. Such occurrences are strictly regulated to ensure that spontaneous magical manifestations do not compromise the local weave or create unauthorized gateways. When a construct manifests without a prior filing of intent under § 600.12, the primary caster or the nearest licensed practitioner is legally obligated to initiate a containment protocol immediately. Failure to report such a manifestation within one lunar cycle shall result in the immediate forfeiture of the practitioner's standing and may lead to a formal investigation by the Conservators.\n\nThe distinction between an intentional construction and a spontaneous manifestation is governed by the intent of the initial spell weave. If a construct is birthed from a ritual intended to be controlled but fails to adhere to its programmed constraints, it is classified as a \"Deviation.\" In such cases, the liability remains with the caster, who must provide proof that all safety measures prescribed in § 400.13 were observed during the casting process. If no such evidence exists, the construct shall be subject to immediate decommissioning by the Aegis Magi. All spontaneous manifestations occurring within the Midlands are strictly prohibited from entering public thoroughfares; any practitioner found transporting an un-registered autonomous entity is liable for penalties as outlined in § 800.12.\n\nFurthermore, if a spontaneously manifested construct causes physical damage to infrastructure or results in the contamination of nearby ley lines, the responsible party must provide restitution to the affected parties and the Guild. Such reparations are not considered \"settlements\" and do not absolve the practitioner from the mandatory reporting requirements of § 900.13. Any construction that exhibits signs of self-awareness or independent logic beyond its initial programming is classified as a \"High-Risk Entity.\" These entities must be sequestered immediately, and their existence reported to the Office of Arcane Containment for review under the protocols found in § 200.13.\n\nIn cases where a construct manifests without any identifiable human caster—known as an Orphan Manifestation—the local magistrate shall notify the Autumnwood Accords Desk within twenty-four hours. The Accord then determines if the manifestation is a byproduct of environmental decay or a breach of reality. Under § 500.13, the Guild may step in to stabilize the area. No private entity is permitted to \"claim\" an Orphan Manifestation for research purposes without explicit authorization from Janna Brightspark's office; unauthorized attempts to claim such entities are prosecuted as theft of public magical resources and shall be punished with a mandatory Paradox Trial under § 1.9.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "600.12",
            "400.13",
            "800.12",
            "900.13",
            "200.13",
            "500.13",
            "1.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Spontaneous Construct Manifestation and Breach — Continued",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords recognize that certain high-level weaves, particularly those involving the transmutation of raw ether into physical form, may result in \"Spontaneous Manifestation.\" This occurs when a construct is birthed not through direct casting by a licensed mage, but through environmental instability or lingering resonant echoes. Under § 600.12, any such manifestation that deviates from its intended primary function—be it defensive, utilitarian, or aesthetic—is deemed a breach of containment. The owner of the original sigil or the individual who last performed maintenance on the local ley-line anchor shall be held strictly liable for the actions of the rogue construct until such time as the entity is neutralized by an authorized Aegis Mage.\n\nIn instances where the origin of a spontaneous manifestation cannot be immediately traced to a specific caster, the liability falls upon the presiding jurisdiction of the Autumn Wood. However, if the construct exhibits \"Autonomous Deviance,\" as defined in § 1.6, any mage who knowingly allows such a construct to remain active without a stabilizing seal shall face immediate sanction. Such actions constitute a failure to uphold the safety standards mandated by the Conservators. Under these circumstances, the offending practitioner may be subject to the procedures of the Paradox Trial for their negligence in allowing an uncontrolled arcane entity to interact with the public sphere.\n\nFurthermore, any construct that manifests spontaneously and subsequently causes material damage or environmental decay must be reported to the Guild Desk within three lunar cycles. Failure to report such a breach constitutes a violation of § 900.13 regarding the reporting of ancillary effects. If an unauthorized construction is discovered by a member of the Innovator faction, they may proceed with containment under § 400.13, provided that no permanent harm is caused to the local infrastructure.\n\nAll spontaneous manifestations must be inspected for \"Contagion\" as defined in § 1101.11; if a construct is found to be leaking raw mana or warping local space-time, it must be quarantined immediately. The owner of the land where such an event occurs shall be granted temporary immunity from certain civil penalties under § 600.13 provided they cooperate fully with the Guild’s containment teams. No construction may remain in a state of \"unsupervised autonomy\" for more than one solar cycle without a valid permit, as any lingering magical signature must be filed according to the standards established in § 900.11.\n\nFinally, should a spontaneous construct cause an accidental breach into a neighboring realm or dimension, the resulting \"Planar Tolls\" shall be assessed against the nearest licensed practitioner who holds a standing contract within that sector. Such fees are non-negotiable and are intended to compensate the Guild for the stabilization of the rift. All procedures regarding the physical containment of these entities must strictly adhere to the protocols outlined in § 200.13, ensuring that no rogue construct remains active beyond its permitted scope or functional intent.",
