    {
      "cite": "700.13",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "Admissibility of Artifact-Derived Testimony and Relics",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Admissibility of Artifact-Derived Testimony and Relics",
          "text": "The admissibility of evidence derived from sentient or semi-sentient artifacts, including but not limited to enchanted blades, self-aware constructs, or bound spirits, shall be governed by the principles of intent and containment established under § 1.7 regarding the construction of arcane interactions. When such an artifact provides testimony or data that informs a dispute before the High Council or during a Paradox Trial, it must first be certified as stable. Any evidence extracted from an object possessing a degree of autonomy is subject to scrutiny for \"echo-taint,\" wherein the memories or desires of previous owners may bleed into the current evidentiary record. Such tainted testimony shall be struck unless the presiding magistrate can determine that the information provided is functionally distinct from the artifact’s inherent magical persona.\n\nIn instances where a non-sentient item—such as a ritual focus or an enchanted scroll—is used to relay information, the evidence must be scrutinized for \"magical drift.\" Under § 700.12, any arcane residue that suggests a deliberate alteration of the truth by the presenting party shall result in immediate disqualification of the artifact from the proceedings. Furthermore, if a piece of evidence is retrieved from a site of significant magical instability, such as those identified in the reports on environmental decay under § 500.13, it must be undergo a purification rite before it may be presented to the court. The presence of \"wild mana\" or lingering enchantments that could influence the perception of the adjudicators shall render the evidence inadmissible.\n\nWhere an artifact is claimed by multiple parties, its status as a primary witness or source of truth is governed by the rules of possession and custody. If an item was previously held by a member of the Innovator faction under a sanctioned research permit, it may still carry \"legacy permissions\" for limited testimony; however, such evidence must be cross-referenced against § 400.13 to ensure that no deviant methodologies were employed during its acquisition or use. Conversely, any artifact seized from an individual currently undergoing a Paradox Trial shall be sequestered immediately and may not serve as a source of evidence until the trial is concluded and the offender’s status is finalized under § 800.12.\n\nFinally, should a piece of evidence be recovered from a location outside the standard jurisdiction—specifically those regions noted for high-density magical anomalies—the burden of proof shifts to the presenting party to demonstrate that no external forces influenced the artifact's \"voice.\" This is particularly critical when dealing with items sourced from contested zones or areas under the influence of rival powers such as the Iron Legion. In such cases, the evidence shall only be admitted if accompanied by a certified seal of authenticity issued by an authorized official, as defined in § 1.9, to ensure that the testimony provided has not been manipulated by external entities or competing magical influences.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1.7",
            "700.12",
            "500.13",
            "400.13",
            "800.12",
            "1.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Evidentiary Standards for Non-Sentient Arcane Relics",
          "text": "In the determination of arcane litigation, specific protocols must govern the introduction of non-sentient artifacts as evidence within the courts of the Autumnwood Accords. While primary testimony from a sentient being is governed by standard procedures under § 1.4, the \"voice\" of an artifact—defined herein as residual psychic imprints, spectral echoes, or magically encoded data—must be subjected to rigorous scrutiny before it may be admitted into the record. Such evidence is deemed \"Artifact-Derived Testimony\" and shall only be admissible if the presiding magistrate determines that the relic contains a distinct, lingering intent from its previous wielder that remains coherent enough to inform the current inquiry.\n\nUnder § 1.7, such interpretations of an object’s history must be constructed with extreme caution; any inference drawn from a relic must be supported by a secondary physical fact or a verified historical record. For instance, if an artifact is used to prove a past violation of the Accords, the prosecution must demonstrate that the arcane resonance within the item is not a product of modern tampering or environmental contamination. If the integrity of the relic’s echo cannot be established beyond reasonable doubt, it shall be struck from the record as \"muddled\" and may only be used for internal investigative purposes by the Aegis Magi.\n\nFurthermore, any artifact admitted into evidence must undergo a ritualistic purification to ensure that no lingering \"malice\" or \"sentient haunting\" influences the adjudicating council. This process ensures that the testimony of the object remains tethered to its historical purpose rather than becoming an active conduit for unwanted spectral presence. Such procedures are consistent with the containment standards established in § 200.13, ensuring that the court remains a neutral ground. Relics that exhibit signs of \"active\" haunting or possessive spirits shall be immediately sequestered and deemed inadmissible under any circumstances, as they no longer constitute an object but rather a breach of safety protocols.\n\nIn cases involving the Innovator faction’s research, artifacts derived from experimental methodologies must be scrutinized even more closely to ensure that their testimonies do not contain \"shadow-data\" or prohibited arcane signatures. Any artifact found to hold such data shall be subjected to immediate seizure and confiscation under § 800.12. The court acknowledges the distinction between a relic as a witness and a relic as a tool; while a tool may be examined for its construction, only a relic possessing a verifiable \"echo\" of intent may be treated as testimony. All such evidence must be formally logged in the central registry to ensure that the same artifact is not presented multiple times across different proceedings, thereby maintaining the integrity of the judicial record and ensuring consistent application of the law as outlined under § 100.3.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "1.7",
            "200.13",
            "800.12",
            "100.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Evidence of Non-Sentient Material Resonance",
          "text": "The determination of evidentiary weight regarding non-sentient arcane relics shall be governed by the principles of material resonance and historical continuity. Under § 1.6, an \"artifact\" is defined as any physical object imbued with residual mana or lingering intent that does not possess independent sapience. Such objects may provide testimony through recorded fluctuations in their latent magical signatures; however, such data is only admissible in a formal proceeding if it can be isolated from environmental noise and secondary tampering. The court must distinguish between the inherent \"voice\" of an object—as permitted under § 300.13 for ritualistic variance—and the deliberate manipulations imposed by a third party.\n\nIn cases involving relics recovered from sites of high magical instability, the prosecution or defense may submit data regarding the relic's resonance patterns as prima facie evidence of its prior location or usage. However, such evidence must be scrutinized under § 700.12 to ensure that no \"collateral contamination\" has occurred during the extraction process. If a relic’s signature is found to be warped by subsequent proximity to unauthorized casting, the court may strike the testimony as unreliable. The Accord maintains that while an object cannot testify in words, its physical degradation and magical decay under § 500.13 constitute a measurable record of historical interaction with arcane forces.\n\nFurthermore, any artifact utilized as evidence must be subjected to a mandatory certification process before it is admitted into the record. This certification ensures that the relic's current state is documented against known standards of decay, thereby preventing the fabrication of \"new\" magical signatures by unscrupulous practitioners. Evidence derived from artifacts shall not be considered sufficient for a conviction on its own unless corroborated by secondary sources or verified through the protocols established in § 100.13 regarding inter-factional arbitration. This prevents the Innovator faction from utilizing experimental, high-variance resonance techniques to bypass standard evidentiary hurdles set forth by the Conservators.\n\nThe court shall strictly prohibit the admission of any artifact whose provenance is obscured by \"shadowed\" history or which has been subjected to unapproved modifications under § 400.13. If a relic’s origin cannot be traced back at least three cycles before its current discovery, it may only be admitted as circumstantial evidence regarding environmental conditions rather than specific acts of intent. Any attempt to present \"living\" artifacts—those possessing even the most rudimentary form of semi-sentience—as witnesses shall be summarily rejected under § 200.13, as such entities fall outside the scope of non-sentient material resonance and are subject to different legal standards for sentient agency. All evidence must ultimately conform to the overarching requirements of construction and aggregation as defined in § 1.7.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "300.13",
            "700.12",
            "500.13",
            "100.13",
            "400.13",
            "200.13",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Evidentiary Standards for Non-Sentient Material Resonance",
          "text": "The adjudication of arcane disputes within the Autumnwood Accords requires a rigorous distinction between sentient testimony and the material resonance emitted by non-sentient artifacts. Under § 1.6, \"resonance\" is defined as the involuntary acoustic or magical signature produced by an object reacting to external arcane stimuli. When such resonance is introduced into a legal proceeding to establish intent or presence, it must satisfy the standards of physical stability as outlined in § 1.7. The court shall not accept raw, unshielded resonance as primary evidence; rather, it must be captured within a stabilized containment field to ensure that environmental fluctuations do not contaminate the data.\n\nIn cases where an artifact is not sentient, yet its resonance provides proof of historical usage or proximity to forbidden schools, the prosecution must demonstrate that the resonance is \"stable.\" A stable resonance is one that remains consistent across multiple observations. If the resonance fluctuates wildly, it may be deemed inadmissible under § 700.12 due to potential contamination by external magical interference. Any artifact-derived evidence must be accompanied by a certification from an approved investigator—typically an Aegis Mage—confirming that the material has not been tampered with or subjected to unauthorized \"Innovator\" modifications as defined in § 400.13.\n\nFurthermore, when non-sentient objects are used to prove the presence of prohibited magic, such as those restricted by the Conservators under § 800.11, the evidence must be cross-referenced with a physical survey. A mere \"echo\" of an arcane event is insufficient for a conviction; the court requires proof that the artifact itself was physically present at the site of the transgression. This distinction is vital to ensure that phantom signals or lingering magical residue are not mistaken for active violations of the Accords.\n\nFinally, any material evidence derived from non-sentient objects must be filed with a corresponding certification of origin. If an object’s provenance is unclear, its resonance may only be used as circumstantial evidence rather than primary proof of guilt. This protects the accused from being prosecuted based on \"ghost\" signals that lack a clear physical anchor. All such proceedings regarding artifact-based testimony shall adhere to the procedural requirements for arcane litigation found in § 700.12, and any items seized during the investigation must be cataloged according to the protocols for containment and breach mitigation under § 200.13.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "1.7",
            "700.12",
            "400.13",
            "800.11",
            "200.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Evidentiary Standards for Non-Sentient Material Resonance — Continued",
          "text": "The admissibility of evidence derived from non-sentient arcane relics is governed by the principles of resonance stability and historical purity. Under § 1.6, \"resonance\" is defined as the measurable sympathetic vibration of an object’s magical signature when exposed to a specific inquiry or ritualistic query. When a relic—defined herein as any non-living object imbued with lingering mana—exhibits such a response, the resulting data may be admitted into a formal proceeding only if the resonance remains consistent across three consecutive diagnostic sweeps. Such consistency is required to ensure that the evidence reflects the inherent properties of the item rather than a transient fluctuation caused by external environmental factors or unauthorized proximity by unlicensed practitioners.\n\nIn cases where a relic’s material composition has been altered by significant magical trauma, the court shall apply the standards established in § 700.12 regarding evidentiary weight. If a relic displays \"unstable resonance,\" it may be excluded as evidence of intent, though it may remain admissible to establish the physical location or duration of a past arcane event. This distinction is vital when dealing with artifacts from the forbidden schools; while the object itself may be seized under § 800.12, its testimony regarding historical events must be strictly quarantined to prevent the contamination of current legal proceedings. Any evidence gathered from an artifact that shows signs of \"haunting\" or sentient-echoing shall be redirected to a specialized inquiry under the protocols for containment and breach mitigation found in § 200.13.\n\nFurthermore, any non-sentient material resonance used as primary evidence must be verified by a certified adjudicator to ensure no unauthorized manipulation has occurred. If an artifact is suspected of being \"tainted\" by external influence—such as the lingering signatures of the Iron Legion or other hostile entities—the prosecution must prove that the resonance was captured within a vacuum-sealed, magically shielded environment. Failure to provide such proof may result in the immediate dismissal of the evidence and a subsequent investigation into the handler's credentials under § 100.2. The Guild maintains that while objects do not possess voices, their material echoes serve as a silent record; however, these records are only legally binding when they conform to the strict interpretative nuances of the Accord’s foundational definitions regarding what constitutes \"automatic\" versus \"induced\" resonance.\n\nFinally, any relic utilized during a trial must be submitted for a mandatory inspection to confirm it does not contain active parasitic entities or sentient remnants that could influence the proceedings through subtle telepathic means. If an artifact is found to possess such traits, it shall be immediately sequestered as per § 200.13 and its testimony struck from the record. The prosecution of a mage under these circumstances must rely solely on physical evidence or witness testimony, as the \"voice\" of the object is deemed inadmissible if it can be shown that the object possesses any level of sapience or independent agency. All such determinations regarding the nature of an artifact's sentience are governed by the definitions provided in § 1.6 and the overarching construction rules found under § 1.7.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "700.12",
            "800.12",
            "200.13",
            "100.2",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Authentication of Resonance Records in Non-Sentient Artifacts",
          "text": "The admissibility of evidence derived from non-sentient arcane artifacts within the Autumnwood Accords is governed by strict evidentiary standards to ensure that \"resonance\" is not conflated with intentional testimony. Under § 1.6, a distinction must be maintained between sentient communication and the passive discharge of magical signatures. When an artifact lacks a conscious consciousness—thereby excluding it from the protections afforded to persons under § 1.4—it may still yield data regarding its history, proximity to high-level casting, or exposure to forbidden schools. Such data is classified as \"Material Resonance\" rather than testimony. For these records to be admitted in a formal inquiry, they must be verified by an Aegis Mage to ensure the resonance has not been artificially inflated or spoofed by unauthorized manipulation of the local mana field.\n\nFurthermore, any evidence derived from such artifacts must be cross-referenced with the protocols for containment and breach mitigation as outlined under § 200.13. If an artifact exhibits \"volatile\" resonance—defined as fluctuations that suggest a lingering instability in the object’s physical form—the resulting data may only be admitted if it is accompanied by a certification of stability from the Conservators. The Innovator faction may argue for the inclusion of \"unstable\" data to prove the existence of experimental research; however, such claims must still adhere to the overarching safety mandates of § 400.13 regarding deviant methodology. Evidence suggesting that an artifact was used in a manner inconsistent with its intended design shall be flagged for immediate scrutiny by the Archmage’s office.\n\nIn cases involving potential breaches of the Quiet List, evidence of resonance from non-sentient objects is only admissible if it can be proven that the resonance reflects a \"natural\" occurrence of magical decay rather than an intentional act of communication. This distinction is critical to prevent the unauthorized dissemination of restricted information in public spheres. Any artifact found to have been modified to broadcast its internal state must be seized under the provisions for illegal casting as defined in § 800.12. Furthermore, if a piece of evidence involves material that has been exposed to \"corrupted\" mana sources, it must undergo a purification process before being entered into the record to prevent magical contagion from affecting the court’s proceedings, consistent with the standards set forth in § 1101.12.\n\nFinally, any artifacts presented as evidence must be accompanied by a certified provenance document. This document shall certify that the item is not currently under an active seal or sequestration order. If an artifact's origin cannot be verified, it may be held in a pocket-prison for observation before its resonance is deemed admissible for use in determining guilt during a Paradox Trial. Any attempt to bypass these verification steps by claiming \"urgent necessity\" shall be viewed as an attempt to circumvent the established legal safeguards of the Autumnwood Accords and may result in immediate sanctions against the presenting party under § 1301.12.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "1.4",
            "200.13",
            "400.13",
            "800.12",
            "1101.12",
            "1301.12"
          ]
        }
