    {
      "cite": "800.13",
      "part": "part_viii",
      "title": "Mandatory Reporting of Arcane Artifact Malfunction and Degradation",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Mandatory Reporting of Arcane Artifact Malfunction and Degradation",
          "text": "The scope of this provision encompasses the mandatory reporting obligations imposed upon all practitioners, scholars, and associated laborers who come into contact with arcane artifacts exhibiting signs of structural instability or inherent magical volatility. Pursuant to the standards established under § 800.11, any artifact that demonstrates a deviation from its baseline magical signature—specifically those fluctuations resulting in uncontrolled energy discharge or spatial distortion—must be immediately flagged for assessment by an authorized officer of the Autumnwood Accords. Such reporting is not optional; it constitutes a foundational duty of care to ensure the safety of the public and the integrity of the local weave. Failure to report such malfunctions may result in severe penalties under § 800.12, as the omission of data regarding unstable artifacts poses a significant risk of collateral contamination.\n\nThe criteria for \"malfunction\" are defined by the degree of deviation from sanctioned parameters. If an artifact's output exceeds its permitted threshold or if it begins to manifest effects unrelated to its primary function—such as unintended necrotic seepage, uncontrolled kinetic discharge, or minor rift-tears—the user shall immediately initiate a containment protocol. In instances where such containment is impossible without the intervention of specialized personnel, the user must invoke the protocols for emergency reporting and secure the site. Under § 1.6, any artifact found in a state of advanced degradation that has not been reported by its previous custodian shall be subject to immediate seizure under the provisions of § 800.10. The determination of \"degradation\" is a matter of technical assessment; however, if an item exhibits signs of being influenced by forbidden schools or unauthorized experiments as defined in § 400.13, it must be surrendered immediately without delay for inspection by the Conservators.\n\nFurthermore, any artifact that has been subjected to prolonged exposure to high-density mana zones or \"unstable fields\" must undergo a mandatory re-certification period every thirty days. During this period, the artifact’s signature is compared against the standard logs maintained in the Autumn Wood archives. Should an artifact fail this verification, it shall be marked as \"Unstable\" and its use by non-certified personnel shall be prohibited under § 800.1. The reporting of such failures must be filed within forty-eight hours of the discovery. Any individual who knowingly conceals the degradation of a tool or relic to maintain their license to practice may be subject to the Paradox Trial as outlined in § 800.2, and may face immediate revocation of their credentials under § 1.4.\n\nThe procedures for reporting shall follow the established channels for non-emergency documentation unless an active breach is occurring. In cases where a breach is active, the Aegis Magi are authorized to intervene and seize the artifact immediately. The physical location of such artifacts must be recorded in accordance with standard inventory practices; however, any item that has been flagged as \"at risk\" due to its proximity to restricted zones or unauthorized research sites shall be subject to heightened scrutiny under § 100.3. All reports of malfunction must include a detailed log of the circumstances leading to the degradation, including the date of last maintenance and the identities of all individuals who operated the artifact in the twenty-four hours preceding the report. Failure to provide such documentation may result in an automatic presumption of negligence under § 700.11.\n\nIn matters involving artifacts that have been modified by Innovators without the explicit approval granted under § 400.13, any and all malfunctions shall be treated as a breach of the core Accord protections. Such items are subject to immediate confiscation and may lead to the permanent revocation of the user's right to conduct research. The identification of such modifications is handled through standard forensic analysis; if an artifact cannot be verified against its original manufacturing stamp, it must be quarantined until a determination can be made by the presiding council under § 100.1. All parties are reminded that the Autumnwood Accords prioritize the stability of the realm over individual research interests or property rights. Consequently, any item deemed by the Guild to be a threat to public safety due to its malfunctioning state shall be permanently removed from circulation and destroyed if necessary to prevent further harm, as per the enforcement measures detailed in § 800.13.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "800.11",
            "800.12",
