        },
        {
          "heading": "Protocol for the Identification and Reporting of Volatile Relic Decay",
          "text": "Any arcane artifact, whether it be an item of common utility or a relic of significant historical weight, is subject to the oversight requirements established under § 800.12 regarding unlawful casting and confiscation. When such an object exhibits signs of structural degradation, mana-leakage, or erratic behavioral shifts—hereafter referred to as \"Malfunction\"—the holder of said artifact is bound by these Accords to initiate a formal report. A malfunction is defined under § 1.6 as any deviation from the artifact's intended primary function that poses a risk to local inhabitants, environmental stability, or the sanctity of the weave. Such instances must be reported immediately to the nearest authorized repository or through a registered courier to the Autumn Wood headquarters.\n\nThe determination of whether an artifact is in a state of \"Degradation\" shall be evaluated based on the criteria for physical and metaphysical integrity as outlined in § 1.7. If an object begins to emit uncontrolled radiation, exhibit spontaneous animation, or display unpredictable elemental outbursts, it is deemed a public hazard. In such cases, the holder must secure the item within a lead-lined or magically dampened container before transport. Failure to contain a malfunctioning artifact during transit may result in a finding of negligence under § 500.13 regarding environmental arcane decay. The reporting process must include a detailed log of the symptoms observed, the duration of the malfunction, and any attempts made by the holder to stabilize the item using approved methods as described in § 400.13.\n\nFurthermore, certain artifacts identified on the \"Quiet List\" are subject to specialized handling protocols; however, the requirement for reporting a critical failure remains absolute regardless of the object's classification. Under § 100.13, mediation between the Conservators and the Innovators may occur regarding whether a malfunctioning item should be sequestered or allowed to remain in the hands of its owner for study. However, if an artifact demonstrates a \"Class-A\" breach—defined as a rupture in local reality or unauthorized spatial folding—the Aegis Magi are authorized to seize the object immediately under § 800.12 without prior notice. Such seizures do not constitute a violation of ownership but rather a mandatory protective action to prevent catastrophic consequences.\n\nAny artifact that has undergone significant degradation and was subsequently repaired by an unlicensed practitioner shall be subject to investigation for \"Contamination\" as defined in § 1101.11. The reporting of such a malfunction serves as the primary mechanism for determining whether the item still meets the safety standards required for public circulation. Documentation must be filed within forty-eight hours of the initial detection of any erratic behavior. Failure to report a known degradation may result in the forfeiture of the holder's right to possess arcane items and could lead to the initiation of a Paradox Trial under § 1301.13, where the severity of the non-reporting will dictate whether the punishment is a simple seal or a permanent relocation to a pocket-prison.\n\nAll reports concerning artifacts that have been modified by Innovator-sanctioned research must be cross-referenced with the research permits issued under § 400.13. If an artifact's failure is linked to unauthorized modifications, the subsequent penalties shall be escalated according to the severity of the breach of protocol. No report shall be deemed complete unless it includes a certified seal as defined in § 1.9; any document bearing an unofficial or forged mark will be discarded and may result in immediate prosecution for fraud against the Autumnwood Accords. All materials must remain within the jurisdiction of the Guild until a final determination of stability is issued by an appointed evaluator.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "800.12",
            "1.6",
            "1.7",
            "500.13",
            "400.13",
            "100.13",
            "1101.11",
            "1301.13",
            "1.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Protocols for the Containment of Degenerative Relic Resonance",
          "text": "Any arcane artifact exhibiting signs of structural instability, spontaneous energy discharge, or erratic behavioral fluctuations must be immediately reported to the nearest regional oversight officer under § 500.12. Such artifacts are deemed \"Degenerating\" when their internal magical matrices begin to fracture, leading to unpredictable outputs that may endanger the public or compromise the stability of local ley lines. The identification of these symptoms is critical; a relic showing signs of volatile decay must be isolated from other mundane and magical objects to prevent environmental contamination as outlined in § 1101.11.\n\nUpon the preliminary identification of a Degenerating Relic, the discovering party shall secure the item within a lead-lined or magically dampened container to suppress its resonance. This containment is not merely a precautionary measure but a mandatory procedural step for all practitioners seeking to maintain their standing under the Autumnwood Accords. If the artifact demonstrates \"volatile\" behavior—defined as an uncontrolled release of mana that threatens nearby life or property—the practitioner must initiate a localized dampening field. Failure to do so may result in immediate seizure of the item and potential prosecution under § 800.11 for the unauthorized possession of hazardous materials.\n\nThe reporting process must be initiated within three lunar cycles of the initial detection of degradation. The reporter shall file a formal declaration detailing the artifact’s origin, its current stability rating, and a log of all observed malfunctions. This documentation is essential for determining whether the item qualifies for stabilization by the Conservators or if it must be purged under the protocols for high-risk materials. During this period, any attempt to \"repair\" or modify the artifact without express permission from Archmage Veyra’s office shall be considered a violation of research integrity standards as defined in § 400.12.\n\nFurthermore, all artifacts that exhibit a significant degradation of their primary enchantment are subject to mandatory inspection by the Aegis Magi. If an item is found to have been modified or \"patched\" using unauthorized methods, the practitioner may be subjected to a peer review process to determine if such actions were necessitated by emergency circumstances. In cases where a relic’s decay poses a direct threat to public safety, the reporting party must act with extreme haste; any delay in reporting that leads to an uncontrolled discharge will be treated as gross negligence. For specific details regarding the legal consequences of failing to report known hazards, see § 1301.13.\n\nFinally, items that are deemed beyond salvage by the Guild’s technical examiners shall be moved into a permanent containment facility. These items are then removed from circulation and their associated licenses may be suspended until the owner can provide proof of adequate safety measures. All such actions are governed by the overarching rules regarding the management of hazardous arcane materials and the specific penalties for negligence in handling volatile artifacts as outlined under § 800.12. Practitioners who fail to comply with these containment and reporting mandates risk having their magic sealed through a formal Paradox Trial, as prescribed in § 1.6.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "500.12",
            "1101.11",
            "800.11",
            "400.12",
            "1301.13",
            "800.12",
