        {
          "heading": "Protocol for the Identification and Reporting of Volatile Relic Decay — Continued",
          "text": "All practitioners, regardless of their affiliation with the Conservators or the Innovators, are bound by this mandate to identify and report any instance of volatile relic decay. Under § 1.6, an \"Arcane Artifact\" is defined as any non-biological object possessing a persistent magical signature; therefore, any such object exhibiting degradation in its primary function or secondary resonance must be reported immediately. This obligation applies even if the artifact is currently held by a third party who does not possess a formal registration under § 1.4. The detection of volatility—defined as an unpredictable fluctuation in mana output exceeding three standard units per hour—triggers an immediate reporting requirement to the nearest Aegis station or a designated Guild liaison.\n\nThe identification process for such decay shall involve a preliminary assessment of the artifact’s core integrity. Practitioners must determine if the degradation is localized or systemic. Localized instability, while less severe than systemic collapse, still necessitates a formal filing under § 800.13 to ensure that any potential spillover does not contaminate nearby environments. In cases where a relic’s containment field shows signs of thinning or \"bleeding\" into the surrounding atmosphere, the practitioner must initiate an immediate stabilization protocol as outlined in § 200.13. Failure to report such a breach may result in the automatic initiation of a Paradox Trial for the responsible party, as the threat posed by uncontrolled relic decay is considered a primary risk to regional stability.\n\nFurthermore, any artifact exhibiting \"Volatile Relic Decay\" must be isolated from public spaces and common thoroughfares. If an artifact’s degradation results in the physical manifestation of spectral residue or unintended arcane echoes, it shall be deemed a hazardous material under § 1101.11. In such instances, the reporting party is required to provide a detailed log of the decay's progression over the preceding forty-eight hours. This data is essential for the Guild’s internal monitoring and helps distinguish between natural degradation and intentional tampering or illicit modification by unauthorized elements.\n\nThe scope of this mandate extends to artifacts that are not currently in active use but remain within the possession of a known practitioner. Under § 100.3, the presence of such items requires ongoing maintenance; if an artifact becomes unstable while in storage, it is no longer considered \"dormant\" and must be treated as an active threat. Any individual found to have knowingly concealed the degradation of a relic for the purpose of continuing its use shall face severe penalties under § 800.12. The Guild maintains that transparency regarding the physical health of magical objects is paramount to preventing catastrophic failures that could breach local reality constraints or necessitate intervention by the Accord’s defense forces.\n\nFinally, any artifact that has been declared \"Critically Degraded\" and subsequently quarantined shall be subject to a mandatory audit. During this process, the original owner's permissions for restricted research under § 400.13 may be suspended until such time as the site of the decay is deemed stable. The reporting of these incidents serves not only as a safety measure but also as an evidentiary foundation should a dispute arise regarding the legality of a practitioner's current holdings or their fitness to hold high-level certifications. All reports must be filed in triplicate, with one copy retained by the local Aegis station and two copies submitted to the central archives at Autumn Wood for review by Archmage Veyra’s appointed scribes.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "1.4",
            "800.13",
            "200.13",
            "1101.11",
            "100.3",
            "800.12",
            "400.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Identification and Containment of Degenerative Relic Resonance",
          "text": "Any arcane artifact manifesting signs of structural instability, erratic mana discharge, or ontological drift is hereby classified as a \"Degenerating Relic.\" Under the Autumnwood Accords, any practitioner—regardless of their affiliation with the Conservators or the Innovators—who identifies such an object is legally bound to initiate immediate containment procedures. This obligation applies specifically to relics that exhibit signs of resonance decay, wherein the magic within the item begins to bleed into the surrounding environment, potentially causing localized spatial warping or material degradation. Such occurrences must be reported immediately to the nearest Aegis Magistrate under § 100.2, as failure to contain a deteriorating relic may constitute an act of negligence toward the stability of the Midlands.\n\nThe containment process for a Degenerating Relic must prioritize the isolation of the resonance from commoner populations and non-magical structures. In cases where the artifact’s magical signature begins to fluctuate beyond the standard variance allowed under § 1.6, the practitioner shall establish a physical and arcane perimeter. If the degradation is deemed \"volatile,\" as defined by the specific energy spikes recorded in historical logs of the Quiet List (which may not be disclosed in public forums), the responder must utilize stabilization wards to dampen the output until a containment team can arrive. Any attempt to bypass these protocols for the purpose of \"salvaging\" components from a failing relic is strictly prohibited and shall be treated as an intentional violation of safety mandates under § 400.13.\n\nIn instances where a relic’s decay produces significant environmental arcane decay, the responsible party must initiate remediation protocols as outlined in § 500.13 to ensure that the local ley lines are not permanently scarred by the artifact's expiration. Furthermore, any artifact exhibiting \"High-Level Decay\" (Type III or higher) must be immediately surrendered to the Guild for study and possible sequestration. Practitioners who attempt to conceal a degrading relic rather than reporting it are subject to immediate investigation. If such an object causes significant property damage or public harm, the practitioner shall face the penalties outlined in § 800.12 regarding the unauthorized possession of unstable materials.\n\nThe distinction between a malfunctioning tool and a deteriorating relic is critical for determining liability. A malfunction typically involves a failure of intent, whereas degradation implies a fundamental decay of the object's magical integrity. Pursuant to § 700.13, evidence of intentional tampering with a known decaying relic will result in an automatic referral to the Paradox Trial. No practitioner may claim \"Good Faith\" defense if they knowingly bypass reporting requirements for artifacts exhibiting signs of resonance bleed. All documentation regarding the specific nature of the decay must be filed with the Autumnwood Accords Desk within forty-eight hours of the initial detection, ensuring that the records remain consistent with the definitions provided in § 1.7.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "100.2",
            "1.6",
            "400.13",
            "500.13",
            "800.12",
            "700.13",
            "1.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Deterioration Analysis and Stabilization Protocols for Unstable Relics",
          "text": "Any arcane artifact, construct, or focus displaying symptoms of structural instability or ontological degradation must be immediately flagged for review by the presiding Aegis Magi. This obligation applies to all practitioners holding a valid permit under § 1.4, as well as those operating under special consideration from the Innovator faction. The standard for \"malfunction\" includes not only mechanical failure but also any deviation from the item's baseline magical signature, such as spontaneous heat emission, rhythmic humming outside of ritual use, or localized atmospheric distortion. Such occurrences may indicate a breach of containment protocols; therefore, practitioners are required to secure the object and initiate a formal report to the Autumnwood Accords Desk. Under § 1.9, any physical seals placed upon these artifacts for safety purposes must remain intact until an official surveyor can verify the integrity of the item's core.\n\nWhen a relic is identified as showing \"volatile decay,\" it enters a state of provisional quarantine. During this period, the artifact shall not be transported beyond the immediate precinct unless authorized by Archmage Veyra’s office. The deterioration of an artifact often results from improper handling or the unauthorized application of high-level casting, which may lead to \"leakage\" into the surrounding environment. Such leakage is governed under § 1101.12 regarding collateral contamination and must be mitigated before the item can be cleared for continued use. If a relic exhibits signs of resonance—where its magical signature begins to bleed into nearby objects or surfaces—the practitioner must immediately invoke the containment procedures described in § 200.13, ensuring that the degradation does not propagate through the local weave.\n\nFurthermore, any artifacts derived from sources outside the known sanctioned catalogs are subject to heightened scrutiny under § 400.13 regarding deviant methodology. These items often possess less stable internal structures and are prone to rapid degradation when exposed to standard environmental factors. Should an artifact fail during a transition or while being moved between jurisdictions, it must be reported as a \"breach of stability\" regardless of the intent of the caster. Failure to report such malfunctions promptly may result in a mandatory audit of the practitioner’s credentials and potential escalation to a Paradox Trial under § 100.3. All reports must include a detailed log of the artifact's behavior leading up to the point of failure, as well as any observed fluctuations in local mana density to ensure compliance with the standards established for environmental safety.\n\nIn cases where an artifact is deemed \"non-recoverable\" due to extreme decay or structural collapse, it shall be surrendered to the Conservators for permanent decommissioning. This process ensures that no unstable magic remains in circulation to pose a threat to the public or the sanctity of the Autumn Wood. Practitioners who fail to surrender such objects may find their licenses suspended under § 800.12. Furthermore, any artifact showing signs of sentient-like behavior or independent agency following a degradation event must be isolated immediately and reported as a potential high-risk anomaly. Such items are strictly prohibited from being handled without the direct supervision of Archmage Theron's agents, who specialize in the containment of volatile relics that have begun to manifest erratic properties due to their failing magical constraints.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
