    {
      "cite": "1101.13",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Liability for Residue and Lingering Arcane Echoes",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Residue and Lingering Arcane Echoes",
          "text": "The determination of liability regarding lingering magical signatures, hereinafter referred to as \"Residual Echoes,\" is governed by the principles of containment and stability established within the Autumnwood Accords. Under § 1101.6, any magic that persists within a physical vessel after its primary casting or utility has concluded is categorized as a collateral risk. The legal status of such echoes depends upon their activity level; \"Passive Resonance\" may be maintained under strict observation as permitted by the definitions in § 1.6, while \"Active Echoes\" are strictly prohibited from circulation without an official variance permit.\n\nIn instances where ownership is transferred via conveyance or gift, as outlined in § 1101.5, the transferor must provide a certified declaration of all known lingering signatures to the recipient. Failure to disclose active echoes during such a transaction constitutes a willful violation of the Accord and may result in the immediate seizure of the item under § 800.13. If an artifact’s signature originates from a \"blessing\" by a competing vendor, see § 1101.4, the current holder may be granted immunity from liability for malfunctions arising specifically from that blessing, provided the original seal remains intact as defined in § 1.9.\n\nThe Innovator faction may argue for the preservation of complex echoes for data collection; however, such claims are strictly subject to the oversight protocols and mediation procedures established under § 100.13. Any attempt to manipulate or \"harvest\" these signals without an explicit permit from Archmage Veyra shall be treated as unauthorized research and may result in a referral for a Paradox Trial if the resulting discharge causes environmental harm. Furthermore, any artifact that exhibits spontaneous manifestation of its internal echoes is deemed a breach of containment under § 1101.7.\n\nShould a lingering echo cause environmental degradation or collateral taint, the current holder remains liable for all remediation costs as prescribed by § 800.12. Any item found to possess an \"unstable\" signature that bypasses standard containment protocols must be surrendered to the Aegis Magi immediately for evaluation and potential sealing. This mandatory reporting requirement is non-negotiable and serves to ensure the continued stability of the Midlands region against unpredictable arcane fluctuations.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.6",
            "1.6",
            "1101.5",
            "800.13",
            "1101.4",
            "1.9",
            "100.13",
            "1101.7",
            "800.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Management of Persistent Aetheric Harmonics",
          "text": "The occurrence of residual aetheric resonance—herein referred to as \"Harmonics\"—within the jurisdictions governed by the Autumnwood Accords shall be subject to strict containment and remediation protocols. A Harmonic is defined as any lingering arcane signature, oscillation, or spectral echo that persists in a physical location for more than one lunar cycle following the cessation of an active casting. Such manifestations are deemed liabilities rather than active magic; however, if a mage fails to neutralize these echoes within the prescribed timeframe, they shall be held liable for any environmental degradation or collateral harm caused by the lingering energy. For specific definitions regarding the distinction between active spells and residual remnants, see § 1.6.\n\nThe determination of whether an intentional \"grace period\" may be granted for a persistent Harmonic is often a point of contention between the Conservators and the Innovators. Under these circumstances, any mage seeking to maintain an active resonance for research purposes must submit a petition to Archmage Veyra. Such petitions are subject to the scrutiny of the oversight protocols established under § 400.13. If the petition is denied or if the mage fails to secure express consent from the Accord’s mediation office, any resulting instability shall be deemed a willful violation of the safety mandates, and the offender may face immediate prosecution by the Aegis Magi.\n\nThe Aegis Magi are authorized to conduct proactive patrols in areas known for high-density casting to identify and neutralize unmanaged Harmonics. These agents possess the authority to seize any equipment or materials involved in the creation of a persistent resonance if that resonance is deemed unstable. Pursuant to § 500.12, these officers may establish cordons around \"hot zones\" where lingering echoes threaten to bleed into neighboring territories or public thoroughfares. Any mage found within such a zone without a valid permit for specialized field operations shall be subject to immediate detention and the confiscation of their casting focus.\n\nFurthermore, any Harmonic that manifests as an unpredictable anomaly—such as spontaneous environmental warping or temporal flickering—must be reported immediately to the central registry. Failure to report such occurrences may result in severe penalties under § 900.13 regarding the reporting of ancillary and sub-layered effects. If a lingering resonance is linked to a malfunctioning artifact, the owner of said artifact shall be held strictly liable for any damages, regardless of whether the malfunction was accidental or intentional, as per the mandates found in § 800.13.\n\nIn cases where a persistent Harmonic creates a localized hazard that poses a risk to non-magical personnel or public infrastructure, the responsible mage must provide a remediation plan within twenty-four hours of identification by the Aegis Magi. If no such plan is provided, or if the remediation is deemed insufficient by the presiding adjudicator, the matter shall be escalated for formal review. The presence of lingering magic does not constitute an exception to standard conduct; all mages are expected to maintain the sanctity of the physical realm as dictated by the overarching safety standards of the Accord.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "400.13",
            "500.12",
            "900.13",
            "800.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Stabilization of Residual Arcane Signatures",
          "text": "Any practitioner performing magical acts within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords shall be held strictly liable for the stabilization of their work. This obligation encompasses not only the primary intended effect of a spell or ritual but also any secondary \"echoes,\" lingering harmonics, or mana-residue left in the wake of casting. Such residue is defined under § 1.6 as any arcane signature that persists beyond the standard dissipation period required for local environmental stability. It is the duty of the mage to ensure that no volatile remnants remain to interfere with the natural flow of the weave or to pose a risk to adjacent areas.\n\nThe distinction between \"lingering\" effects and \"unintentional residue\" is critical in determining the severity of a violation. While certain high-level rituals may necessitate temporary arcane stability, these must be documented and approved prior to execution. Research conducted under the oversight of the Innovator faction may involve higher risks of residual build-up; however, such practitioners are still bound by the requirement to mitigate any \"scars\" left upon the local environment. In cases where experimental methods result in persistent signatures that exceed safety thresholds, these instances shall be governed by the protocols for innovation oversight found under § 400.13.\n\nShould a site be identified as containing hazardous or lingering arcane fluctuations, the Aegis Magi are authorized to intervene and demand immediate remediation from the responsible party. If the mage is unable or unwilling to stabilize the area within the timeframe mandated by the presiding officer, the matter shall proceed toward formal penalty assessment under § 1301.12. Any equipment, conduits, or catalysts found to be actively contributing to a persistent resonance may be seized and impounded as evidence of non-compliance.\n\nFurthermore, if a mage’s failure to manage residue leads to tangible environmental degradation—such as the crystallization of local flora or the thinning of the veil—the practitioner shall face enhanced penalties for negligence. Such instances of significant environmental impact fall under the oversight of the Conservators and are subject to the specific remediation requirements outlined in § 500.13. Failure to proactively manage these risks may result in a formal review of the mage’s standing within the Guild, potentially leading to the revocation of their right to perform high-level research or public casting.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "400.13",
            "1301.12",
            "500.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Purification of Recurrent Aetheric Harmonics and Environmental Stabilizing Proce",
          "text": "Any arcane manifestation that leaves a discernible, measurable trace upon the physical or ethereal planes following the conclusion of a casting event shall be classified as \"Residual Flux.\" Under § 1.6, such flux encompasses both immediate magical waste products and delayed harmonic echoes which may destabilize local weaves if left unaddressed. Practitioners are strictly mandated to ensure that all ritualistic or practical magic concludes with a definitive stabilization phase. The failure to perform these procedures constitutes a breach of the safety protocols established by the Autumnwood Accords, particularly when such casting occurs in public spaces or near sensitive ecological zones where lingering vibrations could interfere with natural ley-lines.\n\nThe determination of liability for these lingering signatures rests solely upon the primary caster of record. Should an arcane signature persist beyond the standard dissipation window—typically defined as three lunar cycles unless a specific variance is granted—the mage shall be held liable for all subsequent remediation costs and mandated containment measures. This obligation persists regardless of whether the instability was a result of intentional experimentation or technical oversight. In instances where the complexity of the spell exceeds the caster's current clearance, they must seek additional guidance under § 400.13 to ensure that the resulting harmonics do not contribute to broader environmental decay as described in § 500.13.\n\nFurthermore, any mage found to be intentionally cultivating \"persistent echoes\" for research purposes—a practice frequently debated between the Innovator and Conservator factions—must submit a formal declaration of intent. These declarations are reviewed under the criteria for innovation oversight to ensure that such lingering harmonics do not pose a threat to the public. If an echo is deemed volatile or uncontainable, the Aegis Magi may intervene to impose immediate containment measures as outlined in § 200.13. Any mage failing to stabilize their output and allowing \"hot zones\" to form shall be subject to review for potential disciplinary action under the Paradox Trial if the resulting instability causes significant harm to non-magical entities or infrastructure.\n\nFinal stabilization procedures must be documented and filed with the appropriate local authority upon the completion of any large-scale ritual or high-energy casting. These documents serve as legal proof that the caster has fulfilled their duty to neutralize lingering signatures and stabilize the local environment. In cases where a signature is deemed \"volatile,\" the mage must employ specialized containment sigurals to prevent the leakage of essence into adjacent planes, ensuring full compliance with § 1.9 regarding the proper application of seals and stamps to secure volatile magical zones. Failure to provide such documentation may result in the suspension of casting privileges within the Midlands.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "400.13",
            "500.13",
            "200.13",
            "1.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Remediation Protocols for Lingering Aetheric Decay",
          "text": "Any arcane manifestation that persists beyond the intended duration of a spell, ritual, or sustained magical construct is officially classified as \"Aetheric Residue\" under § 1.6. Such residue frequently manifests as lingering harmonics, ghost-echoes, or decaying mana signatures that remain in the physical plane after the casting agent has ceased their work. While the Innovator faction argues that certain residual signatures are essential for documenting advanced research and high-level experimentation, the Conservators mandate the immediate neutralization of such remnants to ensure environmental stability. Practitioners are held strictly liable for any lingering echoes that manifest within public thoroughfares or private holdings without explicit authorization from the office of Archmage Veyra.\n\nThe determination of liability is predicated upon the duration and intensity of the arcane decay. If a signature remains active or detectable for more than three lunar cycles, it is deemed \"Persistent\" and requires mandatory remediation as outlined under § 1301.12. In such cases, the caster must provide documentation to the Accord’s registry confirming that the residue has been effectively scrubbed from the local environment. Failure to do so may result in a formal inquiry into the mage's professional standing. Furthermore, any aura or echo that begins to exhibit \"contagion\" symptoms—defined as the unsolicited growth of arcane flora or the warping of local physical constants—shall be treated under § 1101.11, necessitating immediate containment and remediation by authorized personnel.\n\nShould the neutralization of these harmonics require specialized equipment or intervention from outside the practitioner's primary circle, it must be requested through official channels. The Aegis Magi are empowered to intervene if an unmanaged signature poses a threat to public safety or creates a \"hazard zone\" that could destabilize neighboring regions. In such instances, the mage may have their research privileges restricted under § 400.13 until they can demonstrate mastery over the stabilization of their methods. Any residue that manifests as a self-sustaining loop or an autonomous magical entity is strictly prohibited and shall be treated as an unauthorized equipment malfunction under § 800.13, regardless of whether the mage intended for such a result.\n\nNon-compliance with these remediation protocols may lead to severe disciplinary actions against the practitioner’s standing within the Autumnwood Accords. If a mage intentionally leaves a lingering signature to serve as a waypoint or other unauthorized purpose, they shall be subject to the Paradox Trial to determine if their magic should be sealed or if they must face relocation to a pocket-prison. This determination is made in accordance with § 800.12 regarding the punishment of those who risk public safety through reckless arcane conduct. All remediation logs and purification records must be submitted to the Accord’s archives within thirty days of the casting's conclusion, ensuring that all active sites are cleared of lingering aetheric decay as required by § 500.13.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1.6",
            "1301.12",
            "1101.11",
            "400.13",
            "800.13",
            "800.12",
            "500.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Allocation of Remedial Costs Among Construct Owners and Their Principal Mages",
          "text": "In the administration of the Autumnwood Accords, the financial and thaumaturgical burden associated with lingering arcane echoes must be clearly apportioned between the entity that initiated the casting and any constructs or familiars utilized to sustain it. While § 1101.13 establishes the general principle of liability for residue, it is imperative that specific mechanisms exist to prevent a single mage from being held responsible for the cumulative decay generated by autonomous constructs operating under their command. Under § 1.4 Persons Bound, any construct deemed sufficiently intelligent to possess an independent magical signature shall be treated as a separate legal actor regarding its own output; however, the ultimate liability for the residue remains with the principal mage unless proof of gross negligence or unauthorized deviation is presented in accordance with § 1.9 Rules of Construction and Interpretation. This distinction is vital when assessing claims against Goodstyle Artisans, whose support structures often involve complex networks of minor constructs that may inadvertently leave behind persistent aetheric imprints long after their activation cycles have ended.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords must address the scenario where an arcane echo persists not because of initial casting error, but due to environmental factors that interact with residual signatures in unforeseen ways. As noted under § 500.14 Remediation and Obligations for Lingering Arcane Effects, certain acts of magical casting may yield unintended consequences that persist beyond their intended duration; however, the obligation to mitigate these effects shifts if the environment itself catalyzes the decay into a hazardous state. In such instances, the primary liability falls upon the local jurisdiction's containment officers rather than the original caster, provided that the caster adhered to all protocols set forth in § 200.13 Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation. This allocation of responsibility ensures that mages are not penalized for natural environmental interactions with their work, thereby encouraging continued research into high-yield thaumaturgical applications without fear of unjustified retribution for every variable outcome.\n\nThe concept of \"lingering\" also extends to the metaphysical realm, where ancient pacts and forgotten wards may reactivate due to proximity to recent magical activity. Under § 1301.13 Liability for Residue and Lingering Arcane Echoes in the Autumnwood Accords, it is imperative that mages and their constructs adhere to strict protocols regarding the aftermath of their magical activities, yet this duty does not extend to dormant entities unless they are knowingly activated or disturbed. The distinction between active residue and passive reawakening is critical for determining liability, as the latter often falls under the purview of § 500.13 Mitigation of Environmental Arcane Decay rather than direct caster negligence. Consequently, a mage who cleanses an area immediately after casting but then returns days later to find a dormant ward has flared due to shifting ley lines is not automatically liable for the resulting arcane storm, unless they failed to file the necessary report as required by § 1001.16 Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Members.\n\nFinally, the Accords must provide a framework for the transfer of liability when constructs are sold or transferred between guild members without proper clearance. If a construct known to harbor significant residual echoes is transferred without disclosure, the receiving party assumes full responsibility for any subsequent decay under § 700.8 Admissibility of Magical Traces and Residues, which deems evidence derived from residual enchantments admissible in determining fault. This provision serves as a deterrent against the careless disposal of hazardous magical artifacts and ensures that the history of an object's thaumaturgical usage is transparent to all parties involved in its ownership chain. By strictly enforcing these allocation principles, the Mages' Guild Accords Desk maintains a balance between fostering innovation and ensuring public safety, preventing the chaotic fallout of unchecked arcane echoes from undermining the stability of the Midlands jurisdiction.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1101.13",
            "1.4",
            "1.9",
            "500.14",
            "200.13",
            "1301.13",
