    {
      "cite": "1201.13",
      "part": "part_xii",
      "title": "Additional Provisions for Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Additional Provisions for Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords Desk hereby promulgates additional provisions concerning planar transit and the obligations thereof. These provisions are designed to ensure compliance with the mandates of this Codex while preserving the integrity and stability of planar travel. The following rules apply:\n\nUnder § 1201.13, all mages engaging in planar transit must adhere strictly to the protocols established for such crossings. This includes the requirement to obtain a valid transit stamp from an authorized Aegis Mage before commencing any planar journey. The purpose of this requirement is not only to ensure the safety and stability of planar travel but also to prevent unauthorized or harmful incursions into other planes.\n\nThe obligation to secure a transit stamp is mandatory for all mages, regardless of their affiliation with the Mages' Guild or any other organization. Failure to obtain a valid stamp constitutes a violation of the Autumnwood Accords, subjecting the offending party to the penalties detailed in § 800.11. See also § 200.13 for protocols regarding arcane containment and breach mitigation.\n\nTransit stamps must be applied for at least forty-eight hours prior to intended travel, unless otherwise approved by an Aegis Mage. The application process involves a survey of the mage's intended destination to ensure compliance with all relevant Accords. This includes verifying that no violations have occurred in the target plane and ensuring that any necessary environmental or stability measures are in place.\n\nUpon approval, the transit stamp shall be issued as proof of compliance with these provisions. Mages who attempt to circumvent this requirement or forge a transit stamp will face severe consequences under § 1001.11. The intent behind such acts is considered a direct affront to the stability and integrity of planar travel.\n\nThe Aegis Magi are responsible for monitoring all planar crossings, ensuring that all mages comply with these provisions. Any mage found in violation shall be subject to immediate sanctions under § 800.11, including but not limited to having their magic sealed or being cast into a pocket dimension. The enforcement of these measures is critical to maintaining the balance and harmony of planar existence.\n\nIt should be noted that while this section focuses on the transit stamps required for planar travel, it also encompasses the broader obligations related to planar sovereignty and jurisdiction. These obligations are designed to protect the integrity of other planes from potential breaches or incursions by rogue mages. Under § 1201.8, extraterritorial jurisdiction applies in such cases, ensuring that all violations are addressed promptly and effectively.\n\nFurthermore, any mage found guilty under these provisions may seek a waiver or modification through express consent as detailed in § 1201.9. However, this process is subject to the discretion of the Aegis Magi, who must ensure that any such modifications do not compromise the stability of planar travel.\n\nIn conclusion, adherence to these additional provisions is crucial for maintaining the integrity and balance of planar transit. All mages are required to comply with the protocols outlined in this section to prevent unauthorized or harmful incursions into other planes. Any breach of these provisions will be met with severe consequences under § 800.11.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1201.13",
            "800.11",
            "200.13",
            "1001.11",
            "1201.8",
            "1201.9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Enforcement Protocols for Arcane Cartography and Surveying",
          "text": "In the wake of recent developments in arcane cartography, the Autumnwood Accords Desk has seen an increase in requests for survey licenses to map planar transit routes. While such requests are valid under § 1201.12, the process of stamping obligations is not merely a formality but a crucial step in ensuring compliance with the Accords and preventing the spread of collateral taints (see § 1101.11). This additional provision aims to clarify and standardize the enforcement protocols for those seeking to map or survey planar transit routes, thereby fostering greater transparency and accountability among mages.\n\nThe process begins with an application for a survey license, which must be submitted in writing to the Accords Desk (see § 100.1). The applicant shall provide detailed plans of the proposed route along with any relevant maps or surveys that have been conducted previously. These documents are critical as they form the basis upon which the Desk will evaluate potential risks and benefits.\n\nOnce received, the Desk conducts an initial review to ensure compliance with § 400.13’s protocols for innovative methodologies. If there is any doubt regarding the legality of certain techniques or materials used in the survey process, immediate consultation with the relevant peers is mandated (see § 900.13). This step ensures that all parties involved are aware of potential pitfalls and can work together to mitigate risks.\n\nUpon approval, the Desk issues a provisional stamping order for the route, which must be carried out by a licensed surveyor or team of mages under the supervision of an Archmage (see § 200.13). The process involves several stages:\n\nFirst, all involved parties must undergo a thorough training session on the Accords’ protocols for planar transit and stampering obligations (see § 700.13). This ensures that everyone understands their roles and responsibilities.\n\nNext, the survey team proceeds to map the route using advanced techniques and tools, ensuring that every detail is meticulously recorded. Critical data includes environmental conditions, magical residues, and potential points of disruption or taint. All findings must be reported back to the Desk for review (see § 900.13).\n\nAfter completion, a final report is submitted detailing all observations and recommendations for further action if necessary. This document serves as proof of compliance and can be used in future disputes regarding planar transit routes.\n\nIn cases where the route involves crossing into territories governed by other laws or agreements (see § 1201.12), special considerations apply. The Desk must consult with relevant authorities to ensure that all parties involved are aware of potential legal implications and can work together towards a mutually acceptable solution.\n\nViolation of these protocols is taken very seriously. Any mage found guilty of unauthorized mapping or surveying without proper authorization may face severe penalties, including the sealing of their magic (see § 800.11). Furthermore, failure to follow established procedures could result in the revocation of one's Guild pass and potential involvement in a Paradox Trial under § 400.12.\n\nIn conclusion, these additional provisions aim not only to streamline the process but also to enhance safety and compliance across all planar transit routes. By adhering to strict protocols and maintaining open lines of communication, we can ensure that our guild remains at the forefront of magical innovation while remaining true to its principles of integrity and responsibility (see § 1401.12).\n\nThe Desk encourages all applicants to approach their requests with thoroughness and respect for the Accords. Together, we can preserve the integrity of our craft and protect the very essence of magic itself.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1201.12",
            "1101.11",
            "100.1",
            "400.13",
            "900.13",
            "200.13",
            "700.13",
            "800.11",
            "400.12",
            "1401.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Enforcement Protocols for Arcane Cartography and Surveying — Continued",
          "text": "In the practice of magical cartography, precision is paramount. The accurate depiction of planes and their interconnections is not merely an academic exercise; it is a matter of jurisdictional clarity and safety. This section outlines the protocols that govern the enforcement of arcane cartography and surveying practices within the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords.\n\nUnder § 1201.13, enforcement protocols for planar transit and stamping obligations are paramount to ensuring that mages adhere to their responsibilities in mapping planes. The primary concern here is the accurate representation of planar boundaries and the proper application of seals and stamps to ensure the integrity of the map. A mage who fails to comply with these protocols risks not only a reprimand but also potential legal action under § 1000.12.\n\nThe enforcement process begins with the initial survey, where the cartographer must adhere to strict guidelines set forth in § 400.13 and § 900.13. These sections emphasize the importance of research integrity and the reporting of ancillary effects that may arise during the creation or modification of a map. The mage conducting the survey is required to detail any anomalies, ensuring that no aspect of the plane is overlooked.\n\nOnce the initial survey is complete, the cartographer must then proceed with the mapping process. During this phase, adherence to § 600.13 and § 700.13 is critical. These sections address the liability for autonomous construct malfeasance and admissibility of artifact-derived testimony and relics. The mage must ensure that any constructs used in the surveying process are under strict control and do not inadvertently alter the plane's integrity.\n\nUpon completion of the mapping, the cartographer must apply stamps to the map as per § 1000.12. These stamps serve multiple purposes: they provide official certification of the mage’s work, establish jurisdiction over the mapped area, and act as a legal safeguard against unauthorized use or abuse. The application of these stamps is not merely a formalistic exercise but also serves to mitigate potential risks associated with the plane's cartography.\n\nThe enforcement protocols for arcane cartography and surveying extend beyond initial mapping. Regular maintenance and updates are required under § 1201.13. These updates ensure that the map remains accurate and relevant, reflecting any changes in the plane’s structure or magical activity. The mage responsible for maintaining the map must provide periodic reports on these changes to the appropriate authorities.\n\nIn cases where a mage fails to adhere to these protocols, severe penalties may be imposed under § 800.11. These penalties are designed not only to punish but also to deter future breaches of protocol. The Paradox Trial can be invoked if a mage’s actions pose a significant risk to the integrity of the plane or its inhabitants.\n\nIt is worth noting that the enforcement protocols for arcane cartography and surveying apply equally to both mages within and outside the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords. This ensures uniformity in practices across all planes, fostering greater cooperation and reducing the likelihood of misunderstandings or conflicts. Mages who operate under the auspices of other governing bodies are advised to consult these protocols when conducting cartographic work that intersects with the boundaries of the Autumnwood Accords.\n\nFinally, it is crucial for mages to recognize their responsibility in maintaining the integrity of planar transit and stamping obligations. The accuracy and reliability of maps directly impact the safety and well-being of all who traverse those planes. By adhering to these protocols, mages uphold the trust placed upon them by the Autumnwood Accords and contribute to a harmonious and secure magical community.\n\nUnder § 1000.12, it is imperative for mages to understand that any deviation from these protocols not only risks their professional standing but also can have broader implications for the entire community. The enforcement of these protocols ensures that all cartographic work is conducted with the highest standards of integrity and responsibility.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1201.13",
            "1000.12",
            "400.13",
            "900.13",
            "600.13",
            "700.13",
            "800.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Enforcement Protocols for Arcane Cartography and Surveying — Historical Preceden",
          "text": "In the realm of the Autumnwood Accords, the conduct of arcane cartography and surveying is subject to strict enforcement protocols designed to safeguard both the integrity of magical constructs and the stability of the planes themselves. The task of mapping the complex and often unpredictable nature of magic is not merely a matter of recording physical geography but involves deep considerations of legal precedent and the maintenance of planar order. This topic explores the historical development of these protocols, their legal implications, and the role they play in ensuring that all mages operate within the bounds set forth by the Accords.\n\nThe practice of arcane cartography is not a straightforward one; it requires an intricate understanding of both the physical and metaphysical landscapes. Historically, this has led to significant disputes between mages regarding the accuracy and legitimacy of their surveys. One notable example involves the Cartographer's Guild controversy in the early years of the Accords. The Guild sought to monopolize the practice of cartography, claiming that only those who had completed a rigorous training regimen could produce accurate maps. This stance was met with significant opposition from mages who argued for open access and free flow of information regarding magical territories.\n\nThe Paradox Trial, as outlined in § 800.11, played a crucial role in resolving such disputes by establishing the criteria that legitimate cartography must meet. It was determined that maps produced under these guidelines would be considered official and could not be challenged without due cause. This decision laid the foundation for the enforcement protocols we see today, which emphasize accuracy, transparency, and adherence to established standards.\n\nUnder § 1201.13, it is explicitly stated that all mages are required to obtain a surveyor's license before engaging in any form of arcane cartography. These licenses are not merely a formality; they serve as a testament to the mage’s commitment to upholding the Accords. The process involves rigorous examination and ongoing audits to ensure that the mage remains competent and adheres to best practices.\n\nThe legal implications of these protocols extend beyond mere compliance; they also have significant consequences for those who violate them. Under § 100.12, any coercive or degradational magic used in surveying operations is strictly prohibited. Violators face severe penalties, including the sealing of their magic and potential confinement to a pocket dimension as per the Paradox Trial protocol described in § 800.11.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords recognize that the misuse of cartographic data can have far-reaching consequences on the stability of magical planes. Thus, under § 600.13, liability for autonomous construct malfeasance is strictly regulated. This ensures that mages are held accountable not just for their direct actions but also for any unintended outcomes of their surveying endeavors.\n\nThe enforcement protocols for arcane cartography and surveying are thus a critical component in maintaining the balance between innovation and stability within the Mages' Guild Accords. They serve to protect both the practitioners themselves and the planes they inhabit, ensuring that all activities are conducted with the utmost care and respect for the magical order.\n\nIn conclusion, the enforcement of protocols for arcane cartography and surveying is not merely a technical issue but a legal one with profound implications. The Accords have established clear guidelines and stringent penalties to ensure that mages operate within acceptable boundaries, thereby safeguarding the integrity of their craft and the planes they traverse. These protocols are essential in maintaining harmony and stability within the magical community, ensuring that innovation and tradition coexist in a balanced manner.\n\nSee § 100.12 for prohibitions on coercive and degradational magic; see § 800.11 for sanctions against rogue mages; under § 600.13, liability for autonomous construct malfeasance is strictly regulated.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "800.11",
            "1201.13",
            "100.12",
            "600.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Enforcement Protocols for Arcane Cartography and Surveying: Ethical Consideratio",
          "text": "In the governance of magic, the realm of cartography and surveying holds a unique position. This section outlines the ethical considerations that mages must adhere to when engaging in any form of planar transit or stumping, ensuring that their actions do not merely serve the immediate needs of magical expeditions but also respect the broader implications for both the mage and the environment.\n\nUnder § 1201.13 Additional Provisions for Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations, it is imperative that mages consider the potential impact of their cartographic and surveying activities on the natural balance of the plane they are traversing. The Accords mandate that all surveys be conducted with a sense of responsibility, acknowledging the role that magic plays in maintaining the integrity of the planes themselves.\n\n### Ethical Responsibility to the Plane\n\nThe mage is required to conduct thorough preliminary research before commencing any cartographic or surveying activity. This includes an assessment of potential hazards and disturbances that may arise from the survey process (see § 100.3). The mage must ensure that the survey does not inadvertently disrupt local ecosystems, destabilize natural boundaries between planes, or create magical anomalies (see § 400.2). In instances where such risks are identified, the mage must seek alternative methods of achieving their objectives without compromising the integrity of the plane.\n\n### Consent and Community Engagement\n\nBefore engaging in any survey activity within a region inhabited by sentient beings, mages must obtain consent from the relevant authorities or representatives. This includes communities, guilds, and other organizations that may be affected by the survey (see § 100.4). Failure to do so is considered disrespectful of local customs and traditions and may result in sanctions under the Paradox Trial (§ 800.11). The mage must also engage with community leaders to understand their concerns and incorporate these into the survey plan, thereby ensuring that the activity benefits all parties involved.\n\n### Preservation of Cultural Heritage\n\nCultural heritage is a non-negotiable aspect of any survey conducted within regions rich in cultural significance (see § 600.13). Mages are required to take special care to avoid damage to historical sites and artifacts. If such damage occurs, immediate steps must be taken to restore the site or artifact to its original condition. In cases where restoration is not possible, compensation may be required under § 900.12.\n\n### Transparency in Reporting\n\nAll findings from cartographic surveys must be reported transparently to the relevant authorities and made available for public review (see § 700.13). This includes both positive outcomes and any unintended side effects of the survey. The mage is responsible for ensuring that this information is disseminated accurately and promptly, thereby fostering a culture of accountability and trust.\n\n### Continuous Learning and Improvement\n\nFinally, mages must commit to continuous learning and improvement in their methods of cartographic and surveying practices. This includes staying abreast of new technologies and techniques that can enhance the accuracy and safety of these activities (see § 400.13). The mage is encouraged to seek out peer review and feedback from colleagues, ensuring that each survey is conducted with the highest standards of integrity.\n\nIn conclusion, the ethical considerations outlined in this section are designed to ensure that mages operate within a framework that respects both the natural environment and the cultural heritage of the regions they traverse. By adhering to these protocols, mages can contribute positively to the preservation of the planes and enhance their reputation as custodians of magic (see § 100.5). The Accords emphasize the importance of responsible behavior in all aspects of magical practice, including cartography and surveying.\n\nUnderstandably, there will be instances where unforeseen challenges arise during a survey. In such cases, mages are advised to seek guidance from their peers or supervisors (see § 100.6) and to engage in open dialogue with those affected by the activities. This approach not only mitigates risks but also strengthens the bonds between different factions within the Guild.\n\nBy adhering to these protocols, mages can ensure that their actions are both responsible and beneficial to all parties involved. The Accords stand as a testament to the commitment of the Autumnwood Accords to maintaining balance and harmony across the planes of existence.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1201.13",
            "100.3",
            "400.2",
            "100.4",
            "800.11",
            "600.13",
            "900.12",
            "700.13",
            "400.13",
            "100.5",
            "100.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Additional Provisions for Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations: Regulatory Me",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords Desk, pursuant to the authority vested in it by § 1.2, hereby promulgates additional provisions governing the regulatory measures applicable to surveying and mapping activities within the jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild. These provisions are designed to ensure that all cartographic endeavors conducted under the auspices of the Guild adhere to rigorous standards and ethical considerations. The enforcement protocols outlined herein are intended to safeguard against unauthorized and potentially harmful uses of mapping magic, which could disrupt planar stability or compromise the integrity of surveyed regions.\n\nUnder § 1201.13, it is made clear that all acts of surveying and mapping conducted within the jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild must be formally recorded and registered with the Accords Desk. This requirement serves to prevent the unauthorized use of surveying magic, which could otherwise lead to unintended consequences for both mages and the planar fabric itself. The registration process is designed to ensure that all cartographic endeavors are conducted in a manner consistent with the established protocols laid out in § 400.12.\n\nThe Accords Desk emphasizes the importance of ethical considerations in surveying activities, as outlined under § 1301.12. Any mage engaging in surveying magic must demonstrate a thorough understanding of the potential risks and benefits associated with their work. The Desk mandates that all mages involved in such activities adhere to the principles of minimal interference and respect for the surveyed environment. This includes avoiding any actions that could disrupt local planar stability, disturb natural elements, or inadvertently create dangerous anomalies.\n\nFurthermore, under § 500.13, it is explicitly stated that all surveyors must take measures to mitigate environmental arcane decay, particularly when conducting operations in areas of known magical activity or sensitivity. This provision underscores the Guild's commitment to preserving the balance and harmony of the planar realms, a responsibility enshrined in the foundational principles articulated in § 100.13.\n\nThe enforcement protocols for surveying activities are designed to ensure compliance with these provisions. Mages found engaging in unauthorized mapping or failing to adhere to regulatory requirements risk facing sanctions under § 800.11. The Aegis Magi, who enforce the will of the dominant faction as outlined in § 100.2, play a crucial role in ensuring that all surveying activities are conducted in accordance with the Guild's policies.\n\nUnder § 700.13, it is established that any evidence derived from properly registered and surveyed maps can be admitted in legal proceedings under the jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild. This ensures that the results of legitimate mapping endeavors are recognized and respected by all parties involved. Conversely, those who engage in unauthorized or unethical surveying activities may have their findings disqualified as evidence, a measure designed to reinforce the importance of compliance with regulatory standards.\n\nThe Accords Desk also mandates that all cartographic surveys conducted within the jurisdiction must be completed using standardized techniques and methodologies, which are outlined in § 300.12. This ensures consistency across all surveying efforts, facilitating accurate and reliable mapping data. The use of non-standardized or experimental techniques is strictly limited to those projects explicitly authorized by the Guild's leadership under § 400.13.\n\nIn the event that a mage engages in surveying activities without proper authorization or fails to adhere to regulatory requirements, the Accords Desk reserves the right to impose sanctions under § 800.11. These sanctions may range from a temporary suspension of mapping privileges to more severe penalties, depending on the nature and extent of the violation.\n\nLastly, it is important to note that all mages involved in surveying activities must undergo regular training and certification programs, as outlined in § 400.12. This ensures that they remain up-to-date with the latest regulatory standards and best practices. Failure to maintain this level of proficiency may result in disciplinary action.\n\nIn summary, these additional provisions for planar transit and stamping obligations are designed to ensure that all surveying activities conducted within the jurisdiction of the Mages' Guild are carried out ethically and in accordance with established protocols. By adhering to these regulations, mages can contribute to the preservation of planar stability and harmony while ensuring the integrity and reliability of their cartographic endeavors.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1.2",
            "1201.13",
            "400.12",
            "1301.12",
            "500.13",
            "100.13",
            "800.11",
