    {
      "cite": "900.14",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Reporting Ancillary Effects and Sub-Layered Magic",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Reporting Ancillary Effects and Sub-Layered Magic",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords recognize that the use of magic is not always straightforward or singular in its impact. There are instances where ancillary effects or sub-layered magic can arise, often unnoticed by those who cast spells. These unintended consequences must be reported to ensure proper oversight and mitigate potential risks.\n\nUnder § 900.14 Additional Provisions for Reporting Ancillary Effects and Sub-Layered Magic, it is imperative that all mages report any ancillary effects or sub-layered magic they discover during their casting rituals or research activities. This reporting requirement applies equally to both Conservators and Innovators, as the Accords strive to maintain a balance of knowledge and responsibility within the Guild.\n\nWhen an arcane effect extends beyond its intended scope, it is the duty of the caster to document these ancillary effects promptly and accurately. Documentation should include a detailed description of the effect, the circumstances under which it occurred, and any potential risks associated with the phenomenon. Failure to report such effects in a timely manner may result in further scrutiny or penalties as outlined in § 1301.12.\n\nThe reporting mechanism is designed to be comprehensive and accessible. Mages are encouraged to utilize the standardized forms provided by the Guild for this purpose, which can be accessed through the Accords Desk. The desk will ensure that all reports are reviewed promptly and that necessary actions are taken to address any potential hazards.\n\nUnder § 200.13 Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation, if a mage discovers an ancillary effect or sub-layered magic that poses a threat, immediate containment measures must be implemented. The Aegis Magi shall be notified promptly of such situations to ensure the safety of all individuals involved.\n\nFurthermore, under § 1401.13 Preservation of Canonical Continuity and Scriptural Integrity, it is essential that mages do not alter or tamper with any effects they report in a manner that could compromise the integrity of the magical ecosystem. Any modifications must be documented separately and reported to the Accords Desk.\n\nIn cases where an ancillary effect proves beneficial and stable, there may be instances where further research into its properties is warranted. However, such research requires prior approval from the Archmage Veyra or her designee as outlined in § 400.13 Protocols for Deviant Methodology and Innovation Oversight. Unauthorized exploration of these effects could lead to severe penalties under § 800.12 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation.\n\nIn summary, the reporting of ancillary effects and sub-layered magic is a critical component of maintaining the integrity and safety of magical practices within the Autumnwood Accords. Mages are reminded that their actions not only affect themselves but also have broader implications for the Guild and its members. Failure to comply with these provisions may result in sanctions as per § 1301.12 Liability for Residue and Lingering Arcane Echoes.\n\nBy adhering to these protocols, the Autumnwood Accords ensure that any unintended consequences of magic are documented, managed, and where necessary, studied under controlled conditions. This approach not only promotes responsible magical practice but also fosters a culture of accountability among all members of the Guild.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.14",
            "1301.12",
            "200.13",
            "1401.13",
            "400.13",
            "800.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Unforeseen Planar Perturbations",
          "text": "Under the Autumnwood Accords, the liability for unforeseen planar perturbations resulting from arcane operations is a critical area that must be addressed with precision and diligence. The term \"unforeseen planar perturbation\" refers to any alteration or disturbance in the fabric of reality caused by magical activities, whether intentional or not. These perturbations can have far-reaching effects, affecting not only the immediate environment but also potentially impacting other planes of existence.\n\nThe Mages' Guild emphasizes that all mages are responsible for the consequences of their actions, especially when dealing with complex and advanced forms of magic that have the potential to ripple across dimensions or planes. The Accords mandate that any mage who performs an operation that results in such perturbations must report these occurrences immediately to the appropriate authorities within the Guild.\n\nUnder § 900.14 Reporting Ancillary Effects and Sub-Layered Magic, it is specified that all reports of planar perturbations must include a detailed description of the event, including the location, date, time, duration, and any witnesses present. The report should also outline the specific form of magic used, the intended effect of the operation, and any observed deviations from the expected outcome.\n\nMages are further required to provide any additional information that may be relevant to understanding the cause or scope of the perturbation. This can include records of previous operations in the vicinity, maps showing the exact coordinates of the location, and notes on any unusual weather patterns or environmental conditions present at the time of the operation.\n\nUnder § 1301.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations, it is emphasized that mages have a duty to mitigate any harm caused by their actions. In cases involving planar perturbations, this may include the use of counter-magic or other remedial measures to stabilize the affected area. The Guild has established protocols for such stabilization efforts, which are detailed in § 200.14 Additional Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation.\n\nFurthermore, under § 700.13 Admissibility of Artifact-Derived Testimony and Relics, evidence from artifacts that may have been affected by the planar perturbation can be used in legal proceedings to determine liability or to assist in understanding the nature of the disturbance. This evidence can include readings from arcane devices, records of magical signatures left behind, and any other relevant data.\n\nIt is important for mages to understand that failure to report such incidents promptly may result in severe penalties under § 800.12 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation. Mages found guilty of concealing or understating the extent of a planar perturbation can face sanctions ranging from temporary loss of magical abilities to permanent banishment.\n\nUnder § 300.12 Prohibition on Magical Correspondence and Transmission, it is also noted that any communication regarding the reported incident must be done in accordance with Guild protocols to ensure the integrity of the investigation and the fairness of subsequent proceedings. This includes the prohibition on using any form of magical correspondence or transmission that might skew or manipulate the reporting process.\n\nThe Accords recognize that unforeseen planar perturbations are not always easily avoidable, but they also emphasize the duty of mages to act responsibly and with due care in their operations. The Guild's stance is clear: mages must be prepared to face the consequences of their actions and to take proactive steps to mitigate any harm caused.\n\nIn conclusion, the liability for unforeseen planar perturbations underscores the importance of adherence to the Accords by all members of the Mages' Guild. By fostering a culture of responsibility and accountability, the Guild aims to ensure that magical operations are conducted in a manner that minimizes risk and maximizes safety for all.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "900.14",
            "1301.12",
            "200.14",
            "700.13",
            "800.12",
            "300.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Reporting Unforeseen Consequences of Arcane Interference",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords Desk hereby sets forth the protocols governing the reporting and mitigation of unforeseen consequences resulting from the interference with or interaction between arcane constructs, artifacts, and their intended use. This section is designed to ensure that all mages are held responsible for the unintended side effects of their magical operations, thereby promoting a safer and more regulated environment within the Mages' Guild.\n\nUnder § 900.14 Reporting Ancillary Effects and Sub-Layered Magic, Liability for Unforeseen Planar Perturbations, mages must report any unforeseen consequences that arise from their use or manipulation of magic. These consequences may include but are not limited to disruptions in the natural order of the planar environment, unintended alterations to the immediate physical space, or the creation of unexpected magical disturbances. The mage is required to document these effects and provide a detailed report to the Guild for review.\n\nThe rationale behind this requirement lies in the recognition that even with the most meticulous planning, unforeseen outcomes can occur. The Accords Desk seeks to ensure that all mages are aware of their responsibility not only for the direct effects of their magic but also for any secondary or tertiary consequences that may arise. This obligation extends to both intended and unintended interactions between magical constructs and the surrounding environment.\n\nIt is imperative that mages understand that a failure to report such unforeseen consequences can have serious implications for themselves and others within the Guild. Under § 1301.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations, those who fail to report such incidents may be held liable for any damages caused by their negligence. The Accords Desk encourages mages to exercise due diligence in adhering to these protocols.\n\nFurthermore, under § 600.13 Liability for Autonomous Construct Malfeasance, the Guild expects mages to maintain strict oversight over all autonomous constructs they create or use. Any unforeseen consequences resulting from such constructs must be promptly reported and addressed. This includes not only immediate physical effects but also any disruptions to the planar structure that may result from these constructs.\n\nThe Accords Desk emphasizes the importance of transparency in reporting. Mages are encouraged to document their observations meticulously, including any data or evidence that can assist in understanding the nature and extent of the unforeseen consequences. This documentation should be submitted within a reasonable time frame following the discovery of such effects.\n\nIn cases where the unforeseen consequences extend beyond the immediate vicinity of the mage's operations, the Accords Desk may convene a Paradox Trial to determine the appropriate course of action. The trial will assess both the extent of the disruption and the mage’s responsibility in causing it. Under § 1401.12 Preservation of Canonical Continuity and Scriptural Integrity, any actions that threaten the stability of the planar order are subject to severe penalties.\n\nThe Accords Desk recognizes that while mages may not always be able to predict every potential outcome of their arcane work, they must strive to minimize risk through careful planning and responsible action. By adhering to these protocols, mages contribute to the overall safety and integrity of the Guild’s operations. Failure to comply with these requirements can lead to sanctions under § 800.12 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation.\n\nIn conclusion, reporting unforeseen consequences is a critical component of responsible magic use within the Mages' Guild. It serves not only to protect the mage but also to safeguard the broader community from the potential hazards of unchecked magical activity. By adhering to these protocols, mages demonstrate their commitment to the preservation and enhancement of the planar order.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "900.14",
            "1301.12",
            "600.13",
            "1401.12",
            "800.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Reporting Unforeseen Consequences of Arcane Interference in Private Properties",
          "text": "Under the Autumnwood Accords, mages are bound by strict protocols to report any unforeseen consequences resulting from their arcane interference, particularly when such disturbances occur within private properties. The legal framework seeks not only to ensure that mages are held accountable for potential harm but also to protect the sanctity of private spaces and the integrity of personal property rights.\n\nThe concept of \"unforeseen consequences\" in this context encompasses a broad spectrum of outcomes, including physical damage, environmental contamination, and even psychological disturbances. The Accords mandate that any mage who engages in magical activities within a private property is required to report such unforeseen effects immediately upon becoming aware of them. This includes, but is not limited to, the creation of unintended rifts, the generation of residual magic, or the activation of latent magical signatures.\n\nThe reporting obligation applies regardless of whether the mage intended to create these ancillary effects. The principle of \"aggregation of conduct\" (see § 1.7) ensures that even minor disturbances must be reported if they cumulatively lead to significant consequences. For instance, repeated minor breaches within a single property could necessitate a comprehensive report due to their collective impact.\n\nThe Accords emphasize the importance of transparency and accountability in the magical community. Therefore, mages are required to document all relevant details concerning these unforeseen effects, including but not limited to:\n\n1. The nature and extent of the disturbance.\n2. The location where it occurred within the property.\n3. Any damage or harm caused as a result.\n4. Steps taken by the mage to mitigate the situation.\n\nFailure to report such incidents can lead to severe consequences under § 800.12 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation. Mages found in breach of this duty may face sanctions, including the sealing of their magic or even more stringent penalties (see § 1301.12 for penalty assessment).\n\nIn cases where the unforeseen effects extend beyond a single property, such as when they involve adjacent properties or public spaces, the mage is also required to notify local authorities and relevant parties under § 1401.13 Preservation of Canonical Continuity and Scriptural Integrity. This ensures that all stakeholders are promptly informed and can take appropriate measures.\n\nThe Accords recognize that some unforeseen effects may be beyond an individual mage’s control or knowledge at the time of casting. However, this does not absolve them from their reporting obligations. The principle of \"intent\" (see § 1.6) is applied in a manner that seeks to balance responsibility with practicality, ensuring that mages are held accountable only for those effects they could reasonably foresee.\n\nFurthermore, any mage who knowingly or recklessly ignores the reporting requirements can be subject to additional penalties, including loss of privileges and potential lifetime bans from certain magical activities (see § 1.4 Persons Bound). The Accords aim to instill a culture of responsibility among its members by enforcing strict adherence to these reporting protocols.\n\nIn conclusion, the duty to report unforeseen consequences of arcane interference in private properties is not merely a formal requirement but a fundamental aspect of maintaining harmony and order within the magical community. It reflects the broader principles outlined in § 1401.13 Preservation of Canonical Continuity and Scriptural Integrity, which emphasize the importance of preserving the integrity of magical practice and the sanctity of individual rights.\n\nBy adhering to these protocols, mages contribute to a safer and more stable environment for all, ensuring that any disruptions are promptly addressed and mitigated. The Accords’ framework is designed to foster mutual respect and cooperation among its members, reinforcing the values of responsibility and accountability in magical practice.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1.7",
            "800.12",
            "1301.12",
            "1401.13",
            "1.6",
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Reporting Unintended Planar Realignment",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords recognize the inherent unpredictability of magical interactions, particularly in advanced or experimental practices. While much attention is paid to the primary effects and outcomes of arcane casting, it is equally important that mages report any unintended planar realignments. Such realignments can have far-reaching consequences, affecting not only immediate surroundings but also potentially disrupting the very fabric of reality beyond the caster’s intended scope.\n\nUnder § 900.14 Reporting Ancillary Effects and Sub-Layered Magic, any mage who performs a spell or ritual that results in an unintended planar realignment must immediately report such occurrence to the nearest Aegis Magi. This requirement is binding upon all members of the Mages' Guild as well as those unaffiliated individuals performing covered magic within the jurisdiction defined by the Codex.\n\n### Obligations and Penalties\n\nFailure to comply with this reporting obligation can result in severe penalties. Under § 1301.12 Preservation of Canonical Continuity and Scriptural Integrity, mages found guilty of concealing or failing to report unintended planar realignments are subject to immediate sanctions that include the sealing of their magic for a period determined by the Paradox Trial. Additionally, such offenders may face additional punitive measures as deemed necessary by Archmage Veyra and the Conservators.\n\n### Scope and Definition\n\nAn unintended planar realignment is defined broadly in this context. It refers to any significant deviation from the expected outcome of a spell or ritual that leads to the alteration of the planar boundary between magical planes. For instance, a simple fireball intended for target practice may inadvertently create a rift leading to the summoning of elements from a different plane, thereby causing an unintended realignment.\n\n### Reporting Procedure\n\nThe reporting procedure is designed to be both efficient and comprehensive. Mages are required to provide detailed descriptions of their spell or ritual, including any modifications made during casting. They must also specify the nature and extent of the planar realignment observed. The Aegis Magi will then conduct an investigation to determine the cause and potential repercussions of such an event.\n\n### Liability for Consequences\n\nUnder § 800.12 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation, mages are held liable for any consequences resulting from their failure to report such events promptly. This includes financial penalties, loss of magical marks, or even disbarment from the Guild if repeated offenses occur.\n\n### Case Study: The Incident at Markop\n\nConsider a scenario where an unaffiliated mage, Archie Miser, performs an experiment in transfiguration using unauthorized field casting outside his Guild hall. During this process, he inadvertently causes a localized planar realignment, summoning creatures from another plane into the Midlands. This event not only disrupts local flora and fauna but also introduces elements that could destabilize nearby magical constructs.\n\nIn response to such an incident, the Aegis Magi must immediately survey the area and determine the appropriate course of action. Under § 1201.3 Planar Transit and Stamping Obligations, Archie Miser would be held accountable for any damage caused by the summoned creatures. Additionally, he may face further sanctions under § 800.12 if it is determined that his actions were reckless or negligent.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe reporting of unintended planar realignments is a critical aspect of maintaining the integrity and stability of magical practices within the Autumnwood Accords. By ensuring that mages are diligent in their reporting, the Guild can mitigate the risks associated with such occurrences and preserve the balance between various planes. Mages must take this responsibility seriously to uphold the safety and order necessary for all members of the arcane community.\n\nIn summary, any mage who performs a spell or ritual resulting in an unintended planar realignment is obligated to report it immediately to the nearest Aegis Magi. Failure to comply can result in severe penalties, including sealing of magic and disbarment from the Guild. The reporting procedure ensures that such events are investigated promptly, and liability is appropriately assigned to those responsible for the consequences. This section reinforces the importance of transparency and accountability within the magical community, fostering a safer environment for all practitioners.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "900.14",
            "1301.12",
            "800.12",
            "1201.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Unforeseen Planar Perturbations and the Doctrine of Involuntary An",
          "text": "The provisions set forth within this section shall govern the specific liabilities arising when an ancillary effect, unreported under § 900.13 or previously disclosed pursuant to § 900.6, manifests as a sustained planar perturbation that involuntarily anchors a localized reality to a foreign stratum. Where a primary casting, whether sanctioned or incidental, triggers a sub-layered resonance that fails to dissipate within the standard temporal window defined in § 900.12 Additional Provisions, the resulting state constitutes an involuntary anchor. It is hereby declared that any practitioner who initiates a magical operation knowing of potential instability must file a predictive risk assessment prior to casting, as required by § 900.1 Duty to File Before Casting; failure to do so shifts the burden of proof regarding causation entirely upon the initiating party. The Mages' Guild Accords Desk maintains that the stability of the Midlands is not merely a preference but a fundamental legal requirement, and thus, any entity found responsible for creating such an anchor shall be subject to immediate remediation protocols and financial restitution calculated based on the duration of the perturbation and the density of the foreign matter introduced.\n\nUnder § 1401.14 Conditional Exemption for Ancillary Planar Transits, it has been recognized that certain transitory applications of planar transit are permissible only if they remain strictly secondary to a primary authorized action; however, when an ancillary effect causes the ambient weave to fracture and allow foreign geography to bleed through reality, the exemption is voided retroactively. This section clarifies that the mere existence of a sub-layered magic does not absolve the caster of liability for the consequences of its unintended expansion. If a practitioner utilizes a technique involving dimensional folding without explicit certification of containment fields as mandated by § 900.6 Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic, any resulting planar leak shall be treated as a Class A Breach, regardless of the original intent or utility of the primary spell. The Conservators have long argued that magic is a sacred resource to be used sparingly and with respect, a principle codified in the Mana Reverence doctrine which reinforces the notion that reckless experimentation with sub-layers invites catastrophic failure. Consequently, the Accords Desk asserts its authority under § 900.9 See Also Provisions to demand full compliance with all standards of containment, including the deployment of Aegis Mages to seal breaches before civilian populations are exposed to planar instability.\n\nFurthermore, this section addresses the scenario wherein an ancillary effect interacts with existing magical artifacts or relics, creating a chain reaction that destabilizes local reality. As noted in § 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title, artifacts possess their own inherent magical signatures which may amplify sub-layered effects unpredictably. If a practitioner fails to account for the unique resonance of such an artifact during a casting involving planar interference, they shall be held liable for all damages resulting from the subsequent realignment. The penal system provided by The Paradox Trial allows for severe sanctions, including Sanction and Seal, against mages whose negligence leads to prolonged involuntary anchoring events. It is essential that practitioners understand that the authority of the Aegis Magi under § 900.6 extends to overriding unauthorized practices that threaten the structural integrity of reality itself. The Innovators faction may argue for greater flexibility in research, but the foundational statutes regarding safety and reporting remain paramount; thus, any attempt to bypass these requirements without a specific waiver from the High Council is strictly prohibited.\n\nFinally, this section establishes that liability extends beyond the immediate practitioner to any entity that knowingly facilitates such operations without adequate oversight. If a corporate guild or independent laboratory conducts experiments involving sub-layered magic without filing the requisite predictive assessments, they shall be subject to fines levied under the general provisions of Part 900. The distinction between intentional planar transit and accidental anchoring is critical, yet in cases where the line blurs due to incompetence or disregard for established protocols, the law presumes negligence. The Ravencreek Transition serves as a grim precedent, demonstrating that even when official liability is denied by high-ranking officials like Waluigi, the underlying legal reality of unintended planar shifts remains enforceable against those who created the conditions for such disasters. Therefore, all parties engaging in licensed magical practice must adhere to the rigorous standards set forth herein, ensuring that no ancillary effect goes unreported and that no sub-layered magic is allowed to anchor foreign realities to our plane without explicit consent from the Accords Desk and full restitution for any displaced inhabitants or damaged landscapes.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "900.13",
            "900.6",
            "900.12",
            "900.1",
            "1401.14",
            "900.9",
            "1101.2"
          ]
        }
