        {
          "heading": "Remediation and Obligations for Lingering Arcane Effects in Private Properties",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords Desk acknowledges the significance of safeguarding private properties against the adverse effects of lingering arcane effects. While the Codex has provided extensive guidelines on the mitigation of such effects, it is incumbent upon each mage to take responsibility for the integrity and safety of their own property. This section aims to outline the obligations and remediation protocols applicable when a mage discovers or encounters lingering arcane echoes within private spaces.\n\nUnder § 500.14, the doctrine of \"Lingering Arcane Effects in Private Properties\" mandates that all mages are required to report any detected anomalies or residues within their property immediately upon discovery. The mage must document the nature and extent of the effect, including any potential risks associated with continued presence, and submit this documentation to the nearest Aegis Magi for immediate assessment.\n\nThe Aegis Magi have the authority to issue directives based on a comprehensive evaluation of the situation, which may include containment protocols, remediation efforts, or even relocation if deemed necessary. It is imperative that mages comply fully with such directives to prevent further spread and potential harm. Failure to report or address lingering effects in private properties can lead to severe penalties under § 1401.12.\n\nThe mage must also maintain a log of all remediation activities undertaken, including any spells cast or materials used. This documentation is essential for future reference and compliance with the Codex's stringent requirements regarding preservation of canonical continuity and scriptural integrity (see § 1401.13). The Aegis Magi may request access to these logs at any time during their investigation.\n\nFurthermore, mages are obligated to provide a comprehensive restoration plan that ensures the property is returned to its original state or condition as closely as possible. This includes the removal of all traces of arcane residues and ensuring that no lingering effects persist. If the mage is unable to achieve this independently, they must seek assistance from authorized personnel who have the necessary expertise and resources.\n\nIn cases where a mage fails to comply with these obligations, the Aegis Magi may impose additional sanctions beyond those outlined in § 1401.13. These could include fines, suspension of magical abilities, or even relocation if it is determined that continued habitation poses an ongoing risk (see § 800.12 for unlawful casting and confiscation). The mage must also bear the costs associated with any remediation efforts undertaken by authorized personnel.\n\nIt is crucial to note that the Codex does not limit obligations to direct property owners but extends these responsibilities to all tenants, guests, or visitors within such properties. If a guest or tenant discovers lingering arcane effects, they are required to inform both the owner and the Aegis Magi immediately (see § 500.14 for reporting requirements).\n\nThe Autumnwood Accords Desk emphasizes that the preservation of private spaces from lingering arcane effects is not only an individual responsibility but also a collective duty. By adhering to these protocols, mages contribute to maintaining the integrity and safety of their communities. Failure in this regard may lead to broader implications affecting public order and safety.\n\nIn summary, the obligations imposed under § 500.14 are designed to ensure that all mages take proactive measures to prevent the adverse effects of lingering arcane echoes within private properties. Compliance with these guidelines is not only a legal requirement but also a moral responsibility in maintaining the balance and harmony of magical practices across the Midlands.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "500.14",
            "1401.12",
            "1401.13",
            "800.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Remediation and Obligations for Lingering Arcane Effects in Sacred Sites",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords Desk recognizes the unique challenges posed by the lingering arcane effects within sacred sites. These areas, often imbued with powerful magical artifacts or ancient rituals, can become reservoirs of residual magic that pose risks to both the integrity of the site and the safety of its visitors. This section addresses the obligations placed upon mages who conduct operations in such locations and provides protocols for ensuring the proper remediation and containment of any lingering arcane effects.\n\nUnder § 500.14, it is imperative that all operations within sacred sites be conducted with due diligence and adherence to strict safety protocols. Mages are required to secure explicit permission from the site's custodian or religious authority before engaging in any arcane activities. Such authorization must be obtained through formal channels and is subject to review by the Aegis Magi, who ensure compliance with § 500.12.\n\nSection § 500.14 mandates that mages report all instances of residual magic within sacred sites immediately upon discovery. Failure to do so may result in penalties under § 800.12 for unlawful casting and confiscation. The mage is responsible for the immediate containment of any such effects, using methods approved by the Aegis Magi or as outlined in previous agreements with the custodian.\n\nThe mage must provide a detailed report under § 900.13, which includes the nature of the residual magic, its source, and an assessment of potential risks to visitors or sacred artifacts. This report is subject to review by the Paradox Trial, which may impose additional sanctions if deemed necessary for public safety.\n\nFor cases where the residual effects are deemed hazardous, the mage must implement remediation protocols approved by the Aegis Magi. These protocols typically involve the use of containment fields and neutralization spells, as described in § 200.12. In severe cases, the Paradox Trial may order the sealing or relocation of affected artifacts under § 400.12.\n\nThe mage is further obligated to maintain a public record of all remediation efforts for at least ten years after the completion of the project, as specified in § 700.13. This ensures transparency and allows for future review if necessary.\n\nIn instances where the residual effects are not fully contained but remain within acceptable levels deemed safe by the Aegis Magi, ongoing monitoring is required. The mage must provide periodic reports under § 900.12 to ensure that no further escalation occurs. If any such escalation is detected, immediate action must be taken.\n\nIt is also important for mages to consider the cultural and spiritual significance of the sacred site when conducting operations. Any actions taken should respect local traditions and customs. Failure to do so may result in public backlash and legal repercussions under § 100.14.\n\nIn summary, the obligations placed upon mages operating within sacred sites are stringent but necessary for ensuring the safety and integrity of these important locations. Mages are encouraged to approach such operations with a deep sense of responsibility, recognizing that any failure to comply could have far-reaching consequences. By adhering to these protocols, mages can contribute positively to the preservation of these invaluable cultural assets.\n\nUnder § 500.14, it is further emphasized that any mage found responsible for causing lingering arcane effects within sacred sites shall be held accountable under § 800.12 and face penalties proportional to the severity of the breach. The Paradox Trial has the authority to impose additional measures as deemed necessary.\n\nThe Aegis Magi are tasked with ensuring compliance with these protocols, conducting regular inspections where required, and providing guidance on best practices for working in such sensitive environments. Their role is critical in maintaining balance between the need for magical innovation and respect for sacred sites.\n\nIn conclusion, mages must approach operations within sacred sites with a commitment to safety and cultural preservation. By adhering to these protocols and protocols under § 500.14, they can contribute to the maintenance of these important locations while upholding the highest standards of conduct expected by the Autumnwood Accords Desk.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "500.14",
            "500.12",
            "800.12",
            "900.13",
            "200.12",
