        {
          "heading": "§ 500.14 Subsection E: Disposal of Resonant Debris and Transient Anomalies in Mu",
          "text": "Under the comprehensive framework established for Remediation and Obligations for Lingering Arcane Effects, this subsection addresses the specific logistical and legal requirements regarding the physical removal and neutralization of resonant debris found within municipal zones. When a magical event, whether sanctioned or incidental, results in the accumulation of unstable particulate matter, crystallized mana residue, or semi-stable dimensional rifts embedded in public infrastructure, the immediate responsibility falls upon the local Wardens to coordinate with the Mages' Guild Accords Desk for retrieval. It shall be unlawful for any private citizen, non-accredited artisan, or unauthorized mage to attempt the dismantling of such debris without a formal extraction permit issued under § 100.15 Protocols for Immediate and Expedited Remediation. Failure to comply with these disposal mandates may result in the automatic application of sanctions described in The Paradox Trial, including the temporary sealing of one's magical aptitude until proper certification is obtained.\n\nThe classification of such debris varies based on its resonance stability and potential for interaction with ambient ley lines; however, all forms must be contained within lead-lined or dampening containers prior to transport. Transport vehicles designated for the removal of arcane materials must adhere strictly to the routing guidelines set forth in § 1301.14 Remediation Protocols for Ancillary Arcane Disruption, ensuring that no transit occurs through densely populated residential sectors or near sensitive natural sites. The Accords Desk maintains a registry of approved disposal facilities where resonant debris is either stabilized for archival study or safely incinerated using high-grade thaumaturgical combustion techniques. Any facility operating outside this registry shall be deemed an unlicensed arcane forge, subject to immediate confiscation of tools and personnel under provisions governing Unlawful Casting found in § 800.15 Remediation Protocols for Ancillary Arcane Disruption.\n\nFurthermore, the obligations extend beyond mere physical removal; they encompass the mitigation of lingering magical echoes that may persist within the air or soil after the primary event has concluded. If a public space exhibits persistent anomalies such as spontaneous weather distortions, localized gravity shifts, or auditory hallucinations induced by residual magic, the site must be quarantined until a certified neutralization team can perform the necessary counter-casting procedures. This quarantine status is not merely administrative but carries legal weight, rendering access to the area restricted for all non-accredited individuals until written clearance is granted by the High Council of Magic Regulation. Any unauthorized entry into a quarantined zone constitutes a breach of the Unsanctioned Arts policy and may trigger an investigation into potential negligence or malice under the liability frameworks outlined in § 500.15 Liability for Ancillary Arcane Events and Casualties.\n\nThe Accords Desk further mandates that all parties involved in the remediation process, including the original caster if identified, must submit a detailed report detailing the nature of the debris, the methods used for its containment, and the specific rituals employed for its disposal. These reports serve as the primary evidence should future incidents arise from improper disposal practices or contamination events linked to the initial cleanup operation. The integrity of these records is paramount, as they form the basis for determining whether an ancillary disruption was successfully resolved or if it evolved into a larger catastrophe requiring intervention from higher authorities such as Archmage Veyra. Consequently, falsification of remediation logs shall be treated with the same severity as unauthorized casting, leading to potential exile or permanent sealing of magical faculties depending on the scope of the deception.\n\nFinally, this subsection reinforces the principle that the safety of the public is paramount in all matters concerning lingering arcane effects. While the Innovators may argue for a more permissive approach to experimental disposal methods, the Conservators hold firm to the belief that precedent must not be sacrificed for innovation without rigorous oversight. Therefore, any new technique proposed for the disposal of resonant debris must undergo a validation process akin to that required for Arcane Artifacts and Relics under § 700.16 Validation Protocols for Arcane Artifacts and Relics before it can be adopted as standard procedure. Only after such rigorous testing and approval by the Council will a method be deemed acceptable for widespread use in municipal zones, ensuring that the delicate balance between magical progress and public safety remains intact within the realm of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "100.15",
            "1301.14",
            "800.15",
