      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and Artifacts",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords Desk recognizes that arcane testimony and artifacts are critical to the adjudication of disputes in which magic has played a role. This section outlines the standards by which such evidence must be evaluated to ensure its admissibility and relevance in court.\n\nUnder § 700.13, Admissibility of Artifact-Derived Testimony and Relics, it is already established that artifacts derived from magical sources are subject to rigorous scrutiny before being admitted into evidence. The same principle applies to arcane testimony provided by witnesses who possess knowledge or experiences directly related to the magic in question. This section seeks to extend these principles further, ensuring a consistent framework for assessing the reliability and relevance of all arcane testimonies.\n\nWhen considering arcane testimony, the court must evaluate several factors under § 700.14. First, the authenticity of the testimony is paramount; it shall be established that the witness possesses direct knowledge or experience relevant to the magic involved in the dispute (see § 600.13). This ensures that only those with credible expertise provide evidence. The court may employ various methods to verify this, including cross-examination and expert witnesses.\n\nSecond, the context of the testimony must be considered. The circumstances under which the testimony was given can significantly influence its reliability. For instance, if a witness claims to have seen a particular magical event but did so in a setting where hallucinations or illusions were plausible, their testimony may be deemed unreliable (see § 700.6 for further considerations on competent witnesses).\n\nThird, the consistency of the testimony is crucial. Repeated statements that vary widely without explanation can cast doubt on the witness's reliability and credibility. The court should seek to reconcile inconsistencies where possible or exclude such testimony if they cannot be reconciled (under § 900.14 for reporting ancillary effects and sub-layered magic).\n\nFourth, the potential for spoliation must also be considered. Memory charms or similar spells that alter a witness's memory could render their testimony unreliable or potentially prejudicial to either party in the dispute (see § 700.3 on memory charm as spoliation). The use of such charms without proper authorization is strictly prohibited.\n\nFinally, the court should consider whether the testimony provides a reasonable explanation for any magical phenomenon at issue. Testimony that lacks coherence or plausibility may not be admitted (see § 800.14 regarding unauthorized research permits and exceeding boundaries).\n\nArtifacts derived from arcane sources are similarly subject to stringent evaluation under § 700.13. The court must determine the authenticity of the artifact, its provenance, and whether it has been tampered with in any way that might affect its integrity or reliability as evidence (see § 1101.12 for collateral contamination and remedial obligations). If an artifact is shown to have been altered or contaminated after its creation, such alteration may render the testimony derived from it unreliable.\n\nIn conclusion, this section emphasizes the need for a comprehensive assessment of arcane testimonies and artifacts before they can be admitted into evidence. The court must consider authenticity, context, consistency, potential spoliation, and the plausibility of the explanations provided by both witnesses and artifacts. Failure to adhere to these standards could lead to misinterpretation or misuse of magical evidence, potentially skewing the outcome of the dispute (see § 700.12 for additional provisions regarding arcane litigation and evidence).",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "700.13",
