        {
          "heading": "Admissibility Standards for Arcane Artifacts as Evidence",
          "text": "Under the Autumnwood Accords, the admissibility of arcane artifacts in legal proceedings is governed by strict standards designed to ensure that all evidence presented before the Mages' Guild is both reliable and relevant. This section outlines the criteria that must be met for an arcane artifact to be admitted into a case involving alleged breaches of magical law or disputes over the use of magic.\n\nThe foundational principle underlying the admissibility of such artifacts lies in their authenticity, integrity, and relevance (see § 700.13). An arcane artifact's authenticity is paramount; it must be demonstrably related to the events or allegations at issue. The integrity of an artifact refers to its physical condition and whether it has been tampered with or altered after its creation or last known use (see § 1301.12 for remedial obligations). Relevance, however, extends beyond mere connection to the case; it must also be capable of aiding in the resolution of a specific legal inquiry.\n\nOne critical aspect is the corroboration requirement (see § 700.12). Artifacts are generally only admissible if they can be corroborated by other forms of evidence or testimony from qualified individuals, such as mages who have firsthand knowledge of their creation or use. This ensures that the artifact's claims are not isolated and unsupported, thereby enhancing its credibility in court.\n\nAnother significant factor is the age of the artifact. Older artifacts may carry more historical weight but are also subject to greater deterioration and potential tampering (see § 500.13 for mitigation protocols). The age of an artifact must be considered alongside its condition and relevance when determining admissibility. Artifacts that have been in use or custody for extended periods may require additional verification to establish their reliability.\n\nThe provenance of the artifact is also a crucial consideration (see § 400.13 for oversight protocols). The history and origins of an arcane artifact can provide context regarding its creation, purpose, and potential relevance to the case. Artifacts with unclear or disputed provenances may face additional scrutiny before being admitted into evidence.\n\nFurthermore, the context in which an artifact is presented must be carefully considered (see § 700.12 for protocols). The circumstances under which an artifact was discovered, its location at the time of discovery, and any associated testimonies should be thoroughly examined to ensure that the artifact's presentation does not introduce bias or mislead the court. This includes examining whether the artifact’s discovery was made in a legally permissible manner (see § 800.12 for provisions on unlawful casting).\n\nFinally, the admissibility of an arcane artifact is subject to ongoing review throughout the legal process. Any new evidence, such as further corroboration or new discoveries regarding the artifact's provenance, can prompt a reassessment of its admissibility (see § 300.14 for protocols on variance and deviation).\n\nIn conclusion, the admissibility of arcane artifacts in legal proceedings is a complex and multifaceted process that balances the need for reliable evidence with the requirement to ensure fairness and integrity in the judicial system. By adhering to these standards, the Mages' Guild can maintain its reputation as a bastion of justice while upholding the principles enshrined in the Autumnwood Accords (see § 1.4 for persons bound).",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "700.13",
