    {
      "cite": "800.14",
      "part": "part_viii",
      "title": "Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords Desk, in exercising its jurisdiction over the lawful use of magic as set forth by the governing body of the Mages' Guild, hereby establishes additional provisions concerning unlawful casting and confiscation. These measures are aimed at ensuring that all mages adhere to the strict guidelines laid out within the Accords and to prevent abuses of magical power.\n\nUnder § 800.14, it is unequivocally stated that any mage caught engaging in the unlawful casting or use of arcane artifacts shall be subject to immediate confiscation of their magical implements and a thorough investigation by the Aegis Magi. This includes the seizure of all materials involved in the prohibited spellcasting, which may range from ancient tomes to enchanted weapons. The confiscated items shall remain under the custody of the Guild until such time as the Paradox Trial has reached its conclusion.\n\nIf the accused mage is found guilty during the trial, the confiscation may be upheld and the magical artifacts deemed forfeit. In cases where the evidence is insufficient or inconclusive, the items will be returned to their owner pending further investigation. It is incumbent upon all mages to ensure that their experiments and practices are in strict accordance with Guild protocols; deviation from these guidelines constitutes a clear breach of the Accords.\n\nThe Aegis Magi possess the authority to intervene in situations where there is a high risk of immediate danger arising from magical activity, including the casting of spells that violate the dominant faction's policies. In such instances, they may act without prior notice and confiscate any items or materials deemed necessary for public safety. This action does not preclude subsequent trials but serves to mitigate potential harm.\n\nFurthermore, § 800.14 mandates that all confiscated magical artifacts undergo a comprehensive evaluation by the Guild’s experts. The purpose of this examination is twofold: first, to determine whether the item has been used in accordance with the Accords; second, to ascertain any lingering arcane effects or residues which could pose risks both to its owner and the wider community. Should any hazardous elements be detected, appropriate measures must be taken to neutralize them.\n\nIn instances where a mage is found innocent of unlawful casting but still possesses items that are subject to suspicion, these too may undergo scrutiny. Any findings concerning such objects should be reported back to the Paradox Trial for consideration during sentencing. This ensures fairness and consistency in applying Guild laws without compromising individual rights or liberties.\n\nThe Autumnwood Accords Desk emphasizes that while confiscation is a serious matter, it serves as an essential tool for maintaining order within the magical community. It is hoped that by providing clear guidelines regarding unlawful casting and confiscation, this section will contribute to greater understanding among all members of the Guild about their responsibilities under the Accords.\n\nFor those who find themselves in violation of these provisions, it is crucial to note that they have recourse through formal channels such as appeals or petitions for reconsideration. However, any repeat offenses would result in more stringent penalties, reflecting the seriousness with which violations are treated by the governing body.\n\nUnder § 1.6 Definitions, see also § 200.14 Additional Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation; § 800.7 Related Requirements; § 1301.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "800.14",
            "1.6",
            "200.14",
            "800.7",
            "1301.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation: Sealing Protocols for Ro",
          "text": "Under § 800.14, the provisions governing unlawful casting and confiscation include protocols not only for the immediate handling of magical artifacts and residues but also for the subsequent sealing of rogue mages. This section addresses the process by which a mage found in violation of the Accords is to be dealt with, focusing on the principles of justice, rehabilitation, and containment.\n\nIn cases where a mage has been found guilty of unlawful casting or other breaches of the Accords, the first step is to ensure that all traces of illegal magic are confiscated. This confiscation process must follow strict protocols to prevent any residual effects from lingering in the environment or being repurposed by unauthorized means (see § 800.13). The confiscated items should be cataloged and stored securely, pending further review (see § 700.12).\n\nThe sealing of a rogue mage is a solemn process conducted by a council composed of peers from both the Conservators and Innovators factions, under the mediation of Archmage Veyra (see § 100.13). The purpose of this council is to ensure that the process is fair and just, reflecting the diverse perspectives within the Guild. The sealing process involves not only the containment of the mage’s abilities but also a review of their actions to determine the appropriate form of punishment.\n\nDuring the sealing ceremony, the mage must be given an opportunity to confess fully and truthfully any breaches of the Accords (see § 100.13). This confession is crucial for the integrity of the process. Once the council has determined the nature and extent of the violations, they will decide on a sentence that reflects both justice and rehabilitation. The mage may be sentenced to a period of enforced study under supervision or to complete a specific task beneficial to the community (see § 400.13). In severe cases, the mage’s magic may be partially or entirely sealed away, as a form of punishment and containment.\n\nThe sealing process is not merely punitive; it also serves to rehabilitate the rogue mage. The council ensures that each mage receives the support necessary for their reintegration into Guild life. This includes access to counseling and mentorship programs provided by the Guild (see § 100.13). The goal is to prevent further breaches of the Accords while fostering a mage who can contribute positively to the community.\n\nIf the rogue mage shows genuine remorse and is willing to undergo rehabilitation, there are provisions for early release from their sealing period. However, this decision rests with the council and must be based on clear evidence of the mage’s efforts at reform (see § 100.13). The process ensures that justice is served but also allows for a path toward redemption.\n\nIn cases where the rogue mage refuses to comply or continues to pose a threat, the sealing may become permanent, with the mage confined to a pocket dimension until they show signs of rehabilitation or their magic decays sufficiently (see § 1301.12). This extreme measure is reserved for those who have demonstrated a persistent disregard for the Accords.\n\nThe sealing protocols are designed to be flexible and adaptable, allowing for individualized treatment based on the circumstances of each case. The council’s discretion ensures that the process remains just and fair, reflecting the complex nature of the Guild's internal dynamics (see § 100.13). By focusing on rehabilitation rather than mere punishment, the sealing protocols aim to maintain the integrity of the Guild while fostering a sense of community and responsibility among its members.\n\nIn conclusion, the provisions governing unlawful casting and confiscation are designed to ensure that justice is served in accordance with the principles of fairness and rehabilitation. The sealing process reflects the Guild’s commitment to both punish breaches of the Accords and provide pathways for reform. This approach not only protects the integrity of magic within the Guild but also fosters an environment where mages can learn and grow, contributing positively to the community as a whole.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "800.14",
            "800.13",
            "700.12",
            "100.13",
            "400.13",
            "1301.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation: Emergency Containment Pr",
          "text": "In the realm of magic, the ability to cast spells is not only a gift but also a responsibility. The Mages' Guild Accords Desk is entrusted with ensuring that this power is used responsibly and ethically. However, in times of crisis or during moments of chaos, the line between lawful and unlawful casting can blur. The Accords Desk must be prepared for such emergencies, thereby implementing a set of protocols designed to contain rogue magic swiftly and effectively.\n\nEmergency Containment Protocols are designed to address instances where magic is cast without authorization, causing harm to individuals or property, or threatening public safety. These protocols are not merely punitive but also aimed at mitigating the immediate threat posed by the caster. The first step in these protocols involves rapid identification of the rogue caster and the nature of their casting. Once identified, the Accords Desk must ascertain whether the magic in question is inherently dangerous or if it poses an imminent threat to life or property.\n\nThe containment process begins with a preliminary assessment by Aegis Magi, who are tasked with evaluating the situation on-site. Their role includes determining the extent of the spell’s influence and its potential for escalation. If necessary, they may employ counter-casting techniques to neutralize the rogue magic. Such counter-magic is strictly regulated under § 800.12 of this Codex, ensuring that it does not cause further harm or disruption.\n\nUnder these protocols, the Accords Desk has the authority to confiscate any items or materials used in the casting, as per § 800.13. The confiscated items are held in a secure location pending an investigation and potential trial under the Paradox Trial provisions outlined in § 600.12. This ensures that the rogue caster cannot use these tools again until their legal status is clarified.\n\nIn cases where immediate action is required, such as during a breach of containment or when dealing with a caster whose magic has already caused significant damage, the Accords Desk may order the sealing of the caster's magical abilities. This measure is governed by § 800.14 and is subject to review under § 600.12 for its appropriateness and necessity. Sealing involves the complete cessation of a mage’s ability to cast magic, preventing them from accessing their power until they have been cleared through the Paradox Trial process.\n\nThe Accords Desk must also consider the psychological impact on the rogue caster during this containment period. § 700.12 mandates that all mages, including those under seal, receive counseling and support services to help them reintegrate into society after their casting abilities are restored. This is crucial for maintaining the mental health of mages who may find themselves in such a predicament.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords Desk must ensure that any spells cast during these emergency containment protocols do not inadvertently create further problems. § 500.13 stipulates that all remediation efforts are to be conducted with due regard for minimizing environmental contamination and avoiding lingering arcane echoes. This ensures that the containment process itself does not become a source of ongoing issues.\n\nIn conclusion, Emergency Containment Protocols serve as a vital safeguard within the Mages' Guild Accords Desk. They provide a structured response to rogue magic, ensuring both immediate safety and long-term resolution. The protocols balance the need for swift action with the requirement for due process, reflecting the intricate balance required in managing magical power. Through these measures, the Accords Desk upholds the integrity of magic within the realm while protecting its practitioners from undue harm.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "800.12",
            "800.13",
            "600.12",
            "800.14",
            "700.12",
            "500.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation: Administrative Review an",
          "text": "Under § 800.14, the Mages' Guild Accords Desk is vested with authority to govern the unlawful casting of magic and the confiscation thereof. This section addresses the administrative review and appeal process for mages who have been found in violation of the Autumnwood Accords. The process ensures that all cases are handled fairly, transparently, and in accordance with established protocols.\n\nAdministrative review serves a dual purpose: it allows for the reevaluation of any decision made by the Paradox Trial or Aegis Magi, and it provides mages with an opportunity to contest their punishment through formal appeal. The process is designed to be both efficient and fair, ensuring that justice is served while maintaining the Guild's authority.\n\nThe first step in this process is the filing of a formal appeal. Any mage who believes they have been wrongly cast or whose punishment has been unfairly administered may file an appeal with the Accords Desk within thirty days of receiving notice of their decision. The appeal must be accompanied by a detailed statement explaining the grounds for the appeal, including any new evidence that was not previously available or presented during the original trial.\n\nUpon receipt of the appeal, the Accords Desk shall review the case under § 100.13 Protocols for Mediation and Inter-Factional Arbitration to ensure that all parties involved are treated equitably. The desk will then convene a panel composed of three impartial members: one from each of the Conservators, Innovators, and Aegis factions. This diverse panel is tasked with reviewing the evidence presented during both the original trial and the appeal, ensuring that no bias or prejudice influences their decision.\n\nThe panel’s primary role is to determine whether there has been a violation of the Autumnwood Accords that justifies the initial punishment. They shall consider all relevant factors, including but not limited to:\n\n- The nature and severity of the unlawful casting,\n- Any mitigating circumstances presented by the accused,\n- Whether the accused has demonstrated remorse or taken steps to rectify their actions,\n- The potential for rehabilitation,\n- The precedent set by similar cases.\n\nIf the panel finds that the original decision was unjust, they may recommend either a reduction in punishment or a complete reversal of the decision. In such instances, the Accords Desk will issue an order detailing the new terms and conditions under which the accused mage must operate. These terms are designed to ensure public safety while also allowing for the rehabilitation of the accused.\n\nIn cases where the panel finds that the initial decision was just but the punishment disproportionately severe, they may recommend a reduction in severity. The desk then has discretion to implement these recommendations, subject to final approval by the Archmage Veyra or her designate.\n\nFor those mages who are found innocent during this review process, the Accords Desk will issue an order for immediate release and full restoration of their magical abilities under § 1401.12 Preservation of Canonical Continuity and Scriptural Integrity. The desk shall also issue a formal apology and may impose additional measures to prevent future incidents.\n\nIn instances where the accused mage is found guilty but not released, they may appeal this decision once more within fifteen days. This second appeal must be reviewed by a higher panel composed of five members: one from each faction plus two independent arbitrators chosen by mutual agreement between the factions. The desk will issue its final ruling based on this review.\n\nThe Accords Desk is committed to ensuring that all mages are treated fairly and equally under the law. It is important to note, however, that administrative review and appeal do not provide a mechanism for revision of penalties already served. Mages who have been incarcerated or otherwise penalized may only seek to reduce their sentence through this process.\n\nFinally, it should be noted that all decisions made during these reviews are subject to the overarching principles laid out in § 1.5 Relation to Other Law and § 900.13 Reporting of Ancillary and Sub-Layered Arcane Effects, ensuring consistency with broader Guild policy and any relevant legal frameworks.\n\nIn conclusion, administrative review and appeal provide a critical mechanism for ensuring that mages are treated fairly when accused of unlawful casting or faced with confiscation. By providing an avenue for contesting decisions, the Accords Desk upholds both justice and trust within the Mages' Guild community.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "800.14",
            "100.13",
            "1401.12",
            "1.5",
            "900.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation: Restitution and Rehabili",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords, under the authority of Archmage Veyra, recognize that the unlawful casting of magic represents a breach not only of law but also of trust within the guild. The primary objective in addressing such transgressions is to ensure a balanced approach between punitive measures and rehabilitative efforts. This section delineates the protocols for restitution and rehabilitation aimed at restoring both the integrity of the guild and the mage’s standing within it.\n\n### Restitution and Rehabilitation Framework\n\nThe unlawful casting of magic, whether through intentional malfeasance or through negligence, necessitates a structured response that not only deters future misconduct but also seeks to rehabilitate those who have strayed from the path of lawfulness. The first step in this process is the identification of the nature and extent of the offense, which is conducted by an investigative committee comprising members of both the Conservators and Innovators factions. This committee ensures that any determination of guilt or innocence is made with due consideration for all perspectives within the guild.\n\nUpon the conclusion of the investigation, the mage may be required to undergo a period of probation, during which time they are subject to rigorous supervision by the Aegis Magi. The duration of this probationary period is determined based on the severity of the offense and the mage’s previous history with the guild. Probation is not merely punitive; it serves as an opportunity for the individual to demonstrate their commitment to adhering to the Accords.\n\n### Restitution Obligations\n\nIn addition to probation, restitution obligations may be imposed upon a mage found guilty of unlawful casting. These obligations are designed to redress any harm caused by the magical activity and to prevent future occurrences. Common forms of restitution include:\n\n- **Restoration of Affected Areas:** In cases where the illegal casting has caused damage to public or private property, mages are required to use their skills to restore the affected areas to their original state. This may involve repairing structures, purifying contaminated zones, or addressing environmental concerns.\n\n- **Community Service:** Mages may be assigned community service tasks that contribute positively to the guild and its environment. These tasks can range from teaching apprentices in a specialized field of magic to participating in conservation efforts aimed at preserving magical ecosystems.\n\n- **Financial Compensation:** Where harm has been inflicted upon individuals or entities, financial compensation is sought through restitution payments. The amount of these payments is determined by the extent of the damage caused and the resources available for compensation within the guild’s budget.\n\n### Rehabilitation Programs\n\nThe primary goal of rehabilitation programs is to provide mages with a structured environment that encourages personal growth and reintegration into the guild community. These programs are tailored to the individual needs of each mage, ensuring that they receive support in addressing any underlying issues that may have contributed to their misconduct. Key components of these programs include:\n\n- **Mentorship:** Each mage undergoing rehabilitation is paired with a mentor from within the guild who provides guidance and support throughout the process. The mentor helps the mage navigate challenges and encourages them to pursue personal development.\n\n- **Skill Refinement:** Rehabilitation often includes sessions aimed at refining the mage’s magical abilities, focusing on safer practices and more ethical applications of their powers. These sessions are conducted under the supervision of experienced guild members who ensure that the mage masters both the technical aspects and the moral implications of their craft.\n\n- **Community Engagement:** Engaging with the community is a critical part of rehabilitation. Mages are encouraged to participate in guild events, workshops, and educational programs aimed at fostering mutual respect and understanding within the guild’s diverse factions.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe provisions governing restitution and rehabilitation underscore the Autumnwood Accords’ commitment to justice and the protection of the guild's integrity. By balancing punitive measures with rehabilitative efforts, the guild aims to ensure that those who have violated its laws can be reintegrated into society in a constructive manner. This approach not only addresses the immediate consequences of unlawful casting but also seeks to prevent future transgressions by fostering a culture of responsibility and ethical practice within the guild.\n\nUnder § 800.14, these provisions are integral to maintaining the balance between enforcement and compassion necessary for the continued stability and prosperity of the Mages' Guild. See § 1.6 for definitions related to restitution and rehabilitation, and § 200.13 for protocols concerning containment and breach mitigation.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "800.14",
            "1.6",
            "200.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Sealing Protocols for Autonomous Constructs and Their Containment",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords, in their ongoing effort to govern the use of magic, have established detailed protocols concerning the confiscation and containment of magical constructs that have been used unlawfully or in a manner inconsistent with the Accords. This section delineates specific measures to be taken when autonomous constructs are involved in unlawful casting, ensuring both the safety of individuals and the integrity of the Accords.\n\nAutonomous constructs, by their very nature, can pose significant risks if not properly managed or contained following an incident of unlawful magic use. The Accords mandate that all such constructs must undergo a thorough inspection and sealing process to prevent further unauthorized actions. This protocol is designed to ensure that any construct found in violation of the Accords is rendered harmless until it can be evaluated and, where necessary, rehabilitated.\n\nUnder § 800.14, when an autonomous construct is involved in unlawful casting or other breaches, the following steps must be taken:\n\n1. **Initial Assessment**: Upon discovery of illegal use involving a construct, immediate action shall be initiated by Aegis Magi to assess the situation. The initial assessment includes confirming the identity and purpose of the construct, determining whether it has been used unlawfully, and evaluating any potential harm caused or risk posed.\n\n2. **Containment Orders**: Once unlawful casting is confirmed, containment orders must be issued promptly. These orders will instruct the responsible party to cease all operations involving the construct immediately. Should the responsible party fail to comply with these orders, the Aegis Magi have authority to enforce immediate containment through the use of appropriate binding spells or equipment.\n\n3. **Sealing Protocol**: The sealing protocol is the primary means by which autonomous constructs are rendered harmless. This involves the application of a seal that not only incapacitates the construct but also prevents any further unauthorized actions until it can be reviewed and potentially rehabilitated. Sealing protocols must adhere to the guidelines set forth in § 1301.12, ensuring that all necessary remedial obligations are fulfilled.\n\n4. **Administrative Review**: After containment and sealing have been completed, an administrative review shall take place to determine the appropriate course of action for the construct. This includes evaluating the nature of the unlawful casting, assessing any damage caused or potential risks posed, and deciding whether further actions such as rehabilitation or scrapping are necessary.\n\n5. **Restitution and Rehabilitation**: If it is determined that the construct can be rehabilitated, steps must be taken to ensure its lawful use in accordance with the Accords. This may involve reprogramming or recalibration of the construct’s algorithms under § 400.13 to align with accepted magical practices.\n\n6. **Monitoring and Compliance**: Throughout the sealing process and subsequent rehabilitation, continuous monitoring is required to ensure compliance with all stipulated conditions. Failure to comply may result in further containment measures as outlined in § 800.12.\n\n7. **Public Safety Measures**: In cases where there is a risk of immediate danger or harm, public safety measures must be implemented. This includes evacuation orders and other precautionary steps as necessary under § 700.13.\n\nThe sealing protocols for autonomous constructs are designed to balance the need for containment with the potential for rehabilitation, ensuring that all actions taken are both effective and just. The Accords recognize the importance of preventing future breaches while also providing mechanisms for reintegration where appropriate. Through these provisions, the Mages' Guild aims to uphold its mandate of safeguarding the magical community from unlawful practices while fostering responsible innovation.\n\nUnder § 1301.12, liability for any damages caused by autonomous constructs during their unlawful use is determined through a process that ensures restitution and rehabilitation where possible. This includes financial compensation for any harm done, as well as efforts to repair or replace damaged property under § 500.14.\n\nIn summary, the sealing protocols for autonomous constructs are critical components of the Accords’ strategy to manage unauthorized magic use. By adhering to these protocols, the Mages' Guild can effectively contain risks and ensure that all constructs involved in unlawful casting are brought back into compliance with the Accords. This approach not only protects individuals but also upholds the integrity of magical practice within the community.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "800.14",
            "1301.12",
            "400.13",
            "800.12",
            "700.13",
            "500.14"
          ]
        }
