    {
      "cite": "1101.14",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations",
          "text": "Under § 1101.12, collateral contamination refers to the unintended consequences resulting from the proximity or interaction between magical artifacts, constructs, or spells that are under the control of one party with those controlled by another. This section aims to clarify the obligations of parties in mitigating such contamination and addressing any resultant damage.\n\nThe Mages' Guild recognizes that the use of magic is inherently unpredictable and can lead to unforeseen side effects. Therefore, it is incumbent upon all mages bound by the Autumnwood Accords to ensure that their magical activities do not compromise the integrity or functionality of artifacts belonging to others.\n\nMagical contamination may manifest in various forms, including but not limited to residue from planar transit, lingering arcane echoes, and collateral taints. The nature and extent of such contamination are subject to evaluation by a competent mage, who will determine whether remedial action is necessary.\n\nWhen contamination occurs, the party responsible for the magical activity must take immediate steps to mitigate any adverse effects. This may include but is not limited to neutralizing the source of contamination, repairing or replacing damaged artifacts, and restoring any disrupted planar conditions. The costs associated with such remediation are generally borne by the party responsible.\n\nUnder § 100.14 and § 200.14, jurisdictional clarity must be established before any remedial measures can commence. This includes identifying the parties involved, determining their respective responsibilities, and ensuring that all necessary protocols for containment and breach mitigation are followed.\n\nIn instances where contamination is caused by a rogue or unauthorized spell, the Paradox Trial may be invoked to determine culpability and impose appropriate sanctions. See § 700.12 for further details on arcane litigation and evidence.\n\nIt is important to note that the burden of proof regarding collateral contamination lies with the party alleging such contamination. This includes providing evidence of proximity or interaction between their artifact and another, as well as demonstrating causation. Under § 900.14, reporting ancillary effects and sub-layered magic is mandatory, ensuring transparency in all magical transactions.\n\nThe Mages' Guild encourages proactive measures to prevent collateral contamination through the use of containment protocols and careful planning. See § 200.13 for additional protocols for arcane containment and breach mitigation. In cases where contamination has occurred despite such precautions, the party responsible must take immediate action to mitigate any damage caused.\n\nFinally, the Mages' Guild recognizes that in some instances, collateral contamination may be unavoidable due to unforeseen circumstances or the inherent nature of certain spells. In such cases, parties involved are encouraged to engage in mediation and negotiation to reach a mutually acceptable resolution. See § 100.13 for protocols for mediation and inter-factional arbitration.\n\nIn conclusion, while the Mages' Guild seeks to promote responsible magical practices, it acknowledges that accidents can happen. Parties must act in good faith and take reasonable steps to mitigate any contamination or damage caused by their magical activities. Failure to do so may result in sanctions under the Paradox Trial, as outlined in § 700.12.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.12",
            "100.14",
            "200.14",
            "700.12",
            "900.14",
            "200.13",
            "100.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Unintended Planar Shifts",
          "text": "In the complex realm of magical practice, the accidental creation or amplification of unintended planar shifts poses significant risks to both the environment and those who dwell within it. While the Autumnwood Accords aim to minimize such occurrences through strict protocols and containment measures, unforeseen circumstances may still lead to collateral contamination and resultant remedial obligations.\n\nUnder § 1401.13, the preservation of canonical continuity and scriptural integrity is paramount. However, in instances where an unintended planar shift occurs, it is incumbent upon the mage responsible to take immediate action to mitigate any potential harm. The mage must first ascertain the nature and extent of the contamination through a thorough survey of the affected area (see § 100.13 for protocols on mediation). If the mage fails to act promptly or adequately, they are subject to additional penalties under § 1401.12.\n\nOnce the scope of the planar shift is determined, the mage shall implement remedial measures as necessary. These may include, but are not limited to, neutralizing any foreign elements that have been introduced into the local plane (see § 800.13 for provisions governing unlawful casting and confiscation). The mage must also ensure that no lingering arcane echoes remain, which could disrupt the natural equilibrium of the area (see § 500.14 on remediation and obligations).\n\nIn cases where a planar shift has caused significant collateral contamination, the mage is obligated to notify the nearest Aegis Magi and seek their assistance in containing and neutralizing any threats (see § 200.13 for protocols on arcane containment and breach mitigation). The mage must also provide a detailed report of the incident to the Autumnwood Accords Desk within forty-eight hours, outlining all actions taken and measures implemented. Failure to comply with this reporting requirement shall result in enhanced penalties under § 700.12.\n\nFurthermore, if the planar shift has caused damage to local structures or ecosystems, the responsible mage is liable for any resulting costs of repair or restoration (see § 1301.12 on penalty assessment and enforcement). This liability extends not only to direct physical damages but also to indirect effects that may arise from the contamination.\n\nIt is important to note that the responsibility for unintended planar shifts does not lie solely with the mage who caused them. Any individual or entity that discovers an ongoing or potential planar shift must report it immediately, regardless of their relation to the original event (see § 100.13 on protocols for mediation and inter-factional arbitration). Failure to do so could result in shared liability under the principles of joint tortfeasors.\n\nIn instances where a mage is found culpable of multiple unintended planar shifts or significant collateral contamination, the Autumnwood Accords Desk may impose stricter measures beyond those outlined in § 1301.12. Such actions are intended to ensure that mages recognize and take responsibility for their magical practices, thereby preventing future occurrences.\n\nIn conclusion, while the Autumnwood Accords strive to minimize the risks associated with planar shifts through strict protocols and containment measures, it is the responsibility of every mage to act promptly and responsibly in the event of an unintended shift. By adhering to these guidelines, mages not only uphold the integrity of canonical continuity but also contribute to the preservation of balance within the magical realm (see § 1401.13 for further details on this principle).",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1401.13",
            "100.13",
            "1401.12",
            "800.13",
            "500.14",
            "200.13",
            "700.12",
            "1301.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Unplanned Planar Resonance",
          "text": "In the administration of the Accords, the Mages' Guild is tasked not merely with the regulation and enforcement of arcane practices but also with safeguarding the very fabric of reality itself. The accidental or unauthorized use of magic that results in unintended planar resonance can have far-reaching consequences, affecting not only the immediate environment but potentially entire planes of existence. This section outlines the liability for such events, ensuring that mages are held accountable for their actions and that the Guild has mechanisms to mitigate these risks.\n\nUnder § 1101.14, Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations, Liability for Unintended Planar Shifts, mages who inadvertently cause a shift in planes of existence through their magic may be subject to severe penalties. The Accords recognize that while it is impossible to prevent every mishap, mages are responsible for the outcomes of their actions. Therefore, any mage who causes an unintended planar resonance must bear responsibility for the collateral consequences.\n\nThe liability for such events extends beyond mere financial compensation or sanctions. It includes a comprehensive approach aimed at mitigating the risks and ensuring that future incidents can be prevented. This involves a thorough investigation into the circumstances surrounding the event to determine whether it was due to negligence, ignorance, or deliberate misconduct. If negligence is found, the mage may face additional penalties, including a period of mandatory training under the supervision of experienced Aegis Magi.\n\nFurthermore, in cases where unintended planar shifts occur, immediate action must be taken by Guild personnel. The primary objective is to ensure that no harm comes to innocent individuals or structures beyond the immediate vicinity. This includes sealing off the affected area and preventing any further unauthorized access until the situation has been stabilized. The Guild's response team is responsible for ensuring that all necessary precautions are taken to prevent escalation.\n\nThe Accords also mandate that mages involved in such incidents provide a detailed report of their actions leading up to the event. This report must include an explanation of what went wrong, any steps taken to rectify the situation, and measures implemented to avoid similar occurrences in the future. Failure to comply with this requirement could result in additional sanctions, including suspension from practice.\n\nIn instances where the unintended planar resonance results in significant collateral damage or risk, mages may be required to contribute to a fund dedicated to restoring balance within affected areas. This fund is managed by the Guild and is used to support efforts aimed at stabilizing planes of existence, repairing breaches, and ensuring that such incidents do not recur.\n\nThe Accords emphasize that while every mage is expected to take reasonable precautions against such risks, it is also understood that accidents can happen. However, the responsibility lies with the individual to ensure that such events are handled appropriately and that lessons are learned from them. The Guild's role in this context is to provide a framework for accountability, ensuring that mages act responsibly and contribute positively to the overall balance of magical practice.\n\nIn summary, liability for unintended planar resonances underscores the importance of responsible magic use within the Accords. By holding mages accountable for their actions and implementing measures aimed at mitigating risks, the Guild ensures that the integrity of reality is maintained. This approach not only protects the mage community but also upholds the broader interests of all planes of existence.\n\nSee § 100.13 Protocols for Mediation and Inter-Factional Arbitration; under § 700.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence; see § 1.6 Definitions; § 400.14 Unauthorized Research Permits and Exceeding Boundaries; see § 1301.13 Liability for Residue and Lingering Arcane Echoes;",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1101.14",
            "100.13",
            "700.12",
            "1.6",
            "400.14",
            "1301.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Unplanned Planar Resonance — Continued",
          "text": "Under the auspices of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, liability for unplanned planar resonance is a critical concern in the administration and enforcement of the Mages' Guild Accords. Planar resonance occurs when an arcane casting inadvertently triggers a disturbance in the fabric of reality, leading to unintended alterations or disturbances across planes. Such occurrences are not only perilous but also pose significant challenges in terms of liability and remediation.\n\nThe primary responsibility for preventing unplanned planar resonances lies with the caster who initiates the spell or magical act. However, as is often the case within the Mages' Guild, jurisdictional clarity can be ambiguous. The Accords Desk seeks to provide a comprehensive framework that delineates responsibilities and mitigates potential conflicts.\n\n### Liability for Unplanned Planar Resonance\n\nSection 1101.14.1 of the Codex sets forth the general principles governing liability for unplanned planar resonances. It establishes that, in cases where an uncontrolled planar resonance occurs, the caster is primarily liable unless they can demonstrate due diligence and adherence to standard protocols. This includes compliance with all applicable Accords, the use of appropriate containment measures, and the avoidance of casting in areas known to be susceptible to such disturbances.\n\n### Due Diligence and Standard Protocols\n\nThe Accords Desk recognizes that accidental planar resonances can occur despite best efforts. To this end, § 1101.14.2 mandates strict adherence to standard protocols designed to minimize the risk of unplanned resonance. These protocols include:\n\n- Conducting a thorough survey of the intended casting area before any spell is cast.\n- Ensuring that all involved parties are aware and prepared for potential resonant effects.\n- Utilizing containment measures such as planar barriers or stabilizers when necessary.\n\n### Remedial Obligations\n\nUpon identifying an unplanned planar resonance, the caster must immediately report the incident to the nearest Aegis Magus. The responsible party is also obligated to take immediate remedial actions aimed at restoring stability and mitigating any adverse effects. This includes:\n\n- Conducting a comprehensive survey of the affected area.\n- Implementing containment protocols to prevent further spread or escalation of the resonance.\n- Collaborating with the Accords Desk and other relevant authorities in assessing damages and planning long-term mitigation strategies.\n\n### Cross-Responsibility\n\nIn instances where multiple parties are involved, § 1101.14.3 outlines a framework for determining liability based on cross-responsibilities. This includes scenarios where:\n\n- The resonance was caused by the negligence of an allied party.\n- Multiple casters were present during the casting and subsequent incident.\n\nThe Accords Desk emphasizes that cooperation between involved parties is paramount in such cases, ensuring a collaborative approach to resolving issues effectively.\n\n### Consequences for Non-Compliance\n\nFailure to adhere to these protocols can result in severe consequences. § 1101.14.4 mandates strict adherence to the outlined responsibilities and sets out penalties for non-compliance. These penalties include:\n\n- Suspension of casting privileges.\n- Mandatory participation in remediation efforts.\n- Financial reparations or community service.\n\n### Interpretative Nuance\n\nInterpretation of liability and remedial obligations is not without nuance. § 1.13 provides guidelines on interpretative consistency, emphasizing that ambiguities shall be resolved in favor of the caster's safety and the stability of the planar fabric. This ensures a balanced approach to accountability and responsibility.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nIn summary, liability for unplanned planar resonances is a complex yet necessary aspect of the Mages' Guild Accords. By establishing clear protocols and responsibilities, the Codex aims to mitigate risks associated with such occurrences while ensuring that appropriate measures are taken in response. This not only upholds the integrity of magical practice but also safeguards the broader community from potential hazards.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1101.14.2",
            "1101.14.3",
            "1101.14.4",
            "1.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Unplanned Planar Displacement",
          "text": "In the complex tapestry of magical activity within the Autumnwood Accords, the concept of \"unplanned planar displacement\" poses significant challenges to the mages and scholars who navigate the intricate dimensions that are integral to their craft. The unintended shifting between planes can lead to unforeseen consequences, ranging from localized distortions in the fabric of reality to catastrophic disruptions that threaten both the stability of the planes themselves and the safety of those within them. This section addresses the liability for such displacements and the obligations mages have towards mitigating any resulting harm.\n\n### The Nature of Unplanned Planar Displacement\n\nUnplanned planar displacement occurs when a mage's casting or experimentation inadvertently opens a rift between different dimensions, disrupting the natural balance that maintains the integrity of those planes. This phenomenon is often the result of errors in incantation, misaligned focus, or poorly controlled magical energies. While it may seem trivial to some, such displacements can have profound and lasting effects on the affected areas.\n\nUnder § 1301.12, Liability for Residue and Lingering Arcane Echoes, mages are held accountable for any residual effects of their magic that linger beyond the immediate casting. This includes both physical and planar disruptions caused by their actions. In the case of unplanned displacements, the mage must demonstrate due diligence in preventing such occurrences. Failure to do so can result in severe penalties under § 1401.12 Preservation of Canonical Continuity and Scriptural Integrity.\n\n### Obligations and Remediation\n\nThe mage has a primary obligation to ensure that any unintended planar displacement is promptly addressed. This involves immediate containment efforts, which may include the use of specialized barriers or counter-casting techniques detailed in § 200.13 Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation. Once contained, the mage must then work towards restoring the affected area to its original state as far as possible.\n\n### Determining Liability\n\nDetermining liability for unplanned planar displacements requires a careful examination of the circumstances surrounding the incident. § 100.14 Additional Provisions for Office Boundaries and Jurisdictional Clarity specifies that the Autumnwood Accords Desk shall have exclusive jurisdiction over such matters. The desk's role is to mediate between mages, ensuring that each party’s responsibility is clearly delineated.\n\nThe mage is presumed responsible until proven otherwise. § 1401.13 Preservation of Canonical Continuity and Scriptural Integrity mandates that all actions taken by a mage must be consistent with the established rules and guidelines set forth in the Accords. Any deviation, even unintentional, can have far-reaching implications on both the mage’s reputation and the integrity of the planes.\n\n### Remedial Obligations\n\nIn cases where an unplanned displacement has caused significant damage or disruption, mages are obligated to provide remediation services. These services may include extensive magical interventions aimed at restoring balance to the affected area. § 1101.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations underscores that the mage is responsible for any collateral contamination resulting from their actions. This includes not only the immediate effects of the displacement but also any secondary consequences, such as the introduction of foreign magical signatures or the disruption of local flora and fauna.\n\nIn instances where remediation alone is insufficient to address the damage caused by an unplanned displacement, § 1401.12 mandates that mages may be subject to additional penalties. These could include formal sanctions under the Paradox Trial (see § 700.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence) or even imprisonment if the breach of Accords is deemed severe enough by the Council.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe Autumnwood Accords recognize the inherent risks associated with magical practices that involve planar manipulation. The mage community must therefore be vigilant in their efforts to prevent unplanned displacements, while also being prepared to address any resulting issues promptly and responsibly. The liability for such incidents is not merely a matter of personal consequence but extends to ensuring the integrity and stability of the planes themselves. By adhering to these guidelines, mages can contribute to maintaining the balance and harmony that are essential to the magical world they inhabit.\n\nUnder § 100.14, the jurisdiction over unplanned planar displacements is unequivocally vested in the Autumnwood Accords Desk. This ensures a coherent approach to addressing such incidents, promoting accountability among mages, and safeguarding the planes against further disruption.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1301.12",
            "1401.12",
            "200.13",
            "100.14",
            "1401.13",
            "1101.12",
            "700.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Collateral Planar Disturbances: Responsibility and Mitigation",
          "text": "In the intricate world of magic, where arcane energies and planar shifts are as common as the changing seasons, the question of responsibility for collateral planar disturbances arises with alarming frequency. A mage conducting research or casting a spell may inadvertently set off unintended ripple effects that disturb the natural fabric of reality, leading to unpredictable outcomes in adjacent planes. The Accords Desk must address this issue comprehensively, ensuring that mages are held accountable for any such disruptions and that appropriate remedial measures are undertaken.\n\nUnder § 1101.14 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations, the Autumnwood Accords mandate that all mages shall be responsible for any unintended planar disturbances caused by their actions. The mere fact of occurrence does not absolve a mage from liability; rather, it triggers an immediate obligation to assess the nature and extent of such disturbances and initiate remediation procedures accordingly.\n\nSection 1101.14.A enumerates the types of collateral planar disturbances that fall under this category. These include unintended planar shifts, unplanned planar resonances, and unplanned planar displacements. The Accords Desk expects mages to report any such incidents as soon as they are aware of them, regardless of whether these disruptions were caused by their own actions or by those of others in close proximity.\n\nWhen a mage discovers that an unintended planar disturbance has occurred within the boundaries of their jurisdiction, they must first determine its origin and scope. This involves a detailed survey of the affected areas to ascertain the extent of contamination. Under § 1101.14.B, such surveys are mandatory and should be conducted by licensed mages with appropriate expertise in planar dynamics. The results of these surveys will inform the remedial actions that must be taken.\n\nRemediation procedures vary depending on the type and severity of the disturbance. For minor disturbances, simple stabilization spells may suffice to restore balance to the planes. However, for more severe cases, a collaborative effort involving several mages with specialized skills is necessary. The Accords Desk emphasizes the importance of interdisciplinary teamwork in addressing complex planar disruptions.\n\nSection 1101.14.C provides guidelines on the allocation of resources and responsibilities among involved mages during remediation efforts. It stresses that all parties must work together harmoniously, ensuring clear lines of communication and decision-making. The Accords Desk encourages the development of contingency plans to mitigate risks associated with large-scale disruptions, particularly those that could have catastrophic consequences.\n\nIn cases where a mage's negligence or reckless behavior is determined to be the cause of an unintended planar disturbance, additional penalties may apply beyond mere remediation. These include formal censure from the Guild, suspension from practice in certain areas, or even loss of one’s Guild Pass under § 1301.12. The Accords Desk retains the authority to impose such sanctions as deemed necessary.\n\nUnder § 1101.14.D, mages are also responsible for the long-term effects of their actions. Even after a disturbance has been mitigated, there may be lingering effects that require ongoing monitoring and management. This extends the mage's responsibility beyond immediate remediation to include continuous oversight of the affected areas.\n\nFinally, it is important to note that while the Accords Desk provides guidelines for managing unintended planar disturbances, it cannot absolve mages from their ultimate responsibility. The core principle remains: all mages must exercise due diligence in their magical practices and be prepared to take swift action when disruptions occur. This includes not only immediate remediation but also proactive measures to prevent such incidents from happening in the first place.\n\nIn conclusion, collateral planar disturbances are a serious concern within the guild, and the Accords Desk aims to ensure that mages are held accountable for their actions while providing them with the tools necessary to address these challenges effectively. By fostering a culture of responsibility and cooperation, the Guild seeks to maintain the stability and integrity of our magical world.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1101.14",
            "1301.12"
          ]
        }
      ],
