            "700.13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Ancillary Arcane Disruption Reporting and Remediation Protocols",
          "text": "In the intricate web of arcane law, the Autumnwood Accords acknowledge that not all disruptions are intentional or within a mage's direct control. Ancillary arcane disruption, defined as unintended side effects resulting from planar transit, can sometimes lead to unforeseen consequences. The protocols outlined herein aim to ensure that such disruptions are promptly reported and effectively remediated.\n\nUnder § 1401.12 Additional Provisions for Penalty Assessment and Enforcement (see § 1301.12), the Autumnwood Accords establish a framework for assessing penalties, but this section does not exhaustively cover all potential scenarios involving ancillary arcane disruptions. The codex thus introduces conditional exemptions for ancillary planar transits, ensuring that mages are not unfairly penalized for events beyond their control.\n\n### Ancillary Arcane Disruption Reporting Protocols\n\nThe first step in addressing ancillary arcane disruption is timely and accurate reporting. Mages engaged in planar transit must be aware of the potential for unintended side effects. These disruptions can include lingering arcane echoes, residue contamination, or even minor breaches that do not meet the threshold for full containment measures (see § 1401.13). It is incumbent upon mages to report any ancillary arcane disruption immediately.\n\nMages are required to submit a preliminary report via the Accords Desk within twenty-four hours of detecting such an event (see § 900.12 Reporting Ancillary and Sub-Layered Arcane Effects, see also § 1401.13). This report must detail the nature and extent of the disruption, including any potential hazards or risks to the surrounding area. Failure to report promptly can result in additional penalties (see § 800.12 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation).\n\n### Remediation Protocols\n\nOnce a report is filed, the Accords Desk will assess the severity of the disruption and determine appropriate remediation protocols. For minor disruptions that do not pose significant risk, the mage may be instructed to conduct basic containment measures (see § 200.12 Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation). In more serious cases, additional resources or assistance from the Aegis Magi may be required.\n\nIn instances where a disruption is linked to unauthorized field use of magic (as in the case of Titan growth on Markop), remediation protocols will include a detailed investigation into the source of the breach. This ensures that mages are not unfairly penalized for incidents beyond their control, such as accidental contamination from neighboring jurisdictions or breaches caused by external forces (see § 600.12 Oversight and Compliance Protocols for Construct Labor).\n\n### Mitigation Protocols\n\nMitigation protocols are designed to prevent ancillary arcane disruptions from escalating into more significant issues. These measures include periodic monitoring of planar transit points, the establishment of buffer zones around sensitive areas, and the development of contingency plans (see § 500.12 Remediation and Obligations for Lingering Arcane Effects). Mages involved in complex or high-risk transits are advised to undergo additional training on mitigating ancillary arcane effects.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords Desk will review reports of ancillary disruptions periodically, seeking patterns that might indicate systemic issues with certain transit points. Such reviews can lead to adjustments in protocols and enhanced safeguards (see § 1401.12 Additional Provisions for Penalty Assessment and Enforcement).\n\n### Accountability and Penalties\n\nWhile the Accords aim to provide conditional exemptions for unintended disruptions, accountability remains paramount. Mages who fail to report or mishandle ancillary arcane effects can face penalties under § 800.12 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation. The severity of these penalties will depend on the nature and extent of the disruption, as well as any failures in reporting or remediation.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe Ancillary Arcane Disruption Reporting and Remediation Protocols are a critical component of maintaining the integrity of planar transit within the Autumnwood Accords. By ensuring that mages are aware of their obligations and responsibilities, these protocols help to mitigate risks and promote safer magical practices. The codex recognizes that not all disruptions can be prevented or controlled directly, but through prompt reporting and effective remediation, the impact of such events can be minimized.\n\nUnder § 1401.12 Additional Provisions for Penalty Assessment and Enforcement (see § 1301.12), the Accords Desk maintains a robust framework for addressing these issues. By balancing conditional exemptions with accountability, the codex seeks to foster an environment where mages can innovate while remaining responsible stewards of their craft.\n\nIn conclusion, the Ancillary Arcane Disruption Reporting and Remediation Protocols serve as a vital safeguard against unintended consequences of planar transit. They ensure that mages are not unfairly penalized for events beyond their control while maintaining the integrity of the magical community.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1401.12",
            "1301.12",
            "1401.13",
