          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Ancillary Arcane Contamination and Secondary Liability",
          "text": "In the realm of planar transits, the Mages' Guild Accords Desk recognizes that ancillary activities inevitably occur in conjunction with primary magical operations. These ancillary activities can sometimes result in unintended consequences such as arcane contamination. The Codex must address these potential liabilities to ensure responsible conduct by all members and guests who traverse planar boundaries.\n\nUnder § 1401.14, the Mages' Guild provides a conditional exemption for ancillary planar transits that arise from unforeseen circumstances or necessary secondary actions. This section aims to delineate the conditions under which such exemptions are granted and the obligations that accompany them. Ancillary activities refer to those actions undertaken as a direct result of the primary planar transit, but not part of it. These may include temporary storage spells, quick containment measures, or immediate response to emergent anomalies.\n\nWhen a caster initiates a planar transit, they undertake an obligation to ensure that any ancillary activities do not create undue risk or harm. This includes avoiding the introduction of harmful substances into the target plane and ensuring that any necessary secondary actions are conducted safely and with due care. Under § 1401.14, if unforeseen circumstances necessitate such secondary actions, a conditional exemption may be granted provided certain criteria are met.\n\nTo qualify for this exemption, the caster must demonstrate:\n(a) That the ancillary activity was an unavoidable consequence of the primary planar transit;\n(b) That all reasonable precautions were taken to mitigate any potential harm or contamination; and\n(c) That immediate steps were undertaken upon detection of any harmful effects to contain and neutralize them.\n\nThe conditional nature of this exemption implies that the caster remains liable for any ancillary arcane contamination resulting from their actions. If such contamination occurs, the caster is required under § 1301.12 to provide prompt notice and take all necessary steps to mitigate the contamination under § 500.13. Failure to comply with these obligations may result in sanctions or penalties as outlined in § 400.14.\n\nFurthermore, § 900.13 mandates that any ancillary activities resulting in significant disruption must be reported immediately. This ensures that all relevant authorities are informed and can respond appropriately under § 200.14. The report should include a detailed description of the activity, the measures taken to prevent contamination, and any steps undertaken to mitigate it.\n\nIn cases where the caster fails to meet these obligations, the Guild may impose additional penalties as outlined in § 1301.12. These penalties can range from warnings to financial sanctions or even temporary restrictions on magical activities under § 400.14. The severity of the penalty will depend on the extent of any harm caused and the caster’s previous record.\n\nIt is important to note that this conditional exemption does not apply if the caster was aware of potential risks but chose to ignore them. Awareness of such risks would negate the claim for a conditional exemption, thereby subjecting the caster to full liability under § 1301.12. Additionally, any deliberate or reckless actions leading to contamination will be treated as serious violations and subject to harsher penalties.\n\nUnder § 1401.14, the Mages' Guild aims to balance the practical needs of its members with the responsibility to ensure that ancillary activities do not introduce undue harm into planar environments. By providing this conditional exemption for unforeseen circumstances, the Codex encourages responsible conduct while recognizing the complexities inherent in magical operations.\n\nIn conclusion, § 1401.14 serves as a vital tool for managing the potential risks associated with ancillary arcane contamination arising from planar transits. It provides a framework within which casters can operate responsibly, ensuring that any unintended consequences are addressed promptly and effectively. This approach not only protects the integrity of the planes but also fosters a culture of accountability among all members of the Guild and its guests.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1401.14",
            "1301.12",
            "500.13",
            "400.14",
            "900.13",
            "200.14"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Secondary Liability and Compensation Schedules for Unforeseen Arcane Resonance E",
          "text": "Under § 1401.14 Conditional Exemption for Ancillary Planar Transits, the Mages' Guild Accords Desk has established protocols to mitigate the risks associated with ancillary planar transits; however, this exemption does not absolve the primary operator of all consequences arising from such operations. Specifically, when an ancillary transit triggers a secondary resonance event that results in property damage or personal injury to third parties who were not participants in the ritual, liability shall be determined by the severity of the arcane contamination and the degree of negligence exhibited during the pre-transit assessment. This section establishes a tiered compensation schedule designed to ensure that victims of accidental planar breaches receive adequate restitution while preventing frivolous claims against mages acting in good faith under the exemptions granted by § 1201.14. It is imperative that all mages engaging in activities requiring such exemptions maintain a comprehensive log of pre-transit stability readings, as failure to document these readings shall constitute prima facie evidence of negligence in the event of a catastrophic resonance cascade.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords Desk recognizes that the nature of ancillary planar transits often involves interactions with unstable manifolds where the laws of physics are merely suggestions rather than dictates; consequently, the standard statutes governing civil liability under § 500.15 may be insufficient to address the unique metaphysical damages incurred during such events. Therefore, any claim arising from an event classified as ancillary arcane contamination shall be adjudicated according to the specific mitigation protocols outlined in this section, which supersede general tort laws for a period of one hundred and forty-four hours following the incident. During this window, the primary operator must secure the affected area and initiate immediate remediation procedures as mandated by § 800.15 Remediation Protocols for Ancillary Arcane Disruption to prevent the spread of contamination into adjacent spatial coordinates. The cost of these mandatory remediation efforts shall be borne entirely by the party seeking the exemption, regardless of whether the event was deemed unforeseeable at the time of planning, provided that the operator failed to adhere to the safety standards set forth in § 1201.16 Conditional Exemption for Ancillary Planar Transits and Associated Protocols.\n\nIn cases where the ancillary transit results in the accidental opening of a portal that leads to a hostile or uninhabited plane, resulting in the loss of life or permanent dislocation of individuals, the liability framework shifts significantly from mere compensation to restorative justice involving the binding of ancient pacts. Under these circumstances, the primary operator shall be required to enter into a binding agreement with the affected parties or their representatives, stipulating not only financial reparations but also the transfer of specific magical resources or artifacts as restitution. This requirement is grounded in the belief that certain metaphysical debts cannot be settled solely through currency, echoing the principles found in the governance of ancient pacts where breaking agreements risks apocalypse. The Accords Desk shall review all such agreements to ensure they meet the threshold of fairness and adequacy before granting final release from further liability, a process that may involve peer review protocols detailed in § 1.17 Additional Provisions for Mages' Guild Mediation and Peer Review Protocols. It is the explicit intent of this legislation to balance the innovative spirit of the Innovator faction, which seeks to expand the boundaries of permissible research, with the cautionary traditions of the Conservators who insist that no advancement should come at the cost of unchecked peril to the innocent populace.\n\nFinally, any attempt to circumvent these liability obligations by mischaracterizing a primary operation as an ancillary transit shall be treated as a violation of the fundamental trust required within the Mages' Guild community and may result in sanctions including the sealing of magical abilities under the penal provisions of The Paradox Trial. The distinction between a true ancillary activity and a pretext for avoiding liability is to be determined by the Accords Desk based on the complexity of the primary goal versus the magnitude of the potential risk, ensuring that the exemption granted under § 1401.14 serves its intended purpose of facilitating necessary magical progress rather than becoming a loophole for irresponsibility. Mages are reminded that the safety of the realm is paramount, and the pursuit of knowledge must never be pursued at the expense of the well-being of those who share the same spatial plane, reinforcing the ethical obligations inherent in the practice of magic as codified throughout the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
