      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Oversight Protocols for Quiet List Reinstatement Hearings",
          "text": "In the ongoing administration and oversight of the Quiet List, it is imperative that mages bound by these protocols adhere to strict procedures when seeking reinstatement or re-evaluation of their listings. The Quiet List, while not a public catalogue, maintains a significant influence over the reputation and standing of its members within the Autumnwood Accords. Therefore, any process for review must be meticulously conducted to ensure fairness and transparency.\n\nUnder § 100.13 Protocols for Mediation and Inter-Factional Arbitration (see § 100.13), the oversight committee responsible for Quiet List management operates with a commitment to balance the interests of all involved parties, including the mage seeking review, the Aegis Magi enforcing the Accords, and the broader community. The process begins with the initiation of a request by the mage in question (see § 400.13 Unauthorized Research Permits and Exceeding Boundaries), which must be accompanied by evidence demonstrating that they have addressed their previous transgressions satisfactorily.\n\nOnce this initial request is received, the oversight committee reviews the case under § 900.13 Reporting of Ancillary and Sub-Layered Arcane Effects, ensuring that any lingering effects or residual issues are accounted for before proceeding further. A mage's application can be denied if it is found that they have not sufficiently mitigated their previous actions (see § 800.14 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation). If the initial review indicates a viable case, a formal hearing is scheduled to allow for detailed discussion of the circumstances leading to the original listing.\n\nDuring these hearings, under § 200.15 Catchline Title: Emergency Protocols for Arcane Breach Mitigation (see § 200.15), the mage may present evidence and testimony from peers or other relevant parties who can attest to their efforts in correcting past behavior. The oversight committee must ensure that these hearings are conducted under strict confidentiality, as stipulated in § 700.14 Admissibility Standards for Arcane Testimony and Artifacts (see § 700.14). This ensures that the mage's reputation is not compromised through premature or unregulated disclosure.\n\nIf the oversight committee finds sufficient cause to overturn the listing, they must provide a reasoned decision outlining the rationale behind their determination. In such cases, the mage will be reinstated and all previous restrictions lifted (see § 800.14 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation). However, should the committee determine that further measures are necessary, they may impose additional conditions or require further rehabilitation before reconsidering the listing.\n\nIt is important to note that once a mage has been reinstated, their continued compliance must be monitored under § 500.13 Mitigation of Environmental Arcane Decay (see § 500.13). Any breach of these conditions can result in immediate re-listing and additional restrictions, highlighting the importance of sustained effort post-reinstatement.\n\nIn conclusion, the protocols governing Quiet List reinstatement hearings are designed to uphold the integrity and fairness of the system while providing a mechanism for mages to demonstrate their commitment to ethical practice. These protocols reflect the broader principles enshrined in the Autumnwood Accords, emphasizing the need for balance between protection and opportunity within the magical community (see § 1401.14 Conditional Exemption for Ancillary Planar Transits). By adhering strictly to these procedures, the Quiet List remains a vital tool in maintaining the health and stability of the magical arts, ensuring that only those who demonstrate genuine reform are allowed to return to active practice.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "100.13",
            "400.13",
            "900.13",
