    {
      "cite": "400.15",
      "part": "part_iv",
      "title": "Oversight Protocols for Exceptional Research Permits and Supervised Experiments",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Oversight Protocols for Exceptional Research Permits and Supervised Experiments",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords Desk recognizes that innovation and advancement in the realm of arcane studies are critical to the progress of magic within the Guild. However, these pursuits must be conducted under strict supervision to ensure safety and compliance with established protocols. § 400.15 Additional Provisions sets forth specific oversight protocols for exceptional research permits and supervised experiments, ensuring that mages conduct their work responsibly and ethically.\n\nUnder § 400.15, all requests for exceptional research permits must be thoroughly vetted by a panel of peers. This includes mages from both the Conservator and Innovator factions to ensure balanced oversight. The panel evaluates the request based on the potential risks involved, the necessity of the research, and the proposed containment measures. Only those projects that demonstrate a clear benefit to the Guild or the wider magical community may proceed.\n\nOnce approved, the mage conducting the research must adhere strictly to the terms outlined in the permit. These terms include detailed protocols for containment and disposal of any specimens or artifacts generated during the experiment. § 400.7 Specimen Containment Standards and Disposal Protocols provides specific guidelines on how these items are to be handled. Failure to comply with these standards can result in immediate revocation of research privileges under § 800.13.\n\nThe mage must also submit regular reports documenting the progress and findings of their experiments. These reports are reviewed by the Oversight Committee, which ensures that all activities are conducted safely and ethically. The Oversight Committee reserves the right to intervene if it deems the research to be unsafe or unethical under § 400.13 Protocols for Deviant Methodology and Innovation Oversight.\n\nAdditionally, the mage must maintain a journal of their experiments, detailing every step taken in the process. This journal is subject to audit by the Oversight Committee at any time. The purpose of these audits is not only to verify compliance with established protocols but also to identify potential areas of improvement for future research.\n\nIn cases where the research involves unauthorized field casting or exceeds the boundaries set forth in the permit, immediate action must be taken. § 400.12 Research Integrity and Oversight Protocols outlines the steps that should be followed in such scenarios. The mage responsible will be subject to a Paradox Trial under § 300.13 to determine appropriate sanctions.\n\nThe Oversight Committee also has the authority to impose additional conditions on any research permit, ensuring that safety measures are stringent and comprehensive. These conditions can include limiting the duration of the experiment, imposing strict containment protocols, or requiring the mage to work with a supervising mage from another faction. This ensures that no single faction exerts undue influence over the research process.\n\nFurthermore, § 400.15 mandates that any innovative methodology proposed must undergo rigorous peer review under § 400.9 Research Integrity and Peer Review before it is approved for experimentation. The committee will consider both the theoretical soundness of the methodology and its potential real-world application.\n\nIn conclusion, § 400.15 Additional Provisions ensures that all exceptional research permits are conducted in a manner that promotes innovation while maintaining the safety and integrity of the Guild. By adhering to these protocols, mages can contribute meaningfully to the advancement of magic without compromising the well-being of their fellow mages or the community at large.\n\nSee § 400.13 for more on innovative methodology oversight, § 800.13 on unlawful casting and confiscation, and § 900.14 on reporting ancillary effects.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "400.15",
            "400.7",
            "800.13",
            "400.13",
