        {
          "heading": "Conditional Release Protocols for Sealed Mages — Continued — Continued",
          "text": "Under the auspices of the Autumnwood Accords, the conditional release protocols for sealed mages have been meticulously crafted to balance the imperative of public safety with the necessity of allowing individuals to retain their autonomy and dignity. Section § 200.16 sets forth these protocols, which are critical in ensuring that a mage who has had their magic sealed can be reintegrated into society safely and effectively. This section addresses not only the initial stages of release but also the ongoing monitoring and support required for such individuals to navigate life post-sealing.\n\nWhen a mage is found guilty of breaches that warrant sealing, the decision is made by a Paradox Trial panel, which considers all relevant factors before determining the appropriate measure. Upon the issuance of a seal, the mage undergoes an evaluation process to determine if they are capable of living independently without posing a risk to themselves or others. This evaluation includes psychological assessments, community service records, and the assessment of any ongoing support needs.\n\nThe first phase of conditional release involves supervised reintroduction into society. The mage is required to report regularly to their local Aegis Mage, who oversees their activities and ensures compliance with all stipulated conditions. These conditions typically include restrictions on certain types of magic or environments in which they can practice. Supervision may also involve regular meetings with a mentor or counselor provided by the Guild.\n\nFollowing this initial phase, the mage is evaluated for further release based on demonstrated adherence to the conditions imposed. If deemed suitable, the mage progresses to a more independent monitoring system. This involves periodic checks and reports but with fewer direct interactions from the Aegis Mage. The mage must continue to demonstrate responsible behavior and maintain a stable environment conducive to their continued recovery.\n\nIn cases where a sealed mage shows significant improvement and poses no ongoing threat, they may be considered for full release. Full release requires approval from both the Paradox Trial panel that originally imposed the seal and the local Archmage. Once approved, the mage is removed from any formal oversight, although they remain subject to the general provisions of the Codex.\n\nFor those mages who have been sealed due to exceptional circumstances, such as a severe breach or an inability to control their magic in public spaces, additional conditions may be imposed. These could include restrictions on travel beyond certain boundaries or limitations on participation in specific activities that might lead to harm.\n\nThe process for conditional release is designed to ensure that mages who have been sealed are reintegrated into society in a manner that promotes both their recovery and the safety of others. It recognizes the complexity of magic and its potential impact on individuals and communities, ensuring that even after sealing, mages continue to receive the support necessary for long-term success.\n\nThe conditional release protocols also emphasize the importance of community involvement in the rehabilitation process. Local Guild members play a crucial role in providing resources and support to sealed mages. This includes offering counseling services, facilitating access to education and employment opportunities, and ensuring that the mage has access to all necessary supports within their community.\n\nMoreover, the Codex mandates regular reviews of the conditions imposed on sealed mages. These reviews are conducted at least annually and involve a comprehensive assessment of the mage's progress towards full release. The goal is not only to ensure continued safety but also to identify any additional support needed for long-term recovery.\n\nIn instances where a sealed mage demonstrates exceptional improvement, they may be eligible for early conditional release. Early release is contingent upon approval from both the Paradox Trial panel and local Aegis Mage, ensuring that all parties agree on the readiness of the individual for more independent living. This process underscores the collaborative nature of the Codex, emphasizing shared responsibility in managing and supporting individuals with sealed magic.\n\nUltimately, the conditional release protocols serve as a testament to the Guild's commitment to rehabilitation and reintegration. By providing structured pathways for sealed mages to regain their autonomy while ensuring public safety, these protocols reflect the balance between discipline and compassion that is at the heart of the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "200.16"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Protocol for Sealed Artifacts in Transit During Breach Events",
          "text": "In the event that an arcane breach occurs while a sealed artifact is being transported or stored under the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords Desk, the immediate cessation of movement becomes mandatory regardless of the cargo manifest status. Under § 200.15, the primary directive for emergency protocols establishes that unforeseen eruptions of magical energy require decisive action to prevent widespread chaos; however, this general mandate must be specifically tailored when the breach is coupled with the displacement of a sealed artifact. Such artifacts, often bound by ancient pacts or subjected to the sanctions of § 1301.14 regarding ancillary arcane disruption, possess unique resonance frequencies that can interact catastrophically with active breach energies if not neutralized instantly. Consequently, any mages present at the scene are required to invoke the provisions found in § 200.13 concerning mandatory protocols for volatile phenomena, treating the sealed object not merely as cargo but as a secondary containment vessel that may itself become a source of instability if its seal is compromised by the ambient magical surge.\n\nThe procedural hierarchy for handling such a scenario dictates that the lead responding mage shall first attempt to establish a localized null-field around the transport container or storage unit, thereby decoupling the artifact from the primary breach signature. This operation must be executed in strict accordance with § 1.15, which addresses expedited remediation and immediate response protocols, ensuring that no time is wasted on administrative queries when seconds count against structural integrity. If the seal proves permeable under the stress of the breach event, the personnel on site are authorized to initiate a controlled transfer of the artifact into a pre-designated high-vacuum stasis field located within the nearest secured containment wing, as referenced in § 200.17 regarding advanced provisions for mitigating catastrophic effects. This transfer must be conducted without engaging the artifact directly with offensive magic, lest one trigger the Paradox Trial sanctions outlined under the penal system of the Mages' Guild Accords, where rogue mages face sealing of their abilities or trial by arcane duel for endangering public safety.\n\nFurthermore, any ancillary disruptions resulting from the interaction between the breach energy and the sealed artifact must be logged and remediated in accordance with § 800.16, which outlines protocols for addressing environmental impacts arising from arcane leakage. The clerks of the Accords Desk shall ensure that all witnesses to the incident are debriefed immediately regarding the potential release of forbidden schools of magic, such as necromancy or blood magic, which may have been inadvertently activated by the artifact's instability during the breach. Should the artifact contain remnants of ancient pacts, its handling must also align with § 700.16, the validation protocols for arcane artifacts and relics, to ensure that no binding agreements are violated in the haste of emergency response. The Conservators and Innovators factions within the Guild may hold divergent views on the aggressiveness of the containment strategy; however, the Accords Desk maintains authority under § 1.9 to enforce a unified standard of caution that prioritizes the safety of the realm over theoretical research interests.\n\nIn cases where the sealed artifact is deemed irretrievable or its seal has fully ruptured, releasing the contained entity into the wild without containment fails to meet the requirements set forth in § 200.14 concerning additional protocols for preventing rogue magic from escaping into the public domain. Therefore, any residual energy signatures left behind must be scrubbed by qualified remediation teams using techniques approved under § 1301.15, which details specific remediation protocols for ancillary arcane disruption caused by deliberate or accidental transgression. The Archmage Veyra, acting as Sovereign under the governance structure of the Guild, retains the final authority to authorize the destruction of such artifacts if they pose an existential threat that cannot be mitigated through standard containment measures. Failure to adhere to these specific sub-protocols during a breach event involving sealed artifacts shall be treated as a grave violation of the Accords, subjecting the offending mage to the full extent of penal sanctions available under the current legal framework, including potential exile to a pocket dimension or permanent sealing of their magical aptitude until such time as peer review protocols under § 1.17 deem them fit for reintegration into society.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "200.15",
            "1301.14",
