        {
          "heading": "Verification Standards for Anomalous Signatures on Quiet List Entries",
          "text": "The administration of the Quiet List requires that any entry designated as \"anomalous\" or requiring further scrutiny must undergo a rigorous process of signature verification before it is formally recorded in the Guild's ledgers. This section establishes the mandatory protocols for validating the arcane signatures attached to petitions, transfer orders, and exclusion notices involving mages currently under review. Under § 1.6 Definitions, a valid signature is understood to be an energetic imprint that binds the signatory to the truth of the declaration; however, entries on the Quiet List present a unique challenge wherein the signatory may be unwilling or unable to provide a traditional binding. Consequently, any petition lacking a verifiable arcane signature must instead bear the official seal of a Peer Reviewer designated under § 1.17 Additional Provisions for Mages' Guild Mediation and Peer Review Protocols. Such designations are not arbitrary; they require prior approval from the Council of Oversight to ensure that the reviewer possesses the requisite authority to vouch for the claims being submitted on behalf of a restricted individual. Any attempt to forge or simulate a signature using non-canonical mana sources will result in immediate revocation of the petition and potential referral to the Paradox Trial under the penal provisions of the Accords, as seen in the strictures against false provenance outlined in § 900.5 False Provenance.\n\nFurthermore, the verification process must account for the integrity of the transmission medium used to convey the signature from the point of origin to the Accords Desk. In accordance with § 300.14 Catchline Title: Transmission Protocols and Oversight, any artifact or document bearing a Quiet List entry must traverse a secure conduit that prevents unauthorized alteration of the magical resonance embedded within the paper or digital equivalent. Should a transmission fail or show signs of tampering, the entry shall be deemed void ab initio until re-submitted through an expedited channel authorized by § 100.15 Catchline Title: Protocols for Immediate and Expedited Remediation. This is particularly critical when dealing with mages whose own magical signatures have been dampened or suppressed by previous sanctions, as their inability to produce a strong resonance could be mistaken for inauthenticity by automated sorting algorithms. To prevent such erroneous exclusions, the Clerk of the Accords Desk must manually validate the context of the submission against the historical record found in § 1201.14 Catchline Title: Conditional Exemption for Ancillary Planar Transits, ensuring that any anomalies are not dismissed as procedural failures but rather investigated as potential indicators of deeper structural issues within the Quiet List itself.\n\nThe burden of proof regarding the authenticity of a signature or seal rests entirely with the submitting party unless the Clerk has reason to suspect foul play based on patterns observed in previous filings under § 300.15 Catchline Title: Oversight Protocols for Quiet List Procedures. When a dispute arises concerning whether a specific energetic imprint constitutes a valid signature, the matter shall be referred to the Peer Review panel convened under § 100.17 Catchline Title: Protocols for Exclusionary Hearings and Peer Review. These panels are empowered to determine not only the technical validity of the signature but also the intent behind its creation, distinguishing between genuine attempts at compliance and calculated obfuscation. It is imperative that all reviewers adhere to the speech protocols outlined in § 100.16 Catchline Title: Speech Protocols and Oversight, ensuring that their deliberations remain free from bias or external influence that could compromise the impartiality of the verification process. Failure to adhere to these standards may result in the invalidation of the entire Quiet List entry and the imposition of sanctions upon the responsible official, reinforcing the Guild's commitment to maintaining a transparent and just administrative framework even within its most secretive operations.\n\nIn cases where the signature cannot be physically verified due to the absence of a signatory or the destruction of the original document, alternative methods of validation shall be employed, provided they are sanctioned by the High Council under § 100.16 Catchline Title: Speech Protocols and Oversight. These alternatives may include corroboration from trusted allies or witnesses who can attest to the circumstances surrounding the creation of the entry, effectively creating a chain of testimony that serves as a substitute for the direct arcane signature. Such corroboration must be recorded in the official archives with full citation to the relevant sections governing witness testimony and evidentiary standards, ensuring that no single point of failure exists within the verification protocol. By adhering strictly to these layered requirements, the Mages' Guild ensures that the Quiet List remains a tool for protection rather than oppression, balancing the need for secrecy with the fundamental rights of all mages subject to its provisions. Any deviation from these established procedures shall be treated as a breach of duty under § 1.9 General Liability for Clerks and Administrators, subjecting the offending party to disciplinary action proportional to the severity of the compromise.",
