    {
      "cite": "1101.16",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Catchline Title: Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Merging and Consequential Colla",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Catchline Title: Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Merging and Consequential Colla",
          "text": "Under § 1101.14, the remedial protocols concerning collateral contamination are well established; however, there remains a critical gap in addressing scenarios where unlawful casting has resulted in an unintended merging of magical energies or entities, leading to unforeseen consequences. This section introduces a new protocol designed specifically for such situations.\n\nIn cases where magic is cast unlawfully and leads to the merging of different magical signatures, whether due to improper spellwork, unauthorized experimentation, or malicious intent, the resulting amalgamation may propagate beyond the immediate area. Such mergers can create hazardous conditions, affecting not only the immediate vicinity but also potentially distant locales through a chain reaction of collateral damage.\n\nThe protocol under § 1101.16 mandates that all mages involved in such unlawful casting are required to immediately cease their activities and report the incident to the nearest Aegis Magi or local Conservator authority. Failure to comply with this directive may result in increased penalties, as detailed in § 800.14.\n\nUpon receiving notification of a merged magical signature, the Aegis Magi are responsible for initiating an immediate assessment. This involves surveying the area and identifying all affected parties and entities involved in or subjected to the merging effect. The protocol requires that all mages present at the site must assist in the containment process. Under § 100.16, they are also required to provide any relevant information regarding their magical practices, materials used, and any known effects of previous castings.\n\nThe Aegis Magi then proceed with a thorough remediation effort aimed at reversing the merging effect. This may involve targeted counter-casting or other arcane techniques deemed necessary by the peer review committee formed under § 1601.16 to oversee such protocols. The committee is responsible for ensuring that all remedial actions are conducted safely and effectively, minimizing further collateral damage.\n\nIn instances where the merged magical signatures have already caused significant harm or disruption, the protocol requires an immediate containment zone to be established around the affected area. Under § 200.16, this zone must remain secure until further assessment by the peer review committee can determine a safe course of action. The containment may include the sealing of specific areas within the zone to prevent the spread of magical residue or lingering echoes.\n\nAdditionally, any mage involved in the unlawful casting is liable for all remediation costs incurred and must contribute to the cleanup effort under § 500.16. This includes not only financial compensation but also personal service hours dedicated to community projects aimed at restoring balance to affected areas, as determined by the peer review committee.\n\nThe protocol also addresses the issue of liability for any lingering effects or echoes that persist after initial remediation efforts. Under § 700.16, mages involved are required to provide ongoing oversight and support until these effects have been fully mitigated. This ensures that all potential risks associated with the merged signatures are thoroughly addressed.\n\nIn conclusion, the protocol set forth in this new section aims to address the complexities of unintended magical mergings by establishing a clear framework for immediate response, containment, and remediation. It emphasizes the importance of cooperation between all involved parties and the responsibility of mages to ensure that their actions do not lead to further harm or disruption. By adhering to these protocols, the Mages' Guild seeks to maintain order and stability within its jurisdiction while minimizing collateral damage and promoting responsible magical practice.\n\nSee § 800.14 for specific penalties related to unlawful casting; under § 1601.16, peer review committees oversee all remediation efforts; § 500.16 details mage liabilities for cleanup costs; § 700.16 outlines ongoing oversight and support requirements post-remediation.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.14",
