    {
      "cite": "1201.16",
      "part": "part_xii",
      "title": "Conditional Exemption for Ancillary Planar Transits and Associated Protocols",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Conditional Exemption for Ancillary Planar Transits and Associated Protocols",
          "text": "The Mages' Guild Accords Desk recognizes the necessity to regulate the use of planar transit across all jurisdictions, including those beyond the immediate control of the Midlands region. While standard protocols are in place under § 1201.14, certain situations may arise where such transits are ancillary and do not require the full rigors of oversight typically applied. This section provides a framework for these exceptional cases.\n\nUnder § 1201.16, mages intending to use planar transit in an ancillary manner must first demonstrate that their purpose aligns with one or more of the following criteria:\n\n(a) The transit is necessary for emergency purposes, such as immediate response to a breach of the Quiet List or a Paradox Trial. In these cases, § 1001.16 ensures confidentiality and reporting obligations are met; see also § 300.16 for peer review protocols.\n\n(b) The transit concerns the transport of arcane artifacts that are not subject to direct supervision due to their nature or previous use in exceptional research permits (§ 400.15).\n\n(c) Ancillary transits are required for the relocation of constructs and structures under § 600.16, provided such activities do not interfere with local sovereignty or jurisdiction.\n\nIn all cases where an exemption is sought under § 1201.16, the mage must provide a detailed plan to the Archmage Sovereign for review. The Sovereign may then grant the exemption if it is determined that no undue risk is posed and compliance with Guild protocols can be ensured through alternative measures (§ 1600.15).\n\nThe granting of an exemption does not preclude the mage from reporting such transits to local authorities or other relevant bodies, as outlined in § 1001.16.\n\nFor those seeking conditional exemptions, it is imperative that they understand their obligations under § 300.16 regarding peer review and oversight. Additionally, any ancillary transit must not contravene the jurisdictional sovereignty of any local authorities or planar entities (§ 800.15).\n\nThe granting of an exemption does not nullify the mage's responsibility to adhere to all relevant protocols under § 900.16 concerning ancillary arcane events and casualties. In instances where such events occur, remediation must be immediate and in accordance with § 500.16.\n\nFurthermore, mages granted conditional exemptions are required to maintain records of their activities for at least one year after completion, to ensure accountability (§ 700.16).\n\nIn conclusion, while § 1201.16 offers a path for ancillary planar transits that align with Guild protocols and guidelines, it is crucial that all mages understand the responsibilities and potential risks associated with such activities. The Archmage Sovereign reserves the right to review and modify any exemption granted under this section (§ 300.16).\n\nSee § 1201.14 for additional protocols regarding ancillary transits; see also § 500.16 for remediation obligations in the event of collateral contamination or disruption.\n\nUnder § 300.16, peer review and oversight are paramount to ensure compliance with Guild standards during all planar transit activities.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1201.14",
            "1201.16",
            "1001.16",
            "300.16",
