    {
      "cite": "300.17",
      "part": "part_iii",
      "title": "Contingencies for Unauthorized Arcane Breaches",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Contingencies for Unauthorized Arcane Breaches",
          "text": "Whereas the Accords recognize that the integrity of the arcane lattice is paramount to the safety of the natural world, it is hereby mandated that all practitioners licensed under § 300.11 shall maintain rigorous internal protocols to detect and neutralize unauthorized breaches before they escalate into public calamity. This obligation extends beyond mere passive observation; any mage who detects an anomaly in the local mana field or suspects infiltration by agents of the Unsanctioned Arts must immediately initiate containment procedures as outlined under § 300.12 Prohibition on Magical Correspondence and Transmission, ensuring that no illicit information regarding the Quiet List escapes the secure perimeter. Failure to act with such diligence shall be construed not merely as negligence but as complicity in the dissemination of forbidden knowledge, particularly when such knowledge pertains to the research subjects detailed in § 400.1 Research Permits. The Office of Archmage Sovereignty retains the authority to suspend any permit holder found to have compromised these containment measures, effectively sealing their magical faculties until a full review of their conduct is completed pursuant to the penal provisions found in § 800.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Unlawful Casting and Confiscation. It shall be further noted that the mere possession of artifacts derived from prohibited schools, such as those associated with necromancy or blood magic listed in § 300.2 Necromancy, constitutes a prima facie case of violation regardless of the intent behind their acquisition.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords impose a strict duty to report any irregularities regarding the employment of constructs or automated systems that may have been altered by hostile forces. As established in § 600.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence, evidence gathered from such compromised entities shall be admissible only if the chain of custody remains unbroken and the original integrity of the construct is verified through authorized diagnostic rituals. Any attempt to conceal damage, corruption, or destabilization resulting from exposure to prohibited energies will be deemed a serious breach of trust, triggering immediate remedial obligations under § 1101.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations. The Conservators argue that these measures are essential to prevent the radical Innovators from pushing the boundaries of acceptable research too far, while the Innovators contend that such rigid oversight stifles progress; however, the Accords remain silent on this debate, instead enforcing a neutral standard where safety trumps theoretical advancement. Consequently, any research conducted in a pocket dimension designated as a laboratory under § 400.2 Pocket Dimensions as Labs must adhere to these same containment standards, ensuring that no unauthorized transmission of magical data occurs between the inner sanctum and the outer world. The Office of Archmage Sovereignty shall review all such incidents quarterly to ensure compliance, and any deviation from the established schedules of arts found in § 300.1 Schedules of Arts will be subject to immediate audit. It is the solemn duty of every practitioner to uphold these protocols, for the collapse of one containment field could unravel the delicate balance maintained by the Accords for centuries.\n\nAdditionally, practitioners must be aware that exceptions granted under § 300.13 Exceptions for Ritualistic Variance and Deviation do not absolve them of their primary responsibility to prevent unauthorized transmission. Even in cases where ritualistic variance is permitted to explore new avenues of magic, the prohibition on conveying information pertaining to the Unsanctioned Arts remains absolute unless expressly authorized in writing by the Office of Archmage Sovereignty. This includes any form of magical correspondence that might inadvertently reveal the location or nature of restricted research projects. The Edicts of the Flame have long argued that all unnatural forces must be purged, yet the Accords provide a structured path for those who wish to study dangerous arts under strict supervision. Therefore, no mage shall engage in commercial spellcasting activities outside the bounds of their licensed practice without express authorization from the Guild's High Council, as mandated by § 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct. This restriction applies equally to any attempt to sell or distribute magical items tainted by residual influences from previous owners who utilized forbidden methods. The legacy of such taints must be fully disclosed in accordance with the principles set forth in § 1101.11 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion, ensuring that potential users are fully informed of the inherent risks associated with the item's history. By adhering to these comprehensive guidelines, the Autumnwood Accords ensure that the study of magic remains a force for good, safeguarding both the practitioners and the world they inhabit from the catastrophic consequences of unchecked arcane power.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "300.11",
            "300.12",
            "400.1",
