        },
        {
          "heading": "Procedural Integrity in the Transfer of Living Specimens",
          "text": "The mandate to maintain an unbroken chain of evidence regarding living arcane specimens extends beyond mere physical possession; it requires a rigorous audit of every hand that touches a subject, ensuring that the biological or metaphysical integrity of the specimen remains attributable to its original custodian. Under § 400.12, which establishes the foundational protocols for research integrity and oversight, any deviation from the prescribed transfer procedures renders the subsequent data derived from said specimen inadmissible in proceedings under § 700.8. This section governs the specific mechanics of transit between authorized laboratories housed within pocket dimensions and field sites, a concept further elaborated in § 400.2. When a researcher requests the relocation of a living entity, whether it be a crystalline golem or a volatile elemental, the request must include a detailed manifest listing the current condition of the specimen's containment matrix and the specific magical signatures currently stabilizing its form. Failure to provide this manifest constitutes a breach of the custody chain mandated by § 400.17 and may trigger penalties outlined in § 800.9 for unauthorized casting or mishandling of artifacts.\n\nThe physical act of transferring a living specimen must be witnessed by at least one certified Guild scribe or an overseer designated under the exceptional permit protocols found in § 400.15. This witness is not merely a passive observer but an active guarantor of the chain of custody, required to affix their personal seal to the transfer manifest immediately upon completion of the exchange. Without this dual-signature verification, the receiving party assumes liability for any degradation or alteration of the specimen prior to their arrival, a principle that aligns with the broader standards of relic provenance set forth in § 1101.2 regarding chain of title. It is imperative that the witness record includes a timestamp calibrated against the Guild's central clock and a description of the ambient magical pressure at the time of transfer, as these factors are critical in later adjudicating disputes over specimen behavior post-transfer. Such documentation ensures that if a specimen exhibits anomalous properties after arriving at a new lab, the original custodian cannot be held responsible for events occurring during transit, provided the transfer adhered to the strictures of § 400.12.\n\nFurthermore, the custody chain is not broken by the mere passage of time or distance but only by unauthorized intervention or the failure to secure the specimen against environmental hazards that could alter its fundamental nature. The Accords recognize that certain specimens possess inherent instabilities that require continuous monitoring; therefore, any transfer involving such entities must be accompanied by a supplemental agreement detailing the specific containment requirements and energy sources necessary to maintain their state. This requirement reinforces the mandate in § 1301.13 concerning liability for residue and lingering arcane echoes, ensuring that the burden of maintaining a specimen's stability does not shift unexpectedly to an unprepared institution. If a transfer is conducted without these supplemental safeguards, the receiving party may invoke the dispute resolution mechanisms referenced in § 400.12 to seek restitution or compensation, as detailed in the schedules found within § 400.11. Ultimately, the integrity of the research depends on the ability to trace every action back to a lawful origin, a principle that is as vital for living specimens as it is for static artifacts or enchanted objects traded under the laws of the Guild.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "400.12",
            "700.8",
            "400.2",
            "400.17",
            "800.9",
