            "1301.13",
            "400.11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Mandatory Sealing and Isolation Protocols for Living Specimens During Inter-Depa",
          "text": "The integrity of any magical study relies fundamentally upon the physical and metaphysical security of its constituent materials, particularly those biological or elemental entities classified as living specimens under the jurisdiction of Part 400. When a researcher is granted a permit pursuant to § 400.1 Research Permits and Supervised Experiments, the initial grant covers the scope of inquiry but does not implicitly authorize the movement of volatile subjects without specific adherence to containment protocols detailed in § 400.15 Oversight Protocols for Exceptional Research Permits and Supervised Experiments. Any transfer of a living specimen from one authorized laboratory or pocket dimension to another must be conducted under a sealed chain of custody that remains unbroken from the moment of extraction until final deposition, ensuring that no unauthorized casting occurs during transit. This requirement is not merely administrative but serves as a critical safeguard against the accidental release of hazardous forces, a concern explicitly addressed in § 200.13 Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation where liability for such breaches falls heavily upon the custodian failing to maintain proper seals.\n\nFurthermore, the legal standing of a specimen is contingent upon its ability to be positively identified at every juncture of its journey, a principle that mirrors the strict chain of title requirements found in § 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title regarding enchanted objects and artifacts. Just as an artifact lacking provenance cannot be lawfully traded without risking severe penalties under § 900.5 False Provenance, a living specimen transported without continuous verification of its identity constitutes a breach of research integrity mandated by § 400.12 Research Integrity and Oversight Protocols. The transition of custody between individuals requires the presence of at least one accredited observer or a remote scrying link approved by the Accords Desk to verify that the entity transferred matches the manifest description recorded in the primary ledger. Failure to establish this visual or magical confirmation invalidates the transfer, rendering the receiving party liable for any subsequent anomalies arising from the specimen's true nature, which may differ from its cataloged classification.\n\nIn instances where a specimen must pass through public spaces or transit hubs between secure facilities, the custodian must employ active dampening fields to prevent unauthorized interaction or resonance with ambient magic, thereby upholding the safety standards implied in § 1301.15 Catchline Title: Remediation Protocols for Ancillary Arcane Disruption regarding unforeseen hazardous effects of arcane phenomena. The burden of proof for demonstrating that a specimen remained secure and unaltered during transit rests upon the custodian initiating the transfer, as established under § 700.6 Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcan, which dictates that the party asserting a fact—in this case, the integrity of the transport—must provide sufficient evidence to satisfy the adjudicative body. If a specimen is found to have undergone unauthorized metamorphosis or has been influenced by external magical forces during transit, the investigating council will look first to the custodian's failure to maintain the required isolation protocols before considering claims of force majeure or accidental exposure.\n\nConsequently, any deviation from the prescribed sealing procedures, whether due to negligence or an attempt to conceal a specimen's true origin, may trigger sanctions ranging from the revocation of research privileges to formal disciplinary action under the penal provisions of § 800.8 800.9 Penalties for Unlawful Casting and Use of Arcane Artifacts. The Accords Desk maintains that the distinction between a permitted study and an unauthorized field casting is often blurred by inadequate containment measures during transport, effectively converting a sanctioned experiment into a rogue operation. Therefore, researchers must ensure that their logistics plans are reviewed and approved prior to any physical movement of living subjects, aligning their operational procedures with the rigorous oversight expectations set forth in § 1001.16 Catchline Title: Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Memb to prevent the leakage of sensitive research data or the escape of dangerous entities. Only by maintaining an unbroken chain of evidence and adhering to these stringent transfer mandates can the guild preserve the sanctity of its research endeavors while protecting the broader community from the unpredictable dangers inherent in manipulating life forces.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "400.1",
            "400.15",
            "200.13",
            "1101.2",
