        {
          "heading": "Mandatory Sealing and Isolation Protocols for Living Specimens During Inter-Depa — Continued",
          "text": "The integrity of the chain of custody is rendered null and void if a living specimen escapes containment during transit between the research facilities of the Mages' Guild. Under § 400.17, it is hereby mandated that any living entity, whether biological, elemental, or constructed of arcane matter, must be housed within a sealed stasis cell prior to the initiation of movement across Guild borders. This requirement supersedes all logistical conveniences; no research permit holder may utilize a pocket dimension for transport unless the specimen has been explicitly cleared for such transit under the strictures of § 400.12 Research Integrity and Oversight Protocols, which further details the ethical boundaries of handling sentient materials. The failure to secure a specimen within a certified stasis field before departure constitutes a breach of contract with the Guild and violates the foundational principles of containment established in § 200.13 Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation. Furthermore, any transport vehicle, be it a carriage, a floating skiff, or an arcane construct, must bear the official seal of the Archmage's Office, serving as immediate visual proof that the cargo is authorized and monitored, a practice reinforced by the evidentiary standards set forth in § 1101.2 Relics; Chain of Title regarding the provenance and lawful ownership of magical objects.\n\nIn the event that a specimen requires transfer between departments where the receiving facility lacks compatible stasis technology, the transporting party is obligated to maintain continuous active suppression fields until handover is physically complete. This obligation is not merely procedural but carries significant liability implications under § 1301.13 Liability for Residue and Lingering Arcane Echoes, as any damage caused by an unsealed or improperly isolated specimen during transit is deemed a direct result of the transporter's negligence. The burden of proving that a breach occurred due to external sabotage rather than custodial failure shall rest upon the accused party, adhering to the general evidentiary standards outlined in § 700.6 Burden of Proof and Standard of Evidence in Arcan. Moreover, if a specimen is found loose within a transport pocket dimension during an audit, such as those conducted under the supervision of the Peer Review Committee referenced in § 1.17 Additional Provisions for Mages' Guild Mediation and Peer Review Protocols, the entire chain of evidence associated with that research project shall be automatically flagged for re-examination. This automatic flagging is a necessary precaution to ensure that no unauthorized alterations or escapes have compromised the validity of the data collected, a principle that aligns with the strict evidentiary rules governing magical traces found in § 700.8 Admissibility of Magical Traces and Residues.\n\nThe Act of Transport must be accompanied by a sealed manifest detailing the containment class, the specific suppression spells active within the stasis field, and the identity of the authorized custodian. This manifest serves as a critical component of the chain of evidence, ensuring that if a dispute arises regarding the origin or condition of the specimen, the provenance can be traced back to its lawful source without ambiguity. Any deviation from the prescribed sealing protocols, including the use of makeshift containment devices or the relaxation of suppression fields due to perceived safety risks, shall be considered an act of gross misconduct under the Research Integrity and Oversight Protocols mandated by § 400.12. Consequently, such deviations will result in the immediate suspension of the research permit and potential forfeiture of any findings derived from the specimen until a full inquiry can determine if the data was obtained through lawful means. The Guild maintains that the safety of the public and the stability of the natural world are paramount, and thus, the rigorous application of these sealing and isolation mandates is not optional but an absolute requirement for all authorized arcane researchers operating within the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "400.17",
            "400.12",
            "200.13",
