    {
      "cite": "900.17",
      "part": "part_ix",
      "title": "Certification of Sub-Layered and Latent Arcane Signatures",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Certification of Sub-Layered and Latent Arcane Signatures",
          "text": "It is hereby provided that the integrity of any arcane construct, artifact, or field must be verified not merely by its overt function but by the comprehensive authentication of all sub-layered signatures and latent magical frequencies. Where an item bears a primary inscription or active enchantment, the examiner shall further investigate for residual signatures from previous owners, unauthorized alterations, or hidden sub-routines that may activate under specific conditions. This requirement applies to all transactions involving high-level artifacts, research prototypes, and constructs created under exceptional permits. The duty to certify these latent layers is distinct from the general disclosure of material facts; it requires a technical analysis of the magical structure itself to ensure no forbidden schools or destabilizing influences have been concealed within the code of the enchantment. Failure to disclose such sub-layered signatures shall constitute a violation of § 900.7 Duty to Disclose Material Facts, and may additionally trigger penalties under § 800.11 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation if the hidden layers involve prohibited energies or unauthorized alterations.\n\nAny certification conducted pursuant to this section must explicitly state the presence or absence of collateral taints and magical contagion that might compromise the safety of the user or the stability of the local arcane field. As noted in § 1101.11 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion, residual influences from previous creators are deemed part of an artifact's inherent risk profile unless fully disclosed and mitigated. A certificate issued without addressing these latent taints shall be deemed void ab initio, regardless of the overt functionality of the item. Furthermore, the certification process must align with the oversight protocols established for exceptional research permits under § 900.15 Oversight Protocols for Exceptional Research Permits and Supervised Experiments. Where an artifact originates from a supervised experiment, the certifying authority must confirm that all safety measures regarding sub-layered effects were strictly adhered to during its creation. Any deviation from these protocols invalidates the certification and subjects the creator to disciplinary action under § 900.12 Additional Provisions, which preserves the full force of all other provisions within the Accords.\n\nThe admissibility of a certificate regarding sub-layered signatures in any legal proceeding shall be governed by the standards set forth in § 700.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence. In disputes concerning the authenticity or safety of an enchanted item, the original certification documents must be produced as primary evidence. If a defendant claims that a latent sub-layer was unknown at the time of sale, the burden of proof shifts to them to demonstrate that such a layer existed independently of any prior unauthorized alteration. This standard ensures that the duty to certify extends beyond mere surface-level inspection into a deep structural analysis of the magical composition. Such rigorous standards are necessary to maintain public trust and prevent the proliferation of dangerous constructs that operate with hidden agendas or unstable frequencies.\n\nMoreover, this section operates in conjunction with the foundational provisions regarding the provenance and authenticity of all magical items under § 900.8 Provenance and Authenticity. The title and history of an item are insufficient to establish its safety; the internal architecture of its magic must also be clear of illicit additions. Where an item is found to harbor sub-layered effects that violate the restrictions on forbidden schools or unauthorized energy sources, the item shall be subject to immediate seizure and remediation in accordance with § 800.12 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation. The authorities retain the right to dismantle or neutralize such constructs if the risk of activation outweighs any potential benefit to the public. This preventative approach is consistent with the mandate for research integrity found in § 400.12 Research Integrity and Oversight Protocols, which emphasizes that the safety of the arcane field must never be compromised by negligence or concealment.\n\nFinally, it is mandated that all practitioners engaged in the creation or modification of high-level artifacts shall maintain a log of every sub-layer added or removed during their work. These logs must be accessible to the Guild's High Council upon request and are subject to random audits to ensure compliance with the certification requirements. Where a practitioner fails to maintain accurate records of sub-layered modifications, they shall be liable for any damages resulting from the activation of hidden effects. This strict liability is intended to discourage the practice of hiding complex magical structures within simple-looking constructs. By requiring full transparency into the deepest layers of an enchantment, the Accords ensure that no magical deception can undermine the safety and order of the realm. Any act of omission in this regard shall be treated as a material violation subject to the full range of sanctions available under these Accords.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "900.7",
