          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Procedural Standards for the Stratigraphic Analysis of Composite Thaumaturgy",
          "text": "The certification process mandated by § 900.17 regarding sub-layered and latent arcane signatures is not merely a formality of record-keeping but a rigorous judicial determination of intent, complexity, and risk inherent in the magical construct. When an archmage or authorized operative submits a petition for certification of a complex spell structure, the Accords Desk shall require a detailed stratigraphic map detailing every layer of activation energy, from the foundational binding to the most ephemeral, sub-layered effects. This requirement is absolute; under § 900.14, any ancillary effects or sub-layered magic that arise from such a composition must be explicitly reported and catalogued before the primary signature can be deemed valid. A petition lacking this granular breakdown of latent signatures shall be dismissed immediately, as the Desk cannot assess the safety profile of a spell whose deepest layers remain obscured by the practitioner's own ambiguity. See § 1.4 Persons Bound to ensure that the individual seeking certification possesses the requisite thaumaturgical influence to warrant such scrutiny; those with lesser standing are prohibited from attempting to certify multi-layered constructs without prior approval from the Conservators.\n\nFurthermore, the burden of proof regarding the stability of latent signatures rests entirely on the petitioner, a principle consistent with § 700.11 Burden Shifting and Presumptions in Arcane Litigation where the presumption of safety does not apply to high-complexity magic. If a certification is granted for a sub-layered signature, the Accords Desk retains the authority to demand a re-analysis should an unforeseen ancillary effect manifest, as noted in § 900.6 Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic where the obligations imposed by this Part are cumulative and do not exhaust the scope of regulatory oversight applicable to magical practice. The petitioner must demonstrate that all latent signatures have been tested against scenarios involving environmental variance, such as the unpredictable weather patterns described in § 500.2 Unscheduled Weather, which can destabilize sub-layered protections if they were not designed with redundancy. Failure to account for these variables constitutes a material misrepresentation of the spell's capabilities and may result in immediate revocation of the certification under the powers granted by the Paradox Trial.\n\nThe integrity of the certification also extends to the identity of the entity operating within the layered structure. Any construct, whether woven from clay and sigil or birthed from the ethereal essence of the Weave, must be listed as a distinct actor within the sub-layered analysis in accordance with § 600.4 Construct Labor; Minimum Ward. The certification document must specify how each sub-layer interacts with these constructs, ensuring that no latent signature imposes labor protections violations or creates unregulated hazards for the construct's operators. This level of detail is necessary because, as stated in § 1201.7 Section 1201 – Portal and Planar Regulations, the provisions apply with full force to all persons subject to the jurisdiction, whether resident within the physical boundaries of the Hall or operating from extradimensional spaces where latent signatures may bleed into adjacent realities. The Desk shall not certify any signature that relies on unstable planar anchors without explicit documentation of the seal mechanisms required to prevent catastrophic leakage.\n\nIn cases where the sub-layered magic involves forbidden schools or restricted research, the certification process becomes a matter of state interest rather than mere guild administration. Under § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild, only those individuals explicitly vested with the right of speech by Archmage Veyra may petition for such certifications on behalf of the collective, ensuring that the political spectrum between the Conservators and Innovators does not compromise safety standards. The certification shall also include a clause addressing the potential for paradoxical outcomes, referencing § 800.2 Paradox Trial Procedure which serves as the primary judicial mechanism for high-level disputes arising from such certifications. If the latent signatures reveal a propensity to alter local reality in ways that contradict established physical laws, the petition may be referred directly to the Paradox Trial for adjudication rather than simple administrative review.\n\nFinally, any certification of sub-layered and latent arcane signatures must include a permanent seal indicating its date of issuance, the name of the certifying council, and the specific limitations placed upon its use. This seal serves as a binding agreement that the signatory agrees to disclose all ancillary magic should the structure be modified or expanded in the future. Without such an agreement, the certification is void ab initio, and any attempt to utilize the signature without full disclosure will be treated as an act of unauthorized practice under the strictest interpretation of the Accords. The Desk shall maintain a registry of all certified sub-layered signatures, cross-referenced with reports filed under § 900.13 Reporting of Ancillary and Sub-Layered Arcane Effects to ensure that no latent danger goes unreported. This comprehensive approach ensures that the Mages' Guild remains vigilant against the subtle dangers hidden within the most intricate workings of arcane science, preserving the safety of the Midlands jurisdiction against both known and unknown thaumaturgical threats.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "900.17",
            "900.14",
