            "800.2",
            "800.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Verification of Stratum Integrity and Temporal Dissonance in Composite Signature",
          "text": "The certification process mandated by § 900.17 extends beyond mere identification; it requires a rigorous assessment of the structural integrity inherent to sub-layered arcane signatures. When a composite thaumaturgical construct is submitted for review, the examining officer must first determine if the constituent layers exhibit signs of temporal dissonance or causal inversion, conditions often precipitated by unauthorized interference with the foundational weave. A signature deemed stable under § 900.17 is one where the primary intent remains causally prior to its secondary effects, ensuring that the latent magic does not act as a precursor to the overt spell rather than a consequence thereof. Failure to account for this inversion renders the certification void, and such defects must be reported immediately in accordance with § 900.13 regarding the reporting of ancillary and sub-layered arcane effects. Furthermore, any construct found to possess a latent layer that actively undermines the stability of the primary sigil shall be subject to immediate containment protocols under the authority granted by § 1201.7 for planar regulations, as unstable composites often manifest spatial anomalies indistinguishable from unauthorized portal activity.\n\nThe procedural standards for excising or analyzing these layers require the application of a stratigraphic lens, a specialized diagnostic tool designed to isolate specific thaumaturgical frequencies without triggering the dormant mechanisms within the composite. This analysis is not merely observational but interpretive, requiring the certifier to reconstruct the sequence of casting intent versus execution variance. If the reconstruction reveals that a latent layer was introduced through coercion or manipulation of a minor construct—such as a clay sigil-woven entity—the certifier must invoke § 600.4 regarding construct labor protections to ensure that no sentient or semi-sentient component within the sub-layer was exploited against its will during the composite's formation. Such exploitation is a violation of the fundamental principles governing magical practice and may elevate the offense from a regulatory infraction to a criminal matter triable under § 800.2 for paradox trial procedure, particularly if the manipulation involved high-level thaumaturgical influence affecting the Midlands jurisdiction.\n\nMoreover, the certification document itself must explicitly state whether the latent signatures are dormant or actively fluctuating, as this distinction dictates the level of monitoring required post-certification. Under § 900.6, the disclosure and certification of ancillary magic mandates that any active fluctuation in a sub-layered signature be treated with the same urgency as a direct violation of the Accords, regardless of the intent of the original caster. The certifier must also cross-reference the source authority; if the composite signature was generated by an entity acting under duress or within a compromised state of mind, the certification carries a provisional status until further inquiry is conducted by the Paradox Trial council as outlined in § 800.3 regarding the duration and review of sanctions. It is imperative that the certification does not inadvertently validate a magical construct that relies on the suppression of free will or the distortion of memory, as these are hallmarks of the forbidden schools often associated with necromancy and blood magic under § 1201.7.\n\nFinally, the legal presumption regarding sub-layered signatures operates such that any ambiguity in the strata is interpreted strictly against the holder of the composite artifact. This burden of clarity ensures that hidden dangers within a magical construct are not concealed behind layers of obfuscation designed to evade detection. Should the analysis reveal that the latent layer serves as a failsafe for catastrophic release or if it contains a paradox loop capable of collapsing local reality, the certifier is obligated to initiate an emergency containment protocol immediately. This obligation supersedes any claims of proprietary interest or research privilege, reinforcing the Guild's stance that safety and stability within the Midlands take precedence over arcane innovation. As noted in § 100.2, only those explicitly vested with the right of speech by Archmage Veyra may issue a final certification on such matters, ensuring that the determination of sub-layered integrity remains a function of high-ranking authority rather than bureaucratic convenience. Any attempt to bypass these stratigraphic requirements will result in the automatic revocation of the license and potential referral to the Pyre of Judgment for severe infractions, though the Autumnwood Accords generally prefer rehabilitation through the Paradox Trial unless the threat to the Weave is existential.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "900.17",
            "900.13",
            "1201.7",
            "600.4",
            "800.2",
            "900.6",
            "800.3",
            "100.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Protocol for the Stratum Integrity Verification and Temporal Dissonance Assessme",
          "text": "When a mage seeks certification under this section for a signature exhibiting sub-layered or latent properties, the primary obligation rests upon establishing the integrity of the lower strata prior to any attempt at disclosure. Under § 900.6 Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic, it is established that the obligations imposed by this Part are cumulative; however, the specific mechanics of verifying that a foundational layer has not been corrupted by temporal dissonance require adherence to stricter procedural standards than those applicable to singular casting events. The Autumnwood Accords recognize that the use of magic is not always straightforward or singular in its impact (§ 900.14 Reporting Ancillary Effects and Sub-Layered Magic), particularly when a primary sigil masks a secondary, latent function intended for activation only under specific conditions such as high atmospheric pressure or the presence of specific celestial alignments. Consequently, the certification process must include a mandatory temporal dissonance assessment to ensure that the latent layer does not reactivate in a manner inconsistent with the original intent of the caster. This assessment is not merely a formality but a critical safety measure, as the reactivation of a dormant sub-layer can introduce chaotic variables into the magical ecosystem, potentially leading to unforeseen consequences for bystanders or even the structural integrity of the surrounding environment.\n\nThe procedural standards for this verification mandate the presence of an independent third-party auditor, often referred to as a Stratigraphic Witness, who must possess authorization from the Guild Council before commencing any analysis of the composite signature. This requirement aligns with § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild, ensuring that only those explicitly vested with the right of speech by Archmage Veyra or the presiding authority may interpret the results of such complex analyses. The Stratigraphic Witness shall employ specialized scrying techniques to peer beneath the surface signature without altering its current state, a process distinct from the intrusive methods used in standard evidence gathering. Under § 700.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence, any attempt to modify the signature during the verification phase is strictly prohibited unless an emergency exception is granted by the Paradox Trial; such exceptions are rare and typically reserved for cases where the latent layer poses an immediate threat of spontaneous combustion or reality fracture. Furthermore, the witness must document the temporal stability of the lower strata, noting any signs of drift that might suggest the signature was originally cast from a different epoch than its current location in the Weave.\n\nIf the verification reveals significant temporal dissonance, where the latent layer appears to originate from a divergent timeline or possesses properties incompatible with the current magical consensus, the certification application shall be immediately suspended pursuant to § 1201.7 Section 1201 – Portal and Planar Regulations. The provisions of this Part apply with full force to all persons subject to the jurisdiction of the Autumnwood Accords, whether resident within or outside the physical boundaries of the Guild's Hall, and the presence of a temporally unstable signature constitutes a violation of that jurisdictional boundary. In such instances, the applicant must submit a detailed report explaining the origin of the dissonance and propose a remediation plan that involves either the stabilization of the temporal anchor or the complete excision of the latent layer. Failure to provide satisfactory documentation may result in the initiation of proceedings under § 800.2 Paradox Trial Procedure, where the Paradox Trial shall serve as the primary judicial mechanism for the resolution of high-level arcane disputes and the adjudication of severe violations of the Autumnwood Accords. The burden rests heavily on the applicant to demonstrate that the latent layer poses no risk to the public or the fabric of reality, a standard that is often difficult to meet when dealing with signatures that exhibit signs of corruption from the Shadowfell or other extraplanar sources.\n\nMoreover, the certification process must account for the potential interaction between the sub-layered signature and constructs or artificial intelligences that may be employed in its activation. Under § 600.4 Construct Labor; Minimum Ward, all constructs, regardless of their method of manufacture, are subject to the labor protections of the Autumnwood Accords, and a composite signature that inadvertently enslaves or manipulates a construct without proper consent is deemed an act of exploitation rather than lawful magic. The Stratigraphic Witness must therefore evaluate whether the latent layer relies on such non-consensual manipulation and, if so, recommend appropriate sanctions or modifications to the signature structure. This evaluation is crucial in maintaining the ethical standards of the Guild and preventing the rise of new forms of magical tyranny that could destabilize the political landscape of the Midlands.\n\nFinally, the certification document issued upon successful verification must include a clear statement regarding the temporal integrity of the signature and any limitations on its use based on the findings of the dissonance assessment. This document serves as a permanent record of the signature's status and is subject to review by the Aegis Magi under their continuing authority. As noted in § 900.6 Disclosure and Certification of Ancillary Magic, nothing herein shall be construed as limiting the authority of the Aegis Magi under the Accords, and they retain the right to revoke certification if subsequent events reveal that the initial verification was flawed or incomplete. The integrity of the magical landscape depends upon the rigorous application of these standards, ensuring that every sub-layered signature is brought into full compliance with the laws governing its existence before it is released into the world at large.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "900.6",
            "900.14",
            "100.2",
            "700.12",
            "1201.7",
            "800.2",
            "600.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Stratum-Displacement Liability and the Burden of Unintended Temporal Anchors",
          "text": "The certification process mandated under § 900.17 is not merely an administrative formality but a rigorous judicial prerequisite designed to mitigate the catastrophic risks inherent in stratigraphic magic. When a mage performs an operation intended to layer new thaumaturgical signatures over existing magical structures—be it a residential ward, a fortress gate, or a pocket dimension interface—the resulting composite signature must be subjected to a mandatory integrity scan before any public disclosure is permitted. This requirement exists because, as noted in the foundational provisions regarding persons bound by these Accords (§ 1.4), the mere presence of an entity within a jurisdiction creates a liability that extends to the stability of the environment they inhabit. A failure to properly certify the interaction between a sub-layered signature and its latent parent structure can result in temporal dissonance, where the physical reality of the Midgrual mountains or the Regal Capital begins to flicker between conflicting versions of itself. Therefore, any practitioner found violating the protocols for stratigraphic analysis shall be subject to immediate suspension of their license and a mandatory review by the Aegis Magi under § 900.6, which establishes the overarching duty to disclose all ancillary magic regardless of intent.\n\nThe procedural standards for this certification are distinct from standard magical audits because they require a specific understanding of how latent effects migrate across temporal strata. Under § 900.14, the Reporting of Ancillary Effects and Sub-Layered Magic, practitioners are already obligated to report any unforeseen consequences of their work; however, § 900.17 adds a layer of complexity by demanding that the *potential* for such effects be predicted and mitigated prior to the final sealing of a signature. This is particularly critical in cases involving constructs or artificial intelligences, where the labor protections outlined in § 600.4 regarding Construct Labor must be balanced against the risk of a construct's internal logic conflicting with the host world's fundamental laws. If a sub-layered signature causes a construct to perceive time linearly while the host reality is experiencing a paradox loop, this constitutes a breach of the Stratum Integrity Verification requirements. Consequently, any certification document submitted to the Accords Desk must include a detailed mathematical and thaumaturgical model demonstrating that no latent anchor points have been created which could destabilize the local timeline.\n\nFurthermore, the authority vested in the Archmage Veyra to regulate who may speak for the Guild (§ 100.2) applies with equal force to the enforcement of these certification standards. No unauthorized individual, whether a rogue mage from the Planar Sanctum or an agent of the Edicts of the Flame seeking to purge \"unnatural\" phenomena, may claim that a signature has been properly certified without first undergoing the full stratigraphic excavation protocol. The recent incidents involving the Estragon Island Incident and the collapse of the library at the Planar Sanctum serve as grim reminders that ignoring the integrity of composite signatures can lead to reality-shattering outcomes that no amount of post-hoc scrying can fix. As such, any attempt to bypass these certification requirements by claiming that a signature is \"too subtle\" or \"too latent\" to require review will be rejected outright under § 700.2, which governs the limitations of scrying closed books when it comes to verifying structural integrity. The law is clear: if the layers are not visible, they must be proven stable through empirical testing, not theoretical dismissal.\n\nFinally, the penal consequences for failing to adhere to these certification standards are severe and cumulative. Unlike minor infractions that might result in a fine or community service, violations of § 900.17 regarding sub-layered signatures often trigger the Paradox Trial (§ 800.2) because they threaten the very fabric of existence within the Midlands jurisdiction. If a mage certifies a signature that later manifests as a temporal anchor pulling the city into a pocket dimension, the presiding council of the Paradox Trial will likely impose a Seal of Silence (§ 800.3) lasting for multiple centuries to prevent further leakage of reality. This is not merely punishment but a necessary measure to contain the damage caused by the initial failure of certification. The Accords Desk retains full authority under § 1201.7 to enforce these regulations even on those operating outside the physical boundaries of the Guild's Hall, ensuring that no mage can hide behind the veil of distance or planar displacement to avoid accountability. Ultimately, the safety of the realm depends on the rigorous application of these standards, and any deviation from them will be met with swift and decisive action by the Mages' Guild.",
