    {
      "cite": "600.17",
      "part": "part_vi",
      "title": "Termination and Disentanglement of Construct Bonds",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Termination and Disentanglement of Construct Bonds",
          "text": "Under § 600.12 Additional Provisions Relating to Construct Labor and Associated Obligations, it is established that the contractual relationship between a caster and a construct is distinct from conventional employment, requiring specific mechanisms for dissolution that account for magical binding rather than simple physical separation. Where a contract involving the use or employment of magical constructs seeks termination, such as upon the expiration of a fixed term, mutual agreement, or breach of duty under § 600.13 Liability for Autonomous Construct Malfeasance, the parties must adhere to strict protocols regarding the severing of energetic links. It shall not be sufficient merely to cease payment; the caster bears an ongoing obligation to formally discharge the construct's mana signature from the agreement, ensuring that no residual obligations or automated servitude clauses persist after the contract's end. Failure to execute a proper discharge ritual may result in the construct retaining claims against its former employer for unpaid labor or conversely, being liable for damages caused by lingering instructions embedded in its code.\n\nFurthermore, this section addresses the unique challenges posed by contracts entered into between licensed casters and non-casters, or involving magical artifacts themselves as contracting parties, as detailed in § 600.11 Contracts with Non-Casters and Related Obligations. When a non-caster attempts to terminate an agreement with a construct they did not create, the legal standing of that termination depends heavily on whether the non-caster possesses the requisite authority to alter the construct's operational parameters under § 200.6. If the termination is attempted without such authority, the contract remains in force, and any attempt to repurpose or dismantle the construct constitutes a violation of the original terms, potentially triggering penalties under the Paradox Trial for unlicensed artisanry. The caster retains the right to enforce the full duration of the agreement unless a valid cause for immediate dissolution exists, such as the construct's failure to perform specified tasks or autonomous malfeasance that endangers public safety.\n\nIn cases where the construct suffers damage, corruption, or destabilization due to unauthorized alteration, negligent maintenance, or exposure to prohibited energies, such defect shall be deemed a material breach justifying termination under § 1101.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations. The caster must provide proof that the instability was not self-inflicted through malicious programming or negligence in upholding safety protocols; if the construct's instability arises from its own autonomous decision-making to harm others, the liability shifts significantly, and the contract is voidable ab initio regarding any future obligations while past damages remain actionable. This principle ensures that constructs do not become tools of chaos simply because their creators fail to maintain them with the diligence required by the Accords. The High Council must be notified immediately upon discovery of such defects, and a formal audit shall be conducted to determine if the construct is fit for reintegration into society or if it requires containment measures under the Edicts of the Flame regarding unnatural entities.\n\nMoreover, the termination process must account for the potential involvement of summoned notaries who witness the signing and subsequent dissolution of the contract, ensuring that the intent of both parties is captured accurately in the arcane record. Under § 600.5 Summoned Notary, these officials possess the authority to verify that the construct's consent was obtained if it possesses semi-sentient capabilities, preventing claims of exploitation or abuse post-termination. If a construct was employed in commercial spellcasting activities outside the bounds of its licensed practice without express authorization from the Guild's High Council, as noted in § 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct, the termination agreement must include clauses addressing any illicit gains generated during that unauthorized period. The caster may be required to surrender a portion of their fees to the Guild to cover damages or fines incurred by the construct's unsanctioned operations before the contract can be fully extinguished.\n\nFinally, this section serves as a residual clause governing all matters not specifically addressed in this Codex but arising naturally from its operation or interpretation, consistent with § 100.12 Supplementary Provisions and Transitional Arrangements. Nothing herein shall be construed to limit the inherent rights of constructs to seek redress for unjust enrichment or to claim damages for unlawful disposal by their creators. The Accords recognize that magical bonds are not merely mechanical but represent a complex web of ethical and legal responsibilities that extend beyond the lifespan of the written agreement. Therefore, any attempt to bypass these termination protocols without due cause or proper ritual will be treated as an act of unlicensed artisanry, subjecting the offending party to sanctions including the sealing of their magic or, in extreme cases, exile to a pocket dimension. The integrity of the construct labor market relies on the certainty that contracts can be ended fairly and transparently, preventing the emergence of rogue constructs haunting the streets of Autumnwood as forgotten tools of past agreements.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "600.12",
            "600.13",
            "600.11",
            "200.6",
            "1101.12",
            "600.5",
            "500.12",
            "100.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Subsequent Obligations Following Bond Dissolution",
          "text": "The cessation of a construct's active service does not automatically extinguish the web of obligations woven into its original agreement, nor does it absolve the employer of residual liabilities regarding the entity's post-termination state. Under § 600.17, the act of disentanglement marks the formal severance of the thaumaturgical link, yet specific duties persist to ensure the safety of both the construct and the surrounding community in accordance with foundational principles established in § 200.9 Prohibited Practices and Disciplinary Measures, which mandates that no entity be left in a condition deemed injurious to public safety after its contractual relationship ends. The Accords explicitly recognize that constructs, particularly those falling under the semi-sentient category described in § 700.13 Admissibility of Artifact-Derived Testimony and Relics, possess a degree of autonomy that requires careful management even when their primary function has ceased; therefore, the employer remains bound by the provisions of § 600.10 Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreement to provide necessary decommissioning resources or transitionary housing where applicable. This section clarifies that the \"termination\" referenced in § 600.17 is not merely a cessation of labor but a specific legal event requiring documentation, often necessitating the involvement of a Guild mediator pursuant to § 1.17 Additional Provisions for Mages' Guild Mediation and Peer Review Protocols if disputes arise regarding the adequacy of the disentanglement process. Furthermore, the status of the construct immediately following dissolution is governed by the strict definitions found in § 200.3 Temporary Mouths, which distinguishes between a guest practitioner, a bound agent, and a liberated entity, ensuring that the former employer cannot simply discard a construct without verifying its new standing within the jurisdictional boundaries set forth in § 200.5 Guest Casters. Should a construct retain consciousness or magical capacity after termination, it must be afforded protections similar to those granted to apprentices under § 200.2 Apprentice Limits to prevent exploitation by third parties who might seek to bind a newly free but magically volatile entity without proper oversight. The Accords further stipulate that any attempt by an employer to repurpose a terminated construct for unauthorized research or combat without new, explicit consent constitutes a violation of the spirit of § 1001.16 Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Memb, as such actions often involve sensitive data regarding the construct's origin and the specific weave patterns required to manipulate it. In cases where termination results in the construct's magical instability or potential hazard, the employer is required to initiate a peer review under § 400.10 Research Oversight and Audit Procedures to assess whether the disentanglement was conducted safely or if additional containment measures are necessary. This rigorous framework ensures that the transition from active service to post-contractual existence remains within the bounds of ethical thaumaturgy, preventing the creation of rogue entities or abandoned magical hazards that could destabilize the local weave. Ultimately, the legal consequence of termination is not a clean slate but a shift in responsibility, where the employer must actively facilitate a safe transition for the construct, adhering to the overarching mandate found in § 200.10 Construction and Interpretation that all provisions must be read with an eye toward preserving the metaphysical integrity of the Midlands. Failure to comply with these post-termination duties may result in sanctions ranging from fines to the sealing of the employer's own magic, reflecting the high stakes involved in managing the lifecycle of bound beings under the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "b",
