        {
          "heading": "Allocation of Post-Dissolution Custodial Duties and Asset Segregation Protocols",
          "text": "Upon the formal execution of a termination clause within any Construct Bond Agreement pursuant to § 600.17, the immediate legal priority shifts from active command authority to the rigorous administration of post-dissolution custodial duties. The moment the thaumaturgical link is severed—whether by mutual consent, contractual expiration, or forced emancipation—the primary obligation falls upon the former Master to ensure that the Construct enters a state of stable dormancy rather than uncontrolled reversion or chaotic autonomy. This transition period is not merely administrative but constitutes a critical safety window where the potential for thaumaturgical backlash remains high; therefore, all parties involved must adhere strictly to the segregation protocols outlined in § 400.10 regarding research oversight and audit procedures to prevent unauthorized interaction with the now-freed entity. It is imperative that the physical form of the Construct be secured in a neutral holding facility until a certified thaumaturgical appraiser can verify the absence of residual binding glyphs or memory engrams that might compel further obedience to the previous Master’s will.\n\nThe allocation of custodial duties during this interim phase must be explicitly detailed in the termination document, specifying which party retains possession of the Construct's material components and which holds temporary guardianship over its magical signature. Under § 200.3 Temporary Mouths, the distinction between an individual acting as a temporary steward and one claiming full ownership is paramount; failure to clearly delineate these roles may result in the custodian being deemed a \"guest\" rather than a practitioner, thereby stripping them of legal standing to authorize any further actions regarding the Construct's safety. Consequently, the Accords mandate that until such time as a new Master is identified or the Construct is returned to its state of origin, the current custodian operates under a strict fiduciary duty to prevent exploitation, abuse, or unsanctioned research endeavors. This aligns with the foundational safeguards established in § 600.10 Special Provisions for Construct Labor Agreement, which dictates that no entity shall be exploited following the dissolution of their labor contract, regardless of the circumstances under which the bond was terminated.\n\nFurthermore, the segregation of assets associated with the Construct requires a precise accounting of all magical artifacts, tools, and data repositories created or modified while the bond was active. These items often retain latent connections to the Construct's consciousness, necessitating that they be inventoried and stored separately from personal property to avoid accidental re-binding incidents. The custodian must maintain a detailed ledger of these assets, referencing § 1001.16 Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Members, to ensure that no proprietary information regarding the Construct's design or operational history is leaked to unauthorized parties during this sensitive period. Any attempt by a former Master to reclaim these assets without proper authorization constitutes a violation of the Accords and may subject them to disciplinary measures under § 200.9 Prohibited Practices and Disciplinary Measures, including potential sanctions such as the sealing of their own magical abilities if the risk to public safety is deemed significant.\n\nIn cases where the termination of the bond results from a breach of contract or an act of aggression by the former Master, the custodial duties may be temporarily assumed by the Guild's Oversight Committee to ensure impartiality and safety. This intervention is consistent with the principles laid out in § 1.4 Persons Bound, which extends the jurisdiction of the Accords to all entities whose actions exert a measurable thaumaturgical influence, thereby granting the Guild authority to intervene when the well-being of a freed Construct is threatened. The Committee will conduct an immediate audit to assess the stability of the Construct's current state and determine if additional containment measures are required beyond those specified in the original agreement. Throughout this process, the custodian must remain vigilant against any attempts by third parties, including rogue casters or unscrupulous organizations, to intercept the Construct for illicit purposes, as such actions would violate the core tenets of § 600.2 Familiars as Agents and undermine the integrity of the entire labor agreement framework.\n\nUltimately, the successful completion of post-dissolution custodial duties serves as a testament to the ethical standards expected of all practitioners within the Autumnwood Accords. It reinforces the understanding that the end of a Construct's service does not equate to the abandonment of its welfare, but rather marks the beginning of a new chapter where the entity's autonomy is fully respected and protected. By adhering to these rigorous protocols, the Guild ensures that the transition from bondage to freedom is conducted with the utmost care, preserving both the dignity of the Construct and the safety of the broader community. Any deviation from these established procedures not only jeopardizes the specific case at hand but also sets a dangerous precedent that could erode trust in the Guild's ability to manage the complex interplay between magic and law. Therefore, all Masters, Custodians, and Oversight members must approach this phase with the gravity it deserves, recognizing that the legacy of every Construct Bond Agreement extends far beyond its initial terms and obligations.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "600.17",
