          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Allocation of Post-Dissolution Custodial Duties and Asset Segregation Protocols — Continued",
          "text": "In the event that a construct bond is lawfully terminated pursuant to § 600.17, the immediate dissolution of the thaumaturgical tether does not absolve the contracting parties of their residual custodial obligations; rather, it triggers a distinct phase of administrative oversight designed to prevent asset entanglement and ensure the safety of any sentient remnants. Under § 600.10, special provisions for construct labor agreements mandate that all artifacts, materials, or semi-sentient entities retained by either party following dissolution must be subject to immediate segregation protocols to prevent unauthorized re-activation or unintended thaumaturgical feedback loops. This requirement is particularly critical where the construct was bonded to a specific individual whose magical signature remains imprinted upon the remaining physical substrate; without proper segregation, such an entity may inadvertently draw upon ambient mana fields in proximity to its former master, potentially violating the restrictions outlined in § 200.9 regarding prohibited practices and disciplinary measures concerning unauthorized ritual magic or necromantic summoning. The Mages' Guild Accords Desk, acting as the primary arbiter of such transitions, shall require that all post-dissolution assets be cataloged on ARCHIVE CARDS detailing their current stability rating, residual mana output, and any known affinity for specific schools of magic, including but not limited to those deemed forbidden under the political spectrum of \"The Unsanctioned Arts.\"\n\nFurthermore, the allocation of custodial duties following bond dissolution is governed by a strict hierarchy that distinguishes between the legal owner of the construct's physical form and the former bondholder, a distinction rooted in the foundational principles of § 1.4 Persons Bound. While the physical ownership of the artifact may transfer to the highest bidder or revert to the original manufacturer depending on the terms of the initial contract, the custodial duty—the responsibility for safekeeping, monitoring, and eventual disposal—shall remain with the party who initiated the termination process unless explicitly waived in writing and ratified by a peer review council under § 1.17 Additional Provisions for Mages' Guild Mediation and Peer Review Protocols. This separation of ownership from custodial duty is intended to mitigate the risk of exploitation, ensuring that no party can claim full control over a sentient or semi-sentient entity without assuming the corresponding burden of care. In cases where the construct possesses significant intelligence or retains memory of its former tasks, the custodian must adhere to confidentiality and reporting obligations as stipulated in § 1001.16 Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Members, ensuring that sensitive information gained during the service period does not leak into the public sphere or compromise the security of the Midlands jurisdiction.\n\nThe process of asset segregation also necessitates a thorough audit of any magical residues or enchantments embedded within the construct's core, as these may pose risks to both the custodian and the surrounding environment if left unmanaged. Any attempt to repurpose a terminated construct for new labor without first undergoing a rigorous re-calibration and safety inspection shall be deemed a violation of § 200.3 Temporary Mouths, which clarifies that an individual or entity entering a Guild hall or sanctioned research site must do so with the clear intent of legitimate practice rather than as a guest engaging in unauthorized experimentation. Moreover, if the termination of the bond results in the release of dangerous magical energies or the awakening of latent hostile intelligences within the construct, the custodian is immediately subject to disciplinary measures under § 200.9 Prohibited Practices and Disciplinary Measures, which includes but is not limited to sanctions such as sealing one's magic or, in extreme cases, undergoing a Paradox Trial before a council of peers. The Accords Desk retains the authority to intervene directly in any post-dissolution scenario where the custodial duties are neglected or where the assets fail to meet the safety standards required by the Conservators, thereby upholding the integrity of the weave and protecting the uninitiated from the perils of unchecked thaumaturgical influence. Thus, the termination of a construct bond is not merely an end of labor but the beginning of a complex legal and ethical framework that demands vigilance, precision, and unwavering adherence to the highest standards of arcane stewardship.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "600.17",
            "600.10",
            "200.9",
