          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Post-Dissolution Custodial Duties and Asset Segregation Protocols — Continued",
          "text": "Upon the formal severance of a Construct Bond pursuant to § 600.17, the immediate legal obligation shifts from active maintenance to the rigorous segregation of all assets, data streams, and residual thaumaturgical signatures associated with the dissolved entity. This phase, often referred to as the \"Cool-Down Period,\" is not merely a procedural formality but a critical safety mechanism designed to prevent accidental reactivation or unauthorized access by third parties who may have retained partial knowledge of the binding rituals. Under § 200.10, construction and interpretation of these post-dissolution clauses must prioritize the containment of any lingering magical inertia, ensuring that the Construct does not revert to a dormant but unstable state where it might inadvertently harm its former master or environment. The custodial duties assigned to the primary handler during this interval are absolute; they must physically isolate the Construct in a designated holding cell compatible with its original containment fields, as defined under § 600.14, while simultaneously initiating a comprehensive audit of all digital and analog records generated during the tenure of the bond. This includes, but is not limited to, the segregation of any proprietary algorithms, memory banks, or enchanted tools that were integrated into the Construct’s operational framework before termination.\n\nThe allocation of custodial responsibility following dissolution is strictly hierarchical and cannot be delegated without explicit written consent from the Mages' Guild Oversight Committee, a requirement grounded in the foundational principles of § 1.4 Persons Bound regarding liability assignment. If the original caster has been terminated due to violations under § 200.9 Prohibited Practices and Disciplinary Measures, the custodial duties automatically revert to the Guild’s Emergency Containment Division until a new custodian is vetted and approved. This transition period is governed by the same rigorous standards applied to temporary mouths under § 200.3, where the distinction between a guest observing the process and a practitioner actively managing the segregation determines the scope of legal authority required. Furthermore, any attempt to bypass these asset segregation protocols constitutes a breach of contract that triggers the provisions outlined in § 600.12 Additional Provisions Relating to Construct Labor and Associated Obligations, specifically those clauses detailing financial restitution for damages incurred during improper handling. It is imperative that all assets be cataloged and sealed within forty-eight hours of bond dissolution; failure to do so may result in the imposition of a Paradox Trial under the penal system described in § 1.17 Additional Provisions for Mages' Guild Mediation and Peer Review Protocols, where peers will adjudicate the severity of the negligence based on the potential for catastrophic magical feedback.\n\nIn instances where the Construct possesses independent cognitive functions or retains memory fragments that could be exploited by hostile entities, the custodial duties must include active monitoring to ensure no unauthorized data exfiltration occurs. This monitoring is subject to the confidentiality requirements set forth in § 1001.16 Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Members, ensuring that sensitive information regarding the Construct’s former operations remains protected from public dissemination or rival factions such as The Edicts of the Flame, who might seek to purge the artifact under their doctrine of The Pyre of Judgment. Additionally, the segregation process must account for any artifacts recovered during the dissolution event, governed by the evidentiary standards in § 700.13 Admissibility of Artifact-Derived Testimony and Relics, which dictates how such items can be stored, analyzed, or destroyed without violating the sanctity of the magical weave. The custodian must also file a detailed report with the Accords Desk within three business days, detailing the condition of all assets and confirming that no residual enchantments remain active; this report serves as the primary legal defense against any future claims of liability arising from the Construct’s behavior post-termination.\n\nFinally, the allocation of these duties extends to the financial restitution mechanisms required when asset damage occurs during the Cool-Down Period. Under § 400.10 Research Oversight and Audit Procedures, any deviation from the standard segregation protocol must be logged and audited by an independent third party to ensure compliance with safety standards previously established for laboratory environments. The custodian is personally liable for any negligence that results in the escape of hazardous magical elements or the corruption of adjacent magical fields, a liability that cannot be waived by contract clauses attempting to limit responsibility below the statutory minimum. This strict liability framework ensures that the transition from active service to inert storage is managed with the same care and precision demanded of active research, thereby upholding the integrity of the Accords and preventing the kind of unchecked magical proliferation that could lead to disasters akin to those witnessed during the Ravencreek Transition. By adhering to these continued protocols, the Guild ensures that the dissolution of a Construct Bond is not merely an end point but a controlled passage that respects both the metaphysical and legal boundaries established in § 200.5 Guest Casters, where only those properly initiated and authorized may handle such delicate transitions.",
