    {
      "cite": "700.17",
      "part": "part_vii",
      "title": "Protocol for Cross-Referencing Arcane Artifacts in Dispute",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Protocol for Cross-Referencing Arcane Artifacts in Dispute",
          "text": "The provisions set forth in this section govern the procedural mechanics by which arcane artifacts are introduced into evidentiary proceedings before the Autumnwood Accords Desk or any other adjudicative body authorized under these Accords, ensuring that such items do not merely sit as static objects but function as active participants in the determination of fact. This protocol is designed to complement the foundational principles of evidence generally established in § 700.1 and the specific standards for admissibility outlined in § 700.14, while also respecting the distinct validation protocols required for relics detailed under § 700.16. When a litigant seeks to introduce an artifact derived from unauthorized casting or suspected corruption, the presiding officer must first determine whether the item falls within the scope of the additional provisions regarding arcane litigation found in § 700.12, which dictates that any magical evidence presented must be free from contamination or destabilization that would compromise the integrity of the adjudication process. Furthermore, this section operates in tandem with the rules concerning burden shifting and presumptions established in § 700.11, as the mere presence of a contested artifact may trigger a presumption of illegality unless the proponent can satisfy the rigorous validation requirements set forth herein.\n\nThe procedural framework for introducing an artifact requires that the item be accompanied by a certified chain-of-custody manifest, which shall explicitly detail the origin of the magical energy contained within and any prior interactions with the Unsanctioned Arts prohibited under § 300.12. If the artifact in question is alleged to have suffered damage, corruption, or destabilization due to negligent maintenance or exposure to prohibited energies, such defect shall be deemed a critical factor in its admissibility pursuant to the remedial obligations articulated in § 1101.12. In cases where the artifact is claimed to be the result of an unlawful act requiring confiscation, the court must apply the supplementary provisions governing penalties for unauthorized arcane activity found in § 800.11 before accepting the item as valid testimony or physical evidence. It is imperative that the validation protocols for artifacts and relics referenced in § 700.15 be strictly observed prior to any attempt to use the item to establish a prima facie case, ensuring that the magical properties have not been altered by external forces such as the forbidden schools of necromancy or blood magic which remain absolutely forbidden under the political spectrum of these Accords.\n\nWhere an artifact is utilized to support testimony regarding the presence of unauthorized casting, the court shall refer to the standards for admissibility of arcane testimony and artifacts in § 700.14 to determine if the item possesses sufficient evidentiary weight to override the default allocation of proof that remains with the accuser as under common law. If the defendant is charged with an act requiring a shift in burden, the artifact must be examined against the criteria set forth in § 700.9 regarding the standard of evidence in arcane disputes, ensuring that the magical resonance does not violate the prohibition on magical correspondence and transmission utilized to convey information pertaining to the Unsanctioned Arts. Additionally, when the artifact serves as a witness through its own magical traces or residues, the admissibility of such artifact-derived testimony must align with the specific regulations governing magical traces found in § 700.8, preventing the introduction of data that relies on unscheduled weather patterns or field casting anomalies described in § 500.12.\n\nThe integrity of the legal proceeding depends heavily on the competence of those assessing the artifact's authenticity and the weight of its magical signature, requiring the application of the rules concerning competent witnesses and expert testimony as detailed in § 700.10. Any attempt to utilize a corrupted or unstable item for the purpose of a trial by arcane duel or sanction and seal punishment under the penal provisions of The Paradox Trial must first pass through the validation gates established in this section, as failure to do so may result in the collateral contamination described in § 1101.12 affecting the entire court session. The provisions herein shall be read in conjunction with the foundational provisions concerning the rights of persons and the specific restrictions on commercial spellcasting found in § 500.12, ensuring that no caster engages in unauthorized activities outside the bounds of their licensed practice while presenting evidence. Ultimately, this protocol ensures that the complex body of laws governing magic remains fiercely debated yet consistently applied between the traditionalist Conservators and the radical Innovators, maintaining the delicate balance required to prevent the escalation of magical conflict into unregulated chaos.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "700.1",
            "700.14",
            "700.16",
            "700.12",
            "700.11",
