        },
        {
          "heading": "Determination of Chain-of-Custody Integrity for Transmitted Relics",
          "text": "In the intricate web of arcane protocols that govern the Mages' Guild, the transmission of artifacts and relics is a critical aspect that demands meticulous care and adherence to strict guidelines. When an artifact enters the evidentiary chain following its initial discovery or seizure, the integrity of that custody must be preserved through every subsequent handover, storage interval, and review session conducted by authorized personnel. Under § 700.16, validation protocols establish the baseline for confirming an item's magical signature, yet these initial checks do not absolve the custodian of the obligation to document the temporal continuity of possession. Any gap in the custody log, whether caused by a clerical error or an unrecorded shift change, renders the artifact suspect under the admissibility standards outlined in § 700.14. The Accords Desk shall treat any break in the documented chain as prima facie evidence of potential tampering, unless the custodian can produce a corroborating magical seal that verifies the artifact's state remained unchanged during the lapse. This burden of proof rests heavily on the senior archivist or designated guardian, who must ensure that every transfer is witnessed by at least one peer-reviewed mage possessing an active license to handle high-risk relics.\n\nFurthermore, the protocols for cross-referencing require that all metadata associated with a specific relic be updated simultaneously with any physical movement of the object. As detailed in § 300.14, transmission oversight mandates that the destination node receive both the physical artifact and its corresponding digital or runic manifest within a timeframe dictated by the severity of the containment class. Failure to align these records constitutes a breach of protocol equivalent to a minor containment failure under § 200.14. The Accords Desk maintains a strict policy wherein any discrepancy between the physical properties of an artifact and its recorded history triggers an immediate audit, often involving a peer review panel convened under the additional provisions found in § 1.17. This rigorous scrutiny is necessary because artifacts are not merely static objects; they are dynamic entities whose magical resonance can shift if subjected to unauthorized exposure or improper storage conditions. Consequently, the cross-referencing process must account for subtle variations in mana density that might indicate an external influence attempting to alter the artifact's testimony before it reaches the adjudicative body.\n\nThe legal weight of an artifact is intrinsically linked to the purity of its provenance, a principle that extends beyond simple physical possession to include the ethical and procedural history of its acquisition. As noted in § 700.12, the admissibility of magical evidence depends heavily on whether the methods used to secure or retrieve the item violated any prohibitions against the Unsanctioned Arts. An artifact recovered through forceful means that compromised the safety of surrounding civilians or endangered stable magical zones may be deemed inadmissible regardless of its evidentiary value, a stance reinforced by the remediation protocols for ancillary disruption found in § 1301.16. Therefore, the cross-referencing phase serves not only as a logistical checkpoint but also as a preliminary judicial filter to ensure that the means of acquisition do not taint the ends of justice. If an artifact was seized during a raid where containment protocols were breached, as described in § 200.13, the entire chain of custody is retroactively flagged for review, and its testimony may be suppressed until the breach is remediated and the integrity of the relic is re-verified by an independent validator.\n\nMoreover, the cross-referencing protocol must accommodate the unique nature of sentient or semi-sentient artifacts, which possess their own internal memories and potential biases that can influence the consistency of their recorded history. Under § 700.13, the admissibility of testimony derived from such entities is governed by principles of intent and coherence, requiring that the artifact's internal narrative align with external observations made during custody transfers. If an artifact begins to exhibit signs of personality alteration or memory corruption between two custodians, this divergence must be logged and analyzed as part of the cross-referencing dossier. The Mages' Guild Accords recognize that these living relics can be targeted by malicious actors seeking to manipulate judicial outcomes, making the verification of their continuity a paramount concern for the Autumnwood Accords Desk. To safeguard against such manipulation, all artifacts in dispute must undergo a resonance scan before and after every significant custody transfer, with the results archived alongside the physical item for future reference.\n\nFinally, the cross-referencing process is not merely an administrative formality but a foundational element of the Mages' Guild's commitment to truth and magical safety. By enforcing strict adherence to these procedures, the Accords ensure that no artifact enters the courtroom without a complete and unbroken history that validates its authenticity and integrity. This diligence serves as a bulwark against fraud, tampering, and the introduction of false evidence that could distort the course of justice in matters involving high-stakes magical disputes. The provisions governing this aspect of evidence handling are designed to maintain the highest standards of accountability, ensuring that every artifact presented before the bench is not only legally admissible but also magically secure. As such, any deviation from the prescribed chain-of-custody protocols shall be treated with the utmost seriousness, potentially leading to the exclusion of the artifact's testimony or even sanctions against the responsible custodian if negligence is proven beyond a reasonable doubt.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "700.16",
            "700.14",
            "300.14",
