        {
          "heading": "Protocol for Hierarchical Attribution and Canonical Source Mapping of Contested ",
          "text": "Where multiple parties present conflicting accounts regarding the origin, activation history, or current state of an arcane artifact, the adjudicative body shall not rely solely upon the physical presentation of the item but must instead engage in a rigorous process of hierarchical attribution. As established under § 700.15, validation protocols serve as the initial gatekeeper for admissibility; however, once an artifact clears this threshold, the burden shifts to establishing its canonical lineage within the dispute. This section governs the specific mechanics by which cross-referencing is performed when an item claims multiple lineages or possesses conflicting resonance signatures that challenge its primary designation. The clerk shall first examine the internal registry of the object, utilizing authorized arcane sensors to map the depth of its magical etching against the known database of sanctioned artifacts and relics. If the artifact displays signs of modification, grafting, or unauthorized infusions—common in cases involving restricted research under § 1101.14—the clerk must cross-reference these anomalies against the transmission logs detailed in § 300.14 to determine if the current state represents a legitimate evolution or an illicit alteration. It is imperative that the chain of custody remains unbroken; any gap in the physical handling record renders the artifact's claimed history suspect, necessitating a deeper forensic analysis as outlined in § 200.13 regarding containment and breach mitigation. Furthermore, testimony derived from sentient artifacts must be weighed against their own internal memory banks, ensuring that the narrative provided by the object itself aligns with the historical records filed under § 700.12. When an artifact is presented as evidence of a breach or a violation of the Unsanctioned Arts, the cross-referencing process must explicitly check for any prohibited schools of magic, such as necromancy or blood magic, which are strictly forbidden under the political mandates of the Accords. The clerk shall consult § 1301.16 to assess whether any ancillary arcane disruption has occurred that might have altered the artifact's fundamental properties during transmission, thereby invalidating its current testimony. In instances where the artifact's identity is ambiguous due to rapid metamorphosis or shapeshifting properties common in higher-tier relics, the adjudicator may invoke peer review protocols pursuant to § 1.17 to seek consensus from a council of specialists regarding the canonical interpretation of the item's nature. The ultimate goal of this cross-referencing phase is to synthesize disparate data points—from physical resonance to historical transcript—into a single, coherent legal identity that can be reliably used for judgment. Failure to perform these checks with sufficient diligence may result in the artifact being deemed inadmissible under the admissibility standards set forth in § 700.14, as its foundational truth cannot be established. Therefore, every cross-reference must be logged in the official docket, citing the specific sections of the Accords that authorize the comparison and the specific data points that were found to align or diverge. This meticulous approach ensures that the integrity of the magical evidence is preserved, protecting both the Conservators' adherence to tradition and the Innovators' pursuit of advanced understanding without compromising safety. As noted in § 200.14, additional protocols for containment are relevant whenever cross-referencing reveals potential instability or a risk of breach, requiring immediate remedial action before the hearing can proceed. Ultimately, the determination of an artifact's canonical status is not merely a technical exercise but a legal imperative that upholds the solemnity of the Autumnwood Accords and prevents the proliferation of false evidence in matters of arcane litigation.\n\nThe process of hierarchical attribution also extends to the verification of external endorsements or seals attached to the artifact. If a relic bears a seal from an authorized entity, such as the Office of Archmage Sovereign, this must be cross-referenced with the registry of active permits and supervised experiments listed in § 900.15. Any discrepancy between the seal's claimed authority and the actual permissions granted at the time of creation shall result in the artifact being classified as compromised. Moreover, when an artifact is alleged to be a construct or a bound spirit, the cross-referencing phase must include an analysis of its binding contracts against the principles of intent and cooperation described in § 700.13. This ensures that the artifact's testimony regarding its own creation is consistent with the known laws of contract magic. The clerk shall further review any collateral contamination claims under § 1101.14 to determine if the artifact has been influenced by nearby magical fields, which could skew its cross-referenced data. By integrating these various layers of verification, the Mages' Guild ensures that every artifact brought before the desk is not only physically present but legally and magically authenticated in a manner that satisfies the stringent requirements of the Accords. This comprehensive approach prevents the misuse of powerful items as mere props in a dispute and maintains the high standard of evidence required for fair adjudication in the complex tapestry of magical law.",
