        {
          "heading": "Protocol for Hierarchical Attribution and Canonical Source Mapping of Contested",
          "text": "In the adjudication of disputes involving arcane artifacts, the primary challenge lies not merely in the identification of an object's magical signature, but in establishing its authoritative origin relative to the contested claim. When multiple parties assert conflicting histories regarding a relic, such as whether a sentient construct was bound by a Conservator-led ritual or liberated by an Innovator faction experiment, the Accords Desk shall apply the principle of Canonical Source Mapping. This process requires the adjudicator to trace the lineage of the artifact through all known transmission logs, containment breaches, and peer-reviewed research permits. As established in § 700.16, validation protocols serve as the baseline for confirming an artifact's current magical state; however, these validation checks are insufficient when determining historical precedence. Therefore, under § 700.15, the Desk must prioritize canonical source documents—such as original binding pacts or initial containment orders—over derivative testimonies or secondary magical scans. If a contested artifact displays contradictory signatures indicative of tampering, the burden of proof shifts immediately to the party claiming alteration, requiring them to produce evidence consistent with § 300.14 transmission protocols.\n\nThe determination of hierarchy in attribution follows a strict stratification designed to prevent the dilution of historical truth by conflicting narratives. First, any artifact derived from an Ancient Pact or governed by Mana Reverence principles shall be presumed canonical unless proven otherwise via a formal Paradox Trial under § 300.12 prohibitions on unauthorized magical transmission. Second, artifacts resulting from supervised experiments authorized under § 900.15 shall be attributed to the original grantee of the exceptional research permit, provided that the artifact has not suffered collateral contamination as defined in § 1101.14. Third, when an artifact's origin is obscured by deliberate obfuscation spells or unauthorized modifications, the Accords Desk shall default to the most recent verified containment log found in § 200.13 protocols for breach mitigation. This tiered approach ensures that the legal weight of an artifact remains anchored to its original intent, rather than being distorted by subsequent illegal interventions. It is imperative that clerks cross-reference any new validation data against the foundational standards set forth in § 1.4 through § 1.9 regarding persons, seals, and general construction of the Accords, ensuring that no single interpretation overrides the collective wisdom of the Guild without explicit Council approval.\n\nFurthermore, the mapping process must account for the political spectrum of the Mages' Guild, recognizing the inherent tension between traditionalist Conservators and radical Innovators. An artifact claimed by an Innovator faction may be reattributed to a Conservator if evidence suggests it was seized during a breach or transferred without proper oversight under § 300.12. Similarly, claims that an artifact possesses forbidden properties, such as necromantic resonance, must be rigorously tested against the prohibition on the Unsanctioned Arts outlined in § 300.14. The Desk shall not accept attribution based solely on magical resonance or emotional testimony from sentient constructs without corroborating physical evidence of their origin chain. In instances where an artifact's history is fragmented due to a pocket dimension exile or similar penal sanction under the Paradox Trial system, the mapping protocol requires the reconstruction of the timeline using all available archival cards and cross-referenced logs. This ensures that the final attribution reflects the full scope of the artifact's existence within the legal framework, preventing parties from exploiting gaps in knowledge to gain unfair advantage.\n\nFinally, any attempt to manipulate the canonical source mapping for evidentiary purposes shall be treated as a violation of the core tenets of the Autumnwood Accords, potentially invoking sanctions under § 1301.14 or remediation protocols for ancillary arcane disruption found in § 1301.15. The integrity of the chain-of-custody is not merely administrative; it is a safeguard against the destabilization of magical reality itself. Clerks must ensure that every step of the attribution process is documented with precision, citing all relevant sections including § 700.12 regarding the admissibility of artifact-derived testimony and § 700.13 concerning sentient relics. By adhering to these hierarchical rules, the Accords Desk maintains the stability of the magical order while providing a clear, defensible path for resolving complex disputes over contested artifacts.",
