        {
          "heading": "Protocol for Sequential Resonance Mapping and Temporal Provenance Attribution",
          "text": "When an arcane artifact or relic is introduced into a formal dispute before the Autumnwood Accords Desk, the primary function of the adjudicative body shifts from mere observation to active forensic reconstruction of the item's ontological history. Under § 700.14, standards for admissibility are established, but these standards assume a baseline continuity in the artifact’s existence that must often be rigorously tested when the object claims multiple origins or possesses shifting properties. Consequently, the Clerk shall require a sequential resonance map prior to any validation attempt under § 700.16, ensuring that the temporal lineage of the relic is understood before its current magical signature is interpreted. This mapping process involves tracing the object’s interaction with local mana currents at every point where it changed hands, was activated, or underwent modification, thereby creating a continuous thread of evidence that binds the physical reality of the artifact to the legal record. Any break in this chain of resonance, whether caused by unauthorized containment under § 200.13 or by the passage through a rift in reality, must be explicitly documented, as such discontinuities may render the current state of the artifact legally distinct from its antecedent forms.\n\nThe determination of temporal provenance requires the application of canonical source mapping, a technique detailed further in related provisions governing exceptional research permits under § 900.15 and remediation protocols for ancillary disruption found in § 1301.16. When two parties present conflicting testimonies regarding an artifact’s origin, the Accords Desk shall not simply accept the verbal assertions of either litigant; rather, the Clerk must cross-reference the magical signatures embedded within the object against known historical repositories and active research logs. If an artifact purports to be an ancient pact-bound item, the verification process must align with the strict prohibitions outlined in § 300.12 regarding unauthorized transmission, ensuring that no information derived from the relic was conveyed via forbidden magical means during its journey through the dispute. Furthermore, if the artifact exhibits signs of sentient agency or semi-sentient constructs as described in § 700.13, the mapping protocol must account for the potential influence of the artifact’s own will on its recorded history, distinguishing between passive evolution and active manipulation of its own provenance to suit a specific legal narrative.\n\nIn cases where the hierarchical attribution of an artifact suggests conflicting claims of ownership or stewardship, the Desk shall invoke the principles of mediation and peer review set forth in § 1.17 to determine which canonical source holds precedence. The authority of a claimed origin is not inherent but must be demonstrated through a rigorous chain of custody that survives scrutiny under the transmission protocols of § 300.14. Should the resonance map reveal that an artifact has been subjected to collateral contamination, as defined in § 1101.14, the Desk shall order a complete re-mapping of the object’s core essence prior to any ruling on its admissibility or weight in the case. This precautionary measure prevents the introduction of extraneous magical variables that could distort the legal truth, ensuring that the adjudication rests solely on the intrinsic properties and history of the contested relic.\n\nMoreover, the protocol for sequential resonance mapping serves as a critical filter against the radical Innovators’ tendency to push the boundaries of acceptable research, balancing their innovations against the traditionalist Conservators’ insistence on established ontological stability referenced in § 1.9. By mandating a deep dive into the temporal layers of an artifact, the Accords Desk ensures that disputes are resolved based on the immutable facts of magical history rather than transient illusions or deceptive enchantments. If the cross-referencing process reveals that an artifact’s current form is a result of prohibited necromantic practices or other forbidden schools detailed under § 1.4, the entire chain of attribution shall be nullified, and the item shall be treated as contraband subject to immediate containment under § 200.13. Ultimately, this rigorous approach to temporal provenance ensures that the Mages' Guild maintains a clear and unbreakable understanding of how magic interacts with the law, preventing any single party from exploiting the fluid nature of arcane objects to subvert the judicial process or evade accountability for past actions involving powerful relics.",
          "key": "e",
