        {
          "heading": "Protocol for Sequential Resonance Mapping and Temporal Provenance Attribution — Continued",
          "text": "Under the intricate web of arcane protocols that govern the Mages' Guild, the transmission of artifacts and relics is a critical aspect that demands meticulous care and adherence to strict guidelines, particularly when such items possess the capacity to alter reality or shift temporal states. This section outlines the mandatory procedures for establishing a continuous chain of resonance mapping, ensuring that every artifact introduced into a dispute possesses a verifiable history of existence prior to its current invocation. In cases where an artifact claims to originate from a pre-Accord era or asserts a causal link to events predating the current calendar cycle, the adjudicative body must first apply the validation protocols set forth in § 700.16 before considering any testimony regarding its origin. Without such foundational validation, any claim of ancient provenance shall be deemed inadmissible under § 700.14, as the burden of proof rests squarely upon the party asserting the artifact's history to demonstrate that its resonance signature has not been artificially manipulated or synthesized through forbidden means.\n\nThe determination of temporal provenance requires the application of a sequential resonance map, a complex lattice of magical frequencies recorded at each point of custody transfer. This mapping process is not merely administrative; it is a metaphysical necessity designed to detect anomalies in the artifact's timeline. When an artifact is presented as evidence, the examining mage must cross-reference its current resonance signature against the canonical records maintained by the Office of Archmage Sovereignty. If the artifact exhibits signs of \"temporal drift\"—whereby its magical signature fluctuates inconsistently with known physical laws or contradicts established historical timelines—the matter shall be referred to the Remediation Protocols for Ancillary Arcane Disruption detailed in § 1301.16. Furthermore, any attempt to utilize the artifact's history as testimony must adhere to the strictures of § 700.12 regarding the prohibition on magical correspondence, ensuring that no unauthorized spells were employed to forge or obscure the artifact's past. The integrity of this chain is paramount; a single break in the resonance mapping invalidates the entire evidentiary value of the relic, rendering it akin to a mundane object devoid of legal standing in arcane disputes.\n\nIn situations involving sentient artifacts or constructs that actively manipulate their own perceived history, the court must employ additional measures to separate the entity's subjective narrative from objective fact. Such entities may attempt to rewrite their own provenance to align with the desires of their current possessors, a phenomenon known as \"mnestic corruption.\" To counteract this, the adjudicator shall consult the peer review protocols established in § 1.17, utilizing a council of mages specialized in temporal mechanics to verify the artifact's true age and origin. If the artifact is found to be a construct designed specifically to evade scrutiny under the guise of being an ancient relic, the party introducing it into evidence shall face sanctions under the Penal System Policy regarding the Unsanctioned Arts, as detailed in § 300.14. The court possesses the authority to seal the artifact's resonance temporarily using the Sanction and Seal procedure if its continued presence threatens to destabilize the local temporal field.\n\nIt is imperative that all parties recognize that the attribution of an artifact's source must be hierarchical, moving from direct observation by a certified magistrate before escalating to broader cross-referencing of external databases. This hierarchy ensures that the highest levels of scrutiny are applied only when lower-level validations fail or when the stakes involve significant magical infrastructure. Any claim that an artifact was recovered from a pocket dimension or a forbidden school of magic must be corroborated by independent witnesses who were present at the moment of retrieval, in strict accordance with the admissibility standards outlined in § 700.13. Furthermore, the court shall maintain a log of all resonance mappings performed under this protocol, ensuring that future disputes can reference past validations without requiring redundant testing. This systematic approach prevents the proliferation of counterfeit artifacts and maintains the sanctity of the Accords against those who would exploit temporal loopholes to gain an unfair advantage. Ultimately, the stability of the magical community relies on the unwavering commitment to truth in the provenance of every artifact brought before the Autumnwood Accords Desk.",
