        {
          "heading": "The Temporal Decay of Sealed Artifacts in Transit",
          "text": "Under § 800.14 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation, the Accords Desk recognizes that sealed capacities are not static reservoirs but dynamic states subject to environmental degradation, particularly when transferred between jurisdictions or stored during extended transit periods. The phenomenon of temporal decay must be addressed immediately upon the discovery of a breach in containment integrity, as prolonged exposure to ambient magical radiation can cause the seal matrix to fracture irreparably, rendering the contents indistinguishable from wild mana. Consequently, any practitioner authorized under § 300.11 who is tasked with transporting sealed artifacts must adhere to strict monitoring protocols detailed in § 800.5 Pocket-Prison Procedure, ensuring that the dimensional stability of the seal remains consistent with the original calibration. Failure to detect early signs of decay, such as a dulling of the luminescent halo or a sudden drop in ambient temperature readings, constitutes negligence under the Autumnwood Accords and may trigger an expedited remediation process pursuant to § 100.15 Catchline Title: Protocols for Immediate and Expedited Remediation. It is imperative that all transport vessels be equipped with active stabilization wards capable of compensating for minor fluctuations in local reality, a requirement explicitly outlined in the emergency protocols found under § 200.16 Emergency Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation.\n\nFurthermore, the reclamation process for artifacts exhibiting signs of advanced decay requires the intervention of a High Conservator or an equivalent peer reviewer designated under § 1.17 Additional Provisions for Mages' Guild Mediation and Peer Review Protocols. The Desk shall not authorize the reopening of a sealed capacity unless the structural integrity of the containment field has been mathematically verified to withstand the release of its contents without catastrophic dispersion. This verification process often involves cross-referencing the artifact's original manifest against current dimensional telemetry, ensuring that no unauthorized modifications have occurred during transit. As noted in § 400.7 Specimen Containment Standards and Disposal Proto, any specimen or capacity deemed beyond safe reclamation must be immediately flagged for disposal via a sanctioned pocket dimension incineration, preventing the leakage of unstable energy into the natural lattice. The Desk maintains that the safety of the arcane lattice is paramount, and thus, no amount of potential utility justifies the risk of an uncontrolled release. Practitioners found attempting to bypass these decay checks will be subject to immediate sanctioning under the Penal System Policy of the Paradox Trial, where the severity of the penalty depends on whether the breach was accidental or willful.\n\nIn cases where the sealed capacity contains sentient entities, such as familiars defined under § 600.2 Familiars as Agents, the reclamation procedure must include a mandatory consultation with the entity itself to assess its willingness to be reintegrated into the seal matrix. The legal standing of such an entity is protected by the principle of vicarious weaving, meaning that any attempt to forcibly reclaim a unwilling familiar constitutes a breach of contract akin to unlawful casting. This specific nuance was highlighted during recent incidents involving transported entities where the lack of consent led to spontaneous dimensional ruptures, necessitating the activation of contingency measures described in § 300.17 Contingencies for Unauthorized Arcane Breaches. The Desk emphasizes that the integrity of the arcane lattice is not merely a physical constraint but a legal covenant; therefore, respecting the autonomy of sealed entities is as much a matter of law as it is of physics. Any deviation from this standard protocol will be treated as an unauthorized arcane breach, triggering an automatic response from the Emergency Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation outlined in § 200.16.\n\nFinally, the documentation of decay rates and reclamation success metrics must be archived under the strict confidentiality guidelines established in § 1001.16 Catchline Title: Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Memb. These records are essential for future calibration of seal matrices and should not be disseminated without the explicit permission of the Archmage Sovereign or the High Council. The Desk retains the authority to revoke transport licenses for any practitioner who fails to report decay indicators within the prescribed timeframe, ensuring that the collective knowledge of the Guild remains robust against unforeseen catastrophic events. By adhering to these rigorous standards, the Autumnwood Accords ensure that the allocation and reclamation of sealed capacities proceed with the utmost precision, safeguarding both the practitioners and the world from the unpredictable dangers of unstable magical energy.",
