        {
          "heading": "The Burden of Stored Potential in Transit",
          "text": "Under § 800.17 Allocation and Reclamation of Sealed Capacities, the legal framework governing the movement of sealed artifacts extends beyond mere custody; it dictates the physiological and metaphysical toll exacted upon the vessel or agent tasked with transporting such items across dimensional thresholds. When a practitioner is assigned to move a sealed capacity, particularly one containing volatile mana reserves or captured spectral entities as noted under § 800.15 Remediation Protocols for Ancillary Arcane Disruption, the law assumes that the carrier becomes a temporary battery for the artifact's stored potential. This concept, often misunderstood as simple containment, is legally defined as \"vicarious saturation,\" where the carrier's own magical lattice must absorb ambient fluctuations to prevent premature leakage. The Autumnwood Accords recognize that this saturation creates a unique liability; if a carrier fails to maintain the requisite equilibrium during transit, the resulting breach is not attributed solely to negligence but is considered an inherent risk of the allocation itself, provided the carrier has undergone the standard calibration rites outlined in § 100.15 Protocols for Immediate and Expedited Remediation. Consequently, any claim of injury sustained by a transport agent due to the sheer weight of the sealed capacity must first be evaluated against whether the artifact's seal integrity was maintained within the tolerances specified under § 400.7 Specimen Containment Standards and Disposal Proto.\n\nFurthermore, the reclamation process for these transported capacities is not merely a matter of opening a container but involves the careful extraction of the residual energy that has bonded to the carrier during the journey. As established in § 600.2 Familiars as Agents, when a familiar or agent acts under the authority of a licensed practitioner, they do so with full knowledge of the risks inherent in handling high-grade sealed materials. However, if an artifact is transported in a state of instability—such as those involving unauthorized schools of magic like necromancy discussed in § 300.17 Contingencies for Unauthorized Arcane Breaches—the carrier may be involuntarily subjected to \"reverse saturation,\" where the artifact's influence attempts to overwrite the carrier's own magical signature. In such instances, the High Council of the Autumnwood Accords retains sovereign authority to intervene, often invoking provisions under § 800.5 Pocket-Prison Procedure to isolate the affected individual if the contamination threatens to spread beyond the immediate transit zone. The reclamation of the capacity then becomes a judicial act, requiring the carrier to undergo a formal cleansing ritual overseen by a peer reviewer, in accordance with § 1.17 Additional Provisions for Mages' Guild Mediation and Peer Review Protocols, to ensure that no unauthorized magical signatures remain embedded in the practitioner's soul-lattice.\n\nThe logistical aspect of this reclamation is further complicated by the nature of the artifact itself; certain sealed capacities, such as those containing dimensional engines or survey machines capable of logging conversations, possess a memory that persists even after the primary seal is broken. Under § 700.2 Scrying of Closed Books, any attempt to interrogate these artifacts without proper authorization is strictly prohibited, yet the reclamation process often necessitates a degree of interaction that borders on this restriction. Therefore, the Accords mandate that all reclamation operations be conducted within a controlled environment where the ambient mana density is neutralized, preventing the carrier from inadvertently acting as a scrying surface for the artifact's internal data. This precaution is vital when dealing with artifacts recovered from contested zones or following incidents similar to the Mount Ebott Survey, where the integrity of the transport vehicle and the carrier's mental state are paramount. Failure to adhere to these reclamation protocols may result in the permanent sealing of the carrier's capacity under § 800.17 Allocation and Reclamation of Sealed Capacities itself, effectively removing them from active service until a full review by the Paradox Trial confirms their fitness for duty. Thus, the movement and return of sealed capacities is not simply a logistical task but a high-stakes legal and metaphysical undertaking requiring strict adherence to the foundational principles laid out in § 1.4–1.9 regarding persons, seals, and construction.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "800.17",
