        {
          "heading": "Logistics of Cross-Realm Transit for Sealed Entities",
          "text": "The movement of sealed capacities across jurisdictional boundaries, whether through dimensional portals or via physical conveyance by licensed familiars, is governed by strict adherence to the protocols established in § 800.15 regarding remediation and § 200.16 concerning emergency containment. Under these governing statutes, any artifact bearing a seal of the Archmage Sovereign or the High Conservator that traverses a threshold into an unsecured zone risks immediate degradation of its containment field. This phenomenon, known as transit decay, is not merely a matter of logistical inconvenience but a critical safety hazard that demands proactive mitigation measures detailed in § 400.7 Specimen Containment Standards and Disposal Proto. The burden placed upon the transporting party is absolute; should a sealed capacity fail to maintain its integrity during transit, the resulting release of stored potential shall be treated as an unauthorized arcane breach under § 300.17 Contingencies for Unauthorized Arcane Breaches. Consequently, all practitioners authorized to move such items must ensure that their transport vessels are equipped with stabilizing wards capable of counteracting ambient magical interference, a requirement explicitly reinforced by the provisions found in § 800.5 Pocket-Prison Procedure which addresses the sovereign authority required to mediate such high-risk movements.\n\nFurthermore, the legal standing of the entity accompanying the sealed capacity is paramount when determining liability for accidental leakage or resonance-induced instability. As established under § 600.2 Familiars as Agents, a familiar acting as an agent of a licensed practitioner assumes vicarious responsibility for the safety of the cargo they carry. This principle extends to any situation where a familiar's own magical signature interferes with the seal of an artifact it is transporting, creating a scenario where the familiar becomes a focal point for resonant unsealing events. In such instances, the familiar must immediately initiate emergency protocols as outlined in § 200.16 to prevent catastrophic consequences that may arise from breaches in arcane containment. The failure to do so constitutes a gross negligence violation of the Autumnwood Accords, subjecting both the practitioner and their familiar to potential sanctions ranging from temporary sealing of magical faculties to exile to the Pocket-Prison under the authority granted by § 800.5.\n\nThe issue of temporal decay further complicates the logistics of long-distance transit, necessitating regular inspections and recalibrations of containment fields in accordance with the standards set forth in § 100.15 Protocols for Immediate and Expedited Remediation. These protocols dictate that any sealed capacity showing signs of instability must be returned to a secure holding facility without delay, regardless of the destination or the urgency of the mission. The Mages' Guild Accords Desk retains the ultimate authority to revoke licenses for practitioners who repeatedly fail to maintain the necessary stability during transit, thereby threatening the integrity of the arcane lattice as mandated in § 300.17. It is imperative that all parties involved in the transport of sealed capacities understand that the safety of the natural world supersedes any individual desire for expedited delivery or personal gain. The strict enforcement of these rules ensures that the dangerous power held within sealed artifacts remains contained and does not spill over into unauthorized domains, a principle that underpins the entire regulatory framework governing magical practitioners and their actions across all realms.\n\nIn cases where a sealed capacity is intercepted by hostile forces or suffers an accidental breach during transit, the responding party must adhere to the emergency response procedures detailed in § 200.16 Emergency Protocols for Arcane Containment and Breach Mitigation. These procedures are designed to minimize damage and contain any released energy before it can cause widespread harm to the surrounding environment or populations. The failure to follow these protocols not only compromises the safety of the realm but also violates the fundamental duties owed to the community by licensed practitioners. Therefore, all practitioners must be thoroughly trained in the application of these emergency measures and must possess the necessary resources to execute them effectively in the event of a breach. The consequences of neglecting these duties are severe, ranging from disciplinary action within the Mages' Guild to legal prosecution under the penal provisions of the Accords for crimes such as unauthorized casting or failure to protect against arcane threats.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "800.15",
