        {
          "heading": "Liability for Resonant Drift During Long-Distance Transit",
          "text": "The administration of sealed capacities, particularly when those entities are transported across dimensional vectors or subjected to prolonged periods of stasis within the Arcane Lattice, introduces a specific class of liability known as Resonant Drift. As established in § 800.15 regarding remediation for ancillary arcane disruption, any variance in the stability of a sealed entity must be addressed immediately; however, the Accords Desk recognizes that drift often accumulates quietly during transit, necessitating a distinct framework for assessment and correction prior to final reclamation or deployment. Under § 100.15, which mandates immediate expedited remediation for any breach, the burden of proof regarding the safety of a sealed capacity in transit lies with the transporter, not the recipient. This principle is critical given the volatile nature of stored potential, where even minor fluctuations in ambient mana density can cause a sealed entity to \"hum\" or emit low-frequency dissonance that weakens its structural seals. Consequently, any vessel designated for cross-realm transit must be equipped with dampening fields calibrated according to the specific magical signature of the cargo, ensuring that the artifact remains inert until it reaches its designated coordinate.\n\nThe mechanics of this drift are governed by the same principles found in § 300.17 concerning contingencies for unauthorized arcane breaches, as an uncontained resonance during transit is functionally indistinguishable from an active breach of the lattice. When a sealed capacity is moved, it carries with it not only its own potential but also the residual history of its previous existence; this \"ghost load\" can interact unpredictably with the transport medium itself. For instance, if a Gold Dragon egg, classified under § 800.17 as a high-risk sealed capacity, is transported through a region of unstable reality, the egg may absorb the chaotic energies of that zone, thereby altering its internal sealing matrix without external observers noticing. This phenomenon requires rigorous monitoring protocols, similar to those applied to familiars acting as agents under § 600.2, where the familiar's status and safety are intrinsically linked to the actions of its master. In this context, the transporter acts as the master, bearing full vicarious responsibility for any degradation of the seal caused by environmental factors or poor logistical planning.\n\nFurthermore, the issue of \"phantom leakage\" must be addressed, a condition where potential energy escapes the primary seal and dissipates into the surrounding environment rather than being reclaimed upon arrival. This is a direct violation of § 800.5 regarding pocket-prison procedure, as it effectively releases a fraction of the entity's power prematurely while still technically \"in custody.\" To prevent this, all transit logs must be cross-referenced with real-time resonance monitors, and any reading indicating a drift exceeding the threshold defined in § 100.15 for immediate remediation must trigger an automatic halt to the journey. The Accords Desk maintains that no sealed capacity shall be considered fully reclaimed until a certified auditor confirms that its resonant frequency has stabilized within acceptable margins, a standard that often requires the presence of a High Conservator if the entity is of significant historical or destructive value.\n\nFinally, the legal distinction between natural decay and induced drift must be maintained to ensure fair adjudication in cases involving the Paradox Trial. If a sealed capacity suffers damage or degradation due to the negligence of its transporters, the resulting instability is treated as a punishable offense under the penal spectrum for Sanction and Seal. However, if the drift occurs spontaneously due to unknown anomalies in the ambient lattice, the responsibility shifts to the Mages' Guild to implement emergency containment protocols as outlined in § 200.16. This nuance ensures that innovators are not unfairly penalized for phenomena beyond their control, while traditionalists maintain strict accountability over their wards and agents. The Desk will cite any failure to adhere to these transit standards in the peer review process, ensuring that the integrity of the arcane lattice remains paramount regardless of the factional debates raging between the Conservators and the Innovators regarding the nature of such containment.\n\nIn summary, the allocation and reclamation of sealed capacities is not a binary event but a continuous process of stabilization that extends throughout the journey. Transporters must adhere to the strictest interpretations of § 400.7 regarding specimen containment standards, treating every sealed entity as a volatile hazard until proven otherwise. The Desk reserves the right to revoke transport licenses for any practitioner who repeatedly fails to account for resonant drift or who attempts to bypass monitoring equipment in an effort to expedite delivery. By integrating these safeguards into the broader framework of the Autumnwood Accords, the Guild ensures that the reclamation process remains safe, predictable, and just, protecting both the practitioners and the natural world from the catastrophic consequences of unmanaged arcane potential.",
