    {
      "cite": "1001.17",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Prohibition on Magical Counterfeiting and Alchemical Debasement",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Prohibition on Magical Counterfeiting and Alchemical Debasement",
          "text": "Under § 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold Reporting Thresholds and the foundational principles established in § 1.4 regarding the definition of valuable property, the Accords expressly forbid any attempt to alter the intrinsic nature of currency or trade goods for the purpose of deception. A hat that later bills its wearer, as noted under § 1001.6 Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets, creates a latent liability; however, this liability does not extend to the active transmutation of base metals into gold without adherence to the assay desk protocols mandated by § 1001.8. The aggregation of materials described in § 1001.5 Material Alteration of Traded Goods permits permissible transfiguration for legitimate craft, but such processes are strictly distinguished from the fraudulent creation of assets that mimic the value of recognized currency. Any construct or artifact produced under a false guise of official backing, where the composition does not match the filed description as required by § 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk, shall be deemed counterfeit and subject to immediate confiscation pursuant to § 800.11 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation. The distinction between a legitimate enhancement and an illicit debasement relies heavily on the intent of the caster and the transparency of the resulting object's properties; therefore, any magical operation designed to obscure the true value of a commodity until after the transaction is complete violates the spirit of § 1001.7 and the strict construction found in § 1.9 regarding fraudulent conveyances.\n\nFurthermore, the use of spatial containment devices, such as those referenced in § 1001.13 Bag of Holding as Off-Books Vault, to store counterfeit assets or to artificially inflate inventory values constitutes a severe breach of fiduciary duty and statutory compliance. The mere presence of an item within a bound object does not alter its legal classification; if the contents are illicitly transmuted goods, the container itself becomes complicit under the joint liability framework implied by § 1001.15 Layered Transfiguration Schemes. This is particularly relevant when considering the historical annotations found in C.C.D. 6125 and 7355, which clarify that the burden of proof for the authenticity of magical currency rests entirely on the issuer or creator. A wish used to rewrite a filed return, as explicitly ruled out in § 1001.11 Wish Used to Rewrite a Filed Return, similarly cannot be invoked to retroactively legitimize a batch of alchemically debased coins or artifacts. The stamp of the Guild remains immutable against supernatural manipulation, and any attempt to bypass this administrative reality through magical fiat is an act of unauthorized spellcasting in public spaces under § 500.11 Unsanctioned Field Operations and Liability.\n\nThe Conservators argue that such counterfeiting undermines the economic stability of the realm by introducing unstable energetic signatures into the standard market, while the Innovators contend that strict regulation stifles legitimate research into material properties; however, this debate does not diminish the statutory prohibition against fraud. As established in § 1001.9 Polymorph of Coinage and reinforced by the broader safety protocols in § 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct, the transformation of currency must be declared prior to circulation. Failure to disclose the composition or the inherent risks associated with a magical asset, as detailed in § 1001.7, renders the transaction voidable at the discretion of the Accords Desk. Moreover, if a gifted item bears residual magical influences from previous owners that were not disclosed, such taints are considered part of the collateral's risk profile under § 1101.11 Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion, and using them to simulate new currency constitutes both a breach of trust and a violation of safety standards. The penalties for engaging in such activities range from sealing one's magic, as per the penal policy of § 500.12, to full confiscation of assets under § 800.11. Ultimately, the integrity of the economic system relies on the absolute truthfulness of disclosures and the impossibility of rewriting administrative records through magical means, a principle codified in § 1001.16 Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Members and upheld by the residual clauses in § 100.12 Supplementary Provisions and Transitional Arrangements.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1001.8",
