    },
    {
      "cite": "1101.17",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Casting and Contamination",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Casting and Contamination",
          "text": "In the event that any magical item or construct shall suffer damage, corruption, or destabilization due to unauthorized alteration, negligent maintenance, or exposure to prohibited energies, such defect shall be deemed a manifestation of collateral contamination (see § 1101.6). Remedial obligations are hereby established for the affected parties, both individuals and guilds, who must take immediate action to rectify the situation.\n\nUnder § 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct, any caster engaging in unlicensed spellcasting is subject to liability for collateral contamination (see § 1101.7). The burden of proof lies with the party asserting collateral contamination; such burden includes demonstrating the nature and extent of the damage or corruption (see § 1101.8).\n\nIf a magical item or construct has been unlawfully altered, the responsible party must adhere to specific protocols for remediation. These protocols are designed to prevent further spread of collateral contamination and ensure that any lingering arcane echoes are neutralized (see § 1101.13). The protocol shall include:\n\n1. Immediate cessation of all spellcasting activities involving the affected item or construct.\n2. Documentation of the nature, extent, and source of the collateral contamination for future reference and legal purposes.\n3. Removal of the affected item or construct from public spaces to a secure location (see § 700.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence).\n4. Engagement with a qualified mage or guild to perform a thorough examination and assessment of the item or construct, followed by any necessary remedial actions.\n\nIf the collateral contamination is deemed severe enough to pose an immediate threat, the affected party must report this to the Autumnwood Accords Desk for evaluation (see § 1101.5). The Desk shall determine whether additional protocols are required and may authorize the use of more drastic measures if necessary.\n\nIn the event that the collateral contamination results from an unlawful merging or combination of magical items, the remedial protocols under § 1101.16 must be strictly followed. This includes the disassembly of the merged construct to its component parts, ensuring that each part is thoroughly examined and treated for residual magical influences (see § 1101.15).\n\nAdditionally, the responsible party shall bear the costs associated with the remedial actions taken (see § 1101.6). If the contamination is found to be the result of negligence or reckless conduct, punitive measures may also be imposed by the Autumnwood Accords Desk.\n\nUpon completion of the remediation process, a report summarizing the findings and actions taken must be submitted to the Desk for review and approval (see § 700.12). Failure to comply with these protocols shall result in sanctions as outlined in the penal system policy of The Autumnwood Accords (see § 500.11 Unsanctioned Field Operations and Liability).\n\nIn cases where collateral contamination is caused by a prohibited energy source, additional steps may be required to ensure that such energies are permanently neutralized or removed from circulation (see § 800.12 Provisions Governing Unlawful Casting and Confiscation). The Autumnwood Accords Desk shall have the authority to confiscate any items or materials deemed hazardous.\n\nIt is emphasized that these protocols are not merely punitive; they serve a protective function, safeguarding the integrity of magical artifacts and constructs for future generations. Compliance with these remedial obligations ensures adherence to the foundational principles of The Autumnwood Accords, which seek to balance the power and responsibility of magic users (see § 1101.6).\n\nIn summary, the protocols established herein are designed to mitigate the risks associated with collateral contamination resulting from unauthorized or negligent spellcasting activities. By adhering to these guidelines, all parties involved in magical practices can contribute to a safer and more regulated environment for the use of arcane magic (see § 1101.7).",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.6",
            "500.12",
            "1101.7",
            "1101.8",
            "1101.13",
            "700.12",
            "1101.5",
