    {
      "cite": "100.18",
      "part": "part_i",
      "title": "Protocols for Unsanctioned Spellcasting Incidents",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Protocols for Unsanctioned Spellcasting Incidents",
          "text": "In the event that a member of the Mages' Guild engages in unapproved spellcasting activities outside their licensed practice, such incidents shall be governed by the provisions set forth herein. These protocols are designed to ensure immediate and effective remediation of any breaches while maintaining the integrity of the Guild’s authority and public safety.\n\nUnder § 500.11 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces; Safety Protocols and Civil Remedies, the casting of spells without prior clearance by the Autumnwood Accords Desk constitutes an act of unlicensed artisanry. Upon receiving notification or evidence of such activity, the Desk shall immediately undertake a preliminary investigation. If the activity is determined to be unauthorized, the Desk may issue a cease-and-desist order under § 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct.\n\nThe Desk’s response must consider both the safety implications of the spellcasting and the potential harm to bystanders or infrastructure. In cases where there are no immediate risks, the Desk will issue a warning letter pursuant to § 800.11 Additional Provisions Regarding Penalties for Unauthorized Arcane Activity. However, if the circumstances indicate potential danger or widespread disruption, the Desk may take more decisive action.\n\nWhere appropriate, the Desk shall coordinate with local authorities and other relevant entities to ensure public safety. Under § 700.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence, evidence of unauthorized spellcasting can be admitted in any proceedings before the Desk or other authorized bodies. The Desk may also seek assistance from the Guild’s Research Oversight Committee under § 400.12 Research Integrity and Oversight Protocols to determine whether the spellcasting activity falls within permissible research guidelines.\n\nIn cases where the spellcaster is a member of the Guild, the Desk shall refer the matter for further investigation by the relevant factional or guild committees. Such committees will have the authority to impose disciplinary action under § 1401.11 Supplementary Provisions and Transitional Arrangements. If the spellcasting activity involves multiple factions or jurisdictions, the Desk may convene an inter-factional arbitration panel as outlined in § 1301.11 Supplementary Provisions and Transitional Arrangements.\n\nUpon conclusion of any investigation or proceeding, the Desk will issue a final order. This order shall detail all findings and the prescribed remedial actions to be taken by the spellcaster. The spellcaster may appeal such an order under § 1401.12 Appeal Procedures for Final Orders. If the spellcasting activity is determined to be malicious or reckless, penalties ranging from reprimand to suspension of practice may be imposed.\n\nThe Desk shall maintain a record of all unapproved spellcasting incidents and their resolutions. This information will be used to inform future policy decisions and training programs within the Guild. The Desk’s protocols are designed to ensure that any breach of protocol is promptly addressed, thereby maintaining public trust in the Guild’s regulatory framework. See § 100.7 Foreign Halls and Courtesy for guidance on how foreign halls may handle similar incidents.\n\nThe Desk shall also ensure compliance with § 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct regarding the unauthorized commercial spellcasting activities. Any individual or entity engaging in such activities without express authorization from the Guild’s High Council is subject to swift and stringent penalties, including confiscation of tools and materials used for such acts.\n\nIn all instances, the Desk shall act with due diligence to ensure that any breach does not compromise public safety or the integrity of the Guild. The Desk’s protocols are thus designed not only to address immediate issues but also to prevent recurrence by providing clear guidelines and consequences for unauthorized spellcasting activities.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "500.11",
            "500.12",
            "800.11",
            "700.12",
            "400.12",
            "1401.11",
            "1301.11",
            "1401.12",
            "100.7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Handling of Publicly Observed Spells",
          "text": "In the administration of the Mages' Guild Accords Desk, the protocol for handling publicly observed spells that have not been sanctioned by the appropriate authorities is critical to maintaining public safety and the integrity of magic's governance. The immediate response to such incidents ensures that any potential harm or disruption can be addressed swiftly, thereby preventing escalation and collateral damage.\n\nThe first step in addressing a publicly observed spell involves the identification of the caster and the nature of the spell. Under § 100.2 (Who May Speak for the Guild), it is imperative that only those authorized to speak on behalf of the Guild are permitted to communicate official responses or concerns regarding unlicensed activities. This ensures that any communication is both accurate and within the bounds of propriety.\n\nUpon identification, the Autumnwood Accords Desk shall issue a directive under § 1001.16 (Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Members) to ensure that all parties involved in the incident are aware of their confidentiality obligations. This includes both the caster and any witnesses who may have observed or been affected by the spell.\n\nThe Desk shall then proceed with immediate action under § 500.11 (Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces; Safety Protocols and Civil Remedies). The aim is to ensure that public spaces remain safe, free from harmful or disruptive magical activities. This includes a review of any potential risks posed by the spell and the implementation of necessary safety protocols.\n\nIf the spell involves hazardous or potentially dangerous elements, immediate remediation under § 100.15 (Protocols for Immediate and Expedited Remediation) shall be initiated. This may include the neutralization of the spell’s effects, if possible, to mitigate any ongoing harm or disruption. The Desk will work closely with local authorities and other relevant entities as needed.\n\nIn cases where the caster is identified as a member of the Guild, additional protocols under § 100.17 (Protocols for Exclusionary Hearings and Peer Review) shall be followed. This includes an evaluation by a panel of peers to determine if further sanctions or education are necessary. The panel’s findings will guide any actions taken against the caster, including potential reprimands, additional training, or more severe penalties.\n\nWhen dealing with publicly observed spells that may have garnered public attention, it is crucial to manage media interactions carefully. Under § 100.18 (Protocols for Unsanctioned Spellcasting Incidents), the Desk shall coordinate responses to minimize panic and misinformation while ensuring accurate information is conveyed to the public. This includes working with official spokespeople who can provide clear explanations of the situation, the steps being taken, and any potential outcomes.\n\nThe Autumnwood Accords Desk must also ensure that records are properly maintained for future reference. Under § 800.15 (Remediation Protocols for Ancillary Arcane Disruption), detailed reports shall be compiled to document all aspects of the incident, including the nature of the spell, its effects, and the measures taken in response. These records will be invaluable for training purposes and future planning.\n\nFurthermore, under § 900.16 (Ancillary Arcane Disruption Remediation Protocols), ongoing support may be provided to those affected by the incident or to the community at large if necessary. This can include counseling services or other forms of assistance that ensure a holistic approach to managing such events.\n\nIn conclusion, handling publicly observed spells requires a multifaceted approach that prioritizes public safety and Guild integrity. By following these protocols, the Desk ensures that any unlicensed spellcasting is managed efficiently and effectively, minimizing risk and promoting trust between the Guild and the community. Through adherence to established guidelines, the Mages' Guild Accords Desk can maintain its role as a cornerstone of magical governance in the realm.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "100.2",
            "1001.16",
            "500.11",
            "100.15",
            "100.17",
            "100.18",
            "800.15",
            "900.16"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Procedures for Reporting and Initial Assessment of Publicly Observed Spells",
          "text": "In the administration of the Autumnwood Accords, ensuring the integrity and safety of public spaces is paramount. This responsibility falls under the purview of the Mages' Guild Accords Desk, which must remain vigilant in addressing unlicensed spellcasting activities that may be observed by members of the public. The protocols established for such incidents are critical to maintaining order and preventing escalation into larger issues.\n\n### Reporting Mechanisms\n\nThe first step in handling publicly observed spells is the reporting mechanism itself. Under § 100.2, it is imperative that any individual who witnesses an unlicensed spellcasting incident immediately report it to the nearest Accords Desk. The public is encouraged to do so through various channels, including emergency hotlines and online reporting platforms provided by the Guild (see § 500.12 for additional provisions regarding field operations). It is crucial that these reports are made as soon as possible after the incident occurs to ensure that all necessary parties can respond promptly.\n\n### Initial Assessment\n\nUpon receipt of a report, the Accords Desk must conduct an initial assessment of the situation (see § 800.15 for remediation protocols). This process involves evaluating whether the spellcasting is indeed unlicensed and determining its potential impact on public safety. The assessors are trained to identify common signs that may indicate unauthorized use of magic, such as unusual atmospheric disturbances or observable effects in populated areas. If the assessment confirms the presence of an unlicensed spell, further actions must be taken immediately.\n\n### Coordination with Local Authorities\n\nIn cases where publicly observed spells pose immediate threats to public safety, coordination between the Mages' Guild and local law enforcement is essential (see § 100.15 for protocols on immediate and expedited remediation). The Accords Desk should work closely with police forces or other relevant authorities to ensure that appropriate measures are taken swiftly. This includes setting up containment zones where necessary, evacuating affected areas if needed, and providing information to the public about what is happening (see § 1301.14 for ancillary arcane disruption remediation protocols).\n\n### Documentation and Follow-Up\n\nAfter addressing any immediate threats posed by an unlicensed spellcasting incident, thorough documentation of all events must be completed. This includes records of spells cast, their effects on the environment or population, and actions taken by both the Guild and local authorities (see § 1001.16 for confidentiality and reporting obligations). Follow-up measures may involve further investigation into the source of the spellcasting (if identified) and ensuring that all involved parties understand their responsibilities moving forward.\n\n### Public Communication\n\nEffective communication with the public is crucial during these situations to prevent panic or misinformation from spreading. The Accords Desk should issue clear statements outlining what has been observed, any risks present, and how they are being managed (see § 1001.16 for confidentiality requirements). Providing updates on ongoing investigations or resolutions can help maintain trust between the Guild and its community members.\n\nBy adhering to these procedures, the Mages' Guild Accords Desk ensures that incidents of unlicensed spellcasting observed by the public receive prompt attention without causing undue alarm among citizens. These protocols reflect the broader goals outlined in § 100.2 regarding communication with the public and maintaining trust between magical practitioners and those they serve.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "100.2",
            "500.12",
            "800.15",
            "100.15",
            "1301.14",
            "1001.16"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Reporting and Immediate Response Protocols for Publicly Observed Spells",
          "text": "In the administration of the Autumnwood Accords, it is imperative that protocols are established to address ancillary disruptions in arcane applications which may result from both deliberate and accidental transgressions. Under § 100.18 Protocols for Unsanctioned Spellcasting Incidents, the Mages' Guild Accords Desk is entrusted with ensuring that any breach or deviation from established protocols does not linger unaddressed. This section provides guidelines on how to handle publicly observed spells and the immediate response mechanisms in place.\n\nUnder § 500.11 Unauthorized Spellcasting in Public Spaces; Safety Protocols and Civil Remedies, it is made clear that the casting of spells without prior clearance by the Autumnwood Accords Desk constitutes an act of unlicensed artisanry under § 200.13. This section emphasizes the importance of immediate reporting to the Accords Desk upon observing such incidents, as outlined in § 100.18. The desk’s protocol mandates that all publicly observed spells must be reported immediately to ensure a swift and effective response.\n\nThe initial assessment process begins with the identification and categorization of the spell cast. Under § 1301.14 Remediation Protocols for Ancillary Arcane Disruption, it is stated that protocols are in place to address and mitigate the effects of such disruptions. The desk will determine whether the spell falls under an unlicensed practice or if there was an accidental occurrence. If the casting is found to be unauthorized, the desk must coordinate with the caster for immediate remediation.\n\nUpon receiving a report of a publicly observed spell, the desk will dispatch a team for an initial assessment, as outlined in § 100.15 Protocols for Immediate and Expedited Remediation. The team's primary responsibility is to ensure the safety of the public and any bystanders who may be affected by the spell. This includes assessing the immediate impact on the environment or individuals involved, and determining whether further action is necessary.\n\nThe desk has authority under § 100.2 Who May Speak for the Guild to assign personnel capable of handling such situations effectively. This ensures that the response team comprises mages trained in both arcane knowledge and public safety protocols. The immediate response may include deactivating the spell, mitigating any harm caused by it, or guiding the caster towards proper licensing if necessary.\n\nIf the spell is found to be an accident rather than a deliberate act of unlicensed magic, the desk will provide guidance on how to prevent such occurrences in the future. Under § 100.17 Protocols for Exclusionary Hearings and Peer Review, it is emphasized that peer review plays a crucial role in ensuring that mages understand their responsibilities under the Accords. This could include recommending further training or resources for the caster.\n\nIn cases where the spell has caused significant disruption or harm to public safety, the desk may escalate the matter to a formal investigation, as outlined in § 800.15 Remediation Protocols for Ancillary Arcane Disruption. The investigation will determine whether there was any negligence on the part of the caster and what measures should be taken to ensure compliance with the Accords in the future.\n\nThe desk also has the authority under § 1.14 Additional Provisions for Expedited Enforcement and Immediate Remedies to act swiftly, ensuring that any breaches or deviations from established protocols are addressed without delay. This includes immediate enforcement actions such as confiscation of materials used in unlicensed magic and potential penalties for those who repeatedly engage in such practices.\n\nIn instances where the spell caster is a novice or apprentice mage, the desk will provide support and guidance to ensure they understand their responsibilities under the Accords. Under § 1001.16 Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Members, it is emphasized that confidentiality must be maintained in all aspects of the investigation and response process to protect both the caster and the public.\n\nThe desk’s protocols are designed to ensure that any publicly observed spell is handled efficiently and effectively, minimizing disruption and harm. By following these guidelines, the Autumnwood Accords Desk can maintain order within the arcane community and uphold the integrity of the Accords. Through swift and decisive action, the desk ensures that mages understand their responsibilities and the consequences of unlicensed practice.\n\nThe importance of reporting publicly observed spells cannot be understated. This early intervention is crucial in preventing any escalation or collateral damage resulting from unlicensed spellcasting. By adhering to these protocols, the Mages' Guild Accords Desk can ensure a safer environment for all practitioners of magic within its jurisdiction.",
          "key": "d",
