        {
          "heading": "Remediation Protocols for Collateral Interference and Consequential Damage",
          "text": "In the event that magical artifacts or constructs come into proximity with one another, thereby resulting in unintended merging events, such occurrences are not only destructive but also perilous to the integrity of the magical ecosystem. Under § 1101.14, collateral contamination refers to the unintended consequences arising from the interaction between magical artifacts, constructs, or spells under the control of a single party with another. However, this section addresses scenarios where such contamination extends beyond mere physical proximity and results in a more insidious form of interference—collateral interference.\n\nCollateral interference occurs when the influence of one magical construct inadvertently alters the properties or functions of an adjacent artifact without direct contact. This phenomenon can lead to unpredictable outcomes, including but not limited to, the destabilization of nearby magical constructs, the corruption of artifacts, and the creation of paradoxical zones that threaten the safety and stability of the environment.\n\nUnder these protocols, mages are required to report any instances of collateral interference immediately upon discovery. The reporting obligation is outlined in § 1101.12, which mandates that mages document the nature, extent, and location of such events within twenty-four hours of their occurrence. Failure to comply with this requirement may result in additional penalties under § 800.11.\n\nUpon receiving a report of collateral interference or consequential damage, the Mages' Guild Accords Desk shall initiate an investigation. The desk will consult with experts in the field of magical contamination and remedial protocols (see § 1101.16) to determine the appropriate course of action. Remediation efforts may include but are not limited to, disassembly of affected constructs, reconfiguration of spells, or the use of counter-curses designed specifically for such events.\n\nThe Accords Desk shall ensure that all remedial protocols are carried out under strict safety guidelines (see § 1800.5) to prevent further contamination and potential hazards. In cases where the collateral interference cannot be fully reversed, alternative measures may be implemented, including the relocation of affected artifacts or constructs to safer environments.\n\nUnder § 1101.17, mages are responsible for the cost of remedial actions taken to mitigate collateral interference. If the mage is found culpable in causing the event, additional penalties under § 800.8 may be imposed. The Accords Desk reserves the right to impose stricter measures if it determines that negligence or deliberate intent was involved.\n\nIn instances where the collateral interference leads to the creation of a paradoxical zone, mages are required to report such zones immediately and to take steps to neutralize them as per § 1800.6. Failure to comply may result in severe sanctions under § 800.12. The Accords Desk will coordinate with other guilds or organizations involved to ensure the safety of all parties.\n\nIt is important to note that collateral interference and consequential damage are not limited to physical proximity but can also occur through indirect means such as magical resonance, aura overlap, or even the sharing of a common source of power. These protocols apply equally to all mages regardless of rank or status within the guild (see § 1.7).\n\nThe Accords Desk encourages continuous monitoring and reporting to prevent future occurrences of collateral interference. By adhering to these remedial protocols, mages contribute not only to their own safety but also to the broader magical community's stability and harmony.\n\nIn summary, the remediation of collateral interference is critical in maintaining the integrity of magical constructs and artifacts. Mages are reminded that their actions have far-reaching consequences, and they must take responsibility for any unintended outcomes of their casting or manipulation (see § 1.9). By following these protocols, mages can help ensure a safer and more stable environment for all practitioners of magic.",
