    {
      "cite": "500.18",
      "part": "part_v",
      "title": "Liability for Ancillary Arcane Disruptions and Casualties",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Liability for Ancillary Arcane Disruptions and Casualties",
          "text": "In the conduct of arcane operations, mages and their apprentices may encounter unintended side effects known as ancillary events. These disturbances can range from minor inconveniences to severe hazards that threaten public safety or cause property damage. This section outlines the liabilities that accrue when such disruptions occur.\n\nUnder § 500.18, a mage is strictly liable for any ancillary arcane disruption resulting from their field operations (see § 500.12). The mere presence of an arcane cast at the site of a disruption shall suffice to establish liability unless the caster can demonstrate that the disruption was wholly unforeseeable and unavoidable (under § 700.11). Liability may be mitigated if the caster can prove that reasonable precautions were taken to prevent such disruptions or that the ancillary events were caused by an intervening act of nature, a third party, or another independent cause beyond their control.\n\nThe Autumnwood Accords Desk retains jurisdiction over all ancillary events (see § 700.12). In cases involving significant harm or widespread disruption, the Desk may order remediation protocols to mitigate the effects and restore affected areas to their previous state of safety (see § 500.14). The costs associated with such remediation shall be borne by the caster unless a third party is found responsible for contributing to the event's causation.\n\nFor ancillary events that result in casualties, liability extends beyond mere property damage or inconvenience. Casualties include both physical injuries and psychological harm (see § 500.17). The mage or their practice shall bear responsibility for all medical expenses incurred by individuals injured as a direct result of the disruption (under § 500.10). If the caster fails to provide adequate care, they may be subject to additional penalties or sanctions (see § 800.12).\n\nIn instances where an ancillary event leads to fatalities, the Desk shall determine whether negligence on the part of the caster contributed to the loss of life (under § 500.15). If found liable for such a death, the caster may be required to provide compensation to the deceased’s dependents or survivors. The amount of compensation is determined by the Desk based on factors including the nature and extent of the loss, duration of dependency, and other relevant considerations (see § 800.12).\n\nIt is further incumbent upon a mage to ensure that any ancillary events are promptly reported to the Autumnwood Accords Desk for review and record-keeping purposes (under § 500.7). Failure to comply with this requirement can result in increased liability and potential sanctions from the Guild's High Council.\n\nFurthermore, mages engaged in field operations must maintain a comprehensive log of their activities, including spells cast, materials used, and any ancillary events observed or experienced during those operations (see § 400.12). This log shall be made available to the Desk upon request for review and potential legal action.\n\nIn conclusion, liability for ancillary arcane disruptions is an integral part of responsible spellcasting. Mages must take every reasonable step to minimize such risks while being prepared to bear the consequences when they occur. The Autumnwood Accords provide a framework within which mages can both conduct their practice safely and ethically while remaining accountable for any unintended effects that arise from their work.\n\nUnder § 500.18, mages are not merely passive observers of these disruptions but active participants in mitigating their impact (see § 500.14). By adhering to these provisions, mages uphold the integrity and reputation of their profession while ensuring public safety remains a priority.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "500.18",
            "500.12",
            "700.11",
            "700.12",
            "500.14",
            "500.17",
