    {
      "cite": "1101.19",
      "part": "part_xi",
      "title": "Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Merging and Consequential Collateral Damage",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Merging and Consequential Collateral Damage",
          "text": "Under §1101.16, the remedial protocols concerning collateral contamination are well established; however, there remains a critical gap in addressing scenarios where unlawful casting has resulted in an unintended merging of magical artifacts or constructs, leading to unforeseen collateral damage. Section 1101.19 Additional Provisions aims to fill this gap by delineating specific protocols and obligations for mages involved in such mishaps.\n\nWhen an artifact or construct suffers damage due to unlawful merging during the casting process, it is deemed a form of collateral contamination governed under §1101.7 (Collateral Taints and Magical Contagion). This contamination can propagate magical effects that are both harmful and unpredictable, thereby imposing additional burdens on the affected parties.\n\nThe mage responsible for the unlawful casting bears primary liability under §1101.14 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations; however, under §500.11 Unsanctioned Field Operations and Liability, the mage must also adhere to safety protocols that include precautionary measures against such accidents. If an unintended merging occurs despite adherence to these protocols, remediation efforts are prioritized through §1101.16 Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Merging and Consequential Collateral Damage.\n\nThe mage is obligated under §1101.14 to mitigate the collateral damage resulting from such mergers by restoring or repairing the affected artifact or construct, removing any residual magical influences that may cause further harm (see §1101.13 Liability for Residue and Lingering Arcane Echoes), and ensuring that the artifact can no longer pose a risk to public safety.\n\nFurthermore, under §1101.8 Burden of Proof and Presumptions, it shall be presumed that any unintended merging resulting in collateral damage is due to the mage’s negligence unless proven otherwise by competent evidence. In instances where the mage cannot demonstrate adherence to §500.11 Unsanctioned Field Operations and Liability or failure to implement necessary safeguards, the burden of proof shifts to the mage.\n\nIn cases involving magical items that have been inadvertently merged with other artifacts or constructs, §700.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Litigation and Evidence provides guidelines for the admissibility and weight of such evidence in proceedings before the Autumnwood Accords Desk. The desk may consider expert testimony from mages specializing in artifact preservation or magical forensics to establish the extent of collateral contamination and the appropriate remedial measures.\n\nThe mage responsible for the unlawful casting shall also bear the cost of any necessary repairs, restoration, and containment efforts (see §1101.14). This includes the costs associated with neutralizing lingering arcane echoes that may persist due to the merging process (see §1101.13).\n\nIn instances where the collateral contamination cannot be fully remediated or poses an ongoing risk, the mage must seek authorization from the Autumnwood Accords Desk for the safe disposal of the affected artifact or construct (see §1101.14). The desk may order the mage to implement additional safeguards or restrictions on future casting activities to prevent recurrence of similar incidents.\n\nIn summary, under §1101.19 Additional Provisions, the responsibility for mitigating collateral damage from unlawful mergers lies with the responsible mage. This includes adherence to safety protocols, prompt remediation efforts, and compliance with any orders issued by the Autumnwood Accords Desk. The mage must also bear the financial burden of such remedial actions, ensuring that future incidents are avoided through stringent adherence to established guidelines.\n\nThese provisions are intended not only to protect public safety but also to foster a culture of responsibility among mages practicing arcane arts. By clarifying the obligations and liabilities associated with unlawful mergers, §1101.19 seeks to uphold the integrity of magical practice and enhance public trust in the Autumnwood Accords.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1101.16",
            "1101.7",
            "1101.14",
            "500.11",
            "1101.13",
            "1101.8",
