            "1101.19"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Remediation of Affected Constructs Following Unlawful Merging",
          "text": "In the administration of justice and the regulation of magical practice, the Autumnwood Accords have long sought to balance the freedom to innovate with the responsibility to uphold public safety (see § 1.4). The unlawful merging of magical constructs can result in unpredictable and often dangerous outcomes. Such mergers are not only a violation of the Mages' Guild's accords but also pose significant risks to the stability of the arcane environment. This section outlines remedial protocols designed to address and mitigate the consequences of such unlawful mergings, particularly when they involve constructs that have been destabilized or corrupted.\n\nWhen an unlawful merging occurs, it is critical to first assess the extent of the damage and contamination (see § 1101.14). The immediate response must be to isolate the affected construct from its environment to prevent further spread of contamination and escalation of risk (see § 1.15 for expedited remediation protocols). The next step is to conduct a thorough evaluation of the construct's integrity, focusing on any signs of destabilization or corruption that could lead to catastrophic failure. This assessment should be conducted by qualified mages with expertise in the specific arcane disciplines involved (see § 800.18 for remediation protocols and reporting obligations).\n\nOnce the extent of the damage is determined, the next protocol involves the application of counter-curses and containment spells designed to neutralize any residual effects of the unlawful merging. These spells should be tailored to address the specific nature of the contamination (see § 1101.18 for remedial protocols related to collateral contamination). The use of such spells must be guided by a detailed understanding of the construct's original design and function, as well as the exact conditions under which it was merged unlawfully.\n\nIn cases where the construct presents an immediate threat to public safety or stability, emergency measures may be necessary. These can include the disassembly or deconstruction of the construct into manageable components (see § 500.11 for unauthorized spellcasting in public spaces and liability). Such actions must be conducted with utmost care to avoid further contamination or escalation of risk.\n\nAfter stabilizing the construct, it is crucial to evaluate whether any parts can be salvaged for reuse. This assessment should take into account not only the physical integrity of the components but also their potential for future use in a controlled and regulated environment (see § 1101.2 Relics for considerations regarding relics). If certain parts are deemed safe for recovery, they must be placed under strict supervision until further analysis determines their stability and compatibility with other constructs.\n\nFinally, post-remediation protocols involve ongoing monitoring of the construct to ensure that no residual effects persist (see § 1101.16 for protocols concerning collateral contamination). This includes regular inspections and periodic checks by qualified mages to verify that the construct remains stable and free from any signs of instability or corruption. The construct should only be reintroduced into service after thorough validation through these processes, ensuring public safety and environmental stability.\n\nIn summary, the remediation process following an unlawful merging must prioritize both immediate containment and long-term stabilization. By adhering to these protocols, the Mages' Guild aims to ensure that such incidents do not compromise the integrity of the arcane environment or endanger the public (see § 1.17 for additional provisions related to mediation and peer review).",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1.4",
            "1101.14",
            "1.15",
            "800.18",
            "1101.18",
            "500.11",
