            "1.17"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Remediation of Affected Constructs Following Unlawful Merging — Continued",
          "text": "In the administration of justice under the auspices of The Autumnwood Accords, the remedial protocols outlined in this Part are intended to ensure that when magical constructs and artifacts undergo unlawful merging, the resulting collateral damage is addressed with due diligence. This section specifically addresses the remediation of affected constructs following such mergers, ensuring that both the integrity of magical constructs and the safety of the public remain paramount considerations.\n\nUnder § 1101.19 Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Merging and Consequential Collateral Damage, when a construct or artifact has undergone an unlawful merging due to negligent or unauthorized actions, it is incumbent upon the owner or responsible party to initiate remediation protocols forthwith. The process of remediation involves a comprehensive evaluation of the affected construct to determine its stability, functionality, and potential for further harm. This evaluation should be conducted by qualified mages who are knowledgeable in the specific arcane disciplines involved in the merging.\n\nThe first step in the remediation process is the containment of the affected construct within controlled environments. Containment ensures that any hazardous materials or unstable energies produced during the merging do not pose a threat to nearby individuals or constructs. The § 1101.14 Additional Provisions for Expedited Remediation and Immediate Response Protocols (see under § 1.15) mandate that such containment be initiated immediately following the discovery of an unlawful merging, regardless of the time of day or nature of the construct.\n\nOnce contained, a thorough inspection should be performed to identify any structural weaknesses or energy distortions resulting from the unlawful merging. The § 1101.17 Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Casting and Contamination (see under § 1.4) provides guidelines on how to approach such inspections, emphasizing the importance of avoiding further destabilization during the process. Where necessary, structural reinforcements or energy realignment must be undertaken to restore stability.\n\nIn some cases, the affected construct may require complete dismantling and reconstruction to ensure it functions safely and effectively. The § 1101.12 Collateral Contamination and Remedial Obligations (see under § 1.6) stipulates that such actions are permissible only when they do not exacerbate the existing conditions of contamination or instability. Dismantling should be carried out by skilled artisans, with all materials being subjected to further testing to ensure that no residual contaminants remain.\n\nFollowing successful remediation, a final inspection is conducted to verify that the construct is stable and functional as intended. This step ensures compliance with § 1101.16 Catchline Title: Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Merging and Consequential Collateral Damage, which mandates that all constructs must be returned to their original or improved state without causing additional damage. If any issues remain unresolved, the owner or responsible party is obligated to report them immediately to the Autumnwood Accords Desk.\n\nIn instances where the construct's condition cannot be restored through conventional means, alternative solutions may be required. These could include the use of advanced arcane technologies or the application of rare materials not typically found in standard constructs. The § 1101.18 Remedial Protocols for Unlawful Casting and Contamination (see under § 1.4) provides a framework within which such alternatives can be explored, emphasizing the importance of maintaining public safety throughout the process.\n\nUnder exceptional circumstances, where a construct poses an imminent threat to the public or other constructs, the Autumnwood Accords Desk may authorize its destruction if no viable remediation options are available. This decision is made in consultation with qualified experts and must be justified under § 1.15 Additional Provisions for Expedited Remediation and Immediate Response Protocols (see also § 1.8). Destruction of a construct should only occur as a last resort, with due consideration given to the potential risks involved.\n\nIn conclusion, the remediation protocols outlined in this section are designed to address the collateral damage resulting from unlawful mergers effectively and safely. By adhering to these guidelines, mages and their constructs can continue to operate within the bounds of The Autumnwood Accords, ensuring that both public safety and magical integrity remain paramount considerations.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1101.19",
            "1101.14",
            "1.15",
            "1101.17",
