    {
      "cite": "1001.20",
      "part": "part_x",
      "title": "Prohibition on Unlawful Alchemy and Counterfeiting in Magical Transactions",
      "kind": "related",
      "status": "filed",
      "body": [
        {
          "heading": "Prohibition on Unlawful Alchemy and Counterfeiting in Magical Transactions",
          "text": "In the practice of the arcane arts, it behooves all members of The Autumnwood Accords to adhere strictly to the laws governing the creation, manipulation, and exchange of magical artifacts. The prohibition against unlawful alchemy and counterfeiting is a cornerstone of these regulations, aimed at preserving the integrity of magical transactions and protecting the unsuspecting from fraud.\n\nUnder § 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk, all parties involved in a transaction must provide full and accurate disclosure regarding the nature and risks associated with any magical item being exchanged. This principle extends to the prevention of fraudulent practices such as alchemy that seeks to deceive through subterfuge or misrepresentation.\n\nAlchemical deceptions, whether in the form of counterfeit coins, potions, or artifacts, are strictly forbidden. § 1001.17 Prohibition on Magical Counterfeiting and Alchemical Debasement explicitly states that no individual may engage in any act of counterfeiting magical items or debasing their value for personal gain. This prohibition applies not only to the creation but also to the sale, trade, or distribution of such fraudulent goods.\n\nThe penalties for engaging in unlawful alchemy are severe and include fines levied by the Guild's Treasury, forfeiture of assets, and, in egregious cases, confiscation of magical items or even expulsion from The Autumnwood Accords. § 600.12 Additional Provisions Relating to Construct Labor and Associated Obligations further emphasizes that any construct involved in such unlawful activities shall be subject to immediate deactivation until the matter is resolved.\n\nIt is crucial for all members of the Mages' Guild to understand their obligations under these laws. Failure to comply can lead not only to personal liability but also to broader implications affecting the Guild as a whole. § 1001.6 Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets provides additional context on the proper handling of magical items, emphasizing that any alteration or manipulation must be done with the utmost care and in accordance with established protocols.\n\nMoreover, under § 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct, any unauthorized spellcasting that results in harm to others or disruption of public order is strictly prohibited. This includes practices that could inadvertently lead to the creation of counterfeit items through unintended side effects.\n\nThe Guild’s Office of Archmage Sovereign has the authority to oversee these matters, ensuring compliance and enforcing penalties as necessary. § 300.12 Prohibition on Magical Correspondence and Transmission mandates that any communication regarding such unlawful activities be reported promptly for investigation, further underscoring the importance of transparency and accountability.\n\nIn summary, members of The Autumnwood Accords must take utmost care to ensure that their practices adhere to these stringent standards. Any deviation from the rules not only risks personal legal repercussions but also damages the reputation of the Guild. Compliance with these regulations is paramount for maintaining the integrity and respectability of magical transactions within our community. See § 1001.7 Disclosure of Composition, Value, and Risk; § 1001.17 Prohibition on Magical Counterfeiting and Alchemical Debasement; § 500.12 Additional Provisions Regarding Arcane Field Operations and Conduct; § 600.12 Additional Provisions Relating to Construct Labor and Associated Obligations.",
          "key": "a",
          "refs": [
            "1001.7",
            "1001.17",
            "600.12",
            "1001.6",
            "500.12",
            "300.12"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibitions Against Illicit Substitution in Spellcraft",
          "text": "In the intricate tapestry of the Mages' Guild Accords, the prohibition against illicit substitution in spellcraft stands as a bulwark against the pernicious practice of substituting key components or ingredients required for genuine magical transactions. This prohibition is particularly relevant where such substitutions are made with the intent to deceive or defraud, thereby undermining the integrity and reliability that form the very foundation of magical commerce.\n\nUnder § 1001.20 Prohibition on Unlawful Alchemy and Counterfeiting in Magical Transactions, it has long been established that substitution of a genuine component for an inferior one during a spell-casting or alchemical procedure is inherently unlawful. The Accords are unequivocal: mages who engage in such practices risk not only the personal penalties outlined under § 800.1 Discipline but also the potential confiscation of their materials and equipment, as detailed in § 500.6 Destruction of Third-Party Property by Spell.\n\nThis prohibition extends to all forms of magical transactions where substitution might lead to a reduction in efficacy or potency, thereby compromising the intended outcome. For instance, if an apprentice mage were to substitute an inferior grade of dragon's blood for genuine dragon's blood during the crafting of an artifact, such an act would be considered unlawful and subject to the strictures outlined in § 1001.20. The intent behind the substitution is not merely a point of secondary concern; it is a critical determinant in the severity of any potential penalties.\n\nUnder § 300.12 Prohibition on Magical Correspondence and Transmission, mages are also prohibited from communicating information regarding illicit substitutions through any form of magical means. This further reinforces the principle that such practices must be conducted openly and transparently to maintain public trust and confidence in the integrity of magical commerce. Any mage found guilty of engaging in such activities or facilitating their commission may face sanctions under § 800.1 Discipline.\n\nThe prohibition against illicit substitution extends beyond mere commercial transactions, encompassing all forms of collaborative spell-casting where multiple mages are involved. § 1001.6 Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets underscores the importance of maintaining the authenticity and integrity of such gifts, particularly those that involve the transfer of components or materials between parties. The Accords explicitly prohibit any substitution that would lead to a reduction in the value or efficacy of the asset.\n\nMoreover, § 1001.8 Lead-to-Gold; Reporting Threshold provides additional context regarding the prohibition against illicit substitutions. Any attempt to substitute key components with lesser alternatives for financial gain constitutes a form of fraud and is subject to strict reporting requirements as outlined in this section. The Accords recognize that such practices not only undermine trust within the guild but also threaten the broader magical community.\n\nIn conclusion, the prohibition against illicit substitution in spellcraft serves to uphold the principles of integrity and transparency that are essential to the functioning of the Mages' Guild Accords. By prohibiting mages from engaging in such practices, whether for personal gain or otherwise, the Accords ensure a level playing field within the magical community and protect the public from potential harm. Any mage found guilty of such activities not only risks the loss of their license but also faces the possibility of expulsion from the guild, as explicitly stated under § 800.1 Discipline.\n\nUnder § 300.12 Prohibition on Magical Correspondence and Transmission, mages must ensure that all communications regarding legitimate transactions are conducted openly and without deceit. Any attempt to communicate information pertaining to illicit substitutions through any form of magical means is strictly prohibited, further emphasizing the need for transparency in all aspects of magical commerce.\n\nThis prohibition against illicit substitution applies equally to both novice and seasoned mages alike, serving as a reminder that even minor deviations from established practices can have significant consequences. The Accords emphasize the importance of adhering to these principles not only for personal integrity but also for the broader good of the community.",
          "key": "b",
          "refs": [
            "1001.20",
            "800.1",
            "500.6",
            "300.12",
            "1001.6",
            "1001.8"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Illicit Substitution in Spellcraft",
          "text": "In the realm of magical transactions, the integrity of casting and the purity of materials are paramount to ensuring that all spellwork adheres to the Accords and maintains the balance of magic within Elyria. The prohibition against illicit substitution in spellcraft is one such tenet, which aims to prevent the adulteration or substitution of components intended for a specific spell's efficacy. Under this section, mages are prohibited from altering the composition of ingredients or substituting materials in a manner that would render the resulting spell ineffective or harmful.\n\nThe prohibition against illicit substitution applies not only to base materials but also to enchanted items and components imbued with magical properties. For instance, the use of counterfeit runes, altered potions, or even the reconfiguration of known magical objects is strictly forbidden. To safeguard against such practices, mages are required to ensure that all components used in their spellcraft are authentic and unaltered according to § 1001.20. This includes the prohibition on using any substance that could be considered a counterfeit or a debased version of the intended component.\n\nUnder § 300.12, mages must not employ magical correspondence or transmission to communicate information related to the Unsanctioned Arts unless explicitly authorized in writing by the Office of Archmage Sovereign. This prohibition extends to all forms of communication that could potentially facilitate the illicit sharing of knowledge or materials related to forbidden practices. The intent is clear: to prevent the dissemination of dangerous and corrupting magic.\n\nThe Accords further stipulate that any mage found guilty of substituting a component in such a way as to alter its intended magical effect must face severe penalties, including a potential Seal of Silence under § 800.3. This penalty ensures that rogue mages are held accountable for their actions and that the integrity of magic is preserved.\n\nMoreover, it is incumbent upon all members of the Mages' Guild to report any instances they witness or become aware of through § 1001.16. Failure to do so could result in additional penalties, including a loss of standing within the guild and potential legal repercussions under § 800.2.\n\nIn instances where substitution is deemed unintentional but results in an altered spell effect, mages must rectify the situation as quickly as possible by neutralizing or replacing the affected components with authentic materials. This requirement is laid out in detail under § 1001.18, which provides specific guidelines for handling such situations to ensure that any harm caused by the illicit substitution can be mitigated.\n\nThe prohibition against illicit substitution underscores the importance of maintaining the sanctity and efficacy of magical transactions. It ensures that all practitioners adhere to the Accords and contribute to the preservation of balance in Elyria's weave of magic. By upholding this standard, mages protect not only themselves but also the broader community from the potential dangers posed by corrupted or debased spellcraft.\n\nGiven the complexity of magical interactions, it is essential for mages to be aware of the consequences of their actions and to comply fully with the provisions outlined in § 1001.20. The Accords are designed to foster a harmonious environment where all practitioners can engage in spellwork without fear of corrupting or destabilizing the weave. By adhering to these guidelines, mages uphold the integrity of magic and contribute to the maintenance of balance within Elyria's mystical ecosystem.",
          "key": "c",
          "refs": [
            "1001.20",
            "300.12",
            "800.3",
            "1001.16",
            "800.2",
            "1001.18"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Prohibitions Against Illicit Spell Component Manipulation",
          "text": "The Autumnwood Accords strictly prohibit the manipulation of spell components intended for use in sanctioned magical transactions, whether through alchemical means or other illicit methods. This prohibition is designed to ensure that all spells are cast with integrity and that no mage seeks to alter or substitute components in a manner that would compromise the efficacy or safety of the spell being performed.\n\nUnder § 1001.20 Prohibition on Unlawful Alchemy and Counterfeiting in Magical Transactions, it is explicitly stated that any substitution or alteration of an approved component—such as rare herbs, enchanted stones, or even animal parts—shall be deemed illicit if undertaken without the express permission of the Mages' Guild Accords Desk. The intent behind this rule is to maintain a standard of purity and authenticity in spellcraft, which is essential for both the safety of the caster and the recipient.\n\nThe prohibition against illicit substitution extends not only to the physical components themselves but also includes the processes used to prepare or alter them. Any mage attempting to adulterate or counterfeit an approved component by adding impurities, diluting its potency, or otherwise manipulating it in a manner that would render it unsafe for use is subject to severe penalties. This includes not just potions and elixirs but also more complex components such as enchanted dusts, oils, and even the very essence of magical creatures.\n\nIllicit substitution can have dire consequences. A spell crafted with tampered components may fail to achieve its intended effect or, worse yet, produce unexpected side effects that endanger all present. Such behavior is not only a violation of the Accords but also a breach of ethical conduct within the guild. The Accords Desk has the authority to confiscate any improperly prepared components and to revoke the license of any mage found guilty of such transgressions.\n\nFurthermore, under § 1001.17 Prohibition on Magical Counterfeiting and Alchemical Debasement, it is emphasized that the intrinsic nature of magical components must remain untouched. This means that any attempt to degrade or counterfeit an approved component by replacing genuine elements with inferior substitutes is strictly prohibited. For instance, if a spell requires an ingredient derived from the heart of a basilisk, substituting it with another substance—even one that superficially appears similar—would be considered illegal.\n\nThe Accords also recognize that some mages may seek to circumvent these regulations through more subtle means. Therefore, under § 1001.16 Confidentiality and Reporting Obligations for Mages' Guild Members, all members are required to report any instance of suspected illicit substitution immediately. This ensures that the Mages' Guild can swiftly address such cases and prevent further harm.\n\nIn practice, the Accords Desk conducts regular inspections and audits to ensure compliance with these provisions. Any mage found guilty of engaging in illicit substitution is subject to penalties ranging from a temporary suspension of casting privileges to permanent expulsion from the guild. In extreme cases, the Paradox Trial may be convened to determine if additional sanctions are warranted.\n\nIt is important for all members to understand that the integrity of spell components is paramount. The Accords strictly prohibit any unauthorized manipulation or substitution of these components. Any mage contemplating such actions should seek guidance and approval from the Mages' Guild Accords Desk before proceeding, as the risks involved far outweigh any potential benefits.\n\nUnder § 1001.6 Endowments and Gifts of Magical Assets, it is noted that any gift or transfer of magical assets must be conducted with due diligence to ensure they meet the highest standards of purity and authenticity. This extends to spell components, which should never be treated as commodities but rather as sacred elements necessary for the proper casting of spells.\n\nIn conclusion, the prohibition against illicit substitution in spellcraft is a cornerstone of the Autumnwood Accords, reflecting a commitment to maintaining the integrity and safety of magical transactions. Any mage engaging in such practices risks not only their professional standing within the guild but also the trust and respect of fellow mages. The Accords Desk remains vigilant in enforcing these regulations, ensuring that all spells are crafted with honesty and integrity.",
          "key": "d",
          "refs": [
            "1001.20",
            "1001.17",
            "1001.16",
            "1001.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Illicit Mixture and Contamination in Magical Components",
          "text": "In the complex interplay of magical transactions, the integrity of components is paramount to ensuring that spells and enchantments function as intended. The prohibition against illicit mixture and contamination in magical components seeks to safeguard this integrity by preventing any form of adulteration or substitution that could undermine the efficacy or safety of a spell. This prohibition extends not only to physical alchemical concoctions but also to the very essence of mystical components, which are integral to the casting process.\n\nThe Accords define illicit mixture as the unauthorized alteration of the properties of a magical component through the introduction of foreign substances or elements that do not belong in the original composition. This can occur during the preparation phase of an alchemical process or even after the component has been sealed into a spell formula. An example of such contamination would be adding lead to the components used for creating a potion that was designed specifically for purifying gold. Such actions are strictly prohibited under § 1001.20, as they not only compromise the effectiveness of the spell but also risk endangering the caster and those around them.\n\nContamination can take various forms, including the introduction of harmful or incompatible elements into a potion, the adulteration of ingredients in an enchanted weapon, or the substitution of components with properties that are diametrically opposed to their intended function. For instance, mixing a stabilizing agent meant for fire spells with a component designed for water-based enchantments would result in unpredictable and potentially dangerous outcomes. Such practices not only violate the foundational principles established in § 1001.8 regarding the integrity of magical materials but also contravene the broader ethical standards set forth by the Accords.\n\nUnder § 300.12, mages are expressly forbidden from employing any form of magical correspondence or transmission to convey information pertaining to illicit mixture or contamination. This prohibition is in place to prevent the dissemination of harmful or misleading practices that could proliferate within the guild and beyond. Only authorized communications regarding such violations may be transmitted under specific conditions outlined by the Office of Archmage Sovereign.\n\nThe Accords further specify that any mage found guilty of engaging in illicit mixture or contamination shall face severe penalties, as detailed in § 800.1 Discipline; Range of Sanctions. The punishment for such offenses can range from a formal reprimand to a lifetime ban from the guild and, in extreme cases, the imposition of a Seal of Silence, as described in § 800.3 Seal of Silence; Duration and Review.\n\nThe prohibition against illicit mixture and contamination underscores the importance of maintaining purity and authenticity within the realm of magical components. This safeguard is not merely an administrative measure but a fundamental aspect of ensuring that magic remains a force for good and not for malevolent or chaotic purposes. The Accords emphasize that any deviation from the intended properties of a component can lead to unintended consequences, both in terms of the spell's effectiveness and the safety of those involved.\n\nFurthermore, the Accords highlight the interconnectedness between the integrity of magical components and the broader ethical standards of the guild. Any form of contamination or adulteration is not only a breach of trust but also an affront to the sanctity of magic itself. The prohibition on such practices reflects the guild's commitment to upholding the highest standards of conduct and responsibility among its members.\n\nIn conclusion, the prohibition against illicit mixture and contamination in magical components serves as a critical safeguard for the integrity and efficacy of spells and enchantments within the Mages' Guild. It reinforces the ethical responsibilities of all mages, ensuring that magic is used responsibly and with the utmost respect for its inherent power. This prohibition is not merely regulatory but reflects the guild's broader mission to uphold the balance and harmony that define the practice of magic.",
          "key": "e",
          "refs": [
            "1001.20",
            "1001.8",
            "300.12",
            "800.1",
            "800.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Illicit Alteration of Spell Effects Through Mixture",
          "text": "Under the auspices of the Autumnwood Accords, it is unequivocally proscribed that any mage, conservator or innovator alike, shall not tamper with the effects of a spell by introducing illicit mixtures. The prohibition against such unlawful practices is rooted in both § 1001.20 and § 1001.17, which collectively aim to safeguard the integrity and sanctity of magical transactions.\n\nIllicit alteration refers to the practice of blending components or substances with spells that are not sanctioned by the Mages' Guild. Such practices are inherently pernicious as they can disrupt the balance of a spell’s intended effect. The Accords specifically forbid the adulteration of any component of a spell, whether it be an arcane ingredient, a focus object, or a verbal incantation. This prohibition extends to all forms of magical transactions, ensuring that spells are cast and performed in their original, unadulterated form.\n\nIllicit alteration poses several hazards. First, it can lead to unpredictable outcomes, as the intended properties of the spell may not be realized. This unpredictability can cause harm not only to those who commission the spell but also to unintended bystanders. The Accords recognize that spells are tools of immense power and consequence; therefore, any deviation from their original design constitutes a breach of trust between the mage and the client.\n\nSecondly, illicit alteration undermines the Guild's authority and the public's confidence in magical practices. When mages engage in such practices, it reflects poorly on the entire profession. The Guild is charged with maintaining standards that ensure the safety and efficacy of all spells, and any deviation from these standards can have far-reaching implications for the public. Therefore, under § 1001.20, any mage found guilty of altering a spell's effects in an illicit manner will be subject to severe penalties.\n\nFurthermore, § 300.12, which governs magical correspondence and transmission, reinforces this prohibition by stipulating that no communication utilizing magical means shall convey information regarding such practices. This ensures that mages do not share or disseminate the methods of illicit alteration, thus safeguarding the Guild's authority and integrity.\n\nThe Accords also impose stringent reporting obligations on mages who are aware of any instances of illicit alteration. Under § 1001.16, any member of the Mages' Guild is required to report such activities, ensuring that all violations are brought to light promptly. Failure to do so will result in penalties under § 800.3, which details the consequences for failing to comply with confidentiality and reporting obligations.\n\nIn instances where a mage has been found guilty of altering spells through illicit mixtures, the Paradox Trial (§ 800.1) shall determine an appropriate penalty. The trial ensures that justice is served, taking into account not only the severity of the offense but also any extenuating circumstances. Penalties may include sanctions and seals, as well as a ban on engaging in certain types of spells or magical transactions.\n\nThe Guild's commitment to maintaining the integrity of its practices is paramount. Illicit alteration of spell effects through mixture is thus strictly prohibited. Mages are advised to adhere to the Accords’ stipulations and to uphold the highest standards of professionalism and ethical conduct. Failure to do so will not only result in legal ramifications but also erode public trust, which is critical for the Guild’s continued success.\n\nIn conclusion, § 1001.20 and its sister provisions underscore the importance of maintaining the integrity of magical transactions. Illicit alteration of spell effects through mixture jeopardizes both the safety of those involved and the reputation of the Mages' Guild. Therefore, all mages are advised to exercise utmost caution and adhere strictly to the Accords’ stipulations to uphold the highest standards of professionalism and ethical conduct.",
          "key": "f",
          "refs": [
            "1001.20",
            "1001.17",
            "300.12",
            "1001.16",
            "800.3",
            "800.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "brief": "Prohibition on Unlawful Alchemy and Counterfeiting in Magical Transactions",
